- **RISC** designs favour fixed-length, few, simple instructions with load/store memory access; **CISC** has variable-length, many, complex instructions.', 2, 0),
  ('gate-computer-organization-computer-arithmetic-m01', 'gate-computer-organization-computer-arithmetic', 'Computer Arithmetic', '## Integers
Signed integers use **two''s complement**: negate by inverting all bits and adding 1. Addition and subtraction use the same adder hardware. A **half adder** adds two bits; a **full adder** also takes a carry-in. **Overflow** occurs when the result exceeds the representable range (e.g., two positives giving a negative).

## Multiplication
**Booth''s algorithm** efficiently multiplies signed numbers in two''s complement by examining pairs of bits.

## Floating point (IEEE 754)
A floating-point number is stored as **sign · exponent · mantissa (fraction)**:
| Format | Bits | Sign | Exponent | Mantissa |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Single** | 32 | 1 | 8 | 23 |
| **Double** | 64 | 1 | 11 | 52 |

The exponent uses a **bias** (127 for single, 1023 for double), and the mantissa has an implicit leading 1 (normalised form).', 2, 0),
  ('gate-discrete-math-lattices-boolean-algebra-m01', 'gate-discrete-math-lattices-boolean-algebra', 'Lattices & Boolean Algebra', '## Posets and lattices
A **partially ordered set (poset)** has a relation that is reflexive, antisymmetric and transitive, often drawn as a **Hasse diagram**.

A **lattice** is a poset in which **every pair of elements** has:
- a **least upper bound (LUB / join, ∨)**, and
- a **greatest lower bound (GLB / meet, ∧)**.

A **bounded lattice** has a greatest element **1** and least element **0**.

## Boolean algebra
A **Boolean algebra** is a **complemented, distributive lattice**. It satisfies:
- **Commutative, associative, distributive** laws,
- **Identity** (a ∨ 0 = a, a ∧ 1 = a),
- **Complement** (a ∨ a'' = 1, a ∧ a'' = 0), and
- **De Morgan''s laws**: (a ∨ b)'' = a'' ∧ b''.

Digital logic is built directly on Boolean algebra.', 2, 0),
  ('gate-discrete-math-number-theory-modular-m01', 'gate-discrete-math-number-theory-modular', 'Number Theory & Modular Arithmetic', '## Divisibility, GCD and LCM
The **greatest common divisor** is found by **Euclid''s algorithm**: gcd(a, b) = gcd(b, a mod b). For any two positive integers, **gcd(a,b) × lcm(a,b) = a × b**.

## Modular arithmetic
Working ''mod n'' keeps only remainders:
```
(a + b) mod n = ((a mod n) + (b mod n)) mod n
(a × b) mod n = ((a mod n) × (b mod n)) mod n
```
**a ≡ b (mod n)** means n divides (a − b) — a **congruence**.

## Key results
- A **prime** has exactly two divisors, 1 and itself.
- **Fermat''s little theorem**: if p is prime and gcd(a, p) = 1, then **aᵖ⁻¹ ≡ 1 (mod p)**.
- **Euler''s totient φ(n)** counts integers ≤ n that are coprime to n; for a prime p, φ(p) = p − 1.
These underpin cryptography (e.g., RSA).', 2, 0),
  ('gate-programming-ds-trees-bst-m01', 'gate-programming-ds-trees-bst', 'Trees & Binary Search Trees', 'A **tree** is a connected acyclic graph with a **root**; nodes have children, and leaves have none. A **binary tree** has at most two children per node.

## Binary search tree (BST)
A BST keeps, for every node, **left subtree keys < node < right subtree keys**. Search, insert and delete take **O(h)** time, where h is the height — **O(log n)** if balanced, but **O(n)** in the worst (skewed) case.

## Balanced trees
- **AVL tree**: height-balanced; the balance factor (height difference of subtrees) is kept in {−1, 0, +1} using rotations.
- **Red-black tree**: balanced via colouring rules, guaranteeing O(log n) height.

## Traversals
| Traversal | Order |
| --- | --- |
| **In-order** | Left, Root, Right → **sorted** for a BST |
| **Pre-order** | Root, Left, Right |
| **Post-order** | Left, Right, Root |
| **Level-order** | Breadth-first (uses a queue) |', 2, 0),
  ('gate-programming-ds-searching-sorting-m01', 'gate-programming-ds-searching-sorting', 'Searching & Sorting Algorithms', '## Searching
- **Linear search** — O(n), works on any list.
- **Binary search** — O(log n), but requires a **sorted** array.

## Sorting complexities
| Algorithm | Best | Average | Worst | Stable? |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Bubble / Insertion** | O(n) | O(n²) | O(n²) | Yes |
| **Selection** | O(n²) | O(n²) | O(n²) | No |
| **Merge Sort** | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | Yes |
| **Quick Sort** | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | **O(n²)** | No |
| **Heap Sort** | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | No |

- A **stable** sort keeps equal keys in their original order; an **in-place** sort uses O(1) extra space.
- Any **comparison-based** sort needs at least **Ω(n log n)** comparisons.
- **Counting** and **radix** sort are non-comparison sorts that can run in **O(n)** for suitable integer keys.', 2, 0),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-reading-comprehension-m01', 'gate-general-aptitude-reading-comprehension', 'Reading Comprehension', '**Reading comprehension** tests whether you can understand a passage and answer questions **from the text itself**, not from outside knowledge.

## What questions ask
- **Main idea** — the central point the author is making.
- **Inference** — a conclusion the passage supports but does not state directly.
- **Vocabulary in context** — the meaning of a word as used in the passage.
- **Tone / attitude** — the author''s stance (e.g., critical, neutral, enthusiastic).
- **Supporting detail** — a specific fact that backs up the main idea.

## Strategy
1. Skim for the **main idea** first, then read closely.
2. Answer strictly from **what is written** — avoid bringing in your own assumptions.
3. For ''inference'' questions, choose the option that **must** follow from the text.
4. Watch **transition words** (however, therefore, although) — they signal contrast, cause, or concession.', 1, 0),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-mensuration-geometry-m01', 'gate-general-aptitude-mensuration-geometry', 'Mensuration & Geometry', '**Mensuration** computes lengths, areas and volumes of shapes.

## 2D shapes
| Shape | Area | Perimeter |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Rectangle** | l × b | 2(l + b) |
| **Square** | a² | 4a |
| **Triangle** | ½ × base × height | sum of sides |
| **Circle** | πr² | 2πr (circumference) |

## 3D solids
| Solid | Volume | Surface area |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Cube** | a³ | 6a² |
| **Cuboid** | l × b × h | 2(lb + bh + hl) |
| **Cylinder** | πr²h | 2πr(r + h) |
| **Sphere** | (4/3)πr³ | 4πr² |

## Geometry facts
- Interior angles of a **triangle** sum to **180°**; of a **quadrilateral**, **360°**.
- The angle in a **semicircle** is **90°**. Use **π ≈ 22/7** unless told otherwise.', 2, 0),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-spatial-aptitude-m01', 'gate-general-aptitude-spatial-aptitude', 'Analytical & Spatial Aptitude', '**Analytical aptitude** tests logical deduction; **spatial aptitude** tests reasoning about shapes and space.

## Analytical (deductive) reasoning
- **Transitivity**: if A > B and B > C, then A > C.
- **Syllogism**: All A are B, all B are C ⇒ all A are C.
- **Modus ponens**: if P then Q; P is true ⇒ Q is true.
- A universal negative converts: ''No A is B'' ⇒ ''No B is A''.

## Spatial reasoning
- A **cube** has **6 faces, 12 edges, 8 vertices**; a **cross-shaped net** of six squares folds into a cube.
- A **plane mirror** produces **left-right (lateral) inversion**; a **water reflection** inverts **top-to-bottom**.
- **Rotations**: a 90° turn is a quarter turn; a 180° turn is a half turn.
- **Cube cutting**: an n×n×n painted cube has 8 corner pieces (3 painted faces), 12(n−2) edge pieces (2 faces), 6(n−2)² face pieces (1 face), and (n−2)³ hidden pieces (0 faces).', 2, 0),
  ('gate-computer-organization-sequential-circuits-m01', 'gate-computer-organization-sequential-circuits', 'Sequential Circuits & Registers', 'A **combinational circuit''s** output depends only on the **current inputs** (e.g. adders, multiplexers). A **sequential circuit''s** output depends on the current inputs **and** the stored past — it has **memory**.

## Flip-flops (1-bit memory)
A **flip-flop** stores exactly **one bit** and changes state on a **clock edge**.
| Type | Behaviour |
| --- | --- |
| **SR** | Set/Reset; input S=R=1 is **forbidden** (NOR latch) |
| **D** | Output Q **follows** the D input on each clock |
| **JK** | Like SR but J=K=1 **toggles** (no forbidden state) |
| **T** | T=1 **toggles**, T=0 holds |

A **latch** is **level-triggered** (transparent while enable is high); a **flip-flop** is **edge-triggered** (changes only at the clock edge).

## Registers & counters
- A **register** is a group of flip-flops storing a multi-bit word — **n flip-flops store n bits**.
- A **shift register** moves data one bit per clock pulse.
- A **counter** cycles through states; a **mod-m** counter needs ⌈log₂m⌉ flip-flops.
- **n flip-flops** can represent **2ⁿ distinct states**, so a mod-8 counter needs **3** flip-flops.', 2, 0),
  ('gate-computer-organization-memory-organization-m01', 'gate-computer-organization-memory-organization', 'Memory Organization & Storage', 'Memory is organised as a **hierarchy** ordered by speed and cost: **registers → cache → main memory (RAM) → secondary storage (disk/SSD)**. Faster memory sits closer to the CPU and costs more per bit.

## Semiconductor memory
| Type | Volatile? | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **SRAM** | Yes | Fast, uses flip-flops, **no refresh** — used for cache |
| **DRAM** | Yes | One **capacitor + transistor** per bit, needs **refresh**, dense — used for main memory |
| **ROM / PROM / EPROM / EEPROM / Flash** | No | Non-volatile; **EEPROM/Flash** erase **electrically** |

## Addressing
With **n address lines** the CPU can address **2ⁿ locations**. So **1 KB = 1024 bytes** needs **10** address lines (2¹⁰ = 1024).

## Secondary storage & RAID
- A **magnetic disk** stores data on concentric **tracks**, each split into **sectors**.
- **RAID 0** = **striping** (speed, no redundancy); **RAID 1** = **mirroring** (full redundancy); **RAID 5** = block striping with **distributed parity** (survives one disk failure).', 2, 0),
  ('gate-discrete-math-functions-m01', 'gate-discrete-math-functions', 'Functions & Mappings', 'A **function** f: A → B assigns to **every** element of the domain A **exactly one** element of the codomain B.

## Types of function
- **Injective (one-to-one)**: distinct inputs give distinct outputs — f(x₁)=f(x₂) ⇒ x₁=x₂.
- **Surjective (onto)**: every element of B is hit by some input.
- **Bijective**: both injective **and** surjective. A function has an **inverse iff it is bijective**.

## Counting functions
| Count | Formula |
| --- | --- |
| Functions A→B, \|A\|=m, \|B\|=n | **nᵐ** |
| Injections A→B, m ≤ n | n·(n−1)···(n−m+1) |
| Bijections on an n-set | **n!** |

## Composition & pigeonhole
- **Composition**: (g∘f)(x) = **g(f(x))** — apply f first, then g.
- The **identity function** maps each element to **itself**.
- **Pigeonhole principle**: placing **n+1** items into **n** boxes forces at least one box to hold **≥ 2** items (so among 13 people, two share a birth month).', 2, 0),
  ('gate-discrete-math-proof-techniques-m01', 'gate-discrete-math-proof-techniques', 'Proof Techniques & Induction', 'A **proof** is a logically valid argument establishing a statement''s truth.

## Direct & indirect proofs
- **Direct proof**: assume the hypothesis P, derive the conclusion Q.
- **Contrapositive**: prove "if ¬Q then ¬P" — logically equivalent to "if P then Q".
- **Proof by contradiction**: assume the statement is **false** and derive an absurdity (e.g. **√2 is irrational** is proved this way).
- **Counterexample**: a **single** case disproves a universal claim — "all primes are odd" is refuted by **2**.

## Mathematical induction
To prove P(n) holds for all n ≥ n₀:
1. **Base case** — verify P(n₀) (usually P(1)).
2. **Inductive step** — assume P(k) (the *hypothesis*) and prove **P(k+1)**.

**Strong induction** assumes P holds for **all** values up to k, not just k, when proving P(k+1). The identity **1+2+···+n = n(n+1)/2** is a classic induction exercise.', 2, 0),
  ('gate-core-cs-process-scheduling-synchronization-m01', 'gate-core-cs-process-scheduling-synchronization', 'Process Scheduling & Synchronization', '**CPU scheduling** decides which ready process runs next. GATE tests you on computing **waiting time** and **turnaround time**.

## Key formulas
- **Turnaround time (TAT)** = Completion − Arrival.
- **Waiting time (WT)** = Turnaround − Burst.
- **Response time** = first CPU time − Arrival.

## Scheduling algorithms
| Algorithm | Idea | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **FCFS** | first come, first served | non-preemptive; **convoy effect** |
| **SJF** | shortest job first | **optimal** average WT; needs burst prediction |
| **SRTF** | preemptive SJF | shortest remaining time |
| **Round Robin** | fixed time quantum | fair; high context-switch overhead |
| **Priority** | highest priority first | risk of **starvation** → fix with **aging** |

## Synchronization
- **Critical section** must satisfy **mutual exclusion, progress, bounded waiting**.
- A **semaphore** is an integer with atomic **wait (P)** and **signal (V)**. A **binary semaphore / mutex** allows one process in.
- The **producer–consumer, readers–writers and dining-philosophers** problems are classic synchronization exercises.', 2, 0),
  ('gate-core-cs-deadlocks-memory-management-m01', 'gate-core-cs-deadlocks-memory-management', 'Deadlocks & Memory Management', '## Deadlock
A **deadlock** needs **all four Coffman conditions** to hold at once:
1. **Mutual exclusion**, 2. **Hold and wait**, 3. **No preemption**, 4. **Circular wait**.

- **Prevention** breaks one condition; **avoidance** uses the **Banker''s algorithm** to stay in a **safe state**.
- A cycle in the **resource-allocation graph** implies deadlock **only** when each resource has a single instance.

## Memory management
- **Paging** splits memory into fixed **frames** and logical memory into equal **pages** — no external fragmentation, but internal fragmentation.
- A **logical address** = (page number, offset); the **page table** maps page → frame.
- **Segmentation** uses variable-size logical segments.

## Page replacement
On a page fault the OS evicts a frame using **FIFO**, **LRU** or **Optimal**.
- **Belady''s anomaly**: more frames can cause **more** faults — possible under **FIFO**, never under LRU or Optimal.
- **Effective access time** = (1 − p)·mem + p·page-fault-time, where p is the fault rate.', 2, 0),
  ('gate-core-cs-database-normalization-transactions-m01', 'gate-core-cs-database-normalization-transactions', 'Normalization & Transactions', '## Normal forms
Normalization removes redundancy using **functional dependencies (FDs)**.
| Form | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| **1NF** | atomic (indivisible) attribute values |
| **2NF** | 1NF + no **partial** dependency on part of a candidate key |
| **3NF** | 2NF + no **transitive** dependency of non-prime attributes |
| **BCNF** | every FD''s left side is a **superkey** |

A **candidate key** minimally determines all attributes; a **prime attribute** belongs to some candidate key.

## Transactions — ACID
**Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability.**

- A **schedule** is **conflict-serializable** if its **precedence graph is acyclic**.
- **Two-Phase Locking (2PL)** guarantees serializability; **strict 2PL** also avoids cascading rollbacks.
- Conflicting operations = two operations on the **same item** where at least one is a **write**.', 2, 0),
  ('gate-core-cs-tcp-ip-subnetting-m01', 'gate-core-cs-tcp-ip-subnetting', 'TCP/IP & Subnetting', '## The stack
The **TCP/IP model** has 4 layers; the **OSI model** has 7. Key protocols by layer:
- **Application** — HTTP, DNS, SMTP, FTP.
- **Transport** — **TCP** (reliable, connection-oriented) and **UDP** (unreliable, connectionless).
- **Network** — **IP**, routing, ICMP.
- **Link** — Ethernet, ARP, MAC.

## TCP vs UDP
- TCP: 3-way handshake (**SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK**), flow control (sliding window), congestion control.
- UDP: no handshake, lower overhead — used for DNS, streaming, VoIP.

## Subnetting
An IPv4 address is **32 bits**. A **/n prefix** fixes the first n network bits.
- **Hosts per subnet** = 2^(32−n) − 2 (subtract network + broadcast address).
- Example: **/24** → 2⁸ − 2 = **254** usable hosts; **/26** → 2⁶ − 2 = **62** hosts.
- The **subnet mask** for /26 is 255.255.255.192.', 2, 0),
  ('gate-core-cs-regular-languages-finite-automata-m01', 'gate-core-cs-regular-languages-finite-automata', 'Regular Languages & Finite Automata', '## Finite automata
A **DFA** has exactly one transition per (state, symbol); an **NFA** may have many or **ε-moves**. Both recognise exactly the **regular languages** — every NFA has an equivalent DFA (subset construction), so they are equal in power.

- A DFA is a 5-tuple (Q, Σ, δ, q₀, F).
- Converting an n-state NFA to a DFA can need up to **2ⁿ** states.

## Regular languages
Regular languages are **closed** under union, concatenation, star, intersection and complement.

- **Regular expressions**, DFAs, NFAs and regular grammars all describe the same class.
- The **pumping lemma** proves a language is **not** regular — e.g. { aⁿbⁿ } is not regular because it needs unbounded counting.
- **Myhill–Nerode**: a language is regular iff it has **finitely many** distinguishable classes; this also gives the **minimum DFA**.', 2, 0),
  ('gate-core-cs-context-free-grammars-parsing-m01', 'gate-core-cs-context-free-grammars-parsing', 'Context-Free Grammars & Parsing', '## Context-free grammars
A **CFG** has productions with a **single non-terminal** on the left. CFGs generate the **context-free languages**, recognised by **pushdown automata (PDA)** — the stack supplies the memory a finite automaton lacks (so { aⁿbⁿ } **is** context-free).

- A grammar is **ambiguous** if some string has two distinct parse trees / leftmost derivations.
- CFLs are closed under union, concatenation and star, but **not** under intersection or complement.

## Parsing
- **Top-down (LL)** parsers build the tree from the root; they need a grammar free of **left recursion** and require **left factoring**.
- **Bottom-up (LR/SLR/LALR)** parsers are more powerful and handle a larger grammar class.
- **FIRST** and **FOLLOW** sets drive predictive-parser table construction.
- A **deterministic PDA** is strictly weaker than a general (non-deterministic) PDA.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-ethics-code-of-ethics-standards-m01', 'cfa-ethics-code-of-ethics-standards', 'Code of Ethics & Standards', 'The **Code of Ethics** and the **Standards of Professional Conduct** are the ethical backbone of the CFA Program and the most heavily weighted topic at every level.

## The Seven Standards
| # | Standard | Core idea |
| --- | --- | --- |
| I | Professionalism | Knowledge of law, independence, no misrepresentation |
| II | Integrity of Capital Markets | No material nonpublic information, no manipulation |
| III | Duties to Clients | Loyalty, fair dealing, suitability, performance, confidentiality |
| IV | Duties to Employers | Loyalty, no improper compensation, supervision |
| V | Investment Analysis | Diligence, communication, record retention |
| VI | Conflicts of Interest | Disclosure, priority of transactions, referral fees |
| VII | Responsibilities as a Member | No conduct compromising the CFA designation |

## Key Principles
- **Loyalty, prudence, and care** to clients require placing **client interests before your own** and the employer''s.
- Under **Standard II(A)**, members must not act or cause others to act on **material nonpublic information**.
- **Standard III(B) Fair Dealing** requires treating all clients fairly when disseminating recommendations; it does not require identical treatment.
- The **Mosaic Theory** lets analysts combine public and **nonmaterial nonpublic** information into a recommendation without violating insider rules.

### When Laws Conflict
- Members must follow the **stricter** of the applicable law or the Code and Standards.

### Tip
- When in doubt, the more conservative action that protects client interests and market integrity is almost always the compliant answer.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-time-value-of-money-m01', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-time-value-of-money', 'Time Value of Money', 'The **time value of money (TVM)** captures the core idea that a dollar today is worth more than a dollar tomorrow because it can earn a return.

## Core Formulas
The future value of a single sum compounds at rate r over n periods:

```
FV = PV * (1 + r)^n
PV = FV / (1 + r)^n
```

- **PV** is present value, **FV** is future value, **r** is the periodic rate, **n** is the number of periods.

## Annuities
- An **ordinary annuity** pays at the **end** of each period; an **annuity due** pays at the **beginning**.
- An annuity due is worth more: multiply the ordinary annuity value by **(1 + r)**.
- A **perpetuity** pays forever: PV = PMT / r.

## Compounding Frequency
With m compounding periods per year and stated rate r:

```
EAR = (1 + r/m)^m - 1
```

| Frequency | m |
| --- | --- |
| Annual | 1 |
| Semiannual | 2 |
| Quarterly | 4 |
| Monthly | 12 |

- More frequent compounding raises the **effective annual rate (EAR)** above the stated rate.
- **Continuous compounding** gives FV = PV * e^(r*n).

### Tip
- Always match the rate and the number of periods to the same frequency before computing; a mismatch is the most common TVM error.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-economics-market-structures-m01', 'cfa-economics-market-structures', 'Market Structures', '**Market structure** describes how the number of firms, product differentiation, and barriers to entry shape pricing power and long-run profits.

## The Four Structures
| Structure | Sellers | Barriers | Pricing power |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Perfect competition | Many | None | Price taker |
| Monopolistic competition | Many | Low | Some |
| Oligopoly | Few | High | Considerable |
| Monopoly | One | Very high | Greatest |

## Perfect Competition
- Firms are **price takers**; the demand curve facing a single firm is **perfectly elastic** (horizontal).
- In the long run, economic profit is driven to **zero** because entry is free.
- Profit is maximized where **marginal revenue equals marginal cost (MR = MC)**.

## Monopoly
- A single seller faces the **downward-sloping market demand** curve and sets MR = MC, then charges the price the demand curve allows.
- Sources of monopoly include **economies of scale**, patents, control of resources, and government licensing.

## Measuring Concentration
- The **Herfindahl-Hirschman Index (HHI)** sums the squared market shares of all firms; higher HHI means more concentration.
- The **N-firm concentration ratio** adds the shares of the largest N firms but ignores mergers among leaders.

### Tip
- In every structure a profit-maximizing firm produces where MR = MC; what differs is the shape of the demand curve it faces.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-financial-ratios-analysis-m01', 'cfa-financial-reporting-financial-ratios-analysis', 'Financial Ratios & Analysis', '**Ratio analysis** converts financial statements into comparable measures of liquidity, solvency, efficiency, and profitability.

## Liquidity Ratios
- **Current ratio** = current assets / current liabilities.
- **Quick ratio** = (cash + marketable securities + receivables) / current liabilities; it excludes inventory.

## Solvency Ratios
```
Debt-to-equity = Total debt / Total equity
Interest coverage = EBIT / Interest expense
```

## Profitability Ratios
| Ratio | Formula |
| --- | --- |
| Gross margin | Gross profit / Revenue |
| Net margin | Net income / Revenue |
| Return on assets (ROA) | Net income / Total assets |
| Return on equity (ROE) | Net income / Equity |

## DuPont Analysis
The three-part **DuPont** decomposition breaks ROE into drivers:

```
ROE = Net margin * Asset turnover * Financial leverage
```

- **Net margin** measures profitability, **asset turnover** measures efficiency, and **financial leverage** (assets / equity) measures the use of debt.
- A rising ROE driven mainly by leverage signals more risk, not better operations.

### Activity Ratios
- **Inventory turnover** = COGS / average inventory; higher turnover means inventory sells faster.
- **Receivables turnover** = revenue / average receivables.

### Tip
- Always compare a ratio against the company''s history and industry peers; a single number in isolation tells you little.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital-m01', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital', 'Cost of Capital', 'The **cost of capital** is the required return a firm must earn to satisfy its providers of capital, and it serves as the **discount rate** for evaluating projects.

## Weighted Average Cost of Capital
The **WACC** weights each source by its market-value proportion:

```
WACC = (E/V)*Re + (D/V)*Rd*(1 - t)
```

- **E** is equity value, **D** is debt value, **V = E + D**, **Re** is cost of equity, **Rd** is cost of debt, and **t** is the marginal tax rate.
- Interest on debt is **tax-deductible**, so the after-tax cost of debt is **Rd * (1 - t)**.

## Cost of Equity
The most common estimate uses the **CAPM**:

```
Re = Rf + beta * (Rm - Rf)
```

| Input | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| Rf | Risk-free rate |
| beta | Sensitivity to market risk |
| Rm - Rf | Equity risk premium |

- An alternative for dividend-paying firms is the **dividend discount model**: Re = D1 / P0 + g.

## Using WACC Correctly
- Use **target** (not historical) capital structure weights and **market** values, not book values.
- Apply WACC only to projects with risk similar to the firm''s average; riskier projects need a higher discount rate.

### Tip
- Forgetting the (1 - t) tax shield on debt is the single most common cost-of-capital mistake.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-equity-equity-valuation-ddm-m01', 'cfa-equity-equity-valuation-ddm', 'Equity Valuation: DDM', 'The **dividend discount model (DDM)** values a stock as the present value of all expected future dividends, discounted at the required return on equity.

## Gordon Growth Model
For a dividend growing at a constant rate g forever:

```
V0 = D1 / (r - g)
```

- **D1** is next year''s dividend (D0 * (1 + g)), **r** is the required return, and **g** is the constant growth rate.
- The model requires **r greater than g**; otherwise the value is undefined or negative.

## Sustainable Growth
The growth rate a firm can sustain from retained earnings:

```
g = Retention ratio * ROE = b * ROE
```

- **b** is the retention ratio (1 minus the dividend payout ratio).

## Multistage DDM
| Stage | Assumption |
| --- | --- |
| High growth | Above-normal g for n years |
| Transition | g declines toward maturity |
| Terminal | Constant g via Gordon model |

- The **terminal value** is found with the Gordon model at the start of the stable phase, then discounted back to today.

## Strengths and Limits
- DDM works best for **stable, dividend-paying** firms with predictable payout policies.
- It is unreliable for firms that pay **no dividends** or have growth above the discount rate; use free-cash-flow models instead.

### Tip
- Sensitivity to (r - g) is extreme: small changes in either input swing the Gordon value sharply, so stress-test your assumptions.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-duration-convexity-m01', 'cfa-fixed-income-duration-convexity', 'Duration & Convexity', '**Duration** and **convexity** measure how a bond''s price responds to changes in yield, the cornerstone of interest-rate risk management.

## Duration
- **Macaulay duration** is the weighted average time to receive a bond''s cash flows, measured in years.
- **Modified duration** estimates the percentage price change for a 1% change in yield:

```
Modified duration = Macaulay duration / (1 + y/m)
%change in price ~= -Modified duration * change in yield
```

- Duration and price move **inversely** to yield: when yields rise, prices fall.

## Drivers of Duration
| Factor | Effect on duration |
| --- | --- |
| Longer maturity | Higher duration |
| Lower coupon | Higher duration |
| Lower yield | Higher duration |

- A **zero-coupon bond** has Macaulay duration equal to its maturity.

## Convexity
- Duration is a **linear** approximation; the true price-yield curve is **convex**.
- For large yield moves, add the **convexity** adjustment for accuracy:

```
%change in price ~= -ModDur * dy + 0.5 * Convexity * (dy)^2
```

- Positive convexity helps the investor: prices rise more when yields fall than they drop when yields rise by the same amount.

### Tip
- Use modified duration for small yield changes; always include convexity once the yield shift is large.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-derivatives-options-put-call-parity-m01', 'cfa-derivatives-options-put-call-parity', 'Options: Put-Call Parity', '**Put-call parity** is a no-arbitrage relationship that links the prices of a European call, a European put, the underlying asset, and a risk-free bond.

## The Parity Equation
For options with the same strike K and expiration T on the same underlying:

```
C + PV(K) = P + S
```

- **C** is the call price, **P** is the put price, **S** is the spot price, and **PV(K)** is the present value of the strike, K / (1 + r)^T.
- The left side (call plus a bond paying K) is the **fiduciary call**; the right side (put plus stock) is the **protective put**. Both produce identical payoffs.

## Rearranging Parity
| Solve for | Expression |
| --- | --- |
| Call | C = P + S - PV(K) |
| Put | P = C - S + PV(K) |
| Stock | S = C - P + PV(K) |
| Bond | PV(K) = P + S - C |

## Synthetic Positions
- A **synthetic call** = put + stock - bond; a **synthetic put** = call - stock + bond.
- If the parity relation does not hold, an **arbitrage** opportunity exists: buy the cheaper side and sell the dearer side.

### Notes
- Parity holds strictly for **European** options on non-dividend-paying assets; dividends reduce S by their present value.

### Tip
- Memorize fiduciary call equals protective put; from that single identity you can derive every synthetic position on the exam.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-private-equity-m01', 'cfa-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-private-equity', 'Hedge Funds & Private Equity', '**Hedge funds** and **private equity** are alternative investments that pursue absolute returns using strategies and structures unavailable to traditional long-only funds.

## Hedge Fund Strategies
| Strategy | Approach |
| --- | --- |
| Equity long/short | Long undervalued, short overvalued names |
| Event-driven | Mergers, distressed, special situations |
| Relative value | Exploit pricing differences between related assets |
| Macro | Bets on rates, currencies, and economies |

- Hedge funds use **leverage**, **short selling**, and **derivatives** to seek returns uncorrelated with markets.

## Fee Structure
The classic **"2 and 20"** model charges a management fee plus an incentive fee:

```
Total fee = 2% of assets + 20% of profits above a hurdle
```

- A **high-water mark** ensures incentive fees are paid only on new net profits, not on recovering prior losses.

## Private Equity
- **Venture capital** funds early-stage companies; **buyout (LBO)** funds acquire mature firms using heavy debt.
- The **J-curve** describes early negative returns from fees and write-downs, followed by gains as investments mature and exit.
- Exits occur via **IPO**, **trade sale**, or **secondary sale** to another fund.

### Risks and Liquidity
- Both are **illiquid**, have long lock-up periods, and carry **survivorship** and **backfill** biases in reported index returns.

### Tip
- Net-of-fee returns matter most; the 2-and-20 structure can consume a large share of gross performance.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-portfolio-risk-return-capm-m01', 'cfa-portfolio-management-portfolio-risk-return-capm', 'Portfolio Risk, Return & CAPM', '**Modern portfolio theory** shows how combining assets reduces risk, and the **CAPM** prices the systematic risk that diversification cannot remove.

## Portfolio Return and Risk
- Portfolio return is the **weighted average** of asset returns.
- Two-asset portfolio variance depends on the **correlation** between the assets:

```
Var(p) = w1^2*s1^2 + w2^2*s2^2 + 2*w1*w2*Cov(1,2)
```

- The lower the **correlation**, the greater the **diversification** benefit; perfect positive correlation gives no benefit.

## Systematic vs Unsystematic Risk
- **Unsystematic** (firm-specific) risk is **diversifiable** and earns no premium.
- **Systematic** (market) risk, measured by **beta**, cannot be diversified away and is the only risk rewarded.

## The CAPM
The required return rises linearly with beta along the **Security Market Line**:

```
E(R) = Rf + beta * (Rm - Rf)
```

| Term | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| Rf | Risk-free rate |
| beta | Systematic risk measure |
| Rm - Rf | Market risk premium |

- A **beta of 1** moves with the market; beta above 1 is more volatile, below 1 less volatile.

### Performance Measures
- The **Sharpe ratio** = (Rp - Rf) / standard deviation rewards total-risk-adjusted return.

### Tip
- Only systematic risk is priced; a stock with high total volatility but low beta still has a modest required return under CAPM.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-ethics-material-non-public-info-m01', 'cfa-ethics-material-non-public-info', 'Material Non-Public Information & Market Manipulation', '**Material nonpublic information (MNPI)** is information that is both **material** — meaning a reasonable investor would consider it important in making an investment decision or it would likely affect a security''s price — and **nonpublic**, meaning it has not been disseminated to the general investing public.

## Standard II(A): Material Nonpublic Information

CFA Institute Standard II(A) prohibits members and candidates from acting or causing others to act on MNPI. This includes trading for personal accounts, client accounts, or employer accounts. The prohibition also extends to "tipping" — passing MNPI to another person who then trades on it.

**Key questions to ask:**
- Is the information material? (Would it move the price or influence a reasonable investor?)
- Is the information public? (Has it been broadly disseminated through press releases, SEC filings, or major news outlets?)

If both answers point toward MNPI, trading is prohibited.

## Mosaic Theory

Analysts are permitted to use **mosaic theory**: combining individually nonmaterial nonpublic information with publicly available information to reach an investment conclusion. The resulting conclusion is not considered a violation even if it resembles what the MNPI would have revealed, as long as no single piece of nonpublic information was itself material.

## Standard II(B): Market Manipulation

Standard II(B) prohibits members from engaging in practices that distort prices or artificially inflate trading volume. This includes:

| Type | Example |
|------|---------|
| Information-based manipulation | Spreading false rumors to move a price |
| Transaction-based manipulation | Wash trading or painting the tape |

## Firewall Procedures

Firms must establish **information barriers** (Chinese walls) between departments that handle MNPI (e.g., investment banking) and those that trade in public markets. Members who inadvertently receive MNPI should stop trading and report the situation to their compliance department immediately.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-ethics-duties-to-clients-m01', 'cfa-ethics-duties-to-clients', 'Duties to Clients & Portfolio Integrity', 'Standard III of the CFA Institute Code of Ethics governs a member''s obligations to clients. These duties reflect the principle that client interests must be placed above the member''s own interests and, in most cases, above the employer''s interests.

## Standard III(A): Loyalty, Prudence, and Care

Members must act in the **best interest of clients** and exercise the care, judgment, and skill that a reasonable and prudent investment professional would use. Key obligations include:

- Determining the client''s **investment objectives and risk tolerance** before recommending any investment
- Voting proxies in a manner consistent with client interests
- Keeping client assets separate from the firm''s own assets

## Standard III(B): Fair Dealing

Members must deal **fairly and objectively** with all clients when disseminating investment recommendations or taking investment action. Fair dealing does not mean equal treatment — clients may legitimately receive different services — but it prohibits selective disclosure that favors certain clients over others.

## Standard III(C): Suitability

Before making any recommendation, a member must determine that it is **suitable** for the client based on:

| Factor | Consideration |
|--------|---------------|
| Risk tolerance | Conservative vs. aggressive profile |
| Time horizon | Short-term vs. long-term goals |
| Liquidity needs | Access to funds required |
| Tax situation | Tax-exempt vs. taxable investor |
| Unique constraints | Socially responsible investing, legal restrictions |

## Standard III(D): Performance Reporting

Members must not misrepresent their investment performance. Performance should be reported in a manner that is **fair, accurate, and complete**, including relevant benchmarks and risk disclosures.

## Standard III(E): Preservation of Confidentiality

Client information must be kept **confidential** unless the client consents to disclosure, disclosure is required by law, or the information relates to illegal activity.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-ethics-conflicts-of-interest-m01', 'cfa-ethics-conflicts-of-interest', 'Conflicts of Interest & Priority of Transactions', 'A **conflict of interest** arises when a member''s personal interests, or the interests of their employer, could impair their ability to act in the best interest of clients. CFA Institute Standard VI addresses disclosure of conflicts and the priority of transactions.

## Standard VI(A): Disclosure of Conflicts

Members must make **full and fair disclosure** of all matters that could reasonably be expected to impair their independence or objectivity, or interfere with their duties to clients. Disclosures must be prominent and in plain language. Common conflicts include:

- **Ownership of securities** being recommended to clients
- **Compensation arrangements** such as commissions, referral fees, or bonuses tied to products sold
- **Board directorships** or other relationships with companies under coverage
- **Gifts and entertainment** received from third parties above a de minimis threshold

## Standard VI(B): Priority of Transactions

Client transactions must take priority over transactions in accounts in which the member has a **beneficial ownership interest**. The order of priority is:

1. Client accounts
2. Employer (proprietary) accounts
3. Member''s personal accounts

**Front-running** — trading for personal accounts ahead of known client orders — is a clear violation of this standard.

## Standard VI(C): Referral Fees

Members must disclose any **compensation received for referring clients** to other service providers. This allows clients to assess whether the referral is truly in their best interest or motivated by compensation.

## Managing Conflicts

Best practices for conflict management include:

| Practice | Purpose |
|----------|---------|
| Pre-clearance of personal trades | Prevents front-running |
| Blackout periods | Restricts trading around client order flow |
| Independent supervisory review | Ensures objectivity of recommendations |
| Written disclosure to clients | Maintains transparency |', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-ethics-gips-standards-m01', 'cfa-ethics-gips-standards', 'GIPS Standards & Performance Reporting', 'The **Global Investment Performance Standards (GIPS)** are a set of ethical principles established by CFA Institute to standardize how investment firms calculate and present their historical investment performance. Compliance is voluntary but is widely recognized as a mark of credibility and transparency.

## Purpose and Scope

GIPS apply to **investment management firms**, not to individual practitioners. A firm claiming GIPS compliance must apply the standards on a **firm-wide basis** — it cannot selectively claim compliance for only some strategies or products. GIPS are designed to give prospective clients a fair, comparable view of a firm''s track record.

## Key Concepts

**Composites** are the centerpiece of GIPS. A composite is an aggregation of all discretionary portfolios managed to a similar investment mandate, objective, or strategy. Firms must include all actual, fee-paying, discretionary portfolios in at least one composite.

| Requirement | Detail |
|-------------|--------|
| Composite construction | All discretionary, fee-paying portfolios must be included |
| Minimum history | Firms must present at least 5 years of performance (or since inception if less than 5 years), building to 10 years |
| Returns | Must be calculated on a **time-weighted return (TWR)** basis to eliminate the effect of external cash flows |
| Fees | Gross-of-fees and net-of-fees returns should both be presented |

## Time-Weighted vs. Money-Weighted Returns

GIPS requires **time-weighted returns (TWR)** because they eliminate the distorting effect of client-driven cash flows, making performance comparable across managers. **Money-weighted returns (MWR)** reflect the timing of cash flows and are appropriate for measuring investor returns but not manager skill.

## Verification

A firm may hire an independent third party to conduct **GIPS verification**, which provides additional assurance to prospective clients. Verification is performed on a firm-wide basis, not composite by composite.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-m01', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-probability', 'Probability & Expected Value', '**Probability** is a numerical measure of the likelihood that an event will occur, ranging from 0 (impossible) to 1 (certain). In investment analysis, probability concepts underpin risk measurement, scenario analysis, and portfolio construction.

## Types of Probability

- **Empirical probability**: based on historical frequency of an event
- **Subjective probability**: based on personal judgment or expert opinion
- **A priori probability**: based on logical reasoning from equally likely outcomes

## Key Rules

**Addition Rule** (for mutually exclusive events):

```
P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B)
```

For events that are **not** mutually exclusive:

```
P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A and B)
```

**Multiplication Rule** (for independent events):

```
P(A and B) = P(A) x P(B)
```

**Conditional Probability**: the probability of A given that B has occurred:

```
P(A | B) = P(A and B) / P(B)
```

## Expected Value

The **expected value** E(X) of a random variable is the probability-weighted average of all possible outcomes:

```
E(X) = sum of [P(xi) x xi]
```

| Scenario | Probability | Return |
|----------|-------------|--------|
| Bull market | 0.40 | 20% |
| Flat market | 0.35 | 5% |
| Bear market | 0.25 | -15% |

E(Return) = 0.40(20%) + 0.35(5%) + 0.25(-15%) = 8% + 1.75% - 3.75% = **6%**

## Bayes'' Theorem

**Bayes'' theorem** allows updating probability estimates when new information arrives:

```
P(A | B) = [P(B | A) x P(A)] / P(B)
```

This is particularly useful in investment contexts for revising the probability of economic states given new data releases.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-statistical-inference-m01', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-statistical-inference', 'Sampling & Statistical Inference', '**Statistical inference** is the process of drawing conclusions about a **population** based on information from a **sample**. Because it is rarely feasible to observe every member of a population, analysts rely on samples and inferential statistics to make investment decisions.

## Sampling Methods

| Method | Description |
|--------|-------------|
| Simple random sampling | Every member of the population has an equal chance of selection |
| Systematic sampling | Every nth member of the population is selected |
| Stratified random sampling | Population is divided into subgroups (strata); random samples taken from each |
| Cluster sampling | Population is divided into clusters; entire clusters are randomly selected |

**Stratified random sampling** is especially common in fixed-income index replication, where bonds are grouped by maturity, credit rating, and sector.

## The Central Limit Theorem

The **Central Limit Theorem (CLT)** states that for a sufficiently large sample size (n >= 30), the sampling distribution of the sample mean will be approximately **normal**, regardless of the population''s distribution. The standard deviation of this sampling distribution — the **standard error** — is:

```
Standard Error = sigma / sqrt(n)
```

where sigma is the population standard deviation and n is the sample size.

## Sampling Bias

- **Data-snooping bias**: finding patterns by repeatedly mining the same dataset; results may not hold out-of-sample
- **Sample selection bias**: certain data are systematically excluded (e.g., survivorship bias in mutual fund databases)
- **Look-ahead bias**: using information that was not available at the time of the decision
- **Time-period bias**: results that depend heavily on the specific time period chosen

## Point and Interval Estimation

A **point estimate** gives a single value for an unknown parameter. A **confidence interval** provides a range likely to contain the true parameter at a specified level of confidence (e.g., 95%). As sample size increases, the interval narrows and precision improves.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing-m01', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing', 'Hypothesis Testing', '**Hypothesis testing** is a formal statistical procedure for evaluating claims about population parameters using sample data. It is widely used in investment research to validate quantitative strategies and assess the significance of empirical findings.

## Structure of a Hypothesis Test

Every hypothesis test consists of:

- **Null hypothesis (H0)**: the default assumption, typically a statement of "no effect" or "no difference"
- **Alternative hypothesis (Ha)**: the claim to be tested; what we seek evidence for

Tests can be **one-tailed** (directional) or **two-tailed** (non-directional).

## Test Statistic and Decision Rule

A **test statistic** measures how far the sample result is from what the null hypothesis predicts, scaled by the standard error. For a test of a population mean with known variance:

```
z = (x_bar - mu0) / (sigma / sqrt(n))
```

The test statistic is compared against a **critical value** from the relevant distribution (z or t). If |test statistic| > critical value, we **reject H0**.

## Type I and Type II Errors

| Error Type | Definition | Probability |
|------------|-----------|-------------|
| Type I error | Rejecting a true null hypothesis | alpha (significance level) |
| Type II error | Failing to reject a false null hypothesis | beta |
| Power of test | Probability of correctly rejecting a false H0 | 1 - beta |

Reducing alpha (e.g., from 5% to 1%) decreases Type I error but increases Type II error for a fixed sample size.

## p-Value

The **p-value** is the smallest significance level at which the null hypothesis can be rejected given the sample data. If p-value < alpha, reject H0. A very small p-value provides strong evidence against the null hypothesis.

## Common Tests

- **t-test**: used when population variance is unknown (most common in practice)
- **Chi-square test**: used for testing variance and goodness-of-fit
- **F-test**: used for comparing variances across two populations', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-regression-m01', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-regression', 'Simple & Multiple Regression', '**Regression analysis** is a statistical technique that models the relationship between a **dependent variable** (Y) and one or more **independent variables** (X). It is one of the most widely used tools in quantitative finance and investment research.

## Simple Linear Regression

The simple linear regression model takes the form:

```
Y = b0 + b1*X + epsilon
```

where b0 is the intercept, b1 is the slope coefficient, and epsilon is the error term. The **ordinary least squares (OLS)** method estimates b0 and b1 by minimizing the sum of squared residuals.

**Interpretation**: b1 represents the expected change in Y for a one-unit increase in X, holding all else constant.

## Key Regression Statistics

| Statistic | Interpretation |
|-----------|----------------|
| R-squared | Proportion of variance in Y explained by the model (0 to 1) |
| Adjusted R-squared | R-squared penalized for additional predictors; used in multiple regression |
| Standard error of estimate (SEE) | Measures the accuracy of predictions; lower is better |
| F-statistic | Tests overall significance of the regression model |
| t-statistic | Tests significance of individual coefficients |

## Multiple Regression

Multiple regression includes two or more independent variables:

```
Y = b0 + b1*X1 + b2*X2 + ... + bk*Xk + epsilon
```

**Adjusted R-squared** should be used instead of R-squared when comparing models with different numbers of predictors because R-squared always increases when variables are added.

## Regression Violations

- **Heteroskedasticity**: variance of residuals is not constant; causes inefficient estimates and biased standard errors
- **Serial correlation (autocorrelation)**: residuals are correlated across observations; common in time-series data
- **Multicollinearity**: independent variables are highly correlated with each other; inflates standard errors, making it difficult to isolate individual effects

Each violation can be detected with specific tests (e.g., Breusch-Pagan for heteroskedasticity, Durbin-Watson for serial correlation) and corrected through appropriate adjustments.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-economics-gdp-business-cycles-m01', 'cfa-economics-gdp-business-cycles', 'GDP, Business Cycles & Economic Indicators', '**Gross Domestic Product (GDP)** is the total market value of all final goods and services produced within a country in a given period. It is the primary measure of economic output and living standards.

## Measuring GDP

GDP can be measured three equivalent ways:

| Approach | Formula |
| --- | --- |
| Expenditure | GDP = C + I + G + (X - M) |
| Income | GDP = wages + profits + rents + interest |
| Value-added | Sum of value added at each production stage |

- **C** is private consumption, **I** is gross investment, **G** is government spending, and **(X - M)** is net exports.
- **Real GDP** adjusts for price changes using a base-year price level; **nominal GDP** uses current prices.

## Business Cycle Phases

Economies move through recurring expansions and contractions:

| Phase | Characteristics |
| --- | --- |
| Expansion | Rising output, employment, and confidence |
| Peak | Output at maximum; inflation pressures build |
| Contraction | Falling output and rising unemployment |
| Trough | Output at minimum; recovery begins |

- A **recession** is conventionally defined as two consecutive quarters of negative real GDP growth.

## Economic Indicators

Indicators are classified by their timing relative to the cycle:

- **Leading indicators** turn before the economy: equity prices, building permits, yield curve slope, new orders.
- **Coincident indicators** move with the economy: employment, personal income, industrial production.
- **Lagging indicators** turn after the economy: CPI, commercial loan levels, unemployment duration.

### Tip
- The yield curve is among the most reliable leading indicators; an **inverted yield curve** (short rates above long rates) has historically preceded recessions.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-economics-monetary-fiscal-policy-m01', 'cfa-economics-monetary-fiscal-policy', 'Monetary & Fiscal Policy', '**Monetary policy** and **fiscal policy** are the two main tools governments and central banks use to manage economic activity, inflation, and unemployment.

## Monetary Policy

Conducted by the **central bank**, monetary policy controls the money supply and interest rates:

- **Expansionary**: lower policy rates, asset purchases (QE) — stimulates borrowing and growth.
- **Contractionary**: raise rates, sell assets — cools inflation.

The **quantity theory of money** relates money supply to nominal output:

```
M * V = P * Y
```

where **M** is money supply, **V** is velocity, **P** is price level, and **Y** is real output.

## Fiscal Policy

Conducted by the **government**, fiscal policy adjusts spending and taxation:

| Tool | Expansionary | Contractionary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Government spending | Increase | Decrease |
| Taxes | Cut | Raise |

- The **fiscal multiplier** amplifies the effect of spending changes on GDP; it is larger when the marginal propensity to consume is higher.
- **Automatic stabilisers** (e.g., unemployment insurance, progressive taxes) dampen cycles without discretionary action.

## Policy Interactions

- **Crowding out**: heavy government borrowing can raise real interest rates, reducing private investment.
- Expansionary fiscal policy combined with tight monetary policy tends to raise real rates and appreciate the currency.
- Central bank **independence** from political pressure is associated with lower and more stable inflation.

### Tip
- On the exam, distinguish the **tool** (interest rate vs. government spending), the **objective** (price stability vs. growth), and the **institution** (central bank vs. treasury).', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-economics-international-trade-forex-m01', 'cfa-economics-international-trade-forex', 'International Trade & Foreign Exchange', '**International trade** and **foreign exchange** markets are closely linked: trade creates demand for currencies, and exchange rate movements influence competitiveness.

## Comparative Advantage

A country has a **comparative advantage** in goods it can produce at a lower **opportunity cost**, even if another country is absolutely more efficient at everything. Specialisation and trade raise total output for both parties.

## Balance of Payments

The **BOP** records all economic transactions between residents and the rest of the world:

| Account | Records |
| --- | --- |
| Current account | Trade in goods, services, income, transfers |
| Capital account | Non-financial asset transfers |
| Financial account | Portfolio, FDI, reserve flows |

- The current account and financial account **must sum to zero** (with statistical discrepancy).
- A **current account deficit** is financed by a financial account surplus (net capital inflows).

## Exchange Rate Determination

- The **spot rate** is for immediate delivery; the **forward rate** locks in a future exchange.
- **Purchasing Power Parity (PPP)**: currencies adjust so identical baskets cost the same across countries; high-inflation currencies depreciate.
- **Covered interest rate parity**: the forward premium/discount equals the interest rate differential, eliminating arbitrage.

```
F/S = (1 + r_d) / (1 + r_f)
```

### Tip
- A country with higher inflation or lower real interest rates tends to see its currency **depreciate** over time — remember both PPP and the interest rate channel.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-economics-geopolitics-globalisation-m01', 'cfa-economics-geopolitics-globalisation', 'Geopolitics & Globalisation', '**Geopolitics** studies how geography, power, and political relationships shape economic outcomes. **Globalisation** refers to the increasing integration of economies through trade, capital, labour, and information flows.

## Drivers of Globalisation

- Trade liberalisation through **WTO** rules, bilateral and multilateral trade agreements.
- **Foreign direct investment (FDI)** and the rise of **multinational corporations**.
- Technology reducing transportation and communication costs.

## Geopolitical Risk Factors

Analysts assess geopolitical risks across several dimensions:

| Risk Type | Examples |
| --- | --- |
| Political instability | Elections, coups, policy reversals |
| Trade restrictions | Tariffs, sanctions, export controls |
| Resource nationalism | Expropriation of natural resources |
| Military conflict | Supply chain disruption, commodity shocks |

## Impact on Investments

- **Tariffs and sanctions** raise input costs, alter supply chains, and compress margins for affected firms.
- Geopolitical shocks create **risk-off** episodes: investors shift to safe-haven assets (USD, Treasuries, gold).
- **Country risk premiums** are added to discount rates for investments in politically unstable markets.

## Deglobalisation Trends

Recent trends include reshoring, friend-shoring, and strategic decoupling in semiconductors and critical minerals, reflecting a shift from pure efficiency to **resilience** in global supply chains.

### Tip
- CFA candidates should evaluate geopolitical risk through the lens of **investment implications**: which asset classes, sectors, and currencies are affected, and how does the risk premium change?', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-income-statement-m01', 'cfa-financial-reporting-income-statement', 'Income Statement & Revenue Recognition', 'The **income statement** (profit and loss statement) reports a company''s revenues, expenses, and profits over a period. It is the primary gauge of operating performance.

## Income Statement Structure

```
Revenue
- Cost of goods sold (COGS)
= Gross profit
- Operating expenses (SG&A, R&D, D&A)
= Operating income (EBIT)
- Interest expense
+ Other income / (expense)
= Pretax income (EBT)
- Income tax expense
= Net income
```

## Revenue Recognition (IFRS 15 / ASC 606)

The five-step model for recognising revenue:

| Step | Action |
| --- | --- |
| 1 | Identify the contract with the customer |
| 2 | Identify the performance obligations |
| 3 | Determine the transaction price |
| 4 | Allocate price to performance obligations |
| 5 | Recognise revenue when each obligation is satisfied |

- Revenue is recognised when **control** of goods or services is transferred to the customer, not necessarily when cash is received.
- **Long-term contracts** use the **percentage-of-completion** method under IFRS; completed-contract is generally not permitted.

## Analyst Adjustments

- **Non-recurring items** (restructuring charges, asset write-downs) should be excluded from normalised earnings.
- **EBITDA** (earnings before interest, taxes, depreciation, and amortisation) is widely used as a proxy for operating cash flow but ignores capex and working capital changes.

### Tip
- Focus on whether revenue recognition timing accelerates or delays income; aggressive recognition inflates near-term earnings and inflates receivables.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-balance-sheet-equity-m01', 'cfa-financial-reporting-balance-sheet-equity', 'Balance Sheet & Shareholders Equity', 'The **balance sheet** (statement of financial position) presents a company''s assets, liabilities, and shareholders equity at a point in time. The fundamental identity is:

```
Assets = Liabilities + Shareholders Equity
```

## Asset Classification

| Category | Examples |
| --- | --- |
| Current assets | Cash, receivables, inventory, prepaid expenses |
| Non-current assets | PP&E, intangibles, goodwill, long-term investments |

- Assets are listed in **decreasing liquidity** under US GAAP; IFRS allows the reverse.

## Liability Classification

- **Current liabilities** are due within one year: accounts payable, accrued expenses, current portion of long-term debt.
- **Non-current liabilities**: bonds payable, deferred tax liabilities, pension obligations.

## Shareholders Equity Components

```
Shareholders equity = Paid-in capital + Retained earnings
                      - Treasury stock +/- AOCI
```

- **Paid-in capital**: par value plus additional paid-in capital from share issuances.
- **Retained earnings**: cumulative net income less dividends paid.
- **AOCI (Accumulated Other Comprehensive Income)**: unrealised gains/losses on AFS securities, foreign currency translation adjustments, pension remeasurements.
- **Treasury stock** reduces equity; it is shares repurchased and not yet retired.

### Book Value vs Market Value

- **Book value per share** = shareholders equity / shares outstanding.
- Market value usually differs from book value; a **price-to-book ratio** below 1 may indicate distress or value.

### Tip
- AOCI items bypass the income statement; analysts must watch for large swings that affect reported equity but not net income.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-cash-flow-statement-m01', 'cfa-financial-reporting-cash-flow-statement', 'Cash Flow Statement & FCF Analysis', 'The **cash flow statement** reconciles net income to actual cash flows and discloses how a company generates and uses cash across three activities.

## Three Sections

| Section | Includes |
| --- | --- |
| Operating (CFO) | Cash from core business; net income adjusted for non-cash items and working capital changes |
| Investing (CFI) | Capital expenditures, acquisitions, purchases/sales of investments |
| Financing (CFF) | Debt issuance/repayment, equity issuance, dividends, share buybacks |

## Direct vs Indirect Method (CFO)

- **Direct method**: lists actual cash receipts and payments (preferred by analysts, required disclosures differ by standard).
- **Indirect method**: starts with net income and adjusts for non-cash charges and working capital changes (most common in practice).

Key adjustments under indirect method:
- Add back **depreciation and amortisation** (non-cash charges).
- Subtract increases in current assets (e.g., receivables, inventory).
- Add increases in current liabilities (e.g., payables).

## Free Cash Flow

```
FCFF = CFO + Interest*(1-t) - Capex
FCFE = CFO - Capex + Net borrowing
```

- **FCFF** (free cash flow to the firm) is available to all capital providers and is discounted at WACC.
- **FCFE** (free cash flow to equity) is available to equity holders and is discounted at the cost of equity.

### Tip
- High net income but low CFO is a red flag; it often signals aggressive revenue recognition or deteriorating working capital management.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-inventory-ppe-taxes-m01', 'cfa-financial-reporting-inventory-ppe-taxes', 'Inventories, PP&E & Deferred Taxes', 'Inventory, property/plant/equipment, and deferred taxes are major balance sheet items with significant accounting choices that affect comparability across firms.

## Inventory Cost Flow Methods

| Method | Allowed under IFRS | Allowed under US GAAP |
| --- | --- | --- |
| FIFO | Yes | Yes |
| Weighted average | Yes | Yes |
| LIFO | No | Yes |

- In a **rising price** environment, **LIFO** produces higher COGS, lower inventory, and lower taxable income.
- To compare a LIFO firm with FIFO peers, add the **LIFO reserve** to inventory and subtract the tax-adjusted reserve from retained earnings.

## PP&E

- Recorded at **cost** initially; subsequently at cost less accumulated depreciation (cost model) or revalued fair value under IFRS.
- **Depreciation methods**: straight-line (SL), declining balance (accelerated), and units-of-production.
- Accelerated depreciation raises early-year expenses and reduces taxable income sooner.

```
Annual SL depreciation = (Cost - Salvage value) / Useful life
```

## Deferred Taxes

Differences between **book income** (GAAP) and **taxable income** (tax code) create deferred tax assets and liabilities:

- **Deferred tax liability (DTL)**: tax is owed in the future (e.g., accelerated depreciation for tax purposes).
- **Deferred tax asset (DTA)**: tax savings in the future (e.g., warranty expenses recognised for book but not yet for tax).

### Tip
- Under IFRS, inventory write-downs can be **reversed** if value recovers; US GAAP prohibits inventory write-up reversals — this creates permanent comparability differences.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-m01', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-structure', 'Capital Structure & MM Theory', '**Capital structure** refers to the mix of debt and equity a firm uses to finance its assets. The Modigliani-Miller (MM) framework provides the theoretical foundation.

## MM Without Taxes (Proposition I & II)

In a world with no taxes, no transaction costs, and efficient markets, **MM Proposition I** states that firm value is independent of capital structure — the pie metaphor holds regardless of how it is sliced.

**MM Proposition II** (no taxes) states that the cost of equity rises linearly with leverage:

```
Re = R0 + (R0 - Rd) * (D/E)
```

- **R0** is the unlevered cost of equity, **Rd** is the cost of debt, and **D/E** is the debt-to-equity ratio.
- Higher leverage raises financial risk for equity holders, so they demand a higher return — exactly offsetting the benefit of cheaper debt.

## MM With Corporate Taxes

When interest is **tax-deductible**, debt creates a **tax shield** equal to the tax rate times the debt balance:

```
Value of levered firm = Value of unlevered firm + t * D
```

This suggests firms should use maximum debt — but real-world frictions prevent this.

## Trade-Off Theory

The **trade-off theory** balances the tax shield against **financial distress costs** (direct legal costs plus indirect costs such as lost customers, suppliers, and employees):

| Factor | Effect on optimal leverage |
| --- | --- |
| Higher tax rate | More debt preferred |
| Higher distress costs | Less debt preferred |
| More tangible assets | More debt preferred |
| Higher profitability | More debt preferred |

The **optimal capital structure** minimises WACC and maximises firm value where the marginal tax shield benefit equals the marginal cost of distress.

## Pecking Order Theory

Firms prefer **internal financing** first, then debt, and equity last, because information asymmetry makes external equity the most expensive signal to the market.

### Tip
On exam questions, MM with taxes always favours maximum debt; real-world optimal structure requires adding distress costs via the trade-off theory.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-dividends-buybacks-m01', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-dividends-buybacks', 'Dividends & Share Buybacks', '**Dividends** and **share buybacks** are the two primary mechanisms through which firms return cash to shareholders. Understanding when and why firms choose each method is essential for the CFA exam.

## Miller-Modigliani Dividend Irrelevance

Under MM assumptions (no taxes, no transaction costs, perfect information), **dividend policy is irrelevant** to firm value. Shareholders can create their own dividend by selling shares ("homemade dividend"), so payout policy does not change total return.

## Real-World Factors

In practice, several frictions make payout policy relevant:

| Factor | Implication |
| --- | --- |
| Taxes on dividends | Investors may prefer capital gains if taxed lower |
| Clientele effect | Different investor groups prefer different payout policies |
| Signaling theory | Dividend increases signal management confidence |
| Agency costs | Paying out cash reduces free cash flow available for waste |

## Dividends vs Share Buybacks

- A **cash dividend** distributes earnings proportionally to all shareholders and signals stable, recurring income.
- A **share repurchase** reduces shares outstanding, boosts EPS and book value per share, and gives shareholders the choice of whether to receive cash (by tendering) or to defer taxation.
- Under tax neutrality, both methods produce identical outcomes for the shareholder — **payout equivalence**.

## Signaling Theory

- An **unexpected dividend increase** signals that management expects higher future earnings.
- A **dividend cut** is typically interpreted negatively and causes a share price decline, even if the firm redirects cash to positive-NPV investments.

## Types of Dividends

- **Regular cash dividend**: recurring, committed payout.
- **Special dividend**: one-time, non-recurring distribution.
- **Stock dividend**: additional shares issued in lieu of cash; total equity value unchanged.
- **Liquidating dividend**: return of capital that reduces the cost basis.

### Tip
When a question mentions signaling, think dividend increase equals positive signal; cut equals negative signal regardless of stated rationale.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-governance-m01', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-governance', 'Corporate Governance & ESG', '**Corporate governance** is the system of rules, practices, and processes by which a company is directed and controlled. It addresses the **principal-agent problem** that arises when ownership and management are separated.

## The Agency Problem

- **Principals** (shareholders) hire **agents** (managers) to run the firm, but managers may pursue their own interests at shareholders'' expense.
- **Agency costs** include monitoring costs, bonding costs, and residual losses from imperfect alignment.
- Mechanisms to reduce agency costs include executive compensation tied to share performance, independent boards, and concentrated ownership.

## Board Structure

| Feature | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| Independent directors | Provide objective oversight of management |
| Audit committee | Oversees financial reporting and external audit |
| Compensation committee | Aligns executive pay with shareholder interests |
| Staggered board | Directors elected in tranches, limiting hostile takeover speed |

- A **majority-independent board** is considered a best-practice governance feature.
- **Dual-class shares** concentrate voting power with founders and can entrench management against shareholder wishes.

## Stakeholder Theory

Beyond shareholders, firms have obligations to a broader set of **stakeholders** including employees, creditors, customers, suppliers, and communities. Governance frameworks increasingly incorporate these interests.

## ESG Integration

**Environmental, Social, and Governance (ESG)** factors are non-financial criteria that can affect long-term performance and risk:

- **Environmental**: carbon emissions, resource use, climate risk exposure.
- **Social**: labour practices, supply chain standards, data privacy.
- **Governance**: board composition, executive pay, shareholder rights.

ESG analysis is used in **negative screening** (excluding certain sectors), **positive/best-in-class screening**, and **integration** into fundamental valuations through adjusted discount rates or cash-flow estimates.

### Tip
On CFA exams, governance questions often test whether a given board or ownership structure strengthens or weakens shareholder protection — independent boards and one-share-one-vote structures favour shareholders.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-leverage-analysis-m01', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-leverage-analysis', 'Business & Financial Leverage', '**Leverage** amplifies both gains and losses. The CFA curriculum distinguishes between operating leverage (from fixed operating costs) and financial leverage (from fixed financing costs).

## Degree of Operating Leverage (DOL)

DOL measures the sensitivity of **EBIT** to changes in sales:

```
DOL = % change in EBIT / % change in sales
    = (Sales - Variable costs) / EBIT
    = Contribution margin / EBIT
```

- High **fixed operating costs** (e.g., manufacturing plants) raise DOL, making EBIT more sensitive to revenue swings.

## Degree of Financial Leverage (DFL)

DFL measures the sensitivity of **EPS** (or net income) to changes in EBIT:

```
DFL = % change in EPS / % change in EBIT
    = EBIT / (EBIT - Interest)
```

- Higher debt and fixed interest charges raise DFL, amplifying EPS volatility.

## Degree of Total Leverage (DTL)

DTL combines both effects and measures sensitivity of EPS to a change in sales:

```
DTL = DOL * DFL
    = Contribution margin / (EBIT - Interest)
```

## Breakeven Analysis

| Concept | Formula |
| --- | --- |
| Operating breakeven (units) | Fixed operating costs / (Price - Variable cost per unit) |
| Financial breakeven | Sales level at which EPS = 0 |

- **Operating breakeven** is the sales quantity at which EBIT = 0.
- A firm with high DOL and high DFL (high DTL) has **amplified earnings sensitivity** in both directions — greater upside in good times and greater downside in bad times.

## Sensitivity and Risk

The higher the leverage, the more volatile the **earnings per share** relative to sales, raising the risk perceived by equity investors and increasing the required return on equity.

### Tip
Remember DOL is about the income statement above EBIT and DFL is below EBIT; DTL captures the whole statement from revenue to EPS.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-equity-market-organisation-efficiency-m01', 'cfa-equity-market-organisation-efficiency', 'Market Organisation & Efficiency', '**Market efficiency** describes how quickly and accurately security prices reflect available information. The **Efficient Market Hypothesis (EMH)** has direct implications for investment strategy.

## Three Forms of Market Efficiency

| Form | Information reflected | Implication |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Weak | All past trading data (prices, volume) | Technical analysis cannot earn excess returns |
| Semi-strong | All publicly available information | Fundamental analysis cannot earn excess returns |
| Strong | All information (including private/inside) | Even insiders cannot consistently earn excess returns |

- In practice, markets appear roughly **semi-strong efficient** for large-cap equities in developed markets.

## Market Anomalies

Several documented anomalies appear to challenge semi-strong efficiency:

- **Size effect**: small-cap stocks have historically outperformed risk-adjusted benchmarks.
- **Value effect**: high book-to-market stocks tend to outperform growth stocks.
- **Momentum**: recent winners continue to outperform in the short run.
- **Calendar effects**: January effect and weekend effect.

Debate continues over whether anomalies represent genuine mispricings, risk factors, or data-mining artefacts.

## Implications for Active Management

- If markets are **weak-form efficient**, technical analysis adds no value over passive strategies.
- If **semi-strong efficient**, neither technical nor fundamental analysis consistently generates alpha after costs.
- Passive **index investing** is the rational response under semi-strong efficiency.

## Market Organisation

- **Primary markets** issue new securities (IPOs, seasoned equity offerings).
- **Secondary markets** provide liquidity for existing securities (exchanges, OTC).
- **Quote-driven** (dealer) markets use bid-ask spreads; **order-driven** markets use a limit order book.

### Tip
The key exam point: the form of efficiency determines which type of analysis is rendered useless — weak form kills technical, semi-strong kills fundamental, strong form kills all analysis including insider trading.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-equity-industry-company-analysis-m01', 'cfa-equity-industry-company-analysis', 'Industry & Company Analysis', '**Industry analysis** evaluates the structural forces that shape profitability and competitive dynamics within a sector. **Company analysis** then identifies which firms are best positioned within the industry.

## Porter''s Five Forces

Michael Porter''s framework identifies five forces that determine industry attractiveness and long-run profitability:

| Force | High threat means lower profitability |
| --- | --- |
| Threat of new entrants | Easy entry erodes margins |
| Bargaining power of buyers | Strong buyers force prices down |
| Bargaining power of suppliers | Strong suppliers raise input costs |
| Threat of substitutes | Substitutes cap pricing power |
| Rivalry among existing firms | Intense competition compresses margins |

Industries protected by **high barriers to entry**, **switching costs**, and **limited substitutes** tend to sustain above-average returns.

## Industry Life Cycle

| Stage | Characteristics |
| --- | --- |
| Embryonic | Slow growth, high prices, small customers |
| Growth | Rapid expansion, new entrants, improving margins |
| Shakeout | Slowing growth, consolidation, falling margins |
| Mature | Slow growth, high competition, stable margins |
| Decline | Falling demand, exits, shrinking margins |

## Company-Level Analysis

Within an industry, competitive advantage is assessed through:

- **Cost leadership**: producing at the lowest cost in the industry.
- **Differentiation**: offering a unique product that commands a price premium.
- **SWOT analysis**: Strengths, Weaknesses, Opportunities, Threats.
- **Return on invested capital (ROIC)** relative to WACC signals value creation.

A firm with **ROIC > WACC** is creating economic value; one with **ROIC < WACC** is destroying it.

### Tip
When Porter''s forces are weak (i.e., barriers to entry are high and rivals are few), the industry is attractive; translate this into higher terminal growth rate assumptions in your valuation model.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-equity-dcf-fcf-valuation-m01', 'cfa-equity-dcf-fcf-valuation', 'DCF & Free Cash Flow Valuation', '**Discounted cash flow (DCF)** valuation estimates intrinsic value as the present value of expected future free cash flows. Two main variants differ in which stakeholders'' cash flows are discounted.

## FCFF vs FCFE

| Metric | Definition | Discount rate |
| --- | --- | --- |
| FCFF (Free Cash Flow to the Firm) | Cash available to all capital providers after reinvestment | WACC |
| FCFE (Free Cash Flow to Equity) | Cash available to equity holders after debt obligations | Cost of equity (r) |

Key formulas:

```
FCFF = Net income + Depreciation - Capital expenditure
       - Change in working capital + Interest*(1-t)

FCFE = FCFF - Interest*(1-t) + Net borrowing
```

## Discounting and Terminal Value

A two-stage DCF model sums the present value of explicit forecast cash flows and a **terminal value**:

```
Terminal value = FCF(n+1) / (r - g)
```

- **g** is the long-run sustainable growth rate (typically close to nominal GDP growth).
- The terminal value often represents 60-80% of total DCF value, making the growth rate assumption critically important.

## WACC as the Discount Rate

For FCFF, the **WACC** reflects the blended required return of all capital providers, incorporating the tax shield on debt.

## Sensitivity Analysis

Because DCF value is highly sensitive to input assumptions, analysts should stress-test:

- The terminal growth rate (g) — small changes have a large impact.
- The discount rate (WACC or r) — a 1% increase significantly reduces value.
- Near-term growth and margin assumptions in the explicit forecast period.

### Tip
Always check whether the question uses FCFF (discount at WACC to get total firm value, then subtract debt) or FCFE (discount at cost of equity to get equity value directly).', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-equity-price-multiples-ev-m01', 'cfa-equity-price-multiples-ev', 'Price Multiples & EV Analysis', '**Price multiples** and **enterprise value (EV) multiples** are relative valuation tools that compare a stock or firm to peers or to its own history. They are faster to compute than DCF but require careful comparability.

## Common Price Multiples

| Multiple | Formula | Best used when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| P/E | Price / EPS | Mature, profitable firms |
| P/B | Price / Book value per share | Financial firms, asset-heavy companies |
| P/S | Price / Revenue per share | Loss-making or cyclical firms |
| P/CF | Price / Cash flow per share | Capital-intensive industries |

- **Trailing P/E** uses the most recent 12 months of EPS; **leading P/E** (forward P/E) uses next year''s consensus estimate.
- A stock trading at a **P/E below peers** may be undervalued — or may reflect lower growth, higher risk, or weaker profitability.

## Enterprise Value Multiples

**EV** captures the total value to all capital providers:

```
EV = Market cap + Debt + Preferred equity + Minority interest - Cash
```

- **EV/EBITDA** is the most widely used EV multiple because EBITDA is less affected by capital structure and depreciation policy differences.
- **EV/EBIT** is preferred when comparing firms with very different capital expenditure and depreciation policies.

## Comparable Company Analysis

Steps in a comparable company ("comps") analysis:

1. Select a universe of comparable firms (similar size, business model, geography).
2. Calculate and spread relevant multiples for the peer group.
3. Apply the median (or mean) peer multiple to the target firm''s fundamental metric.
4. Triangulate with DCF and precedent transaction analysis.

## Limitations

- Multiples reflect the **market''s current sentiment**, which may itself be mispriced.
- Accounting differences across firms can distort P/B and P/E comparisons.
- Negative earnings make P/E undefined; use P/S or EV/EBITDA instead.

### Tip
EV/EBITDA is capital-structure neutral and depreciation-policy neutral, making it the best multiple for comparing firms with different leverage and asset bases.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-bond-features-valuation-m01', 'cfa-fixed-income-bond-features-valuation', 'Bond Features & Valuation', '## Bond Features & Valuation

A **bond** is a fixed income instrument representing a loan from an investor to a borrower. Key structural features determine how a bond is valued.

## Coupon Types

| Coupon Type | Description |
|---|---|
| Fixed-rate | Constant periodic payment throughout life |
| Floating-rate | Resets periodically based on a reference rate (e.g., SOFR + spread) |
| Zero-coupon | No periodic payments; issued at a discount to par |
| Step-up | Coupon rate increases at scheduled dates |

**Par value** (face value) is the principal repaid at maturity, typically 1,000 per bond.

## Embedded Options

**Callable bonds** give the issuer the right to redeem the bond before maturity, typically when interest rates fall. **Putable bonds** give the holder the right to sell the bond back to the issuer. **Convertible bonds** allow the holder to exchange the bond for a specified number of equity shares.

## Clean vs. Dirty Price

The **dirty price** (full price) is the actual amount paid by the buyer and equals the present value of all future cash flows. The **clean price** (flat price) is the dirty price minus **accrued interest**:

> Dirty Price = Clean Price + Accrued Interest

**Accrued interest** compensates the seller for interest earned since the last coupon date. Bonds are quoted on a clean price basis but settle at the dirty price.

## Bond Valuation

A bond''s value is the present value of its future cash flows (coupons plus par) discounted at the required yield:

> Price = Sum of [Coupon / (1 + r)^t] + [Par / (1 + r)^N]

When the coupon rate equals the required yield, the bond trades **at par**. When the required yield exceeds the coupon rate, the bond trades at a **discount**; when below, at a **premium**.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-yield-measures-term-structure-m01', 'cfa-fixed-income-yield-measures-term-structure', 'Yield Measures & Term Structure', '## Yield Measures & Term Structure

Yield measures quantify the return an investor earns from a fixed income security under specific assumptions.

## Common Yield Measures

**Current yield** = Annual coupon / Bond price. Simple but ignores capital gains and time value.

**Yield to maturity (YTM)** is the internal rate of return assuming the bond is held to maturity and all coupons are reinvested at the YTM. It is the most widely used yield measure.

**Yield to call (YTC)** is calculated to the first call date rather than maturity, relevant for callable bonds.

## Spot Rates and Forward Rates

A **spot rate** is the yield on a zero-coupon bond for a specific maturity. The collection of spot rates across maturities forms the **spot curve** (zero curve). Bond prices should theoretically equal the sum of cash flows discounted at their respective spot rates (**no-arbitrage pricing**).

A **forward rate** is the interest rate agreed today for a loan beginning at a future date. The relationship between spot and forward rates:

> (1 + S2)^2 = (1 + S1)(1 + 1f1)

where 1f1 is the one-period forward rate one period from now.

## Par Curve and Yield Spreads

The **par curve** shows YTMs on coupon bonds priced at par across maturities.

| Spread Measure | Description |
|---|---|
| G-spread | Yield minus interpolated government benchmark yield |
| Z-spread | Constant spread added to all spot rates to price the bond |
| OAS | Z-spread adjusted for the value of embedded options |

**OAS (option-adjusted spread)** is the most meaningful spread for bonds with embedded options, isolating credit and liquidity risk from option risk.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-credit-risk-analysis-m01', 'cfa-fixed-income-credit-risk-analysis', 'Credit Risk & Credit Analysis', '## Credit Risk & Credit Analysis

**Credit risk** is the risk that a borrower fails to make required debt payments. It has two primary components: **default risk** (probability of default) and **loss severity** (loss given default).

## Key Credit Risk Metrics

> Expected Loss = Probability of Default x Loss Given Default

**Loss given default (LGD)** = 1 - Recovery Rate. Investment-grade bonds historically have recovery rates around 40-50% of par.

**Credit spread** is the yield difference between a corporate bond and a comparable government bond. Wider spreads signal higher perceived credit risk.

## Credit Ratings

Rating agencies (Moody''s, S&P, Fitch) assign ratings from **AAA/Aaa** (highest quality) down to **D** (default). Bonds rated BBB-/Baa3 or above are **investment grade**; below are **high yield** (speculative).

| Category | S&P | Moody''s |
|---|---|---|
| Highest quality | AAA | Aaa |
| Investment grade cutoff | BBB- | Baa3 |
| Speculative | BB+ and below | Ba1 and below |

## The Four Cs of Credit Analysis

Credit analysts evaluate issuers using the **four Cs**:

- **Capacity**: Ability to repay debt (cash flow generation, leverage ratios)
- **Collateral**: Assets pledged as security; quality of the asset base
- **Covenants**: Bond indenture provisions protecting bondholders (affirmative and negative covenants)
- **Character**: Management''s track record, governance quality, and willingness to repay

Covenant analysis is particularly important: **negative covenants** restrict issuer actions (e.g., limits on additional debt), while **affirmative covenants** require specific actions (e.g., maintaining insurance).', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-securitisation-abs-m01', 'cfa-fixed-income-securitisation-abs', 'Securitisation & Asset-Backed Securities', '## Securitisation & Asset-Backed Securities

**Securitisation** is the process of pooling financial assets and issuing securities backed by those cash flows. It converts illiquid assets into tradeable instruments, improving liquidity and enabling risk transfer.

## Key Structures

**Mortgage-backed securities (MBS)** are backed by pools of mortgage loans. **Agency MBS** are issued or guaranteed by government-sponsored enterprises (Fannie Mae, Freddie Mac) and carry minimal credit risk. **Non-agency MBS** carry credit risk and are structured with credit enhancements.

**Asset-backed securities (ABS)** are backed by non-mortgage assets: auto loans, credit card receivables, student loans, or equipment leases.

**Collateralised debt obligations (CDOs)** are backed by pools of debt instruments (bonds, loans). **CLOs** (collateralised loan obligations) use leveraged loans as collateral.

## Prepayment Risk

Mortgage borrowers may repay principal ahead of schedule when rates fall (**refinancing**) or when homes are sold. **Prepayment risk** has two components:

- **Contraction risk**: When rates fall, prepayments accelerate and investors receive principal early, forced to reinvest at lower rates
- **Extension risk**: When rates rise, prepayments slow and the security''s duration extends

## Tranching and Waterfall Structure

**Tranching** allocates cash flows and losses among classes (tranches) with different risk/return profiles. The **waterfall structure** distributes principal and interest: senior tranches receive payments first and bear losses last, while junior/equity tranches absorb first losses but earn higher yields.

**Credit enhancement** techniques include overcollateralisation, excess spread, reserve accounts, and subordination to protect senior bondholders.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-derivatives-forwards-futures-m01', 'cfa-derivatives-forwards-futures', 'Forwards & Futures Pricing', '## Forwards & Futures Pricing

**Forwards** and **futures** are contracts obligating both parties to transact a specified asset at a predetermined price on a future date. Forwards are private OTC contracts; futures are standardised and exchange-traded with daily mark-to-market settlement.

## Cost of Carry and Forward Price

The **no-arbitrage forward price** is derived from the **cost of carry model**:

> F0 = S0 x (1 + r)^T

where S0 is the current spot price, r is the risk-free rate, and T is time to expiry. For assets with carrying costs (storage) or benefits (dividends, convenience yield):

> F0 = S0 x (1 + r + storage - income)^T

**Carrying costs** (storage, insurance, financing) push the forward price above the spot. **Carrying benefits** (dividends, coupons, convenience yield) reduce the forward price.

## Contango and Backwardation

| Market Structure | Forward Price vs Spot | Typical Condition |
|---|---|---|
| Contango | F0 > S0 | Normal for financial assets |
| Backwardation | F0 < S0 | High convenience yield or tight supply |

In **contango**, the futures curve slopes upward with maturity. In **backwardation**, it slopes downward.

## Basis Risk

**Basis** = Spot price - Futures price. **Basis risk** arises when the asset being hedged does not perfectly match the futures contract (cross hedging) or when the hedge is lifted before contract expiry. As expiry approaches, basis converges to zero (**convergence**).

Futures require a **margin deposit** and daily **mark-to-market** (variation margin), unlike forwards which settle only at expiration.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-derivatives-swaps-m01', 'cfa-derivatives-swaps', 'Swaps: Interest Rate & Currency', '## Swaps: Interest Rate & Currency

A **swap** is a derivative contract in which two counterparties agree to exchange a series of cash flows over a specified period. Swaps are OTC instruments typically used to manage interest rate or currency exposure.

## Plain Vanilla Interest Rate Swap

In a **plain vanilla interest rate swap**, one party pays a **fixed rate** and receives a **floating rate** (referenced to SOFR or a similar benchmark) on the same notional principal. No principal is exchanged.

> Net payment = (Fixed rate - Floating rate) x Notional x Day count fraction

The party paying fixed benefits when rates rise; the party receiving fixed benefits when rates fall. Corporations often use pay-fixed swaps to convert floating-rate debt to fixed-rate, reducing interest rate uncertainty.

## Swap Pricing and Valuation

At inception, the **fixed swap rate** (par rate) is set so the swap has zero value to both parties. It equals the rate that equates the present value of fixed payments to the present value of expected floating payments, using the current term structure of interest rates.

After initiation, swap value changes as interest rates move. The long position (receive fixed) gains value when rates fall.

## Currency Swaps

In a **currency swap**, counterparties exchange principal and interest payments in different currencies. Unlike interest rate swaps, **notional principal is exchanged** at initiation and at maturity at the agreed exchange rate.

| Feature | Interest Rate Swap | Currency Swap |
|---|---|---|
| Principal exchange | No | Yes (at start and end) |
| Currency | Single | Two different currencies |
| Use case | Rate risk | FX + rate risk |

Currency swaps help firms raise funding in foreign markets and swap back to their home currency, often at lower cost than direct foreign borrowing.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-derivatives-option-strategies-m01', 'cfa-derivatives-option-strategies', 'Option Strategies & Greeks', '## Option Strategies & Greeks

Options give the holder the right but not the obligation to buy (**call**) or sell (**put**) an asset at a specified **strike price** before or at expiry. The seller (writer) collects the **premium** in exchange for bearing the obligation.

## Common Option Strategies

**Covered call**: Long the underlying asset + short a call. Generates income but caps upside above the strike. Suitable when the investor expects modest price appreciation.

**Protective put**: Long the underlying asset + long a put. Acts as insurance, limiting downside below the strike while preserving upside. The put premium is the cost of protection.

**Straddle**: Long a call and long a put at the same strike and expiry. Profits from large moves in either direction; loses the combined premium if the price stays near the strike.

**Bull call spread**: Long a lower-strike call + short a higher-strike call. Reduces premium cost but caps maximum gain. Profits from moderate upward moves.

**Bear put spread**: Long a higher-strike put + short a lower-strike put. Profits from moderate downward moves at reduced cost.

## The Option Greeks

| Greek | Measures | Direction |
|---|---|---|
| **Delta** | Price sensitivity to underlying price change | Calls: 0 to +1; Puts: -1 to 0 |
| **Gamma** | Rate of change of delta | Always positive for long options |
| **Theta** | Time decay (value lost per day) | Negative for long options |
| **Vega** | Sensitivity to implied volatility | Positive for long options |

**Delta hedging** involves holding a position that offsets the delta of an option, requiring continuous rebalancing as delta changes (**dynamic hedging**). Gamma measures the cost of this rebalancing.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-derivatives-risk-management-applications-m01', 'cfa-derivatives-risk-management-applications', 'Risk Management Applications of Derivatives', '## Risk Management Applications of Derivatives

Derivatives are essential tools for managing financial risk exposures. Hedging with derivatives does not eliminate risk but transfers it to parties more willing or able to bear it.

## Equity Risk Management

A portfolio manager can reduce equity market exposure by **selling (shorting) equity index futures**. The number of contracts needed:

> Number of contracts = (Target beta - Portfolio beta) / Futures beta x (Portfolio value / Futures price x Multiplier)

To eliminate all market risk (target beta = 0), the manager sells futures equal in value to the portfolio. **Protective puts** on an equity portfolio cap downside while preserving upside.

## Interest Rate Risk Management

**Duration** measures a bond portfolio''s sensitivity to interest rate changes. To reduce duration, a manager can **pay fixed in an interest rate swap** (effectively converting a long-duration portfolio to a shorter one) or **short interest rate futures**.

> Dollar duration change = (MDtarget - MDportfolio) x Portfolio value

Interest rate caps and floors protect floating-rate borrowers and lenders respectively from adverse rate moves.

## Currency Risk Management

A firm with foreign currency receivables faces **transaction exposure**: the risk that exchange rates move unfavourably before settlement. Hedging tools include:

| Tool | Mechanism |
|---|---|
| Forward contract | Lock in today''s exchange rate for future delivery |
| Currency futures | Exchange-traded equivalent of a forward |
| Currency swap | Exchange principal and interest in two currencies |
| Currency options | Right (not obligation) to exchange at a set rate |

**Economic exposure** (long-run impact on firm value) and **translation exposure** (accounting impact of consolidating foreign subsidiaries) also require active management. Forwards and swaps are most commonly used for transaction hedging due to their precision and flexibility.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-real-estate-m01', 'cfa-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'Real Estate Investments', '**Real estate** is one of the largest asset classes globally, offering income, capital appreciation, and inflation-hedging properties.

## Forms of Real Estate Investment
| Form | Description |
| --- | --- |
| Direct ownership | Investor holds the physical property |
| Real Estate Investment Trust (REIT) | Publicly traded, liquid, pass-through vehicle |
| Real estate limited partnerships | Illiquid, private, pass-through |
| Commingled real estate funds | Pooled vehicles for institutional investors |

**Direct investment** provides maximum control but requires large capital, active management, and accepts illiquidity. **REITs** offer liquidity and diversification but expose investors to equity market correlation.

## Key Metrics
- **Net Operating Income (NOI)** = Gross potential rent - Vacancy losses - Operating expenses (excludes debt service and taxes).
- **Capitalization rate (cap rate)** = NOI / Property value. A lower cap rate implies a higher valuation relative to income.
- **Gross Income Multiplier (GIM)** = Property price / Gross income; used for quick comparisons.

## Property Types
- **Residential**: apartments, single-family homes; demand driven by demographics.
- **Commercial (office/retail)**: sensitive to economic cycles and remote-work trends.
- **Industrial**: warehouses and logistics; strong secular demand from e-commerce.
- **Hospitality**: highest operational complexity; most cyclical.

## The Real Estate Cycle
Real estate moves through four phases: **recovery**, **expansion**, **hyper-supply**, and **recession**. Cap rates typically compress during expansion and widen during recession as values fall relative to income.

### Tip
- The cap rate is used to value property from NOI: Value = NOI / Cap rate. Memorize this relationship and recognize that rising cap rates mean falling values.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-commodities-m01', 'cfa-alternative-investments-commodities', 'Commodities & Commodity Derivatives', '**Commodities** include energy, metals, and agricultural products. Investors gain exposure primarily through **futures contracts** rather than physical ownership.

## Spot vs. Futures
- The **spot price** is the current market price for immediate delivery.
- A **futures price** is the agreed price for delivery at a future date.
- When futures prices exceed spot prices the market is in **contango**; when futures are below spot it is in **backwardation**.

## Sources of Commodity Futures Return
| Source | Description |
| --- | --- |
| Spot return | Change in the spot price of the commodity |
| Roll yield | Gain or loss from rolling expiring futures to the next contract |
| Collateral yield | Return on collateral posted to margin the futures position |

**Roll yield** is positive in backwardation (rolling into cheaper contracts) and negative in contango (rolling into more expensive contracts).

## Convenience Yield
**Convenience yield** is the non-monetary benefit of holding physical inventory of a commodity (e.g., uninterrupted production). It explains why some markets remain in backwardation: holders of physical goods demand a premium to sell forward.

## Commodity Indices
Major indices such as the **S&P GSCI** and **Bloomberg Commodity Index** differ in sector weights. The GSCI is energy-heavy; the Bloomberg index is more diversified.

## Role in a Portfolio
Commodities provide a partial **inflation hedge** because commodity prices often rise with inflation. Correlation with equities and bonds is historically low, improving portfolio diversification.

### Tip
- The total return of a long futures position equals spot return + roll yield + collateral yield. In backwardation, roll yield is positive and boosts total return.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-infrastructure-m01', 'cfa-alternative-investments-infrastructure', 'Infrastructure & Other Alternatives', '**Infrastructure** investments include assets such as toll roads, airports, utilities, pipelines, and telecommunications networks that provide essential services to the public.

## Defining Characteristics
- **Long economic life**: assets often operate for decades, matching long-duration liabilities.
- **Regulated or contracted revenues**: tariffs and concession agreements provide cash flow predictability.
- **Essential services**: demand is relatively inelastic; assets form natural monopolies.
- **High capital intensity**: large upfront costs create significant barriers to entry.

## Brownfield vs. Greenfield
| Type | Description | Risk profile |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Brownfield | Existing, operating asset | Lower risk; established cash flows |
| Greenfield | New construction project | Higher risk; construction and ramp-up risk |

Brownfield assets trade at lower expected returns; greenfield projects command an illiquidity and construction risk premium.

## Public-Private Partnerships (PPP)
In a **PPP**, a government entity grants a private operator the right to build and/or operate infrastructure for a defined period in exchange for agreed tariffs or availability payments. Risk is shared between the public and private sectors.

## Other Alternative Categories
- **Timberland**: income from timber harvests plus biological growth; inflation-linked revenues.
- **Farmland**: income from crop production; storage flexibility allows timing of harvests.
- **Natural resources**: oil and gas royalties; highly commodity-price sensitive.

### Tip
- Infrastructure is attractive to pension funds because long-dated, inflation-linked cash flows match long-duration liabilities. Brownfield assets behave more like bonds; greenfield assets behave more like equity.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-due-diligence-m01', 'cfa-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'Due Diligence & Performance Evaluation', 'Investing in alternatives requires a rigorous **due diligence** process because of illiquidity, complexity, and limited transparency relative to traditional assets.

## Manager Selection Framework
Investors evaluate managers across several dimensions:

| Dimension | Key questions |
| --- | --- |
| Investment process | Is the strategy well-defined and consistently applied? |
| People | What is the team''s experience, stability, and alignment of interests? |
| Portfolio | Does the portfolio match the stated strategy? Is concentration appropriate? |
| Performance | Is the track record long enough, and is attribution understood? |
| Operations | Are back-office controls, compliance, and reporting robust? |

## Operational Due Diligence
**Operational due diligence (ODD)** focuses on non-investment risks: fund administration, custody arrangements, valuation procedures, IT systems, and regulatory compliance. Many fraud cases (e.g., Madoff) would have been flagged by adequate ODD.

## Strategy Fit
An investor must confirm that the manager''s strategy fits the **portfolio''s objectives**, risk budget, liquidity needs, and return expectations before committing capital.

## Performance Benchmarks
Alternative investment performance is evaluated against **peer universes**, custom benchmarks, or absolute-return targets (e.g., LIBOR/SOFR + spread). Common metrics include **IRR** for private equity, **TVPI** (total value to paid-in capital), and **DPI** (distributed to paid-in capital).

## Risk Assessment
Key risks include **liquidity risk**, **leverage risk**, **counterparty risk**, and **key-person risk** (dependence on a single portfolio manager).

### Tip
- A strong track record alone is insufficient. Verify that past performance was achieved with the same team, strategy, and risk level as what is being offered today.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-ips-planning-m01', 'cfa-portfolio-management-ips-planning', 'Investment Policy Statement & Planning', 'The **Investment Policy Statement (IPS)** is the governing document for a portfolio. It translates a client''s situation into actionable guidelines for the portfolio manager.

## Components of an IPS
A well-structured IPS addresses **objectives** and **constraints**:

### Objectives
- **Return objective**: Can be stated as an absolute target (e.g., 7% per year) or relative to a benchmark. Must be consistent with the risk objective.
- **Risk tolerance**: Willingness (psychological comfort) and ability (financial capacity) to bear risk. The binding constraint is whichever is lower.

### Constraints (TTLLU)
| Constraint | Description |
| --- | --- |
| Time horizon | Investment period; longer horizons allow more risk |
| Tax concerns | Tax bracket, tax-exempt status, location of assets |
| Liquidity needs | Scheduled or contingent cash requirements |
| Legal & regulatory | Fiduciary duties, investment restrictions |
| Unique circumstances | ESG preferences, concentrated positions, restrictions |

## The Planning Process
1. Determine the client''s current situation (assets, liabilities, income, goals).
2. Identify objectives and constraints.
3. Draft and agree on the IPS.
4. Construct the portfolio consistent with the IPS.
5. Monitor, review, and update the IPS when circumstances change.

## Institutional vs. Individual Investors
Institutions (endowments, pension funds) typically have longer horizons, higher risk tolerance, and simpler tax situations than individuals. Defined-benefit pension plans must match asset duration to liability duration.

### Tip
- On the exam, always identify both willingness and ability to take risk separately. If they conflict, the lower tolerance governs the portfolio''s risk level.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-asset-allocation-m01', 'cfa-portfolio-management-asset-allocation', 'Asset Allocation & Multi-Asset Portfolios', '**Asset allocation** is the decision of how to distribute a portfolio across broad asset classes (equities, fixed income, alternatives, cash). Research suggests it explains the majority of long-run portfolio return variability.

## Strategic vs. Tactical Allocation
- **Strategic asset allocation (SAA)**: long-term target weights based on the investor''s IPS; reflects capital market expectations over a full market cycle.
- **Tactical asset allocation (TAA)**: short-term deliberate deviations from SAA to exploit perceived mispricings. TAA is an active decision that adds tracking error.

## Mean-Variance Optimization (MVO)
MVO uses expected returns, standard deviations, and correlations to identify the **efficient frontier**: the set of portfolios with the highest expected return for a given level of risk. The **minimum-variance portfolio** sits at the leftmost point of the frontier.

Limitations of MVO: sensitive to input assumptions, often produces concentrated portfolios, and ignores parameter estimation error.

## Black-Litterman Model
The **Black-Litterman model** begins with market equilibrium returns (implied by market cap weights) and adjusts them based on the investor''s views with a degree of confidence. The result is a more diversified, stable set of portfolio weights than raw MVO.

## Rebalancing
As asset prices drift, portfolio weights diverge from SAA targets. **Rebalancing** restores target weights and controls risk. Common approaches include:
- **Calendar rebalancing**: rebalance on a fixed schedule.
- **Threshold (corridor) rebalancing**: rebalance when any weight drifts beyond a set band around its target.

### Tip
- Wider rebalancing corridors reduce trading costs but allow greater style drift; narrower corridors maintain tighter risk control at higher cost.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-performance-evaluation-m01', 'cfa-portfolio-management-performance-evaluation', 'Performance Evaluation & Attribution', '**Performance evaluation** measures how well a portfolio achieved its objectives relative to risk taken and an appropriate benchmark.

## Risk-Adjusted Performance Measures
| Measure | Formula | Risk measure used |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Sharpe ratio | (Rp - Rf) / sigma_p | Total risk (standard deviation) |
| Treynor ratio | (Rp - Rf) / beta_p | Systematic risk (beta) |
| Jensen''s alpha | Rp - [Rf + beta(Rm - Rf)] | Systematic risk |
| M-squared (M2) | (Sharpe_p - Sharpe_m) * sigma_m + Rf | Total risk |
| Information ratio | Active return / Tracking error | Active risk |

- **Sharpe ratio** is best for evaluating a total portfolio; **Treynor ratio** is better for a sub-portfolio within a diversified fund.
- **Jensen''s alpha** measures excess return relative to CAPM expectation; positive alpha indicates manager skill.
- The **information ratio (IR)** measures consistency of active management: IR = (Rp - Rb) / TE.

## Performance Attribution: BHB Model
The **Brinson, Hood, and Beebower (BHB)** model decomposes active return into three components:
- **Allocation effect**: did the manager over/underweight the right asset classes?
- **Selection effect**: did the manager pick better securities within each class?
- **Interaction effect**: combined impact of allocation and selection decisions.

## Benchmark Requirements
A valid benchmark should be **specified in advance**, **investable**, **measurable**, **appropriate**, **reflective of current investment opinion**, **accountable**, and **unambiguous**.

### Tip
- The Sharpe ratio uses total risk and is the right metric when the portfolio is the investor''s only investment. The Treynor ratio and Jensen''s alpha use beta and are appropriate when the portfolio is one of many holdings.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-risk-management-m01', 'cfa-portfolio-management-risk-management', 'Risk Management in Portfolios', '**Portfolio risk management** is the process of identifying, measuring, and controlling the risks that could prevent a portfolio from meeting its objectives.

## Risk Budgeting
**Risk budgeting** allocates a total risk budget (expressed as volatility, VaR, or tracking error) across portfolio positions or asset classes. Each allocation consumes part of the budget; managers seek to maximize return per unit of risk budget consumed.

## Value at Risk (VaR)
**VaR** estimates the maximum loss expected over a given time horizon at a specified confidence level (e.g., 95% or 99%). For example, a daily 95% VaR of $1 million means losses exceeding $1 million are expected only 5% of trading days.

| VaR Method | Approach |
| --- | --- |
| Parametric (variance-covariance) | Assumes normal returns; uses mean and standard deviation |
| Historical simulation | Uses actual past return distribution; no normality assumption |
| Monte Carlo simulation | Generates thousands of scenarios; flexible but computationally intensive |

Limitations: VaR does not describe the magnitude of losses beyond the threshold; **Conditional VaR (CVaR)** or **Expected Shortfall** addresses this by measuring the average loss in the tail.

## Drawdown Management
**Maximum drawdown** is the largest peak-to-trough decline over a period. Drawdown limits are often set in mandates to protect capital and manage investor psychology. **Time to recovery** measures how long it takes to return to the prior peak.

## Factor Risk
Portfolio risk can be decomposed into **factor exposures** (market, size, value, momentum) and **idiosyncratic risk**. Factor-based risk models help identify unintended bets and improve diversification.

### Tip
- VaR is additive only under normal distributions with linear positions. For portfolios with options or fat tails, historical simulation or Monte Carlo methods are more reliable.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-ethics-independence-objectivity-m01', 'cfa-ethics-independence-objectivity', 'Independence, Objectivity & Misrepresentation', '**Standard I(B) Independence and Objectivity** requires members to use reasonable care and judgement to maintain independence and avoid anything that could compromise it.

## Key rules
- **Gifts from clients** (for past work) may be accepted but **must be disclosed** to the employer. **Gifts/benefits from third parties** seeking to influence (e.g. an issuer paying for analyst travel) should be **declined** — pay your own way; modest, non-influential items only.
- **Buy-side and sell-side** pressure (issuers, own firm''s IB division) must not bias research. Firms should use **firewalls**.
- **Credit-rating agencies**: keep rating and fee-negotiation functions separate.

## Standard I(C) Misrepresentation
- No untrue statements; no guaranteeing returns on volatile investments.
- **Plagiarism** = using others'' work without attribution. You may use recognized statistical/factual data (e.g. from a government source) without attribution, but not another''s analysis or models.
- Misrepresenting qualifications or performance also violates I(C).

> Disclose conflicts, decline influence-seeking perks, and attribute others'' work.', 2, 5),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-distributions-m01', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-distributions', 'Common Probability Distributions', '## Discrete vs continuous
- **Binomial**: n independent Bernoulli trials, success prob p. Mean = **np**, variance = **np(1−p)**.
- **Uniform (continuous)**: equal density over [a,b].
- **Normal**: symmetric, fully described by **μ and σ²**. ~**68% / 95% / 99%** of observations fall within 1 / 2 / 3 σ (precisely 1.65σ → 90%, 1.96σ → 95%, 2.58σ → 99%).

## Standard normal & safety-first
- **Z = (x − μ) / σ** standardizes any normal variable.
- **Roy''s safety-first ratio** = (E[Rp] − R_threshold) / σ_p. **Maximize** it; the optimal portfolio minimizes P(return < threshold). The **Sharpe ratio** is the special case where the threshold is the risk-free rate.

## Lognormal
- If ln(X) is normal, X is **lognormal** — bounded below by 0 and **right-skewed**, so it is used to model **asset prices** (which can''t go negative). Continuously compounded returns are normally distributed.

| Distribution | Use |
| --- | --- |
| Binomial | # of defaults / up-moves |
| Normal | returns |
| Lognormal | prices |', 2, 5),
  ('cfa-economics-currency-parity-m01', 'cfa-economics-currency-parity', 'Currency Exchange Rate Parity Conditions', 'Quote convention used here: **price/base** (e.g. USD/EUR = 1.20 means 1 EUR costs 1.20 USD).

## The parity conditions
- **Covered interest rate parity (CIRP)** holds by arbitrage: the **forward** premium/discount exactly offsets the interest-rate differential. Forward ≈ Spot × (1 + i_price)/(1 + i_base). The **higher-yield currency trades at a forward discount.**
- **Uncovered interest rate parity (UIRP)**: expected spot change = interest-rate differential (no risk premium). Often fails short-term ("forward rate bias" / carry trade profits).
- **Purchasing power parity (PPP)**: **relative PPP** — the currency of the higher-inflation country **depreciates** by the inflation differential. **Absolute PPP** rests on the law of one price.
- **International Fisher effect**: nominal rate differentials reflect **expected inflation** differentials (equal real rates).

> Memory hook: CIRP is the only one enforced by arbitrage and therefore always holds; the others are equilibrium tendencies.', 2, 5),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-reporting-quality-m01', 'cfa-financial-reporting-reporting-quality', 'Financial Reporting Quality', '**Reporting quality ≠ results (earnings) quality.** High *reporting* quality = decision-useful, faithful representation. High *earnings* quality = sustainable, high-return results. You can have high reporting quality reporting low (but honest) earnings.

## Quality spectrum (high → low)
1. GAAP, decision-useful, sustainable & adequate returns
2. GAAP, decision-useful, but **low earnings quality**
3. GAAP, but **biased/aggressive choices** ("earnings management")
4. GAAP, but **earnings management** (e.g. real activities like channel stuffing)
5. **Non-compliant** accounting
6. **Fraudulent** / fictitious transactions

## Red flags & motivations
- Motivations: meet **analyst forecasts**, hit **debt covenants**, boost **management compensation**.
- Aggressive choices: **early revenue recognition**, capitalizing costs that should be expensed, lengthening depreciable lives, understating reserves/allowances.
- **Beneish M-score** and accruals ratios help detect manipulation; large **accruals** relative to cash flow signal lower earnings quality.', 2, 5),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-working-capital-m01', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-working-capital', 'Working Capital & Liquidity Management', '## The cash conversion cycle (CCC)
**CCC = DSO + DOH − DPO**
- **DSO** (days sales outstanding) = 365 / receivables turnover
- **DOH** (days of inventory on hand) = 365 / inventory turnover
- **DPO** (days payables outstanding) = 365 / payables turnover

A **shorter CCC** ties up less cash — better liquidity. A negative CCC (e.g. some retailers) means suppliers fund operations.

## Liquidity ratios
- **Current ratio** = current assets / current liabilities
- **Quick (acid-test)** = (cash + marketable securities + receivables) / current liabilities — excludes inventory
- **Cash ratio** = (cash + marketable securities) / current liabilities

## Short-term funding & investing
- Sources: lines of credit (committed > uncommitted > revolving), **commercial paper**, factoring receivables.
- **Cost of trade credit** if forgoing a discount "2/10 net 30": (1 + 0.02/0.98)^(365/20) − 1 ≈ **44.6%** — usually worth taking the discount.
- Invest excess cash in safe, liquid instruments; measure with **money-market yields** (bond-equivalent yield for comparability).', 2, 5),
  ('cfa-equity-residual-income-valuation-m01', 'cfa-equity-residual-income-valuation', 'Residual Income & Private Company Valuation', '## Residual income (RI)
**RI = Net income − (Equity charge)** = NI − (r_e × Beginning book value of equity). It is the profit *after* charging for the cost of equity.

**Value = B₀ + Σ RI_t / (1+r)^t.** With constant growth:
**V₀ = B₀ + [(ROE − r) × B₀] / (r − g)**

- RI models recognize value **earlier** than DDM/FCF and rely less on a terminal value — useful for firms that **don''t pay dividends** or have unpredictable cash flows.
- Weakness: relies on **clean-surplus accounting** and book-value/accounting quality.

## Private company valuation — three approaches
1. **Income** (free cash flow / capitalized cash flow / excess earnings)
2. **Market** (guideline public companies, guideline transactions, prior transactions)
3. **Asset-based** (net asset value)

Apply a **discount for lack of marketability (DLOM)** and a **discount for lack of control** (or a **control premium** for controlling stakes). Private-company discount rates are higher — add size and company-specific risk premia.', 2, 5),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-spot-forward-rates-m01', 'cfa-fixed-income-spot-forward-rates', 'Spot, Forward & Par Rates', '## Definitions
- **Spot rate** z(t): YTM of a zero-coupon bond maturing at t. Price = Σ CF_t / (1 + z_t)^t.
- **Par rate**: the coupon rate that prices a bond at par given the spot curve.
- **Forward rate** f(a,b−a): rate agreed today for a loan starting at a, ending at b.

## The no-arbitrage link
**(1 + z_b)^b = (1 + z_a)^a × (1 + f(a, b−a))^(b−a)**

Example: (1+z₂)² = (1+z₁)(1+f(1,1)). So the implied **1y1y forward** = (1+z₂)²/(1+z₁) − 1.

## Curve shapes
- **Upward-sloping** spot curve ⇒ forward rates **above** spot rates; par < spot at long end.
- A bond''s **return = spot rate** only if the curve is flat and unchanged ("rolling down the curve" adds return when upward-sloping and stable).
- **Forward rates are the market''s break-even** future spot rates: you profit from buying long vs. rolling short only if future spots come in **below** today''s forwards.', 2, 5),
  ('cfa-derivatives-binomial-option-valuation-m01', 'cfa-derivatives-binomial-option-valuation', 'Binomial Option Valuation', '## One-period binomial model
Underlying S can move **up** to S·u or **down** to S·d. Value the option by **risk-neutral pricing** (no real probabilities needed):

**Risk-neutral up-probability:** π = (1 + r − d) / (u − d)

**Option value:** c₀ = [π·c_u + (1 − π)·c_d] / (1 + r)

where c_u, c_d are the option payoffs in each state.

## Key ideas
- **No-arbitrage = replication**: a portfolio of the underlying plus borrowing/lending reproduces the option payoff; the option must cost the same (the **hedge ratio** n = (c_u − c_d)/(S·u − S·d)).
- **Real probabilities don''t appear** — investors'' risk preferences are irrelevant once we hedge.
- **American options**: at each node compare the continuation value with the **early-exercise** value; early exercise can matter for **American puts** and calls on dividend-paying stocks.
- Multi-period: build the tree forward, then discount payoffs back node-by-node.', 2, 5),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-private-debt-m01', 'cfa-alternative-investments-private-debt', 'Private Debt & Direct Lending', '**Private debt** = loans/bonds extended directly to companies (often mid-market) outside public markets, typically by funds rather than banks.

## Main strategies
- **Direct lending** — senior, often **floating-rate**, secured loans to private firms; income-oriented.
- **Mezzanine debt** — subordinated, higher yield, often with **equity warrants** (hybrid).
- **Venture debt** — loans to venture-backed startups, alongside VC equity.
- **Distressed debt** — buying the debt of troubled firms, sometimes to convert to control ("loan-to-own").
- **Specialty/structured** — infrastructure debt, real estate debt, fund financing.

## Risk & return characteristics
- Return drivers: **interest income** + fees (origination, prepayment) + capital gains in distressed/mezzanine.
- Investors are compensated for **illiquidity** and **credit risk**; floating rates reduce duration risk.
- Key risks: **default/credit risk**, weakening **covenants** ("cov-lite"), illiquidity, and leverage at the fund level.
- Grew rapidly post-2008 as **bank lending retrenched** under tighter capital rules.', 2, 5),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-behavioral-finance-m01', 'cfa-portfolio-management-behavioral-finance', 'Behavioral Finance & Biases', 'Behavioral finance relaxes the "rational economic man" assumption. Biases are split into **cognitive errors** (faulty reasoning — can be reduced with education/data) and **emotional biases** (feeling-based — harder to correct, accommodate them).

## Cognitive errors
- **Belief perseverance**: conservatism, **confirmation**, representativeness, illusion of control, hindsight.
- **Information processing**: **anchoring**, mental accounting, framing, availability.

## Emotional biases
- **Loss aversion** (losses hurt ~2× gains → **disposition effect**: sell winners, hold losers), **overconfidence**, **self-control**, **status quo**, **endowment**, **regret aversion**.

## Market-level effects
- **Prospect theory**: value is judged vs. a reference point; people are risk-averse in gains, risk-seeking in losses.
- Anomalies: **momentum**, **bubbles/crashes**, value/size effects, **herding**.
- Advisers should **moderate** cognitive biases and **adapt to** emotional biases when building the IPS.', 2, 5),
  ('cfa-ethics-duties-to-employers-m01', 'cfa-ethics-duties-to-employers', 'Duties to Employers (Standard IV)', '**Standard IV — Duties to Employers** governs a member''s conduct toward the firm that employs them.

## The three sub-standards
| Sub-standard | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| **IV(A) Loyalty** | Act for the employer''s benefit; do not deprive it of your skills, divulge confidential information, or harm it. You may prepare to compete, but not compete while employed. |
| **IV(B) Additional Compensation Arrangements** | Do not accept gifts, benefits or compensation that competes with, or creates a conflict with, the employer''s interest **without written consent** from all parties. |
| **IV(C) Responsibilities of Supervisors** | Make reasonable efforts to **prevent and detect violations** by anyone under your supervision; ensure adequate compliance procedures exist. |

## Key points
- **Loyalty** does not prohibit whistle-blowing that protects clients or the market — protecting the integrity of the market can override loyalty to the employer.
- Independent practice for compensation while employed requires employer consent.
- A supervisor who lacks the authority or tools to enforce compliance should **decline supervisory responsibility** in writing until adequate procedures are adopted.
- ''Skills and abilities'' developed on the job generally belong to the member, but client lists, files and confidential data belong to the employer.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-ethics-investment-analysis-recommendations-m01', 'cfa-ethics-investment-analysis-recommendations', 'Investment Analysis & Recommendations (Standard V)', '**Standard V — Investment Analysis, Recommendations, and Actions** governs the research and advice members provide.

## The three sub-standards
| Sub-standard | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| **V(A) Diligence and Reasonable Basis** | Exercise diligence, independence and thoroughness; have a **reasonable and adequate basis**, supported by research, for any analysis, recommendation or action. |
| **V(B) Communication with Clients & Prospects** | **Distinguish fact from opinion**, disclose the basic format and general principles of the investment process, and identify limitations and risks. |
| **V(C) Record Retention** | Keep records supporting analyses, recommendations and actions. Absent a regulatory requirement, CFA Institute recommends a **7-year** minimum. |

## Key points
- When relying on a third party''s (or group) research, a member must make **reasonable efforts to verify** its soundness.
- Members must communicate significant **changes** in risk characteristics and identify which factors are material.
- Records are the property of the **firm**; a member cannot take them to a new employer.
- ''Reasonable basis'' scales with the significance of the recommendation and the sophistication of the client.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-descriptive-statistics-m01', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-descriptive-statistics', 'Descriptive Statistics & Data', '**Descriptive statistics** summarise a data set''s central tendency, dispersion and shape.

## Central tendency
- **Arithmetic mean** — the simple average; sensitive to outliers.
- **Median** — the middle value; robust to outliers.
- **Mode** — the most frequent value.
- **Geometric mean** — used for compound growth/returns; always **≤ arithmetic mean**.
- **Harmonic mean** — used for averaging ratios (e.g., cost averaging).

## Dispersion
- **Range, mean absolute deviation, variance, standard deviation.**
- **Coefficient of variation (CV) = σ / mean** — risk per unit of return; **lower is better**.

## Shape
| Feature | Description |
| --- | --- |
| **Positive (right) skew** | Long right tail; **mean > median > mode** |
| **Negative (left) skew** | Long left tail; **mean < median < mode** |
| **Kurtosis** | ''Peakedness''/tails. **Leptokurtic** (excess > 0) has fat tails; **platykurtic** (< 0) thin tails; normal is **mesokurtic** (excess = 0) |

- **Quantiles** (quartiles, deciles, percentiles) divide data into equal parts.
- **Chebyshev''s inequality**: at least 1 − 1/k² of observations lie within k standard deviations of the mean.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-rates-of-return-m01', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-rates-of-return', 'Rates of Return: TWR & MWR', '## Holding period return
```
HPR = (P_end − P_begin + income) / P_begin
```

## Money-weighted vs time-weighted return
| Measure | Definition | Sensitive to cash-flow timing? |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Money-weighted return (MWR)** | The **IRR** of all portfolio cash flows | **Yes** — reflects the investor''s timing/size of contributions |
| **Time-weighted return (TWR)** | The **geometric mean** of sub-period returns | **No** — neutral to external flows |

- The **TWR** is preferred for evaluating a **manager**, because the manager usually does not control the timing of client deposits and withdrawals.
- The **MWR** better reflects the **investor''s** actual experience.

## Mean returns
- **Geometric mean return** measures compound growth; it is **≤ the arithmetic mean** (equal only when all returns are identical).
- **Arithmetic mean** is the best estimate of a single-period expected return.

## Adjustments
- **Gross vs net** (before/after fees), **pre-tax vs after-tax**, and **nominal vs real** (adjusted for inflation) returns. A **leveraged** return magnifies both gains and losses.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-economics-demand-supply-elasticity-m01', 'cfa-economics-demand-supply-elasticity', 'Demand, Supply & Elasticity', 'The **law of demand** says quantity demanded falls as price rises (downward-sloping demand); the **law of supply** says quantity supplied rises with price. **Equilibrium** is where the two curves intersect.

## Price elasticity of demand
```
E_d = (% change in quantity demanded) / (% change in price)
```
| |E_d| | Interpretation |
| --- | --- |
| **> 1** | Elastic (quantity very responsive) |
| **< 1** | Inelastic (quantity insensitive) |
| **= 1** | Unit elastic |

Demand is **more elastic** when there are close substitutes, the good is a luxury, it is a large share of the budget, and over a **longer time horizon**.

## Other elasticities
- **Income elasticity**: positive for **normal** goods, negative for **inferior** goods.
- **Cross-price elasticity**: positive for **substitutes**, negative for **complements**.

## Surplus
- **Consumer surplus** = value to buyers − price paid; **producer surplus** = price received − cost. Total surplus is maximised at the competitive equilibrium.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-economics-aggregate-output-growth-m01', 'cfa-economics-aggregate-output-growth', 'Aggregate Output, Demand & Growth', '## Aggregate demand and supply
- **Aggregate demand (AD)** = C + I + G + (X − M); it slopes downward in price-level/output space.
- **Short-run aggregate supply (SRAS)** slopes upward; **long-run aggregate supply (LRAS)** is vertical at **potential GDP** (full employment).
- Shifts in AD or AS move the economy''s equilibrium output and price level.

## The business cycle
Phases: **expansion → peak → contraction (recession) → trough**, and back. Inflation tends to build near peaks; unemployment rises in contractions.

## Sources of economic growth
Output is described by a **production function** using **labour, capital, and technology**:
- **Capital deepening** — more capital per worker (subject to diminishing returns).
- **Total factor productivity (TFP)** — technological progress and efficiency; the main driver of sustained per-capita growth.

## Key measures
- **Real vs nominal GDP** (deflated by the GDP deflator).
- **Potential GDP** — the sustainable, non-inflationary level of output.
- The **Solow (neoclassical) model** shows economies converging to a steady state where only technology raises long-run per-capita output.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-standards-framework-m01', 'cfa-financial-reporting-standards-framework', 'Financial Reporting Standards (IFRS vs GAAP)', 'Financial reporting aims to give **useful information** to capital providers. The two dominant frameworks are **IFRS** (set by the **IASB**) and **US GAAP** (set by the **FASB**).

## Qualitative characteristics (IFRS Conceptual Framework)
| Fundamental | Enhancing |
| --- | --- |
| **Relevance**, **Faithful representation** | Comparability, Verifiability, Timeliness, Understandability |

- **Constraints**: cost (benefit vs cost) and materiality.
- Underlying assumptions: **accrual basis** and **going concern**.

## Elements
Assets, liabilities, equity, income, and expenses.

## Selected IFRS vs US GAAP differences
| Item | IFRS | US GAAP |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Inventory (LIFO)** | Prohibited | Permitted |
| **Inventory write-down reversal** | Allowed | Prohibited |
| **Development costs** | Capitalise if criteria met | Generally expensed |
| **Revaluation of PP&E** | Allowed (revaluation model) | Not allowed |

- IFRS is more **principles-based**; US GAAP is more **rules-based**. Both are moving toward convergence in key areas (e.g., revenue recognition, leases).', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-liabilities-leases-m01', 'cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-liabilities-leases', 'Long-term Liabilities & Leases', '## Bonds payable
A bond is issued at **par, a premium, or a discount** depending on how its coupon compares with the market rate at issuance:
| Coupon vs market rate | Issued at |
| --- | --- |
| Coupon > market | **Premium** |
| Coupon = market | **Par** |
| Coupon < market | **Discount** |

- Interest expense is recognised using the **effective interest method**: interest expense = carrying value × market yield at issuance.
- For a **discount** bond the carrying value rises toward par over time; for a **premium** bond it falls toward par.

## Leases
- A **finance (capital) lease** transfers substantially all the risks and rewards of ownership; the lessee records a **right-of-use asset** and a **lease liability**.
- Under **IFRS 16**, lessees recognise nearly **all** leases on the balance sheet (a single model), removing most operating-lease off-balance-sheet treatment.
- An **operating lease** (under US GAAP for lessees) still records a right-of-use asset and liability but expenses a single straight-line lease cost.
- Covenants restrict the borrower to protect lenders; breaching them can trigger default.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting-m01', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting', 'Capital Budgeting: NPV & IRR', '**Capital budgeting** evaluates long-term investment projects. Analysts use **incremental after-tax cash flows**, ignore **sunk costs**, and include **opportunity costs** and **externalities**.

## Decision rules
| Method | Rule | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Net Present Value (NPV)** | Accept if **NPV > 0** | PV of cash flows − initial outlay; adds value directly |
| **Internal Rate of Return (IRR)** | Accept if **IRR > required return** | The rate that makes NPV = 0 |
| **Payback period** | Shorter is better | Ignores the time value of money and cash flows after payback |
| **Discounted payback** | Shorter is better | Uses discounted cash flows but still ignores later flows |
| **Profitability index (PI)** | Accept if **PI > 1** | PI = PV of future cash flows / initial outlay = 1 + NPV/outlay |

## NPV vs IRR
- For **independent** projects, NPV and IRR give the same accept/reject decision.
- For **mutually exclusive** projects they can conflict (due to scale or timing). **Always prefer NPV** — it assumes reinvestment at the cost of capital and measures value added, whereas IRR assumes reinvestment at the IRR and can give multiple or misleading answers.
- A project''s NPV equals the theoretical increase in shareholder wealth.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-business-models-m01', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-business-models', 'Business Models & Structures', '## Business model
A **business model** describes how a firm creates and captures value: **who** the customers are, **what** is offered, **how** it is delivered (channels), and the **pricing/revenue** approach. Examples: **subscription**, **freemium**, **razor-and-blades**, **licensing**, and platform/network models.

## Legal structures
| Form | Liability | Life / features |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Sole proprietorship** | Unlimited personal liability | Simplest; owner and firm are one |
| **Partnership** | Partners liable (general) | Shared ownership; limited partners have limited liability |
| **Corporation** | **Limited liability** for shareholders | Separate legal entity, perpetual life, easier capital raising, but potential double taxation |

## Stakeholders
A firm balances **shareholders** (owners) with other **stakeholders** — creditors, employees, customers, suppliers, government and the community. Corporate governance manages the conflicts among them.

## Company types
- **Growth** companies reinvest heavily; **defensive** companies have stable demand across the cycle; **cyclical** companies'' fortunes swing with the economy. Understanding the model clarifies a firm''s revenue durability, cost structure and competitive position.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-equity-securities-types-m01', 'cfa-equity-securities-types', 'Equity Securities: Types & Features', 'Equity represents a **residual ownership claim** on a company''s assets and income.

## Common vs preferred
| Feature | Common shares | Preferred shares |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Voting** | Usually yes | Usually no |
| **Dividend** | Discretionary, variable | Fixed, paid before common |
| **Claim priority** | Last (residual) | Ahead of common, behind debt |

## Preferred-share variations
- **Cumulative** — unpaid (in-arrears) dividends accumulate and must be paid before common dividends; **non-cumulative** do not.
- **Participating** — may receive extra dividends beyond the fixed rate.
- **Convertible** — can convert into common shares.
- **Callable / putable** — issuer can redeem / holder can sell back.

## Other features
- **Public vs private equity** — private equity is illiquid and not exchange-listed.
- **Depository receipts (ADRs/GDRs)** let foreign shares trade on a domestic exchange in local currency.
- **Book value** is accounting equity; **market value** is the price investors pay. **ROE** (net income ÷ equity) links the two and gauges profitability.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-equity-security-market-indexes-m01', 'cfa-equity-security-market-indexes', 'Security Market Indexes', 'A **security market index** represents the value of a group of securities, used as a **benchmark**, a market gauge, and the basis for index funds and ETFs.

## Weighting methods
| Method | How weighted | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Price-weighted** | By share price (÷ divisor) | High-priced stocks dominate; affected by splits (e.g., Dow Jones) |
| **Market-cap-weighted** | By market capitalisation | Large caps dominate; usually **float-adjusted** (e.g., S&P 500) |
| **Equal-weighted** | Each constituent equally | Requires frequent rebalancing; tilts to small caps |
| **Fundamental-weighted** | By fundamentals (sales, book value) | Breaks the price-weight link; a value tilt |

## Return types
- A **price-return** index reflects only price changes.
- A **total-return** index also reinvests **dividends/income**, so it is higher over time.

## Maintenance
- **Rebalancing** resets weights to target; **reconstitution** changes the membership. Market-cap indexes largely rebalance themselves as prices move.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-markets-instruments-m01', 'cfa-fixed-income-markets-instruments', 'Fixed-Income Markets & Instruments', '## Issuers and sectors
Bonds are issued by **sovereign governments**, **non-sovereign** (local) governments, **quasi-government** agencies, **supranationals** (e.g., the World Bank), and **corporations** (including financial institutions).

## Money vs capital market
| Market | Maturity | Examples |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Money market** | ≤ 1 year | Treasury bills, commercial paper, CDs, repos |
| **Capital market** | > 1 year | Government and corporate bonds |

## Primary vs secondary
- **Primary market**: new issues via **underwritten** offerings, best-efforts, **auctions** (common for government bonds), or **private placements**.
- **Secondary market**: trading of existing bonds, largely **over the counter**.

## Instruments
- **Fixed-rate** bonds pay a constant coupon; **floating-rate notes (FRNs)** pay a **reference rate + spread** that resets periodically; **zero-coupon** bonds pay only par at maturity.
- **Repurchase agreements (repos)** are short-term collateralised loans — sell a security now and agree to buy it back later at a higher price (the repo rate).', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-interest-rate-risk-measures-m01', 'cfa-fixed-income-interest-rate-risk-measures', 'Interest Rate Risk Measures', 'Duration and related measures quantify a bond''s price sensitivity to yield changes.

## Money duration & PVBP
- **Money (dollar) duration** = **modified duration × full price** (per 100 of par or per position). It gives the money change in price for a 1% (100 bp) yield change.
- The **price value of a basis point (PVBP / PV01)** is the money-price change for a **1 basis point** move — money duration × 0.0001.

## Effective vs analytical duration
- **Modified duration** works for option-free bonds (a fixed cash-flow schedule).
- **Effective duration** must be used for bonds with **embedded options** (callable/putable, MBS), because their cash flows change with yields.

## Key rate (partial) duration
Measures sensitivity to a change in yield at a **specific maturity point** on the curve, capturing non-parallel shifts that a single duration misses.

## Duration and the horizon
When a bond''s **Macaulay duration equals the investment horizon**, price risk and reinvestment risk offset — the basis of **immunisation**. **Convexity** refines the duration estimate for larger yield moves.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-derivatives-markets-instruments-m01', 'cfa-derivatives-markets-instruments', 'Derivative Markets & Instruments', 'A **derivative** is a financial instrument whose value derives from an **underlying** asset, rate or index.

## Two broad families
| Family | Instruments | Payoff |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Forward commitments** | Forwards, futures, swaps | Both parties **obligated** to transact |
| **Contingent claims** | Options | Buyer has the **right, not the obligation** |

## Exchange-traded vs OTC
| | Exchange-traded | Over the counter (OTC) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Terms | **Standardised** | **Customised** |
| Counterparty risk | Low (**central clearing**) | Higher (bilateral) |
| Examples | Futures, listed options | Forwards, swaps |

## Purposes and criticisms
- **Benefits**: risk transfer/hedging, **price discovery**, lower transaction costs, and efficient exposure with less capital.
- **Concerns**: **leverage** can magnify losses, complexity, and potential **systemic risk**.
- A **clearinghouse** guarantees performance and requires **margin**, reducing default risk on exchanges.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-derivatives-arbitrage-cost-of-carry-m01', 'cfa-derivatives-arbitrage-cost-of-carry', 'Arbitrage & Cost of Carry', '## Arbitrage and the law of one price
**Arbitrage** is a **riskless profit** earned with no net investment by exploiting a price discrepancy between equivalent assets. The **law of one price** says identical cash flows must have the same price; if they don''t, arbitrageurs trade until prices realign. This **no-arbitrage principle** underlies all derivatives pricing.

## Replication
Any derivative''s payoff can be **replicated** by a portfolio of the underlying and risk-free borrowing/lending. Because the replicating portfolio and the derivative have identical payoffs, they must have the same price.

## Cost-of-carry forward pricing
For a forward on an asset with financing cost r over time T:
```
F₀ = S₀ × (1 + r)^T − FV(benefits) + FV(costs)
```
- **Carry costs** (storage, insurance) **raise** the forward price.
- **Carry benefits** (dividends, coupons, convenience yield) **lower** it.
- If the observed forward deviates from this, a **cash-and-carry** (or reverse) arbitrage locks in a riskless profit — buying the cheap side and selling the expensive side.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-overview-m01', 'cfa-alternative-investments-overview', 'Alternative Investments: Overview & Fees', '**Alternative investments** fall outside traditional stocks, bonds and cash, and include **hedge funds**, **private capital** (private equity and private debt), **real estate**, **infrastructure**, and **natural resources/commodities**.

## Common characteristics
- **Illiquidity** and long lock-ups, **less regulation** and transparency, specialised strategies, and often **low correlation** with traditional assets (diversification), but **higher fees**.

## Fee structure
- Typically **''2 and 20''** — a ~2% **management fee** plus a ~20% **incentive (performance) fee**.
- A **hurdle rate** is a minimum return before incentive fees apply.
- A **high-water mark** means performance fees are charged only on gains **above the prior peak**, so investors don''t pay twice for recovering losses.

## Fund structures
- Usually **limited partnerships**: the **general partner (GP)** manages; **limited partners (LPs)** invest.
- Private funds draw down **committed capital** over time and often show a **J-curve** (early negative returns before gains). Redemptions may face **lock-ups and gates**. Thorough **due diligence** is essential.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-digital-assets-m01', 'cfa-alternative-investments-digital-assets', 'Digital Assets & Tokenisation', '**Digital assets** are assets that exist digitally, typically recorded on a **distributed ledger (blockchain)**.

## Distributed ledger technology (DLT)
A **blockchain** is a decentralised, cryptographically secured ledger shared across a network, made trustworthy by a **consensus mechanism** (e.g., **proof of work** or **proof of stake**) and by **immutability** — records are extremely difficult to alter.

## Types of digital assets
| Asset | Description |
| --- | --- |
| **Cryptocurrencies** | Decentralised digital money (e.g., Bitcoin) with no central issuer |
| **Stablecoins** | Tokens pegged to a reference (often a fiat currency) |
| **Tokens** | Utility, security, or governance tokens created on a blockchain |
| **NFTs** | Non-fungible tokens representing unique digital items |

- **Smart contracts** are self-executing code that runs when conditions are met.
- **Tokenisation** puts claims on real assets (e.g., real estate) onto a blockchain to improve divisibility and liquidity.

## Investment forms and risks
Investors gain exposure directly (coins/tokens), or via **futures** and **funds**. Key risks: extreme **volatility**, **regulatory uncertainty**, **custody/security** (hacking, lost keys), and fraud.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-process-m01', 'cfa-portfolio-management-process', 'The Portfolio Management Process', 'Portfolio management is a continuous, three-step process:
| Step | Activities |
| --- | --- |
| **1. Planning** | Understand the client, set objectives and constraints, and write the **Investment Policy Statement (IPS)** |
| **2. Execution** | Decide **asset allocation**, then security analysis and selection; construct the portfolio |
| **3. Feedback** | **Monitor** and **rebalance**; evaluate **performance** against the benchmark |

## Types of investors
- **Individual** investors vary widely in goals and risk tolerance.
- **Institutional** investors include **pension plans** (defined benefit vs defined contribution), **endowments and foundations** (long horizons, spending needs), **insurance companies** and **banks** (liability-driven), and **sovereign wealth funds**.

## Pooled vehicles
- **Mutual funds** (open-end trade at NAV; closed-end trade on an exchange), **ETFs** (exchange-traded, usually index-tracking, tax-efficient), **separately managed accounts (SMAs)**, and **hedge/private funds**.
- **Diversification** across imperfectly correlated assets reduces risk for a given return — the core rationale for holding a portfolio rather than single securities.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-fintech-m01', 'cfa-portfolio-management-fintech', 'Fintech in Investment Management', '**Fintech** is technology-driven innovation in financial products and services. Several strands are transforming investment management.

## Big data
Characterised by the **''three Vs'' — volume, velocity, and variety**. Sources span **traditional** data (financial statements, prices) and **alternative** data (social media, satellite/sensor data, credit-card records).

## Artificial intelligence & machine learning
- **Machine learning** finds patterns in data: **supervised** learning (labelled data → prediction/classification), **unsupervised** learning (finding structure, e.g., clustering), and **deep learning** (neural networks).
- **Natural language processing (NLP)** extracts information from text (news, filings, sentiment).

## Applications and risks
- **Algorithmic and high-frequency trading** execute orders by rule at speed.
- **Robo-advisers** deliver low-cost automated advice and asset allocation.
- **Distributed ledger technology** enables faster settlement and tokenisation.
- Considerations: **data quality**, **overfitting** models to noise, privacy and model risk.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-ethics-ethics-and-trust-m01', 'cfa-ethics-ethics-and-trust', 'Ethics & Trust in the Investment Profession', 'The investment industry runs on **trust**: clients hand over capital and rely on professionals to act in their interest. **Ethics** are the shared beliefs about what is good/acceptable conduct — they go **beyond the law**, which sets only a minimum.

## Ethics vs legal standards
Some acts are legal but unethical, and some ethical acts may be illegal. High-quality conduct meets the **stricter** of law or ethics.

## Why conduct lapses
- **Situational influences** (money, loyalty, peer pressure) and cognitive limitations can crowd out principles.
- **Overconfidence** leads people to overrate their own ethical judgement.

## A framework for ethical decision-making
1. **Identify** the relevant facts, stakeholders, duties, and conflicts.
2. **Consider** guidance (the Code and Standards), alternatives, and consequences.
3. **Decide and act.**
4. **Reflect** on the outcome.

## The role of the profession
A **profession** serves society and holds members to a code. The **CFA Institute** promotes ethics and professional excellence. Unethical behaviour by a few damages trust for all — trust is hard to build and easily lost, and its loss raises costs and harms markets.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-portfolio-mathematics-m01', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-portfolio-mathematics', 'Portfolio Mathematics', '## Expected return
A portfolio''s expected return is the **weighted average** of the assets'' expected returns:
```
E(Rp) = w₁E(R₁) + w₂E(R₂) + …
```

## Risk depends on covariance
Portfolio **variance is not** a simple weighted average — it depends on how assets move together. For two assets:
```
σ²p = w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + 2w₁w₂ρ₁₂σ₁σ₂
```
- **Covariance** measures the co-movement of two returns; **correlation (ρ)** standardises it to a range of **−1 to +1**.
- `Cov(1,2) = ρ₁₂ × σ₁ × σ₂`.

## Diversification
The lower the correlation, the greater the **diversification benefit** — risk falls below the weighted average of the individual risks. With **ρ = +1** there is no diversification; with **ρ = −1** risk can be driven toward zero. Because covariance drives portfolio risk, adding an asset with low or negative correlation reduces total portfolio variance.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-simulation-methods-m01', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-simulation-methods', 'Simulation Methods', 'Simulation models uncertainty by generating many possible outcomes.

## Monte Carlo simulation
Repeatedly draws **random values** for the uncertain inputs (from assumed distributions), computes the result each time, and builds a **distribution of outcomes**.
- **Uses**: valuing complex options, estimating **Value at Risk**, retirement/financial planning, and stress testing.
- **Strengths**: handles complex, path-dependent problems with no closed-form solution.
- **Limitations**: results are only as good as the input assumptions (''garbage in, garbage out''); it is a **statistical estimate**, not an analytical/exact answer.

## Bootstrapping
**Resamples** (with replacement) from an **observed** data set to build a sampling distribution, rather than assuming a theoretical distribution. It is useful when the true distribution is unknown.

## Asset prices
Asset **prices** are often modelled as **lognormal** (they cannot fall below zero), while continuously compounded **returns** are modelled as normal.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-big-data-techniques-m01', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-big-data-techniques', 'Introduction to Big Data Techniques', '**Big data** is characterised by the **''three Vs'' — volume, velocity, variety** (some add veracity/value).

## Data types
- **Structured** data fit neatly in rows/columns (e.g., prices, financial statements).
- **Unstructured** data have no predefined format (text, images, audio, social media, sensor/satellite data — ''alternative data'').

## Processing steps
Data must be **captured, cleansed** (fix errors/missing values), and **wrangled/transformed** into a usable format before analysis.

## Analytical techniques
- **Machine learning**: **supervised** (labelled data → prediction/classification), **unsupervised** (find structure, e.g., clustering), and **deep learning** (neural networks).
- **Natural language processing (NLP)** extracts meaning and sentiment from text.

## Applications & cautions
Big-data techniques power sentiment analysis, factor discovery and risk models. Key risks: poor **data quality**, and **overfitting** a model to noise so it fails on new data.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-economics-capital-flows-fx-m01', 'cfa-economics-capital-flows-fx', 'Capital Flows & the FX Market', 'The **foreign exchange (FX) market** is the largest financial market; participants include banks, corporations, investors, governments and central banks.

## Quotes
- A **spot** rate is for near-immediate delivery; a **forward** rate is for a future date.
- The **real exchange rate** adjusts the nominal rate for relative price levels (purchasing power).

## Balance of payments
A country''s international transactions are recorded in the:
- **Current account** (trade in goods/services, income, transfers), and the
- **Capital and financial account** (cross-border investment flows). A current-account deficit is financed by a financial-account surplus (net capital inflows).

## Drivers of exchange rates
- **Relative interest rates** and **inflation** (higher relative inflation tends to weaken a currency).
- **Capital flows**: strong investment inflows raise demand for a currency.
- **Trade balances**, risk sentiment, and central-bank/exchange-rate **regimes** (fixed, pegged, floating).

A stronger domestic currency makes exports dearer and imports cheaper, affecting the trade balance.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-assets-m01', 'cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-assets', 'Analysis of Long-Term Assets', '## Capitalise vs expense
Costs that provide future benefits beyond one period are **capitalised** (recorded as assets and depreciated/amortised); costs consumed now are **expensed**. Capitalising raises current income and assets but lowers future income (via depreciation).

## Property, plant & equipment
Depreciated over useful life. Methods: **straight-line** (equal each year), **accelerated/double-declining balance** (more early), and **units-of-production**.

## Intangibles
- **Finite-life** intangibles (patents) are **amortised**; **indefinite-life** intangibles are **not amortised** but tested for impairment.
- **Goodwill** (from acquisitions) is **not amortised** — it is tested annually for **impairment**.

## Impairment & revaluation
- An asset is **impaired** when its carrying value exceeds its recoverable amount; the write-down reduces income.
- **IFRS** permits the **revaluation model** (upward revaluation to fair value); **US GAAP** uses historical cost only. Under IFRS, impairment losses (except goodwill) may be reversed; under US GAAP they generally may not.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-financial-modeling-m01', 'cfa-financial-reporting-financial-modeling', 'Introduction to Financial Statement Modeling', 'Financial modelling builds **pro-forma (forecast) statements** to value a company or test scenarios.

## Building the model
1. **Forecast revenue** — **top-down** (from the economy/industry to market share) or **bottom-up** (from the company''s units/segments up).
2. **Model costs and margins** (fixed vs variable; economies of scale).
3. Link the **income statement → balance sheet → cash flow statement**; the statements must **articulate** (tie together), often using a financing **''plug''** (revolving debt or cash) to balance.

## Techniques
- **Sensitivity analysis** varies one input; **scenario analysis** varies a coherent set (e.g., recession vs boom).
- Consider the **competitive environment** (Porter''s forces), pricing power and industry structure.

## Behavioural pitfalls
Analysts are prone to **overconfidence**, **anchoring** to past figures, **confirmation bias**, and **base-rate neglect** — all of which can distort forecasts. Good modelling checks assumptions against history and the competitive reality.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring-m01', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring', 'Corporate Restructuring', 'Corporate restructuring changes a firm''s **structure, operations, or ownership**. Actions fall into three types:
| Type | Examples |
| --- | --- |
| **Investment** (expansion) | Acquisitions, mergers, joint ventures, equity investments |
| **Divestment** (contraction) | **Spin-offs**, split-offs, **carve-outs**, sales of divisions |
| **Restructuring** | Cost cuts, balance-sheet restructuring, reorganisation, **bankruptcy** |

## Mergers & acquisitions
- Motivations: **synergies** (cost or revenue), growth, diversification, market power, and acquiring undervalued targets.
- **Synergy** means the combined firm is worth more than the sum of the parts.

## Valuation of targets
Three common approaches:
- **Comparable company analysis** — multiples of similar public firms (plus a takeover premium).
- **Comparable transaction analysis** — multiples paid in similar past deals (premium already embedded).
- **Premium-paid analysis** — the premium over the target''s pre-announcement price.

Firms restructure to unlock value, respond to distress, refocus on core operations, or exploit perceived mispricing.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-equity-company-forecasting-m01', 'cfa-equity-company-forecasting', 'Company Analysis: Forecasting', 'Company analysis forecasts a firm''s future **revenues, costs, and cash flows** to support valuation.

## Forecasting revenue
- Identify **growth drivers**: volume, price, market size, and **market share**.
- Approaches: **top-down** (economy → industry → company) and **bottom-up** (product/segment build-up). A **hybrid** blends both.

## Costs and profitability
Separate **fixed vs variable** costs, model **operating leverage**, and consider **economies of scale** and input-cost trends. This yields forecast margins.

## Competitive position
A firm''s **competitive advantage** (a ''moat'' — brands, patents, network effects, cost advantage) determines whether high returns are **sustainable**. Porter''s five forces frame the industry''s attractiveness.

## Scenarios and pitfalls
Use **scenario analysis** (base/bull/bear) to reflect uncertainty. Beware behavioural biases — **overconfidence**, **anchoring**, and extrapolating recent trends too far. Forecasts feed directly into DCF and multiple-based valuations.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-cash-flows-types-m01', 'cfa-fixed-income-cash-flows-types', 'Fixed-Income Cash Flows & Types', 'Bonds differ in how **principal** is repaid and how **coupons** are paid.

## Principal repayment structures
| Structure | Description |
| --- | --- |
| **Bullet** | Coupons over the life; **entire principal at maturity** |
| **Fully amortising** | Level payments repay principal gradually to zero |
| **Partially amortising** | Amortises partly, leaving a **balloon** payment at maturity |
| **Sinking fund** | Issuer retires part of the principal on a schedule |

## Coupon structures
- **Fixed-rate**, **floating-rate (FRN)** (reference rate + spread), **step-up** (rises on a schedule), **deferred/split** (no early coupons), and **payment-in-kind (PIK)** (pays coupons in more bonds, not cash).
- **Index-linked** bonds tie coupon/principal to an index (e.g., inflation-linked).

## Contingency provisions (embedded options)
- **Callable** (issuer may redeem early — benefits the issuer), **putable** (holder may sell back early — benefits the investor), and **convertible** (holder may convert into equity).', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-government-corporate-markets-m01', 'cfa-fixed-income-government-corporate-markets', 'Government & Corporate Fixed-Income Markets', '## Government (sovereign) debt
- **Treasury bills** (≤1 year, zero-coupon, sold at a discount), **notes** (2–10 years) and **bonds** (>10 years).
- The most recently issued, most liquid security is **on-the-run**; older issues are **off-the-run**.
- **Non-sovereign** (local government) and **quasi-government/agency** bonds also exist, as do **supranationals**.

## Corporate & financial issuers
Non-financial corporations raise debt through:
- **Bank loans** and **syndicated loans** (a group of lenders),
- **Commercial paper** (short-term, unsecured),
- **Medium-term notes (MTNs)**, and **corporate bonds**.

Financial issuers (banks) rely heavily on **deposits**, **interbank funding**, and the **repo market** for funding.

## Markets & seniority
Corporate bonds carry a **credit spread** over government yields for default and liquidity risk. In the capital structure, **secured** debt ranks above **senior unsecured**, which ranks above **subordinated** debt.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-curve-empirical-duration-m01', 'cfa-fixed-income-curve-empirical-duration', 'Curve-Based & Empirical Duration', 'Beyond a single yield duration, several measures capture interest-rate risk more richly.

## Curve-based (effective) duration
**Effective duration** estimates price sensitivity to a **parallel shift in the benchmark yield curve**, computed from the bond''s prices under small up/down shifts. It is essential for bonds with **embedded options**, whose cash flows change with rates.

## Key rate durations
**Key rate (partial) durations** measure sensitivity to a yield change at a **specific maturity**, capturing the effect of **non-parallel** (steepening/flattening) curve moves that one duration number misses.

## Empirical duration
**Analytical (yield) durations** are computed from a pricing formula. **Empirical duration** is instead **estimated statistically** — by regressing a bond''s actual price changes on benchmark yield changes using historical data.
- It captures how yields and **credit spreads move together**. For example, in a risk-off episode government yields fall while high-yield spreads widen, so a high-yield bond''s **empirical duration is often lower** than its analytical duration.
- Empirical duration is especially useful for **credit-risky** bonds and portfolios.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-active-management-m01', 'cfa-portfolio-management-active-management', 'Analysis of Active Portfolio Management', '**Active management** seeks returns above a benchmark; its value is judged by return **relative to the benchmark**.

## Active return and active risk
- **Active return** = portfolio return − benchmark return (also ''value added'').
- **Active risk** = **tracking error** = the standard deviation of active returns.

## The information ratio
```
Information Ratio (IR) = active return / active risk
```
The IR measures **risk-adjusted active performance** — value added per unit of tracking error. A higher IR is better; it is unaffected by the amount of leverage/scaling of active positions.

## The fundamental law of active management
```
IR ≈ IC × √(breadth)
```
- **IC (information coefficient)** — the skill/quality of the manager''s forecasts.
- **Breadth (BR)** — the number of **independent** active decisions per year.

So performance improves with **more skill** and **more independent bets**. Active management must overcome its **higher fees and costs**; if the expected value added does not exceed those costs, low-cost passive/index investing is preferable.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-intercorporate-investments-m01', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-intercorporate-investments', 'Intercorporate Investments', 'Accounting for an investment in another company depends on **influence/control**, not just ownership %.

| Category | Typical stake | Method |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Financial assets | < 20% | **Fair value** (FVPL or FVOCI) |
| Associate | 20–50% (significant influence) | **Equity method** |
| Business combination | > 50% (control) | **Consolidation** (acquisition method) |
| Joint venture | shared control | **Equity method** (IFRS & US GAAP) |

## Equity method
- One-line: investment carried at cost + share of investee earnings − dividends received. Dividends **reduce the carrying value** (return of investment), not income.

## Consolidation
- Combine 100% of subsidiary assets/liabilities; recognize **non-controlling interest** for the portion not owned.
- **Goodwill** = purchase price − fair value of identifiable net assets. Goodwill is **not amortized** but tested for impairment. (Partial vs full goodwill differs under IFRS vs US GAAP.)
- Acquisition-method: assets/liabilities recorded at **fair value** at acquisition.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-equity-fcf-valuation-m01', 'cfa2-equity-fcf-valuation', 'Free Cash Flow Valuation (FCFF/FCFE)', '## Definitions
- **FCFF** = cash to *all* capital providers. FCFF = NI + NCC + Int×(1−t) − FCInv − WCInv.
- **FCFE** = cash to *equity* holders. FCFE = FCFF − Int×(1−t) + Net borrowing. Or FCFE = NI + NCC − FCInv − WCInv + Net borrowing.

## Discounting
- Discount **FCFF at WACC** → firm value; subtract debt → equity value.
- Discount **FCFE at the cost of equity (r)** → equity value directly.
- Single-stage: Firm = FCFF₁/(WACC − g); Equity = FCFE₁/(r − g).

## Notes
- Use FCFE when capital structure is stable; FCFF when leverage is changing/high (FCFE can be negative).
- **Dividends ≠ FCFE**; FCF models value the capacity to pay, not the policy.
- Add back **non-cash charges (NCC)** like depreciation; subtract reinvestment (FCInv, WCInv).', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-equity-residual-income-m01', 'cfa2-equity-residual-income', 'Residual Income Valuation', '**Residual income** = net income − equity charge = NI − (r × beginning book value).

**V₀ = B₀ + Σ RI_t/(1+r)^t.** Single-stage: **V₀ = B₀ + [(ROE − r)/(r − g)] × B₀**.

## Why use it
- Recognizes value **earlier** and depends **less on a terminal value** than DDM/FCF — useful for non-dividend payers and firms with negative near-term FCF.

## Cautions
- Relies on **clean-surplus** accounting (all income flows through retained earnings; bypassed by some OCI items).
- Sensitive to **book value and accounting quality**; adjust for off-balance-sheet items and aggressive accruals.
- A **persistence factor (ω)** scales how long abnormal RI continues before fading to the cost of equity.', 2, 1),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-arbitrage-free-oas-m01', 'cfa2-fixed-income-arbitrage-free-oas', 'Arbitrage-Free Valuation & OAS', '## Arbitrage-free value
Discount each cash flow at its **spot rate**; otherwise stripping/reconstitution would create arbitrage. For bonds **with embedded options**, value along an **interest-rate tree** (calibrated to be arbitrage-free) using backward induction.

## Embedded options
- **Callable bond** = straight bond − value of call (issuer''s option). Value_callable = Value_straight − Call.
- **Putable bond** = straight bond + value of put. Value_putable = Value_straight + Put.
- Higher **volatility** raises both option values → **lowers** callable price, **raises** putable price.

## Spreads
- **Z-spread**: constant spread over the spot curve that prices the bond — ignores optionality.
- **OAS (option-adjusted spread)**: Z-spread **minus** the option cost; it''s the spread after removing the embedded option''s effect, so OAS is comparable across bonds.
- For callables, **OAS < Z-spread**; for putables, **OAS > Z-spread**.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-option-valuation-m01', 'cfa2-derivatives-option-valuation', 'Option Valuation: Binomial & BSM', '## Binomial (risk-neutral)
π = (1 + r − d)/(u − d); option = [π·c_u + (1−π)·c_d]/(1+r). Real probabilities are irrelevant once the option is replicated/hedged.

## Black-Scholes-Merton (BSM)
c = S·N(d₁) − Ke^(−rT)·N(d₂); p via put-call parity. Assumes lognormal prices, constant volatility & rate, no early exercise (European).

## The Greeks
| Greek | Measures sensitivity to |
| --- | --- |
| Delta | Underlying price (call 0→1) |
| Gamma | Rate of change of delta (largest ATM) |
| Vega | Volatility (largest ATM, long-dated) |
| Theta | Time decay |
| Rho | Interest rate |

- **Delta hedging** neutralizes small moves; rebalancing is needed because of **gamma**.
- Carry/dividends lower call value; **only volatility is unobservable** → implied vol drives quoted prices.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-multiple-regression-m01', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-multiple-regression', 'Multiple Regression & Model Misspecification', '## The model
Y = b₀ + b₁X₁ + … + bₖXₖ + ε. Each slope is the effect of that X **holding the others constant**.

## Inference
- **t-test** on each coefficient; **F-test** for joint significance of all slopes.
- **R²** always rises with more regressors → use **adjusted R²** to penalize added variables.

## Assumption violations
| Problem | Effect | Detect | Fix |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Heteroskedasticity** | biased standard errors (not coefficients) | Breusch-Pagan | robust (White) SE |
| **Serial correlation** | biased SE, inflated t | Durbin-Watson, Breusch-Godfrey | Newey-West SE |
| **Multicollinearity** | high R² but insignificant t''s | high VIF | drop/combine variables |

## Misspecification
- Omitting a relevant variable → **biased** coefficients. Wrong functional form, or using a lagged dependent variable with serial correlation, also bias results. Good models are grounded in **economic reasoning**, not data mining.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-economics-currency-forecasting-m01', 'cfa2-economics-currency-forecasting', 'Currency Exchange Rates & Forecasting', '## Parity (recap)
- **Covered IRP** holds by arbitrage; **uncovered IRP**, **PPP** and the **international Fisher effect** are equilibrium tendencies that often fail short-term (carry-trade profits).

## Mark-to-market on a forward
A long base-currency forward gains when the spot/forward moves in its favor; value = PV of (current forward − contracted forward) on the remaining term.

## Approaches to forecasting FX
- **Balance of payments / current account**: persistent deficits pressure a currency lower (flow effects), but capital flows can dominate.
- **Monetary models**: money supply growth and inflation drive long-run value.
- **Portfolio-balance / asset-market**: relative returns and risk premia drive capital flows.
- **Carry trade**: borrow low-yield, invest high-yield — profits if UIRP fails; risk of sudden crashes ("picking up nickels in front of a steamroller").

## Intervention & controls
- Central-bank intervention and capital controls can move rates short-term; effectiveness depends on reserves and credibility.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-equity-price-multiples-m01', 'cfa2-equity-price-multiples', 'Price & Enterprise-Value Multiples', '## Price multiples
- **P/E** — most common. **Trailing** uses last 12m EPS; **forward** uses expected EPS. Justified leading P/E = (payout)/(r − g). Use for profitable, stable firms; distorted by cyclicality and accounting.
- **P/B** — useful for banks/financials and when earnings are negative; reflects asset intensity.
- **P/S** — robust to accounting choices and usable for unprofitable firms; ignores cost structure/leverage.
- **P/CF** — harder to manipulate than earnings.

## Enterprise value (EV)
EV = market cap + debt + preferred + minority interest − cash.
- **EV/EBITDA** — capital-structure neutral; good for comparing firms with different leverage and for capital-intensive businesses. **EV/Sales** for early-stage.

## Method of comparables vs. fundamentals
- **Comparables**: compare a multiple to peers/benchmark; cheap if below peers with similar fundamentals.
- **Justified multiple**: derived from a DCF/DDM, telling you what the multiple *should* be given r and g.
- Watch the **trailing vs leading** mismatch and one-off items (normalize EPS).', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-pe-real-estate-m01', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-pe-real-estate', 'Private Equity & Real Estate Valuation', '## Private equity
- **Buyout vs venture capital**: buyouts use leverage on mature cash-flow firms; VC funds early-stage growth.
- **Exit routes**: trade sale, secondary sale, **IPO**, recapitalization.
- Fund economics: **2-and-20** (management fee + carried interest), often over a **hurdle rate** with a **clawback**; **J-curve** (early negative returns from fees before exits).
- Performance: **IRR** and multiples (**MOIC/TVPI, DPI, RVPI**).

## Real estate
Four quadrants: **private/public × equity/debt** (direct property, REITs, mortgages, MBS).

| Approach | Idea |
| --- | --- |
| **Income (cap rate)** | Value = NOI / cap rate |
| **DCF** | PV of forecast NOI + reversion |
| **Cost** | Replacement cost − depreciation + land |
| **Comparable sales** | Adjust recent transactions |

- **Cap rate** ≈ discount rate − growth; lower cap rate ⇒ higher price/lower yield.
- **REITs** valued via NAV, price-to-FFO/AFFO, and dividend discount.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-ethics-application-soft-dollar-m01', 'cfa2-ethics-application-soft-dollar', 'Application of the Code & Soft-Dollar Standards', 'Level II tests the Standards through **cases**. Work the facts against the seven Standards and pick the *least* compliant action.

## High-frequency themes
- **Standard III(B) Fair Dealing**: disseminate recommendations/changes to all clients fairly — don''t tip favored clients first.
- **Standard VI(A) Disclosure of Conflicts**: disclose ownership, board seats, referral fees (VI(C)).
- **Standard III(A) Loyalty, Prudence, Care**: act for the client/beneficiary, not the plan sponsor.

## Soft dollars (client brokerage)
- Client brokerage **belongs to the client** and must be used for their benefit — to buy **research that aids the investment decision**, not the manager''s overhead (rent, software unrelated to research, travel).
- "**Best execution**" cannot be sacrificed to direct soft-dollar business.
- **Directed brokerage** (client directs trades to a broker for the client''s own benefit, e.g. to offset fees) is allowed but may forgo best execution/soft-dollar benefits — disclose.
- The **CFA Institute Soft Dollar Standards** are *voluntary* best practice on top of the Code.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-payout-m01', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-payout', 'Capital Structure & Payout Policy', '## Capital structure
- **Modigliani-Miller (no taxes)**: capital structure is irrelevant; WACC is constant.
- **MM with taxes**: debt adds a **tax shield** → value rises with leverage (favoring 100% debt in theory).
- **Static trade-off**: optimal leverage balances the tax shield against **financial distress / bankruptcy costs**; the WACC is U-shaped.
- **Pecking order** (asymmetric info): firms prefer **internal funds → debt → equity**; issuing equity signals overvaluation.
- Higher business risk and asset intangibility argue for **less** debt.

## Payout policy
- **Dividend irrelevance** (MM) vs. signaling: dividend **initiations/increases** signal confidence; **cuts** are penalized.
- **Clientele effect**: investors self-select by payout/tax preference.
- **Share repurchases** vs dividends: buybacks are more **flexible**, are tax-advantaged where capital gains < dividend tax, and boost EPS if the buyback yield > earnings yield (financed cheaply).
- A buyback at a price **below** intrinsic value transfers wealth to remaining holders.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-multifactor-models-m01', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-multifactor-models', 'Multifactor Models & Risk Attribution', '## Arbitrage Pricing Theory (APT)
E[R] = R_f + Σ βₖ·(factor risk premiumₖ). Built on **no-arbitrage**, multiple systematic factors, and well-diversified portfolios — a generalization of the single-factor CAPM.

## Types of multifactor models
- **Macroeconomic** (factors = surprises in GDP, inflation, credit spreads, term structure).
- **Fundamental** (factors = firm attributes: size, value, momentum, quality).
- **Statistical** (factors extracted via PCA/factor analysis).

## Uses
- **Return attribution**: active return = Σ (factor tilt × factor return) + security selection.
- **Risk attribution**: active risk² = **factor (systematic) variance + active specific variance**.
- **Information ratio** = active return / active risk; the **fundamental law**: IR ≈ IC × √breadth.
- Construct **factor-mimicking portfolios** to hedge or target a single factor (e.g. interest-rate or inflation hedges).', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-credit-analysis-m01', 'cfa2-fixed-income-credit-analysis', 'Credit Analysis & Credit Strategies', '## Measuring credit risk
- **Expected loss = PD × LGD × EAD** (LGD = 1 − recovery rate).
- **Credit spread** compensates for expected loss + a risk premium + liquidity.
- **Structural (Merton)** models: equity is a call on assets; default if assets < debt. **Reduced-form** models: default is a statistical hazard driven by macro variables.

## Spread analysis
- **G-spread** (over government), **I-spread** (over swaps), **Z-spread** (over the spot curve), **OAS** (Z-spread minus option cost). Use **OAS** to compare bonds with embedded options.
- Return impact of a spread change ≈ **−(spread duration × Δspread)** + convexity term.

## Strategies
- **Bottom-up** (pick mispriced issuers) vs **top-down** (sector/quality rotation over the cycle).
- In an expected **downturn**, move up in quality and shorten spread duration; in a **recovery**, add lower-rated/higher-spread credit.
- Watch **liquidity** and **spread curve** roll-down; diversify idiosyncratic default risk.', 2, 1),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-pensions-m01', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-pensions', 'Pensions & Post-Employment Benefits', '## Plan types
- **Defined contribution (DC)**: employer pays a fixed contribution; the **employee bears investment risk**. Expense = the contribution; no balance-sheet liability beyond unpaid amounts.
- **Defined benefit (DB)**: employer promises a future benefit; the **employer bears the risk**. Requires actuarial estimates.

## Funded status (DB)
**Funded status = fair value of plan assets − PBO** (projected benefit obligation). A deficit is a net pension **liability** on the balance sheet; a surplus is an asset.

## Periodic pension cost (DB)
- Components: **service cost**, **net interest** (discount rate × net liability), **remeasurements/actuarial gains-losses**, and past service cost.
- **IFRS**: service cost + net interest go to **P&L**; remeasurements to **OCI** (not recycled).
- **US GAAP**: service cost in operating income; interest & expected return can sit in non-operating; actuarial gains/losses often in OCI with **corridor/amortization**.

## Key assumptions
- A **higher discount rate** lowers the PBO and usually service cost; a higher assumed **compensation growth** raises the PBO. Analysts adjust for aggressive assumptions and reclassify components for comparability.', 2, 1),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-multinational-m01', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-multinational', 'Multinational Operations & Currency Translation', 'When a parent consolidates a foreign subsidiary, the sub''s statements must be translated into the presentation currency.

## Two methods
| | Current-rate method | Temporal method |
| --- | --- | --- |
| When | Functional currency = local currency | Functional currency = parent''s |
| Assets/liabs | **Current** rate | Monetary at current; **non-monetary at historical** |
| Revenue/exp | Average rate | Average; COGS & depreciation at **historical** |
| Gain/loss to | **OCI** (CTA) | **Net income** (remeasurement gain/loss) |

## Choosing the functional currency
- Determined by the **primary economic environment** (where cash flows, prices, financing are denominated).
- In a **hyperinflationary** economy: US GAAP uses the **temporal** method (functional = parent''s); IFRS **restates** for inflation then translates at the current rate.

## Effects
- Current-rate method preserves financial **ratios** of the sub (all at current rate); the temporal method distorts them.
- A depreciating local currency with a net asset exposure produces a **negative CTA** (current-rate) or remeasurement loss (temporal).', 2, 2),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-swaps-fra-m01', 'cfa2-derivatives-swaps-fra', 'Swaps & FRA Valuation', '## Forward rate agreements (FRA)
- An FRA locks a future interest rate. The **long** gains if the reference rate **rises** above the contract rate.
- Payoff is settled at the FRA expiry on a **discounted** basis: (reference − FRA rate) × notional × period, divided by (1 + reference × period).

## Interest-rate swaps
- A plain-vanilla swap = exchange **fixed for floating**; it is economically a **portfolio of FRAs** (or a long/short bond pair).
- The **swap fixed rate** is set so the swap''s initial value is **zero** — it''s the rate that equates the PV of fixed and floating legs (a par-rate calculation from discount factors).
- **Value over time**: as rates move, the swap gains/loses. Value to the fixed-rate payer ≈ (PV of floating leg) − (PV of fixed leg). The floating leg resets to par at each payment date.

## Other swaps
- **Currency swap**: exchange principal + interest in two currencies. **Equity swap**: exchange equity return for fixed/floating. Used to convert exposures (e.g., fixed↔floating debt) cheaply without re-issuing.', 2, 1),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-time-series-m01', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-time-series', 'Time-Series Analysis', '## Models
- **Trend models**: linear (constant change) or **log-linear** (constant growth rate). Check residuals for serial correlation (Durbin-Watson).
- **Autoregressive AR(p)**: x_t depends on its own lags. **Covariance-stationary** is required (constant mean/variance; autocovariances depend only on lag).

## Diagnostics
- **AR(1) stationarity**: |slope| < 1. A **unit root** (slope = 1, random walk) ⇒ non-stationary; test with **Dickey-Fuller**. Fix by **first-differencing**.
- Check residual autocorrelations (t-tests / Ljung-Box); add lags or seasonal terms if significant.
- **Mean reversion** level = b₀ / (1 − b₁) for an AR(1).

## Other tools
- **RMSE** compares out-of-sample forecast accuracy (lower is better).
- **Seasonality**: add a seasonal lag (e.g., x_{t-4} for quarterly).
- **ARCH**: if error variance depends on prior squared errors, variance is not constant — use generalized least squares / model the variance (ARCH/GARCH).
- **Cointegration**: two unit-root series can be regressed if cointegrated (long-run relationship); otherwise the regression is **spurious**.', 2, 1),
  ('cfa2-economics-growth-m01', 'cfa2-economics-growth', 'Economic Growth & the Investment Setting', '## Sources of growth
- **Labor** (size, participation, hours, human capital) + **capital** (physical + human + technology) + **total factor productivity (TFP)**.
- **Production function**: Y = A·F(K, L). Growth in output = TFP growth + α·ΔK + (1−α)·ΔL (growth accounting).

## Theories
- **Neoclassical (Solow)**: diminishing returns to capital → economies reach a **steady state**; long-run per-capita growth comes only from **exogenous technology**. Poorer economies grow faster (**convergence**).
- **Endogenous growth**: R&D and human capital generate **constant/increasing** returns; policy can raise the long-run growth rate (no forced convergence).
- **Club convergence**: only economies with similar institutions converge.

## Investment implications
- **Sustainable growth** raises equity returns over the long run via earnings growth.
- Drivers analysts watch: **savings/investment rates, demographics, productivity, property rights, openness to trade, and infrastructure**.
- Government policy (taxes, education, rule of law) and **capital deepening** vs. genuine TFP gains matter for durability.', 2, 1),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-m01', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-hedge-funds', 'Hedge Fund Strategies', '## Strategy buckets
- **Equity**: long/short equity, equity market-neutral (beta ≈ 0), dedicated short.
- **Event-driven**: merger (risk) arbitrage, distressed, special situations.
- **Relative value**: fixed-income arbitrage, convertible arbitrage, volatility.
- **Macro / managed futures**: discretionary global macro, **CTA/trend-following**.
- **Multi-strategy** and **fund-of-funds** (added diversification but **double fees**).

## Characteristics
- Often use **leverage, shorting, and derivatives**; seek **absolute return** / alpha.
- Fees **2-and-20** with **hurdle** and **high-water mark**; lock-ups, gates, and notice periods limit liquidity.
- Returns are often **non-normal** (negative skew, fat tails) — many strategies are "short volatility," paying steady returns then large losses in stress.

## In a portfolio
- Low average correlation to equities but **correlations rise in crises**; report-smoothing understates risk.
- **Operational due diligence** is critical; conditional/strategy-specific risk (e.g., liquidity in distressed) must be modeled, not just volatility.', 2, 1),
  ('cfa2-ethics-analysis-recommendations-m01', 'cfa2-ethics-analysis-recommendations', 'Investment Analysis, Recommendations & Record Retention', '**Standard V — Investment Analysis, Recommendations, and Actions.**

## V(A) Diligence & reasonable basis
- Have a **reasonable, adequate basis** supported by research before making a recommendation.
- When using **third-party/secondary research or quant models**, make reasonable efforts to verify their soundness; you remain responsible.
- Group research: you may disagree with a team''s consensus and need not dissent publicly as long as the basis is sound.

## V(B) Communication with clients
- Distinguish **fact from opinion**; disclose the **basic process and risks**; promptly disclose **material changes** in process.
- Identify **limitations** and key assumptions.

## V(C) Record retention
- Keep records supporting analysis, recommendations, and actions. Absent a regulator''s rule, CFA Institute recommends **≥ 7 years**. Records are the **firm''s property**; you cannot take files to a new employer.', 2, 1),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-governance-esg-m01', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-governance-esg', 'Corporate Governance, ESG & Stakeholders', '## Stakeholder management
Balancing the interests of **shareholders, board, management, employees, creditors, suppliers, customers, and regulators**. Key conflicts: **principal-agent** (managers vs. shareholders) and **shareholder vs. creditor** (e.g., risk-shifting).

## Governance mechanisms
- **Board**: independent, diverse directors; separate chair/CEO; committees (audit, remuneration, nomination).
- **Shareholder rights**: voting (one-share-one-vote vs. dual-class), proxy access, say-on-pay, takeover defenses (poison pills reduce accountability).
- Strong governance lowers risk and the cost of capital; weak governance is a red flag.

## ESG integration
- **E** (climate, emissions, resource use), **S** (labor, safety, data privacy), **G** (board, ethics, controls).
- Approaches: **negative/exclusionary screening, positive/best-in-class, thematic, impact investing, ESG integration** into valuation, and **active ownership/engagement**.
- **Materiality** matters — focus on ESG factors that affect cash flows/risk; watch for **greenwashing**. ESG risks feed into discount rates and cash-flow forecasts.', 2, 1),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-market-risk-var-m01', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-market-risk-var', 'Measuring & Managing Market Risk (VaR)', '**Value at Risk (VaR)**: the **minimum** loss expected over a period at a given confidence (e.g. "5% 1-day VaR = $1m" → ≥ $1m loss on the worst 5% of days).

## Three methods
| Method | Idea | Pros / cons |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Parametric (variance-covariance)** | Assumes normal returns; uses σ | Simple; poor for options/fat tails |
| **Historical simulation** | Reuses actual past returns | No distribution assumption; backward-looking |
| **Monte Carlo** | Simulate many scenarios | Flexible for non-linear payoffs; compute-heavy |

## Extensions & limits
- **Conditional VaR (expected shortfall)** = average loss **beyond** VaR — captures tail severity that VaR ignores.
- VaR says nothing about the **size** of losses past the threshold, can be gamed, and depends on assumptions.
- Complements: **scenario analysis, stress tests, and sensitivity measures** (beta, duration, delta/gamma).
- Other constraints: **risk budgeting, position limits, stop-losses**.', 2, 1),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-machine-learning-m01', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-machine-learning', 'Machine Learning & Big Data', '## Learning types
- **Supervised** (labeled data): regression & classification — penalized regression/**LASSO**, **SVM**, KNN, CART, random forests, neural nets.
- **Unsupervised** (no labels): **clustering** (k-means, hierarchical) and **dimension reduction** (**PCA**).
- **Deep learning / reinforcement learning** for complex, non-linear problems.

## Overfitting — the central risk
- A model that fits noise has **low bias, high variance** and fails out of sample.
- Control with **train/validation/test splits, cross-validation, regularization (LASSO/ridge), and pruning**.
- **Bias-variance tradeoff**: too simple → underfit; too complex → overfit.

## Big data & the data project
Steps: **conceptualization → data collection → preparation/wrangling (cleaning, handling outliers/missing) → exploration → model training → tuning**.
- Text/NLP: **tokenization, stemming, bag-of-words, TF-IDF**.
- Big data traits: **volume, velocity, variety** (and veracity). ML finds patterns but needs sound economic reasoning and care with data quality and look-ahead bias.', 2, 2),
  ('cfa2-equity-multistage-ddm-m01', 'cfa2-equity-multistage-ddm', 'Multistage Dividend Discount Models', 'When growth isn''t constant, use a **multistage DDM**: forecast dividends explicitly, then a terminal value.

## Gordon (single-stage)
**V₀ = D₁ / (r − g)** — only for stable, mature firms where g < r.

## Two-stage
High growth for n years, then a stable terminal growth. Terminal value at year n: **TV_n = D_{n+1}/(r − g_L)**; discount each dividend + TV back to today.

## H-model
Growth **declines linearly** from g_S to g_L over 2H years:
**V₀ = [D₀(1+g_L) + D₀·H·(g_S − g_L)] / (r − g_L)** — a shortcut for a fading high-growth phase.

## Three-stage
Growth, transition, and maturity phases — for young firms.

## Notes
- Terminal value usually dominates; check sensitivity to **r and g_L**.
- **Sustainable growth g = b × ROE** (retention ratio × ROE).
- Use DDM for dividend-paying firms; for non-payers prefer FCFE/residual income.', 2, 3),
  ('cfa2-equity-private-company-m01', 'cfa2-equity-private-company', 'Private Company Valuation', '## Three approaches
1. **Income** — free-cash-flow / capitalized-cash-flow / **excess-earnings**; discount at a build-up or expanded-CAPM rate (add size + company-specific premia).
2. **Market** — guideline public companies, guideline transactions, prior-transaction multiples.
3. **Asset-based** — net asset value (less common for going concerns).

## Normalizing earnings
Adjust for **owner compensation, related-party transactions, and one-offs** to reflect true economic earnings.

## Discounts & premiums
- **DLOM** (discount for lack of marketability) — private interests can''t be sold quickly.
- **DLOC** (discount for lack of control) for minority stakes; a **control premium** for controlling stakes.
- Private discount rates exceed comparable public rates (illiquidity, size, specific risk).

## Definitions (standard) of value
Fair market value, fair value, market value, investment value, intrinsic value — the **purpose** (tax, litigation, M&A, financial reporting) drives which applies.', 2, 4),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-term-structure-m01', 'cfa2-fixed-income-term-structure', 'Term Structure & Interest Rate Models', '## Theories of the yield-curve shape
- **Pure expectations**: forwards = expected future spot rates (no risk premium).
- **Liquidity preference**: investors demand a premium for longer maturities → upward bias.
- **Market segmentation / preferred habitat**: supply-demand in maturity buckets; investors shift only for a premium.

## Spot, forward, swap, par rates
Linked by no-arbitrage: (1+z₂)² = (1+z₁)(1+f₁,₁). The **swap-rate curve** is a common benchmark; the **swap spread** over governments reflects credit/liquidity.

## Risk drivers
- The curve moves via **level, slope (steepness), and curvature** — the first principal component (level) explains most variance.
- **Key rate (partial) durations** capture exposure to specific maturities (non-parallel shifts) better than a single effective duration.

## Models
- **Equilibrium** (e.g. CIR, Vasicek) start from economic assumptions; **arbitrage-free** (e.g. Ho-Lee) are calibrated to fit the current curve exactly — used to value bonds with embedded options on a tree.', 2, 2),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-cds-m01', 'cfa2-fixed-income-cds', 'Credit Default Swaps', 'A **CDS** is insurance against a borrower''s default: the **protection buyer** pays a periodic **premium (spread)**; the **seller** pays out on a **credit event** (bankruptcy, failure to pay, restructuring).

## Mechanics
- **Single-name** (one issuer) or **index** (e.g. CDX, iTraxx, a basket).
- **Standard coupon** (e.g. 1% or 5%); the difference vs. the fair spread is settled via an **upfront payment**: upfront ≈ (CDS spread − coupon) × **duration**.
- Payout ≈ notional × **(1 − recovery rate)** = notional × LGD.

## Pricing intuition
- CDS spread ≈ **hazard rate × (1 − recovery)** — compensation for expected loss.
- Value to the protection buyer rises as **credit quality deteriorates** (spread widens).

## Uses
- **Hedge** credit exposure, **express a view** (buy protection = short credit), or exploit the **basis** (CDS spread vs. cash-bond spread). Enables **synthetic** long/short credit without trading the bond.', 2, 3),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-forwards-futures-m01', 'cfa2-derivatives-forwards-futures', 'Forward & Futures Pricing', '## No-arbitrage forward price
**F₀ = S₀ × (1 + r)^T** (cost-of-carry). With income/costs:
**F₀ = (S₀ − PV(income) + PV(costs)) × (1 + r)^T**.
- **Carry benefits** (dividends, coupons, convenience yield) **lower** the forward; **carry costs** (storage) **raise** it.

## Value over time
A forward''s value at t: **V_t = (F_t − F₀) × discount factor** (zero at initiation). The price is fixed; the *value* drifts as spot/rates move.

## Forwards vs futures
- **Futures** are exchange-traded, standardized, daily **marked-to-market** with margin (minimal counterparty risk); **forwards** are OTC, customized, settled at maturity (counterparty risk).
- When rates correlate with the asset, futures and forward prices differ slightly (mark-to-market timing).

## Backwardation vs contango
- **Contango**: futures > spot (carry costs dominate). **Backwardation**: futures < spot (high convenience yield/benefits).', 2, 2),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-income-taxes-m01', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-income-taxes', 'Income Taxes (Deferred Tax)', 'Differences between **accounting** profit and **taxable** profit create deferred taxes.

## Temporary vs permanent
- **Temporary differences** reverse over time → create **deferred tax assets/liabilities**. Example: accelerated tax depreciation > book depreciation → a **deferred tax liability (DTL)**.
- **Permanent differences** (e.g. tax-exempt interest, non-deductible fines) never reverse → affect the **effective tax rate**, not deferred taxes.

## DTA vs DTL
- **DTL**: tax payable now is *less* than book expense (e.g. faster tax depreciation, installment sales).
- **DTA**: tax payable now is *more* than book (e.g. **loss carryforwards**, warranty/bad-debt provisions). A **valuation allowance** (US GAAP) reduces a DTA if realization is unlikely.

## Analyst points
- A change in the **tax rate** remeasures DTAs/DTLs (hits the period''s tax expense).
- DTLs expected to **reverse** are treated as debt-like; those that keep growing/never reverse may be treated as **equity** in analysis.
- **Income tax expense = taxes payable + ΔDTL − ΔDTA**.', 2, 3),
  ('cfa2-equity-industry-analysis-m01', 'cfa2-equity-industry-analysis', 'Industry & Competitive Analysis', '## Framework
1. **Classify** the industry (by business cycle sensitivity — cyclical vs. defensive — and by life-cycle stage: embryonic → growth → shakeout → mature → decline).
2. **Analyze structure** with **Porter''s Five Forces**: rivalry, new entrants, substitutes, supplier power, buyer power.
3. **Competitive position** & strategy (Porter: **cost leadership, differentiation, focus**).

## Key drivers
- **Industry life cycle** shapes margins and growth: growth stage = high growth, falling prices; mature = consolidation, stable margins.
- **Barriers to entry**, pricing power, industry concentration (few players → more pricing power), and exposure to **macro, demographic, technological, governmental, and social** influences.
- **Experience curve**: unit costs fall with cumulative output (a barrier favoring incumbents).

## For valuation
- A company can only sustain returns above its cost of capital with a durable **competitive advantage (moat)**.
- Map industry forces to forecasts of **revenue growth, margins, and reinvestment** used in the DCF.', 2, 5),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-option-strategies-m01', 'cfa2-derivatives-option-strategies', 'Option Strategies & Greeks', '## Core positions
- **Covered call** (long stock + short call): income, caps upside. **Protective put** (long stock + long put): floors downside.
- **Collar**: protective put financed by a short call — bounded payoff.

## Spreads & combinations
- **Bull call spread** / **bear put spread**: directional, limited risk & reward.
- **Straddle** (long call + put, same strike): profits from large moves **either** direction (long volatility). **Strangle**: cheaper, wider strikes.
- **Butterfly**: profits if the underlying stays near the middle strike (short volatility).

## The Greeks
| Greek | Sensitivity to | Note |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Delta** | underlying price | hedge ratio |
| **Gamma** | change in delta | largest ATM near expiry |
| **Vega** | volatility | largest ATM, long-dated |
| **Theta** | time decay | hurts long options |
| **Rho** | interest rates | small for short-dated |

- **Delta hedging** is dynamic (rebalance due to gamma). Maximum gain/loss and **breakevens** are standard exam computations.', 2, 3),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-simulation-m01', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-simulation', 'Simulation & Probabilistic Methods', '## Monte Carlo simulation
Generates thousands of random scenarios from assumed input distributions to build a **distribution of outcomes** (e.g. option values, VaR, retirement wealth).
- Strengths: handles **complex, path-dependent, non-linear** payoffs and many interacting variables.
- Limits: only as good as the input assumptions ("garbage in, garbage out"); **statistical**, not analytical.

## Other tools
- **Historical simulation**: resample actual past data (no distribution assumption).
- **Bootstrapping**: repeatedly resample (with replacement) from observed data to estimate a sampling distribution / standard errors.
- **Sensitivity analysis**: vary one input at a time. **Scenario analysis**: change a coherent set of inputs.

## Probability essentials
- **Bayes'' formula** updates a prior probability with new evidence: P(A|B) = P(B|A)·P(A) / P(B).
- Track **correlations** between inputs — ignoring them understates or overstates portfolio risk. Use enough trials for the estimate to **converge**.', 2, 3),
  ('cfa2-economics-regulation-m01', 'cfa2-economics-regulation', 'Economics of Regulation', '## Why regulate
- **Market failures**: externalities, public goods, information asymmetry, and **natural monopoly** (one provider is most efficient → price regulation).
- Regulators also protect investors/consumers and ensure financial stability.

## Types
- **Statutes** (legislatures), **administrative regulations** (agencies), and **judicial law**.
- **Economic** regulation (prices, entry — e.g. utilities) vs. **prudential** (safety/soundness of financial firms) vs. **disclosure/conduct**.
- **Self-regulating organizations (SROs)** can be granted authority (e.g. exchanges).

## Analyst lens
- **Regulatory capture**: regulators serve the regulated industry''s interests.
- **Regulatory arbitrage**: firms exploit gaps/differences across jurisdictions.
- Assess **costs vs. benefits** (compliance costs, unintended consequences). Regulation changes industry economics — barriers to entry, pricing, and risk — so it feeds directly into valuation and risk assessment.', 2, 2),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-commodities-m01', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-commodities', 'Commodities & Commodity Derivatives', 'Commodity **spot** exposure is hard to hold (storage), so investors use **futures**.

## Total return of a futures position
**Price return + roll return + collateral return.**
- **Roll return** depends on the curve: **backwardation** (futures < spot) → **positive** roll yield as you roll up to spot; **contango** (futures > spot) → **negative** roll yield.

## Pricing theories
- **Insurance/hedging-pressure (Keynes)**: producers hedge by selling futures → futures sit below expected spot (normal backwardation).
- **Theory of storage**: futures ≈ spot + storage cost − **convenience yield**. High convenience yield (tight supply) → backwardation.

## Portfolio role
- **Inflation hedge** and diversifier; commodities often rise with unexpected inflation when stocks/bonds fall.
- No income/dividends; returns driven by supply-demand, the curve shape, and the **collateral** (cash) yield. Sub-sectors: energy, metals, agriculture, livestock.', 2, 2),
  ('cfa2-ethics-gips-m01', 'cfa2-ethics-gips', 'GIPS Standards', '**GIPS** are voluntary, ethical standards for **fair representation and full disclosure** of investment performance.

## Core rules
- Compliance is **firm-wide** (all or nothing). Define the **firm** appropriately.
- **Composites**: include **all actual fee-paying, discretionary** portfolios of a similar strategy — prevents cherry-picking and survivorship bias. Non-discretionary accounts are excluded.
- Show a minimum of **5 years** (or since inception), building to **10**.
- Returns are **time-weighted** (removing client cash-flow timing); present a **benchmark** and a measure of **dispersion**.

## Other points
- **Verification** is firm-wide, performed by an independent third party, and **recommended but not required**.
- Must not present **false or misleading** performance; disclose fees, methodology, and significant events.
- GIPS does not replace local law; where law conflicts, follow law and disclose. The standards reduce the information asymmetry between managers and prospective clients.', 2, 2),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-mergers-acquisitions-m01', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-mergers-acquisitions', 'Mergers, Acquisitions & Restructuring', '## Types & motives
- **Horizontal** (same industry — synergies/market power), **vertical** (supply chain), **conglomerate** (diversification).
- Motives: **synergies**, growth, economies of scale, tax benefits, undervaluation. Beware **managerial hubris** and empire-building.

## Payment & defenses
- **Cash vs. stock** offer: stock shares risk with the target; cash transfers all risk/reward to the acquirer.
- Takeover defenses: **poison pill, staggered board, golden parachute, white knight, greenmail, Pac-Man**.

## Valuation of the deal
- **DCF**, comparable companies, and **comparable transactions** (which include a control premium).
- **Synergies** and the **premium** paid determine who wins: **Gains_target = premium**; **Gains_acquirer = synergies − premium**. Overpaying destroys acquirer value (the **winner''s curse**).

## Restructuring
- **Divestitures, spin-offs, carve-outs, leveraged buyouts** — often to unlock value or refocus. Regulators review for **antitrust** (e.g. HHI concentration).', 2, 2),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-trading-execution-m01', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-trading-execution', 'Trading, Execution & Rebalancing', '## Execution costs
- **Explicit** (commissions, fees, taxes) + **implicit** (bid-ask **spread**, **market impact**, **delay/slippage**, **opportunity cost** of unfilled orders).
- **Implementation shortfall** = paper-portfolio return − actual return — the most complete cost measure. **VWAP** is a common benchmark.

## Order types & algos
- **Market** (immediacy, pays impact) vs. **limit** (price control, risks non-execution).
- Algorithms: **VWAP/TWAP**, **implementation-shortfall** (balances impact vs. timing risk), and **liquidity-seeking**. Slice large orders to limit impact.

## Rebalancing
- **Calendar** vs. **percentage-range (corridor)**. Wider corridors for **higher transaction costs**, higher risk tolerance, and lower correlation; narrower for higher volatility.
- Rebalancing is **contrarian** (sell winners, buy losers) and can earn a **diversification return**; it controls risk, not return.
- Trade **urgency**: aggressive trades pay impact but cut timing risk; patient trades risk adverse price moves.', 2, 2),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-analysis-techniques-m01', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-analysis-techniques', 'Financial Analysis Techniques & Earnings Quality', '## Tools
- **Common-size** statements (% of revenue / total assets), **ratio analysis**, and **trend/horizontal** analysis.
- **DuPont decomposition** of ROE:
  - 3-way: ROE = **net margin × asset turnover × leverage**.
  - 5-way: tax burden × interest burden × EBIT margin × asset turnover × leverage — isolates operating vs. financing vs. tax effects.

## Ratio categories
- **Activity** (turnover), **liquidity** (current, quick), **solvency** (debt/equity, interest coverage), **profitability** (margins, ROE/ROA), **valuation** (P/E, P/B).

## Earnings quality
- **High-quality earnings** are sustainable and backed by cash. Red flags: **earnings >> operating cash flow** (high accruals), rising DSO/inventory, frequent "one-offs", aggressive revenue recognition, capitalizing costs.
- **Accruals ratio** and the **cash-flow-to-net-income** ratio gauge quality.
- Compare against **peers and the firm''s own history**; adjust statements for off-balance-sheet items and non-recurring effects before forecasting.', 2, 4),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-mbs-abs-m01', 'cfa2-fixed-income-mbs-abs', 'Mortgage- & Asset-Backed Securities', '**Securitization** pools loans (mortgages, auto, cards) into tradable securities, moving them off the originator''s balance sheet via a bankruptcy-remote **SPV**.

## Mortgage-backed securities (MBS)
- **Agency** (gov-backed, minimal credit risk) vs. **non-agency**.
- **Prepayment risk** is the key feature: borrowers refinance when rates fall (**contraction risk**) and prepay slowly when rates rise (**extension risk**) → **negative convexity** for pass-throughs.
- **CMOs** carve cash flows into **tranches** to redistribute prepayment risk (e.g., sequential-pay, PAC vs. support tranches).

## Asset-backed securities (ABS)
- Backed by auto loans, credit cards, etc.; use **credit tranching** (senior/subordinated **waterfall**) and credit enhancement (overcollateralization, reserve accounts).
- **Amortizing** (autos) vs. **non-amortizing/revolving** (cards, with a lockout/revolving period).

## Analysis
- Value with prepayment models (**SMM/CPR**, PSA benchmark) on an interest-rate tree; use **OAS** to compare.', 2, 4),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-interest-rate-m01', 'cfa2-derivatives-interest-rate', 'Interest-Rate Derivatives & Swaptions', '## Building blocks
- **FRA**: locks a single future rate. **Interest-rate swap**: a strip of FRAs (fixed ↔ floating).
- **Caps & floors**: a **cap** = a series of **caplets** (calls on a reference rate) — protects a floating-rate **borrower** when rates rise. A **floor** = a series of **floorlets** (puts) — protects a floating-rate **lender/investor** when rates fall.
- A **collar** = long cap + short floor (cheaper protection).

## Swaptions
- A **payer swaption**: the right to **pay fixed / receive floating** — gains value as rates **rise** (like a call on rates). Used to hedge against rising rates or to enter a swap later.
- A **receiver swaption**: right to **receive fixed** — gains as rates **fall**.
- Swaptions are used to hedge **callable/putable debt** and to monetize rate views.

## Uses
- Convert fixed↔floating exposure, hedge a future borrowing, or cap funding costs. Valuation uses an arbitrage-free model calibrated to the current curve and rate **volatility**.', 2, 4),
  ('cfa2-economics-currency-determination-m01', 'cfa2-economics-currency-determination', 'Currency Exchange Rate Determination', '## Quote conventions
**Price/base** (P/B): units of price currency per 1 base. A **cross rate** is derived from two pairs sharing a currency.

## Spot vs forward
Forward points come from the **interest-rate differential** (covered interest rate parity). The higher-yield currency trades at a forward **discount**.

## Determination frameworks
- **Mundell-Fleming**: links monetary/fiscal policy to exchange rates via interest rates and trade. Expansionary **monetary** policy → lower rates → currency **depreciates** (with high capital mobility).
- **Monetary models**: relative money supply and inflation drive long-run value.
- **Portfolio-balance**: sustained fiscal deficits eventually pressure the currency lower.

## Other
- **Real exchange rate** adjusts the nominal rate for relative price levels.
- Capital flows often dominate trade flows short-term; **central-bank intervention** and **capital controls** can influence rates temporarily.', 2, 3),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-real-estate-m01', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'Real Estate & REITs', '## Four quadrants
Private/public × equity/debt: **direct property, REITs, mortgages, MBS**.

## Valuing direct property
| Approach | Idea |
| --- | --- |
| **Income (direct capitalization)** | Value = **NOI / cap rate** |
| **DCF** | PV of forecast NOI + reversion (terminal) value |
| **Cost** | Replacement cost − depreciation + land |
| **Comparable sales** | Adjust recent transactions |

- **Cap rate ≈ discount rate − NOI growth**; a lower cap rate ⇒ higher value/lower yield.
- **NOI** = rental income − operating expenses (before financing and taxes).

## REITs
- Pass-through vehicles that must distribute most income (tax-advantaged); valued by **NAV**, **price-to-FFO/AFFO**, and **dividend discount**.
- **FFO** = net income + real-estate depreciation − gains on sales; **AFFO** subtracts maintenance capex (a cleaner cash measure).
- Provide income, diversification, and inflation sensitivity; public REITs are more **liquid** but more **equity-correlated** than direct property.', 2, 3),
  ('cfa2-ethics-professionalism-integrity-m01', 'cfa2-ethics-professionalism-integrity', 'Professionalism & Market Integrity (Standards I & II)', '## Standard I — Professionalism
- **I(A) Knowledge of the Law**: follow the **stricter** of law or the Code; dissociate from violations.
- **I(B) Independence & Objectivity**: avoid gifts/pressure that could bias you; modest items only.
- **I(C) Misrepresentation**: no untrue statements; **plagiarism** is misrepresentation.
- **I(D) Misconduct**: no dishonesty, fraud, or conduct reflecting on professional integrity.

## Standard II — Integrity of Capital Markets
- **II(A) Material Nonpublic Information (MNPI)**: do not act or cause others to act on MNPI. Use the **mosaic theory** (combining public + non-material non-public is fine) and **firewalls**.
- **II(B) Market Manipulation**: no transaction-based (e.g. **wash trades, spoofing, pump-and-dump**) or information-based manipulation.

> Exam approach: read the vignette, match the action to the precise sub-standard, and choose the least-compliant option. Recommended procedures (firewalls, restricted lists, compliance review) often resolve cases.', 2, 3),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting-m01', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting', 'Capital Budgeting & Investment Decisions', '## Decision rules
- **NPV** = PV(inflows) − initial outlay. **Accept if NPV > 0** — the theoretically correct rule (adds shareholder value).
- **IRR**: the discount rate making NPV = 0. Accept if IRR > cost of capital. Beware **multiple/no IRRs** with non-conventional cash flows and **ranking conflicts** with NPV for mutually exclusive or different-scale projects — **follow NPV**.
- Others: **payback** (ignores time value and post-payback flows), **discounted payback**, **profitability index** = PV(inflows)/outlay.

## Cash-flow principles
- Use **incremental, after-tax** cash flows; include **opportunity costs** and **externalities**; **exclude sunk costs** and ignore **financing costs** (captured in the discount rate).
- Account for **depreciation tax shields**, **net working-capital** changes, and **terminal/salvage** value.

## Analytical tools
- **Sensitivity, scenario, and Monte Carlo** analysis; **real options** (timing, expansion, abandonment) add value not captured by static NPV.', 2, 3),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-economics-markets-m01', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-economics-markets', 'Economics & Investment Markets', '## Discount-rate building blocks
Any asset''s value is the PV of expected cash flows. The required return = **real risk-free rate + expected inflation + risk premium**. The **real risk-free rate** tracks expected real GDP growth and the inter-temporal rate of substitution.

## Business cycle & asset classes
- **Default-free bond** yields ≈ expected future short rates + a **term premium**; the yield curve embeds growth/inflation expectations.
- **Credit spreads** widen in recessions (rising default risk) and narrow in expansions — credit is **pro-cyclical**.
- **Equities** carry a higher risk premium and are most sensitive to the cycle; expected return ≈ risk-free + equity risk premium.
- **Real estate** blends bond-like (lease income) and equity-like (cyclical) features.

## Implications
- Discount rates rise with growth and risk aversion. **Pro-cyclical** assets (equities, credit) earn premia for poor recession-time payoffs. Inflation expectations shift nominal yields and the relative appeal of real vs. nominal assets.', 2, 3),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-long-lived-assets-m01', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-long-lived-assets', 'Long-Lived Assets & Impairment', '## Capitalize vs. expense
- **Capitalize** costs that provide future benefits (PP&E, qualifying development); **expense** routine costs. Capitalizing boosts current income/assets and smooths earnings; expensing front-loads costs.
- **Borrowing costs** on qualifying assets are capitalized (IFRS/GAAP differ on details).

## Depreciation/amortization
- Methods: **straight-line**, **accelerated** (DDB), units-of-production. Estimates: useful life, salvage. Longer lives / higher salvage → lower expense, higher income (a quality lever).

## Measurement models
- **Cost model** (cost − accumulated depreciation − impairment). **IFRS revaluation model** allows upward revaluation (to OCI). **US GAAP** prohibits upward revaluation.

## Impairment
- **IFRS**: impaired if carrying value > recoverable amount (higher of fair-value-less-costs and value-in-use); **reversals allowed**.
- **US GAAP**: two-step — if carrying value > undiscounted future cash flows, write down to fair value; **no reversal**.
- Intangibles: finite-life are amortized & tested on indicators; **indefinite-life and goodwill** are not amortized but tested annually.', 2, 5),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-yield-curve-strategies-m01', 'cfa2-fixed-income-yield-curve-strategies', 'Yield-Curve & Active FI Strategies', '## Positioning for a curve view
- **Stable curve**: **buy-and-hold**, **roll down** (ride the curve), sell convexity, use carry/leverage.
- **Level change (rates fall)**: **extend duration**; (rates rise) **shorten duration**.
- **Slope**: expect **flattening** → **barbell**/long the long end; expect **steepening** → **bullet**/short the long end. A **bull steepener** vs **bear flattener** etc. describe level+slope combos.
- **Curvature**: **butterfly** trades (long the wings vs the belly, or vice-versa).

## Structures
- **Bullet** (concentrated at one maturity), **barbell** (short + long), **ladder** (even spread). For a given duration, the **barbell** has more **convexity** (helps with large/volatile moves) but usually a lower yield.

## Tools & risks
- **Key rate durations** target specific maturities; **duration-neutral** trades isolate slope/curvature.
- Active managers also rotate **sector/credit** and use **leverage** (repo). Watch **rolldown vs. forward-implied** returns: profit only if realized rates beat the curve''s forwards.', 2, 5),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-currency-equity-forwards-m01', 'cfa2-derivatives-currency-equity-forwards', 'Currency & Equity Forward Pricing', 'Each forward applies **cost-of-carry**, adjusting for the underlying''s income/cost.

## Equity forwards
- **F₀ = (S₀ − PV(dividends)) × (1 + r)^T**. Dividends are a carry benefit, lowering the forward. For an index, use a continuous **dividend yield q**: F₀ = S₀·e^((r − q)T).

## Fixed-income (bond) forwards
- Subtract the **PV of coupons**: F₀ = (S₀ − PV(coupons))(1 + r)^T. Quoted relative to the cheapest-to-deliver and a conversion factor for futures.

## Currency forwards (covered interest rate parity)
- **F (price/base) = S × (1 + r_price)^T / (1 + r_base)^T**. The currency with the **higher** interest rate trades at a forward **discount**.

## Valuation over time
- A forward''s value = PV of (current forward price − contracted forward price). Zero at initiation; drifts with spot, rates, and carry. Arbitrage (cash-and-carry / reverse) enforces these prices.', 2, 5),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing-m01', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing', 'Hypothesis Testing', '## Steps
State **H₀** (null) and **Hₐ** (alternative) → choose a test statistic and **significance level α** → compute → compare to the critical value / **p-value** → decide.

## Errors
- **Type I**: reject a true H₀ (probability = **α**). **Type II**: fail to reject a false H₀ (probability β). **Power = 1 − β**.
- Lowering α reduces Type I but raises Type II.

## Common tests
| Test | Use |
| --- | --- |
| **z-test** | known variance / large sample |
| **t-test** | unknown variance, normal population |
| **Chi-square** | a single variance |
| **F-test** | equality of two variances; overall regression |

- **One-tailed vs two-tailed** depends on the alternative.
- A **p-value < α** ⇒ reject H₀. **Statistical** significance ≠ **economic** significance (after costs/taxes).
- **Non-parametric** tests (e.g. Spearman rank) apply when distribution assumptions fail.', 2, 4),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-probability-bayes-m01', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-probability-bayes', 'Probability Concepts & Bayes', '## Core rules
- **Addition**: P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B).
- **Multiplication**: P(A and B) = P(A|B)·P(B); independent ⇒ P(A and B) = P(A)P(B).
- **Total probability**: P(A) = Σ P(A|Sᵢ)P(Sᵢ) over a mutually exclusive, exhaustive set.

## Bayes'' formula
**P(event | info) = [P(info | event) / P(info)] × P(event)** — updates a **prior** into a **posterior** with new evidence.

## Expected value & moments
- **E[X] = Σ p·x**; **Var = Σ p·(x − E[X])²**.
- **Covariance/correlation** drive portfolio variance: Var(aX+bY) = a²σ_X² + b²σ_Y² + 2ab·Cov(X,Y).

## Counting
- **Combinations** nCr (order irrelevant) vs **permutations** nPr (order matters); the **multiplication rule** for sequential choices. Useful for scenario/labeling problems and binomial-tree node counts.', 2, 5),
  ('cfa2-economics-business-cycles-m01', 'cfa2-economics-business-cycles', 'Aggregate Output & Business Cycles', '## Measuring output
- **GDP** = C + I + G + (X − M). **Nominal** vs **real** (inflation-adjusted via the GDP deflator). **Potential GDP** is full-employment output; the **output gap** is actual − potential.

## Aggregate demand & supply
- **AD** slopes down; **SRAS** up; **LRAS** vertical at potential. Shocks shift them, moving price level and output.

## Business cycle phases
Expansion → **peak** → contraction/recession → **trough** → recovery.
- **Inventory-sales** ratio rises early in a downturn; unemployment is **lagging**.
- **Indicators**: leading (new orders, building permits, yield curve), coincident (industrial production, employment), lagging (CPI, average duration of unemployment).

## Inflation & unemployment
- **Demand-pull** vs **cost-push** inflation; the **Phillips curve** trades off (short-run) inflation and unemployment; **NAIRU** is the non-accelerating-inflation rate.
- Schools: **Keynesian** (manage AD), **Monetarist** (steady money growth), **New Classical** (rational expectations).', 2, 4),
  ('cfa2-economics-trade-capital-flows-m01', 'cfa2-economics-trade-capital-flows', 'International Trade & Capital Flows', '## Gains from trade
- **Comparative advantage** (Ricardo): trade benefits both parties even if one has an absolute advantage, by specializing where opportunity cost is lowest. **Heckscher-Ohlin**: countries export goods intensive in their abundant factor.

## Trade restrictions
- **Tariffs, quotas, voluntary export restraints, subsidies**. They protect domestic producers but create a **deadweight loss** and raise consumer prices; a large country may gain via terms-of-trade.

## Balance of payments
- **Current account** (trade in goods/services, income) + **capital/financial account** ≈ 0. A current-account **deficit** is financed by a financial-account **surplus** (capital inflows).
- Persistent deficits relate to low **savings vs. investment** and can pressure the currency.

## Institutions
- **WTO** (trade rules), **IMF** (balance-of-payments support/stability), **World Bank** (development). Trading blocs (FTAs, customs unions, common markets) deepen integration and cause **trade creation** vs **trade diversion**.', 2, 5),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-infrastructure-m01', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-infrastructure', 'Infrastructure & Real Assets', '## Infrastructure
- Long-lived physical assets — transport (roads, airports), utilities, social (hospitals, schools), and **digital** (towers, data centers).
- **Brownfield** (existing, lower risk, income now) vs **greenfield** (to-be-built, higher risk, development upside).
- Characteristics: **stable, often inflation-linked cash flows**, high barriers, long horizons, and **regulatory** sensitivity. Provides income, diversification, and an **inflation hedge**; low correlation to equities.

## Other real assets
- **Timberland & farmland**: returns from biological growth, commodity prices, and land appreciation; inflation hedges.
- **Commodities** (covered separately) and natural resources.

## Portfolio role & risks
- Match the **liability/horizon** profile (e.g., pensions like long, inflation-linked infrastructure cash flows).
- Risks: **regulatory/political**, leverage, construction (greenfield), illiquidity, and demand/usage risk. Access via **direct, listed (e.g. infra funds/REITs), or private funds**.', 2, 4),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-due-diligence-m01', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'Alternatives: Due Diligence & Appraisal', '## Performance appraisal
- Alternatives have **non-normal** returns, so volatility/Sharpe understate risk. Use **downside** measures (Sortino, max drawdown), **VaR/CVaR**, and scenario analysis.
- **Smoothed** (appraisal-based) returns understate volatility/correlation — **unsmooth** before optimizing.
- Private funds: **IRR** (money-weighted) and multiples — **TVPI, DPI (realized), RVPI (unrealized)**; compare to **vintage-year** peers, not a public index alone.

## Fees
- **2-and-20** with a **hurdle** and **high-water mark**; **clawback** protects LPs; **management fee on committed vs invested** capital matters.

## Due diligence
- **Investment DD**: strategy, edge, repeatability, team. **Operational DD** (a leading cause of failure): independent **administrator/custodian/auditor**, valuation policy, controls, compliance, key-person and business risk.
- Assess **liquidity terms** (lockups, gates), leverage, capacity, and alignment of interests.', 2, 5),
  ('cfa2-ethics-duties-clients-employers-m01', 'cfa2-ethics-duties-clients-employers', 'Duties to Clients & Employers (Standards III & IV)', '## Standard III — Duties to Clients
- **III(A) Loyalty, Prudence & Care**: act for the client''s/beneficiary''s benefit; for pensions, the **beneficiaries** come first.
- **III(B) Fair Dealing**: disseminate recommendations/changes fairly; no favoring select clients.
- **III(C) Suitability**: match recommendations to each client''s IPS (objectives & constraints); for advisory relationships consider the **total portfolio**.
- **III(D) Performance Presentation**: fair, accurate, complete (don''t cherry-pick).
- **III(E) Confidentiality**: keep current/former/prospective client information confidential (unless illegal activity / legal requirement).

## Standard IV — Duties to Employers
- **IV(A) Loyalty**: act for the employer; don''t deprive it of your skills; **no taking client lists/records**; whistle-blowing protects the market/clients.
- **IV(B) Additional Compensation Arrangements**: get **written consent** for outside compensation that competes with the employer''s interest.
- **IV(C) Responsibilities of Supervisors**: take reasonable steps to **prevent and detect** violations by those you supervise (adequate compliance procedures).', 2, 4),
  ('cfa2-ethics-conflicts-priority-m01', 'cfa2-ethics-conflicts-priority', 'Conflicts of Interest & Priority of Transactions (Standard VI)', '## Standard VI — Conflicts of Interest
- **VI(A) Disclosure of Conflicts**: **fully and fairly disclose** anything that could impair independence/objectivity (ownership, board seats, compensation structures, broker relationships) — prominently and in plain language.
- **VI(B) Priority of Transactions**: **client and employer trades take precedence** over a member''s own. Personal trading must never disadvantage clients (restricted lists, pre-clearance, blackout/restricted periods for IPOs and private placements).
- **VI(C) Referral Fees**: disclose to clients and the employer any compensation for referrals so clients can assess potential bias.

## Putting it together
Many vignettes mix VI with I(B) Independence and III(B) Fair Dealing. Recommended procedures: **disclosure**, **personal-trading policies/pre-clearance**, **restricted lists**, and **compliance review**. The default cure for a conflict is **disclosure + management**, not concealment.', 2, 5),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-working-capital-m01', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-working-capital', 'Working Capital & Liquidity Management', '## Cash conversion cycle
**CCC = DSO + DOH − DPO** (days sales outstanding + days inventory − days payables). A **shorter** CCC frees up cash and improves liquidity; some retailers run a **negative** CCC (suppliers finance them).

## Liquidity ratios
- **Current** = CA/CL; **quick** = (cash + marketable securities + receivables)/CL (excludes inventory); **cash ratio** = (cash + marketable securities)/CL (strictest).

## Short-term funding & investing
- Sources: committed > uncommitted lines, **revolvers**, **commercial paper**, factoring.
- **Cost of trade credit** when forgoing a discount ("2/10 net 30"): (1 + 0.02/0.98)^(365/20) − 1 ≈ **44.6%** — usually worth taking the discount.
- Invest excess cash safely/liquidly; measure with money-market yields (bond-equivalent yield for comparison).

## Management
- Optimize receivables (credit terms, collections), inventory (turnover vs. stockouts), and payables (stretch without harming suppliers). Maintain adequate liquidity buffers for operating needs.', 2, 4),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital-m01', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital', 'Cost of Capital (WACC)', '## WACC
**WACC = w_d·r_d·(1 − t) + w_p·r_p + w_e·r_e**, using **target (market-value) weights**. The after-tax cost of debt reflects the interest **tax shield**.

## Component costs
- **Debt**: yield-to-maturity on current debt (or matrix/spread for unrated).
- **Preferred**: r_p = dividend / price.
- **Equity**: usually **CAPM** r_e = R_f + β(market risk premium); or bond-yield-plus-risk-premium / dividend-discount.

## Beta estimation
- Use a comparable''s beta: **unlever** (remove its leverage) then **relever** at the subject firm''s capital structure (Hamada): β_L = β_U[1 + (1 − t)(D/E)].

## Use & cautions
- WACC is the discount rate for **average-risk** projects; adjust up/down for project-specific risk. Use a **marginal** (forward) cost of capital. The **MCC schedule** rises with the amount of new capital raised (break points).', 2, 5),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-risk-return-capm-m01', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-risk-return-capm', 'Portfolio Risk, Return & CAPM', '## Diversification
- Portfolio variance depends on weights, variances, and **covariances/correlations**: combining assets with correlation < 1 reduces risk. **Systematic** (market) risk can''t be diversified away; **unsystematic** can.

## The efficient frontier & CAL
- The **efficient frontier** holds the best return per unit of total risk. Adding a **risk-free asset** creates the **Capital Allocation Line**; the tangency portfolio gives the **CML** (uses total risk σ).

## CAPM & SML
- **E[R_i] = R_f + β_i(E[R_m] − R_f)**. **Beta** = Cov(i, m)/Var(m) measures systematic risk.
- The **Security Market Line** plots expected return vs. beta; assets above the SML are **undervalued** (positive alpha).
- **Assumptions** (frictionless markets, homogeneous expectations, one period) are unrealistic, but CAPM remains the workhorse for the cost of equity. Multifactor models extend it.', 2, 4),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-ips-allocation-m01', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-ips-allocation', 'IPS & Asset Allocation', '## The portfolio management process
**Planning → Execution → Feedback.** The **Investment Policy Statement (IPS)** governs planning.

## IPS = objectives + constraints
- **Objectives**: **risk** tolerance (ability + willingness — use the **lower**) and **return** requirement.
- **Constraints (mnemonic: T-T-L-L-U)**: **Time horizon, Taxes, Liquidity, Legal/regulatory, Unique circumstances**.

## Asset allocation
- **Strategic (SAA)**: long-run policy weights from the IPS and capital market expectations — the dominant driver of long-run return variability.
- **Tactical (TAA)**: short-run deviations to exploit views. **Rebalancing** keeps the portfolio near policy.
- Approaches: **asset-only** (mean-variance optimization), **liability-relative**, **goals-based**.

## Execution & review
- Implement via security selection/trading; monitor, rebalance, and revise the IPS as circumstances change. Diversify across asset classes per the chosen allocation.', 2, 5),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-financial-institutions-m01', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-financial-institutions', 'Analysis of Financial Institutions', 'Banks and insurers differ from industrial firms — their assets are largely **financial** and they are heavily regulated.

## Frameworks
- **CAMELS**: Capital adequacy, Asset quality, Management, Earnings, Liquidity, Sensitivity to market risk — the supervisory rating system for banks.
- **Basel III** sets minimum **capital ratios** (CET1, Tier 1, total), a **liquidity coverage ratio (LCR)** and a **net stable funding ratio (NSFR)**.

## Key metrics
- **Net interest margin** = net interest income / average earning assets.
- **Provision for loan losses** signals expected credit deterioration.
- Insurers: analyse **combined ratio** (P&C) and the quality of reserves.

Financial institutions carry systemic importance, so leverage, funding stability and off-balance-sheet exposures are central to the analysis.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-share-based-compensation-m01', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-share-based-compensation', 'Share-Based Compensation', 'Share-based pay aligns employees with shareholders but creates **expense** and **dilution**.

## Accounting
- Compensation expense is based on the **fair value at grant date** and recognised over the **vesting period**.
- **Stock options** are valued with an option-pricing model (e.g., Black–Scholes–Merton); inputs include volatility, expected life and the risk-free rate.
- **Restricted stock units (RSUs)** are valued at the grant-date share price.

## Analyst adjustments
- Higher assumed **volatility** or **expected life** raises option fair value and expense.
- Options create potential dilution captured in **diluted EPS** via the treasury-stock method.
- Compare grant practices across peers — aggressive assumptions can understate expense.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-reporting-quality-m01', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-reporting-quality', 'Evaluating Quality of Financial Reports', '**Reporting quality** (faithful representation) is distinct from **earnings quality** (sustainable, adequate returns). Both matter.

## Quality spectrum
From high to low: GAAP & decision-useful → within-GAAP but biased choices → non-compliant → fictitious transactions.

## Warning signs
- Revenue recognised too early or **bill-and-hold** arrangements.
- Growing gap between **net income and operating cash flow** (accruals).
- Capitalising costs that should be expensed; frequent one-time charges.
- **Beneish M-score** and accruals ratios flag manipulation risk.

## Motivations & conditions
Fraud typically needs **opportunity, motivation, and rationalization**. Weak governance and aggressive incentive pay raise the risk.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-equity-valuation-process-m01', 'cfa2-equity-valuation-process', 'Equity Valuation: Applications & Processes', 'Valuation estimates an asset''s **intrinsic value** to compare against market price.

## Model families
- **Present-value (DCF)**: dividend discount, free cash flow, residual income.
- **Multiplier (relative)**: P/E, P/B, EV/EBITDA versus peers.
- **Asset-based**: value of net assets.

## Choosing a model
- Use **DDM** for dividend-paying, stable firms; **FCF** when dividends differ from capacity to pay; **residual income** when cash flows are negative or accounting is informative.
- The **intrinsic value vs price** gap, if the market is efficient enough to correct, drives the expected **alpha**.

Analysts also distinguish **going-concern** value from **liquidation** value and apply control or marketability adjustments.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-equity-return-concepts-m01', 'cfa2-equity-return-concepts', 'Return Concepts & Required Return', 'The **required return** is the minimum an investor demands for the risk taken; it is the discount rate in valuation.

## Building the required return
- **CAPM**: r = Rf + β(ERP), where ERP is the equity risk premium.
- **Bond-yield-plus-risk-premium**: r = YTM on the firm''s debt + equity risk premium.
- **Multifactor (Fama–French, BIRR)** add size, value and macro factors.

## Related concepts
- **Expected return** vs **required return**: a positive difference implies the asset is undervalued.
- The **equity risk premium** can be estimated with historical or forward-looking (Gordon growth) methods.
- Small, illiquid stocks may warrant a **size or illiquidity premium** added to CAPM.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-equity-dividend-discount-m01', 'cfa2-equity-dividend-discount', 'Discounted Dividend Valuation', 'The **dividend discount model (DDM)** values a share as the present value of future dividends.

## Gordon growth model
V₀ = D₁ / (r − g), valid when g is constant and g < r.
- Implied growth: g = r − D₁/V₀.
- **Sustainable growth** g = retention ratio (b) × ROE.

## Multistage models
- **Two-stage**: high growth then a terminal Gordon phase.
- **H-model** approximates a linearly declining growth rate: V₀ = [D₀(1+gL) + D₀·H·(gS−gL)] / (r−gL), where H is half the high-growth period.

## Cautions
The Gordon model is highly sensitive to the **(r − g)** denominator — small changes in g produce large value swings.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-embedded-options-m01', 'cfa2-fixed-income-embedded-options', 'Bonds with Embedded Options', 'Embedded options change a bond''s cash flows and risk.

## Value relationships
- **Callable bond** = straight bond − value of the call (issuer''s option). Value_call-option benefits the issuer.
- **Putable bond** = straight bond + value of the put (investor''s option).

## Analytics
- Use a **binomial interest-rate tree** and **backward induction**; at each call node the value is capped at the call price.
- **Option-adjusted spread (OAS)** removes the option''s effect, allowing comparison of bonds with different optionality.
- **Effective duration** and **effective convexity** (which can be **negative** for callables at low rates) capture rate sensitivity with optionality.

Higher interest-rate volatility raises both option values, lowering a callable''s price and raising a putable''s.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-securitization-m01', 'cfa2-fixed-income-securitization', 'Introduction to Fixed-Income Securitization', '**Securitization** pools financial assets and issues tradable securities backed by their cash flows.

## Structure
- The originator sells assets to a bankruptcy-remote **special purpose entity (SPE/SPV)**, isolating them from the originator''s credit.
- The SPE issues **tranches** with differing priority (senior/mezzanine/subordinated) — the **waterfall** directs cash flows.

## Benefits
- Lowers funding cost, transfers risk, and creates securities tailored to investor risk appetite.

## Mortgage examples
- **Pass-through** securities share pooled mortgage cash flows pro rata.
- **CMOs** carve prepayment risk into tranches; **subordination** provides credit enhancement.

Key risks are **prepayment/extension** risk and the credit quality of the underlying pool.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-structured-instruments-m01', 'cfa2-fixed-income-structured-instruments', 'Structured Instruments: CDOs & CLOs', '**Collateralized debt obligations (CDOs)** repackage a pool of debt into tranches sold to investors with different risk appetites.

## Mechanics
- A manager assembles collateral (bonds, loans, or other CDOs); an SPE issues **senior, mezzanine and equity** tranches.
- The **equity tranche** absorbs first losses and earns the residual; senior tranches are protected by **subordination**.
- **CLOs** are CDOs backed specifically by **leveraged loans**.

## Analysis
- Requires assessing collateral credit quality, correlation of defaults, and the **cash-flow waterfall**.
- The arbitrage is the spread between collateral yield and the cost of the funding tranches.

The 2008 crisis highlighted model risk from underestimating **default correlation** in these structures.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-forward-commitments-m01', 'cfa2-derivatives-forward-commitments', 'Pricing & Valuation of Forward Commitments', 'Forwards, futures and swaps are **forward commitments** priced by **no-arbitrage**.

## Forward price
- F₀ = S₀ × (1 + r)^T for a non-income asset (cost-of-carry).
- Adjust for **carry benefits** (dividends, coupons, convenience yield) and **carry costs** (storage): F₀ = (S₀ − PV(benefits) + PV(costs))(1+r)^T.

## Value over life
- At initiation the value is **zero** (price set so no cash changes hands).
- Later, Vt = PV of (current forward price − original forward price).

## Futures vs forwards
Daily **marking-to-market** and margin distinguish futures; prices converge to spot at expiry. Interest-rate correlation can make futures and forward prices differ slightly.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-contingent-claims-m01', 'cfa2-derivatives-contingent-claims', 'Valuation of Contingent Claims', '**Contingent claims** (options) have asymmetric payoffs valued via **risk-neutral** methods.

## Binomial model
- Risk-neutral probability π = (1 + r − d)/(u − d).
- Option value = [π·c⁺ + (1−π)·c⁻] / (1 + r), discounting expected payoffs at the risk-free rate.

## Black–Scholes–Merton (BSM)
- Values European options using S, X, r, T, volatility σ and (for continuous carry) dividend yield.
- **Put–call parity**: c + PV(X) = p + S.

## Greeks
- **Delta** (∂price/∂S), **gamma** (∂delta/∂S), **vega** (volatility), **theta** (time), **rho** (rate).
- Delta hedging offsets small underlying moves; gamma measures how often the hedge must be rebalanced.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-binomial-trees-m01', 'cfa2-derivatives-binomial-trees', 'Binomial Trees & Risk-Neutral Valuation', 'The **binomial tree** models the underlying as moving up (u) or down (d) each period.

## Risk-neutral valuation
- Under the risk-neutral measure, expected returns equal the risk-free rate.
- π = (1 + r − d)/(u − d); (1 − π) is the down probability.
- Discount expected payoffs at r — actual probabilities and risk preferences are not needed.

## Multi-period & American options
- Build the tree forward, then value **backward** from expiry.
- For **American options**, at each node take the greater of the exercise value and the continuation value — capturing early exercise (relevant for American puts and dividend-paying calls).

More time steps make the binomial value converge to the Black–Scholes–Merton price.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-regression-diagnostics-m01', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-regression-diagnostics', 'Evaluating Regression Model Fit', 'After estimating a regression, assess **goodness of fit** and **validity of assumptions**.

## Fit measures
- **R²** and **adjusted R²** (which penalises added regressors).
- **F-test** for overall significance; **t-tests** for individual coefficients.
- Standard error of the estimate (SEE) measures typical residual size.

## Assumption violations
| Problem | Effect | Detection |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Heteroskedasticity** | biased SEs | Breusch–Pagan test |
| **Serial correlation** | biased SEs | Durbin–Watson |
| **Multicollinearity** | inflated SEs, unstable coefficients | high R² but few significant t; VIF |

Use robust (White) standard errors to correct heteroskedasticity/serial correlation.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-logistic-regression-m01', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-logistic-regression', 'Model Extensions: Logistic Regression', '**Logistic regression** models a **binary** (0/1) outcome — e.g., default vs no default.

## The model
- Models the **log-odds**: ln[p/(1−p)] = b₀ + b₁X₁ + … , so predicted probability p = 1/(1 + e^(−(b₀+…))) lies in (0,1).
- Coefficients are estimated by **maximum likelihood**, not least squares.
- e^(bᵢ) gives the **odds ratio** for a one-unit change in Xᵢ.

## Interpretation & fit
- A positive coefficient raises the probability of the event.
- Fit is judged by **likelihood ratio tests** and classification accuracy, not R².

Logistic regression is standard for probability-of-default and other classification problems where a linear probability model would give values outside 0–1.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-big-data-projects-m01', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-big-data-projects', 'Big Data Projects & Data Processing', 'A financial **data-analysis project** follows a structured pipeline.

## Steps
1. **Conceptualise** the modeling task and outcome.
2. **Collect** structured/unstructured data.
3. **Prepare & wrangle**: cleanse (fix errors, missing values), then **preprocess** (extract, aggregate, transform).
4. **Explore**: exploratory data analysis, **feature selection** and **feature engineering**.
5. **Train** the model; **tune** hyperparameters.

## Text (unstructured) data
- Steps: text cleansing, tokenization, then building a **document-term matrix**; techniques include **stemming** and removing stop words.

## Model evaluation
- Metrics: **precision, recall, F1 score, accuracy**; a **confusion matrix** summarises errors.
- Guard against **overfitting** using out-of-sample (cross-)validation.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-economics-exchange-rate-parity-m01', 'cfa2-economics-exchange-rate-parity', 'Exchange Rate Parity Conditions', 'Parity conditions link **spot rates, forward rates, interest rates and inflation**.

## The conditions
- **Covered interest rate parity (CIRP)**: enforced by arbitrage; the forward premium equals the interest-rate differential.
- **Uncovered interest rate parity (UIP)**: the expected spot change equals the interest-rate differential (no arbitrage forces it, so it often fails short-term).
- **Purchasing power parity (PPP)**: exchange rates adjust to inflation differentials.
- **International Fisher effect**: interest-rate differentials reflect expected inflation differentials.

## Practical use
The **carry trade** profits precisely because UIP tends to fail — high-yield currencies do not depreciate as UIP predicts, on average, until crises hit.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-economics-carry-trade-m01', 'cfa2-economics-carry-trade', 'The FX Carry Trade & Volatility', 'The **carry trade** borrows a low-yield (funding) currency and invests in a high-yield currency.

## Mechanics & risk
- Profits if the high-yield currency does not depreciate by the interest differential (i.e., **UIP fails**).
- Returns show **negative skew and fat tails**: many small gains, occasional large losses when funding currencies rally sharply ("crash risk").
- Losses cluster in periods of **high volatility / risk aversion** (VIX spikes).

## Related trades
- **Volatility trading** uses options/variance swaps to take a view on FX volatility.
- Diversifying across several currency pairs reduces, but does not remove, the crash risk inherent in carry.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-economics-growth-theories-m01', 'cfa2-economics-growth-theories', 'Theories of Economic Growth', 'Growth theory explains long-run increases in output per capita.

## Main models
- **Classical (Malthusian)**: growth in output is offset by population growth, so per-capita income reverts to subsistence.
- **Neoclassical (Solow)**: output depends on capital, labour and technology; because capital has **diminishing returns**, long-run per-capita growth depends on **exogenous technological progress**. Economies converge to a steady state.
- **Endogenous growth**: technology and knowledge are produced within the model (R&D), with **constant returns** to capital broadly defined, so policy can affect the long-run growth rate.

## Convergence
- **Absolute convergence**: poor countries grow faster and catch up.
- **Conditional convergence**: catch-up only among countries with similar savings, technology and institutions.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-private-capital-m01', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-private-capital', 'Private Capital: Structures & Returns', '**Private capital** spans private equity and private debt.

## Fund structure
- Organised as **limited partnerships**: the **GP** manages; **LPs** provide capital.
- Fees: typically **"2 and 20"** — ~2% management fee plus ~20% **carried interest** above a **hurdle rate**, often with a **high-water mark** and **clawback**.
- Capital is drawn over time (**committed vs called capital**); the **J-curve** shows early negative returns before value is realised.

## Performance measures
- **IRR** (money-weighted) and multiples: **MOIC**, **DPI** (distributed/paid-in), **RVPI**, **TVPI**.
- Vintage-year benchmarking compares funds raised in the same year.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-digital-assets-m01', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-digital-assets', 'Digital Assets & Tokenization', '**Digital assets** record ownership on a **distributed ledger (blockchain)**.

## Types
- **Cryptocurrencies** (e.g., a medium of exchange), **tokens** (utility or security), and **stablecoins** (pegged to fiat or collateral).
- **Tokenization** represents real assets (real estate, art) as blockchain tokens, potentially improving **liquidity and fractional ownership**.

## Investment characteristics
- Largely **non-income-producing**; value is driven by adoption and scarcity, giving **high volatility**.
- Investable via direct holding, **futures**, or exchange-traded products.

## Risks
Custody/security (key management), **regulatory uncertainty**, and immature market infrastructure. Diversification benefit claims should be tested, as correlations rise in stress.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-natural-resources-m01', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-natural-resources', 'Natural Resources & Timberland', 'Natural resources include **commodities, farmland and timberland**.

## Timberland
- Returns come from **biological growth**, **timber price** changes, and **land** value.
- Trees can be stored "on the stump" — harvest can be delayed when prices are low, a valuable flexibility.

## Farmland
- Returns from **crop income** (rents) plus land appreciation; row crops vs permanent crops differ in risk.

## Role in a portfolio
- Provide **inflation protection** and low correlation with financial assets.
- Illiquid, with valuation based on appraisals; subject to weather, environmental and regulatory risk.

Commodities themselves earn no income; their return comes from spot changes plus the **roll yield** in futures.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-ethics-diligence-records-m01', 'cfa2-ethics-diligence-records', 'Diligence, Records & Conduct (Standards V & VII)', '## Standard V — Investment Analysis, Recommendations & Actions
- **V(A) Diligence and Reasonable Basis**: have adequate research supporting any action; when using others'' research, verify its soundness.
- **V(B) Communication**: distinguish **fact from opinion**; disclose the basic process and limitations.
- **V(C) Record Retention**: keep records supporting analysis (CFA Institute recommends **7 years**).

## Standard VII — Responsibilities as a CFA Member/Candidate
- **VII(A)**: do not compromise the integrity of the **CFA exam** or Institute.
- **VII(B)**: reference the CFA designation **properly** — no exaggeration; "CFA" is an adjective, not a noun, and there is no partial designation.

Diligence violations often arise from relying on third-party research without verification.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-ethics-gips-composites-m01', 'cfa2-ethics-gips-composites', 'GIPS: Composite Construction & Presentation', 'The **Global Investment Performance Standards (GIPS)** ensure fair, comparable performance reporting.

## Composites
- A **composite** aggregates all **actual, fee-paying, discretionary** portfolios managed to a similar strategy.
- Firms must **include every eligible portfolio** — no cherry-picking — and define composites by strategy.
- New portfolios are added on a timely, consistent basis; terminated portfolios stay in the composite through their last full measurement period.

## Presentation
- Show at least **5 years** of GIPS-compliant history, building to **10 years**.
- Returns are **time-weighted**; disclose fees, benchmark, and dispersion.
- Compliance is **firm-wide**; firms cannot claim partial compliance and should undergo **verification**.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-ethics-ethics-cases-m01', 'cfa2-ethics-ethics-cases', 'Ethics: Application Cases', 'Applying the Code and Standards to realistic scenarios.

## Common patterns
- **Material nonpublic information (II-A)**: do not trade or cause others to trade; use a **firewall**. The **mosaic theory** permits combining public and non-material nonpublic pieces.
- **Loyalty when leaving an employer (IV-A)**: no soliciting clients or taking records before departure; skills and memory are fine.
- **Suitability (III-C)**: match recommendations to the client''s IPS and circumstances.
- **Priority of transactions (VI-B)**: client and employer trades come before personal trades.
- **Independence (I-B)**: refuse gifts/pressure that could compromise objectivity; modest client gifts must be disclosed.

Best practice when in doubt: **disclose, document, and put client interests first**.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-dividends-repurchases-m01', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-dividends-repurchases', 'Analysis of Dividends & Share Repurchases', 'Firms return cash via **dividends** or **share repurchases**.

## Equivalence and differences
- In a perfect market, a buyback and a cash dividend of equal size leave shareholder wealth the same.
- **Buybacks** offer flexibility and tax timing; they raise EPS only if the after-tax earnings yield exceeds the after-tax cost of funds.
- A buyback **increases** EPS when shares are repurchased at a P/E where earnings yield > financing cost.

## Theories
- **Dividend irrelevance** (MM): payout policy doesn''t affect value in perfect markets.
- **Bird-in-the-hand**: investors prefer certain dividends (disputed).
- **Tax preference** and **signaling**: dividend changes convey management''s confidence; cuts are strong negative signals.

## Safety metrics
Dividend **coverage ratio** and **FCFE coverage** gauge sustainability.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring-m01', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring', 'Corporate Restructuring & Divestitures', 'Restructuring alters a firm''s **portfolio of businesses or capital structure**.

## Forms
- **Expansion**: acquisitions, mergers, joint ventures.
- **Contraction**: **divestitures**, **spin-offs** (shares distributed to existing holders), **split-offs**, **carve-outs** (partial IPO of a unit), **liquidations**.
- **Financial restructuring**: recapitalizations, debt-for-equity swaps, leveraged buyouts.

## Rationale & analysis
- Firms restructure to **focus** (unlock a "conglomerate discount"), raise cash, or improve efficiency.
- Evaluate with the same **valuation tools** (DCF, comparables) applied to the affected units; assess the effect on leverage and cash flow.

Spin-offs often create value by improving management focus and transparency.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-esg-analysis-m01', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-esg-analysis', 'ESG Considerations in Analysis', '**ESG** integrates **Environmental, Social and Governance** factors into investment analysis.

## The pillars
- **Environmental**: carbon emissions, resource use, climate risk.
- **Social**: labour practices, product safety, community relations.
- **Governance**: board independence, shareholder rights, executive pay, ownership structure.

## Integration approaches
- **Negative/exclusionary screening**, **positive/best-in-class**, **thematic**, **impact investing**, and **ESG integration** into financial models.

## Materiality
- Focus on **financially material** ESG issues, which differ by industry.
- ESG risks can affect **cash flows, cost of capital, and franchise value** — e.g., stranded-asset risk for carbon-heavy firms or litigation from poor governance.

Data challenges include inconsistent disclosure and divergent third-party ratings.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-active-management-m01', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-active-management', 'Analysis of Active Portfolio Management', 'Active management seeks **excess return (alpha)** relative to a benchmark.

## Value added and the IR
- **Active return** = portfolio return − benchmark return; **active risk** (tracking error) is its volatility.
- **Information ratio (IR)** = active return / active risk — the key measure of skill per unit of active risk.

## The fundamental law of active management
Expected active return ≈ **IC × √BR × TC × σ_active**, where:
- **IC** = information coefficient (skill/forecasting accuracy),
- **BR** = breadth (number of independent decisions per year),
- **TC** = transfer coefficient (how fully signals reach the portfolio given constraints).

Doubling breadth raises the IR by only **√2**. The **Sharpe ratio** of an optimal active-plus-benchmark portfolio rises with IR² added to the benchmark''s Sharpe².', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-algorithmic-trading-m01', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-algorithmic-trading', 'Algorithmic Trading & HF Markets', '**Algorithmic trading** uses computer programs to execute orders by rules.

## Execution algorithms
- **VWAP/TWAP**: trade to match a volume- or time-weighted average price, minimising market impact.
- **Implementation shortfall** algorithms balance market impact against the risk of price drift while waiting.
- **Liquidity-seeking** algorithms hunt hidden liquidity across venues (including **dark pools**).

## High-frequency trading (HFT)
- Extremely low-latency strategies: market making, statistical arbitrage, latency arbitrage.
- Benefits: tighter spreads, more liquidity. Risks: **flash crashes**, fleeting liquidity, and system/technology risk.

Regulation focuses on market integrity, circuit breakers and preventing manipulative practices such as spoofing.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-backtesting-m01', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-backtesting', 'Backtesting & Simulation', '**Backtesting** applies a strategy to historical data to estimate how it would have performed.

## Process
- Specify the strategy and investment universe, then roll forward: form portfolios, rebalance, and record returns.
- Report risk-adjusted performance (Sharpe, IR) net of realistic costs.

## Pitfalls
- **Survivorship bias** (dead firms excluded) and **look-ahead bias** (using data not available at the time).
- **Data snooping / overfitting**: testing many variants inflates the best result.
- Historical scenarios may not repeat — a single path understates tail risk.

## Beyond backtesting
- **Scenario analysis** stresses specific events; **Monte Carlo simulation** generates many random paths to reveal the distribution of outcomes, capturing non-normal, fat-tailed risks.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-biases-portfolio-m01', 'cfa3-behavioral-biases-portfolio', 'Behavioral Biases in Portfolio Construction', 'Biases split into **cognitive errors** (faulty reasoning — *moderate* them with education/data) and **emotional biases** (feeling-based — *adapt* the plan around them).

## Cognitive
- Belief perseverance: **conservatism, confirmation, representativeness, illusion of control, hindsight.**
- Information processing: **anchoring, mental accounting, framing, availability.**

## Emotional
- **Loss aversion** (→ disposition effect), **overconfidence, self-control, status quo, endowment, regret aversion.**

## In portfolios
- Biases cause **inertia/under-diversification** (status quo, endowment, home bias), **excessive trading** (overconfidence), and **mis-set risk** (overconfidence lowers, loss aversion raises perceived risk).
- A **behaviorally modified asset allocation** lets clients deviate modestly from the rational optimum so they can stick with the plan. Higher willingness/standard-of-living-risk tolerance → larger allowable deviations.
- **Goals-based investing** uses mental-accounting tendencies constructively (separate risk buckets per goal).', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-forecasting-m01', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-forecasting', 'Forecasting Returns & Economic Analysis', '**Capital market expectations (CME)** are forecasts of risk, return, and correlations used to set asset allocation.

## Pitfalls
- Anchoring, status-quo and confirmation bias; **data-mining**; using **non-stationary** historical data; survivorship bias; **time-period bias**.

## Tools
- **Grinold-Kroner** equity return ≈ dividend yield − Δshares + nominal earnings growth + Δ(P/E):  D/P − ΔS + i + g + ΔPE.
- **Building-block** fixed income: real risk-free + inflation + term + credit + liquidity premia.
- **Singer-Terhaar** (ICAPM): blends fully integrated and fully segmented market risk premia using the degree of integration; RP = ρ·σ·(market Sharpe).
- **Taylor rule** for the policy rate: r* + inflation + 0.5(inflation gap) + 0.5(output gap).

## Business cycle
- Map phases (initial recovery → late expansion → slowdown → contraction) to asset-class returns; leading/coincident/lagging indicators inform timing.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-strategic-m01', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-strategic', 'Strategic Asset Allocation', '**Strategic asset allocation (SAA)** sets long-run policy weights from the IPS objectives and constraints; it is the dominant driver of long-run return variability.

## Approaches
- **Asset-only** (e.g. mean-variance optimization, MVO) — maximize utility = E[R] − ½λσ². MVO is **sensitive to inputs** ("error maximization"); remedies: constraints, **resampling**, Black-Litterman, reverse optimization.
- **Liability-relative** — manage assets against liabilities (pensions, insurers); surplus optimization.
- **Goals-based** — sub-portfolios per goal, each with its own time horizon and required probability of success.

## Risk concepts
- **Risk budgeting** allocates risk (not just capital); **risk parity** equalizes risk contributions.
- **Rebalancing**: calendar vs **percentage-range** (corridor); wider corridors for illiquid/high-cost/low-correlation assets. Rebalancing harvests a **diversification return** and is contrarian.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-ldi-m01', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-ldi', 'Liability-Driven Investing & Immunization', '**LDI** manages assets to fund a known set of liabilities.

## Single liability — immunization
- **Match Macaulay duration** of assets to the liability (investment) horizon, set **PV(assets) ≥ PV(liability)**, and **minimize convexity/dispersion** of the asset portfolio so it tracks the liability under parallel shifts.
- Immunization locks in a target return against **small parallel** rate moves; rebalance as durations drift.

## Multiple liabilities
- **Cash-flow matching** (dedication) buys bonds whose cash flows match each liability — lowest risk, least flexible.
- **Duration matching** matches the portfolio''s BPV to the liabilities'' BPV — more flexible, exposes some structural/curve risk.

## Risks
- **Structural risk** (non-parallel/twist moves), **basis/spread risk**, model and rebalancing risk. Minimizing dispersion reduces structural risk.
- Contingent immunization allows **active management** while a safety cushion (surplus) exists.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-performance-attribution-m01', 'cfa3-performance-attribution', 'Attribution & Appraisal Measures', 'Performance evaluation = **measurement** (return), **attribution** (sources), and **appraisal** (skill vs luck).

## Attribution
- **Brinson** decomposes active return into **allocation** (over/underweighting sectors), **selection** (picking within sectors), and **interaction**.
- **Returns-based** uses a factor model; **holdings/transaction-based** uses actual positions (more accurate, more data).

## Appraisal (risk-adjusted) ratios
| Measure | Risk used |
| --- | --- |
| **Sharpe** | Total volatility |
| **Treynor** | Beta |
| **Information ratio** | Tracking error (active risk) |
| **M² (Modigliani)** | Total risk, expressed in return units |
| **Jensen''s alpha** | CAPM beta (excess over required) |

- **IR ≈ IC × √breadth** (fundamental law of active management).
- Use the **appropriate, investable, specified-in-advance** benchmark; check for **style/benchmark misfit**.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-passive-active-factor-m01', 'cfa3-equity-pm-passive-active-factor', 'Passive, Active & Factor Investing', '## The spectrum
- **Passive** — track an index; low cost, low tracking error. Methods: full replication, **stratified sampling**, optimization.
- **Active** — seek alpha; higher fees and tracking error. Judged by the **information ratio** (active return / active risk).
- **Factor / smart beta** — rules-based tilts to rewarded factors (value, size, momentum, quality, low-volatility) — between passive and active.

## Building exposure
- **Index funds/ETFs** and **derivatives** (equity futures, total-return swaps) give cheap, liquid beta; a **completion overlay** fills gaps.
- **Long-only vs long/short**: market-neutral isolates alpha by removing beta.

## Costs & implementation
- Watch **management fees, transaction costs, market impact, and taxes**; rebalancing and turnover erode returns.
- **Tracking error** measures deviation from benchmark; passive minimizes it, active budgets it.
- Fundamental law: **IR ≈ IC × √breadth** — more independent, skillful bets raise the achievable information ratio.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-derivatives-m01', 'cfa3-risk-management-derivatives', 'Risk Management with Derivatives', 'Derivatives let managers **adjust exposures** cheaply without trading the underlying.

## Common overlays
- **Equity futures**: change beta. Contracts = [(β_target − β_port)/β_futures] × (Portfolio/Futures price). Sell to lower beta, buy to raise it.
- **Interest-rate futures/swaps**: adjust portfolio **duration**. Number of contracts ≈ [(D_target − D_port)/D_futures] × (Portfolio/Futures BPV).
- **Currency forwards**: hedge foreign-asset FX exposure (hedge ratio choice).
- **Options**: asymmetric protection — **protective put** (floor), **covered call** (income), **collar** (cap + floor cheaply).

## Discipline
- Define the **risk to hedge**, choose the instrument, size it, and monitor **basis risk** (the hedge and exposure not moving identically).
- Futures need **margin** and daily settlement; swaps/forwards are OTC with **counterparty risk** (mitigated by clearing/collateral).
- Hedging reduces both downside and upside; **dynamic** delta hedging requires rebalancing due to gamma.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-tax-estate-m01', 'cfa3-private-wealth-tax-estate', 'Private Wealth: Tax & Estate Planning', '## Taxes and returns
- **Tax drag** compounds: after-tax return r·(1 − t) over many years sharply reduces terminal wealth.
- **Asset location**: hold tax-inefficient assets (bonds, high-turnover) in **tax-deferred/exempt** accounts; tax-efficient (low-turnover equity) in taxable accounts.
- **Tax-loss harvesting** and deferring gains raise after-tax returns; **step-up in basis** at death can eliminate embedded gains.

## Account types
- **Tax-deferred** (contributions pre-tax, taxed on withdrawal) vs **tax-exempt** (after-tax in, tax-free out). Choice depends on current vs expected future tax rates.

## Estate planning
- **Gifting during life** (uses the lower of gift/estate tax, removes future growth from the estate) vs **bequests** at death.
- **Trusts** (revocable vs irrevocable), **life insurance**, and **charitable** vehicles manage transfer taxes and control.
- Core IPS inputs for individuals: **time horizon, liquidity needs, taxes, legal/regulatory, and unique circumstances** (the constraints), plus return/risk objectives. Human capital and **mortality/longevity risk** shape asset allocation and insurance needs.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-institutional-pensions-endowments-m01', 'cfa3-institutional-pensions-endowments', 'Pensions & Endowments', '## Defined-benefit pensions
- **Risk tolerance** rises with a younger workforce, a well-funded status, a lower plan/sponsor correlation, and a smaller plan relative to the sponsor.
- Liabilities are **bond-like** → **LDI** (match duration of assets to liabilities). Time horizon often long but tied to the active/retired mix.
- Liquidity needs depend on the **retired-lives proportion** and contributions.

## Endowments & foundations
- Goal: fund spending **in perpetuity** while preserving **real** value → high equity/alternatives allocation, long horizon, high risk tolerance.
- **Spending rules**: simple (% of beginning assets), rolling 3-year average (smooths), or **geometric/Yale** (weights prior spending and market value) to stabilize distributions.
- Return objective ≈ **spending rate + inflation + fees**; **US foundations** must typically distribute ~5% annually.

## Other institutions
- **Banks/insurers**: heavily regulated, liability-driven, lower risk tolerance, strong liquidity and ALM focus.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-rebalancing-execution-m01', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-rebalancing-execution', 'Rebalancing & Trade Execution', '## Rebalancing
- **Calendar** rebalancing (e.g. quarterly) is simple but ignores market moves; **percentage-range (corridor)** rebalances when a weight breaches a band.
- Optimal corridor **width** is *wider* for higher transaction costs, higher risk tolerance, and lower correlation with the rest of the portfolio; *narrower* for higher volatility.
- Rebalancing is **contrarian** (sells winners, buys losers) and can capture a **diversification return**; it controls risk, it doesn''t chase return.

## Execution costs
- **Implementation shortfall** = paper-portfolio return − actual return = explicit costs (commissions, fees, taxes) + implicit costs (**spread, market impact, delay/slippage, opportunity cost** of unfilled orders).
- **VWAP** benchmarks fills against volume-weighted average price.

## Trade strategy
- **Urgency vs cost**: aggressive (liquidity-demanding) trades pay more impact but less timing risk; patient (liquidity-supplying) trades risk adverse price moves.
- Algorithms: VWAP/TWAP, implementation-shortfall, and liquidity-seeking; large orders are **sliced** to limit impact.', 2, 1),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-currency-m01', 'cfa3-risk-management-currency', 'Currency Management', 'Foreign assets carry **two** return sources: the local asset return and the **currency** return.

## The strategic decision
- **Hedging ratio** ranges from fully unhedged → fully hedged. Drivers toward **more hedging**: short horizon, risk-averse client, high hedging-cost tolerance, low correlation of currency with the asset.
- Bonds: currency risk is large relative to return → often **hedged**. Equities: currency is a smaller share and adds diversification → often **partially** hedged.

## Tools
- **Forwards** (most common — precise, low cost; roll at maturity), **currency futures**, **options** (asymmetric), and **cross-hedges/proxy hedges** when a direct forward is costly.
- **Minimum-variance hedge ratio** = regression beta of asset (domestic-currency) returns on the exchange-rate return.

## Active overlays
- A **currency overlay** manager seeks alpha from FX separately from the asset managers. Tactics use carry, value (PPP), momentum, and volatility trading. **Roll yield** matters: hedging a forward at a premium/discount adds or subtracts return.', 2, 1),
  ('cfa3-ethics-asset-manager-code-m01', 'cfa3-ethics-asset-manager-code', 'Asset Manager Code & GIPS', '## Asset Manager Code (AMC)
Voluntary, firm-level conduct code (vs. the individual Code & Standards). Firms **claim compliance** for the whole firm; CFA Institute does not verify it. Six pillars of ethical responsibilities:
1. **Loyalty to clients** (place client interests first; confidentiality)
2. **Investment process & actions** (reasonable basis, fair dealing, no manipulation)
3. **Trading** (best execution; client brokerage for client benefit; priority of client trades)
4. **Risk management, compliance & support** (designated compliance officer, controls, recordkeeping)
5. **Performance & valuation** (fair, accurate, complete; no cherry-picking)
6. **Disclosures** (truthful, timely; conflicts, fees, costs)

## GIPS (Global Investment Performance Standards)
- Voluntary, ethical performance-presentation standards ensuring **fair representation and full disclosure**; compliance is **firm-wide**, not per-composite.
- **Composites** group all fee-paying discretionary portfolios of similar mandate (prevents survivorship/cherry-picking). Must show ≥ history (building to 10 years).
- Returns are **time-weighted** (TWR removes the effect of client cash flows); show benchmark and dispersion. **Verification** by a third party is recommended but optional.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-portfolio-role-m01', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-portfolio-role', 'Alternatives in Portfolio Management', 'Alternatives (private equity, hedge funds, real assets, private debt, commodities) are added for **diversification, return enhancement, and inflation protection**.

## Portfolio role & allocation
- Low (and unstable) correlations with stocks/bonds — but correlations **rise in crises**.
- Reported returns are **smoothed** (appraisal-based/stale pricing) → **understated volatility and correlation**; "unsmooth" before optimizing or MVO over-allocates to alternatives.
- Liquidity, long lock-ups, the **J-curve**, high fees (2-and-20), and operational/manager risk constrain sizing.

## Approaches
- Allocate by **role** (capital growth, income, diversification, inflation hedge) rather than by traditional asset-class labels.
- Use **liquidity tiers** and commitment pacing for private funds; model **vintage-year** diversification.
- Risk tools must capture **non-normal** returns (fat tails, negative skew) — VaR/volatility alone understate risk; use scenario analysis and downside measures.
- Benchmarks are difficult: peer-group/IRR for private; absolute or risk-factor for hedge funds.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-due-diligence-m01', 'cfa3-manager-selection-due-diligence', 'Manager Selection & Due Diligence', 'Manager selection has three steps: **universe definition → quantitative analysis → qualitative (operational) due diligence.**

## Type I vs Type II error
- **Type I**: hiring/keeping a manager with **no real skill** (false positive).
- **Type II**: rejecting/firing a **genuinely skilled** manager (false negative).
- Tightening selection criteria reduces Type I but raises Type II (and vice-versa).

## Quantitative
- Style/returns-based and holdings-based analysis; capture **alpha vs. factor exposures** (don''t pay alpha fees for cheap beta). Assess **information ratio**, consistency, drawdowns, and capacity. Beware short, noisy track records and **backfill/survivorship bias**.

## Qualitative & operational due diligence
- **Investment**: philosophy, process repeatability, team, decision rights.
- **Operational** (a major source of fund failure): independent **administrator/custodian/auditor**, valuation policy, controls, compliance, key-person and business risk, fee terms, and alignment.
- Output: a conviction-weighted recommendation, not just past returns.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-market-behavior-m01', 'cfa3-behavioral-market-behavior', 'Behavioral Finance & Market Anomalies', 'Behavioral finance challenges the **efficient market hypothesis** and rational-investor assumptions.

## Anomalies (apparent EMH violations)
- **Momentum** (returns persist short-term), **value & size** premia, **January/calendar** effects, **overreaction/reversal**, **post-earnings-announcement drift**.
- Many shrink after discovery or vanish net of costs/risk — possibly compensation for risk, data mining, or true mispricing.

## Bubbles & crashes
- Driven by **herding, overconfidence, regret, and disposition** effects; "**this time is different**" narratives. Prices detach from fundamentals, then reverse violently.

## Theories of market behavior
- **Adaptive Markets Hypothesis (Lo)**: applies evolution/ecology — efficiency varies with conditions; strategies "survive" until competed away.
- **Behavioral asset pricing** adds a sentiment premium to required return.

> Implication: markets are *mostly* efficient but exhibit periodic, behaviorally driven mispricing that disciplined investors may exploit (carefully).', 2, 1),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-investor-types-m01', 'cfa3-behavioral-investor-types', 'Behavioral Investor Types', 'Classifying clients helps advisers tailor portfolios and communication. The **BIT (Pompian)** framework:

| Type | Dominant biases | Risk tolerance |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Passive Preserver** | emotional (loss aversion, status quo, endowment, regret) | low |
| **Friendly Follower** | cognitive (availability, recency); follows trends | low-medium |
| **Independent Individualist** | cognitive (overconfidence, confirmation, self-attribution) | high |
| **Active Accumulator** | emotional (overconfidence, illusion of control) | high |

## Using the types
- **Emotional** biases → **adapt** the portfolio (build around them). **Cognitive** errors → **moderate** with education and data.
- Wealthier/higher-standard-of-living clients can tolerate larger deviations from the rational optimum.

## Limitations
- Investors don''t fit neatly into one box, can show multiple biases, and change over time. Use the BIT as a starting diagnostic, then confirm via discussion and a bias questionnaire.', 2, 2),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-adviser-client-m01', 'cfa3-behavioral-adviser-client', 'Adviser-Client Relations', 'Understanding client psychology improves the relationship and outcomes.

## Why it matters
- Better **risk profiling** (ability + willingness), more **suitable** portfolios, stronger trust, and clients who **stick with the plan** through volatility.
- A **behaviorally modified asset allocation** lets clients deviate modestly from the mean-variance optimum so they can stay invested.

## Practical steps
- Use **risk-tolerance questionnaires** and conversation to identify biases and the **behavioral investor type**.
- **Educate** to moderate cognitive errors; **accommodate** deep emotional biases.
- Frame information carefully (avoid triggering loss aversion); set expectations; agree the IPS jointly.

## Limits on deviation
- Larger allowable deviations when the client has **higher standard-of-living risk tolerance** and **emotional** (hard-to-change) biases; smaller deviations when biases are cognitive and the client''s financial security depends on staying near the optimum.', 2, 3),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-analyst-biases-m01', 'cfa3-behavioral-analyst-biases', 'Behavioral Biases of Analysts', 'Analysts are not immune to bias.

## Forecasting biases
- **Overconfidence**: too-narrow confidence intervals (**illusion of knowledge**), **illusion of control**, **self-attribution** (credit skill, blame luck). → over-precise forecasts.
- **Representativeness** & **base-rate neglect**: over-weighting recent or vivid data; **gambler''s fallacy**.
- **Confirmation bias**: seeking data that supports the thesis; **anchoring** to prior estimates or management guidance.
- **Availability**: over-weighting easily recalled scenarios.

## Company-interaction biases
- Over-reliance on **management**; halo effect (good company ≠ good stock); social pressure to match consensus.

## Remedies
- Use **systematic processes, base rates, and prior records**; pre-commit to decision rules; seek **disconfirming** evidence; perform **post-mortems**; use **structured** analysis and counter-arguments to widen confidence intervals.', 2, 4),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-committee-bias-m01', 'cfa3-behavioral-committee-bias', 'Biases in Committees & Markets', '## Investment committees
- **Social proof / groupthink**: members defer to the group or a dominant voice, suppressing dissent.
- Remedies: **anonymous input**, diverse membership, a **devil''s advocate**, structured voting, and a leader who solicits dissent before stating their own view.

## Market-level effects of bias
- **Momentum & herding** can create **bubbles** (overreaction up) and **crashes** (overreaction down). The **disposition effect** (holding losers, selling winners) contributes to **under-reaction** and momentum.
- **Halo effect** and **home bias** distort allocation; **trend-chasing** flows amplify cycles.

## Anomalies linked to biases
- **Value and momentum** premia, post-earnings drift, and calendar effects are partly behavioral.
- Whether anomalies are exploitable depends on **persistence, capacity, and costs** — many fade once widely known.', 2, 5),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-economic-analysis-m01', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-economic-analysis', 'Economic Analysis & the Business Cycle', 'Capital market expectations rest on macro analysis.

## Business cycle & assets
- Phases (initial recovery → early/late expansion → slowdown → contraction) map to returns: equities/credit do best early; defensives and bonds later.
- **Inflation**: rising inflation hurts bonds (and often stocks at high levels); deflation helps quality bonds.

## Monetary & fiscal policy
- **Taylor rule** for the neutral policy rate: r* + inflation + 0.5(inflation gap) + 0.5(output gap).
- The **yield curve** signals expectations: an **inverted** curve often precedes recession.

## Forecasting tools & pitfalls
- **Econometric models, leading indicators, surveys**. Drivers of trend growth: labor, capital, **productivity (TFP)**.
- Pitfalls: status-quo/anchoring bias, **data mining**, using **non-stationary** data, model uncertainty, and ignoring **regime shifts**. Distinguish **secular** trends from cyclical noise.', 2, 1),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-fixed-income-m01', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-fixed-income', 'Forecasting Fixed-Income Returns', '## Building-block expected return
**Real risk-free rate + expected inflation + term premium + credit premium + liquidity premium.** The **DCF/YTM** is the base forecast for a bond held to maturity (assuming reinvestment).

## Drivers
- **Government bonds**: expectations of short-rate path + a term premium; tied to growth/inflation and policy.
- **Credit**: spread compensates for **expected loss + risk/liquidity premium**; spreads are **pro-cyclical** (widen in recession). Return impact ≈ yield/carry + roll − (spread duration × Δspread) − expected loss.
- **Emerging-market debt**: add country/political risk and currency considerations.

## Methods
- **Survey/judgment**, building-block, and **DCF/scenario** approaches.
- Mind **reinvestment and rolldown**; forwards are the **break-even** — active return requires realized rates to differ from forwards. Watch liquidity and the credit cycle.', 2, 2),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-equity-m01', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-equity', 'Forecasting Equity Returns', '## Models
- **Grinold-Kroner**: E[R] ≈ **dividend yield − Δshares + nominal earnings growth + Δ(P/E)** = (D/P − ΔS) + (i + g) + ΔPE. Repricing (ΔPE) averages ~0 long-run.
- **Historical** averages (adjust for survivorship/time-period bias) and **risk-premium/build-up** (R_f + ERP).
- **Singer-Terhaar (ICAPM)**: blends fully integrated and segmented risk premia by the degree of market integration; RP_i = ρ_{i,M} · σ_i · (Sharpe of global market).

## Inputs
- The **equity risk premium** can be estimated via historical, forward (DDM-implied), or survey methods.
- Earnings growth ≈ trend GDP growth ± margin/share effects.

## Cautions
- Avoid extrapolating recent returns; account for **mean reversion** in margins and valuations, and regime changes. Cross-check models against each other.', 2, 3),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-real-estate-m01', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-real-estate', 'Forecasting Real Estate & Alternatives', '## Real estate expected return
**Cap rate (income) + NOI growth ± valuation change.** The **cap rate ≈ discount rate − growth**; expected return ≈ cap rate + NOI growth − cap-rate expansion.
- Real estate blends **bond-like** (lease income) and **equity-like** (cyclical value) behavior; it is sensitive to rates, the economy, and credit availability.
- **Smoothed appraisals** understate volatility/correlation — unsmooth before use.

## Other alternatives
- **Commodities**: price + **roll** (backwardation positive, contango negative) + collateral yield; an inflation hedge.
- **Private equity/credit**: build up an illiquidity premium over public comparables; model **vintage** and J-curve effects.
- **Hedge funds**: factor-based or absolute-return assumptions; account for **non-normality** and fees.

## Caution
- Reported alternative returns are **stale/smoothed**; adjust risk and correlation inputs before optimization.', 2, 4),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-exchange-rates-m01', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-exchange-rates', 'Forecasting Exchange Rates', '## Frameworks
- **Parity conditions** (PPP, uncovered IRP, international Fisher) — useful long-run anchors but poor short-run predictors.
- **Balance of payments / current account**: persistent deficits pressure a currency lower (flows), though capital flows can dominate.
- **Monetary & portfolio-balance** models: relative money growth, inflation, and asset demand.

## Short-run drivers
- **Capital flows** chasing relative returns (carry), **risk sentiment** (safe havens — USD, JPY, CHF — rally in stress), and **policy/intervention**.
- **Carry trades** profit when UIRP fails but carry **crash risk** (negative skew).

## In CME
- Currency forecasts feed **hedging decisions** for international assets (hedge ratio) and the home-currency expected returns. Combine fundamentals with **technical/sentiment** and acknowledge high forecast uncertainty.', 2, 5),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-approaches-m01', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-approaches', 'Asset Allocation Approaches', '## Asset-only methods
- **Mean-variance optimization (MVO)**: maximize utility = E[R] − ½λσ². Powerful but an **"error maximizer"** — highly sensitive to expected-return inputs, producing concentrated, unstable weights.
- Remedies: **constraints**, **resampled** efficiency, **Black-Litterman** (blend equilibrium reverse-optimized returns with views), and **reverse optimization**.

## Risk-based methods
- **Risk budgeting** allocates risk (not capital); **risk parity** equalizes each asset''s risk contribution (often levered).
- **Minimum-variance** and **maximum-diversification** portfolios.

## Factor-based
- Allocate to **rewarded factors** (value, size, momentum, quality, carry) rather than asset-class labels.

## Choosing
- Match to the investor: **asset-only** vs **liability-relative** vs **goals-based**. Account for costs, taxes, and the **rebalancing** policy. MVO with sensible constraints remains the workhorse.', 2, 2),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-liability-relative-m01', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-liability-relative', 'Liability-Relative Asset Allocation', 'Manages assets against the **liabilities** they must fund (pensions, insurers, banks).

## Approaches
- **Surplus optimization**: mean-variance on the **surplus** (assets − liabilities); maximize expected surplus return per unit of surplus volatility.
- **Hedging/return-seeking** split: a **liability-hedging** portfolio (duration-matched bonds) plus a **return-seeking** portfolio (growth assets) sized by the funded ratio and risk appetite.
- **Integrated asset-liability management (ALM)** jointly models both sides.

## Key concepts
- **Funded ratio** = assets / PV(liabilities); a surplus deficit raises risk.
- Liabilities are **bond-like**, so **interest-rate (duration) risk** dominates → match BPV; mind **inflation-linked** liabilities (use linkers).
- A better-funded plan can **de-risk** (glide path) toward more hedging assets.

## Risks
- **Surplus** volatility, **non-parallel** curve moves, longevity (pensions), and contribution/regulatory risk.', 2, 3),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-goals-based-m01', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-goals-based', 'Goals-Based Asset Allocation', 'Used mainly for **individuals**: build separate **sub-portfolios** for distinct goals, each with its own time horizon and **required probability of success**.

## Mechanics
- Assign each goal a **minimum-expectations** return and a confidence level; higher-priority/near-term goals get **safer** sub-portfolios; aspirational/long-term goals can hold **riskier** assets.
- Aggregates into an **overall** allocation, but the framing leverages clients'' natural **mental accounting** so they stay disciplined.

## Pros & cons
- **Pros**: intuitive, improves client behavior/adherence, ties risk to each goal.
- **Cons**: can be **sub-optimal** at the total-portfolio level (ignores cross-goal diversification), and more complex to manage.

## Module/overlay approach
- Use standardized **risk modules** (sub-portfolios at set risk levels) and allocate each goal to a module by its horizon and required success probability; an overlay manages the aggregate exposures and taxes.', 2, 4),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-constraints-m01', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-constraints', 'Asset Allocation with Constraints', 'Real-world allocation faces frictions beyond the textbook optimum.

## Constraints
- **Asset size** (too small to access some alternatives; too large faces capacity limits), **liquidity needs**, **time horizon**, **regulatory/legal**, and **tax**.
- **Illiquid assets** (PE, real estate, infrastructure): model **commitment pacing**, the **J-curve**, and a liquidity reserve; they can''t be rebalanced freely.

## Tax-aware allocation
- **After-tax** return and risk differ from pre-tax; **asset location** (tax-inefficient assets in sheltered accounts) and **tax-loss harvesting** add value. Rebalancing has tax costs — use **wider corridors** for taxable accounts.

## Other adjustments
- **Mental accounting/behavioral** modifications, **ESG** constraints, and home-country/currency limits.
- Rebalancing policy: **calendar vs corridor**; wider bands for illiquid, high-cost, or low-correlation assets.
- **Tactical** shifts and **automatic adjustments** (glide paths) respond to changing circumstances and capital market expectations.', 2, 5),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-indexing-m01', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-indexing', 'Index-Based Fixed-Income Investing', 'Replicating a bond index is harder than equities: indices have **thousands of issues**, many illiquid, with constantly changing composition (maturities, calls, new issuance).

## Replication methods
- **Full replication**: rarely feasible — too many illiquid bonds.
- **Stratified sampling (cell matching)**: divide the index into **cells** by key risk factors (duration/maturity bucket, sector, quality) and hold representative bonds matching each cell''s weight.
- **Enhanced indexing**: match the index''s **risk factors (duration, key-rate durations, sector/quality)** but make small bets to add value at low tracking error.

## Tracking risk
- Sources: mismatches in **duration, key-rate, sector, quality, and convexity**; transaction costs; and index reconstitution.
- Match **portfolio BPV/duration** and key-rate durations to the benchmark to control interest-rate tracking error.

## Vehicles
- **Bond ETFs/index funds**, **total-return swaps**, and **futures** give cheap, liquid exposure; mind liquidity and the **cash-bond basis**.', 2, 1),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-yield-curve-m01', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-yield-curve', 'Yield-Curve Strategies', 'Position for an expected change in the **level, slope, or curvature** of the yield curve.

## Level (duration)
- Expect **rates to fall** → **extend duration**; **rise** → **shorten**. Use **key-rate durations** to target specific maturities.

## Slope
- Expect **flattening** → **barbell** / overweight the long end; expect **steepening** → **bullet** / overweight the short-intermediate. Combinations: bull/bear steepeners and flatteners.

## Curvature
- **Butterfly** trades: long the wings vs the belly (or vice-versa), usually **duration-neutral**, to profit from curvature changes.

## Stable curve
- **Buy-and-hold**, **rolldown** (ride the curve), sell convexity, and use **carry/leverage**.

## Tools & caveats
- A given duration via a **barbell** has more **convexity** (helps in large moves) but lower yield than a **bullet**. Profit from active trades only if realized rates differ from the **forward-implied** rates.', 2, 2),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-credit-m01', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-credit', 'Credit Strategies', '## Top-down vs bottom-up
- **Top-down**: rotate **sector, quality, and credit-cycle** exposure based on the macro/credit outlook (add risk in recovery, de-risk before downturns).
- **Bottom-up**: pick individual **mispriced issuers** via fundamental/relative-value analysis.

## Spread analysis
- **G/I/Z-spread** and **OAS**; spread return ≈ carry/roll − **spread duration × Δspread** − expected loss.
- **Expected loss = PD × LGD**; spreads are **pro-cyclical** (widen in recessions).
- The **credit-spread curve** and **rolldown** matter, as do **liquidity** and the **CDS-cash basis**.

## Implementation & risks
- Cash bonds, **CDS/CDX index** (efficient long/short credit), and **total-return swaps**.
- Diversify idiosyncratic default risk; watch **liquidity, downgrade/migration**, and **tail** (jump-to-default) risk.
- **Spread duration** measures sensitivity to spread changes — the key risk metric for a credit portfolio.', 2, 3),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-leverage-m01', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-leverage', 'Leverage, Carry & Roll-Down', '## Leverage methods
- **Repo** (sale + repurchase agreement — the main tool), **futures/swaps** (embedded leverage), **securities lending**, and structured vehicles.
- **Leveraged return** ≈ r_assets + (borrowed/equity) × (r_assets − r_borrow). Leverage **magnifies gains and losses** and adds **funding/rollover risk** (repo haircuts can rise in stress).

## Carry & rolldown
- **Carry** = income earned (coupon/yield) minus financing cost. A positive **carry trade** profits if the curve is unchanged.
- **Rolldown return**: on a stable upward-sloping curve, a bond''s yield falls as it ages toward shorter maturities, producing price gains beyond the coupon.

## Risks
- Leverage amplifies **duration/spread** risk; **margin calls** can force selling at the worst time.
- The **repo haircut** and rate determine financing cost; a flat/steep curve and stable rates favor carry/rolldown strategies, but they lose if rates rise sharply.', 2, 4),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-multiple-liabilities-m01', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-multiple-liabilities', 'Managing Multiple Liabilities', '## Approaches (lowest to highest flexibility/risk)
- **Cash-flow matching (dedication)**: buy bonds whose cash flows **directly fund** each liability — minimal risk, least flexible, often higher cost.
- **Duration matching (immunization of multiple liabilities)**: match the portfolio''s **BPV (money duration)** to the liabilities'', keep **PV(assets) ≥ PV(liabilities)**, and **minimize dispersion/convexity difference** to limit structural risk. More flexible, exposes some curve risk.
- **Contingent immunization**: manage **actively** while a surplus cushion exists; revert to pure immunization if the cushion is exhausted.
- **Horizon matching**: cash-flow match near-term liabilities and duration-match longer ones.

## Risks
- **Structural risk** from **non-parallel (twist)** curve shifts — reduced by minimizing dispersion. Also **rebalancing**, **spread**, and **model** risk.
- Immunization locks in a return against **small, parallel** rate moves; rebalance as durations drift.', 2, 5),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-passive-m01', 'cfa3-equity-pm-passive', 'Passive Equity Investing', 'Track an index at low cost and low tracking error.

## Index construction matters
- **Market-cap weighted** (most common; self-rebalancing, but concentrates in large/overvalued names), **price-weighted**, **equal-weighted** (small-cap tilt, needs rebalancing), and **fundamental/factor-weighted** (smart beta).

## Replication methods
- **Full replication** (hold all constituents — best for liquid, narrow indices), **stratified sampling**, and **optimization** (minimize tracking error subject to constraints) for large/illiquid indices.

## Implementation
- **Index mutual funds/ETFs**, **derivatives** (equity futures, total-return swaps) for cheap, liquid beta and **completion overlays**.
- Costs: management fee, **transaction costs, market impact**, and **index reconstitution** (front-running at rebalance dates). Securities lending can offset some costs.
- **Tracking error** is the primary success metric for a passive manager; minimize it.', 2, 1),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-active-fundamental-m01', 'cfa3-equity-pm-active-fundamental', 'Active Equity: Fundamental Strategies', 'Discretionary, research-driven stock selection.

## Styles
- **Value** (low multiples, contrarian), **growth** (earnings momentum), **GARP** (growth at a reasonable price); **market-cap** and **sector** tilts.
- **Top-down** (macro/sector themes → stocks) vs **bottom-up** (company-by-company).

## Process & metrics
- Build a thesis (competitive position, earnings power, valuation) with a **margin of safety**.
- **Pitfalls/behavioral risks**: confirmation bias, anchoring, overconfidence, illusion of control, and **style drift**.
- Concentration vs diversification; **active share** measures how much holdings differ from the benchmark.

## Evaluation
- Judge skill with the **information ratio** (active return / active risk) and **active share**; high active share + high IR signals genuine, well-rewarded conviction.
- Capacity and turnover/costs constrain how much alpha survives.', 2, 2),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-active-quant-m01', 'cfa3-equity-pm-active-quant', 'Active Equity: Quantitative Strategies', 'Systematic, rules-based selection using factors and models.

## Process
1. Define the **investment thesis** and **factors** (value, momentum, quality, size, low-volatility, growth).
2. **Backtest** (out-of-sample/walk-forward) and build a **multifactor model**.
3. **Portfolio construction** via optimization with risk/turnover constraints.

## Risks & pitfalls
- **Overfitting/data mining**, **survivorship & look-ahead bias**, **factor crowding/decay**, and **regime change** (a model that worked can break).
- **Quant meltdowns** occur when crowded factor trades unwind simultaneously.

## Fundamental vs quant
- Quant: **breadth** (many small bets), discipline, low cost. Fundamental: **depth** (few high-conviction bets), adaptability.
- The **fundamental law**: **IR ≈ IC × √breadth** — quant relies on modest skill applied across many independent positions.', 2, 3),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-construction-m01', 'cfa3-equity-pm-construction', 'Equity Portfolio Construction & Risk', 'Translate insights into a portfolio while controlling risk and cost.

## Key building blocks (Active return decomposition)
Active return comes from **factor (rewarded) exposures** + **idiosyncratic (security selection)** + (minus) **costs**.

## Risk controls & metrics
- **Active share** (how different from the benchmark) and **active risk / tracking error** (volatility of active return). Pure stock-pickers have high active share; factor bets show up as active risk.
- Limit position sizes, sector/factor exposures, and **turnover**; manage **liquidity**.

## Approaches
- **Concentrated** (few high-conviction names — higher active share/risk) vs **diversified/closet-index** (low active share).
- **Long-only** vs **long/short** (market-neutral isolates alpha by removing beta).
- Mind **implementation costs** (impact, spread) — they erode paper alpha. Set a **risk budget** consistent with the mandate.', 2, 4),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-corporate-governance-m01', 'cfa3-equity-pm-corporate-governance', 'Corporate Governance & ESG Integration', '## Corporate governance (for equity owners)
- Strong governance — **independent, diverse boards**, aligned incentives, one-share-one-vote, sound audit/controls — lowers risk and the cost of capital.
- **Shareholder activism & engagement**: proxy voting, dialogue, and (for activists) board changes/capital-return campaigns to unlock value.
- Red flags: entrenchment (poison pills, staggered boards), dual-class shares, weak audit.

## ESG integration in equity
- **E/S/G** factors that are **material** affect cash flows and risk → adjust forecasts and discount rates.
- Approaches: **negative/exclusionary screening, positive/best-in-class, thematic, impact, ESG integration, and active ownership/engagement**.
- Watch **greenwashing** and data inconsistency; focus on financially **material** issues (e.g., SASB framework).
- **Stewardship** is part of the manager''s fiduciary duty; voting and engagement are tools to protect/enhance long-term value.', 2, 5),
  ('cfa3-performance-return-measurement-m01', 'cfa3-performance-return-measurement', 'Return Measurement: TWR & MWR', '## Two return measures
- **Time-weighted return (TWR)**: removes the effect of **external cash flows** (client deposits/withdrawals). It compounds sub-period returns between cash flows. **Best for judging the manager** (they don''t control flows) — the GIPS default.
- **Money-weighted return (MWR / IRR)**: the internal rate of return on actual cash flows. It **reflects the timing and size of flows**, so it measures the **investor''s** experience.

## When they differ
- They diverge when there are **large external cash flows** and volatile sub-period returns.
- If money flows in **before** a strong period, MWR > TWR; flows in before a weak period → MWR < TWR.

## Practical points
- Use TWR for **manager comparison/GIPS**; use MWR when the manager **controls the timing** of flows (e.g., private equity, where IRR is standard).
- Sub-period valuation at each cash flow improves TWR accuracy; **linked internal rate of return** approximates TWR when frequent valuations aren''t available.', 2, 1),
  ('cfa3-performance-attribution-approaches-m01', 'cfa3-performance-attribution-approaches', 'Return Attribution Approaches', 'Decompose **active return** (portfolio − benchmark) into its sources.

## Equity — Brinson
- **Allocation effect**: over/underweighting sectors vs the benchmark.
- **Selection effect**: picking out/under-performing securities within sectors.
- **Interaction**: the cross term. (Brinson-Hood-Beebower vs Brinson-Fachler differ in the allocation benchmark.)

## Types of attribution
- **Returns-based**: uses only periodic returns and a factor model — easy, less precise, can be **gamed**.
- **Holdings-based**: uses portfolio weights each period — more accurate, more data.
- **Transaction-based**: uses holdings **and trades** — most accurate, most data-intensive.

## Fixed income
- Decompose into **yield/income, roll, duration (level), curve (slope/curvature), spread/credit, and currency** effects.

## Use
- Attribution explains **how** active return was earned, distinguishing **allocation vs selection** skill and identifying unintended bets.', 2, 2),
  ('cfa3-performance-risk-attribution-m01', 'cfa3-performance-risk-attribution', 'Risk Attribution', 'Where return attribution explains realized return, **risk attribution** explains the sources of **risk** taken.

## Decomposition
- **Active risk (tracking error)²** = **active factor risk** + **active specific (selection) risk**. A factor-bet manager shows mostly factor risk; a pure stock-picker shows mostly specific risk.
- For absolute mandates, decompose **total risk** into factor and idiosyncratic contributions; **marginal/component VaR** attributes total risk to positions.

## Tying risk to process
- The **dominant risk source should match the stated strategy** (e.g., a "stock picker" with mostly factor risk has style drift).
- **Risk budgeting** sets ex-ante limits; risk attribution checks the **ex-post** outcome against them.

## Metrics
- Tracking error, beta, factor exposures, VaR/ES, and drawdowns. Combine with return attribution to judge whether the **risk taken was rewarded** (information ratio).', 2, 3),
  ('cfa3-performance-benchmarks-m01', 'cfa3-performance-benchmarks', 'Benchmarks & Benchmark Quality', '## Properties of a valid benchmark (mnemonic: SAMURAI)
**S**pecified in advance · **A**ppropriate · **M**easurable · **U**nambiguous · **R**eflective of current investment opinions · **A**ccountable (owned) · **I**nvestable.

## Types
- **Absolute** (a target return), **broad market index**, **style index**, **factor/risk model**, **manager universe (peer group)** — but peer groups are **not investable** and suffer survivorship bias.
- **Custom/strategy benchmarks** and **normal portfolios** best fit specialized mandates.

## Quality tests
- **Systematic biases**: low correlation between the manager''s active return and the benchmark''s active return vs the market signals a poor fit.
- **Tracking error** vs the benchmark should be reasonable; **coverage** (% of holdings in the benchmark) should be high.

## Why it matters
- A flawed (style-mismatched) benchmark distorts attribution and appraisal — making allocation/selection effects meaningless and unfairly judging the manager.', 2, 4),
  ('cfa3-performance-appraisal-m01', 'cfa3-performance-appraisal', 'Manager Continuation & Appraisal', 'Distinguishing **skill from luck** and deciding whether to retain a manager.

## Risk-adjusted appraisal ratios
| Measure | Risk used |
| --- | --- |
| **Sharpe** | total volatility |
| **Treynor** | beta |
| **Information ratio** | tracking error (active risk) |
| **M² (Modigliani)** | total risk, in return units |
| **Jensen''s alpha** | CAPM beta |

- **IR ≈ IC × √breadth** (fundamental law). An IR ~0.5 is good, ~1.0 exceptional.

## Continuation policy
- Avoid the **Type I / Type II** trade-off: firing a skilled manager after bad luck (Type II) vs keeping an unskilled one (Type I).
- A **manager continuation policy (MCP)** sets objective criteria and a **zone of acceptable performance** to reduce churn driven by noise.
- Short, volatile records make skill hard to confirm statistically; weight **process and operational** quality, not just past returns.', 2, 5),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-ips-m01', 'cfa3-private-wealth-ips', 'Individual IPS & Goals', 'An individual IPS = **objectives + constraints**, like institutions, but with personal nuances.

## Objectives
- **Return**: derived from goals (retirement, education, bequest) — solve for the required return given spending and horizon.
- **Risk**: combine **ability** (wealth vs. needs, horizon, flexibility) and **willingness** (psychological) — use the **lower**, then educate.

## Constraints (T-T-L-L-U)
- **Time horizon** (often multi-stage: pre- and post-retirement), **Taxes** (a major individual factor), **Liquidity** (spending, emergencies, large purchases), **Legal/regulatory** (trusts, jurisdiction), **Unique** (ESG, concentrated stock, illiquid business).

## Distinctive features
- **Goals-based** framing; **human capital** and **mortality/longevity** risk shape allocation and insurance.
- Multi-stage horizons and changing circumstances mean the IPS is **reviewed regularly**.', 2, 1),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-human-capital-m01', 'cfa3-private-wealth-human-capital', 'Human Capital & Life-Cycle Investing', '**Total wealth = financial capital + human capital** (the present value of future labor income).

## Implications
- **Bond-like human capital** (stable salary, e.g. tenured professor) → tilt financial assets **toward equities**.
- **Equity-like human capital** (volatile, correlated with markets, e.g. a trader) → tilt **toward bonds**.
- Human capital **declines** with age while financial capital grows → the equity share of *financial* assets typically **falls over the life cycle** (a glide path), even though total-wealth risk may stay similar.

## Risks to human capital
- **Mortality risk** (death cuts future income) → **life insurance**.
- **Longevity risk** (outliving assets) → **annuities**.
- **Earnings/disability risk** → disability insurance and an emergency reserve.

## Integration
- Optimal asset allocation considers human capital''s size and its **correlation** with risky assets, plus insurance to hedge its risks.', 2, 2),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-tax-efficiency-m01', 'cfa3-private-wealth-tax-efficiency', 'Tax-Efficient Investing & Asset Location', '## Tax drag compounds
After-tax return r(1 − t) over many years sharply reduces terminal wealth. Minimizing the tax drag is a major source of value for individuals.

## Levers
- **Asset location**: hold **tax-inefficient** assets (taxable bonds, high-turnover, REITs) in **tax-deferred/exempt** accounts; **tax-efficient** assets (low-turnover equity, municipals) in taxable accounts.
- **Tax-loss harvesting**: realize losses to offset gains/income.
- **Defer gains** (lower turnover) and use the **step-up in basis** at death.
- **Account types**: tax-deferred (pre-tax in, taxed out) vs tax-exempt (after-tax in, tax-free out) — choose by current vs expected future rates.
- Hold appreciated assets to be **gifted/bequeathed** rather than sold.

## Taxes & risk
- Taxes reduce both **return and risk** (the government shares gains/losses), so optimize on an **after-tax** basis. Wider rebalancing corridors for taxable accounts limit taxable trades.', 2, 3),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-estate-planning-m01', 'cfa3-private-wealth-estate-planning', 'Estate Planning & Wealth Transfer', 'Transfer wealth efficiently across generations and to charity.

## Lifetime gifts vs bequests
- **Gifting during life** removes future **appreciation** from the estate and may use a lower gift-tax rate/annual exclusion; **bequests** transfer at death (often with a **step-up in basis**).
- The relative benefit depends on gift vs estate tax rates, the donor''s vs recipient''s tax rates, and expected growth.

## Tools
- **Trusts** (revocable — flexible, in the estate; **irrevocable** — removes assets/control from the estate), **life insurance** (liquidity to pay estate taxes; often held in an irrevocable trust), and **charitable** vehicles (donor-advised funds, charitable trusts).
- **Generation-skipping** transfers and **forced heirship** rules vary by jurisdiction.

## Core ideas
- **Relative after-tax value** of gifting vs bequest drives the decision.
- Coordinate with the IPS: liquidity for taxes, concentrated-position management, and the client''s **legacy goals**.', 2, 4),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-risk-insurance-m01', 'cfa3-private-wealth-risk-insurance', 'Risk Management for Individuals', 'Individuals face risks beyond market risk; insurance transfers those that are **low-probability, high-severity**.

## Major personal risks & tools
- **Mortality** (premature death cuts human capital) → **life insurance** (term vs whole/permanent).
- **Longevity** (outliving assets) → **annuities** (immediate/deferred; longevity annuities).
- **Health/disability** → health and disability insurance.
- **Property/liability** → P&C and umbrella liability coverage.

## Risk management framework
- **Retain** (small, frequent), **reduce/avoid**, **transfer** (insure low-frequency/high-severity), and **self-insure** where wealth allows.
- The **economic (holistic) balance sheet** includes human capital and the PV of pensions/liabilities, not just financial assets.

## Insurance vs investing
- Life insurance hedges **early death**; annuities hedge **long life** — they are complementary. Need analysis sizes coverage from the gap between resources and obligations.', 2, 5),
  ('cfa3-institutional-db-pension-m01', 'cfa3-institutional-db-pension', 'Defined-Benefit Pension Management', '## Risk tolerance drivers
A DB plan''s ability to take risk **rises** with: a **younger** workforce (longer horizon), a **higher funded ratio**, a **smaller** plan relative to the sponsor, a **financially strong** sponsor, and **low correlation** between plan assets and the sponsor''s business.

## Objectives & constraints
- **Return**: at minimum, fund the liabilities (the discount/actuarial rate is a benchmark).
- **Risk**: surplus risk; balance funding cost vs contribution volatility.
- **Liquidity**: driven by the **retired-lives proportion** (benefit payments) and contributions; **time horizon** long but tied to workforce age.
- **Legal**: ERISA-type fiduciary rules; **Taxes**: usually tax-exempt.

## LDI
- Liabilities are **bond-like** → **liability-driven investing** (duration/BPV match) plus a return-seeking sleeve. Inflation-linked benefits → linkers. Better-funded plans **de-risk** along a glide path.', 2, 1),
  ('cfa3-institutional-foundations-m01', 'cfa3-institutional-foundations', 'Foundations & Endowments IPS', 'Both support a mission **in perpetuity**, implying long horizons, high risk tolerance, and a real-return focus.

## Return objective
≈ **spending rate + inflation + investment fees**. **US private foundations** must distribute ~**5%** annually; endowments set their own spending rule.

## Spending rules (endowments)
- **Simple** (% of beginning assets — volatile), **rolling 3-year average** (smooths), **geometric/Yale** (weights prior spending + a % of market value — most stable).

## Constraints
- **Time horizon**: very long/perpetual. **Liquidity**: modest near-term (spending) but constrained by large **illiquid alternatives** allocations. **Taxes**: largely tax-exempt. **Legal/regulatory**: UPMIFA-type prudence; donor restrictions. **Unique**: mission/ESG constraints.

## Allocation
- The **endowment model**: heavy equities and **alternatives** (PE, real assets, hedge funds) for real returns over long horizons, accepting illiquidity.', 2, 2),
  ('cfa3-institutional-insurance-m01', 'cfa3-institutional-insurance', 'Insurance Companies', 'Insurers invest premiums (the "**float**") to fund future claims; they are **liability-driven** and heavily regulated.

## Life vs P&C
| | Life insurers | Property-casualty (P&C) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Liabilities | Long-duration, predictable | Shorter, **lumpy/uncertain** (catastrophes) |
| Investing | Long bonds, ALM/duration match, some equities | Shorter bonds + **more liquidity**; equities for surplus |
| Key risk | Interest-rate/disintermediation | Underwriting/cat risk, liquidity |

## Constraints
- **Risk**: low — protect policyholders/solvency. **Liquidity**: high for P&C (claims), lower for life. **Time horizon**: long (life), shorter (P&C). **Taxes**: taxable, complex. **Legal/regulatory**: solvency capital (e.g., RBC, Solvency II) drives asset choices.

## Themes
- **Asset-liability management** and **duration matching** dominate; credit quality is emphasized; a **surplus** portfolio can take more risk than reserves backing liabilities.', 2, 3),
  ('cfa3-institutional-banks-m01', 'cfa3-institutional-banks', 'Banks & Asset-Liability Management', 'A bank''s **securities portfolio** is a residual/liquidity buffer managed within an **asset-liability management (ALM)** framework.

## Goals
- Manage the **net interest margin** and the **duration gap** between assets (loans, securities) and liabilities (deposits, borrowings).
- Maintain **liquidity** (e.g., LCR/NSFR), meet **regulatory capital** (Basel risk-weights favor high-quality, liquid assets), and provide **collateral**.

## Constraints
- **Risk**: low for the securities book (it backs deposits/regulatory needs). **Liquidity**: high. **Time horizon**: tied to the liability structure. **Taxes**: taxable. **Legal/regulatory**: capital and liquidity rules dominate.

## Tools
- Use **duration/key-rate** measures and derivatives (swaps, futures) to manage the duration gap and interest-rate risk; tilt to **high-quality, liquid** securities (governments, agencies) for regulatory and liquidity reasons.', 2, 4),
  ('cfa3-institutional-sovereign-m01', 'cfa3-institutional-sovereign', 'Sovereign Wealth Funds', 'State-owned funds investing national reserves/resource revenues. Objectives vary by **type**:

| Type | Purpose | Horizon/risk |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Budget stabilization** | smooth commodity/revenue swings | short horizon, low risk, liquid |
| **Savings/future generations** | preserve wealth across generations | very long, high risk tolerance |
| **Development** | fund domestic projects | long, project-linked |
| **Reserve investment** | earn more on FX reserves | varies, often liquid/high-grade |
| **Pension reserve** | pre-fund future pension liabilities | long, liability-aware |

## Constraints
- **Time horizon/risk** depend on type (stabilization = conservative; savings = aggressive). **Liquidity**: high for stabilization, low for savings/development. **Legal/regulatory & political**: governance, transparency (Santiago Principles), and political scrutiny.

## Themes
- Long-horizon savings funds resemble **endowments** (equity/alternatives heavy); stabilization funds resemble **liquidity reserves** (short bonds).', 2, 5),
  ('cfa3-ethics-code-standards-m01', 'cfa3-ethics-code-standards', 'Code of Ethics & the Seven Standards', 'The **Code of Ethics** sets the ethical principles; the **Standards of Professional Conduct** make them actionable. The most heavily tested area at **every** level.

## The seven Standards
| # | Standard | Core idea |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **I** | Professionalism | Knowledge of law, independence, no misrepresentation, no misconduct |
| **II** | Integrity of Capital Markets | No MNPI use, no manipulation |
| **III** | Duties to Clients | Loyalty/care, fair dealing, suitability, performance, confidentiality |
| **IV** | Duties to Employers | Loyalty, additional compensation, supervision |
| **V** | Investment Analysis & Actions | Diligence/reasonable basis, communication, record retention |
| **VI** | Conflicts of Interest | Disclose conflicts, priority of transactions, referral fees |
| **VII** | Responsibilities as a Member/Candidate | Don''t compromise CFA/CFA Institute integrity; conduct in the program |

## Approach
- Follow the **stricter** of law or the Code. Read each case, map the action to the precise sub-standard, and pick the **least-compliant** choice.', 2, 1),
  ('cfa3-ethics-professionalism-m01', 'cfa3-ethics-professionalism', 'Professionalism & Market Integrity (I & II)', '## Standard I — Professionalism
- **I(A) Knowledge of the Law**: follow the **stricter** of law/Code; **dissociate** from violations.
- **I(B) Independence & Objectivity**: avoid gifts/pressure that bias you; modest items only; firewalls.
- **I(C) Misrepresentation**: no untrue statements, no guaranteed returns on risky assets; **plagiarism** is misrepresentation.
- **I(D) Misconduct**: no dishonesty/fraud reflecting on professional integrity.

## Standard II — Integrity of Capital Markets
- **II(A) Material Nonpublic Information**: don''t act/cause others to act on **MNPI**. **Mosaic theory** (public + non-material nonpublic) is allowed; use **firewalls**.
- **II(B) Market Manipulation**: no **transaction-based** (wash trades, spoofing, pump-and-dump) or **information-based** manipulation.

> Recommended procedures — restricted lists, firewalls, compliance review — frequently resolve I- and II-type vignettes.', 2, 2),
  ('cfa3-ethics-duties-clients-m01', 'cfa3-ethics-duties-clients', 'Duties to Clients (Standard III)', '## Standard III — Duties to Clients
- **III(A) Loyalty, Prudence & Care**: place clients'' interests first; for pensions, loyalty runs to the **beneficiaries**. Use **client brokerage (soft dollars)** for the client''s benefit; seek **best execution**.
- **III(B) Fair Dealing**: disseminate recommendations and changes **fairly** to all clients — don''t favor select clients.
- **III(C) Suitability**: match recommendations to each client''s **IPS** (objectives/constraints); in advisory relationships consider the **total portfolio**.
- **III(D) Performance Presentation**: **fair, accurate, and complete** — no cherry-picking.
- **III(E) Preservation of Confidentiality**: keep current/former/prospective client information confidential (unless legally required or to report illegal activity).

> Watch vignettes mixing III with **VI(B)** Priority of Transactions and **I(B)** Independence.', 2, 3),
  ('cfa3-ethics-conflicts-m01', 'cfa3-ethics-conflicts', 'Conflicts & Priority of Transactions (VI)', '## Standard VI — Conflicts of Interest
- **VI(A) Disclosure of Conflicts**: **fully and fairly** disclose anything that could impair independence/objectivity — ownership, board seats, compensation arrangements, broker relationships — **prominently** and in plain language.
- **VI(B) Priority of Transactions**: **client and employer trades take precedence** over a member''s own. Personal trading must never disadvantage clients; use **restricted lists, pre-clearance, and blackout/restricted periods** (especially for IPOs and private placements).
- **VI(C) Referral Fees**: disclose to clients and the employer any compensation for referrals so clients can assess bias.

## Standard IV (Duties to Employers) — overlap
- **IV(A) Loyalty** (no taking records/clients), **IV(B) Additional Compensation** (written consent), **IV(C) Supervisors** (prevent/detect violations).

> The default cure for a conflict is **disclosure + management**, never concealment.', 2, 4),
  ('cfa3-ethics-application-cases-m01', 'cfa3-ethics-application-cases', 'Application of the Code & Standards', 'Level III tests ethics through **cases/vignettes**. A reliable method:

## Method
1. **Identify the actors and actions** in the scenario.
2. **Map each action to the precise sub-standard** (e.g., taking a client list → IV(A) Loyalty; trading on a tip → II(A) MNPI).
3. Pick the **least compliant** action / the response that **best** remedies it.
4. When law and the Code differ, follow the **stricter**.

## Common case themes
- **Soft dollars** must benefit the client; **best execution** cannot be sacrificed.
- **Mosaic theory** is permissible; trading on MNPI is not.
- **Gifts**: from clients (disclose), from third parties seeking influence (decline).
- **GIPS/AMC** claims must be accurate and firm-wide.
- **Recommended procedures** (firewalls, restricted lists, disclosure, supervision, record retention) usually resolve the case.', 2, 5),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-market-risk-m01', 'cfa3-risk-management-market-risk', 'Measuring & Managing Market Risk (VaR)', '**Value at Risk (VaR)** is the minimum loss expected over a horizon at a given probability — e.g., a 5% one-day VaR of $1m means losses should exceed $1m on ~5% of days.

## Estimating VaR
- **Parametric (variance–covariance)**: assumes normally distributed returns; fast but understates tail risk.
- **Historical simulation**: re-prices the portfolio over an actual past return window; no distributional assumption but anchored to history.
- **Monte Carlo**: simulates many return paths from an assumed model; flexible but model- and compute-intensive.

## Limits & complements
- VaR says nothing about losses **beyond** the threshold → use **Conditional VaR (expected shortfall)**, the average loss in the tail.
- Add **sensitivity measures** (beta, duration, delta/gamma, vega) and **scenario/stress tests** (historical and hypothetical) to capture non-normal, regime-shift risk.

## Using risk measures
- **Risk budgeting** allocates a total risk limit across positions/managers.
- Constraints include **position limits, stop-losses, and VaR/scenario limits**; market participants (banks, asset managers, pensions) weight these differently per their mandates.', 2, 2),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-swaps-forwards-m01', 'cfa3-risk-management-swaps-forwards', 'Swaps, Forwards & Futures Strategies', 'Linear (symmetric) derivatives let managers alter exposures **without trading the underlying**.

## Duration & equity exposure
- **Interest-rate swaps**: receive-fixed/pay-floating **adds** duration; pay-fixed/receive-floating **reduces** it. The swap''s duration ≈ difference between fixed- and floating-leg durations.
- **Bond futures** adjust portfolio duration toward a target via the basis-point value (BPV) hedge ratio.
- **Equity index futures** change beta: contracts ≈ [(β_target − β_port)/β_fut] × (portfolio value / futures value).

## Currency & asset-class overlays
- **Currency forwards/swaps** hedge or take FX exposure; **total-return / equity swaps** synthetically gain or shed an asset-class exposure cheaply.
- A **cash-equitization** ("cash drag") overlay uses index futures to keep idle cash market-exposed.

## Trade-offs
- Symmetric instruments remove **both** downside and upside. Futures bring **margin and daily mark-to-market**; swaps are OTC with counterparty risk (mitigated by collateral/clearing). **Basis risk** remains when the derivative imperfectly matches the exposure.', 2, 3),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-options-strategies-m01', 'cfa3-risk-management-options-strategies', 'Options Strategies', 'Options give **asymmetric** payoffs — protection or income — for a premium.

## Directional & protective
- **Protective put**: long asset + long put → floor on losses, upside kept (less premium).
- **Covered call**: long asset + short call → premium income, upside capped at the strike.
- **Collar**: protective put financed by a short call → bounded downside *and* upside.

## Spreads & volatility
- **Bull call / bear put spreads** reduce cost by capping the payoff; **calendar spreads** trade time decay.
- **Straddle/strangle** (long): profit from large moves either way (long volatility); short versions earn premium if the market is quiet.

## The Greeks
- **Delta** (≈ directional exposure), **gamma** (delta''s sensitivity → why delta hedges must be rebalanced), **theta** (time decay), **vega** (volatility), **rho** (rates).
- Implied volatility and the **volatility smile/skew** shape option prices; managers choose strategies by their volatility and directional view.', 2, 4),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-enterprise-m01', 'cfa3-risk-management-enterprise', 'Risk Governance & Enterprise Risk Management', '**Enterprise Risk Management (ERM)** is a centralized, top-down framework that manages risk **across the whole organization** against its goals and risk tolerance.

## Risk governance
- The **board/senior management** set risk tolerance aligned to objectives; an effective framework is **enterprise-wide**, not siloed.
- Steps: (1) identify & classify risks, (2) measure & aggregate, (3) set a **risk budget/limits**, (4) monitor & report, (5) adjust/mitigate.

## Risk types
- **Financial**: market, credit, liquidity. **Non-financial**: operational, model, solvency, regulatory, legal, settlement, tail risk.
- Aggregation must account for **correlations** — risks are not simply additive.

## Mitigation choices
- **Prevent/avoid**, **accept** (self-insure), **transfer** (insurance), or **shift/hedge** (derivatives). Choice weighs cost vs. benefit and the firm''s comparative advantage in bearing each risk.
- Good governance places risk decisions where the firm has the most knowledge and gives a **centralized** view of total exposure.', 2, 5),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-m01', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-hedge-funds', 'Hedge Fund Strategies', 'Hedge funds pursue **absolute return** using leverage, shorting, and derivatives, grouped by strategy.

## Major categories
- **Equity hedge**: long/short equity, market-neutral, short-biased.
- **Event-driven**: merger arbitrage, distressed, special situations.
- **Relative value**: fixed-income/convertible arbitrage — small mispricings, high leverage.
- **Opportunistic/macro**: global macro, managed futures (CTAs/trend-following).
- **Multi-strategy**: allocate dynamically across the above.

## Risk & return profile
- Strategies vary from **equity-like** (long/short) to **diversifying/convex** (managed futures often gains in crises).
- Many carry **tail risk**: arbitrage and short-volatility styles can show steady gains then sharp drawdowns ("picking up pennies in front of a steamroller").

## Practicalities
- Conditional, **time-varying correlations** to equities (low in calm, higher in stress).
- **Fees** (commonly ~1.5-and-15/20), lock-ups, and gates. Benchmarking uses **peer groups, absolute targets, or risk-factor models** rather than a single index.', 2, 1),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-private-equity-m01', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-private-equity', 'Private Equity & Venture Capital', 'Private equity buys, improves, and exits private (or take-private) companies via long-lived, illiquid funds.

## Structure
- **Closed-end LP funds**: investors (LPs) commit capital that a **GP** draws down over an investment period. Typical terms: **2% management fee + 20% carried interest** over an **8% hurdle**, often with a **clawback**.
- **Capital is committed, then called** — investors hold dry powder and pace commitments across **vintage years**.

## Value creation & the J-curve
- Buyouts add value through **operational improvement, financial leverage, and multiple expansion**; venture funds back early-stage growth with high dispersion and failure rates.
- The **J-curve**: fees and early markdowns create initial negative returns before exits drive gains.

## Performance & risk
- Measured by **IRR and MOIC/TVPI** (money-weighted), plus **DPI** (realized). Compared against a **public-market equivalent (PME)**.
- Risks: illiquidity, leverage, manager dispersion (skill persistence matters), and valuation **smoothing** that understates true volatility.', 2, 2),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-real-estate-m01', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'Real Estate Investment', 'Real estate spans **private** (direct, funds) and **public** (REITs) plus **equity** and **debt** (mortgages) quadrants.

## Valuation
- **Income/direct-capitalization**: Value = NOI / **cap rate**; a lower cap rate implies a higher price (and lower expected return).
- **Discounted cash flow** of NOI plus a terminal value; **cost** and **sales-comparison** approaches cross-check.
- **NOI** = rental and other income − operating expenses (excludes financing and taxes).

## Portfolio role
- Provides **income, diversification, and partial inflation protection**; returns driven by income yield plus appreciation.
- **Leverage** amplifies both return and risk; vacancy, financing, and liquidity are key risks.

## Vehicles
- **REITs** offer liquidity and daily pricing but behave more **equity-like** in the short run; **private real estate** is appraisal-priced → **smoothed**, understated volatility.
- Core → value-add → opportunistic styles trade rising risk/return and leverage.', 2, 3),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-real-assets-m01', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-real-assets', 'Commodities & Real Assets', 'Commodities and other real assets are added mainly for **inflation sensitivity and diversification**.

## Commodity returns
- Most exposure is via **futures**, not physical holding. Total return = **spot + roll + collateral** yield.
- **Roll yield** is positive in **backwardation** (futures below spot) and negative in **contango** (futures above spot).
- Theories: **insurance/hedging-pressure** and **theory of storage** (convenience yield) explain the futures curve.

## Role & risks
- Historically **positive correlation with inflation surprises**, a diversifier versus stocks/bonds — but volatile and producing **no income**.
- Storage, seasonality, and curve shape drive returns; correlations can rise in macro shocks.

## Other real assets
- **Infrastructure, timber, farmland, and natural resources** offer long-duration, inflation-linked cash flows.
- Like other privates, valuations may be **appraisal-based (smoothed)**; sizing must respect illiquidity.', 2, 4),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-private-debt-m01', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-private-debt', 'Private Debt & Infrastructure', 'Private debt and infrastructure provide **contractual, often inflation-linked income** with illiquidity premia.

## Private debt
- **Direct lending** (senior secured loans to mid-market firms), **mezzanine** (subordinated, equity kicker), **distressed**, and **specialty finance** (venture debt, real-estate/asset-backed).
- Return drivers: **credit spread + illiquidity premium**, often **floating-rate** (low duration, rises with rates). Key risk is **credit/default and recovery**, plus weak covenants in frothy markets.

## Infrastructure
- Long-lived physical assets (transport, utilities, energy, social). **Brownfield** (operating, stable yield) vs. **greenfield** (development, higher risk).
- Cash flows are often **regulated or contracted and inflation-linked**, giving bond-like, diversifying returns over long horizons.

## Portfolio fit
- Both suit long-horizon investors seeking **income and inflation protection**; illiquidity, leverage, and (for infrastructure) regulatory/political risk constrain sizing and require careful pacing.', 2, 5),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-quantitative-m01', 'cfa3-manager-selection-quantitative', 'Quantitative Manager Evaluation', 'Quantitative evaluation tests whether a track record reflects **skill** rather than luck, beta, or biases.

## Returns-based analysis
- Regress manager returns on factor/benchmark returns to separate **alpha (skill)** from **factor (beta) exposure** — don''t pay active fees for cheap beta.
- Gauge consistency with the **information ratio** (active return / tracking error) and risk-adjusted measures (Sharpe, Sortino).

## Statistical caution
- Short, volatile records have **low statistical power** to distinguish skill from luck.
- **Backfill and survivorship biases** inflate database track records; returns may be **smoothed** for illiquid holdings.

## Style & drift
- Confirm the manager actually delivers the intended exposures; watch for **style drift** away from the mandate.
- Outputs feed a **forward-looking** judgment — past returns alone are weak predictors, so combine the quant evidence with qualitative due diligence.', 2, 1),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-fees-m01', 'cfa3-manager-selection-fees', 'Fee Structures & Alignment', 'Fee terms shape **net** returns and the alignment between manager and client incentives.

## Common structures
- **Ad valorem (AUM) fees**: a fixed % of assets — simple, but rewards asset gathering.
- **Performance fees**: a share of gains, raising upside alignment but also **risk-taking incentives**.
- **High-water mark**: performance fees only on new net profits (no double-charging after a drawdown). **Hurdle rate**: performance fee applies only above a threshold return.

## Alignment tools
- **Clawbacks** (private funds) return excess carry if later results disappoint; **caps** limit how much performance fee accrues.
- A **symmetric (fulcrum) fee** rewards out- and penalizes under-performance, improving alignment.

## Evaluation
- Compare **gross vs. net** of fees and assess whether the structure encourages **appropriate risk**.
- High fixed fees on a **beta-heavy** strategy destroy value; selection should weigh fee level *and* structure against expected, repeatable skill.', 2, 2),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-style-analysis-m01', 'cfa3-manager-selection-style-analysis', 'Style & Holdings-Based Analysis', 'Style analysis verifies **what a manager actually does** versus what they claim.

## Two approaches
- **Returns-based style analysis (RBSA)**: regress fund returns on a set of style indexes; the constrained coefficients estimate the **effective style mix**. Cheap and timely but backward-looking and can miss rapid changes.
- **Holdings-based style analysis (HBSA)**: classify the **actual positions** by size, value/growth, sector, region. More precise and current but **data-intensive** and a snapshot.

## What it reveals
- **Style drift** away from mandate, hidden **factor tilts**, and concentration.
- Whether reported alpha is genuine or simply **persistent factor exposure**.

## Use in selection
- Confirms the manager fits the intended **portfolio role** and won''t overlap or double-count exposures with existing managers.
- Combine RBSA (history/consistency) with HBSA (current positioning) for a fuller picture before hiring or monitoring.', 2, 3),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-monitoring-m01', 'cfa3-manager-selection-monitoring', 'Monitoring & Manager Termination', 'Selection doesn''t end at hiring — managers must be **monitored** and replaced when the thesis breaks.

## What to monitor
- **Performance** vs. benchmark and peers (in the right risk context), **style consistency**, and the original **investment thesis**.
- **People, process, and firm**: team turnover/**key-person** departures, ownership changes, asset growth toward **capacity** limits, and **operational** red flags.

## Termination triggers
- Loss of the rationale for hiring: **style drift**, **process change**, eroding edge, or operational/ethical problems — not just a bad quarter.
- Beware **performance chasing**: firing after underperformance and hiring recent winners often **destroys value** (mean reversion).

## Errors revisited
- Premature firing of a skilled manager is a **Type II error**; retaining an unskilled one is **Type I**. Set decisions on a sound, ex-ante framework and watch **transaction/transition costs** of replacement.', 2, 4),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-universe-m01', 'cfa3-manager-selection-universe', 'Universe Definition & Process Screening', 'Sound selection starts by **defining the opportunity set** and screening it down to candidates worth deep diligence.

## Universe definition
- Specify the **mandate**: asset class, style, region, vehicle (separate account vs. fund), liquidity, and constraints (ESG, leverage).
- A poorly defined universe yields **apples-to-oranges** comparisons and misleading peer rankings.

## Screening
- Apply **quantitative filters** (track-record length, AUM/capacity, risk-adjusted performance, fees) and **qualitative filters** (firm stability, transparency, alignment).
- Adjust databases for **survivorship/backfill** bias so the screen isn''t built on inflated histories.

## From screen to shortlist
- Surviving managers proceed to full **investment + operational due diligence**.
- The aim is a **repeatable, documented process** that controls both error types and produces a **conviction-weighted** recommendation grounded in skill and operations — not a ranking by last year''s return.', 2, 5),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-cognitive-emotional-m01', 'cfa3-behavioral-cognitive-emotional', 'Cognitive Errors vs Emotional Biases', 'Behavioral biases split into **cognitive errors** (faulty reasoning) and **emotional biases** (feelings). Cognitive errors are easier to correct with education and data; emotional biases are harder and are usually **accommodated**.

## Cognitive errors
- **Belief perseverance**: conservatism, confirmation, representativeness, illusion of control, hindsight.
- **Information processing**: anchoring/adjustment, mental accounting, framing, availability.

## Emotional biases
- **Loss aversion**, **overconfidence**, **self-control**, **status quo**, **endowment**, **regret aversion**.

## Practical response
- **Moderate** or **adapt** to a bias depending on the client''s wealth relative to their standard of living and the bias type.
- Emotional biases are generally accommodated; cognitive errors are corrected.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-portfolio-theory-m01', 'cfa3-behavioral-portfolio-theory', 'Behavioral Portfolio Theory & Goals', '**Behavioral portfolio theory (BPT)** says investors build portfolios as **layered pyramids**, each layer meeting a goal with its own risk attitude — rather than one mean-variance optimal portfolio.

## Key ideas
- Investors hold a **low-risk layer** (safety) and **high-risk layer** (aspiration) simultaneously, ignoring correlations across layers.
- This mirrors **mental accounting** and **goals-based investing**.

## Consequences
- Resulting portfolios may be **inefficient** relative to the mean-variance frontier.
- Advisers can harness this by framing allocations around **client goals** (near-term needs vs long-term aspirations), improving adherence even if slightly suboptimal.

BPT helps explain why real portfolios differ from the single tangency portfolio of modern portfolio theory.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-anomalies-m01', 'cfa3-behavioral-anomalies', 'Behavioral Explanations of Anomalies', 'Market **anomalies** appear to contradict market efficiency; behavioral finance offers explanations.

## Common anomalies
- **Momentum**: winners keep winning short-term — linked to **herding** and under-reaction.
- **Value/size effects**: cheap and small stocks outperform — possibly risk or mispricing.
- **Calendar effects** (January effect), **overreaction** and long-term reversals.
- **Bubbles and crashes**: driven by herding, overconfidence and regret.

## Cautions
- Some anomalies vanish after discovery (arbitraged away) or reflect **data mining**.
- Others may be **compensation for risk** rather than true inefficiency.

Behavioral biases (overconfidence, anchoring, herding) provide plausible mechanisms, but rational risk-based explanations must also be considered.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-framework-m01', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-framework', 'CME Framework & Challenges', '**Capital market expectations (CME)** are forecasts of risk and return for asset classes, feeding strategic allocation.

## A disciplined framework
1. Specify the expectations needed and the horizon.
2. Research historical/current conditions.
3. Select forecasting models and gather data.
4. Interpret and formulate expectations; monitor and adjust.

## Common problems
- **Limitations of economic data** (revisions, lags), **data-measurement errors and biases** (survivorship, appraisal smoothing).
- **The limits of historical estimates** and **non-stationarity** (regime changes).
- **Ex-post risk as a biased measure of ex-ante risk**, **psychological biases** (anchoring, overconfidence) and **model/input uncertainty**.

Good CME require combining statistical tools with sound economic judgement.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-macro-linkages-m01', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-macro-linkages', 'Macro Linkages & Emerging Markets', 'Economies are linked through **trade and capital flows**, so business cycles and policy spill across borders.

## Linkages
- **Inflation/deflation** and monetary policy affect real rates and currencies.
- Countries with strong trade/capital ties tend to have **more correlated** cycles.

## Emerging markets — extra risks
- **Political and legal** risk (weaker property rights, unstable policy).
- **Economic** risk (commodity dependence, fiscal/current-account deficits, high debt).
- **Currency** risk and possible capital controls.

## Forecasting approaches
- **Econometric models**, **leading indicators**, and **checklist** approaches.
- Assess an emerging economy''s growth via factors like savings/investment, demographics, and structural policy.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-risk-premium-models-m01', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-risk-premium-models', 'Discount-Rate & Risk-Premium Models', 'Expected returns are often built as a **risk-free rate plus risk premia**.

## Building blocks
- **Bond returns** ≈ real risk-free rate + inflation premium + default premium + illiquidity premium + maturity (term) premium.
- **Equity returns** via the **Grinold–Kroner** model: expected return ≈ dividend yield − Δshares + inflation + real earnings growth + Δ(P/E).

## Other tools
- **Building-block / risk-premium** approach sums compensations for each risk.
- **DCF (Gordon growth)** implies expected return = D₁/P + g.
- **Singer–Terhaar** (extended ICAPM) blends fully segmented and fully integrated market expectations using a degree of integration.

These models translate macro views into asset-class return forecasts.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-risk-budgeting-m01', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-risk-budgeting', 'Risk Budgeting in Allocation', '**Risk budgeting** allocates a portfolio''s total risk across positions or factors, rather than allocating capital alone.

## Concepts
- **Marginal contribution to risk (MCTR)** = sensitivity of portfolio risk to a small increase in a position.
- **Absolute contribution to risk (ACTR)** = weight × MCTR; the ACTRs sum to total portfolio risk.
- An allocation is **optimal** when the **ratio of excess return to MCTR is equal** across positions.

## Uses
- Ensures risk isn''t unintentionally concentrated (e.g., ''equity'' dominating a balanced fund''s risk).
- **Risk parity** equalizes risk contributions across assets, often adding leverage to low-risk assets.

Risk budgeting complements return forecasting by focusing on where risk is actually taken.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-currency-m01', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-currency', 'Currency Management in Allocation', 'Global portfolios carry **currency exposure** that can be managed separately from asset selection.

## Strategic choices
- **Currency-neutral** (fully hedged) to **unhedged** are the poles; most managers sit in between with a **strategic hedge ratio**.
- Rationale for hedging: currencies add volatility but little expected return over the long run; hedging costs and manager skill matter.

## Tools & tactics
- **Forwards** and **currency options**; **cross-hedges** and **proxy hedges** when direct hedges are costly.
- **Minimum-variance hedge ratio** from regressing asset returns on currency returns.
- Active managers may take currency views via carry, value (PPP) and momentum, treating currency as an alpha source.

Discretion is set in the IPS''s currency-risk policy.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-tactical-m01', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-tactical', 'Tactical Asset Allocation', '**Tactical asset allocation (TAA)** makes short-term deviations from the strategic (policy) weights to exploit perceived opportunities.

## Approaches
- **Discretionary TAA** relies on manager judgement about markets.
- **Systematic TAA** uses rules/signals such as value, momentum and carry.

## Considerations
- TAA is measured against the strategic policy portfolio; success is judged by **information ratio** of the tactical bets.
- Costs: **transaction costs**, **taxes**, and higher **tracking error** versus policy.
- Constraints from the IPS bound how far weights may deviate.

TAA can add value but introduces active risk; it must be disciplined and cost-aware.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-liquidity-m01', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-liquidity', 'Bond Market Liquidity & Trading', 'Bond markets are largely **over-the-counter** and far less liquid than equities.

## Liquidity features
- Most trading is **dealer-intermediated**; liquidity varies hugely by sector (governments most liquid; high-yield and structured least).
- **Newly issued (on-the-run)** bonds are more liquid than **off-the-run**.
- Liquidity dries up in stress, widening bid-ask spreads.

## Managing liquidity
- Hold liquid instruments (governments, futures) as a **liquidity buffer**.
- Use **credit default swaps** and **bond futures** to adjust exposure without trading cash bonds.
- Trading costs are largely **implicit** (bid-ask, market impact) rather than explicit commissions.

Liquidity risk is a key consideration in fixed-income portfolio construction.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-inflation-linked-m01', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-inflation-linked', 'Inflation-Linked Bonds & Real Return', '**Inflation-linked bonds** (e.g., TIPS) protect purchasing power by tying principal or coupons to a price index.

## Mechanics
- Principal accretes with inflation; coupons are paid on the adjusted principal.
- The yield quoted is a **real yield**; the gap between nominal and real yields is the **breakeven inflation rate**.

## Uses
- Hedge **inflation risk** for liabilities linked to prices (pensions, endowments'' spending).
- Low correlation with nominal bonds when inflation surprises occur.

## Risks
- **Real-rate risk** remains (duration), and **deflation** can hurt (though many have a principal floor).
- Liquidity and tax treatment (phantom income on accretion) are practical concerns.

Breakeven inflation is a market-based inflation expectation used in CME.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-international-m01', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-international', 'International Bond Investing & Hedging', 'Global bonds add **diversification** but introduce **currency** and **cross-market** risk.

## Return components
- Local-currency bond return + **currency return** (spot change plus any hedging carry).
- Fully hedging currency converts the foreign yield roughly into the domestic-cash yield plus the foreign bond''s excess return.

## Strategies
- **Interest-rate differentials** and expected currency moves drive country allocation.
- **Duration** can be taken in the most attractive markets, with currency hedged back if desired.
- The **hedged return** relationship is governed by covered interest rate parity.

## Considerations
- Diversification benefit depends on low correlation of global rate cycles.
- Emerging-market debt adds credit and political risk but higher yield.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-performance-gips-m01', 'cfa3-performance-gips', 'GIPS & Performance Presentation', 'The **Global Investment Performance Standards (GIPS)** standardize how firms present investment results.

## Core requirements
- Compliance is **firm-wide**; no partial compliance.
- Group portfolios into **composites** by strategy, including **all actual, fee-paying, discretionary** portfolios.
- Returns are **time-weighted**; show at least **5 years** building to 10.

## Presentation
- Disclose benchmark, fees (gross/net), **composite dispersion**, and number of portfolios.
- **Verification** is firm-wide and voluntary but recommended, adding credibility.

## Why it matters
GIPS makes performance **comparable across managers** and prevents cherry-picking, supporting fair client decisions.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-performance-macro-micro-m01', 'cfa3-performance-macro-micro', 'Macro vs Micro Attribution', 'Attribution decomposes performance into its **sources**.

## Macro attribution
- Applied at the **fund-sponsor** level; attributes total fund return to decisions such as net contributions, the policy (benchmark) allocation, asset-category allocation, and manager selection.

## Micro attribution
- Applied at the **individual portfolio/manager** level.
- **Brinson–Hood–Beebower**: splits active return into **allocation** (over/underweighting sectors), **selection** (picking within sectors), and an **interaction** term.

## Fixed-income attribution
- Decomposes return into **duration/curve** effects, **spread** changes and **selection**.

Choosing macro vs micro depends on whether the sponsor or the individual manager is being evaluated.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-performance-risk-adjusted-m01', 'cfa3-performance-risk-adjusted', 'Risk-Adjusted Performance Measures', 'Risk-adjusted measures compare return **per unit of risk**.

## Common ratios
| Measure | Risk used |
| --- | --- |
| **Sharpe** | total risk (σ) |
| **Treynor** | systematic risk (β) |
| **Information ratio** | active risk (tracking error) |
| **Sortino** | downside deviation |

- **Jensen''s alpha** = actual return − CAPM-required return.
- **M² (Modigliani)** restates the Sharpe ratio as a benchmark-relative return.
- **Appraisal ratio** = alpha / non-systematic risk.

## Choosing a measure
- Use **Sharpe** for a standalone portfolio; **Treynor/Jensen** when the portfolio is one of many (only systematic risk matters); **information ratio** to judge active management.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-index-construction-m01', 'cfa3-equity-pm-index-construction', 'Index Construction & Weighting', 'Equity indexes differ by **weighting scheme**, which shapes their risk and turnover.

## Weighting methods
- **Market-cap weighted**: reflects the investable opportunity set; low turnover but tilts to large, possibly overvalued names.
- **Price weighted** (e.g., some legacy indexes): higher-priced stocks dominate — largely an artifact.
- **Equal weighted**: more small-cap and value tilt; higher turnover from rebalancing.
- **Fundamental weighted**: weights by sales, earnings or book — breaks the price link, adding a value tilt.

## Implications for passive investors
- Choice of index defines the passive exposure; **reconstitution** and **rebalancing** create turnover and tracking considerations.
- Full replication vs **stratified sampling** vs **optimization** are the tracking methods.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-long-short-m01', 'cfa3-equity-pm-long-short', 'Long/Short & Market-Neutral Equity', 'Long/short strategies buy expected winners and **short** expected losers.

## Structures
- **Market-neutral**: balance long and short betas to ≈ 0, isolating stock selection (alpha) from market direction.
- **Long-biased (e.g., 130/30)**: 130% long, 30% short — retains market exposure while adding shorting to express negative views.
- **Pairs trading**: long/short two related securities to capture relative mispricing.

## Considerations
- Shorting adds **borrowing costs**, potential **unlimited loss**, and **short-squeeze** risk.
- Enables a higher **transfer coefficient** by acting on negative signals (fundamental law of active management).
- Leverage and financing must be managed carefully.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-costs-fees-m01', 'cfa3-equity-pm-costs-fees', 'Equity Costs, Fees & Turnover', 'Costs erode active returns and must be managed.

## Types of cost
- **Explicit**: commissions, fees, taxes.
- **Implicit**: **bid-ask spread**, **market impact**, and **delay/opportunity cost** — together the **implementation shortfall**.

## Turnover and taxes
- Higher **turnover** raises trading costs and, for taxable investors, realizes gains sooner (tax drag).
- Tax-aware strategies use **loss harvesting** and holding-period management.

## Fees
- Management fees plus performance fees; evaluate net-of-fee alpha.
- A strategy with modest gross alpha can deliver **negative net alpha** after costs, so cost control is itself a source of value.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-exposure-adjustment-m01', 'cfa3-risk-management-exposure-adjustment', 'Adjusting Exposure with Futures', 'Derivatives let managers change exposure quickly and cheaply without trading the underlying.

## Equity beta adjustment
- Number of futures = ((β_target − β_current)/β_futures) × (Portfolio value / (futures price × multiplier)).
- To **equitize cash**, buy index futures to gain equity exposure on a cash balance.

## Duration adjustment
- Use bond futures to move portfolio duration toward a target: contracts ≈ ((D_target − D_current) × Portfolio value) / (D_futures × futures price).

## Uses
- Tactical shifts, transition management, and hedging.
- Futures are capital-efficient (margin only) and highly liquid, but require managing basis risk and rolls.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-fx-hedging-m01', 'cfa3-risk-management-fx-hedging', 'Currency Hedging Strategies', 'Managers hedge currency to reduce volatility or take active currency views.

## Instruments
- **Forwards** (most common), **currency futures**, **options**, and **cross-currency swaps**.
- **Options** provide asymmetric protection (a floor) at the cost of a premium.

## Hedge ratios
- Range from **0% (unhedged)** to **100% (fully hedged)**; a **minimum-variance hedge ratio** may differ from 1 due to correlation between the asset and currency.
- **Dynamic hedging** adjusts the ratio as markets move.

## Active currency tactics
- **Carry** (buy high-yield/sell low-yield), **value** (PPP-based), and **momentum**.
- Roll yield on forwards and hedging cost depend on interest-rate differentials.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-tail-risk-m01', 'cfa3-risk-management-tail-risk', 'Tail Risk & Stress Testing', '**Tail risk** is the risk of rare, extreme losses that normal (VaR) models understate.

## Measures beyond VaR
- **Conditional VaR (expected shortfall)**: the average loss **given** the VaR threshold is breached — captures tail severity.
- VaR tells you a threshold; CVaR tells you how bad it gets beyond it.

## Techniques
- **Stress testing** applies severe but plausible shocks (rate spikes, equity crashes).
- **Scenario analysis** models specific historical or hypothetical events.
- **Reverse stress testing** starts from a failure and finds what could cause it.

## Managing tail risk
- **Tail hedges** (out-of-the-money puts), diversification across truly uncorrelated assets, and holding liquidity buffers.
- Fat tails and rising correlations in crises make tail risk larger than a normal model implies.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-concentrated-m01', 'cfa3-private-wealth-concentrated', 'Managing Concentrated Positions', 'Wealthy clients often hold a **concentrated single-stock** or private-business position, creating outsized idiosyncratic risk.

## Objectives & constraints
- Goals: **reduce risk**, **generate liquidity**, and **defer/minimize tax** — often in tension.
- Constraints: capital-gains tax, restricted/legacy shares, emotional attachment, control considerations.

## Strategies
- **Outright sale** (simple but taxable).
- **Equity monetization**: a **collar** (buy put, sell call) plus borrowing against the position to raise cash while deferring tax.
- **Exchange funds** pool concentrated holdings for diversification with tax deferral.
- **Charitable trusts** (CRTs) and **completion portfolios** to diversify around the position.

The right approach balances tax efficiency against risk reduction and the client''s goals.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-family-governance-m01', 'cfa3-private-wealth-family-governance', 'Family Governance & Education', '**Family governance** structures how a wealthy family makes decisions and transfers values across generations.

## Elements
- A **family mission statement** and **constitution** articulate shared goals and rules.
- **Family councils/meetings** provide a forum for communication and conflict resolution.
- **Education** prepares heirs to be responsible stewards of wealth.

## Why it matters
- Most multigenerational wealth erosion stems from **breakdowns in communication and trust**, not poor investing.
- Good governance addresses the classic ''shirtsleeves to shirtsleeves in three generations'' risk.

## Family office
- A **single-family office** or **multi-family office** coordinates investments, tax, estate and philanthropy for the family.

Governance complements the financial plan by managing the human/relational side of wealth.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-philanthropy-m01', 'cfa3-private-wealth-philanthropy', 'Charitable Giving & Philanthropy', 'Philanthropy achieves personal goals while often providing **tax efficiency**.

## Vehicles
- **Direct gifts** of cash or **appreciated securities** (avoids capital-gains tax and gives a deduction).
- **Donor-advised funds**: immediate deduction, grants over time.
- **Private foundations**: more control, higher administration and payout rules.
- **Charitable remainder trusts (CRT)**: income to the donor now, remainder to charity — useful for concentrated, low-basis stock.
- **Charitable lead trusts (CLT)**: income to charity first, remainder to heirs.

## Planning
- Gifting **appreciated assets** is usually more tax-efficient than gifting cash then selling.
- Coordinate philanthropy with the overall **estate plan** and the family''s values/governance.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-institutional-dc-pensions-m01', 'cfa3-institutional-dc-pensions', 'Defined-Contribution Plans', 'In a **defined-contribution (DC)** plan, the **employee bears the investment risk**, unlike a defined-benefit plan where the sponsor does.

## Features
- Benefits depend on contributions and investment returns; there is **no guaranteed payout**.
- The plan sponsor''s duties focus on offering a **suitable menu**, controlling fees, and fiduciary oversight.

## Design considerations
- **Default options** (target-date funds) leverage inertia to improve outcomes.
- **Auto-enrollment** and **auto-escalation** raise participation and savings.
- Address participant **behavioral biases** (inertia, naive diversification, employer-stock over-concentration).

Compared with DB, DC shifts **longevity and market risk** to individuals, raising the importance of good defaults and education.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-institutional-endowment-model-m01', 'cfa3-institutional-endowment-model', 'The Endowment Model & Spending', 'Endowments and foundations support an institution''s mission **in perpetuity**, giving them a long horizon and high risk tolerance.

## The endowment model
- Heavy allocation to **alternatives** (private equity, hedge funds, real assets) to earn an illiquidity premium.
- Requires strong governance and the ability to bear illiquidity.

## Spending rules
- **Constant growth**: prior spending grown by inflation.
- **Market-value rule**: a fixed % of a moving average of asset values (smooths spending).
- **Hybrid (Yale) rule**: a weighted blend of the two, balancing stability and responsiveness.

## Objectives
- Maintain **intergenerational equity** (real value preserved) while funding current spending.
- Required return ≈ spending rate + inflation + costs.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-institutional-alm-m01', 'cfa3-institutional-alm', 'Asset-Liability Management Frameworks', '**Asset-liability management (ALM)** manages assets in explicit relation to liabilities, central for pensions, insurers and banks.

## Approaches
- **ALM (liability-relative)**: choose assets to hedge or match liabilities (e.g., **immunization**, **cash-flow matching**, LDI).
- **Asset-only** ignores liabilities; ALM is preferred when liabilities are significant and well-defined.

## Institution examples
- **Banks** manage the **duration gap** between assets and deposits.
- **Insurers** match asset duration/cash flows to policy liabilities and consider surrender risk.
- **DB pensions** hedge the **funded-ratio** volatility via LDI.

## Key metrics
- **Surplus** (assets − liabilities) and **funded ratio**; managing **surplus risk** (volatility of the surplus) is the ALM objective.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-ethics-duties-employers-m01', 'cfa3-ethics-duties-employers', 'Duties to Employers (Standard IV)', '**Standard IV — Duties to Employers.**

## IV(A) Loyalty
- Act for the employer''s benefit; do not deprive it of your skills or divulge confidential information.
- **Leaving a firm**: you may use general knowledge/skills but must not take records, client lists, or solicit clients before departure. Independent practice needs employer consent.

## IV(B) Additional Compensation Arrangements
- Do not accept gifts/compensation that compete with or create a conflict with the employer''s interest **without written consent** from all parties.

## IV(C) Responsibilities of Supervisors
- Members with supervisory roles must take **reasonable steps to prevent and detect** violations by those under their supervision (adequate compliance systems).

Loyalty duties are balanced against the duty to comply with law and not engage in illegal activity for an employer.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-ethics-investment-actions-m01', 'cfa3-ethics-investment-actions', 'Investment Analysis & Actions (Standard V)', '**Standard V — Investment Analysis, Recommendations, and Actions.**

## V(A) Diligence and Reasonable Basis
- Exercise diligence and have a **reasonable, adequate basis** for analysis and recommendations; verify third-party research and quantitative models.

## V(B) Communication with Clients
- Disclose the **basic format and general principles** of the investment process; distinguish **fact from opinion**; identify **material limitations and risks**.

## V(C) Record Retention
- Maintain records supporting analysis and recommendations (recommended **7 years**); records are the employer''s property.

Violations often involve acting on inadequate research or presenting opinion as fact.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-ethics-gips-verification-m01', 'cfa3-ethics-gips-verification', 'GIPS Verification & Compliance', '**Verification** is a third-party review testing whether a firm''s **processes** comply with GIPS.

## What verification does
- Confirms the firm has followed GIPS **on a firm-wide basis** for composite construction and calculation.
- Verification applies to the **whole firm**, not a single composite; a firm cannot claim a specific composite is ''verified'' alone.
- Verification is **voluntary but recommended**; it adds credibility and cannot be partial.

## Compliance basics
- Claiming compliance requires meeting **all** requirements — no partial compliance.
- Firms must show a compliant track record (5 years, building to 10) and use the official compliance statement.

Verification does not guarantee the accuracy of any specific composite presentation, only that firm-wide processes meet the standards.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-hf-fees-m01', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-hf-fees', 'Hedge Fund Fees & Funds-of-Funds', 'Hedge funds typically charge **management + incentive fees**, classically **''2 and 20''**.

## Fee mechanics
- Incentive (performance) fees often apply above a **hurdle rate** and subject to a **high-water mark** (no incentive fee until prior losses are recovered).
- Fees materially reduce net returns; compounding of fees matters over time.

## Funds-of-funds
- Provide **diversification** and **due-diligence/access**, but add a **second layer** of fees (e.g., ''1 and 10'' on top).
- Can suffer from **over-diversification** (diluting alpha) and still bear underlying-fund fees.

## Netting risk
- In multi-manager funds, investors may pay incentive fees to winning managers even if the fund overall is flat (**netting risk**), unless there is a fund-level netting arrangement.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-due-diligence-m01', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'Alternatives Due Diligence', 'Due diligence on alternative managers spans **investment** and **operational** review.

## Investment due diligence
- Strategy, edge, and **repeatability** of returns; risk management; use of leverage; capacity constraints.
- Analyze the **return drivers** and whether performance is skill or beta.

## Operational due diligence (ODD)
- **Independent** administrator, custodian and auditor; valuation policy for illiquid assets.
- Controls, compliance, key-person risk, and **business continuity**.
- Operational failures (fraud, weak controls) are a leading cause of hedge-fund losses.

## Terms
- **Lockups, gates, redemption** frequency and notice; fee structure and alignment.

Robust ODD is as important as investment analysis for alternatives.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-risk-return-m01', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-risk-return', 'Risk-Return & Diversification of Alternatives', 'Alternatives are added for **return enhancement**, **diversification**, or both — but their statistics can mislead.

## Measurement issues
- Reported returns are often **smoothed** (appraisal-based), understating volatility and correlation and overstating Sharpe ratios.
- Returns show **negative skew and fat tails** (e.g., many strategies resemble selling insurance).
- **Illiquidity** and **survivorship/backfill bias** in indexes flatter historical results.

## Portfolio role
- Assess **correlation in stress**, which often rises, reducing diversification just when needed.
- Use **downside measures** (Sortino, max drawdown, CVaR) rather than Sharpe alone.
- The **illiquidity premium** is compensation, not free return; size allocations to the ability to bear illiquidity.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-operational-dd-m01', 'cfa3-manager-selection-operational-dd', 'Operational Due Diligence', '**Operational due diligence (ODD)** evaluates a manager''s business and controls, separate from investment merit.

## Focus areas
- **Independence** of key service providers: administrator, custodian, auditor.
- **Valuation** policy (especially for illiquid assets) and pricing sources.
- **Compliance, controls, and cybersecurity**; segregation of duties.
- **Key-person risk** and succession; business viability and AUM stability.

## Red flags
- Self-administration and self-custody, unqualified or tiny auditors, opaque valuation, high staff turnover, past regulatory issues.

Many manager failures are **operational, not investment-driven**; ODD can be a veto over an otherwise attractive strategy.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-type-errors-m01', 'cfa3-manager-selection-type-errors', 'Type I & II Errors in Selection', 'Manager selection decisions carry two error types.

## The errors
- **Type I error**: **hiring/keeping a manager who adds no value** (rejecting the null ''no skill'' when it''s true — a false positive).
- **Type II error**: **rejecting/firing a manager who does add value** (a false negative).

## Implications
- Chasing recent outperformance raises Type I errors (paying for luck).
- Firing after short-term underperformance raises Type II errors (dropping skilled managers at the wrong time).
- A robust process weighs **statistical significance** of past returns against costs of switching.

Because performance is noisy, both errors are common; disciplined, evidence-based processes reduce them.', 2, 0),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-capture-ratios-m01', 'cfa3-manager-selection-capture-ratios', 'Capture Ratios & Manager Metrics', '**Capture ratios** describe how a manager performs in up versus down markets.

## Definitions
- **Upside capture**: manager return ÷ benchmark return in **up** markets (>100% is good).
- **Downside capture**: manager return ÷ benchmark return in **down** markets (<100% is good).
- **Capture ratio** = upside capture ÷ downside capture; >1 indicates a favorable asymmetry.

## Other selection metrics
- **Batting average** (fraction of periods beating the benchmark) and **up/down number ratios**.
- **Drawdown** statistics and **consistency** of the information ratio.

These metrics reveal a manager''s **convexity** — the ideal is to capture more of the upside than the downside.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-management-capm-m01', 'frm-foundations-risk-management-capm', 'Risk Management & CAPM', '**Risk management** is the process of identifying, measuring and controlling the exposures that threaten an institution''s value. The FRM curriculum begins here, distinguishing risk types and grounding the trade-off between **risk and return**.

## Types of Risk
- **Market risk** arises from movements in prices, rates and volatility.
- **Credit risk** is the loss from a counterparty''s failure to pay.
- **Operational risk** stems from failed processes, people or systems.
- **Liquidity risk** is the inability to transact at fair prices.

## Systematic vs Idiosyncratic
- **Systematic (market) risk** cannot be diversified away and is rewarded.
- **Idiosyncratic (firm-specific) risk** is diversifiable and earns no premium.

## The CAPM
The **Capital Asset Pricing Model** prices only non-diversifiable risk through **beta**:

```
E(R_i) = R_f + beta_i * (E(R_m) - R_f)
```

where **beta** measures sensitivity to the market and (E(R_m) - R_f) is the **market risk premium**.

| Measure | Formula |
| --- | --- |
| Sharpe ratio | (R_p - R_f) / sigma_p |
| Treynor ratio | (R_p - R_f) / beta_p |
| Jensen''s alpha | R_p - [R_f + beta_p*(R_m - R_f)] |

## Risk-Adjusted Performance
- The **Sharpe ratio** divides excess return by total risk; the **Treynor ratio** uses beta instead.
- **Jensen''s alpha** is the return above the CAPM prediction, a measure of manager skill.

### Tip
- Beta of 1.0 means the asset moves with the market; only systematic risk earns a premium under CAPM.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-volatility-ewma-garch-m01', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-volatility-ewma-garch', 'Volatility, EWMA & GARCH', '**Volatility** is the standard deviation of returns and the central input to most risk models. Because it changes over time (**volatility clustering**), the FRM tests several estimation schemes.

## Equally Weighted Estimate
The simple historical variance gives each of the past m returns the same weight, which reacts slowly and shows a **ghosting** effect when large moves drop out of the window.

## EWMA
The **exponentially weighted moving average** places more weight on recent returns through a decay factor lambda:

```
sigma_n^2 = lambda * sigma_(n-1)^2 + (1 - lambda) * u_(n-1)^2
```

- RiskMetrics uses **lambda = 0.94** for daily data.
- A higher lambda gives a smoother, slower-reacting series.

## GARCH(1,1)
**GARCH(1,1)** adds a long-run variance term, so estimates revert toward a mean:

```
sigma_n^2 = omega + alpha * u_(n-1)^2 + beta * sigma_(n-1)^2
```

| Term | Role |
| --- | --- |
| omega | Constant linked to long-run variance |
| alpha | Weight on the most recent squared return |
| beta | Weight on the previous variance |

- **Persistence** is alpha + beta; it must be below 1 for the model to be **mean-reverting**.
- The long-run variance equals **omega / (1 - alpha - beta)**.
- EWMA is the special case where omega = 0 and alpha + beta = 1.

### Tip
- If alpha + beta is close to 1, shocks to volatility decay very slowly and persistence is high.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-financial-markets-futures-forwards-hedging-m01', 'frm-financial-markets-futures-forwards-hedging', 'Futures, Forwards & Hedging', '**Forwards** and **futures** are contracts to buy or sell an asset at a fixed price on a future date. They are the building blocks for hedging market exposure in the FRM Products section.

## Forwards vs Futures
| Feature | Forward | Future |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Trading | Over-the-counter | Exchange-traded |
| Standardization | Customized | Standardized |
| Settlement | At maturity | Daily marking-to-market |
| Credit risk | Counterparty | Clearinghouse |

## Cost-of-Carry Pricing
For an asset with no income, the **forward price** is:

```
F_0 = S_0 * e^(r*T)
```

- With a dividend or convenience yield q, F_0 = S_0 * e^((r - q)*T).
- **Contango** is F above spot; **backwardation** is F below spot.

## The Minimum-Variance Hedge
The **hedge ratio** that minimizes variance is:

```
h* = rho * (sigma_S / sigma_F)
```

where rho is the correlation between spot and futures returns. The **optimal number of contracts** is h* times the position size divided by the futures contract size.

## Basis Risk
- **Basis** = spot price - futures price; it converges to zero at delivery.
- A hedge is imperfect when the asset, maturity or quantity does not match the contract, leaving **basis risk**.

### Tip
- A short hedge protects a holder of the asset; a long hedge protects a future buyer against price rises.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-value-at-risk-m01', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-value-at-risk', 'Value at Risk (VaR)', '**Value at Risk (VaR)** estimates the maximum loss over a horizon at a given confidence level. It is the single most tested concept in the FRM Valuation section.

## Definition
A one-day 99% VaR of 1 million means there is a **1% chance** of losing more than 1 million in a day. VaR answers "how bad can things get" up to a confidence threshold, not beyond it.

## Parametric (Delta-Normal) VaR
Assuming normally distributed returns:

```
VaR = z * sigma * sqrt(t) * V
```

where z is the standard-normal quantile (**2.33 for 99%, 1.65 for 95%**), sigma is the return volatility, t is the horizon, and V is the portfolio value.

| Confidence | z-value |
| --- | --- |
| 95% | 1.65 |
| 97.5% | 1.96 |
| 99% | 2.33 |

## Scaling and Aggregation
- VaR scales with the **square root of time**: T-day VaR = 1-day VaR * sqrt(T).
- Portfolio VaR is below the sum of component VaRs whenever assets are imperfectly correlated, reflecting **diversification**.

## Expected Shortfall
**Expected Shortfall (ES)**, also called CVaR, is the average loss **beyond** the VaR threshold. It is a **coherent** risk measure that captures tail severity, which VaR ignores.

### Tip
- VaR can violate **subadditivity** for non-normal portfolios, which is the main reason regulators favor Expected Shortfall.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-types-taxonomy-m01', 'frm-foundations-risk-types-taxonomy', 'Risk Types & Enterprise Risk Taxonomy', '## Enterprise Risk Taxonomy

A structured **risk taxonomy** classifies the full universe of risks a firm faces, enabling consistent identification, measurement, and reporting across business lines.

## Core Risk Categories

**Market risk** arises from adverse movements in prices — interest rates, equity prices, foreign exchange rates, and commodity prices. It is measured using tools such as Value-at-Risk (VaR) and Expected Shortfall (ES).

**Credit risk** is the potential loss from a counterparty''s failure to meet its contractual obligations. It encompasses **default risk**, **migration risk** (credit quality deterioration), and **spread risk**.

**Liquidity risk** splits into two forms: **funding liquidity risk** (inability to meet cash obligations as they fall due) and **market liquidity risk** (inability to exit a position without materially moving the price).

**Operational risk** covers losses from inadequate or failed internal processes, people, systems, or external events. Regulatory capital under Basel III is explicitly required for operational risk.

**Legal and compliance risk** stems from unenforceable contracts, litigation, regulatory sanctions, or breaches of laws and rules. Closely linked is **reputational risk** — the threat of damage to the firm''s standing that reduces future revenue.

**Strategic risk** reflects the impact of adverse business decisions, poor implementation of strategies, or failure to respond to industry changes.

## Risk Interactions

| Risk Type | Primary Driver | Key Metric |
|-----------|---------------|------------|
| Market | Price volatility | VaR / ES |
| Credit | Counterparty quality | PD, LGD, EAD |
| Liquidity | Cash-flow timing | LCR, NSFR |
| Operational | Process failures | Loss frequency / severity |

Risks are rarely independent. A credit event can trigger liquidity stress (2008 crisis), and operational failures can amplify market losses. An enterprise taxonomy ensures no category is overlooked and that **risk aggregation** across types is coherent.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-foundations-corporate-governance-m01', 'frm-foundations-corporate-governance', 'Corporate Governance & Risk Culture', '## Corporate Governance & Risk Culture

**Corporate governance** refers to the system by which a firm is directed and controlled. Effective governance aligns management actions with shareholder interests while protecting other stakeholders, including creditors and regulators.

## Board Oversight

The **board of directors** bears ultimate responsibility for setting risk strategy and approving the **risk appetite statement**. A dedicated **Board Risk Committee** (BRC) provides focused oversight, reviewing significant risk exposures and ensuring management''s risk-taking remains within approved boundaries.

The **Chief Risk Officer (CRO)** leads the independent risk management function, reporting directly to the CEO and — critically — to the board. Independence from revenue-generating units is essential to avoid conflicts of interest.

## Three Lines of Defense

The **three lines of defense** model is the standard governance framework in financial institutions:

| Line | Role | Examples |
|------|------|---------|
| First | Business units own and manage risk | Trading desks, lending officers |
| Second | Risk and compliance functions provide oversight | Risk management, compliance, finance |
| Third | Internal audit provides independent assurance | Internal audit, inspection teams |

Regulators expect clear separation between the lines to prevent the second line from becoming captured by commercial pressures.

## Risk Culture

**Risk culture** encompasses the values, beliefs, and behaviours that determine how employees identify and respond to risk. A strong risk culture is characterised by **tone from the top**: senior leaders visibly endorse prudent risk-taking, and there is no tolerance for circumventing controls.

Key indicators of a weak risk culture include siloed reporting, suppression of bad news, excessive focus on short-term profit, and inadequate whistleblowing mechanisms. Regulators including the Basel Committee increasingly assess culture as a standalone governance dimension alongside formal structures.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-foundations-erp-risk-appetite-m01', 'frm-foundations-erp-risk-appetite', 'Enterprise Risk Programme & Risk Appetite', '## Enterprise Risk Programme & Risk Appetite

An **Enterprise Risk Programme (ERP)** — sometimes called Enterprise Risk Management (ERM) — provides a holistic, firm-wide framework for identifying, assessing, monitoring, and controlling all material risks. Unlike traditional **silo-based risk management**, which optimises individual risk categories in isolation, ERM recognises interdependencies and aggregates risk at the enterprise level.

## Risk Appetite Framework

The **risk appetite framework** translates strategic objectives into quantitative and qualitative risk boundaries:

- **Risk appetite**: the aggregate level and types of risk a firm is willing to accept in pursuit of its strategic objectives.
- **Risk tolerance**: the acceptable deviation around the risk appetite — a tighter band within which day-to-day operations are managed.
- **Risk limits**: specific quantitative thresholds (e.g., VaR limits by desk, single-name credit concentration limits) cascaded from the appetite statement.

## Key Tools

**Key Risk Indicators (KRIs)** are forward-looking metrics that signal when risk levels are approaching limits, allowing pre-emptive action before a limit breach.

The **risk register** is a centralised inventory cataloguing identified risks, their likelihood, potential impact, the responsible owner, and the current control environment. It is a living document, updated as risks evolve.

| Component | Purpose |
|-----------|---------|
| Risk appetite statement | Defines acceptable risk boundaries |
| Risk tolerance bands | Guides operational decision-making |
| KRIs | Early warning signals |
| Risk register | Comprehensive risk inventory |
| Stress testing | Validates resilience under adverse scenarios |

## ERM vs. Traditional Risk Management

Traditional risk management treats credit, market, and operational risk independently, often within separate departments. ERM integrates these streams, enabling **portfolio-level risk diversification** assessment and ensuring that risk limits remain coherent across the organisation.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-foundations-financial-disasters-m01', 'frm-foundations-financial-disasters', 'Financial Disasters & Risk Lessons', '## Financial Disasters & Risk Lessons

Studying past financial failures provides some of the most durable lessons in risk management. Each major disaster reveals a recurring pattern of inadequate controls, governance failures, or model overconfidence.

## Key Case Studies

**Barings Bank (1995)**: Nick Leeson accumulated unauthorised speculative positions in Nikkei futures, concealing losses in a hidden error account (88888). Barings collapsed because the same individual controlled both trading and back-office settlement — a fundamental breach of **segregation of duties**. Lesson: operational controls and independent oversight are non-negotiable.

**Metallgesellschaft (1993)**: MG Refining used short-dated futures to hedge long-dated oil supply commitments, creating massive mark-to-market margin calls when oil prices fell. The strategy was theoretically sound but ignored **liquidity risk**. Lesson: hedges must account for funding liquidity as well as price exposure.

**Long-Term Capital Management (1998)**: LTCM used extreme leverage (over 25:1) and relied on models calibrated to historical correlations. The Russian debt default caused correlations to converge to 1 during the crisis, destroying the assumed diversification benefits. Lesson: **model risk** and tail dependencies invalidate normal-period correlations in stress.

**2008 Global Financial Crisis**: Systemic failures included over-reliance on ratings for structured products, flawed correlation assumptions in Gaussian copula models for CDOs, excessive leverage, and inadequate liquidity buffers. Lesson: systemic risk and **wrong-way risk** (correlation between counterparty default and exposure) must be explicitly modelled.

## Common Threads

| Disaster | Primary Failure | Key Lesson |
|----------|----------------|-----------|
| Barings | Operational / governance | Segregation of duties |
| Metallgesellschaft | Liquidity risk | Funding vs. price hedge mismatch |
| LTCM | Model risk / leverage | Tail correlations and stress testing |
| 2008 Crisis | Systemic / model risk | Wrong-way risk and concentration |

**Model risk** — the risk that a model is incorrectly specified or misused — is a cross-cutting lesson from every major disaster.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-probability-distributions-m01', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-probability-distributions', 'Probability Distributions in Risk', '## Probability Distributions in Risk

Selecting the appropriate probability distribution is foundational to quantitative risk modelling. The distribution chosen shapes every downstream risk metric, including VaR, Expected Shortfall, and scenario analysis outputs.

## Key Distributions

The **normal distribution** N(mu, sigma^2) is defined entirely by its mean and variance. Its symmetry and thin tails make it analytically convenient, but financial returns routinely exhibit **excess kurtosis** (fat tails) and negative skew, making the normal distribution a poor standalone model for extreme losses.

The **lognormal distribution** is used to model asset prices directly, since a lognormally distributed price cannot fall below zero. If log-returns are normally distributed, the underlying price follows a lognormal process — the foundation of the Black-Scholes model.

The **Student-t distribution** with nu degrees of freedom introduces fatter tails than the normal. As nu increases the t-distribution converges to normal. With nu between 4 and 8, it better captures the empirical tail behaviour of financial returns and is widely used in **VaR models** to avoid underestimating extreme losses.

The **binomial distribution** models the number of successes (e.g., defaults) in n independent Bernoulli trials with probability p. It underpins simple credit portfolio models.

The **Poisson distribution** models the number of rare events (e.g., operational loss events) in a fixed time interval with mean lambda. It is central to **Loss Distribution Approach (LDA)** modelling in operational risk.

## Application to VaR

| Distribution | Tail Behaviour | Common Use |
|-------------|---------------|-----------|
| Normal | Thin tails | Parametric VaR (baseline) |
| Student-t | Fat tails | Improved parametric VaR |
| Lognormal | Right-skewed | Option pricing, asset price models |
| Poisson | Discrete events | Operational loss frequency |

The choice between normal and fat-tailed distributions directly affects capital requirements: underestimating tail thickness leads to systematic **VaR underestimation**.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-statistical-inference-m01', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-statistical-inference', 'Statistical Inference & Estimation', '## Statistical Inference & Estimation

Statistical inference allows risk managers to draw conclusions about population parameters from sample data — essential when fitting loss distributions, backtesting models, and calibrating risk metrics.

## Central Limit Theorem

The **Central Limit Theorem (CLT)** states that the sampling distribution of the sample mean converges to a normal distribution as sample size n grows, regardless of the underlying population distribution. In practice, n >= 30 is often cited as sufficient. The CLT justifies the use of normal-based confidence intervals for estimated means and is the theoretical foundation for many backtesting procedures.

## Confidence Intervals

A **(1-alpha) confidence interval** for a parameter theta is a random interval that contains the true parameter value with probability (1-alpha) in repeated sampling. For a population mean with known variance:

CI = x-bar +/- z_(alpha/2) * (sigma / sqrt(n))

Risk managers use confidence intervals to quantify estimation uncertainty around VaR estimates and to evaluate whether backtesting exception rates are statistically significant.

## Parameter Estimation

**Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE)** selects parameter values that maximise the likelihood of observing the sample data. MLE estimators are consistent and asymptotically efficient, making them the standard approach for fitting distributions such as the Student-t, GEV, or GPD to loss data.

**Method of Moments (MoM)** equates theoretical moments (mean, variance, skewness) to their sample equivalents and solves for parameters. MoM is simpler than MLE but can be less efficient, particularly in small samples.

| Method | Strengths | Limitations |
|--------|-----------|------------|
| MLE | Efficient, consistent | Computationally intensive |
| Method of Moments | Simple, closed-form | Less efficient in small samples |
| Bayesian estimation | Incorporates prior information | Requires prior specification |

**Hypothesis testing** complements estimation: backtesting VaR models involves testing whether the observed exception frequency matches the stated confidence level, using tests such as the Kupiec proportion-of-failures (POF) test.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-regression-m01', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-regression', 'Linear & Non-Linear Regression', '## Linear & Non-Linear Regression

Regression analysis is the workhorse of quantitative finance, used to estimate factor exposures, forecast returns, and model the conditional mean and variance of financial time series.

## OLS and Its Assumptions

**Ordinary Least Squares (OLS)** minimises the sum of squared residuals to estimate coefficients in y = X*beta + epsilon. The **Gauss-Markov theorem** guarantees that OLS is the Best Linear Unbiased Estimator (BLUE) when the classical assumptions hold:

1. Linearity in parameters
2. No perfect multicollinearity
3. Zero conditional mean of errors: E(epsilon | X) = 0
4. **Homoskedasticity**: constant error variance Var(epsilon | X) = sigma^2
5. No autocorrelation: Cov(epsilon_i, epsilon_j) = 0 for i not equal to j

## Violations and Consequences

**Heteroskedasticity** occurs when error variance changes with X (common in financial data where volatility clusters). OLS remains unbiased but is no longer efficient, and standard errors are incorrect. Remedies include **White''s heteroskedasticity-consistent standard errors** or WLS.

**Autocorrelation** means residuals are correlated across time. It inflates R-squared and distorts t-statistics. The **Durbin-Watson test** detects first-order autocorrelation; remedies include including lagged variables or using Newey-West standard errors.

**Multicollinearity** arises when regressors are highly correlated, inflating standard errors and making individual coefficient estimates unstable. The **Variance Inflation Factor (VIF)** quantifies its severity.

## GARCH Models

Standard OLS cannot capture **volatility clustering** — the empirical tendency of large return changes to cluster together. **GARCH(1,1)** models the conditional variance as:

sigma^2_t = omega + alpha * epsilon^2_(t-1) + beta * sigma^2_(t-1)

GARCH is the foundation for time-varying volatility estimates used in dynamic VaR calculation and options pricing.

| Issue | Test | Remedy |
|-------|------|--------|
| Heteroskedasticity | White / Breusch-Pagan | Robust SEs, WLS |
| Autocorrelation | Durbin-Watson | Newey-West SEs, GLS |
| Multicollinearity | VIF | Remove/combine regressors |', 2, 0),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-monte-carlo-m01', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-monte-carlo', 'Monte Carlo Simulation & Copulas', '## Monte Carlo Simulation & Copulas

**Monte Carlo simulation** uses repeated random sampling to approximate the distribution of outcomes that cannot be derived analytically. It is the most flexible risk measurement technique, applicable to path-dependent derivatives, portfolio credit risk, and operational risk capital modelling.

## Simulation Steps

1. Specify the stochastic model (e.g., geometric Brownian motion for equity prices, or a jump-diffusion process).
2. Generate **pseudo-random numbers** from a uniform distribution using algorithms such as the Mersenne Twister.
3. Transform uniforms to the target distribution using the **inverse CDF (probability integral transform)** or Cholesky decomposition for correlated variables.
4. Simulate many paths (typically 10,000 to 1,000,000) and compute the portfolio value at each path''s end.
5. Construct the empirical loss distribution and read off VaR and ES at the required confidence level.

## Variance Reduction Techniques

Naive Monte Carlo can require enormous sample sizes for accurate tail estimates. **Variance reduction** techniques improve efficiency:

- **Antithetic variates**: pair each random draw u with (1-u) to reduce variance.
- **Control variates**: use a correlated variable with a known analytical price to adjust simulated values.
- **Importance sampling**: oversample the tail region and reweight results, dramatically reducing the number of paths needed for accurate extreme quantile estimation.

## Copulas for Dependency Modelling

A **copula** is a multivariate distribution that links marginal distributions through a specified dependency structure, independent of the marginals themselves. Sklar''s theorem guarantees that any joint distribution can be decomposed into its marginals and a copula.

| Copula | Tail Dependency | Use Case |
|--------|----------------|---------|
| Gaussian | None | Baseline portfolio models |
| Student-t | Symmetric tail dep. | Improved credit portfolio |
| Clayton | Lower tail dep. | Default clustering models |
| Gumbel | Upper tail dep. | Extreme value applications |

The **Gaussian copula** was central to CDO pricing pre-2008 and its failure to capture **tail dependency** — the tendency for defaults to cluster in stress — was a key contributor to systemic underestimation of portfolio credit risk.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-financial-markets-options-pricing-m01', 'frm-financial-markets-options-pricing', 'Options & Black-Scholes Pricing', '**Options** give the buyer the right, but not the obligation, to buy or sell an asset at a fixed **strike price** before or at expiry. Understanding payoffs and the Black-Scholes model is essential for the FRM.

## Call and Put Payoffs
- **Call option** payoff at expiry: max(S - K, 0), where S is the asset price and K is the strike.
- **Put option** payoff at expiry: max(K - S, 0).
- The buyer pays a **premium** upfront; the seller collects it and bears unlimited (call) or large (put) downside.

## The Black-Scholes Model
Black-Scholes prices a European option using five inputs:

| Input | Symbol | Role |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Current asset price | S | Starting point of the stock |
| Strike price | K | Exercise threshold |
| Time to expiry | T | Remaining life in years |
| Risk-free rate | r | Discounting factor |
| Volatility | sigma | Standard deviation of log-returns |

The formula produces **d1** and **d2** and prices the call as S*N(d1) - K*e^(-rT)*N(d2), where N( ) is the cumulative normal distribution.

## Implied vs Historical Volatility
- **Historical volatility** is calculated from past price data.
- **Implied volatility** is the volatility backed out of the market option price; it reflects the market''s forward-looking expectation.
- The **volatility smile** (or skew) shows that implied volatility varies across strikes, violating strict Black-Scholes assumptions.

## Greeks Overview
The **Greeks** measure option price sensitivity to each input. Delta, gamma, vega, theta, and rho are the primary risk measures used to manage option positions and are covered in depth in the Option Valuation topic.

### Tip
- Black-Scholes assumes continuous trading, constant volatility, and no dividends; real markets deviate from each assumption.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-financial-markets-swaps-m01', 'frm-financial-markets-swaps', 'Swaps & Interest Rate Products', 'A **swap** is an agreement between two parties to exchange cash flows over time. Interest rate swaps are the most traded derivative in the world and a central topic for the FRM.

## Plain Vanilla Interest Rate Swap
In a **fixed-for-floating swap**, one party pays a fixed rate (the **swap rate**) and receives a floating reference rate, while the counterparty does the opposite. Only net cash flows are exchanged.

| Party | Pays | Receives |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Fixed payer | Fixed swap rate | Floating (SOFR/LIBOR) |
| Floating payer | Floating (SOFR/LIBOR) | Fixed swap rate |

## Swap Valuation
A swap can be valued as the difference between two bond positions: the fixed-rate bond (fixed leg) and a floating-rate bond (floating leg). At initiation, the swap rate is set so that the swap has **zero value**. As rates move, the swap gains or loses value to each party.

## OIS Discounting
After the 2008 crisis, market practice shifted to discounting swap cash flows using the **overnight indexed swap (OIS) rate** rather than LIBOR, because OIS better reflects the risk-free rate for collateralized transactions.

## LIBOR Transition to SOFR
The scandal-driven end of LIBOR prompted a global transition to alternative risk-free rates. In the United States, the **Secured Overnight Financing Rate (SOFR)** is the preferred replacement. SOFR is based on actual overnight repo transactions, making it more robust than LIBOR''s panel submissions.

### Tip
- Remember that the fixed swap rate is chosen so that the present value of fixed cash flows equals the present value of expected floating cash flows at inception.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-financial-markets-fixed-income-products-m01', 'frm-financial-markets-fixed-income-products', 'Fixed Income & Bond Markets', '**Fixed income securities** promise defined cash flows and are exposed to interest rate, credit, and liquidity risks. The FRM requires understanding bond pricing, yield measures, and risk metrics.

## Bond Pricing
A bond''s price is the present value of its coupon payments and face value discounted at the **yield to maturity (YTM)**:

```
Price = sum of [C / (1+y)^t] + [F / (1+y)^n]
```

where C is the coupon, F is the face value, y is the YTM per period, and n is the number of periods.

## Yield Measures
| Measure | Description |
| --- | --- |
| Coupon rate | Annual coupon divided by face value |
| Current yield | Annual coupon divided by market price |
| Yield to maturity | Discount rate equating price to cash flows |
| Yield to call | YTM assuming the bond is called at first call date |

- **YTM** is the single most important yield measure but assumes all coupons are reinvested at the same rate.

## Duration and Convexity in Risk Context
- **Macaulay duration** is the weighted average time to cash flows.
- **Modified duration** approximates the percentage price change per 1% change in yield: dP/P = -ModD * dy.
- **Convexity** corrects for the curvature in the price-yield relationship; bonds with higher convexity gain more when yields fall and lose less when yields rise.

## Repo Markets
In a **repurchase agreement (repo)**, a dealer sells securities and agrees to buy them back at a higher price. Repos are the primary short-term funding mechanism for fixed-income positions. The **haircut** is the discount applied to collateral value.

### Tip
- Duration measures linear price sensitivity; convexity is the second-order correction that improves accuracy for large yield moves.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-financial-markets-credit-products-m01', 'frm-financial-markets-credit-products', 'Credit Derivatives & Structured Products', '**Credit derivatives** transfer credit risk without transferring the underlying asset. The FRM tests the mechanics of **credit default swaps** and the structure of complex credit products.

## Credit Default Swap (CDS) Mechanics
A **CDS** is a contract in which the **protection buyer** pays a periodic spread (in basis points per year) to the **protection seller**. If a **reference entity** experiences a **credit event** (default, restructuring, etc.), the seller compensates the buyer for the loss.

| Party | Role |
| --- | --- |
| Protection buyer | Pays CDS spread; hedged against default |
| Protection seller | Receives spread; absorbs loss on credit event |

## CDS Pricing
The CDS **spread** is set so that the present value of premium payments equals the present value of expected loss payments. A wider spread reflects higher default risk or lower recovery rates.

## CDO Tranching
A **collateralized debt obligation (CDO)** pools loans or bonds and issues tranched securities:
- **Senior tranche** absorbs losses last; rated AAA; lowest yield.
- **Mezzanine tranche** takes losses after the equity tranche; intermediate yield.
- **Equity (first-loss) tranche** absorbs the first losses; highest yield; unrated.

## Synthetic CDO
A **synthetic CDO** gains credit exposure through CDS contracts rather than actual bond holdings, allowing investors to take on credit risk without purchasing the underlying assets. Correlation among reference entities determines how losses flow through the tranches.

### Tip
- Higher correlation between reference entities in a CDO hurts mezzanine holders and benefits equity holders, since defaults become more clustered.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-bond-valuation-m01', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-bond-valuation', 'Bond Valuation & Duration', 'In the risk context, bond valuation focuses on **interest rate sensitivity** rather than just pricing. The FRM tests DV01, key rate durations, spread risk, and option-adjusted spread.

## DV01 and PV01
- **DV01** (dollar value of a basis point) is the change in bond price for a 1-basis-point (0.01%) decrease in yield:

```
DV01 = -dP / dy * 0.0001
```

- **PV01** is often used interchangeably for the present value impact of a 1-bp shift in the discount curve.
- A portfolio''s total DV01 is the sum of DV01 across all positions and is the primary tool for hedging interest rate risk.

## Key Rate Durations
**Key rate duration** (also called **partial duration**) measures price sensitivity to a shift in a specific maturity point on the yield curve, holding other rates constant. Key rate durations sum to total modified duration and allow hedging of **yield curve shape risk**.

## Spread Risk
- **Spread duration** measures sensitivity to changes in the **credit spread** rather than the risk-free rate.
- A widening spread lowers bond prices; spread duration and DV01 combine to give total sensitivity for credit-risky bonds.

## Option-Adjusted Spread
The **option-adjusted spread (OAS)** is the constant spread added to the risk-free curve that equates the model price to the market price after accounting for **embedded options** (calls, puts, prepayment). OAS strips out optionality, enabling comparison across bonds with different option features.

### Tip
- DV01 is the go-to tool for rate hedging; OAS is the go-to measure for comparing callable and bullet bonds on a like-for-like basis.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-option-valuation-m01', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-option-valuation', 'Option Valuation & Greeks', 'The **Greeks** measure how an option''s price changes with each input. Managing an options book requires understanding each Greek and the limitations of dynamic hedging.

## Delta
**Delta** is the rate of change of the option price with respect to the underlying asset price. A call has delta between 0 and 1; a put has delta between -1 and 0. **Delta hedging** involves holding an offsetting position in the underlying to make the portfolio insensitive to small price moves.

## Gamma
**Gamma** is the rate of change of delta with respect to the underlying price. It is highest for at-the-money options near expiry. A positive gamma position benefits from large moves; a negative gamma position (short options) suffers. **Gamma risk** makes delta hedges deteriorate quickly.

| Greek | Sensitivity to | Sign for long call |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Delta | Underlying price | Positive |
| Gamma | Delta (second-order price) | Positive |
| Vega | Volatility | Positive |
| Theta | Time (time decay) | Negative |
| Rho | Risk-free rate | Positive |

## Vega and Theta
- **Vega** measures sensitivity to implied volatility; long options have positive vega.
- **Theta** captures **time decay**, the loss of option value as expiry approaches; long options have negative theta.

## Dynamic Hedging Limitations
Delta hedging must be **rebalanced continuously** as the underlying moves. In practice, discrete rebalancing leaves residual gamma risk. Transaction costs, jumps in the underlying, and changing volatility all impair the effectiveness of dynamic hedging.

### Tip
- Long gamma and long vega always go together in standard options; they profit from high realized volatility but pay positive time decay.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-stress-testing-m01', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-stress-testing', 'Stress Testing & Scenario Analysis', '**Stress testing** examines how a portfolio or institution performs under severe but plausible scenarios that VaR may miss. It is a cornerstone of post-crisis risk management and regulation.

## Purpose
VaR captures typical losses but understates losses in extreme, correlated market dislocations. Stress tests evaluate **tail scenarios** that are too rare or too severe for statistical models to capture reliably.

## Regulatory Stress Tests
| Test | Jurisdiction | Key Feature |
| --- | --- | --- |
| DFAST | United States | Annual Dodd-Frank Act Stress Test for large banks |
| CCAR | United States | Comprehensive Capital Analysis and Review; includes capital planning |
| EBA stress test | European Union | Biennial test across major European banks |

- Both DFAST and CCAR require banks to project losses, revenues, and capital under **adverse** and **severely adverse** macroeconomic scenarios provided by regulators.

## Historical Scenarios
**Historical scenarios** replay the losses from past crises — the 1987 equity crash, the 1997 Asian crisis, the 2008 financial crisis — using current portfolio positions. They are concrete but limited to events that have already occurred.

## Narrative (Hypothetical) Scenarios
**Hypothetical scenarios** are forward-looking stories built around plausible but unprecedented shocks (e.g., a sudden sovereign debt crisis or a pandemic-driven shutdown). They are more flexible but require expert judgment.

## Reverse Stress Testing
**Reverse stress testing** works backward: it asks what scenario would cause the institution to fail or breach a capital threshold, then assesses how plausible that scenario is. It complements forward-looking stress tests by identifying hidden vulnerabilities.

### Tip
- Reverse stress testing is especially powerful because it forces management to confront scenarios that threaten viability, not just painful but survivable losses.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-model-risk-m01', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-model-risk', 'Model Risk & Validation', '**Model risk** is the risk of adverse outcomes from errors in model development, implementation, or use. The FRM tests the regulatory framework and best practices for managing it.

## SR 11-7 Guidance
The Federal Reserve''s **SR 11-7** (Supervisory Guidance on Model Risk Management) is the primary U.S. regulatory standard. It defines a model as having three components: an information input, a processing component, and a reporting output. Key requirements include:

- Independent **model validation** separate from model development.
- Clear documentation of model assumptions, limitations, and intended use.
- An **inventory** of all models in use, with tiering by materiality.

## Model Validation
**Validation** assesses whether a model is conceptually sound and performs as intended. Core activities include:

| Activity | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| Conceptual review | Assess theory and methodology |
| Benchmarking | Compare to alternative models |
| Backtesting | Test performance against historical outcomes |
| Sensitivity analysis | Test behavior under varied inputs |

## Backtesting
**Backtesting** compares model predictions to realized outcomes. A VaR model is backtested by counting exceptions; a credit scoring model by comparing predicted default rates to actual defaults.

## Model Limitations
All models simplify reality. Common limitations include incorrect distributional assumptions, parameter instability, and use outside the intended range (**model misuse**).

## Challenger Models
A **challenger model** is an independently built alternative used to validate or benchmark the primary (champion) model. Running challenger models reduces dependence on a single approach and helps identify blind spots.

### Tip
- SR 11-7 requires validation to be independent of development; using the model builder to validate their own model is a governance failure.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-foundations-apt-multifactor-m01', 'frm-foundations-apt-multifactor', 'APT & Multifactor Models', '**Arbitrage Pricing Theory (APT)** explains expected return as a linear function of **multiple systematic risk factors**, not just the single market factor of the CAPM.

**E[R_i] = R_f + β_{i,1}·RP_1 + β_{i,2}·RP_2 + … + β_{i,k}·RP_k**

- Each **β** is the asset''s sensitivity (factor loading) to a factor; each **RP** is that factor''s risk premium.
- APT rests on **no-arbitrage**, not on the mean-variance / market-portfolio assumptions of CAPM.
- APT does **not** specify the factors. Common macro factors: inflation surprises, GDP/industrial production, term-structure (slope) shifts, credit spreads.

## Multifactor models
- **Macroeconomic** factor models use economic surprises; **fundamental** models (e.g. Fama-French) use firm characteristics (size SMB, value HML, plus market and momentum).
- Used for **return attribution**, **risk decomposition**, and **hedging** specific factor exposures.

> APT generalizes CAPM: CAPM is the special one-factor case where the only priced factor is the market.', 2, 5),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-time-series-m01', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-time-series', 'Time Series: AR, MA & Stationarity', '## Stationarity
A **covariance-stationary** series has constant **mean**, constant **variance**, and an autocovariance that depends only on the lag. Most modeling requires it; non-stationary series are often **differenced** first.

## Building blocks
- **AR(p)** — autoregressive: x_t depends on its own past values. An **AR(1)** x_t = c + φ·x_{t−1} + ε_t is stationary iff **|φ| < 1**; its mean reverts to c/(1−φ).
- **MA(q)** — moving average: x_t is a function of current and past **shocks** (ε terms).
- **ARMA(p,q)** combines both; **ARIMA** adds differencing (the "I") for non-stationary data.

## Diagnostics
- A **unit root** (φ = 1, a random walk) means non-stationarity; test with the **(Augmented) Dickey-Fuller** test.
- Choose lag order with **AIC/BIC** (lower is better); check residuals are **white noise** (no remaining autocorrelation, e.g. Ljung-Box).
- **Seasonality** is handled with seasonal lags/differencing.', 2, 5),
  ('frm-financial-markets-market-structure-m01', 'frm-financial-markets-market-structure', 'Exchanges, OTC Markets & Central Clearing', '## Exchange-traded vs OTC
| | Exchange (futures) | OTC (forwards/swaps) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Terms | Standardized | Customized |
| Counterparty | **Clearinghouse (CCP)** | Bilateral |
| Credit risk | Minimal (margin) | Counterparty risk |
| Liquidity | High | Variable |

## Central counterparties (CCPs)
After 2008, regulators pushed standardized OTC derivatives into **central clearing**. The CCP becomes **buyer to every seller and seller to every buyer** (novation), and manages risk via:
- **Initial margin** (covers potential future exposure) and **variation margin** (daily mark-to-market).
- A **default waterfall**: defaulter''s margin → defaulter''s default-fund contribution → CCP "skin in the game" → mutualized default fund → assessments.
- **Multilateral netting**, which sharply reduces gross exposures.

## Trade-offs
CCPs concentrate risk (a CCP failure is systemic) and require robust margining and a **guarantee fund**. Non-cleared OTC trades face bilateral margin rules and **CVA** charges.', 2, 5),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-volatility-smiles-m01', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-volatility-smiles', 'Volatility Smiles & Surfaces', 'Black-Scholes assumes a **single, constant volatility** for all strikes. In reality, implied volatilities **vary by strike and maturity** — evidence the lognormal assumption is imperfect.

## Shapes
- **Volatility smile**: implied vol higher for deep ITM/OTM options than at-the-money — common in **currency** options (fat tails, jumps in both directions).
- **Volatility skew (smirk)**: implied vol higher for **low strikes** (OTM puts) than high strikes — typical of **equity** options, reflecting crash fear and leverage effects.
- **Volatility surface**: implied vol plotted across both strike and maturity; traders interpolate it to price exotics.

## Interpretation
- A smile implies the **risk-neutral distribution has fatter tails** than lognormal.
- An equity skew implies a distribution **left-skewed** (heavier downside).
- Smiles tend to **flatten as maturity lengthens**.
- Implied vol from the smile is the market''s forward-looking volatility; using a single ATM vol misprices wing options.', 2, 5),
  ('frm-foundations-garp-code-m01', 'frm-foundations-garp-code', 'GARP Code of Conduct & Ethics', 'The **GARP Code of Conduct** sets the ethical standard for FRMs, similar in spirit to the CFA Code.

## Core principles
1. **Professional integrity and ethical conduct** — act honestly, avoid conduct that discredits the profession.
2. **Conflicts of interest** — disclose and manage; place the interests of clients and employers appropriately.
3. **Confidentiality** — protect client and employer information.
4. **Fundamental responsibilities** — comply with laws/regulations and maintain competence.
5. **General accepted practices** — follow prevailing risk-management standards.

## Enforcement
- Violations can lead to loss of the FRM designation and removal from membership.
- The Code applies to **candidates and certified members** alike.

Ethics questions test applying these principles to realistic risk-management dilemmas — disclosure, independence, and professional competence.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-data-m01', 'frm-foundations-risk-data', 'Risk Data Aggregation & Reporting', 'After 2008, the Basel Committee issued **BCBS 239** — principles for effective **risk data aggregation and risk reporting (RDARR)**.

## Key principles
- **Governance and infrastructure**: strong data architecture and IT.
- **Accuracy and integrity**: aggregate data reliably, largely automated, with reconciliation.
- **Completeness**: capture all material risks across the group.
- **Timeliness**: produce aggregated risk data rapidly, especially in stress.
- **Adaptability**: meet ad-hoc requests and new scenarios.

## Reporting principles
Reports should be **accurate, comprehensive, clear, useful and timely**, distributed to the right recipients.

Poor data aggregation was a key failing in the crisis — banks could not quickly measure aggregate exposures (e.g., to a single counterparty).', 2, 0),
  ('frm-foundations-capital-allocation-m01', 'frm-foundations-capital-allocation', 'Capital Allocation & RAROC', '**RAROC (risk-adjusted return on capital)** measures profitability relative to the **economic capital** at risk.

## The formula
RAROC = (Expected return − Expected loss − Costs + Return on capital) / **Economic capital**.
- **Economic capital** is the capital needed to absorb unexpected losses at a chosen confidence level.
- A project/business adds value when **RAROC > hurdle rate** (often the cost of equity).

## Uses
- **Capital allocation** across business lines by risk-adjusted performance.
- **Pricing** — ensuring each transaction earns enough to cover its risk.
- **Performance measurement** and incentive design.

## Adjusted RAROC (ARAROC)
Adjusts for the business''s beta so comparisons account for systematic risk, comparing ARAROC against the risk-free rate.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-hypothesis-testing-m01', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-hypothesis-testing', 'Hypothesis Testing & Confidence Intervals', '**Hypothesis testing** evaluates a claim about a population parameter.

## Framework
- **Null (H₀)** vs **alternative (H₁)** hypotheses.
- **Test statistic** (z or t) compared to a critical value; or use the **p-value**.
- Reject H₀ if the p-value < significance level **α** (e.g., 5%).

## Errors
- **Type I error** (α): rejecting a true H₀ (false positive).
- **Type II error** (β): failing to reject a false H₀; **power** = 1 − β.

## Confidence intervals
- A 95% CI is the estimate ± (critical value × standard error).
- Use the **z** distribution when the population variance is known/large samples; the **t** distribution for small samples with unknown variance.
- **One-tailed vs two-tailed** tests change the critical value.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-correlation-copulas-m01', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-correlation-copulas', 'Correlation, Covariance & Copulas', '**Covariance** measures how two variables move together; **correlation** ρ standardises it to [−1, 1].

## Key facts
- Portfolio variance uses covariances: σ²_p = w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + 2w₁w₂ρσ₁σ₂.
- Correlation captures only **linear** dependence and can miss tail dependence.

## Copulas
- A **copula** joins marginal distributions into a joint distribution, modelling the **dependence structure** separately from the marginals.
- **Gaussian copulas** underprice **tail dependence** — a key lesson from the 2008 CDO crisis.
- **t-copulas** and **Gumbel/Clayton** copulas capture **tail dependence** better.

Copulas are essential in credit portfolio and structured-product modelling where joint defaults matter.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-bayesian-m01', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-bayesian', 'Bayesian Analysis & Estimation', '**Bayesian analysis** updates beliefs with new evidence using **Bayes'' theorem**:
P(A|B) = P(B|A)·P(A) / P(B).

## Terminology
- **Prior**: initial belief before data.
- **Likelihood**: probability of the data given the hypothesis.
- **Posterior**: updated belief after data (∝ prior × likelihood).

## In risk management
- Update **probability of default** as new information arrives.
- Combine expert judgement (prior) with sparse loss data — useful for **operational risk** where data are scarce.
- Contrast with the **frequentist** view, which treats parameters as fixed and uses long-run frequencies.

A classic application is revising the probability a borrower is ''good'' after observing a payment, or diagnosing base-rate problems.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-financial-markets-commodity-forwards-m01', 'frm-financial-markets-commodity-forwards', 'Commodity Forwards & Futures', 'Commodity forward pricing follows **cost-of-carry** but with commodity-specific twists.

## Pricing
F₀ = S₀·e^((r + u − y)T), where **u** = storage cost, **y** = convenience yield.
- **Convenience yield**: benefit of holding the physical commodity (avoiding stock-outs).

## Curve shapes
- **Contango**: futures above spot (carry costs dominate).
- **Backwardation**: futures below spot (high convenience yield / tight supply).

## Return components
- Total return = **spot return + roll yield + collateral yield**.
- **Roll yield** is positive in backwardation, negative in contango.

Commodities include energy, metals and agriculturals; each has distinct storage, seasonality and convenience-yield behaviour.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-financial-markets-foreign-exchange-m01', 'frm-financial-markets-foreign-exchange', 'Foreign Exchange Markets & Risk', 'The **FX market** is the largest, most liquid market, quoting currency pairs.

## Key relationships
- **Covered interest rate parity (CIRP)**: F/S = (1 + r_domestic)/(1 + r_foreign); enforced by arbitrage.
- **Uncovered interest rate parity (UIP)**: expected spot change equals the interest differential (often fails short-term).
- A currency trades at a **forward premium** when its interest rate is lower.

## FX risk types
- **Transaction risk** (contracted cash flows), **translation risk** (accounting), and **economic risk** (competitive position).

## Hedging
- **Forwards, futures, options and swaps** hedge FX exposure.
- **Multi-currency VaR** aggregates positions; correlations between currencies matter.

FX exposure is a major driver of risk for global banks and corporates.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-financial-markets-mortgages-mbs-m01', 'frm-financial-markets-mortgages-mbs', 'Mortgages & Mortgage-Backed Securities', 'A **mortgage-backed security (MBS)** pools mortgages and passes their cash flows to investors.

## Key risks
- **Prepayment risk**: borrowers refinance when rates fall, shortening the security (**contraction risk**); when rates rise, prepayments slow (**extension risk**).
- Prepayment makes MBS exhibit **negative convexity** — price gains are capped as rates fall.

## Structures
- **Pass-throughs** share pooled cash flows pro rata.
- **CMOs** carve prepayment risk into tranches (e.g., PAC, support).
- Measured with the **PSA prepayment model** and **option-adjusted spread (OAS)**.

## Agency vs non-agency
- **Agency MBS** carry a government/GSE guarantee (little credit risk); **non-agency** carry credit risk requiring subordination.

Prepayment modelling is central to valuing and hedging MBS.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-fixed-income-risk-m01', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-fixed-income-risk', 'Fixed-Income Risk: DV01 & Convexity', 'Bond price sensitivity to rates is captured by **duration** and **convexity**.

## Measures
- **DV01** (dollar value of a basis point): price change for a 1 bp yield move.
- **Modified duration**: % price change per 1% yield change; ΔP/P ≈ −D·Δy.
- **Convexity** corrects the linear duration estimate: ΔP/P ≈ −D·Δy + ½·C·(Δy)².

## Hedging
- **Duration hedging**: match DV01 of the hedge to the position.
- **Key-rate (partial) durations** capture non-parallel curve shifts.

## Behaviour
- Positive convexity benefits the holder (price rises more than duration predicts when yields fall, falls less when they rise).
- Callable bonds and MBS can show **negative convexity**.

DV01 and duration are the workhorses of interest-rate risk management.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-country-risk-m01', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-country-risk', 'Country & Sovereign Risk', '**Country risk** is the risk of investing in a particular nation; **sovereign risk** is the risk the government defaults.

## Drivers
- **Economic**: growth, inflation, fiscal and current-account deficits, debt/GDP.
- **Political/legal**: stability, rule of law, expropriation risk.
- **Ability vs willingness to pay**: a sovereign may be able but unwilling to repay (especially foreign-currency debt).

## Assessment tools
- **Sovereign credit ratings** and **CDS spreads** signal default risk.
- Local-currency vs foreign-currency debt differ — a government can print its own currency but not foreign currency.

## Contagion
Country risk can spread across similar economies (contagion), as seen in emerging-market and euro-area crises.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-credit-ratings-m01', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-credit-ratings', 'External & Internal Credit Ratings', '**Credit ratings** assess the creditworthiness of a borrower or issue.

## External ratings
- Agencies (S&P, Moody''s, Fitch) map issuers to grades: **investment grade** (BBB−/Baa3 and above) vs **speculative/high-yield** below.
- Ratings are **through-the-cycle** and can lag; **rating transitions** are summarised in **transition matrices**.

## Issues & criticisms
- **Issuer-pays conflict of interest**, procyclicality, and slow reaction (as in 2008).
- **Rating cliffs** can force selling when a bond is downgraded below investment grade.

## Internal ratings
- Banks build **internal rating-based (IRB)** models estimating **PD, LGD and EAD** for Basel capital.
- Internal ratings can be **point-in-time** (reflecting current conditions) or through-the-cycle.

A **transition matrix** gives the probability of moving between rating grades over a horizon.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-market-risk-var-methods-backtesting-m01', 'frm-market-risk-var-methods-backtesting', 'VaR Methods & Backtesting', '**Market risk measurement** compares three ways to compute **VaR** and then validates the chosen model through **backtesting** against realized losses.

## Three VaR Approaches
| Method | Key trait |
| --- | --- |
| Parametric | Assumes a distribution; fast but misses fat tails |
| Historical simulation | Reuses actual past returns; no distribution assumption |
| Monte Carlo | Simulates many paths; flexible but compute-heavy |

- **Historical simulation** ranks past profit-and-loss and reads off the percentile.
- **Monte Carlo** can price nonlinear positions but is the slowest method.

## Backtesting
**Backtesting** counts how often actual losses **exceed** the VaR estimate. At 99% one-day VaR, roughly 1% of days should be **exceptions**.

```
Expected exceptions = (1 - confidence) * number of days
```

## The Basel Traffic-Light Test
Over 250 days at 99% VaR:

| Zone | Exceptions | Action |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Green | 0 to 4 | Model accepted |
| Yellow | 5 to 9 | Multiplier increased |
| Red | 10 or more | Model rejected |

- Too many exceptions implies the model **understates** risk; too few implies it is overly conservative.
- A **conditional coverage** test also checks that exceptions are not clustered in time (independence).

### Tip
- Backtesting needs both correct unconditional coverage and independence of exceptions to validate a VaR model.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-credit-risk-default-probability-credit-var-m01', 'frm-credit-risk-default-probability-credit-var', 'Default Probability & Credit VaR', '**Credit risk** is the loss from a borrower or counterparty failing to meet obligations. The FRM measures it through expected loss, default probability and **Credit VaR**.

## The Loss Building Blocks
**Expected Loss (EL)** combines three drivers:

```
EL = PD * LGD * EAD
```

| Term | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| PD | Probability of default |
| LGD | Loss given default (1 - recovery) |
| EAD | Exposure at default |

- **LGD** equals one minus the recovery rate.

## Expected vs Unexpected Loss
- **Expected loss** is priced into spreads and covered by provisions.
- **Unexpected loss** is the volatility of losses, covered by **economic capital**.

## Default Probability
- A bond''s **credit spread** approximates PD * LGD over the horizon.
- The **Merton model** treats equity as a call option on firm assets, defaulting when asset value falls below debt.
- **Cumulative** and **marginal** default probabilities link through survival rates.

## Credit VaR
**Credit VaR** is the unexpected loss at a confidence level, the worst credit loss minus the expected loss:

```
Credit VaR = Worst-case loss (at confidence) - Expected Loss
```

- Default correlation raises portfolio Credit VaR; a higher **copula** correlation fattens the loss tail.

### Tip
- Economic capital is sized to unexpected loss, not expected loss, since expected loss is already provisioned.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-operational-risk-basel-accords-raroc-m01', 'frm-operational-risk-basel-accords-raroc', 'Basel Accords & RAROC', '**Operational risk** is the risk of loss from inadequate processes, people, systems or external events. The Basel framework sets capital for it and **RAROC** links risk to performance.

## The Basel Accords
| Accord | Focus |
| --- | --- |
| Basel I | Credit risk, simple risk weights |
| Basel II | Three pillars; adds operational risk |
| Basel III | Liquidity and capital quality |

- **Pillar 1** sets minimum capital, **Pillar 2** supervisory review, **Pillar 3** market discipline.

## Operational Risk Capital
- Approaches evolved from the **Basic Indicator** and **Standardized** methods toward the loss-data-driven **Standardized Measurement Approach**.
- Loss data is mapped across **event types** and **business lines**.

## RAROC
**Risk-Adjusted Return on Capital** measures return per unit of risk capital:

```
RAROC = (Revenue - Costs - Expected Loss) / Economic Capital
```

- A project adds value when **RAROC exceeds the hurdle rate** (the cost of equity).
- **Adjusted RAROC** subtracts the risk-free rate from RAROC before comparing to the equity premium.

## Why It Matters
| Use | Benefit |
| --- | --- |
| Capital allocation | Directs capital to high-RAROC units |
| Pricing | Sets risk-based loan pricing |
| Performance | Compares units on a like-for-like basis |

### Tip
- Accept a business line only when its RAROC clears the firm''s hurdle rate, not merely when it is positive.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-liquidity-risk-liquidity-risk-management-m01', 'frm-liquidity-risk-liquidity-risk-management', 'Liquidity Risk Management', '**Liquidity risk** is the danger of being unable to meet obligations or trade positions without large losses. The FRM splits it into funding and market liquidity.

## Two Faces of Liquidity
- **Funding liquidity risk** is the inability to raise cash to meet cash-flow needs.
- **Market (trading) liquidity risk** is the inability to sell an asset near its fair value.

## Measuring Market Liquidity
The **liquidity-adjusted VaR** adds a cost-of-liquidation term based on the bid-ask spread:

```
LVaR = VaR + 0.5 * (spread) * position value
```

| Driver | Effect on liquidity cost |
| --- | --- |
| Wider bid-ask spread | Higher liquidation cost |
| Larger position size | Greater market impact |
| Higher spread volatility | Larger exogenous cost add-on |

## Basel III Liquidity Ratios
- The **Liquidity Coverage Ratio (LCR)** requires enough **high-quality liquid assets** to survive a 30-day stress:

```
LCR = HQLA / Net cash outflows over 30 days >= 100%
```

- The **Net Stable Funding Ratio (NSFR)** ensures stable funding over a one-year horizon.

## Managing the Risk
- Hold a **liquidity buffer** of HQLA and diversify funding sources and maturities.
- Run **cash-flow gap** analysis and stress scenarios to anticipate shortfalls.

### Tip
- Funding and market liquidity reinforce each other in a crisis, producing a downward **liquidity spiral**.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-investment-risk-portfolio-risk-budgeting-m01', 'frm-investment-risk-portfolio-risk-budgeting', 'Portfolio Risk Budgeting', '**Risk budgeting** allocates a portfolio''s total risk among positions and factors, the core of risk management in the investment process.

## Portfolio Variance
For two assets, variance depends on weights, volatilities and correlation:

```
sigma_p^2 = w1^2*sigma1^2 + w2^2*sigma2^2 + 2*w1*w2*rho*sigma1*sigma2
```

- Lower **correlation** reduces portfolio variance, the engine of diversification.

## Marginal and Component Risk
| Measure | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| Marginal VaR | Change in VaR from a small position increase |
| Incremental VaR | VaR change from adding a whole position |
| Component VaR | Each position''s share of total VaR (sums to total) |

- **Component VaR** decomposes total VaR so the parts add back to the whole.

## Tracking Error and Information Ratio
- **Tracking error** is the volatility of active (portfolio minus benchmark) returns.
- The **information ratio** rewards active return per unit of tracking error:

```
IR = (R_portfolio - R_benchmark) / Tracking error
```

## Risk Parity
- A **risk-parity** portfolio equalizes each asset''s risk contribution rather than its capital weight.
- Surplus risk and funded-ratio analysis extend budgeting to **liability-relative** investors.

### Tip
- Component VaRs sum to total VaR, making them the right tool for assigning a risk budget across desks.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-current-issues-basel-iii-iv-emerging-risks-m01', 'frm-current-issues-basel-iii-iv-emerging-risks', 'Basel III/IV & Emerging Risks', '**Current issues** track how the Basel framework and the risk landscape are evolving, from post-crisis capital rules to climate and cyber threats.

## Basel III Capital Strengthening
- Raised the quality and quantity of capital, centered on **Common Equity Tier 1 (CET1)**.
- Added a **capital conservation buffer** and a **countercyclical buffer**.
- Introduced a non-risk-based **leverage ratio** as a backstop:

```
Leverage ratio = Tier 1 capital / Total exposure >= 3%
```

## Basel III/IV Finalization
| Reform | Aim |
| --- | --- |
| Output floor | Caps internal-model capital relief at 72.5% of standardized |
| Revised standardized approaches | More risk-sensitive credit and operational rules |
| FRTB | Overhauls the trading-book market-risk framework |

- The **Fundamental Review of the Trading Book (FRTB)** replaces VaR with **Expected Shortfall** for regulatory market risk.

## Emerging Risks
- **Climate risk** splits into **physical** and **transition** risk and now enters stress tests.
- **Cyber and operational resilience** risks grow with digital dependence and outsourcing.
- **Model risk** and AI governance demand robust validation of increasingly complex models.
- **Geopolitical** shocks and supply-chain disruptions add fresh sources of correlated loss.

### Tip
- Remember that FRTB shifts the regulatory measure from VaR to Expected Shortfall at a 97.5% confidence level, while the output floor curbs internal-model relief.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-market-risk-parametric-var-m01', 'frm-market-risk-parametric-var', 'Parametric VaR & Normal Distribution', '## Parametric VaR & Normal Distribution

**Parametric VaR** (also called the **variance-covariance method**) estimates the maximum loss over a given horizon at a specified confidence level, assuming returns are normally distributed.

## Core Formula

For a single position, VaR is calculated as:

**VaR = Position Value x z-score x Daily Volatility x sqrt(T)**

The **z-score** maps the chosen confidence level to the standard normal distribution:

| Confidence Level | z-score |
|-----------------|---------|
| 90% | 1.28 |
| 95% | 1.65 |
| 99% | 2.33 |
| 99.9% | 3.09 |

## Portfolio VaR and Correlation

For a two-asset portfolio, the **portfolio VaR** accounts for the correlation between positions:

**VaR_p = sqrt(VaR_1^2 + VaR_2^2 + 2 x rho x VaR_1 x VaR_2)**

When **correlation (rho)** is less than 1, portfolio VaR is less than the sum of individual VaRs — this reduction is the **diversification benefit**. A correlation of 1 eliminates diversification, while a correlation of -1 can theoretically reduce portfolio risk to zero.

## Scaling VaR Across Time

VaR scales with the **square root of time**. A 1-day VaR at 99% can be scaled to a 10-day VaR by multiplying by sqrt(10), a common regulatory requirement under Basel.

## Advantages and Limitations

**Advantages:** Analytically simple, computationally fast, easy to decompose into risk factor contributions.

**Limitations:** The normality assumption understates **tail risk** because actual return distributions exhibit **fat tails** (excess kurtosis) and **negative skewness**. Parametric VaR also relies on stable covariance estimates, which break down during market stress. For portfolios with options or nonlinear instruments, the linear approximation introduces additional error.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-market-risk-historical-simulation-m01', 'frm-market-risk-historical-simulation', 'Historical Simulation & Full Valuation', '## Historical Simulation & Full Valuation

**Historical simulation (HS)** estimates VaR by replaying actual historical market moves against the current portfolio. Unlike parametric VaR, it makes no distributional assumption about returns.

## Methodology

The standard approach uses a **lookback window** (typically 250 to 500 trading days) of historical risk factor changes. Each historical scenario is applied to today''s portfolio holdings, generating a distribution of hypothetical P&L. VaR is then read directly from the percentile of this distribution — the 5th percentile for 95% VaR or the 1st percentile for 99% VaR.

Because the portfolio is revalued under each scenario using full pricing models, this is known as **full valuation**, which naturally captures nonlinear exposures such as options gamma and vega.

## Variants

**Age-weighted historical simulation** assigns exponentially declining weights to older observations, making the VaR estimate more responsive to recent market conditions. The decay factor (lambda) controls how quickly old data loses influence.

**Filtered historical simulation (FHS)** combines HS with volatility scaling: raw historical returns are standardised by a GARCH-estimated volatility, then rescaled by current volatility. This corrects for the fact that raw HS treats all past periods as equally volatile, which is a significant flaw during volatility regime changes.

## Advantages vs. Parametric VaR

| Feature | Historical Simulation | Parametric VaR |
|---|---|---|
| Distributional assumption | None | Normal |
| Tail risk capture | Better (fat tails included) | Understated |
| Computation | Slower (full repricing) | Fast |
| Nonlinear instruments | Handles well | Approximation only |

## Key Limitation

HS is entirely backward-looking. It cannot capture risks from events not present in the historical window and may be slow to reflect structural market changes. Window length is a critical and somewhat arbitrary choice.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-market-risk-expected-shortfall-m01', 'frm-market-risk-expected-shortfall', 'Expected Shortfall & Coherent Risk Measures', '## Expected Shortfall & Coherent Risk Measures

**Expected Shortfall (ES)**, also called **Conditional VaR (CVaR)** or **Expected Tail Loss (ETL)**, measures the average loss in scenarios that exceed the VaR threshold. Where VaR only asks "what is the loss at the Xth percentile?", ES asks "given that we are in the worst (1-X)% of outcomes, what is our average loss?"

## Formula

**ES(alpha) = E[Loss | Loss > VaR(alpha)]**

For example, a 97.5% ES is the expected loss conditional on being in the worst 2.5% of the distribution. This is the confidence level adopted under the **FRTB** framework to replace 99% VaR.

## Coherent Risk Measures

A risk measure is **coherent** if it satisfies four axioms:

1. **Monotonicity** — a portfolio with higher losses in all scenarios has higher risk
2. **Subadditivity** — the risk of a combined portfolio cannot exceed the sum of individual risks (diversification is rewarded)
3. **Positive homogeneity** — scaling a portfolio scales the risk measure proportionally
4. **Translation invariance** — adding cash reduces risk by that amount

**VaR fails subadditivity** for non-normal distributions, meaning the sum of two positions'' VaRs can understate combined portfolio risk. ES satisfies all four axioms and is therefore coherent.

## Practical Implications

Because ES averages across the entire tail rather than reading a single quantile, it is more sensitive to extreme events and better reflects the shape of the loss distribution beyond the VaR cutoff. This makes ES a superior risk measure for stress-scenario assessment and regulatory capital purposes. However, ES is statistically harder to **backtest** because it conditions on tail events that are, by definition, rare.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-market-risk-regulatory-capital-m01', 'frm-market-risk-regulatory-capital', 'Market Risk Regulatory Capital (Basel)', '## Market Risk Regulatory Capital (Basel)

The **Fundamental Review of the Trading Book (FRTB)**, finalised under Basel III/IV, overhauled the market risk capital framework. Banks must choose between the **Standardised Approach (SA)** and the **Internal Models Approach (IMA)**, subject to regulatory approval.

## Standardised Approach (SA)

The SA uses a **sensitivities-based method (SBM)** that aggregates capital charges across risk classes (interest rate, credit spread, equity, FX, commodity). For each risk class, banks compute **delta, vega, and curvature** sensitivities, which are then bucketed and aggregated using prescribed correlations. The SA also includes a **residual risk add-on** for instruments with exotic features.

## Internal Models Approach (IMA)

Under the IMA, banks use their own models to compute **Expected Shortfall at 97.5%** (replacing the pre-FRTB 99% VaR) over a 10-day horizon. Key features include:

- **Liquidity horizons** that vary by risk factor (10 days for major FX to 120 days for illiquid credit spreads)
- **Non-modellable risk factors (NMRFs)** must be capitalised using stress scenarios if insufficient price observations exist
- Desk-level IMA approval — each trading desk must independently pass approval tests

## Backtesting and the Traffic Light System

Banks using the IMA must backtest their VaR models by comparing daily VaR forecasts to actual P&L. Exceptions (days where the loss exceeds VaR) are counted over a 250-day window:

| Exceptions | Zone | Capital Multiplier |
|---|---|---|
| 0-4 | Green | 1.5 |
| 5-9 | Amber | 1.5 to 2.0 (scaling) |
| 10+ | Red | 2.0 (IMA revoked) |

The multiplier applies to the ES-based capital charge, incentivising accurate model calibration.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-credit-risk-credit-derivatives-m01', 'frm-credit-risk-credit-derivatives', 'Credit Default Swaps & Credit Derivatives', '## Credit Default Swaps & Credit Derivatives

A **Credit Default Swap (CDS)** is a bilateral contract that transfers the credit risk of a **reference entity** from one party to another. It is the most widely traded credit derivative and a foundational instrument in credit risk management.

## Structure

The **protection buyer** pays a periodic premium (the **CDS spread**, quoted in basis points per annum on the notional) to the **protection seller**. In return, if a **credit event** occurs, the seller compensates the buyer for the loss.

- **Premium leg:** The stream of periodic payments from buyer to seller, which ceases upon a credit event or contract maturity
- **Protection leg:** The contingent payment from seller to buyer upon a credit event

## Credit Events

Standard ISDA-defined credit events include:
- **Failure to pay** on debt obligations
- **Bankruptcy** or insolvency
- **Restructuring** of debt terms (haircut, maturity extension)

## Settlement

Upon a credit event, settlement is typically **physical** (buyer delivers defaulted bonds, receives par) or **cash** (seller pays par minus recovery value). The **recovery rate** determines the actual payout: protection payment = Notional x (1 - Recovery Rate).

## CDS Spread and Credit Risk

The CDS spread reflects the market''s assessment of the reference entity''s default probability and expected recovery. A higher spread indicates greater perceived credit risk. The relationship is approximately:

**CDS Spread ≈ PD x LGD** (where LGD = 1 - Recovery Rate)

## Applications

CDS are used to **hedge** credit exposure in loan portfolios, to take **short credit positions** without owning the underlying bond, and as building blocks for synthetic **CDOs** and **index products** such as CDX (North America) and iTraxx (Europe).', 2, 0),
  ('frm-credit-risk-securitisation-m01', 'frm-credit-risk-securitisation', 'Securitisation, CDOs & Structured Credit', '## Securitisation, CDOs & Structured Credit

**Securitisation** is the process of pooling financial assets (mortgages, auto loans, corporate loans) and issuing new securities backed by the cash flows from that pool. It enables the **originate-to-distribute** model, where banks originate loans but transfer the credit risk to capital market investors.

## Waterfall Structure

Cash flows from the asset pool are distributed to investors in a strict **priority waterfall**. Losses, conversely, are absorbed from the bottom up:

| Tranche | Seniority | Risk | Yield |
|---|---|---|---|
| Senior | Highest | Lowest | Lowest |
| Mezzanine | Middle | Moderate | Moderate |
| Equity (first loss) | Lowest | Highest | Highest |

The **senior tranche** is protected by credit enhancement from subordinate tranches and typically achieves a AAA rating even when the underlying assets are sub-investment grade.

## Collateralised Debt Obligations (CDOs)

A **CDO** securitises a pool of debt instruments — bonds, loans, or CDS contracts (synthetic CDO). A **CDO-squared** securitises tranches of other CDOs, creating additional complexity and correlation sensitivity.

## Correlation Risk

**Default correlation** is the central risk driver in CDOs. The value of the equity tranche increases with higher correlation (losses are concentrated, so equity either survives or is wiped out). The senior tranche value falls with higher correlation because the protective buffer of subordinate tranches is more likely to be simultaneously exhausted. Mispricing of correlation was a principal cause of CDO losses during the 2007-2009 financial crisis.

## Risk Retention

Post-crisis regulation (the "skin in the game" rule) requires originators to retain at least **5% of the economic interest** in securitised exposures to align incentives between originators and investors.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-credit-risk-counterparty-credit-risk-m01', 'frm-credit-risk-counterparty-credit-risk', 'Counterparty Credit Risk & CVA', '## Counterparty Credit Risk & CVA

**Counterparty Credit Risk (CCR)** is the risk that a counterparty to a derivative or securities financing transaction will default before the final settlement of the transaction''s cash flows. Unlike loan credit risk, CCR exposure is bilateral and changes with market moves.

## Exposure Metrics

**Current Exposure (CE):** The replacement cost of the derivative at today''s market value (floored at zero, since the bank is not at risk if the contract has negative value to it).

**Potential Future Exposure (PFE):** The maximum exposure at a specified confidence level (e.g., 95%) over a future time horizon, used for credit limit monitoring and capital purposes.

**Expected Exposure (EE):** The probability-weighted average exposure at a future date across all simulation paths.

**Expected Positive Exposure (EPE):** The time-averaged EE over the life of the transaction, used in the Basel regulatory capital calculation for CCR.

## Credit Valuation Adjustment (CVA)

**CVA** is the market value of counterparty credit risk — the difference between the risk-free value of a derivative and its true value accounting for the possibility of counterparty default:

**CVA ≈ LGD x integral of EE(t) x PD(t) dt**

where PD(t) is the marginal default probability at time t (derived from CDS spreads or internal models) and LGD is (1 - Recovery Rate). CVA is a cost charged to trading desks and represents the expected loss from counterparty default.

## Netting and Collateral

**Netting agreements** (ISDA Master Agreement) allow offsetting of positive and negative exposures across transactions with the same counterparty, significantly reducing net exposure. **Collateral agreements** (CSAs) further reduce exposure by requiring periodic posting of variation margin. Both are critical tools for managing CCR.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-credit-risk-regulatory-capital-m01', 'frm-credit-risk-regulatory-capital', 'Credit Risk Regulatory Capital', '## Credit Risk Regulatory Capital

Basel III requires banks to hold minimum capital against credit risk in their banking book. The framework offers two broad approaches: the **Standardised Approach (SA)** and the **Internal Ratings-Based (IRB) Approach**.

## Standardised Approach

Under the SA, **risk weights** are assigned to exposures based on external credit ratings or asset class. For example, AAA-rated corporate exposures attract a 20% risk weight, while unrated corporates receive 100%. **Credit Risk-Weighted Assets (RWA)** are the sum of exposure amounts multiplied by their respective risk weights.

**Minimum capital = 8% x RWA** (under Pillar 1)

## Internal Ratings-Based (IRB) Approach

Under IRB, banks use internal estimates of credit risk parameters:

| Parameter | Definition |
|---|---|
| **PD** | Probability of Default over 1 year |
| **LGD** | Loss Given Default (1 - Recovery Rate) |
| **EAD** | Exposure at Default |
| **M** | Effective Maturity |

Under the **Foundation IRB (F-IRB)**, banks estimate PD internally; regulators supply LGD and EAD. Under **Advanced IRB (A-IRB)**, banks estimate all four parameters. The Basel IRB formula applies a **supervisory correlation** factor and an **adjustment for maturity** to derive a risk weight function grounded in the Vasicek single-factor model.

**RWA = EAD x IRB Risk Weight Function(PD, LGD, M, rho)**

## Output Floor

A key Basel IV constraint is the **output floor**: IRB-derived capital cannot fall below **72.5%** of the SA capital requirement, limiting the capital benefit of internal models and ensuring a minimum level of standardisation across banks. This floor is being phased in through 2028.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-operational-risk-loss-distribution-m01', 'frm-operational-risk-loss-distribution', 'Loss Distribution Approach', 'The **Loss Distribution Approach (LDA)** is the primary statistical method for quantifying operational risk capital under the **Advanced Measurement Approach (AMA)** of Basel II. It models the aggregate annual loss by combining two separate distributions.

## Frequency Distribution
The number of loss events in a period is typically modelled with a **Poisson distribution**, characterised by a single parameter lambda (the expected event count). When variance exceeds the mean, a **negative binomial** distribution is used instead.

## Severity Distribution
The size of each individual loss is modelled independently, most commonly with a **lognormal** or **Pareto** distribution. The lognormal is preferred for its positive support and heavy right tail, which captures the rare but catastrophic losses that dominate operational risk capital.

## Convolution
The aggregate loss distribution is obtained by **convolving** the frequency and severity distributions, usually via **Monte Carlo simulation**:
1. Draw a random event count N from the frequency distribution.
2. Draw N independent loss amounts from the severity distribution.
3. Sum to get aggregate loss; repeat thousands of times.

The result is the full **aggregate loss distribution**, from which the **99.9th percentile** is taken as the regulatory capital charge.

## Data Requirements

| Source | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| Internal loss data | Calibrate severity for own business lines |
| External loss data | Fill tail with industry events |
| Scenario analysis | Capture low-frequency, high-impact events |
| Business environment factors | Adjust for current risk profile |

## AMA Under Basel

Under the **AMA**, banks could use LDA provided the model captured at least four data elements per event. Basel III/IV replaced the AMA with the **Standardised Measurement Approach (SMA)**, reducing model flexibility but improving comparability. Understanding LDA remains central to FRM because it underpins internal stress models.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-operational-risk-key-risk-indicators-m01', 'frm-operational-risk-key-risk-indicators', 'Key Risk Indicators & RCSA', '**Key Risk Indicators (KRIs)** are quantitative metrics that provide early warning of rising operational risk exposure. They complement loss data by capturing the environment before losses materialise.

## Risk Control Self-Assessment (RCSA)
**RCSA** is a structured process in which business units identify their risks, evaluate the effectiveness of existing controls, and rate residual risk. The typical RCSA cycle involves:
1. **Risk identification** workshops with front-line staff.
2. **Inherent risk** scoring (likelihood x impact before controls).
3. **Control effectiveness** assessment.
4. **Residual risk** scoring and action planning.

## Designing Effective KRIs
A good KRI is **measurable, timely, predictive**, and directly linked to a specific risk. Examples include:
- Number of system downtime minutes per month (IT failure risk).
- Staff turnover rate in key control functions (people risk).
- Number of failed trades or settlement breaks (process risk).

## Thresholds and Escalation
KRIs are assigned **green / amber / red** thresholds. When a metric breaches amber, management is notified; red triggers escalation to senior risk committees. Thresholds must be calibrated to historical data and updated periodically.

## Heat Maps

| Likelihood | Low Impact | Medium Impact | High Impact |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| High | Amber | Red | Red |
| Medium | Green | Amber | Red |
| Low | Green | Green | Amber |

**Risk heat maps** visualise the residual risk landscape from RCSA, allowing prioritisation of mitigation resources. When KRI trends and RCSA outcomes are integrated, risk managers gain a forward-looking view that pure loss-history analysis cannot provide.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-operational-risk-cyber-technology-risk-m01', 'frm-operational-risk-cyber-technology-risk', 'Cyber & Technology Risk', '**Cyber risk** is the potential for financial loss, reputational damage, or regulatory penalty arising from the failure, misuse, or deliberate compromise of information technology systems. It is a subcategory of operational risk and one of the fastest-growing concerns for financial institutions.

## Types of Cyber Attacks
- **Phishing / spear-phishing**: Deceptive communications that trick employees into revealing credentials.
- **Ransomware**: Malware that encrypts data and demands payment for decryption keys.
- **Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS)**: Floods systems with traffic to cause outages.
- **Insider threats**: Malicious or negligent acts by employees or contractors.
- **Supply chain attacks**: Compromise of third-party software or vendors to reach the target.

## NIST Cybersecurity Framework
The **NIST CSF** organises cyber risk management into five functions: **Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond, Recover**. Regulators including the Basel Committee endorse this framework for financial institutions.

## Quantification
Cyber risk is difficult to quantify because loss data are sparse and events are correlated. Common approaches include **scenario analysis**, **factor-based models**, and **cyber insurance pricing benchmarks**. The **FAIR (Factor Analysis of Information Risk)** model provides a probabilistic framework.

## Third-Party Risk
Banks rely heavily on cloud providers, payment processors, and software vendors. **Third-party cyber risk** requires due diligence assessments, contractual security requirements, and ongoing monitoring. A breach at a vendor can cascade to the bank even if internal systems are secure.

## Regulatory Expectations
Regulators require boards to oversee cyber risk, institutions to maintain cyber incident response plans, and material incidents to be reported within defined timeframes. Capital allocations for cyber risk increasingly draw on operational risk AMA or scenario-based stress tests.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-operational-risk-business-continuity-m01', 'frm-operational-risk-business-continuity', 'Business Continuity & Resilience', '**Business Continuity Planning (BCP)** is the proactive process of ensuring critical business functions can continue during and after a disruption. It is distinct from but closely related to **Disaster Recovery Planning (DRP)**, which focuses specifically on restoring IT systems and data.

## BCP vs DRP

| Dimension | BCP | DRP |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Scope | All critical business functions | IT systems and data recovery |
| Goal | Sustain operations | Restore technology |
| Ownership | Business units + risk | IT department |

## Key Metrics
- **Recovery Time Objective (RTO)**: The maximum acceptable downtime before a function must be restored.
- **Recovery Point Objective (RPO)**: The maximum tolerable data loss, measured in time (e.g., backups must be no older than 4 hours).

## Crisis Management
An effective crisis management framework includes a **Crisis Management Team (CMT)** with defined roles, pre-approved communication templates, and escalation triggers. The CMT coordinates between business, IT, legal, communications, and regulators during an event.

## Operational Resilience
Regulators now emphasise **operational resilience** over traditional BCP: the ability to prevent, adapt to, respond to, recover from, and learn from disruptions. Key steps include identifying **important business services**, setting **impact tolerances**, and mapping end-to-end dependencies including third parties.

## Testing
BCP and DRP must be tested regularly through:
- **Tabletop exercises**: Discussion-based walkthroughs.
- **Simulation exercises**: Functional rehearsals of response procedures.
- **Full failover tests**: Live switching to backup systems.

Test results must feed back into plan improvements, and lessons learned must be documented and acted upon.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-liquidity-risk-funding-liquidity-m01', 'frm-liquidity-risk-funding-liquidity', 'Funding Liquidity Risk & Metrics', '**Funding liquidity risk** is the risk that a firm cannot meet its cash flow obligations as they fall due without incurring unacceptable cost. Basel III introduced two binding quantitative standards to address the structural and short-term dimensions of this risk.

## Liquidity Coverage Ratio (LCR)
The **LCR** ensures banks hold enough high-quality liquid assets (HQLA) to survive a 30-day stress scenario:

```
LCR = HQLA / Net Cash Outflows (30-day stress) >= 100%
```

**HQLA** are split into Level 1 (cash, central bank reserves, sovereign bonds - no haircut) and Level 2 (corporate bonds, covered bonds - haircuts apply). Net cash outflows apply prescribed run-off rates to deposits and funding lines.

## Net Stable Funding Ratio (NSFR)
The **NSFR** addresses structural funding mismatches over a one-year horizon:

```
NSFR = Available Stable Funding / Required Stable Funding >= 100%
```

Stable funding sources (equity, long-term debt, retail deposits) are weighted more favourably than short-term wholesale funding.

## Intraday Liquidity
Beyond overnight positions, banks must manage **intraday liquidity** - the cash needed to settle payment and securities obligations throughout the trading day. Central bank payment systems create peak demand that can differ substantially from end-of-day positions.

## Central Bank Facilities
In stress, banks access **central bank standing facilities** and **repo operations** as a backstop. The availability and cost of these facilities affect the practical LCR buffer needed. Collateral eligibility rules determine which assets can be pledged.

| Metric | Horizon | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| LCR | 30 days | Short-term stress survival |
| NSFR | 1 year | Structural funding stability |
| Intraday | Same day | Payment system settlement |', 2, 0),
  ('frm-liquidity-risk-market-liquidity-m01', 'frm-liquidity-risk-market-liquidity', 'Market (Asset) Liquidity Risk', '**Market liquidity risk** (also called asset liquidity risk) is the risk that a position cannot be liquidated quickly at or near its fair value. Unlike funding liquidity, which concerns cash flows, market liquidity concerns transaction costs and price impact when trading.

## Bid-Ask Spread
The **bid-ask spread** is the simplest liquidity measure: the difference between the price at which a market-maker will buy (bid) and sell (ask). A wider spread signals lower liquidity. The half-spread represents the immediate cost of executing a trade.

## Market Depth and Price Impact
**Market depth** refers to the volume of orders at each price level. A deep market absorbs large trades with little price movement. The **price impact** (or market impact) of a trade measures how much the execution price deviates from the pre-trade mid price for a given order size.

## Liquidity-Adjusted VaR (LVaR)
Standard VaR assumes positions can be liquidated instantly at mid-market. **Liquidity-adjusted VaR** adds the cost of unwinding positions:

```
LVaR = VaR + Liquidity Cost
Liquidity Cost = (1/2) * Spread * Position Value
```

For large positions, the price impact must also be incorporated, scaling the cost with position size relative to average daily volume.

## Liquidity Risk Premium
Investors demand a **liquidity risk premium** for holding illiquid assets. This premium varies over time and spikes during stress, driving asset prices down and amplifying market dislocations. The **Amihud illiquidity ratio** (absolute return divided by dollar volume) quantifies this.

| Indicator | Measures |
| --- | --- |
| Bid-ask spread | Transaction cost per unit |
| Market depth | Order book resilience |
| Price impact | Cost of large trades |
| Amihud ratio | Return-to-volume illiquidity |', 2, 0),
  ('frm-liquidity-risk-stress-testing-m01', 'frm-liquidity-risk-stress-testing', 'Liquidity Stress Testing', '**Liquidity stress testing** assesses a bank''s ability to survive adverse scenarios that impair its ability to fund itself. It is a core component of the **Internal Liquidity Adequacy Assessment Process (ILAAP)** required by supervisors under Basel III Pillar 2.

## Survival Horizon
The **survival horizon** is the period over which the bank projects it can meet all obligations using available liquid assets, even if wholesale markets are closed and retail deposits run off at stressed rates. Regulators typically require a minimum horizon of 30 days (aligned with LCR) and often longer for ILAAP purposes.

## Stress Scenarios
Liquidity stress tests generally cover three scenario types:

| Scenario | Description |
| --- | --- |
| Idiosyncratic | Bank-specific crisis (rating downgrade, reputational event) |
| Market-wide | System-wide funding freeze (like 2008) |
| Combined | Both occurring simultaneously |

Each scenario applies stressed run-off rates to deposits and drawdown rates to committed credit lines.

## Cash Flow Stress Modelling
Under stress, the bank projects **contractual cash inflows** (loan repayments, securities maturities) versus **stressed cash outflows** (deposit withdrawals, collateral calls, derivatives margin). The **net cumulative outflow** profile reveals when the buffer is exhausted.

## Liquidity Buffer
The **liquidity buffer** (or contingency liquidity reserve) must exceed net stressed outflows throughout the survival horizon. It consists of unencumbered HQLA that can be monetised rapidly without significant haircut even in stressed markets.

## ILAAP and Supervisory Review
Banks submit ILAAP documents to supervisors, who challenge scenario severity and buffer adequacy. Supervisors may impose **Pillar 2 liquidity add-ons** if the internal stress test is deemed insufficient.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-liquidity-risk-alm-m01', 'frm-liquidity-risk-alm', 'Asset-Liability Management', '**Asset-Liability Management (ALM)** is the practice of managing a bank''s balance sheet to control interest rate risk and liquidity risk arising from the mismatch between assets and liabilities. The **ALCO (Asset-Liability Committee)** oversees these decisions.

## Gap Analysis
**Gap analysis** compares the volume of assets and liabilities repricing within defined time buckets. A **positive gap** (more assets than liabilities repricing) benefits from rising rates; a **negative gap** benefits from falling rates.

```
Repricing Gap = Rate-Sensitive Assets - Rate-Sensitive Liabilities
```

## Duration Gap
**Duration gap** extends gap analysis to capture the present-value sensitivity of equity to interest rate changes:

```
Duration Gap = Duration(Assets) - [Liabilities/Assets] * Duration(Liabilities)
```

A positive duration gap means equity value falls when rates rise.

## NII vs EVE
Two complementary metrics dominate ALM:

| Metric | Focus | Horizon |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Net Interest Income (NII) | Earnings impact of rate shift | Short term (1-2 years) |
| Economic Value of Equity (EVE) | Present-value impact on equity | Long term (full balance sheet) |

Regulators under Basel IRRBB require banks to monitor both and disclose EVE sensitivity to a standard 200bp parallel shift.

## Immunisation Strategies
**Immunisation** aims to make portfolio value insensitive to interest rate changes. Techniques include:
- **Duration matching**: Matching asset and liability duration.
- **Cash flow matching**: Aligning cash flows in each period.
- **Interest rate swaps**: Exchanging fixed for floating payments to adjust repricing profiles.

These strategies require rebalancing as rates change and portfolio composition evolves.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-investment-risk-hedge-fund-risk-m01', 'frm-investment-risk-hedge-fund-risk', 'Hedge Fund Risk & Due Diligence', '## Hedge Fund Risk & Due Diligence

Hedge funds employ diverse strategies, each carrying distinct risk profiles that require careful analysis during **due diligence**.

## Strategy Risks

**Long/short equity** funds carry net market exposure risk and stock-selection risk. **Global macro** strategies face event risk from unexpected policy shifts. **Statistical arbitrage** relies on mean-reversion assumptions that can break down during market stress. **Convertible arbitrage** is sensitive to credit spreads and equity volatility simultaneously.

## Liquidity Risk

A critical structural issue is **liquidity mismatch**: funds may hold illiquid assets (private credit, distressed debt) while offering investors quarterly or monthly redemptions. During stress events, redemption gates and **side pockets** may be imposed to segregate illiquid positions, creating uncertainty for investors.

## Leverage Risk

Hedge funds use both **balance sheet leverage** (borrowing) and **synthetic leverage** (derivatives). Prime brokers can increase margin requirements or withdraw financing rapidly, forcing **deleveraging** at adverse prices. Gross leverage ratios for some strategies can exceed 10x.

## Manager & Operational Risk

**Key-person risk** arises when performance depends on one or a few portfolio managers. **Operational risk** encompasses trade errors, valuation conflicts of interest, and inadequate back-office controls. Investors should assess administrator independence, auditor quality, and segregation of duties.

## Due Diligence Framework

| Category | Key Questions |
|---|---|
| Strategy | Is the edge durable? Capacity constraints? |
| Liquidity | Do terms match portfolio liquidity? |
| Leverage | What are gross/net exposures? Prime broker relationships? |
| Operations | Who values positions? Is the fund administrator independent? |
| Track Record | How long? Survivorship bias? |

**Operational due diligence (ODD)** has become a standalone discipline after high-profile fraud cases, focusing on controls, compliance, and infrastructure independent of investment merits.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-investment-risk-pension-risk-m01', 'frm-investment-risk-pension-risk', 'Pension Fund Risk Management', '## Pension Fund Risk Management

Pension funds face a distinctive risk landscape shaped by long-dated liabilities and obligations to beneficiaries.

## Defined Benefit vs. Defined Contribution

In a **defined benefit (DB)** plan, the sponsor guarantees a specific retirement income, bearing all investment and longevity risk. The **funded status** is the ratio of plan assets to the present value of liabilities; a ratio below 100% indicates an underfunded plan requiring additional contributions. In a **defined contribution (DC)** plan, investment risk is transferred entirely to the participant.

## Asset-Liability Management (ALM)

DB plans use **ALM** to align asset duration with liability duration. Liabilities are typically discounted at a rate tied to high-quality corporate bond yields. Rising interest rates reduce both asset values and liability present values, but the **net effect on funded status depends on duration mismatch**. Plans often use **liability-driven investing (LDI)** — holding long-duration bonds, interest rate swaps, or strips — to hedge this mismatch.

## Longevity Risk

**Longevity risk** is the risk that beneficiaries live longer than actuarial assumptions predict, increasing total payouts. Sponsors hedge this via **longevity swaps** or by transferring liabilities to insurance companies through **buy-out or buy-in** transactions.

## Interest Rate Sensitivity

| Duration Gap | Impact of Rising Rates |
|---|---|
| Assets < Liabilities (positive gap) | Funded status improves |
| Assets > Liabilities (negative gap) | Funded status deteriorates |

## Contribution & Regulatory Risk

Underfunded plans face regulatory pressure from bodies such as the Pension Benefit Guaranty Corporation (PBGC) in the US, requiring **minimum funding contributions** that can strain corporate cash flow. **Actuarial assumptions** — discount rate, mortality tables, salary growth — materially affect reported funded status and must be disclosed.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-investment-risk-factor-models-m01', 'frm-investment-risk-factor-models', 'Factor Models & Alpha/Beta Risk', '## Factor Models & Alpha/Beta Risk

Factor models decompose portfolio returns into systematic (factor) exposures and idiosyncratic (alpha) components.

## Fama-French Three-Factor Model

Fama and French extended the CAPM by adding two factors beyond the market:

- **MKT**: Excess market return
- **SMB** (Small Minus Big): Return of small-cap stocks minus large-cap stocks
- **HML** (High Minus Low): Return of high book-to-market stocks minus low book-to-market stocks

This model explains a substantially larger portion of cross-sectional return variation than CAPM alone.

## Carhart Four-Factor Model

Carhart added a **momentum factor (MOM or UMD — Up Minus Down)** to capture the tendency of recent winners to outperform recent losers over 3-12 month horizons. The four-factor model is widely used to benchmark actively managed funds.

## Alpha vs. Beta

**Beta** captures return attributable to systematic factor exposures. **Alpha** is the residual return after accounting for all factor exposures — genuine skill or an unidentified factor exposure. Many apparent alphas in practice reflect compensation for liquidity, leverage, or complexity risks rather than true skill.

## Smart Beta & Factor Investing

**Smart beta** strategies systematically tilt portfolios toward rewarded factors (value, momentum, quality, low volatility) using rules-based approaches. They sit between passive indexing and active management in cost and return profile.

## Risk Decomposition

| Return Component | Source | Measurement |
|---|---|---|
| Market beta | Systematic market risk | Regression beta |
| Factor alpha | Exposure to SMB, HML, MOM | Factor loadings |
| True alpha | Manager skill | Regression intercept |
| Idiosyncratic risk | Stock-specific | Residual variance |

**Information ratio (IR)** — alpha divided by tracking error — measures the consistency of alpha generation and is the primary metric for evaluating active managers.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-investment-risk-esg-m01', 'frm-investment-risk-esg', 'ESG & Sustainable Investing Risk', '## ESG & Sustainable Investing Risk

**ESG** (Environmental, Social, and Governance) factors have become central to investment risk assessment, driven by regulatory pressure, investor demand, and evidence that ESG issues can be material to financial performance.

## ESG Risk Categories

**Environmental risks** include carbon emissions, water usage, biodiversity loss, and exposure to physical climate hazards. **Social risks** cover labor practices, supply chain standards, data privacy, and community relations. **Governance risks** relate to board composition, executive compensation, shareholder rights, and accounting transparency.

## Integration Approaches

| Approach | Description |
|---|---|
| ESG Integration | Incorporate ESG factors into financial analysis alongside traditional metrics |
| Negative Screening | Exclude sectors or companies (tobacco, weapons) |
| Positive Screening | Tilt toward best-in-class ESG performers within each sector |
| Impact Investing | Target measurable environmental/social outcomes alongside financial returns |

## Greenwashing Risk

**Greenwashing** occurs when a fund or company overstates its ESG credentials. This creates **reputational risk** for investors and potential **regulatory liability** as regulators in the EU (SFDR) and US (SEC) tighten disclosure requirements for sustainability claims.

## Regulatory ESG Requirements

The EU **Sustainable Finance Disclosure Regulation (SFDR)** classifies funds as Article 6 (no sustainability integration), Article 8 (promoting ESG characteristics), or Article 9 (sustainable investment objective). The **EU Taxonomy** provides a classification system for environmentally sustainable economic activities.

## Impact Measurement

Measuring ESG outcomes remains challenging due to inconsistent data and methodologies. **Double materiality** — the idea that companies should report both how ESG factors affect them financially and how their activities affect society and the environment — is increasingly demanded by regulators and stakeholders.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-current-issues-climate-risk-m01', 'frm-current-issues-climate-risk', 'Climate Risk & Transition Risk', '## Climate Risk & Transition Risk

Climate risk has emerged as a priority for financial regulators and institutions, with the **Task Force on Climate-related Financial Disclosures (TCFD)** providing the dominant framework for risk identification and disclosure.

## Physical vs. Transition Risk

**Physical risk** arises from climate change itself:
- **Acute physical risk**: extreme weather events (hurricanes, floods, wildfires) causing sudden asset damage
- **Chronic physical risk**: gradual shifts (sea-level rise, temperature increases) eroding asset values over time

**Transition risk** arises from the shift to a low-carbon economy:
- **Policy risk**: carbon taxes, emissions trading schemes, fuel efficiency standards
- **Technology risk**: disruption from renewable energy and clean technology
- **Reputational risk**: changing consumer and investor preferences

## TCFD Framework

The TCFD recommends disclosure across four pillars: **Governance**, **Strategy**, **Risk Management**, and **Metrics & Targets**. Scenario analysis — including a 1.5C, 2C, and business-as-usual scenario — is central to TCFD-aligned reporting.

## Stranded Assets

**Stranded assets** are assets that lose economic value before the end of their expected life due to changes in regulation, market conditions, or technology. Fossil fuel reserves and coal-fired power plants face stranding risk as carbon pricing increases and renewable costs fall.

## Carbon Pricing

**Carbon taxes** set a price per tonne of CO2 directly. **Emissions trading schemes (ETS)** like the EU ETS cap total emissions and allow firms to trade allowances. Rising carbon prices increase operating costs for carbon-intensive industries and affect credit risk.

## Climate Stress Testing

Regulators including the Bank of England and ECB conduct **climate stress tests** to assess bank and insurer resilience to physical and transition risk scenarios over 10-30 year horizons, far longer than traditional stress test windows.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-current-issues-digital-assets-m01', 'frm-current-issues-digital-assets', 'Digital Assets & Crypto Risk', '## Digital Assets & Crypto Risk

Digital assets — including cryptocurrencies, stablecoins, and tokenized assets — introduce novel risk categories that sit outside traditional financial risk frameworks.

## Crypto Market Structure Risks

Crypto markets operate 24/7 with thin liquidity relative to traditional markets, producing **extreme volatility**. **Market manipulation** risks are elevated due to fragmented, lightly regulated exchanges, the prevalence of algorithmic bots, and the absence of circuit breakers.

## DeFi Risks

**Decentralized finance (DeFi)** protocols execute financial transactions via **smart contracts** on blockchains without intermediaries. Key risks include:
- **Smart contract risk**: code vulnerabilities and exploits (hacks resulting in billions of losses)
- **Oracle risk**: manipulation of price feeds that DeFi protocols rely on
- **Governance risk**: token-based governance can be captured by large holders

## Custody Risk

Unlike traditional assets held by regulated custodians, crypto assets require **private key management**. Loss or theft of private keys results in permanent loss. **Exchange custody risk** became prominent after high-profile exchange collapses (e.g., FTX in 2022), where customer assets were commingled with exchange proprietary funds.

## Stablecoin Risks

**Algorithmic stablecoins** maintain their peg through market mechanisms rather than collateral, as demonstrated by the collapse of TerraUSD in 2022. **Fiat-backed stablecoins** carry counterparty risk on the issuer''s reserve management.

## Regulatory Uncertainty

The regulatory landscape for digital assets is fragmented globally. Key questions include asset classification (security vs. commodity), AML/KYC requirements for DeFi, and reserve requirements for stablecoin issuers. Regulatory clarity is a precondition for institutional adoption.

| Risk Type | Example |
|---|---|
| Market risk | Bitcoin volatility |
| Custody risk | Exchange collapse (FTX) |
| Smart contract risk | Protocol exploit |
| Regulatory risk | Asset reclassification |', 2, 0),
  ('frm-current-issues-ai-machine-learning-m01', 'frm-current-issues-ai-machine-learning', 'AI & Machine Learning in Risk Management', '## AI & Machine Learning in Risk Management

AI and machine learning (ML) are transforming risk management, from credit scoring and fraud detection to algorithmic trading and model risk governance.

## Algorithmic Trading Risks

**High-frequency trading (HFT)** and ML-driven strategies can amplify market volatility. Flash crashes — rapid, deep price dislocations — have been associated with algorithmic feedback loops. **Model crowding** occurs when many strategies share similar signals, increasing correlated drawdowns.

## Model Explainability

**Black-box models** — deep neural networks, gradient-boosted trees — often outperform traditional statistical models but lack interpretability. Regulatory guidance (e.g., SR 11-7 in the US, EBA guidelines in the EU) requires **model validation** to include understanding of model mechanics, not just backtested performance. **Explainability techniques** such as SHAP values and LIME help decompose model outputs.

## Data Quality Risks

ML models are highly sensitive to **training data quality**. Key issues include:
- **Survivorship bias**: using only historical data from entities that survived
- **Look-ahead bias**: inadvertently including future information in training
- **Data drift**: the statistical properties of inputs change over time, degrading model performance

## AI Governance

Effective **AI governance** requires model inventories, defined ownership, ongoing performance monitoring, and clear escalation protocols. **Model risk management (MRM)** frameworks must evolve to cover the distinct failure modes of ML models versus traditional statistical models.

## Regulatory Perspective

Regulators are increasingly focused on AI in high-stakes financial decisions. The EU **AI Act** classifies credit scoring and fraud detection as high-risk AI applications subject to strict requirements. Supervisors expect firms to demonstrate that ML models are **fair, explainable, and auditable**.

| ML Risk | Mitigation |
|---|---|
| Overfitting | Out-of-sample testing, regularization |
| Data bias | Data audit, fairness metrics |
| Model drift | Ongoing monitoring, retraining triggers |
| Explainability | SHAP, LIME, simpler surrogate models |', 2, 0),
  ('frm-current-issues-systemic-risk-m01', 'frm-current-issues-systemic-risk', 'Systemic Risk & Macroprudential Policy', '## Systemic Risk & Macroprudential Policy

**Systemic risk** is the risk of a breakdown in the entire financial system rather than failure of individual institutions — a cascade of failures that impairs the real economy.

## Too-Big-To-Fail

**Systemically important financial institutions (SIFIs)** are so large and interconnected that their failure would cause widespread disruption. The implicit government guarantee enjoyed by these institutions creates **moral hazard** — they may take on excessive risk. Post-2008 reforms require SIFIs to hold higher capital buffers (**GSIB surcharge**) and prepare credible **recovery and resolution plans (living wills)**.

## Interconnectedness & Contagion

Financial institutions are linked through interbank lending, derivatives counterparty exposures, payment systems, and common asset holdings. **Contagion** can spread through:
- **Direct linkages**: default of one institution triggers losses at counterparties
- **Indirect linkages**: fire sales of common assets depress prices for all holders

## Macroprudential Tools

| Tool | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Countercyclical Capital Buffer (CCyB) | Build capital in booms to absorb losses in downturns |
| GSIB Surcharge | Additional capital for globally systemic banks (1-3.5%) |
| Loan-to-Value (LTV) caps | Limit mortgage leverage to cool housing bubbles |
| Liquidity Coverage Ratio (LCR) | Ensure banks hold sufficient liquid assets for 30-day stress |
| Systemic Risk Buffer (SRB) | EU-specific buffer for domestic systemic risk |

## FSB Framework

The **Financial Stability Board (FSB)** coordinates global macroprudential policy, designates global SIFIs (G-SIBs, G-SIIs), and monitors emerging systemic vulnerabilities including non-bank financial intermediation (**shadow banking**). Annual G-SIB lists determine which institutions face enhanced supervision and capital requirements.

**Procyclicality** — the tendency of financial system behavior to amplify economic cycles — is a central concern; macroprudential policy aims to counteract this amplification.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-market-risk-risk-factor-mapping-m01', 'frm-market-risk-risk-factor-mapping', 'Mapping Portfolios to Risk Factors', '**Risk mapping** decomposes positions into a manageable set of standardized **risk factors** so VaR can be computed across a large portfolio.

## Approaches for fixed income
- **Principal (notional) mapping**: map a bond to a single zero at its maturity — crude.
- **Duration mapping**: map to the zero whose maturity matches the bond''s **duration**.
- **Cash-flow mapping**: split each cash flow onto adjacent standard vertices, **preserving market value and risk (variance)** — the most accurate.

## Other instruments
- **Forwards/FX forwards**: decompose into spot, foreign bill, and domestic bill positions.
- **Options**: map via **delta** (linear/delta-normal) plus **gamma/vega** for non-linearity; delta-normal VaR understates risk for options.

## Why map?
- Reduces dimensionality, lets you build a **factor covariance matrix**, and isolates **diversified vs undiversified** (component) VaR.
- Trade-off: mapping introduces **basis/specific risk** not captured by the chosen factors.', 2, 5),
  ('frm-credit-risk-merton-model-m01', 'frm-credit-risk-merton-model', 'Merton Model & Distance to Default', 'The **Merton (structural) model** treats a firm''s **equity as a call option on its assets**, struck at the face value of debt (K), maturing when the debt is due.

- **Default** occurs if asset value V_T < K at maturity. Equity holders exercise (repay debt) only if assets exceed debt.
- Equity value = call on assets; **risky debt = risk-free debt − a put** on the assets (the put is the value of the default option to shareholders).

## Distance to default (DD)
**DD = [ln(V₀/K) + (μ − ½σ²)T] / (σ√T)**

- Higher DD ⇒ lower default probability. In the model, **PD = N(−DD)**.
- Drivers: more **leverage** (K↑) and higher **asset volatility** (σ↑) raise PD; more equity cushion lowers it.

## Strengths & limits
- Strength: links default to **market-based** firm value and volatility (basis for **KMV/Moody''s EDF**).
- Limits: assets aren''t directly observable; assumes default only at maturity and a simple capital structure; underestimates short-horizon spreads.', 2, 5),
  ('frm-operational-risk-scenario-analysis-op-m01', 'frm-operational-risk-scenario-analysis-op', 'External Loss Data & Scenario Analysis', 'Operational risk capital (AMA-style) blends **four elements**: internal loss data, **external loss data**, **scenario analysis**, and business-environment/internal-control factors (BEICFs).

## External loss data
- Sources: **consortium** data (e.g. ORX, member banks pool losses) and **public/vendor** databases (news-based).
- Vital for the **tail** — rare, severe events a single firm hasn''t experienced.
- Must be **scaled** to the firm''s size/business mix and corrected for **reporting bias** (public data over-weights very large, public losses; thresholds truncate small losses).

## Scenario analysis
- Structured **expert workshops** estimate frequency and severity of plausible severe events (e.g. rogue trader, major cyber breach).
- Strength: forward-looking, captures risks **absent from history**.
- Weakness: **subjective**, prone to behavioral biases (anchoring, availability); needs governance and challenge.

## Combining
Fit a **loss distribution** (frequency × severity), use internal+external data for the body and scenarios for the tail, then derive op-risk capital at a high confidence level (e.g. 99.9%).', 2, 5),
  ('frm-liquidity-risk-lvar-m01', 'frm-liquidity-risk-lvar', 'Liquidity-Adjusted VaR (LVaR)', 'Standard VaR ignores the cost of **liquidating** a position. **Liquidity-adjusted VaR (LVaR)** adds that cost.

## Bid-ask (exogenous) spread approach
**LVaR = VaR + ½ × P × spread**, where spread = (ask − bid)/mid and P is the position value. The ½ converts the round-trip spread into a one-way liquidation cost. With **uncertain spreads**, add a multiple of the spread''s volatility: ½·P·(μ_spread + k·σ_spread).

## Endogenous (price-impact) liquidity
Large positions move the price. **Optimal execution** spreads trading over time, trading off:
- **Market-impact cost** (trade fast) vs **price-risk cost** (trade slow, more exposure).
- The **liquidation horizon** lengthens VaR: a longer holding period scales VaR by ≈ √(horizon).

## Key ideas
- Liquidity risk has **two parts**: bid-ask (transaction cost) and market impact (depth).
- Liquidity dries up in stress — spreads widen exactly when VaR is largest, so LVaR add-ons should be **stressed**.', 2, 5),
  ('frm-investment-risk-performance-measurement-m01', 'frm-investment-risk-performance-measurement', 'Performance Measurement: Sharpe, Sortino & Information Ratio', 'Risk-adjusted performance ratios standardize return per unit of risk.

| Ratio | Numerator | Denominator (risk) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Sharpe** | R_p − R_f | Total volatility σ_p |
| **Sortino** | R_p − R_target | **Downside** deviation |
| **Treynor** | R_p − R_f | **Beta** (systematic risk) |
| **Information ratio** | R_p − R_benchmark (active return) | **Tracking error** |

## Notes
- **Sharpe** uses total risk — best for an investor''s whole portfolio. **Treynor** uses beta — best for a well-diversified sub-portfolio.
- **Sortino** penalizes only downside volatility, fitting asymmetric/loss-averse mandates.
- **Information ratio** measures skill at generating active return per unit of active risk; **IR = IC × √breadth** (fundamental law of active management).
- **Jensen''s alpha** = R_p − [R_f + β(R_m − R_f)]: return above CAPM-required.
- Sharpe and IR are **not** robust when returns are highly non-normal (option-like payoffs) — they ignore skew/kurtosis.', 2, 5),
  ('frm-current-issues-libor-transition-m01', 'frm-current-issues-libor-transition', 'Reference Rate Reform: LIBOR to SOFR', '**LIBOR** (London Interbank Offered Rate) was a **survey-based**, unsecured term rate. After manipulation scandals and the collapse of underlying interbank lending, it was **discontinued** (USD LIBOR ceased mid-2023).

## Replacement: risk-free rates (RFRs)
- USD: **SOFR** (Secured Overnight Financing Rate) — based on actual **overnight Treasury repo** transactions; others: SONIA (GBP), €STR (EUR), TONA (JPY).
- RFRs are **secured/near-risk-free, overnight, backward-looking, transaction-based** — versus LIBOR''s unsecured, term, forward-looking, judgement-based nature.

## Key differences & challenges
- LIBOR embedded a **bank credit/term premium**; SOFR does not — so a **credit-spread adjustment** is added for legacy contracts (ISDA fallbacks).
- Overnight RFRs are **compounded in arrears** to make a term rate, so the period rate is known only near the end — operational change for loans.
- **Fallback language** and repapering of trillions in legacy contracts created **legal/basis risk**; term SOFR (forward-looking) emerged for some cash products.

> Theme: move from judgement-based to **transaction-based**, more robust benchmarks.', 2, 5),
  ('frm-market-risk-fixed-income-var-m01', 'frm-market-risk-fixed-income-var', 'Fixed-Income VaR & Rate Risk', 'Estimating **VaR for bond portfolios** requires mapping to interest-rate risk factors.

## Approaches
- **Duration-based VaR**: VaR ≈ portfolio value × modified duration × Δy(at chosen confidence).
- **Cash-flow mapping**: decompose bonds into **zero-coupon** exposures at standard maturities (vertices), then apply the covariance matrix.
- **Full revaluation** reprices under yield-curve scenarios.

## Curve risk
- Parallel shifts are captured by duration; **key-rate durations** and **principal component analysis (PCA)** capture non-parallel (steepening/flattening) moves.
- The first three PCA factors — **level, slope, curvature** — explain most yield-curve variation.

## Considerations
- Convexity adjustments improve large-move estimates.
- Credit spread risk is a separate factor from the risk-free curve.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-market-risk-correlation-basis-m01', 'frm-market-risk-correlation-basis', 'Correlation & Basis Risk', '**Basis risk** is the risk that a hedge and the hedged item do not move together.

## Sources of basis risk
- **Cross-hedging** with an imperfectly correlated instrument.
- Maturity or delivery mismatches between the hedge and exposure.
- The **basis** = spot price − futures price; it varies over time.

## Correlation risk
- Portfolios rely on assumed correlations; **correlations rise toward 1 in crises**, reducing diversification.
- **Correlation trading** (e.g., in tranches) takes explicit views on correlation.

## Managing it
- Choose hedges with high, stable correlation to the exposure.
- Stress-test the hedge under scenarios where correlations break down.

Underestimating basis and correlation risk has caused many hedging failures.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-market-risk-frtb-m01', 'frm-market-risk-frtb', 'FRTB: Fundamental Review of the Trading Book', '**FRTB** is the Basel overhaul of market-risk capital rules after the 2008 crisis.

## Key changes
- Replaces VaR with **Expected Shortfall (ES)** at 97.5% for the internal models approach — ES better captures tail risk and is **coherent** (subadditive).
- A stricter, more risk-sensitive **standardised approach (SA)** that all banks must compute.
- A clearer **boundary between the trading book and banking book** to curb regulatory arbitrage.

## Model approval
- **Internal Models Approach (IMA)** requires **P&L attribution** and **backtesting** tests at the desk level; failing desks revert to the SA.
- **Non-modellable risk factors (NMRFs)** attract add-on capital.

FRTB raises capital sensitivity and comparability across banks.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-credit-risk-credit-scoring-m01', 'frm-credit-risk-credit-scoring', 'Credit Scoring & Rating Models', '**Credit scoring** models estimate the probability a borrower defaults.

## Model types
- **Logistic regression** (scorecards): outputs a default probability; widely used for retail lending.
- **Discriminant analysis** (e.g., **Altman Z-score**) classifies firms as safe or distressed from financial ratios.
- **Machine-learning** models (trees, neural nets) improve accuracy but reduce interpretability.

## Key outputs (Basel)
- **PD** (probability of default), **LGD** (loss given default), **EAD** (exposure at default).
- **Expected loss** = PD × LGD × EAD.

## Validation
- Discriminatory power via the **ROC/AUC** and the **KS statistic**; calibration via observed vs predicted defaults.

Scorecards must be monitored for **population drift** and periodically recalibrated.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-credit-risk-portfolio-models-m01', 'frm-credit-risk-portfolio-models', 'Credit Portfolio Models', 'Credit portfolio models estimate the **loss distribution** of a portfolio, capturing **default correlation**.

## Major frameworks
- **CreditMetrics** (J.P. Morgan): rating-transition based; uses a transition matrix and asset-return correlations to simulate portfolio value changes.
- **KMV / Moody''s**: structural (Merton) approach using **distance to default**.
- **CreditRisk+** (Credit Suisse): actuarial, models default counts with a Poisson-type distribution.

## Key concepts
- **Default correlation** drives the tail of the loss distribution — higher correlation fattens the tail.
- **Unexpected loss** (economic capital) is the difference between a high quantile and the expected loss.
- **Concentration risk** (single-name or sector) raises tail losses.

Diversification reduces unexpected loss but cannot remove systematic credit risk.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-credit-risk-wrong-way-risk-m01', 'frm-credit-risk-wrong-way-risk', 'Wrong-Way Risk & Netting', '**Wrong-way risk** is when exposure to a counterparty **rises** as the counterparty''s credit quality **worsens**.

## Types
- **General wrong-way risk**: exposure and default probability driven by common macro factors.
- **Specific wrong-way risk**: a direct link (e.g., buying protection on a firm from that same firm''s affiliate).

## Mitigants
- **Netting agreements (ISDA)**: offset positive and negative mark-to-markets to a single net exposure.
- **Collateral / margin (CSA)**: reduce exposure; **initial** and **variation** margin.
- **CVA** (credit valuation adjustment) prices counterparty risk; wrong-way risk raises CVA.

## Right-way risk
The opposite — exposure falls as the counterparty deteriorates (favourable).

Netting and collateral dramatically reduce counterparty exposure but must be legally enforceable.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-operational-risk-model-validation-m01', 'frm-operational-risk-model-validation', 'Model Risk & Model Validation', '**Model risk** is the risk of loss from using an incorrect or misused model.

## Sources
- **Incorrect model** (wrong assumptions/specification), **implementation errors**, and **inappropriate use** (applying a model outside its valid domain).

## Validation (SR 11-7 framework)
Three core elements:
1. **Conceptual soundness** — is the theory and design appropriate?
2. **Ongoing monitoring** — benchmarking and process verification.
3. **Outcomes analysis** — **backtesting** predictions against actuals.

## Governance
- **Independent** validation function separate from model developers.
- A **model inventory** and tiering by materiality.
- Address **model limitations** with conservative adjustments or overlays.

Complex, AI-driven models heighten model risk, demanding stronger validation and governance.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-operational-risk-third-party-m01', 'frm-operational-risk-third-party', 'Third-Party & Outsourcing Risk', 'Reliance on **vendors and outsourcing** creates operational risk that remains the firm''s responsibility.

## Key risks
- **Concentration**: many firms depending on one provider (e.g., a cloud host) creates systemic fragility.
- **Fourth-party risk**: a vendor''s own subcontractors.
- **Business continuity**: a vendor failure disrupts the firm.

## Management lifecycle
1. **Due diligence** before onboarding (financials, controls, security).
2. **Contract** with clear SLAs, audit rights and exit clauses.
3. **Ongoing monitoring** of performance and risk.
4. **Exit / substitutability** planning.

## Regulatory focus
Regulators increasingly treat critical third parties (especially cloud) as sources of **systemic operational risk**.

You can outsource the activity but not the accountability.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-operational-risk-conduct-culture-m01', 'frm-operational-risk-conduct-culture', 'Conduct & Culture Risk', '**Conduct risk** is the risk of harm to customers or markets from inappropriate behaviour; **culture** shapes whether conduct is sound.

## Conduct risk examples
- Mis-selling, market manipulation (e.g., LIBOR/FX rigging), and unfair treatment of customers.
- These caused enormous fines and reputational damage after 2008.

## Risk culture drivers
- **Tone from the top**, incentives, accountability, and effective challenge.
- A strong risk culture rewards **speaking up** and does not punish messengers.

## Measurement & tools
- Surveys, conduct metrics, complaints data, and **behavioural indicators**.
- **Three lines of defence**: business (1st), risk/compliance (2nd), internal audit (3rd).

Culture is hard to measure but is a root cause of many operational and conduct failures.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-liquidity-risk-regulation-m01', 'frm-liquidity-risk-regulation', 'Liquidity Regulation: LCR & NSFR', 'Basel III introduced two quantitative **liquidity standards**.

## Liquidity Coverage Ratio (LCR)
- **LCR = High-Quality Liquid Assets (HQLA) / Net cash outflows over 30 days ≥ 100%.**
- Ensures survival of a **30-day** acute stress scenario.
- HQLA are tiered (Level 1: cash, government bonds; Level 2: high-grade corporates with haircuts).

## Net Stable Funding Ratio (NSFR)
- **NSFR = Available stable funding / Required stable funding ≥ 100%.**
- Promotes **longer-term (1-year)** structural funding stability, reducing reliance on short-term wholesale funding.

## Purpose
- LCR addresses **short-term** resilience; NSFR addresses **structural** funding.
- Both were responses to the funding runs of 2007–08.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-liquidity-risk-intraday-m01', 'frm-liquidity-risk-intraday', 'Intraday Liquidity & Collateral', '**Intraday liquidity** is the funding a bank needs **within the day** to settle payments as they fall due.

## Why it matters
- Banks must meet payment and settlement obligations in real time through systems (RTGS, CLS).
- A shortfall intraday can cause **settlement failures** and cascade to counterparties.

## Monitoring (BCBS tools)
- Daily maximum liquidity usage, available intraday liquidity, total payments, and time-specific obligations.

## Collateral management
- **Collateral** (HQLA) is pledged to central banks and CCPs; efficient **collateral optimisation** allocates the cheapest eligible collateral.
- **Margin calls** (initial and variation) create intraday funding needs, especially in volatile markets.

Managing intraday liquidity and collateral is now a core treasury function.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-liquidity-risk-contingency-funding-m01', 'frm-liquidity-risk-contingency-funding', 'Contingency Funding Planning', 'A **Contingency Funding Plan (CFP)** is a documented playbook for a liquidity crisis.

## Components
- **Early-warning indicators (EWIs)**: rising funding costs, deposit outflows, widening CDS, rating pressure.
- **Stress scenarios**: idiosyncratic (firm-specific), market-wide, and combined.
- **Action plans**: which liquidity sources to tap and in what order (asset sales, secured borrowing, central-bank facilities).
- **Governance**: roles, escalation triggers, and communication.

## Liquidity sources & buffer
- A **liquidity buffer** of HQLA is the first line of defence.
- Diversify funding by **source, tenor and currency** to avoid concentration.

## Testing
The CFP must be **regularly tested** and integrated with recovery/resolution planning.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-investment-risk-portfolio-construction-m01', 'frm-investment-risk-portfolio-construction', 'Portfolio Construction & Optimization', 'Portfolio construction turns forecasts into weights while controlling risk.

## Mean-variance optimization (MVO)
- Maximises expected return for a given risk, tracing the **efficient frontier**.
- Highly sensitive to **input estimation error**, especially expected returns (''error maximisation'').

## Robust techniques
- **Resampling** and **Black–Litterman** blend a market equilibrium prior with investor views for more stable weights.
- **Constraints** (position limits, turnover) reduce extreme allocations.
- **Risk parity** allocates by risk contribution rather than capital.

## Risk control
- Set **tracking-error** budgets and factor exposures.
- Account for **transaction costs** and **liquidity** in the optimisation.

Good construction balances expected alpha against estimation risk and implementation costs.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-investment-risk-attribution-m01', 'frm-investment-risk-attribution', 'Risk Monitoring & Performance Attribution', 'Ongoing **monitoring** and **attribution** explain where risk and return come from.

## Performance attribution
- **Brinson**: splits active return into **allocation**, **selection**, and **interaction**.
- **Factor-based attribution**: decomposes return into factor exposures × factor returns plus specific return.

## Risk monitoring
- Track **ex-ante (predicted)** vs **ex-post (realised)** risk and tracking error.
- Monitor factor exposures, concentrations, and limit breaches.
- **Style drift**: a manager deviating from the stated mandate.

## Metrics
- **Information ratio** (active return / active risk) gauges skill.
- Compare realised risk to the risk budget; investigate large gaps.

Attribution links results back to decisions, supporting accountability and process improvement.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-investment-risk-illiquid-assets-m01', 'frm-investment-risk-illiquid-assets', 'Illiquid Assets & Alternative Risk', 'Illiquid alternatives (private equity, real estate, private credit) require special risk treatment.

## Measurement pitfalls
- **Appraisal-based valuations** smooth returns, understating volatility and correlation and overstating Sharpe ratios.
- **Stale pricing** and infrequent marks distort risk metrics.

## Risk considerations
- **Liquidity risk**: capital is locked up; account for **drawdowns and capital calls**.
- **J-curve**: early negative returns before value is realised.
- Correlations to public markets **rise in stress**, reducing diversification.

## Managing it
- Size illiquid allocations to the investor''s **ability to bear illiquidity**.
- Use **downside measures** (max drawdown, CVaR) rather than Sharpe alone.
- Model **commitment pacing** and liquidity needs across the portfolio.

The **illiquidity premium** is compensation for bearing these risks, not a free lunch.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-current-issues-fintech-m01', 'frm-current-issues-fintech', 'Fintech & Financial Innovation', '**Fintech** applies technology to financial services, reshaping risk.

## Areas of innovation
- **Digital lending / marketplace lending**, **robo-advisers**, **payments**, **insurtech**, and **decentralised finance (DeFi)**.
- **Big data and AI** for credit scoring and fraud detection.

## Risk implications
- **New entrants** and **disintermediation** pressure incumbents.
- **Operational and cyber risk** rise with digital delivery.
- **Regulatory arbitrage** and gaps as innovation outpaces rules; **regtech** helps compliance.
- **Financial inclusion** benefits, but also new consumer-protection and data-privacy risks.

## Supervisory response
- **Regulatory sandboxes** let firms test innovations under supervision.
- Regulators watch for **systemic risk** from concentration in tech platforms.

Fintech creates both efficiency gains and novel risk-management challenges.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-current-issues-operational-resilience-m01', 'frm-current-issues-operational-resilience', 'Cyber & Operational Resilience', '**Operational resilience** is the ability to deliver critical operations through disruption.

## Cyber risk
- Threats: ransomware, data breaches, DDoS, and supply-chain attacks.
- Frameworks: **NIST** (Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond, Recover).
- Rising with digitalisation and third-party dependence.

## Operational resilience approach
- Identify **important business services**, set **impact tolerances** (max tolerable disruption), and map dependencies.
- **Scenario test** severe-but-plausible disruptions and ensure the firm stays within tolerance.

## Key concepts
- **Recovery time objective (RTO)** and **recovery point objective (RPO)**.
- Resilience assumes disruptions **will** happen — the goal is to withstand and recover, not just prevent.

Regulators now treat operational resilience as a supervisory priority alongside financial resilience.', 2, 0),
  ('frm-current-issues-cbdc-m01', 'frm-current-issues-cbdc', 'CBDCs & Payment Innovation', 'A **Central Bank Digital Currency (CBDC)** is a digital form of a nation''s fiat money, issued by the central bank.

## Types
- **Retail CBDC**: for the general public (like digital cash).
- **Wholesale CBDC**: for interbank settlement.

## Potential benefits
- Faster, cheaper payments; financial inclusion; a risk-free digital settlement asset.

## Risks & design issues
- **Disintermediation of banks**: if households move deposits to CBDC, banks lose funding (a structural risk), so designs often include **holding limits**.
- **Bank-run acceleration**: digital flight to a risk-free CBDC in a crisis.
- **Privacy** vs **AML/CFT** trade-offs, and **cyber** and operational resilience of the infrastructure.

## Related innovation
- **Stablecoins** and **fast-payment systems** compete with or complement CBDCs.

CBDCs illustrate how payment innovation reshapes monetary and financial-stability risk.', 2, 0)
