  ('jee-adv-math-area-under-curves-q05', 'jee-adv-math-area-under-curves', 'The value of ∫ from 0 to π of sin x dx is:', '2', '0', '1', 'π', 'A', 'Evaluating [−cos x] from 0 to π gives ∫₀^π sin x dx = 2.', 4),
  ('jee-adv-math-area-under-curves-q06', 'jee-adv-math-area-under-curves', 'To find area, one should first:', 'Sketch the region and find intersection points', 'Differentiate', 'Ignore the limits', 'Guess', 'A', 'Sketching and locating intersections set up the correct integral.', 5),
  ('jee-adv-math-area-under-curves-q07', 'jee-adv-math-area-under-curves', 'The area of a circle x² + y² = a² using integration is:', 'πa²', '2πa', 'πa', 'a²', 'A', 'Integrating gives the standard circle area πa².', 6),
  ('jee-adv-math-area-under-curves-q08', 'jee-adv-math-area-under-curves', 'When it is convenient, area can be found by integrating with respect to:', 'y (∫(right − left) dy)', 'Only x', 'Time', 'The slope', 'A', 'Integrating in y (right minus left) can simplify some regions.', 7),
  ('jee-adv-math-area-under-curves-q09', 'jee-adv-math-area-under-curves', 'The value of ∫ from 0 to 2 of x dx is:', '2', '1', '4', '0', 'A', '∫₀² x dx = [x²/2]₀² = 2.', 8),
  ('jee-adv-math-area-under-curves-q10', 'jee-adv-math-area-under-curves', 'For a region symmetric about the y-axis, the area can be computed as:', '2 × area of one half', 'Zero', 'Half of one side', 'Negative', 'A', 'Symmetry lets you compute one half and double it.', 9),
  ('jee-adv-math-area-under-curves-q11', 'jee-adv-math-area-under-curves', 'The area bounded by y = x² and y = x from 0 to 1 is:', '1/6', '1/2', '1/3', '1', 'A', '∫₀¹ (x − x²) dx = 1/2 − 1/3 = 1/6.', 10),
  ('jee-adv-math-area-under-curves-q12', 'jee-adv-math-area-under-curves', 'The area enclosed by a curve crossing the x-axis is found by:', 'Splitting the integral at the crossing points', 'One integral always', 'Ignoring the sign', 'Differentiating', 'A', 'Split at zeros so each piece''s sign is handled correctly.', 11),
  ('jee-adv-math-statistics-q01', 'jee-adv-math-statistics', 'The mean of n observations is their sum divided by:', 'n', 'n − 1', 'n + 1', 'n²', 'A', 'The arithmetic mean divides the total by the count n.', 0),
  ('jee-adv-math-statistics-q02', 'jee-adv-math-statistics', 'Variance is the square of the:', 'Standard deviation', 'Mean', 'Median', 'Mode', 'A', 'Variance = (standard deviation)².', 1),
  ('jee-adv-math-statistics-q03', 'jee-adv-math-statistics', 'The most frequently occurring value in data is the:', 'Mode', 'Mean', 'Median', 'Range', 'A', 'The mode is the value that appears most often.', 2),
  ('jee-adv-math-statistics-q04', 'jee-adv-math-statistics', 'By De Morgan''s law, ¬(p ∧ q) equals:', '¬p ∨ ¬q', '¬p ∧ ¬q', 'p ∨ q', 'p ∧ ¬q', 'A', 'De Morgan''s law: ¬(p∧q) = ¬p ∨ ¬q.', 3),
  ('jee-adv-math-statistics-q05', 'jee-adv-math-statistics', 'The middle value of an ordered data set is the:', 'Median', 'Mean', 'Mode', 'Range', 'A', 'The median is the central value when data are ordered.', 4),
  ('jee-adv-math-statistics-q06', 'jee-adv-math-statistics', 'The empirical relation is Mode ≈:', '3 Median − 2 Mean', 'Mean + Median', '2 Mean − Median', 'Mean × Median', 'A', 'Empirically, Mode = 3 Median − 2 Mean.', 5),
  ('jee-adv-math-statistics-q07', 'jee-adv-math-statistics', 'The statement equivalent to an implication p → q is its:', 'Contrapositive (¬q → ¬p)', 'Converse', 'Inverse', 'Negation', 'A', 'An implication is logically equivalent to its contrapositive.', 6),
  ('jee-adv-math-statistics-q08', 'jee-adv-math-statistics', 'Adding a constant to every observation ______ the standard deviation.', 'Does not change', 'Doubles', 'Halves', 'Removes', 'A', 'SD is unaffected by adding a constant (only spread matters).', 7),
  ('jee-adv-math-statistics-q09', 'jee-adv-math-statistics', 'Variance equals:', '(Σx²/n) − (mean)²', 'mean²', 'Σx/n', 'range²', 'A', 'Variance = mean of squares minus square of the mean.', 8),
  ('jee-adv-math-statistics-q10', 'jee-adv-math-statistics', 'By De Morgan''s law, ¬(p ∨ q) equals:', '¬p ∧ ¬q', '¬p ∨ ¬q', 'p ∧ q', 'p ∨ ¬q', 'A', 'De Morgan''s law: ¬(p∨q) = ¬p ∧ ¬q.', 9),
  ('jee-adv-math-statistics-q11', 'jee-adv-math-statistics', 'Multiplying each observation by a constant k multiplies the standard deviation by:', '|k|', 'k²', '1', '0', 'A', 'SD scales by |k| under multiplication.', 10),
  ('jee-adv-math-statistics-q12', 'jee-adv-math-statistics', 'A single counterexample is enough to prove a general statement is:', 'False', 'True', 'Undecidable', 'A tautology', 'A', 'One counterexample disproves a universal claim.', 11),
  ('jee-adv-phy-capacitors-q01', 'jee-adv-phy-capacitors', 'A parallel-plate capacitor with a dielectric constant K has capacitance:', 'Kε₀A/d', 'ε₀A/(Kd)', 'ε₀A/d', 'Kd/(ε₀A)', 'A', 'A dielectric multiplies the vacuum capacitance by K: C = Kε₀A/d.', 0),
  ('jee-adv-phy-capacitors-q02', 'jee-adv-phy-capacitors', 'Energy stored in a capacitor of charge Q and capacitance C is:', 'Q²/2C', 'Q²C/2', '2C/Q²', 'QC²', 'A', 'U = ½CV² = ½QV = Q²/2C.', 1),
  ('jee-adv-phy-capacitors-q03', 'jee-adv-phy-capacitors', 'A charged capacitor is disconnected from the battery, then a dielectric is inserted. The stored energy:', 'Decreases', 'Increases', 'Stays the same', 'Becomes zero', 'A', 'At constant Q, C rises so U = Q²/2C falls; the field pulls the slab in.', 2),
  ('jee-adv-phy-capacitors-q04', 'jee-adv-phy-capacitors', 'With the battery still connected, inserting a dielectric makes the charge:', 'Increase', 'Decrease', 'Stay constant', 'Reverse sign', 'A', 'At constant V, Q = CV rises because C increases.', 3),
  ('jee-adv-phy-capacitors-q05', 'jee-adv-phy-capacitors', 'Two capacitors 2 μF and 2 μF in series give:', '1 μF', '4 μF', '2 μF', '0.5 μF', 'A', '1/C = 1/2 + 1/2 = 1, so C = 1 μF.', 4),
  ('jee-adv-phy-capacitors-q06', 'jee-adv-phy-capacitors', 'The electric-field energy density in vacuum is:', '½ε₀E²', 'ε₀E²', '½ε₀E', 'ε₀E/2', 'A', 'Energy per unit volume of an electric field is u = ½ε₀E².', 5),
  ('jee-adv-phy-capacitors-q07', 'jee-adv-phy-capacitors', 'In steady state, the current through a fully charged capacitor is:', 'Zero', 'Maximum', 'V/R', 'Infinite', 'A', 'A fully charged capacitor blocks DC, so steady-state current is zero.', 6),
  ('jee-adv-phy-capacitors-q08', 'jee-adv-phy-capacitors', 'In a balanced capacitor Wheatstone bridge (C₁/C₂ = C₃/C₄), the bridge capacitor:', 'Carries no charge', 'Carries maximum charge', 'Breaks down', 'Doubles its value', 'A', 'A balanced bridge has equal potentials across the bridge element, so no charge.', 7),
  ('jee-adv-phy-capacitors-q09', 'jee-adv-phy-capacitors', 'The time constant of an RC circuit is:', 'RC', 'R/C', 'C/R', '1/(RC)', 'A', 'The charging time constant is τ = RC.', 8),
  ('jee-adv-phy-capacitors-q10', 'jee-adv-phy-capacitors', 'Three 2 μF capacitors in parallel give a total capacitance of:', '6 μF', '2/3 μF', '2 μF', '8 μF', 'A', 'Parallel capacitances add: 2 + 2 + 2 = 6 μF.', 9),
  ('jee-adv-phy-capacitors-q11', 'jee-adv-phy-capacitors', 'If the plate separation d of an isolated charged capacitor is doubled, its capacitance:', 'Halves', 'Doubles', 'Quadruples', 'Is unchanged', 'A', 'C = ε₀A/d is inversely proportional to d, so doubling d halves C.', 10),
  ('jee-adv-phy-capacitors-q12', 'jee-adv-phy-capacitors', 'At constant voltage, doubling the capacitance changes the stored energy by a factor of:', '2', '4', '½', '1', 'A', 'U = ½CV²; at fixed V, doubling C doubles the energy.', 11),
  ('jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-waves-q01', 'jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-waves', 'In an electromagnetic wave, the E and B fields are:', 'Mutually perpendicular and in phase', 'Parallel to each other', '90° out of phase', 'Both along the direction of travel', 'A', 'E and B are perpendicular to each other and oscillate in phase.', 0),
  ('jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-waves-q02', 'jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-waves', 'The speed of EM waves in vacuum is given by:', '1/√(μ₀ε₀)', '√(μ₀ε₀)', 'μ₀ε₀', '1/(μ₀ε₀)', 'A', 'c = 1/√(μ₀ε₀) ≈ 3 × 10⁸ m/s.', 1),
  ('jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-waves-q03', 'jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-waves', 'The ratio E₀/B₀ of the field amplitudes in an EM wave equals:', 'c', '1/c', 'c²', 'μ₀', 'A', 'For an EM wave E₀ = cB₀, so E₀/B₀ = c.', 2),
  ('jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-waves-q04', 'jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-waves', 'EM waves are:', 'Transverse', 'Longitudinal', 'Neither', 'Both simultaneously', 'A', 'Electromagnetic waves are transverse — fields oscillate perpendicular to propagation.', 3),
  ('jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-waves-q05', 'jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-waves', 'Which has the highest frequency?', 'Gamma rays', 'Microwaves', 'Infrared', 'Radio waves', 'A', 'Gamma rays sit at the high-frequency end of the EM spectrum.', 4),
  ('jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-waves-q06', 'jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-waves', 'EM waves carry momentum p related to energy U by:', 'p = U/c', 'p = Uc', 'p = U/c²', 'p = Uc²', 'A', 'The momentum of radiation is p = U/c, giving radiation pressure.', 5),
  ('jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-waves-q07', 'jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-waves', 'The displacement current introduced by Maxwell is proportional to:', 'dΦ_E/dt', 'dΦ_B/dt', 'The conduction current only', 'The magnetic flux', 'A', 'Displacement current I_d = ε₀ dΦ_E/dt, the rate of change of electric flux.', 6),
  ('jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-waves-q08', 'jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-waves', 'In an EM wave the wave travels along the direction of:', 'E × B', 'B × E', 'E only', 'B only', 'A', 'The Poynting vector E × B gives the propagation direction.', 7),
  ('jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-waves-q09', 'jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-waves', 'In an EM wave, the energy is:', 'Shared equally by E and B fields', 'Only in the E field', 'Only in the B field', 'Zero on average', 'A', 'The electric and magnetic fields carry equal average energy densities.', 8),
  ('jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-waves-q10', 'jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-waves', 'Visible light lies between which two regions of the spectrum?', 'Infrared and ultraviolet', 'Radio and microwave', 'X-rays and gamma rays', 'Microwave and infrared', 'A', 'Visible light falls between infrared (lower f) and ultraviolet (higher f).', 9),
  ('jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-waves-q11', 'jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-waves', 'In a medium of refractive index n, the speed of an EM wave is:', 'c/n', 'cn', 'c', 'n/c', 'A', 'The wave slows to v = c/n in a medium of index n.', 10),
  ('jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-waves-q12', 'jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-waves', 'Radiation pressure is exerted because EM waves carry:', 'Momentum', 'Mass', 'Charge', 'Temperature', 'A', 'EM waves transport momentum, which produces radiation pressure on absorption.', 11),
  ('jee-adv-phy-nuclear-physics-q01', 'jee-adv-phy-nuclear-physics', 'The energy equivalent of 1 atomic mass unit is approximately:', '931.5 MeV', '13.6 eV', '1 MeV', '511 keV', 'A', '1 u corresponds to about 931.5 MeV via E = mc².', 0),
  ('jee-adv-phy-nuclear-physics-q02', 'jee-adv-phy-nuclear-physics', 'Binding energy per nucleon is maximum near which nucleus?', 'Iron (A ≈ 56)', 'Hydrogen', 'Uranium', 'Helium', 'A', 'The binding-energy-per-nucleon curve peaks near iron (A ≈ 56).', 1),
  ('jee-adv-phy-nuclear-physics-q03', 'jee-adv-phy-nuclear-physics', 'The radioactive decay law is:', 'N = N₀e^(−λt)', 'N = N₀e^(λt)', 'N = N₀λt', 'N = N₀/t', 'A', 'Nuclei decay exponentially: N = N₀e^(−λt).', 2),
  ('jee-adv-phy-nuclear-physics-q04', 'jee-adv-phy-nuclear-physics', 'The half-life T½ in terms of the decay constant λ is:', '0.693/λ', 'λ/0.693', '1/λ', 'λ²', 'A', 'T½ = ln2/λ = 0.693/λ.', 3),
  ('jee-adv-phy-nuclear-physics-q05', 'jee-adv-phy-nuclear-physics', 'In alpha decay, the mass number A of the nucleus changes by:', '−4', '−2', '+1', '0', 'A', 'Emitting a ⁴₂He nucleus reduces A by 4 (and Z by 2).', 4),
  ('jee-adv-phy-nuclear-physics-q06', 'jee-adv-phy-nuclear-physics', 'In β⁻ decay, the atomic number Z:', 'Increases by 1', 'Decreases by 1', 'Decreases by 2', 'Stays the same', 'A', 'A neutron becomes a proton, so Z increases by 1 (A unchanged).', 5),
  ('jee-adv-phy-nuclear-physics-q07', 'jee-adv-phy-nuclear-physics', 'After 3 half-lives, the fraction of the original sample remaining is:', '1/8', '1/3', '1/6', '3/4', 'A', '(½)³ = 1/8 of the nuclei remain after three half-lives.', 6),
  ('jee-adv-phy-nuclear-physics-q08', 'jee-adv-phy-nuclear-physics', 'Energy is released in nuclear fusion because light nuclei have:', 'Lower binding energy per nucleon than the product', 'Higher binding energy per nucleon', 'Zero binding energy', 'No mass defect', 'A', 'Fusing light nuclei raises binding energy per nucleon, releasing energy.', 7),
  ('jee-adv-phy-nuclear-physics-q09', 'jee-adv-phy-nuclear-physics', 'Activity A of a sample is related to N by:', 'A = λN', 'A = N/λ', 'A = λ/N', 'A = λ²N', 'A', 'Activity is the decay rate A = λN.', 8),
  ('jee-adv-phy-nuclear-physics-q10', 'jee-adv-phy-nuclear-physics', 'Gamma emission changes the nucleus''s:', 'Neither Z nor A', 'Z only', 'A only', 'Both Z and A', 'A', 'A gamma photon carries away energy but not nucleons, so Z and A are unchanged.', 9),
  ('jee-adv-phy-nuclear-physics-q11', 'jee-adv-phy-nuclear-physics', 'The mean life τ of a radioactive nucleus equals:', '1/λ', 'λ', '0.693/λ', 'λ/2', 'A', 'Mean life τ = 1/λ, longer than the half-life.', 10),
  ('jee-adv-phy-nuclear-physics-q12', 'jee-adv-phy-nuclear-physics', 'The mass defect Δm of a nucleus is converted to:', 'Binding energy', 'Kinetic energy of the nucleus', 'Charge', 'Angular momentum', 'A', 'The mass defect appears as the nuclear binding energy E = Δm·c².', 11),
  ('jee-adv-phy-experimental-physics-q01', 'jee-adv-phy-experimental-physics', 'The least count of a screw gauge is:', 'Pitch / number of circular-scale divisions', 'Pitch × divisions', '1 main-scale division', 'Divisions / pitch', 'A', 'Screw-gauge least count = pitch ÷ head-scale divisions.', 0),
  ('jee-adv-phy-experimental-physics-q02', 'jee-adv-phy-experimental-physics', 'When quantities are multiplied or divided, the errors that add are the:', 'Relative (fractional) errors', 'Absolute errors', 'Squared errors', 'Zero errors', 'A', 'For products and quotients, fractional errors add.', 1),
  ('jee-adv-phy-experimental-physics-q03', 'jee-adv-phy-experimental-physics', 'For Z = A²B/C, the maximum fractional error in Z is:', '2(ΔA/A) + (ΔB/B) + (ΔC/C)', '(ΔA/A) + (ΔB/B) + (ΔC/C)', '2(ΔA/A) − (ΔB/B)', '(ΔA/A)²', 'A', 'Powers multiply their fractional errors: A² contributes 2(ΔA/A).', 2),
  ('jee-adv-phy-experimental-physics-q04', 'jee-adv-phy-experimental-physics', 'In a metre-bridge experiment, error is minimised when the balance point is near the:', 'Middle of the wire', 'Left end', 'Right end', 'Galvanometer', 'A', 'Sensitivity is greatest and error least near the wire''s midpoint.', 3),
  ('jee-adv-phy-experimental-physics-q05', 'jee-adv-phy-experimental-physics', 'For g from a pendulum, g = 4π²L/T². The fractional error in g includes the T term as:', '2(ΔT/T)', '(ΔT/T)', '½(ΔT/T)', '4(ΔT/T)', 'A', 'Since T is squared, it contributes 2(ΔT/T) to the fractional error.', 4),
  ('jee-adv-phy-experimental-physics-q06', 'jee-adv-phy-experimental-physics', 'A potentiometer measures EMF accurately because at balance it draws:', 'No current from the cell', 'Maximum current', 'A constant 1 A', 'Alternating current', 'A', 'At the null point no current flows, so the true EMF is measured.', 5),
  ('jee-adv-phy-experimental-physics-q07', 'jee-adv-phy-experimental-physics', 'The reading 0.00450 has how many significant figures?', '3', '5', '2', '6', 'A', 'Leading zeros don''t count; 4, 5 and the trailing 0 give 3 sig figs.', 6),
  ('jee-adv-phy-experimental-physics-q08', 'jee-adv-phy-experimental-physics', 'For a sum or difference of quantities, the errors that add are the:', 'Absolute errors', 'Relative errors', 'Percentage errors', 'Fractional errors', 'A', 'Absolute errors add for sums and differences.', 7),
  ('jee-adv-phy-experimental-physics-q09', 'jee-adv-phy-experimental-physics', 'Zero error of a screw gauge must be:', 'Algebraically subtracted from each reading', 'Ignored', 'Squared', 'Added twice', 'A', 'The systematic zero error is subtracted (with sign) from every reading.', 8),
  ('jee-adv-phy-experimental-physics-q10', 'jee-adv-phy-experimental-physics', 'Random errors are reduced by:', 'Averaging repeated measurements', 'Using a biased scale', 'Reading only once', 'Increasing zero error', 'A', 'Averaging many readings cancels random fluctuations.', 9),
  ('jee-adv-phy-experimental-physics-q11', 'jee-adv-phy-experimental-physics', 'A vernier calliper typically has a least count of:', '0.1 mm', '1 mm', '0.5 mm', '1 cm', 'A', 'A standard vernier calliper resolves to 0.1 mm.', 10),
  ('jee-adv-phy-experimental-physics-q12', 'jee-adv-phy-experimental-physics', 'A calculated result should be quoted to the:', 'Least number of significant figures among the data', 'Most decimal places possible', 'Nearest whole number always', 'Twelve significant figures', 'A', 'Precision is limited by the least precise measurement used.', 11),
  ('jee-adv-chem-metallurgy-q01', 'jee-adv-chem-metallurgy', 'Froth flotation is used to concentrate which type of ore?', 'Sulphide ores', 'Oxide ores', 'Carbonate ores', 'Chloride ores', 'A', 'Froth flotation concentrates sulphide ores using their preference for oil.', 0),
  ('jee-adv-chem-metallurgy-q02', 'jee-adv-chem-metallurgy', 'Highly reactive metals such as sodium and aluminium are extracted by:', 'Electrolysis', 'Carbon reduction', 'Roasting', 'Froth flotation', 'A', 'Reactive metals are obtained by electrolysis of their molten salts/oxides.', 1),
  ('jee-adv-chem-metallurgy-q03', 'jee-adv-chem-metallurgy', 'The Ellingham diagram plots:', 'ΔG° of oxide formation vs temperature', 'ΔH vs pressure', 'Ksp vs temperature', 'EMF vs concentration', 'A', 'Ellingham diagrams plot standard free energy of oxide formation against T.', 2),
  ('jee-adv-chem-metallurgy-q04', 'jee-adv-chem-metallurgy', 'The thermite (aluminothermy) process is used to extract:', 'Chromium and manganese', 'Sodium', 'Aluminium', 'Copper', 'A', 'Aluminium reduces Cr₂O₃ and MnO₂ in the highly exothermic thermite reaction.', 3),
  ('jee-adv-chem-metallurgy-q05', 'jee-adv-chem-metallurgy', 'Bauxite is purified in the Bayer process by leaching with:', 'NaOH solution', 'Dilute HCl', 'Cyanide solution', 'Water only', 'A', 'Bauxite dissolves in hot NaOH, separating from insoluble impurities.', 4),
  ('jee-adv-chem-metallurgy-q06', 'jee-adv-chem-metallurgy', 'Ultrapure silicon and germanium for semiconductors are obtained by:', 'Zone refining', 'Electrolysis', 'Froth flotation', 'Roasting', 'A', 'Zone refining exploits impurities'' preference for the molten zone.', 5),
  ('jee-adv-chem-metallurgy-q07', 'jee-adv-chem-metallurgy', 'Converting a sulphide ore to its oxide by heating in air is called:', 'Roasting', 'Calcination', 'Smelting', 'Leaching', 'A', 'Roasting heats sulphide ores in air to form the oxide (releasing SO₂).', 6),
  ('jee-adv-chem-metallurgy-q08', 'jee-adv-chem-metallurgy', 'Calcination is the heating of an ore:', 'In limited air to expel volatile matter', 'In excess oxygen', 'With carbon', 'In water', 'A', 'Calcination heats carbonate/hydrated ores in limited air to drive off CO₂/H₂O.', 7),
  ('jee-adv-chem-metallurgy-q09', 'jee-adv-chem-metallurgy', 'Gold and silver are extracted from their ores by leaching with:', 'Sodium cyanide solution', 'Sodium hydroxide', 'Sulphuric acid', 'Ammonia', 'A', 'Cyanide leaching forms soluble complexes of Au and Ag (MacArthur–Forrest).', 8),
  ('jee-adv-chem-metallurgy-q10', 'jee-adv-chem-metallurgy', 'In the Ellingham diagram, a metal can reduce an oxide whose line lies:', 'Above its own line', 'Below its own line', 'Parallel to its line', 'At the same point', 'A', 'A metal reduces oxides whose formation lines lie above (less negative) its own.', 9),
  ('jee-adv-chem-metallurgy-q11', 'jee-adv-chem-metallurgy', 'Nickel is purified by the Mond process, which involves its volatile:', 'Carbonyl complex', 'Chloride', 'Sulphide', 'Oxide', 'A', 'Ni forms volatile Ni(CO)₄, which decomposes to give pure nickel.', 10),
  ('jee-adv-chem-metallurgy-q12', 'jee-adv-chem-metallurgy', 'The reduction of a metal oxide by carbon in a furnace is called:', 'Smelting', 'Leaching', 'Flotation', 'Calcination', 'A', 'Smelting reduces oxide ore with carbon (coke) at high temperature.', 11),
  ('jee-adv-chem-qualitative-analysis-q01', 'jee-adv-chem-qualitative-analysis', 'Group I cations in qualitative analysis are precipitated by:', 'Dilute HCl', 'H₂S in acidic medium', 'NH₄OH', '(NH₄)₂CO₃', 'A', 'Group I (e.g. Pb²⁺) precipitates as chlorides with dilute HCl.', 0),
  ('jee-adv-chem-qualitative-analysis-q02', 'jee-adv-chem-qualitative-analysis', 'Group II sulphides are precipitated using H₂S in the presence of:', 'Dilute HCl (low S²⁻ concentration)', 'NH₄OH', 'Excess NaOH', 'Concentrated HNO₃', 'A', 'Dilute HCl suppresses S²⁻ via the common-ion effect so only low-Ksp sulphides precipitate.', 1),
  ('jee-adv-chem-qualitative-analysis-q03', 'jee-adv-chem-qualitative-analysis', 'The separation of Group II from Group IV cations relies on the:', 'Common-ion effect controlling [S²⁻]', 'Flame test', 'Density difference', 'Magnetic property', 'A', 'Adjusting [S²⁻] via acidity selectively precipitates group II then group IV.', 2),
  ('jee-adv-chem-qualitative-analysis-q04', 'jee-adv-chem-qualitative-analysis', 'A blood-red colour with potassium thiocyanate confirms:', 'Fe³⁺', 'Cu²⁺', 'Zn²⁺', 'Al³⁺', 'A', 'Fe³⁺ forms the blood-red [Fe(SCN)]²⁺ complex.', 3),
  ('jee-adv-chem-qualitative-analysis-q05', 'jee-adv-chem-qualitative-analysis', 'A deep-blue solution with excess ammonia confirms:', 'Cu²⁺', 'Fe³⁺', 'Ca²⁺', 'Pb²⁺', 'A', 'Cu²⁺ forms the deep-blue [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺ complex.', 4),
  ('jee-adv-chem-qualitative-analysis-q06', 'jee-adv-chem-qualitative-analysis', 'The rosy-red precipitate with dimethylglyoxime (DMG) confirms:', 'Ni²⁺', 'Co²⁺', 'Mn²⁺', 'Zn²⁺', 'A', 'Ni²⁺ gives a characteristic rosy-red complex with DMG.', 5),
  ('jee-adv-chem-qualitative-analysis-q07', 'jee-adv-chem-qualitative-analysis', 'The brown-ring test confirms the presence of:', 'Nitrate ion', 'Sulphate ion', 'Chloride ion', 'Carbonate ion', 'A', 'The brown ring of [Fe(H₂O)₅NO]²⁺ confirms nitrate.', 6),
  ('jee-adv-chem-qualitative-analysis-q08', 'jee-adv-chem-qualitative-analysis', 'The Group III reagent (for Al³⁺, Fe³⁺) is:', 'NH₄Cl with NH₄OH', 'Dilute HCl', 'H₂S in HCl', '(NH₄)₂CO₃', 'A', 'NH₄OH in presence of NH₄Cl precipitates group III hydroxides.', 7),
  ('jee-adv-chem-qualitative-analysis-q09', 'jee-adv-chem-qualitative-analysis', 'A white precipitate with BaCl₂ insoluble in dilute HCl indicates:', 'Sulphate', 'Chloride', 'Nitrate', 'Carbonate', 'A', 'BaSO₄ is white and acid-insoluble, confirming sulphate.', 8),
  ('jee-adv-chem-qualitative-analysis-q10', 'jee-adv-chem-qualitative-analysis', 'Group V cations (Ba²⁺, Sr²⁺, Ca²⁺) are precipitated by:', 'Ammonium carbonate', 'Dilute HCl', 'H₂S', 'NaOH', 'A', '(NH₄)₂CO₃ precipitates the group V carbonates.', 9),
  ('jee-adv-chem-qualitative-analysis-q11', 'jee-adv-chem-qualitative-analysis', 'AgCl precipitate dissolves in ammonia, distinguishing chloride from:', 'Iodide', 'Nitrate', 'Sulphate', 'Carbonate', 'A', 'AgCl dissolves in NH₃ whereas AgI does not, separating Cl⁻ from I⁻.', 10),
  ('jee-adv-chem-qualitative-analysis-q12', 'jee-adv-chem-qualitative-analysis', 'In systematic analysis, the acidic radical refers to the:', 'Anion', 'Cation', 'Metal ion', 'Ligand', 'A', 'The anion is termed the acidic radical; the cation is the basic radical.', 11),
  ('jee-adv-chem-gaseous-state-q01', 'jee-adv-chem-gaseous-state', 'The ideal gas equation is:', 'PV = nRT', 'PV = nRT²', 'P/V = nRT', 'PVT = nR', 'A', 'The ideal gas law relates P, V, n and T by PV = nRT.', 0),
  ('jee-adv-chem-gaseous-state-q02', 'jee-adv-chem-gaseous-state', 'The RMS speed of a gas molecule is:', '√(3RT/M)', '√(2RT/M)', '√(8RT/πM)', '3RT/M', 'A', 'The root-mean-square speed is v_rms = √(3RT/M).', 1),
  ('jee-adv-chem-gaseous-state-q03', 'jee-adv-chem-gaseous-state', 'The correct order of molecular speeds is:', 'v_mp < v_avg < v_rms', 'v_rms < v_avg < v_mp', 'v_avg < v_mp < v_rms', 'All equal', 'A', 'Most probable < average < root-mean-square speed.', 2),
  ('jee-adv-chem-gaseous-state-q04', 'jee-adv-chem-gaseous-state', 'In the van der Waals equation, the constant ''a'' accounts for:', 'Intermolecular attraction', 'Molecular volume', 'Temperature', 'Pressure units', 'A', '''a'' corrects the pressure for intermolecular attractive forces.', 3),
  ('jee-adv-chem-gaseous-state-q05', 'jee-adv-chem-gaseous-state', 'The van der Waals constant ''b'' represents:', 'The finite volume of molecules', 'Attractive forces', 'Kinetic energy', 'The gas constant', 'A', '''b'' is the excluded volume due to the finite size of molecules.', 4),
  ('jee-adv-chem-gaseous-state-q06', 'jee-adv-chem-gaseous-state', 'The compressibility factor Z for an ideal gas is:', '1', '0', 'Greater than 1 always', 'Less than 1 always', 'A', 'Z = PV/nRT = 1 for an ideal gas.', 5),
  ('jee-adv-chem-gaseous-state-q07', 'jee-adv-chem-gaseous-state', 'When attractive forces dominate in a real gas, Z is:', 'Less than 1', 'Greater than 1', 'Exactly 1', 'Negative', 'A', 'Attraction makes the gas more compressible, so Z < 1.', 6),
  ('jee-adv-chem-gaseous-state-q08', 'jee-adv-chem-gaseous-state', 'Above the critical temperature, a gas:', 'Cannot be liquefied by pressure alone', 'Liquefies immediately', 'Becomes a solid', 'Loses all pressure', 'A', 'No amount of pressure liquefies a gas above its critical temperature.', 7),
  ('jee-adv-chem-gaseous-state-q09', 'jee-adv-chem-gaseous-state', 'The average kinetic energy of a gas molecule is proportional to:', 'Absolute temperature', 'Pressure', 'Volume', 'Molar mass', 'A', 'Average KE = (3/2)kT, proportional to absolute temperature only.', 8),
  ('jee-adv-chem-gaseous-state-q10', 'jee-adv-chem-gaseous-state', 'At the Boyle temperature a real gas:', 'Behaves ideally over a range of pressure', 'Cannot exist', 'Has Z = 0', 'Always liquefies', 'A', 'At the Boyle temperature a real gas obeys the ideal law over an appreciable pressure range.', 9),
  ('jee-adv-chem-gaseous-state-q11', 'jee-adv-chem-gaseous-state', 'Which gas will behave most ideally under ordinary conditions?', 'Hydrogen', 'Carbon dioxide', 'Ammonia', 'Sulphur dioxide', 'A', 'Small, weakly-attracting H₂ behaves most ideally; CO₂, NH₃, SO₂ deviate more.', 10),
  ('jee-adv-chem-gaseous-state-q12', 'jee-adv-chem-gaseous-state', 'According to kinetic theory, gas pressure arises from:', 'Molecular collisions with the walls', 'Intermolecular attraction', 'The gas''s weight', 'Chemical bonds', 'A', 'Pressure results from momentum transfer during molecule–wall collisions.', 11),
  ('jee-adv-math-properties-triangles-q01', 'jee-adv-math-properties-triangles', 'The sine rule states a/sin A =', '2R', 'R', 'R/2', '4R', 'A', 'a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C = 2R (circumdiameter).', 0),
  ('jee-adv-math-properties-triangles-q02', 'jee-adv-math-properties-triangles', 'The cosine rule gives cos A =', '(b² + c² − a²)/(2bc)', '(a² + b² − c²)/(2ab)', '(b² + c² + a²)/(2bc)', 'a/(2bc)', 'A', 'cos A = (b² + c² − a²)/(2bc).', 1),
  ('jee-adv-math-properties-triangles-q03', 'jee-adv-math-properties-triangles', 'Heron''s formula for the area uses the semi-perimeter s =', '(a + b + c)/2', 'a + b + c', 'abc/2', '(a + b + c)/3', 'A', 's is half the perimeter; Area = √[s(s−a)(s−b)(s−c)].', 2),
  ('jee-adv-math-properties-triangles-q04', 'jee-adv-math-properties-triangles', 'The circumradius R equals:', 'abc/(4Δ)', 'Δ/s', 'abc/Δ', 's/Δ', 'A', 'R = abc divided by four times the area Δ.', 3),
  ('jee-adv-math-properties-triangles-q05', 'jee-adv-math-properties-triangles', 'The inradius r equals:', 'Δ/s', 's/Δ', 'abc/4', 'Δ·s', 'A', 'The inradius is area over semi-perimeter, r = Δ/s.', 4),
  ('jee-adv-math-properties-triangles-q06', 'jee-adv-math-properties-triangles', 'tan(A/2) equals:', '√[(s−b)(s−c)/(s(s−a))]', '√[s(s−a)/bc]', '√[(s−b)(s−c)/bc]', '(s−a)/s', 'A', 'The half-angle tangent formula is tan(A/2) = √[(s−b)(s−c)/(s(s−a))].', 5),
  ('jee-adv-math-properties-triangles-q07', 'jee-adv-math-properties-triangles', 'For sides 5, 12, 13, the triangle''s area is:', '30', '60', '78', '39', 'A', 'It is right-angled (5² + 12² = 13²), so area = ½·5·12 = 30.', 6),
  ('jee-adv-math-properties-triangles-q08', 'jee-adv-math-properties-triangles', 'For a right triangle, the circumradius equals:', 'Half the hypotenuse', 'The hypotenuse', 'Half a leg', 'The inradius', 'A', 'The hypotenuse is a diameter of the circumcircle, so R = hypotenuse/2.', 7),
  ('jee-adv-math-properties-triangles-q09', 'jee-adv-math-properties-triangles', 'The projection formula gives a =', 'b cos C + c cos B', 'b sin C + c sin B', 'b cos C − c cos B', 'b + c', 'A', 'a = b cos C + c cos B (projection rule).', 8),
  ('jee-adv-math-properties-triangles-q10', 'jee-adv-math-properties-triangles', 'The SSA configuration is known as the:', 'Ambiguous case', 'Right-angle case', 'Equilateral case', 'Degenerate case', 'A', 'SSA can give zero, one, or two triangles — the ambiguous case.', 9),
  ('jee-adv-math-properties-triangles-q11', 'jee-adv-math-properties-triangles', 'The area of a triangle in terms of two sides and the included angle is:', '½ab sin C', '½ab cos C', 'ab sin C', 'ab cos C', 'A', 'Δ = ½ab sin C where C is the included angle.', 10),
  ('jee-adv-math-properties-triangles-q12', 'jee-adv-math-properties-triangles', 'In any triangle, r = 4R·sin(A/2)·sin(B/2)·sin(C/2) relates the inradius to the:', 'Circumradius', 'Semi-perimeter only', 'Longest side', 'Median', 'A', 'This identity links the inradius r to the circumradius R.', 11),
  ('jee-adv-math-mathematical-induction-q01', 'jee-adv-math-mathematical-induction', 'The first step of induction is to verify the:', 'Base case', 'Inductive step', 'Conclusion', 'Converse', 'A', 'You start by verifying the base case, usually P(1).', 0),
  ('jee-adv-math-mathematical-induction-q02', 'jee-adv-math-mathematical-induction', 'In the inductive step, assuming P(k), you prove:', 'P(k+1)', 'P(1)', 'P(k−1)', 'The base case', 'A', 'The inductive step derives P(k+1) from the hypothesis P(k).', 1),
  ('jee-adv-math-mathematical-induction-q03', 'jee-adv-math-mathematical-induction', 'Σ from 1 to n of k² equals:', 'n(n+1)(2n+1)/6', '[n(n+1)/2]²', 'n(n+1)/2', 'n³', 'A', 'The sum of the first n squares is n(n+1)(2n+1)/6.', 2),
  ('jee-adv-math-mathematical-induction-q04', 'jee-adv-math-mathematical-induction', 'Bernoulli''s inequality states that for x > −1 and n ≥ 1:', '(1 + x)ⁿ ≥ 1 + nx', '(1 + x)ⁿ ≤ 1 + nx', '(1 + x)ⁿ = nx', '(1 + x)ⁿ < 0', 'A', 'Bernoulli''s inequality (1 + x)ⁿ ≥ 1 + nx is a classic induction result.', 3),
  ('jee-adv-math-mathematical-induction-q05', 'jee-adv-math-mathematical-induction', 'For all natural n, xⁿ − yⁿ is divisible by:', 'x − y', 'x + y', 'xy', 'n', 'A', 'xⁿ − yⁿ is always divisible by (x − y), provable by induction.', 4),
  ('jee-adv-math-mathematical-induction-q06', 'jee-adv-math-mathematical-induction', 'Strong induction assumes the statement holds for:', 'All values up to k', 'Only k', 'Only the base case', 'No previous values', 'A', 'Strong induction uses P for all values up to k when proving P(k+1).', 5),
  ('jee-adv-math-mathematical-induction-q07', 'jee-adv-math-mathematical-induction', 'The expression 3²ⁿ − 1 is divisible by:', '8', '5', '7', '6', 'A', '3²ⁿ − 1 = 9ⁿ − 1 is divisible by 8 for all natural n.', 6),
  ('jee-adv-math-mathematical-induction-q08', 'jee-adv-math-mathematical-induction', 'Σ from 1 to n of k³ equals:', '[n(n+1)/2]²', 'n(n+1)(2n+1)/6', 'n²(n+1)/2', 'n(n+1)/2', 'A', 'The sum of the first n cubes is the square of the first-power sum.', 7),
  ('jee-adv-math-mathematical-induction-q09', 'jee-adv-math-mathematical-induction', 'Why is the base case indispensable?', 'Without it, false statements could be ''proved''', 'It is decorative', 'It proves the converse', 'It replaces the hypothesis', 'A', 'The inductive step alone, lacking a true base, can propagate falsehoods.', 8),
  ('jee-adv-math-mathematical-induction-q10', 'jee-adv-math-mathematical-induction', 'The assumption P(k) is called the:', 'Inductive hypothesis', 'Axiom', 'Base case', 'Lemma', 'A', 'Assuming P(k) true is the inductive hypothesis.', 9),
  ('jee-adv-math-mathematical-induction-q11', 'jee-adv-math-mathematical-induction', 'For n ≥ 1, 2ⁿ compares with n as:', '2ⁿ > n', '2ⁿ < n', '2ⁿ = n', '2ⁿ = 2n', 'A', '2ⁿ > n for every natural n, provable by induction.', 10),
  ('jee-adv-math-mathematical-induction-q12', 'jee-adv-math-mathematical-induction', 'Induction proves statements indexed by:', 'Natural numbers', 'Real numbers', 'Complex numbers', 'Irrational numbers', 'A', 'PMI applies to statements over the natural numbers.', 11),
  ('jee-adv-math-mean-value-theorems-q01', 'jee-adv-math-mean-value-theorems', 'Rolle''s theorem additionally requires:', 'f(a) = f(b)', 'f(a) = 0', 'f is linear', 'f(a) > f(b)', 'A', 'Rolle''s theorem needs equal endpoint values f(a) = f(b).', 0),
  ('jee-adv-math-mean-value-theorems-q02', 'jee-adv-math-mean-value-theorems', 'Rolle''s theorem guarantees a c in (a,b) with:', 'f′(c) = 0', 'f(c) = 0', 'f″(c) = 0', 'f(c) = c', 'A', 'It guarantees a stationary point f′(c) = 0.', 1),
  ('jee-adv-math-mean-value-theorems-q03', 'jee-adv-math-mean-value-theorems', 'Lagrange''s MVT gives f′(c) =', '[f(b) − f(a)]/(b − a)', 'f(b) − f(a)', '0', '(b − a)/[f(b) − f(a)]', 'A', 'LMVT: the derivative at c equals the average rate of change.', 2),
  ('jee-adv-math-mean-value-theorems-q04', 'jee-adv-math-mean-value-theorems', 'Cauchy''s Mean Value Theorem underlies which rule?', 'L''Hôpital''s rule', 'The chain rule', 'The product rule', 'The binomial theorem', 'A', 'L''Hôpital''s rule follows from Cauchy''s generalised MVT.', 3),
  ('jee-adv-math-mean-value-theorems-q05', 'jee-adv-math-mean-value-theorems', 'Geometrically, LMVT means the tangent at c is parallel to the:', 'Chord joining the endpoints', 'X-axis always', 'Y-axis', 'Normal at b', 'A', 'The tangent at c is parallel to the chord over [a,b].', 4),
  ('jee-adv-math-mean-value-theorems-q06', 'jee-adv-math-mean-value-theorems', 'Between two distinct real roots of a differentiable f, there is at least one root of:', 'f′', 'f″ only', '1/f', 'f itself', 'A', 'By Rolle''s theorem, f′ has a root between two roots of f.', 5),
  ('jee-adv-math-mean-value-theorems-q07', 'jee-adv-math-mean-value-theorems', 'If f′(x) = 0 throughout an interval, then f is:', 'Constant there', 'Increasing', 'Decreasing', 'Discontinuous', 'A', 'A vanishing derivative on an interval means f is constant (a corollary of MVT).', 6),
  ('jee-adv-math-mean-value-theorems-q08', 'jee-adv-math-mean-value-theorems', 'For f(x) = x² on [0, 2], LMVT gives c =', '1', '2', '0', '1.5', 'A', '2c = (4 − 0)/2 = 2, so c = 1.', 7),
  ('jee-adv-math-mean-value-theorems-q09', 'jee-adv-math-mean-value-theorems', 'Rolle''s theorem is the special case of LMVT when:', 'f(a) = f(b)', 'a = b', 'f is constant everywhere', 'f′(a) = 0', 'A', 'With f(a) = f(b) the chord slope is 0, giving Rolle''s f′(c) = 0.', 8),
  ('jee-adv-math-mean-value-theorems-q10', 'jee-adv-math-mean-value-theorems', 'Both theorems require differentiability on:', 'The open interval (a,b)', 'The closed interval [a,b]', 'The endpoints only', 'All of R', 'A', 'Differentiability is required on the open interval (a,b).', 9),
  ('jee-adv-math-mean-value-theorems-q11', 'jee-adv-math-mean-value-theorems', 'The bound |sin x − sin y| ≤ |x − y| follows from:', 'The Mean Value Theorem', 'Rolle''s theorem only', 'The binomial theorem', 'Cauchy''s inequality', 'A', 'Applying LMVT to sine with |cos c| ≤ 1 gives this Lipschitz bound.', 10),
  ('jee-adv-math-mean-value-theorems-q12', 'jee-adv-math-mean-value-theorems', 'For f(x) = x² − 4 on [−2, 2], Rolle''s theorem gives c =', '0', '2', '−2', '1', 'A', 'f(−2) = f(2) = 0 and f′(c) = 2c = 0 gives c = 0.', 11),
  ('jee-adv-phy-rotational-dynamics-q13', 'jee-adv-phy-rotational-dynamics', 'The moment of inertia of a solid sphere about its diameter is:', '(2/5)MR²', '(2/3)MR²', 'MR²', '½MR²', 'A', 'A solid sphere has I = (2/5)MR² about a diameter.', 12),
  ('jee-adv-phy-rotational-dynamics-q14', 'jee-adv-phy-rotational-dynamics', 'For a body rolling without slipping, the fraction of kinetic energy that is rotational for a solid sphere is:', '2/7', '5/7', '1/2', '1/3', 'A', 'Rotational KE fraction = I/(I+MR²) = (2/5)/(7/5) = 2/7.', 13),
  ('jee-adv-phy-rotational-dynamics-q15', 'jee-adv-phy-rotational-dynamics', 'Which body rolls down an incline fastest (smallest I/MR²)?', 'Solid sphere', 'Hollow sphere', 'Ring', 'Hollow cylinder', 'A', 'The solid sphere has the smallest I/MR², so it accelerates fastest.', 14),
  ('jee-adv-phy-rotational-dynamics-q16', 'jee-adv-phy-rotational-dynamics', 'The parallel-axis theorem states that I = I_cm +', 'Md²', 'MR²', '½Md²', 'Md²/2', 'A', 'I = I_cm + Md² for an axis a distance d from the centre of mass.', 15),
  ('jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-induction-q13', 'jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-induction', 'Faraday''s law states that the induced EMF equals the negative rate of change of:', 'Magnetic flux', 'Electric field', 'Charge', 'Current', 'A', 'EMF = −dΦ_B/dt, the rate of change of magnetic flux.', 12),
  ('jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-induction-q14', 'jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-induction', 'Lenz''s law is a consequence of the conservation of:', 'Energy', 'Charge', 'Momentum', 'Mass', 'A', 'The induced current opposes the change, conserving energy.', 13),
  ('jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-induction-q15', 'jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-induction', 'In a series LCR circuit at resonance, the impedance equals:', 'R (a minimum)', 'X_L', 'X_C', 'Zero', 'A', 'At resonance X_L = X_C, so Z = R is minimum and current is maximum.', 14),
  ('jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-induction-q16', 'jee-adv-phy-electromagnetic-induction', 'The energy stored in an inductor carrying current I is:', '½LI²', 'LI²', '½L²I', 'LI', 'A', 'Magnetic energy stored in an inductor is U = ½LI².', 15),
  ('jee-adv-phy-modern-physics-q13', 'jee-adv-phy-modern-physics', 'The maximum kinetic energy of a photoelectron equals hν minus the:', 'Work function', 'Rest energy', 'Binding energy of the nucleus', 'Kinetic energy of the photon', 'A', 'Einstein''s equation: KE_max = hν − φ (the work function).', 12),
  ('jee-adv-phy-modern-physics-q14', 'jee-adv-phy-modern-physics', 'The de Broglie wavelength of a particle of momentum p is:', 'h/p', 'p/h', 'hp', 'h/p²', 'A', 'λ = h/p, the de Broglie relation.', 13),
  ('jee-adv-phy-modern-physics-q15', 'jee-adv-phy-modern-physics', 'The energy of the nth hydrogen level is proportional to:', '−1/n²', '−n²', '−1/n', '−n', 'A', 'The Bohr energy level is Eₙ = −13.6/n² eV, scaling as −1/n².', 14),
  ('jee-adv-phy-modern-physics-q16', 'jee-adv-phy-modern-physics', 'The stopping potential in the photoelectric effect depends on the light''s:', 'Frequency', 'Intensity', 'Polarisation', 'Phase', 'A', 'Stopping potential depends on frequency, not intensity.', 15),
  ('jee-adv-phy-wave-optics-q13', 'jee-adv-phy-wave-optics', 'In Young''s double-slit experiment, the fringe width is proportional to the:', 'Wavelength', 'Slit width', 'Frequency', 'Intensity', 'A', 'Fringe width β = λD/d, proportional to wavelength.', 12),
  ('jee-adv-phy-wave-optics-q14', 'jee-adv-phy-wave-optics', 'Constructive interference occurs when the path difference is:', 'An integer multiple of λ', 'A half-integer multiple of λ', 'λ/4', 'Zero only', 'A', 'Constructive interference needs path difference = nλ.', 13),
  ('jee-adv-phy-wave-optics-q15', 'jee-adv-phy-wave-optics', 'The condition for the first minimum in single-slit diffraction is a sin θ =', 'λ', 'λ/2', '2λ', '0', 'A', 'The first diffraction minimum occurs at a sin θ = λ.', 14),
  ('jee-adv-phy-wave-optics-q16', 'jee-adv-phy-wave-optics', 'Light waves can interfere observably only if the sources are:', 'Coherent', 'Incoherent', 'Of different frequency', 'Unpolarised only', 'A', 'A stable interference pattern requires coherent sources (constant phase difference).', 15),
  ('jee-adv-chem-organic-mechanisms-q13', 'jee-adv-chem-organic-mechanisms', 'An SN1 reaction proceeds through a ____ intermediate.', 'Carbocation', 'Carbanion', 'Free radical', 'Carbene', 'A', 'SN1 forms a carbocation in the rate-determining step.', 12),
  ('jee-adv-chem-organic-mechanisms-q14', 'jee-adv-chem-organic-mechanisms', 'The rate of an SN2 reaction depends on:', 'Both substrate and nucleophile concentrations', 'Only the substrate', 'Only the nucleophile', 'Neither', 'A', 'SN2 is bimolecular — rate depends on both substrate and nucleophile.', 13),
  ('jee-adv-chem-organic-mechanisms-q15', 'jee-adv-chem-organic-mechanisms', 'SN2 reactions proceed with ____ of configuration.', 'Inversion', 'Retention', 'Racemisation', 'No change', 'A', 'The backside attack in SN2 inverts the stereocentre (Walden inversion).', 14),
  ('jee-adv-chem-organic-mechanisms-q16', 'jee-adv-chem-organic-mechanisms', 'Markovnikov''s rule predicts that in HX addition to an alkene, H adds to the carbon with:', 'More hydrogens', 'Fewer hydrogens', 'A halogen', 'A double bond only', 'A', 'H adds to the carbon with more H''s, forming the more stable carbocation.', 15),
  ('jee-adv-chem-thermo-equilibrium-q13', 'jee-adv-chem-thermo-equilibrium', 'For a spontaneous process at constant T and P, the Gibbs free energy change is:', 'Negative', 'Positive', 'Zero', 'Infinite', 'A', 'ΔG < 0 indicates a spontaneous process.', 12),
  ('jee-adv-chem-thermo-equilibrium-q14', 'jee-adv-chem-thermo-equilibrium', 'At equilibrium, ΔG equals:', 'Zero', 'Negative', 'Positive', 'ΔH', 'A', 'At equilibrium the Gibbs free energy change is zero.', 13),
  ('jee-adv-chem-thermo-equilibrium-q15', 'jee-adv-chem-thermo-equilibrium', 'The relationship ΔG° = −RT ln K shows that a large K corresponds to a:', 'Large negative ΔG°', 'Large positive ΔG°', 'Zero ΔG°', 'Zero K only', 'A', 'A large equilibrium constant means a strongly negative standard free-energy change.', 14),
  ('jee-adv-chem-thermo-equilibrium-q16', 'jee-adv-chem-thermo-equilibrium', 'According to Le Chatelier''s principle, increasing pressure shifts an equilibrium toward the side with:', 'Fewer moles of gas', 'More moles of gas', 'More solids', 'Higher temperature', 'A', 'Higher pressure favours the side with fewer gas moles.', 15),
  ('jee-adv-chem-coordination-q13', 'jee-adv-chem-coordination', 'The coordination number of the metal in [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ is:', '6', '3', '4', '2', 'A', 'Six ammonia ligands give a coordination number of 6.', 12),
  ('jee-adv-chem-coordination-q14', 'jee-adv-chem-coordination', 'A strong-field ligand generally produces a ____ complex.', 'Low-spin', 'High-spin', 'Colourless', 'Non-magnetic always', 'A', 'Strong-field ligands cause large splitting, favouring electron pairing (low spin).', 13),
  ('jee-adv-chem-coordination-q15', 'jee-adv-chem-coordination', 'In an octahedral field, the d-orbitals split into t₂g and:', 'e_g', 'a₁g', 't₁u', 's and p', 'A', 'Octahedral crystal field splits d-orbitals into lower t₂g and higher e_g sets.', 14),
  ('jee-adv-chem-coordination-q16', 'jee-adv-chem-coordination', 'EDTA is a ____ ligand.', 'Hexadentate', 'Monodentate', 'Bidentate', 'Ambidentate', 'A', 'EDTA binds through six donor sites, making it hexadentate.', 15),
  ('jee-adv-chem-electrochemistry-q13', 'jee-adv-chem-electrochemistry', 'In a galvanic cell, oxidation occurs at the:', 'Anode', 'Cathode', 'Salt bridge', 'Electrolyte', 'A', 'Oxidation always occurs at the anode; reduction at the cathode.', 12),
  ('jee-adv-chem-electrochemistry-q14', 'jee-adv-chem-electrochemistry', 'The Nernst equation relates cell potential to the:', 'Reaction quotient Q', 'Coupon', 'Bond order', 'Molar mass', 'A', 'E = E° − (RT/nF) ln Q relates potential to the reaction quotient.', 13),
  ('jee-adv-chem-electrochemistry-q15', 'jee-adv-chem-electrochemistry', 'The standard free-energy change relates to the cell potential by ΔG° =', '−nFE°', 'nFE°', '−RT ln E°', 'nF/E°', 'A', 'ΔG° = −nFE°, linking free energy and standard cell potential.', 14),
  ('jee-adv-chem-electrochemistry-q16', 'jee-adv-chem-electrochemistry', 'According to Faraday''s first law, the mass deposited is proportional to the:', 'Charge passed', 'Voltage only', 'Time squared', 'Resistance', 'A', 'Mass deposited is proportional to the total charge (Q = It) passed.', 15),
  ('jee-adv-math-definite-integrals-q13', 'jee-adv-math-definite-integrals', 'The value of ∫₀^(π/2) sin x dx is:', '1', '0', 'π/2', '2', 'A', '[−cos x]₀^(π/2) = 0 − (−1) = 1.', 12),
  ('jee-adv-math-definite-integrals-q14', 'jee-adv-math-definite-integrals', 'The property ∫₀^a f(x) dx = ∫₀^a f(a−x) dx is a:', 'King property of definite integrals', 'Product rule', 'Chain rule', 'Quotient rule', 'A', 'This is the ''king'' reflection property used to simplify definite integrals.', 13),
  ('jee-adv-math-definite-integrals-q15', 'jee-adv-math-definite-integrals', 'For an odd function f, ∫₋a^a f(x) dx equals:', '0', '2∫₀^a f(x) dx', 'a²', '1', 'A', 'The integral of an odd function over a symmetric interval is zero.', 14),
  ('jee-adv-math-definite-integrals-q16', 'jee-adv-math-definite-integrals', 'The area under y = f(x) from a to b is given by:', '∫ₐ^b f(x) dx', 'f(b) − f(a)', 'f''(x)', 'The average of f', 'A', 'The definite integral gives the (signed) area under the curve.', 15),
  ('jee-adv-math-probability-q13', 'jee-adv-math-probability', 'For independent events A and B, P(A ∩ B) equals:', 'P(A)·P(B)', 'P(A) + P(B)', 'P(A) − P(B)', 'P(A)/P(B)', 'A', 'Independent events multiply: P(A ∩ B) = P(A)P(B).', 12),
  ('jee-adv-math-probability-q14', 'jee-adv-math-probability', 'Bayes'' theorem gives P(A|B) = P(B|A)·P(A) /', 'P(B)', 'P(A)', 'P(B|A)', '1', 'A', 'The denominator is the total probability of the evidence, P(B).', 13),
  ('jee-adv-math-probability-q15', 'jee-adv-math-probability', 'For mutually exclusive events, P(A ∪ B) equals:', 'P(A) + P(B)', 'P(A)·P(B)', 'P(A) − P(B)', '0', 'A', 'Mutually exclusive events cannot co-occur, so probabilities add.', 14),
  ('jee-adv-math-probability-q16', 'jee-adv-math-probability', 'The mean (expected value) of a binomial distribution B(n, p) is:', 'np', 'np(1−p)', 'p/n', 'n/p', 'A', 'The mean of a binomial distribution is np.', 15),
  ('jee-adv-math-conics-q13', 'jee-adv-math-conics', 'The eccentricity of a parabola is:', '1', '0', 'Between 0 and 1', 'Greater than 1', 'A', 'A parabola has eccentricity exactly 1.', 12),
  ('jee-adv-math-conics-q14', 'jee-adv-math-conics', 'For the ellipse x²/a² + y²/b² = 1 (a > b), the eccentricity is:', '√(1 − b²/a²)', '√(1 + b²/a²)', 'b/a', 'a/b', 'A', 'e = √(1 − b²/a²) for an ellipse with a > b.', 13),
  ('jee-adv-math-conics-q15', 'jee-adv-math-conics', 'The length of the latus rectum of y² = 4ax is:', '4a', '2a', 'a', '8a', 'A', 'For y² = 4ax the latus rectum has length 4a.', 14),
  ('jee-adv-math-conics-q16', 'jee-adv-math-conics', 'The eccentricity of a hyperbola is:', 'Greater than 1', 'Equal to 1', 'Less than 1', 'Zero', 'A', 'A hyperbola always has eccentricity greater than 1.', 15),
  ('jee-adv-math-complex-numbers-q13', 'jee-adv-math-complex-numbers', 'The modulus of the complex number 3 + 4i is:', '5', '7', '12', '1', 'A', '|3 + 4i| = √(3² + 4²) = √25 = 5.', 12),
  ('jee-adv-math-complex-numbers-q14', 'jee-adv-math-complex-numbers', 'The value of i² is:', '−1', '1', 'i', '0', 'A', 'By definition of the imaginary unit, i² = −1.', 13),
  ('jee-adv-math-complex-numbers-q15', 'jee-adv-math-complex-numbers', 'The sum of the n nth roots of unity is:', '0', '1', 'n', '−1', 'A', 'The n nth roots of unity sum to zero for n > 1.', 14),
  ('jee-adv-math-complex-numbers-q16', 'jee-adv-math-complex-numbers', 'By De Moivre''s theorem, (cos θ + i sin θ)ⁿ equals:', 'cos nθ + i sin nθ', 'cos θ + i sin nθ', 'n cos θ', 'cos(θ/n)', 'A', 'De Moivre''s theorem: (cos θ + i sin θ)ⁿ = cos nθ + i sin nθ.', 15),
  ('jee-adv-phy-com-collisions-q13', 'jee-adv-phy-com-collisions', 'In a perfectly elastic collision, which quantities are conserved?', 'Both momentum and kinetic energy', 'Only momentum', 'Only kinetic energy', 'Neither', 'A', 'Elastic collisions conserve both momentum and kinetic energy.', 12),
  ('jee-adv-phy-com-collisions-q14', 'jee-adv-phy-com-collisions', 'In a perfectly inelastic collision, the colliding bodies:', 'Move together after impact', 'Bounce apart elastically', 'Gain kinetic energy', 'Conserve kinetic energy', 'A', 'In a perfectly inelastic collision the bodies stick and move with a common velocity.', 13),
  ('jee-adv-phy-com-collisions-q15', 'jee-adv-phy-com-collisions', 'The coefficient of restitution for a perfectly elastic collision is:', '1', '0', '0.5', 'Infinite', 'A', 'e = 1 for a perfectly elastic collision; e = 0 for perfectly inelastic.', 14),
  ('jee-adv-phy-com-collisions-q16', 'jee-adv-phy-com-collisions', 'In the absence of external forces, the velocity of the centre of mass:', 'Remains constant', 'Increases', 'Decreases to zero', 'Reverses', 'A', 'With no net external force, the centre of mass moves at constant velocity.', 15),
  ('jee-adv-phy-gauss-law-q13', 'jee-adv-phy-gauss-law', 'Gauss''s law states that the electric flux through a closed surface equals the enclosed charge divided by:', 'ε₀', 'μ₀', '4π', 'c', 'A', 'Φ = q_enclosed/ε₀ by Gauss''s law.', 12),
  ('jee-adv-phy-gauss-law-q14', 'jee-adv-phy-gauss-law', 'The electric field due to an infinite charged plane sheet is:', 'σ/2ε₀ and uniform', 'kQ/r²', 'Zero', 'σ/ε₀', 'A', 'An infinite sheet produces a uniform field of magnitude σ/2ε₀ on each side.', 13),
  ('jee-adv-phy-gauss-law-q15', 'jee-adv-phy-gauss-law', 'The electric field inside a uniformly charged conducting shell is:', 'Zero', 'kQ/r²', 'kQ/R²', 'Uniform and non-zero', 'A', 'Inside a conductor''s cavity with no enclosed charge, the field is zero.', 14),
  ('jee-adv-phy-gauss-law-q16', 'jee-adv-phy-gauss-law', 'The relationship between electric field and potential is E =', '−dV/dr', 'dV/dr', 'V·r', 'V/r²', 'A', 'The field is the negative gradient of potential, E = −dV/dr.', 15),
  ('jee-adv-phy-shm-waves-q13', 'jee-adv-phy-shm-waves', 'In simple harmonic motion, the acceleration is proportional to and directed:', 'Opposite to the displacement', 'Along the displacement', 'Perpendicular to it', 'Constant', 'A', 'In SHM, a = −ω²x — restoring, opposite to displacement.', 12),
  ('jee-adv-phy-shm-waves-q14', 'jee-adv-phy-shm-waves', 'The time period of a simple pendulum of length L is:', '2π√(L/g)', '2π√(g/L)', '2π√(Lg)', '2πL/g', 'A', 'T = 2π√(L/g) for small oscillations.', 13),
  ('jee-adv-phy-shm-waves-q15', 'jee-adv-phy-shm-waves', 'The total mechanical energy of an SHM oscillator is proportional to the square of the:', 'Amplitude', 'Period', 'Phase', 'Mass only', 'A', 'Energy E = ½mω²A² is proportional to amplitude squared.', 14),
  ('jee-adv-phy-shm-waves-q16', 'jee-adv-phy-shm-waves', 'Beats are produced by the superposition of two waves of slightly different:', 'Frequencies', 'Amplitudes', 'Phases only', 'Directions', 'A', 'Beats arise from two waves with slightly different frequencies.', 15),
  ('jee-adv-phy-transient-circuits-q13', 'jee-adv-phy-transient-circuits', 'The time constant of an RC circuit is:', 'RC', 'R/C', 'C/R', '1/RC', 'A', 'The charging/discharging time constant is τ = RC.', 12),
  ('jee-adv-phy-transient-circuits-q14', 'jee-adv-phy-transient-circuits', 'The time constant of an RL circuit is:', 'L/R', 'R/L', 'LR', '1/LR', 'A', 'For an RL circuit the time constant is τ = L/R.', 13),
  ('jee-adv-phy-transient-circuits-q15', 'jee-adv-phy-transient-circuits', 'After one time constant, a charging capacitor reaches about what fraction of full charge?', '63%', '37%', '50%', '100%', 'A', 'Q = Q₀(1 − e⁻¹) ≈ 0.63 Q₀ after one time constant.', 14),
  ('jee-adv-phy-transient-circuits-q16', 'jee-adv-phy-transient-circuits', 'The energy stored in a charged capacitor is:', '½CV²', 'CV²', '½C²V', 'QV²', 'A', 'Stored energy U = ½CV² = ½QV = Q²/2C.', 15),
  ('jee-adv-chem-stereochemistry-q13', 'jee-adv-chem-stereochemistry', 'A carbon bonded to four different groups is called:', 'A chiral (asymmetric) centre', 'A carbanion', 'A carbocation', 'Achiral', 'A', 'A carbon with four different substituents is a chiral stereocentre.', 12),
  ('jee-adv-chem-stereochemistry-q14', 'jee-adv-chem-stereochemistry', 'Enantiomers are stereoisomers that are:', 'Non-superimposable mirror images', 'Identical', 'Structural isomers', 'Superimposable', 'A', 'Enantiomers are non-superimposable mirror images of each other.', 13),
  ('jee-adv-chem-stereochemistry-q15', 'jee-adv-chem-stereochemistry', 'A racemic mixture contains equal amounts of two enantiomers and is:', 'Optically inactive', 'Strongly dextrorotatory', 'Strongly levorotatory', 'A single compound', 'A', 'Equal enantiomers cancel each other''s rotation, so the mixture is optically inactive.', 14),
  ('jee-adv-chem-stereochemistry-q16', 'jee-adv-chem-stereochemistry', 'The maximum number of stereoisomers for a molecule with n stereocentres is:', '2ⁿ', 'n²', '2n', 'n!', 'A', 'A molecule with n chiral centres has at most 2ⁿ stereoisomers.', 15),
  ('jee-adv-chem-kinetics-q13', 'jee-adv-chem-kinetics', 'For a first-order reaction, the half-life is:', 'Independent of initial concentration', 'Proportional to concentration', 'Inversely proportional to concentration', 'Zero', 'A', 'A first-order half-life t½ = 0.693/k is independent of the initial concentration.', 12),
  ('jee-adv-chem-kinetics-q14', 'jee-adv-chem-kinetics', 'The Arrhenius equation relates the rate constant to:', 'Temperature and activation energy', 'Pressure only', 'Volume', 'Concentration only', 'A', 'k = A·e^(−Ea/RT) links k to temperature and activation energy.', 13),
  ('jee-adv-chem-kinetics-q15', 'jee-adv-chem-kinetics', 'A catalyst increases the reaction rate by:', 'Lowering the activation energy', 'Raising the activation energy', 'Changing ΔH', 'Shifting the equilibrium', 'A', 'A catalyst provides an alternative pathway with lower activation energy.', 14),
  ('jee-adv-chem-kinetics-q16', 'jee-adv-chem-kinetics', 'The order of a reaction is determined by:', 'The rate law (experimentally)', 'The balanced equation always', 'The molar mass', 'The temperature', 'A', 'Reaction order comes from the experimentally determined rate law, not stoichiometry.', 15),
  ('jee-adv-chem-atomic-structure-q13', 'jee-adv-chem-atomic-structure', 'The maximum number of electrons in a shell with principal quantum number n is:', '2n²', 'n²', '2n', 'n', 'A', 'A shell holds a maximum of 2n² electrons.', 12),
  ('jee-adv-chem-atomic-structure-q14', 'jee-adv-chem-atomic-structure', 'The Pauli exclusion principle states that no two electrons can have the same:', 'Set of four quantum numbers', 'Energy ever', 'Spin ever', 'Orbital shape', 'A', 'No two electrons in an atom share all four quantum numbers.', 13),
  ('jee-adv-chem-atomic-structure-q15', 'jee-adv-chem-atomic-structure', 'Hund''s rule states that electrons fill degenerate orbitals:', 'Singly first, with parallel spins', 'In pairs first', 'Randomly', 'Only after pairing', 'A', 'Degenerate orbitals are singly occupied with parallel spins before pairing.', 14),
  ('jee-adv-chem-atomic-structure-q16', 'jee-adv-chem-atomic-structure', 'The azimuthal quantum number l determines the orbital''s:', 'Shape', 'Size only', 'Orientation only', 'Spin', 'A', 'The azimuthal (angular momentum) quantum number l defines orbital shape.', 15),
  ('jee-adv-chem-ionic-equilibrium-q13', 'jee-adv-chem-ionic-equilibrium', 'The pH of a neutral aqueous solution at 25°C is:', '7', '0', '14', '1', 'A', 'At 25°C neutral water has [H⁺] = 10⁻⁷ M, so pH = 7.', 12),
  ('jee-adv-chem-ionic-equilibrium-q14', 'jee-adv-chem-ionic-equilibrium', 'The Henderson–Hasselbalch equation relates pH to pKa and the ratio of:', 'Conjugate base to acid', 'Acid to water', 'Salt to solvent', 'Cation to anion', 'A', 'pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA]).', 13),
  ('jee-adv-chem-ionic-equilibrium-q15', 'jee-adv-chem-ionic-equilibrium', 'A buffer solution resists changes in pH when small amounts of ____ are added.', 'Acid or base', 'Water only', 'Salt only', 'Nothing', 'A', 'Buffers resist pH change on adding small amounts of acid or base.', 14),
  ('jee-adv-chem-ionic-equilibrium-q16', 'jee-adv-chem-ionic-equilibrium', 'The solubility product Ksp applies to:', 'Sparingly soluble salts', 'Highly soluble salts', 'Gases only', 'Pure water', 'A', 'Ksp describes the equilibrium of a sparingly soluble ionic salt with its ions.', 15),
  ('jee-adv-math-limits-continuity-q13', 'jee-adv-math-limits-continuity', 'The limit of (sin x)/x as x → 0 is:', '1', '0', '∞', 'Undefined', 'A', 'The standard limit lim_{x→0} (sin x)/x = 1.', 12),
  ('jee-adv-math-limits-continuity-q14', 'jee-adv-math-limits-continuity', 'A function is continuous at x = a if the limit equals:', 'f(a)', '0', 'f''(a)', 'Infinity', 'A', 'Continuity requires lim_{x→a} f(x) = f(a).', 13),
  ('jee-adv-math-limits-continuity-q15', 'jee-adv-math-limits-continuity', 'Differentiability of a function at a point implies:', 'Continuity at that point', 'Discontinuity', 'A vertical tangent', 'Nothing', 'A', 'If a function is differentiable at a point, it is continuous there.', 14),
  ('jee-adv-math-limits-continuity-q16', 'jee-adv-math-limits-continuity', 'L''Hôpital''s rule applies to limits of the indeterminate form:', '0/0 or ∞/∞', '1 + 1', '2/3', 'A finite number', 'A', 'L''Hôpital''s rule handles 0/0 and ∞/∞ indeterminate forms.', 15),
  ('jee-adv-math-differential-equations-q13', 'jee-adv-math-differential-equations', 'The order of a differential equation is the order of its:', 'Highest derivative', 'Lowest derivative', 'Constant term', 'Degree only', 'A', 'The order is the highest derivative appearing in the equation.', 12),
  ('jee-adv-math-differential-equations-q14', 'jee-adv-math-differential-equations', 'The equation dy/dx = ky has the general solution:', 'y = Ce^(kx)', 'y = kx + C', 'y = C/x', 'y = ln(kx)', 'A', 'Separating variables gives the exponential solution y = Ce^(kx).', 13),
  ('jee-adv-math-differential-equations-q15', 'jee-adv-math-differential-equations', 'A first-order linear ODE dy/dx + Py = Q is solved using an:', 'Integrating factor', 'Partial fraction', 'Binomial expansion', 'Substitution only', 'A', 'The integrating factor e^(∫P dx) solves a first-order linear ODE.', 14),
  ('jee-adv-math-differential-equations-q16', 'jee-adv-math-differential-equations', 'A differential equation is separable if it can be written as:', 'f(y)dy = g(x)dx', 'y = mx + c', 'ax² + bx + c = 0', 'A matrix equation', 'A', 'Separable equations put y-terms on one side and x-terms on the other.', 15),
  ('jee-adv-math-vectors-3d-q13', 'jee-adv-math-vectors-3d', 'The dot product of two perpendicular vectors is:', '0', '1', 'Their magnitudes'' product', 'Undefined', 'A', 'Perpendicular vectors have a dot product of zero (cos 90° = 0).', 12),
  ('jee-adv-math-vectors-3d-q14', 'jee-adv-math-vectors-3d', 'The cross product of two parallel vectors is:', 'The zero vector', 'A unit vector', 'Their dot product', 'Undefined', 'A', 'Parallel vectors have sin 0° = 0, so their cross product is the zero vector.', 13),
  ('jee-adv-math-vectors-3d-q15', 'jee-adv-math-vectors-3d', 'The magnitude of a × b equals |a||b| times:', 'sin θ', 'cos θ', 'tan θ', '1', 'A', '|a × b| = |a||b| sin θ, where θ is the angle between them.', 14),
  ('jee-adv-math-vectors-3d-q16', 'jee-adv-math-vectors-3d', 'The scalar triple product a·(b × c) gives the volume of a:', 'Parallelepiped', 'Sphere', 'Cone', 'Cylinder', 'A', 'The scalar triple product equals the volume of the parallelepiped formed by the vectors.', 15),
  ('jee-adv-math-application-derivatives-q13', 'jee-adv-math-application-derivatives', 'At a local maximum of a smooth function, the first derivative is:', 'Zero (with f'''' < 0)', 'Positive', 'Undefined always', 'Equal to f', 'A', 'At a smooth local maximum f'' = 0 and f'''' < 0.', 12),
  ('jee-adv-math-application-derivatives-q14', 'jee-adv-math-application-derivatives', 'A function is increasing on an interval where its derivative is:', 'Positive', 'Negative', 'Zero', 'Undefined', 'A', 'A positive derivative means the function is increasing there.', 13),
  ('jee-adv-math-application-derivatives-q15', 'jee-adv-math-application-derivatives', 'The point where the concavity of a curve changes is called a:', 'Point of inflection', 'Maximum', 'Minimum', 'Root', 'A', 'A point of inflection is where the second derivative changes sign (concavity changes).', 14),
  ('jee-adv-math-application-derivatives-q16', 'jee-adv-math-application-derivatives', 'If dy/dx = 3 at a point and dx/dt = 2, then dy/dt equals:', '6', '1.5', '5', '3', 'A', 'By the chain rule dy/dt = (dy/dx)(dx/dt) = 3 × 2 = 6.', 15),
  ('neet-biology-cell-structure-function-q01', 'neet-biology-cell-structure-function', 'Which organelle is known as the ''powerhouse of the cell''?', 'Ribosome', 'Lysosome', 'Golgi apparatus', 'Mitochondrion', 'D', 'Mitochondria generate most cellular ATP through aerobic respiration.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-cell-structure-function-q02', 'neet-biology-cell-structure-function', 'Ribosomes found in the cytoplasm of eukaryotic cells are of the type:', '80S', '70S', '50S', '30S', 'A', 'Eukaryotic cytoplasmic ribosomes are 80S; the 70S type occurs in mitochondria and chloroplasts.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-cell-structure-function-q03', 'neet-biology-cell-structure-function', 'Which structure is present in plant cells but absent in animal cells?', 'Mitochondrion', 'Nucleus', 'Ribosome', 'Cellulose cell wall', 'D', 'A rigid cellulose cell wall surrounds plant cells but is absent in animal cells.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-cell-structure-function-q04', 'neet-biology-cell-structure-function', 'Lysosomes are often called the ''suicide bags'' because they contain:', 'Photosynthetic pigments', 'DNA', 'Hydrolytic enzymes', 'Ribosomes', 'C', 'Lysosomes are packed with hydrolytic enzymes that can digest the cell''s own components.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-cell-structure-function-q05', 'neet-biology-cell-structure-function', 'The fluid-mosaic model of the plasma membrane was proposed by:', 'Schleiden and Schwann', 'Robert Hooke', 'Singer and Nicolson', 'Watson and Crick', 'C', 'Singer and Nicolson (1972) described the membrane as a lipid bilayer with mobile proteins.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-cell-structure-function-q06', 'neet-biology-cell-structure-function', 'Which feature distinguishes a prokaryotic from a eukaryotic cell?', 'Presence of cytoplasm', 'Presence of a plasma membrane', 'Presence of ribosomes', 'Absence of a membrane-bound nucleus', 'D', 'Prokaryotes lack a true membrane-bound nucleus; their DNA lies in a nucleoid region.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-cell-structure-function-q07', 'neet-biology-cell-structure-function', 'The nucleolus is the site of synthesis of:', 'DNA', 'rRNA', 'Lipids', 'ATP', 'B', 'The nucleolus is the centre of ribosomal RNA synthesis and ribosome assembly.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-cell-structure-function-q08', 'neet-biology-cell-structure-function', '''Omnis cellula e cellula'', meaning new cells arise from existing cells, was stated by:', 'Robert Hooke', 'Rudolf Virchow', 'Theodor Schwann', 'Anton van Leeuwenhoek', 'B', 'Virchow extended the cell theory with the principle that every cell comes from a pre-existing cell.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-human-physiology-digestion-q01', 'neet-biology-human-physiology-digestion', 'Salivary amylase (ptyalin) acts on:', 'Proteins', 'Fats', 'Starch', 'Nucleic acids', 'C', 'Ptyalin begins starch digestion in the mouth, converting it to maltose.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-human-physiology-digestion-q02', 'neet-biology-human-physiology-digestion', 'Pepsin is secreted in an inactive form called:', 'Pepsinogen', 'Prorennin', 'Trypsinogen', 'Chymotrypsinogen', 'A', 'Inactive pepsinogen is activated to pepsin by the HCl of gastric juice.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-human-physiology-digestion-q03', 'neet-biology-human-physiology-digestion', 'Bile, secreted by the liver, helps digestion by:', 'Breaking proteins', 'Emulsifying fats', 'Hydrolysing starch', 'Absorbing water', 'B', 'Bile salts emulsify large fat globules into smaller droplets for lipase to act on.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-human-physiology-digestion-q04', 'neet-biology-human-physiology-digestion', 'Most absorption of digested food occurs in the:', 'Stomach', 'Oesophagus', 'Small intestine', 'Large intestine', 'C', 'Villi and microvilli of the small intestine provide a huge surface for absorption.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-human-physiology-digestion-q05', 'neet-biology-human-physiology-digestion', 'The adult human dental formula 2123/2123 corresponds to how many teeth?', '20', '28', '36', '32', 'D', 'Each half-jaw has 2 incisors, 1 canine, 2 premolars, 3 molars; total 32 teeth.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-human-physiology-digestion-q06', 'neet-biology-human-physiology-digestion', 'The largest gland in the human body is the:', 'Liver', 'Pancreas', 'Salivary gland', 'Thyroid', 'A', 'The liver is the largest gland; it produces bile and stores glycogen.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-human-physiology-digestion-q07', 'neet-biology-human-physiology-digestion', 'Intrinsic factor secreted by the stomach is essential for the absorption of:', 'Vitamin C', 'Calcium', 'Vitamin B₁₂', 'Iron', 'C', 'Intrinsic factor binds vitamin B₁₂, enabling its absorption in the ileum.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-human-physiology-digestion-q08', 'neet-biology-human-physiology-digestion', 'Which enzyme from the pancreas digests fats?', 'Amylase', 'Lipase', 'Trypsin', 'Pepsin', 'B', 'Pancreatic lipase breaks fats into fatty acids and glycerol.', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-organic-chemistry-basics-q01', 'neet-chemistry-organic-chemistry-basics', 'The correct order of stability of carbocations is:', 'Methyl > 1° > 2° > 3°', '2° > 3° > 1° > methyl', '3° > 2° > 1° > methyl', '1° > 2° > 3° > methyl', 'C', 'Hyperconjugation and the +I effect make tertiary carbocations the most stable.', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-organic-chemistry-basics-q02', 'neet-chemistry-organic-chemistry-basics', 'An electrophile is a species that is:', 'Electron-rich', 'A free radical', 'Always negatively charged', 'Electron-deficient', 'D', 'Electrophiles are electron-poor and accept an electron pair from a nucleophile.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-organic-chemistry-basics-q03', 'neet-chemistry-organic-chemistry-basics', 'Which group shows a +M (electron-donating resonance) effect?', '−NO₂', '−C≡N', '−COOH', '−NH₂', 'D', 'The lone pair on −NH₂ is donated into the ring, a +M effect.', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-organic-chemistry-basics-q04', 'neet-chemistry-organic-chemistry-basics', 'The hybridisation of the positively charged carbon in a carbocation is:', 'sp', 'sp²', 'sp³', 'sp³d', 'B', 'A carbocation carbon is sp² hybridised with an empty p-orbital, giving planar geometry.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-organic-chemistry-basics-q05', 'neet-chemistry-organic-chemistry-basics', 'Which of the following is the most acidic?', 'Ethanol', 'Acetic acid', 'Phenol', 'Water', 'B', 'Carboxylic acids are more acidic than phenols, alcohols and water due to resonance-stabilised anions.', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-organic-chemistry-basics-q06', 'neet-chemistry-organic-chemistry-basics', 'Hyperconjugation involves the delocalisation of electrons from:', 'A σ (C–H) bond', 'A π bond only', 'A lone pair', 'A d orbital', 'A', 'Hyperconjugation delocalises σ(C–H) bond electrons into an adjacent empty or π orbital.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-organic-chemistry-basics-q07', 'neet-chemistry-organic-chemistry-basics', 'A chiral carbon atom must be bonded to:', 'A double bond', 'Two identical groups', 'Four different groups', 'A hydrogen only', 'C', 'Optical activity requires a carbon attached to four different groups (a stereocentre).', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-organic-chemistry-basics-q08', 'neet-chemistry-organic-chemistry-basics', 'Electron-withdrawing groups attached to an acid generally:', 'Decrease acidity', 'Make it basic', 'Have no effect', 'Increase acidity', 'D', 'They stabilise the conjugate base by dispersing negative charge, increasing acidity.', 7),
  ('neet-physics-ray-optics-q01', 'neet-physics-ray-optics', 'The focal length of a spherical mirror is related to its radius of curvature by:', 'f = R', 'f = 2R', 'f = R/4', 'f = R/2', 'D', 'For a spherical mirror the focal length is half the radius of curvature, f = R/2.', 0),
  ('neet-physics-ray-optics-q02', 'neet-physics-ray-optics', 'Total internal reflection occurs when light travels from a:', 'Denser to a rarer medium beyond the critical angle', 'Denser to a rarer medium at normal incidence', 'Medium to vacuum at any angle', 'Rarer to a denser medium', 'A', 'TIR needs a denser-to-rarer transition with the incidence angle exceeding the critical angle.', 1),
  ('neet-physics-ray-optics-q03', 'neet-physics-ray-optics', 'The power of a lens of focal length 50 cm is:', '0.5 D', '5 D', '2 D', '50 D', 'C', 'P = 1/f(m) = 1/0.5 = 2 dioptres.', 2),
  ('neet-physics-ray-optics-q04', 'neet-physics-ray-optics', 'A convex lens always forms which type of image of a distant object?', 'Virtual and erect', 'Real and erect', 'Virtual and inverted', 'Real and inverted', 'D', 'A converging lens forms a real, inverted image of a distant real object on the far side.', 3),
  ('neet-physics-ray-optics-q05', 'neet-physics-ray-optics', 'Snell''s law relates the angles of incidence and refraction through:', 'n₁ cos i = n₂ cos r', 'n₁ sin i = n₂ sin r', 'n₁ tan i = n₂ tan r', 'n₁ i = n₂ r', 'B', 'Snell''s law states n₁ sin i = n₂ sin r at a refracting boundary.', 4),
  ('neet-physics-ray-optics-q06', 'neet-physics-ray-optics', 'Two thin lenses of power +3 D and +2 D placed in contact have a combined power of:', '1.2 D', '1 D', '6 D', '5 D', 'D', 'Powers add for lenses in contact: P = 3 + 2 = 5 D.', 5),
  ('neet-physics-ray-optics-q07', 'neet-physics-ray-optics', 'A prism produces a spectrum of white light because the refractive index:', 'Is constant for all colours', 'Depends on wavelength', 'Depends on the prism mass', 'Is zero for violet light', 'B', 'Refractive index varies with wavelength, so violet bends most and red least (dispersion).', 6),
  ('neet-physics-ray-optics-q08', 'neet-physics-ray-optics', 'A convex mirror always forms an image that is:', 'Real and inverted', 'Virtual and magnified', 'Real and magnified', 'Virtual, erect and diminished', 'D', 'A convex (diverging) mirror always gives a virtual, erect, diminished image, useful as a rear-view mirror.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division-q01', 'neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division', 'DNA replication during the cell cycle occurs in which phase?', 'S phase', 'G1 phase', 'G2 phase', 'M phase', 'A', 'DNA is replicated during the S (synthesis) phase, doubling the chromosomal DNA content.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division-q02', 'neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division', 'Separation of sister chromatids to opposite poles takes place during:', 'Prophase', 'Anaphase', 'Metaphase', 'Telophase', 'B', 'In anaphase the centromeres split and sister chromatids move to opposite poles.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division-q03', 'neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division', 'Crossing over, which recombines genetic material, occurs during which sub-stage of prophase I?', 'Leptotene', 'Pachytene', 'Zygotene', 'Diakinesis', 'B', 'Crossing over occurs at the chiasmata during pachytene of meiotic prophase I.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division-q04', 'neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division', 'Meiosis in a human cell produces gametes with how many chromosomes?', '46', '22', '92', '23', 'D', 'Meiosis is reductional, halving the diploid 46 to the haploid 23 in gametes.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division-q05', 'neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division', 'At which stage of mitosis do chromosomes align at the equatorial plate?', 'Anaphase', 'Metaphase', 'Telophase', 'Prophase', 'B', 'During metaphase the chromosomes line up at the equatorial (metaphase) plate.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division-q06', 'neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division', 'The quiescent stage in which cells exit the cell cycle is called:', 'M phase', 'S phase', 'G2 phase', 'G0 phase', 'D', 'G0 is a quiescent stage where cells leave the cycle, as seen in mature neurons.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division-q07', 'neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division', 'Reduction in chromosome number during meiosis occurs at:', 'Meiosis II', 'Cytokinesis', 'Meiosis I', 'Interphase', 'C', 'The chromosome number is halved at meiosis I; meiosis II resembles mitosis.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division-q08', 'neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division', 'Mitosis is described as equational division because the daughter cells are:', 'Diploid and identical to the parent', 'Haploid and different', 'Haploid and identical', 'Tetraploid', 'A', 'Mitosis produces two diploid daughter cells genetically identical to the parent cell.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-biomolecules-q01', 'neet-biology-biomolecules', 'Proteins are polymers of amino acids joined by which type of bond?', 'Glycosidic bond', 'Hydrogen bond', 'Phosphodiester bond', 'Peptide bond', 'D', 'Amino acids are linked by peptide bonds to form polypeptides and proteins.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-biomolecules-q02', 'neet-biology-biomolecules', 'The most abundant protein in the entire biosphere is:', 'Haemoglobin', 'Collagen', 'Keratin', 'RuBisCO', 'D', 'RuBisCO, the CO2-fixing enzyme of photosynthesis, is the most abundant protein in the biosphere.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-biomolecules-q03', 'neet-biology-biomolecules', 'The alpha-helix and beta-pleated sheet describe which level of protein structure?', 'Secondary', 'Primary', 'Tertiary', 'Quaternary', 'A', 'Secondary structure refers to local folding such as the alpha-helix and beta-pleated sheet.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-biomolecules-q04', 'neet-biology-biomolecules', 'The storage polysaccharide in animals is:', 'Starch', 'Glycogen', 'Cellulose', 'Chitin', 'B', 'Glycogen is the storage polysaccharide in animals, while starch stores energy in plants.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-biomolecules-q05', 'neet-biology-biomolecules', 'Enzymes act as biological catalysts mainly by:', 'Lowering activation energy', 'Raising activation energy', 'Changing the equilibrium constant', 'Supplying heat', 'A', 'Enzymes accelerate reactions by lowering the activation energy without altering equilibrium.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-biomolecules-q06', 'neet-biology-biomolecules', 'Haemoglobin, with four polypeptide subunits, illustrates which level of protein organisation?', 'Primary', 'Secondary', 'Quaternary', 'Tertiary', 'C', 'Quaternary structure arises from the association of multiple subunits, as in haemoglobin.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-biomolecules-q07', 'neet-biology-biomolecules', 'An inhibitor that resembles the substrate and binds the active site is a:', 'Coenzyme', 'Non-competitive inhibitor', 'Allosteric activator', 'Competitive inhibitor', 'D', 'Competitive inhibitors structurally resemble the substrate and compete for the active site.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-biomolecules-q08', 'neet-biology-biomolecules', 'Cellulose is a structural homopolymer made of:', 'Alpha-D-glucose', 'Amino acids', 'Fructose', 'Beta-D-glucose', 'D', 'Cellulose is a structural homopolymer of beta-D-glucose units.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants-q01', 'neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants', 'The light reaction of photosynthesis takes place in the:', 'Stroma', 'Thylakoid membrane', 'Cytoplasm', 'Mitochondrial matrix', 'B', 'The light reaction occurs on the thylakoid membranes, producing ATP, NADPH and O2.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants-q02', 'neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants', 'Oxygen released during photosynthesis comes from:', 'Photolysis of water', 'Glucose', 'Carbon dioxide', 'RuBP', 'A', 'The splitting (photolysis) of water in the light reaction releases molecular oxygen.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants-q03', 'neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants', 'The enzyme that fixes CO2 onto RuBP in the Calvin cycle is:', 'PEP carboxylase', 'ATP synthase', 'RuBisCO', 'Carbonic anhydrase', 'C', 'RuBisCO fixes CO2 onto RuBP, the first stable product being 3-PGA in the C3 pathway.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants-q04', 'neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants', 'The first stable product of the C3 (Calvin) cycle is a compound with how many carbons?', '3-carbon (3-PGA)', '4-carbon (OAA)', '5-carbon (RuBP)', '6-carbon (glucose)', 'A', 'In C3 plants the first stable product is the 3-carbon 3-phosphoglyceric acid (3-PGA).', 3),
  ('neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants-q05', 'neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants', 'In C4 plants CO2 is first fixed in the mesophyll by which enzyme?', 'RuBisCO', 'PEP carboxylase', 'Hexokinase', 'Dehydrogenase', 'B', 'C4 plants fix CO2 as oxaloacetate via PEP carboxylase, minimising photorespiration.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants-q06', 'neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants', 'The primary photosynthetic pigment in green plants is:', 'Carotenoid', 'Xanthophyll', 'Chlorophyll a', 'Anthocyanin', 'C', 'Chlorophyll a is the primary pigment; carotenoids serve as accessory pigments.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants-q07', 'neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants', 'Which wavelengths of light are most effective for photosynthesis?', 'Green and yellow', 'Orange only', 'Red and blue', 'Ultraviolet', 'C', 'Red and blue wavelengths are absorbed best by chlorophyll and drive photosynthesis effectively.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants-q08', 'neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants', 'The dark reaction (Calvin cycle) occurs in which part of the chloroplast?', 'Thylakoid lumen', 'Stroma', 'Thylakoid membrane', 'Outer membrane', 'B', 'The Calvin cycle, which synthesises sugar, takes place in the stroma of the chloroplast.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-plant-animal-tissues-q01', 'neet-biology-plant-animal-tissues', 'Conduction of water from roots to leaves is carried out by:', 'Phloem', 'Xylem', 'Collenchyma', 'Parenchyma', 'B', 'Xylem conducts water and minerals upward; phloem translocates food.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-plant-animal-tissues-q02', 'neet-biology-plant-animal-tissues', 'Which animal tissue has plasma as its fluid matrix?', 'Muscular tissue', 'Nervous tissue', 'Epithelial tissue', 'Blood', 'D', 'Blood is a fluid connective tissue with plasma serving as its liquid matrix.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-plant-animal-tissues-q03', 'neet-biology-plant-animal-tissues', 'Tendons, which connect muscle to bone, are an example of which tissue?', 'Connective', 'Epithelial', 'Muscular', 'Nervous', 'A', 'Tendons are dense connective tissue joining muscle to bone; ligaments join bone to bone.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-plant-animal-tissues-q04', 'neet-biology-plant-animal-tissues', 'Flexible mechanical support in young plant stems is provided mainly by:', 'Sclerenchyma', 'Phloem', 'Xylem', 'Collenchyma', 'D', 'Collenchyma provides flexible support; sclerenchyma gives rigid support.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-plant-animal-tissues-q05', 'neet-biology-plant-animal-tissues', 'Cardiac muscle is correctly described as:', 'Smooth and multinucleate', 'Smooth and voluntary', 'Striated and voluntary', 'Striated, branched and involuntary', 'D', 'Cardiac muscle is striated, branched, uninucleate and involuntary.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-plant-animal-tissues-q06', 'neet-biology-plant-animal-tissues', 'Active cell division in plants takes place in tissue called:', 'Parenchyma', 'Sclerenchyma', 'Phloem', 'Meristematic tissue', 'D', 'Meristematic tissue consists of dividing cells responsible for plant growth.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-plant-animal-tissues-q07', 'neet-biology-plant-animal-tissues', 'Which tissue lines and covers body surfaces in animals?', 'Connective', 'Muscular', 'Epithelial', 'Nervous', 'C', 'Epithelial tissue covers and lines body surfaces and cavities.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-plant-animal-tissues-q08', 'neet-biology-plant-animal-tissues', 'Translocation of food in plants occurs through sieve tubes of the:', 'Phloem', 'Xylem', 'Cambium', 'Cortex', 'A', 'Phloem, through its sieve tubes, translocates the food prepared in leaves.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-breathing-exchange-of-gases-q01', 'neet-biology-breathing-exchange-of-gases', 'The exchange of gases in the lungs occurs across the walls of the:', 'Bronchi', 'Trachea', 'Larynx', 'Alveoli', 'D', 'Gaseous exchange occurs by diffusion across the thin walls of the alveoli.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-breathing-exchange-of-gases-q02', 'neet-biology-breathing-exchange-of-gases', 'Carbon dioxide is transported in blood mainly as:', 'Bicarbonate ions', 'Carbaminohaemoglobin', 'Dissolved CO2', 'Carbonic acid gas', 'A', 'About 70 percent of CO2 is carried as bicarbonate ions in the plasma.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-breathing-exchange-of-gases-q03', 'neet-biology-breathing-exchange-of-gases', 'During inspiration the diaphragm:', 'Relaxes and domes up', 'Flattens and contracts', 'Stays unchanged', 'Moves sideways only', 'B', 'The diaphragm flattens and contracts during inspiration, increasing thoracic volume.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-breathing-exchange-of-gases-q04', 'neet-biology-breathing-exchange-of-gases', 'The respiratory rhythm centre that controls breathing is located in the:', 'Cerebellum', 'Hypothalamus', 'Medulla oblongata', 'Spinal cord', 'C', 'The breathing centre lies in the medulla oblongata of the brain.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-breathing-exchange-of-gases-q05', 'neet-biology-breathing-exchange-of-gases', 'Oxygen is transported in blood predominantly as:', 'Oxyhaemoglobin', 'Dissolved O2 in plasma', 'Bicarbonate', 'Carbamino compound', 'A', 'About 97 percent of oxygen is carried bound to haemoglobin as oxyhaemoglobin.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-breathing-exchange-of-gases-q06', 'neet-biology-breathing-exchange-of-gases', 'The approximate tidal volume of a normal adult human is:', '4500 mL', '1500 mL', '3500 mL', '500 mL', 'D', 'Tidal volume, the air exchanged in a normal breath, is about 500 mL.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-breathing-exchange-of-gases-q07', 'neet-biology-breathing-exchange-of-gases', 'The enzyme in RBCs that speeds conversion of CO2 to bicarbonate is:', 'Pepsin', 'Carbonic anhydrase', 'Catalase', 'Amylase', 'B', 'Carbonic anhydrase in red blood cells accelerates the CO2-to-bicarbonate reaction.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-breathing-exchange-of-gases-q08', 'neet-biology-breathing-exchange-of-gases', 'A rightward shift of the oxygen dissociation curve (Bohr effect) is favoured by:', 'Low CO2 and high pH', 'High pH alone', 'Low temperature only', 'High CO2, low pH and high temperature', 'D', 'High CO2, low pH and high temperature shift the curve right, releasing more O2 to tissues.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-body-fluids-circulation-q01', 'neet-biology-body-fluids-circulation', 'The natural pacemaker of the human heart is the:', 'AV node', 'Bundle of His', 'SA node', 'Purkinje fibres', 'C', 'The sino-atrial (SA) node generates impulses and acts as the heart''s pacemaker.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-body-fluids-circulation-q02', 'neet-biology-body-fluids-circulation', 'Mature mammalian red blood cells are unusual because they:', 'Have two nuclei', 'Lack a nucleus', 'Contain chlorophyll', 'Are amoeboid', 'B', 'Mature mammalian RBCs lack a nucleus and live for about 120 days.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-body-fluids-circulation-q03', 'neet-biology-body-fluids-circulation', 'The valve located between the left atrium and left ventricle is the:', 'Tricuspid valve', 'Pulmonary valve', 'Bicuspid (mitral) valve', 'Aortic valve', 'C', 'The bicuspid (mitral) valve lies between the left atrium and left ventricle.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-body-fluids-circulation-q04', 'neet-biology-body-fluids-circulation', 'Blood clotting is the primary function of:', 'Erythrocytes', 'Leukocytes', 'Platelets', 'Plasma proteins only', 'C', 'Platelets (thrombocytes) play the central role in blood coagulation.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-body-fluids-circulation-q05', 'neet-biology-body-fluids-circulation', 'In the ABO system, which blood group is the universal donor?', 'A', 'B', 'AB', 'O', 'D', 'Group O lacks A and B surface antigens, making it the universal donor.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-body-fluids-circulation-q06', 'neet-biology-body-fluids-circulation', 'Roughly what percentage of blood volume is plasma?', 'About 10 percent', 'About 25 percent', 'About 90 percent', 'About 55 percent', 'D', 'Plasma forms about 55 percent of blood volume and is its fluid matrix.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-body-fluids-circulation-q07', 'neet-biology-body-fluids-circulation', 'The human heart is described as myogenic because the heartbeat originates in:', 'Nerves of the brain', 'Cardiac muscle itself', 'The spinal cord', 'Adrenal glands', 'B', 'A myogenic heart generates its own beat from cardiac muscle (the SA node), not from nerves.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-body-fluids-circulation-q08', 'neet-biology-body-fluids-circulation', 'Double circulation in humans is advantageous because it:', 'Mixes oxygenated and deoxygenated blood', 'Keeps oxygenated and deoxygenated blood separate', 'Removes the need for a heart', 'Slows blood flow to tissues', 'B', 'Double circulation keeps oxygenated and deoxygenated blood separate for efficient delivery.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-excretory-products-elimination-q01', 'neet-biology-excretory-products-elimination', 'The functional unit of the kidney is the:', 'Neuron', 'Glomerulus', 'Alveolus', 'Nephron', 'D', 'The nephron is the structural and functional unit of the kidney.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-excretory-products-elimination-q02', 'neet-biology-excretory-products-elimination', 'Humans are ureotelic, meaning the chief nitrogenous waste excreted is:', 'Urea', 'Uric acid', 'Ammonia', 'Amino acids', 'A', 'Ureotelic animals like humans excrete urea, formed in the liver by the ornithine cycle.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-excretory-products-elimination-q03', 'neet-biology-excretory-products-elimination', 'Glomerular filtration in the nephron takes place at the:', 'Loop of Henle', 'Distal tubule', 'Collecting duct', 'Bowman''s capsule', 'D', 'Filtration occurs at Bowman''s capsule, which encloses the glomerulus.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-excretory-products-elimination-q04', 'neet-biology-excretory-products-elimination', 'ADH (vasopressin) acts on the kidney to:', 'Increase urine volume', 'Excrete more glucose', 'Promote water reabsorption', 'Block filtration', 'C', 'ADH promotes water reabsorption, concentrating the urine and conserving water.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-excretory-products-elimination-q05', 'neet-biology-excretory-products-elimination', 'Birds and reptiles, which excrete mainly uric acid, are termed:', 'Uricotelic', 'Ureotelic', 'Ammonotelic', 'Osmotic', 'A', 'Uricotelic animals such as birds and reptiles excrete nitrogen mainly as uric acid.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-excretory-products-elimination-q06', 'neet-biology-excretory-products-elimination', 'The glomerular filtration rate (GFR) in a healthy person is about:', '25 mL per minute', '500 mL per minute', '125 mL per minute', '1000 mL per minute', 'C', 'A healthy GFR is about 125 mL per minute (roughly 180 L filtered per day).', 5),
  ('neet-biology-excretory-products-elimination-q07', 'neet-biology-excretory-products-elimination', 'The medullary concentration gradient of the kidney is maintained by the:', 'Counter-current mechanism in the loop of Henle', 'Glomerulus', 'Bowman''s capsule', 'Renal pelvis', 'A', 'The counter-current mechanism in the loop of Henle maintains the medullary osmotic gradient.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-excretory-products-elimination-q08', 'neet-biology-excretory-products-elimination', 'The hormone renin, which raises blood pressure via RAAS, is released by:', 'JG cells of the kidney', 'Adrenal cortex', 'Liver', 'Pancreas', 'A', 'Juxtaglomerular (JG) cells release renin, triggering the RAAS to raise blood pressure.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-neural-control-coordination-q01', 'neet-biology-neural-control-coordination', 'The resting membrane potential of a neuron is approximately:', '+70 mV', '0 mV', '-30 mV', '-70 mV', 'D', 'The resting potential is about -70 mV, with more sodium ions outside the membrane.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-neural-control-coordination-q02', 'neet-biology-neural-control-coordination', 'Conduction of the nerve impulse in myelinated fibres is called:', 'Continuous conduction', 'Saltatory conduction', 'Synaptic conduction', 'Reflex conduction', 'B', 'In myelinated axons the impulse jumps between nodes of Ranvier, called saltatory conduction.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-neural-control-coordination-q03', 'neet-biology-neural-control-coordination', 'Which part of the brain controls balance and coordination of movement?', 'Cerebrum', 'Cerebellum', 'Medulla oblongata', 'Hypothalamus', 'B', 'The cerebellum controls balance, posture and coordination of voluntary movement.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-neural-control-coordination-q04', 'neet-biology-neural-control-coordination', 'During depolarisation in an action potential, which ions rush into the neuron?', 'Potassium ions', 'Chloride ions', 'Sodium ions', 'Calcium ions only', 'C', 'Depolarisation occurs as sodium ions rush into the neuron, reversing membrane polarity.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-neural-control-coordination-q05', 'neet-biology-neural-control-coordination', 'A commonly cited chemical neurotransmitter at synapses is:', 'Insulin', 'Renin', 'Haemoglobin', 'Acetylcholine', 'D', 'Acetylcholine is a neurotransmitter that carries signals across chemical synapses.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-neural-control-coordination-q06', 'neet-biology-neural-control-coordination', 'The resting potential of a neuron is maintained chiefly by the:', 'Nucleus', 'Myelin sheath', 'Sodium-potassium pump', 'Golgi body', 'C', 'The sodium-potassium pump actively maintains the ionic gradient and resting potential.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-neural-control-coordination-q07', 'neet-biology-neural-control-coordination', 'The central nervous system consists of the:', 'Sense organs', 'Nerves and ganglia', 'Brain and spinal cord', 'Autonomic nerves only', 'C', 'The central nervous system comprises the brain and the spinal cord.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-neural-control-coordination-q08', 'neet-biology-neural-control-coordination', 'A rapid involuntary response such as withdrawing the hand from heat is integrated at the:', 'Cerebrum', 'Cerebellum', 'Spinal cord (reflex arc)', 'Thalamus', 'C', 'A reflex arc allows rapid involuntary responses, integrated at the spinal cord.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation-q01', 'neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation', 'The phenotypic ratio of a monohybrid cross in the F2 generation is:', '1:1', '3:1', '9:3:3:1', '1:2:1', 'B', 'A monohybrid cross gives a 3:1 phenotypic ratio in the F2 generation.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation-q02', 'neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation', 'Mendel performed his classic genetic experiments using:', 'Drosophila', 'Maize', 'Garden pea', 'Snapdragon', 'C', 'Mendel used the garden pea, Pisum sativum, to establish the laws of inheritance.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation-q03', 'neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation', 'Inheritance of the ABO blood group, where both alleles express, is an example of:', 'Incomplete dominance', 'Codominance', 'Epistasis', 'Linkage', 'B', 'ABO blood groups show codominance, where alleles I-A and I-B are both expressed.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation-q04', 'neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation', 'Down syndrome is caused by:', 'Trisomy of chromosome 21', 'Monosomy of chromosome 21', 'Trisomy of chromosome 18', 'XXY genotype', 'A', 'Down syndrome results from trisomy of chromosome 21 (three copies of chromosome 21).', 3),
  ('neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation-q05', 'neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation', 'Mendel''s law of segregation states that:', 'Alleles assort independently', 'One allele always masks another', 'Alleles separate during gamete formation', 'Genes are linked', 'C', 'The law of segregation states that paired alleles separate during gamete formation.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation-q06', 'neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation', 'Haemophilia and red-green colour blindness are inherited as:', 'Autosomal dominant traits', 'Y-linked traits', 'X-linked recessive traits', 'Mitochondrial traits', 'C', 'Both haemophilia and colour blindness are X-linked recessive disorders.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation-q07', 'neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation', 'A dihybrid cross between two heterozygotes gives an F2 phenotypic ratio of:', '9:3:3:1', '1:2:1', '3:1', '1:1:1:1', 'A', 'A dihybrid cross yields a 9:3:3:1 phenotypic ratio in the F2 generation.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation-q08', 'neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation', 'Crossing an individual of unknown genotype with a homozygous recessive parent is a:', 'Test cross', 'Back cross to dominant', 'Reciprocal cross', 'Dihybrid cross', 'A', 'A test cross with a homozygous recessive parent reveals the unknown genotype.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance-q01', 'neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance', 'The double-helix model of DNA was proposed in 1953 by:', 'Watson and Crick', 'Meselson and Stahl', 'Hershey and Chase', 'Griffith and Avery', 'A', 'Watson and Crick proposed the antiparallel double-helix structure of DNA in 1953.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance-q02', 'neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance', 'In DNA, adenine pairs with thymine through how many hydrogen bonds?', 'One', 'Two', 'Three', 'Four', 'B', 'A=T pairing involves two hydrogen bonds, whereas G=C uses three.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance-q03', 'neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance', 'DNA replication is described as semiconservative, a fact proved experimentally by:', 'Meselson and Stahl', 'Watson and Crick', 'Mendel', 'Morgan', 'A', 'Meselson and Stahl demonstrated the semiconservative nature of DNA replication.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance-q04', 'neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance', 'The start codon that signals the beginning of translation is:', 'UAA', 'AUG', 'UAG', 'UGA', 'B', 'AUG is the start codon coding for methionine; UAA, UAG and UGA are stop codons.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance-q05', 'neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance', 'The classic model of inducible gene regulation in E. coli is the:', 'trp operon', 'ara operon', 'lac operon', 'gal operon', 'C', 'The lac operon of E. coli is the classic example of inducible gene regulation.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance-q06', 'neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance', 'The enzyme that synthesises mRNA from a DNA template during transcription is:', 'RNA polymerase', 'DNA polymerase', 'DNA ligase', 'Helicase', 'A', 'RNA polymerase transcribes DNA into mRNA during transcription.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance-q07', 'neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance', 'According to the central dogma, the correct flow of genetic information is:', 'Protein to RNA to DNA', 'RNA to DNA to Protein', 'DNA to Protein to RNA', 'DNA to RNA to Protein', 'D', 'The central dogma describes information flowing from DNA to RNA to protein.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance-q08', 'neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance', 'The portion of tRNA that base-pairs with the mRNA codon is the:', 'Anticodon', 'Codon', 'Promoter', 'Operator', 'A', 'The tRNA anticodon reads and base-pairs with the complementary mRNA codon.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-evolution-q01', 'neet-biology-evolution', 'The theory of natural selection was proposed by:', 'Lamarck', 'De Vries', 'Darwin and Wallace', 'Mendel', 'C', 'Darwin and Wallace independently proposed evolution by natural selection.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-evolution-q02', 'neet-biology-evolution', 'Homologous organs, having a common origin but different functions, indicate:', 'Convergent evolution', 'Parallel evolution', 'Divergent evolution', 'Coevolution', 'C', 'Homologous organs share a common origin and indicate divergent evolution.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-evolution-q03', 'neet-biology-evolution', 'The wings of a bird and a butterfly are analogous organs that show:', 'Divergent evolution', 'Adaptive radiation only', 'Convergent evolution', 'Genetic drift', 'C', 'Analogous organs have different origins but similar function, indicating convergent evolution.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-evolution-q04', 'neet-biology-evolution', 'The inheritance of acquired characters was the central idea of:', 'Darwin', 'Wallace', 'Lamarck', 'Hardy', 'C', 'Lamarck proposed the now-discredited inheritance of acquired characters.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-evolution-q05', 'neet-biology-evolution', 'Industrial melanism in peppered moths is a classic example of:', 'Genetic drift', 'Natural selection', 'Mutation pressure', 'Gene flow', 'B', 'The rise of dark peppered moths during industrialisation illustrates natural selection.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-evolution-q06', 'neet-biology-evolution', 'The Miller-Urey experiment provided evidence for:', 'Spontaneous generation', 'Natural selection', 'Chemical evolution of life', 'Genetic drift', 'C', 'The Miller-Urey experiment supported the chemical (abiogenic) origin of organic molecules.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-evolution-q07', 'neet-biology-evolution', 'The Hardy-Weinberg principle states that allele frequencies remain constant when:', 'No evolutionary forces act', 'Mutation is frequent', 'Migration is high', 'Selection is strong', 'A', 'Allele frequencies stay constant in the absence of evolutionary forces (Hardy-Weinberg).', 6),
  ('neet-biology-evolution-q08', 'neet-biology-evolution', 'The correct sequence in human evolution is:', 'Homo sapiens to Homo erectus to Homo habilis', 'Australopithecus to Homo habilis to Homo erectus to Homo sapiens', 'Homo erectus to Australopithecus to Homo sapiens', 'Homo habilis to Australopithecus to Homo sapiens', 'B', 'Human evolution proceeded Australopithecus to Homo habilis to Homo erectus to Homo sapiens.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-human-reproduction-q01', 'neet-biology-human-reproduction', 'In humans fertilisation normally takes place in the:', 'Uterus', 'Ovary', 'Ampulla of the fallopian tube', 'Cervix', 'C', 'Fertilisation occurs in the ampulla of the fallopian tube, forming a diploid zygote.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-human-reproduction-q02', 'neet-biology-human-reproduction', 'Ovulation in a typical 28-day menstrual cycle is triggered by a surge of:', 'FSH', 'LH', 'Progesterone', 'Oxytocin', 'B', 'A mid-cycle LH surge around day 14 triggers ovulation.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-human-reproduction-q03', 'neet-biology-human-reproduction', 'Spermatogenesis takes place within the:', 'Epididymis', 'Seminiferous tubules of the testis', 'Prostate gland', 'Vas deferens', 'B', 'Sperm are produced in the seminiferous tubules of the testis.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-human-reproduction-q04', 'neet-biology-human-reproduction', 'The hormone detected by most pregnancy tests is:', 'Oestrogen', 'Progesterone', 'FSH', 'hCG', 'D', 'Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) is the basis of common pregnancy tests.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-human-reproduction-q05', 'neet-biology-human-reproduction', 'Progesterone that maintains the uterine lining is secreted mainly by the:', 'Corpus luteum', 'Anterior pituitary', 'Placenta in early days only', 'Hypothalamus', 'A', 'The corpus luteum secretes progesterone that maintains the uterine endometrium.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-human-reproduction-q06', 'neet-biology-human-reproduction', 'The hormone that stimulates uterine contractions during childbirth is:', 'Oxytocin', 'Insulin', 'Thyroxine', 'Aldosterone', 'A', 'Oxytocin stimulates strong uterine contractions during parturition (childbirth).', 5),
  ('neet-biology-human-reproduction-q07', 'neet-biology-human-reproduction', 'Normal human gestation lasts approximately:', '6 months', '9 months (about 280 days)', '12 months', '3 months', 'B', 'Human gestation lasts about 9 months (roughly 280 days).', 6),
  ('neet-biology-human-reproduction-q08', 'neet-biology-human-reproduction', 'After fertilisation, the structure that implants in the uterine wall is the:', 'Zygote', 'Corpus luteum', 'Ovum', 'Blastocyst', 'D', 'The blastocyst implants in the uterine wall, after which the placenta develops.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-biotechnology-principles-q01', 'neet-biology-biotechnology-principles', 'Enzymes that cut DNA at specific recognition sequences are called:', 'DNA ligases', 'Polymerases', 'Restriction endonucleases', 'Helicases', 'C', 'Restriction endonucleases are molecular scissors that cut DNA at specific sites.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-biotechnology-principles-q02', 'neet-biology-biotechnology-principles', 'The heat-stable enzyme used in PCR is:', 'DNA ligase', 'Reverse transcriptase', 'Restriction enzyme', 'Taq polymerase', 'D', 'PCR uses heat-stable Taq polymerase to extend primers during repeated cycles.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-biotechnology-principles-q03', 'neet-biology-biotechnology-principles', 'In gel electrophoresis, DNA fragments move towards the:', 'Negative electrode', 'Neither electrode', 'Positive electrode', 'Both equally', 'C', 'Negatively charged DNA migrates towards the positive electrode (anode) in electrophoresis.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-biotechnology-principles-q04', 'neet-biology-biotechnology-principles', 'The enzyme that joins two DNA fragments together is:', 'Amylase', 'Restriction enzyme', 'DNA ligase', 'Lipase', 'C', 'DNA ligase seals the nicks and joins DNA fragments into recombinant molecules.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-biotechnology-principles-q05', 'neet-biology-biotechnology-principles', 'A widely used restriction endonuclease in genetic engineering is:', 'Taq', 'RNase', 'DNA ligase', 'EcoRI', 'D', 'EcoRI is a commonly used restriction enzyme that cuts DNA at a specific palindromic site.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-biotechnology-principles-q06', 'neet-biology-biotechnology-principles', 'The vehicle most commonly used to carry foreign DNA into a host cell is a:', 'Ribosome', 'Lysosome', 'Plasmid vector', 'Centriole', 'C', 'A plasmid acts as a vector, carrying foreign DNA into a host such as E. coli.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-biotechnology-principles-q07', 'neet-biology-biotechnology-principles', 'The three repeating steps of one PCR cycle are:', 'Ligation, cutting, sealing', 'Denaturation, annealing, extension', 'Transcription, translation, folding', 'Replication, transcription, translation', 'B', 'Each PCR cycle consists of denaturation, primer annealing and extension.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-biotechnology-principles-q08', 'neet-biology-biotechnology-principles', 'Large-scale culture of cells for biotechnological products is carried out in:', 'Petri dishes only', 'Autoclaves', 'Test tubes', 'Bioreactors', 'D', 'Bioreactors allow large-scale culture of cells to obtain biotechnology products.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity-q01', 'neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity', 'Green plants in an ecosystem occupy the trophic level of:', 'Primary consumers', 'Decomposers', 'Producers', 'Secondary consumers', 'C', 'Green plants are producers (autotrophs), the first trophic level of an ecosystem.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity-q02', 'neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity', 'The 10 percent law of energy transfer between trophic levels was given by:', 'Odum', 'Tansley', 'Lindeman', 'Darwin', 'C', 'Lindeman''s 10 percent law states only about 10 percent of energy passes to the next trophic level.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity-q03', 'neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity', 'The pyramid of energy in an ecosystem is always:', 'Upright', 'Inverted', 'Spindle-shaped', 'Horizontal', 'A', 'The pyramid of energy is always upright because energy decreases at each higher level.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity-q04', 'neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity', 'Conservation of species within their natural habitat, as in national parks, is:', 'Ex situ conservation', 'Tissue culture', 'Cryopreservation', 'In situ conservation', 'D', 'In situ conservation protects species in their natural habitats like parks and reserves.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity-q05', 'neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity', 'Energy flow through an ecosystem is described as:', 'Cyclic', 'Unidirectional', 'Bidirectional', 'Random', 'B', 'Energy flow in an ecosystem is unidirectional, moving from producers to consumers.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity-q06', 'neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity', 'The greatest species richness on Earth is found in the:', 'Polar regions', 'Deserts', 'Tropics', 'Temperate grasslands', 'C', 'Species richness is highest in the tropics, which support the greatest biodiversity.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity-q07', 'neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity', 'Threatened species are officially classified by the:', 'WWF', 'UNESCO', 'IUCN Red List', 'FAO', 'C', 'The IUCN Red List classifies the conservation status of threatened species.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity-q08', 'neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity', 'A species with a disproportionately large effect on its ecosystem is called a:', 'Pioneer species', 'Keystone species', 'Endemic species', 'Invasive species', 'B', 'A keystone species has an effect on its community far larger than its abundance suggests.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-human-health-disease-q01', 'neet-biology-human-health-disease', 'Malaria in humans is caused by which pathogen?', 'Plasmodium', 'Salmonella', 'Entamoeba', 'HIV', 'A', 'Malaria is caused by the protozoan Plasmodium, transmitted by female Anopheles mosquitoes.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-human-health-disease-q02', 'neet-biology-human-health-disease', 'Malaria is transmitted to humans by the bite of the female:', 'Culex mosquito', 'Anopheles mosquito', 'Aedes mosquito', 'Housefly', 'B', 'The female Anopheles mosquito is the vector that transmits Plasmodium causing malaria.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-human-health-disease-q03', 'neet-biology-human-health-disease', 'HIV, the cause of AIDS, primarily attacks which cells of the immune system?', 'Red blood cells', 'Helper T-cells', 'Platelets', 'Neurons', 'B', 'HIV targets and destroys helper T-cells, weakening the immune response in AIDS.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-human-health-disease-q04', 'neet-biology-human-health-disease', 'Vaccination protects against disease by inducing:', 'Passive immunity', 'Allergy', 'Innate immunity only', 'Active immunity', 'D', 'Vaccines induce active immunity by exposing the body to weakened or inactivated antigens.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-human-health-disease-q05', 'neet-biology-human-health-disease', 'Immunity that is present from birth and is non-specific is termed:', 'Acquired immunity', 'Active immunity', 'Innate immunity', 'Passive immunity', 'C', 'Innate immunity is non-specific and present from birth, forming the first line of defence.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-human-health-disease-q06', 'neet-biology-human-health-disease', 'Typhoid fever is caused by the bacterium:', 'Plasmodium', 'Entamoeba', 'Salmonella', 'Wuchereria', 'C', 'Typhoid is caused by the bacterium Salmonella typhi.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-human-health-disease-q07', 'neet-biology-human-health-disease', 'A key feature distinguishing cancer cells from normal cells is the loss of:', 'Contact inhibition', 'Cell membrane', 'Ribosomes', 'Mitochondria', 'A', 'Cancer cells lose contact inhibition, allowing uncontrolled division and tumour formation.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-human-health-disease-q08', 'neet-biology-human-health-disease', 'The injection of ready-made antibodies provides:', 'Active immunity', 'Passive immunity', 'Innate immunity', 'Lifelong immunity', 'B', 'Supplying ready-made antibodies gives passive immunity, which is immediate but short-lived.', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-atomic-structure-q01', 'neet-chemistry-atomic-structure', 'The neutron was discovered by:', 'J. J. Thomson', 'Rutherford', 'Niels Bohr', 'James Chadwick', 'D', 'James Chadwick discovered the neutron, a neutral subatomic particle.', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-atomic-structure-q02', 'neet-chemistry-atomic-structure', 'The principle that no two electrons in an atom can have all four quantum numbers identical is:', 'Hund''s rule', 'Pauli exclusion principle', 'Aufbau principle', 'Heisenberg principle', 'B', 'The Pauli exclusion principle forbids two electrons from sharing all four quantum numbers.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-atomic-structure-q03', 'neet-chemistry-atomic-structure', 'The energy of the nth Bohr orbit of the hydrogen atom is given by:', 'E = -13.6 n^2 eV', 'E = 13.6 n eV', 'E = -13.6 / n^2 eV', 'E = -13.6 / n eV', 'C', 'The energy of the nth hydrogen Bohr orbit is E = -13.6/n^2 eV.', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-atomic-structure-q04', 'neet-chemistry-atomic-structure', 'Hund''s rule of maximum multiplicity states that orbitals of a subshell are:', 'Filled in pairs first', 'Filled randomly', 'Left empty', 'Filled singly before pairing', 'D', 'Hund''s rule says degenerate orbitals are singly occupied before any pairing of electrons.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-atomic-structure-q05', 'neet-chemistry-atomic-structure', 'The de Broglie relation for the wavelength of a moving particle is:', 'lambda = mv/h', 'lambda = h/mv', 'lambda = hmv', 'lambda = h/m', 'B', 'The de Broglie wavelength is lambda = h/mv, giving matter its wave nature.', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-atomic-structure-q06', 'neet-chemistry-atomic-structure', 'The nuclear model of the atom was proposed after the gold-foil experiment by:', 'Rutherford', 'Thomson', 'Bohr', 'Chadwick', 'A', 'Rutherford proposed the nuclear model based on the alpha-particle scattering experiment.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-atomic-structure-q07', 'neet-chemistry-atomic-structure', 'The Aufbau principle states that electrons first occupy orbitals of:', 'Highest energy', 'Equal energy only', 'Lowest energy', 'The outermost shell', 'C', 'By the Aufbau principle, electrons fill the lowest-energy available orbitals first.', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-atomic-structure-q08', 'neet-chemistry-atomic-structure', 'Heisenberg''s uncertainty principle places a limit on simultaneously knowing a particle''s:', 'Position and momentum', 'Charge and mass', 'Spin and charge', 'Energy and charge', 'A', 'Heisenberg''s principle limits simultaneous precise knowledge of position and momentum.', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure-q01', 'neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure', 'The shape of a methane (CH4) molecule is:', 'Tetrahedral', 'Trigonal planar', 'Linear', 'Bent', 'A', 'CH4 is tetrahedral with bond angles of 109.5 degrees due to sp3 hybridisation.', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure-q02', 'neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure', 'An ionic bond is typically formed between:', 'Two non-metals', 'Two metals', 'A metal and a non-metal', 'Two noble gases', 'C', 'Ionic bonds form by electron transfer, typically between a metal and a non-metal.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure-q03', 'neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure', 'The paramagnetic behaviour of the O2 molecule is best explained by:', 'VSEPR theory', 'Molecular orbital theory', 'Valence bond theory', 'Octet rule', 'B', 'Molecular orbital theory predicts two unpaired electrons in O2, explaining its paramagnetism.', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure-q04', 'neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure', 'The hybridisation of the boron atom in BF3 is:', 'sp', 'sp2', 'sp3', 'sp3d', 'B', 'BF3 is trigonal planar with sp2 hybridisation of the central boron atom.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure-q05', 'neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure', 'A pi bond is formed by which kind of orbital overlap?', 'Head-on (axial) overlap', 'No overlap', 'Sideways (lateral) overlap', 'sp3 overlap only', 'C', 'Pi bonds form by sideways overlap of p-orbitals; sigma bonds form by head-on overlap.', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure-q06', 'neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure', 'The unusually high boiling point of water is mainly due to:', 'Hydrogen bonding', 'Ionic bonding', 'Metallic bonding', 'Van der Waals forces only', 'A', 'Extensive hydrogen bonding among water molecules gives water its high boiling point.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure-q07', 'neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure', 'The geometry of molecules is predicted from electron-pair repulsion by:', 'Band theory', 'Crystal field theory', 'VSEPR theory', 'Kinetic theory', 'C', 'VSEPR theory predicts molecular geometry from repulsion between electron pairs.', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure-q08', 'neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure', 'The bond order of a diatomic molecule is calculated as:', '(bonding - antibonding electrons)/2', '(bonding + antibonding electrons)/2', 'bonding electrons only', 'antibonding electrons / 2', 'A', 'Bond order equals (number of bonding minus antibonding electrons) divided by two.', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-periodic-classification-q01', 'neet-chemistry-periodic-classification', 'The modern periodic law arranges elements by increasing:', 'Atomic mass', 'Valency', 'Density', 'Atomic number', 'D', 'Moseley''s modern periodic law arranges elements by increasing atomic number.', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-periodic-classification-q02', 'neet-chemistry-periodic-classification', 'The most electronegative element in the periodic table is:', 'Oxygen', 'Chlorine', 'Fluorine', 'Nitrogen', 'C', 'Fluorine is the most electronegative element.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-periodic-classification-q03', 'neet-chemistry-periodic-classification', 'As we move across a period from left to right, the atomic radius generally:', 'Decreases', 'Increases', 'Remains constant', 'First increases then decreases', 'A', 'Atomic radius decreases across a period as effective nuclear charge increases.', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-periodic-classification-q04', 'neet-chemistry-periodic-classification', 'Down a group in the periodic table, ionisation enthalpy generally:', 'Decreases', 'Increases', 'Stays the same', 'Becomes zero', 'A', 'Ionisation enthalpy decreases down a group as atomic size and shielding increase.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-periodic-classification-q05', 'neet-chemistry-periodic-classification', 'Transition elements belong to which block of the periodic table?', 'd-block', 'p-block', 's-block', 'f-block', 'A', 'Transition elements are d-block elements; lanthanoids and actinoids are f-block.', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-periodic-classification-q06', 'neet-chemistry-periodic-classification', 'Mendeleev arranged the elements in his periodic table primarily according to:', 'Atomic number', 'Density', 'Electronegativity', 'Atomic mass', 'D', 'Mendeleev arranged elements by increasing atomic mass and even predicted unknown elements.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-periodic-classification-q07', 'neet-chemistry-periodic-classification', 'The diagonal relationship correctly pairs which two elements?', 'Li and Mg', 'Na and K', 'He and Ne', 'Cl and Br', 'A', 'The diagonal relationship links Li-Mg, Be-Al and B-Si due to similar properties.', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-periodic-classification-q08', 'neet-chemistry-periodic-classification', 'Across a period, metallic character of elements generally:', 'Increases', 'Doubles', 'Is unchanged', 'Decreases', 'D', 'Metallic character decreases across a period and increases down a group.', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-thermodynamics-q01', 'neet-chemistry-thermodynamics', 'The mathematical statement of the first law of thermodynamics is:', 'delta U = q + w', 'delta U = q - w only', 'delta G = delta H - T delta S', 'delta S = q/T', 'A', 'The first law states delta U = q + w, expressing conservation of energy.', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-thermodynamics-q02', 'neet-chemistry-thermodynamics', 'A process is spontaneous when the Gibbs free energy change is:', 'Greater than zero', 'Equal to zero', 'Infinite', 'Less than zero', 'D', 'A negative delta G (less than zero) indicates a spontaneous process.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-thermodynamics-q03', 'neet-chemistry-thermodynamics', 'The statement that the total enthalpy change is independent of the path taken is:', 'Le Chatelier''s principle', 'Raoult''s law', 'Hess''s law', 'Henry''s law', 'C', 'Hess''s law states the total enthalpy change of a reaction is independent of the path.', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-thermodynamics-q04', 'neet-chemistry-thermodynamics', 'For an isothermal process involving an ideal gas, the change in internal energy is:', 'Maximum', 'Zero', 'Negative always', 'Equal to the work done', 'B', 'For an ideal gas at constant temperature, internal energy change delta U is zero.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-thermodynamics-q05', 'neet-chemistry-thermodynamics', 'An exothermic reaction is characterised by an enthalpy change (delta H) that is:', 'Positive', 'Undefined', 'Zero', 'Negative', 'D', 'Exothermic reactions release heat, so delta H is negative.', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-thermodynamics-q06', 'neet-chemistry-thermodynamics', 'Enthalpy (H) is correctly defined as:', 'H = U + PV', 'H = U - PV', 'H = U + TS', 'H = U - TS', 'A', 'Enthalpy is defined as H = U + PV.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-thermodynamics-q07', 'neet-chemistry-thermodynamics', 'According to the third law of thermodynamics, the entropy of a perfect crystal at 0 K is:', 'Maximum', 'Infinite', 'Zero', 'Negative', 'C', 'The third law states the entropy of a perfect crystal is zero at absolute zero (0 K).', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-thermodynamics-q08', 'neet-chemistry-thermodynamics', 'Which of the following is a state function?', 'Work', 'Heat', 'Path length', 'Enthalpy', 'D', 'Enthalpy is a state function depending only on initial and final states, not the path.', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-equilibrium-q01', 'neet-chemistry-equilibrium', 'At chemical equilibrium, the rates of the forward and reverse reactions are:', 'Equal', 'Both zero', 'Forward greater', 'Reverse greater', 'A', 'At equilibrium the forward and reverse reaction rates are equal, so concentrations stay constant.', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-equilibrium-q02', 'neet-chemistry-equilibrium', 'The pH of pure water at 25 degrees Celsius is:', '0', '14', '7', '1', 'C', 'Pure water is neutral with a pH of 7 at 25 degrees Celsius.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-equilibrium-q03', 'neet-chemistry-equilibrium', 'According to Le Chatelier''s principle, increasing the temperature shifts equilibrium in the:', 'Exothermic direction', 'Direction of fewer moles', 'Endothermic direction', 'No change', 'C', 'Raising temperature shifts the equilibrium in the endothermic direction to absorb the heat.', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-equilibrium-q04', 'neet-chemistry-equilibrium', 'According to the Arrhenius concept, a base is a substance that in water gives:', 'H+ ions', 'Electrons', 'OH- ions', 'Protons', 'C', 'An Arrhenius base produces hydroxide (OH-) ions in aqueous solution.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-equilibrium-q05', 'neet-chemistry-equilibrium', 'A Bronsted-Lowry acid is defined as a substance that:', 'Accepts a proton', 'Donates a proton', 'Donates an electron pair', 'Accepts an electron pair', 'B', 'A Bronsted-Lowry acid is a proton (H+) donor.', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-equilibrium-q06', 'neet-chemistry-equilibrium', 'The ionic product of water, Kw, at 25 degrees Celsius is:', '1.0 x 10^-7', '1.0 x 10^-14', '1.0 x 10^7', '1.0 x 10^14', 'B', 'Kw = [H+][OH-] = 1.0 x 10^-14 at 25 degrees Celsius.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-equilibrium-q07', 'neet-chemistry-equilibrium', 'Suppression of the ionisation of a weak electrolyte by adding a common ion is the:', 'Buffer action', 'Solubility effect', 'Le Chatelier shift', 'Common ion effect', 'D', 'The common ion effect suppresses the ionisation of a weak electrolyte.', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-equilibrium-q08', 'neet-chemistry-equilibrium', 'A solution of CH3COOH and CH3COONa is an example of a:', 'Strong acid', 'Buffer solution', 'Neutral salt', 'Strong base', 'B', 'A mixture of a weak acid and its salt forms a buffer that resists pH change.', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-hydrocarbons-q01', 'neet-chemistry-hydrocarbons', 'The general molecular formula of saturated hydrocarbons (alkanes) is:', 'CnH2n', 'CnH(2n-2)', 'CnH(2n+2)', 'CnHn', 'C', 'Alkanes are saturated hydrocarbons with the general formula CnH(2n+2).', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-hydrocarbons-q02', 'neet-chemistry-hydrocarbons', 'Markovnikov''s rule predicts that in the addition of HX to an alkene, hydrogen adds to the carbon:', 'With fewer hydrogens', 'Bearing the halogen', 'In the middle', 'With more hydrogens', 'D', 'By Markovnikov''s rule, H adds to the carbon already bearing more hydrogen atoms.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-hydrocarbons-q03', 'neet-chemistry-hydrocarbons', 'Benzene is aromatic because it obeys Huckel''s rule, having a number of pi electrons equal to:', '2n', '4n', 'n + 1', '4n + 2', 'D', 'Aromatic compounds obey Huckel''s rule with (4n + 2) pi electrons; benzene has six.', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-hydrocarbons-q04', 'neet-chemistry-hydrocarbons', 'Alkanes characteristically undergo which type of reaction?', 'Addition', 'Polymerisation only', 'Elimination', 'Substitution', 'D', 'Saturated alkanes undergo free-radical substitution, such as halogenation.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-hydrocarbons-q05', 'neet-chemistry-hydrocarbons', 'Ethyne (acetylene) can be prepared in the laboratory from water and:', 'Calcium oxide', 'Sodium chloride', 'Calcium carbide', 'Calcium carbonate', 'C', 'Calcium carbide reacts with water to give ethyne (acetylene).', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-hydrocarbons-q06', 'neet-chemistry-hydrocarbons', 'The anti-Markovnikov (peroxide) effect is observed in the addition of which reagent to alkenes?', 'HCl', 'H2O', 'HI', 'HBr', 'D', 'The peroxide (Kharasch) effect reverses addition only with HBr, not HCl or HI.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-hydrocarbons-q07', 'neet-chemistry-hydrocarbons', 'Complete combustion of a hydrocarbon yields:', 'CO and H2', 'CO2 and H2O', 'C and H2O', 'CO2 and H2', 'B', 'Complete combustion of hydrocarbons produces carbon dioxide and water.', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-hydrocarbons-q08', 'neet-chemistry-hydrocarbons', 'Which class of hydrocarbon contains a carbon-carbon triple bond?', 'Alkane', 'Alkyne', 'Alkene', 'Cycloalkane', 'B', 'Alkynes contain a carbon-carbon triple bond, as in ethyne.', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-haloalkanes-haloarenes-q01', 'neet-chemistry-haloalkanes-haloarenes', 'An SN2 reaction at a chiral carbon proceeds with:', 'Retention of configuration', 'Inversion of configuration', 'Racemisation', 'No stereochemical change', 'B', 'SN2 reactions occur with inversion of configuration (Walden inversion) at the chiral carbon.', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-haloalkanes-haloarenes-q02', 'neet-chemistry-haloalkanes-haloarenes', 'The reactivity order of haloalkanes towards SN2 is:', '3 degree > 2 degree > 1 degree', '1 degree > 2 degree > 3 degree', '2 degree > 1 degree > 3 degree', 'All react equally', 'B', 'SN2 reactivity is 1 degree > 2 degree > 3 degree because of decreasing steric hindrance.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-haloalkanes-haloarenes-q03', 'neet-chemistry-haloalkanes-haloarenes', 'A Grignard reagent (R-Mg-X) is prepared by reacting a haloalkane with:', 'Magnesium', 'Sodium', 'Zinc', 'Copper', 'A', 'A Grignard reagent forms from a haloalkane and magnesium in dry ether.', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-haloalkanes-haloarenes-q04', 'neet-chemistry-haloalkanes-haloarenes', 'Two molecules of a haloalkane reacting with sodium to form a higher alkane is the:', 'Aldol reaction', 'Cannizzaro reaction', 'Wurtz reaction', 'Friedel-Crafts reaction', 'C', 'The Wurtz reaction couples two haloalkanes using sodium to give a higher alkane.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-haloalkanes-haloarenes-q05', 'neet-chemistry-haloalkanes-haloarenes', 'On standing in air and light, chloroform forms the poisonous gas:', 'Methane', 'Phosgene', 'Carbon dioxide', 'Mustard gas', 'B', 'Chloroform is slowly oxidised in air and light to poisonous phosgene (carbonyl chloride).', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-haloalkanes-haloarenes-q06', 'neet-chemistry-haloalkanes-haloarenes', 'Among the C-X bonds, which haloalkane is most reactive in substitution due to the weakest bond?', 'Fluoride', 'Chloride', 'Bromide', 'Iodide', 'D', 'The C-I bond is the weakest, so iodides react fastest in nucleophilic substitution.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-haloalkanes-haloarenes-q07', 'neet-chemistry-haloalkanes-haloarenes', 'An SN1 reaction proceeds through which intermediate?', 'Carbanion', 'Carbene', 'Free radical', 'Carbocation', 'D', 'SN1 is a two-step reaction proceeding through a carbocation intermediate.', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-haloalkanes-haloarenes-q08', 'neet-chemistry-haloalkanes-haloarenes', 'Haloarenes are less reactive than haloalkanes towards nucleophilic substitution because:', 'The C-X bond has partial double-bond character due to resonance', 'They lack any C-X bond', 'They are ionic', 'They are saturated', 'A', 'Resonance gives the C-X bond in haloarenes partial double-bond character, lowering reactivity.', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-alcohols-phenols-ethers-q01', 'neet-chemistry-alcohols-phenols-ethers', 'Phenol is more acidic than ethanol because its conjugate base (phenoxide) is:', 'Less stable', 'Resonance-stabilised', 'A free radical', 'Non-polar', 'B', 'The phenoxide ion is resonance-stabilised, making phenol more acidic than alcohols.', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-alcohols-phenols-ethers-q02', 'neet-chemistry-alcohols-phenols-ethers', 'The Williamson synthesis is used to prepare:', 'Ethers', 'Alcohols', 'Aldehydes', 'Carboxylic acids', 'A', 'The Williamson synthesis prepares ethers from an alkyl halide and a sodium alkoxide.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-alcohols-phenols-ethers-q03', 'neet-chemistry-alcohols-phenols-ethers', 'The test used to distinguish primary, secondary and tertiary alcohols by reaction speed is the:', 'Tollens'' test', 'Iodoform test', 'Fehling''s test', 'Lucas test', 'D', 'The Lucas test distinguishes 1, 2 and 3 degree alcohols by the rate of turbidity formation.', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-alcohols-phenols-ethers-q04', 'neet-chemistry-alcohols-phenols-ethers', 'On controlled oxidation, a primary alcohol is first converted to a(n):', 'Ketone', 'Ether', 'Aldehyde', 'Ester', 'C', 'Primary alcohols oxidise first to aldehydes and then to carboxylic acids.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-alcohols-phenols-ethers-q05', 'neet-chemistry-alcohols-phenols-ethers', 'Phenol, used as an antiseptic, is also commonly known as:', 'Formic acid', 'Carbolic acid', 'Acetic acid', 'Oxalic acid', 'B', 'Phenol is also called carbolic acid and was historically used as an antiseptic.', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-alcohols-phenols-ethers-q06', 'neet-chemistry-alcohols-phenols-ethers', 'Dehydration of an alcohol typically yields a(n):', 'Ester', 'Alkane', 'Alkyne', 'Alkene', 'D', 'Acid-catalysed dehydration of an alcohol removes water to give an alkene.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-alcohols-phenols-ethers-q07', 'neet-chemistry-alcohols-phenols-ethers', 'The functional group present in an alcohol is:', '-CHO', '-COOH', '-OH on an sp3 carbon', '-O- between two carbons', 'C', 'Alcohols contain a hydroxyl (-OH) group attached to an sp3 (saturated) carbon.', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-alcohols-phenols-ethers-q08', 'neet-chemistry-alcohols-phenols-ethers', 'Which of the following has the highest acidity?', 'Ethanol', 'Phenol', 'Water', 'Methanol', 'B', 'Among these, phenol is the most acidic because its phenoxide ion is resonance-stabilised.', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids-q01', 'neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids', 'The functional group common to aldehydes, ketones and carboxylic acids is the:', 'Carbonyl group', 'Hydroxyl group', 'Amino group', 'Nitro group', 'A', 'All three contain the carbonyl group (C=O) as their key functional feature.', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids-q02', 'neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids', 'Tollens'' test (silver mirror) gives a positive result with:', 'Ketones', 'Alkanes', 'Ethers', 'Aldehydes', 'D', 'Tollens'' reagent is reduced by aldehydes, giving a silver mirror; ketones do not react.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids-q03', 'neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids', 'The Cannizzaro reaction occurs only in aldehydes that have:', 'No alpha-hydrogen', 'An alpha-hydrogen', 'A double bond', 'A hydroxyl group', 'A', 'The Cannizzaro reaction occurs with aldehydes lacking an alpha-hydrogen, like formaldehyde.', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids-q04', 'neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids', 'The only carboxylic acid that gives a positive Tollens'' test is:', 'Acetic acid', 'Benzoic acid', 'Formic acid', 'Oxalic acid', 'C', 'Formic acid has an aldehyde-like group and reduces Tollens'' reagent.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids-q05', 'neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids', 'The carbonyl carbon in aldehydes and ketones is hybridised as:', 'sp2', 'sp', 'sp3', 'sp3d', 'A', 'The carbonyl carbon is sp2 hybridised and electrophilic, favouring nucleophilic addition.', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids-q06', 'neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids', 'The aldol condensation requires the reacting aldehyde or ketone to have:', 'An alpha-hydrogen', 'No alpha-hydrogen', 'A halogen', 'An aromatic ring', 'A', 'Aldol condensation needs at least one alpha-hydrogen on the carbonyl compound.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids-q07', 'neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids', 'Compared with alcohols and phenols, carboxylic acids are:', 'Less acidic', 'Neutral', 'Equally acidic', 'More acidic', 'D', 'Carboxylic acids are more acidic owing to the resonance-stabilised carboxylate ion.', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids-q08', 'neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids', 'The characteristic reaction of the carbonyl group in aldehydes and ketones is:', 'Electrophilic substitution', 'Nucleophilic addition', 'Free-radical addition', 'Elimination', 'B', 'The polar carbonyl group undergoes nucleophilic addition reactions.', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-biomolecules-chemistry-q01', 'neet-chemistry-biomolecules-chemistry', 'Glucose is chemically classified as a(n):', 'Aldohexose', 'Ketohexose', 'Aldopentose', 'Ketopentose', 'A', 'Glucose is an aldohexose (a six-carbon sugar with an aldehyde group).', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-biomolecules-chemistry-q02', 'neet-chemistry-biomolecules-chemistry', 'Sucrose is a non-reducing sugar because it lacks a:', 'Glycosidic bond', 'Carbon atom', 'Free anomeric -OH group', 'Hydroxyl group entirely', 'C', 'Sucrose is non-reducing because both anomeric -OH groups are involved in the glycosidic bond.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-biomolecules-chemistry-q03', 'neet-chemistry-biomolecules-chemistry', 'The sugar present in DNA is:', 'Ribose', 'Fructose', 'Glucose', 'Deoxyribose', 'D', 'DNA contains the sugar deoxyribose, while RNA contains ribose.', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-biomolecules-chemistry-q04', 'neet-chemistry-biomolecules-chemistry', 'Which group of vitamins is fat-soluble?', 'B and C', 'C only', 'A, D, E and K', 'B-complex only', 'C', 'Vitamins A, D, E and K are fat-soluble; the B-complex and C are water-soluble.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-biomolecules-chemistry-q05', 'neet-chemistry-biomolecules-chemistry', 'Amino acids in a protein are joined together by:', 'Glycosidic bonds', 'Ether bonds', 'Ester bonds', 'Peptide bonds', 'D', 'Alpha-amino acids are linked by peptide bonds (-CO-NH-) to form proteins.', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-biomolecules-chemistry-q06', 'neet-chemistry-biomolecules-chemistry', 'The bonds joining monosaccharide units in a polysaccharide are:', 'Peptide bonds', 'Disulphide bonds', 'Phosphodiester bonds', 'Glycosidic bonds', 'D', 'Glycosidic bonds link monosaccharide units in disaccharides and polysaccharides.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-biomolecules-chemistry-q07', 'neet-chemistry-biomolecules-chemistry', 'Insulin, which regulates blood glucose, is chemically a:', 'Carbohydrate', 'Lipid', 'Protein hormone', 'Nucleic acid', 'C', 'Insulin is a protein (peptide) hormone that lowers and regulates blood glucose.', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-biomolecules-chemistry-q08', 'neet-chemistry-biomolecules-chemistry', 'Heating or extreme pH disrupts the secondary and tertiary structure of a protein in a process called:', 'Denaturation', 'Hydrolysis', 'Condensation', 'Esterification', 'A', 'Denaturation disrupts the secondary and tertiary structure of proteins by heat or pH change.', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds-q01', 'neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds', 'In K4[Fe(CN)6], the oxidation state of iron is:', '+3', '0', '+2', '+6', 'C', 'In K4[Fe(CN)6] iron is in the +2 oxidation state with coordination number 6.', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds-q02', 'neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds', 'EDTA, widely used in complexometric titration, is a ligand that is:', 'Hexadentate', 'Bidentate', 'Monodentate', 'Unidentate', 'A', 'EDTA is a hexadentate ligand, donating through six donor atoms.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds-q03', 'neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds', 'In Werner''s theory, the coordination number corresponds to the metal''s:', 'Primary valency', 'Secondary valency', 'Atomic number', 'Mass number', 'B', 'Secondary valency in Werner''s theory equals the coordination number of ligand donor atoms.', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds-q04', 'neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds', 'Ethylenediamine (en) is an example of a ligand that is:', 'Bidentate', 'Monodentate', 'Tridentate', 'Hexadentate', 'A', 'Ethylenediamine (en) is a bidentate ligand with two nitrogen donor atoms.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds-q05', 'neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds', 'The colour of transition-metal complexes is mainly due to:', 's-s transitions', 'd-d transitions', 'Nuclear transitions', 'p-p transitions', 'B', 'd-d electronic transitions absorb visible light, giving transition-metal complexes colour.', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds-q06', 'neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds', 'The splitting of d-orbitals into t2g and eg sets in a complex is explained by:', 'Valence bond theory', 'Molecular orbital theory of diatomics', 'VSEPR theory', 'Crystal field theory', 'D', 'Crystal field theory explains the splitting of d-orbitals into t2g and eg levels.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds-q07', 'neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds', 'A ring structure formed by a polydentate ligand bound to a metal is called a:', 'Salt', 'Isomer', 'Complex ion only', 'Chelate', 'D', 'A chelate is a ring formed by a polydentate ligand, which adds extra stability.', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds-q08', 'neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds', 'In the spectrochemical series, which ligand is a strong-field ligand?', 'I-', 'CO', 'Cl-', 'F-', 'B', 'CO is a strong-field ligand in the spectrochemical series, while I- is weak-field.', 7),
  ('neet-physics-kinematics-q01', 'neet-physics-kinematics', 'Which equation of motion relates final velocity, initial velocity, acceleration and displacement?', 'v^2 = u^2 + 2as', 's = ut + (1/2)at^2', 'v = u + at', 's = vt', 'A', 'The relation v^2 = u^2 + 2as connects velocities, acceleration and displacement (no time term).', 0),
  ('neet-physics-kinematics-q02', 'neet-physics-kinematics', 'The maximum range of a projectile (for a given speed) is achieved at a launch angle of:', '30 degrees', '60 degrees', '45 degrees', '90 degrees', 'C', 'For a given speed, the horizontal range of a projectile is maximum at a 45 degree launch angle.', 1),
  ('neet-physics-kinematics-q03', 'neet-physics-kinematics', 'The slope of a velocity-time graph represents the:', 'Displacement', 'Speed', 'Acceleration', 'Distance', 'C', 'The slope of a velocity-time graph gives the acceleration of the body.', 2),
  ('neet-physics-kinematics-q04', 'neet-physics-kinematics', 'At the highest point of a projectile''s path, its vertical velocity is:', 'Maximum', 'Equal to horizontal velocity', 'Zero', 'Infinite', 'C', 'At the highest point the vertical component of a projectile''s velocity is zero.', 3),
  ('neet-physics-kinematics-q05', 'neet-physics-kinematics', 'Which of the following is a vector quantity?', 'Speed', 'Displacement', 'Distance', 'Time', 'B', 'Displacement is a vector (has magnitude and direction); distance and speed are scalars.', 4),
  ('neet-physics-kinematics-q06', 'neet-physics-kinematics', 'The area under a velocity-time graph gives the:', 'Displacement', 'Acceleration', 'Force', 'Power', 'A', 'The area under a velocity-time graph equals the displacement of the body.', 5),
  ('neet-physics-kinematics-q07', 'neet-physics-kinematics', 'The equations of motion (v = u + at etc.) are valid only when the acceleration is:', 'Uniform (constant)', 'Variable', 'Zero', 'Negative', 'A', 'The standard equations of motion hold only for uniform (constant) acceleration.', 6),
  ('neet-physics-kinematics-q08', 'neet-physics-kinematics', 'The path followed by a projectile under gravity (ignoring air resistance) is:', 'A straight line', 'A parabola', 'A circle', 'An ellipse', 'B', 'A projectile follows a parabolic path because horizontal velocity is constant while vertical velocity changes.', 7),
  ('neet-physics-laws-of-motion-q01', 'neet-physics-laws-of-motion', 'Newton''s first law of motion is also known as the law of:', 'Inertia', 'Acceleration', 'Action-reaction', 'Gravitation', 'A', 'Newton''s first law defines inertia, the tendency of a body to resist a change in its state.', 0),
  ('neet-physics-laws-of-motion-q02', 'neet-physics-laws-of-motion', 'The SI unit of force is the:', 'Newton', 'Watt', 'Joule', 'Pascal', 'A', 'The newton (N), equal to kg m/s^2, is the SI unit of force.', 1),
  ('neet-physics-laws-of-motion-q03', 'neet-physics-laws-of-motion', 'Newton''s second law states that force equals the rate of change of:', 'Displacement', 'Acceleration', 'Energy', 'Momentum', 'D', 'Newton''s second law gives F = dp/dt, the rate of change of momentum (F = ma).', 2),
  ('neet-physics-laws-of-motion-q04', 'neet-physics-laws-of-motion', 'The centripetal force needed for circular motion is directed:', 'Inward (toward the centre)', 'Outward (away from centre)', 'Tangentially', 'Vertically up', 'A', 'Centripetal force is directed toward the centre of the circular path, with magnitude mv^2/r.', 3),
  ('neet-physics-laws-of-motion-q05', 'neet-physics-laws-of-motion', 'Impulse delivered to a body is equal to its change in:', 'Velocity', 'Momentum', 'Kinetic energy', 'Acceleration', 'B', 'Impulse (force multiplied by time) equals the change in momentum of the body.', 4),
  ('neet-physics-laws-of-motion-q06', 'neet-physics-laws-of-motion', 'The recoil of a gun when fired is best explained by the conservation of:', 'Energy', 'Momentum', 'Mass', 'Charge', 'B', 'The gun''s recoil follows from conservation of linear momentum (and Newton''s third law).', 5),
  ('neet-physics-laws-of-motion-q07', 'neet-physics-laws-of-motion', 'Which type of friction must be overcome to start a stationary body moving?', 'Kinetic friction', 'Limiting (maximum static) friction', 'Rolling friction', 'Fluid friction', 'B', 'A body starts moving only after the applied force exceeds the limiting static friction.', 6),
  ('neet-physics-laws-of-motion-q08', 'neet-physics-laws-of-motion', 'Newton''s third law of motion states that for every action there is:', 'No reaction', 'A smaller reaction', 'An equal and opposite reaction', 'A larger reaction', 'C', 'Newton''s third law states every action has an equal and opposite reaction.', 7),
  ('neet-physics-work-energy-power-q01', 'neet-physics-work-energy-power', 'The net work done on a body equals its change in:', 'Potential energy', 'Kinetic energy', 'Momentum', 'Temperature', 'B', 'By the work-energy theorem, net work done equals the change in kinetic energy.', 0),
  ('neet-physics-work-energy-power-q02', 'neet-physics-work-energy-power', 'Work done by a force acting perpendicular to the displacement is:', 'Maximum', 'Zero', 'Negative', 'Equal to the force', 'B', 'Since W = Fs cos(theta) and cos 90 degrees = 0, perpendicular force does zero work.', 1),
  ('neet-physics-work-energy-power-q03', 'neet-physics-work-energy-power', 'The kinetic energy of a body of mass m moving with speed v is:', 'mgh', 'Fs', 'mv', '(1/2)mv^2', 'D', 'Kinetic energy is KE = (1/2)mv^2.', 2),
  ('neet-physics-work-energy-power-q04', 'neet-physics-work-energy-power', 'A collision is called elastic when there is conservation of:', 'Both momentum and kinetic energy', 'Only kinetic energy', 'Only momentum', 'Neither', 'A', 'An elastic collision conserves both momentum and kinetic energy.', 3),
  ('neet-physics-work-energy-power-q05', 'neet-physics-work-energy-power', 'The SI unit of power is the:', 'Joule', 'Watt', 'Newton', 'Pascal', 'B', 'Power, the rate of doing work, is measured in watts (W = J/s).', 4),
  ('neet-physics-work-energy-power-q06', 'neet-physics-work-energy-power', 'One horsepower is approximately equal to:', '100 watts', '746 watts', '1000 watts', '550 watts', 'B', 'One horsepower equals about 746 watts.', 5),
  ('neet-physics-work-energy-power-q07', 'neet-physics-work-energy-power', 'In a conservative field such as gravity, the total mechanical energy of a body is:', 'Always increasing', 'Always decreasing', 'Zero', 'Constant', 'D', 'If only conservative forces act, the total mechanical energy (KE + PE) stays constant.', 6),
  ('neet-physics-work-energy-power-q08', 'neet-physics-work-energy-power', 'The gravitational potential energy of a body of mass m at height h is:', '(1/2)mv^2', 'mv', 'mgh', 'Fvt', 'C', 'Gravitational potential energy near the surface is PE = mgh.', 7),
  ('neet-physics-gravitation-q01', 'neet-physics-gravitation', 'The escape velocity from the surface of the Earth is approximately:', '7.9 km/s', '3.0 x 10^5 km/s', '9.8 km/s', '11.2 km/s', 'D', 'The escape velocity from Earth''s surface is about 11.2 km/s.', 0),
  ('neet-physics-gravitation-q02', 'neet-physics-gravitation', 'Kepler''s law of periods states that the square of the orbital period is proportional to the:', 'Radius', 'Square of the radius', 'Cube of the semi-major axis', 'Mass of the planet', 'C', 'Kepler''s third law states T^2 is proportional to a^3, the cube of the semi-major axis.', 1),
  ('neet-physics-gravitation-q03', 'neet-physics-gravitation', 'The value of g at a planet''s surface is given by:', 'g = GM/R', 'g = GMR', 'g = G/R^2', 'g = GM/R^2', 'D', 'Acceleration due to gravity at the surface is g = GM/R^2.', 2),
  ('neet-physics-gravitation-q04', 'neet-physics-gravitation', 'A geostationary satellite has an orbital period of:', '24 hours', '12 hours', '1 hour', '365 days', 'A', 'A geostationary satellite orbits with a period of 24 hours, matching Earth''s rotation.', 3),
  ('neet-physics-gravitation-q05', 'neet-physics-gravitation', 'The acceleration due to gravity g is maximum at the:', 'Poles', 'Equator', 'Centre of the Earth', 'Top of a mountain', 'A', 'g is maximum at the poles and decreases with altitude and depth.', 4),
  ('neet-physics-gravitation-q06', 'neet-physics-gravitation', 'The weightlessness felt by an astronaut in orbit is due to the state of:', 'Zero gravity in space', 'High speed only', 'Free fall', 'Absence of mass', 'C', 'Orbital weightlessness arises because the satellite and astronaut are in continuous free fall.', 5),
  ('neet-physics-gravitation-q07', 'neet-physics-gravitation', 'According to Kepler''s law of areas, a planet sweeps out:', 'Unequal areas in equal times', 'Equal areas in unequal times', 'Equal areas in equal times', 'No area at all', 'C', 'Kepler''s second law states a planet sweeps equal areas in equal time intervals.', 6),
  ('neet-physics-gravitation-q08', 'neet-physics-gravitation', 'Newton''s law of gravitation states that the force between two masses is:', 'Inversely proportional to the square of the distance', 'Directly proportional to the distance', 'Independent of distance', 'Inversely proportional to the distance', 'A', 'Gravitational force varies as 1/r^2, the inverse square of the separation between masses.', 7),
  ('neet-physics-thermodynamics-q01', 'neet-physics-thermodynamics', 'In an adiabatic process, the quantity that stays zero is:', 'Heat exchanged with surroundings', 'Change in internal energy', 'Work done', 'Pressure', 'A', 'An adiabatic process exchanges no heat with the surroundings (delta Q = 0).', 0),
  ('neet-physics-thermodynamics-q02', 'neet-physics-thermodynamics', 'The first law of thermodynamics is expressed as:', 'delta Q = delta U - delta W', 'delta Q = delta W - delta U', 'delta Q = delta U + delta W', 'delta U = 0', 'C', 'The first law states delta Q = delta U + delta W, conserving energy.', 1),
  ('neet-physics-thermodynamics-q03', 'neet-physics-thermodynamics', 'The most efficient engine operating between two given temperatures is the:', 'Petrol engine', 'Carnot engine', 'Diesel engine', 'Steam engine', 'B', 'The Carnot engine is the most efficient possible engine between two temperatures.', 2),
  ('neet-physics-thermodynamics-q04', 'neet-physics-thermodynamics', 'In an isochoric process (constant volume), the work done by the gas is:', 'Zero', 'Maximum', 'Equal to delta U', 'Negative', 'A', 'At constant volume no work is done (W = P delta V = 0) since delta V = 0.', 3),
  ('neet-physics-thermodynamics-q05', 'neet-physics-thermodynamics', 'For an ideal gas, Mayer''s relation states that:', 'Cp - Cv = R', 'Cp + Cv = R', 'Cp = Cv', 'Cv - Cp = R', 'A', 'Mayer''s relation for an ideal gas is Cp - Cv = R.', 4),
  ('neet-physics-thermodynamics-q06', 'neet-physics-thermodynamics', 'According to the second law, heat flows spontaneously from a body at:', 'High to low temperature', 'Low to high temperature', 'Equal temperatures only', 'Any direction', 'A', 'Heat flows spontaneously from a hotter body to a colder body (second law).', 5),
  ('neet-physics-thermodynamics-q07', 'neet-physics-thermodynamics', 'The efficiency of a Carnot engine working between hot (Th) and cold (Tc) reservoirs is:', '1 - Tc/Th', 'Tc/Th', 'Th/Tc', '1 + Tc/Th', 'A', 'Carnot efficiency is 1 - Tc/Th, with temperatures in kelvin.', 6),
  ('neet-physics-thermodynamics-q08', 'neet-physics-thermodynamics', 'In an isothermal process for an ideal gas, the change in internal energy is:', 'Maximum', 'Negative', 'Zero', 'Equal to the heat supplied', 'C', 'At constant temperature, an ideal gas has delta U = 0.', 7),
  ('neet-physics-oscillations-waves-q01', 'neet-physics-oscillations-waves', 'In simple harmonic motion, the restoring force is proportional to and directed:', 'Same as the displacement', 'Opposite to the displacement', 'Perpendicular to displacement', 'Independent of displacement', 'B', 'In SHM the restoring force is proportional to and directed opposite to the displacement.', 0),
  ('neet-physics-oscillations-waves-q02', 'neet-physics-oscillations-waves', 'Sound waves are an example of which type of wave?', 'Transverse', 'Standing only', 'Electromagnetic', 'Longitudinal', 'D', 'Sound is a longitudinal wave in which particles vibrate parallel to wave propagation.', 1),
  ('neet-physics-oscillations-waves-q03', 'neet-physics-oscillations-waves', 'The time period of a simple pendulum of length L is given by:', 'T = 2 pi sqrt(m/k)', 'T = 2 pi L g', 'T = 2 pi sqrt(g/L)', 'T = 2 pi sqrt(L/g)', 'D', 'The period of a simple pendulum is T = 2 pi sqrt(L/g).', 2),
  ('neet-physics-oscillations-waves-q04', 'neet-physics-oscillations-waves', 'Sound cannot travel through:', 'Air', 'A vacuum', 'Steel', 'Water', 'B', 'Sound needs a material medium and cannot propagate through a vacuum.', 3),
  ('neet-physics-oscillations-waves-q05', 'neet-physics-oscillations-waves', 'Two sound waves of slightly different frequencies superpose to produce:', 'Beats', 'Resonance', 'Echo', 'Reverberation', 'A', 'Beats arise from two waves of slightly different frequencies; beat frequency = |f1 - f2|.', 4),
  ('neet-physics-oscillations-waves-q06', 'neet-physics-oscillations-waves', 'The relation between wave speed, frequency and wavelength is:', 'v = f/lambda', 'v = f x lambda', 'v = lambda/f', 'v = f + lambda', 'B', 'Wave speed equals frequency multiplied by wavelength (v = f times lambda).', 5),
  ('neet-physics-oscillations-waves-q07', 'neet-physics-oscillations-waves', 'The apparent change in observed frequency due to relative motion of source and observer is the:', 'Bohr effect', 'Photoelectric effect', 'Doppler effect', 'Compton effect', 'C', 'The Doppler effect changes the observed frequency when source and observer move relative to each other.', 6),
  ('neet-physics-oscillations-waves-q08', 'neet-physics-oscillations-waves', 'Resonance occurs when the forcing frequency equals the system''s:', 'Beat frequency', 'Natural frequency', 'Half-frequency', 'Zero frequency', 'B', 'Resonance occurs when the driving frequency matches the natural frequency, giving large amplitude.', 7),
  ('neet-physics-electrostatics-q01', 'neet-physics-electrostatics', 'According to Coulomb''s law, the force between two point charges is inversely proportional to:', 'The distance', 'The square of the distance', 'The cube of the distance', 'The product of charges', 'B', 'Coulomb''s law states the force varies as 1/r^2, the inverse square of the separation.', 0),
  ('neet-physics-electrostatics-q02', 'neet-physics-electrostatics', 'The SI unit of capacitance is the:', 'Farad', 'Coulomb', 'Volt', 'Tesla', 'A', 'Capacitance (C = Q/V) is measured in farads (F).', 1),
  ('neet-physics-electrostatics-q03', 'neet-physics-electrostatics', 'The electric field inside a conductor in electrostatic equilibrium is:', 'Maximum', 'Infinite', 'Equal to the surface field', 'Zero', 'D', 'In electrostatic equilibrium the electric field inside a conductor is zero.', 2),
  ('neet-physics-electrostatics-q04', 'neet-physics-electrostatics', 'Inserting a dielectric between the plates of a capacitor causes its capacitance to:', 'Decrease', 'Become zero', 'Stay the same', 'Increase', 'D', 'A dielectric increases the capacitance of a capacitor.', 3),
  ('neet-physics-electrostatics-q05', 'neet-physics-electrostatics', 'Gauss''s law relates the electric flux through a closed surface to the:', 'Surface area', 'Volume', 'External field only', 'Enclosed charge', 'D', 'Gauss''s law states the total flux equals the enclosed charge divided by epsilon0.', 4),
  ('neet-physics-electrostatics-q06', 'neet-physics-electrostatics', 'The energy stored in a capacitor of capacitance C charged to voltage V is:', 'CV', '(1/2)CV^2', 'C/V', 'CV^2', 'B', 'The energy stored in a charged capacitor is U = (1/2)CV^2.', 5),
  ('neet-physics-electrostatics-q07', 'neet-physics-electrostatics', 'Electric field lines:', 'Cross each other', 'Start on negative and end on positive charges', 'Start on positive and end on negative charges', 'Form closed loops around charges', 'C', 'Electric field lines begin on positive charges and end on negative charges and never cross.', 6),
  ('neet-physics-electrostatics-q08', 'neet-physics-electrostatics', 'The SI unit of electric field intensity is:', 'Newton per coulomb', 'Volt-metre', 'Coulomb per metre', 'Joule per coulomb', 'A', 'Electric field intensity is force per unit charge, measured in N/C (or V/m).', 7),
  ('neet-physics-current-electricity-q01', 'neet-physics-current-electricity', 'Ohm''s law is correctly expressed as:', 'I = VR', 'V = IR', 'R = VI', 'V = I/R', 'B', 'Ohm''s law states V = IR for an ohmic conductor at constant temperature.', 0),
  ('neet-physics-current-electricity-q02', 'neet-physics-current-electricity', 'The equivalent resistance of resistors connected in series is:', 'Less than the smallest resistance', 'The sum of the individual resistances', 'Reciprocal of the sum', 'Always equal to one resistor', 'B', 'In series the equivalent resistance equals the sum R1 + R2 + ...', 1),
  ('neet-physics-current-electricity-q03', 'neet-physics-current-electricity', 'Kirchhoff''s junction rule is a direct consequence of the conservation of:', 'Energy', 'Mass', 'Momentum', 'Charge', 'D', 'The junction rule (current in equals current out) follows from conservation of charge.', 2),
  ('neet-physics-current-electricity-q04', 'neet-physics-current-electricity', 'The resistance of a wire is related to its length L and area A by:', 'R = rho L / A', 'R = rho A / L', 'R = L / (rho A)', 'R = rho L A', 'A', 'Resistance R = rho L / A, increasing with length and decreasing with cross-section.', 3),
  ('neet-physics-current-electricity-q05', 'neet-physics-current-electricity', 'Electrical power dissipated in a resistor can be written as:', 'P = V/I', 'P = I^2 R', 'P = IR^2', 'P = V/R', 'B', 'Power dissipated is P = VI = I^2 R = V^2/R.', 4),
  ('neet-physics-current-electricity-q06', 'neet-physics-current-electricity', 'Kirchhoff''s loop rule is based on the conservation of:', 'Charge', 'Mass', 'Energy', 'Momentum', 'C', 'The loop rule (sum of EMFs equals sum of IR drops) follows from energy conservation.', 5),
  ('neet-physics-current-electricity-q07', 'neet-physics-current-electricity', 'A Wheatstone bridge is said to be balanced when:', 'P = Q', 'P/Q = R/S', 'R = S', 'P x Q = R x S', 'B', 'A Wheatstone bridge is balanced when the ratio P/Q equals R/S, giving zero galvanometer current.', 6),
  ('neet-physics-current-electricity-q08', 'neet-physics-current-electricity', 'In a parallel combination of resistors, the quantity that is the same across each resistor is the:', 'Current', 'Power', 'Resistance', 'Voltage', 'D', 'In a parallel circuit the same voltage is applied across each resistor.', 7),
  ('neet-physics-moving-charges-magnetism-q01', 'neet-physics-moving-charges-magnetism', 'The magnetic force on a charged particle moving in a magnetic field is:', 'Parallel to the velocity', 'Along the field', 'Perpendicular to both velocity and field', 'Zero always', 'C', 'The magnetic (Lorentz) force F = q(v x B) is perpendicular to both velocity and field.', 0),
  ('neet-physics-moving-charges-magnetism-q02', 'neet-physics-moving-charges-magnetism', 'The SI unit of magnetic field strength is the:', 'Weber', 'Tesla', 'Henry', 'Gauss', 'B', 'The SI unit of magnetic field (flux density) is the tesla (T).', 1),
  ('neet-physics-moving-charges-magnetism-q03', 'neet-physics-moving-charges-magnetism', 'Two parallel wires carrying current in the same direction will:', 'Rotate', 'Repel each other', 'Exert no force', 'Attract each other', 'D', 'Parallel currents in the same direction attract each other.', 2),
  ('neet-physics-moving-charges-magnetism-q04', 'neet-physics-moving-charges-magnetism', 'A charged particle entering a uniform magnetic field perpendicular to its velocity follows a:', 'Straight line', 'Parabola', 'Circular path', 'Hyperbola', 'C', 'A charge moving perpendicular to a uniform field moves in a circle of radius r = mv/(qB).', 3),
  ('neet-physics-moving-charges-magnetism-q05', 'neet-physics-moving-charges-magnetism', 'A device that accelerates charged particles using a magnetic field is the:', 'Cyclotron', 'Transformer', 'Galvanometer', 'Voltmeter', 'A', 'A cyclotron uses a magnetic field and alternating voltage to accelerate charged particles.', 4),
  ('neet-physics-moving-charges-magnetism-q06', 'neet-physics-moving-charges-magnetism', 'The magnetic field inside a long solenoid carrying current is given by:', 'B = mu0 n I', 'B = mu0 I / (2 pi r)', 'B = mu0 I / (2R)', 'B = mu0 I R', 'A', 'The field inside a long solenoid is B = mu0 n I, where n is turns per unit length.', 5),
  ('neet-physics-moving-charges-magnetism-q07', 'neet-physics-moving-charges-magnetism', 'A charged particle moving parallel to a magnetic field experiences a force that is:', 'Maximum', 'Zero', 'Perpendicular to B', 'Along B', 'B', 'When velocity is parallel to B, sin(theta) = 0, so the magnetic force is zero.', 6),
  ('neet-physics-moving-charges-magnetism-q08', 'neet-physics-moving-charges-magnetism', 'A moving-coil galvanometer detects small currents by measuring the:', 'Heat produced', 'Charge stored', 'Voltage drop', 'Torque on a current-carrying coil', 'D', 'A galvanometer works on the torque experienced by a current-carrying coil in a magnetic field.', 7),
  ('neet-physics-electromagnetic-induction-ac-q01', 'neet-physics-electromagnetic-induction-ac', 'Faraday''s law states that the induced EMF is equal to the rate of change of:', 'Magnetic flux', 'Current', 'Resistance', 'Charge', 'A', 'Faraday''s law: the induced EMF equals the negative rate of change of magnetic flux.', 0),
  ('neet-physics-electromagnetic-induction-ac-q02', 'neet-physics-electromagnetic-induction-ac', 'Lenz''s law, which says the induced current opposes the change causing it, is a consequence of conservation of:', 'Charge', 'Mass', 'Momentum', 'Energy', 'D', 'Lenz''s law is a statement of energy conservation; the induced current opposes the flux change.', 1),
  ('neet-physics-electromagnetic-induction-ac-q03', 'neet-physics-electromagnetic-induction-ac', 'A transformer can change the voltage of:', 'Direct current only', 'Both AC and DC', 'Alternating current only', 'Neither', 'C', 'A transformer works only on alternating current, using mutual induction.', 2),
  ('neet-physics-electromagnetic-induction-ac-q04', 'neet-physics-electromagnetic-induction-ac', 'The RMS value of an alternating current of peak value I0 is:', 'I0', '2 I0', 'I0 x sqrt(2)', 'I0 / sqrt(2)', 'D', 'The RMS current equals the peak value divided by the square root of two, I0/sqrt(2).', 3),
  ('neet-physics-electromagnetic-induction-ac-q05', 'neet-physics-electromagnetic-induction-ac', 'At resonance in a series LCR circuit, the impedance is:', 'Maximum', 'Infinite', 'Zero', 'Minimum (equal to R)', 'D', 'At resonance XL = XC, so impedance is minimum and equal to the resistance R.', 4),
  ('neet-physics-electromagnetic-induction-ac-q06', 'neet-physics-electromagnetic-induction-ac', 'Inductive reactance XL of a coil in an AC circuit is given by:', '1/(2 pi f L)', '1/(2 pi f C)', '2 pi f C', '2 pi f L', 'D', 'Inductive reactance is XL = 2 pi f L, increasing with frequency.', 5),
  ('neet-physics-electromagnetic-induction-ac-q07', 'neet-physics-electromagnetic-induction-ac', 'For an ideal transformer, the relation between turns and voltages is:', 'Vs/Vp = Ns/Np', 'Vs/Vp = Np/Ns', 'Vs x Vp = Ns x Np', 'Vs = Vp', 'A', 'In an ideal transformer Vs/Vp = Ns/Np, relating secondary and primary turns to voltages.', 6),
  ('neet-physics-electromagnetic-induction-ac-q08', 'neet-physics-electromagnetic-induction-ac', 'The phenomenon by which a coil opposes changes in its own current is called:', 'Mutual induction', 'Hysteresis', 'Eddy currents', 'Self-induction', 'D', 'Self-induction is a coil opposing changes in its own current via an induced back-EMF.', 7),
  ('neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics-q01', 'neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics', 'In the photoelectric effect, the emission of electrons depends on the light''s:', 'Frequency', 'Intensity only', 'Colour temperature', 'Phase', 'A', 'Photoelectron emission depends on frequency; below the threshold frequency no electrons emit.', 0),
  ('neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics-q02', 'neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics', 'The minimum energy required to eject an electron from a metal surface is the:', 'Work function', 'Kinetic energy', 'Binding energy', 'Ionisation energy of gas', 'A', 'The work function is the minimum energy needed to free an electron from the metal surface.', 1),
  ('neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics-q03', 'neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics', 'The de Broglie wavelength of a particle of momentum p is:', 'lambda = h/p', 'lambda = p/h', 'lambda = hp', 'lambda = h + p', 'A', 'The de Broglie wavelength is lambda = h/p, linking a particle''s momentum to its wavelength.', 2),
  ('neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics-q04', 'neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics', 'One electronvolt (eV) is equal to:', '1.6 x 10^-19 joule', '1.6 x 10^19 joule', '9.1 x 10^-31 joule', '6.6 x 10^-34 joule', 'A', 'One electronvolt equals 1.6 x 10^-19 joule.', 3),
  ('neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics-q05', 'neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics', 'The joining of light nuclei to release energy, as in the Sun, is called:', 'Nuclear fission', 'Radioactivity', 'Nuclear fusion', 'Ionisation', 'C', 'Nuclear fusion joins light nuclei into heavier ones, releasing large amounts of energy.', 4),
  ('neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics-q06', 'neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics', 'The energy of a photon of frequency f is given by:', 'E = h/f', 'E = hf', 'E = f/h', 'E = h + f', 'B', 'A photon''s energy is E = hf, where h is Planck''s constant.', 5),
  ('neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics-q07', 'neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics', 'The rest mass of a photon is:', 'Zero', 'Equal to that of an electron', 'Infinite', 'Equal to that of a proton', 'A', 'A photon has zero rest mass yet carries momentum p = h/lambda.', 6),
  ('neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics-q08', 'neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics', 'The Bohr model of the atom introduced the idea of:', 'Quantised energy levels', 'Continuous energy levels', 'A solid nucleus only', 'Electron-free atoms', 'A', 'Bohr proposed quantised energy levels (stationary orbits) to explain atomic spectra.', 7),
  ('neet-physics-semiconductor-electronics-q01', 'neet-physics-semiconductor-electronics', 'In an n-type semiconductor the majority charge carriers are:', 'Holes', 'Protons', 'Electrons', 'Neutrons', 'C', 'n-type semiconductors are doped with pentavalent atoms, making electrons the majority carriers.', 0),
  ('neet-physics-semiconductor-electronics-q02', 'neet-physics-semiconductor-electronics', 'A p-n junction diode conducts current when it is:', 'Reverse biased', 'Forward biased', 'Unbiased', 'At absolute zero', 'B', 'A diode conducts in forward bias and blocks current in reverse bias.', 1),
  ('neet-physics-semiconductor-electronics-q03', 'neet-physics-semiconductor-electronics', 'A p-type semiconductor is produced by doping silicon with a:', 'Pentavalent impurity', 'Trivalent impurity', 'Tetravalent impurity', 'Noble gas', 'B', 'Doping with a trivalent impurity (like boron) creates holes, giving a p-type semiconductor.', 2),
  ('neet-physics-semiconductor-electronics-q04', 'neet-physics-semiconductor-electronics', 'A diode is most commonly used in a circuit as a:', 'Amplifier', 'Transformer', 'Oscillator', 'Rectifier', 'D', 'A diode acts as a rectifier, converting alternating current (AC) to direct current (DC).', 3),
  ('neet-physics-semiconductor-electronics-q05', 'neet-physics-semiconductor-electronics', 'As temperature increases, the resistance of a pure semiconductor:', 'Increases', 'Becomes infinite', 'Stays constant', 'Decreases', 'D', 'In a semiconductor, more carriers are generated with rising temperature, so resistance decreases.', 4),
  ('neet-physics-semiconductor-electronics-q06', 'neet-physics-semiconductor-electronics', 'The diode used for voltage regulation operating in reverse breakdown is the:', 'LED', 'Rectifier diode', 'Photodiode', 'Zener diode', 'D', 'A Zener diode operates in reverse breakdown and is used for voltage regulation.', 5),
  ('neet-physics-semiconductor-electronics-q07', 'neet-physics-semiconductor-electronics', 'The forbidden energy gap of silicon is approximately:', '0.1 eV', '1.1 eV', '5.5 eV', '11 eV', 'B', 'The forbidden energy gap of silicon is about 1.1 eV.', 6),
  ('neet-physics-semiconductor-electronics-q08', 'neet-physics-semiconductor-electronics', 'A transistor can be used to:', 'Store charge only', 'Generate magnetic fields', 'Amplify signals or act as a switch', 'Rectify only', 'C', 'A transistor can amplify signals or act as an electronic switch.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-cell-structure-function-q09', 'neet-biology-cell-structure-function', 'The ''powerhouse of the cell'' is the:', 'Ribosome', 'Mitochondrion', 'Golgi apparatus', 'Lysosome', 'B', 'Mitochondria carry out aerobic respiration, producing most of the cell''s ATP.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-cell-structure-function-q10', 'neet-biology-cell-structure-function', 'Which organelle is called the ''suicidal bag'' of the cell?', 'Lysosome', 'Nucleus', 'Peroxisome', 'Vacuole', 'A', 'Lysosomes contain hydrolytic enzymes and can digest the cell''s own components.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-cell-structure-function-q11', 'neet-biology-cell-structure-function', 'The fluid mosaic model of the plasma membrane was proposed by:', 'Watson and Crick', 'Singer and Nicolson', 'Robert Hooke', 'Schleiden and Schwann', 'B', 'Singer and Nicolson (1972) described the membrane as a fluid lipid bilayer with mobile proteins.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-cell-structure-function-q12', 'neet-biology-cell-structure-function', 'Which feature is present in prokaryotic but NOT eukaryotic cells?', 'A true membrane-bound nucleus', '70S ribosomes and no membrane-bound organelles', 'Mitochondria', 'Endoplasmic reticulum', 'B', 'Prokaryotes lack a true nucleus and membrane-bound organelles and have 70S ribosomes.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-human-physiology-digestion-q09', 'neet-biology-human-physiology-digestion', 'The enzyme that begins starch digestion in the mouth is:', 'Pepsin', 'Salivary amylase (ptyalin)', 'Trypsin', 'Lipase', 'B', 'Salivary amylase hydrolyses starch into maltose in the buccal cavity.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-human-physiology-digestion-q10', 'neet-biology-human-physiology-digestion', 'Protein digestion in the stomach is carried out by:', 'Amylase', 'Pepsin (activated by HCl)', 'Maltase', 'Bile salts', 'B', 'Pepsinogen is converted by HCl to pepsin, which digests proteins into peptides.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-human-physiology-digestion-q11', 'neet-biology-human-physiology-digestion', 'Bile, which emulsifies fats, is produced by the:', 'Pancreas', 'Liver', 'Gall bladder', 'Stomach', 'B', 'The liver produces bile (stored in the gall bladder), which emulsifies fats for digestion.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-human-physiology-digestion-q12', 'neet-biology-human-physiology-digestion', 'Most absorption of digested food occurs in the:', 'Stomach', 'Small intestine (villi)', 'Large intestine', 'Oesophagus', 'B', 'The small intestine''s villi provide a large surface area for absorbing nutrients.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division-q09', 'neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division', 'DNA replication occurs during which phase of the cell cycle?', 'G1 phase', 'S phase', 'G2 phase', 'M phase', 'B', 'The S (synthesis) phase of interphase is when DNA is replicated.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division-q10', 'neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division', 'Crossing over, which increases genetic variation, occurs during:', 'Prophase I of meiosis', 'Metaphase of mitosis', 'Anaphase II', 'Telophase', 'A', 'Crossing over between homologous chromosomes occurs in the pachytene of prophase I.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division-q11', 'neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division', 'Meiosis results in daughter cells that are:', 'Diploid and identical', 'Haploid with half the chromosome number', 'Tetraploid', 'Identical to the parent', 'B', 'Meiosis halves the chromosome number, producing four haploid cells for gametes.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division-q12', 'neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division', 'Sister chromatids separate during:', 'Anaphase of mitosis', 'Prophase', 'Metaphase I', 'Prophase I', 'A', 'In mitotic anaphase, sister chromatids are pulled to opposite poles.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-biomolecules-q09', 'neet-biology-biomolecules', 'The monomers of proteins are:', 'Nucleotides', 'Amino acids', 'Monosaccharides', 'Fatty acids', 'B', 'Proteins are polymers of amino acids linked by peptide bonds.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-biomolecules-q10', 'neet-biology-biomolecules', 'Which bond links two monosaccharides in a disaccharide?', 'Peptide bond', 'Glycosidic bond', 'Phosphodiester bond', 'Hydrogen bond', 'B', 'Monosaccharides join through glycosidic bonds to form di- and polysaccharides.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-biomolecules-q11', 'neet-biology-biomolecules', 'Enzymes lower the ______ of a reaction:', 'Activation energy', 'Temperature', 'Substrate concentration', 'Product amount', 'A', 'Enzymes are biological catalysts that lower activation energy, speeding reactions.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-biomolecules-q12', 'neet-biology-biomolecules', 'The secondary structure of proteins (α-helix, β-pleated sheet) is stabilised by:', 'Peptide bonds', 'Hydrogen bonds', 'Disulphide bonds', 'Ionic bonds', 'B', 'Hydrogen bonds between backbone atoms stabilise α-helices and β-sheets.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants-q09', 'neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants', 'The light reactions of photosynthesis occur in the:', 'Stroma', 'Thylakoid membranes', 'Cytoplasm', 'Mitochondria', 'B', 'Light-dependent reactions occur on the thylakoid membranes of chloroplasts.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants-q10', 'neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants', 'The Calvin cycle (dark reaction) fixes CO₂ in the:', 'Thylakoid lumen', 'Stroma', 'Grana', 'Nucleus', 'B', 'Carbon fixation via the Calvin cycle takes place in the stroma of the chloroplast.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants-q11', 'neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants', 'The primary CO₂ acceptor in C3 plants is:', 'PEP', 'RuBP', 'Glucose', 'Oxaloacetate', 'B', 'In C3 plants, RuBP accepts CO₂ via the enzyme RuBisCO to form 3-PGA.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants-q12', 'neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants', 'C4 plants (e.g., maize) minimise photorespiration using the anatomy called:', 'Palisade anatomy', 'Kranz anatomy', 'Spongy anatomy', 'Reticulate anatomy', 'B', 'Kranz anatomy concentrates CO₂ around RuBisCO in bundle-sheath cells, reducing photorespiration.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-plant-animal-tissues-q09', 'neet-biology-plant-animal-tissues', 'Which plant tissue conducts water from roots to leaves?', 'Phloem', 'Xylem', 'Collenchyma', 'Epidermis', 'B', 'Xylem transports water and minerals upward; phloem transports food.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-plant-animal-tissues-q10', 'neet-biology-plant-animal-tissues', 'Cardiac muscle is characterised as:', 'Voluntary and striated', 'Involuntary and striated', 'Voluntary and smooth', 'Involuntary and smooth', 'B', 'Cardiac muscle is striated but involuntary, contracting rhythmically without fatigue.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-plant-animal-tissues-q11', 'neet-biology-plant-animal-tissues', 'Blood is classified as which type of animal tissue?', 'Epithelial', 'Connective', 'Muscular', 'Neural', 'B', 'Blood is a fluid connective tissue with a liquid matrix (plasma).', 10),
  ('neet-biology-plant-animal-tissues-q12', 'neet-biology-plant-animal-tissues', 'Which meristem is responsible for increase in girth (secondary growth)?', 'Apical meristem', 'Lateral meristem', 'Intercalary meristem', 'Ground meristem', 'B', 'Lateral meristems (vascular and cork cambium) add to the plant''s thickness.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-breathing-exchange-of-gases-q09', 'neet-biology-breathing-exchange-of-gases', 'Most oxygen is transported in blood as:', 'Dissolved in plasma', 'Oxyhaemoglobin', 'Bicarbonate', 'Carbaminohaemoglobin', 'B', 'About 97% of O₂ is carried bound to haemoglobin as oxyhaemoglobin.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-breathing-exchange-of-gases-q10', 'neet-biology-breathing-exchange-of-gases', 'The exchange of gases in the lungs occurs at the:', 'Bronchi', 'Alveoli', 'Trachea', 'Larynx', 'B', 'Alveoli provide a thin, large surface for gas exchange by diffusion.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-breathing-exchange-of-gases-q11', 'neet-biology-breathing-exchange-of-gases', 'Most carbon dioxide is transported in blood as:', 'Bicarbonate ions', 'Dissolved gas only', 'Carbonic acid crystals', 'Bound to plasma proteins only', 'A', 'About 70% of CO₂ is carried as bicarbonate (HCO₃⁻) formed in red blood cells.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-breathing-exchange-of-gases-q12', 'neet-biology-breathing-exchange-of-gases', 'The volume of air breathed in or out during normal quiet breathing is the:', 'Tidal volume', 'Vital capacity', 'Residual volume', 'Total lung capacity', 'A', 'Tidal volume (~500 mL) is the air exchanged in a normal breath.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-body-fluids-circulation-q09', 'neet-biology-body-fluids-circulation', 'The natural pacemaker of the human heart is the:', 'AV node', 'SA node', 'Purkinje fibres', 'Bundle of His', 'B', 'The sino-atrial (SA) node initiates each heartbeat and sets the rhythm.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-body-fluids-circulation-q10', 'neet-biology-body-fluids-circulation', 'Which blood cells are involved in clotting?', 'Erythrocytes', 'Platelets (thrombocytes)', 'Lymphocytes', 'Monocytes', 'B', 'Platelets release clotting factors and help form the blood clot.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-body-fluids-circulation-q11', 'neet-biology-body-fluids-circulation', 'The universal blood donor group is:', 'AB positive', 'O negative', 'A positive', 'B negative', 'B', 'O negative has no A, B, or Rh antigens, so it can be given to any recipient.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-body-fluids-circulation-q12', 'neet-biology-body-fluids-circulation', 'In double circulation, blood passes through the heart:', 'Once per cycle', 'Twice per cycle', 'Thrice per cycle', 'Never', 'B', 'Double circulation sends blood through the heart twice — pulmonary and systemic circuits.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-excretory-products-elimination-q09', 'neet-biology-excretory-products-elimination', 'The functional unit of the kidney is the:', 'Neuron', 'Nephron', 'Alveolus', 'Villus', 'B', 'The nephron filters blood and forms urine; each kidney has about a million.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-excretory-products-elimination-q10', 'neet-biology-excretory-products-elimination', 'Humans are ureotelic, meaning they mainly excrete:', 'Ammonia', 'Urea', 'Uric acid', 'Amino acids', 'B', 'Humans convert toxic ammonia to less toxic urea for excretion (ureotelic).', 9),
  ('neet-biology-excretory-products-elimination-q11', 'neet-biology-excretory-products-elimination', 'Filtration of blood occurs in the:', 'Loop of Henle', 'Malpighian corpuscle (glomerulus + Bowman''s capsule)', 'Collecting duct', 'Ureter', 'B', 'Ultrafiltration occurs at the glomerulus into Bowman''s capsule.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-excretory-products-elimination-q12', 'neet-biology-excretory-products-elimination', 'ADH (vasopressin) regulates urine by promoting:', 'Water reabsorption in the kidney', 'Water loss', 'Salt excretion', 'Glucose excretion', 'A', 'ADH increases water reabsorption from the collecting ducts, concentrating urine.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-neural-control-coordination-q09', 'neet-biology-neural-control-coordination', 'The structural and functional unit of the nervous system is the:', 'Nephron', 'Neuron', 'Neuroglia', 'Axon hillock', 'B', 'The neuron is the basic unit transmitting nerve impulses.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-neural-control-coordination-q10', 'neet-biology-neural-control-coordination', 'The resting membrane potential of a neuron is maintained mainly by the:', 'Sodium–potassium pump', 'Calcium pump', 'Proton pump', 'Chloride channel', 'A', 'The Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase pumps 3 Na⁺ out and 2 K⁺ in, maintaining the resting potential.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-neural-control-coordination-q11', 'neet-biology-neural-control-coordination', 'Across a synapse, the nerve impulse is transmitted by:', 'Direct electrical contact only', 'Neurotransmitters', 'Hormones in blood', 'The myelin sheath', 'B', 'Chemical synapses release neurotransmitters (e.g., acetylcholine) into the synaptic cleft.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-neural-control-coordination-q12', 'neet-biology-neural-control-coordination', 'The part of the brain controlling balance and coordination is the:', 'Cerebrum', 'Cerebellum', 'Medulla oblongata', 'Hypothalamus', 'B', 'The cerebellum coordinates muscular activity and maintains balance and posture.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation-q09', 'neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation', 'Mendel''s law of segregation states that:', 'Alleles blend in offspring', 'Paired alleles separate during gamete formation', 'All genes are linked', 'Traits skip generations always', 'B', 'Each gamete receives only one of the two alleles for a trait (segregation).', 8),
  ('neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation-q10', 'neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation', 'The phenotypic ratio of a monohybrid cross (F2) is:', '9:3:3:1', '3:1', '1:1', '1:2:1', 'B', 'A monohybrid F2 shows a 3:1 dominant-to-recessive phenotypic ratio.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation-q11', 'neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation', 'Colour blindness and haemophilia are examples of:', 'Autosomal dominant traits', 'X-linked recessive traits', 'Y-linked traits', 'Mitochondrial traits', 'B', 'These are X-linked recessive disorders, more common in males.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation-q12', 'neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation', 'A dihybrid cross F2 gives a phenotypic ratio of:', '3:1', '9:3:3:1', '1:1', '1:2:1', 'B', 'Independent assortment of two traits gives the classic 9:3:3:1 ratio.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance-q09', 'neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance', 'In DNA, adenine pairs with:', 'Guanine', 'Thymine', 'Cytosine', 'Uracil', 'B', 'In DNA, A pairs with T (two hydrogen bonds) and G with C (three).', 8),
  ('neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance-q10', 'neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance', 'The process of making mRNA from a DNA template is called:', 'Replication', 'Transcription', 'Translation', 'Transduction', 'B', 'Transcription synthesises RNA from a DNA template using RNA polymerase.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance-q11', 'neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance', 'The experiment establishing DNA as the genetic material in bacteriophages was by:', 'Hershey and Chase', 'Mendel', 'Darwin', 'Morgan', 'A', 'Hershey and Chase used radioactive labelling to show DNA (not protein) is the genetic material.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance-q12', 'neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance', 'DNA replication is described as semi-conservative, meaning each new molecule has:', 'Two new strands', 'One parental and one new strand', 'Two parental strands', 'Only RNA', 'B', 'Meselson and Stahl showed each daughter DNA keeps one original strand and one new one.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-evolution-q09', 'neet-biology-evolution', 'Natural selection as the mechanism of evolution was proposed by:', 'Lamarck', 'Charles Darwin', 'Mendel', 'Hugo de Vries', 'B', 'Darwin proposed natural selection (''survival of the fittest'') in On the Origin of Species.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-evolution-q10', 'neet-biology-evolution', 'Homologous organs (e.g., forelimbs of humans and whales) indicate:', 'Convergent evolution', 'Divergent evolution (common ancestry)', 'No relationship', 'Spontaneous generation', 'B', 'Homologous organs share structure from a common ancestor — divergent evolution.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-evolution-q11', 'neet-biology-evolution', 'The Hardy–Weinberg principle describes conditions under which:', 'Allele frequencies remain constant across generations', 'Mutations always occur', 'Natural selection is strong', 'Populations always evolve', 'A', 'In an idealised population (no selection, mutation, migration, drift), allele frequencies stay constant.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-evolution-q12', 'neet-biology-evolution', 'Analogous organs (e.g., wings of insects and birds) are the result of:', 'Convergent evolution', 'Divergent evolution', 'Common ancestry', 'Genetic drift', 'A', 'Analogous organs have different origins but similar function — convergent evolution.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-human-reproduction-q09', 'neet-biology-human-reproduction', 'The site of fertilisation in humans is the:', 'Uterus', 'Ampulla of the fallopian tube', 'Ovary', 'Cervix', 'B', 'Fertilisation normally occurs in the ampullary region of the oviduct.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-human-reproduction-q10', 'neet-biology-human-reproduction', 'The process of sperm formation in the testes is called:', 'Oogenesis', 'Spermatogenesis', 'Ovulation', 'Implantation', 'B', 'Spermatogenesis produces sperm in the seminiferous tubules of the testes.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-human-reproduction-q11', 'neet-biology-human-reproduction', 'The hormone that maintains pregnancy by sustaining the corpus luteum early on is:', 'hCG', 'FSH', 'LH', 'Oxytocin', 'A', 'Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) from the embryo maintains the corpus luteum.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-human-reproduction-q12', 'neet-biology-human-reproduction', 'Implantation of the blastocyst occurs in the:', 'Ovary', 'Endometrium of the uterus', 'Fallopian tube', 'Vagina', 'B', 'The blastocyst embeds in the uterine endometrium during implantation.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-biotechnology-principles-q09', 'neet-biology-biotechnology-principles', 'Restriction enzymes are used in genetic engineering to:', 'Join DNA fragments', 'Cut DNA at specific sequences', 'Replicate DNA', 'Translate mRNA', 'B', 'Restriction endonucleases cut DNA at specific palindromic recognition sites (''molecular scissors'').', 8),
  ('neet-biology-biotechnology-principles-q10', 'neet-biology-biotechnology-principles', 'The enzyme that joins DNA fragments is:', 'DNA ligase', 'DNA polymerase', 'Restriction enzyme', 'Helicase', 'A', 'DNA ligase seals the phosphodiester backbone, joining fragments (''molecular glue'').', 9),
  ('neet-biology-biotechnology-principles-q11', 'neet-biology-biotechnology-principles', 'PCR (polymerase chain reaction) is used to:', 'Cut DNA', 'Amplify DNA in vitro', 'Translate proteins', 'Sequence proteins', 'B', 'PCR amplifies specific DNA segments using primers and a thermostable Taq polymerase.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-biotechnology-principles-q12', 'neet-biology-biotechnology-principles', 'A commonly used vector to carry foreign DNA into bacteria is a:', 'Ribosome', 'Plasmid', 'Lysosome', 'Centriole', 'B', 'Plasmids are small, self-replicating circular DNA used as cloning vectors.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity-q09', 'neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity', 'The flow of energy through an ecosystem is:', 'Cyclic', 'Unidirectional', 'Bidirectional', 'Absent', 'B', 'Energy flows one way (sun → producers → consumers), dissipating as heat, unlike cycling nutrients.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity-q10', 'neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity', 'Organisms that make their own food are called:', 'Producers (autotrophs)', 'Consumers', 'Decomposers', 'Parasites', 'A', 'Autotrophs (e.g., green plants) synthesise organic food and form the base of food chains.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity-q11', 'neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity', 'According to the 10% law, energy transferred to the next trophic level is about:', '10%', '50%', '90%', '100%', 'A', 'Only about 10% of energy passes to the next trophic level; the rest is lost as heat.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity-q12', 'neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity', 'The greatest biodiversity on Earth is found in:', 'Deserts', 'Tropical rainforests', 'Tundra', 'Grasslands', 'B', 'Tropical rainforests, especially the Amazon, harbour the highest species diversity.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-human-health-disease-q09', 'neet-biology-human-health-disease', 'Malaria is caused by which pathogen?', 'A bacterium', 'Plasmodium (a protozoan)', 'A virus', 'A fungus', 'B', 'Malaria is caused by Plasmodium, transmitted by the female Anopheles mosquito.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-human-health-disease-q10', 'neet-biology-human-health-disease', 'Antibodies are produced by which type of cell?', 'T lymphocytes', 'B lymphocytes', 'Red blood cells', 'Platelets', 'B', 'B lymphocytes differentiate into plasma cells that secrete antibodies (humoral immunity).', 9),
  ('neet-biology-human-health-disease-q11', 'neet-biology-human-health-disease', 'AIDS is caused by:', 'HIV, which attacks helper T-cells', 'A bacterium', 'A protozoan', 'A fungus', 'A', 'HIV infects and destroys helper T-lymphocytes, weakening the immune system.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-human-health-disease-q12', 'neet-biology-human-health-disease', 'Active immunity is acquired by:', 'Receiving ready-made antibodies', 'Exposure to an antigen (infection or vaccine)', 'Mother''s milk only', 'Blood transfusion', 'B', 'Active immunity develops when the body makes its own antibodies after antigen exposure.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-the-living-world-q01', 'neet-biology-the-living-world', 'The system of giving two-word Latin names to species is called:', 'Vernacular naming', 'Binomial nomenclature', 'Trinomial nomenclature', 'Common naming', 'B', 'Binomial nomenclature (Linnaeus) gives each species a Genus + species name.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-the-living-world-q02', 'neet-biology-the-living-world', 'The correct sequence of taxonomic categories from broad to narrow is:', 'Species → Genus → Family → Kingdom', 'Kingdom → Phylum → Class → Order → Family → Genus → Species', 'Genus → Species → Family → Order', 'Order → Class → Kingdom → Species', 'B', 'The hierarchy runs Kingdom → Phylum → Class → Order → Family → Genus → Species.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-the-living-world-q03', 'neet-biology-the-living-world', 'The only defining characteristic common to all living organisms is:', 'Consciousness', 'Metabolism', 'Locomotion', 'Photosynthesis', 'B', 'Metabolism (the sum of chemical reactions) is a defining property of all living things.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-the-living-world-q04', 'neet-biology-the-living-world', 'In a scientific name, the genus name is:', 'Written in lowercase', 'Capitalised', 'Never italicised', 'Placed second', 'B', 'The genus is capitalised and the species name is lowercase; both are italicised.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-the-living-world-q05', 'neet-biology-the-living-world', 'A group of organisms that can interbreed to produce fertile offspring is a:', 'Genus', 'Species', 'Family', 'Order', 'B', 'A species is the basic unit — a group of interbreeding, reproductively isolated organisms.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-the-living-world-q06', 'neet-biology-the-living-world', 'Which is a taxonomic aid that stores dried, pressed plant specimens?', 'Zoo', 'Herbarium', 'Aquarium', 'Botanical key', 'B', 'A herbarium is a store of dried, pressed, and preserved plant specimens on sheets.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-the-living-world-q07', 'neet-biology-the-living-world', 'Taxonomy that deals with naming organisms is:', 'Nomenclature', 'Identification only', 'Ecology', 'Physiology', 'A', 'Nomenclature is the process of assigning standardised scientific names.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-the-living-world-q08', 'neet-biology-the-living-world', 'The defining property unique to human beings among living organisms is:', 'Growth', 'Consciousness (self-awareness)', 'Reproduction', 'Respiration', 'B', 'Self-consciousness is regarded as the defining feature distinguishing humans.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-the-living-world-q09', 'neet-biology-the-living-world', 'ICBN stands for the international code that governs naming of:', 'Animals', 'Plants (botanical names)', 'Bacteria only', 'Viruses', 'B', 'The International Code of Botanical (now algae, fungi and plants) Nomenclature governs plant names.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-the-living-world-q10', 'neet-biology-the-living-world', 'A monograph contains information on:', 'All members of a group at one time', 'A single taxon in detail', 'Only fossils', 'Only local flora', 'B', 'A monograph gives comprehensive information on any one taxon.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-the-living-world-q11', 'neet-biology-the-living-world', 'Growth in living organisms is defined as an increase in:', 'Only external size', 'Mass and number of cells', 'Temperature', 'Colour', 'B', 'Biological growth is an increase in cell number and body mass (intrinsic in living things).', 10),
  ('neet-biology-the-living-world-q12', 'neet-biology-the-living-world', 'The taxonomic category between Class and Family is:', 'Order', 'Genus', 'Phylum', 'Species', 'A', 'Order lies between Class and Family in the taxonomic hierarchy.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-biological-classification-q01', 'neet-biology-biological-classification', 'The five-kingdom classification was proposed by:', 'Linnaeus', 'R. H. Whittaker', 'Ernst Haeckel', 'Carl Woese', 'B', 'Whittaker (1969) proposed the five kingdoms: Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae, Animalia.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-biological-classification-q02', 'neet-biology-biological-classification', 'Kingdom Monera consists of:', 'Eukaryotic algae', 'Prokaryotes (bacteria)', 'Fungi', 'Protozoa', 'B', 'Monera includes all prokaryotic organisms — the bacteria.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-biological-classification-q03', 'neet-biology-biological-classification', 'Which kingdom includes heterotrophic organisms with a chitinous cell wall?', 'Plantae', 'Fungi', 'Monera', 'Animalia', 'B', 'Fungi are heterotrophs with cell walls made of chitin.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-biological-classification-q04', 'neet-biology-biological-classification', 'Archaebacteria that live in extreme salty habitats are called:', 'Halophiles', 'Methanogens', 'Thermoacidophiles', 'Cyanobacteria', 'A', 'Halophiles are archaebacteria adapted to extreme salt concentrations.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-biological-classification-q05', 'neet-biology-biological-classification', 'Lichens are a symbiotic association between:', 'A fungus and an alga', 'Two fungi', 'A plant and a bacterium', 'Two algae', 'A', 'A lichen is a mutualism between a fungus (mycobiont) and an alga/cyanobacterium (phycobiont).', 4),
  ('neet-biology-biological-classification-q06', 'neet-biology-biological-classification', 'Viruses are not included in the five-kingdom system because they:', 'Are too large', 'Are non-cellular and need a host to reproduce', 'Have chlorophyll', 'Are prokaryotes', 'B', 'Viruses are acellular obligate parasites, so they fall outside the cellular kingdoms.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-biological-classification-q07', 'neet-biology-biological-classification', 'Dinoflagellates and diatoms belong to which kingdom?', 'Monera', 'Protista', 'Fungi', 'Plantae', 'B', 'These unicellular eukaryotic algae are placed in kingdom Protista.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-biological-classification-q08', 'neet-biology-biological-classification', 'The infective, protein-only agents causing diseases like mad-cow disease are:', 'Viroids', 'Prions', 'Bacteria', 'Plasmids', 'B', 'Prions are abnormally folded infectious proteins responsible for spongiform encephalopathies.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-biological-classification-q09', 'neet-biology-biological-classification', 'Which is an example of a symbiotic, nitrogen-fixing cyanobacterium/bacterium?', 'Rhizobium', 'Escherichia coli', 'Lactobacillus', 'Clostridium botulinum', 'A', 'Rhizobium fixes nitrogen symbiotically in the root nodules of legumes.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-biological-classification-q10', 'neet-biology-biological-classification', 'Slime moulds are classified under:', 'Fungi', 'Protista', 'Monera', 'Animalia', 'B', 'Slime moulds are saprophytic protists that form plasmodia.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-biological-classification-q11', 'neet-biology-biological-classification', 'Viroids differ from viruses in that they consist of:', 'Only free RNA without a protein coat', 'Only protein', 'DNA and protein', 'A cell wall', 'A', 'Viroids are small, naked, single-stranded RNA molecules lacking a protein coat.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-biological-classification-q12', 'neet-biology-biological-classification', 'The mode of nutrition in most fungi is:', 'Autotrophic', 'Heterotrophic (saprophytic/parasitic)', 'Chemosynthetic', 'Photosynthetic', 'B', 'Fungi are heterotrophs, absorbing nutrients as saprophytes, parasites, or symbionts.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-plant-kingdom-q01', 'neet-biology-plant-kingdom', 'Bryophytes are often called the ''amphibians of the plant kingdom'' because they:', 'Live only in water', 'Need water for fertilisation but live on land', 'Are aquatic animals', 'Have no cells', 'B', 'Bryophytes live on land but require water for the sperm to reach the egg.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-plant-kingdom-q02', 'neet-biology-plant-kingdom', 'The first vascular plants (with xylem and phloem) are the:', 'Algae', 'Bryophytes', 'Pteridophytes', 'Fungi', 'C', 'Pteridophytes (e.g., ferns) are the earliest plants with true vascular tissue.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-plant-kingdom-q03', 'neet-biology-plant-kingdom', 'Gymnosperms are distinguished by having:', 'Flowers and fruits', 'Naked seeds (not enclosed in an ovary)', 'No seeds', 'Spores only', 'B', 'Gymnosperms bear naked seeds because there is no ovary enclosing the ovules.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-plant-kingdom-q04', 'neet-biology-plant-kingdom', 'In bryophytes, the dominant, photosynthetic phase of the life cycle is the:', 'Sporophyte', 'Gametophyte', 'Zygote', 'Seed', 'B', 'In bryophytes the haploid gametophyte is dominant and the sporophyte is dependent on it.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-plant-kingdom-q05', 'neet-biology-plant-kingdom', 'Double fertilisation and triploid endosperm are characteristic of:', 'Angiosperms', 'Gymnosperms', 'Pteridophytes', 'Bryophytes', 'A', 'Only angiosperms show double fertilisation forming a triploid endosperm.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-plant-kingdom-q06', 'neet-biology-plant-kingdom', 'The life cycle showing an alternation between haploid and diploid phases is called:', 'Alternation of generations', 'Metagenesis of animals', 'Parthenogenesis', 'Binary fission', 'A', 'Plants alternate between a haploid gametophyte and a diploid sporophyte generation.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-plant-kingdom-q07', 'neet-biology-plant-kingdom', 'Red algae owe their colour to the pigment:', 'Chlorophyll a only', 'Phycoerythrin', 'Carotene', 'Xanthophyll', 'B', 'The red pigment phycoerythrin gives Rhodophyceae (red algae) their colour.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-plant-kingdom-q08', 'neet-biology-plant-kingdom', 'In pteridophytes, the dominant phase is the:', 'Gametophyte', 'Sporophyte', 'Zygote', 'Spore', 'B', 'In ferns and other pteridophytes the diploid sporophyte is the main, dominant plant body.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-plant-kingdom-q09', 'neet-biology-plant-kingdom', 'Which of these is a gymnosperm?', 'Mango', 'Pinus (pine)', 'Fern', 'Moss', 'B', 'Pinus is a conifer, a classic gymnosperm bearing naked seeds in cones.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-plant-kingdom-q10', 'neet-biology-plant-kingdom', 'Angiosperms are divided into two classes based on the number of:', 'Petals', 'Cotyledons (monocots and dicots)', 'Roots', 'Stamens', 'B', 'Angiosperms are split into monocotyledons (one cotyledon) and dicotyledons (two).', 9),
  ('neet-biology-plant-kingdom-q11', 'neet-biology-plant-kingdom', 'The reserve food material commonly stored in brown algae is:', 'Starch', 'Laminarin and mannitol', 'Glycogen', 'Cellulose', 'B', 'Brown algae (Phaeophyceae) store food as laminarin and mannitol.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-plant-kingdom-q12', 'neet-biology-plant-kingdom', 'Ferns reproduce by means of:', 'Seeds', 'Spores', 'Flowers', 'Fruits', 'B', 'Pteridophytes like ferns reproduce by spores borne on the underside of fronds.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-animal-kingdom-q01', 'neet-biology-animal-kingdom', 'The largest animal phylum, characterised by jointed appendages, is:', 'Chordata', 'Arthropoda', 'Mollusca', 'Annelida', 'B', 'Arthropoda (insects, crustaceans, etc.) is the largest phylum, with jointed legs and an exoskeleton.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-animal-kingdom-q02', 'neet-biology-animal-kingdom', 'The presence of a notochord at some stage of life is characteristic of:', 'Porifera', 'Chordata', 'Cnidaria', 'Arthropoda', 'B', 'Chordates possess a notochord, dorsal hollow nerve cord, and pharyngeal slits.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-animal-kingdom-q03', 'neet-biology-animal-kingdom', 'Sponges (Porifera) have a body organisation at the level of:', 'Organ system', 'Cellular level', 'Organ level', 'Tissue level', 'B', 'Poriferans show only cellular-level organisation with a canal system.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-animal-kingdom-q04', 'neet-biology-animal-kingdom', 'Stinging cells (nematocysts) are characteristic of the phylum:', 'Cnidaria (Coelenterata)', 'Platyhelminthes', 'Mollusca', 'Echinodermata', 'A', 'Cnidarians (Hydra, jellyfish) have cnidoblasts with stinging nematocysts.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-animal-kingdom-q05', 'neet-biology-animal-kingdom', 'The water vascular system is a unique feature of:', 'Annelida', 'Echinodermata', 'Mollusca', 'Arthropoda', 'B', 'Echinoderms (starfish) have a water vascular system with tube feet for locomotion.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-animal-kingdom-q06', 'neet-biology-animal-kingdom', 'Flatworms belong to the phylum:', 'Aschelminthes', 'Platyhelminthes', 'Annelida', 'Nematoda', 'B', 'Platyhelminthes are dorsoventrally flattened, acoelomate worms (e.g., Planaria, tapeworm).', 5),
  ('neet-biology-animal-kingdom-q07', 'neet-biology-animal-kingdom', 'Metameric segmentation (true segmentation) is first seen in:', 'Annelida', 'Porifera', 'Cnidaria', 'Protozoa', 'A', 'Annelids (earthworm, leech) show true metameric segmentation of the body.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-animal-kingdom-q08', 'neet-biology-animal-kingdom', 'Radial symmetry is characteristic of adult:', 'Humans', 'Echinoderms and cnidarians', 'Insects', 'Earthworms', 'B', 'Cnidarians and adult echinoderms show radial symmetry; most higher animals are bilateral.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-animal-kingdom-q09', 'neet-biology-animal-kingdom', 'Animals having a body cavity lined by mesoderm are called:', 'Acoelomates', 'Coelomates', 'Pseudocoelomates', 'Diploblastic', 'B', 'Coelomates have a true coelom fully lined by mesoderm.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-animal-kingdom-q10', 'neet-biology-animal-kingdom', 'Which class of vertebrates has a three-chambered heart (in adults, except crocodiles)?', 'Mammalia', 'Reptilia', 'Aves', 'Amphibia and Reptilia', 'D', 'Amphibians and most reptiles have a three-chambered heart; birds and mammals have four.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-animal-kingdom-q11', 'neet-biology-animal-kingdom', 'Presence of feathers and a four-chambered heart identifies the class:', 'Aves (birds)', 'Reptilia', 'Amphibia', 'Pisces', 'A', 'Birds (Aves) are feathered, warm-blooded vertebrates with a four-chambered heart.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-animal-kingdom-q12', 'neet-biology-animal-kingdom', 'Mammals are characterised by the presence of:', 'Feathers', 'Mammary glands and hair', 'Scales only', 'Gills throughout life', 'B', 'Mammals have mammary glands (to nourish young) and body hair.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-morphology-flowering-plants-q01', 'neet-biology-morphology-flowering-plants', 'The tap root system is characteristic of:', 'Monocots', 'Dicots', 'Ferns', 'Mosses', 'B', 'Dicots typically have a tap root system; monocots have a fibrous root system.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-morphology-flowering-plants-q02', 'neet-biology-morphology-flowering-plants', 'Parallel venation in leaves is typical of:', 'Dicots', 'Monocots', 'Gymnosperms', 'Algae', 'B', 'Monocot leaves show parallel venation; dicots show reticulate venation.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-morphology-flowering-plants-q03', 'neet-biology-morphology-flowering-plants', 'The green, leaf-like outermost floral whorl is the:', 'Corolla', 'Calyx (sepals)', 'Androecium', 'Gynoecium', 'B', 'The calyx, made of sepals, is the outermost protective whorl of a flower.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-morphology-flowering-plants-q04', 'neet-biology-morphology-flowering-plants', 'The male reproductive part of a flower is the:', 'Gynoecium', 'Androecium (stamens)', 'Calyx', 'Corolla', 'B', 'The androecium consists of stamens, each with an anther and filament.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-morphology-flowering-plants-q05', 'neet-biology-morphology-flowering-plants', 'Placentation where ovules are borne on the central axis of a multi-chambered ovary is:', 'Marginal', 'Axile', 'Parietal', 'Free central', 'B', 'Axile placentation has ovules attached to a central axis in a septate ovary (e.g., tomato).', 4),
  ('neet-biology-morphology-flowering-plants-q06', 'neet-biology-morphology-flowering-plants', 'An inflorescence with the main axis of unlimited growth and flowers borne laterally is:', 'Cymose', 'Racemose', 'Solitary', 'Compound', 'B', 'In racemose inflorescences the main axis grows indefinitely; flowers open bottom-up.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-morphology-flowering-plants-q07', 'neet-biology-morphology-flowering-plants', 'The pea plant (Pisum) belongs to which family, noted for its typical flower?', 'Solanaceae', 'Fabaceae (Leguminosae)', 'Liliaceae', 'Poaceae', 'B', 'Pea is a member of Fabaceae, with characteristic papilionaceous flowers and pod fruits.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-morphology-flowering-plants-q08', 'neet-biology-morphology-flowering-plants', 'The edible part of a mango fruit develops from the:', 'Ovule', 'Mesocarp of the pericarp', 'Sepals', 'Anther', 'B', 'In a mango (a drupe) the fleshy edible portion is the mesocarp of the pericarp.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-morphology-flowering-plants-q09', 'neet-biology-morphology-flowering-plants', 'A modified underground stem for storage, as in potato, is a:', 'Tuber', 'Tap root', 'Rhizoid', 'Corm-less bulb', 'A', 'A potato is a stem tuber, an underground stem modification with ''eyes'' (buds).', 8),
  ('neet-biology-morphology-flowering-plants-q10', 'neet-biology-morphology-flowering-plants', 'The arrangement of leaves on a stem is called:', 'Aestivation', 'Phyllotaxy', 'Placentation', 'Venation', 'B', 'Phyllotaxy is the pattern of leaf arrangement (alternate, opposite, whorled).', 9),
  ('neet-biology-morphology-flowering-plants-q11', 'neet-biology-morphology-flowering-plants', 'A flower with both androecium and gynoecium is called:', 'Unisexual', 'Bisexual (perfect)', 'Sterile', 'Neuter', 'B', 'A bisexual flower contains both stamens and carpels.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-morphology-flowering-plants-q12', 'neet-biology-morphology-flowering-plants', 'Aestivation refers to the arrangement of:', 'Leaves on a stem', 'Sepals/petals in a floral bud', 'Ovules in an ovary', 'Roots in soil', 'B', 'Aestivation is the mode of arrangement of sepals or petals in a floral bud.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-anatomy-flowering-plants-q01', 'neet-biology-anatomy-flowering-plants', 'Which tissue conducts food in plants?', 'Xylem', 'Phloem', 'Collenchyma', 'Sclerenchyma', 'B', 'Phloem (sieve tubes and companion cells) transports food from source to sink.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-anatomy-flowering-plants-q02', 'neet-biology-anatomy-flowering-plants', 'The dead, lignified supporting tissue in plants is:', 'Parenchyma', 'Sclerenchyma', 'Collenchyma', 'Epidermis', 'B', 'Sclerenchyma cells are dead with thick lignified walls, providing mechanical support.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-anatomy-flowering-plants-q03', 'neet-biology-anatomy-flowering-plants', 'Secondary growth in dicot stems arises from the:', 'Apical meristem', 'Vascular cambium', 'Ground tissue', 'Epidermis', 'B', 'The vascular cambium produces secondary xylem and phloem, increasing girth.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-anatomy-flowering-plants-q04', 'neet-biology-anatomy-flowering-plants', 'In monocot stems, the vascular bundles are:', 'Arranged in a ring', 'Scattered', 'Absent', 'In the pith only', 'B', 'Monocot stems have scattered vascular bundles; dicot stems have them in a ring.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-anatomy-flowering-plants-q05', 'neet-biology-anatomy-flowering-plants', 'Water-conducting elements found only in angiosperms are:', 'Tracheids', 'Vessels', 'Sieve tubes', 'Companion cells', 'B', 'Vessels (efficient water conduits) are characteristic of angiosperm xylem.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-anatomy-flowering-plants-q06', 'neet-biology-anatomy-flowering-plants', 'Annual rings in a tree trunk are due to:', 'Activity of the vascular cambium varying with seasons', 'Leaf fall', 'Root growth', 'Flowering', 'A', 'Seasonal differences in cambial activity produce distinct early and late wood — annual rings.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-anatomy-flowering-plants-q07', 'neet-biology-anatomy-flowering-plants', 'Guard cells regulate the opening and closing of:', 'Lenticels', 'Stomata', 'Vessels', 'Sieve pores', 'B', 'Turgid guard cells open the stomatal pore for gas exchange and transpiration.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-anatomy-flowering-plants-q08', 'neet-biology-anatomy-flowering-plants', 'The living companion cells are associated with:', 'Vessels', 'Sieve tube elements', 'Tracheids', 'Fibres', 'B', 'Companion cells support the enucleate sieve tube elements in phloem.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-anatomy-flowering-plants-q09', 'neet-biology-anatomy-flowering-plants', 'Collenchyma provides support especially in:', 'Woody stems', 'Young, growing stems and petioles', 'Roots only', 'Dead tissues', 'B', 'Collenchyma, with thickened corners, gives flexible support to young growing organs.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-anatomy-flowering-plants-q10', 'neet-biology-anatomy-flowering-plants', 'The outermost protective tissue of the primary plant body is the:', 'Epidermis', 'Endodermis', 'Pericycle', 'Cortex', 'A', 'The epidermis is the outer covering, often coated with a waxy cuticle.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-anatomy-flowering-plants-q11', 'neet-biology-anatomy-flowering-plants', 'The Casparian strips are found in the:', 'Epidermis', 'Endodermis of the root', 'Phloem', 'Cortex', 'B', 'The endodermal Casparian strips (suberin) regulate water entry into the vascular cylinder.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-anatomy-flowering-plants-q12', 'neet-biology-anatomy-flowering-plants', 'Meristematic tissue is characterised by cells that:', 'Are dead and thick-walled', 'Actively divide', 'Store food only', 'Conduct water', 'B', 'Meristems are regions of actively dividing cells responsible for growth.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-structural-organisation-animals-q01', 'neet-biology-structural-organisation-animals', 'The tissue that lines body cavities and covers surfaces is:', 'Connective tissue', 'Epithelial tissue', 'Muscular tissue', 'Neural tissue', 'B', 'Epithelial tissue forms coverings and linings and rests on a basement membrane.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-structural-organisation-animals-q02', 'neet-biology-structural-organisation-animals', 'Which tissue has an abundant extracellular matrix?', 'Epithelial', 'Connective', 'Neural', 'Squamous', 'B', 'Connective tissues (blood, bone, cartilage) are defined by their extensive matrix.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-structural-organisation-animals-q03', 'neet-biology-structural-organisation-animals', 'Tendons, which connect muscle to bone, are made of:', 'Cardiac muscle', 'Dense regular connective tissue', 'Adipose tissue', 'Epithelium', 'B', 'Tendons are dense regular connective tissue rich in collagen fibres.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-structural-organisation-animals-q04', 'neet-biology-structural-organisation-animals', 'The junction that prevents leakage of substances between cells is the:', 'Gap junction', 'Tight junction', 'Adhering junction', 'Synapse', 'B', 'Tight junctions seal adjacent cells, stopping substances from leaking between them.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-structural-organisation-animals-q05', 'neet-biology-structural-organisation-animals', 'Adipose tissue is specialised to store:', 'Water', 'Fat', 'Glycogen only', 'Minerals', 'B', 'Adipose is a loose connective tissue storing fat for energy and insulation.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-structural-organisation-animals-q06', 'neet-biology-structural-organisation-animals', 'Gap junctions function to:', 'Seal cells together', 'Allow rapid communication between adjacent cells', 'Anchor cells to matrix', 'Secrete enzymes', 'B', 'Gap junctions form channels allowing ions and small molecules to pass between cells.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-structural-organisation-animals-q07', 'neet-biology-structural-organisation-animals', 'Which muscle type is branched and shows intercalated discs?', 'Skeletal', 'Cardiac', 'Smooth', 'Visceral', 'B', 'Cardiac muscle cells are branched and joined by intercalated discs for synchronised contraction.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-structural-organisation-animals-q08', 'neet-biology-structural-organisation-animals', 'Neuroglia are cells that:', 'Transmit impulses', 'Support and protect neurons', 'Store fat', 'Secrete hormones', 'B', 'Neuroglial cells provide structural and metabolic support to neurons in neural tissue.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-structural-organisation-animals-q09', 'neet-biology-structural-organisation-animals', 'Cartilage differs from bone in that its matrix is:', 'Calcified and hard', 'Flexible and non-calcified (chondroitin)', 'Fluid', 'Absent', 'B', 'Cartilage has a pliable, non-mineralised matrix, unlike the calcified matrix of bone.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-structural-organisation-animals-q10', 'neet-biology-structural-organisation-animals', 'In the earthworm, locomotion is aided by:', 'Tube feet', 'Setae (chaetae)', 'Wings', 'Fins', 'B', 'Earthworms use S-shaped setae and muscle contraction to move through soil.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-structural-organisation-animals-q11', 'neet-biology-structural-organisation-animals', 'The cockroach has an open circulatory system in which blood is called:', 'Plasma', 'Haemolymph', 'Lymph', 'Serum', 'B', 'Insects have haemolymph flowing through body spaces (haemocoel) in an open system.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-structural-organisation-animals-q12', 'neet-biology-structural-organisation-animals', 'Glandular epithelium is specialised for:', 'Contraction', 'Secretion', 'Impulse conduction', 'Storage of fat', 'B', 'Glandular epithelium is modified to secrete substances (exocrine or endocrine).', 11),
  ('neet-biology-transport-in-plants-q01', 'neet-biology-transport-in-plants', 'The upward movement of water in xylem is best explained by the:', 'Root pressure theory alone', 'Transpiration pull–cohesion–tension theory', 'Active transport theory', 'Imbibition theory', 'B', 'Transpiration creates tension transmitted through cohesive water columns, pulling water up.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-transport-in-plants-q02', 'neet-biology-transport-in-plants', 'The loss of water vapour from aerial plant parts is called:', 'Guttation', 'Transpiration', 'Osmosis', 'Imbibition', 'B', 'Transpiration is water loss as vapour, mostly through stomata.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-transport-in-plants-q03', 'neet-biology-transport-in-plants', 'Water potential of pure water at standard conditions is:', 'Positive', 'Zero', 'Always negative', 'Infinite', 'B', 'Pure water is assigned a water potential of zero; adding solutes makes it negative.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-transport-in-plants-q04', 'neet-biology-transport-in-plants', 'Phloem transport (translocation) is explained by the:', 'Pressure-flow (mass-flow) hypothesis', 'Cohesion-tension theory', 'Root pressure', 'Diffusion only', 'A', 'The pressure-flow hypothesis explains sugar movement from source to sink in phloem.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-transport-in-plants-q05', 'neet-biology-transport-in-plants', 'Stomata generally open when the guard cells become:', 'Flaccid', 'Turgid', 'Plasmolysed', 'Dead', 'B', 'Turgid guard cells bow apart, opening the stomatal pore.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-transport-in-plants-q06', 'neet-biology-transport-in-plants', 'Movement of water molecules into a cell down a water-potential gradient is:', 'Active transport', 'Osmosis', 'Translocation', 'Transpiration', 'B', 'Osmosis is the passive movement of water across a semipermeable membrane down its potential gradient.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-transport-in-plants-q07', 'neet-biology-transport-in-plants', 'Guttation, the loss of liquid water from leaf margins, is driven mainly by:', 'Transpiration pull', 'Root pressure', 'Photosynthesis', 'Wind', 'B', 'Root pressure forces water out through hydathodes as droplets (guttation), typically at night.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-transport-in-plants-q08', 'neet-biology-transport-in-plants', 'Active transport of solutes across membranes requires:', 'ATP energy', 'No energy', 'Only a concentration gradient', 'Only water', 'A', 'Active transport moves solutes against a gradient using energy (ATP) and carrier proteins.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-transport-in-plants-q09', 'neet-biology-transport-in-plants', 'The swelling of dry seeds when placed in water is an example of:', 'Osmosis', 'Imbibition', 'Transpiration', 'Guttation', 'B', 'Imbibition is the adsorption of water by hydrophilic colloids like seed proteins/starch.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-transport-in-plants-q10', 'neet-biology-transport-in-plants', 'The apoplast pathway of water movement is through:', 'The cytoplasm and plasmodesmata', 'Cell walls and intercellular spaces', 'The phloem', 'The vacuoles only', 'B', 'The apoplast is the non-living continuum of cell walls and spaces, offering little resistance.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-transport-in-plants-q11', 'neet-biology-transport-in-plants', 'Adding solute to pure water changes its water potential to become:', 'More positive', 'More negative', 'Unchanged', 'Zero exactly', 'B', 'Solutes lower (make more negative) the water potential via the solute potential term.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-transport-in-plants-q12', 'neet-biology-transport-in-plants', 'Most transpiration in plants occurs through the:', 'Cuticle', 'Stomata', 'Lenticels', 'Roots', 'B', 'The bulk of transpiration is stomatal, through the pores on leaf surfaces.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-mineral-nutrition-q01', 'neet-biology-mineral-nutrition', 'Elements required by plants in large amounts are called:', 'Micronutrients', 'Macronutrients', 'Trace elements', 'Beneficial elements', 'B', 'Macronutrients (C, H, O, N, P, K, Ca, Mg, S) are needed in relatively large quantities.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-mineral-nutrition-q02', 'neet-biology-mineral-nutrition', 'Biological nitrogen fixation by Rhizobium occurs in the:', 'Leaves', 'Root nodules of legumes', 'Stem', 'Flowers', 'B', 'Rhizobium fixes atmospheric N₂ symbiotically inside legume root nodules.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-mineral-nutrition-q03', 'neet-biology-mineral-nutrition', 'The enzyme responsible for biological nitrogen fixation is:', 'Nitrogenase', 'Amylase', 'Catalase', 'RuBisCO', 'A', 'Nitrogenase reduces atmospheric N₂ to ammonia; it is highly sensitive to oxygen.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-mineral-nutrition-q04', 'neet-biology-mineral-nutrition', 'Leghaemoglobin in root nodules functions to:', 'Fix carbon', 'Scavenge oxygen (protect nitrogenase)', 'Store starch', 'Transport water', 'B', 'Leghaemoglobin binds oxygen, protecting the oxygen-sensitive nitrogenase enzyme.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-mineral-nutrition-q05', 'neet-biology-mineral-nutrition', 'Which is a micronutrient for plants?', 'Nitrogen', 'Zinc', 'Potassium', 'Calcium', 'B', 'Zinc (like Fe, Mn, Cu, B, Mo) is a micronutrient needed in trace amounts.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-mineral-nutrition-q06', 'neet-biology-mineral-nutrition', 'Growing plants in a nutrient solution without soil is called:', 'Hydroponics', 'Photoperiodism', 'Vernalisation', 'Grafting', 'A', 'Hydroponics is soil-less culture in a defined nutrient solution.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-mineral-nutrition-q07', 'neet-biology-mineral-nutrition', 'Deficiency of nitrogen causes:', 'Chlorosis (yellowing), especially in older leaves', 'Increased flowering', 'Root thickening', 'No effect', 'A', 'Nitrogen is mobile, so its deficiency yellows older leaves first (chlorosis).', 6),
  ('neet-biology-mineral-nutrition-q08', 'neet-biology-mineral-nutrition', 'An essential element must be:', 'Required for a plant''s normal life cycle and irreplaceable', 'Present in soil only', 'A heavy metal', 'Toxic', 'A', 'An essential element is indispensable and cannot be replaced by another for the plant''s life cycle.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-mineral-nutrition-q09', 'neet-biology-mineral-nutrition', 'Magnesium is important in plants because it is a component of:', 'Chlorophyll', 'Cellulose', 'DNA', 'Starch', 'A', 'Magnesium is the central atom of the chlorophyll molecule and activates enzymes.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-mineral-nutrition-q10', 'neet-biology-mineral-nutrition', 'Free-living nitrogen-fixing bacteria in soil include:', 'Azotobacter', 'Lactobacillus', 'E. coli', 'Salmonella', 'A', 'Azotobacter (aerobic) and Clostridium (anaerobic) fix nitrogen free-living in soil.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-mineral-nutrition-q11', 'neet-biology-mineral-nutrition', 'The conversion of ammonia to nitrate by soil bacteria is called:', 'Nitrification', 'Denitrification', 'Ammonification', 'Fixation', 'A', 'Nitrifying bacteria (Nitrosomonas, Nitrobacter) convert NH₃ → nitrite → nitrate.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-mineral-nutrition-q12', 'neet-biology-mineral-nutrition', 'Denitrification returns nitrogen to the atmosphere by converting:', 'Nitrate to N₂ gas', 'N₂ to ammonia', 'Ammonia to nitrate', 'Protein to amino acids', 'A', 'Denitrifying bacteria reduce nitrate to gaseous N₂, completing the nitrogen cycle.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-respiration-in-plants-q01', 'neet-biology-respiration-in-plants', 'Glycolysis takes place in the:', 'Mitochondrial matrix', 'Cytoplasm', 'Chloroplast', 'Nucleus', 'B', 'Glycolysis occurs in the cytoplasm and splits glucose into two pyruvate molecules.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-respiration-in-plants-q02', 'neet-biology-respiration-in-plants', 'The Krebs cycle occurs in the:', 'Cytoplasm', 'Mitochondrial matrix', 'Thylakoid', 'Ribosome', 'B', 'The TCA (Krebs) cycle takes place in the mitochondrial matrix.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-respiration-in-plants-q03', 'neet-biology-respiration-in-plants', 'The net ATP yield from glycolysis (per glucose) is:', '2 ATP', '36 ATP', '4 ATP', '38 ATP', 'A', 'Glycolysis produces 4 ATP but uses 2, giving a net of 2 ATP (plus 2 NADH).', 2),
  ('neet-biology-respiration-in-plants-q04', 'neet-biology-respiration-in-plants', 'The final electron acceptor in aerobic respiration is:', 'Water', 'Oxygen', 'NAD⁺', 'Carbon dioxide', 'B', 'Oxygen accepts electrons at the end of the electron transport chain, forming water.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-respiration-in-plants-q05', 'neet-biology-respiration-in-plants', 'In the absence of oxygen, yeast carries out fermentation producing:', 'Lactic acid', 'Ethanol and CO₂', 'Water only', 'Glucose', 'B', 'Alcoholic fermentation in yeast converts pyruvate to ethanol and carbon dioxide.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-respiration-in-plants-q06', 'neet-biology-respiration-in-plants', 'The respiratory quotient (RQ) for carbohydrates is:', '1', '0.7', 'Greater than 1', '0', 'A', 'For carbohydrate oxidation, CO₂ released equals O₂ consumed, so RQ = 1.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-respiration-in-plants-q07', 'neet-biology-respiration-in-plants', 'Oxidative phosphorylation (ATP synthesis using the ETC) occurs on the:', 'Inner mitochondrial membrane', 'Cytoplasm', 'Outer membrane', 'Ribosomes', 'A', 'The electron transport chain and ATP synthase are on the inner mitochondrial membrane.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-respiration-in-plants-q08', 'neet-biology-respiration-in-plants', 'Complete aerobic oxidation of one glucose molecule yields approximately:', '2 ATP', '36–38 ATP', '12 ATP', '100 ATP', 'B', 'Aerobic respiration of glucose yields about 36–38 ATP in total.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-respiration-in-plants-q09', 'neet-biology-respiration-in-plants', 'The connecting link between glycolysis and the Krebs cycle is the formation of:', 'Lactic acid', 'Acetyl-CoA', 'Ethanol', 'Glucose', 'B', 'Pyruvate is oxidatively decarboxylated to acetyl-CoA, which enters the Krebs cycle.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-respiration-in-plants-q10', 'neet-biology-respiration-in-plants', 'An RQ less than 1 indicates the respiratory substrate is a:', 'Carbohydrate', 'Fat', 'Organic acid', 'Protein only', 'B', 'Fats consume more oxygen relative to CO₂ released, giving RQ < 1 (about 0.7).', 9),
  ('neet-biology-respiration-in-plants-q11', 'neet-biology-respiration-in-plants', 'In muscle cells under oxygen shortage, glucose is converted to:', 'Ethanol', 'Lactic acid', 'Acetyl-CoA', 'Starch', 'B', 'Anaerobic respiration in animal muscle produces lactic acid, causing fatigue.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-respiration-in-plants-q12', 'neet-biology-respiration-in-plants', 'Amphibolic pathway means the respiratory pathway is involved in:', 'Only breakdown (catabolism)', 'Both breakdown and synthesis', 'Only synthesis (anabolism)', 'Neither', 'B', 'Respiration is amphibolic: its intermediates serve both catabolic and anabolic (biosynthetic) roles.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-plant-growth-development-q01', 'neet-biology-plant-growth-development', 'The plant hormone responsible for apical dominance is:', 'Auxin', 'Ethylene', 'Abscisic acid', 'Cytokinin', 'A', 'Auxin produced at the shoot tip suppresses growth of lateral buds (apical dominance).', 0),
  ('neet-biology-plant-growth-development-q02', 'neet-biology-plant-growth-development', 'Which hormone promotes fruit ripening and is a gas?', 'Gibberellin', 'Ethylene', 'Auxin', 'Cytokinin', 'B', 'Ethylene is the gaseous hormone that hastens fruit ripening and senescence.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-plant-growth-development-q03', 'neet-biology-plant-growth-development', 'The ''stress hormone'' that promotes stomatal closure and dormancy is:', 'Auxin', 'Abscisic acid', 'Gibberellin', 'Cytokinin', 'B', 'Abscisic acid (ABA) closes stomata under water stress and induces bud/seed dormancy.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-plant-growth-development-q04', 'neet-biology-plant-growth-development', 'Gibberellins are best known for causing:', 'Stem elongation and bolting', 'Stomatal closure', 'Apical dominance', 'Leaf abscission', 'A', 'Gibberellins promote internode elongation, bolting, and break seed/bud dormancy.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-plant-growth-development-q05', 'neet-biology-plant-growth-development', 'Cytokinins primarily promote:', 'Cell division and delay senescence', 'Fruit ripening', 'Stomatal closure', 'Root dormancy', 'A', 'Cytokinins stimulate cytokinesis (cell division) and delay leaf ageing.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-plant-growth-development-q06', 'neet-biology-plant-growth-development', 'The response of plants to the relative lengths of day and night is:', 'Vernalisation', 'Photoperiodism', 'Phototropism', 'Geotropism', 'B', 'Photoperiodism is the flowering response to day/night length, sensed via phytochrome.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-plant-growth-development-q07', 'neet-biology-plant-growth-development', 'The promotion of flowering by exposure to low temperature is called:', 'Photoperiodism', 'Vernalisation', 'Dormancy', 'Etiolation', 'B', 'Vernalisation is the requirement of a cold period to induce or accelerate flowering.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-plant-growth-development-q08', 'neet-biology-plant-growth-development', 'The site that perceives the photoperiodic stimulus for flowering is the:', 'Root tip', 'Leaf', 'Flower', 'Seed', 'B', 'Leaves perceive the day-length signal (via phytochrome) and induce flowering.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-plant-growth-development-q09', 'neet-biology-plant-growth-development', 'Auxin is commonly used commercially to:', 'Promote rooting in stem cuttings', 'Ripen fruit', 'Close stomata', 'Delay germination', 'A', 'Auxins (e.g., IBA, NAA) are applied to induce adventitious roots in cuttings.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-plant-growth-development-q10', 'neet-biology-plant-growth-development', 'Plant growth is described as ''open'' because it:', 'Occurs only once', 'Continues throughout life via meristems', 'Stops at maturity', 'Is determinate', 'B', 'Meristems allow indeterminate (open) growth throughout the plant''s life.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-plant-growth-development-q11', 'neet-biology-plant-growth-development', 'The phase of cell growth where cells enlarge and vacuolate is the:', 'Meristematic phase', 'Elongation phase', 'Maturation phase', 'Dormant phase', 'B', 'In the elongation phase, cells increase in size with vacuolation and new wall material.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-plant-growth-development-q12', 'neet-biology-plant-growth-development', 'The pigment that regulates photoperiodic and light responses is:', 'Chlorophyll', 'Phytochrome', 'Carotene', 'Anthocyanin', 'B', 'Phytochrome perceives red/far-red light to regulate flowering and germination.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-locomotion-movement-q01', 'neet-biology-locomotion-movement', 'The contractile proteins of skeletal muscle are:', 'Actin and myosin', 'Collagen and elastin', 'Keratin and tubulin', 'Albumin and globulin', 'A', 'Actin (thin) and myosin (thick) filaments slide past each other during contraction.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-locomotion-movement-q02', 'neet-biology-locomotion-movement', 'According to the sliding filament theory, during contraction:', 'Actin filaments slide over myosin, shortening the sarcomere', 'Filaments themselves shorten', 'Only myosin shortens', 'The A-band shortens', 'A', 'Thin filaments slide over thick ones; the sarcomere shortens while filament lengths stay constant.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-locomotion-movement-q03', 'neet-biology-locomotion-movement', 'The ion essential for triggering muscle contraction is:', 'Na⁺', 'Ca²⁺', 'K⁺', 'Cl⁻', 'B', 'Calcium ions released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum expose actin binding sites for contraction.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-locomotion-movement-q04', 'neet-biology-locomotion-movement', 'The total number of bones in the adult human skeleton is:', '206', '300', '150', '246', 'A', 'The adult human skeleton has 206 bones (axial + appendicular).', 3),
  ('neet-biology-locomotion-movement-q05', 'neet-biology-locomotion-movement', 'The freely movable joints of the body are called:', 'Fibrous joints', 'Synovial joints', 'Cartilaginous joints', 'Sutures', 'B', 'Synovial joints (e.g., knee, shoulder) permit free movement with a lubricated cavity.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-locomotion-movement-q06', 'neet-biology-locomotion-movement', 'During contraction, which region of the sarcomere shortens?', 'A-band', 'I-band and H-zone', 'Myosin filament', 'Z-line thickness', 'B', 'The I-band and H-zone shorten during contraction; the A-band length remains unchanged.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-locomotion-movement-q07', 'neet-biology-locomotion-movement', 'The axial skeleton includes the:', 'Limbs and girdles', 'Skull, vertebral column, ribs, and sternum', 'Hands and feet', 'Pelvic girdle only', 'B', 'The axial skeleton comprises the skull, vertebral column, ribs, and sternum.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-locomotion-movement-q08', 'neet-biology-locomotion-movement', 'A disorder caused by autoimmune damage at the neuromuscular junction is:', 'Gout', 'Myasthenia gravis', 'Osteoporosis', 'Arthritis', 'B', 'Myasthenia gravis is autoimmune, causing fatigue and weakness of skeletal muscles.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-locomotion-movement-q09', 'neet-biology-locomotion-movement', 'The energy currency directly powering muscle contraction is:', 'Glucose', 'ATP', 'Creatine only', 'Fat', 'B', 'ATP hydrolysis on the myosin head drives the cross-bridge cycle of contraction.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-locomotion-movement-q10', 'neet-biology-locomotion-movement', 'The proteins that regulate the binding of myosin to actin are:', 'Troponin and tropomyosin', 'Collagen and elastin', 'Albumin and casein', 'Keratin and myoglobin', 'A', 'Troponin–tropomyosin blocks actin sites until Ca²⁺ binds troponin, allowing contraction.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-locomotion-movement-q11', 'neet-biology-locomotion-movement', 'Osteoporosis, common in older women, is characterised by:', 'Decreased bone mass and density', 'Increased bone density', 'Joint inflammation', 'Muscle wasting only', 'A', 'Osteoporosis reduces bone mass (often with reduced oestrogen), raising fracture risk.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-locomotion-movement-q12', 'neet-biology-locomotion-movement', 'The functional contractile unit of a myofibril, between two Z-lines, is the:', 'Sarcomere', 'Sarcolemma', 'Myofilament', 'Fascicle', 'A', 'The sarcomere, the region between two Z-lines, is the functional unit of contraction.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-chemical-coordination-hormones-q01', 'neet-biology-chemical-coordination-hormones', 'The ''master gland'' of the endocrine system is the:', 'Thyroid', 'Pituitary', 'Adrenal', 'Pancreas', 'B', 'The pituitary is called the master gland because it controls other endocrine glands.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-chemical-coordination-hormones-q02', 'neet-biology-chemical-coordination-hormones', 'The hormone insulin is secreted by which cells?', 'Alpha cells', 'Beta cells of the islets of Langerhans', 'Adrenal cortex', 'Thyroid follicles', 'B', 'Beta cells of the pancreatic islets secrete insulin, which lowers blood glucose.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-chemical-coordination-hormones-q03', 'neet-biology-chemical-coordination-hormones', 'Deficiency of iodine leads to under-production of thyroxine and causes:', 'Diabetes', 'Goitre', 'Dwarfism', 'Cretinism only in adults', 'B', 'Iodine deficiency reduces thyroxine, enlarging the thyroid gland (goitre).', 2),
  ('neet-biology-chemical-coordination-hormones-q04', 'neet-biology-chemical-coordination-hormones', 'The ''fight or flight'' hormone from the adrenal medulla is:', 'Insulin', 'Adrenaline (epinephrine)', 'Thyroxine', 'Parathormone', 'B', 'Adrenaline prepares the body for emergencies, raising heart rate and blood glucose.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-chemical-coordination-hormones-q05', 'neet-biology-chemical-coordination-hormones', 'The hormone raising blood calcium levels is:', 'Calcitonin', 'Parathormone (PTH)', 'Insulin', 'Glucagon', 'B', 'Parathormone increases blood Ca²⁺ by acting on bone, kidney, and intestine.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-chemical-coordination-hormones-q06', 'neet-biology-chemical-coordination-hormones', 'Steroid hormones act by:', 'Binding intracellular receptors and altering gene expression', 'Second messengers only at the membrane', 'Being pumped into cells', 'Blocking enzymes outside cells', 'A', 'Lipid-soluble steroid hormones enter cells and bind receptors that regulate transcription.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-chemical-coordination-hormones-q07', 'neet-biology-chemical-coordination-hormones', 'Peptide/protein hormones typically act via:', 'Membrane receptors and second messengers (e.g., cAMP)', 'Entering the nucleus directly', 'Diffusing into DNA', 'Being stored in bone', 'A', 'Water-soluble hormones bind cell-surface receptors, triggering second messengers.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-chemical-coordination-hormones-q08', 'neet-biology-chemical-coordination-hormones', 'The hypothalamus controls the pituitary by secreting:', 'Digestive enzymes', 'Releasing and inhibiting hormones', 'Bile', 'Insulin', 'B', 'The hypothalamus produces releasing/inhibiting hormones that regulate the pituitary.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-chemical-coordination-hormones-q09', 'neet-biology-chemical-coordination-hormones', 'The hormone glucagon:', 'Raises blood glucose', 'Lowers blood glucose', 'Stores glucose as fat', 'Has no effect on glucose', 'A', 'Glucagon (from alpha cells) raises blood glucose by promoting glycogen breakdown.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-chemical-coordination-hormones-q10', 'neet-biology-chemical-coordination-hormones', 'Diabetes mellitus results from deficiency or ineffectiveness of:', 'Thyroxine', 'Insulin', 'Adrenaline', 'Growth hormone', 'B', 'Inadequate insulin action raises blood glucose, causing diabetes mellitus.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-chemical-coordination-hormones-q11', 'neet-biology-chemical-coordination-hormones', 'Melatonin, which regulates the sleep–wake cycle, is secreted by the:', 'Pineal gland', 'Thyroid', 'Pancreas', 'Adrenal', 'A', 'The pineal gland secretes melatonin, regulating circadian (diurnal) rhythms.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-chemical-coordination-hormones-q12', 'neet-biology-chemical-coordination-hormones', 'Most hormone secretion is regulated by:', 'Feedback mechanisms', 'Random release', 'Digestion', 'Muscle contraction', 'A', 'Negative feedback loops keep hormone levels within a regulated range.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-sexual-reproduction-flowering-plants-q01', 'neet-biology-sexual-reproduction-flowering-plants', 'Pollen grains are produced in the:', 'Ovule', 'Anther (microsporangia)', 'Stigma', 'Ovary', 'B', 'Microspores (pollen) develop in the pollen sacs (microsporangia) of the anther.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-sexual-reproduction-flowering-plants-q02', 'neet-biology-sexual-reproduction-flowering-plants', 'Double fertilisation in angiosperms produces a zygote and a:', 'Diploid endosperm', 'Triploid endosperm', 'Haploid embryo', 'Second zygote', 'B', 'One male gamete forms the zygote; the other fuses with two polar nuclei to form triploid endosperm.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-sexual-reproduction-flowering-plants-q03', 'neet-biology-sexual-reproduction-flowering-plants', 'The transfer of pollen from anther to stigma is called:', 'Fertilisation', 'Pollination', 'Germination', 'Dispersal', 'B', 'Pollination is the transfer of pollen to a receptive stigma (self- or cross-pollination).', 2),
  ('neet-biology-sexual-reproduction-flowering-plants-q04', 'neet-biology-sexual-reproduction-flowering-plants', 'The typical mature embryo sac is:', '8-nucleate and 7-celled', '4-celled', '2-nucleate', '16-celled', 'A', 'The Polygonum-type embryo sac is 7-celled and 8-nucleate.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-sexual-reproduction-flowering-plants-q05', 'neet-biology-sexual-reproduction-flowering-plants', 'After fertilisation, the ovule develops into the:', 'Fruit', 'Seed', 'Flower', 'Endosperm', 'B', 'The fertilised ovule matures into the seed; the ovary becomes the fruit.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-sexual-reproduction-flowering-plants-q06', 'neet-biology-sexual-reproduction-flowering-plants', 'The nutritive tissue that nourishes the developing embryo is the:', 'Endosperm', 'Cotyledon only', 'Testa', 'Pericarp', 'A', 'The triploid endosperm provides nutrition to the developing embryo.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-sexual-reproduction-flowering-plants-q07', 'neet-biology-sexual-reproduction-flowering-plants', 'Formation of seeds without fertilisation is called:', 'Apomixis', 'Polyembryony', 'Parthenocarpy', 'Vivipary', 'A', 'Apomixis is asexual seed formation without meiosis and fertilisation.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-sexual-reproduction-flowering-plants-q08', 'neet-biology-sexual-reproduction-flowering-plants', 'The occurrence of more than one embryo in a seed is:', 'Apomixis', 'Polyembryony', 'Parthenocarpy', 'Pollination', 'B', 'Polyembryony is the presence of multiple embryos in a single seed (e.g., in citrus).', 7),
  ('neet-biology-sexual-reproduction-flowering-plants-q09', 'neet-biology-sexual-reproduction-flowering-plants', 'Fruit development without fertilisation, giving seedless fruit, is:', 'Parthenocarpy', 'Apomixis', 'Polyembryony', 'Double fertilisation', 'A', 'Parthenocarpy produces seedless fruits without fertilisation (e.g., banana).', 8),
  ('neet-biology-sexual-reproduction-flowering-plants-q10', 'neet-biology-sexual-reproduction-flowering-plants', 'Cross-pollination between two flowers of different plants is called:', 'Autogamy', 'Xenogamy', 'Geitonogamy', 'Cleistogamy', 'B', 'Xenogamy is pollination between flowers of genetically different plants.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-sexual-reproduction-flowering-plants-q11', 'neet-biology-sexual-reproduction-flowering-plants', 'The male gametophyte in angiosperms is the:', 'Pollen grain', 'Embryo sac', 'Ovule', 'Endosperm', 'A', 'The germinated pollen grain (with the pollen tube and two male gametes) is the male gametophyte.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-sexual-reproduction-flowering-plants-q12', 'neet-biology-sexual-reproduction-flowering-plants', 'The phenomenon of double fertilisation was discovered by:', 'Nawaschin', 'Mendel', 'Darwin', 'Strasburger', 'A', 'S. Nawaschin first described double fertilisation in angiosperms.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-reproductive-health-q01', 'neet-biology-reproductive-health', 'The Copper-T is an example of which contraceptive method?', 'Barrier method', 'Intrauterine device (IUD)', 'Oral pill', 'Surgical method', 'B', 'Copper-T is an intrauterine device that prevents implantation/fertilisation.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-reproductive-health-q02', 'neet-biology-reproductive-health', 'Which is a surgical method of contraception in males?', 'Tubectomy', 'Vasectomy', 'IUD', 'Oral pill', 'B', 'Vasectomy cuts/ties the vas deferens in males; tubectomy is the female equivalent.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-reproductive-health-q03', 'neet-biology-reproductive-health', 'IVF, where fertilisation occurs outside the body, produces a so-called:', 'Clone', 'Test-tube baby', 'Transgenic baby', 'Surrogate', 'B', 'In-vitro fertilisation (IVF) fertilises the egg in the lab — a ''test-tube baby''.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-reproductive-health-q04', 'neet-biology-reproductive-health', 'MTP stands for:', 'Medical Termination of Pregnancy', 'Male Testis Protein', 'Menstrual Timing Programme', 'Maternal Tissue Preservation', 'A', 'MTP is the legal, medically supervised termination of pregnancy within limits.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-reproductive-health-q05', 'neet-biology-reproductive-health', 'Which is a sexually transmitted infection (STI)?', 'Malaria', 'Syphilis', 'Diabetes', 'Tuberculosis', 'B', 'Syphilis, gonorrhoea, and HIV/AIDS are sexually transmitted infections.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-reproductive-health-q06', 'neet-biology-reproductive-health', 'Amniocentesis is a technique used to:', 'Detect foetal genetic disorders', 'Fertilise eggs', 'Prevent pregnancy', 'Deliver babies', 'A', 'Amniocentesis samples amniotic fluid to detect foetal abnormalities; its misuse for sex determination is banned.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-reproductive-health-q07', 'neet-biology-reproductive-health', 'GIFT (gamete intrafallopian transfer) involves transferring:', 'An embryo to the uterus', 'Gametes into the fallopian tube', 'Sperm into the vagina', 'An ovary', 'B', 'GIFT places collected gametes into the fallopian tube for fertilisation in vivo.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-reproductive-health-q08', 'neet-biology-reproductive-health', 'Oral contraceptive pills mainly work by:', 'Preventing ovulation (hormonal)', 'Physically blocking sperm', 'Killing sperm', 'Surgery', 'A', 'Hormonal pills inhibit ovulation and alter the endometrium/cervical mucus.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-reproductive-health-q09', 'neet-biology-reproductive-health', 'The barrier method of contraception includes:', 'Condoms and diaphragms', 'Copper-T', 'Oral pills', 'Vasectomy', 'A', 'Barrier methods (condoms, diaphragms) physically prevent sperm meeting the egg.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-reproductive-health-q10', 'neet-biology-reproductive-health', 'ICSI (intracytoplasmic sperm injection) involves:', 'Injecting a single sperm directly into an egg', 'Transferring an embryo', 'Removing the uterus', 'Freezing gametes only', 'A', 'ICSI injects a sperm directly into the ovum''s cytoplasm, aiding male-factor infertility.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-reproductive-health-q11', 'neet-biology-reproductive-health', 'A natural (fertility-awareness) contraceptive method is the:', 'Rhythm/periodic abstinence method', 'Copper-T', 'Condom', 'Pill', 'A', 'The rhythm method avoids intercourse during the fertile period of the cycle.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-reproductive-health-q12', 'neet-biology-reproductive-health', 'Reproductive and Child Health (RCH) programmes primarily aim to:', 'Increase population rapidly', 'Create awareness and provide reproductive health facilities', 'Ban all contraception', 'Promote large families', 'B', 'RCH programmes spread awareness and improve access to reproductive health care.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-microbes-human-welfare-q01', 'neet-biology-microbes-human-welfare', 'Curd is formed from milk by the action of:', 'Yeast', 'Lactic acid bacteria (Lactobacillus)', 'Penicillium', 'Rhizobium', 'B', 'Lactobacillus (LAB) curdles milk and also increases vitamin B₁₂ content.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-microbes-human-welfare-q02', 'neet-biology-microbes-human-welfare', 'The antibiotic penicillin was obtained from:', 'Penicillium notatum', 'Bacillus', 'Streptococcus', 'Aspergillus', 'A', 'Alexander Fleming discovered penicillin from the mould Penicillium notatum.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-microbes-human-welfare-q03', 'neet-biology-microbes-human-welfare', 'The organism used to make bread and alcohol is:', 'Saccharomyces cerevisiae (yeast)', 'Lactobacillus', 'Rhizobium', 'E. coli', 'A', 'Baker''s/brewer''s yeast ferments sugars, producing CO₂ (bread) and ethanol (alcohol).', 2),
  ('neet-biology-microbes-human-welfare-q04', 'neet-biology-microbes-human-welfare', 'Biogas (mainly methane) is produced by:', 'Methanogens', 'Yeast', 'Lactobacillus', 'Penicillium', 'A', 'Methanogenic archaea (e.g., Methanobacterium) produce methane in biogas plants.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-microbes-human-welfare-q05', 'neet-biology-microbes-human-welfare', 'The ''holes'' in Swiss cheese are produced by CO₂ from:', 'Propionibacterium', 'Lactobacillus', 'Yeast', 'Aspergillus', 'A', 'Propionibacterium sharmanii releases CO₂, forming the large holes in Swiss cheese.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-microbes-human-welfare-q06', 'neet-biology-microbes-human-welfare', 'The immunosuppressive drug cyclosporin A is obtained from:', 'The fungus Trichoderma polysporum', 'A bacterium', 'A yeast', 'A virus', 'A', 'Cyclosporin A, used in organ transplants, is produced by the fungus Trichoderma.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-microbes-human-welfare-q07', 'neet-biology-microbes-human-welfare', 'Statins, used to lower blood cholesterol, are produced by:', 'Monascus purpureus (a yeast)', 'Rhizobium', 'E. coli', 'Lactobacillus', 'A', 'Statins from the yeast Monascus purpureus inhibit cholesterol synthesis.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-microbes-human-welfare-q08', 'neet-biology-microbes-human-welfare', 'In sewage treatment, the primary role of microbes is to:', 'Decompose organic matter, reducing BOD', 'Add nutrients', 'Increase BOD', 'Produce antibiotics', 'A', 'Aerobic microbes digest organic waste, lowering the biochemical oxygen demand (BOD).', 7),
  ('neet-biology-microbes-human-welfare-q09', 'neet-biology-microbes-human-welfare', 'A biofertiliser that fixes nitrogen in rice fields is:', 'Anabaena/Cyanobacteria', 'Penicillium', 'Yeast', 'Aspergillus', 'A', 'Cyanobacteria like Anabaena and Nostoc fix nitrogen, enriching paddy fields.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-microbes-human-welfare-q10', 'neet-biology-microbes-human-welfare', 'Bacillus thuringiensis (Bt) is used as a:', 'Biocontrol agent (biopesticide)', 'Antibiotic', 'Vaccine', 'Biofertiliser', 'A', 'Bt produces insecticidal crystal proteins used to control insect pests.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-microbes-human-welfare-q11', 'neet-biology-microbes-human-welfare', 'Mycorrhiza is a symbiotic association between a fungus and:', 'Plant roots', 'Animal cells', 'Bacteria', 'Algae only', 'A', 'Mycorrhizae associate fungi with plant roots, aiding phosphorus uptake.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-microbes-human-welfare-q12', 'neet-biology-microbes-human-welfare', 'Citric acid, used in food, is produced industrially by:', 'Aspergillus niger', 'Lactobacillus', 'Yeast', 'Rhizobium', 'A', 'The fungus Aspergillus niger is used for industrial citric acid production.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-biotechnology-applications-q01', 'neet-biology-biotechnology-applications', 'Bt cotton is genetically engineered to be resistant to:', 'Insect pests', 'Drought', 'Herbicides only', 'Fungi', 'A', 'Bt cotton carries cry genes producing insecticidal proteins against bollworms.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-biotechnology-applications-q02', 'neet-biology-biotechnology-applications', 'The cry genes in Bt crops are derived from:', 'Bacillus thuringiensis', 'Agrobacterium', 'E. coli', 'Yeast', 'A', 'cry genes from Bacillus thuringiensis encode insecticidal Cry proteins.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-biotechnology-applications-q03', 'neet-biology-biotechnology-applications', 'Genetically engineered human insulin is marketed as:', 'Humulin', 'Interferon', 'Cyclosporin', 'Statin', 'A', 'Humulin is recombinant human insulin produced in E. coli.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-biotechnology-applications-q04', 'neet-biology-biotechnology-applications', 'Gene therapy is a technique to:', 'Correct a defective gene by inserting a functional one', 'Clone whole animals', 'Make antibiotics', 'Ferment food', 'A', 'Gene therapy inserts a normal gene to compensate for a defective one (e.g., ADA deficiency).', 3),
  ('neet-biology-biotechnology-applications-q05', 'neet-biology-biotechnology-applications', 'RNA interference (RNAi) is used in crops to:', 'Silence specific genes (e.g., in pests)', 'Add new proteins', 'Increase mutation', 'Fix nitrogen', 'A', 'RNAi silences target genes; e.g., it protects tobacco against a nematode.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-biotechnology-applications-q06', 'neet-biology-biotechnology-applications', 'The molecular diagnostic technique that detects very low amounts of a pathogen''s nucleic acid is:', 'PCR', 'Gel electrophoresis only', 'Chromatography', 'Centrifugation', 'A', 'PCR amplifies pathogen DNA/RNA, enabling early, sensitive diagnosis.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-biotechnology-applications-q07', 'neet-biology-biotechnology-applications', 'ELISA is used to detect diseases by identifying:', 'Antigens or antibodies', 'Whole organisms only', 'Bones', 'Fats', 'A', 'ELISA detects antigen–antibody interactions, e.g., in HIV diagnosis.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-biotechnology-applications-q08', 'neet-biology-biotechnology-applications', 'Transgenic animals are those that have:', 'A foreign gene deliberately introduced', 'No genes', 'Only natural mutations', 'Been cloned only', 'A', 'Transgenic animals carry a manipulated/foreign gene (e.g., for producing useful proteins).', 7),
  ('neet-biology-biotechnology-applications-q09', 'neet-biology-biotechnology-applications', 'The regulatory body (in India) overseeing GM organisms is:', 'GEAC', 'ICMR only', 'WHO', 'FDA', 'A', 'The Genetic Engineering Appraisal Committee (GEAC) regulates GM research and release in India.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-biotechnology-applications-q10', 'neet-biology-biotechnology-applications', 'Biopiracy refers to:', 'Unauthorised exploitation of bioresources/traditional knowledge', 'Farming with GM crops', 'Gene therapy', 'Making insulin', 'A', 'Biopiracy is the use of bio-resources or knowledge without authorisation or fair benefit-sharing.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-biotechnology-applications-q11', 'neet-biology-biotechnology-applications', 'Human insulin (Humulin) consists of which chains joined by disulphide bonds?', 'Chains A and B', 'Chains X and Y', 'Only one chain', 'Three chains', 'A', 'Mature insulin has A and B polypeptide chains linked by disulphide bridges (C-peptide removed).', 10),
  ('neet-biology-biotechnology-applications-q12', 'neet-biology-biotechnology-applications', '''Golden rice'' was engineered to be rich in:', 'Vitamin A (beta-carotene) precursor', 'Vitamin C', 'Iron only', 'Protein', 'A', 'Golden rice produces beta-carotene (provitamin A) to combat vitamin A deficiency.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-organisms-populations-q01', 'neet-biology-organisms-populations', 'The J-shaped growth curve represents:', 'Logistic growth', 'Exponential growth (unlimited resources)', 'Zero growth', 'Declining population', 'B', 'Exponential (J-shaped) growth occurs when resources are unlimited: dN/dt = rN.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-organisms-populations-q02', 'neet-biology-organisms-populations', 'The S-shaped (sigmoid) curve reflects:', 'Exponential growth', 'Logistic growth with a carrying capacity', 'Instant extinction', 'No limiting factors', 'B', 'Logistic growth levels off at the carrying capacity K, forming an S-shaped curve.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-organisms-populations-q03', 'neet-biology-organisms-populations', 'In the interaction where both species benefit, the relationship is:', 'Mutualism (+/+)', 'Predation (+/−)', 'Competition (−/−)', 'Amensalism (−/0)', 'A', 'Mutualism benefits both partners (e.g., pollinators and flowering plants).', 2),
  ('neet-biology-organisms-populations-q04', 'neet-biology-organisms-populations', 'The ''carrying capacity'' (K) of an environment is the:', 'Maximum population it can sustainably support', 'Minimum population', 'Birth rate', 'Death rate', 'A', 'K is the maximum population size the resources of a habitat can support long-term.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-organisms-populations-q05', 'neet-biology-organisms-populations', 'An interaction where one benefits and the other is unaffected is:', 'Commensalism (+/0)', 'Mutualism', 'Predation', 'Competition', 'A', 'Commensalism benefits one species with no effect on the other (e.g., an orchid on a tree).', 4),
  ('neet-biology-organisms-populations-q06', 'neet-biology-organisms-populations', 'In parasitism, the relationship is:', '+/− (parasite benefits, host harmed)', '+/+', '0/0', '−/−', 'A', 'A parasite benefits at the expense of its host (+/−).', 5),
  ('neet-biology-organisms-populations-q07', 'neet-biology-organisms-populations', 'Organisms that maintain a constant internal environment despite external changes are:', 'Conformers', 'Regulators', 'Migrators', 'Dormant', 'B', 'Regulators maintain homeostasis (e.g., birds and mammals thermoregulate).', 6),
  ('neet-biology-organisms-populations-q08', 'neet-biology-organisms-populations', 'Allen''s rule states that mammals in cold regions have:', 'Larger ears and limbs', 'Shorter ears and limbs (reduce heat loss)', 'No fur', 'Thin skin', 'B', 'Allen''s rule: colder climates favour shorter extremities to minimise heat loss.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-organisms-populations-q09', 'neet-biology-organisms-populations', 'The number of individuals per unit area or volume is population:', 'Density', 'Emigration', 'Natality', 'Mortality', 'A', 'Population density measures the number of individuals in a given area or volume.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-organisms-populations-q10', 'neet-biology-organisms-populations', 'In the logistic equation dN/dt = rN[(K−N)/K], growth stops when:', 'N = K', 'N = 0', 'r = 0 only', 'K = 0', 'A', 'When N equals K, the term (K−N)/K becomes zero and net growth stops.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-organisms-populations-q11', 'neet-biology-organisms-populations', 'The desert kangaroo rat is adapted to survive with little water by:', 'Producing concentrated urine and using metabolic water', 'Drinking a lot of water', 'Living underwater', 'Sweating heavily', 'A', 'It meets water needs internally (metabolic water) and excretes very concentrated urine.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-organisms-populations-q12', 'neet-biology-organisms-populations', 'A population''s age pyramid that is broad at the base indicates:', 'A growing population', 'A declining population', 'A stable population', 'Extinction', 'A', 'A broad base (many young individuals) signals an expanding population.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-environmental-issues-q01', 'neet-biology-environmental-issues', 'Eutrophication of a water body is caused by:', 'Excess nutrients (nitrogen, phosphorus)', 'Lack of nutrients', 'High oxygen', 'Low temperature', 'A', 'Nutrient enrichment triggers algal blooms, later depleting oxygen and harming aquatic life.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-environmental-issues-q02', 'neet-biology-environmental-issues', 'BOD (biochemical oxygen demand) of water indicates the amount of:', 'Organic matter (pollution level)', 'Dissolved salt', 'Heavy metals', 'Temperature', 'A', 'A high BOD reflects more organic waste and greater pollution.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-environmental-issues-q03', 'neet-biology-environmental-issues', 'The main greenhouse gas responsible for global warming is:', 'Oxygen', 'Carbon dioxide', 'Nitrogen', 'Argon', 'B', 'CO₂ is the principal greenhouse gas, along with methane, N₂O, and CFCs.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-environmental-issues-q04', 'neet-biology-environmental-issues', 'Ozone depletion in the stratosphere is mainly caused by:', 'Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs)', 'Carbon dioxide', 'Oxygen', 'Water vapour', 'A', 'CFCs release chlorine radicals that catalytically destroy ozone.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-environmental-issues-q05', 'neet-biology-environmental-issues', 'Electrostatic precipitators are used to control:', 'Air pollution (particulates)', 'Water pollution', 'Noise pollution', 'Soil erosion', 'A', 'Electrostatic precipitators remove fine particulate matter from industrial exhaust.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-environmental-issues-q06', 'neet-biology-environmental-issues', 'The thickness of the ozone layer is measured in:', 'Dobson units', 'Decibels', 'Pascals', 'Lux', 'A', 'Ozone column thickness is measured in Dobson units (DU).', 5),
  ('neet-biology-environmental-issues-q07', 'neet-biology-environmental-issues', 'Catalytic converters in vehicles reduce emission of:', 'Harmful gases like CO and NOx', 'Water vapour', 'Oxygen', 'Nitrogen gas', 'A', 'Catalytic converters convert CO, hydrocarbons, and NOx to less harmful gases.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-environmental-issues-q08', 'neet-biology-environmental-issues', 'The international agreement to control CFCs and protect the ozone layer is the:', 'Montreal Protocol', 'Kyoto Protocol', 'Ramsar Convention', 'Rio Summit', 'A', 'The Montreal Protocol (1987) phases out ozone-depleting substances like CFCs.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-environmental-issues-q09', 'neet-biology-environmental-issues', 'Biomagnification refers to the increasing concentration of a toxicant:', 'At successive trophic levels', 'In water only', 'In soil only', 'In the atmosphere', 'A', 'Non-degradable toxins (e.g., DDT) accumulate and increase up the food chain.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-environmental-issues-q10', 'neet-biology-environmental-issues', 'The Kyoto Protocol primarily aims to reduce emissions of:', 'Greenhouse gases', 'CFCs only', 'Particulates', 'Noise', 'A', 'The Kyoto Protocol targets reduction of greenhouse gas emissions to curb global warming.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-environmental-issues-q11', 'neet-biology-environmental-issues', 'Increased UV radiation from ozone depletion can cause:', 'Skin cancer and cataracts', 'Improved eyesight', 'Cooling of Earth', 'More rainfall', 'A', 'More UV-B reaching the surface increases skin cancer, cataracts, and immune suppression.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-environmental-issues-q12', 'neet-biology-environmental-issues', 'A sustainable approach to solid waste is to:', 'Reduce, reuse, and recycle', 'Burn all waste openly', 'Dump in rivers', 'Bury without treatment', 'A', 'The 3 R''s — reduce, reuse, recycle — form the basis of sustainable waste management.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-organic-chemistry-basics-q09', 'neet-chemistry-organic-chemistry-basics', 'A species with an incomplete octet and positive charge on carbon is a:', 'Carbanion', 'Carbocation', 'Free radical', 'Carbene', 'B', 'A carbocation has a positively charged, electron-deficient carbon with six electrons.', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-organic-chemistry-basics-q10', 'neet-chemistry-organic-chemistry-basics', 'The +I (inductive) effect refers to a group that:', 'Withdraws electrons', 'Donates electron density through sigma bonds', 'Forms pi bonds', 'Breaks bonds', 'B', 'An electron-donating group shows a +I effect, pushing electron density through sigma bonds.', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-organic-chemistry-basics-q11', 'neet-chemistry-organic-chemistry-basics', 'Resonance structures differ only in the position of:', 'Atoms', 'Electrons', 'Nuclei', 'Molecular mass', 'B', 'Resonance structures differ only in electron distribution, not in the positions of atoms.', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-organic-chemistry-basics-q12', 'neet-chemistry-organic-chemistry-basics', 'A nucleophile is a species that is:', 'Electron-loving (electron deficient)', 'Electron-rich and attacks positive centres', 'Always neutral', 'A strong acid', 'B', 'A nucleophile is electron-rich and donates a pair of electrons to an electrophilic centre.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-atomic-structure-q09', 'neet-chemistry-atomic-structure', 'The maximum number of electrons in a shell is given by:', '2n²', 'n²', '2n', 'n', 'A', 'A shell of principal quantum number n holds a maximum of 2n² electrons.', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-atomic-structure-q10', 'neet-chemistry-atomic-structure', 'The principle that no two electrons in an atom have identical quantum numbers is:', 'Hund''s rule', 'Pauli exclusion principle', 'Aufbau principle', 'Heisenberg principle', 'B', 'The Pauli exclusion principle forbids two electrons from having the same set of four quantum numbers.', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-atomic-structure-q11', 'neet-chemistry-atomic-structure', 'Heisenberg''s uncertainty principle states that we cannot simultaneously know exactly an electron''s:', 'Charge and mass', 'Position and momentum', 'Spin and colour', 'Energy only', 'B', 'The uncertainty principle limits simultaneous precise knowledge of position and momentum.', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-atomic-structure-q12', 'neet-chemistry-atomic-structure', 'The shape of an orbital is described by which quantum number?', 'Principal (n)', 'Azimuthal / angular momentum (l)', 'Magnetic (m)', 'Spin (s)', 'B', 'The azimuthal quantum number l determines the shape (s, p, d, f) of the orbital.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure-q09', 'neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure', 'According to VSEPR theory, the shape of a molecule is determined by:', 'Repulsion between electron pairs around the central atom', 'Nuclear mass', 'The colour', 'The number of neutrons', 'A', 'VSEPR predicts geometry from minimising repulsions between bonding and lone electron pairs.', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure-q10', 'neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure', 'The hybridisation of carbon in methane (CH₄) is:', 'sp', 'sp²', 'sp³', 'sp³d', 'C', 'Carbon in CH₄ is sp³ hybridised, giving a tetrahedral shape (109.5°).', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure-q11', 'neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure', 'A coordinate (dative) bond is one in which:', 'Both electrons are supplied by one atom', 'Each atom supplies one electron', 'Electrons are transferred completely', 'No electrons are shared', 'A', 'In a coordinate bond, the shared electron pair comes entirely from one atom.', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure-q12', 'neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure', 'Hydrogen bonding is strongest when H is bonded to:', 'Carbon', 'Highly electronegative F, O, or N', 'Sulphur', 'Chlorine only', 'B', 'Hydrogen bonds form when H is attached to small, highly electronegative F, O, or N atoms.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-periodic-classification-q09', 'neet-chemistry-periodic-classification', 'Across a period (left to right), atomic radius generally:', 'Increases', 'Decreases', 'Stays constant', 'Doubles', 'B', 'Increasing nuclear charge pulls electrons closer, decreasing atomic radius across a period.', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-periodic-classification-q10', 'neet-chemistry-periodic-classification', 'Down a group, ionisation energy generally:', 'Increases', 'Decreases', 'Stays constant', 'Becomes zero', 'B', 'Larger size and shielding down a group make it easier to remove an electron.', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-periodic-classification-q11', 'neet-chemistry-periodic-classification', 'The most electronegative element in the periodic table is:', 'Oxygen', 'Fluorine', 'Chlorine', 'Nitrogen', 'B', 'Fluorine is the most electronegative element (~4.0 on the Pauling scale).', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-periodic-classification-q12', 'neet-chemistry-periodic-classification', 'The modern periodic law states that properties are a periodic function of atomic:', 'Mass', 'Number', 'Radius', 'Volume', 'B', 'Moseley''s modern periodic law bases periodicity on atomic number, not atomic mass.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-thermodynamics-q09', 'neet-chemistry-thermodynamics', 'For a spontaneous process at constant T and P, the Gibbs free energy change is:', 'Positive', 'Negative', 'Zero', 'Infinite', 'B', 'A spontaneous process has ΔG < 0.', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-thermodynamics-q10', 'neet-chemistry-thermodynamics', 'The first law of thermodynamics is a statement of:', 'Entropy increase', 'Conservation of energy', 'Spontaneity', 'Equilibrium', 'B', 'The first law (ΔU = q + w) expresses conservation of energy.', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-thermodynamics-q11', 'neet-chemistry-thermodynamics', 'Entropy is a measure of the:', 'Energy content', 'Disorder/randomness of a system', 'Temperature only', 'Pressure', 'B', 'Entropy (S) measures the degree of disorder or randomness in a system.', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-thermodynamics-q12', 'neet-chemistry-thermodynamics', 'An exothermic reaction has an enthalpy change (ΔH) that is:', 'Positive', 'Negative', 'Zero', 'Undefined', 'B', 'Exothermic reactions release heat, so ΔH is negative.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-equilibrium-q09', 'neet-chemistry-equilibrium', 'According to Le Chatelier''s principle, increasing pressure shifts a gaseous equilibrium toward:', 'More moles of gas', 'Fewer moles of gas', 'The reactants always', 'No change', 'B', 'Higher pressure favours the side with fewer gas molecules.', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-equilibrium-q10', 'neet-chemistry-equilibrium', 'The pH of a neutral aqueous solution at 25 °C is:', '0', '7', '14', '1', 'B', 'At 25 °C, neutral water has [H⁺] = 10⁻⁷ M, giving pH = 7.', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-equilibrium-q11', 'neet-chemistry-equilibrium', 'A catalyst affects a reaction at equilibrium by:', 'Shifting it toward products', 'Speeding attainment of equilibrium without changing its position', 'Increasing yield', 'Changing K', 'B', 'A catalyst speeds both directions equally, so equilibrium is reached faster but is unchanged.', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-equilibrium-q12', 'neet-chemistry-equilibrium', 'According to the Brønsted–Lowry concept, an acid is a substance that:', 'Donates a proton (H⁺)', 'Accepts a proton', 'Donates OH⁻', 'Accepts electrons', 'A', 'A Brønsted–Lowry acid is a proton donor; a base is a proton acceptor.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-hydrocarbons-q09', 'neet-chemistry-hydrocarbons', 'The general formula of alkanes is:', 'CnH2n', 'CnH2n+2', 'CnH2n-2', 'CnHn', 'B', 'Saturated alkanes have the general formula CnH2n+2.', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-hydrocarbons-q10', 'neet-chemistry-hydrocarbons', 'Alkenes undergo which characteristic type of reaction?', 'Substitution', 'Electrophilic addition', 'Elimination only', 'No reaction', 'B', 'The C=C double bond of alkenes undergoes electrophilic addition reactions.', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-hydrocarbons-q11', 'neet-chemistry-hydrocarbons', 'Benzene is an example of a(n):', 'Aliphatic alkane', 'Aromatic hydrocarbon', 'Alkyne', 'Cycloalkane without resonance', 'B', 'Benzene is aromatic, with delocalised π electrons obeying Hückel''s rule (4n+2).', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-hydrocarbons-q12', 'neet-chemistry-hydrocarbons', 'Markovnikov''s rule governs the addition of HX to:', 'Alkanes', 'Unsymmetrical alkenes', 'Aromatic rings', 'Alcohols', 'B', 'Markovnikov''s rule predicts the major product when HX adds to an unsymmetrical alkene.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-haloalkanes-haloarenes-q09', 'neet-chemistry-haloalkanes-haloarenes', 'An SN2 reaction proceeds with:', 'Retention of configuration', 'Inversion of configuration', 'Racemisation', 'No stereochemistry', 'B', 'SN2 involves backside attack, inverting the configuration (Walden inversion).', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-haloalkanes-haloarenes-q10', 'neet-chemistry-haloalkanes-haloarenes', 'SN1 reactions are fastest with which type of halide?', 'Primary', 'Tertiary', 'Methyl', 'Vinyl', 'B', 'Tertiary halides form the most stable carbocation, favouring the SN1 pathway.', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-haloalkanes-haloarenes-q11', 'neet-chemistry-haloalkanes-haloarenes', 'Haloarenes are less reactive toward nucleophilic substitution because of:', 'Partial double-bond character of the C–X bond (resonance)', 'Weak C–X bonds', 'High reactivity of the ring', 'Absence of halogen', 'A', 'Resonance gives the C–X bond partial double-bond character, making it stronger and less reactive.', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-haloalkanes-haloarenes-q12', 'neet-chemistry-haloalkanes-haloarenes', 'A polar aprotic solvent generally favours which mechanism?', 'SN1', 'SN2', 'E1', 'Free radical', 'B', 'Polar aprotic solvents enhance nucleophile strength, favouring the SN2 mechanism.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-alcohols-phenols-ethers-q09', 'neet-chemistry-alcohols-phenols-ethers', 'Phenol is more acidic than ethanol because the phenoxide ion is:', 'Destabilised', 'Stabilised by resonance', 'Neutral', 'A strong base', 'B', 'The negative charge in phenoxide is delocalised into the ring by resonance, stabilising it.', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-alcohols-phenols-ethers-q10', 'neet-chemistry-alcohols-phenols-ethers', 'Oxidation of a primary alcohol first gives:', 'A ketone', 'An aldehyde', 'A carboxylic acid only', 'An ether', 'B', 'Primary alcohols oxidise to aldehydes, then further to carboxylic acids.', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-alcohols-phenols-ethers-q11', 'neet-chemistry-alcohols-phenols-ethers', 'Ethers are prepared by the Williamson synthesis from an alkyl halide and:', 'An alkoxide', 'An acid', 'A ketone', 'An amine', 'A', 'Williamson ether synthesis reacts an alkyl halide with a sodium alkoxide (SN2).', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-alcohols-phenols-ethers-q12', 'neet-chemistry-alcohols-phenols-ethers', 'Oxidation of a secondary alcohol gives a:', 'Ketone', 'Aldehyde', 'Carboxylic acid', 'Ester', 'A', 'Secondary alcohols oxidise to ketones, which resist further easy oxidation.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids-q09', 'neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids', 'The characteristic reaction of the carbonyl group is:', 'Nucleophilic addition', 'Electrophilic substitution', 'Free-radical addition', 'Elimination', 'A', 'The polar C=O group of aldehydes and ketones undergoes nucleophilic addition.', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids-q10', 'neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids', 'Tollens'' reagent gives a silver mirror with:', 'Ketones', 'Aldehydes', 'Ethers', 'Alkanes', 'B', 'Aldehydes reduce Tollens'' reagent to metallic silver (a mirror); ketones do not.', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids-q11', 'neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids', 'Carboxylic acids are more acidic than phenols because the carboxylate ion is:', 'Stabilised by resonance over two equivalent oxygens', 'Non-resonating', 'Positively charged', 'A strong base', 'A', 'Resonance over two equivalent oxygen atoms stabilises the carboxylate ion strongly.', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids-q12', 'neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids', 'Aldehydes and ketones with an α-hydrogen undergo the:', 'Aldol condensation', 'Friedel–Crafts reaction', 'Wurtz reaction', 'Kolbe reaction', 'A', 'α-Hydrogen-bearing carbonyl compounds undergo the base-catalysed aldol condensation.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-biomolecules-chemistry-q09', 'neet-chemistry-biomolecules-chemistry', 'Glucose and fructose are examples of:', 'Disaccharides', 'Monosaccharides', 'Polysaccharides', 'Proteins', 'B', 'Glucose and fructose are simple sugars (monosaccharides).', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-biomolecules-chemistry-q10', 'neet-chemistry-biomolecules-chemistry', 'The bond joining two amino acids in a protein is the:', 'Glycosidic bond', 'Peptide bond', 'Ester bond', 'Phosphodiester bond', 'B', 'Amino acids link through peptide (amide) bonds to form proteins.', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-biomolecules-chemistry-q11', 'neet-chemistry-biomolecules-chemistry', 'Starch and cellulose are polymers of:', 'Amino acids', 'Glucose', 'Fatty acids', 'Nucleotides', 'B', 'Both starch and cellulose are polysaccharides made of glucose units.', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-biomolecules-chemistry-q12', 'neet-chemistry-biomolecules-chemistry', 'Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) is:', 'Fat-soluble', 'Water-soluble', 'A mineral', 'A protein', 'B', 'Vitamin C is water-soluble (like the B vitamins); A, D, E, K are fat-soluble.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds-q09', 'neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds', 'The number of ligand donor atoms bonded to the central metal is the:', 'Oxidation state', 'Coordination number', 'Atomic number', 'Valency only', 'B', 'The coordination number is the count of donor atoms directly bonded to the metal.', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds-q10', 'neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds', 'EDTA is a ligand of denticity:', 'Monodentate', 'Hexadentate', 'Bidentate', 'Tridentate', 'B', 'EDTA binds through six donor atoms (2 N + 4 O), so it is hexadentate.', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds-q11', 'neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds', 'According to CFT, the colour of transition-metal complexes is due to:', 'd–d electronic transitions', 'Nuclear transitions', 'Ionic bonds', 'Sigma bonds only', 'A', 'Absorption promoting a d-electron across the split (Δ) gives the observed colour.', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds-q12', 'neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds', 'A strong-field ligand such as CN⁻ tends to give a complex that is:', 'High-spin', 'Low-spin', 'Colourless always', 'Tetrahedral always', 'B', 'Strong-field ligands cause a large splitting, pairing electrons to form low-spin complexes.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-basic-concepts-mole-q01', 'neet-chemistry-basic-concepts-mole', 'One mole of any substance contains how many particles?', '6.022 × 10²³', '3.011 × 10²³', '6.022 × 10²²', '1.008 × 10²³', 'A', 'A mole contains Avogadro''s number, 6.022 × 10²³ particles.', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-basic-concepts-mole-q02', 'neet-chemistry-basic-concepts-mole', 'The volume occupied by one mole of an ideal gas at STP is:', '22.4 L', '1 L', '11.2 L', '24 L', 'A', 'One mole of an ideal gas occupies 22.4 L at standard temperature and pressure.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-basic-concepts-mole-q03', 'neet-chemistry-basic-concepts-mole', 'The empirical formula gives the:', 'Actual number of atoms', 'Simplest whole-number ratio of atoms', 'Molar mass', 'Bond angles', 'B', 'The empirical formula expresses the simplest whole-number ratio of the elements.', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-basic-concepts-mole-q04', 'neet-chemistry-basic-concepts-mole', 'The reactant that is completely consumed in a reaction is the:', 'Excess reagent', 'Limiting reagent', 'Catalyst', 'Product', 'B', 'The limiting reagent is used up first and determines the amount of product formed.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-basic-concepts-mole-q05', 'neet-chemistry-basic-concepts-mole', 'Molarity is defined as moles of solute per:', 'Litre of solution', 'Kilogram of solvent', 'Mole of solution', 'Gram of solute', 'A', 'Molarity (M) = moles of solute per litre of solution.', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-basic-concepts-mole-q06', 'neet-chemistry-basic-concepts-mole', 'Which concentration term is independent of temperature?', 'Molarity', 'Molality', 'Normality', 'Formality', 'B', 'Molality (mol/kg) uses mass, so it does not change with temperature; molarity does.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-basic-concepts-mole-q07', 'neet-chemistry-basic-concepts-mole', 'The number of moles in 36 g of water (molar mass 18) is:', '1', '2', '0.5', '4', 'B', 'Moles = mass/molar mass = 36/18 = 2 mol.', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-basic-concepts-mole-q08', 'neet-chemistry-basic-concepts-mole', 'The law of conservation of mass states that in a chemical reaction, mass is:', 'Created', 'Neither created nor destroyed', 'Destroyed', 'Doubled', 'B', 'Mass is conserved in chemical reactions (Lavoisier''s law).', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-basic-concepts-mole-q09', 'neet-chemistry-basic-concepts-mole', 'The molar mass of CO₂ (C = 12, O = 16) is:', '28 g/mol', '44 g/mol', '32 g/mol', '12 g/mol', 'B', '12 + 2(16) = 44 g/mol.', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-basic-concepts-mole-q10', 'neet-chemistry-basic-concepts-mole', 'The law of definite proportions states that a compound always contains elements in a fixed ratio by:', 'Volume', 'Mass', 'Number of atoms only', 'Density', 'B', 'A pure compound has a fixed proportion of elements by mass (Proust''s law).', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-basic-concepts-mole-q11', 'neet-chemistry-basic-concepts-mole', 'The number of atoms in 1 mole of oxygen gas (O₂) is:', '6.022 × 10²³ molecules and 1.2 × 10²⁴ atoms', 'Exactly 1 atom', '22.4 atoms', '18 atoms', 'A', '1 mole O₂ has 6.022 × 10²³ molecules, each with 2 atoms → 1.204 × 10²⁴ atoms.', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-basic-concepts-mole-q12', 'neet-chemistry-basic-concepts-mole', 'Percentage yield is calculated as:', '(Actual yield / theoretical yield) × 100', 'Theoretical yield × 100', 'Actual yield only', 'Moles of reactant × 100', 'A', '% yield = (actual/theoretical) × 100.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-states-of-matter-q01', 'neet-chemistry-states-of-matter', 'Boyle''s law states that at constant temperature, pressure is:', 'Directly proportional to volume', 'Inversely proportional to volume', 'Independent of volume', 'Equal to volume', 'B', 'At constant T, P ∝ 1/V (Boyle''s law).', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-states-of-matter-q02', 'neet-chemistry-states-of-matter', 'Charles''s law relates, at constant pressure, volume to:', 'Absolute temperature (directly)', 'Pressure', 'Inverse temperature', 'Mass', 'A', 'At constant P, V ∝ T (in kelvin) — Charles''s law.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-states-of-matter-q03', 'neet-chemistry-states-of-matter', 'The ideal gas equation is:', 'PV = nRT', 'PV = RT/n', 'P/V = nRT', 'PVT = nR', 'A', 'The ideal gas law combines the gas laws: PV = nRT.', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-states-of-matter-q04', 'neet-chemistry-states-of-matter', 'Dalton''s law of partial pressures says total pressure equals the:', 'Product of partial pressures', 'Sum of partial pressures', 'Average pressure', 'Difference of pressures', 'B', 'Total pressure of a gas mixture equals the sum of the partial pressures.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-states-of-matter-q05', 'neet-chemistry-states-of-matter', 'According to Graham''s law, the rate of diffusion of a gas is inversely proportional to:', 'Its temperature', 'The square root of its molar mass', 'Its volume', 'Its pressure', 'B', 'Rate ∝ 1/√(molar mass); lighter gases diffuse faster.', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-states-of-matter-q06', 'neet-chemistry-states-of-matter', 'Real gases deviate most from ideal behaviour at:', 'High temperature and low pressure', 'Low temperature and high pressure', 'Standard conditions', 'Zero pressure', 'B', 'At low T and high P, intermolecular forces and molecular volume matter, causing deviation.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-states-of-matter-q07', 'neet-chemistry-states-of-matter', 'The van der Waals constant ''a'' accounts for:', 'Molecular volume', 'Intermolecular attractive forces', 'Temperature', 'Mass', 'B', '''a'' corrects for intermolecular attractions; ''b'' corrects for finite molecular volume.', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-states-of-matter-q08', 'neet-chemistry-states-of-matter', 'A gas can be liquefied only below its:', 'Boiling point', 'Critical temperature', 'Melting point', 'Triple point', 'B', 'Above the critical temperature, no amount of pressure can liquefy a gas.', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-states-of-matter-q08b', 'neet-chemistry-states-of-matter', 'Surface tension of a liquid generally ______ with rising temperature.', 'Increases', 'Decreases', 'Stays constant', 'Becomes infinite', 'B', 'Higher temperature weakens intermolecular forces, lowering surface tension.', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-states-of-matter-q10', 'neet-chemistry-states-of-matter', 'At absolute zero (0 K), the volume of an ideal gas theoretically becomes:', 'Maximum', 'Zero', 'Infinite', '22.4 L', 'B', 'Extrapolating Charles''s law, volume tends to zero at 0 K (a theoretical limit).', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-states-of-matter-q11', 'neet-chemistry-states-of-matter', 'Viscosity of a liquid is a measure of its:', 'Resistance to flow', 'Colour', 'Volatility', 'Density only', 'A', 'Viscosity measures internal resistance to flow; it decreases with temperature.', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-states-of-matter-q12', 'neet-chemistry-states-of-matter', 'The value of the gas constant R in SI units is approximately:', '8.314 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹', '22.4 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹', '0.0821 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹', '6.022 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹', 'A', 'R = 8.314 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ (or 0.0821 L·atm K⁻¹ mol⁻¹).', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-redox-reactions-q01', 'neet-chemistry-redox-reactions', 'Oxidation is defined as:', 'Gain of electrons', 'Loss of electrons', 'Gain of protons', 'Loss of neutrons', 'B', 'Oxidation is loss of electrons (increase in oxidation number).', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-redox-reactions-q02', 'neet-chemistry-redox-reactions', 'A reducing agent is a species that:', 'Is itself oxidised', 'Is itself reduced', 'Gains electrons', 'Does not change', 'A', 'A reducing agent donates electrons and is oxidised in the process.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-redox-reactions-q03', 'neet-chemistry-redox-reactions', 'The oxidation number of oxygen in most compounds is:', '+2', '−2', '0', '+1', 'B', 'Oxygen is usually −2 (except in peroxides −1 and OF₂ +2).', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-redox-reactions-q04', 'neet-chemistry-redox-reactions', 'In a redox reaction, the oxidising agent undergoes:', 'Oxidation', 'Reduction', 'No change', 'Sublimation', 'B', 'The oxidising agent gains electrons and is reduced.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-redox-reactions-q05', 'neet-chemistry-redox-reactions', 'A reaction in which the same element is both oxidised and reduced is:', 'Combination', 'Disproportionation', 'Displacement', 'Neutralisation', 'B', 'In disproportionation, one species is simultaneously oxidised and reduced.', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-redox-reactions-q06', 'neet-chemistry-redox-reactions', 'The oxidation number of hydrogen in most compounds is:', '−1', '+1', '0', '+2', 'B', 'Hydrogen is +1 in most compounds (but −1 in metal hydrides).', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-redox-reactions-q07', 'neet-chemistry-redox-reactions', 'The oxidation state of an element in its free (elemental) form is:', 'Zero', '+1', '−1', 'Equal to its group number', 'A', 'Free elements have an oxidation number of zero.', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-redox-reactions-q08', 'neet-chemistry-redox-reactions', 'The mnemonic ''OIL RIG'' means:', 'Oxidation Is Loss, Reduction Is Gain (of electrons)', 'Oxidation Is Gain', 'Reduction Is Loss', 'Oxygen Is Lost', 'A', 'OIL RIG: Oxidation Is Loss, Reduction Is Gain of electrons.', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-redox-reactions-q09', 'neet-chemistry-redox-reactions', 'The oxidation number of Mn in KMnO₄ is:', '+2', '+7', '+4', '+6', 'B', 'K(+1) + Mn + 4O(−2) = 0 → Mn = +7.', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-redox-reactions-q10', 'neet-chemistry-redox-reactions', 'Redox reactions are commonly balanced using the:', 'Ion-electron (half-reaction) method', 'Trial method only', 'Mass number method', 'Density method', 'A', 'The ion-electron method balances atoms and charge via separate half-reactions.', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-redox-reactions-q11', 'neet-chemistry-redox-reactions', 'In the reaction Zn + Cu²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cu, the reducing agent is:', 'Cu²⁺', 'Zn', 'Zn²⁺', 'Cu', 'B', 'Zn loses electrons (is oxidised), so it is the reducing agent.', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-redox-reactions-q12', 'neet-chemistry-redox-reactions', 'The sum of oxidation numbers in a neutral molecule is:', 'Zero', 'Equal to the number of atoms', 'Always positive', '+1', 'A', 'In a neutral compound, oxidation numbers sum to zero.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-solutions-q01', 'neet-chemistry-solutions', 'Colligative properties depend on the:', 'Nature of the solute', 'Number of solute particles', 'Colour of the solution', 'Solute mass only', 'B', 'Colligative properties depend on the number, not the identity, of solute particles.', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-solutions-q02', 'neet-chemistry-solutions', 'Raoult''s law states that the vapour pressure of a component is proportional to its:', 'Mass', 'Mole fraction', 'Density', 'Volume', 'B', 'For an ideal solution, a component''s partial vapour pressure ∝ its mole fraction.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-solutions-q03', 'neet-chemistry-solutions', 'Henry''s law relates the solubility of a gas to its:', 'Partial pressure', 'Temperature only', 'Colour', 'Molar mass', 'A', 'Henry''s law: the solubility of a gas is proportional to its partial pressure.', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-solutions-q04', 'neet-chemistry-solutions', 'Adding a non-volatile solute to a solvent causes the boiling point to:', 'Increase (elevation)', 'Decrease', 'Stay the same', 'Become zero', 'A', 'Boiling-point elevation: ΔTb = Kb·m, a colligative property.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-solutions-q05', 'neet-chemistry-solutions', 'The freezing point of a solution compared with the pure solvent is:', 'Higher', 'Lower (depression)', 'The same', 'Infinite', 'B', 'Freezing-point depression: ΔTf = Kf·m; solutions freeze at a lower temperature.', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-solutions-q06', 'neet-chemistry-solutions', 'Osmotic pressure is given by the equation:', 'π = CRT', 'π = PV', 'π = mRT/V²', 'π = nF', 'A', 'Osmotic pressure π = CRT, where C is molar concentration.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-solutions-q07', 'neet-chemistry-solutions', 'The van ''t Hoff factor (i) accounts for the:', 'Colour of solute', 'Dissociation or association of solute particles', 'Temperature only', 'Solvent volume', 'B', 'i corrects colligative properties for how the solute dissociates or associates.', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-solutions-q08', 'neet-chemistry-solutions', 'For NaCl fully dissociating in water, the van ''t Hoff factor i is approximately:', '1', '2', '3', '0.5', 'B', 'NaCl → Na⁺ + Cl⁻ gives two particles, so i ≈ 2.', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-solutions-q09', 'neet-chemistry-solutions', 'A solution that obeys Raoult''s law over the whole range is called:', 'An ideal solution', 'A colloidal solution', 'A supersaturated solution', 'An azeotrope', 'A', 'Ideal solutions obey Raoult''s law and have zero enthalpy/volume change on mixing.', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-solutions-q10', 'neet-chemistry-solutions', 'Osmosis is the movement of solvent molecules from:', 'High to low solute concentration', 'Low to high solute concentration (through a semipermeable membrane)', 'High to high', 'No net movement', 'B', 'Solvent moves toward higher solute concentration through a semipermeable membrane.', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-solutions-q11', 'neet-chemistry-solutions', 'The solubility of most gases in water ______ with rising temperature.', 'Increases', 'Decreases', 'Stays constant', 'Becomes infinite', 'B', 'Gas dissolution is exothermic, so gas solubility falls as temperature rises.', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-solutions-q12', 'neet-chemistry-solutions', 'Two liquids that boil at a constant composition (like an azeotrope) cannot be separated by:', 'Simple/fractional distillation', 'Filtration', 'Decantation', 'Evaporation', 'A', 'Azeotropes distil at constant composition, so distillation cannot separate them.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-electrochemistry-q01', 'neet-chemistry-electrochemistry', 'In a galvanic cell, oxidation occurs at the:', 'Cathode', 'Anode', 'Salt bridge', 'Both electrodes', 'B', 'Oxidation happens at the anode; reduction at the cathode.', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-electrochemistry-q02', 'neet-chemistry-electrochemistry', 'The standard electrode potential of the standard hydrogen electrode is:', '0 V', '+1 V', '−1 V', '+0.34 V', 'A', 'The SHE is the reference electrode, defined as 0 V.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-electrochemistry-q03', 'neet-chemistry-electrochemistry', 'The standard cell potential is calculated as:', 'E°cathode − E°anode', 'E°anode − E°cathode', 'E°cathode + E°anode', 'E°anode × E°cathode', 'A', 'E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode using reduction potentials.', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-electrochemistry-q04', 'neet-chemistry-electrochemistry', 'A cell reaction is spontaneous when E°cell is:', 'Positive', 'Negative', 'Zero', 'Infinite', 'A', 'Positive E°cell means ΔG° = −nFE°cell is negative (spontaneous).', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-electrochemistry-q05', 'neet-chemistry-electrochemistry', 'The Nernst equation at 298 K includes the term:', '(0.059/n) log Q', '(0.059 × n) Q', 'nRT/F only', 'log P only', 'A', 'E = E° − (0.059/n) log Q at 298 K.', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-electrochemistry-q06', 'neet-chemistry-electrochemistry', 'Faraday''s first law relates the mass deposited to the:', 'Quantity of charge passed', 'Temperature', 'Colour', 'Pressure', 'A', 'Mass deposited ∝ charge (Q = It) passed through the electrolyte.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-electrochemistry-q07', 'neet-chemistry-electrochemistry', 'Molar conductivity of an electrolyte ______ with dilution.', 'Increases', 'Decreases', 'Stays constant', 'Becomes zero', 'A', 'Molar conductivity increases on dilution as ions move more freely.', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-electrochemistry-q08', 'neet-chemistry-electrochemistry', 'A more positive standard reduction potential indicates a stronger:', 'Reducing agent', 'Oxidising agent', 'Acid', 'Base', 'B', 'A higher reduction potential means a greater tendency to be reduced — a strong oxidiser.', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-electrochemistry-q09', 'neet-chemistry-electrochemistry', 'One faraday of charge equals approximately:', '96,500 C', '22,400 C', '6,022 C', '1,000 C', 'A', 'One faraday (charge of 1 mole of electrons) is about 96,500 coulombs.', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-electrochemistry-q10', 'neet-chemistry-electrochemistry', 'An electrolytic cell uses electrical energy to drive a reaction that is:', 'Spontaneous', 'Non-spontaneous', 'At equilibrium', 'Exothermic only', 'B', 'Electrolysis supplies energy to force a non-spontaneous redox reaction.', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-electrochemistry-q11', 'neet-chemistry-electrochemistry', 'Kohlrausch''s law is used to determine limiting molar conductivity of:', 'Strong electrolytes only', 'Weak electrolytes', 'Metals', 'Gases', 'B', 'Kohlrausch''s law lets us find limiting molar conductivity of weak electrolytes from ion contributions.', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-electrochemistry-q12', 'neet-chemistry-electrochemistry', 'Rusting of iron is an example of:', 'Electrochemical corrosion', 'Sublimation', 'Neutralisation', 'Polymerisation', 'A', 'Rusting is an electrochemical (redox) corrosion process forming hydrated iron oxide.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemical-kinetics-q01', 'neet-chemistry-chemical-kinetics', 'The order of a reaction is:', 'Determined experimentally from the rate law', 'Equal to the stoichiometric coefficient always', 'Always one', 'The number of reactants', 'A', 'Order is found experimentally as the sum of exponents in the rate law.', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemical-kinetics-q02', 'neet-chemistry-chemical-kinetics', 'The half-life of a first-order reaction is:', 'Independent of initial concentration', 'Proportional to concentration', 'Inversely proportional to k²', 'Always 1 second', 'A', 'For first order, t½ = 0.693/k, independent of starting concentration.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemical-kinetics-q03', 'neet-chemistry-chemical-kinetics', 'The Arrhenius equation is:', 'k = A e^(−Ea/RT)', 'k = Ea/RT', 'k = A/RT', 'k = A e^(Ea/RT)', 'A', 'k = A·e^(−Ea/RT); rate rises with temperature and falls with activation energy.', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemical-kinetics-q04', 'neet-chemistry-chemical-kinetics', 'The units of the rate constant for a first-order reaction are:', 's⁻¹', 'mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹', 'L mol⁻¹ s⁻¹', 'L² mol⁻²', 'A', 'First-order rate constants have units of s⁻¹.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemical-kinetics-q05', 'neet-chemistry-chemical-kinetics', 'A catalyst increases the reaction rate by:', 'Raising the activation energy', 'Lowering the activation energy', 'Increasing temperature', 'Shifting equilibrium', 'B', 'A catalyst provides an alternative pathway with lower activation energy.', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemical-kinetics-q06', 'neet-chemistry-chemical-kinetics', 'The rate of a zero-order reaction is:', 'Independent of reactant concentration', 'Proportional to concentration squared', 'Proportional to concentration', 'Zero always', 'A', 'A zero-order rate equals k and does not change with concentration.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemical-kinetics-q07', 'neet-chemistry-chemical-kinetics', 'A rough rule of thumb is that reaction rate roughly doubles for every ______ rise in temperature.', '1 °C', '10 °C', '100 °C', '50 °C', 'B', 'Rates commonly increase about two- to three-fold per 10 °C rise.', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemical-kinetics-q08', 'neet-chemistry-chemical-kinetics', 'The minimum energy required for a reaction to occur is the:', 'Bond energy', 'Activation energy', 'Kinetic energy', 'Lattice energy', 'B', 'Activation energy (Ea) is the energy barrier reactants must overcome.', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemical-kinetics-q09', 'neet-chemistry-chemical-kinetics', 'In collision theory, an effective collision requires sufficient energy and proper:', 'Colour', 'Orientation', 'Mass', 'Volume', 'B', 'Molecules must collide with adequate energy (≥ Ea) and correct orientation.', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemical-kinetics-q10', 'neet-chemistry-chemical-kinetics', 'The slowest step in a reaction mechanism is the:', 'Rate-determining step', 'Fastest step', 'First step always', 'Last step always', 'A', 'The slowest elementary step determines the overall reaction rate.', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemical-kinetics-q11', 'neet-chemistry-chemical-kinetics', 'The molecularity of an elementary reaction is:', 'Always fractional', 'The number of molecules colliding in the step', 'Always zero', 'The order minus one', 'B', 'Molecularity is the number of reacting species in an elementary step (a whole number).', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemical-kinetics-q12', 'neet-chemistry-chemical-kinetics', 'For a first-order reaction, a plot of ln[A] versus time gives a:', 'Straight line with slope −k', 'Curve', 'Horizontal line', 'Parabola', 'A', 'First-order integrated law gives ln[A] = ln[A]₀ − kt, a straight line of slope −k.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-surface-chemistry-q01', 'neet-chemistry-surface-chemistry', 'Adsorption is the accumulation of a substance:', 'Throughout the bulk', 'At a surface', 'In the gas phase only', 'In solution only', 'B', 'Adsorption concentrates molecules at a surface, unlike absorption (uniform in bulk).', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-surface-chemistry-q02', 'neet-chemistry-surface-chemistry', 'Physisorption is characterised by:', 'Strong chemical bonds', 'Weak van der Waals forces (reversible)', 'High specificity', 'High activation energy', 'B', 'Physisorption involves weak van der Waals forces and is easily reversible.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-surface-chemistry-q03', 'neet-chemistry-surface-chemistry', 'The scattering of light by colloidal particles is called the:', 'Tyndall effect', 'Brownian motion', 'Raman effect', 'Photoelectric effect', 'A', 'The Tyndall effect is the scattering of a light beam by colloidal particles.', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-surface-chemistry-q04', 'neet-chemistry-surface-chemistry', 'The random zig-zag motion of colloidal particles is:', 'Tyndall effect', 'Brownian motion', 'Diffusion only', 'Sedimentation', 'B', 'Brownian motion is the ceaseless random movement of colloidal particles.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-surface-chemistry-q05', 'neet-chemistry-surface-chemistry', 'Enzymes act as which type of catalyst?', 'Highly specific biocatalysts', 'Non-specific', 'Inorganic catalysts', 'Poisons', 'A', 'Enzymes are highly specific biological catalysts.', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-surface-chemistry-q06', 'neet-chemistry-surface-chemistry', 'The size range of colloidal particles is approximately:', '1–1000 nm', '< 1 nm', '> 1 mm', '> 1 cm', 'A', 'Colloidal particles range from about 1 to 1000 nanometres.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-surface-chemistry-q07', 'neet-chemistry-surface-chemistry', 'The Hardy–Schulze rule concerns the coagulating power of ions and states it increases with:', 'Ionic charge', 'Ionic colour', 'Ion size only', 'Temperature', 'A', 'The higher the charge of the coagulating ion, the greater its coagulating power.', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-surface-chemistry-q08', 'neet-chemistry-surface-chemistry', 'Milk is an example of which type of colloid?', 'Emulsion (liquid in liquid)', 'Aerosol', 'Gel', 'Solid sol', 'A', 'Milk is an emulsion of liquid fat dispersed in water.', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-surface-chemistry-q08b', 'neet-chemistry-surface-chemistry', 'Chemisorption differs from physisorption in that it:', 'Involves chemical bond formation (specific, strong)', 'Is weaker', 'Is always reversible', 'Has no activation energy', 'A', 'Chemisorption forms strong, specific chemical bonds and is generally irreversible.', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-surface-chemistry-q10', 'neet-chemistry-surface-chemistry', 'A catalyst that is in the same phase as the reactants is called:', 'Heterogeneous', 'Homogeneous', 'Enzymatic', 'Negative', 'B', 'In homogeneous catalysis, the catalyst and reactants are in the same phase.', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-surface-chemistry-q11', 'neet-chemistry-surface-chemistry', 'Adsorption is generally an ______ process (releases heat).', 'Endothermic', 'Exothermic', 'Isothermal only', 'Athermal', 'B', 'Adsorption is exothermic; it decreases surface energy, releasing heat.', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-surface-chemistry-q12', 'neet-chemistry-surface-chemistry', 'The process of removing dissolved impurities from a colloid using a membrane is:', 'Dialysis', 'Distillation', 'Filtration', 'Sublimation', 'A', 'Dialysis purifies colloids by letting small ions/molecules diffuse through a membrane.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-s-block-elements-q01', 'neet-chemistry-s-block-elements', 'Group 1 elements are known as the:', 'Alkaline earth metals', 'Alkali metals', 'Halogens', 'Noble gases', 'B', 'Group 1 (Li, Na, K…) are the alkali metals with ns¹ configuration.', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-s-block-elements-q02', 'neet-chemistry-s-block-elements', 'Alkali metals are strong ______ agents.', 'Oxidising', 'Reducing', 'Dehydrating', 'Neutral', 'B', 'Alkali metals readily lose their single valence electron, acting as strong reducing agents.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-s-block-elements-q03', 'neet-chemistry-s-block-elements', 'The flame colour imparted by sodium is:', 'Yellow', 'Lilac', 'Brick-red', 'Green', 'A', 'Sodium gives a characteristic golden-yellow flame colour.', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-s-block-elements-q04', 'neet-chemistry-s-block-elements', 'Down group 1, the reactivity of alkali metals:', 'Increases', 'Decreases', 'Stays constant', 'Becomes zero', 'A', 'Ionisation energy falls down the group, so reactivity increases.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-s-block-elements-q05', 'neet-chemistry-s-block-elements', 'Washing soda is chemically:', 'NaHCO₃', 'Na₂CO₃·10H₂O', 'NaOH', 'NaCl', 'B', 'Washing soda is hydrated sodium carbonate, Na₂CO₃·10H₂O.', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-s-block-elements-q06', 'neet-chemistry-s-block-elements', 'Group 2 elements are called the:', 'Alkali metals', 'Alkaline earth metals', 'Chalcogens', 'Lanthanoids', 'B', 'Group 2 (Be, Mg, Ca…) are the alkaline earth metals with ns² configuration.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-s-block-elements-q07', 'neet-chemistry-s-block-elements', 'The diagonal relationship pairs lithium with:', 'Sodium', 'Magnesium', 'Beryllium', 'Calcium', 'B', 'Lithium shows a diagonal relationship with magnesium (similar charge/size ratio).', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-s-block-elements-q08', 'neet-chemistry-s-block-elements', 'Baking soda is chemically:', 'Na₂CO₃', 'NaHCO₃', 'NaOH', 'CaCO₃', 'B', 'Baking soda is sodium bicarbonate, NaHCO₃.', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-s-block-elements-q09', 'neet-chemistry-s-block-elements', 'Plaster of Paris is chemically:', 'CaSO₄·½H₂O', 'CaCO₃', 'CaO', 'Ca(OH)₂', 'A', 'Plaster of Paris is calcium sulphate hemihydrate, CaSO₄·½H₂O.', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-s-block-elements-q10', 'neet-chemistry-s-block-elements', 'Quicklime is chemically:', 'CaO', 'Ca(OH)₂', 'CaCO₃', 'CaCl₂', 'A', 'Quicklime is calcium oxide (CaO), formed by heating limestone.', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-s-block-elements-q11', 'neet-chemistry-s-block-elements', 'Calcium imparts which flame colour?', 'Yellow', 'Brick-red', 'Lilac', 'Blue', 'B', 'Calcium produces a brick-red flame colour.', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-s-block-elements-q12', 'neet-chemistry-s-block-elements', 's-block metals generally form compounds that are:', 'Covalent', 'Ionic', 'Metallic only', 'Non-bonded', 'B', 'Their low ionisation energies favour formation of ionic compounds.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-p-block-elements-q01', 'neet-chemistry-p-block-elements', 'Group 17 elements are called the:', 'Noble gases', 'Halogens', 'Chalcogens', 'Alkali metals', 'B', 'Group 17 (F, Cl, Br, I) are the halogens, the most reactive non-metals.', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-p-block-elements-q02', 'neet-chemistry-p-block-elements', 'The most reactive halogen is:', 'Iodine', 'Fluorine', 'Bromine', 'Chlorine', 'B', 'Fluorine is the most reactive halogen and strongest oxidising agent.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-p-block-elements-q03', 'neet-chemistry-p-block-elements', 'Noble gases (Group 18) are generally unreactive because they have:', 'A complete octet/stable configuration', 'One valence electron', 'High reactivity', 'Unpaired electrons', 'A', 'Their full valence shells make noble gases very stable and largely inert.', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-p-block-elements-q04', 'neet-chemistry-p-block-elements', 'The property of carbon to form long chains by self-linking is called:', 'Catenation', 'Allotropy', 'Isomerism', 'Polymerisation', 'A', 'Catenation is the self-linking of atoms; it is strongest in carbon.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-p-block-elements-q05', 'neet-chemistry-p-block-elements', 'The inert pair effect stabilises which oxidation state down a group?', 'Higher oxidation state', 'Lower oxidation state', 'Zero only', 'Negative only', 'B', 'The inert pair effect makes lower oxidation states more stable down a group (e.g., Pb²⁺ > Pb⁴⁺).', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-p-block-elements-q06', 'neet-chemistry-p-block-elements', 'Which element in group 15 exists as an inert diatomic gas at room temperature?', 'Phosphorus', 'Nitrogen', 'Arsenic', 'Bismuth', 'B', 'Nitrogen exists as a stable N₂ molecule with a strong triple bond.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-p-block-elements-q07', 'neet-chemistry-p-block-elements', 'Down group 17, the oxidising power of halogens:', 'Increases', 'Decreases', 'Stays constant', 'Becomes zero', 'B', 'Oxidising power decreases down the halogens (F₂ strongest, I₂ weakest).', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-p-block-elements-q08', 'neet-chemistry-p-block-elements', 'The first element of each p-block group shows anomalous behaviour mainly due to:', 'Small size and absence of d-orbitals', 'Large size', 'High mass', 'Radioactivity', 'A', 'The small size and lack of available d-orbitals give the first element unique properties.', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-p-block-elements-q09', 'neet-chemistry-p-block-elements', 'The ''king of chemicals'', widely used industrially, is:', 'HNO₃', 'H₂SO₄', 'HCl', 'NH₃', 'B', 'Sulphuric acid (H₂SO₄) is called the ''king of chemicals'' for its industrial importance.', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-p-block-elements-q10', 'neet-chemistry-p-block-elements', 'Which noble gas can form compounds like XeF₂ and XeF₄?', 'Helium', 'Xenon', 'Neon', 'Argon', 'B', 'Xenon, being larger and more polarisable, forms fluorides such as XeF₂, XeF₄, XeF₆.', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-p-block-elements-q11', 'neet-chemistry-p-block-elements', 'Boron is best classified as a:', 'Metal', 'Metalloid', 'Non-metal', 'Noble gas', 'B', 'Boron is a metalloid, showing properties intermediate between metals and non-metals.', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-p-block-elements-q12', 'neet-chemistry-p-block-elements', 'The gas responsible for the pungent smell and used in fertilisers is:', 'Ammonia (NH₃)', 'Oxygen', 'Argon', 'Neon', 'A', 'Ammonia (NH₃), a group-15 hydride, is key to nitrogenous fertilisers.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-d-f-block-elements-q01', 'neet-chemistry-d-f-block-elements', 'Transition elements are characterised by partially filled:', 's-orbitals', 'd-orbitals', 'p-orbitals', 'f-orbitals only', 'B', 'd-block (transition) elements have partially filled d-orbitals in their atoms or ions.', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-d-f-block-elements-q02', 'neet-chemistry-d-f-block-elements', 'Transition metals commonly show:', 'A single oxidation state', 'Variable oxidation states', 'No oxidation state', 'Only +1', 'B', 'Due to close energies of ns and (n−1)d electrons, transition metals show variable oxidation states.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-d-f-block-elements-q03', 'neet-chemistry-d-f-block-elements', 'The colour of transition-metal ions arises from:', 'd–d electronic transitions', 'Nuclear reactions', 's–s transitions', 'Ionic size', 'A', 'd–d transitions of electrons between split d-orbitals give characteristic colours.', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-d-f-block-elements-q04', 'neet-chemistry-d-f-block-elements', 'The steady decrease in size across the lanthanoid series is the:', 'Inert pair effect', 'Lanthanoid contraction', 'Diagonal relationship', 'Shielding effect', 'B', 'The lanthanoid contraction is the gradual size decrease across the 4f series.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-d-f-block-elements-q05', 'neet-chemistry-d-f-block-elements', 'Which is a strong oxidising agent among transition-metal compounds?', 'KMnO₄', 'NaCl', 'KNO₃ only', 'CaCO₃', 'A', 'Potassium permanganate (KMnO₄) is a powerful oxidising agent (Mn in +7).', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-d-f-block-elements-q06', 'neet-chemistry-d-f-block-elements', 'Transition metals often act as good catalysts because they:', 'Provide variable oxidation states and surfaces for adsorption', 'Are radioactive', 'Have full d-orbitals', 'Are non-metals', 'A', 'Variable oxidation states and adsorptive surfaces make transition metals effective catalysts.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-d-f-block-elements-q07', 'neet-chemistry-d-f-block-elements', 'The f-block actinoids are notable for being:', 'All radioactive', 'Non-metallic', 'Gaseous', 'Diamagnetic only', 'A', 'All actinoids (5f series) are radioactive elements.', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-d-f-block-elements-q08', 'neet-chemistry-d-f-block-elements', 'Zn, Cd, and Hg are not regarded as typical transition metals because their d-orbitals are:', 'Empty', 'Completely filled (d¹⁰)', 'Half-filled', 'Absent', 'B', 'With a full d¹⁰ configuration, they lack partially filled d-orbitals in common states.', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-d-f-block-elements-q09', 'neet-chemistry-d-f-block-elements', 'The paramagnetism of transition-metal ions is due to:', 'Unpaired d-electrons', 'Paired electrons', 'No electrons', 'Protons', 'A', 'Unpaired d-electrons make many transition-metal ions paramagnetic.', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-d-f-block-elements-q10', 'neet-chemistry-d-f-block-elements', 'In K₂Cr₂O₇, the oxidation state of chromium is:', '+3', '+6', '+2', '+7', 'B', 'In dichromate, Cr is +6 (a strong oxidising agent).', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-d-f-block-elements-q11', 'neet-chemistry-d-f-block-elements', 'The lanthanoid contraction causes the atomic sizes of the second and third transition series to be:', 'Very similar', 'Very different', 'Zero', 'Infinitely large', 'A', 'The contraction makes 4d and 5d elements nearly the same size (e.g., Zr and Hf).', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-d-f-block-elements-q12', 'neet-chemistry-d-f-block-elements', 'Transition metals frequently form alloys because their atoms have:', 'Very different sizes', 'Similar atomic sizes (can substitute in lattices)', 'No metallic character', 'Full outer shells', 'B', 'Similar atomic radii let transition metals substitute for one another, forming alloys.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-metallurgy-q01', 'neet-chemistry-metallurgy', 'Concentration of a sulphide ore is usually done by:', 'Froth flotation', 'Calcination', 'Electrolysis', 'Distillation', 'A', 'Froth flotation concentrates sulphide ores using their preferential wetting by oil.', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-metallurgy-q02', 'neet-chemistry-metallurgy', 'Roasting is the heating of an ore in:', 'Excess air (for sulphides)', 'Absence of air', 'Water', 'Vacuum', 'A', 'Roasting heats a (usually sulphide) ore in excess air to convert it to the oxide.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-metallurgy-q03', 'neet-chemistry-metallurgy', 'Calcination involves heating an ore:', 'In the absence/limited air (for carbonates/hydroxides)', 'In excess oxygen', 'In water', 'With acid', 'A', 'Calcination heats carbonate/hydroxide ores in limited air to drive off CO₂ or H₂O, giving the oxide.', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-metallurgy-q04', 'neet-chemistry-metallurgy', 'Highly reactive metals like sodium and aluminium are extracted by:', 'Electrolysis', 'Carbon reduction', 'Roasting only', 'Distillation', 'A', 'Very reactive metals cannot be reduced by carbon, so electrolysis of their molten salts is used.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-metallurgy-q05', 'neet-chemistry-metallurgy', 'Aluminium is extracted industrially by the:', 'Hall–Héroult process', 'Bessemer process', 'Haber process', 'Contact process', 'A', 'The Hall–Héroult process electrolyses alumina dissolved in molten cryolite.', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-metallurgy-q06', 'neet-chemistry-metallurgy', 'The thermite (aluminothermite) reaction uses aluminium to reduce:', 'Metal oxides like Fe₂O₃ and Cr₂O₃', 'Water', 'Sulphides', 'Carbonates', 'A', 'Aluminium reduces oxides such as Fe₂O₃ and Cr₂O₃ in the highly exothermic thermite reaction.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-metallurgy-q07', 'neet-chemistry-metallurgy', 'Ellingham diagrams help choose a suitable:', 'Reducing agent at a given temperature', 'Colour of a metal', 'Crystal structure', 'Melting point only', 'A', 'Ellingham diagrams plot ΔG° vs T to indicate feasible reductions.', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-metallurgy-q08', 'neet-chemistry-metallurgy', 'Zone refining is used to obtain very pure:', 'Silicon and germanium', 'Sodium', 'Aluminium', 'Iron', 'A', 'Zone refining purifies semiconductors like Si and Ge, exploiting solubility differences.', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-metallurgy-q09', 'neet-chemistry-metallurgy', 'In the extraction of iron, the reducing agent in the blast furnace is mainly:', 'Carbon monoxide', 'Aluminium', 'Hydrogen', 'Chlorine', 'A', 'CO produced from coke reduces iron oxide to iron in the blast furnace.', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-metallurgy-q10', 'neet-chemistry-metallurgy', 'Leaching is a method of ore concentration based on:', 'Selective dissolving of the ore in a suitable reagent', 'Magnetism', 'Heating', 'Density difference only', 'A', 'Leaching dissolves the desired mineral in a chemical, separating it from gangue (e.g., bauxite in NaOH).', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-metallurgy-q11', 'neet-chemistry-metallurgy', 'The naturally occurring material from which a metal is profitably extracted is a(n):', 'Ore', 'Alloy', 'Slag', 'Flux', 'A', 'An ore is a mineral deposit from which a metal can be economically extracted.', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-metallurgy-q12', 'neet-chemistry-metallurgy', 'The most widely used method to refine impure metals like copper is:', 'Electrolytic refining', 'Roasting', 'Calcination', 'Froth flotation', 'A', 'Electrolytic refining deposits pure metal at the cathode from an impure anode.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-amines-q01', 'neet-chemistry-amines', 'Amines are considered organic derivatives of:', 'Water', 'Ammonia', 'Methane', 'Benzene', 'B', 'Amines are formed by replacing H atoms of NH₃ with alkyl/aryl groups.', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-amines-q02', 'neet-chemistry-amines', 'A primary amine has the general structure:', 'R–NH₂', 'R₂NH', 'R₃N', 'R–OH', 'A', 'A primary amine has one alkyl group and two hydrogens on nitrogen (R–NH₂).', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-amines-q03', 'neet-chemistry-amines', 'Amines are basic because the nitrogen has a:', 'Lone pair of electrons', 'Positive charge', 'Double bond', 'Vacant orbital only', 'A', 'The lone pair on nitrogen can accept a proton, making amines basic.', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-amines-q04', 'neet-chemistry-amines', 'Aniline is a weaker base than aliphatic amines because its lone pair is:', 'Delocalised into the benzene ring', 'Absent', 'On oxygen', 'Strongly localised', 'A', 'In aniline the N lone pair is delocalised into the ring, reducing its availability and basicity.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-amines-q05', 'neet-chemistry-amines', 'The Gabriel phthalimide synthesis is used to prepare only:', 'Primary amines', 'Secondary amines', 'Tertiary amines', 'Aromatic amines', 'A', 'Gabriel synthesis gives pure primary aliphatic amines (not aromatic).', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-amines-q06', 'neet-chemistry-amines', 'The Hoffmann bromamide degradation converts an amide to an amine with:', 'One fewer carbon atom', 'One more carbon atom', 'The same carbons', 'Double the carbons', 'A', 'Hoffmann bromamide reaction gives a primary amine with one fewer carbon than the amide.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-amines-q07', 'neet-chemistry-amines', 'Diazonium salts are formed by treating aromatic primary amines with nitrous acid at:', '0–5 °C', '100 °C', 'Room temperature only', '−50 °C', 'A', 'Aryl diazonium salts form from aniline + HNO₂ (NaNO₂/HCl) at low temperature (0–5 °C).', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-amines-q08', 'neet-chemistry-amines', 'Amines can be prepared by the reduction of:', 'Nitro compounds or nitriles', 'Alcohols', 'Ethers', 'Alkanes', 'A', 'Reducing nitro compounds or nitriles yields amines.', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-amines-q09', 'neet-chemistry-amines', 'A tertiary amine has the structure:', 'R₃N', 'R–NH₂', 'R₂NH', 'R–CHO', 'A', 'A tertiary amine has three alkyl/aryl groups on nitrogen (R₃N) and no N–H.', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-amines-q10', 'neet-chemistry-amines', 'Diazonium salts are important because they can be converted into:', 'A wide variety of substituted benzenes and azo dyes', 'Alkanes only', 'Water', 'Metals', 'A', 'Diazonium salts undergo substitution and coupling, giving many aromatic compounds and dyes.', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-amines-q11', 'neet-chemistry-amines', 'In the gas phase, the order of basicity is generally:', '3° > 2° > 1° > NH₃', 'NH₃ > 1° > 2° > 3°', '1° > 2° > 3°', 'All equal', 'A', 'In the gas phase, more alkyl groups increase electron density on N, so 3° > 2° > 1° > NH₃.', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-amines-q12', 'neet-chemistry-amines', 'The coupling of a diazonium salt with phenol/aniline produces:', 'Azo dyes', 'Alcohols', 'Alkenes', 'Esters', 'A', 'Diazo coupling gives brightly coloured azo compounds used as dyes.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-polymers-q01', 'neet-chemistry-polymers', 'The small repeating units that make up a polymer are called:', 'Isotopes', 'Monomers', 'Ions', 'Radicals', 'B', 'Polymers are formed by joining many small monomer units.', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-polymers-q02', 'neet-chemistry-polymers', 'Polythene is an example of a(n):', 'Addition polymer', 'Condensation polymer', 'Natural polymer', 'Protein', 'A', 'Polythene forms by addition polymerisation of ethene.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-polymers-q03', 'neet-chemistry-polymers', 'Nylon-6,6 is an example of a(n):', 'Addition polymer', 'Condensation polymer', 'Elastomer', 'Natural rubber', 'B', 'Nylon-6,6 forms by condensation polymerisation (with loss of water).', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-polymers-q04', 'neet-chemistry-polymers', 'Rubber is an example of a(n):', 'Fibre', 'Elastomer', 'Thermosetting plastic', 'Protein', 'B', 'Rubber is an elastomer, with weak intermolecular forces allowing elasticity.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-polymers-q05', 'neet-chemistry-polymers', 'Bakelite is an example of a(n):', 'Thermoplastic', 'Thermosetting polymer', 'Elastomer', 'Natural polymer', 'B', 'Bakelite is a thermosetting polymer that sets permanently on heating.', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-polymers-q06', 'neet-chemistry-polymers', 'Teflon is a polymer of:', 'Tetrafluoroethene', 'Ethene', 'Vinyl chloride', 'Styrene', 'A', 'Teflon (PTFE) is polymerised tetrafluoroethene, valued for its non-stick, inert surface.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-polymers-q07', 'neet-chemistry-polymers', 'Terylene (Dacron) is a:', 'Polyester', 'Polyamide', 'Natural rubber', 'Polythene', 'A', 'Terylene is a polyester made from ethylene glycol and terephthalic acid.', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-polymers-q08', 'neet-chemistry-polymers', 'Vulcanisation of rubber involves adding:', 'Sulphur (cross-links chains)', 'Water', 'Nitrogen', 'Chlorine only', 'A', 'Vulcanisation introduces sulphur cross-links, making rubber harder and more elastic.', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-polymers-q09', 'neet-chemistry-polymers', 'PVC is a polymer of:', 'Vinyl chloride', 'Ethene', 'Propene', 'Styrene', 'A', 'Polyvinyl chloride (PVC) is polymerised vinyl chloride, used in pipes and insulation.', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-polymers-q10', 'neet-chemistry-polymers', 'A thermoplastic polymer:', 'Softens on heating and can be remoulded', 'Sets permanently', 'Is always natural', 'Cannot be melted', 'A', 'Thermoplastics soften on heating and harden on cooling, allowing reshaping.', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-polymers-q11', 'neet-chemistry-polymers', 'Which is a natural polymer?', 'Cellulose', 'Nylon', 'PVC', 'Bakelite', 'A', 'Cellulose (a glucose polymer) is a natural polymer; the others are synthetic.', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-polymers-q12', 'neet-chemistry-polymers', 'A biodegradable polymer is:', 'PHBV', 'Polythene', 'PVC', 'Teflon', 'A', 'PHBV (a polyhydroxyalkanoate) is biodegradable, unlike common synthetic plastics.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemistry-everyday-life-q01', 'neet-chemistry-chemistry-everyday-life', 'Antacids are used to treat:', 'Excess stomach acidity', 'Fever', 'Bacterial infection', 'Pain only', 'A', 'Antacids neutralise excess hydrochloric acid in the stomach.', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemistry-everyday-life-q02', 'neet-chemistry-chemistry-everyday-life', 'Antibiotics such as penicillin act by:', 'Killing or inhibiting microorganisms', 'Reducing acidity', 'Relieving pain only', 'Sweetening food', 'A', 'Antibiotics inhibit or kill microbes; penicillin disrupts bacterial cell-wall synthesis.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemistry-everyday-life-q03', 'neet-chemistry-chemistry-everyday-life', 'Antiseptics are substances applied to:', 'Living tissue to prevent infection', 'Floors and drains', 'Food as sweeteners', 'Metals', 'A', 'Antiseptics (e.g., Dettol) are applied to living tissue; disinfectants are for non-living surfaces.', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemistry-everyday-life-q04', 'neet-chemistry-chemistry-everyday-life', 'Aspirin is an example of a(n):', 'Analgesic (and antipyretic)', 'Antibiotic', 'Antacid', 'Sweetener', 'A', 'Aspirin relieves pain (analgesic) and fever, and has antiplatelet action.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemistry-everyday-life-q05', 'neet-chemistry-chemistry-everyday-life', 'Saccharin and aspartame are examples of:', 'Artificial sweeteners', 'Preservatives', 'Antibiotics', 'Antacids', 'A', 'Saccharin and aspartame are non-nutritive artificial sweeteners.', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemistry-everyday-life-q06', 'neet-chemistry-chemistry-everyday-life', 'Sodium benzoate is used in food as a:', 'Preservative', 'Sweetener', 'Colouring agent', 'Flavour', 'A', 'Sodium benzoate is a common food preservative that inhibits microbial growth.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemistry-everyday-life-q07', 'neet-chemistry-chemistry-everyday-life', 'Soaps are sodium or potassium salts of:', 'Long-chain fatty acids', 'Amino acids', 'Nucleic acids', 'Sugars', 'A', 'Soaps are salts of long-chain fatty acids made by saponification of fats.', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemistry-everyday-life-q08', 'neet-chemistry-chemistry-everyday-life', 'Detergents are preferred over soaps in hard water because they:', 'Do not form insoluble scum with Ca²⁺/Mg²⁺', 'Are natural', 'Are cheaper only', 'Do not clean', 'A', 'Detergents work in hard water because their calcium/magnesium salts are soluble (no scum).', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemistry-everyday-life-q09', 'neet-chemistry-chemistry-everyday-life', 'The cleansing action of soap involves the formation of:', 'Micelles around grease', 'Crystals', 'Precipitates', 'Gases', 'A', 'Soap molecules surround grease forming micelles, which are washed away with water.', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemistry-everyday-life-q10', 'neet-chemistry-chemistry-everyday-life', 'Antihistamines are used to relieve:', 'Allergy symptoms', 'Bacterial infection', 'Acidity', 'Pain from injury', 'A', 'Antihistamines counter the effects of histamine in allergic reactions.', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemistry-everyday-life-q11', 'neet-chemistry-chemistry-everyday-life', 'A disinfectant such as phenol (at high concentration) is used on:', 'Non-living surfaces (floors, drains)', 'Open wounds', 'Food', 'Skin', 'A', 'Disinfectants are applied to inanimate objects; they are too harsh for living tissue.', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemistry-everyday-life-q12', 'neet-chemistry-chemistry-everyday-life', 'Ranitidine and cimetidine are drugs used as:', 'Antacids (H₂-receptor blockers)', 'Antibiotics', 'Analgesics', 'Sweeteners', 'A', 'These H₂-receptor antagonists reduce stomach acid secretion, acting as antacids.', 11),
  ('neet-physics-ray-optics-q09', 'neet-physics-ray-optics', 'The power of a lens is measured in:', 'Watt', 'Dioptre', 'Newton', 'Joule', 'B', 'Lens power (P = 1/f in metres) is measured in dioptres (D).', 8),
  ('neet-physics-ray-optics-q10', 'neet-physics-ray-optics', 'For a concave mirror, a real image is formed when the object is:', 'Between the pole and focus', 'Beyond the focus', 'At the focus', 'At infinity only', 'B', 'A concave mirror forms a real image when the object lies beyond the focal point.', 9),
  ('neet-physics-ray-optics-q11', 'neet-physics-ray-optics', 'Total internal reflection occurs when light travels from a denser to a rarer medium and the angle of incidence:', 'Exceeds the critical angle', 'Is zero', 'Is less than the critical angle', 'Equals 90°', 'A', 'Beyond the critical angle, light is totally internally reflected (basis of optical fibres).', 10),
  ('neet-physics-ray-optics-q12', 'neet-physics-ray-optics', 'The refractive index of a medium is defined as:', 'Speed of light in vacuum / speed in the medium', 'Speed in medium / speed in vacuum', 'Frequency ratio', 'Wavelength product', 'A', 'μ = c/v: the ratio of light''s speed in vacuum to its speed in the medium.', 11),
  ('neet-physics-kinematics-q09', 'neet-physics-kinematics', 'For uniformly accelerated motion, v = u + at is which equation of motion?', 'First', 'Second', 'Third', 'Fourth', 'A', 'v = u + at is the first equation of motion relating velocity, acceleration, and time.', 8),
  ('neet-physics-kinematics-q10', 'neet-physics-kinematics', 'The area under a velocity–time graph gives:', 'Acceleration', 'Displacement', 'Force', 'Jerk', 'B', 'The area under a v–t graph equals the displacement of the object.', 9),
  ('neet-physics-kinematics-q11', 'neet-physics-kinematics', 'The slope of a velocity–time graph represents:', 'Displacement', 'Acceleration', 'Speed', 'Distance', 'B', 'The slope (rate of change of velocity) of a v–t graph is the acceleration.', 10),
  ('neet-physics-kinematics-q12', 'neet-physics-kinematics', 'A body thrown vertically upward has, at its highest point, a velocity of:', 'Maximum', 'Zero (acceleration g downward)', 'Equal to g', 'Infinite', 'B', 'At the top, velocity is momentarily zero while acceleration remains g downward.', 11),
  ('neet-physics-laws-of-motion-q09', 'neet-physics-laws-of-motion', 'Newton''s second law expresses force as:', 'F = ma (rate of change of momentum)', 'F = mv', 'F = m/a', 'F = a/m', 'A', 'Force equals the rate of change of momentum, F = ma for constant mass.', 8),
  ('neet-physics-laws-of-motion-q10', 'neet-physics-laws-of-motion', 'Newton''s third law states that action and reaction are:', 'Equal and in the same direction', 'Equal and opposite, on different bodies', 'Unequal', 'On the same body', 'B', 'Every action has an equal and opposite reaction, acting on different bodies.', 9),
  ('neet-physics-laws-of-motion-q11', 'neet-physics-laws-of-motion', 'The principle of conservation of linear momentum follows from:', 'Newton''s first law', 'Newton''s third law (and no external force)', 'Hooke''s law', 'Ohm''s law', 'B', 'With no external force, momentum is conserved — a consequence of the third law.', 10),
  ('neet-physics-laws-of-motion-q12', 'neet-physics-laws-of-motion', 'The maximum static friction before sliding is:', 'μ_s N', 'μ_k N only', 'Zero', 'mg always', 'A', 'Limiting static friction equals μ_s N, usually greater than kinetic friction μ_k N.', 11),
  ('neet-physics-work-energy-power-q09', 'neet-physics-work-energy-power', 'The work–energy theorem states that net work done equals the change in:', 'Potential energy', 'Kinetic energy', 'Momentum', 'Power', 'B', 'Net work done on a body equals its change in kinetic energy.', 8),
  ('neet-physics-work-energy-power-q10', 'neet-physics-work-energy-power', 'The SI unit of power is the:', 'Joule', 'Watt', 'Newton', 'Pascal', 'B', 'Power (rate of doing work) is measured in watts (1 W = 1 J/s).', 9),
  ('neet-physics-work-energy-power-q11', 'neet-physics-work-energy-power', 'In an elastic collision, the quantity that is conserved besides momentum is:', 'Kinetic energy', 'Temperature', 'Charge', 'Volume', 'A', 'Elastic collisions conserve both momentum and kinetic energy.', 10),
  ('neet-physics-work-energy-power-q12', 'neet-physics-work-energy-power', 'The work done by a force perpendicular to the displacement is:', 'Maximum', 'Zero', 'Negative', 'Equal to the force', 'B', 'Work = Fd cosθ; at θ = 90°, cosθ = 0, so the work is zero.', 11),
  ('neet-physics-gravitation-q09', 'neet-physics-gravitation', 'Newton''s law of gravitation states that the force between two masses is proportional to:', 'The product of the masses and inversely to the square of the distance', 'The sum of masses', 'The distance', 'The cube of the distance', 'A', 'F = Gm₁m₂/r², proportional to the masses'' product and inverse-square in distance.', 8),
  ('neet-physics-gravitation-q10', 'neet-physics-gravitation', 'The acceleration due to gravity g at the Earth''s centre is:', 'Maximum', 'Zero', '9.8 m/s²', 'Infinite', 'B', 'At the centre, the net gravitational field (and g) is zero.', 9),
  ('neet-physics-gravitation-q11', 'neet-physics-gravitation', 'The escape velocity from the Earth''s surface is approximately:', '11.2 km/s', '7.9 km/s', '3 × 10⁸ m/s', '1 km/s', 'A', 'Escape velocity from Earth is about 11.2 km/s.', 10),
  ('neet-physics-gravitation-q12', 'neet-physics-gravitation', 'A geostationary satellite has an orbital period of:', '24 hours', '1 hour', '12 hours', '1 month', 'A', 'A geostationary satellite orbits with a 24-hour period, matching Earth''s rotation.', 11),
  ('neet-physics-thermodynamics-q09', 'neet-physics-thermodynamics', 'The first law of thermodynamics is written as:', 'ΔU = Q − W', 'Q = ΔU × W', 'W = QΔU', 'ΔU = QW', 'A', 'First law: change in internal energy equals heat added minus work done by the system.', 8),
  ('neet-physics-thermodynamics-q10', 'neet-physics-thermodynamics', 'In an isothermal process, the temperature:', 'Increases', 'Remains constant', 'Decreases', 'Becomes zero', 'B', 'An isothermal process occurs at constant temperature.', 9),
  ('neet-physics-thermodynamics-q11', 'neet-physics-thermodynamics', 'The efficiency of a Carnot engine depends only on the:', 'Working substance', 'Temperatures of the two reservoirs', 'Pressure', 'Volume', 'B', 'Carnot efficiency η = 1 − T_c/T_h depends only on the reservoir temperatures.', 10),
  ('neet-physics-thermodynamics-q12', 'neet-physics-thermodynamics', 'The second law of thermodynamics implies that the entropy of an isolated system:', 'Always decreases', 'Never decreases', 'Is constant', 'Is zero', 'B', 'Entropy of an isolated system never decreases (it stays constant or increases).', 11),
  ('neet-physics-oscillations-waves-q09', 'neet-physics-oscillations-waves', 'The time period of a simple pendulum depends on:', 'Its mass', 'Its length and g', 'The amplitude only', 'The material', 'B', 'T = 2π√(L/g); the period depends on length and gravitational acceleration, not mass.', 8),
  ('neet-physics-oscillations-waves-q10', 'neet-physics-oscillations-waves', 'In SHM, the total energy is proportional to the square of the:', 'Amplitude', 'Mass', 'Time', 'Phase', 'A', 'Total energy E = ½mω²A² ∝ amplitude².', 9),
  ('neet-physics-oscillations-waves-q11', 'neet-physics-oscillations-waves', 'The speed of a wave is related to frequency and wavelength by:', 'v = fλ', 'v = f/λ', 'v = λ/f', 'v = f + λ', 'A', 'Wave speed v = frequency × wavelength = fλ.', 10),
  ('neet-physics-oscillations-waves-q12', 'neet-physics-oscillations-waves', 'Sound waves are:', 'Transverse', 'Longitudinal', 'Electromagnetic', 'Stationary only', 'B', 'Sound propagates as longitudinal (compression) waves requiring a medium.', 11),
  ('neet-physics-electrostatics-q09', 'neet-physics-electrostatics', 'Coulomb''s law states that the force between two point charges is proportional to:', 'The product of charges / (distance)²', 'The sum of charges', 'The distance', '1/charge', 'A', 'F = kq₁q₂/r²: proportional to the product of charges and inverse-square in distance.', 8),
  ('neet-physics-electrostatics-q10', 'neet-physics-electrostatics', 'The electric field inside a hollow charged conductor is:', 'Maximum', 'Zero', 'Uniform and nonzero', 'Infinite', 'B', 'The field inside a conductor in electrostatic equilibrium is zero.', 9),
  ('neet-physics-electrostatics-q11', 'neet-physics-electrostatics', 'The capacitance of a parallel-plate capacitor increases when a dielectric is inserted because the dielectric:', 'Reduces the field, storing more charge for the same voltage', 'Increases the plate distance', 'Removes charge', 'Has no effect', 'A', 'A dielectric of constant K increases capacitance by factor K (C = Kε₀A/d).', 10),
  ('neet-physics-electrostatics-q12', 'neet-physics-electrostatics', 'The energy stored in a capacitor is:', '½CV²', 'CV', '½C²V', 'CV²', 'A', 'Energy stored = ½CV² = ½QV = Q²/2C.', 11),
  ('neet-physics-current-electricity-q09', 'neet-physics-current-electricity', 'Ohm''s law states that voltage equals:', 'IR (current × resistance)', 'I/R', 'I + R', 'R/I', 'A', 'Ohm''s law: V = IR, where R is the resistance.', 8),
  ('neet-physics-current-electricity-q10', 'neet-physics-current-electricity', 'Resistances in series combine as:', 'R = R₁ + R₂ + …', '1/R = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂', 'R = R₁R₂', 'R = R₁ − R₂', 'A', 'In series, total resistance is the sum of individual resistances.', 9),
  ('neet-physics-current-electricity-q11', 'neet-physics-current-electricity', 'The power dissipated in a resistor is:', 'I²R', 'IR only', 'I/R', 'R/I²', 'A', 'Power P = I²R = V²/R = VI (Joule heating).', 10),
  ('neet-physics-current-electricity-q12', 'neet-physics-current-electricity', 'Kirchhoff''s junction (current) rule is based on conservation of:', 'Energy', 'Charge', 'Momentum', 'Mass', 'B', 'The junction rule (sum of currents in = out) expresses conservation of charge.', 11),
  ('neet-physics-moving-charges-magnetism-q09', 'neet-physics-moving-charges-magnetism', 'The force on a charge q moving with velocity v in a magnetic field B is:', 'qv × B', 'qB/v', 'q/vB', 'qvB parallel to v', 'A', 'The magnetic (Lorentz) force is F = qv × B, perpendicular to both v and B.', 8),
  ('neet-physics-moving-charges-magnetism-q10', 'neet-physics-moving-charges-magnetism', 'A charged particle moving parallel to a magnetic field experiences a force that is:', 'Maximum', 'Zero', 'Perpendicular', 'Equal to qE', 'B', 'When v is parallel to B, the angle is 0 and F = qvB sin0 = 0.', 9),
  ('neet-physics-moving-charges-magnetism-q11', 'neet-physics-moving-charges-magnetism', 'The magnetic field inside a long solenoid is:', 'Uniform and along the axis', 'Zero', 'Radial', 'Maximum at the ends', 'A', 'A long solenoid has a nearly uniform axial field B = μ₀nI inside.', 10),
  ('neet-physics-moving-charges-magnetism-q12', 'neet-physics-moving-charges-magnetism', 'A device that measures small currents using a coil in a magnetic field is a:', 'Galvanometer', 'Voltmeter only', 'Transformer', 'Capacitor', 'A', 'A moving-coil galvanometer detects and measures small currents via torque on a coil.', 11),
  ('neet-physics-electromagnetic-induction-ac-q09', 'neet-physics-electromagnetic-induction-ac', 'Faraday''s law states that the induced EMF equals the rate of change of:', 'Charge', 'Magnetic flux', 'Current only', 'Resistance', 'B', 'Induced EMF = −dΦ/dt, the negative rate of change of magnetic flux.', 8),
  ('neet-physics-electromagnetic-induction-ac-q10', 'neet-physics-electromagnetic-induction-ac', 'Lenz''s law is a consequence of the conservation of:', 'Charge', 'Energy', 'Momentum', 'Mass', 'B', 'Lenz''s law (opposing the change) ensures energy conservation.', 9),
  ('neet-physics-electromagnetic-induction-ac-q11', 'neet-physics-electromagnetic-induction-ac', 'A transformer works on the principle of:', 'Self-induction only', 'Mutual induction', 'Electrostatics', 'Resistance heating', 'B', 'A transformer transfers energy between coils via mutual induction (AC only).', 10),
  ('neet-physics-electromagnetic-induction-ac-q12', 'neet-physics-electromagnetic-induction-ac', 'The RMS value of an AC of peak current I₀ is:', 'I₀/√2', 'I₀√2', 'I₀', '2I₀', 'A', 'For sinusoidal AC, I_rms = I₀/√2 ≈ 0.707 I₀.', 11),
  ('neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics-q09', 'neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics', 'The photoelectric equation gives the maximum kinetic energy of electrons as:', 'hν − φ', 'hν + φ', 'φ − hν', 'hν', 'A', 'KE_max = hν − φ (work function), Einstein''s photoelectric equation.', 8),
  ('neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics-q10', 'neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics', 'The de Broglie wavelength of a particle is inversely proportional to its:', 'Charge', 'Momentum', 'Volume', 'Temperature', 'B', 'λ = h/p, so wavelength is inversely proportional to momentum.', 9),
  ('neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics-q11', 'neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics', 'The stopping potential in the photoelectric effect depends on the light''s:', 'Intensity', 'Frequency', 'Colourless nature', 'Polarisation', 'B', 'Stopping potential depends on frequency (via KE_max), not on intensity.', 10),
  ('neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics-q12', 'neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics', 'The photon is a packet (quantum) of:', 'Charge', 'Electromagnetic energy', 'Mass', 'Momentum only', 'B', 'A photon is a quantum of electromagnetic energy, E = hν.', 11),
  ('neet-physics-semiconductor-electronics-q09', 'neet-physics-semiconductor-electronics', 'Adding a pentavalent impurity to silicon produces a(n):', 'p-type semiconductor', 'n-type semiconductor', 'Insulator', 'Conductor', 'B', 'Pentavalent (donor) doping adds free electrons, giving an n-type semiconductor.', 8),
  ('neet-physics-semiconductor-electronics-q10', 'neet-physics-semiconductor-electronics', 'A p–n junction diode allows current to flow easily when it is:', 'Forward biased', 'Reverse biased', 'Unbiased', 'At absolute zero', 'A', 'In forward bias the depletion region narrows and the diode conducts.', 9),
  ('neet-physics-semiconductor-electronics-q11', 'neet-physics-semiconductor-electronics', 'The main function of a diode in a circuit is to act as a:', 'Rectifier', 'Amplifier', 'Oscillator', 'Capacitor', 'A', 'A diode conducts in one direction, rectifying AC to DC.', 10),
  ('neet-physics-semiconductor-electronics-q12', 'neet-physics-semiconductor-electronics', 'Adding a trivalent impurity to silicon creates a p-type semiconductor with majority carriers being:', 'Electrons', 'Holes', 'Protons', 'Neutrons', 'B', 'Trivalent (acceptor) doping creates holes as the majority charge carriers.', 11),
  ('neet-physics-units-measurements-q01', 'neet-physics-units-measurements', 'How many base units are there in the SI system?', '5', '7', '9', '3', 'B', 'The SI system has seven base units (m, kg, s, A, K, mol, cd).', 0),
  ('neet-physics-units-measurements-q02', 'neet-physics-units-measurements', 'The dimensional formula of force is:', 'MLT⁻²', 'ML²T⁻²', 'MLT⁻¹', 'ML⁻¹T⁻²', 'A', 'Force = mass × acceleration = M × LT⁻² = MLT⁻².', 1),
  ('neet-physics-units-measurements-q03', 'neet-physics-units-measurements', 'When quantities are multiplied or divided, the ______ errors add.', 'Absolute', 'Relative (fractional)', 'Random', 'Zero', 'B', 'For products/quotients, the fractional (relative) errors add.', 2),
  ('neet-physics-units-measurements-q04', 'neet-physics-units-measurements', 'Accuracy of a measurement refers to how close it is to the:', 'True value', 'Mean of readings', 'Least count', 'Zero error', 'A', 'Accuracy is closeness to the true value; precision is reproducibility.', 3),
  ('neet-physics-units-measurements-q05', 'neet-physics-units-measurements', 'The dimensional formula of energy (work) is:', 'ML²T⁻²', 'MLT⁻²', 'ML²T⁻¹', 'MLT⁻¹', 'A', 'Energy = force × distance = MLT⁻² × L = ML²T⁻².', 4),
  ('neet-physics-units-measurements-q06', 'neet-physics-units-measurements', 'Dimensional analysis can be used to:', 'Check the consistency of an equation', 'Find numerical constants exactly', 'Determine direction', 'Measure mass', 'A', 'Dimensional analysis checks homogeneity/consistency but cannot find dimensionless constants.', 5),
  ('neet-physics-units-measurements-q07', 'neet-physics-units-measurements', 'The number of significant figures in 0.00420 is:', '2', '3', '5', '6', 'B', 'Leading zeros are not significant; 4, 2, and the trailing 0 give three significant figures.', 6),
  ('neet-physics-units-measurements-q08', 'neet-physics-units-measurements', 'The smallest value an instrument can measure is its:', 'Range', 'Least count', 'Zero error', 'Accuracy', 'B', 'The least count is the smallest measurable increment of an instrument.', 7),
  ('neet-physics-units-measurements-q09', 'neet-physics-units-measurements', 'The dimensional formula of pressure is:', 'ML⁻¹T⁻²', 'MLT⁻²', 'ML²T⁻²', 'MT⁻²', 'A', 'Pressure = force/area = MLT⁻²/L² = ML⁻¹T⁻².', 8),
  ('neet-physics-units-measurements-q10', 'neet-physics-units-measurements', 'When quantities are added or subtracted, the ______ errors add.', 'Absolute', 'Relative', 'Percentage only', 'No', 'A', 'For sums/differences, the absolute errors add.', 9),
  ('neet-physics-units-measurements-q11', 'neet-physics-units-measurements', 'The candela is the SI base unit of:', 'Luminous intensity', 'Electric current', 'Temperature', 'Amount of substance', 'A', 'The candela measures luminous intensity.', 10),
  ('neet-physics-units-measurements-q12', 'neet-physics-units-measurements', 'A dimensionless quantity among the following is:', 'Strain', 'Force', 'Velocity', 'Momentum', 'A', 'Strain is a ratio of lengths, so it is dimensionless.', 11),
  ('neet-physics-motion-in-a-plane-q01', 'neet-physics-motion-in-a-plane', 'The horizontal component of a projectile''s velocity during flight (ignoring air resistance):', 'Increases', 'Remains constant', 'Decreases', 'Becomes zero at the top', 'B', 'With no horizontal force, the horizontal velocity u cosθ stays constant.', 0),
  ('neet-physics-motion-in-a-plane-q02', 'neet-physics-motion-in-a-plane', 'The range of a projectile is maximum at a launch angle of:', '30°', '45°', '60°', '90°', 'B', 'R = u²sin2θ/g is maximum when sin2θ = 1, i.e., θ = 45°.', 1),
  ('neet-physics-motion-in-a-plane-q03', 'neet-physics-motion-in-a-plane', 'The path (trajectory) of a projectile is a:', 'Straight line', 'Parabola', 'Circle', 'Hyperbola', 'B', 'Combining uniform horizontal and accelerated vertical motion gives a parabolic path.', 2),
  ('neet-physics-motion-in-a-plane-q04', 'neet-physics-motion-in-a-plane', 'The centripetal acceleration in uniform circular motion is:', 'v²/r toward the centre', 'v²r outward', 'Zero', 'vr', 'A', 'Centripetal acceleration a = v²/r points toward the centre of the circle.', 3),
  ('neet-physics-motion-in-a-plane-q05', 'neet-physics-motion-in-a-plane', 'At the highest point of its trajectory, a projectile''s vertical velocity is:', 'Maximum', 'Zero', 'Equal to u', 'Equal to g', 'B', 'The vertical velocity becomes zero at the peak before the descent.', 4),
  ('neet-physics-motion-in-a-plane-q06', 'neet-physics-motion-in-a-plane', 'The time of flight of a projectile launched at speed u and angle θ is:', '2u sinθ/g', 'u cosθ/g', 'u²/g', 'u sinθ/2g', 'A', 'Time of flight T = 2u sinθ/g.', 5),
  ('neet-physics-motion-in-a-plane-q07', 'neet-physics-motion-in-a-plane', 'In uniform circular motion, the quantity that changes continuously is the:', 'Speed', 'Direction of velocity', 'Kinetic energy', 'Radius', 'B', 'Speed is constant but velocity''s direction changes, requiring centripetal acceleration.', 6),
  ('neet-physics-motion-in-a-plane-q08', 'neet-physics-motion-in-a-plane', 'The maximum height of a projectile is given by:', 'u²sin²θ/2g', 'u²sin2θ/g', '2u sinθ/g', 'u cosθ/g', 'A', 'Maximum height H = u²sin²θ/2g.', 7),
  ('neet-physics-motion-in-a-plane-q09', 'neet-physics-motion-in-a-plane', 'Two vectors of equal magnitude have a resultant equal to either one when the angle between them is:', '60°', '120°', '90°', '180°', 'B', 'For equal vectors, the resultant equals their magnitude when the angle is 120°.', 8),
  ('neet-physics-motion-in-a-plane-q10', 'neet-physics-motion-in-a-plane', 'The horizontal range is the same for launch angles of:', 'θ and (90° − θ)', 'θ and 90°', 'θ and 180°', 'Only 45°', 'A', 'Complementary angles (θ and 90°−θ) give the same range since sin2θ = sin(180°−2θ).', 9),
  ('neet-physics-motion-in-a-plane-q11', 'neet-physics-motion-in-a-plane', 'The centripetal force required for circular motion is directed:', 'Along the velocity', 'Toward the centre', 'Away from the centre', 'Tangentially', 'B', 'Centripetal force points toward the centre, providing the acceleration v²/r.', 10),
  ('neet-physics-motion-in-a-plane-q12', 'neet-physics-motion-in-a-plane', 'A scalar quantity among the following is:', 'Velocity', 'Speed', 'Displacement', 'Acceleration', 'B', 'Speed is a scalar (magnitude only); the others are vectors.', 11),
  ('neet-physics-rotational-motion-q01', 'neet-physics-rotational-motion', 'The rotational analogue of force is:', 'Torque', 'Momentum', 'Power', 'Impulse', 'A', 'Torque (τ = r × F) is the rotational analogue of force.', 0),
  ('neet-physics-rotational-motion-q02', 'neet-physics-rotational-motion', 'The moment of inertia of a solid sphere about a diameter is:', '(2/5)MR²', 'MR²', '(1/2)MR²', '(2/3)MR²', 'A', 'A solid sphere has I = (2/5)MR² about a diameter.', 1),
  ('neet-physics-rotational-motion-q03', 'neet-physics-rotational-motion', 'Angular momentum is conserved when the net external ______ is zero.', 'Force', 'Torque', 'Momentum', 'Energy', 'B', 'Angular momentum L = Iω is conserved in the absence of a net external torque.', 2),
  ('neet-physics-rotational-motion-q04', 'neet-physics-rotational-motion', 'The rotational form of Newton''s second law is:', 'τ = Iα', 'F = ma', 'L = Iω', 'p = mv', 'A', 'Net torque equals moment of inertia times angular acceleration, τ = Iα.', 3),
  ('neet-physics-rotational-motion-q05', 'neet-physics-rotational-motion', 'The parallel-axis theorem is written as:', 'I = I_cm + Md²', 'I = I_cm − Md²', 'I = Md²', 'I = I_cm/2', 'A', 'I about a parallel axis is I_cm + Md², where d is the axis separation.', 4),
  ('neet-physics-rotational-motion-q06', 'neet-physics-rotational-motion', 'For rolling without slipping, the linear and angular speeds are related by:', 'v = ωR', 'v = ω/R', 'v = ωR²', 'v = R/ω', 'A', 'Rolling without slipping requires v = ωR.', 5),
  ('neet-physics-rotational-motion-q07', 'neet-physics-rotational-motion', 'The moment of inertia of a ring about its central axis is:', 'MR²', '(1/2)MR²', '(2/5)MR²', '(1/3)MR²', 'A', 'A thin ring has all its mass at radius R, so I = MR².', 6),
  ('neet-physics-rotational-motion-q08', 'neet-physics-rotational-motion', 'When a ballerina pulls in her arms, her angular speed increases because her:', 'Moment of inertia decreases (L conserved)', 'Torque increases', 'Mass decreases', 'Energy decreases', 'A', 'With L conserved, reducing I raises ω.', 7),
  ('neet-physics-rotational-motion-q09', 'neet-physics-rotational-motion', 'The kinetic energy of a rolling body is:', '½mv² + ½Iω²', '½mv² only', '½Iω² only', 'mv²', 'A', 'A rolling body has both translational (½mv²) and rotational (½Iω²) kinetic energy.', 8),
  ('neet-physics-rotational-motion-q10', 'neet-physics-rotational-motion', 'The moment of inertia of a disc about its central axis is:', '(1/2)MR²', 'MR²', '(2/5)MR²', '(1/4)MR²', 'A', 'A uniform disc has I = ½MR² about its central axis.', 9),
  ('neet-physics-rotational-motion-q11', 'neet-physics-rotational-motion', 'The centre of mass of a uniform rod lies at its:', 'One end', 'Geometric centre (midpoint)', 'Quarter length', 'Outside the rod', 'B', 'For a uniform rod, the centre of mass is at the midpoint.', 10),
  ('neet-physics-rotational-motion-q12', 'neet-physics-rotational-motion', 'Radius of gyration k relates to moment of inertia by:', 'I = Mk²', 'I = Mk', 'k = MI', 'I = k/M', 'A', 'The radius of gyration is defined by I = Mk².', 11),
  ('neet-physics-mechanical-properties-solids-q01', 'neet-physics-mechanical-properties-solids', 'Stress is defined as:', 'Force per unit area', 'Change in length', 'Force × area', 'Length per unit force', 'A', 'Stress = restoring force per unit cross-sectional area.', 0),
  ('neet-physics-mechanical-properties-solids-q02', 'neet-physics-mechanical-properties-solids', 'Strain is a:', 'Dimensionless ratio', 'Force', 'Pressure', 'Vector with units', 'A', 'Strain is the fractional deformation (change/original), so it is dimensionless.', 1),
  ('neet-physics-mechanical-properties-solids-q03', 'neet-physics-mechanical-properties-solids', 'Hooke''s law states that within the elastic limit, stress is:', 'Proportional to strain', 'Inversely proportional to strain', 'Independent of strain', 'Equal to strain squared', 'A', 'Hooke''s law: stress ∝ strain within the elastic limit.', 2),
  ('neet-physics-mechanical-properties-solids-q04', 'neet-physics-mechanical-properties-solids', 'Young''s modulus measures resistance to change in:', 'Length', 'Volume', 'Shape only', 'Temperature', 'A', 'Young''s modulus (Y) = longitudinal stress/strain, resisting length change.', 3),
  ('neet-physics-mechanical-properties-solids-q05', 'neet-physics-mechanical-properties-solids', 'The bulk modulus is related to a change in:', 'Volume', 'Length', 'Shape', 'Mass', 'A', 'Bulk modulus measures resistance to uniform volume compression.', 4),
  ('neet-physics-mechanical-properties-solids-q06', 'neet-physics-mechanical-properties-solids', 'The shear (rigidity) modulus concerns a change in:', 'Shape', 'Volume', 'Length', 'Density', 'A', 'The shear modulus measures resistance to a change in shape (shear strain).', 5),
  ('neet-physics-mechanical-properties-solids-q07', 'neet-physics-mechanical-properties-solids', 'A material with a higher Young''s modulus is:', 'More elastic/stiffer', 'More plastic', 'Softer', 'Weaker', 'A', 'A higher Young''s modulus means the material resists deformation more (stiffer).', 6),
  ('neet-physics-mechanical-properties-solids-q08', 'neet-physics-mechanical-properties-solids', 'The SI unit of stress and Young''s modulus is the:', 'Pascal (N/m²)', 'Newton', 'Joule', 'Watt', 'A', 'Stress (and modulus) has units of pascals, N/m².', 7),
  ('neet-physics-mechanical-properties-solids-q09', 'neet-physics-mechanical-properties-solids', 'The elastic potential energy stored per unit volume is:', '½ × stress × strain', 'stress × strain', '½ × stress²', 'strain²', 'A', 'Elastic energy density = ½ × stress × strain.', 8),
  ('neet-physics-mechanical-properties-solids-q10', 'neet-physics-mechanical-properties-solids', 'The point beyond which a material does not return to its original shape is the:', 'Elastic limit', 'Yield increase', 'Origin', 'Melting point', 'A', 'Beyond the elastic limit, permanent (plastic) deformation occurs.', 9),
  ('neet-physics-mechanical-properties-solids-q11', 'neet-physics-mechanical-properties-solids', 'A brittle material breaks:', 'Soon after the elastic limit with little plastic deformation', 'Only after large stretching', 'Never', 'At zero stress', 'A', 'Brittle materials fracture shortly beyond the elastic region with little plasticity.', 10),
  ('neet-physics-mechanical-properties-solids-q12', 'neet-physics-mechanical-properties-solids', 'The ratio of lateral strain to longitudinal strain is called:', 'Poisson''s ratio', 'Young''s modulus', 'Bulk modulus', 'Shear strain', 'A', 'Poisson''s ratio is the ratio of lateral to longitudinal strain.', 11),
  ('neet-physics-mechanical-properties-fluids-q01', 'neet-physics-mechanical-properties-fluids', 'Pascal''s law states that pressure applied to an enclosed fluid is transmitted:', 'Equally in all directions', 'Only downward', 'Only upward', 'In one direction', 'A', 'Pascal''s law is the basis of hydraulic presses — pressure is transmitted undiminished.', 0),
  ('neet-physics-mechanical-properties-fluids-q02', 'neet-physics-mechanical-properties-fluids', 'Archimedes'' principle states that the buoyant force equals the weight of:', 'The object', 'The fluid displaced', 'The container', 'Air above', 'B', 'Buoyant force equals the weight of the fluid displaced by the submerged part.', 1),
  ('neet-physics-mechanical-properties-fluids-q03', 'neet-physics-mechanical-properties-fluids', 'The pressure at depth h in a liquid of density ρ is:', 'P₀ + ρgh', 'ρg/h', 'P₀ − ρgh', 'ρh/g', 'A', 'Fluid pressure increases with depth: P = P₀ + ρgh.', 2),
  ('neet-physics-mechanical-properties-fluids-q04', 'neet-physics-mechanical-properties-fluids', 'According to Bernoulli''s theorem, where fluid speed is high, the pressure is:', 'Low', 'High', 'Unchanged', 'Infinite', 'A', 'Bernoulli''s principle: faster-moving fluid has lower pressure (energy conservation).', 3),
  ('neet-physics-mechanical-properties-fluids-q05', 'neet-physics-mechanical-properties-fluids', 'The equation of continuity (Av = constant) is based on conservation of:', 'Mass', 'Charge', 'Momentum', 'Energy', 'A', 'Continuity (A₁v₁ = A₂v₂) expresses conservation of mass for an incompressible fluid.', 4),
  ('neet-physics-mechanical-properties-fluids-q06', 'neet-physics-mechanical-properties-fluids', 'Viscosity is a measure of a fluid''s:', 'Resistance to flow', 'Density', 'Surface area', 'Colour', 'A', 'Viscosity is the internal friction resisting relative motion between fluid layers.', 5),
  ('neet-physics-mechanical-properties-fluids-q07', 'neet-physics-mechanical-properties-fluids', 'Stokes'' law gives the viscous force on a small sphere as:', '6πηrv', 'πr²v', 'ρgh', '½ρv²', 'A', 'Stokes'' law: F = 6πηrv for a sphere of radius r moving at speed v.', 6),
  ('neet-physics-mechanical-properties-fluids-q08', 'neet-physics-mechanical-properties-fluids', 'Surface tension arises due to:', 'Cohesive forces between molecules at the surface', 'Gravity only', 'Air pressure', 'Temperature only', 'A', 'Unbalanced cohesive forces on surface molecules produce surface tension.', 7),
  ('neet-physics-mechanical-properties-fluids-q09', 'neet-physics-mechanical-properties-fluids', 'Capillary rise of a liquid is due to:', 'Surface tension and adhesion', 'Gravity alone', 'Viscosity', 'Pressure only', 'A', 'Surface tension with adhesion causes liquids to rise in narrow tubes (capillarity).', 8),
  ('neet-physics-mechanical-properties-fluids-q10', 'neet-physics-mechanical-properties-fluids', 'An object floats when its weight is:', 'Equal to the buoyant force', 'Greater than buoyant force', 'Zero', 'Greater than gravity', 'A', 'Floating occurs when the buoyant force balances the object''s weight.', 9),
  ('neet-physics-mechanical-properties-fluids-q11', 'neet-physics-mechanical-properties-fluids', 'The terminal velocity of a sphere falling through a viscous fluid is reached when:', 'Net force is zero (weight = drag + buoyancy)', 'Speed is zero', 'Drag is zero', 'Buoyancy is zero', 'A', 'At terminal velocity, gravity is balanced by viscous drag and buoyancy, so acceleration is zero.', 10),
  ('neet-physics-mechanical-properties-fluids-q12', 'neet-physics-mechanical-properties-fluids', 'The lift on an aeroplane wing is explained by:', 'Bernoulli''s principle', 'Archimedes'' principle', 'Pascal''s law', 'Hooke''s law', 'A', 'Faster airflow over the curved upper wing lowers pressure, generating lift (Bernoulli).', 11),
  ('neet-physics-thermal-properties-matter-q01', 'neet-physics-thermal-properties-matter', 'The coefficient of volume expansion (γ) is related to linear expansion (α) by:', 'γ = 3α', 'γ = α', 'γ = 2α', 'γ = α/3', 'A', 'For isotropic solids, γ = 3α (and area coefficient β = 2α).', 0),
  ('neet-physics-thermal-properties-matter-q02', 'neet-physics-thermal-properties-matter', 'Heat transfer through a solid without bulk movement of matter is:', 'Conduction', 'Convection', 'Radiation', 'Evaporation', 'A', 'Conduction transfers heat through a material via molecular vibration/electron motion.', 1),
  ('neet-physics-thermal-properties-matter-q03', 'neet-physics-thermal-properties-matter', 'Heat transfer that requires no medium is:', 'Conduction', 'Convection', 'Radiation', 'Diffusion', 'C', 'Radiation transfers heat via electromagnetic waves and can occur through a vacuum.', 2),
  ('neet-physics-thermal-properties-matter-q04', 'neet-physics-thermal-properties-matter', 'The Stefan–Boltzmann law states that radiated power is proportional to:', 'T⁴', 'T', 'T²', '1/T', 'A', 'A body radiates power ∝ T⁴ (absolute temperature).', 3),
  ('neet-physics-thermal-properties-matter-q05', 'neet-physics-thermal-properties-matter', 'Latent heat is the heat absorbed or released during a:', 'Phase change (at constant temperature)', 'Temperature rise', 'Pressure change', 'Volume change only', 'A', 'Latent heat accompanies phase changes with no temperature change (Q = mL).', 4),
  ('neet-physics-thermal-properties-matter-q06', 'neet-physics-thermal-properties-matter', 'Newton''s law of cooling states the rate of heat loss is proportional to the:', 'Temperature difference with surroundings', 'Square of temperature', 'Volume', 'Mass only', 'A', 'Newton''s law of cooling: cooling rate ∝ (T_body − T_surroundings) for small differences.', 5),
  ('neet-physics-thermal-properties-matter-q07', 'neet-physics-thermal-properties-matter', 'The heat required to raise the temperature is Q = mcΔT, where c is the:', 'Specific heat capacity', 'Latent heat', 'Thermal conductivity', 'Emissivity', 'A', 'c is the specific heat capacity, the heat per unit mass per degree.', 6),
  ('neet-physics-thermal-properties-matter-q08', 'neet-physics-thermal-properties-matter', 'A perfect black body is one that:', 'Absorbs and emits all radiation', 'Reflects all radiation', 'Transmits all radiation', 'Emits no radiation', 'A', 'A black body absorbs all incident radiation and is the best possible emitter.', 7),
  ('neet-physics-thermal-properties-matter-q09', 'neet-physics-thermal-properties-matter', 'Wien''s displacement law relates the peak emission wavelength to temperature as:', 'λ_max ∝ 1/T', 'λ_max ∝ T', 'λ_max ∝ T⁴', 'λ_max ∝ T²', 'A', 'Wien''s law: the peak wavelength is inversely proportional to absolute temperature.', 8),
  ('neet-physics-thermal-properties-matter-q10', 'neet-physics-thermal-properties-matter', 'Heat transfer in liquids and gases by actual movement of matter is:', 'Convection', 'Conduction', 'Radiation', 'Sublimation', 'A', 'Convection transfers heat through the bulk movement of heated fluid.', 9),
  ('neet-physics-thermal-properties-matter-q11', 'neet-physics-thermal-properties-matter', 'Water has an unusually high value of:', 'Specific heat capacity', 'Thermal conductivity', 'Density at all temperatures', 'Emissivity', 'A', 'Water''s high specific heat lets it absorb much heat with little temperature rise.', 10),
  ('neet-physics-thermal-properties-matter-q12', 'neet-physics-thermal-properties-matter', 'The area coefficient of expansion β equals:', '2α', '3α', 'α', 'α/2', 'A', 'For isotropic solids, the area expansion coefficient β = 2α.', 11),
  ('neet-physics-kinetic-theory-q01', 'neet-physics-kinetic-theory', 'According to kinetic theory, the pressure of a gas arises from:', 'Molecular collisions with the walls', 'Molecular colour', 'Gravity', 'Chemical bonds', 'A', 'Gas pressure results from momentum transfer during molecular collisions with the container.', 0),
  ('neet-physics-kinetic-theory-q02', 'neet-physics-kinetic-theory', 'The average kinetic energy of a gas molecule is proportional to:', 'Absolute temperature', 'Pressure', 'Volume', 'Molar mass', 'A', 'Average KE per molecule = (3/2)kT, proportional to absolute temperature.', 1),
  ('neet-physics-kinetic-theory-q03', 'neet-physics-kinetic-theory', 'The rms speed of gas molecules is proportional to:', '√T', 'T', 'T²', '1/T', 'A', 'v_rms = √(3RT/M), so it scales as the square root of temperature.', 2),
  ('neet-physics-kinetic-theory-q04', 'neet-physics-kinetic-theory', 'The number of degrees of freedom of a monatomic gas molecule is:', '3', '5', '6', '1', 'A', 'A monatomic gas has 3 translational degrees of freedom.', 3),
  ('neet-physics-kinetic-theory-q05', 'neet-physics-kinetic-theory', 'The equipartition theorem assigns energy per degree of freedom of:', '½kT', 'kT', '(3/2)kT', '2kT', 'A', 'Each degree of freedom carries an average energy of ½kT.', 4),
  ('neet-physics-kinetic-theory-q06', 'neet-physics-kinetic-theory', 'For a diatomic gas, the ratio of specific heats γ is:', '7/5', '5/3', '4/3', '1', 'A', 'A diatomic gas (5 degrees of freedom) has γ = 7/5.', 5),
  ('neet-physics-kinetic-theory-q07', 'neet-physics-kinetic-theory', 'The mean free path of gas molecules ______ as pressure increases.', 'Decreases', 'Increases', 'Stays constant', 'Becomes infinite', 'A', 'Higher pressure means more molecules per volume, so the mean free path decreases.', 6),
  ('neet-physics-kinetic-theory-q08', 'neet-physics-kinetic-theory', 'Among molecular speeds, the correct order is:', 'v_rms > v_avg > v_mp', 'v_mp > v_avg > v_rms', 'All equal', 'v_avg > v_rms > v_mp', 'A', 'For a Maxwell distribution, v_rms > v_average > v_most-probable.', 7),
  ('neet-physics-kinetic-theory-q09', 'neet-physics-kinetic-theory', 'Kinetic theory shows gas pressure P equals:', '(1/3)ρv̄²', 'ρgh', '½ρv²', 'ρv', 'A', 'P = (1/3)ρv̄² relates pressure to density and mean-square molecular speed.', 8),
  ('neet-physics-kinetic-theory-q10', 'neet-physics-kinetic-theory', 'At the same temperature, two different gases have the same average:', 'Kinetic energy per molecule', 'Speed', 'Mass', 'Density', 'A', 'At equal temperature, molecules have the same average KE, though speeds differ with mass.', 9),
  ('neet-physics-kinetic-theory-q11', 'neet-physics-kinetic-theory', 'Absolute zero corresponds to the temperature at which molecular motion:', 'Theoretically ceases (minimum energy)', 'Is maximum', 'Is random and high', 'Doubles', 'A', 'At 0 K, molecular translational motion is at its theoretical minimum.', 10),
  ('neet-physics-kinetic-theory-q12', 'neet-physics-kinetic-theory', 'For a diatomic gas, the number of degrees of freedom (translational + rotational) at room temperature is:', '5', '3', '7', '1', 'A', 'A diatomic molecule has 3 translational + 2 rotational = 5 degrees of freedom.', 11),
  ('neet-physics-wave-optics-q01', 'neet-physics-wave-optics', 'In Young''s double-slit experiment, the fringe width is given by:', 'λD/d', 'λd/D', 'dD/λ', 'λ/dD', 'A', 'Fringe width β = λD/d.', 0),
  ('neet-physics-wave-optics-q02', 'neet-physics-wave-optics', 'Sustained interference requires the two sources to be:', 'Coherent', 'Of different frequency', 'Incoherent', 'Point sources only', 'A', 'Coherent sources with a constant phase difference are needed for stable interference.', 1),
  ('neet-physics-wave-optics-q03', 'neet-physics-wave-optics', 'Diffraction becomes significant when the obstacle/aperture size is comparable to the:', 'Wavelength', 'Frequency', 'Amplitude', 'Speed', 'A', 'Diffraction is pronounced when the aperture is comparable to the wavelength of light.', 2),
  ('neet-physics-wave-optics-q04', 'neet-physics-wave-optics', 'The phenomenon that confirms light is a transverse wave is:', 'Polarisation', 'Interference', 'Diffraction', 'Refraction', 'A', 'Only transverse waves can be polarised, confirming light''s transverse nature.', 3),
  ('neet-physics-wave-optics-q05', 'neet-physics-wave-optics', 'Malus''s law relates the transmitted intensity through a polariser to:', 'cos²θ', 'sinθ', 'tanθ', 'cosθ', 'A', 'Malus''s law: I = I₀cos²θ, where θ is the angle between polariser axes.', 4),
  ('neet-physics-wave-optics-q06', 'neet-physics-wave-optics', 'Huygens'' principle treats each point on a wavefront as a source of:', 'Secondary wavelets', 'Particles', 'Photons only', 'Sound', 'A', 'Huygens'' principle: each wavefront point emits secondary spherical wavelets.', 5),
  ('neet-physics-wave-optics-q07', 'neet-physics-wave-optics', 'For constructive interference, the path difference must be:', 'An integer multiple of λ', 'A half-integer multiple of λ', 'Zero only', 'λ/4', 'A', 'Bright fringes occur at path differences of nλ.', 6),
  ('neet-physics-wave-optics-q08', 'neet-physics-wave-optics', 'In single-slit diffraction, the first minimum occurs at:', 'a sinθ = λ', 'a sinθ = λ/2', 'd sinθ = λ', 'a sinθ = 2λ', 'A', 'The first diffraction minimum from a slit of width a is at a sinθ = λ.', 7),
  ('neet-physics-wave-optics-q09', 'neet-physics-wave-optics', 'When the double-slit apparatus is immersed in water, the fringe width:', 'Decreases', 'Increases', 'Stays the same', 'Becomes zero', 'A', 'Wavelength decreases in water (λ/μ), so the fringe width β decreases.', 8),
  ('neet-physics-wave-optics-q10', 'neet-physics-wave-optics', 'Brewster''s law relates the polarising angle to refractive index by:', 'tan θ_p = μ', 'sin θ_p = μ', 'cos θ_p = μ', 'θ_p = μ', 'A', 'Brewster''s law: tan θ_p = μ, at which reflected light is fully polarised.', 9),
  ('neet-physics-wave-optics-q11', 'neet-physics-wave-optics', 'The resolving power of an optical instrument increases with:', 'Larger aperture', 'Smaller aperture', 'Longer wavelength', 'Lower frequency', 'A', 'Resolving power ∝ aperture/λ, so a larger aperture resolves finer detail.', 10),
  ('neet-physics-wave-optics-q12', 'neet-physics-wave-optics', 'The central fringe in Young''s experiment (zero path difference) is:', 'Bright', 'Dark', 'Coloured', 'Absent', 'A', 'At zero path difference, constructive interference gives a bright central fringe.', 11),
  ('neet-physics-magnetism-matter-q01', 'neet-physics-magnetism-matter', 'Diamagnetic materials are:', 'Weakly repelled by a magnetic field', 'Strongly attracted', 'Permanently magnetic', 'Non-magnetic entirely', 'A', 'Diamagnetic substances are weakly repelled and have small negative susceptibility.', 0),
  ('neet-physics-magnetism-matter-q02', 'neet-physics-magnetism-matter', 'Ferromagnetic materials, such as iron, are:', 'Strongly attracted and can be permanently magnetised', 'Weakly repelled', 'Never magnetic', 'Only paramagnetic', 'A', 'Ferromagnets have magnetic domains and can retain strong permanent magnetism.', 1),
  ('neet-physics-magnetism-matter-q03', 'neet-physics-magnetism-matter', 'Paramagnetic susceptibility varies with temperature according to:', 'Curie''s law (χ ∝ 1/T)', 'Ohm''s law', 'Hooke''s law', 'Stefan''s law', 'A', 'Curie''s law: paramagnetic susceptibility is inversely proportional to temperature.', 2),
  ('neet-physics-magnetism-matter-q04', 'neet-physics-magnetism-matter', 'A ferromagnet loses its magnetism above the:', 'Curie temperature', 'Boiling point', 'Triple point', 'Absolute zero', 'A', 'Above the Curie temperature, thermal agitation destroys domain alignment and ferromagnetism.', 3),
  ('neet-physics-magnetism-matter-q05', 'neet-physics-magnetism-matter', 'The angle between the Earth''s magnetic field and the horizontal is the:', 'Angle of dip (inclination)', 'Declination', 'Azimuth', 'Latitude', 'A', 'The dip (inclination) is the angle the Earth''s field makes with the horizontal.', 4),
  ('neet-physics-magnetism-matter-q06', 'neet-physics-magnetism-matter', 'Soft iron is preferred for electromagnets because it has a:', 'Narrow hysteresis loop (low retentivity)', 'Wide hysteresis loop', 'High permanent magnetism', 'Zero permeability', 'A', 'Soft iron''s narrow hysteresis loop lets it magnetise and demagnetise easily — ideal for electromagnets.', 5),
  ('neet-physics-magnetism-matter-q07', 'neet-physics-magnetism-matter', 'Steel is used for permanent magnets because it has:', 'High retentivity and a wide hysteresis loop', 'Low retentivity', 'Zero coercivity', 'Diamagnetism', 'A', 'Steel retains magnetism well (high retentivity/coercivity), suiting permanent magnets.', 6),
  ('neet-physics-magnetism-matter-q08', 'neet-physics-magnetism-matter', 'The torque on a magnetic dipole of moment m in a field B is:', 'm × B', 'm·B', 'm/B', 'mB²', 'A', 'Torque τ = m × B, maximal when the dipole is perpendicular to the field.', 7),
  ('neet-physics-magnetism-matter-q09', 'neet-physics-magnetism-matter', 'The magnetic susceptibility of a diamagnetic material is:', 'Small and negative', 'Small and positive', 'Large and positive', 'Zero', 'A', 'Diamagnetic materials have small negative susceptibility.', 8),
  ('neet-physics-magnetism-matter-q10', 'neet-physics-magnetism-matter', 'The property by which a ferromagnet''s magnetisation lags behind the applied field is:', 'Hysteresis', 'Resonance', 'Diffraction', 'Polarisation', 'A', 'Hysteresis is the lag of magnetisation behind the magnetising field in ferromagnets.', 9),
  ('neet-physics-magnetism-matter-q11', 'neet-physics-magnetism-matter', 'The Earth behaves approximately like a giant:', 'Bar magnet', 'Electric charge', 'Capacitor', 'Resistor', 'A', 'The geomagnetic field resembles that of a bar magnet tilted from the rotation axis.', 10),
  ('neet-physics-magnetism-matter-q12', 'neet-physics-magnetism-matter', 'Paramagnetic materials are:', 'Weakly attracted by a magnetic field', 'Strongly repelled', 'Permanently magnetic', 'Insulators only', 'A', 'Paramagnetic substances are weakly attracted and have small positive susceptibility.', 11),
  ('neet-physics-electromagnetic-waves-q01', 'neet-physics-electromagnetic-waves', 'Electromagnetic waves travel in a vacuum at a speed of:', '3 × 10⁸ m/s', '3 × 10⁶ m/s', '1500 m/s', '340 m/s', 'A', 'EM waves travel at c ≈ 3 × 10⁸ m/s in vacuum.', 0),
  ('neet-physics-electromagnetic-waves-q02', 'neet-physics-electromagnetic-waves', 'In an electromagnetic wave, the electric and magnetic fields are:', 'Mutually perpendicular and perpendicular to propagation', 'Parallel to each other', 'Along the propagation direction', 'Static', 'A', 'E and B are perpendicular to each other and to the direction of propagation (transverse).', 1),
  ('neet-physics-electromagnetic-waves-q03', 'neet-physics-electromagnetic-waves', 'Which has the highest frequency?', 'Radio waves', 'Gamma rays', 'Infrared', 'Visible light', 'B', 'Gamma rays have the highest frequency (and shortest wavelength) in the EM spectrum.', 2),
  ('neet-physics-electromagnetic-waves-q04', 'neet-physics-electromagnetic-waves', 'Which has the longest wavelength?', 'Radio waves', 'X-rays', 'Ultraviolet', 'Gamma rays', 'A', 'Radio waves have the longest wavelength (lowest frequency).', 3),
  ('neet-physics-electromagnetic-waves-q05', 'neet-physics-electromagnetic-waves', 'Electromagnetic waves require which medium to propagate?', 'No medium (can travel in vacuum)', 'Air only', 'Water only', 'Solid only', 'A', 'Unlike sound, EM waves need no material medium and travel through vacuum.', 4),
  ('neet-physics-electromagnetic-waves-q06', 'neet-physics-electromagnetic-waves', 'Microwaves are commonly used in:', 'Radar and ovens', 'Medical X-rays', 'Cancer therapy', 'Night-vision only', 'A', 'Microwaves are used in radar, communications, and microwave ovens.', 5),
  ('neet-physics-electromagnetic-waves-q07', 'neet-physics-electromagnetic-waves', 'Ultraviolet radiation is used for:', 'Sterilisation of water/equipment', 'Cooking food', 'AM radio', 'Seeing in the dark', 'A', 'UV light kills microorganisms, so it is used for sterilisation.', 6),
  ('neet-physics-electromagnetic-waves-q08', 'neet-physics-electromagnetic-waves', 'The existence of electromagnetic waves was predicted theoretically by:', 'Maxwell', 'Newton', 'Faraday only', 'Bohr', 'A', 'Maxwell predicted EM waves; Hertz later demonstrated them experimentally.', 7),
  ('neet-physics-electromagnetic-waves-q09', 'neet-physics-electromagnetic-waves', 'Infrared radiation is chiefly associated with:', 'Heat (thermal radiation)', 'Ionisation', 'Radio broadcasting', 'Gamma emission', 'A', 'Infrared radiation is felt as heat and used in thermal imaging and heating.', 8),
  ('neet-physics-electromagnetic-waves-q10', 'neet-physics-electromagnetic-waves', 'X-rays are widely used for:', 'Medical imaging of bones', 'Cooking', 'Radio communication', 'Water sterilisation', 'A', 'X-rays penetrate soft tissue and are used to image bones and internal structures.', 9),
  ('neet-physics-electromagnetic-waves-q11', 'neet-physics-electromagnetic-waves', 'Electromagnetic waves carry:', 'Both energy and momentum', 'Only mass', 'Only charge', 'Nothing', 'A', 'EM waves transport energy and momentum (exerting radiation pressure).', 10),
  ('neet-physics-electromagnetic-waves-q12', 'neet-physics-electromagnetic-waves', 'The correct order of increasing frequency is:', 'Radio < infrared < visible < X-rays', 'X-rays < visible < radio', 'Visible < radio < infrared', 'Gamma < radio < visible', 'A', 'Frequency increases: radio < microwave < infrared < visible < UV < X-ray < gamma.', 11),
  ('neet-physics-atoms-q01', 'neet-physics-atoms', 'Rutherford''s alpha-scattering experiment established the existence of the:', 'Nucleus', 'Electron', 'Neutron', 'Photon', 'A', 'The scattering of alpha particles revealed a tiny, dense, positively charged nucleus.', 0),
  ('neet-physics-atoms-q02', 'neet-physics-atoms', 'In the Bohr model, electrons revolve in orbits with quantised:', 'Angular momentum', 'Charge', 'Mass', 'Colour', 'A', 'Bohr postulated quantised angular momentum, mvr = nh/2π.', 1),
  ('neet-physics-atoms-q03', 'neet-physics-atoms', 'The energy of the nth level of a hydrogen atom is:', '−13.6/n² eV', '−13.6n² eV', '13.6/n eV', '−13.6/n eV', 'A', 'For hydrogen, Eₙ = −13.6/n² eV, with the ground state (n=1) at −13.6 eV.', 2),
