  ('neet-biology-strategies-food-production-q09', 'neet-biology-strategies-food-production', 'Breeding crops for higher vitamin, mineral, or protein content is called:', 'Biofortification', 'Hybridisation only', 'Vernalisation', 'Grafting', 'A', 'Biofortification improves the nutritional quality of crops through breeding.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-strategies-food-production-q10', 'neet-biology-strategies-food-production', 'The dramatic rise in food-grain output through high-yielding varieties was the:', 'Green Revolution', 'White Revolution', 'Blue Revolution', 'Industrial Revolution', 'A', 'The Green Revolution boosted crop yields via improved varieties and practices.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-strategies-food-production-q11', 'neet-biology-strategies-food-production', 'Somatic hybridisation involves fusing:', 'Protoplasts of two different plant cells', 'Two gametes', 'Two zygotes', 'Sperm and egg', 'A', 'Somatic hybridisation fuses protoplasts to combine traits without fertilisation.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-strategies-food-production-q12', 'neet-biology-strategies-food-production', 'Outbreeding (out-crossing/hybridisation) is used mainly to:', 'Introduce desirable variation / hybrid vigour', 'Increase homozygosity', 'Reduce yield', 'Cause inbreeding depression', 'A', 'Outbreeding introduces new variation and hybrid vigour (heterosis).', 11),
  ('neet-biology-biodiversity-conservation-q01', 'neet-biology-biodiversity-conservation', 'Biodiversity is generally highest in:', 'Tropical regions', 'Polar regions', 'Deserts', 'Tundra', 'A', 'Species diversity is greatest in the tropics and decreases toward the poles.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-biodiversity-conservation-q02', 'neet-biology-biodiversity-conservation', 'Which is an example of in-situ conservation?', 'National park', 'Zoo', 'Seed bank', 'Botanical garden', 'A', 'In-situ conservation protects species in their natural habitat (parks, sanctuaries, reserves).', 1),
  ('neet-biology-biodiversity-conservation-q03', 'neet-biology-biodiversity-conservation', 'Ex-situ conservation includes:', 'Zoos and seed banks', 'National parks', 'Biosphere reserves', 'Sacred groves', 'A', 'Ex-situ conservation protects species outside their habitat (zoos, gene/seed banks).', 2),
  ('neet-biology-biodiversity-conservation-q04', 'neet-biology-biodiversity-conservation', 'The single largest cause of biodiversity loss is:', 'Habitat loss and fragmentation', 'Volcanoes', 'Earthquakes', 'Rainfall', 'A', 'Habitat loss/fragmentation is the leading driver in the ''Evil Quartet''.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-biodiversity-conservation-q05', 'neet-biology-biodiversity-conservation', 'Areas with very high species richness and high endemism under threat are called:', 'Biodiversity hotspots', 'Deserts', 'Grasslands', 'Estuaries', 'A', 'Hotspots have exceptional endemic diversity and severe habitat loss, prioritised for conservation.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-biodiversity-conservation-q06', 'neet-biology-biodiversity-conservation', 'An invasive alien species can cause biodiversity loss by:', 'Out-competing or preying on native species', 'Increasing native populations', 'Adding rainfall', 'Enriching soil only', 'A', 'Introduced species may outcompete or prey upon natives, causing decline/extinction.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-biodiversity-conservation-q07', 'neet-biology-biodiversity-conservation', 'The species–area relationship, when plotted, is typically a:', 'Rectangular hyperbola (rising curve)', 'Straight vertical line', 'Downward curve', 'Circle', 'A', 'Larger areas hold more species, described by a rising rectangular-hyperbola curve.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-biodiversity-conservation-q08', 'neet-biology-biodiversity-conservation', 'Sacred groves are an example of conservation that is:', 'In-situ (community protected forests)', 'Ex-situ', 'Cryopreservation', 'Seed banking', 'A', 'Sacred groves are traditionally protected forest patches — a form of in-situ conservation.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-biodiversity-conservation-q09', 'neet-biology-biodiversity-conservation', 'The three levels at which biodiversity is described are:', 'Genetic, species, and ecological', 'Small, medium, large', 'Land, water, air', 'Plant, animal, microbe only', 'A', 'Biodiversity spans genetic, species, and ecosystem (ecological) diversity.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-biodiversity-conservation-q10', 'neet-biology-biodiversity-conservation', 'The disappearance of one species leading to loss of others depending on it is:', 'Co-extinction', 'Speciation', 'Migration', 'Adaptation', 'A', 'Co-extinction is one of the ''Evil Quartet'' — obligate dependents die out with their host/partner.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-biodiversity-conservation-q11', 'neet-biology-biodiversity-conservation', 'Cryopreservation of gametes/tissues is a technique of:', 'Ex-situ conservation', 'In-situ conservation', 'Habitat restoration', 'Biological control', 'A', 'Cryopreservation stores germplasm at very low temperatures — an ex-situ method.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-biodiversity-conservation-q12', 'neet-biology-biodiversity-conservation', 'The ''rivet-popper hypothesis'' illustrates the importance of:', 'Every species to ecosystem stability', 'Only large animals', 'Only plants', 'Removing species', 'A', 'Paul Ehrlich''s analogy shows each species (rivet) contributes to ecosystem integrity.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-hydrogen-q01', 'neet-chemistry-hydrogen', 'The isotope of hydrogen that is radioactive is:', 'Protium', 'Tritium', 'Deuterium', 'None', 'B', 'Tritium (³H) is the radioactive isotope of hydrogen.', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-hydrogen-q02', 'neet-chemistry-hydrogen', 'Hydrogen resembles alkali metals because it can form:', 'H⁺ ions (loses an electron)', 'Only H⁻', 'H₂ only', 'No ions', 'A', 'Like alkali metals, hydrogen can lose its electron to form H⁺.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-hydrogen-q03', 'neet-chemistry-hydrogen', 'Temporary hardness of water is due to:', 'Bicarbonates of calcium and magnesium', 'Sulphates of calcium', 'Chlorides of sodium', 'Nitrates', 'A', 'Temporary hardness comes from Ca/Mg bicarbonates, removed by boiling.', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-hydrogen-q04', 'neet-chemistry-hydrogen', 'Permanent hardness of water is removed by:', 'Boiling', 'Adding washing soda / ion exchange', 'Filtration', 'Cooling', 'B', 'Permanent hardness (sulphates/chlorides) is removed chemically, e.g., by washing soda or ion exchange.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-hydrogen-q05', 'neet-chemistry-hydrogen', 'Hydrogen peroxide (H₂O₂) can act as:', 'Both an oxidising and a reducing agent', 'Only an oxidising agent', 'Only a reducing agent', 'Neither', 'A', 'H₂O₂ functions as both an oxidiser and a reducer depending on conditions.', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-hydrogen-q06', 'neet-chemistry-hydrogen', 'Heavy water is chemically:', 'D₂O (deuterium oxide)', 'H₂O₂', 'T₂O', 'H₂O', 'A', 'Heavy water is deuterium oxide, D₂O, used as a moderator in nuclear reactors.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-hydrogen-q07', 'neet-chemistry-hydrogen', 'Interstitial (metallic) hydrides are formed by hydrogen with:', 'Transition (d-block) metals', 'Noble gases', 'Alkali metals only', 'Halogens', 'A', 'Many d-block metals form non-stoichiometric interstitial (metallic) hydrides.', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-hydrogen-q08', 'neet-chemistry-hydrogen', 'Dihydrogen is used industrially in the Haber process to make:', 'Ammonia', 'Nitric acid directly', 'Sulphuric acid', 'Chlorine', 'A', 'The Haber process combines N₂ and H₂ to synthesise ammonia.', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-hydrogen-q09', 'neet-chemistry-hydrogen', 'Hydrogen resembles halogens because it can:', 'Gain one electron to form H⁻ (hydride)', 'Lose two electrons', 'Form a full d-subshell', 'Never bond', 'A', 'Like halogens, hydrogen needs one electron for a stable configuration, forming H⁻.', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-hydrogen-q10', 'neet-chemistry-hydrogen', 'Hydrogenation of vegetable oils (to make vanaspati) uses hydrogen with a catalyst of:', 'Nickel', 'Sodium', 'Copper only', 'Gold', 'A', 'Unsaturated oils are hydrogenated over a nickel catalyst to give solid fats.', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-hydrogen-q11', 'neet-chemistry-hydrogen', 'The most abundant element in the universe is:', 'Hydrogen', 'Oxygen', 'Helium', 'Carbon', 'A', 'Hydrogen is the most abundant element in the universe.', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-hydrogen-q12', 'neet-chemistry-hydrogen', 'Hydrogen is considered a clean fuel because its combustion mainly produces:', 'Water', 'Carbon dioxide', 'Sulphur dioxide', 'Soot', 'A', 'Burning hydrogen yields water, making it a clean, non-polluting fuel.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-environmental-chemistry-q01', 'neet-chemistry-environmental-chemistry', 'Acid rain is mainly caused by oxides of:', 'Sulphur and nitrogen', 'Carbon only', 'Sodium', 'Silicon', 'A', 'SO₂ and NOx form sulphuric and nitric acids, producing acid rain.', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-environmental-chemistry-q02', 'neet-chemistry-environmental-chemistry', 'Photochemical smog is an oxidising smog formed from NOx, hydrocarbons, and:', 'Sunlight', 'Fog only', 'Rain', 'Snow', 'A', 'Photochemical (Los Angeles) smog forms when sunlight acts on NOx and hydrocarbons.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-environmental-chemistry-q03', 'neet-chemistry-environmental-chemistry', 'Carbon monoxide is poisonous because it binds strongly to:', 'Haemoglobin', 'Water', 'Bone', 'Enzymes only', 'A', 'CO binds haemoglobin more strongly than O₂, reducing oxygen transport.', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-environmental-chemistry-q04', 'neet-chemistry-environmental-chemistry', 'The main greenhouse gas contributing to global warming is:', 'Carbon dioxide', 'Oxygen', 'Nitrogen', 'Argon', 'A', 'CO₂ is the principal greenhouse gas driving global warming.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-environmental-chemistry-q05', 'neet-chemistry-environmental-chemistry', 'Classical (London) smog is:', 'Reducing (SO₂ + fog)', 'Oxidising', 'Neutral', 'Radioactive', 'A', 'London smog is a reducing smog of SO₂, soot, and fog.', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-environmental-chemistry-q06', 'neet-chemistry-environmental-chemistry', 'Depletion of the stratospheric ozone layer is caused chiefly by:', 'Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs)', 'Carbon dioxide', 'Oxygen', 'Methane only', 'A', 'CFCs release chlorine radicals that catalytically destroy ozone.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-environmental-chemistry-q07', 'neet-chemistry-environmental-chemistry', 'High BOD of a water body indicates:', 'High organic pollution', 'Clean water', 'High oxygen', 'Low bacteria', 'A', 'A high biochemical oxygen demand means more organic waste and greater pollution.', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-environmental-chemistry-q08', 'neet-chemistry-environmental-chemistry', 'Green chemistry aims to:', 'Reduce or eliminate hazardous substances in processes', 'Increase pollution', 'Use more toxic solvents', 'Avoid catalysts', 'A', 'Green chemistry designs safer, less polluting chemical processes.', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-environmental-chemistry-q09', 'neet-chemistry-environmental-chemistry', 'Eutrophication of lakes is caused by excess:', 'Nutrients (nitrates and phosphates)', 'Oxygen', 'Acids only', 'Salt', 'A', 'Nutrient enrichment causes algal blooms, later depleting dissolved oxygen.', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-environmental-chemistry-q10', 'neet-chemistry-environmental-chemistry', 'The pH below which rain is considered ''acid rain'' is about:', '5.6', '7', '9', '12', 'A', 'Rain with pH below ~5.6 (from dissolved acidic oxides) is termed acid rain.', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-environmental-chemistry-q11', 'neet-chemistry-environmental-chemistry', 'PAN (peroxyacetyl nitrate), an eye irritant, is a component of:', 'Photochemical smog', 'Classical smog', 'Acid rain', 'Ozone layer', 'A', 'PAN is produced in photochemical smog and irritates the eyes and lungs.', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-environmental-chemistry-q12', 'neet-chemistry-environmental-chemistry', 'Stratospheric ozone is beneficial because it:', 'Absorbs harmful UV radiation', 'Causes global warming', 'Is a pollutant near the ground only', 'Provides oxygen to breathe', 'A', 'The ozone layer shields the surface from harmful ultraviolet radiation.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-p-block-15-18-q01', 'neet-chemistry-p-block-15-18', 'The group 15 element that exists as an inert diatomic gas is:', 'Nitrogen', 'Phosphorus', 'Arsenic', 'Bismuth', 'A', 'Nitrogen exists as N₂ with a very stable triple bond, making it relatively inert.', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-p-block-15-18-q02', 'neet-chemistry-p-block-15-18', 'Ammonia is manufactured by the:', 'Haber process', 'Contact process', 'Ostwald process', 'Hall–Héroult process', 'A', 'The Haber process synthesises NH₃ from N₂ and H₂ at high pressure with an iron catalyst.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-p-block-15-18-q03', 'neet-chemistry-p-block-15-18', 'Sulphuric acid is manufactured by the:', 'Contact process', 'Haber process', 'Ostwald process', 'Solvay process', 'A', 'The Contact process produces H₂SO₄ via SO₂ → SO₃ over a V₂O₅ catalyst.', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-p-block-15-18-q04', 'neet-chemistry-p-block-15-18', 'Nitric acid is produced industrially by the:', 'Ostwald process', 'Contact process', 'Haber process', 'Hall process', 'A', 'The Ostwald process oxidises ammonia to nitric acid.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-p-block-15-18-q05', 'neet-chemistry-p-block-15-18', 'Among halogens, the strongest oxidising agent is:', 'Fluorine', 'Iodine', 'Bromine', 'Chlorine', 'A', 'Fluorine is the strongest oxidising agent among the halogens.', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-p-block-15-18-q06', 'neet-chemistry-p-block-15-18', 'The noble gas that forms fluorides such as XeF₂ and XeF₄ is:', 'Xenon', 'Helium', 'Neon', 'Argon', 'A', 'Xenon, being large and polarisable, forms fluorides with fluorine.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-p-block-15-18-q07', 'neet-chemistry-p-block-15-18', 'HF is a weaker acid than HCl mainly because of:', 'Strong hydrogen bonding in HF', 'Its large size', 'Its low electronegativity', 'Its metallic nature', 'A', 'Strong H-bonding and the strong H–F bond make HF the weakest hydrohalic acid.', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-p-block-15-18-q08', 'neet-chemistry-p-block-15-18', 'Group 16 elements are collectively called the:', 'Chalcogens', 'Pnictogens', 'Halogens', 'Noble gases', 'A', 'Group 16 (O, S, Se, Te) are the chalcogens (ore-forming elements).', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-p-block-15-18-q09', 'neet-chemistry-p-block-15-18', 'Down group 17, the oxidising power of the halogens:', 'Decreases', 'Increases', 'Stays constant', 'Becomes zero', 'A', 'Oxidising power decreases down the group (F₂ strongest, I₂ weakest).', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-p-block-15-18-q10', 'neet-chemistry-p-block-15-18', 'The maximum oxidation state shown by nitrogen is:', '+5', '+3', '+7', '+2', 'A', 'Nitrogen shows a maximum oxidation state of +5 (e.g., in HNO₃).', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-p-block-15-18-q11', 'neet-chemistry-p-block-15-18', 'Noble gases are largely unreactive because they have:', 'A completely filled valence shell', 'One valence electron', 'High reactivity', 'Unpaired electrons', 'A', 'A full octet gives noble gases very high stability and low reactivity.', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-p-block-15-18-q12', 'neet-chemistry-p-block-15-18', 'Compounds formed between two different halogens are called:', 'Interhalogens', 'Hydrides', 'Oxides', 'Amines', 'A', 'Interhalogen compounds (e.g., ClF₃, ICl) form between two different halogens.', 11),
  ('neet-physics-alternating-current-q01', 'neet-physics-alternating-current', 'The RMS value of an alternating current with peak I₀ is:', 'I₀/√2', 'I₀√2', 'I₀', '2I₀', 'A', 'For sinusoidal AC, I_rms = I₀/√2 ≈ 0.707 I₀.', 0),
  ('neet-physics-alternating-current-q02', 'neet-physics-alternating-current', 'The inductive reactance X_L is given by:', 'ωL', '1/ωL', 'ωC', '1/ωC', 'A', 'Inductive reactance X_L = ωL, increasing with frequency.', 1),
  ('neet-physics-alternating-current-q03', 'neet-physics-alternating-current', 'The capacitive reactance X_C is given by:', '1/ωC', 'ωC', 'ωL', '1/ωL', 'A', 'Capacitive reactance X_C = 1/ωC, decreasing with frequency.', 2),
  ('neet-physics-alternating-current-q04', 'neet-physics-alternating-current', 'In a purely inductive AC circuit, the current:', 'Lags the voltage by 90°', 'Leads by 90°', 'Is in phase', 'Is zero', 'A', 'In a pure inductor, current lags the voltage by a quarter cycle (90°).', 3),
  ('neet-physics-alternating-current-q05', 'neet-physics-alternating-current', 'In a purely capacitive AC circuit, the current:', 'Leads the voltage by 90°', 'Lags by 90°', 'Is in phase', 'Is constant', 'A', 'In a pure capacitor, current leads the voltage by 90°.', 4),
  ('neet-physics-alternating-current-q06', 'neet-physics-alternating-current', 'Series LCR resonance occurs when:', 'X_L = X_C', 'X_L = 0', 'R = 0', 'X_C = 2X_L', 'A', 'At resonance the reactances cancel (X_L = X_C), leaving impedance = R.', 5),
  ('neet-physics-alternating-current-q07', 'neet-physics-alternating-current', 'At resonance in a series LCR circuit, the current is:', 'Maximum', 'Zero', 'Minimum', 'Negative', 'A', 'Minimum impedance at resonance gives maximum current.', 6),
  ('neet-physics-alternating-current-q08', 'neet-physics-alternating-current', 'The impedance of a series LCR circuit is:', '√(R² + (X_L − X_C)²)', 'R + X_L + X_C', 'R × X_L', 'X_L − X_C only', 'A', 'Impedance Z = √(R² + (X_L − X_C)²).', 7),
  ('neet-physics-alternating-current-q09', 'neet-physics-alternating-current', 'The average power in an AC circuit is:', 'V_rms·I_rms·cosφ', 'V_rms·I_rms only', 'V₀I₀', 'Zero always', 'A', 'Average power = V_rms·I_rms·cosφ, where cosφ is the power factor.', 8),
  ('neet-physics-alternating-current-q10', 'neet-physics-alternating-current', 'The term cosφ in AC power is called the:', 'Power factor', 'Impedance', 'Reactance', 'Resonance', 'A', 'cosφ is the power factor, the ratio of real to apparent power.', 9),
  ('neet-physics-alternating-current-q11', 'neet-physics-alternating-current', 'The resonant frequency of a series LCR circuit is:', '1/(2π√(LC))', '2π√(LC)', '1/(2πLC)', '√(LC)', 'A', 'Resonance occurs at f = 1/(2π√(LC)).', 10),
  ('neet-physics-alternating-current-q12', 'neet-physics-alternating-current', 'A transformer changes AC voltage using the principle of:', 'Mutual induction', 'Self-induction only', 'Electrostatics', 'Resistance', 'A', 'A transformer transfers energy between coils by mutual induction (V_s/V_p = N_s/N_p).', 11),
  ('neet-biology-immunity-immune-system-q01', 'neet-biology-immunity-immune-system', 'Immunity present from birth and acting non-specifically is called:', 'Innate immunity', 'Acquired immunity', 'Passive immunity', 'Active immunity', 'A', 'Innate immunity is the non-specific defence present from birth.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-immunity-immune-system-q02', 'neet-biology-immunity-immune-system', 'Antibody-mediated (humoral) immunity is carried out by:', 'B-lymphocytes', 'T-lymphocytes', 'Neutrophils', 'Platelets', 'A', 'B-cells produce antibodies, providing humoral immunity.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-immunity-immune-system-q03', 'neet-biology-immunity-immune-system', 'An antibody molecule is made up of how many polypeptide chains?', 'Four (two heavy, two light)', 'Two', 'Six', 'One', 'A', 'An antibody has the H₂L₂ structure — two heavy and two light chains.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-immunity-immune-system-q04', 'neet-biology-immunity-immune-system', 'The antibody that can cross the placenta is:', 'IgG', 'IgM', 'IgE', 'IgA', 'A', 'IgG is the most abundant antibody and the only one that crosses the placenta.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-immunity-immune-system-q05', 'neet-biology-immunity-immune-system', 'Immunity acquired by receiving ready-made antibodies is:', 'Passive immunity', 'Active immunity', 'Innate immunity', 'Cellular immunity', 'A', 'Passive immunity involves ready-made antibodies and is fast but short-lived.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-immunity-immune-system-q06', 'neet-biology-immunity-immune-system', 'The antibody chiefly involved in allergic reactions is:', 'IgE', 'IgG', 'IgM', 'IgA', 'A', 'IgE mediates allergic responses via mast cells releasing histamine.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-immunity-immune-system-q07', 'neet-biology-immunity-immune-system', 'Vaccination produces which type of immunity?', 'Active (artificially acquired)', 'Passive', 'Innate', 'None', 'A', 'A vaccine primes the body to make its own antibodies — active, long-lasting immunity.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-immunity-immune-system-q08', 'neet-biology-immunity-immune-system', 'HIV, the cause of AIDS, mainly attacks which cells?', 'Helper T-cells (CD4⁺)', 'Red blood cells', 'Platelets', 'Neurons', 'A', 'HIV destroys helper T (CD4⁺) cells, crippling the immune system.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-immunity-immune-system-q09', 'neet-biology-immunity-immune-system', 'The primary lymphoid organs where lymphocytes mature are the:', 'Bone marrow and thymus', 'Spleen and tonsils', 'Liver and pancreas', 'Lymph nodes only', 'A', 'The bone marrow and thymus are primary lymphoid organs for lymphocyte maturation.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-immunity-immune-system-q10', 'neet-biology-immunity-immune-system', 'Ready-made antibodies passed from mother to infant through colostrum are chiefly:', 'IgA', 'IgG', 'IgD', 'IgM', 'A', 'Colostrum is rich in IgA, giving the newborn passive immunity.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-immunity-immune-system-q11', 'neet-biology-immunity-immune-system', 'When the immune system attacks the body''s own cells, the condition is called:', 'Autoimmunity', 'Allergy', 'Immunodeficiency', 'Active immunity', 'A', 'Autoimmunity is an immune attack on self, as in rheumatoid arthritis.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-immunity-immune-system-q12', 'neet-biology-immunity-immune-system', 'The chemical released during an allergic reaction causing symptoms is:', 'Histamine', 'Insulin', 'Thyroxine', 'Adrenaline only', 'A', 'Mast cells release histamine during allergy; antihistamines counter it.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-human-genetics-disorders-q01', 'neet-biology-human-genetics-disorders', 'Down''s syndrome is caused by an extra copy of chromosome:', '21', '18', '23', '13', 'A', 'Down''s syndrome is trisomy 21 — an extra chromosome 21 (47 total).', 0),
  ('neet-biology-human-genetics-disorders-q02', 'neet-biology-human-genetics-disorders', 'Haemophilia is an example of a disorder that is:', 'X-linked recessive', 'Autosomal dominant', 'Y-linked', 'Mitochondrial', 'A', 'Haemophilia is an X-linked recessive disorder of blood clotting.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-human-genetics-disorders-q03', 'neet-biology-human-genetics-disorders', 'Sickle-cell anaemia results from the substitution of glutamic acid by:', 'Valine', 'Glycine', 'Lysine', 'Alanine', 'A', 'In HbS, valine replaces glutamic acid at the 6th position of the β-globin chain.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-human-genetics-disorders-q04', 'neet-biology-human-genetics-disorders', 'Klinefelter''s syndrome has the sex-chromosome karyotype:', 'XXY', 'X0', 'XYY', 'XXX', 'A', 'Klinefelter''s syndrome is 47, XXY — a sterile male with feminine features.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-human-genetics-disorders-q05', 'neet-biology-human-genetics-disorders', 'Turner''s syndrome is characterised by the karyotype:', '45, X0', '47, XXY', '47, XX+21', '46, XY', 'A', 'Turner''s syndrome is 45, X0 — a sterile female with only one X chromosome.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-human-genetics-disorders-q06', 'neet-biology-human-genetics-disorders', 'Sickle-cell anaemia and thalassemia are both inherited as:', 'Autosomal recessive', 'X-linked dominant', 'Y-linked', 'Autosomal dominant', 'A', 'Both sickle-cell anaemia and thalassemia are autosomal recessive disorders.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-human-genetics-disorders-q07', 'neet-biology-human-genetics-disorders', 'In humans, the sex of the child is determined by the:', 'Father''s sperm (X or Y)', 'Mother''s egg', 'Both eggs equally', 'Number of chromosomes', 'A', 'The father is heterogametic (XY); his sperm (X or Y) determines the child''s sex.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-human-genetics-disorders-q08', 'neet-biology-human-genetics-disorders', 'The failure of chromosomes to separate during meiosis, causing aneuploidy, is:', 'Non-disjunction', 'Crossing over', 'Translocation', 'Duplication', 'A', 'Non-disjunction is the failure of homologous chromosomes/chromatids to separate.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-human-genetics-disorders-q09', 'neet-biology-human-genetics-disorders', 'The study of the inheritance of a trait across generations of a family is:', 'Pedigree analysis', 'Karyotyping', 'Cloning', 'Gene therapy', 'A', 'Pedigree analysis traces how a trait is inherited through a family tree.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-human-genetics-disorders-q10', 'neet-biology-human-genetics-disorders', 'A normal human somatic cell contains how many autosomes?', '44', '46', '22', '23', 'A', 'Humans have 22 pairs (44) autosomes plus one pair of sex chromosomes.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-human-genetics-disorders-q11', 'neet-biology-human-genetics-disorders', 'Phenylketonuria (PKU) is caused by the lack of an enzyme that converts:', 'Phenylalanine to tyrosine', 'Glucose to glycogen', 'Lactose to glucose', 'Fat to glycerol', 'A', 'In PKU the enzyme converting phenylalanine to tyrosine is missing, so phenylalanine accumulates.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-human-genetics-disorders-q12', 'neet-biology-human-genetics-disorders', 'Colour blindness in humans is generally:', 'X-linked recessive', 'Autosomal dominant', 'Y-linked', 'Autosomal recessive', 'A', 'Red-green colour blindness is an X-linked recessive trait, more common in males.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-enzymes-q01', 'neet-biology-enzymes', 'Enzymes speed up reactions by:', 'Lowering the activation energy', 'Raising the activation energy', 'Increasing temperature', 'Being consumed in the reaction', 'A', 'Enzymes lower activation energy without being consumed.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-enzymes-q02', 'neet-biology-enzymes', 'Chemically, most enzymes are:', 'Proteins', 'Lipids', 'Carbohydrates', 'Nucleotides', 'A', 'Most enzymes are proteins; a few RNA molecules (ribozymes) are also catalytic.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-enzymes-q03', 'neet-biology-enzymes', 'The region of an enzyme where the substrate binds is the:', 'Active site', 'Allosteric site only', 'Peptide bond', 'Disulphide bridge', 'A', 'The substrate binds at the enzyme''s active site.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-enzymes-q04', 'neet-biology-enzymes', 'RNA molecules that act as biological catalysts are called:', 'Ribozymes', 'Coenzymes', 'Apoenzymes', 'Isozymes', 'A', 'Ribozymes are catalytic RNA molecules.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-enzymes-q05', 'neet-biology-enzymes', 'Enzymes that catalyse oxidation-reduction reactions belong to the class:', 'Oxidoreductases', 'Hydrolases', 'Ligases', 'Isomerases', 'A', 'Oxidoreductases catalyse oxidation-reduction reactions.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-enzymes-q06', 'neet-biology-enzymes', 'A complete, active enzyme (protein plus cofactor) is called a:', 'Holoenzyme', 'Apoenzyme', 'Coenzyme', 'Zymogen', 'A', 'Apoenzyme (protein) + cofactor = holoenzyme (the active enzyme).', 5),
  ('neet-biology-enzymes-q07', 'neet-biology-enzymes', 'Loosely bound organic cofactors, often derived from vitamins, are:', 'Coenzymes', 'Prosthetic groups', 'Metal ions', 'Substrates', 'A', 'Coenzymes (e.g., NAD, FAD) are loosely bound organic cofactors from vitamins.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-enzymes-q08', 'neet-biology-enzymes', 'The ''lock-and-key'' and ''induced fit'' concepts describe enzyme:', 'Substrate binding/specificity', 'Denaturation', 'Synthesis', 'Excretion', 'A', 'Both models describe how a specific substrate fits the enzyme''s active site.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-enzymes-q09', 'neet-biology-enzymes', 'Malonate inhibiting succinate dehydrogenase by resembling the substrate is an example of:', 'Competitive inhibition', 'Non-competitive inhibition', 'Feedback activation', 'Denaturation', 'A', 'Malonate competes with succinate for the active site — competitive inhibition.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-enzymes-q10', 'neet-biology-enzymes', 'Human enzymes generally work best at a temperature of about:', '37 °C', '0 °C', '100 °C', '60 °C', 'A', 'Most human enzymes have an optimum around body temperature, ~37 °C.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-enzymes-q11', 'neet-biology-enzymes', 'The stomach enzyme pepsin works best in which pH range?', 'Acidic', 'Neutral', 'Strongly alkaline', 'Any pH', 'A', 'Pepsin has an acidic optimum pH (~1.5–2), suited to the stomach.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-enzymes-q12', 'neet-biology-enzymes', 'Enzymes of the class ''ligases'' catalyse the:', 'Joining of two molecules using ATP', 'Hydrolysis of bonds', 'Rearrangement into isomers', 'Removal of groups', 'A', 'Ligases join two molecules together, typically using ATP.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-sense-organs-eye-ear-q01', 'neet-biology-sense-organs-eye-ear', 'The photoreceptor cells responsible for dim-light (night) vision are the:', 'Rods', 'Cones', 'Bipolar cells', 'Ganglion cells', 'A', 'Rods, containing rhodopsin, function in dim light; cones handle colour and bright light.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-sense-organs-eye-ear-q02', 'neet-biology-sense-organs-eye-ear', 'Colour vision in the human eye is due to the:', 'Cones', 'Rods', 'Cornea', 'Sclera', 'A', 'Cones detect colour; there are three types sensitive to different wavelengths.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-sense-organs-eye-ear-q03', 'neet-biology-sense-organs-eye-ear', 'The region of sharpest vision in the retina, containing only cones, is the:', 'Fovea (macula lutea)', 'Blind spot', 'Optic disc', 'Ciliary body', 'A', 'The fovea (in the macula lutea) has only cones and gives the sharpest vision.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-sense-organs-eye-ear-q04', 'neet-biology-sense-organs-eye-ear', 'The visual pigment present in the rods is:', 'Rhodopsin', 'Melanin', 'Haemoglobin', 'Chlorophyll', 'A', 'Rods contain rhodopsin (visual purple), derived from vitamin A.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-sense-organs-eye-ear-q05', 'neet-biology-sense-organs-eye-ear', 'Myopia (short-sightedness) is corrected using a:', 'Concave lens', 'Convex lens', 'Cylindrical lens', 'Plane mirror', 'A', 'Myopia is corrected with a concave (diverging) lens.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-sense-organs-eye-ear-q06', 'neet-biology-sense-organs-eye-ear', 'Hypermetropia (long-sightedness) is corrected using a:', 'Convex lens', 'Concave lens', 'Bifocal only', 'Prism', 'A', 'Hypermetropia is corrected with a convex (converging) lens.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-sense-organs-eye-ear-q07', 'neet-biology-sense-organs-eye-ear', 'The three ear ossicles in correct order from the eardrum are:', 'Malleus, incus, stapes', 'Stapes, incus, malleus', 'Incus, malleus, stapes', 'Cochlea, utricle, saccule', 'A', 'Sound passes eardrum → malleus → incus → stapes → oval window.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-sense-organs-eye-ear-q08', 'neet-biology-sense-organs-eye-ear', 'The hearing receptor in the inner ear is the:', 'Organ of Corti', 'Semicircular canal', 'Utricle', 'Eustachian tube', 'A', 'The organ of Corti in the cochlea converts sound vibrations into nerve impulses.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-sense-organs-eye-ear-q09', 'neet-biology-sense-organs-eye-ear', 'Balance and equilibrium are controlled by which part of the inner ear?', 'The vestibular apparatus (semicircular canals)', 'The cochlea', 'The tympanum', 'The pinna', 'A', 'The vestibular apparatus — semicircular canals, utricle, saccule — maintains balance.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-sense-organs-eye-ear-q10', 'neet-biology-sense-organs-eye-ear', 'The tube that equalises air pressure across the eardrum is the:', 'Eustachian tube', 'Fallopian tube', 'Bronchus', 'Ureter', 'A', 'The Eustachian tube connects the middle ear to the pharynx and equalises pressure.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-sense-organs-eye-ear-q11', 'neet-biology-sense-organs-eye-ear', 'The ''blind spot'' of the eye is the point where:', 'The optic nerve leaves the retina (no receptors)', 'Cones are most dense', 'The lens attaches', 'Tears are produced', 'A', 'The blind spot has no photoreceptors because the optic nerve exits there.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-sense-organs-eye-ear-q12', 'neet-biology-sense-organs-eye-ear', 'Focusing the lens for near and far objects is called:', 'Accommodation', 'Adaptation', 'Refraction only', 'Diffraction', 'A', 'Accommodation is the adjustment of lens curvature by the ciliary muscles to focus.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-gametogenesis-menstrual-cycle-q01', 'neet-biology-gametogenesis-menstrual-cycle', 'Spermatogenesis takes place in the:', 'Seminiferous tubules', 'Epididymis', 'Prostate gland', 'Urethra', 'A', 'Sperm are produced in the seminiferous tubules of the testes.', 0),
  ('neet-biology-gametogenesis-menstrual-cycle-q02', 'neet-biology-gametogenesis-menstrual-cycle', 'The cells that nourish developing sperm are the:', 'Sertoli cells', 'Leydig cells', 'Red blood cells', 'Granulosa cells', 'A', 'Sertoli cells nourish the germ cells; Leydig cells make testosterone.', 1),
  ('neet-biology-gametogenesis-menstrual-cycle-q03', 'neet-biology-gametogenesis-menstrual-cycle', 'Testosterone is secreted by which cells of the testis?', 'Leydig (interstitial) cells', 'Sertoli cells', 'Spermatogonia', 'Spermatids', 'A', 'Leydig (interstitial) cells secrete the male hormone testosterone.', 2),
  ('neet-biology-gametogenesis-menstrual-cycle-q04', 'neet-biology-gametogenesis-menstrual-cycle', 'In oogenesis, the primary oocyte is arrested in which stage until puberty?', 'Prophase I', 'Metaphase II', 'Anaphase I', 'Telophase II', 'A', 'The primary oocyte remains arrested in prophase I of meiosis until puberty.', 3),
  ('neet-biology-gametogenesis-menstrual-cycle-q05', 'neet-biology-gametogenesis-menstrual-cycle', 'The secondary oocyte completes meiosis II only when:', 'Fertilisation occurs', 'Ovulation begins', 'Menstruation starts', 'Puberty begins', 'A', 'Meiosis II of the secondary oocyte is completed only if a sperm fertilises it.', 4),
  ('neet-biology-gametogenesis-menstrual-cycle-q06', 'neet-biology-gametogenesis-menstrual-cycle', 'Ovulation in a typical 28-day menstrual cycle occurs around day:', '14', '1', '5', '28', 'A', 'Ovulation usually occurs around the middle of the cycle, about day 14.', 5),
  ('neet-biology-gametogenesis-menstrual-cycle-q07', 'neet-biology-gametogenesis-menstrual-cycle', 'The surge of which hormone triggers ovulation?', 'Luteinising hormone (LH)', 'Progesterone', 'Insulin', 'Prolactin', 'A', 'A mid-cycle surge of LH triggers the release of the secondary oocyte.', 6),
  ('neet-biology-gametogenesis-menstrual-cycle-q08', 'neet-biology-gametogenesis-menstrual-cycle', 'After ovulation, the ruptured follicle becomes the:', 'Corpus luteum', 'Graafian follicle', 'Corpus albicans immediately', 'Zona pellucida', 'A', 'The empty follicle becomes the corpus luteum, which secretes progesterone.', 7),
  ('neet-biology-gametogenesis-menstrual-cycle-q09', 'neet-biology-gametogenesis-menstrual-cycle', 'The hormone secreted by the corpus luteum to maintain the endometrium is:', 'Progesterone', 'FSH', 'Oestrogen only', 'Oxytocin', 'A', 'The corpus luteum secretes progesterone, which maintains the uterine lining.', 8),
  ('neet-biology-gametogenesis-menstrual-cycle-q10', 'neet-biology-gametogenesis-menstrual-cycle', 'The first menstruation at puberty is called:', 'Menarche', 'Menopause', 'Ovulation', 'Gestation', 'A', 'Menarche is the first menstrual period; menopause is the cessation of menstruation.', 9),
  ('neet-biology-gametogenesis-menstrual-cycle-q11', 'neet-biology-gametogenesis-menstrual-cycle', 'One primary oocyte undergoing oogenesis finally produces:', 'One ovum (and polar bodies)', 'Four functional ova', 'Two ova', 'Eight ova', 'A', 'Oogenesis is unequal — one functional ovum plus non-functional polar bodies form.', 10),
  ('neet-biology-gametogenesis-menstrual-cycle-q12', 'neet-biology-gametogenesis-menstrual-cycle', 'If fertilisation does not occur, the fall in progesterone leads to:', 'Menstruation (shedding of the endometrium)', 'Ovulation', 'Pregnancy', 'Menarche', 'A', 'Without fertilisation the corpus luteum degenerates; falling progesterone triggers menstruation.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-solid-state-q01', 'neet-chemistry-solid-state', 'A solid with a sharp melting point and long-range order is:', 'Crystalline', 'Amorphous', 'A supercooled liquid', 'A gas', 'A', 'Crystalline solids have long-range order and a sharp, definite melting point.', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-solid-state-q02', 'neet-chemistry-solid-state', 'Glass is an example of which type of solid?', 'Amorphous', 'Ionic crystalline', 'Metallic', 'Covalent network', 'A', 'Glass is amorphous — it lacks long-range order and softens over a range.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-solid-state-q03', 'neet-chemistry-solid-state', 'The number of atoms per unit cell in a face-centred cubic (FCC) lattice is:', '4', '1', '2', '8', 'A', 'FCC has 8×1/8 (corners) + 6×1/2 (faces) = 4 atoms per unit cell.', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-solid-state-q04', 'neet-chemistry-solid-state', 'A body-centred cubic (BCC) unit cell contains how many atoms?', '2', '1', '4', '6', 'A', 'BCC has 8×1/8 (corners) + 1 (body centre) = 2 atoms per unit cell.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-solid-state-q05', 'neet-chemistry-solid-state', 'The packing efficiency of a face-centred cubic (close-packed) structure is:', '74%', '68%', '52.4%', '100%', 'A', 'FCC (ccp) and hcp are close-packed with 74% packing efficiency.', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-solid-state-q06', 'neet-chemistry-solid-state', 'A corner atom in a cubic unit cell contributes what fraction to the cell?', '1/8', '1/2', '1/4', '1', 'A', 'A corner atom is shared among 8 cells, contributing 1/8 to each.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-solid-state-q07', 'neet-chemistry-solid-state', 'The coordination number in a simple (primitive) cubic lattice is:', '6', '8', '12', '4', 'A', 'In a simple cubic lattice each atom touches 6 neighbours.', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-solid-state-q08', 'neet-chemistry-solid-state', 'A Schottky defect in an ionic crystal results in:', 'A decrease in density', 'An increase in density', 'No change in mass', 'Increased coordination number', 'A', 'Schottky defects remove equal cations and anions, lowering the density.', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-solid-state-q09', 'neet-chemistry-solid-state', 'In a Frenkel defect, the density of the crystal:', 'Remains unchanged', 'Decreases sharply', 'Increases sharply', 'Becomes zero', 'A', 'In a Frenkel defect an ion merely shifts to an interstitial site, so density is unchanged.', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-solid-state-q10', 'neet-chemistry-solid-state', 'Doping silicon with a group-15 element (e.g., phosphorus) gives a:', 'n-type semiconductor', 'p-type semiconductor', 'Perfect insulator', 'Superconductor', 'A', 'Group-15 dopants add extra electrons, giving an n-type semiconductor.', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-solid-state-q11', 'neet-chemistry-solid-state', 'In the density formula d = zM/(a³N_A), the symbol ''z'' represents:', 'Number of atoms per unit cell', 'Edge length', 'Molar mass', 'Avogadro''s number', 'A', '''z'' is the number of formula units (atoms) per unit cell.', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-solid-state-q12', 'neet-chemistry-solid-state', 'Crystalline solids are anisotropic, meaning their properties:', 'Vary with direction', 'Are the same in all directions', 'Do not exist', 'Change with time only', 'A', 'Anisotropy means physical properties differ along different directions in the crystal.', 11),
  ('neet-chemistry-nomenclature-isomerism-q01', 'neet-chemistry-nomenclature-isomerism', 'Compounds with the same molecular formula but different structures are called:', 'Isomers', 'Isotopes', 'Isobars', 'Allotropes', 'A', 'Isomers share a molecular formula but differ in structure or arrangement.', 0),
  ('neet-chemistry-nomenclature-isomerism-q02', 'neet-chemistry-nomenclature-isomerism', 'n-Butane and isobutane are examples of which isomerism?', 'Chain isomerism', 'Position isomerism', 'Functional isomerism', 'Optical isomerism', 'A', 'They differ in carbon-skeleton branching — chain isomerism.', 1),
  ('neet-chemistry-nomenclature-isomerism-q03', 'neet-chemistry-nomenclature-isomerism', 'Ethanol (an alcohol) and dimethyl ether have the same formula C₂H₆O — this is:', 'Functional isomerism', 'Chain isomerism', 'Metamerism', 'Geometrical isomerism', 'A', 'Different functional groups (alcohol vs ether) with the same formula is functional isomerism.', 2),
  ('neet-chemistry-nomenclature-isomerism-q04', 'neet-chemistry-nomenclature-isomerism', 'Geometrical (cis–trans) isomerism requires:', 'Restricted rotation (a C=C double bond or ring)', 'A chiral carbon', 'A free radical', 'A benzene ring only', 'A', 'Geometrical isomerism arises when rotation is restricted, as around a C=C bond.', 3),
  ('neet-chemistry-nomenclature-isomerism-q05', 'neet-chemistry-nomenclature-isomerism', 'A carbon atom bonded to four different groups is described as:', 'Chiral (asymmetric)', 'Achiral', 'Aromatic', 'Quaternary and symmetric', 'A', 'A chiral (asymmetric) carbon has four different groups, giving optical isomers.', 4),
  ('neet-chemistry-nomenclature-isomerism-q06', 'neet-chemistry-nomenclature-isomerism', 'Non-superimposable mirror-image isomers are called:', 'Enantiomers', 'Diastereomers', 'Metamers', 'Tautomers', 'A', 'Enantiomers are non-superimposable mirror images that rotate light oppositely.', 5),
  ('neet-chemistry-nomenclature-isomerism-q07', 'neet-chemistry-nomenclature-isomerism', 'For a molecule with n chiral centres, the maximum number of optical isomers is:', '2ⁿ', 'n²', '2n', 'n/2', 'A', 'The maximum number of optical isomers is 2ⁿ, where n is the number of chiral centres.', 6),
  ('neet-chemistry-nomenclature-isomerism-q08', 'neet-chemistry-nomenclature-isomerism', 'An equimolar mixture of two enantiomers that is optically inactive is called a:', 'Racemic mixture', 'Meso compound', 'Diastereomer', 'Tautomer', 'A', 'A racemic (±) mixture is a 50:50 mix of enantiomers and is optically inactive.', 7),
  ('neet-chemistry-nomenclature-isomerism-q09', 'neet-chemistry-nomenclature-isomerism', 'The dynamic interconversion between keto and enol forms is:', 'Tautomerism', 'Metamerism', 'Chain isomerism', 'Geometrical isomerism', 'A', 'Keto–enol tautomerism is a rapid interconversion of structural isomers.', 8),
  ('neet-chemistry-nomenclature-isomerism-q10', 'neet-chemistry-nomenclature-isomerism', 'In IUPAC nomenclature, which functional group has the highest priority for the suffix?', 'Carboxylic acid', 'Alcohol', 'Aldehyde', 'Amine', 'A', 'Carboxylic acid ranks highest among these for the principal characteristic (suffix) group.', 9),
  ('neet-chemistry-nomenclature-isomerism-q11', 'neet-chemistry-nomenclature-isomerism', 'The IUPAC root word for a four-carbon chain is:', 'But', 'Prop', 'Pent', 'Eth', 'A', 'The roots run meth (1), eth (2), prop (3), but (4), pent (5)…', 10),
  ('neet-chemistry-nomenclature-isomerism-q12', 'neet-chemistry-nomenclature-isomerism', 'An optically inactive stereoisomer with chiral centres but an internal plane of symmetry is a:', 'Meso compound', 'Racemic mixture', 'Enantiomer', 'Metamer', 'A', 'A meso compound has chiral centres but is achiral overall due to internal symmetry.', 11),
  ('neet-physics-electrostatic-potential-capacitance-q01', 'neet-physics-electrostatic-potential-capacitance', 'Electric potential is a:', 'Scalar quantity', 'Vector quantity', 'Tensor', 'Dimensionless number', 'A', 'Electric potential is a scalar; only its magnitude and sign matter.', 0),
  ('neet-physics-electrostatic-potential-capacitance-q02', 'neet-physics-electrostatic-potential-capacitance', 'The SI unit of electric potential is the:', 'Volt', 'Ampere', 'Coulomb', 'Farad', 'A', 'Potential is measured in volts (1 V = 1 J/C).', 1),
  ('neet-physics-electrostatic-potential-capacitance-q03', 'neet-physics-electrostatic-potential-capacitance', 'The work done in moving a charge along an equipotential surface is:', 'Zero', 'Maximum', 'Negative', 'Infinite', 'A', 'No work is done moving a charge along an equipotential surface (ΔV = 0).', 2),
  ('neet-physics-electrostatic-potential-capacitance-q04', 'neet-physics-electrostatic-potential-capacitance', 'The potential due to a point charge q at distance r is proportional to:', '1/r', '1/r²', 'r', 'r²', 'A', 'V = kq/r, so potential varies as 1/r (whereas field varies as 1/r²).', 3),
  ('neet-physics-electrostatic-potential-capacitance-q05', 'neet-physics-electrostatic-potential-capacitance', 'The SI unit of capacitance is the:', 'Farad', 'Volt', 'Henry', 'Ohm', 'A', 'Capacitance is measured in farads (1 F = 1 C/V).', 4),
  ('neet-physics-electrostatic-potential-capacitance-q06', 'neet-physics-electrostatic-potential-capacitance', 'Inserting a dielectric between the plates of a capacitor:', 'Increases the capacitance', 'Decreases the capacitance', 'Has no effect', 'Makes it zero', 'A', 'A dielectric of constant K increases capacitance by the factor K.', 5),
  ('neet-physics-electrostatic-potential-capacitance-q07', 'neet-physics-electrostatic-potential-capacitance', 'For capacitors in parallel, the net capacitance is:', 'C₁ + C₂ + …', '1/C₁ + 1/C₂', 'C₁ × C₂', 'C₁ − C₂', 'A', 'Parallel capacitors add directly: C = C₁ + C₂ + …', 6),
  ('neet-physics-electrostatic-potential-capacitance-q08', 'neet-physics-electrostatic-potential-capacitance', 'For capacitors in series, the correct relation is:', '1/C = 1/C₁ + 1/C₂ + …', 'C = C₁ + C₂', 'C = C₁C₂', 'C = C₁ − C₂', 'A', 'In series the reciprocals add: 1/C = 1/C₁ + 1/C₂ + …', 7),
  ('neet-physics-electrostatic-potential-capacitance-q09', 'neet-physics-electrostatic-potential-capacitance', 'The energy stored in a capacitor is given by:', '½CV²', 'CV', '2CV²', 'C²V', 'A', 'Energy U = ½CV² = ½QV = Q²/2C.', 8),
  ('neet-physics-electrostatic-potential-capacitance-q10', 'neet-physics-electrostatic-potential-capacitance', 'The capacitance of a parallel-plate capacitor is proportional to:', 'Plate area A', 'Plate separation d', 'The square of the voltage', 'The stored charge only', 'A', 'C = ε₀A/d, so C is proportional to plate area A and inversely to separation d.', 9),
  ('neet-physics-electrostatic-potential-capacitance-q11', 'neet-physics-electrostatic-potential-capacitance', 'The relation between electric field and potential is:', 'E = − dV/dr', 'E = V·r', 'E = dV·r', 'E = V²', 'A', 'The field is the negative gradient of potential: E = − dV/dr.', 10),
  ('neet-physics-electrostatic-potential-capacitance-q12', 'neet-physics-electrostatic-potential-capacitance', 'One volt is equal to:', 'One joule per coulomb', 'One coulomb per second', 'One newton per coulomb', 'One joule per second', 'A', '1 volt = 1 joule/coulomb (work per unit charge).', 11),
  ('neet-physics-waves-q01', 'neet-physics-waves', 'A wave transfers:', 'Energy without transferring matter', 'Matter without energy', 'Both matter and energy equally', 'Neither', 'A', 'Waves carry energy through a medium without net transport of matter.', 0),
  ('neet-physics-waves-q02', 'neet-physics-waves', 'Sound waves in air are:', 'Longitudinal', 'Transverse', 'Electromagnetic', 'Stationary only', 'A', 'Sound in air is a longitudinal wave (compressions and rarefactions).', 1),
  ('neet-physics-waves-q03', 'neet-physics-waves', 'The relation between wave speed, frequency and wavelength is:', 'v = fλ', 'v = f/λ', 'v = λ/f', 'v = f + λ', 'A', 'Wave speed v = frequency × wavelength (v = fλ).', 2),
  ('neet-physics-waves-q04', 'neet-physics-waves', 'Sound travels fastest in:', 'Solids', 'Liquids', 'Gases', 'Vacuum', 'A', 'Sound travels fastest in solids and cannot travel through a vacuum.', 3),
  ('neet-physics-waves-q05', 'neet-physics-waves', 'Points of zero amplitude in a stationary wave are called:', 'Nodes', 'Antinodes', 'Crests', 'Troughs', 'A', 'Nodes are points of zero amplitude; antinodes have maximum amplitude.', 4),
  ('neet-physics-waves-q06', 'neet-physics-waves', 'The beat frequency produced by two sound waves of frequency f₁ and f₂ is:', '|f₁ − f₂|', 'f₁ + f₂', 'f₁ × f₂', 'f₁/f₂', 'A', 'Beat frequency equals the magnitude of the difference, |f₁ − f₂|.', 5),
  ('neet-physics-waves-q07', 'neet-physics-waves', 'A closed organ pipe produces which harmonics?', 'Only odd harmonics', 'Only even harmonics', 'All harmonics', 'No harmonics', 'A', 'A pipe closed at one end supports only odd harmonics.', 6),
  ('neet-physics-waves-q08', 'neet-physics-waves', 'The apparent change in frequency due to relative motion of source and observer is the:', 'Doppler effect', 'Photoelectric effect', 'Compton effect', 'Zeeman effect', 'A', 'The Doppler effect changes the observed frequency with relative motion.', 7),
  ('neet-physics-waves-q09', 'neet-physics-waves', 'As a sound source approaches a stationary observer, the observed frequency:', 'Increases', 'Decreases', 'Stays the same', 'Becomes zero', 'A', 'An approaching source raises the observed frequency (Doppler blue-shift analogue).', 8),
  ('neet-physics-waves-q10', 'neet-physics-waves', 'Waves on a stretched string are:', 'Transverse', 'Longitudinal', 'Electromagnetic', 'Non-mechanical', 'A', 'Waves on a string are transverse — particles move perpendicular to propagation.', 9),
  ('neet-physics-waves-q11', 'neet-physics-waves', 'An open organ pipe (open at both ends) produces:', 'All harmonics', 'Only odd harmonics', 'Only even harmonics', 'Only the fundamental', 'A', 'An open pipe supports all harmonics (both odd and even).', 10),
  ('neet-physics-waves-q12', 'neet-physics-waves', 'Maximum amplitude of vibration when the driving frequency equals the natural frequency is:', 'Resonance', 'Damping', 'Interference', 'Diffraction', 'A', 'Resonance occurs when the driving frequency matches a natural frequency, maximising amplitude.', 11),
  ('neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation-q13', 'neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation', 'A cross between two heterozygous tall plants (Tt × Tt) gives a phenotypic ratio of:', '3 tall : 1 dwarf', '1 tall : 1 dwarf', '9 : 3 : 3 : 1', 'All tall', 'A', 'A monohybrid cross of two heterozygotes yields a 3:1 phenotypic ratio.', 12),
  ('neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation-q14', 'neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation', 'The law of independent assortment is illustrated by which type of cross?', 'Dihybrid cross', 'Monohybrid cross', 'Test cross for one gene', 'Reciprocal cross', 'A', 'Independent assortment is seen in dihybrid crosses (two gene pairs), giving 9:3:3:1.', 13),
  ('neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation-q15', 'neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation', 'In humans, colour blindness is inherited as a(n):', 'X-linked recessive trait', 'Y-linked trait', 'Autosomal dominant trait', 'Mitochondrial trait', 'A', 'Colour blindness is X-linked recessive, so it is more common in males.', 14),
  ('neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation-q16', 'neet-biology-principles-inheritance-variation', 'ABO blood grouping in humans is an example of:', 'Multiple alleles and codominance', 'Simple dominance', 'Sex linkage', 'Polygenic inheritance', 'A', 'The ABO system involves three alleles (I^A, I^B, i) with I^A and I^B codominant.', 15),
  ('neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance-q13', 'neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance', 'In DNA, adenine pairs with thymine through how many hydrogen bonds?', 'Two', 'Three', 'One', 'Four', 'A', 'A–T pairs form two hydrogen bonds; G–C pairs form three.', 12),
  ('neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance-q14', 'neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance', 'The enzyme that synthesises RNA from a DNA template is:', 'RNA polymerase', 'DNA ligase', 'Helicase', 'Primase', 'A', 'RNA polymerase carries out transcription, building RNA from a DNA template.', 13),
  ('neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance-q15', 'neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance', 'The Meselson–Stahl experiment demonstrated that DNA replication is:', 'Semiconservative', 'Conservative', 'Dispersive', 'Random', 'A', 'Meselson and Stahl showed replication is semiconservative — each new duplex has one old strand.', 14),
  ('neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance-q16', 'neet-biology-molecular-basis-inheritance', 'A codon consists of how many nucleotides?', 'Three', 'Two', 'One', 'Four', 'A', 'A codon is a triplet of three nucleotides specifying one amino acid.', 15),
  ('neet-biology-human-reproduction-q13', 'neet-biology-human-reproduction', 'In human females, fertilisation normally occurs in the:', 'Ampulla of the fallopian tube', 'Uterus', 'Ovary', 'Cervix', 'A', 'Fertilisation typically occurs at the ampullary-isthmic junction of the oviduct.', 12),
  ('neet-biology-human-reproduction-q14', 'neet-biology-human-reproduction', 'The hormone that triggers ovulation is a surge of:', 'Luteinising hormone (LH)', 'Progesterone', 'Prolactin', 'Oxytocin', 'A', 'A mid-cycle LH surge triggers ovulation from the mature Graafian follicle.', 13),
  ('neet-biology-human-reproduction-q15', 'neet-biology-human-reproduction', 'The structure that nourishes the developing foetus and exchanges materials is the:', 'Placenta', 'Corpus luteum', 'Endometrium only', 'Amnion', 'A', 'The placenta connects foetus and mother for nutrient, gas and waste exchange.', 14),
  ('neet-biology-human-reproduction-q16', 'neet-biology-human-reproduction', 'The process of release of a secondary oocyte from the ovary is called:', 'Ovulation', 'Implantation', 'Parturition', 'Gastrulation', 'A', 'Ovulation is the release of the secondary oocyte from the mature follicle.', 15),
  ('neet-biology-biotechnology-principles-q13', 'neet-biology-biotechnology-principles', 'Restriction endonucleases are enzymes that:', 'Cut DNA at specific recognition sequences', 'Join DNA fragments', 'Synthesise RNA', 'Replicate whole genomes', 'A', 'Restriction enzymes recognise specific palindromic sequences and cut the DNA there.', 12),
  ('neet-biology-biotechnology-principles-q14', 'neet-biology-biotechnology-principles', 'The enzyme used to join DNA fragments in recombinant technology is:', 'DNA ligase', 'DNA polymerase', 'Restriction enzyme', 'Amylase', 'A', 'DNA ligase seals the phosphodiester backbone, joining fragments.', 13),
  ('neet-biology-biotechnology-principles-q15', 'neet-biology-biotechnology-principles', 'The polymerase chain reaction (PCR) is used to:', 'Amplify specific DNA sequences', 'Cut DNA', 'Translate proteins', 'Sequence proteins', 'A', 'PCR amplifies a target DNA segment through repeated cycles of denaturation, annealing and extension.', 14),
  ('neet-biology-biotechnology-principles-q16', 'neet-biology-biotechnology-principles', 'A commonly used cloning vector derived from bacteria is a:', 'Plasmid', 'Ribosome', 'Lysosome', 'Centriole', 'A', 'Plasmids are extrachromosomal circular DNA used as cloning vectors.', 15),
  ('neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity-q13', 'neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity', 'The 10% law of energy transfer between trophic levels was given by:', 'Lindeman', 'Darwin', 'Mendel', 'Haeckel', 'A', 'Lindeman''s 10% law states only ~10% of energy passes to the next trophic level.', 12),
  ('neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity-q14', 'neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity', 'An inverted pyramid of numbers is typically seen in a:', 'Single tree ecosystem', 'Grassland', 'Pond', 'Desert', 'A', 'A big tree supporting many insects/birds gives an inverted pyramid of numbers.', 13),
  ('neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity-q15', 'neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity', 'Organisms that break down dead organic matter are called:', 'Decomposers', 'Producers', 'Primary consumers', 'Carnivores', 'A', 'Decomposers (saprotrophs) break down detritus, recycling nutrients.', 14),
  ('neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity-q16', 'neet-biology-ecosystem-biodiversity', 'The most species-rich region on Earth in terms of biodiversity is the:', 'Tropical rainforest', 'Tundra', 'Desert', 'Temperate grassland', 'A', 'Tropical rainforests hold the greatest biodiversity of any biome.', 15),
  ('neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division-q13', 'neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division', 'DNA replication (S phase) occurs during which stage of the cell cycle?', 'Interphase', 'Metaphase', 'Anaphase', 'Telophase', 'A', 'DNA is synthesised in the S phase of interphase, before mitosis.', 12),
  ('neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division-q14', 'neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division', 'Sister chromatids separate and move to opposite poles during:', 'Anaphase', 'Prophase', 'Metaphase', 'Interphase', 'A', 'In anaphase, sister chromatids split and migrate to opposite poles.', 13),
  ('neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division-q15', 'neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division', 'Meiosis results in daughter cells that are:', 'Haploid (n)', 'Diploid (2n)', 'Tetraploid', 'Identical to the parent', 'A', 'Meiosis halves the chromosome number, producing haploid gametes.', 14),
  ('neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division-q16', 'neet-biology-cell-cycle-cell-division', 'Crossing over, which increases genetic variation, occurs during:', 'Prophase I of meiosis', 'Metaphase of mitosis', 'Anaphase II', 'The S phase', 'A', 'Crossing over between homologous chromosomes occurs in pachytene of prophase I.', 15),
  ('neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants-q13', 'neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants', 'The light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis occur in the:', 'Thylakoid membranes', 'Stroma', 'Cytoplasm', 'Mitochondria', 'A', 'The light reactions occur on the thylakoid membranes of chloroplasts.', 12),
  ('neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants-q14', 'neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants', 'The Calvin cycle (dark reactions) takes place in the:', 'Stroma', 'Thylakoid lumen', 'Grana', 'Nucleus', 'A', 'Carbon fixation via the Calvin cycle occurs in the stroma.', 13),
  ('neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants-q15', 'neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants', 'The primary CO₂ acceptor in C3 plants is:', 'RuBP', 'PEP', 'OAA', 'Pyruvate', 'A', 'In C3 plants RuBP accepts CO₂, catalysed by RuBisCO.', 14),
  ('neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants-q16', 'neet-biology-photosynthesis-higher-plants', 'In C4 plants, the primary CO₂ fixation product is the four-carbon:', 'Oxaloacetic acid (OAA)', '3-PGA', 'RuBP', 'Glucose', 'A', 'C4 plants first fix CO₂ into OAA via PEP carboxylase in mesophyll cells.', 15),
  ('neet-biology-human-health-disease-q13', 'neet-biology-human-health-disease', 'Malaria is caused by a protozoan of the genus:', 'Plasmodium', 'Entamoeba', 'Trypanosoma', 'Leishmania', 'A', 'Plasmodium species cause malaria, transmitted by female Anopheles mosquitoes.', 12),
  ('neet-biology-human-health-disease-q14', 'neet-biology-human-health-disease', 'Antibodies are produced by which type of cell?', 'B lymphocytes (plasma cells)', 'Red blood cells', 'Platelets', 'Neurons', 'A', 'B lymphocytes differentiate into plasma cells that secrete antibodies.', 13),
  ('neet-biology-human-health-disease-q15', 'neet-biology-human-health-disease', 'The AIDS-causing virus HIV primarily attacks which cells?', 'Helper T lymphocytes', 'Neurons', 'Hepatocytes', 'Osteocytes', 'A', 'HIV infects and destroys helper T (CD4+) cells, weakening immunity.', 14),
  ('neet-biology-human-health-disease-q16', 'neet-biology-human-health-disease', 'Vaccination provides which type of immunity?', 'Active acquired immunity', 'Passive immunity', 'Innate immunity only', 'No immunity', 'A', 'A vaccine stimulates the body to make its own antibodies — active acquired immunity.', 15),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure-q13', 'neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure', 'The shape of a methane (CH₄) molecule is:', 'Tetrahedral', 'Trigonal planar', 'Linear', 'Bent', 'A', 'sp³ hybridised carbon with four bond pairs gives a tetrahedral shape.', 12),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure-q14', 'neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure', 'The bond angle in a water molecule is approximately:', '104.5°', '120°', '180°', '90°', 'A', 'Two lone pairs on oxygen compress the H–O–H angle to about 104.5°.', 13),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure-q15', 'neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure', 'According to MOT, the bond order of the O₂ molecule is:', '2', '1', '3', '0', 'A', 'O₂ has bond order (8−4)/2 = 2 and is paramagnetic.', 14),
  ('neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure-q16', 'neet-chemistry-chemical-bonding-molecular-structure', 'A coordinate (dative) bond involves electron pair sharing where both electrons come from:', 'One atom', 'Both atoms equally', 'No atom', 'A metal only', 'A', 'In a coordinate bond one atom donates both electrons of the shared pair.', 15),
  ('neet-chemistry-equilibrium-q13', 'neet-chemistry-equilibrium', 'For the reaction N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, increasing pressure shifts the equilibrium:', 'Toward the products (fewer moles of gas)', 'Toward the reactants', 'No shift', 'Toward solids', 'A', 'By Le Chatelier''s principle, higher pressure favours the side with fewer gas moles (NH₃).', 12),
  ('neet-chemistry-equilibrium-q14', 'neet-chemistry-equilibrium', 'The pH of a neutral aqueous solution at 25°C is:', '7', '0', '14', '1', 'A', 'At 25°C a neutral solution has [H⁺] = 10⁻⁷ M, so pH = 7.', 13),
  ('neet-chemistry-equilibrium-q15', 'neet-chemistry-equilibrium', 'A buffer solution resists changes in:', 'pH', 'Temperature', 'Volume', 'Colour', 'A', 'A buffer resists pH change on adding small amounts of acid or base.', 14),
  ('neet-chemistry-equilibrium-q16', 'neet-chemistry-equilibrium', 'For a reaction at equilibrium, the value of ΔG is:', 'Zero', 'Positive', 'Negative', 'Infinite', 'A', 'At equilibrium the Gibbs free energy change ΔG equals zero.', 15),
  ('neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids-q13', 'neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids', 'The functional group present in aldehydes is:', '–CHO', '–COOH', '–OH', '–NH₂', 'A', 'Aldehydes contain the terminal carbonyl group –CHO.', 12),
  ('neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids-q14', 'neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids', 'Tollens'' reagent gives a silver mirror with:', 'Aldehydes', 'Ketones', 'Alcohols', 'Ethers', 'A', 'Aldehydes reduce Tollens'' reagent, depositing a silver mirror; ketones do not.', 13),
  ('neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids-q15', 'neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids', 'Carboxylic acids contain which functional group?', '–COOH', '–CHO', '–CO–', '–OH', 'A', 'Carboxylic acids have the carboxyl group –COOH.', 14),
  ('neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids-q16', 'neet-chemistry-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids', 'The aldol condensation requires the carbonyl compound to have a(n):', 'Alpha-hydrogen', 'Benzene ring', 'Halogen', 'Nitro group', 'A', 'Aldol condensation needs an α-hydrogen to form the enolate intermediate.', 15),
  ('neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds-q13', 'neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds', 'The coordination number of the central metal in [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ is:', '6', '3', '4', '2', 'A', 'Six ammonia ligands give a coordination number of 6.', 12),
  ('neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds-q14', 'neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds', 'The oxidation state of iron in [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻ is:', '+3', '+2', '+6', '0', 'A', 'Six CN⁻ (−1 each) with an overall −3 charge gives iron +3.', 13),
  ('neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds-q15', 'neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds', 'EDTA is classified as which type of ligand?', 'Hexadentate', 'Monodentate', 'Bidentate', 'Ambidentate', 'A', 'EDTA binds through six donor atoms, making it hexadentate.', 14),
  ('neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds-q16', 'neet-chemistry-coordination-compounds', 'According to crystal field theory, a strong-field ligand tends to form:', 'Low-spin complexes', 'High-spin complexes always', 'No complex', 'Ionic bonds only', 'A', 'Strong-field ligands cause large splitting, favouring electron pairing (low spin).', 15),
  ('neet-physics-electrostatics-q13', 'neet-physics-electrostatics', 'The electric field inside a hollow charged conductor is:', 'Zero', 'kQ/r²', 'Uniform and non-zero', 'Infinite', 'A', 'By Gauss''s law the field inside a hollow conductor''s cavity is zero.', 12),
  ('neet-physics-electrostatics-q14', 'neet-physics-electrostatics', 'Coulomb''s law states that the force between two point charges is inversely proportional to the:', 'Square of the distance', 'Distance', 'Cube of the distance', 'Product of charges', 'A', 'Coulomb''s force varies as 1/r², inversely with the square of separation.', 13),
  ('neet-physics-electrostatics-q15', 'neet-physics-electrostatics', 'The work done in moving a charge along an equipotential surface is:', 'Zero', 'qV', 'Maximum', 'qE·d', 'A', 'No work is done moving a charge on an equipotential surface (ΔV = 0).', 14),
  ('neet-physics-electrostatics-q16', 'neet-physics-electrostatics', 'The SI unit of electric field intensity is:', 'N/C (or V/m)', 'C/N', 'J/C', 'C·m', 'A', 'Electric field is force per unit charge, N/C, equivalent to V/m.', 15),
  ('neet-physics-current-electricity-q13', 'neet-physics-current-electricity', 'Ohm''s law states that V equals:', 'IR', 'I/R', 'R/I', 'I²R', 'A', 'Ohm''s law: V = IR for an ohmic conductor.', 12),
  ('neet-physics-current-electricity-q14', 'neet-physics-current-electricity', 'The equivalent resistance of two resistors R in parallel is:', 'R/2', '2R', 'R', 'R²', 'A', 'Two equal resistances R in parallel give R/2.', 13),
  ('neet-physics-current-electricity-q15', 'neet-physics-current-electricity', 'The power dissipated in a resistor carrying current I is:', 'I²R', 'IR', 'I/R', 'IR²', 'A', 'Power dissipated = I²R (also VI or V²/R).', 14),
  ('neet-physics-current-electricity-q16', 'neet-physics-current-electricity', 'Kirchhoff''s junction rule is based on the conservation of:', 'Charge', 'Energy', 'Momentum', 'Mass', 'A', 'The junction (current) rule expresses conservation of electric charge.', 15),
  ('neet-physics-rotational-motion-q13', 'neet-physics-rotational-motion', 'The moment of inertia of a solid sphere about its diameter is:', '(2/5)MR²', '(2/3)MR²', 'MR²', '½MR²', 'A', 'A solid sphere has I = (2/5)MR² about a diameter.', 12),
  ('neet-physics-rotational-motion-q14', 'neet-physics-rotational-motion', 'Angular momentum is conserved when the net external:', 'Torque is zero', 'Force is zero', 'Power is zero', 'Energy is zero', 'A', 'Zero net external torque conserves angular momentum.', 13),
  ('neet-physics-rotational-motion-q15', 'neet-physics-rotational-motion', 'Torque is the rotational analogue of:', 'Force', 'Momentum', 'Displacement', 'Energy', 'A', 'Torque plays the role in rotation that force plays in translation.', 14),
  ('neet-physics-rotational-motion-q16', 'neet-physics-rotational-motion', 'For a body rolling without slipping, the velocity of the contact point is:', 'Zero', 'v', '2v', 'ωR/2', 'A', 'In rolling without slipping the point of contact is instantaneously at rest.', 15),
  ('neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics-q13', 'neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics', 'The de Broglie wavelength of a particle of momentum p is:', 'h/p', 'p/h', 'hp', 'h/p²', 'A', 'λ = h/p, the de Broglie relation for matter waves.', 12),
  ('neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics-q14', 'neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics', 'In the photoelectric effect, the stopping potential depends on the light''s:', 'Frequency', 'Intensity', 'Polarisation', 'Phase', 'A', 'Stopping potential depends on frequency, not intensity (Einstein''s relation).', 13),
  ('neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics-q15', 'neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics', 'The work function of a metal is the minimum energy needed to:', 'Eject an electron from the surface', 'Ionise the nucleus', 'Melt the metal', 'Excite an electron to n=2', 'A', 'The work function is the least energy required to free a surface electron.', 14),
  ('neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics-q16', 'neet-physics-dual-nature-modern-physics', 'The energy of a photon of frequency ν is given by:', 'hν', 'h/ν', 'ν/h', 'hν²', 'A', 'Photon energy E = hν, where h is Planck''s constant.', 15),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-percentages-profit-loss-q01', 'ssc-cgl-quant-percentages-profit-loss', 'A price is increased by 20% and then decreased by 20%. The net change is:', '0%', '+4%', '−2%', '−4%', 'D', 'Net% = 20 − 20 − (400/100) = −4%, a net decrease.', 0),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-percentages-profit-loss-q02', 'ssc-cgl-quant-percentages-profit-loss', 'An article bought for ₹400 is sold for ₹500. The profit percentage is:', '30%', '20%', '25%', '40%', 'C', 'Profit% = (100/400) × 100 = 25%.', 1),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-percentages-profit-loss-q03', 'ssc-cgl-quant-percentages-profit-loss', 'What is 35% of 240?', '80', '84', '74', '96', 'B', '35% of 240 = 0.35 × 240 = 84.', 2),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-percentages-profit-loss-q04', 'ssc-cgl-quant-percentages-profit-loss', 'Two items are sold at the same price, one at 10% profit and one at 10% loss. The overall result is:', 'No profit, no loss', '1% profit', '1% loss', '2% loss', 'C', 'Selling at equal SP with ±x% gives a loss of (x/10)² = (10/10)² = 1%.', 3),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-percentages-profit-loss-q05', 'ssc-cgl-quant-percentages-profit-loss', 'A shopkeeper marks an item at ₹800 and gives a 15% discount. The selling price is:', '₹640', '₹700', '₹680', '₹720', 'C', 'SP = 800 × (85/100) = ₹680.', 4),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-percentages-profit-loss-q06', 'ssc-cgl-quant-percentages-profit-loss', 'If 60 is what percent of 150?', '50%', '25%', '45%', '40%', 'D', '(60/150) × 100 = 40%.', 5),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-percentages-profit-loss-q07', 'ssc-cgl-quant-percentages-profit-loss', 'By selling a pen for ₹54, a man loses 10%. The cost price was:', '₹58', '₹62', '₹60', '₹64', 'C', 'CP = 54 / 0.90 = ₹60.', 6),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-percentages-profit-loss-q08', 'ssc-cgl-quant-percentages-profit-loss', 'A number increased by 25% becomes 250. The original number is:', '180', '210', '200', '225', 'C', 'Original = 250 / 1.25 = 200.', 7),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-time-speed-distance-q01', 'ssc-cgl-quant-time-speed-distance', 'A car covers 180 km in 3 hours. Its speed is:', '50 km/h', '70 km/h', '60 km/h', '90 km/h', 'C', 'Speed = 180/3 = 60 km/h.', 0),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-time-speed-distance-q02', 'ssc-cgl-quant-time-speed-distance', 'Convert 72 km/h into m/s:', '18 m/s', '20 m/s', '15 m/s', '25 m/s', 'B', 'To convert km/h to m/s, multiply by 5/18: 72 × 5/18 = 20 m/s.', 1),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-time-speed-distance-q03', 'ssc-cgl-quant-time-speed-distance', 'A man covers equal distances at 30 km/h and 60 km/h. His average speed is:', '40 km/h', '45 km/h', '48 km/h', '50 km/h', 'A', 'Average = 2uv/(u+v) = 2(30)(60)/90 = 40 km/h.', 2),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-time-speed-distance-q04', 'ssc-cgl-quant-time-speed-distance', 'Two trains move towards each other at 40 km/h and 50 km/h. Their relative speed is:', '100 km/h', '45 km/h', '10 km/h', '90 km/h', 'D', 'For opposite directions, relative speed is the sum: 40 + 50 = 90 km/h.', 3),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-time-speed-distance-q05', 'ssc-cgl-quant-time-speed-distance', 'A 150 m long train running at 20 m/s crosses a pole in:', '5 s', '7.5 s', '10 s', '12 s', 'B', 'Time = length/speed = 150/20 = 7.5 s.', 4),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-time-speed-distance-q06', 'ssc-cgl-quant-time-speed-distance', 'A boat goes 12 km/h downstream and 8 km/h upstream. The speed of the stream is:', '2 km/h', '4 km/h', '6 km/h', '10 km/h', 'A', 'Stream speed = (down − up)/2 = (12 − 8)/2 = 2 km/h.', 5),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-time-speed-distance-q07', 'ssc-cgl-quant-time-speed-distance', 'If a journey of 240 km takes 4 hours, how long will 360 km take at the same speed?', '5 hours', '6 hours', '7 hours', '8 hours', 'B', 'Speed = 60 km/h, so 360/60 = 6 hours.', 6),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-time-speed-distance-q08', 'ssc-cgl-quant-time-speed-distance', 'A 120 m train at 15 m/s crosses a 180 m platform in:', '15 s', '20 s', '24 s', '18 s', 'B', 'Time = (120 + 180)/15 = 300/15 = 20 s.', 7),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-coding-decoding-series-q01', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-coding-decoding-series', 'If CAT is coded as DBU, then DOG is coded as:', 'FPH', 'EPF', 'EPH', 'CPH', 'C', 'Each letter shifts forward by one: D→E, O→P, G→H.', 0),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-coding-decoding-series-q02', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-coding-decoding-series', 'Find the next term: 2, 6, 12, 20, 30, ?', '40', '42', '44', '36', 'B', 'Differences are 4, 6, 8, 10, then 12; 30 + 12 = 42.', 1),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-coding-decoding-series-q03', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-coding-decoding-series', 'What is the position of the letter T in the English alphabet?', '20', '19', '18', '21', 'A', 'Using EJOTY, T is the 20th letter.', 2),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-coding-decoding-series-q04', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-coding-decoding-series', 'Complete the series: 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, ?', '11', '15', '12', '13', 'D', 'Fibonacci: each term is the sum of the two before it; 5 + 8 = 13.', 3),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-coding-decoding-series-q05', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-coding-decoding-series', 'In a code, A is written as Z, B as Y, C as X. How is D written?', 'W', 'V', 'X', 'U', 'A', 'Opposite coding: each letter maps to 27 − n, so D (4) → 23 → W.', 4),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-coding-decoding-series-q06', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-coding-decoding-series', 'Find the missing term: 3, 9, 27, 81, ?', '243', '210', '162', '324', 'A', 'Each term is multiplied by 3; 81 × 3 = 243.', 5),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-coding-decoding-series-q07', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-coding-decoding-series', 'If 5 is coded as 36 and 6 as 49, then 7 is coded as:', '56', '63', '64', '81', 'C', 'The code is (n + 1)²; (7 + 1)² = 64.', 6),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-coding-decoding-series-q08', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-coding-decoding-series', 'Find the odd one in the number series: 4, 9, 16, 25, 35, 49', '16', '25', '49', '35', 'D', 'All are perfect squares except 35 (36 would fit the pattern).', 7),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-idioms-one-word-q01', 'ssc-cgl-english-idioms-one-word', 'The idiom ''to let the cat out of the bag'' means:', 'To buy a pet', 'To reveal a secret', 'To run away', 'To take a risk', 'B', 'It means to disclose a secret, usually by accident.', 0),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-idioms-one-word-q02', 'ssc-cgl-english-idioms-one-word', 'One word for ''a person who studies birds'' is:', 'Botanist', 'Ornithologist', 'Zoologist', 'Entomologist', 'B', 'Ornithology is the study of birds, so the practitioner is an ornithologist.', 1),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-idioms-one-word-q03', 'ssc-cgl-english-idioms-one-word', 'The idiom ''a blessing in disguise'' means:', 'A hidden danger', 'A good thing that seemed bad at first', 'A bad omen', 'An obvious gift', 'B', 'It refers to something that turns out beneficial despite seeming unfortunate initially.', 2),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-idioms-one-word-q04', 'ssc-cgl-english-idioms-one-word', 'One word for ''words inscribed on a tomb'' is:', 'Epithet', 'Epitaph', 'Epilogue', 'Epigram', 'B', 'An epitaph is the inscription on a gravestone or tomb.', 3),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-idioms-one-word-q05', 'ssc-cgl-english-idioms-one-word', '''Once in a blue moon'' means an event that happens:', 'Very often', 'Every month', 'At night only', 'Very rarely', 'D', 'The idiom denotes something happening extremely rarely.', 4),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-idioms-one-word-q06', 'ssc-cgl-english-idioms-one-word', 'One word for ''a person who hates mankind'' is:', 'Misanthrope', 'Egotist', 'Misogynist', 'Philanthropist', 'A', 'A misanthrope dislikes or distrusts humankind; a philanthropist loves it.', 5),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-idioms-one-word-q07', 'ssc-cgl-english-idioms-one-word', 'To ''burn the midnight oil'' means to:', 'Waste resources', 'Sleep early', 'Start a fire', 'Work late into the night', 'D', 'The idiom describes studying or working late into the night.', 6),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-idioms-one-word-q08', 'ssc-cgl-english-idioms-one-word', 'One word for ''a person who walks on a tightrope'' is:', 'Funambulist', 'Equestrian', 'Somnambulist', 'Acrobat', 'A', 'A funambulist is a tightrope walker; a somnambulist is a sleepwalker.', 7),
  ('ssc-cgl-general-awareness-indian-polity-facts-q01', 'ssc-cgl-general-awareness-indian-polity-facts', 'The Indian Constitution came into force on:', '26 January 1950', '15 August 1947', '26 November 1949', '2 October 1950', 'A', 'Though adopted on 26 November 1949, it came into force on 26 January 1950.', 0),
  ('ssc-cgl-general-awareness-indian-polity-facts-q02', 'ssc-cgl-general-awareness-indian-polity-facts', 'Who was the Chairman of the Drafting Committee of the Constitution?', 'Jawaharlal Nehru', 'Rajendra Prasad', 'B.R. Ambedkar', 'Sardar Patel', 'C', 'Dr. B.R. Ambedkar chaired the Drafting Committee of the Constituent Assembly.', 1),
  ('ssc-cgl-general-awareness-indian-polity-facts-q03', 'ssc-cgl-general-awareness-indian-polity-facts', 'The Fundamental Duties were added to the Constitution by which amendment?', '42nd Amendment', '44th Amendment', '52nd Amendment', '73rd Amendment', 'A', 'The 42nd Amendment (1976) added the Fundamental Duties under Article 51A.', 2),
  ('ssc-cgl-general-awareness-indian-polity-facts-q04', 'ssc-cgl-general-awareness-indian-polity-facts', 'Which Article of the Constitution deals with the office of the President?', 'Article 63', 'Article 52', 'Article 75', 'Article 76', 'B', 'Article 52 establishes the office of the President of India.', 3),
  ('ssc-cgl-general-awareness-indian-polity-facts-q05', 'ssc-cgl-general-awareness-indian-polity-facts', 'The Eighth Schedule of the Constitution deals with:', 'Anti-defection', 'Panchayats', 'Union and State lists', 'Official languages', 'D', 'The Eighth Schedule lists the recognised official languages, now 22 in number.', 4),
  ('ssc-cgl-general-awareness-indian-polity-facts-q06', 'ssc-cgl-general-awareness-indian-polity-facts', 'The concept of Directive Principles of State Policy was borrowed from:', 'The USA', 'The UK', 'Ireland', 'Canada', 'C', 'India adopted the Directive Principles from the Irish Constitution.', 5),
  ('ssc-cgl-general-awareness-indian-polity-facts-q07', 'ssc-cgl-general-awareness-indian-polity-facts', 'The Finance Commission is constituted under which Article and at what interval?', 'Article 280, every 5 years', 'Article 324, every 6 years', 'Article 148, annually', 'Article 280, every 10 years', 'A', 'Article 280 provides for a Finance Commission constituted every five years.', 6),
  ('ssc-cgl-general-awareness-indian-polity-facts-q08', 'ssc-cgl-general-awareness-indian-polity-facts', 'The 42nd Amendment (1976) added which words to the Preamble?', 'Sovereign and Republic', 'Democratic and Justice', 'Liberty and Equality', 'Socialist, Secular and Integrity', 'D', 'The 42nd Amendment inserted ''Socialist'', ''Secular'' and ''Integrity'' into the Preamble.', 7),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-ratio-proportion-q01', 'ssc-cgl-quant-ratio-proportion', 'Divide ₹600 in the ratio 2 : 3. The smaller share is:', '₹300', '₹360', '₹200', '₹240', 'D', 'Smaller share = 600 × 2/(2+3) = 600 × 2/5 = ₹240.', 0),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-ratio-proportion-q02', 'ssc-cgl-quant-ratio-proportion', 'The mean proportional of 9 and 16 is:', '10', '12', '14', '15', 'B', 'Mean proportional = sqrt(9 × 16) = sqrt(144) = 12.', 1),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-ratio-proportion-q03', 'ssc-cgl-quant-ratio-proportion', 'If a : b = 2 : 3 and b : c = 4 : 5, then a : c is:', '8 : 15', '2 : 5', '3 : 5', '8 : 5', 'A', 'a : b : c = 8 : 12 : 15, so a : c = 8 : 15.', 2),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-ratio-proportion-q04', 'ssc-cgl-quant-ratio-proportion', 'In a proportion a : b :: c : d, if a = 4, b = 6 and c = 10, then d is:', '12', '14', '16', '15', 'D', 'a×d = b×c gives 4d = 60, so d = 15.', 3),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-ratio-proportion-q05', 'ssc-cgl-quant-ratio-proportion', 'Two numbers are in the ratio 5 : 7 and their sum is 96. The larger number is:', '40', '60', '56', '48', 'C', 'Larger = 96 × 7/12 = 56.', 4),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-ratio-proportion-q06', 'ssc-cgl-quant-ratio-proportion', 'The ratio 18 : 24 in its simplest form is:', '2 : 3', '4 : 5', '3 : 4', '6 : 8', 'C', 'Dividing both by HCF 6 gives 3 : 4.', 5),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-ratio-proportion-q07', 'ssc-cgl-quant-ratio-proportion', 'If 40% of A equals 60% of B, then A : B is:', '2 : 3', '3 : 2', '4 : 6', '5 : 3', 'B', '0.4A = 0.6B gives A/B = 0.6/0.4 = 3/2, so A : B = 3 : 2.', 6),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-ratio-proportion-q08', 'ssc-cgl-quant-ratio-proportion', 'A sum is split among P, Q, R in the ratio 1 : 2 : 3. If R gets ₹90, the total sum is:', '₹150', '₹160', '₹120', '₹180', 'D', 'R has 3 parts = ₹90, so 1 part = ₹30 and total 6 parts = ₹180.', 7),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-averages-q01', 'ssc-cgl-quant-averages', 'The average of 10, 20, 30, 40 and 50 is:', '25', '30', '35', '40', 'B', 'Sum = 150, count = 5, average = 150/5 = 30.', 0),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-averages-q02', 'ssc-cgl-quant-averages', 'The average of the first 10 natural numbers is:', '5', '5.5', '6', '4.5', 'B', 'Average of first n naturals = (n+1)/2 = 11/2 = 5.5.', 1),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-averages-q03', 'ssc-cgl-quant-averages', 'The average age of 5 boys is 14 years. The total of their ages is:', '70', '65', '60', '75', 'A', 'Total = average × number = 14 × 5 = 70.', 2),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-averages-q04', 'ssc-cgl-quant-averages', 'The average of 6 numbers is 25. If one number 30 is removed, the average of the rest is:', '22', '23', '24', '26', 'C', 'Sum = 150, remove 30 gives 120 over 5 numbers = 24.', 3),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-averages-q05', 'ssc-cgl-quant-averages', 'The average of first n odd natural numbers is:', 'n + 1', '2n', '(n+1)/2', 'n', 'D', 'The average of the first n odd numbers is exactly n.', 4),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-averages-q06', 'ssc-cgl-quant-averages', 'A batsman''s average in 10 innings is 40. To raise it to 42 in the 11th innings, he must score:', '62', '60', '64', '66', 'A', 'New total needed = 42 × 11 = 462; old total = 400; required = 462 − 400 = 62.', 5),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-averages-q07', 'ssc-cgl-quant-averages', 'The average of three consecutive even numbers is 16. The largest of them is:', '14', '16', '20', '18', 'D', 'Middle number = average = 16, so the numbers are 14, 16, 18; largest is 18.', 6),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-averages-q08', 'ssc-cgl-quant-averages', 'Two groups of 4 and 6 students score average 70 and 80. The combined average is:', '74', '75', '76', '78', 'C', 'Weighted avg = (4×70 + 6×80)/10 = (280+480)/10 = 760/10 = 76.', 7),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-simple-compound-interest-q01', 'ssc-cgl-quant-simple-compound-interest', 'The simple interest on ₹1000 at 5% per annum for 3 years is:', '₹150', '₹120', '₹180', '₹200', 'A', 'SI = (1000 × 5 × 3)/100 = ₹150.', 0),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-simple-compound-interest-q02', 'ssc-cgl-quant-simple-compound-interest', 'The compound interest on ₹2000 at 10% per annum for 2 years is:', '₹420', '₹400', '₹440', '₹460', 'A', 'CI = 2000×(1.1)^2 − 2000 = 2420 − 2000 = ₹420.', 1),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-simple-compound-interest-q03', 'ssc-cgl-quant-simple-compound-interest', 'The difference between CI and SI on ₹1000 at 10% for 2 years is:', '₹5', '₹8', '₹12', '₹10', 'D', 'Difference = P×(R/100)^2 = 1000×(0.1)^2 = ₹10.', 2),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-simple-compound-interest-q04', 'ssc-cgl-quant-simple-compound-interest', 'At what rate will SI on a sum double the principal in 20 years?', '5%', '4%', '6%', '10%', 'A', 'Money doubles under SI when R×T = 100, so R = 100/20 = 5%.', 3),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-simple-compound-interest-q05', 'ssc-cgl-quant-simple-compound-interest', 'The amount on ₹500 at simple interest of 8% for 2 years is:', '₹560', '₹580', '₹600', '₹620', 'B', 'SI = (500×8×2)/100 = ₹80; amount = 500 + 80 = ₹580.', 4),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-simple-compound-interest-q06', 'ssc-cgl-quant-simple-compound-interest', 'A sum gives ₹60 simple interest in 2 years at 5% per annum. The principal is:', '₹500', '₹550', '₹650', '₹600', 'D', 'P = (SI×100)/(R×T) = (60×100)/(5×2) = ₹600.', 5),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-simple-compound-interest-q07', 'ssc-cgl-quant-simple-compound-interest', 'The amount on ₹8000 at 5% compound interest for 1 year is:', '₹8200', '₹8300', '₹8400', '₹8500', 'C', 'Amount = 8000 × 1.05 = ₹8400 (for 1 year CI equals SI).', 6),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-simple-compound-interest-q08', 'ssc-cgl-quant-simple-compound-interest', 'Simple interest on a sum for 8 months at 6% per annum equals ₹40. The principal is:', '₹800', '₹900', '₹1000', '₹1200', 'C', 'T = 8/12 yr; P = (40×100)/(6×8/12) = 4000/4 = ₹1000.', 7),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-time-and-work-q01', 'ssc-cgl-quant-time-and-work', 'A does a job in 10 days and B in 15 days. Together they finish it in:', '5 days', '8 days', '7 days', '6 days', 'D', 'Together = ab/(a+b) = (10×15)/25 = 150/25 = 6 days.', 0),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-time-and-work-q02', 'ssc-cgl-quant-time-and-work', 'If A can do a work in 12 days, his one day''s work is:', '1/10', '1/24', '1/6', '1/12', 'D', 'One day''s work = 1/(time to finish) = 1/12.', 1),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-time-and-work-q03', 'ssc-cgl-quant-time-and-work', 'A and B together finish a job in 8 days; A alone takes 12 days. B alone takes:', '24 days', '20 days', '16 days', '18 days', 'A', 'B''s rate = 1/8 − 1/12 = 3/24 − 2/24 = 1/24, so B takes 24 days.', 2),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-time-and-work-q04', 'ssc-cgl-quant-time-and-work', '12 men finish a work in 10 days. In how many days will 8 men finish the same work?', '12 days', '14 days', '15 days', '16 days', 'C', 'Men×days constant: 12×10 = 8×d, so d = 120/8 = 15 days.', 3),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-time-and-work-q05', 'ssc-cgl-quant-time-and-work', 'A is twice as efficient as B. If B does a job in 18 days, A does it in:', '9 days', '6 days', '12 days', '36 days', 'A', 'Twice the efficiency means half the time: 18/2 = 9 days.', 4),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-time-and-work-q06', 'ssc-cgl-quant-time-and-work', 'A pipe fills a tank in 6 hours and an outlet empties it in 12 hours. Together they fill it in:', '12 hours', '10 hours', '8 hours', '18 hours', 'A', 'Net rate = 1/6 − 1/12 = 1/12, so the tank fills in 12 hours.', 5),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-time-and-work-q07', 'ssc-cgl-quant-time-and-work', 'A and B do a job in 20 and 30 days. Working together, the fraction done in 6 days is:', '1/3', '2/5', '1/2', '3/5', 'C', 'Combined rate = 1/20 + 1/30 = 5/60 = 1/12; in 6 days = 6/12 = 1/2.', 6),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-time-and-work-q08', 'ssc-cgl-quant-time-and-work', 'If 4 workers build a wall in 9 days, how many workers build it in 6 days?', '5', '7', '6', '8', 'C', 'Workers×days constant: 4×9 = w×6, so w = 36/6 = 6 workers.', 7),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-number-system-q01', 'ssc-cgl-quant-number-system', 'The HCF of 12 and 18 is:', '2', '3', '9', '6', 'D', 'Common factors of 12 and 18 give the greatest as 6.', 0),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-number-system-q02', 'ssc-cgl-quant-number-system', 'The LCM of 4, 6 and 8 is:', '12', '24', '16', '48', 'B', 'Smallest number divisible by 4, 6 and 8 is 24.', 1),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-number-system-q03', 'ssc-cgl-quant-number-system', 'If the HCF of two numbers is 6 and their LCM is 36, and one number is 12, the other is:', '24', '20', '18', '30', 'C', 'Product = HCF×LCM = 216; other = 216/12 = 18.', 2),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-number-system-q04', 'ssc-cgl-quant-number-system', 'Which of these numbers is divisible by 9?', '1234', '2345', '4321', '5346', 'D', 'Digit sum of 5346 = 5+3+4+6 = 18, divisible by 9.', 3),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-number-system-q05', 'ssc-cgl-quant-number-system', 'The least number which when divided by 5, 6 and 7 leaves remainder 2 in each case is:', '210', '208', '212', '216', 'C', 'LCM(5,6,7) = 210; required = 210 + 2 = 212.', 4),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-number-system-q06', 'ssc-cgl-quant-number-system', 'Which of the following is a prime number?', '21', '27', '29', '33', 'C', '29 has exactly two factors, 1 and itself, so it is prime.', 5),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-number-system-q07', 'ssc-cgl-quant-number-system', 'Is 3927 divisible by 11?', 'No, remainder 1', 'Yes, it is divisible', 'No, remainder 3', 'No, remainder 5', 'B', 'Alternating sum (7−2+9−3)=11 is divisible by 11, so 3927 is divisible.', 6),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-number-system-q08', 'ssc-cgl-quant-number-system', 'The HCF of the fractions 2/3 and 4/9 is:', '2/9', '2/3', '4/3', '1/9', 'A', 'HCF of fractions = HCF(2,4)/LCM(3,9) = 2/9.', 7),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-mensuration-q01', 'ssc-cgl-quant-mensuration', 'The area of a square with side 7 cm is:', '28 cm^2', '49 cm^2', '14 cm^2', '56 cm^2', 'B', 'Area = side^2 = 7^2 = 49 cm^2.', 0),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-mensuration-q02', 'ssc-cgl-quant-mensuration', 'The perimeter of a rectangle 8 cm by 5 cm is:', '13 cm', '20 cm', '40 cm', '26 cm', 'D', 'Perimeter = 2(l+b) = 2(8+5) = 26 cm.', 1),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-mensuration-q03', 'ssc-cgl-quant-mensuration', 'The area of a circle with radius 7 cm (pi = 22/7) is:', '144 cm^2', '174 cm^2', '164 cm^2', '154 cm^2', 'D', 'Area = pi×r^2 = (22/7)×49 = 154 cm^2.', 2),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-mensuration-q04', 'ssc-cgl-quant-mensuration', 'The volume of a cube of edge 5 cm is:', '25 cm^3', '75 cm^3', '150 cm^3', '125 cm^3', 'D', 'Volume = a^3 = 5^3 = 125 cm^3.', 3),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-mensuration-q05', 'ssc-cgl-quant-mensuration', 'The total surface area of a cube of edge 4 cm is:', '64 cm^2', '80 cm^2', '112 cm^2', '96 cm^2', 'D', 'TSA = 6×a^2 = 6×16 = 96 cm^2.', 4),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-mensuration-q06', 'ssc-cgl-quant-mensuration', 'The area of a triangle with base 10 cm and height 6 cm is:', '60 cm^2', '16 cm^2', '30 cm^2', '40 cm^2', 'C', 'Area = (1/2)×base×height = (1/2)×10×6 = 30 cm^2.', 5),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-mensuration-q07', 'ssc-cgl-quant-mensuration', 'The diagonal of a square of side 5 cm is:', '5 cm', '10 cm', '5sqrt(2) cm', '25 cm', 'C', 'Diagonal = side × sqrt(2) = 5sqrt(2) cm.', 6),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-mensuration-q08', 'ssc-cgl-quant-mensuration', 'If each edge of a cube doubles, its volume becomes:', '8 times', '4 times', '6 times', '2 times', 'A', 'Volume scales by k^3; doubling gives 2^3 = 8 times.', 7),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-algebra-basics-q01', 'ssc-cgl-quant-algebra-basics', 'Expand (a + b)^2:', 'a^2 + b^2', 'a^2 - 2ab + b^2', 'a^2 + 2ab + b^2', '2a + 2b', 'C', '(a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2.', 0),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-algebra-basics-q02', 'ssc-cgl-quant-algebra-basics', 'Factorise a^2 - b^2:', '(a - b)^2', '(a + b)^2', '(a + b)(a - b)', 'a^2 + b^2', 'C', 'a^2 - b^2 = (a + b)(a - b).', 1),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-algebra-basics-q03', 'ssc-cgl-quant-algebra-basics', 'If a + 1/a = 3, then a^2 + 1/a^2 equals:', '7', '5', '9', '11', 'A', 'a^2 + 1/a^2 = (a + 1/a)^2 − 2 = 9 − 2 = 7.', 2),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-algebra-basics-q04', 'ssc-cgl-quant-algebra-basics', 'If a + b + c = 0, then a^3 + b^3 + c^3 equals:', '0', 'abc', 'a^3b^3c^3', '3abc', 'D', 'When a + b + c = 0, a^3 + b^3 + c^3 = 3abc.', 3),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-algebra-basics-q05', 'ssc-cgl-quant-algebra-basics', 'For the equation 2x^2 - 5x + 3 = 0, the sum of the roots is:', '-5/2', '3/2', '5/2', '2/5', 'C', 'Sum of roots = -b/a = -(-5)/2 = 5/2.', 4),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-algebra-basics-q06', 'ssc-cgl-quant-algebra-basics', 'The value of 102^2 - 98^2 is:', '400', '600', '1000', '800', 'D', 'a^2 - b^2 = (102+98)(102-98) = 200×4 = 800.', 5),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-algebra-basics-q07', 'ssc-cgl-quant-algebra-basics', 'For 3x^2 + 6x + 2 = 0, the product of the roots is:', '2', '6', '-2', '2/3', 'D', 'Product of roots = c/a = 2/3.', 6),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-algebra-basics-q08', 'ssc-cgl-quant-algebra-basics', 'If a - 1/a = 2, then a^2 + 1/a^2 equals:', '4', '5', '6', '8', 'C', 'a^2 + 1/a^2 = (a - 1/a)^2 + 2 = 4 + 2 = 6.', 7),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-trigonometry-q01', 'ssc-cgl-quant-trigonometry', 'The value of sin 30 degrees is:', '0', 'sqrt(3)/2', '1', '1/2', 'D', 'sin 30 degrees = 1/2 from the standard table.', 0),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-trigonometry-q02', 'ssc-cgl-quant-trigonometry', 'The value of sin^2(x) + cos^2(x) is:', '0', 'depends on x', '2', '1', 'D', 'The Pythagorean identity gives sin^2(x) + cos^2(x) = 1 for all x.', 1),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-trigonometry-q03', 'ssc-cgl-quant-trigonometry', 'The value of tan 45 degrees is:', '0', '1/sqrt(3)', 'sqrt(3)', '1', 'D', 'tan 45 degrees = 1 from the standard angles.', 2),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-trigonometry-q04', 'ssc-cgl-quant-trigonometry', 'sin(90 degrees - x) is equal to:', 'cos x', 'sin x', 'tan x', 'cosec x', 'A', 'By complementary angles, sin(90 − x) = cos x.', 3),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-trigonometry-q05', 'ssc-cgl-quant-trigonometry', 'The value of cos 60 degrees is:', '1/2', 'sqrt(3)/2', '1', '0', 'A', 'cos 60 degrees = 1/2 from the standard table.', 4),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-trigonometry-q06', 'ssc-cgl-quant-trigonometry', '1 + tan^2(x) equals:', 'cosec^2(x)', 'sec^2(x)', 'cot^2(x)', 'sin^2(x)', 'B', 'The identity states 1 + tan^2(x) = sec^2(x).', 5),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-trigonometry-q07', 'ssc-cgl-quant-trigonometry', 'A tower casts a shadow equal to its height. The angle of elevation of the sun is:', '45 degrees', '30 degrees', '60 degrees', '90 degrees', 'A', 'tan(angle) = height/shadow = 1, so the angle is 45 degrees.', 6),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-trigonometry-q08', 'ssc-cgl-quant-trigonometry', 'The value of sin 0 degrees + cos 0 degrees is:', '0', '2', '1', '1/2', 'C', 'sin 0 = 0 and cos 0 = 1, so the sum is 1.', 7),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-geometry-q01', 'ssc-cgl-quant-geometry', 'The sum of the interior angles of a triangle is:', '180 degrees', '90 degrees', '270 degrees', '360 degrees', 'A', 'The angle sum of any triangle is 180 degrees.', 0),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-geometry-q02', 'ssc-cgl-quant-geometry', 'In a right triangle with legs 6 and 8, the hypotenuse is:', '9', '10', '12', '14', 'B', 'Hypotenuse = sqrt(6^2 + 8^2) = sqrt(100) = 10.', 1),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-geometry-q03', 'ssc-cgl-quant-geometry', 'The angle in a semicircle is:', '45 degrees', '90 degrees', '60 degrees', '120 degrees', 'B', 'The angle subtended in a semicircle is a right angle, 90 degrees.', 2),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-geometry-q04', 'ssc-cgl-quant-geometry', 'The sum of interior angles of a hexagon is:', '540 degrees', '720 degrees', '900 degrees', '1080 degrees', 'B', 'Sum = (n−2)×180 = (6−2)×180 = 720 degrees.', 3),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-geometry-q05', 'ssc-cgl-quant-geometry', 'Each exterior angle of a regular pentagon is:', '60 degrees', '90 degrees', '72 degrees', '108 degrees', 'C', 'Exterior angle = 360/n = 360/5 = 72 degrees.', 4),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-geometry-q06', 'ssc-cgl-quant-geometry', 'The centroid of a triangle divides each median in the ratio:', '1 : 1', '2 : 1', '3 : 1', '3 : 2', 'B', 'The centroid divides each median 2 : 1 from the vertex.', 5),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-geometry-q07', 'ssc-cgl-quant-geometry', 'An exterior angle of a triangle is 110 degrees and one opposite interior angle is 70 degrees. The other is:', '40 degrees', '30 degrees', '50 degrees', '60 degrees', 'A', 'Exterior angle = sum of opposite interiors, so other = 110 − 70 = 40 degrees.', 6),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-geometry-q08', 'ssc-cgl-quant-geometry', 'A tangent to a circle meets the radius at the point of contact at an angle of:', '45 degrees', '90 degrees', '60 degrees', '180 degrees', 'B', 'A tangent is perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact, 90 degrees.', 7),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-data-interpretation-q01', 'ssc-cgl-quant-data-interpretation', 'Sales rose from 200 to 250 units. The percentage increase is:', '20%', '25%', '30%', '50%', 'B', 'Increase = (250−200)/200 × 100 = 25%.', 0),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-data-interpretation-q02', 'ssc-cgl-quant-data-interpretation', 'In a pie chart, a category is 25% of the total. Its slice angle is:', '60 degrees', '72 degrees', '90 degrees', '120 degrees', 'C', 'Angle = 25% × 360 = 90 degrees.', 1),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-data-interpretation-q03', 'ssc-cgl-quant-data-interpretation', 'Sales over three years were 200, 250 and 300. The average annual sales is:', '240', '260', '250', '275', 'C', 'Average = (200+250+300)/3 = 750/3 = 250.', 2),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-data-interpretation-q04', 'ssc-cgl-quant-data-interpretation', 'A pie slice measures 90 degrees and the grand total is 800. The slice value is:', '150', '200', '180', '250', 'B', 'Value = (90/360) × 800 = 200.', 3),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-data-interpretation-q05', 'ssc-cgl-quant-data-interpretation', 'A value falls from 300 to 240. The percentage decrease is:', '20%', '15%', '25%', '30%', 'A', 'Decrease = (300−240)/300 × 100 = 20%.', 4),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-data-interpretation-q06', 'ssc-cgl-quant-data-interpretation', 'If a category is 120 degrees in a pie chart, its share of the total is:', '33.33%', '30%', '25%', '40%', 'A', 'Share = (120/360) × 100 = 33.33%.', 5),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-data-interpretation-q07', 'ssc-cgl-quant-data-interpretation', 'Four products sell 40, 60, 80 and 20 units. The product with the largest share has what percent?', '30%', '45%', '40%', '50%', 'C', 'Total = 200; largest is 80, so share = 80/200 × 100 = 40%.', 6),
  ('ssc-cgl-quant-data-interpretation-q08', 'ssc-cgl-quant-data-interpretation', 'A bar shows 150 in 2022 and 180 in 2023. The percentage growth is:', '15%', '20%', '18%', '25%', 'B', 'Growth = (180−150)/150 × 100 = 20%.', 7),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-analogy-classification-q01', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-analogy-classification', 'Hot is to Cold as Up is to:', 'High', 'Down', 'Top', 'Sky', 'B', 'The relationship is antonyms; the opposite of Up is Down.', 0),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-analogy-classification-q02', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-analogy-classification', '4 : 16 :: 5 : ?', '20', '10', '30', '25', 'D', 'The pattern is n : n^2, so 5 : 25.', 1),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-analogy-classification-q03', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-analogy-classification', 'Find the odd one out:', 'Rose', 'Lotus', 'Jasmine', 'Mango', 'D', 'Rose, Lotus and Jasmine are flowers; Mango is a fruit.', 2),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-analogy-classification-q04', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-analogy-classification', 'Carpenter is to Saw as Tailor is to:', 'Scissors', 'Shirt', 'Cloth', 'Shop', 'A', 'Worker-tool relationship; a tailor uses scissors as a carpenter uses a saw.', 3),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-analogy-classification-q05', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-analogy-classification', 'Find the odd number out:', '20', '16', '25', '9', 'A', '9, 16 and 25 are perfect squares; 20 is not.', 4),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-analogy-classification-q06', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-analogy-classification', 'Wheel is to Car as Petal is to:', 'Flower', 'Garden', 'Stem', 'Leaf', 'A', 'Part-whole relationship; a petal is part of a flower as a wheel is part of a car.', 5),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-analogy-classification-q07', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-analogy-classification', 'Find the odd one out:', '11', '13', '15', '17', 'C', '11, 13 and 17 are prime; 15 is composite (3×5).', 6),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-analogy-classification-q08', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-analogy-classification', 'Big is to Large as Quick is to:', 'Slow', 'Easy', 'Late', 'Fast', 'D', 'Synonym relationship; Quick and Fast mean the same.', 7),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-blood-relations-q01', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-blood-relations', 'Your father''s brother is your:', 'Maternal uncle', 'Paternal uncle', 'Cousin', 'Nephew', 'B', 'The father''s brother is the paternal uncle.', 0),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-blood-relations-q02', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-blood-relations', 'Your mother''s brother is your:', 'Maternal uncle', 'Paternal uncle', 'Brother-in-law', 'Father', 'A', 'The mother''s brother is the maternal uncle.', 1),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-blood-relations-q03', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-blood-relations', 'Pointing to a man, a woman said, "He is the son of my mother''s only son." The man is the woman''s:', 'Brother', 'Son', 'Nephew', 'Cousin', 'B', 'Mother''s only son is the woman''s brother only if she has none; here it is herself, so the man is her son.', 2),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-blood-relations-q04', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-blood-relations', 'Your son''s wife is your:', 'Sister-in-law', 'Niece', 'Daughter-in-law', 'Granddaughter', 'C', 'The wife of your son is your daughter-in-law.', 3),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-blood-relations-q05', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-blood-relations', 'A is B''s father and C is B''s sister. How is A related to C?', 'Uncle', 'Brother', 'Father', 'Grandfather', 'C', 'B and C are siblings, and A is the father of B, so A is C''s father.', 4),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-blood-relations-q06', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-blood-relations', 'Your daughter''s son is your:', 'Grandson', 'Nephew', 'Son', 'Cousin', 'A', 'The son of your daughter is your grandson.', 5),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-blood-relations-q07', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-blood-relations', 'P is the mother of Q. Q is the brother of R. R is the daughter of S. How is P related to S?', 'Sister', 'Wife', 'Daughter', 'Mother', 'B', 'P is mother of Q and R; S is father of R, so P is S''s wife.', 6),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-blood-relations-q08', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-blood-relations', 'Your father''s sister''s husband is your:', 'Maternal uncle', 'Brother-in-law', 'Paternal uncle (by marriage)', 'Cousin', 'C', 'Father''s sister is your aunt; her husband is your uncle by marriage on the paternal side.', 7),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-direction-sense-q01', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-direction-sense', 'A man walks 3 km east then 4 km north. His shortest distance from the start is:', '5 km', '7 km', '6 km', '1 km', 'A', 'Shortest = sqrt(3^2 + 4^2) = sqrt(25) = 5 km.', 0),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-direction-sense-q02', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-direction-sense', 'Facing north, you take a right turn. You now face:', 'East', 'South', 'West', 'North', 'A', 'A right turn from north (clockwise) faces east.', 1),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-direction-sense-q03', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-direction-sense', 'At sunrise a man''s shadow falls towards his:', 'West', 'North', 'East', 'South', 'A', 'The sun rises in the east, so shadows fall towards the west.', 2),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-direction-sense-q04', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-direction-sense', 'A walks 6 km south then 8 km west. His shortest distance from start is:', '10 km', '12 km', '14 km', '2 km', 'A', 'Shortest = sqrt(6^2 + 8^2) = sqrt(100) = 10 km.', 3),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-direction-sense-q05', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-direction-sense', 'Facing south, you make an about-turn. You now face:', 'East', 'West', 'South', 'North', 'D', 'An about-turn is a 180-degree reversal; from south you face north.', 4),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-direction-sense-q06', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-direction-sense', 'Facing east, you turn left. You now face:', 'West', 'South', 'North', 'East', 'C', 'A left turn from east (anticlockwise) faces north.', 5),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-direction-sense-q07', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-direction-sense', 'A man walks 5 km north, 5 km east, 5 km south. He is now from start:', '5 km east', '5 km west', '5 km north', '10 km east', 'A', 'North and south cancel; net displacement is 5 km east.', 6),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-direction-sense-q08', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-direction-sense', 'A path has legs of 5 km and 12 km at a right angle. The shortest distance is:', '15 km', '17 km', '13 km', '7 km', 'C', 'Shortest = sqrt(5^2 + 12^2) = sqrt(169) = 13 km.', 7),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-syllogism-q01', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-syllogism', 'All A are B. All B are C. Which conclusion follows?', 'All C are A', 'No A is C', 'Some C are not A', 'All A are C', 'D', 'All A are B and all B are C together give All A are C.', 0),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-syllogism-q02', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-syllogism', '"Some A are B" always implies which conversion?', 'All B are A', 'Some B are A', 'No B is A', 'All A are B', 'B', 'A particular positive converts: Some A are B implies Some B are A.', 1),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-syllogism-q03', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-syllogism', 'Two particular statements together give:', 'A universal conclusion', 'No definite conclusion', 'A negative conclusion', 'All are related', 'B', 'Two particular premises yield no definite conclusion.', 2),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-syllogism-q04', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-syllogism', 'All pens are books. No book is red. Which follows?', 'Some pens are red', 'No pen is red', 'All books are pens', 'All red are books', 'B', 'If no book is red and all pens are books, then no pen is red.', 3),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-syllogism-q05', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-syllogism', 'Does "All A are B" mean "All B are A"?', 'No, not necessarily', 'Yes, always', 'Only if A equals B', 'Only for particular statements', 'A', 'All A are B does not reverse; All B are A need not hold.', 4),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-syllogism-q06', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-syllogism', 'Some A are B. All B are C. Which follows?', 'No A is C', 'All A are C', 'Some A are C', 'All C are A', 'C', 'Some A are B and all B are C give Some A are C.', 5),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-syllogism-q07', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-syllogism', 'Two negative statements together give:', 'A valid conclusion', 'Some are conclusion', 'A universal positive', 'No definite conclusion', 'D', 'Two negative premises yield no definite conclusion.', 6),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-syllogism-q08', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-syllogism', 'In syllogism, you should judge conclusions based on:', 'The given statements only', 'Real-world facts', 'Common sense', 'Personal opinion', 'A', 'Conclusions must follow strictly from the given statements, not outside knowledge.', 7),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-seating-puzzles-q01', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-seating-puzzles', 'In a row of 5 facing north, who is exactly in the middle position?', '1st', '2nd', '5th', '3rd', 'D', 'In a row of 5, the middle position is the 3rd seat.', 0),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-seating-puzzles-q02', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-seating-puzzles', 'Five friends sit in a row. A is left of B, B is left of C. The leftmost so far is:', 'B', 'A', 'C', 'Cannot say', 'B', 'A is left of B who is left of C, so A is the leftmost of these three.', 1),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-seating-puzzles-q03', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-seating-puzzles', 'In a circle facing the centre, a person''s right hand points toward which side?', 'Outside', 'Anticlockwise neighbour', 'The centre', 'Clockwise neighbour', 'D', 'Facing centre, your right is toward the clockwise neighbour.', 2),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-seating-puzzles-q04', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-seating-puzzles', 'In a row of 7 people, the person 3rd from left is also which from the right?', '3rd', '5th', '4th', '6th', 'B', 'Position from right = total − left position + 1 = 7 − 3 + 1 = 5th.', 3),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-seating-puzzles-q05', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-seating-puzzles', 'Eight people sit evenly around a circle. The person opposite seat 1 is at seat:', '4', '8', '6', '5', 'D', 'Opposite seat in a circle of 8 is 1 + 4 = seat 5.', 4),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-seating-puzzles-q06', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-seating-puzzles', 'In a circle facing outward, a person''s right hand points toward which side?', 'Clockwise neighbour', 'The centre', 'Anticlockwise neighbour', 'Nobody', 'C', 'Facing outward, your right is toward the anticlockwise neighbour.', 5),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-seating-puzzles-q07', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-seating-puzzles', 'In a row of 10, a person is 4th from the left. How many people are to the right of them?', '5', '7', '6', '4', 'C', 'People to the right = 10 − 4 = 6.', 6),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-seating-puzzles-q08', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-seating-puzzles', 'When solving a seating puzzle, you should begin with:', 'A vague clue', 'The most concrete fixed clue', 'The last clue', 'A random guess', 'B', 'Start from the most concrete fixed clue to anchor the arrangement.', 7),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-clock-calendar-q01', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-clock-calendar', 'The angle between the hands of a clock at 3:00 is:', '60 degrees', '120 degrees', '90 degrees', '180 degrees', 'C', 'Angle = |30×3 − 5.5×0| = 90 degrees.', 0),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-clock-calendar-q02', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-clock-calendar', 'How many odd days are there in an ordinary year?', '0', '3', '2', '1', 'D', '365 days give 365 mod 7 = 1 odd day.', 1),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-clock-calendar-q03', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-clock-calendar', 'The minute hand moves how many degrees per minute?', '0.5 degrees', '6 degrees', '1 degree', '12 degrees', 'B', 'The minute hand covers 360 degrees in 60 minutes = 6 degrees per minute.', 2),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-clock-calendar-q04', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-clock-calendar', 'Which of these is a leap year?', '1900', '1800', '2100', '2000', 'D', 'Century years are leap only if divisible by 400; 2000 qualifies.', 3),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-clock-calendar-q05', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-clock-calendar', 'The angle between the clock hands at 6:00 is:', '90 degrees', '120 degrees', '150 degrees', '180 degrees', 'D', 'Angle = |30×6 − 5.5×0| = 180 degrees.', 4),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-clock-calendar-q06', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-clock-calendar', 'How many odd days are there in a leap year?', '2', '1', '3', '0', 'A', '366 days give 366 mod 7 = 2 odd days.', 5),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-clock-calendar-q07', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-clock-calendar', 'In 12 hours, the hands of a clock coincide how many times?', '10', '24', '12', '11', 'D', 'The hands coincide 11 times in every 12 hours.', 6),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-clock-calendar-q08', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-clock-calendar', 'The hour hand moves how many degrees per minute?', '1 degree', '0.5 degrees', '6 degrees', '2 degrees', 'B', 'The hour hand covers 30 degrees in 60 minutes = 0.5 degrees per minute.', 7),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-mirror-paper-folding-q01', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-mirror-paper-folding', 'A vertical mirror image reverses which directions?', 'Top and bottom', 'Left and right', 'Both diagonals', 'Nothing changes', 'B', 'A vertical mirror reverses left and right while keeping top and bottom.', 0),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-mirror-paper-folding-q02', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-mirror-paper-folding', 'A water image reverses which directions?', 'Left and right', 'Both', 'Nothing changes', 'Top and bottom', 'D', 'A water image flips the figure top to bottom about a horizontal line.', 1),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-mirror-paper-folding-q03', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-mirror-paper-folding', 'Which letter looks the same in a vertical mirror?', 'B', 'A', 'F', 'G', 'B', 'A has a vertical axis of symmetry, so it is unchanged in a vertical mirror.', 2),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-mirror-paper-folding-q04', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-mirror-paper-folding', 'A paper folded once, punched with one hole, when opened shows how many holes?', '1', '3', '2', '4', 'C', 'One fold creates a symmetric pair, giving 2 holes.', 3),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-mirror-paper-folding-q05', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-mirror-paper-folding', 'Which letter does NOT have vertical mirror symmetry?', 'H', 'R', 'T', 'O', 'B', 'R lacks a vertical axis of symmetry; H, O and T have one.', 4),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-mirror-paper-folding-q06', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-mirror-paper-folding', 'A paper folded twice, punched once, when opened shows up to how many holes?', '2', '3', '4', '8', 'C', 'Two folds can double twice, giving up to 4 holes.', 5),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-mirror-paper-folding-q07', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-mirror-paper-folding', 'In a mirror, a clock shows 3:00. What time does the actual clock show?', '3:00', '9:00', '6:00', '12:00', 'B', 'Mirror time = 12:00 − shown time, so 12:00 − 3:00 = 9:00.', 6),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-mirror-paper-folding-q08', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-mirror-paper-folding', 'For mirror images you should:', 'Swap top and bottom', 'Rotate 90 degrees', 'Swap left and right', 'Do nothing', 'C', 'A mirror image swaps left and right while keeping top and bottom fixed.', 7),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-statement-conclusion-q01', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-statement-conclusion', 'Statement: "Smoking is injurious to health." Conclusion: "One should avoid smoking." Does it follow?', 'Follows', 'Does not follow', 'Cannot be determined', 'Partially follows', 'A', 'The recommendation to avoid smoking is implied by the statement, so it follows.', 0),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-statement-conclusion-q02', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-statement-conclusion', 'In statement-conclusion questions, conclusions needing outside facts:', 'Do not follow', 'Always follow', 'Follow if true in reality', 'Follow sometimes', 'A', 'Conclusions requiring outside facts do not follow from the statement alone.', 1),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-statement-conclusion-q03', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-statement-conclusion', 'A conclusion that merely restates the statement:', 'Does not follow', 'Needs more data', 'Is always wrong', 'Follows trivially', 'D', 'A restatement of the statement follows trivially.', 2),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-statement-conclusion-q04', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-statement-conclusion', 'An assumption is best described as something that is:', 'Drawn after the statement', 'A restatement', 'An outside fact', 'Taken for granted to make the statement meaningful', 'D', 'An assumption is taken for granted to make the statement meaningful.', 3),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-statement-conclusion-q05', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-statement-conclusion', 'When evaluating two conclusions, you should test them:', 'Independently', 'Together as one', 'Using real-world data', 'By guessing', 'A', 'Each conclusion is tested independently before selecting the combined option.', 4),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-statement-conclusion-q06', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-statement-conclusion', 'Statement: "The library is open from 9 am to 5 pm." Conclusion: "The library is closed at 8 pm." Does it follow?', 'Cannot be determined', 'Does not follow', 'Follows', 'Partially follows', 'C', 'Since hours end at 5 pm, the library is closed at 8 pm, so it follows.', 5),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-statement-conclusion-q07', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-statement-conclusion', 'An overly strong, absolute conclusion drawn from a moderate statement:', 'Does not follow', 'Follows', 'Always follows', 'Is an assumption', 'A', 'An absolute claim beyond a moderate statement does not follow.', 6),
  ('ssc-cgl-reasoning-statement-conclusion-q08', 'ssc-cgl-reasoning-statement-conclusion', 'In these questions, the statement should be treated as:', 'Possibly false', 'One of many facts', 'The only available truth', 'Irrelevant', 'C', 'The statement is treated as the only truth available for the conclusion.', 7),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-synonyms-antonyms-q01', 'ssc-cgl-english-synonyms-antonyms', 'Choose the synonym of ABUNDANT:', 'Scarce', 'Plentiful', 'Empty', 'Rare', 'B', 'Abundant means existing in large quantity, that is, plentiful.', 0),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-synonyms-antonyms-q02', 'ssc-cgl-english-synonyms-antonyms', 'Choose the antonym of TRANSPARENT:', 'Clear', 'Opaque', 'Glassy', 'Bright', 'B', 'The opposite of transparent (see-through) is opaque.', 1),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-synonyms-antonyms-q03', 'ssc-cgl-english-synonyms-antonyms', 'Choose the synonym of CANDID:', 'Frank', 'Secretive', 'Rude', 'Shy', 'A', 'Candid means open and honest, that is, frank.', 2),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-synonyms-antonyms-q04', 'ssc-cgl-english-synonyms-antonyms', 'Choose the antonym of FRUGAL:', 'Extravagant', 'Economical', 'Thrifty', 'Careful', 'A', 'Frugal means sparing with money; its opposite is extravagant.', 3),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-synonyms-antonyms-q05', 'ssc-cgl-english-synonyms-antonyms', 'Choose the synonym of BENEVOLENT:', 'Kind', 'Cruel', 'Greedy', 'Harsh', 'A', 'Benevolent means well-meaning and kind.', 4),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-synonyms-antonyms-q06', 'ssc-cgl-english-synonyms-antonyms', 'Choose the antonym of OPTIMIST:', 'Dreamer', 'Pessimist', 'Realist', 'Believer', 'B', 'An optimist expects the best; the opposite is a pessimist.', 5),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-synonyms-antonyms-q07', 'ssc-cgl-english-synonyms-antonyms', 'Choose the synonym of LETHARGIC:', 'Sluggish', 'Energetic', 'Active', 'Quick', 'A', 'Lethargic means lacking energy, that is, sluggish.', 6),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-synonyms-antonyms-q08', 'ssc-cgl-english-synonyms-antonyms', 'Choose the antonym of AMATEUR:', 'Beginner', 'Novice', 'Professional', 'Learner', 'C', 'An amateur is a non-expert; the opposite is a professional.', 7),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-spotting-errors-q01', 'ssc-cgl-english-spotting-errors', 'Identify the error: "One of the boys (A)/ are absent (B)/ today (C)/ No error (D)."', 'B', 'A', 'C', 'D', 'A', '"One of the boys" is singular, so it should be "is absent".', 0),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-spotting-errors-q02', 'ssc-cgl-english-spotting-errors', 'Identify the error: "Each student (A)/ have submitted (B)/ the form (C)/ No error (D)."', 'A', 'D', 'C', 'B', 'D', '"Each" is singular, so it takes "has submitted".', 1),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-spotting-errors-q03', 'ssc-cgl-english-spotting-errors', 'Identify the error: "The furniture (A)/ in this room (B)/ are very old (C)/ No error (D)."', 'A', 'C', 'B', 'D', 'B', '"Furniture" is uncountable and takes a singular verb "is very old".', 2),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-spotting-errors-q04', 'ssc-cgl-english-spotting-errors', 'Identify the error: "She is (A)/ more better (B)/ than her sister (C)/ No error (D)."', 'A', 'D', 'C', 'B', 'D', '"More better" is a double comparative; it should be just "better".', 3),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-spotting-errors-q05', 'ssc-cgl-english-spotting-errors', 'Identify the error: "He returned (A)/ back home (B)/ late at night (C)/ No error (D)."', 'A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'B', '"Returned back" is redundant; "returned" alone is correct.', 4),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-spotting-errors-q06', 'ssc-cgl-english-spotting-errors', 'Identify the error: "He bought (A)/ a umbrella (B)/ from the market (C)/ No error (D)."', 'A', 'D', 'C', 'B', 'D', '"Umbrella" begins with a vowel sound, so it needs "an umbrella".', 5),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-spotting-errors-q07', 'ssc-cgl-english-spotting-errors', 'Identify the error: "The committee (A)/ comprises of (B)/ ten members (C)/ No error (D)."', 'B', 'A', 'C', 'D', 'A', '"Comprise" takes no preposition; "comprises of" should be "comprises".', 6),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-spotting-errors-q08', 'ssc-cgl-english-spotting-errors', 'Identify the error: "They have (A)/ completed the project (B)/ successfully (C)/ No error (D)."', 'A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'D', 'The sentence is grammatically correct, so the answer is No error.', 7),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-sentence-improvement-spelling-q01', 'ssc-cgl-english-sentence-improvement-spelling', 'Improve: "She has went to the market."', 'has gone', 'have went', 'had went', 'No improvement', 'A', 'The past participle of go is gone, so "has gone" is correct.', 0),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-sentence-improvement-spelling-q02', 'ssc-cgl-english-sentence-improvement-spelling', 'Choose the correctly spelt word:', 'Accomodate', 'Acommodate', 'Accommodate', 'Acomodate', 'C', 'Accommodate has two c''s and two m''s.', 1),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-sentence-improvement-spelling-q03', 'ssc-cgl-english-sentence-improvement-spelling', 'Improve: "Let us discuss about the plan."', 'discuss on', 'discuss about', 'discuss over', 'discuss', 'D', '"Discuss" takes no preposition, so "discuss the plan" is correct.', 2),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-sentence-improvement-spelling-q04', 'ssc-cgl-english-sentence-improvement-spelling', 'Choose the correctly spelt word:', 'Definitely', 'Definately', 'Definitly', 'Defenitely', 'A', 'Definitely is spelt with no a and ends in -itely.', 3),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-sentence-improvement-spelling-q05', 'ssc-cgl-english-sentence-improvement-spelling', 'Improve: "He gave me a free gift."', 'a free gift', 'a gift', 'an free gift', 'free of gift', 'B', 'A gift is already free, so "free gift" is redundant; "a gift" is correct.', 4),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-sentence-improvement-spelling-q06', 'ssc-cgl-english-sentence-improvement-spelling', 'Choose the correctly spelt word:', 'Embarass', 'Embaras', 'Embarrass', 'Embarras', 'C', 'Embarrass has two r''s and two s''s.', 5),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-sentence-improvement-spelling-q07', 'ssc-cgl-english-sentence-improvement-spelling', 'Improve: "She likes reading and to write."', 'reading and writing', 'reading and to write', 'to read and writing', 'read and write', 'A', 'Parallelism requires matching forms: "reading and writing".', 6),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-sentence-improvement-spelling-q08', 'ssc-cgl-english-sentence-improvement-spelling', 'Choose the word meaning paper goods (pens, paper):', 'Stationary', 'Stationry', 'Stationery', 'Stationarry', 'C', 'Stationery (with an e) means paper goods; stationary means still.', 7),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-reading-comprehension-q01', 'ssc-cgl-english-reading-comprehension', 'In RC, a factual question is best answered by:', 'Guessing', 'Prior knowledge', 'Locating the exact line in the passage', 'Choosing the longest option', 'C', 'Factual answers are found by locating the exact line in the passage.', 0),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-reading-comprehension-q02', 'ssc-cgl-english-reading-comprehension', 'An inference answer should:', 'Go far beyond the text', 'Stay one safe step beyond the text', 'Contradict the text', 'Ignore the text', 'B', 'A good inference stays one safe step beyond the passage.', 1),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-reading-comprehension-q03', 'ssc-cgl-english-reading-comprehension', 'Answers in reading comprehension should be based on:', 'The passage only', 'General knowledge', 'Personal opinion', 'Other passages', 'A', 'RC answers must come strictly from the passage, not prior knowledge.', 2),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-reading-comprehension-q04', 'ssc-cgl-english-reading-comprehension', 'Which word describes a neutral author tone?', 'Objective', 'Sceptical', 'Critical', 'Appreciative', 'A', 'Objective and descriptive indicate a neutral tone.', 3),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-reading-comprehension-q05', 'ssc-cgl-english-reading-comprehension', 'A common RC trap is choosing an option that is:', 'True in real life but unsupported by the passage', 'Supported by the passage', 'Directly quoted', 'The main idea', 'A', 'A trap is an option true in real life but not supported by the passage.', 4),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-reading-comprehension-q06', 'ssc-cgl-english-reading-comprehension', 'For vocabulary-in-context questions, the best method is to:', 'Use the dictionary meaning only', 'Pick the hardest word', 'Replace the word with each option and test the fit', 'Skip the question', 'C', 'Replace the word with each option and test the fit in context.', 5),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-reading-comprehension-q07', 'ssc-cgl-english-reading-comprehension', 'A title question asks for the passage''s:', 'Smallest detail', 'Hardest word', 'First sentence', 'Central idea', 'D', 'A title question asks for the central idea of the passage.', 6),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-reading-comprehension-q08', 'ssc-cgl-english-reading-comprehension', 'A good first step in RC is to:', 'Skim for the main idea', 'Memorise the passage', 'Read every option first', 'Answer without reading', 'A', 'Skim for the main idea first, then read the questions.', 7),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-active-passive-voice-q01', 'ssc-cgl-english-active-passive-voice', 'Change to passive: "She writes a letter."', 'A letter was written by her.', 'A letter is written by her.', 'A letter is being written by her.', 'A letter has written by her.', 'B', 'Simple present passive uses is/are + V3: A letter is written by her.', 0),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-active-passive-voice-q02', 'ssc-cgl-english-active-passive-voice', 'Change to passive: "He ate an apple."', 'An apple is eaten by him.', 'An apple has been eaten by him.', 'An apple was ate by him.', 'An apple was eaten by him.', 'D', 'Simple past passive uses was/were + V3: An apple was eaten by him.', 1),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-active-passive-voice-q03', 'ssc-cgl-english-active-passive-voice', 'Change to passive: "They are building a house."', 'A house is built by them.', 'A house is being built by them.', 'A house was being built by them.', 'A house has been built by them.', 'B', 'Present continuous passive uses is/are being + V3.', 2),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-active-passive-voice-q04', 'ssc-cgl-english-active-passive-voice', 'Change to passive: "She has completed the task."', 'The task is completed by her.', 'The task was completed by her.', 'The task has been completed by her.', 'The task had completed by her.', 'C', 'Present perfect passive uses has/have been + V3.', 3),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-active-passive-voice-q05', 'ssc-cgl-english-active-passive-voice', 'Change to passive: "He will finish the work."', 'The work was finished by him.', 'The work is finished by him.', 'The work will been finished by him.', 'The work will be finished by him.', 'D', 'Future simple passive uses will be + V3.', 4),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-active-passive-voice-q06', 'ssc-cgl-english-active-passive-voice', 'Change to passive: "You can solve this problem."', 'This problem can be solved by you.', 'This problem is solved by you.', 'This problem could be solved by you.', 'This problem can solved by you.', 'A', 'Modals stay: "can solve" becomes "can be solved".', 5),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-active-passive-voice-q07', 'ssc-cgl-english-active-passive-voice', 'In passive voice, the object of the active sentence becomes the:', 'Verb', 'Adverb', 'Subject', 'Agent', 'C', 'The object of the active sentence becomes the subject of the passive.', 6),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-active-passive-voice-q08', 'ssc-cgl-english-active-passive-voice', 'Change to passive: "The teacher praised the student."', 'The student is praised by the teacher.', 'The student praised by the teacher.', 'The student has praised by the teacher.', 'The student was praised by the teacher.', 'D', 'Simple past passive: The student was praised by the teacher.', 7),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-cloze-test-q01', 'ssc-cgl-english-cloze-test', 'He worked hard, ____ he failed the exam.', 'so', 'and', 'because', 'but', 'D', 'A contrast between effort and failure needs the connector "but".', 0),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-cloze-test-q02', 'ssc-cgl-english-cloze-test', 'She was tired, ____ she went to sleep early.', 'but', 'yet', 'although', 'so', 'D', 'A cause and result relationship needs "so".', 1),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-cloze-test-q03', 'ssc-cgl-english-cloze-test', 'Please pay ____ to the instructions.', 'notice', 'mind', 'care', 'attention', 'D', 'The fixed collocation is "pay attention".', 2),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-cloze-test-q04', 'ssc-cgl-english-cloze-test', 'We must ____ a decision quickly.', 'make', 'take', 'do', 'give', 'A', 'The collocation is "make a decision".', 3),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-cloze-test-q05', 'ssc-cgl-english-cloze-test', '____ it was raining, we went out.', 'Because', 'Although', 'So', 'Therefore', 'B', 'A contrast between rain and going out needs "Although".', 4),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-cloze-test-q06', 'ssc-cgl-english-cloze-test', 'The students must ____ their homework daily.', 'make', 'give', 'take', 'do', 'D', 'The collocation is "do homework".', 5),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-cloze-test-q07', 'ssc-cgl-english-cloze-test', 'He could not come ____ he was ill.', 'but', 'yet', 'although', 'because', 'D', 'A reason follows, so the cause connector "because" fits.', 6),
  ('ssc-cgl-english-cloze-test-q08', 'ssc-cgl-english-cloze-test', 'After filling all blanks in a cloze test, you should:', 'Submit immediately', 'Reread the full passage to confirm flow', 'Change every answer', 'Ignore the connectors', 'B', 'Reread the full passage to confirm it flows coherently.', 7),
