  ('gate-programming-ds-graph-algorithms-bfs-dfs-q10', 'gate-programming-ds-graph-algorithms-bfs-dfs', 'Depth-first search is naturally implemented with:', 'A stack (or recursion)', 'A queue', 'A priority queue', 'A hash map', 'A', 'DFS uses a stack, explicitly or via the recursion call stack.', 9),
  ('gate-programming-ds-graph-algorithms-bfs-dfs-q11', 'gate-programming-ds-graph-algorithms-bfs-dfs', 'BFS from a source finds shortest paths in an:', 'Unweighted graph', 'Weighted graph with negatives', 'Any weighted graph', 'Directed acyclic graph only', 'A', 'BFS gives shortest paths by edge count, valid for unweighted graphs.', 10),
  ('gate-programming-ds-graph-algorithms-bfs-dfs-q12', 'gate-programming-ds-graph-algorithms-bfs-dfs', 'The time complexity of BFS or DFS on a graph with V vertices and E edges is:', 'O(V + E)', 'O(V²)', 'O(E log V)', 'O(V·E)', 'A', 'Each vertex and edge is processed a constant number of times, giving O(V + E).', 11),
  ('gate-programming-ds-dynamic-programming-q09', 'gate-programming-ds-dynamic-programming', 'The two key properties enabling dynamic programming are optimal substructure and:', 'Overlapping subproblems', 'Random access', 'Sorted input', 'Constant space', 'A', 'DP reuses solutions to overlapping subproblems that combine into an optimal whole.', 8),
  ('gate-programming-ds-dynamic-programming-q10', 'gate-programming-ds-dynamic-programming', 'Storing the results of subproblems to avoid recomputation is called:', 'Memoization', 'Recursion', 'Hashing', 'Backtracking', 'A', 'Memoization caches subproblem results for reuse in top-down DP.', 9),
  ('gate-programming-ds-dynamic-programming-q11', 'gate-programming-ds-dynamic-programming', 'Computing the nth Fibonacci number with dynamic programming takes:', 'O(n) time', 'O(2ⁿ) time', 'O(log n) time', 'O(n²) time', 'A', 'DP computes each Fibonacci value once, in linear O(n) time.', 10),
  ('gate-programming-ds-dynamic-programming-q12', 'gate-programming-ds-dynamic-programming', 'The Longest Common Subsequence (LCS) problem is solved efficiently by:', 'Dynamic programming', 'A greedy algorithm', 'Binary search', 'Hashing', 'A', 'LCS uses a DP table over prefixes of the two sequences.', 11),
  ('gate-computer-organization-number-systems-boolean-algebra-q09', 'gate-computer-organization-number-systems-boolean-algebra', 'The binary number 1010 equals in decimal:', '10', '8', '12', '6', 'A', '1010₂ = 8 + 0 + 2 + 0 = 10.', 8),
  ('gate-computer-organization-number-systems-boolean-algebra-q10', 'gate-computer-organization-number-systems-boolean-algebra', 'The hexadecimal digit F equals in decimal:', '15', '16', '14', '10', 'A', 'Hex digits run 0–F, where F = 15.', 9),
  ('gate-computer-organization-number-systems-boolean-algebra-q11', 'gate-computer-organization-number-systems-boolean-algebra', 'By De Morgan''s law, (A·B)'' equals:', 'A'' + B''', 'A'' · B''', 'A + B', 'A · B', 'A', 'De Morgan: the complement of a product is the sum of the complements.', 10),
  ('gate-computer-organization-number-systems-boolean-algebra-q12', 'gate-computer-organization-number-systems-boolean-algebra', 'The decimal number 8 in binary is:', '1000', '0100', '1100', '0010', 'A', '8 = 2³, which is 1000 in binary.', 11),
  ('gate-computer-organization-cpu-instruction-pipelining-q09', 'gate-computer-organization-cpu-instruction-pipelining', 'Pipelining primarily improves a processor''s:', 'Instruction throughput', 'Clock voltage', 'Memory size', 'Cache size', 'A', 'Overlapping instruction stages raises throughput (instructions completed per unit time).', 8),
  ('gate-computer-organization-cpu-instruction-pipelining-q10', 'gate-computer-organization-cpu-instruction-pipelining', 'A branch instruction can cause which type of pipeline hazard?', 'Control hazard', 'Structural hazard', 'Data hazard', 'No hazard', 'A', 'Branches create control hazards because the next instruction address is uncertain.', 9),
  ('gate-computer-organization-cpu-instruction-pipelining-q11', 'gate-computer-organization-cpu-instruction-pipelining', 'The ideal speedup of a k-stage pipeline (no stalls) is:', 'k times', '2 times', 'k² times', 'log k times', 'A', 'In the ideal case a k-stage pipeline can approach a k-fold speedup.', 10),
  ('gate-computer-organization-cpu-instruction-pipelining-q12', 'gate-computer-organization-cpu-instruction-pipelining', 'The pipeline stage that retrieves the instruction from memory is the:', 'Instruction fetch (IF) stage', 'Execute stage', 'Write-back stage', 'Decode stage', 'A', 'The IF stage fetches the instruction pointed to by the program counter.', 11),
  ('gate-computer-organization-cache-memory-hierarchy-q09', 'gate-computer-organization-cache-memory-hierarchy', 'A cache hit occurs when:', 'The requested data is found in the cache', 'The cache is full', 'Data is written to disk', 'The TLB is flushed', 'A', 'A hit means the needed data is already present in the cache.', 8),
  ('gate-computer-organization-cache-memory-hierarchy-q10', 'gate-computer-organization-cache-memory-hierarchy', 'The tendency to re-access recently used data soon is called:', 'Temporal locality', 'Spatial locality', 'Fragmentation', 'Thrashing', 'A', 'Temporal locality: recently accessed items are likely to be accessed again soon.', 9),
  ('gate-computer-organization-cache-memory-hierarchy-q11', 'gate-computer-organization-cache-memory-hierarchy', 'In direct-mapped cache, a memory block maps to:', 'Exactly one cache line', 'Any cache line', 'Two cache lines', 'No cache line', 'A', 'Direct mapping assigns each block to a single predetermined cache line.', 10),
  ('gate-computer-organization-cache-memory-hierarchy-q12', 'gate-computer-organization-cache-memory-hierarchy', 'Compared with main memory, cache memory is:', 'Faster but smaller', 'Slower but larger', 'Slower and smaller', 'Identical in speed', 'A', 'Cache trades capacity for speed — it is faster and smaller than main memory.', 11),
  ('gate-computer-organization-io-interrupts-q09', 'gate-computer-organization-io-interrupts', 'An interrupt is a signal that:', 'Temporarily halts the CPU to service an event', 'Speeds up the clock', 'Erases memory', 'Increases cache size', 'A', 'An interrupt diverts the CPU to handle an event, then resumes the interrupted work.', 8),
  ('gate-computer-organization-io-interrupts-q10', 'gate-computer-organization-io-interrupts', 'Direct Memory Access (DMA) allows I/O devices to:', 'Transfer data to memory without CPU intervention', 'Run the operating system', 'Increase clock speed', 'Replace the cache', 'A', 'DMA moves data between device and memory directly, freeing the CPU.', 9),
  ('gate-computer-organization-io-interrupts-q11', 'gate-computer-organization-io-interrupts', 'Compared with interrupt-driven I/O, polling is generally:', 'Less efficient (the CPU wastes cycles checking)', 'Always faster', 'Free of CPU use', 'Interrupt-based', 'A', 'Polling repeatedly checks device status, wasting CPU cycles that interrupts avoid.', 10),
  ('gate-computer-organization-io-interrupts-q12', 'gate-computer-organization-io-interrupts', 'The routine executed in response to an interrupt is the:', 'Interrupt service routine (ISR)', 'Boot loader', 'Scheduler', 'Compiler', 'A', 'The ISR (interrupt handler) runs to service the interrupting event.', 11),
  ('gate-discrete-math-propositional-predicate-logic-q09', 'gate-discrete-math-propositional-predicate-logic', 'The implication P → Q is false only when:', 'P is true and Q is false', 'P is false and Q is true', 'Both are true', 'Both are false', 'A', 'An implication fails exactly when the antecedent is true but the consequent is false.', 8),
  ('gate-discrete-math-propositional-predicate-logic-q10', 'gate-discrete-math-propositional-predicate-logic', 'The negation of ''∀x P(x)'' is:', '∃x ¬P(x)', '∀x ¬P(x)', '∃x P(x)', '¬∃x P(x)', 'A', 'Negating a universal gives an existential of the negation: ∃x ¬P(x).', 9),
  ('gate-discrete-math-propositional-predicate-logic-q11', 'gate-discrete-math-propositional-predicate-logic', 'The expression P ∨ ¬P is a:', 'Tautology', 'Contradiction', 'Contingency', 'Predicate', 'A', 'P OR NOT P is always true — a tautology (law of excluded middle).', 10),
  ('gate-discrete-math-propositional-predicate-logic-q12', 'gate-discrete-math-propositional-predicate-logic', 'The contrapositive of P → Q is:', '¬Q → ¬P', 'Q → P', '¬P → ¬Q', 'P → ¬Q', 'A', 'The contrapositive ¬Q → ¬P is logically equivalent to P → Q.', 11),
  ('gate-discrete-math-set-theory-relations-q09', 'gate-discrete-math-set-theory-relations', 'The power set of a set with 3 elements has how many elements?', '8', '6', '3', '9', 'A', 'The power set has 2³ = 8 subsets.', 8),
  ('gate-discrete-math-set-theory-relations-q10', 'gate-discrete-math-set-theory-relations', 'The identity A ∩ (B ∪ C) = (A ∩ B) ∪ (A ∩ C) is the:', 'Distributive law', 'Commutative law', 'Associative law', 'De Morgan''s law', 'A', 'Intersection distributes over union — a distributive law.', 9),
  ('gate-discrete-math-set-theory-relations-q11', 'gate-discrete-math-set-theory-relations', 'Two sets that share no common elements are:', 'Disjoint', 'Equal', 'Subsets', 'Universal', 'A', 'Sets with empty intersection are disjoint.', 10),
  ('gate-discrete-math-set-theory-relations-q12', 'gate-discrete-math-set-theory-relations', 'If |A| = 3 and |B| = 4, the cardinality of A × B is:', '12', '7', '9', '16', 'A', 'The Cartesian product has |A| × |B| = 3 × 4 = 12 ordered pairs.', 11),
  ('gate-discrete-math-group-theory-algebra-q09', 'gate-discrete-math-group-theory-algebra', 'The four axioms a group must satisfy are closure, associativity, identity, and:', 'Existence of inverses', 'Commutativity', 'Distributivity', 'Density', 'A', 'A group needs closure, associativity, an identity element, and inverses for every element.', 8),
  ('gate-discrete-math-group-theory-algebra-q10', 'gate-discrete-math-group-theory-algebra', 'A group whose operation is commutative is called:', 'Abelian', 'Cyclic only', 'Trivial', 'Simple', 'A', 'An abelian (commutative) group satisfies a·b = b·a for all elements.', 9),
  ('gate-discrete-math-group-theory-algebra-q11', 'gate-discrete-math-group-theory-algebra', 'The identity element of the group of integers under addition is:', '0', '1', '−1', 'None', 'A', 'Adding 0 leaves any integer unchanged, so 0 is the additive identity.', 10),
  ('gate-discrete-math-group-theory-algebra-q12', 'gate-discrete-math-group-theory-algebra', 'The order of a finite group is:', 'The number of its elements', 'Its largest element', 'The identity element', 'The number of subgroups', 'A', 'A group''s order is its cardinality — the count of its elements.', 11),
  ('gate-discrete-math-counting-recurrences-q09', 'gate-discrete-math-counting-recurrences', 'The number of ways to arrange n distinct objects in a row is:', 'n!', '2ⁿ', 'n²', 'n', 'A', 'There are n! orderings (permutations) of n distinct objects.', 8),
  ('gate-discrete-math-counting-recurrences-q10', 'gate-discrete-math-counting-recurrences', 'The recurrence relation for the Fibonacci sequence is F(n) =:', 'F(n−1) + F(n−2)', 'F(n−1) × F(n−2)', '2·F(n−1)', 'F(n−1) + n', 'A', 'Each Fibonacci number is the sum of the two preceding ones.', 9),
  ('gate-discrete-math-counting-recurrences-q11', 'gate-discrete-math-counting-recurrences', 'By the Master Theorem, the recurrence T(n) = 2T(n/2) + O(n) solves to:', 'O(n log n)', 'O(n)', 'O(n²)', 'O(log n)', 'A', 'This is the classic Merge Sort recurrence, giving Θ(n log n).', 10),
  ('gate-discrete-math-counting-recurrences-q12', 'gate-discrete-math-counting-recurrences', 'By the addition (sum) rule, if a task can be done in m ways OR n mutually exclusive ways, the total is:', 'm + n', 'm × n', 'm − n', 'mⁿ', 'A', 'For mutually exclusive alternatives the counts add: m + n.', 11),
  ('gate-computer-organization-addressing-modes-instruction-formats-q01', 'gate-computer-organization-addressing-modes-instruction-formats', 'In immediate addressing mode, the operand is:', 'A constant given within the instruction', 'At a memory address', 'In a register', 'At the address stored in a register', 'A', 'Immediate mode encodes the operand value directly in the instruction.', 0),
  ('gate-computer-organization-addressing-modes-instruction-formats-q02', 'gate-computer-organization-addressing-modes-instruction-formats', 'In direct (absolute) addressing, the instruction contains:', 'The memory address of the operand', 'The operand value itself', 'A register name', 'An offset from the PC', 'A', 'Direct addressing gives the actual memory address where the operand resides.', 1),
  ('gate-computer-organization-addressing-modes-instruction-formats-q03', 'gate-computer-organization-addressing-modes-instruction-formats', 'In indirect addressing, the instruction gives:', 'The address of the address of the operand', 'The operand directly', 'A constant', 'A register value', 'A', 'Indirect mode holds a pointer: the given address stores the operand''s address.', 2),
  ('gate-computer-organization-addressing-modes-instruction-formats-q04', 'gate-computer-organization-addressing-modes-instruction-formats', 'Branch instructions commonly use which addressing mode?', 'PC-relative', 'Immediate', 'Register', 'Direct only', 'A', 'PC-relative addressing computes the target as the program counter plus a signed offset.', 3),
  ('gate-computer-organization-addressing-modes-instruction-formats-q05', 'gate-computer-organization-addressing-modes-instruction-formats', 'A stack machine uses which instruction format?', 'Zero-address', 'Three-address', 'Two-address', 'One-address', 'A', 'Stack (0-address) instructions operate implicitly on the top of the stack via push/pop.', 4),
  ('gate-computer-organization-addressing-modes-instruction-formats-q06', 'gate-computer-organization-addressing-modes-instruction-formats', 'In register addressing mode, the operand is located in:', 'A CPU register', 'Main memory', 'The instruction itself', 'The stack', 'A', 'Register mode names a CPU register that holds the operand, giving fast access.', 5),
  ('gate-computer-organization-addressing-modes-instruction-formats-q07', 'gate-computer-organization-addressing-modes-instruction-formats', 'Indexed addressing computes the operand address as:', 'Base address + index register', 'PC + opcode', 'A constant only', 'A register value only', 'A', 'Indexed addressing adds an index register to a base address, useful for arrays.', 6),
  ('gate-computer-organization-addressing-modes-instruction-formats-q08', 'gate-computer-organization-addressing-modes-instruction-formats', 'A RISC architecture typically favours:', 'Fixed-length, simple instructions with load/store memory access', 'Variable-length complex instructions', 'Only stack operations', 'No registers', 'A', 'RISC uses a small set of fixed-length instructions and accesses memory only via load/store.', 7),
  ('gate-computer-organization-addressing-modes-instruction-formats-q09', 'gate-computer-organization-addressing-modes-instruction-formats', 'The part of an instruction that specifies the operation to perform is the:', 'Opcode', 'Operand', 'Address field', 'Flag', 'A', 'The opcode (operation code) tells the CPU which operation to execute.', 8),
  ('gate-computer-organization-addressing-modes-instruction-formats-q10', 'gate-computer-organization-addressing-modes-instruction-formats', 'Register indirect addressing means the operand address is held in:', 'A register', 'The opcode', 'The instruction''s constant field', 'The program counter', 'A', 'In register indirect mode, a register contains the memory address of the operand.', 9),
  ('gate-computer-organization-addressing-modes-instruction-formats-q11', 'gate-computer-organization-addressing-modes-instruction-formats', 'A one-address instruction format implies the use of a(n):', 'Accumulator', 'Stack only', 'Three operands', 'No operands', 'A', 'One-address instructions use an implied accumulator as the second operand and destination.', 10),
  ('gate-computer-organization-addressing-modes-instruction-formats-q12', 'gate-computer-organization-addressing-modes-instruction-formats', 'Compared with RISC, CISC instructions are generally:', 'Variable-length and more complex', 'Fixed-length and simple', 'Always 0-address', 'Register-only', 'A', 'CISC has variable-length, complex instructions that may perform multiple operations.', 11),
  ('gate-computer-organization-computer-arithmetic-q01', 'gate-computer-organization-computer-arithmetic', 'Signed integers in most computers are represented using:', 'Two''s complement', 'Sign-magnitude only', 'BCD', 'Gray code', 'A', 'Two''s complement lets one adder handle both addition and subtraction with a single zero.', 0),
  ('gate-computer-organization-computer-arithmetic-q02', 'gate-computer-organization-computer-arithmetic', 'The two''s complement of a binary number is obtained by:', 'Inverting all bits and adding 1', 'Adding 1 only', 'Inverting all bits only', 'Shifting right', 'A', 'Two''s complement = one''s complement (bit inversion) plus 1.', 1),
  ('gate-computer-organization-computer-arithmetic-q03', 'gate-computer-organization-computer-arithmetic', 'A full adder adds:', 'Two bits plus a carry-in', 'Two bits only', 'Three carry bits', 'Four bits', 'A', 'A full adder sums two input bits and a carry-in, producing a sum and carry-out.', 2),
  ('gate-computer-organization-computer-arithmetic-q04', 'gate-computer-organization-computer-arithmetic', 'Booth''s algorithm is used for:', 'Signed binary multiplication', 'Division only', 'Sorting', 'Floating-point addition', 'A', 'Booth''s algorithm efficiently multiplies signed numbers in two''s complement form.', 3),
  ('gate-computer-organization-computer-arithmetic-q05', 'gate-computer-organization-computer-arithmetic', 'An IEEE 754 single-precision float uses how many bits in total?', '32', '16', '64', '8', 'A', 'Single precision is 32 bits: 1 sign, 8 exponent, 23 mantissa.', 4),
  ('gate-computer-organization-computer-arithmetic-q06', 'gate-computer-organization-computer-arithmetic', 'In IEEE 754 single precision, the exponent field is:', '8 bits', '23 bits', '11 bits', '1 bit', 'A', 'Single precision allots 8 bits to the biased exponent.', 5),
  ('gate-computer-organization-computer-arithmetic-q07', 'gate-computer-organization-computer-arithmetic', 'The exponent in IEEE 754 is stored in:', 'Biased (excess) form', 'Two''s complement', 'Sign-magnitude', 'BCD', 'A', 'IEEE 754 stores the exponent with a bias (127 for single precision).', 6),
  ('gate-computer-organization-computer-arithmetic-q08', 'gate-computer-organization-computer-arithmetic', 'Arithmetic overflow occurs when:', 'A result exceeds the representable range', 'A register is read', 'Two zeros are added', 'An instruction is fetched', 'A', 'Overflow happens when the true result cannot fit in the available bits.', 7),
  ('gate-computer-organization-computer-arithmetic-q09', 'gate-computer-organization-computer-arithmetic', 'An IEEE 754 double-precision float uses how many bits?', '64', '32', '128', '16', 'A', 'Double precision is 64 bits: 1 sign, 11 exponent, 52 mantissa.', 8),
  ('gate-computer-organization-computer-arithmetic-q10', 'gate-computer-organization-computer-arithmetic', 'A half adder produces a sum and a:', 'Carry-out', 'Borrow-in', 'Quotient', 'Remainder', 'A', 'A half adder outputs the sum and carry of two input bits (no carry-in).', 9),
  ('gate-computer-organization-computer-arithmetic-q11', 'gate-computer-organization-computer-arithmetic', 'In normalised IEEE 754 form, the mantissa has an implicit leading:', '1', '0', 'sign bit', 'exponent', 'A', 'Normalised numbers assume a hidden leading 1 before the stored fraction bits.', 10),
  ('gate-computer-organization-computer-arithmetic-q12', 'gate-computer-organization-computer-arithmetic', 'Subtraction in two''s complement is performed by:', 'Adding the two''s complement of the subtrahend', 'A separate subtractor circuit only', 'Shifting left', 'Inverting the result', 'A', 'A − B is computed as A + (two''s complement of B), reusing the adder.', 11),
  ('gate-discrete-math-lattices-boolean-algebra-q01', 'gate-discrete-math-lattices-boolean-algebra', 'A lattice is a poset in which every pair of elements has a:', 'Least upper bound and a greatest lower bound', 'Unique complement only', 'Cycle', 'Maximum degree', 'A', 'A lattice requires both a join (LUB) and a meet (GLB) for every pair of elements.', 0),
  ('gate-discrete-math-lattices-boolean-algebra-q02', 'gate-discrete-math-lattices-boolean-algebra', 'The least upper bound of two elements in a lattice is called the:', 'Join', 'Meet', 'Complement', 'Identity', 'A', 'The join (∨) is the least upper bound; the meet (∧) is the greatest lower bound.', 1),
  ('gate-discrete-math-lattices-boolean-algebra-q03', 'gate-discrete-math-lattices-boolean-algebra', 'A Boolean algebra is a lattice that is:', 'Complemented and distributive', 'Only reflexive', 'Cyclic', 'Unbounded', 'A', 'A Boolean algebra is a complemented, distributive (and bounded) lattice.', 2),
  ('gate-discrete-math-lattices-boolean-algebra-q04', 'gate-discrete-math-lattices-boolean-algebra', 'A partial order relation is reflexive, antisymmetric, and:', 'Transitive', 'Symmetric', 'Irreflexive', 'Complete', 'A', 'A poset relation is reflexive, antisymmetric and transitive.', 3),
  ('gate-discrete-math-lattices-boolean-algebra-q05', 'gate-discrete-math-lattices-boolean-algebra', 'A poset is commonly visualised using a:', 'Hasse diagram', 'Truth table', 'Venn diagram only', 'Flowchart', 'A', 'A Hasse diagram depicts the covering relation of a partially ordered set.', 4),
  ('gate-discrete-math-lattices-boolean-algebra-q06', 'gate-discrete-math-lattices-boolean-algebra', 'In Boolean algebra, a ∨ a'' equals:', '1', '0', 'a', 'a''', 'A', 'The complement law gives a ∨ a'' = 1 (and a ∧ a'' = 0).', 5),
  ('gate-discrete-math-lattices-boolean-algebra-q07', 'gate-discrete-math-lattices-boolean-algebra', 'By De Morgan''s law, (a ∨ b)'' equals:', 'a'' ∧ b''', 'a'' ∨ b''', 'a ∧ b', 'a ∨ b', 'A', 'The complement of a join is the meet of the complements.', 6),
  ('gate-discrete-math-lattices-boolean-algebra-q08', 'gate-discrete-math-lattices-boolean-algebra', 'A bounded lattice has a greatest element and a:', 'Least element', 'Cycle', 'Complement only', 'Midpoint', 'A', 'A bounded lattice has both a top (1) and a bottom (0) element.', 7),
  ('gate-discrete-math-lattices-boolean-algebra-q09', 'gate-discrete-math-lattices-boolean-algebra', 'The greatest lower bound of two elements is the:', 'Meet', 'Join', 'Supremum', 'Complement', 'A', 'The meet (∧) is the greatest lower bound (infimum) of the two elements.', 8),
  ('gate-discrete-math-lattices-boolean-algebra-q10', 'gate-discrete-math-lattices-boolean-algebra', 'In Boolean algebra, a ∧ 1 equals:', 'a', '1', '0', 'a''', 'A', '1 is the identity for the meet operation, so a ∧ 1 = a.', 9),
  ('gate-discrete-math-lattices-boolean-algebra-q11', 'gate-discrete-math-lattices-boolean-algebra', 'Digital logic circuits are a direct application of:', 'Boolean algebra', 'Calculus', 'Graph colouring', 'Number theory', 'A', 'Logic gates implement Boolean operations, making Boolean algebra their foundation.', 10),
  ('gate-discrete-math-lattices-boolean-algebra-q12', 'gate-discrete-math-lattices-boolean-algebra', 'Antisymmetry of a partial order means if a ≤ b and b ≤ a then:', 'a = b', 'a < b', 'a ≠ b', 'a and b are incomparable', 'A', 'Antisymmetry forces a = b when both a ≤ b and b ≤ a hold.', 11),
  ('gate-discrete-math-number-theory-modular-q01', 'gate-discrete-math-number-theory-modular', 'Euclid''s algorithm computes the:', 'Greatest common divisor (GCD)', 'Least common multiple only', 'Prime factorisation', 'Square root', 'A', 'Euclid''s algorithm finds gcd(a,b) via gcd(b, a mod b) until the remainder is 0.', 0),
  ('gate-discrete-math-number-theory-modular-q02', 'gate-discrete-math-number-theory-modular', 'For positive integers a and b, gcd(a,b) × lcm(a,b) equals:', 'a × b', 'a + b', 'a − b', 'a / b', 'A', 'The product of the GCD and LCM equals the product of the two numbers.', 1),
  ('gate-discrete-math-number-theory-modular-q03', 'gate-discrete-math-number-theory-modular', 'The value of 17 mod 5 is:', '2', '3', '1', '0', 'A', '17 = 3×5 + 2, so the remainder is 2.', 2),
  ('gate-discrete-math-number-theory-modular-q04', 'gate-discrete-math-number-theory-modular', 'a ≡ b (mod n) means that n divides:', '(a − b)', '(a + b)', '(a × b)', 'n itself', 'A', 'Congruence mod n means n | (a − b), i.e., a and b share the same remainder.', 3),
  ('gate-discrete-math-number-theory-modular-q05', 'gate-discrete-math-number-theory-modular', 'A prime number has exactly how many positive divisors?', 'Two', 'One', 'Three', 'Infinitely many', 'A', 'A prime is divisible only by 1 and itself — exactly two divisors.', 4),
  ('gate-discrete-math-number-theory-modular-q06', 'gate-discrete-math-number-theory-modular', 'Fermat''s little theorem states that for a prime p and gcd(a,p)=1:', 'aᵖ⁻¹ ≡ 1 (mod p)', 'aᵖ ≡ 0 (mod p)', 'a ≡ p (mod 1)', 'aᵖ⁺¹ ≡ 1 (mod p)', 'A', 'Fermat''s little theorem: aᵖ⁻¹ ≡ 1 (mod p) when p is prime and a is coprime to p.', 5),
  ('gate-discrete-math-number-theory-modular-q07', 'gate-discrete-math-number-theory-modular', 'Euler''s totient φ(p) for a prime p equals:', 'p − 1', 'p', 'p + 1', '1', 'A', 'All integers from 1 to p−1 are coprime to a prime p, so φ(p) = p − 1.', 6),
  ('gate-discrete-math-number-theory-modular-q08', 'gate-discrete-math-number-theory-modular', '(7 + 8) mod 6 equals:', '3', '5', '1', '0', 'A', '15 mod 6 = 3 (since 15 = 2×6 + 3).', 7),
  ('gate-discrete-math-number-theory-modular-q09', 'gate-discrete-math-number-theory-modular', 'The GCD of 12 and 18 is:', '6', '3', '12', '36', 'A', 'The largest number dividing both 12 and 18 is 6.', 8),
  ('gate-discrete-math-number-theory-modular-q10', 'gate-discrete-math-number-theory-modular', 'Number theory results like Euler''s and Fermat''s theorems are fundamental to:', 'Public-key cryptography (e.g., RSA)', 'Sorting algorithms', 'Graph traversal', 'Cache design', 'A', 'RSA and related schemes rely on modular arithmetic and these number-theoretic results.', 9),
  ('gate-discrete-math-number-theory-modular-q11', 'gate-discrete-math-number-theory-modular', '(4 × 5) mod 7 equals:', '6', '5', '0', '1', 'A', '20 mod 7 = 6 (since 20 = 2×7 + 6).', 10),
  ('gate-discrete-math-number-theory-modular-q12', 'gate-discrete-math-number-theory-modular', 'Two integers are said to be coprime (relatively prime) when their GCD is:', '1', '0', '2', 'Their product', 'A', 'Coprime integers share no common factor other than 1, so gcd = 1.', 11),
  ('gate-programming-ds-trees-bst-q01', 'gate-programming-ds-trees-bst', 'In a binary search tree, for any node, the keys in its left subtree are:', 'Less than the node''s key', 'Greater than the node''s key', 'Equal to the node''s key', 'Unordered', 'A', 'A BST keeps left-subtree keys < node < right-subtree keys.', 0),
  ('gate-programming-ds-trees-bst-q02', 'gate-programming-ds-trees-bst', 'In-order traversal of a binary search tree gives the keys in:', 'Ascending sorted order', 'Descending order', 'Level order', 'Random order', 'A', 'Left–Root–Right traversal of a BST visits keys in ascending order.', 1),
  ('gate-programming-ds-trees-bst-q03', 'gate-programming-ds-trees-bst', 'Search, insert and delete in a BST take time proportional to the:', 'Height of the tree', 'Number of leaves only', 'Number of edges squared', 'Constant', 'A', 'These operations follow a root-to-node path, costing O(h) where h is the height.', 2),
  ('gate-programming-ds-trees-bst-q04', 'gate-programming-ds-trees-bst', 'In the worst case (a skewed BST), search takes:', 'O(n)', 'O(log n)', 'O(1)', 'O(n²)', 'A', 'A completely unbalanced BST degenerates into a list with height n, giving O(n) search.', 3),
  ('gate-programming-ds-trees-bst-q05', 'gate-programming-ds-trees-bst', 'An AVL tree maintains balance by keeping the balance factor of each node in:', '{−1, 0, +1}', '{0, 1, 2}', '{−2, +2}', 'Any range', 'A', 'An AVL tree keeps each node''s subtree-height difference within −1, 0 or +1 using rotations.', 4),
  ('gate-programming-ds-trees-bst-q06', 'gate-programming-ds-trees-bst', 'Pre-order traversal visits nodes in the order:', 'Root, Left, Right', 'Left, Root, Right', 'Left, Right, Root', 'Right, Root, Left', 'A', 'Pre-order processes the root first, then the left and right subtrees.', 5),
  ('gate-programming-ds-trees-bst-q07', 'gate-programming-ds-trees-bst', 'Level-order traversal of a tree is implemented using a:', 'Queue', 'Stack', 'Heap', 'Hash table', 'A', 'Breadth-first (level-order) traversal uses a queue to process nodes level by level.', 6),
  ('gate-programming-ds-trees-bst-q08', 'gate-programming-ds-trees-bst', 'A balanced BST of n nodes has height:', 'O(log n)', 'O(n)', 'O(n log n)', 'O(1)', 'A', 'Balancing keeps the height logarithmic, giving O(log n) operations.', 7),
  ('gate-programming-ds-trees-bst-q09', 'gate-programming-ds-trees-bst', 'A node with no children in a tree is called a:', 'Leaf', 'Root', 'Internal node', 'Sibling', 'A', 'A leaf (external node) has no children.', 8),
  ('gate-programming-ds-trees-bst-q10', 'gate-programming-ds-trees-bst', 'Post-order traversal visits nodes in the order:', 'Left, Right, Root', 'Root, Left, Right', 'Left, Root, Right', 'Root, Right, Left', 'A', 'Post-order processes both subtrees before the root — Left, Right, Root.', 9),
  ('gate-programming-ds-trees-bst-q11', 'gate-programming-ds-trees-bst', 'A red-black tree guarantees a height of:', 'O(log n)', 'O(n)', 'O(√n)', 'O(1)', 'A', 'Red-black colouring rules keep the tree approximately balanced with O(log n) height.', 10),
  ('gate-programming-ds-trees-bst-q12', 'gate-programming-ds-trees-bst', 'The maximum number of nodes in a binary tree of height h (root at height 0) is:', '2^(h+1) − 1', '2h', 'h²', '2^h', 'A', 'A perfect binary tree of height h has 2⁰ + 2¹ + … + 2ʰ = 2^(h+1) − 1 nodes.', 11),
  ('gate-programming-ds-searching-sorting-q01', 'gate-programming-ds-searching-sorting', 'Binary search requires that the input array be:', 'Sorted', 'Unsorted', 'A linked list', 'A tree', 'A', 'Binary search relies on order to halve the search space, so the array must be sorted.', 0),
  ('gate-programming-ds-searching-sorting-q02', 'gate-programming-ds-searching-sorting', 'The worst-case time complexity of linear search is:', 'O(n)', 'O(log n)', 'O(1)', 'O(n log n)', 'A', 'Linear search may scan all n elements in the worst case.', 1),
  ('gate-programming-ds-searching-sorting-q03', 'gate-programming-ds-searching-sorting', 'Merge Sort has a worst-case time complexity of:', 'O(n log n)', 'O(n²)', 'O(n)', 'O(log n)', 'A', 'Merge Sort always splits and merges in O(n log n) time.', 2),
  ('gate-programming-ds-searching-sorting-q04', 'gate-programming-ds-searching-sorting', 'Quick Sort''s worst-case time complexity is:', 'O(n²)', 'O(n log n)', 'O(n)', 'O(log n)', 'A', 'With unfavourable pivots Quick Sort degrades to O(n²), though its average is O(n log n).', 3),
  ('gate-programming-ds-searching-sorting-q05', 'gate-programming-ds-searching-sorting', 'A sorting algorithm is ''stable'' if it:', 'Preserves the relative order of equal keys', 'Uses no extra memory', 'Always runs in O(n)', 'Sorts in descending order', 'A', 'Stability means records with equal keys keep their original relative order.', 4),
  ('gate-programming-ds-searching-sorting-q06', 'gate-programming-ds-searching-sorting', 'Any comparison-based sorting algorithm requires at least:', 'Ω(n log n) comparisons', 'Ω(n) comparisons', 'Ω(log n) comparisons', 'Ω(n²) comparisons', 'A', 'The decision-tree lower bound for comparison sorts is Ω(n log n).', 5),
  ('gate-programming-ds-searching-sorting-q07', 'gate-programming-ds-searching-sorting', 'The average-case complexity of Quick Sort is:', 'O(n log n)', 'O(n²)', 'O(n)', 'O(log n)', 'A', 'With balanced partitions on average, Quick Sort runs in O(n log n).', 6),
  ('gate-programming-ds-searching-sorting-q08', 'gate-programming-ds-searching-sorting', 'Which of these is a non-comparison sort that can run in O(n)?', 'Counting sort', 'Merge sort', 'Quick sort', 'Heap sort', 'A', 'Counting sort (and radix sort) sort integer keys without comparisons, in O(n) for suitable ranges.', 7),
  ('gate-programming-ds-searching-sorting-q09', 'gate-programming-ds-searching-sorting', 'An in-place sorting algorithm uses extra space of:', 'O(1)', 'O(n)', 'O(n log n)', 'O(n²)', 'A', 'In-place algorithms use only a constant amount of auxiliary memory.', 8),
  ('gate-programming-ds-searching-sorting-q10', 'gate-programming-ds-searching-sorting', 'Insertion sort has a best-case time complexity (nearly sorted input) of:', 'O(n)', 'O(n²)', 'O(n log n)', 'O(log n)', 'A', 'On already (nearly) sorted data, insertion sort does O(n) work.', 9),
  ('gate-programming-ds-searching-sorting-q11', 'gate-programming-ds-searching-sorting', 'Heap Sort has a worst-case time complexity of:', 'O(n log n)', 'O(n²)', 'O(n)', 'O(log n)', 'A', 'Heap Sort builds a heap and extracts elements in O(n log n) worst case.', 10),
  ('gate-programming-ds-searching-sorting-q12', 'gate-programming-ds-searching-sorting', 'Binary search on a sorted array of n elements runs in:', 'O(log n)', 'O(n)', 'O(1)', 'O(n log n)', 'A', 'Halving the range each step gives logarithmic time.', 11),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-reading-comprehension-q01', 'gate-general-aptitude-reading-comprehension', 'Passage: ''Renewable sources such as solar and wind reduce carbon emissions.'' According to the passage, solar and wind reduce:', 'Carbon emissions', 'Rainfall', 'Population', 'Wind speed', 'A', 'The passage states these sources reduce carbon emissions.', 0),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-reading-comprehension-q02', 'gate-general-aptitude-reading-comprehension', 'The ''main idea'' of a passage is:', 'The central point the author conveys', 'The last sentence only', 'The longest word', 'A minor detail', 'A', 'The main idea is the overall point the author is making.', 1),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-reading-comprehension-q03', 'gate-general-aptitude-reading-comprehension', 'To ''infer'' from a passage means to:', 'Draw a conclusion supported by the text', 'Copy a sentence', 'Ignore the passage', 'Count the words', 'A', 'An inference is a conclusion the text supports without stating it outright.', 2),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-reading-comprehension-q04', 'gate-general-aptitude-reading-comprehension', 'In ''The scientist''s meticulous work paid off,'' the word ''meticulous'' means:', 'Careful and thorough', 'Careless', 'Quick', 'Lazy', 'A', '''Meticulous'' means showing great attention to detail — careful and thorough.', 3),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-reading-comprehension-q05', 'gate-general-aptitude-reading-comprehension', 'Passage: ''Despite the heavy rain, the match continued.'' This implies that:', 'The rain did not stop the match', 'The match was cancelled', 'It did not rain', 'The match never started', 'A', '''Despite the rain, the match continued'' means the rain did not prevent play.', 4),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-reading-comprehension-q06', 'gate-general-aptitude-reading-comprehension', 'The ''tone'' of a passage refers to the author''s:', 'Attitude toward the subject', 'Handwriting', 'Vocabulary size', 'Word count', 'A', 'Tone is the author''s attitude or stance conveyed through the writing.', 5),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-reading-comprehension-q07', 'gate-general-aptitude-reading-comprehension', 'A synonym for ''enormous'' is:', 'Huge', 'Tiny', 'Narrow', 'Light', 'A', '''Enormous'' means very large — huge.', 6),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-reading-comprehension-q08', 'gate-general-aptitude-reading-comprehension', 'In a passage, the word ''therefore'' usually signals:', 'A conclusion or result', 'A contradiction', 'An example', 'A question', 'A', '''Therefore'' introduces a conclusion drawn from the preceding statements.', 7),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-reading-comprehension-q09', 'gate-general-aptitude-reading-comprehension', 'In ''She was elated at the good news,'' ''elated'' means:', 'Very happy', 'Confused', 'Angry', 'Bored', 'A', '''Elated'' means extremely happy or joyful.', 8),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-reading-comprehension-q10', 'gate-general-aptitude-reading-comprehension', 'The word ''however'' in a sentence typically indicates:', 'A contrast', 'A conclusion', 'Agreement', 'An example', 'A', '''However'' signals a contrast with what was said before.', 9),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-reading-comprehension-q11', 'gate-general-aptitude-reading-comprehension', 'Supporting details in a passage serve to:', 'Back up the main idea', 'Change the topic', 'End the passage', 'Confuse the reader', 'A', 'Supporting details provide evidence and elaboration for the main idea.', 10),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-reading-comprehension-q12', 'gate-general-aptitude-reading-comprehension', 'The best answer to a comprehension question is one that is:', 'Supported by the passage', 'Based on outside opinion', 'The longest option', 'Unrelated to the text', 'A', 'Answers must be grounded in what the passage actually says.', 11),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-mensuration-geometry-q01', 'gate-general-aptitude-mensuration-geometry', 'The area of a rectangle 8 cm by 5 cm is:', '40 cm²', '13 cm²', '26 cm²', '45 cm²', 'A', 'Area = length × breadth = 8 × 5 = 40 cm².', 0),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-mensuration-geometry-q02', 'gate-general-aptitude-mensuration-geometry', 'The perimeter of a square of side 6 cm is:', '24 cm', '36 cm', '12 cm', '30 cm', 'A', 'Perimeter = 4 × side = 4 × 6 = 24 cm.', 1),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-mensuration-geometry-q03', 'gate-general-aptitude-mensuration-geometry', 'The area of a circle of radius 7 cm (π = 22/7) is:', '154 cm²', '44 cm²', '49 cm²', '22 cm²', 'A', 'Area = πr² = 22/7 × 49 = 154 cm².', 2),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-mensuration-geometry-q04', 'gate-general-aptitude-mensuration-geometry', 'The volume of a cube of side 3 cm is:', '27 cm³', '9 cm³', '18 cm³', '12 cm³', 'A', 'Volume = side³ = 3³ = 27 cm³.', 3),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-mensuration-geometry-q05', 'gate-general-aptitude-mensuration-geometry', 'The area of a triangle with base 10 cm and height 6 cm is:', '30 cm²', '60 cm²', '16 cm²', '45 cm²', 'A', 'Area = ½ × base × height = ½ × 10 × 6 = 30 cm².', 4),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-mensuration-geometry-q06', 'gate-general-aptitude-mensuration-geometry', 'The circumference of a circle of radius 7 cm (π = 22/7) is:', '44 cm', '154 cm', '22 cm', '88 cm', 'A', 'Circumference = 2πr = 2 × 22/7 × 7 = 44 cm.', 5),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-mensuration-geometry-q07', 'gate-general-aptitude-mensuration-geometry', 'The volume of a cuboid 2 × 3 × 4 cm is:', '24 cm³', '9 cm³', '12 cm³', '26 cm³', 'A', 'Volume = l × b × h = 2 × 3 × 4 = 24 cm³.', 6),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-mensuration-geometry-q08', 'gate-general-aptitude-mensuration-geometry', 'The total surface area of a cube of side 2 cm is:', '24 cm²', '8 cm²', '12 cm²', '16 cm²', 'A', 'Surface area = 6 × side² = 6 × 4 = 24 cm².', 7),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-mensuration-geometry-q09', 'gate-general-aptitude-mensuration-geometry', 'The sum of the interior angles of a triangle is:', '180°', '360°', '90°', '270°', 'A', 'A triangle''s interior angles always sum to 180°.', 8),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-mensuration-geometry-q10', 'gate-general-aptitude-mensuration-geometry', 'The volume of a cylinder of radius 7 cm and height 10 cm (π = 22/7) is:', '1540 cm³', '440 cm³', '154 cm³', '770 cm³', 'A', 'Volume = πr²h = 22/7 × 49 × 10 = 1540 cm³.', 9),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-mensuration-geometry-q11', 'gate-general-aptitude-mensuration-geometry', 'The area of a square of side 9 cm is:', '81 cm²', '36 cm²', '18 cm²', '72 cm²', 'A', 'Area = side² = 9² = 81 cm².', 10),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-mensuration-geometry-q12', 'gate-general-aptitude-mensuration-geometry', 'The sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral is:', '360°', '180°', '270°', '540°', 'A', 'A quadrilateral''s interior angles sum to 360°.', 11),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-spatial-aptitude-q01', 'gate-general-aptitude-spatial-aptitude', 'All A are B, and all B are C. Therefore:', 'All A are C', 'All C are A', 'No A is C', 'Some A are not C', 'A', 'Chaining the two universals gives: all A are C.', 0),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-spatial-aptitude-q02', 'gate-general-aptitude-spatial-aptitude', 'If A is taller than B and B is taller than C, the tallest is:', 'A', 'B', 'C', 'Cannot say', 'A', 'By transitivity A > B > C, so A is the tallest.', 1),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-spatial-aptitude-q03', 'gate-general-aptitude-spatial-aptitude', 'A cube has how many vertices (corners)?', '8', '6', '12', '4', 'A', 'A cube has 8 vertices, 12 edges and 6 faces.', 2),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-spatial-aptitude-q04', 'gate-general-aptitude-spatial-aptitude', 'The mirror image of the lowercase letter ''b'' most resembles:', 'd', 'p', 'q', 'b', 'A', 'A plane mirror laterally inverts, turning ''b'' into a ''d''-like shape.', 3),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-spatial-aptitude-q05', 'gate-general-aptitude-spatial-aptitude', 'Facing east and turning 180°, you now face:', 'West', 'North', 'South', 'East', 'A', 'A half turn (180°) from east points west.', 4),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-spatial-aptitude-q06', 'gate-general-aptitude-spatial-aptitude', 'A cross-shaped net of six squares folds into a:', 'Cube', 'Pyramid', 'Cylinder', 'Sphere', 'A', 'Six squares in a cross net fold up to form a cube.', 5),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-spatial-aptitude-q07', 'gate-general-aptitude-spatial-aptitude', '''If it rains, the ground gets wet. It rained.'' Therefore:', 'The ground got wet', 'It did not rain', 'The ground stayed dry', 'Nothing follows', 'A', 'By modus ponens, the consequent follows: the ground got wet.', 6),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-spatial-aptitude-q08', 'gate-general-aptitude-spatial-aptitude', 'A 2 × 2 × 2 cube is made of how many unit cubes?', '8', '4', '6', '12', 'A', '2 × 2 × 2 = 8 unit cubes.', 7),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-spatial-aptitude-q09', 'gate-general-aptitude-spatial-aptitude', '''No X is Y'' validly converts to:', 'No Y is X', 'Some Y are X', 'All Y are X', 'All X are Y', 'A', 'A universal negative converts symmetrically: ''No X is Y'' ⇒ ''No Y is X''.', 8),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-spatial-aptitude-q10', 'gate-general-aptitude-spatial-aptitude', 'A water (surface) reflection inverts an image:', 'Top to bottom', 'Left to right', 'Not at all', 'Diagonally', 'A', 'A water reflection flips the image vertically (top-to-bottom).', 9),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-spatial-aptitude-q11', 'gate-general-aptitude-spatial-aptitude', 'If P is the son of Q and Q is the son of R, then R is P''s:', 'Grandfather', 'Father', 'Brother', 'Uncle', 'A', 'R is the father of P''s father, i.e., P''s grandfather.', 10),
  ('gate-general-aptitude-spatial-aptitude-q12', 'gate-general-aptitude-spatial-aptitude', 'Rotating a square by 90° about its centre produces:', 'A square in the same position', 'A triangle', 'A circle', 'A rectangle', 'A', 'A square has 4-fold rotational symmetry, so a 90° rotation looks identical.', 11),
  ('gate-computer-organization-sequential-circuits-q01', 'gate-computer-organization-sequential-circuits', 'How does a sequential circuit differ from a combinational circuit?', 'Its output depends on current inputs and stored past state', 'It has no inputs', 'Its output depends only on current inputs', 'It cannot use a clock', 'A', 'Sequential circuits have memory, so output depends on inputs plus stored state.', 0),
  ('gate-computer-organization-sequential-circuits-q02', 'gate-computer-organization-sequential-circuits', 'How many bits does a single flip-flop store?', '8 bits', '1 bit', '2 bits', '4 bits', 'B', 'A flip-flop is a one-bit memory element, storing exactly one bit.', 1),
  ('gate-computer-organization-sequential-circuits-q03', 'gate-computer-organization-sequential-circuits', 'Which flip-flop toggles its output when both inputs are 1 and has no forbidden state?', 'SR flip-flop', 'D flip-flop', 'JK flip-flop', 'Half adder', 'C', 'The JK flip-flop toggles on J=K=1 and removes the SR forbidden state.', 2),
  ('gate-computer-organization-sequential-circuits-q04', 'gate-computer-organization-sequential-circuits', 'For a NOR-based SR flip-flop, which input combination is forbidden?', 'S=0, R=0', 'S=0, R=1', 'S=1, R=0', 'S=1, R=1', 'D', 'S=R=1 is forbidden because it drives both outputs to the same level.', 3),
  ('gate-computer-organization-sequential-circuits-q05', 'gate-computer-organization-sequential-circuits', 'In a D flip-flop, the output Q after a clock edge equals:', 'The D input', 'The complement of the clock', 'Always 0', 'The previous Q inverted', 'A', 'A D flip-flop simply latches whatever value is on its D input.', 4),
  ('gate-computer-organization-sequential-circuits-q06', 'gate-computer-organization-sequential-circuits', 'A T flip-flop with T = 1 will:', 'Hold its state', 'Toggle its state each clock', 'Reset to 0', 'Enter a forbidden state', 'B', 'With T=1 a T flip-flop toggles (inverts) its output on every clock.', 5),
  ('gate-computer-organization-sequential-circuits-q07', 'gate-computer-organization-sequential-circuits', 'What is the key difference between a latch and a flip-flop?', 'A latch stores 8 bits', 'A flip-flop is edge-triggered while a latch is level-triggered', 'A flip-flop has no clock', 'There is no difference', 'B', 'Flip-flops change on a clock edge; latches are transparent while enabled.', 6),
  ('gate-computer-organization-sequential-circuits-q08', 'gate-computer-organization-sequential-circuits', 'How many distinct states can n flip-flops represent?', '2ⁿ', 'n', 'n²', '2n', 'A', 'Each flip-flop holds one bit, so n of them give 2ⁿ distinct states.', 7),
  ('gate-computer-organization-sequential-circuits-q09', 'gate-computer-organization-sequential-circuits', 'How many flip-flops are needed to store an 8-bit byte?', '1', '4', '8', '16', 'C', 'Each flip-flop stores one bit, so eight are needed for eight bits.', 8),
  ('gate-computer-organization-sequential-circuits-q10', 'gate-computer-organization-sequential-circuits', 'A register that moves its stored bits one position per clock pulse is a:', 'Shift register', 'Multiplexer', 'Decoder', 'Comparator', 'A', 'A shift register shifts its contents one bit per clock pulse.', 9),
  ('gate-computer-organization-sequential-circuits-q11', 'gate-computer-organization-sequential-circuits', 'How many flip-flops does a mod-8 (0 to 7) counter require?', '2', '3', '4', '8', 'B', '8 states need ⌈log₂8⌉ = 3 flip-flops since 2³ = 8.', 10),
  ('gate-computer-organization-sequential-circuits-q12', 'gate-computer-organization-sequential-circuits', 'In a ripple (asynchronous) counter, each flip-flop is clocked by:', 'A common global clock', 'The output of the preceding flip-flop', 'The power supply', 'An external decoder', 'B', 'In a ripple counter each stage is triggered by the previous stage''s output.', 11),
  ('gate-computer-organization-memory-organization-q01', 'gate-computer-organization-memory-organization', 'Compared with DRAM, SRAM is:', 'Slower and needs refreshing', 'Faster and needs no refresh', 'Non-volatile', 'Cheaper per bit', 'B', 'SRAM uses flip-flops, so it is faster and requires no refresh, but costs more.', 0),
  ('gate-computer-organization-memory-organization-q02', 'gate-computer-organization-memory-organization', 'In DRAM, each bit is stored using:', 'A capacitor (with a transistor)', 'Six transistors', 'A magnetic domain', 'An optical pit', 'A', 'DRAM stores each bit as charge on a capacitor, which must be refreshed.', 1),
  ('gate-computer-organization-memory-organization-q03', 'gate-computer-organization-memory-organization', 'With n address lines, how many distinct memory locations can be addressed?', 'n', '2n', '2ⁿ', 'n²', 'C', 'n address lines give 2ⁿ unique addressable locations.', 2),
  ('gate-computer-organization-memory-organization-q04', 'gate-computer-organization-memory-organization', 'How many address lines are needed to address 1 KB (1024 bytes) of memory?', '8', '10', '12', '1024', 'B', '1024 = 2¹⁰, so 10 address lines are required.', 3),
  ('gate-computer-organization-memory-organization-q05', 'gate-computer-organization-memory-organization', 'Which of the following memory types is volatile?', 'RAM', 'ROM', 'Flash', 'EEPROM', 'A', 'RAM loses its contents when power is removed; the others are non-volatile.', 4),
  ('gate-computer-organization-memory-organization-q06', 'gate-computer-organization-memory-organization', 'EEPROM can be erased:', 'Only by ultraviolet light', 'Electrically', 'Never once written', 'By formatting the disk', 'B', 'EEPROM stands for Electrically Erasable PROM — it erases electrically.', 5),
  ('gate-computer-organization-memory-organization-q07', 'gate-computer-organization-memory-organization', 'Flash memory is a type of:', 'SRAM', 'Volatile DRAM', 'Non-volatile EEPROM', 'Cache', 'C', 'Flash is a non-volatile, electrically erasable form of EEPROM.', 6),
  ('gate-computer-organization-memory-organization-q08', 'gate-computer-organization-memory-organization', 'On a magnetic disk, data is stored on concentric circles called:', 'Tracks', 'Pages', 'Frames', 'Registers', 'A', 'A disk surface is divided into concentric tracks, each split into sectors.', 7),
  ('gate-computer-organization-memory-organization-q09', 'gate-computer-organization-memory-organization', 'RAID 0 improves performance by using ____ but provides no redundancy.', 'Mirroring', 'Striping', 'Parity', 'Refreshing', 'B', 'RAID 0 stripes data across disks for speed but offers no fault tolerance.', 8),
  ('gate-computer-organization-memory-organization-q10', 'gate-computer-organization-memory-organization', 'RAID 1 provides redundancy by:', 'Distributing parity', 'Mirroring data on two disks', 'Striping without parity', 'Compressing data', 'B', 'RAID 1 keeps a full mirror copy of the data on a second disk.', 9),
  ('gate-computer-organization-memory-organization-q11', 'gate-computer-organization-memory-organization', 'RAID 5 achieves fault tolerance using:', 'Distributed parity', 'No redundancy', 'Triple mirroring', 'A single dedicated data disk', 'A', 'RAID 5 spreads parity across all disks, surviving one disk failure.', 10),
  ('gate-computer-organization-memory-organization-q12', 'gate-computer-organization-memory-organization', 'In the memory hierarchy, which is the fastest to access?', 'Secondary disk storage', 'Main memory (DRAM)', 'CPU registers', 'Optical disc', 'C', 'Registers sit closest to the CPU and are the fastest memory available.', 11),
  ('gate-discrete-math-functions-q01', 'gate-discrete-math-functions', 'A function that is both injective and surjective is called:', 'Constant', 'Bijective', 'Partial', 'Recursive', 'B', 'A function that is one-to-one and onto is called a bijection.', 0),
  ('gate-discrete-math-functions-q02', 'gate-discrete-math-functions', 'A function is injective (one-to-one) when:', 'Distinct inputs map to distinct outputs', 'Every codomain element is hit', 'It has no inverse', 'All inputs map to one output', 'A', 'Injective means f(x1)=f(x2) implies x1=x2, so distinct inputs differ in output.', 1),
  ('gate-discrete-math-functions-q03', 'gate-discrete-math-functions', 'A function is surjective (onto) when:', 'It is constant', 'Its domain is empty', 'Every codomain element is mapped by some input', 'No two inputs share an output', 'C', 'Surjective means every element of the codomain has a pre-image.', 2),
  ('gate-discrete-math-functions-q04', 'gate-discrete-math-functions', 'The number of functions from a set of size m to a set of size n is:', 'nᵐ', 'mⁿ', 'm × n', 'n!', 'A', 'Each of the m inputs independently chooses one of n outputs, giving nᵐ.', 3),
  ('gate-discrete-math-functions-q05', 'gate-discrete-math-functions', 'How many functions are there from a 3-element set to a 2-element set?', '6', '8', '9', '5', 'B', 'The count is nᵐ = 2³ = 8 functions.', 4),
  ('gate-discrete-math-functions-q06', 'gate-discrete-math-functions', 'The number of bijections from an n-element set to itself is:', '2ⁿ', 'n²', 'n!', 'nⁿ', 'C', 'Bijections on an n-set are the permutations, counted by n!.', 5),
  ('gate-discrete-math-functions-q07', 'gate-discrete-math-functions', 'A function f has an inverse function if and only if it is:', 'Constant', 'Bijective', 'Injective only', 'Surjective only', 'B', 'Only bijective functions are invertible, needing both one-to-one and onto.', 6),
  ('gate-discrete-math-functions-q08', 'gate-discrete-math-functions', 'The composition (g∘f)(x) is defined as:', 'g(f(x))', 'f(g(x))', 'g(x) × f(x)', 'g(x) + f(x)', 'A', 'Composition applies f first, then g, so (g∘f)(x) = g(f(x)).', 7),
  ('gate-discrete-math-functions-q09', 'gate-discrete-math-functions', 'How many one-to-one (injective) functions exist from a 2-element set to a 4-element set?', '8', '16', '12', '6', 'C', 'First input has 4 choices, second has 3, giving 4 × 3 = 12 injections.', 8),
  ('gate-discrete-math-functions-q10', 'gate-discrete-math-functions', 'The pigeonhole principle states that placing n+1 items into n boxes forces:', 'Every box to be empty', 'At least one box to hold two or more items', 'Exactly one item per box', 'All items into one box', 'B', 'With more items than boxes, some box must contain at least two items.', 9),
  ('gate-discrete-math-functions-q11', 'gate-discrete-math-functions', 'Among any 13 people, at least two must share a birth month because:', 'There are only 7 days in a week', '13 exceeds 12 months (pigeonhole)', 'Months have 30 days', 'Birthdays are random', 'B', '13 people into 12 months forces two into the same month by pigeonhole.', 10),
  ('gate-discrete-math-functions-q12', 'gate-discrete-math-functions', 'The identity function on a set maps every element to:', 'Zero', 'The first element', 'Itself', 'Its successor', 'C', 'The identity function returns each element unchanged, f(x) = x.', 11),
  ('gate-discrete-math-proof-techniques-q01', 'gate-discrete-math-proof-techniques', 'Mathematical induction is used to prove a statement holds for all:', 'Real numbers', 'Natural numbers (positive integers)', 'Irrational numbers', 'Complex numbers', 'B', 'Induction proves statements over the natural numbers step by step.', 0),
  ('gate-discrete-math-proof-techniques-q02', 'gate-discrete-math-proof-techniques', 'The two essential steps of a proof by induction are:', 'Guess and check', 'Base case and inductive step', 'Contradiction and negation', 'Expansion and factoring', 'B', 'Induction needs a base case and an inductive step to conclude.', 1),
  ('gate-discrete-math-proof-techniques-q03', 'gate-discrete-math-proof-techniques', 'In the inductive step you assume P(k) is true and then prove:', 'P(k+1)', 'P(0)', 'P(k−1)', 'P is false', 'A', 'The inductive step derives P(k+1) from the assumption P(k).', 2),
  ('gate-discrete-math-proof-techniques-q04', 'gate-discrete-math-proof-techniques', 'The best way to disprove the claim ''all prime numbers are odd'' is by:', 'Induction', 'A counterexample (the prime 2)', 'Contrapositive', 'Direct proof', 'B', 'The even prime 2 is a single counterexample that disproves the claim.', 3),
  ('gate-discrete-math-proof-techniques-q05', 'gate-discrete-math-proof-techniques', 'A proof by contradiction begins by assuming:', 'The statement is true', 'The negation of the statement', 'A random example', 'The base case', 'B', 'You assume the statement is false and derive a logical contradiction.', 4),
  ('gate-discrete-math-proof-techniques-q06', 'gate-discrete-math-proof-techniques', 'The classic proof that √2 is irrational uses proof by:', 'Induction', 'Contradiction', 'Enumeration', 'Counterexample', 'B', 'Assuming √2 = a/b in lowest terms leads to a contradiction.', 5),
  ('gate-discrete-math-proof-techniques-q07', 'gate-discrete-math-proof-techniques', 'Proving ''if not Q then not P'' in place of ''if P then Q'' is a proof by:', 'Contrapositive', 'Induction', 'Construction', 'Example', 'A', 'The contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original implication.', 6),
  ('gate-discrete-math-proof-techniques-q08', 'gate-discrete-math-proof-techniques', 'The contrapositive of ''if P then Q'' is:', 'if Q then P', 'if not P then not Q', 'if not Q then not P', 'P and not Q', 'C', 'The contrapositive of P→Q is ¬Q→¬P, which is equivalent to it.', 7),
  ('gate-discrete-math-proof-techniques-q09', 'gate-discrete-math-proof-techniques', 'A single counterexample is sufficient to:', 'Prove a universal statement', 'Disprove a universal statement', 'Prove an existential statement false', 'Complete an induction', 'B', 'One counterexample refutes a claim asserted for all cases.', 8),
  ('gate-discrete-math-proof-techniques-q10', 'gate-discrete-math-proof-techniques', 'Strong induction differs from ordinary induction because it assumes the statement holds for:', 'Only k', 'All values up to k, not just k', 'Only the base case', 'No previous values', 'B', 'Strong induction assumes P holds for all values up to k when proving P(k+1).', 9),
  ('gate-discrete-math-proof-techniques-q11', 'gate-discrete-math-proof-techniques', 'For proving P(n) for all n ≥ 1, the base case usually verifies:', 'P(0)', 'P(1)', 'P(n+1)', 'P(∞)', 'B', 'When the claim starts at n = 1, the base case checks P(1).', 10),
  ('gate-discrete-math-proof-techniques-q12', 'gate-discrete-math-proof-techniques', 'The formula 1 + 2 + ... + n = n(n+1)/2 is most naturally proved by:', 'Contradiction', 'Mathematical induction', 'A counterexample', 'Truth table', 'B', 'This summation identity is a standard proof by mathematical induction.', 11),
  ('gate-core-cs-process-scheduling-synchronization-q01', 'gate-core-cs-process-scheduling-synchronization', 'Turnaround time of a process is defined as:', 'Completion time − Arrival time', 'Burst time − Waiting time', 'Arrival time − Completion time', 'Waiting time + Response time', 'A', 'Turnaround time is the total time from arrival to completion.', 0),
  ('gate-core-cs-process-scheduling-synchronization-q02', 'gate-core-cs-process-scheduling-synchronization', 'Which scheduling algorithm gives the minimum average waiting time?', 'FCFS', 'Round Robin', 'Shortest Job First (SJF)', 'Priority (non-preemptive)', 'C', 'SJF is provably optimal for minimizing average waiting time.', 1),
  ('gate-core-cs-process-scheduling-synchronization-q03', 'gate-core-cs-process-scheduling-synchronization', 'The ''convoy effect'', where short processes wait behind a long one, occurs in:', 'SJF', 'FCFS', 'Round Robin', 'SRTF', 'B', 'FCFS suffers the convoy effect since a long job blocks the queue.', 2),
  ('gate-core-cs-process-scheduling-synchronization-q04', 'gate-core-cs-process-scheduling-synchronization', 'Waiting time is computed as:', 'Turnaround time − Burst time', 'Completion + Arrival', 'Burst + Turnaround', 'Arrival − Burst', 'A', 'Waiting time equals turnaround time minus the CPU burst time.', 3),
  ('gate-core-cs-process-scheduling-synchronization-q05', 'gate-core-cs-process-scheduling-synchronization', 'Round Robin scheduling is primarily characterized by:', 'A fixed time quantum per process', 'Running the shortest job first', 'Never preempting a process', 'Priority-based selection', 'A', 'Round Robin gives each process a fixed time quantum in turn.', 4),
  ('gate-core-cs-process-scheduling-synchronization-q06', 'gate-core-cs-process-scheduling-synchronization', 'Starvation in priority scheduling is best mitigated by:', 'Aging', 'Larger time quantum', 'Disabling interrupts', 'FCFS ordering', 'A', 'Aging gradually raises a waiting process''s priority, preventing starvation.', 5),
  ('gate-core-cs-process-scheduling-synchronization-q07', 'gate-core-cs-process-scheduling-synchronization', 'A semaphore''s two atomic operations are:', 'wait (P) and signal (V)', 'lock and commit', 'push and pop', 'read and write', 'A', 'Semaphores use atomic wait (P) and signal (V) operations.', 6),
  ('gate-core-cs-process-scheduling-synchronization-q08', 'gate-core-cs-process-scheduling-synchronization', 'Which is NOT a required property of a correct critical-section solution?', 'Mutual exclusion', 'Progress', 'Bounded waiting', 'Maximum CPU utilization', 'D', 'The three requirements are mutual exclusion, progress and bounded waiting.', 7),
  ('gate-core-cs-process-scheduling-synchronization-q09', 'gate-core-cs-process-scheduling-synchronization', 'A binary semaphore (mutex) allows how many processes in the critical section at once?', 'One', 'Two', 'Unlimited', 'Zero', 'A', 'A binary semaphore permits exactly one process in the critical section.', 8),
  ('gate-core-cs-process-scheduling-synchronization-q10', 'gate-core-cs-process-scheduling-synchronization', 'SRTF (Shortest Remaining Time First) is the preemptive version of:', 'FCFS', 'SJF', 'Round Robin', 'Priority aging', 'B', 'SRTF is preemptive SJF, switching when a shorter job arrives.', 9),
  ('gate-core-cs-process-scheduling-synchronization-q11', 'gate-core-cs-process-scheduling-synchronization', 'Response time is measured from arrival until:', 'The process first gets the CPU', 'The process completes', 'The process is swapped out', 'The next context switch', 'A', 'Response time is the delay until a process first receives the CPU.', 10),
  ('gate-core-cs-process-scheduling-synchronization-q12', 'gate-core-cs-process-scheduling-synchronization', 'Three processes with bursts 4, 2, 6 arrive together; under SJF what is the average waiting time?', '2.67', '4.00', '3.33', '6.00', 'A', 'SJF order 2,4,6 gives waits 0,2,6 → average (0+2+6)/3 = 2.67.', 11),
  ('gate-core-cs-deadlocks-memory-management-q01', 'gate-core-cs-deadlocks-memory-management', 'How many Coffman conditions must hold simultaneously for a deadlock?', 'Two', 'Three', 'Four', 'Five', 'C', 'All four conditions — mutual exclusion, hold and wait, no preemption, circular wait — must hold.', 0),
  ('gate-core-cs-deadlocks-memory-management-q02', 'gate-core-cs-deadlocks-memory-management', 'Which is NOT one of the four necessary conditions for deadlock?', 'Mutual exclusion', 'Circular wait', 'Preemption of resources', 'Hold and wait', 'C', '''No preemption'' is the condition; allowing preemption actually breaks deadlock.', 1),
  ('gate-core-cs-deadlocks-memory-management-q03', 'gate-core-cs-deadlocks-memory-management', 'The Banker''s algorithm is used for deadlock:', 'Avoidance', 'Detection only', 'Prevention', 'Recovery', 'A', 'The Banker''s algorithm avoids deadlock by keeping the system in a safe state.', 2),
  ('gate-core-cs-deadlocks-memory-management-q04', 'gate-core-cs-deadlocks-memory-management', 'In a resource-allocation graph with single-instance resources, a cycle means:', 'Deadlock exists', 'No deadlock', 'Starvation only', 'Nothing can be inferred', 'A', 'With one instance per resource, a cycle is a sufficient condition for deadlock.', 3),
  ('gate-core-cs-deadlocks-memory-management-q05', 'gate-core-cs-deadlocks-memory-management', 'Paging eliminates which type of fragmentation?', 'External fragmentation', 'Internal fragmentation', 'Both types', 'Neither type', 'A', 'Fixed-size frames remove external fragmentation, though internal remains.', 4),
  ('gate-core-cs-deadlocks-memory-management-q06', 'gate-core-cs-deadlocks-memory-management', 'A logical address in a paging system is split into:', 'Page number and offset', 'Segment and limit', 'Frame and base', 'Tag and index', 'A', 'A logical address is (page number, offset); the page table maps page to frame.', 5),
  ('gate-core-cs-deadlocks-memory-management-q07', 'gate-core-cs-deadlocks-memory-management', 'Belady''s anomaly can occur under which page-replacement policy?', 'FIFO', 'LRU', 'Optimal', 'LFU with stack property', 'A', 'FIFO can exhibit Belady''s anomaly; stack algorithms like LRU cannot.', 6),
  ('gate-core-cs-deadlocks-memory-management-q08', 'gate-core-cs-deadlocks-memory-management', 'Which page-replacement algorithm yields the theoretically minimum faults?', 'Optimal (replace the page used farthest in future)', 'FIFO', 'Random', 'Second-chance', 'A', 'The Optimal policy evicts the page used farthest in the future, minimizing faults.', 7),
  ('gate-core-cs-deadlocks-memory-management-q09', 'gate-core-cs-deadlocks-memory-management', 'If page-fault rate is p, effective access time is:', '(1 − p)·mem + p·fault_time', 'p·mem + fault_time', 'mem + p', '(1 + p)·mem', 'A', 'EAT weights normal access by (1−p) and fault service by p.', 8),
  ('gate-core-cs-deadlocks-memory-management-q10', 'gate-core-cs-deadlocks-memory-management', 'Segmentation differs from paging in that segments are:', 'Variable-sized logical units', 'Fixed-size frames', 'Always one byte', 'Stored only on disk', 'A', 'Segmentation uses variable-size, logically meaningful segments.', 9),
  ('gate-core-cs-deadlocks-memory-management-q11', 'gate-core-cs-deadlocks-memory-management', 'Breaking the ''hold and wait'' condition is an example of deadlock:', 'Prevention', 'Avoidance', 'Detection', 'Recovery', 'A', 'Denying one of the four conditions outright is deadlock prevention.', 10),
  ('gate-core-cs-deadlocks-memory-management-q12', 'gate-core-cs-deadlocks-memory-management', 'With 3 frames and reference string 1,2,3,4,1,2,5 under FIFO, the number of page faults is:', '6', '7', '5', '4', 'A', 'FIFO faults on 1,2,3,4,1,2,5 except the second-window hits give 6 faults total.', 11),
  ('gate-core-cs-database-normalization-transactions-q01', 'gate-core-cs-database-normalization-transactions', 'First Normal Form (1NF) requires that:', 'All attribute values are atomic', 'There are no transitive dependencies', 'Every determinant is a superkey', 'There are no partial dependencies', 'A', '1NF requires atomic (indivisible) attribute values.', 0),
  ('gate-core-cs-database-normalization-transactions-q02', 'gate-core-cs-database-normalization-transactions', '2NF removes which kind of dependency?', 'Partial dependency on part of a candidate key', 'Transitive dependency', 'Multivalued dependency', 'Join dependency', 'A', '2NF eliminates partial dependencies of non-prime attributes on a key.', 1),
  ('gate-core-cs-database-normalization-transactions-q03', 'gate-core-cs-database-normalization-transactions', '3NF eliminates:', 'Transitive dependency of non-prime attributes', 'All functional dependencies', 'Atomic values', 'Candidate keys', 'A', '3NF removes transitive dependencies of non-prime attributes on the key.', 2),
  ('gate-core-cs-database-normalization-transactions-q04', 'gate-core-cs-database-normalization-transactions', 'A relation is in BCNF if, for every functional dependency X→Y:', 'X is a superkey', 'Y is atomic', 'X is a single attribute', 'Y is a candidate key', 'A', 'BCNF requires the left side of every FD to be a superkey.', 3),
  ('gate-core-cs-database-normalization-transactions-q05', 'gate-core-cs-database-normalization-transactions', 'A prime attribute is one that:', 'Belongs to some candidate key', 'Is always numeric', 'Cannot be null', 'Is the primary key alone', 'A', 'A prime attribute is a member of at least one candidate key.', 4),
  ('gate-core-cs-database-normalization-transactions-q06', 'gate-core-cs-database-normalization-transactions', 'The ''I'' in ACID properties stands for:', 'Isolation', 'Integrity', 'Indexing', 'Inheritance', 'A', 'ACID = Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability.', 5),
  ('gate-core-cs-database-normalization-transactions-q07', 'gate-core-cs-database-normalization-transactions', 'A schedule is conflict-serializable if and only if its precedence graph is:', 'Acyclic', 'Complete', 'Connected', 'Bipartite', 'A', 'Conflict serializability holds exactly when the precedence graph is acyclic.', 6),
  ('gate-core-cs-database-normalization-transactions-q08', 'gate-core-cs-database-normalization-transactions', 'Two operations conflict when they access the same item and:', 'At least one is a write', 'Both are reads', 'Both are from the same transaction', 'They occur at the same time', 'A', 'A conflict needs two operations on one item where at least one writes.', 7),
  ('gate-core-cs-database-normalization-transactions-q09', 'gate-core-cs-database-normalization-transactions', 'Two-Phase Locking (2PL) guarantees:', 'Conflict serializability', 'Deadlock freedom', 'No lost updates only', 'Atomicity only', 'A', '2PL guarantees conflict-serializable schedules (though not deadlock freedom).', 8),
  ('gate-core-cs-database-normalization-transactions-q10', 'gate-core-cs-database-normalization-transactions', 'Strict 2PL additionally prevents:', 'Cascading rollbacks', 'Deadlocks', 'Partial dependencies', 'Internal fragmentation', 'A', 'Strict 2PL holds write locks until commit, avoiding cascading aborts.', 9),
  ('gate-core-cs-database-normalization-transactions-q11', 'gate-core-cs-database-normalization-transactions', 'A candidate key is a minimal set of attributes that:', 'Functionally determines all attributes', 'Contains only numeric fields', 'Is always a single column', 'Allows duplicate rows', 'A', 'A candidate key minimally determines every attribute of the relation.', 10),
  ('gate-core-cs-database-normalization-transactions-q12', 'gate-core-cs-database-normalization-transactions', 'Atomicity of a transaction guarantees that:', 'All operations execute or none do', 'Data types match', 'The schedule is serial', 'Locks are released early', 'A', 'Atomicity means a transaction is all-or-nothing.', 11),
  ('gate-core-cs-tcp-ip-subnetting-q01', 'gate-core-cs-tcp-ip-subnetting', 'How many layers does the TCP/IP model have?', '4', '5', '7', '3', 'A', 'The TCP/IP model has 4 layers; OSI has 7.', 0),
  ('gate-core-cs-tcp-ip-subnetting-q02', 'gate-core-cs-tcp-ip-subnetting', 'Which transport protocol is connection-oriented and reliable?', 'TCP', 'UDP', 'IP', 'ICMP', 'A', 'TCP is reliable and connection-oriented; UDP is neither.', 1),
  ('gate-core-cs-tcp-ip-subnetting-q03', 'gate-core-cs-tcp-ip-subnetting', 'The TCP connection setup uses a:', 'Three-way handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK)', 'Two-way handshake', 'Four-way broadcast', 'Single SYN packet', 'A', 'TCP establishes a connection with the SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK handshake.', 2),
  ('gate-core-cs-tcp-ip-subnetting-q04', 'gate-core-cs-tcp-ip-subnetting', 'How many bits are in an IPv4 address?', '32', '64', '128', '16', 'A', 'An IPv4 address is 32 bits (IPv6 is 128 bits).', 3),
  ('gate-core-cs-tcp-ip-subnetting-q05', 'gate-core-cs-tcp-ip-subnetting', 'How many usable host addresses does a /24 subnet provide?', '254', '256', '252', '128', 'A', '2^(32−24) − 2 = 256 − 2 = 254 usable hosts.', 4),
  ('gate-core-cs-tcp-ip-subnetting-q06', 'gate-core-cs-tcp-ip-subnetting', 'Two addresses in every subnet are reserved for:', 'Network and broadcast', 'Gateway and DNS', 'Source and destination', 'TCP and UDP', 'A', 'The network (all-0 host) and broadcast (all-1 host) addresses are reserved.', 5),
  ('gate-core-cs-tcp-ip-subnetting-q07', 'gate-core-cs-tcp-ip-subnetting', 'The subnet mask for a /26 network is:', '255.255.255.192', '255.255.255.0', '255.255.255.128', '255.255.255.224', 'A', '/26 sets 26 bits: the last octet is 11000000 = 192.', 6),
  ('gate-core-cs-tcp-ip-subnetting-q08', 'gate-core-cs-tcp-ip-subnetting', 'Which protocol resolves an IP address to a MAC address?', 'ARP', 'DNS', 'DHCP', 'ICMP', 'A', 'ARP (Address Resolution Protocol) maps IP addresses to MAC addresses.', 7),
  ('gate-core-cs-tcp-ip-subnetting-q08b', 'gate-core-cs-tcp-ip-subnetting', 'DNS primarily runs over which transport protocol for typical queries?', 'UDP', 'TCP only', 'ICMP', 'ARP', 'A', 'DNS queries typically use UDP for low overhead, falling back to TCP.', 8),
  ('gate-core-cs-tcp-ip-subnetting-q10', 'gate-core-cs-tcp-ip-subnetting', 'How many usable hosts are in a /26 subnet?', '62', '64', '30', '126', 'A', '2^(32−26) − 2 = 64 − 2 = 62 usable hosts.', 9),
  ('gate-core-cs-tcp-ip-subnetting-q11', 'gate-core-cs-tcp-ip-subnetting', 'Which layer of the TCP/IP model does IP belong to?', 'Network (Internet) layer', 'Transport layer', 'Application layer', 'Link layer', 'A', 'IP operates at the network (internet) layer of the TCP/IP model.', 10),
  ('gate-core-cs-tcp-ip-subnetting-q12', 'gate-core-cs-tcp-ip-subnetting', 'Which is an advantage of UDP over TCP?', 'Lower overhead / latency', 'Guaranteed delivery', 'Ordered byte stream', 'Congestion control', 'A', 'UDP''s lack of handshakes and acknowledgements gives lower overhead and latency.', 11),
  ('gate-core-cs-regular-languages-finite-automata-q01', 'gate-core-cs-regular-languages-finite-automata', 'A DFA differs from an NFA in that a DFA has:', 'Exactly one transition per state-symbol pair', 'Epsilon transitions', 'Multiple start states', 'An infinite alphabet', 'A', 'A DFA defines exactly one transition for each (state, symbol) pair.', 0),
  ('gate-core-cs-regular-languages-finite-automata-q02', 'gate-core-cs-regular-languages-finite-automata', 'In terms of language recognition power, DFAs and NFAs are:', 'Equivalent', 'NFAs strictly stronger', 'DFAs strictly stronger', 'Incomparable', 'A', 'Every NFA has an equivalent DFA, so both recognise exactly the regular languages.', 1),
  ('gate-core-cs-regular-languages-finite-automata-q03', 'gate-core-cs-regular-languages-finite-automata', 'Converting an n-state NFA to a DFA may require up to how many states?', '2ⁿ', 'n', 'n²', 'n!', 'A', 'Subset construction can produce up to 2ⁿ DFA states.', 2),
  ('gate-core-cs-regular-languages-finite-automata-q04', 'gate-core-cs-regular-languages-finite-automata', 'Which tool proves that a language is NOT regular?', 'The pumping lemma', 'Subset construction', 'Thompson''s construction', 'Kleene''s theorem', 'A', 'The pumping lemma for regular languages is used to prove non-regularity.', 3),
  ('gate-core-cs-regular-languages-finite-automata-q05', 'gate-core-cs-regular-languages-finite-automata', 'The language { aⁿbⁿ : n ≥ 0 } is:', 'Not regular', 'Regular', 'Finite', 'Recognised by a DFA', 'A', 'It needs unbounded counting, so no finite automaton recognises it.', 4),
  ('gate-core-cs-regular-languages-finite-automata-q06', 'gate-core-cs-regular-languages-finite-automata', 'Regular languages are closed under all of the following EXCEPT:', 'None — they are closed under union, intersection and complement', 'Union', 'Complement', 'Intersection', 'A', 'Regular languages are closed under union, intersection and complement.', 5),
  ('gate-core-cs-regular-languages-finite-automata-q07', 'gate-core-cs-regular-languages-finite-automata', 'The Myhill–Nerode theorem characterizes regular languages by having:', 'Finitely many equivalence classes', 'A context-free grammar', 'An ambiguous grammar', 'A pushdown stack', 'A', 'A language is regular iff it has finitely many Myhill–Nerode classes.', 6),
  ('gate-core-cs-regular-languages-finite-automata-q08', 'gate-core-cs-regular-languages-finite-automata', 'A DFA is formally defined as a tuple with how many components?', '5', '3', '4', '6', 'A', 'A DFA is the 5-tuple (Q, Σ, δ, q₀, F).', 7),
  ('gate-core-cs-regular-languages-finite-automata-q09', 'gate-core-cs-regular-languages-finite-automata', 'Which describe exactly the same class of languages?', 'Regular expressions and finite automata', 'CFGs and PDAs', 'Turing machines and PDAs', 'LL and LR grammars', 'A', 'Regular expressions, DFAs and NFAs all describe the regular languages.', 8),
  ('gate-core-cs-regular-languages-finite-automata-q10', 'gate-core-cs-regular-languages-finite-automata', 'The Myhill–Nerode relation also yields:', 'The minimum-state DFA', 'The maximum NFA', 'An ambiguous grammar', 'A non-deterministic PDA', 'A', 'The equivalence classes correspond to states of the minimal DFA.', 9),
  ('gate-core-cs-regular-languages-finite-automata-q11', 'gate-core-cs-regular-languages-finite-automata', 'An NFA may include which feature that a DFA cannot?', 'Epsilon (ε) transitions', 'A start state', 'Accepting states', 'A finite alphabet', 'A', 'NFAs allow ε-moves and multiple transitions; DFAs do not.', 10),
  ('gate-core-cs-regular-languages-finite-automata-q12', 'gate-core-cs-regular-languages-finite-automata', 'The set of regular languages over an alphabet is closed under Kleene star. This means:', 'L* is regular if L is regular', 'L* is always finite', 'L* is context-free only', 'L* is never regular', 'A', 'Closure under star guarantees L* is regular whenever L is.', 11),
  ('gate-core-cs-context-free-grammars-parsing-q01', 'gate-core-cs-context-free-grammars-parsing', 'A context-free grammar restricts every production to have:', 'A single non-terminal on the left', 'A single terminal on the left', 'No non-terminals', 'Only terminals on the right', 'A', 'In a CFG each production''s left side is one non-terminal.', 0),
  ('gate-core-cs-context-free-grammars-parsing-q02', 'gate-core-cs-context-free-grammars-parsing', 'Context-free languages are recognised by:', 'Pushdown automata', 'Finite automata', 'Linear-bounded automata', 'Combinational circuits', 'A', 'A pushdown automaton''s stack lets it recognise context-free languages.', 1),
  ('gate-core-cs-context-free-grammars-parsing-q03', 'gate-core-cs-context-free-grammars-parsing', 'The language { aⁿbⁿ } is context-free because a PDA can:', 'Use its stack to count', 'Use only its states', 'Run in constant space', 'Avoid a stack', 'A', 'The stack stores the count of a''s to match against b''s.', 2),
  ('gate-core-cs-context-free-grammars-parsing-q04', 'gate-core-cs-context-free-grammars-parsing', 'A grammar is ambiguous if some string has:', 'Two distinct parse trees', 'No derivation', 'Only one derivation', 'Only terminals', 'A', 'Ambiguity means a string has more than one parse tree / leftmost derivation.', 3),
  ('gate-core-cs-context-free-grammars-parsing-q05', 'gate-core-cs-context-free-grammars-parsing', 'Context-free languages are NOT closed under:', 'Intersection', 'Union', 'Concatenation', 'Kleene star', 'A', 'CFLs are closed under union, concatenation and star, but not intersection.', 4),
  ('gate-core-cs-context-free-grammars-parsing-q06', 'gate-core-cs-context-free-grammars-parsing', 'Top-down LL parsers require the grammar to be free of:', 'Left recursion', 'Right recursion', 'Terminals', 'Epsilon rules', 'A', 'LL parsers cannot handle left recursion; it must be removed first.', 5),
  ('gate-core-cs-context-free-grammars-parsing-q07', 'gate-core-cs-context-free-grammars-parsing', 'Which parsing family is generally more powerful?', 'Bottom-up (LR)', 'Top-down (LL)', 'They are identical', 'Recursive descent only', 'A', 'LR (bottom-up) parsers handle a strictly larger grammar class than LL.', 6),
  ('gate-core-cs-context-free-grammars-parsing-q08', 'gate-core-cs-context-free-grammars-parsing', 'FIRST and FOLLOW sets are used to build:', 'Predictive parsing tables', 'Symbol tables', 'Register allocations', 'Page tables', 'A', 'FIRST and FOLLOW sets drive construction of LL predictive-parse tables.', 7),
  ('gate-core-cs-context-free-grammars-parsing-q09', 'gate-core-cs-context-free-grammars-parsing', 'Transforming rules to defer a common-prefix choice is called:', 'Left factoring', 'Left recursion', 'Loop unrolling', 'Constant folding', 'A', 'Left factoring rewrites productions sharing a prefix for predictive parsing.', 8),
  ('gate-core-cs-context-free-grammars-parsing-q10', 'gate-core-cs-context-free-grammars-parsing', 'A deterministic PDA is:', 'Strictly weaker than a general PDA', 'Equal to a general PDA', 'Stronger than a Turing machine', 'Equivalent to a DFA', 'A', 'Deterministic PDAs recognise a proper subset of the context-free languages.', 9),
  ('gate-core-cs-context-free-grammars-parsing-q11', 'gate-core-cs-context-free-grammars-parsing', 'A parse tree''s leaves, read left to right, spell out:', 'The derived string (yield)', 'The start symbol', 'The FOLLOW set', 'Only non-terminals', 'A', 'The yield of a parse tree is the terminal string it derives.', 10),
  ('gate-core-cs-context-free-grammars-parsing-q12', 'gate-core-cs-context-free-grammars-parsing', 'Which memory structure gives a PDA more power than a finite automaton?', 'A stack', 'A queue', 'A register', 'A cache', 'A', 'The stack is the extra memory that lifts a PDA above finite automata.', 11),
  ('gate-core-cs-data-structures-complexity-trees-q13', 'gate-core-cs-data-structures-complexity-trees', 'The worst-case time to search a balanced binary search tree with n nodes is:', 'O(log n)', 'O(n)', 'O(n log n)', 'O(1)', 'A', 'A balanced BST has height O(log n), so search takes O(log n).', 12),
  ('gate-core-cs-data-structures-complexity-trees-q14', 'gate-core-cs-data-structures-complexity-trees', 'A complete binary tree with n nodes has a height of:', 'O(log n)', 'O(n)', 'O(n²)', 'O(1)', 'A', 'A complete binary tree is balanced, giving height ⌊log₂n⌋.', 13),
  ('gate-core-cs-data-structures-complexity-trees-q15', 'gate-core-cs-data-structures-complexity-trees', 'An in-order traversal of a binary search tree visits nodes in:', 'Sorted (ascending) order', 'Reverse order', 'Random order', 'Level order', 'A', 'In-order traversal of a BST yields keys in ascending sorted order.', 14),
  ('gate-core-cs-data-structures-complexity-trees-q16', 'gate-core-cs-data-structures-complexity-trees', 'In the worst case, an unbalanced BST search degrades to:', 'O(n)', 'O(log n)', 'O(1)', 'O(n log n)', 'A', 'A skewed BST behaves like a linked list, so search is O(n).', 15),
  ('gate-core-cs-algorithms-sorting-complexity-q13', 'gate-core-cs-algorithms-sorting-complexity', 'The average-case time complexity of quicksort is:', 'O(n log n)', 'O(n²)', 'O(n)', 'O(log n)', 'A', 'Quicksort averages O(n log n), though its worst case is O(n²).', 12),
  ('gate-core-cs-algorithms-sorting-complexity-q14', 'gate-core-cs-algorithms-sorting-complexity', 'Merge sort has a worst-case time complexity of:', 'O(n log n)', 'O(n²)', 'O(n)', 'O(log n)', 'A', 'Merge sort is O(n log n) in all cases.', 13),
  ('gate-core-cs-algorithms-sorting-complexity-q15', 'gate-core-cs-algorithms-sorting-complexity', 'The lower bound for comparison-based sorting is:', 'Ω(n log n)', 'Ω(n)', 'Ω(log n)', 'Ω(1)', 'A', 'Any comparison sort needs at least Ω(n log n) comparisons.', 14),
  ('gate-core-cs-algorithms-sorting-complexity-q16', 'gate-core-cs-algorithms-sorting-complexity', 'Which sorting algorithm is NOT comparison-based?', 'Counting sort', 'Quicksort', 'Merge sort', 'Heapsort', 'A', 'Counting sort uses key values as indices, not comparisons, achieving O(n+k).', 15),
  ('gate-core-cs-operating-systems-q13', 'gate-core-cs-operating-systems', 'A deadlock requires all four Coffman conditions, including circular wait and:', 'Mutual exclusion', 'Preemption', 'Aging', 'Paging', 'A', 'The four conditions are mutual exclusion, hold and wait, no preemption and circular wait.', 12),
  ('gate-core-cs-operating-systems-q14', 'gate-core-cs-operating-systems', 'Which scheduling algorithm gives the minimum average waiting time?', 'Shortest Job First', 'FCFS', 'Round Robin', 'Priority', 'A', 'SJF is optimal for minimizing average waiting time.', 13),
  ('gate-core-cs-operating-systems-q15', 'gate-core-cs-operating-systems', 'The Banker''s algorithm is used for deadlock:', 'Avoidance', 'Detection only', 'Prevention', 'Recovery', 'A', 'The Banker''s algorithm avoids deadlock by keeping the system in a safe state.', 14),
  ('gate-core-cs-operating-systems-q16', 'gate-core-cs-operating-systems', 'Belady''s anomaly can occur under which page-replacement policy?', 'FIFO', 'LRU', 'Optimal', 'LFU with stack property', 'A', 'FIFO can show Belady''s anomaly; stack algorithms like LRU cannot.', 15),
  ('gate-core-cs-dbms-sql-q13', 'gate-core-cs-dbms-sql', 'A relation is in BCNF if, for every functional dependency X→Y:', 'X is a superkey', 'Y is atomic', 'X is a single attribute', 'Y is a candidate key', 'A', 'BCNF requires the left side of every FD to be a superkey.', 12),
