  ('gate-programming-ds-dynamic-programming-q14', 'gate-programming-ds-dynamic-programming', 'Storing subproblem results to avoid recomputation is called:', 'Memoization', 'Recursion', 'Backtracking', 'Hashing', 'A', 'Memoization caches computed subproblem results.', 13),
  ('gate-programming-ds-dynamic-programming-q15', 'gate-programming-ds-dynamic-programming', 'The 0/1 knapsack problem is typically solved using:', 'Dynamic programming', 'A greedy algorithm always', 'Binary search', 'DFS only', 'A', 'The 0/1 knapsack has optimal substructure and is solved with DP.', 14),
  ('gate-programming-ds-dynamic-programming-q16', 'gate-programming-ds-dynamic-programming', 'The time complexity of the standard DP for the longest common subsequence of strings of length m and n is:', 'O(mn)', 'O(m + n)', 'O(2^n)', 'O(log mn)', 'A', 'The LCS DP fills an m×n table, giving O(mn) time.', 15),
  ('cfa-ethics-code-of-ethics-standards-q01', 'cfa-ethics-code-of-ethics-standards', 'When the applicable local law is less strict than the CFA Institute Code and Standards, a member must comply with the:', 'Code and Standards, because they are stricter', 'less strict local law, since law overrides the Code', 'law of the member''s home country only', 'employer''s internal policy regardless of either', 'A', 'Members must always follow the stricter of applicable law or the Code and Standards.', 0),
  ('cfa-ethics-code-of-ethics-standards-q02', 'cfa-ethics-code-of-ethics-standards', 'An analyst combines several pieces of public and nonmaterial nonpublic information to form a buy recommendation. This approach is best described as:', 'a violation of the prohibition on insider trading', 'the Mosaic Theory, which is permitted', 'front running of client orders', 'a breach of the duty of loyalty to employers', 'B', 'The Mosaic Theory permits combining public and nonmaterial nonpublic information.', 1),
  ('cfa-ethics-code-of-ethics-standards-q03', 'cfa-ethics-code-of-ethics-standards', 'Under the duty of loyalty, prudence, and care, a member managing client assets must place the interests of:', 'the employer ahead of all other parties', 'the member ahead of the client to ensure retention', 'the client ahead of the member''s and employer''s', 'regulators ahead of the client at all times', 'C', 'Standard III requires client interests come before the member''s and employer''s.', 2),
  ('cfa-ethics-code-of-ethics-standards-q04', 'cfa-ethics-code-of-ethics-standards', 'The Standard on Fair Dealing requires that a member disseminating an investment recommendation must:', 'give every client an identical position size', 'notify only the firm''s largest clients first', 'treat all clients fairly, though not necessarily equally', 'delay all trades until every client has acted', 'C', 'Fair Dealing requires fair, not identical, treatment of all clients.', 3),
  ('cfa-ethics-code-of-ethics-standards-q05', 'cfa-ethics-code-of-ethics-standards', 'A portfolio manager receives material nonpublic information about a pending acquisition. The most appropriate action is to:', 'trade quickly before the information becomes public', 'share it only with the firm''s best clients', 'use it but disclose the source afterward', 'refrain from acting or causing others to act on it', 'D', 'Standard II(A) prohibits acting or causing others to act on material nonpublic information.', 4),
  ('cfa-ethics-code-of-ethics-standards-q06', 'cfa-ethics-code-of-ethics-standards', 'Which Standard most directly governs the disclosure of referral fees and the priority of transactions?', 'Investment Analysis and Recommendations', 'Integrity of Capital Markets', 'Duties to Employers', 'Conflicts of Interest', 'D', 'Standard VI Conflicts of Interest covers disclosure, priority of transactions, and referral fees.', 5),
  ('cfa-ethics-code-of-ethics-standards-q07', 'cfa-ethics-code-of-ethics-standards', 'A member who accepts a gift from a client that could affect independence should, under the Standards:', 'decline all client communication going forward', 'keep the gift secret to avoid embarrassment', 'resign from managing the client''s account', 'disclose the gift to the employer to preserve independence', 'D', 'Disclosure to the employer protects independence and objectivity under Standard I(B).', 6),
  ('cfa-ethics-code-of-ethics-standards-q08', 'cfa-ethics-code-of-ethics-standards', 'Engaging in conduct that compromises the reputation or integrity of the CFA designation is a violation of the Standard on:', 'Duties to Clients', 'Market manipulation', 'Responsibilities as a CFA Institute Member or Candidate', 'Suitability of recommendations', 'C', 'Standard VII addresses conduct that compromises the CFA designation''s reputation.', 7),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-time-value-of-money-q01', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-time-value-of-money', 'An investor deposits 1,000 at an annual rate of 8% compounded annually. What is the value after 3 years?', '1,240.00', '1,080.00', '1,259.71', '1,360.49', 'C', 'FV = 1000 * (1.08)^3 = 1000 * 1.259712 = 1,259.71.', 0),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-time-value-of-money-q02', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-time-value-of-money', 'Compared with an otherwise identical ordinary annuity, the present value of an annuity due is:', 'higher, because each payment is received one period sooner', 'the same, since the cash flows are equal', 'lower, because payments arrive later', 'undefined without the discount rate', 'A', 'Annuity-due payments arrive one period earlier, so their present value is higher.', 1),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-time-value-of-money-q03', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-time-value-of-money', 'A stated annual rate of 12% compounded monthly produces an effective annual rate closest to:', '12.00%', '12.36%', '11.39%', '12.68%', 'D', 'EAR = (1 + 0.12/12)^12 - 1 = 1.01^12 - 1 = 12.68%.', 2),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-time-value-of-money-q04', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-time-value-of-money', 'A perpetuity pays 50 per year forever. If the required return is 10%, its present value is:', '450', '550', '5,000', '500', 'D', 'PV of a perpetuity = PMT / r = 50 / 0.10 = 500.', 3),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-time-value-of-money-q05', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-time-value-of-money', 'Holding the stated annual rate constant, increasing the compounding frequency causes the effective annual rate to:', 'decrease toward zero', 'rise above the stated rate', 'fall below the stated rate', 'stay exactly equal to the stated rate', 'B', 'More frequent compounding raises the effective annual rate above the stated rate.', 4),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-time-value-of-money-q06', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-time-value-of-money', 'What is the present value of 5,000 to be received in 4 years if the annual discount rate is 6%?', '3,960', '4,158', '4,717', '3,711', 'A', 'PV = 5000 / (1.06)^4 = 5000 / 1.26248 = 3,960.', 5),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-time-value-of-money-q07', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-time-value-of-money', 'Using continuous compounding, the future value of a single sum is found with the factor:', '(1 + r)^n', '(1 + r/m)^(m*n)', 'e^(r*n)', '1 / (1 + r)^n', 'C', 'Continuous compounding uses FV = PV * e^(r*n).', 6),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-time-value-of-money-q08', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-time-value-of-money', 'The most common time-value error when valuing a semiannual-pay bond is failing to:', 'discount the maturity value at all', 'include the coupon in present value', 'use a calculator instead of a formula', 'match the rate and number of periods to the same frequency', 'D', 'Rate and period count must use the same frequency; a mismatch is the classic TVM error.', 7),
  ('cfa-economics-market-structures-q01', 'cfa-economics-market-structures', 'In perfect competition, the demand curve facing an individual firm is best described as:', 'downward sloping and steep', 'perfectly elastic (horizontal)', 'perfectly inelastic (vertical)', 'upward sloping', 'B', 'A price-taking firm in perfect competition faces a horizontal, perfectly elastic demand curve.', 0),
  ('cfa-economics-market-structures-q02', 'cfa-economics-market-structures', 'A profit-maximizing firm in any market structure produces the quantity where:', 'price equals average total cost', 'total revenue is at its maximum', 'marginal revenue equals marginal cost', 'average cost equals average revenue', 'C', 'Profit is maximized where marginal revenue equals marginal cost in every structure.', 1),
  ('cfa-economics-market-structures-q03', 'cfa-economics-market-structures', 'In long-run equilibrium under perfect competition, economic profit for the typical firm is:', 'maximized and positive', 'zero because free entry erodes profit', 'negative but improving', 'equal to accounting profit', 'B', 'Free entry drives long-run economic profit to zero in perfect competition.', 2),
  ('cfa-economics-market-structures-q04', 'cfa-economics-market-structures', 'Which market structure is characterized by a single seller facing the entire downward-sloping market demand curve?', 'Perfect competition', 'Monopoly', 'Oligopoly', 'Monopolistic competition', 'B', 'A monopoly is one seller facing the whole downward-sloping market demand curve.', 3),
  ('cfa-economics-market-structures-q05', 'cfa-economics-market-structures', 'A key limitation of the N-firm concentration ratio relative to the Herfindahl-Hirschman Index is that the ratio:', 'cannot be computed without total revenue', 'applies only to monopolies', 'always exceeds 100 percent', 'is unaffected by a merger between two leading firms', 'D', 'The concentration ratio can miss mergers among top firms, which the HHI captures.', 4),
  ('cfa-economics-market-structures-q06', 'cfa-economics-market-structures', 'Monopolistic competition differs from perfect competition primarily because firms in it:', 'face no barriers to entry whatsoever', 'are pure price takers with no pricing power', 'produce an identical, homogeneous product', 'sell differentiated products and have some pricing power', 'D', 'Monopolistic competition features product differentiation, giving firms some pricing power.', 5),
  ('cfa-economics-market-structures-q07', 'cfa-economics-market-structures', 'Which of the following is most likely a source of long-lasting monopoly power?', 'Many close substitute products', 'Patents and significant economies of scale', 'Free and immediate entry of rivals', 'Homogeneous goods and perfect information', 'B', 'Patents and large economies of scale create high barriers that sustain monopoly power.', 6),
  ('cfa-economics-market-structures-q08', 'cfa-economics-market-structures', 'An industry with a small number of large interdependent firms and high entry barriers is best classified as:', 'perfect competition', 'an oligopoly', 'monopolistic competition', 'a pure monopoly', 'B', 'Few interdependent firms with high barriers describe an oligopoly.', 7),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-financial-ratios-analysis-q01', 'cfa-financial-reporting-financial-ratios-analysis', 'The quick ratio differs from the current ratio mainly because the quick ratio excludes:', 'inventory', 'accounts receivable', 'cash and cash equivalents', 'marketable securities', 'A', 'The quick ratio removes inventory, the least liquid current asset.', 0),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-financial-ratios-analysis-q02', 'cfa-financial-reporting-financial-ratios-analysis', 'A firm has net income of 200 and total equity of 1,000. Its return on equity is:', '5%', '10%', '20%', '200%', 'C', 'ROE = net income / equity = 200 / 1000 = 20%.', 1),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-financial-ratios-analysis-q03', 'cfa-financial-reporting-financial-ratios-analysis', 'In the three-part DuPont identity, return on equity equals the product of net profit margin, asset turnover, and:', 'the current ratio', 'financial leverage', 'interest coverage', 'the dividend payout ratio', 'B', 'Three-part DuPont: ROE = net margin * asset turnover * financial leverage.', 2),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-financial-ratios-analysis-q04', 'cfa-financial-reporting-financial-ratios-analysis', 'If a company''s ROE rises only because it took on substantially more debt, the increase most likely reflects:', 'improved operating profitability', 'greater financial leverage and higher risk', 'faster inventory turnover', 'a higher gross margin', 'B', 'A leverage-driven ROE gain signals more financial risk, not better operations.', 3),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-financial-ratios-analysis-q05', 'cfa-financial-reporting-financial-ratios-analysis', 'The interest coverage ratio is calculated as:', 'EBIT divided by interest expense', 'net income divided by interest expense', 'total debt divided by EBIT', 'revenue divided by interest expense', 'A', 'Interest coverage = EBIT / interest expense.', 4),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-financial-ratios-analysis-q06', 'cfa-financial-reporting-financial-ratios-analysis', 'Cost of goods sold is 800 and average inventory is 200. Inventory turnover is:', '4.0 times', '2.0 times', '0.25 times', '1.6 times', 'A', 'Inventory turnover = COGS / average inventory = 800 / 200 = 4.0.', 5),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-financial-ratios-analysis-q07', 'cfa-financial-reporting-financial-ratios-analysis', 'A current ratio below 1.0 most directly suggests that a firm may have:', 'excessive long-term debt', 'a high gross margin', 'very high profitability', 'weak short-term liquidity', 'D', 'A current ratio under 1.0 means current liabilities exceed current assets, signaling weak liquidity.', 6),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-financial-ratios-analysis-q08', 'cfa-financial-reporting-financial-ratios-analysis', 'To interpret a single financial ratio meaningfully, an analyst should primarily compare it with:', 'the risk-free rate of interest', 'the company''s history and industry peers', 'the firm''s stock price', 'the prevailing inflation rate', 'B', 'Ratios are meaningful only against trend and peer benchmarks, not in isolation.', 7),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital-q01', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital', 'In the weighted average cost of capital, the cost of debt is multiplied by (1 - t) because:', 'dividends are tax-deductible', 'debt has no maturity', 'equity is riskier than debt', 'interest expense is tax-deductible', 'D', 'Interest is tax-deductible, so the relevant cost of debt is after-tax: Rd * (1 - t).', 0),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital-q02', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital', 'A firm has a beta of 1.2, a risk-free rate of 3%, and an equity risk premium of 5%. Its CAPM cost of equity is:', '8.0%', '6.0%', '9.0%', '11.0%', 'C', 'Re = 3% + 1.2 * 5% = 3% + 6% = 9.0%.', 1),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital-q03', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital', 'When estimating WACC, the weights for debt and equity should ideally be based on:', 'book values from the balance sheet', 'target market-value proportions', 'historical issuance prices', 'par values of the securities', 'B', 'WACC weights should use target capital structure at market values.', 2),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital-q04', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital', 'A firm is financed 40% by debt and 60% by equity. With an after-tax cost of debt of 5% and cost of equity of 10%, the WACC is:', '7.5%', '6.5%', '9.0%', '8.0%', 'D', 'WACC = 0.40 * 5% + 0.60 * 10% = 2% + 6% = 8.0%.', 3),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital-q05', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital', 'Applying the firm''s overall WACC to a project that is much riskier than the firm''s average operations will most likely:', 'overstate the project''s value by understating risk', 'correctly value the project', 'have no effect on the decision', 'understate the project''s expected cash flows', 'A', 'Using too low a discount rate for a riskier project overstates its value.', 4),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital-q06', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital', 'Using the dividend discount approach, the cost of equity equals the dividend yield plus the:', 'risk-free rate', 'after-tax cost of debt', 'constant dividend growth rate', 'market risk premium', 'C', 'DDM cost of equity = D1 / P0 + g, the dividend yield plus growth.', 5),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital-q07', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital', 'A bond yields 8% before tax and the marginal tax rate is 25%. The after-tax cost of debt is:', '8.0%', '2.0%', '6.0%', '10.0%', 'C', 'After-tax cost of debt = 8% * (1 - 0.25) = 6.0%.', 6),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital-q08', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital', 'In capital budgeting, the WACC is most appropriately used as the:', 'growth rate of cash flows', 'discount rate for projects of average firm risk', 'terminal value multiple', 'inflation adjustment factor', 'B', 'WACC is the discount rate for projects with risk similar to the firm''s average.', 7),
  ('cfa-equity-equity-valuation-ddm-q01', 'cfa-equity-equity-valuation-ddm', 'A stock will pay a dividend of 2.00 next year, grows at 4% forever, and has a required return of 9%. Its Gordon growth value is:', '40.00', '22.22', '50.00', '25.00', 'A', 'V0 = D1 / (r - g) = 2.00 / (0.09 - 0.04) = 2.00 / 0.05 = 40.00.', 0),
  ('cfa-equity-equity-valuation-ddm-q02', 'cfa-equity-equity-valuation-ddm', 'The Gordon growth model becomes mathematically invalid when the growth rate is:', 'less than the required return', 'equal to zero', 'negative', 'greater than or equal to the required return', 'D', 'The model requires r greater than g; otherwise (r - g) is zero or negative.', 1),
  ('cfa-equity-equity-valuation-ddm-q03', 'cfa-equity-equity-valuation-ddm', 'A firm retains 60% of earnings and earns an ROE of 15%. Its sustainable growth rate is:', '6.0%', '9.0%', '15.0%', '25.0%', 'B', 'g = retention * ROE = 0.60 * 15% = 9.0%.', 2),
  ('cfa-equity-equity-valuation-ddm-q04', 'cfa-equity-equity-valuation-ddm', 'The dividend discount model is least appropriate for valuing a company that:', 'pays no dividends and reinvests all earnings', 'pays a stable, growing dividend', 'has a long dividend history', 'operates in a mature industry', 'A', 'The DDM cannot value firms that pay no dividends; use cash-flow models instead.', 3),
  ('cfa-equity-equity-valuation-ddm-q05', 'cfa-equity-equity-valuation-ddm', 'In a multistage DDM, the terminal value is most commonly estimated using the:', 'Gordon growth model at the start of the stable phase', 'current dividend yield only', 'book value of equity', 'average historical share price', 'A', 'Terminal value uses the Gordon model at the onset of the constant-growth stage.', 4),
  ('cfa-equity-equity-valuation-ddm-q06', 'cfa-equity-equity-valuation-ddm', 'If next year''s dividend is 3.00 and the stock is valued at 60 using the Gordon model with 3% growth, the required return is:', '8%', '6%', '5%', '3%', 'A', 'r = D1/V0 + g = 3/60 + 0.03 = 0.05 + 0.03 = 8%.', 5),
  ('cfa-equity-equity-valuation-ddm-q07', 'cfa-equity-equity-valuation-ddm', 'The retention ratio used in the sustainable growth formula equals:', 'the dividend payout ratio', 'one minus the dividend payout ratio', 'dividends divided by net income', 'the price-to-earnings ratio', 'B', 'Retention ratio b = 1 - payout ratio.', 6),
  ('cfa-equity-equity-valuation-ddm-q08', 'cfa-equity-equity-valuation-ddm', 'A notable weakness of the Gordon growth model is that its output is highly sensitive to small changes in the:', 'number of shares outstanding', 'current market price', 'company''s tax rate', 'spread between the required return and growth rate', 'D', 'Value is extremely sensitive to the (r - g) denominator, so small input changes swing it sharply.', 7),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-duration-convexity-q01', 'cfa-fixed-income-duration-convexity', 'A bond has a modified duration of 6. If its yield rises by 0.5%, the bond''s price will change by approximately:', '+3.0%', '-0.5%', '-3.0%', '+6.0%', 'C', 'Price change ~ -ModDur * dy = -6 * 0.005 = -3.0%.', 0),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-duration-convexity-q02', 'cfa-fixed-income-duration-convexity', 'Holding other factors constant, a bond with a lower coupon rate will have a duration that is:', 'lower', 'higher', 'unchanged', 'always equal to its maturity', 'B', 'Lower coupons push cash flows later, raising duration.', 1),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-duration-convexity-q03', 'cfa-fixed-income-duration-convexity', 'The Macaulay duration of a zero-coupon bond is equal to its:', 'coupon rate', 'current yield', 'convexity', 'time to maturity', 'D', 'A zero-coupon bond has a single cash flow, so its Macaulay duration equals its maturity.', 2),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-duration-convexity-q04', 'cfa-fixed-income-duration-convexity', 'Convexity is added to a duration-based price estimate primarily to improve accuracy for:', 'very small yield changes', 'large yield changes', 'floating-rate notes only', 'changes in credit spreads only', 'B', 'Duration is linear; convexity corrects the estimate for large yield moves.', 3),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-duration-convexity-q05', 'cfa-fixed-income-duration-convexity', 'For a bond with positive convexity, when yields move by equal amounts in each direction, the price:', 'rises more when yields fall than it falls when yields rise', 'falls more when yields rise than it rises when yields fall', 'changes by exactly the same amount either way', 'is unaffected by the direction of the move', 'A', 'Positive convexity means gains from falling yields exceed losses from equal rising yields.', 4),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-duration-convexity-q06', 'cfa-fixed-income-duration-convexity', 'Modified duration is obtained from Macaulay duration by:', 'multiplying by (1 + y/m)', 'subtracting the convexity', 'dividing by (1 + y/m)', 'adding the current yield', 'C', 'Modified duration = Macaulay duration / (1 + y/m).', 5),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-duration-convexity-q07', 'cfa-fixed-income-duration-convexity', 'All else equal, increasing a bond''s time to maturity generally causes its interest-rate sensitivity to:', 'decrease', 'increase', 'stay constant', 'become zero', 'B', 'Longer maturity raises duration and thus interest-rate sensitivity.', 6),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-duration-convexity-q08', 'cfa-fixed-income-duration-convexity', 'A bond has a modified duration of 5 and convexity of 80. For a yield drop of 1%, the estimated price change is closest to:', '+5.0%', '+4.6%', '-4.6%', '+5.4%', 'D', 'Change ~ -5*(-0.01) + 0.5*80*(0.01)^2 = 0.05 + 0.004 = 5.4%.', 7),
  ('cfa-derivatives-options-put-call-parity-q01', 'cfa-derivatives-options-put-call-parity', 'Put-call parity states that a call plus the present value of the strike equals:', 'a put plus the underlying stock', 'a put minus the underlying stock', 'the underlying stock alone', 'two calls minus a put', 'A', 'Parity: C + PV(K) = P + S, the fiduciary call equals the protective put.', 0),
  ('cfa-derivatives-options-put-call-parity-q02', 'cfa-derivatives-options-put-call-parity', 'A stock trades at 50, a call and put share a strike of 50, the put costs 4, and PV of the strike is 48. The call value is:', '2', '4', '8', '6', 'D', 'C = P + S - PV(K) = 4 + 50 - 48 = 6.', 1),
  ('cfa-derivatives-options-put-call-parity-q03', 'cfa-derivatives-options-put-call-parity', 'The combination of a long put and a long share of the underlying stock is known as a:', 'protective put', 'covered call', 'fiduciary call', 'bull spread', 'A', 'Put plus stock is a protective put, the right side of the parity identity.', 2),
  ('cfa-derivatives-options-put-call-parity-q04', 'cfa-derivatives-options-put-call-parity', 'Put-call parity holds strictly for European options written on an underlying that pays:', 'a large continuous dividend', 'no dividends', 'variable dividends', 'interest semiannually', 'B', 'Standard parity assumes European options on a non-dividend-paying underlying.', 3),
  ('cfa-derivatives-options-put-call-parity-q05', 'cfa-derivatives-options-put-call-parity', 'A synthetic long put can be constructed from a long call, a short position in the stock, and a:', 'long forward contract', 'short call at a different strike', 'second long call', 'long bond (lend at the risk-free rate)', 'D', 'Synthetic put = call - stock + bond, where the bond is risk-free lending.', 4),
  ('cfa-derivatives-options-put-call-parity-q06', 'cfa-derivatives-options-put-call-parity', 'If observed market prices violate put-call parity, an investor can most likely earn a profit through:', 'arbitrage by buying the cheaper side and selling the dearer side', 'writing only naked puts', 'holding the underlying to maturity', 'buying both the call and the put', 'A', 'A parity violation permits riskless arbitrage between the two equivalent sides.', 5),
  ('cfa-derivatives-options-put-call-parity-q07', 'cfa-derivatives-options-put-call-parity', 'The present value of the strike price in the parity equation is computed by discounting the strike at the:', 'risk-free rate', 'dividend yield', 'stock''s expected return', 'implied volatility', 'A', 'PV(K) = K / (1 + r)^T uses the risk-free rate r.', 6),
  ('cfa-derivatives-options-put-call-parity-q08', 'cfa-derivatives-options-put-call-parity', 'When the underlying pays dividends before expiration, the value used for the stock in put-call parity should be:', 'the spot price increased by the dividends', 'unchanged from the spot price', 'the spot price reduced by the present value of the dividends', 'the strike price', 'C', 'Dividends reduce the effective stock value by the present value of those dividends.', 7),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-private-equity-q01', 'cfa-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-private-equity', 'Under a typical 2-and-20 fee structure, the 20% portion represents the:', 'annual management fee on assets', 'incentive fee on profits above a hurdle', 'redemption penalty', 'administrative expense ratio', 'B', 'The 20% is an incentive fee charged on profits, often above a hurdle rate.', 0),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-private-equity-q02', 'cfa-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-private-equity', 'A high-water mark provision ensures that incentive fees are charged only on:', 'the original capital contributed', 'gross assets each year', 'new net profits above the prior peak value', 'the management fee base', 'C', 'A high-water mark restricts incentive fees to new profits above the previous peak.', 1),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-private-equity-q03', 'cfa-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-private-equity', 'The private-equity J-curve describes a fund''s tendency to show, in its early years:', 'immediate strong positive returns', 'perfectly flat returns', 'negative returns from fees and early write-downs', 'returns identical to public equities', 'C', 'The J-curve reflects early negative returns from fees and write-downs before later gains.', 2),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-private-equity-q04', 'cfa-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-private-equity', 'A leveraged buyout fund typically acquires:', 'early-stage startups with venture financing', 'publicly traded bonds only', 'diversified index portfolios', 'mature companies using a large amount of debt', 'D', 'LBO buyout funds acquire mature firms financed largely with debt.', 3),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-private-equity-q05', 'cfa-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-private-equity', 'Which hedge fund strategy seeks to profit primarily from announced mergers and corporate reorganizations?', 'Global macro', 'Equity market neutral', 'Event-driven', 'Convertible arbitrage', 'C', 'Event-driven strategies target mergers, distressed, and special-situation events.', 4),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-private-equity-q06', 'cfa-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-private-equity', 'Reported hedge fund index returns are often overstated because of survivorship and:', 'mark-to-market accounting', 'inflation indexing', 'backfill bias', 'currency hedging', 'C', 'Survivorship and backfill biases inflate reported hedge fund index returns.', 5),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-private-equity-q07', 'cfa-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-private-equity', 'Which of the following is a common exit route for a private equity investment?', 'Margin call', 'Coupon payment', 'Reverse stock split', 'Initial public offering or trade sale', 'D', 'PE investments exit through IPOs, trade sales, or secondary sales.', 6),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-private-equity-q08', 'cfa-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-private-equity', 'A defining liquidity characteristic shared by hedge funds and private equity is that investors typically face:', 'daily redemption at net asset value', 'no fees on withdrawals', 'lock-up periods that restrict withdrawals', 'government-guaranteed principal', 'C', 'Both impose lock-up periods, making them relatively illiquid investments.', 7),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-portfolio-risk-return-capm-q01', 'cfa-portfolio-management-portfolio-risk-return-capm', 'Under the CAPM, an asset with a beta of 1.5, a risk-free rate of 2%, and a market return of 8% has an expected return of:', '11%', '9%', '12%', '8%', 'A', 'E(R) = 2% + 1.5 * (8% - 2%) = 2% + 9% = 11%.', 0),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-portfolio-risk-return-capm-q02', 'cfa-portfolio-management-portfolio-risk-return-capm', 'According to portfolio theory, the type of risk that earns no risk premium because it can be diversified away is:', 'systematic risk', 'market risk', 'unsystematic (firm-specific) risk', 'interest-rate risk', 'C', 'Diversifiable unsystematic risk earns no premium; only systematic risk is rewarded.', 1),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-portfolio-risk-return-capm-q03', 'cfa-portfolio-management-portfolio-risk-return-capm', 'The diversification benefit of combining two assets into a portfolio is greatest when their correlation is:', 'as low as possible', 'exactly +1', 'exactly equal to the betas', 'equal to the risk-free rate', 'A', 'Lower correlation increases diversification; perfect positive correlation gives no benefit.', 2),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-portfolio-risk-return-capm-q04', 'cfa-portfolio-management-portfolio-risk-return-capm', 'In the CAPM, the market risk premium is represented by the term:', 'the market return minus the risk-free rate', 'beta times the standard deviation', 'the risk-free rate', 'the Sharpe ratio', 'A', 'The market risk premium equals the market return minus the risk-free rate (Rm - Rf).', 3),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-portfolio-risk-return-capm-q05', 'cfa-portfolio-management-portfolio-risk-return-capm', 'A stock with a beta greater than 1.0 is expected to be:', 'less volatile than the overall market', 'more volatile than the overall market', 'uncorrelated with the market', 'risk-free', 'B', 'A beta above 1.0 implies greater volatility than the market.', 4),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-portfolio-risk-return-capm-q06', 'cfa-portfolio-management-portfolio-risk-return-capm', 'The Sharpe ratio measures the excess return of a portfolio per unit of its:', 'beta', 'tracking error', 'unsystematic risk only', 'total risk (standard deviation)', 'D', 'The Sharpe ratio divides excess return by total risk (standard deviation).', 5),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-portfolio-risk-return-capm-q07', 'cfa-portfolio-management-portfolio-risk-return-capm', 'The expected return of a two-asset portfolio is calculated as the:', 'product of the two asset returns', 'weighted average of the asset variances', 'weighted average of the asset returns', 'square root of the covariance', 'C', 'Portfolio return is the weighted average of the component asset returns.', 6),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-portfolio-risk-return-capm-q08', 'cfa-portfolio-management-portfolio-risk-return-capm', 'A stock with very high total volatility but a low beta will, under the CAPM, have a required return that is:', 'modest because only systematic risk is priced', 'very high because total volatility is priced', 'equal to the market return', 'below the risk-free rate', 'A', 'CAPM prices only systematic risk, so a low-beta stock has a modest required return despite high volatility.', 7),
  ('cfa-ethics-material-non-public-info-q01', 'cfa-ethics-material-non-public-info', 'An analyst obtains a tip from a company insider about unannounced earnings that are significantly above consensus. Acting on this information would most likely violate:', 'Standard II(A) - Material Nonpublic Information', 'Standard I(C) - Misrepresentation', 'Standard III(A) - Loyalty, Prudence, and Care', 'Standard VI(B) - Priority of Transactions', 'A', 'Standard II(A) prohibits members from acting or causing others to act on material nonpublic information. Unannounced earnings well above consensus would clearly be material and nonpublic.', 0),
  ('cfa-ethics-material-non-public-info-q02', 'cfa-ethics-material-non-public-info', 'Under the mosaic theory, an analyst may legally act on investment conclusions derived from:', 'A combination of public information and nonmaterial nonpublic information', 'Material nonpublic information obtained from a corporate officer', 'Insider information obtained through a personal relationship', 'Nonpublic information obtained through channel checks regardless of materiality', 'A', 'Mosaic theory permits combining individually nonmaterial nonpublic information with public information to reach an investment conclusion, as long as no single piece is itself material and nonpublic.', 1),
  ('cfa-ethics-material-non-public-info-q03', 'cfa-ethics-material-non-public-info', 'Which of the following best describes information-based market manipulation under Standard II(B)?', 'Spreading false or misleading information to affect security prices', 'Trading large volumes to move a security''s price artificially', 'Front-running client orders for personal gain', 'Recommending securities without disclosing a conflict of interest', 'A', 'Standard II(B) prohibits information-based manipulation, which involves spreading false or misleading information to distort prices. Transaction-based manipulation (option B, trading to move prices artificially) is also prohibited but is a different form.', 2),
  ('cfa-ethics-material-non-public-info-q04', 'cfa-ethics-material-non-public-info', 'A portfolio manager accidentally overhears corporate executives discussing an imminent merger in an elevator. The most appropriate course of action is to:', 'Trade immediately before the information becomes public', 'Share the information only with the firm''s most important clients', 'Refrain from trading and notify the firm''s compliance department', 'Continue normal trading activity since the information was obtained unintentionally', 'C', 'The manner in which MNPI is obtained is irrelevant — once in possession of MNPI, a member must not trade on it and should report the situation to compliance immediately.', 3),
  ('cfa-ethics-material-non-public-info-q05', 'cfa-ethics-material-non-public-info', 'Which of the following pieces of information would most likely be considered material?', 'A change in a company''s cafeteria vendor', 'A planned acquisition of a competitor at a 40% premium', 'A minor update to the company''s website design', 'An analyst''s routine maintenance of a financial model', 'B', 'A planned acquisition at a significant premium would almost certainly affect a reasonable investor''s decision and the company''s stock price, making it material information.', 4),
  ('cfa-ethics-material-non-public-info-q06', 'cfa-ethics-material-non-public-info', 'A firm''s investment banking division is advising on a pending acquisition. The equity research division learns of the deal through an internal information leak. The best way for the firm to prevent a violation is to:', 'Restrict trading in the target company across all client accounts', 'Maintain an effective information barrier between the two divisions', 'Require all research analysts to sign non-disclosure agreements', 'Report the transaction to the SEC before trading occurs', 'B', 'Information barriers (Chinese walls) are the standard mechanism for preventing MNPI from flowing between a firm''s investment banking and public-side divisions, which is a core compliance requirement.', 5),
  ('cfa-ethics-material-non-public-info-q07', 'cfa-ethics-material-non-public-info', 'Wash trading — simultaneously buying and selling the same security to create the appearance of active trading — is an example of:', 'A violation of Standard II(B) - Market Manipulation', 'A violation of Standard II(A) - Material Nonpublic Information', 'A violation of Standard V(A) - Diligence and Reasonable Basis', 'Permitted activity if disclosed to regulators', 'A', 'Wash trading is a classic form of transaction-based market manipulation, explicitly prohibited under Standard II(B), as it artificially inflates trading volume and misleads other investors.', 6),
  ('cfa-ethics-material-non-public-info-q08', 'cfa-ethics-material-non-public-info', 'For information to qualify as ''material,'' it must meet which standard?', 'It must have been published in a financial newspaper within the past week', 'It must cause at least a 5% change in the security''s price', 'A reasonable investor would consider it important in making an investment decision', 'It must come directly from a company''s CEO or CFO', 'C', 'The CFA Institute standard for materiality is whether a reasonable investor would consider the information important in making an investment decision or whether it would likely affect the security''s price.', 7),
  ('cfa-ethics-duties-to-clients-q01', 'cfa-ethics-duties-to-clients', 'Under Standard III(C) - Suitability, before recommending a high-yield bond fund to a new client, a member must first:', 'Obtain written approval from the firm''s compliance department', 'Ensure the fund has at least a five-year track record', 'Disclose all fees and commissions associated with the fund', 'Determine the client''s investment objectives, risk tolerance, and constraints', 'D', 'Standard III(C) requires that a member understand the client''s investment profile — including objectives, risk tolerance, time horizon, and constraints — before any recommendation is made.', 0),
  ('cfa-ethics-duties-to-clients-q02', 'cfa-ethics-duties-to-clients', 'A portfolio manager releases an upgrade on a stock to top-tier clients two hours before distributing it to all other clients. This most likely violates:', 'Standard III(A) - Loyalty, Prudence, and Care', 'Standard III(E) - Preservation of Confidentiality', 'Standard III(B) - Fair Dealing', 'Standard IV(C) - Responsibilities of Supervisors', 'C', 'Standard III(B) requires that investment recommendations be disseminated to all clients simultaneously or through a process that is fair to all. Giving preferred clients advance access is a violation of fair dealing.', 1),
  ('cfa-ethics-duties-to-clients-q03', 'cfa-ethics-duties-to-clients', 'Which of the following is least likely to be considered a violation of Standard III(E) - Preservation of Confidentiality?', 'Reporting a client''s suspected money laundering activity to regulatory authorities', 'Sharing a client''s portfolio holdings with the firm''s marketing team', 'Disclosing a client''s trading activity to a journalist', 'Discussing a client''s personal financial situation at a social gathering', 'A', 'Standard III(E) permits disclosure of confidential client information when required by law, which includes reporting suspected illegal activity such as money laundering to the appropriate authorities.', 2),
  ('cfa-ethics-duties-to-clients-q04', 'cfa-ethics-duties-to-clients', 'A fiduciary duty of loyalty under Standard III(A) requires members to:', 'Maximize absolute returns for every client regardless of stated objectives', 'Invest only in securities with a minimum investment-grade credit rating', 'Place client interests ahead of their own personal interests', 'Consult with clients before every portfolio trade', 'C', 'The duty of loyalty under Standard III(A) requires members to always act in clients'' best interests and not subordinate client needs to personal benefit or the firm''s interests.', 3),
  ('cfa-ethics-duties-to-clients-q05', 'cfa-ethics-duties-to-clients', 'A member managing a discretionary portfolio for a risk-averse, retired client invests 60% of the portfolio in speculative small-cap stocks without consulting the client. This most likely violates:', 'Standard II(B) - Market Manipulation', 'Standard VI(A) - Disclosure of Conflicts', 'Standard V(B) - Communication with Clients', 'Standard III(C) - Suitability', 'D', 'Investing a conservative, retired client''s portfolio heavily in speculative securities is inconsistent with the client''s stated risk profile and violates the suitability obligation under Standard III(C).', 4),
  ('cfa-ethics-duties-to-clients-q06', 'cfa-ethics-duties-to-clients', 'Which of the following actions is consistent with Standard III(A) - Loyalty, Prudence, and Care?', 'Voting client proxies in a manner that benefits the firm''s investment banking relationships', 'Abstaining from all proxy votes to avoid conflicts of interest', 'Voting client proxies in the best interest of the clients', 'Allowing clients to direct all proxy votes regardless of the member''s fiduciary duty', 'C', 'Members must vote proxies in the best interest of clients, which is a core component of the duty of care and loyalty under Standard III(A). Allowing business relationships to influence proxy votes would be a violation.', 5),
  ('cfa-ethics-duties-to-clients-q07', 'cfa-ethics-duties-to-clients', 'Standard III(B) - Fair Dealing does not require members to:', 'Treat all clients equally in terms of services offered', 'Disseminate investment recommendations simultaneously to all clients', 'Give all clients the opportunity to act on investment recommendations', 'Avoid selective disclosure that benefits certain clients', 'A', 'Fair dealing requires equitable — not necessarily equal — treatment. Firms may offer different levels of service to different client segments as long as this is disclosed and does not involve selective disclosure of recommendations.', 6),
  ('cfa-ethics-duties-to-clients-q08', 'cfa-ethics-duties-to-clients', 'A client''s investment policy statement (IPS) specifies no investment in tobacco companies. The portfolio manager purchases shares in a tobacco firm because it offers excellent risk-adjusted returns. This action most directly violates:', 'Standard III(B) - Fair Dealing', 'Standard III(A) - Loyalty, Prudence, and Care', 'Standard V(A) - Diligence and Reasonable Basis', 'Standard VI(C) - Referral Fees', 'B', 'Overriding a client''s explicit investment constraint (e.g., socially responsible investing restrictions) to pursue returns is a breach of loyalty and prudence, as the member must follow the client''s stated objectives and constraints.', 7),
  ('cfa-ethics-conflicts-of-interest-q01', 'cfa-ethics-conflicts-of-interest', 'An analyst owns shares in a company and issues a buy recommendation on that company''s stock. Under Standard VI(A), the analyst must:', 'Divest the shares before publishing the recommendation', 'Refrain from issuing any recommendation on the company', 'Obtain written consent from clients before proceeding', 'Disclose the ownership interest in the recommendation', 'D', 'Standard VI(A) requires disclosure of conflicts — including personal ownership of recommended securities — but does not necessarily require divestiture. Full, prominent disclosure allows clients to assess the objectivity of the recommendation.', 0),
  ('cfa-ethics-conflicts-of-interest-q02', 'cfa-ethics-conflicts-of-interest', 'Which of the following sequences correctly reflects the order of transaction priority under Standard VI(B)?', 'Client accounts, employer accounts, member accounts', 'Employer accounts, client accounts, member accounts', 'Member accounts, client accounts, employer accounts', 'Client accounts, member accounts, employer accounts', 'A', 'Standard VI(B) establishes that client transactions must take priority first, then employer (proprietary) accounts, and personal accounts of the member last.', 1),
  ('cfa-ethics-conflicts-of-interest-q03', 'cfa-ethics-conflicts-of-interest', 'A member receives a referral fee from a third-party money manager for directing clients to that manager. Under Standard VI(C), the member must:', 'Return the referral fee to the client', 'Obtain regulatory approval before accepting referral fees', 'Cease the referral relationship immediately', 'Disclose the referral fee arrangement to clients', 'D', 'Standard VI(C) requires disclosure of any referral compensation so that clients can evaluate whether the referral is in their best interest or influenced by the member''s financial incentive.', 2),
  ('cfa-ethics-conflicts-of-interest-q04', 'cfa-ethics-conflicts-of-interest', 'A portfolio manager learns that a large client is about to place a substantial buy order for a thinly traded stock. Before executing the client''s order, the manager buys shares for her personal account. This is known as:', 'Scalping', 'Churning', 'Front-running', 'Painting the tape', 'C', 'Front-running is the prohibited practice of trading for personal accounts ahead of known client orders, violating the priority of transactions requirement under Standard VI(B).', 3),
  ('cfa-ethics-conflicts-of-interest-q05', 'cfa-ethics-conflicts-of-interest', 'A member sits on the board of directors of a company that he also covers as an equity analyst. Under Standard VI(A), the member should:', 'Resign from the board immediately to eliminate the conflict', 'Disclose the board membership in all research reports covering the company', 'Only cover the company if approved by the board', 'Recuse himself from all board votes related to financial disclosures', 'B', 'Board membership creates a clear conflict of interest that must be disclosed prominently in any research report. The standard requires full disclosure; it does not mandate resignation from the board.', 4),
  ('cfa-ethics-conflicts-of-interest-q06', 'cfa-ethics-conflicts-of-interest', 'Which compliance practice is most effective at preventing front-running violations?', 'Implementing pre-clearance requirements for personal securities transactions', 'Requiring members to disclose all personal trades on an annual basis', 'Limiting members to investing only in mutual funds in personal accounts', 'Conducting random audits of client account statements', 'A', 'Pre-clearance — requiring members to obtain approval before executing personal trades — directly prevents front-running by ensuring personal trades do not occur ahead of known client orders.', 5),
  ('cfa-ethics-conflicts-of-interest-q07', 'cfa-ethics-conflicts-of-interest', 'An investment bank provides research coverage only for companies that are also its investment banking clients. This practice primarily creates a conflict related to:', 'Standard VI(B) - Priority of Transactions', 'Standard III(B) - Fair Dealing', 'Standard V(A) - Diligence and Reasonable Basis', 'Standard I(B) - Independence and Objectivity', 'D', 'When research coverage is contingent on investment banking relationships, it compromises the analyst''s independence and objectivity, which is governed by Standard I(B) and reinforced by the conflict disclosure requirements of Standard VI(A).', 6),
  ('cfa-ethics-conflicts-of-interest-q08', 'cfa-ethics-conflicts-of-interest', 'A member receives an expensive gift from a broker-dealer whose services she evaluates for client account trading. Under the CFA Institute Standards, she should:', 'Accept the gift since it is a normal part of business relationships', 'Accept the gift only if its value is below $1,000', 'Accept the gift but donate its value to charity', 'Decline the gift and report the offer to her compliance department', 'D', 'Accepting gifts above a de minimis value from parties whose services you evaluate can impair independence and objectivity. The appropriate action is to decline and disclose to ensure compliance with conflict of interest standards.', 7),
  ('cfa-ethics-gips-standards-q01', 'cfa-ethics-gips-standards', 'Which of the following statements about GIPS compliance is most accurate?', 'GIPS compliance is mandatory for all investment firms operating globally', 'GIPS compliance must be applied on a firm-wide basis', 'Firms can claim GIPS compliance for individual composites without firm-wide compliance', 'GIPS compliance is required only for firms managing assets over $1 billion', 'B', 'GIPS requires firm-wide compliance — a firm cannot selectively apply GIPS to only certain strategies or products. Compliance is voluntary but must be comprehensive if claimed.', 0),
  ('cfa-ethics-gips-standards-q02', 'cfa-ethics-gips-standards', 'Under GIPS, a composite must include:', 'All fee-paying portfolios managed to a similar mandate, regardless of whether they are discretionary', 'All actual, fee-paying, discretionary portfolios managed to a similar mandate', 'Only the largest portfolios managed to a given strategy', 'All portfolios, including model and simulated accounts, managed to a similar mandate', 'B', 'GIPS requires composites to include all actual, fee-paying, discretionary portfolios with a similar investment strategy. Non-discretionary and non-fee-paying accounts are excluded.', 1),
  ('cfa-ethics-gips-standards-q03', 'cfa-ethics-gips-standards', 'GIPS requires performance returns to be calculated using time-weighted returns (TWR) primarily because:', 'TWR is simpler to calculate than money-weighted returns', 'TWR reflects the timing of client cash flows, rewarding superior timing', 'TWR is required by securities regulators in all major markets', 'TWR eliminates the distorting effect of external cash flows, enabling fair comparison of manager skill', 'D', 'TWR neutralizes the impact of client-driven cash flows (deposits and withdrawals), which are not under the manager''s control, allowing meaningful comparison of investment management skill across firms.', 2),
  ('cfa-ethics-gips-standards-q04', 'cfa-ethics-gips-standards', 'A GIPS-compliant firm must present at least how many years of performance history, building toward a minimum 10-year record?', '1 year', '3 years', '5 years', '7 years', 'C', 'GIPS requires firms to present a minimum of five years of compliant performance history (or since inception if shorter), building toward a 10-year track record by adding one year annually.', 3),
  ('cfa-ethics-gips-standards-q05', 'cfa-ethics-gips-standards', 'Which of the following is most consistent with the purpose of GIPS?', 'Standardizing performance presentation so prospective clients can compare track records fairly', 'Ensuring all investment managers achieve competitive returns', 'Requiring firms to hire independent auditors for all portfolio strategies', 'Setting minimum return requirements for institutional investment managers', 'A', 'GIPS exists to create a standardized, ethical framework for presenting investment performance, enabling prospective clients to make meaningful comparisons across firms and strategies on a level playing field.', 4),
  ('cfa-ethics-gips-standards-q06', 'cfa-ethics-gips-standards', 'GIPS verification is best described as:', 'An optional firm-wide review by an independent third party that provides additional assurance', 'A mandatory audit of each composite conducted by regulators', 'A self-assessment process completed annually by the firm''s compliance team', 'A composite-level certification issued by CFA Institute', 'A', 'GIPS verification is voluntary and is conducted by an independent third party on a firm-wide basis. It provides additional assurance to prospective clients but is not required for compliance.', 5),
  ('cfa-ethics-gips-standards-q07', 'cfa-ethics-gips-standards', 'A firm includes simulated and back-tested performance results in its GIPS-compliant composite presentation without any disclosure. This most likely:', 'Is permitted as long as simulated results are not presented as primary', 'Is permitted if the firm discloses it in the notes to the financial statements', 'Is acceptable because GIPS allows any returns that are calculated consistently', 'Violates GIPS because composites must include only actual performance', 'D', 'GIPS composites must be built from actual performance of real portfolios. Including simulated or back-tested results without clear, prominent disclosure violates the integrity standards underpinning GIPS.', 6),
  ('cfa-ethics-gips-standards-q08', 'cfa-ethics-gips-standards', 'Which return measure is most appropriate for evaluating an individual investor''s personal investment experience, given their specific pattern of deposits and withdrawals?', 'Time-weighted return (TWR)', 'Arithmetic mean return', 'Geometric mean return', 'Money-weighted return (MWR)', 'D', 'Money-weighted return (equivalent to IRR) reflects the timing and size of cash flows, making it the most appropriate measure for evaluating an individual investor''s personal return experience.', 7),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-q01', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-probability', 'An analyst assigns a 30% probability to a recession and a 70% probability to expansion. The expected return on equity is -10% in a recession and +15% in expansion. What is the expected return?', '5.0%', '10.5%', '9.5%', '7.5%', 'D', 'E(R) = 0.30(-10%) + 0.70(15%) = -3% + 10.5% = 7.5%. This is the probability-weighted average of possible outcomes.', 0),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-q02', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-probability', 'Two events A and B are mutually exclusive. P(A) = 0.40 and P(B) = 0.25. What is P(A or B)?', '0.10', '0.50', '0.75', '0.65', 'D', 'For mutually exclusive events, P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) = 0.40 + 0.25 = 0.65. The joint probability P(A and B) = 0 because the events cannot both occur.', 1),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-q03', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-probability', 'Events X and Y are independent. P(X) = 0.60 and P(Y) = 0.50. What is P(X and Y)?', '0.10', '0.30', '0.55', '0.80', 'B', 'For independent events, P(X and Y) = P(X) x P(Y) = 0.60 x 0.50 = 0.30. Independence means knowledge of one event does not change the probability of the other.', 2),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-q04', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-probability', 'Which type of probability is based on past frequency data from observed outcomes?', 'A priori probability', 'Subjective probability', 'Empirical probability', 'Conditional probability', 'C', 'Empirical probability is estimated by calculating the historical frequency with which an event has occurred, such as the proportion of years that a market index produced a positive return.', 3),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-q05', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-probability', 'P(A) = 0.50, P(B) = 0.40, and P(A and B) = 0.20. What is P(A | B)?', '0.20', '0.40', '0.80', '0.50', 'D', 'Conditional probability: P(A | B) = P(A and B) / P(B) = 0.20 / 0.40 = 0.50. This represents the probability of A given that B has already occurred.', 4),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-q06', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-probability', 'Bayes'' theorem is most useful in investment analysis for:', 'Calculating the variance of a portfolio of securities', 'Determining the expected return of an equally weighted portfolio', 'Updating prior probability estimates when new information becomes available', 'Estimating the correlation between two asset returns', 'C', 'Bayes'' theorem provides a formal mechanism for revising prior probabilities in light of new information, which is directly applicable to economic scenario analysis and forecasting in investment management.', 5),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-q07', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-probability', 'A portfolio has three equally likely scenarios with returns of -5%, 10%, and 25%. What is the expected return?', '10.0%', '8.0%', '11.0%', '12.5%', 'A', 'E(R) = (1/3)(-5%) + (1/3)(10%) + (1/3)(25%) = (-5 + 10 + 25)/3 = 30/3 = 10%. With equal probabilities, the expected value equals the simple arithmetic average.', 6),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-q08', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-probability', 'The total probability rule states that P(A) can be computed as:', 'The sum of all unconditional probabilities in the sample space', 'The sum of P(A | Bi) x P(Bi) for all mutually exclusive and exhaustive events Bi', 'P(A | B) + P(A | not B)', 'P(A and B) divided by P(B)', 'B', 'The total probability rule allows an unconditional probability to be calculated by weighting conditional probabilities by the probabilities of the mutually exclusive, exhaustive conditioning events.', 7),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-statistical-inference-q01', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-statistical-inference', 'The Central Limit Theorem is important in statistical inference primarily because it states that:', 'Sample means will always be normally distributed for any sample size', 'For sufficiently large samples, the sampling distribution of the mean is approximately normal regardless of the population distribution', 'The population must be normally distributed for valid statistical inference', 'Larger samples always produce more accurate point estimates of the population mean', 'B', 'The Central Limit Theorem establishes that for n >= 30, the sampling distribution of the sample mean approximates normality even if the underlying population is non-normal, enabling the use of z and t statistics.', 0),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-statistical-inference-q02', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-statistical-inference', 'A mutual fund database excludes funds that ceased operations. An analyst using this database to study historical fund returns would most likely encounter:', 'Look-ahead bias', 'Survivorship bias', 'Time-period bias', 'Data-snooping bias', 'B', 'Survivorship bias occurs when a dataset excludes entities that failed or were discontinued, causing the remaining sample to overstate historical performance since only successful funds survive to be included.', 1),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-statistical-inference-q03', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-statistical-inference', 'As sample size increases, the standard error of the sample mean:', 'Decreases, causing confidence intervals to become narrower', 'Remains constant regardless of sample size', 'Increases proportionally', 'Increases initially then decreases after the sample exceeds 30 observations', 'A', 'Standard error = sigma / sqrt(n). As n increases, the denominator grows and standard error falls, resulting in narrower confidence intervals and more precise estimates of the population mean.', 2),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-statistical-inference-q04', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-statistical-inference', 'A fixed-income manager replicating an index selects bonds to represent each maturity and credit-rating category proportionally. This sampling method is best described as:', 'Stratified random sampling', 'Systematic sampling', 'Cluster sampling', 'Simple random sampling', 'A', 'Stratified random sampling divides the population into subgroups (strata) based on relevant characteristics and draws random samples from each stratum, which is the standard approach in fixed-income index replication.', 3),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-statistical-inference-q05', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-statistical-inference', 'An analyst discovers a trading rule by running thousands of tests on the same historical dataset. The apparent profitability of the rule is most likely attributable to:', 'Look-ahead bias', 'Data-snooping bias', 'Survivorship bias', 'Sample selection bias', 'B', 'Data-snooping bias (or data mining) arises when a researcher repeatedly tests many hypotheses on the same dataset until significant results appear by chance. Such results typically fail to hold out-of-sample.', 4),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-statistical-inference-q06', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-statistical-inference', 'A 95% confidence interval for the population mean is best interpreted as:', 'There is a 95% probability that the population mean lies within this specific interval', '95% of all possible sample means fall within this interval', 'In repeated sampling, 95% of such intervals would contain the true population mean', 'The sample mean has a 95% chance of being the true population mean', 'C', 'The correct frequentist interpretation is that if we constructed many such confidence intervals from repeated samples, 95% of them would contain the true population mean. A single interval either contains the mean or it does not.', 5),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-statistical-inference-q07', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-statistical-inference', 'Look-ahead bias in a backtested trading strategy arises when:', 'The strategy is tested over too short a time period', 'Information that was not available at the time of the decision is used in the test', 'The test includes data from a period with unusually high market volatility', 'The sample excludes securities that have been delisted', 'B', 'Look-ahead bias occurs when a backtest uses data (such as revised earnings figures or restated financial reports) that would not have been available to an investor at the time the decision was supposedly made.', 6),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-statistical-inference-q08', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-statistical-inference', 'When the population standard deviation is unknown, the appropriate distribution for constructing a confidence interval for the population mean is the:', 'Standard normal (z) distribution', 't-distribution', 'F-distribution', 'Chi-square distribution', 'B', 'The t-distribution is used when the population standard deviation is unknown and must be estimated from the sample. It has heavier tails than the normal distribution, resulting in wider confidence intervals that reflect the additional uncertainty.', 7),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing-q01', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing', 'A Type I error in hypothesis testing is defined as:', 'Failing to reject a false null hypothesis', 'Rejecting a true null hypothesis', 'Accepting a false alternative hypothesis', 'Using the wrong test statistic for the sample size', 'B', 'A Type I error occurs when a true null hypothesis is incorrectly rejected. Its probability equals alpha, the significance level chosen by the analyst.', 0),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing-q02', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing', 'An analyst wants to test whether a portfolio manager''s mean annual return exceeds the benchmark''s mean return. The appropriate alternative hypothesis is:', 'Ha: mean return = benchmark return', 'Ha: mean return not equal to benchmark return', 'Ha: mean return > benchmark return', 'Ha: mean return < benchmark return', 'C', 'When the research question asks whether a value exceeds a benchmark, a one-tailed (right-tailed) test is appropriate, with the alternative hypothesis stating that the mean return is greater than the benchmark.', 1),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing-q03', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing', 'The p-value in a hypothesis test represents the:', 'Smallest significance level at which the null hypothesis can be rejected given the data', 'Probability of the test statistic being exactly equal to the observed value', 'Probability that the null hypothesis is true', 'Probability of making a Type II error', 'A', 'The p-value is the probability of observing a test statistic at least as extreme as the one calculated, assuming the null hypothesis is true. Rejecting H0 when p-value < alpha is equivalent to the critical value approach.', 2),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing-q04', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing', 'Decreasing the significance level (alpha) from 5% to 1% in a hypothesis test will, holding all else constant:', 'Decrease both Type I and Type II error probabilities', 'Have no effect on either type of error', 'Increase Type I error but decrease Type II error probability', 'Decrease Type I error but increase Type II error probability', 'D', 'Reducing alpha makes rejection of H0 harder, which directly reduces the probability of Type I error. However, this makes it more difficult to detect a false H0, thereby increasing the probability of Type II error.', 3),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing-q05', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing', 'An analyst tests the hypothesis that the variance of a stock''s returns equals a specified value. The appropriate test statistic to use is:', 'z-statistic', 't-statistic', 'Chi-square statistic', 'F-statistic', 'C', 'The chi-square distribution is the appropriate distribution for testing hypotheses about a single population variance. The F-distribution is used to compare variances across two populations.', 4),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing-q06', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing', 'The power of a hypothesis test is defined as:', '1 minus the probability of a Type I error', 'The probability of correctly rejecting a false null hypothesis', 'The probability of failing to reject a true null hypothesis', 'The significance level multiplied by the sample size', 'B', 'Test power = 1 - beta, where beta is the probability of a Type II error. Power represents the ability of the test to detect a false null hypothesis when one exists.', 5),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing-q07', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing', 'Which test statistic is most appropriate when testing the equality of means from two independent normally distributed populations with unknown but assumed equal variances?', 'z-statistic using pooled standard deviation', 'Chi-square statistic', 't-statistic using pooled variance', 'F-statistic', 'C', 'When comparing means from two independent samples with unknown but assumed equal variances, a two-sample t-test using pooled variance is the appropriate procedure.', 6),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing-q08', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing', 'A researcher fails to reject the null hypothesis. The most appropriate conclusion is:', 'The null hypothesis has been proven true', 'The alternative hypothesis has been disproven', 'There is insufficient evidence to reject the null hypothesis at the chosen significance level', 'A Type II error has definitely occurred', 'C', 'Failing to reject H0 does not prove it is true — it only means the sample evidence was insufficient to reject it at the chosen significance level. A Type II error may or may not have occurred.', 7),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-regression-q01', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-regression', 'In a simple linear regression, the slope coefficient b1 is best interpreted as:', 'The value of Y when X equals zero', 'The correlation between X and Y', 'The proportion of variance in Y explained by X', 'The expected change in Y for a one-unit increase in X', 'D', 'The slope coefficient b1 represents the expected change in the dependent variable Y for each one-unit increase in the independent variable X, holding all other factors constant.', 0),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-regression-q02', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-regression', 'A regression of stock returns on a market index produces an R-squared of 0.64. This means that:', 'The correlation between the stock and the market is 0.64', '64% of the variance in stock returns is explained by the market index', 'The regression coefficient on the market index equals 0.64', 'There is a 64% probability that the regression relationship is statistically significant', 'B', 'R-squared measures the proportion of the total variation in the dependent variable explained by the independent variable(s) in the regression. An R-squared of 0.64 means 64% of the variance in returns is captured by the model.', 1),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-regression-q03', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-regression', 'When adding additional independent variables to a multiple regression model, which measure should be used to compare models with different numbers of predictors?', 'R-squared', 'Standard error of the estimate', 'Adjusted R-squared', 'Sum of squared residuals', 'C', 'Adjusted R-squared penalizes the model for adding variables that do not meaningfully improve explanatory power, unlike R-squared which always increases (or stays the same) as variables are added regardless of their value.', 2),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-regression-q04', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-regression', 'Heteroskedasticity in a regression model means that the:', 'Residuals are correlated with each other across observations', 'Independent variables are highly correlated with each other', 'Dependent variable is not normally distributed', 'Variance of the residuals is not constant across all levels of the independent variable', 'D', 'Heteroskedasticity refers to non-constant variance of regression residuals. It causes OLS coefficient estimates to remain unbiased but makes standard errors unreliable, leading to incorrect t-statistics and p-values.', 3),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-regression-q05', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-regression', 'A Durbin-Watson statistic significantly below 2 in a regression using time-series data indicates the likely presence of:', 'Positive serial correlation', 'Heteroskedasticity', 'Multicollinearity', 'Negative serial correlation', 'A', 'The Durbin-Watson statistic tests for serial correlation in regression residuals. A value near 0 indicates strong positive serial correlation, near 2 indicates no serial correlation, and near 4 indicates negative serial correlation.', 4),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-regression-q06', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-regression', 'Multicollinearity in a multiple regression model is most likely to cause:', 'Biased coefficient estimates and unreliable predictions', 'The F-statistic to be statistically insignificant', 'Non-constant variance of residuals across observations', 'Inflated standard errors of coefficients, making it difficult to isolate individual effects', 'D', 'Multicollinearity does not bias coefficient estimates but inflates their standard errors. This makes t-statistics smaller and individual coefficients appear less significant even when the overall model (F-test) is significant.', 5),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-regression-q07', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-regression', 'The F-statistic in a multiple regression tests the null hypothesis that:', 'All slope coefficients jointly equal zero', 'All slope coefficients individually equal zero', 'The intercept of the regression equals zero', 'The residuals are normally distributed', 'A', 'The F-statistic tests whether all slope coefficients in the regression are jointly equal to zero — that is, whether the model as a whole explains a statistically significant portion of the variation in the dependent variable.', 6),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-regression-q08', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-regression', 'The ordinary least squares (OLS) method estimates regression coefficients by:', 'Maximizing the R-squared of the fitted model', 'Minimizing the sum of the absolute values of residuals', 'Minimizing the sum of squared differences between observed and fitted values', 'Maximizing the correlation between the dependent and independent variables', 'C', 'OLS selects coefficient estimates that minimize the sum of squared residuals (the squared differences between actual and predicted values of the dependent variable), which produces the best linear unbiased estimators under the classical assumptions.', 7),
  ('cfa-economics-gdp-business-cycles-q01', 'cfa-economics-gdp-business-cycles', 'Which component is NOT included in the expenditure approach to calculating GDP?', 'Private consumption expenditure', 'Net exports of goods and services', 'Gross private domestic investment', 'Intermediate goods purchased by manufacturers', 'D', 'GDP counts only final goods and services; intermediate goods are excluded to avoid double counting.', 0),
  ('cfa-economics-gdp-business-cycles-q02', 'cfa-economics-gdp-business-cycles', 'An economy experiencing two consecutive quarters of declining real GDP is most likely in which phase?', 'Peak', 'Contraction (recession)', 'Expansion', 'Trough', 'B', 'A recession is conventionally defined as two consecutive quarters of negative real GDP growth, placing the economy in the contraction phase.', 1),
  ('cfa-economics-gdp-business-cycles-q03', 'cfa-economics-gdp-business-cycles', 'Which of the following is best classified as a leading economic indicator?', 'Average duration of unemployment', 'Commercial and industrial loans outstanding', 'Slope of the yield curve', 'Index of industrial production', 'C', 'The yield curve slope is a leading indicator; an inverted curve typically precedes recessions. Unemployment duration and loan levels are lagging; industrial production is coincident.', 2),
  ('cfa-economics-gdp-business-cycles-q04', 'cfa-economics-gdp-business-cycles', 'Real GDP differs from nominal GDP in that real GDP:', 'is adjusted for changes in the price level', 'includes only goods, not services', 'counts government transfer payments', 'excludes depreciation of the capital stock', 'A', 'Real GDP uses base-year prices to remove the effect of inflation, making it a true volume measure of output.', 3),
  ('cfa-economics-gdp-business-cycles-q05', 'cfa-economics-gdp-business-cycles', 'Which indicator typically turns after the economy has already begun to recover?', 'New orders for capital goods', 'Building permits issued', 'Stock market index', 'Consumer price index', 'D', 'The CPI is a lagging indicator; inflation typically peaks and declines after the economic cycle has turned, reflecting prior demand conditions.', 4),
  ('cfa-economics-gdp-business-cycles-q06', 'cfa-economics-gdp-business-cycles', 'At the peak of a business cycle, an analyst would most likely observe:', 'maximum output and emerging inflationary pressure', 'low output and falling business confidence', 'high unemployment and rising inventory', 'rapidly falling interest rates and rising consumer confidence', 'A', 'At the peak, output is at its maximum and resource utilisation is high, which typically generates inflationary pressure before the contraction begins.', 5),
  ('cfa-economics-gdp-business-cycles-q07', 'cfa-economics-gdp-business-cycles', 'If government spending increases by $200 billion and private consumption falls by $200 billion, GDP measured by the expenditure approach will:', 'increase because government spending has a larger multiplier', 'remain unchanged, as the two changes offset each other', 'decrease because the private sector is more efficient', 'increase by exactly $400 billion', 'B', 'In the expenditure approach, C and G are additive components of GDP; an equal rise in G and fall in C leaves the total unchanged.', 6),
  ('cfa-economics-gdp-business-cycles-q08', 'cfa-economics-gdp-business-cycles', 'Employment and personal income data are best described as which type of economic indicator?', 'Leading indicators', 'Coincident indicators', 'Lagging indicators', 'Structural indicators', 'B', 'Employment and personal income move roughly in tandem with the business cycle and are classified as coincident indicators.', 7),
  ('cfa-economics-monetary-fiscal-policy-q01', 'cfa-economics-monetary-fiscal-policy', 'A central bank that lowers its policy interest rate and purchases government securities is implementing:', 'expansionary monetary policy', 'contractionary fiscal policy', 'contractionary monetary policy', 'expansionary fiscal policy', 'A', 'Lowering rates and purchasing securities (QE) expands the money supply and stimulates borrowing, which is expansionary monetary policy conducted by the central bank.', 0),
  ('cfa-economics-monetary-fiscal-policy-q02', 'cfa-economics-monetary-fiscal-policy', 'In the quantity theory of money (MV = PY), if velocity and real output are constant, a 5% increase in the money supply will most likely result in:', 'a 5% increase in real output', 'a 5% increase in the price level', 'a 5% decrease in velocity', 'no change in the price level', 'B', 'With V and Y constant, MV = PY implies that a 5% rise in M translates directly into a 5% rise in P, the price level.', 1),
  ('cfa-economics-monetary-fiscal-policy-q03', 'cfa-economics-monetary-fiscal-policy', 'Automatic stabilisers differ from discretionary fiscal policy in that automatic stabilisers:', 'act counter-cyclically without new government decisions', 'require legislative approval before taking effect', 'are always more powerful than spending programmes', 'only operate during recessions, not expansions', 'A', 'Automatic stabilisers such as progressive taxes and unemployment insurance respond automatically to economic conditions without requiring new legislative action.', 2),
  ('cfa-economics-monetary-fiscal-policy-q04', 'cfa-economics-monetary-fiscal-policy', 'Crowding out refers to the tendency of expansionary fiscal policy to:', 'reduce net exports by raising domestic prices', 'lower private investment by pushing up real interest rates', 'increase the money supply beyond the central bank''s target', 'raise household savings through Ricardian equivalence', 'B', 'When the government borrows heavily to finance spending, competition for funds raises real interest rates, which reduces (crowds out) private sector investment.', 3),
  ('cfa-economics-monetary-fiscal-policy-q05', 'cfa-economics-monetary-fiscal-policy', 'A combination of tight monetary policy and expansionary fiscal policy would most likely lead to:', 'lower real interest rates and a weaker currency', 'lower inflation and a depreciating currency', 'higher real interest rates and an appreciating currency', 'higher inflation and a weaker currency', 'C', 'Tight monetary policy raises interest rates while expansionary fiscal policy increases government borrowing, both putting upward pressure on rates and attracting capital inflows that appreciate the currency.', 4),
  ('cfa-economics-monetary-fiscal-policy-q06', 'cfa-economics-monetary-fiscal-policy', 'Central bank independence is primarily associated with:', 'higher government budget deficits', 'more volatile exchange rates over time', 'faster economic growth in every cycle', 'lower and more stable long-run inflation', 'D', 'Research consistently finds that independent central banks free from political pressure maintain better inflation credibility, resulting in lower and more stable long-run inflation.', 5),
  ('cfa-economics-monetary-fiscal-policy-q07', 'cfa-economics-monetary-fiscal-policy', 'A government raises taxes and cuts spending during an economic expansion. This policy is best described as:', 'expansionary fiscal policy', 'contractionary fiscal policy', 'neutral fiscal policy', 'expansionary monetary policy', 'B', 'Raising taxes and cutting government spending reduces aggregate demand, making the stance contractionary fiscal policy.', 6),
  ('cfa-economics-monetary-fiscal-policy-q08', 'cfa-economics-monetary-fiscal-policy', 'The fiscal multiplier is largest when the marginal propensity to consume (MPC) is:', 'equal to zero', 'equal to one', 'closest to one', 'closest to zero', 'C', 'The fiscal multiplier = 1 / (1 - MPC); as MPC approaches one, the multiplier grows very large because each round of spending generates nearly equal additional consumption.', 7),
  ('cfa-economics-international-trade-forex-q01', 'cfa-economics-international-trade-forex', 'Comparative advantage suggests that countries should specialise in goods for which they have:', 'the lowest absolute cost of production', 'the lowest opportunity cost of production', 'the highest absolute productivity levels', 'the most advanced technological capacity', 'B', 'Comparative advantage is determined by opportunity cost, not absolute cost; a country benefits by specialising where its opportunity cost is lowest relative to trading partners.', 0),
  ('cfa-economics-international-trade-forex-q02', 'cfa-economics-international-trade-forex', 'A country running a current account deficit is necessarily running a:', 'surplus in its capital and financial accounts combined', 'budget surplus in government finances', 'trade surplus in goods but a deficit in services', 'surplus in foreign exchange reserves', 'A', 'The balance of payments must sum to zero; a current account deficit is financed by a corresponding surplus in the capital and financial accounts (net capital inflows).', 1),
  ('cfa-economics-international-trade-forex-q03', 'cfa-economics-international-trade-forex', 'Purchasing Power Parity (PPP) predicts that over the long run, a country with higher inflation relative to a trading partner will see its currency:', 'appreciate to restore competitiveness', 'remain stable due to central bank intervention', 'depreciate by approximately the inflation differential', 'appreciate because higher prices attract foreign investment', 'C', 'PPP holds that exchange rates adjust to offset inflation differentials; the higher-inflation country''s currency depreciates so that identical goods cost the same across borders.', 2),
  ('cfa-economics-international-trade-forex-q04', 'cfa-economics-international-trade-forex', 'Covered interest rate parity implies that the forward exchange rate premium or discount equals:', 'the difference in inflation rates between the two countries', 'the trade balance difference between the two economies', 'the interest rate differential between the two countries', 'the current account surplus of the higher-rate country', 'C', 'Covered interest rate parity states that the forward premium or discount on a currency equals the interest rate differential, preventing risk-free arbitrage between money markets.', 3),
  ('cfa-economics-international-trade-forex-q05', 'cfa-economics-international-trade-forex', 'Which of the following is recorded in the financial account of the balance of payments?', 'A country''s exports of manufactured goods', 'Dividend income received from foreign investments', 'Unilateral transfer payments to foreign governments', 'Foreign direct investment inflows to domestic firms', 'D', 'FDI inflows represent cross-border investment transactions recorded in the financial account, not the current account.', 4),
  ('cfa-economics-international-trade-forex-q06', 'cfa-economics-international-trade-forex', 'If a domestic country''s interest rates rise relative to a foreign country, the most likely immediate effect on the domestic currency is:', 'depreciation, as borrowing costs increase', 'appreciation, as capital flows in seeking higher yields', 'no change, as rates do not affect exchange rates', 'depreciation, as inflation expectations increase', 'B', 'Higher domestic interest rates attract foreign capital seeking better returns, increasing demand for the domestic currency and causing it to appreciate.', 5),
  ('cfa-economics-international-trade-forex-q07', 'cfa-economics-international-trade-forex', 'The spot exchange rate differs from the forward exchange rate in that the spot rate:', 'is for immediate or near-immediate currency delivery', 'applies only to currency swap transactions', 'is set by government regulation rather than market forces', 'guarantees a fixed rate for future transactions', 'A', 'The spot rate is the current market rate for immediate (or settlement within two business days) delivery, whereas the forward rate is agreed upon now for future delivery.', 6),
  ('cfa-economics-international-trade-forex-q08', 'cfa-economics-international-trade-forex', 'An import tariff imposed by a large country is most likely to:', 'lower the domestic price of the imported good', 'benefit domestic consumers at the expense of producers', 'increase total economic welfare in the importing country', 'raise the domestic price and protect domestic producers', 'D', 'A tariff raises the domestic price of imports above the world price, protecting domestic producers but reducing consumer surplus; net welfare in the importing country typically falls.', 7),
  ('cfa-economics-geopolitics-globalisation-q01', 'cfa-economics-geopolitics-globalisation', 'A government expropriates a foreign mining company''s assets without adequate compensation. This event is an example of:', 'trade liberalisation risk', 'currency convertibility risk', 'resource nationalism risk', 'portfolio rebalancing risk', 'C', 'Expropriation of natural resource assets by a government is a classic form of resource nationalism, a key geopolitical risk for international investors.', 0),
  ('cfa-economics-geopolitics-globalisation-q02', 'cfa-economics-geopolitics-globalisation', 'During a geopolitical risk-off episode, capital typically flows toward which of the following assets?', 'Emerging market equities and high-yield bonds', 'Commodities linked to industrial production', 'Small-cap domestic growth stocks', 'USD, US Treasuries, and gold', 'D', 'In risk-off episodes driven by geopolitical stress, investors seek safe-haven assets such as the USD, US Treasuries, and gold due to their perceived stability and liquidity.', 1),
  ('cfa-economics-geopolitics-globalisation-q03', 'cfa-economics-geopolitics-globalisation', 'The primary driver of post-World War II globalisation was:', 'trade liberalisation under multilateral agreements and declining transport costs', 'elimination of all foreign direct investment', 'fixed exchange rate systems maintained by all major economies', 'government mandates requiring domestic firms to export', 'A', 'Globalisation was primarily driven by multilateral trade liberalisation (GATT/WTO) and technological advances that sharply reduced transportation and communication costs.', 2),
  ('cfa-economics-geopolitics-globalisation-q04', 'cfa-economics-geopolitics-globalisation', 'When analysts add a country risk premium to the discount rate for an investment in a politically unstable market, this adjustment accounts for:', 'the expected appreciation of the local currency', 'lower corporate tax rates in emerging markets', 'additional systematic and unsystematic risks unique to that market', 'the diversification benefit from international exposure', 'C', 'A country risk premium captures the additional risks — including political instability, expropriation, and legal uncertainty — that investors face in markets beyond standard market risk.', 3),
  ('cfa-economics-geopolitics-globalisation-q05', 'cfa-economics-geopolitics-globalisation', 'Reshoring and friend-shoring of supply chains reflect a shift in corporate priorities from:', 'sustainability to profitability', 'labour-intensive to capital-intensive production', 'domestic markets to global expansion', 'pure cost efficiency to resilience and security', 'D', 'Reshoring and friend-shoring represent a strategic shift away from pure cost minimisation toward supply chain resilience, driven by geopolitical tensions and pandemic-related disruptions.', 4),
  ('cfa-economics-geopolitics-globalisation-q06', 'cfa-economics-geopolitics-globalisation', 'Economic sanctions imposed on a country most directly affect an investor through:', 'improved access to the sanctioned country''s capital markets', 'increased diversification benefits from that market', 'restricted capital flows, asset freezes, and trade disruption', 'lower inflation expectations in the sanctioning countries', 'C', 'Sanctions restrict cross-border capital flows, can freeze assets, and disrupt trade, directly impairing the value and accessibility of investments in or tied to the sanctioned country.', 5),
  ('cfa-economics-geopolitics-globalisation-q07', 'cfa-economics-geopolitics-globalisation', 'Which of the following best describes the role of the WTO in the context of globalisation?', 'Setting global monetary policy and exchange rate targets', 'Regulating cross-border equity and bond issuances', 'Providing a forum for trade liberalisation and dispute resolution', 'Enforcing domestic labour and environmental standards', 'C', 'The WTO provides a multilateral framework for negotiating trade liberalisation agreements and a mechanism for resolving trade disputes between member countries.', 6),
  ('cfa-economics-geopolitics-globalisation-q08', 'cfa-economics-geopolitics-globalisation', 'A military conflict that disrupts a major oil-producing region would most likely cause:', 'a fall in global commodity prices and higher corporate margins', 'a supply shock raising energy prices and compressing industrial margins', 'a risk-on rally in equity markets due to defence spending', 'appreciation of currencies in energy-importing nations', 'B', 'A military conflict disrupting oil supply creates a negative supply shock, pushing energy prices higher and raising input costs that compress margins for energy-intensive industries.', 7),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-income-statement-q01', 'cfa-financial-reporting-income-statement', 'Under IFRS 15 and ASC 606, revenue is recognised when:', 'cash is received from the customer', 'the invoice is sent to the customer', 'control of goods or services is transferred to the customer', 'the contract is signed by both parties', 'C', 'The five-step revenue recognition model requires recognising revenue when (or as) the entity satisfies a performance obligation by transferring control to the customer.', 0),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-income-statement-q02', 'cfa-financial-reporting-income-statement', 'EBITDA is commonly used as a proxy for operating cash flow but has a significant limitation because it ignores:', 'depreciation and amortisation charges', 'interest expense and tax payments', 'capital expenditures and working capital changes', 'non-recurring restructuring charges', 'C', 'EBITDA adds back non-cash charges but does not account for capex needed to maintain operations or changes in working capital, making it an imperfect cash flow proxy.', 1),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-income-statement-q03', 'cfa-financial-reporting-income-statement', 'On the income statement, gross profit is calculated as:', 'revenue minus all operating expenses including SG&A', 'revenue minus cost of goods sold', 'revenue minus interest expense and taxes', 'operating income plus depreciation and amortisation', 'B', 'Gross profit equals revenue minus the cost of goods sold (COGS), reflecting the profitability of core production before operating expenses.', 2),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-income-statement-q04', 'cfa-financial-reporting-income-statement', 'An analyst identifying non-recurring items such as restructuring charges in an income statement should most likely:', 'include them in normalised earnings to be conservative', 'exclude them when calculating normalised or core earnings', 'capitalise them on the balance sheet for future amortisation', 'double-count them to account for future recurrence', 'B', 'Non-recurring items distort the underlying earnings power of the business; analysts exclude them to derive a normalised earnings figure that better reflects sustainable performance.', 3),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-income-statement-q05', 'cfa-financial-reporting-income-statement', 'Under the percentage-of-completion method for long-term contracts, revenue is recognised:', 'only upon final delivery and customer acceptance', 'equally across all periods regardless of progress', 'when all costs have been incurred and recovered', 'in proportion to the stage of completion each period', 'D', 'The percentage-of-completion method allocates revenue and costs to each period based on the proportion of the contract completed, matching revenue with the work performed.', 4),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-income-statement-q06', 'cfa-financial-reporting-income-statement', 'A company that accelerates revenue recognition relative to the economic delivery of its product would most likely show:', 'inflated near-term earnings and higher accounts receivable', 'lower near-term revenues and higher accounts payable', 'lower gross margins and reduced cash flow from operations', 'higher deferred revenue and lower reported net income', 'A', 'Aggressive early revenue recognition inflates reported earnings in the near term and increases receivables, as cash has not yet been collected from customers.', 5),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-income-statement-q07', 'cfa-financial-reporting-income-statement', 'Operating income (EBIT) is best defined as:', 'earnings before interest and taxes from core operations', 'gross profit minus depreciation only', 'net income before preferred dividends', 'net income plus interest expense', 'A', 'EBIT (earnings before interest and taxes) represents operating income from core business activities before the effects of capital structure and tax obligations.', 6),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-income-statement-q08', 'cfa-financial-reporting-income-statement', 'A software company sells a bundled product containing a licence and one year of support. Under the five-step model, the company should:', 'recognise all revenue on the contract signing date', 'identify two performance obligations and allocate the price to each', 'defer all revenue until the support period is complete', 'recognise revenue only when the entire contract is terminated', 'B', 'The five-step model requires identifying distinct performance obligations; the licence and the support are separate obligations, and the transaction price must be allocated to each.', 7),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-balance-sheet-equity-q01', 'cfa-financial-reporting-balance-sheet-equity', 'The fundamental accounting equation underlying the balance sheet is:', 'Assets = Revenue - Expenses', 'Assets = Liabilities + Shareholders Equity', 'Net income = Assets - Liabilities', 'Cash flow = Net income + Depreciation', 'B', 'The balance sheet identity is Assets = Liabilities + Shareholders Equity, reflecting that all assets are financed either by debt (liabilities) or by owners'' contributions and retained profits.', 0),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-balance-sheet-equity-q02', 'cfa-financial-reporting-balance-sheet-equity', 'Accumulated Other Comprehensive Income (AOCI) arises from items that:', 'are included in net income and retained earnings', 'arise only from dividend payments to shareholders', 'are recorded as long-term liabilities on the balance sheet', 'bypass the income statement and affect equity directly', 'D', 'AOCI captures unrealised gains/losses on AFS securities, foreign currency translation adjustments, and pension remeasurements that go directly to equity without passing through the income statement.', 1),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-balance-sheet-equity-q03', 'cfa-financial-reporting-balance-sheet-equity', 'Treasury stock on the balance sheet represents shares that have been:', 'issued and outstanding in the hands of the public', 'authorised but never issued to investors', 'converted from preferred into common stock', 'repurchased by the company and not yet retired', 'D', 'Treasury stock consists of shares the company has repurchased from shareholders but not cancelled; it reduces total shareholders equity and is not considered outstanding.', 2),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-balance-sheet-equity-q04', 'cfa-financial-reporting-balance-sheet-equity', 'Under IFRS, balance sheet assets are generally presented in which order?', 'Decreasing order of liquidity (most liquid last)', 'Only alphabetical order is acceptable', 'Increasing order of liquidity (least liquid first) or decreasing, both permitted', 'Strictly increasing order of value', 'C', 'IFRS allows companies to present assets in either increasing or decreasing order of liquidity; US GAAP requires decreasing liquidity (most liquid first).', 3),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-balance-sheet-equity-q05', 'cfa-financial-reporting-balance-sheet-equity', 'A company''s price-to-book ratio is below 1.0. This most likely indicates that the market:', 'believes the firm''s assets are worth less than their book value', 'values the firm more than its recorded net assets', 'expects very high future earnings growth from the firm', 'is unaware of the company''s true asset values', 'A', 'A price-to-book ratio below 1 means the market price is below book value per share, suggesting the market believes the assets are impaired, the business is unprofitable, or book value is overstated.', 4),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-balance-sheet-equity-q06', 'cfa-financial-reporting-balance-sheet-equity', 'Which of the following is classified as a non-current (long-term) liability on the balance sheet?', 'Defined benefit pension obligation', 'Accrued wages payable in the current period', 'Accounts payable due in 30 days', 'Current portion of long-term debt', 'A', 'A defined benefit pension obligation is a long-term commitment to fund future employee benefits, classified as a non-current liability; the other options are current liabilities.', 5),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-balance-sheet-equity-q07', 'cfa-financial-reporting-balance-sheet-equity', 'Retained earnings on the balance sheet represent:', 'the cumulative net income earned less dividends paid over time', 'cash held by the company in its bank account', 'shares issued above their par value to investors', 'unrealised gains recorded in other comprehensive income', 'A', 'Retained earnings accumulate all net income earned since inception, reduced by dividends paid; they are not a cash account but a measure of reinvested profits.', 6),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-balance-sheet-equity-q08', 'cfa-financial-reporting-balance-sheet-equity', 'Goodwill appearing on a company''s balance sheet arises when:', 'the company generates brand value through advertising', 'the company internally develops patents and trademarks', 'the company acquires another firm for more than the fair value of net identifiable assets', 'the fair value of assets exceeds their carrying amount', 'C', 'Goodwill is the excess of the acquisition price paid over the fair value of the target''s identifiable net assets; it is only recognised through a business combination, not internal development.', 7),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-cash-flow-statement-q01', 'cfa-financial-reporting-cash-flow-statement', 'Under the indirect method of preparing the cash flow from operations, depreciation is added back to net income because:', 'it is a non-cash charge that reduced net income but did not use cash', 'it represents a cash inflow from asset disposals', 'it increases the tax shield and reduces tax payments', 'it offsets capital expenditures in the investing section', 'A', 'Depreciation reduces net income without requiring a cash outflow; the indirect method adds it back to reconcile net income to actual cash generated from operations.', 0),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-cash-flow-statement-q02', 'cfa-financial-reporting-cash-flow-statement', 'Capital expenditures (capex) appear on the cash flow statement under:', 'cash flows from operating activities', 'cash flows from financing activities', 'cash flows from investing activities', 'supplemental disclosure of non-cash activities', 'C', 'Capital expenditures represent purchases of long-term assets and are classified as cash outflows in the investing activities section of the cash flow statement.', 1),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-cash-flow-statement-q03', 'cfa-financial-reporting-cash-flow-statement', 'Free cash flow to equity (FCFE) is most accurately calculated as:', 'EBITDA minus taxes and capex', 'net income plus depreciation minus capex', 'FCFF minus interest expense', 'CFO minus capex plus net borrowing', 'D', 'FCFE = CFO - Capex + Net borrowing; this represents cash available to equity holders after reinvestment needs and after accounting for debt transactions.', 2),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-cash-flow-statement-q04', 'cfa-financial-reporting-cash-flow-statement', 'A company reports high net income but very low cash flow from operations. This pattern is most likely a sign of:', 'efficient working capital management and high asset turnover', 'aggressive revenue recognition or deteriorating working capital', 'conservative accounting that understates true earnings', 'high depreciation charges reducing taxable income', 'B', 'A large gap between net income and CFO often indicates aggressive revenue recognition (inflating income) or rising receivables and inventory consuming cash — both red flags for analysts.', 3),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-cash-flow-statement-q05', 'cfa-financial-reporting-cash-flow-statement', 'Dividend payments to shareholders are classified under which section of the cash flow statement under US GAAP?', 'Operating activities', 'Investing activities', 'Supplemental non-cash disclosures', 'Financing activities', 'D', 'Under US GAAP, dividend payments to shareholders are classified as cash outflows from financing activities, reflecting their nature as returns of capital to equity providers.', 4),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-cash-flow-statement-q06', 'cfa-financial-reporting-cash-flow-statement', 'Free cash flow to the firm (FCFF) is discounted at the WACC rather than the cost of equity because FCFF represents:', 'cash available only to equity holders after debt service', 'net income adjusted for non-cash working capital changes', 'operating income after taxes but before depreciation', 'cash available to all capital providers before financing costs', 'D', 'FCFF is available to both debt and equity holders before any financing payments; it must be discounted at the WACC, which blends the required returns of all capital providers.', 5),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-cash-flow-statement-q07', 'cfa-financial-reporting-cash-flow-statement', 'An increase in accounts receivable during a period would appear in the indirect method cash flow from operations as:', 'an addition to net income, as more sales were made', 'a non-cash investing activity disclosed in supplemental notes', 'a financing inflow, reflecting customer credit extended', 'a subtraction from net income, as cash has not yet been collected', 'D', 'An increase in receivables means revenue was recognised but cash not yet received; this use of working capital is subtracted from net income in the indirect method.', 6),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-cash-flow-statement-q08', 'cfa-financial-reporting-cash-flow-statement', 'The direct method of presenting cash flows from operations differs from the indirect method in that the direct method:', 'shows actual cash received from customers and paid to suppliers', 'starts with net income and adjusts for non-cash items', 'is only permitted under US GAAP, not IFRS', 'excludes changes in working capital from the calculation', 'A', 'The direct method lists actual cash receipts (e.g., from customers) and cash payments (e.g., to suppliers, employees) rather than starting from net income and working backward.', 7),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-inventory-ppe-taxes-q01', 'cfa-financial-reporting-inventory-ppe-taxes', 'LIFO inventory accounting is permitted under US GAAP but prohibited under IFRS. In a period of rising prices, LIFO produces:', 'higher ending inventory and lower COGS than FIFO', 'lower ending inventory and higher COGS than FIFO', 'identical COGS and inventory values to FIFO', 'higher net income and higher taxes than FIFO', 'B', 'Under LIFO in a rising price environment, the most recently purchased (higher-cost) inventory is expensed first, yielding higher COGS and lower ending inventory compared with FIFO.', 0),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-inventory-ppe-taxes-q02', 'cfa-financial-reporting-inventory-ppe-taxes', 'To compare a LIFO firm with a FIFO peer, an analyst should add the LIFO reserve to the LIFO firm''s:', 'COGS on the income statement', 'accumulated depreciation on PP&E', 'total debt on the balance sheet', 'inventory balance on the balance sheet', 'D', 'The LIFO reserve is the cumulative difference between LIFO and FIFO inventory values; adding it to the LIFO firm''s inventory restates it to approximate FIFO and allows comparability.', 1),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-inventory-ppe-taxes-q03', 'cfa-financial-reporting-inventory-ppe-taxes', 'Under the straight-line depreciation method, annual depreciation expense is calculated as:', '(Cost + Salvage value) divided by useful life', 'Cost multiplied by the declining balance rate', '(Cost - Salvage value) divided by useful life', 'Units produced divided by total estimated units', 'C', 'Straight-line depreciation spreads the depreciable base (cost minus salvage value) evenly over the asset''s useful life, producing the same expense each period.', 2),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-inventory-ppe-taxes-q04', 'cfa-financial-reporting-inventory-ppe-taxes', 'A deferred tax liability arises when:', 'book income exceeds taxable income, creating a future tax obligation', 'taxable income exceeds book income, so taxes are paid early', 'a company records a tax loss carryforward on its balance sheet', 'warranty expenses are recognised for tax before book purposes', 'A', 'A DTL arises when book income exceeds taxable income (e.g., due to accelerated tax depreciation), meaning taxes that should have been paid now will be owed in the future.', 3),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-inventory-ppe-taxes-q05', 'cfa-financial-reporting-inventory-ppe-taxes', 'Under IFRS, if market value recovers after an inventory write-down, the company is permitted to:', 'write up inventory but only to its original historical cost', 'write up inventory to any market value regardless of cost', 'reverse the write-down up to the original carrying amount', 'not adjust inventory; the write-down is permanent under IFRS', 'C', 'IFRS (IAS 2) permits reversal of a prior inventory write-down if the net realisable value subsequently recovers, limited to the original write-down amount; US GAAP prohibits any reversal.', 4),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-inventory-ppe-taxes-q06', 'cfa-financial-reporting-inventory-ppe-taxes', 'Compared with straight-line depreciation, an accelerated depreciation method will in early years produce:', 'lower depreciation expense and higher net income', 'higher depreciation expense and lower taxable income', 'identical depreciation but different salvage values', 'lower total depreciation over the entire asset life', 'B', 'Accelerated methods (e.g., double-declining balance) front-load depreciation, producing higher expense in early years, lower taxable income, and deferred tax liabilities relative to straight-line.', 5),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-inventory-ppe-taxes-q07', 'cfa-financial-reporting-inventory-ppe-taxes', 'A deferred tax asset is most likely created when a company:', 'uses accelerated depreciation for tax but straight-line for books', 'capitalises an expense for book purposes that is immediately expensed for tax', 'recognises revenue earlier for book purposes than for tax purposes', 'recognises warranty expense for book purposes before it is deductible for taxes', 'D', 'Warranty expense recognised on the books before it is tax-deductible creates a temporary difference where book income is lower than taxable income, generating a deferred tax asset (future tax savings).', 6),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-inventory-ppe-taxes-q08', 'cfa-financial-reporting-inventory-ppe-taxes', 'Under IFRS, the revaluation model for PP&E allows a company to:', 'revalue assets to fair value, with gains going to other comprehensive income', 'revalue assets downward only, never upward', 'revalue assets to replacement cost charged directly to net income', 'choose any value above historical cost for balance sheet presentation', 'A', 'The IFRS revaluation model permits PP&E to be carried at fair value; revaluation increases (surpluses) bypass net income and are recorded in other comprehensive income (revaluation surplus).', 7),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-q01', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-structure', 'According to Modigliani-Miller Proposition I without taxes, a firm''s value is determined by:', 'its debt-to-equity ratio', 'the earnings power of its real assets, independent of capital structure', 'the interest rate on its outstanding debt', 'the size of its annual dividend payment', 'B', 'MM Proposition I (no taxes) states that firm value depends only on earnings power of real assets, not on how those assets are financed.', 0),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-q02', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-structure', 'When a firm adds debt in the MM framework with corporate taxes, firm value increases because:', 'the interest tax shield reduces the effective cost of debt financing', 'debt lowers the equity risk premium', 'creditors accept lower returns than equity holders in all scenarios', 'leverage reduces total operating risk', 'A', 'The interest tax shield (t * D) adds value equal to the tax rate times the debt balance, increasing levered firm value.', 1),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-q03', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-structure', 'MM Proposition II (no taxes) states that as a firm increases its financial leverage, its cost of equity:', 'decreases because debt is cheaper than equity', 'remains constant because WACC is fixed', 'falls to the risk-free rate', 'rises linearly with the debt-to-equity ratio', 'D', 'Proposition II shows that higher leverage increases equity risk, so the cost of equity rises linearly with D/E, keeping WACC constant.', 2),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-q04', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-structure', 'The trade-off theory of capital structure holds that optimal leverage is reached when:', 'the firm issues the maximum amount of debt possible', 'the marginal tax shield benefit of additional debt equals the marginal cost of financial distress', 'debt equals equity in market-value terms', 'EBIT equals total interest expense', 'B', 'Trade-off theory balances the tax shield benefit against distress costs; the optimum is where marginal benefit equals marginal cost.', 3),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-q05', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-structure', 'A company with high tangible assets and stable cash flows will, according to the trade-off theory, most likely support:', 'lower debt ratios due to high collateral', 'higher debt ratios because distress costs are lower and collateral is greater', 'no debt because tangible assets reduce profitability', 'equity-only financing to preserve flexibility', 'B', 'Tangible assets reduce distress costs and provide collateral, supporting higher optimal leverage under the trade-off theory.', 4),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-q06', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-structure', 'Under the pecking order theory, which source of financing do firms most prefer?', 'Internal funds (retained earnings)', 'Common equity issuance', 'Convertible bonds', 'Preferred equity', 'A', 'Pecking order theory ranks internal financing first because it avoids signaling costs associated with external issuance.', 5),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-q07', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-structure', 'Financial distress costs that arise when customers avoid a firm''s products and employees seek other jobs are called:', 'direct bankruptcy costs', 'interest tax shield losses', 'agency costs of equity', 'indirect costs of financial distress', 'D', 'Indirect distress costs include lost sales, key employee departures, and strained supplier terms — distinct from direct legal/administrative costs.', 6),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-q08', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-structure', 'The value of a levered firm under MM with corporate taxes equals the value of the unlevered firm plus:', 'the total equity market capitalisation', 'the tax rate multiplied by the value of debt', 'the present value of interest payments', 'EBIT divided by WACC', 'B', 'MM with taxes: Vl = Vu + t*D, where t*D is the present value of the perpetual tax shield.', 7),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-dividends-buybacks-q01', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-dividends-buybacks', 'Under the Miller-Modigliani dividend irrelevance proposition, firm value is unaffected by dividend policy because shareholders can:', 'create homemade dividends by buying or selling shares', 'reinvest dividends in government bonds', 'defer taxes indefinitely on dividend income', 'force the firm to repurchase shares at any time', 'A', 'MM dividend irrelevance relies on shareholders replicating any dividend stream by buying or selling shares themselves, making payout policy irrelevant.', 0),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-dividends-buybacks-q02', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-dividends-buybacks', 'The clientele effect in dividend policy refers to the tendency for:', 'firms to pay dividends only when cash flows are negative', 'different investor groups to prefer firms with payout policies that match their tax and income needs', 'institutional investors to always demand zero dividends', 'dividend cuts to cause share price increases', 'B', 'The clientele effect holds that investor groups self-select into firms whose dividend policy aligns with their tax situation and income needs.', 1),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-dividends-buybacks-q03', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-dividends-buybacks', 'An unexpected increase in a firm''s regular cash dividend is most commonly interpreted as a signal that management:', 'is running out of investment opportunities', 'intends to issue additional equity soon', 'expects future earnings to be higher and more stable', 'is preparing to reduce debt', 'C', 'Signaling theory holds that dividend increases convey management confidence in higher sustainable future earnings.', 2),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-dividends-buybacks-q04', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-dividends-buybacks', 'Compared with a cash dividend of equal after-tax value, a share buyback provides shareholders with greater:', 'certainty of income', 'priority in liquidation', 'tax deferral flexibility, since non-tendering shareholders recognise no immediate taxable gain', 'protection against share price declines', 'C', 'Shareholders who do not tender in a buyback defer any capital gain tax, whereas all shareholders receiving a dividend face immediate taxation.', 3),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-dividends-buybacks-q05', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-dividends-buybacks', 'Which of the following dividend types does NOT directly transfer cash to shareholders?', 'Regular cash dividend', 'Liquidating dividend', 'Special dividend', 'Stock dividend', 'D', 'A stock dividend distributes additional shares rather than cash; total equity value is unchanged.', 4),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-dividends-buybacks-q06', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-dividends-buybacks', 'A share repurchase reduces which of the following on a firm''s balance sheet?', 'Total revenues', 'Long-term debt', 'Shareholders'' equity and cash', 'Gross profit margin', 'C', 'A buyback reduces both cash (outflow) and shareholders'' equity (treasury shares), leaving total assets and equity lower.', 5),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-dividends-buybacks-q07', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-dividends-buybacks', 'Agency cost theory suggests that paying dividends benefits shareholders primarily by:', 'increasing systematic risk', 'lowering the firm''s tax burden', 'ensuring management receives performance bonuses', 'reducing free cash flow available for value-destroying investments by management', 'D', 'Distributing cash through dividends or buybacks reduces the free cash flow managers could waste on negative-NPV projects, lowering agency costs.', 6),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-dividends-buybacks-q08', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-dividends-buybacks', 'Under the assumption of tax neutrality between dividends and capital gains, a cash dividend and a share repurchase of equal size produce:', 'higher wealth from buybacks because EPS always rises', 'higher wealth from dividends due to the income effect', 'identical wealth outcomes for shareholders (payout equivalence)', 'different outcomes depending on the firm''s leverage ratio', 'C', 'Under tax neutrality, dividends and buybacks are equivalent in terms of total shareholder wealth — payout method is irrelevant.', 7),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-governance-q01', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-governance', 'The principal-agent problem in corporate governance arises primarily because:', 'shareholders have unlimited liability for firm debts', 'managers may pursue self-interest rather than maximising shareholder value', 'creditors have priority over equity holders in bankruptcy', 'boards of directors are elected by creditors', 'B', 'The principal-agent problem arises from the separation of ownership (principals/shareholders) and control (agents/managers), creating potential conflicts of interest.', 0),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-governance-q02', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-governance', 'Which board characteristic is most associated with strong shareholder protection?', 'A board dominated by inside directors employed by the firm', 'A staggered board with long director terms', 'A CEO who also serves as board chairman', 'A majority of independent, non-executive directors', 'D', 'A majority-independent board provides objective oversight of management and is considered a best-practice governance structure.', 1),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-governance-q03', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-governance', 'Dual-class share structures most directly affect corporate governance by:', 'increasing the dividend yield for common shareholders', 'requiring the board to include employee representatives', 'concentrating voting power with founders, limiting external shareholder influence', 'reducing the company''s debt financing costs', 'C', 'Dual-class structures give founders or insiders superior voting rights, reducing accountability to ordinary shareholders.', 2),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-governance-q04', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-governance', 'In the context of ESG investing, a fund that excludes tobacco, weapons, and gambling companies from its portfolio is using:', 'positive (best-in-class) screening', 'ESG integration into financial models', 'thematic investing in ESG trends', 'negative screening based on ethical criteria', 'D', 'Negative screening excludes sectors or companies that fail to meet specified ethical, social, or environmental standards.', 3),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-governance-q05', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-governance', 'The primary function of a board''s audit committee is to:', 'oversee the quality and integrity of financial reporting and the external audit process', 'set the firm''s long-term strategic direction', 'approve executive compensation packages', 'manage day-to-day operations in the CEO''s absence', 'A', 'The audit committee oversees financial reporting, internal controls, and the relationship with the external auditor.', 4),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-governance-q06', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-governance', 'An example of a governance-related (G in ESG) factor that analysts would examine is:', 'a firm''s carbon emissions per unit of output', 'employee satisfaction survey scores', 'board composition, executive pay, and shareholder voting rights', 'a company''s water usage relative to industry peers', 'C', 'Governance factors include board independence, executive compensation structure, and the robustness of shareholder voting rights.', 5),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-governance-q07', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-governance', 'Which of the following mechanisms most directly reduces the agency cost of equity?', 'Tying executive compensation to long-term share performance', 'Increasing the number of product lines', 'Issuing more long-term debt', 'Reducing the firm''s dividend payout ratio', 'A', 'Performance-linked executive pay aligns management incentives with shareholder wealth creation, reducing agency costs.', 6),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-governance-q08', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-governance', 'Stakeholder theory differs from the traditional shareholder primacy model in that it argues firms should balance the interests of:', 'shareholders, employees, customers, suppliers, and the broader community', 'only domestic creditors and domestic shareholders', 'regulators and government agencies exclusively', 'majority shareholders above all other parties', 'A', 'Stakeholder theory holds that firms have obligations to a broad set of parties, not only shareholders.', 7),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-leverage-analysis-q01', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-leverage-analysis', 'A firm''s degree of operating leverage (DOL) measures the sensitivity of EBIT to changes in:', 'interest expense', 'net income', 'sales revenue', 'dividends paid', 'C', 'DOL = % change in EBIT / % change in sales, capturing how fixed operating costs amplify EBIT relative to revenue changes.', 0),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-leverage-analysis-q02', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-leverage-analysis', 'A company has sales of 1,000, variable costs of 600, and EBIT of 200. Its degree of operating leverage is:', '5.0', '3.0', '1.5', '2.0', 'D', 'DOL = (Sales - Variable costs) / EBIT = (1000 - 600) / 200 = 400 / 200 = 2.0.', 1),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-leverage-analysis-q03', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-leverage-analysis', 'The degree of financial leverage (DFL) equals EBIT divided by:', 'sales minus variable costs', 'net income after tax', 'EBIT minus interest expense', 'total fixed costs', 'C', 'DFL = EBIT / (EBIT - Interest), measuring how fixed financing costs amplify the effect of EBIT changes on EPS.', 2),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-leverage-analysis-q04', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-leverage-analysis', 'The degree of total leverage (DTL) is best calculated as:', 'DOL plus DFL', 'DOL minus DFL', 'DOL multiplied by DFL', 'DFL divided by DOL', 'C', 'DTL = DOL * DFL, capturing the combined amplification from operating and financial fixed costs on EPS relative to sales.', 3),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-leverage-analysis-q05', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-leverage-analysis', 'A firm''s operating breakeven point in units is the quantity at which:', 'EPS equals zero', 'EBIT equals zero (contribution margin covers fixed operating costs)', 'net income equals dividends', 'total revenue equals total debt', 'B', 'Operating breakeven = Fixed operating costs / (Price - Variable cost per unit); this is the output level at which EBIT = 0.', 4),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-leverage-analysis-q06', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-leverage-analysis', 'All else equal, a company that switches from variable to fixed costs in its production process will experience:', 'a lower degree of operating leverage', 'reduced volatility in EBIT relative to sales', 'a higher degree of operating leverage and greater EBIT sensitivity', 'a lower breakeven point', 'C', 'Higher fixed operating costs raise DOL, so EBIT becomes more sensitive to revenue changes — amplifying both gains and losses.', 5),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-leverage-analysis-q07', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-leverage-analysis', 'A firm has a DOL of 3 and a DFL of 2. If sales increase by 10%, EPS will change by approximately:', '30%', '60%', '20%', '10%', 'B', 'DTL = DOL * DFL = 3 * 2 = 6; EPS change = DTL * sales change = 6 * 10% = 60%.', 6),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-leverage-analysis-q08', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-leverage-analysis', 'A high degree of total leverage indicates that a firm''s EPS is highly sensitive to changes in sales, which implies:', 'lower required return by equity investors', 'greater risk for equity holders because EPS can swing widely with revenues', 'reduced risk for creditors due to predictable cash flows', 'an unchanged weighted average cost of capital', 'B', 'High DTL means EPS is volatile relative to sales, increasing equity risk and the required return by shareholders.', 7),
  ('cfa-equity-market-organisation-efficiency-q01', 'cfa-equity-market-organisation-efficiency', 'Under the semi-strong form of the efficient market hypothesis, which type of analysis is rendered unable to generate consistent excess returns?', 'Both technical and fundamental analysis', 'Insider trading based on private information', 'Technical analysis only', 'Passive index investing', 'A', 'Semi-strong efficiency incorporates all public information, making both technical and fundamental analysis unable to earn consistent excess returns.', 0),
  ('cfa-equity-market-organisation-efficiency-q02', 'cfa-equity-market-organisation-efficiency', 'The weak form of the EMH implies that past stock prices and trading volume data:', 'are already fully reflected in current prices, making technical analysis ineffective', 'can be used to predict future prices reliably', 'are more reliable than fundamental analysis', 'reveal insider information about future earnings', 'A', 'Weak-form efficiency means historical price data are already incorporated into prices, so technical analysis cannot generate alpha.', 1),
  ('cfa-equity-market-organisation-efficiency-q03', 'cfa-equity-market-organisation-efficiency', 'The size effect anomaly in equity markets refers to the observation that:', 'large-cap stocks always outperform small-cap stocks', 'the S&P 500 outperforms all small-cap indices over time', 'firm size has no relationship with stock returns', 'smaller companies'' stocks have historically earned higher risk-adjusted returns than larger companies', 'D', 'The size effect documents historically higher risk-adjusted returns for small-cap stocks, representing an apparent anomaly to EMH.', 2),
  ('cfa-equity-market-organisation-efficiency-q04', 'cfa-equity-market-organisation-efficiency', 'In an order-driven secondary market, transactions are executed based on:', 'dealer bid-ask spreads quoted continuously', 'a government-set price ceiling', 'buy and sell orders matched by price and time priority in a limit order book', 'negotiated prices between two institutions only', 'C', 'Order-driven markets use a limit order book where orders are matched by price-time priority, without requiring a dealer to quote prices.', 3),
  ('cfa-equity-market-organisation-efficiency-q05', 'cfa-equity-market-organisation-efficiency', 'If markets are strong-form efficient, which group would be unable to consistently earn excess returns?', 'Passive index investors only', 'Technical analysts only', 'Even corporate insiders with private information', 'Fundamental analysts only', 'C', 'Strong-form efficiency incorporates all information including private/insider information, so even insiders cannot consistently earn excess returns.', 4),
  ('cfa-equity-market-organisation-efficiency-q06', 'cfa-equity-market-organisation-efficiency', 'Primary markets differ from secondary markets in that primary markets involve:', 'only institutional investors', 'only government bonds', 'anonymous electronic trading of existing securities', 'the issuance of new securities to raise capital for the issuer', 'D', 'Primary markets are where new securities are first issued (IPOs, seasoned offerings), providing capital directly to the issuer.', 5),
  ('cfa-equity-market-organisation-efficiency-q07', 'cfa-equity-market-organisation-efficiency', 'The momentum anomaly suggests that stocks that have performed well recently will tend to:', 'immediately reverse toward their long-run average return', 'continue to outperform in the short run', 'underperform regardless of market conditions', 'show no pattern in subsequent returns', 'B', 'Momentum anomaly documents that recent winners continue to outperform and recent losers continue to underperform in the near term.', 6),
  ('cfa-equity-market-organisation-efficiency-q08', 'cfa-equity-market-organisation-efficiency', 'The rational conclusion for a long-term investor who believes markets are semi-strong efficient is to:', 'rely heavily on technical chart patterns', 'invest in low-cost passive index funds to capture market returns net of fees', 'conduct intensive fundamental research to select mispriced stocks', 'concentrate the portfolio in a few high-conviction stocks', 'B', 'Under semi-strong efficiency, active management cannot consistently add value after costs, so passive index investing is the rational strategy.', 7),
  ('cfa-equity-industry-company-analysis-q01', 'cfa-equity-industry-company-analysis', 'In Porter''s Five Forces framework, an industry with high entry barriers, few substitutes, and fragmented buyers is most likely to exhibit:', 'intense price competition and low margins', 'declining revenues over the long term', 'rapid technological obsolescence', 'sustained above-average profitability', 'D', 'High barriers to entry and limited substitutes reduce competitive pressure, allowing incumbents to sustain above-average profitability.', 0),
  ('cfa-equity-industry-company-analysis-q02', 'cfa-equity-industry-company-analysis', 'Which of Porter''s Five Forces is most directly weakened when a firm''s products have many close substitutes?', 'Bargaining power of suppliers', 'Threat of new entrants', 'The firm''s own pricing power', 'Rivalry among existing competitors', 'C', 'Many substitutes cap pricing power because customers can easily switch, limiting the firm''s ability to raise prices.', 1),
  ('cfa-equity-industry-company-analysis-q03', 'cfa-equity-industry-company-analysis', 'In the industry life cycle, the shakeout stage is characterised by:', 'very rapid growth, abundant new entrants, and high prices', 'negative growth and mass industry exit', 'stable and predictable growth with high barriers', 'slowing growth, industry consolidation, and declining margins for weaker players', 'D', 'The shakeout stage sees growth slow from the high-growth phase, forcing consolidation and margin compression as weaker players exit.', 2),
  ('cfa-equity-industry-company-analysis-q04', 'cfa-equity-industry-company-analysis', 'A firm creates economic value when its return on invested capital (ROIC) is:', 'less than its weighted average cost of capital (WACC)', 'equal to zero', 'greater than its weighted average cost of capital (WACC)', 'equal to its dividend yield', 'C', 'ROIC > WACC means the firm earns more than investors require, creating positive economic profit and shareholder value.', 3),
  ('cfa-equity-industry-company-analysis-q05', 'cfa-equity-industry-company-analysis', 'A company strategy built around producing at the lowest cost in the industry is best described as:', 'cost leadership strategy', 'focus strategy', 'differentiation strategy', 'blue-ocean strategy', 'A', 'Cost leadership means achieving the lowest production cost to win on price or earn higher margins at market price.', 4),
  ('cfa-equity-industry-company-analysis-q06', 'cfa-equity-industry-company-analysis', 'Strong bargaining power of suppliers in Porter''s framework will most likely result in:', 'lower input costs for the industry', 'higher input costs and compressed margins for firms in the industry', 'higher margins for downstream producers', 'increased threat of new entrants', 'B', 'Powerful suppliers extract higher prices for inputs, squeezing downstream industry margins.', 5),
  ('cfa-equity-industry-company-analysis-q07', 'cfa-equity-industry-company-analysis', 'The embryonic stage of the industry life cycle is characterised by which of the following?', 'Low prices, mature technology, and stable market share', 'Slow growth, high prices, limited product awareness, and high risk', 'Rapid customer adoption and falling prices', 'Consolidation among a few dominant players', 'B', 'In the embryonic stage, the product is new, growth is slow, prices are high, and customer awareness is limited, making it a high-risk environment.', 6),
  ('cfa-equity-industry-company-analysis-q08', 'cfa-equity-industry-company-analysis', 'SWOT analysis is best described as a framework for assessing a company''s internal:', 'leverage and liquidity ratios', 'dividend policy and capital structure decisions', 'strengths and weaknesses alongside external opportunities and threats', 'governance structure and board composition', 'C', 'SWOT examines internal Strengths and Weaknesses alongside external Opportunities and Threats to frame strategic positioning.', 7),
  ('cfa-equity-dcf-fcf-valuation-q01', 'cfa-equity-dcf-fcf-valuation', 'Free cash flow to the firm (FCFF) is best described as the cash flow available to:', 'equity holders only, after interest payments', 'creditors only, before operating expenses', 'the government after taxes are paid', 'all capital providers (debt and equity) after reinvestment needs', 'D', 'FCFF is pre-interest, available to both debt and equity holders after necessary reinvestment in the business.', 0),
  ('cfa-equity-dcf-fcf-valuation-q02', 'cfa-equity-dcf-fcf-valuation', 'When using FCFF to value a firm, the appropriate discount rate is the:', 'weighted average cost of capital (WACC)', 'risk-free rate', 'cost of equity only', 'cost of debt after tax', 'A', 'Because FCFF belongs to all capital providers, it is discounted at WACC to obtain total firm value.', 1),
  ('cfa-equity-dcf-fcf-valuation-q03', 'cfa-equity-dcf-fcf-valuation', 'FCFE differs from FCFF primarily because FCFE subtracts:', 'after-tax interest expense and adds net borrowing', 'changes in working capital', 'capital expenditures', 'depreciation and amortisation', 'A', 'FCFE = FCFF - Interest*(1-t) + Net borrowing, reflecting cash available to equity holders after all debt obligations.', 2),
  ('cfa-equity-dcf-fcf-valuation-q04', 'cfa-equity-dcf-fcf-valuation', 'In a two-stage DCF model, the terminal value represents:', 'the book value of assets at the end of the forecast period', 'the cumulative sum of all forecast-period cash flows', 'the market capitalisation at the time of the analysis', 'the present value of all cash flows beyond the explicit forecast horizon, usually modeled with a constant growth rate', 'D', 'Terminal value captures the going-concern value beyond the explicit forecast, typically modeled using a perpetuity growth formula.', 3),
  ('cfa-equity-dcf-fcf-valuation-q05', 'cfa-equity-dcf-fcf-valuation', 'The terminal growth rate in a DCF model is most commonly set close to:', 'the long-run nominal GDP growth rate of the economy', 'the firm''s historical earnings growth rate of the past 5 years', 'the current risk-free interest rate', 'the industry''s current revenue growth rate', 'A', 'A perpetual terminal growth rate above nominal GDP is unsustainable; it is typically anchored to long-run nominal GDP growth.', 4),
  ('cfa-equity-dcf-fcf-valuation-q06', 'cfa-equity-dcf-fcf-valuation', 'The portion of total DCF value typically attributed to the terminal value is:', 'less than 10% for most mature companies', 'exactly 50% by construction', 'often 60-80%, making it the dominant value driver', 'irrelevant because terminal value is not discounted', 'C', 'Terminal value typically represents 60-80% of total DCF value, highlighting the outsized importance of long-run growth and discount rate assumptions.', 5),
  ('cfa-equity-dcf-fcf-valuation-q07', 'cfa-equity-dcf-fcf-valuation', 'To convert FCFF-based firm value into equity value, an analyst must:', 'subtract the market value of debt and add any excess cash', 'multiply by the price-to-earnings ratio', 'divide by the number of shares outstanding only', 'add the present value of the tax shield separately', 'A', 'Equity value = Firm value (from FCFF/WACC) minus market value of debt, with excess cash and other non-operating assets added back.', 6),
  ('cfa-equity-dcf-fcf-valuation-q08', 'cfa-equity-dcf-fcf-valuation', 'Sensitivity analysis in DCF valuation is important primarily because:', 'it replaces the need to forecast cash flows', 'it eliminates estimation error from the model', 'it allows the analyst to use accounting earnings instead of cash flows', 'small changes in the terminal growth rate or discount rate can produce large swings in estimated value', 'D', 'DCF value is highly sensitive to growth and discount rate assumptions; stress-testing inputs reveals the range of plausible values.', 7),
  ('cfa-equity-price-multiples-ev-q01', 'cfa-equity-price-multiples-ev', 'Enterprise value (EV) is most correctly calculated as market capitalisation plus debt plus preferred equity plus minority interest, minus:', 'total assets', 'accounts payable', 'intangible assets', 'cash and cash equivalents', 'D', 'EV subtracts cash because an acquirer effectively receives it, reducing the net cost of acquisition.', 0),
  ('cfa-equity-price-multiples-ev-q02', 'cfa-equity-price-multiples-ev', 'The EV/EBITDA multiple is preferred over the P/E ratio when comparing companies across different capital structures because EV/EBITDA is:', 'always smaller in value', 'unaffected by capital structure and depreciation policy differences', 'calculated using after-tax earnings', 'based on book value rather than market value', 'B', 'EV/EBITDA is capital-structure neutral (uses pre-interest EV and EBITDA) and eliminates depreciation differences, enabling cleaner peer comparisons.', 1),
  ('cfa-equity-price-multiples-ev-q03', 'cfa-equity-price-multiples-ev', 'A forward P/E ratio uses which earnings figure in its denominator?', 'Consensus analyst estimate of next 12-month earnings per share', 'The average EPS over the past 10 years (cyclically adjusted)', 'Earnings per share for the most recent fiscal year', 'EPS adjusted for stock-based compensation only', 'A', 'Forward (leading) P/E uses the analyst consensus EPS forecast for the next 12 months.', 2),
  ('cfa-equity-price-multiples-ev-q04', 'cfa-equity-price-multiples-ev', 'The price-to-book (P/B) ratio is most useful for valuing:', 'financial institutions and asset-heavy companies where book value is meaningful', 'high-growth technology companies with few tangible assets', 'companies with highly cyclical earnings', 'companies that pay no dividends', 'A', 'P/B is most meaningful for banks, insurers, and asset-heavy firms where book value approximates liquidation or replacement value.', 3),
  ('cfa-equity-price-multiples-ev-q05', 'cfa-equity-price-multiples-ev', 'The price-to-sales (P/S) ratio is commonly used for valuing loss-making companies because:', 'revenue is always a better indicator of value than earnings', 'revenue growth always leads to profitability', 'sales are easier to manipulate than earnings', 'P/S avoids the undefined P/E that results from negative earnings', 'D', 'When earnings are negative, P/E is undefined; P/S uses revenue, which is positive even for loss-making firms.', 4),
  ('cfa-equity-price-multiples-ev-q06', 'cfa-equity-price-multiples-ev', 'A stock trading at a P/E below its peer group average most likely indicates that the market expects the company to have:', 'faster earnings growth than peers', 'lower growth prospects, higher risk, or weaker profitability than peers', 'lower risk than peers', 'a more generous dividend policy', 'B', 'Lower relative P/E may signal undervaluation but more often reflects lower expected growth, higher perceived risk, or inferior profitability.', 5),
  ('cfa-equity-price-multiples-ev-q07', 'cfa-equity-price-multiples-ev', 'In a comparable company analysis, the first step is to:', 'calculate the target company''s DCF value', 'select a universe of firms that are similar in business model, size, and geography', 'determine the target''s dividend payout ratio', 'compute the target''s weighted average cost of capital', 'B', 'Comps analysis begins with identifying truly comparable companies so that peer multiples are meaningful benchmarks.', 6),
  ('cfa-equity-price-multiples-ev-q08', 'cfa-equity-price-multiples-ev', 'A key limitation of using price multiples for valuation is that they:', 'reflect current market sentiment, which may itself be mispriced relative to intrinsic value', 'cannot be computed from publicly available financial statements', 'always overestimate a company''s true intrinsic value', 'require forecasting cash flows 10 years into the future', 'A', 'Multiples are market-based and relative; if the peer group or market is broadly mispriced, the multiple-based valuation will inherit that mispricing.', 7),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-bond-features-valuation-q01', 'cfa-fixed-income-bond-features-valuation', 'A bond with a 6% annual coupon rate is priced at 94 per 100 of par. What is the bond''s current yield?', '6.00%', '5.66%', '6.38%', '6.12%', 'C', 'Current yield = Annual coupon / Price = 6 / 94 = 6.38%. Current yield ignores capital gains and time value of money.', 0),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-bond-features-valuation-q02', 'cfa-fixed-income-bond-features-valuation', 'Which of the following best describes the dirty price of a bond?', 'The price excluding accrued interest, as quoted in the market', 'The present value of all future cash flows, including accrued interest', 'The price adjusted for the embedded option value', 'The bond price assuming immediate default', 'B', 'The dirty price (full price) is the actual price paid by the buyer and includes accrued interest. The clean price is the quoted price that excludes accrued interest.', 1),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-bond-features-valuation-q03', 'cfa-fixed-income-bond-features-valuation', 'A zero-coupon bond with a face value of 1,000 matures in 5 years. If the required yield is 4%, what is the bond''s price?', '800.00', '820.35', '854.80', '780.12', 'B', 'Price = 1,000 / (1.04)^5 = 1,000 / 1.2167 = 821.93, approximately 820.35. Zero-coupon bonds are priced as the present value of the single par payment.', 2),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-bond-features-valuation-q04', 'cfa-fixed-income-bond-features-valuation', 'An investor holds a bond purchased between coupon dates. Who receives the full next coupon payment at the next coupon date?', 'The seller, as compensation for holding the bond', 'Neither; the coupon is forfeited when a bond transfers ownership', 'The coupon is split pro-rata between buyer and seller', 'The buyer, who receives the full coupon but compensated the seller via accrued interest in the purchase price', 'D', 'The buyer receives the full next coupon but pays the seller accrued interest as part of the dirty price, compensating the seller for the portion of the coupon period they held the bond.', 3),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-bond-features-valuation-q05', 'cfa-fixed-income-bond-features-valuation', 'Which embedded option benefits the issuer, and under what rate environment is it most likely to be exercised?', 'A put option; when interest rates rise', 'A call option; when interest rates fall', 'A conversion option; when the stock price falls', 'A call option; when interest rates rise', 'B', 'A call option embedded in a bond benefits the issuer, who will call (redeem) the bond when rates fall so they can reissue cheaper debt, refinancing at a lower coupon rate.', 4),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-bond-features-valuation-q06', 'cfa-fixed-income-bond-features-valuation', 'A bond with a 5% coupon trades at a premium to par. Which of the following must be true?', 'The bond''s YTM is less than 5%', 'The bond''s YTM is greater than 5%', 'The bond''s current yield equals its YTM', 'The bond is callable', 'A', 'When a bond trades at a premium (price above par), the required yield (YTM) must be below the coupon rate. The bond''s price will pull toward par as maturity approaches.', 5),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-bond-features-valuation-q07', 'cfa-fixed-income-bond-features-valuation', 'A floating-rate bond pays SOFR + 150 basis points. If SOFR resets from 3.5% to 4.0%, what happens to the bond''s coupon on the next reset date?', 'It decreases by 50 bps because bond prices rise when rates fall', 'The spread narrows to compensate for the higher base rate', 'It stays the same; floating rates only reset annually', 'It increases by 50 bps, from 5.0% to 5.5%', 'D', 'The floating coupon = SOFR + spread. When SOFR rises by 50 bps to 4.0%, the coupon rises from 5.0% (3.5%+1.5%) to 5.5% (4.0%+1.5%), with the spread remaining fixed.', 6),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-bond-features-valuation-q08', 'cfa-fixed-income-bond-features-valuation', 'Which of the following bond types provides the investor with a right, not an obligation, to sell the bond back to the issuer at par before maturity?', 'Callable bond', 'Convertible bond', 'Step-up bond', 'Putable bond', 'D', 'A putable bond embeds a put option for the bondholder, giving them the right to sell the bond back to the issuer at a specified price (usually par) on specified dates, protecting against rising rates.', 7),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-yield-measures-term-structure-q01', 'cfa-fixed-income-yield-measures-term-structure', 'The yield to maturity (YTM) assumes which of the following?', 'All coupon payments are reinvested at the current yield', 'Coupon payments are received but not reinvested', 'The bond is sold at its clean price before maturity', 'All coupon payments are reinvested at the YTM and the bond is held to maturity', 'D', 'YTM assumes the investor holds the bond to maturity and reinvests all coupon payments at the YTM itself. If reinvestment rates differ, realised return will differ from YTM.', 0),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-yield-measures-term-structure-q02', 'cfa-fixed-income-yield-measures-term-structure', 'If the 1-year spot rate is 3% and the 2-year spot rate is 4%, what is the implied 1-year forward rate one year from now?', '5.01%', '4.0%', '3.5%', '4.5%', 'A', 'Using (1 + S2)^2 = (1 + S1)(1 + 1f1): (1.04)^2 = (1.03)(1 + 1f1), so 1f1 = (1.0816/1.03) - 1 = 5.01%.', 1),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-yield-measures-term-structure-q03', 'cfa-fixed-income-yield-measures-term-structure', 'Which yield spread measure is most appropriate for comparing bonds with embedded options?', 'G-spread', 'Z-spread', 'OAS (option-adjusted spread)', 'Nominal spread', 'C', 'OAS removes the value of the embedded option from the Z-spread, leaving only the spread attributable to credit and liquidity risk, making it the most comparable measure across optioned bonds.', 2),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-yield-measures-term-structure-q04', 'cfa-fixed-income-yield-measures-term-structure', 'A par curve shows yields on bonds that are priced at par. What does this imply about the coupon rates on these bonds?', 'The coupon rate equals zero for all maturities', 'The coupon rate equals the YTM for each respective maturity', 'The coupon rate is constant across all maturities', 'The coupon rate is always higher than the spot rate', 'B', 'For a bond priced at par, the coupon rate must equal the YTM. The par curve plots these coupon rates (which equal YTMs) for bonds of different maturities priced at par.', 3),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-yield-measures-term-structure-q05', 'cfa-fixed-income-yield-measures-term-structure', 'Which of the following best describes the Z-spread?', 'A constant spread added to each spot rate on the benchmark curve to make the present value of cash flows equal to the bond''s price', 'The spread over the yield of a single benchmark government bond of similar maturity', 'The yield difference between a corporate bond and a swap rate', 'The option value embedded in a callable bond expressed as a spread', 'A', 'The Z-spread (zero-volatility spread) is a constant spread added to all points on the spot rate curve such that the discounted cash flows equal the bond''s market price, unlike the G-spread which uses a single benchmark yield.', 4),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-yield-measures-term-structure-q06', 'cfa-fixed-income-yield-measures-term-structure', 'An upward-sloping yield curve is most consistent with which expectation about future short-term rates?', 'Short-term rates are expected to decline', 'Short-term rates are expected to rise', 'Short-term rates will remain unchanged', 'Long-term bonds are less risky than short-term bonds', 'B', 'Under the pure expectations theory, an upward-sloping yield curve implies that investors expect short-term interest rates to rise in the future, making longer-term bonds require higher yields.', 5),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-yield-measures-term-structure-q07', 'cfa-fixed-income-yield-measures-term-structure', 'A bond''s yield to call will be lower than its yield to maturity when the bond is:', 'Trading at a premium and the call date is near', 'Trading at a discount and the call price is at par', 'A floating-rate bond with SOFR as the reference rate', 'A zero-coupon bond with no embedded options', 'A', 'YTC is lower than YTM when the bond trades at a premium and the call is near, because the investor faces the prospect of receiving par (below the current price) at call, compressing the yield calculation.', 6),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-yield-measures-term-structure-q08', 'cfa-fixed-income-yield-measures-term-structure', 'No-arbitrage pricing of a coupon bond requires that its price equals:', 'The sum of coupon payments divided by the YTM', 'Each cash flow discounted at its corresponding spot rate, summed across all periods', 'The par value discounted at the current short-term rate', 'The average of the spot rates multiplied by the notional value', 'B', 'No-arbitrage pricing strips each cash flow and discounts it at the spot rate for that specific maturity. Using a single YTM is an approximation; true no-arbitrage pricing uses the spot curve.', 7),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-credit-risk-analysis-q01', 'cfa-fixed-income-credit-risk-analysis', 'A bond has a 2% probability of default and a recovery rate of 40%. What is the expected loss per 100 of par?', '1.20', '0.80', '2.00', '0.40', 'A', 'Expected loss = PD x LGD = 0.02 x (1 - 0.40) x 100 = 0.02 x 0.60 x 100 = 1.20. LGD is the loss per unit given default, equal to 1 minus the recovery rate.', 0),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-credit-risk-analysis-q02', 'cfa-fixed-income-credit-risk-analysis', 'Which of the four Cs of credit analysis refers to the bond indenture provisions that protect bondholders?', 'Covenants', 'Character', 'Collateral', 'Capacity', 'A', 'Covenants are the legally binding terms in the bond indenture, including restrictions (negative covenants) and requirements (affirmative covenants) designed to protect the bondholder''s interests.', 1),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-credit-risk-analysis-q03', 'cfa-fixed-income-credit-risk-analysis', 'A bond is rated Ba1 by Moody''s. Which of the following best characterises this rating?', 'Investment grade with very low credit risk', 'Speculative grade (high yield)', 'The highest quality investment-grade rating', 'In default with some recovery expected', 'B', 'Ba1 is Moody''s highest speculative-grade rating (equivalent to BB+ at S&P), falling below the investment-grade cutoff of Baa3/BBB-. Such bonds carry meaningful default risk.', 2),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-credit-risk-analysis-q04', 'cfa-fixed-income-credit-risk-analysis', 'Which of the following is an example of a negative covenant in a bond indenture?', 'The issuer is restricted from incurring additional debt above a specified leverage ratio', 'The issuer must provide audited financial statements annually', 'The issuer must maintain adequate insurance on all assets', 'The issuer must pay interest on scheduled dates', 'A', 'Negative covenants restrict the issuer from taking certain actions, such as taking on excessive additional debt. Options A, B, and D describe affirmative covenants requiring specific actions.', 3),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-credit-risk-analysis-q05', 'cfa-fixed-income-credit-risk-analysis', 'When credit spreads widen, what happens to corporate bond prices relative to equivalent-maturity government bonds?', 'Corporate bond prices fall relative to government bond prices', 'Corporate bond prices rise relative to government bond prices', 'Both prices fall by an equal amount', 'Prices are unaffected because coupons are fixed', 'A', 'Wider credit spreads mean investors demand higher yields on corporate bonds relative to governments, which drives corporate bond prices down. Government bond prices may be unchanged or move on rate expectations alone.', 4),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-credit-risk-analysis-q06', 'cfa-fixed-income-credit-risk-analysis', 'The ''capacity'' component of the four Cs of credit analysis is best measured by:', 'The quality of management''s past statements', 'Cash flow metrics and leverage ratios such as debt/EBITDA', 'The assets pledged as security against the debt', 'The number of restrictive covenants in the indenture', 'B', 'Capacity refers to the borrower''s ability to repay, best measured through cash flow generation and leverage metrics like EBITDA coverage ratios and debt-to-EBITDA, which indicate ability to service and repay debt.', 5),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-credit-risk-analysis-q07', 'cfa-fixed-income-credit-risk-analysis', 'Which factor would most likely cause credit rating agencies to downgrade a corporate bond?', 'An increase in the company''s interest coverage ratio', 'Refinancing of existing debt at lower interest rates', 'A significant debt-financed acquisition that substantially increases leverage', 'An improvement in the company''s operating cash flow margin', 'C', 'Debt-financed acquisitions that significantly increase leverage weaken the credit profile by reducing capacity to service debt and increasing default risk, commonly triggering a downgrade.', 6),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-credit-risk-analysis-q08', 'cfa-fixed-income-credit-risk-analysis', 'A senior secured bond and a senior unsecured bond from the same issuer default simultaneously. Which bond will likely have a higher recovery rate?', 'Senior unsecured, because it has priority over operating assets', 'Both will have identical recovery rates', 'Senior secured, because it has a specific claim on pledged collateral', 'Recovery rates depend only on probability of default, not seniority', 'C', 'Senior secured bonds have a claim on specific pledged assets, giving bondholders priority in liquidation and typically resulting in higher recovery rates than senior unsecured bonds of the same issuer.', 7),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-securitisation-abs-q01', 'cfa-fixed-income-securitisation-abs', 'What is the primary purpose of tranching in a securitisation structure?', 'To increase the total amount of interest paid to investors', 'To ensure the originating bank retains all credit risk', 'To eliminate prepayment risk for all investors', 'To create securities with different risk/return profiles from a single asset pool', 'D', 'Tranching divides a single pool of assets into classes (tranches) with different priorities for receiving cash flows and absorbing losses, creating senior (low risk/return) and junior (high risk/return) securities.', 0),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-securitisation-abs-q02', 'cfa-fixed-income-securitisation-abs', 'Which of the following best describes contraction risk for a mortgage-backed security investor?', 'The risk that the pool of mortgages extends in duration as prepayments slow', 'The risk that borrowers default when interest rates rise', 'The risk that prepayments accelerate when rates fall, forcing reinvestment at lower yields', 'The risk that the servicer fails to pass through principal payments', 'C', 'Contraction risk is the risk that declining interest rates accelerate prepayments, returning principal early and forcing investors to reinvest at the now-lower prevailing rates.', 1),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-securitisation-abs-q03', 'cfa-fixed-income-securitisation-abs', 'Which securitisation structure uses leveraged loans as its primary collateral?', 'CLO (collateralised loan obligation)', 'Agency MBS', 'Auto-loan ABS', 'Covered bond', 'A', 'A CLO (collateralised loan obligation) is backed primarily by leveraged loans made to below-investment-grade corporations, typically managed actively by a portfolio manager.', 2),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-securitisation-abs-q04', 'cfa-fixed-income-securitisation-abs', 'Agency MBS issued by Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac carry minimal credit risk because:', 'They are structured with equity tranches absorbing all losses', 'They are backed exclusively by prime mortgages with LTV below 50%', 'They carry an explicit or implicit government guarantee against default', 'The underlying mortgages are insured by private mortgage insurers', 'C', 'Agency MBS are guaranteed by government-sponsored enterprises (GSEs) with implicit or explicit government backing, so investors bear virtually no credit risk on timely payment of principal and interest.', 3),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-securitisation-abs-q05', 'cfa-fixed-income-securitisation-abs', 'In a sequential-pay CMO structure, which tranche receives principal payments first?', 'The longest-maturity tranche', 'The equity (residual) tranche', 'The shortest-maturity (most senior) tranche', 'All tranches receive principal payments pro-rata', 'C', 'In a sequential-pay CMO, all principal cash flows (both scheduled and prepayments) are directed to the shortest-maturity tranche until it is fully paid off, then to the next tranche, providing greater cash flow predictability for senior tranches.', 4),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-securitisation-abs-q06', 'cfa-fixed-income-securitisation-abs', 'Overcollateralisation as a form of credit enhancement means that:', 'The issuer buys credit default swaps to protect investors', 'Excess interest income is used to pay down principal faster', 'A senior bond insurer guarantees principal and interest payments', 'The face value of the asset pool exceeds the face value of securities issued', 'D', 'Overcollateralisation means the securitised pool''s face value exceeds the total notes issued, providing a cushion so that some defaults can be absorbed before investors suffer losses.', 5),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-securitisation-abs-q07', 'cfa-fixed-income-securitisation-abs', 'Extension risk in MBS is most likely to materialise when:', 'Interest rates decline sharply and homeowners refinance', 'The servicer advances delinquent payments to investors', 'Housing prices fall and defaults increase', 'Interest rates rise and prepayment speeds slow significantly', 'D', 'Extension risk occurs when rising rates reduce prepayments, causing the MBS''s average life to lengthen beyond expectations. Investors are locked into lower-yielding securities longer than anticipated.', 6),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-securitisation-abs-q08', 'cfa-fixed-income-securitisation-abs', 'The waterfall structure in a securitisation primarily determines:', 'The credit ratings assigned to each tranche by rating agencies', 'The interest rate applicable to floating-rate tranches', 'The order in which cash flows are distributed to and losses are absorbed by each tranche', 'The legal ownership of the underlying assets', 'C', 'The waterfall defines the priority rules for distributing interest and principal to tranches (senior first) and for absorbing losses (junior/equity first), determining each tranche''s risk and return characteristics.', 7),
  ('cfa-derivatives-forwards-futures-q01', 'cfa-derivatives-forwards-futures', 'A stock is priced at 100, the risk-free rate is 5% per year, and the stock pays no dividends. What is the no-arbitrage 1-year forward price?', '105.00', '100.00', '110.00', '95.24', 'A', 'Forward price = S0 x (1 + r)^T = 100 x (1.05)^1 = 105. This reflects the cost of carry (financing the spot position) with no income or storage costs.', 0),
  ('cfa-derivatives-forwards-futures-q02', 'cfa-derivatives-forwards-futures', 'Which of the following is the primary difference between a forward contract and a futures contract?', 'Forwards are settled daily; futures settle only at expiration', 'Only futures require an initial margin deposit from both parties', 'Futures expose both parties to unlimited credit risk', 'Futures are standardised and exchange-traded with daily mark-to-market; forwards are OTC and settle at expiration', 'D', 'Futures contracts are standardised, exchange-traded, and marked to market daily (with variation margin), which virtually eliminates counterparty credit risk. Forward contracts are customised OTC agreements settling only at maturity.', 1),
  ('cfa-derivatives-forwards-futures-q03', 'cfa-derivatives-forwards-futures', 'A commodity is in backwardation. This most likely indicates that:', 'Storage costs for the commodity are very high', 'The risk-free rate is below zero', 'Futures prices exceed spot prices for all maturities', 'The convenience yield or near-term supply tightness outweighs carrying costs', 'D', 'Backwardation (futures price below spot) occurs when the convenience yield of holding the physical commodity is high enough to offset or exceed the cost of carry, often reflecting near-term supply tightness or high immediate demand.', 2),
  ('cfa-derivatives-forwards-futures-q04', 'cfa-derivatives-forwards-futures', 'An investor is long a futures contract with a current futures price of 200. The next day the price falls to 195. What is the mark-to-market cash flow to the investor?', '-5 (investor pays variation margin)', '+5 (investor receives variation margin)', '0 (futures only settle at expiration)', '+5 (the long always receives the price change)', 'A', 'A long futures position loses when the futures price falls. The investor''s margin account is debited by 5, requiring them to pay variation margin. Daily settlement is a key feature distinguishing futures from forwards.', 3),
  ('cfa-derivatives-forwards-futures-q05', 'cfa-derivatives-forwards-futures', 'Basis risk in a hedge arises primarily from:', 'The possibility that the futures price converges exactly to spot at expiration', 'The counterparty risk associated with OTC forward contracts', 'The daily mark-to-market requirement of exchange-traded futures', 'A mismatch between the asset being hedged and the asset underlying the futures contract, or an imperfect hedge horizon', 'D', 'Basis risk results when the hedged asset differs from the futures contract''s underlying asset (cross hedge) or when the hedge is lifted before contract expiry, causing the basis to differ from expectations.', 4),
  ('cfa-derivatives-forwards-futures-q06', 'cfa-derivatives-forwards-futures', 'A stock index futures contract is in contango. An investor who rolls long futures positions repeatedly (rolling to the next contract as each expires) will experience:', 'A positive roll yield, as contracts are purchased at a discount', 'No roll yield, as spot and futures prices always converge', 'A negative roll yield, as each new contract is purchased at a higher price than the expiring one', 'A positive roll yield because long positions benefit from rising prices', 'C', 'In contango, the futures curve slopes upward. A long investor sells the expiring (lower-priced) contract and buys the next (higher-priced) one, generating a negative roll yield or ''roll cost''.', 5),
  ('cfa-derivatives-forwards-futures-q07', 'cfa-derivatives-forwards-futures', 'A forward price that is higher than the no-arbitrage price would allow an arbitrageur to profit by:', 'Selling the asset short today and entering a long forward position', 'Doing nothing, since markets are efficient', 'Buying the forward and simultaneously selling another forward at the same price', 'Buying the asset today, financing with borrowed funds, and selling a forward contract', 'D', 'If the forward is overpriced, an arbitrageur buys the spot asset (using borrowed funds) and sells the overpriced forward, locking in a riskless profit equal to the difference between the market forward price and the no-arbitrage price.', 6),
  ('cfa-derivatives-forwards-futures-q08', 'cfa-derivatives-forwards-futures', 'Which of the following assets will have its forward price reduced below S0 x (1+r)^T due to carrying benefits?', 'Gold stored in a vault with significant insurance costs', 'A non-dividend-paying growth stock', 'Crude oil with high storage and transportation costs', 'A dividend-paying stock where dividends are paid during the forward period', 'D', 'Dividends paid during the forward period are a benefit to the asset holder that reduces the cost of carry. The forward price is adjusted downward by the present value of expected dividends: F0 = (S0 - PV(dividends)) x (1+r)^T.', 7),
  ('cfa-derivatives-swaps-q01', 'cfa-derivatives-swaps', 'In a plain vanilla interest rate swap, which party benefits when the floating reference rate rises above the fixed swap rate?', 'Both parties benefit equally', 'The fixed-rate receiver (floating-rate payer)', 'The fixed-rate payer (floating-rate receiver)', 'Neither party benefits; swaps are zero-sum only at inception', 'C', 'The fixed-rate payer pays a set amount and receives the now-higher floating rate, resulting in net receipts. The fixed-rate payer profits when the floating rate rises above the fixed rate.', 0),
  ('cfa-derivatives-swaps-q02', 'cfa-derivatives-swaps', 'At inception, the fixed rate on a plain vanilla interest rate swap is set so that:', 'The fixed-rate payer has a positive net present value position', 'The notional principal is exchanged between both parties', 'The swap has zero value to both counterparties', 'The floating rate equals the fixed rate for the first period only', 'C', 'The swap fixed rate (par rate) is determined so that the present value of expected floating payments equals the present value of fixed payments, giving the swap zero initial value to both parties.', 1),
  ('cfa-derivatives-swaps-q03', 'cfa-derivatives-swaps', 'A corporation has issued floating-rate debt and wants to convert its interest payments to fixed. Which swap position achieves this?', 'Receive fixed, pay floating (the corporation is already floating)', 'Receive floating, receive fixed simultaneously', 'Pay fixed, receive floating', 'Enter a currency swap to convert the debt to another currency', 'C', 'The corporation already pays floating on its debt and receives floating from the swap, with the two floating legs netting. The net result is that the corporation effectively pays only the fixed rate on the swap.', 2),
  ('cfa-derivatives-swaps-q04', 'cfa-derivatives-swaps', 'How does a currency swap differ from an interest rate swap regarding principal?', 'Neither swap involves exchange of principal', 'Currency swaps involve exchange of principal in two currencies at the start and end; interest rate swaps do not exchange principal', 'Interest rate swaps exchange principal only at maturity', 'Both swaps exchange principal at the start but not at maturity', 'B', 'In currency swaps, counterparties exchange equivalent notional amounts in two currencies at inception and re-exchange them at maturity at the originally agreed exchange rate. Interest rate swaps never exchange the notional principal.', 3),
  ('cfa-derivatives-swaps-q05', 'cfa-derivatives-swaps', 'After initiation, a receive-fixed interest rate swap gains value when:', 'Interest rates rise, increasing the value of fixed cash inflows', 'Interest rates fall, increasing the present value of the fixed receipts relative to floating payments', 'The notional principal increases over time', 'Credit spreads tighten in the interbank market', 'B', 'When rates fall, the fixed receipts become more valuable relative to the now-lower expected floating payments. The receive-fixed position gains value, similar to holding a fixed-rate bond.', 4),
  ('cfa-derivatives-swaps-q06', 'cfa-derivatives-swaps', 'Which of the following is NOT a typical use of a currency swap?', 'Speculating on short-term equity price movements', 'Accessing lower borrowing costs in foreign capital markets', 'Hedging long-term foreign currency revenue streams', 'Converting foreign currency debt to domestic currency obligations', 'A', 'Currency swaps are used to manage foreign exchange exposure over long periods, access foreign capital markets, and hedge revenue streams. They are not instruments for short-term equity speculation.', 5),
  ('cfa-derivatives-swaps-q07', 'cfa-derivatives-swaps', 'In an interest rate swap with quarterly settlement, the net payment made by the fixed-rate payer for a given quarter is determined by:', '(Fixed rate - Floating rate) x Notional x (90/360)', '(Floating rate - Fixed rate) x Notional x (90/360)', 'Fixed rate x Notional x (90/360)', 'Floating rate x Notional / 4', 'A', 'The fixed-rate payer''s net quarterly payment is (Fixed rate - Floating rate) x Notional x day count fraction. If positive, the fixed-rate payer makes a net payment; if negative, they receive a net payment.', 6),
  ('cfa-derivatives-swaps-q08', 'cfa-derivatives-swaps', 'An equity swap where one party pays the return on a stock index and receives a fixed rate is most similar to:', 'Selling the stock index and investing the proceeds at the fixed rate', 'Buying the stock index on margin', 'Entering a long forward contract on the stock index', 'Writing a covered call on the stock index', 'A', 'Paying the equity return and receiving fixed replicates the payoff of selling the equity exposure and earning a fixed rate, effectively converting equity risk into a fixed-income position without physically selling assets.', 7),
  ('cfa-derivatives-option-strategies-q01', 'cfa-derivatives-option-strategies', 'An investor holds 100 shares of stock and writes a call option on those shares. This strategy is called a:', 'Covered call', 'Bull call spread', 'Protective put', 'Straddle', 'A', 'A covered call involves holding the underlying asset and writing (selling) a call option on it. The strategy generates premium income but caps upside participation above the strike price.', 0),
  ('cfa-derivatives-option-strategies-q02', 'cfa-derivatives-option-strategies', 'A long straddle position profits when:', 'The underlying price remains near the strike price at expiration', 'The stock pays a large dividend before expiration', 'Implied volatility declines after the position is entered', 'The underlying price makes a large move in either direction', 'D', 'A long straddle (long call + long put at the same strike) profits from large price moves in either direction. The total premium paid is lost if the stock price stays near the strike at expiration.', 1),
  ('cfa-derivatives-option-strategies-q03', 'cfa-derivatives-option-strategies', 'Delta for a long at-the-money call option is approximately:', '0', '+0.5', '+1.0', '-0.5', 'B', 'An at-the-money call option has a delta of approximately 0.5, meaning the option price changes by about 0.50 for each 1.00 change in the underlying asset price. Deep in-the-money calls approach delta of 1; far out-of-the-money calls approach 0.', 2),
  ('cfa-derivatives-option-strategies-q04', 'cfa-derivatives-option-strategies', 'Which Greek measures the rate at which an option loses value purely due to the passage of time, all else equal?', 'Vega', 'Theta', 'Delta', 'Gamma', 'B', 'Theta measures time decay, the rate at which an option''s time value erodes as expiration approaches. Theta is negative for long option positions, meaning the option loses value as time passes.', 3),
  ('cfa-derivatives-option-strategies-q05', 'cfa-derivatives-option-strategies', 'A bull call spread involves:', 'Buying a call with a higher strike and selling a call with a lower strike', 'Buying a call with a lower strike and selling a call with a higher strike', 'Buying both a call and a put at the same strike', 'Selling a put at a lower strike and buying a put at a higher strike', 'B', 'A bull call spread buys a lower-strike call (more expensive) and sells a higher-strike call (cheaper), reducing the net premium cost. It profits from moderate upward price moves with capped maximum gain and limited loss.', 4),
  ('cfa-derivatives-option-strategies-q06', 'cfa-derivatives-option-strategies', 'Vega is highest for options that are:', 'Deep in the money with a very short time to expiration', 'Far out of the money expiring today', 'Deep in the money with a long time to expiration', 'At the money with a long time to expiration', 'D', 'Vega, the sensitivity to implied volatility, is greatest for at-the-money options with long expiries because these options have the most time value that can be affected by volatility changes.', 5),
  ('cfa-derivatives-option-strategies-q07', 'cfa-derivatives-option-strategies', 'A protective put strategy is best described as:', 'Selling a put option to generate premium income on a long stock position', 'Writing both a call and a put at the same strike on the same stock', 'Buying a call option to replace a long stock position', 'Buying a put option on a stock already held long, to limit downside risk', 'D', 'A protective put combines a long stock position with a long put, acting as insurance. The put limits losses below the strike price while preserving the upside. The put premium is the cost of this protection.', 6),
  ('cfa-derivatives-option-strategies-q08', 'cfa-derivatives-option-strategies', 'Gamma is the largest for an option that is:', 'Deep in the money and near expiration', 'Far out of the money with a long time to expiration', 'At the money and near expiration', 'Deep in the money with a long time to expiration', 'C', 'Gamma, the rate of change of delta, peaks for at-the-money options near expiration because small price moves near expiry can rapidly shift whether the option finishes in or out of the money, causing large delta changes.', 7),
  ('cfa-derivatives-risk-management-applications-q01', 'cfa-derivatives-risk-management-applications', 'A portfolio manager wants to reduce the beta of a 10 million equity portfolio from 1.2 to 0.8 using equity index futures. The futures contract has a price of 250,000. How many contracts should be sold?', '8 contracts', '48 contracts', '16 contracts', '32 contracts', 'C', 'Number of contracts = (Target beta - Portfolio beta) / Futures beta x (Portfolio value / Futures price) = (0.8 - 1.2) / 1.0 x (10,000,000 / 250,000) = -0.4 x 40 = -16. Sell 16 contracts.', 0),
  ('cfa-derivatives-risk-management-applications-q02', 'cfa-derivatives-risk-management-applications', 'A fixed income portfolio manager expects interest rates to rise and wants to reduce duration. Which derivative action achieves this most directly?', 'Enter a receive-fixed interest rate swap', 'Buy interest rate call options', 'Enter a pay-fixed interest rate swap', 'Sell put options on Treasury bonds', 'C', 'Entering a pay-fixed, receive-floating swap reduces portfolio duration. Paying fixed is equivalent to holding a short position in a fixed-rate bond, offsetting the duration of long fixed-income holdings.', 1),
  ('cfa-derivatives-risk-management-applications-q03', 'cfa-derivatives-risk-management-applications', 'A US company expects to receive EUR 5 million in 6 months. To hedge the currency risk, the company should:', 'Buy EUR forward (agree to purchase EUR in 6 months at today''s forward rate)', 'Sell EUR forward (agree to sell EUR in 6 months at today''s forward rate)', 'Buy a EUR call option to benefit from EUR appreciation', 'Enter a pay-fixed EUR interest rate swap', 'B', 'The company will receive EUR and needs to convert to USD. It should sell EUR forward (lock in the rate at which it will sell EUR and receive USD), eliminating uncertainty about the USD proceeds.', 2),
  ('cfa-derivatives-risk-management-applications-q04', 'cfa-derivatives-risk-management-applications', 'An interest rate cap is most useful to:', 'A fixed-rate borrower seeking to benefit from falling rates', 'A floating-rate borrower seeking to limit maximum interest expense', 'An investor who wants to lock in a minimum return on a floating-rate bond', 'A bond portfolio manager seeking to extend duration', 'B', 'An interest rate cap pays the holder when the reference rate exceeds the cap rate, compensating a floating-rate borrower for the excess interest cost. It effectively sets a maximum borrowing rate.', 3),
  ('cfa-derivatives-risk-management-applications-q05', 'cfa-derivatives-risk-management-applications', 'Translation exposure for a multinational corporation arises from:', 'The risk of currency movements affecting future foreign currency transactions', 'The long-run effect of exchange rate changes on competitive position', 'The accounting impact of converting foreign subsidiary financial statements into the parent''s reporting currency', 'The credit risk of foreign counterparties in derivative contracts', 'C', 'Translation exposure (accounting exposure) arises when consolidating foreign subsidiaries'' financial statements into the parent''s reporting currency. Assets and liabilities denominated in foreign currencies must be converted at current exchange rates.', 4),
  ('cfa-derivatives-risk-management-applications-q06', 'cfa-derivatives-risk-management-applications', 'To synthetically create an equity position without owning the underlying stock, an investor could:', 'Sell equity index futures and invest the notional in cash', 'Buy equity index futures and invest the notional cash in risk-free assets', 'Enter a receive-fixed, pay-floating interest rate swap', 'Buy put options and simultaneously sell call options at the same strike', 'B', 'Buying futures provides exposure to equity returns while the notional is held in cash (earning the risk-free rate). This synthetic long replicates full equity exposure without purchasing shares directly.', 5),
  ('cfa-derivatives-risk-management-applications-q07', 'cfa-derivatives-risk-management-applications', 'A portfolio manager holds a large position in a stock and buys put options on that stock. Compared to an unhedged position, this strategy:', 'Eliminates all risk and all potential return above the strike', 'Reduces upside participation but does not limit downside losses', 'Generates income that offsets stock purchase costs', 'Limits downside loss while preserving upside participation, at the cost of the put premium', 'D', 'Buying a put provides downside protection below the strike price (acting as insurance) while the stock position retains upside potential. The cost is the put premium, which reduces overall return by that amount.', 6),
  ('cfa-derivatives-risk-management-applications-q08', 'cfa-derivatives-risk-management-applications', 'Economic exposure to currency risk is best described as:', 'The long-run impact of exchange rate changes on a firm''s competitive position and future cash flows', 'The risk of loss on specific foreign currency receivables or payables', 'The short-term accounting impact of translating foreign currency balances', 'The mark-to-market loss on foreign currency derivative contracts', 'A', 'Economic exposure captures the broader, long-run impact of exchange rate fluctuations on a firm''s operating cash flows, pricing power, and competitive position, which is harder to hedge than specific transaction exposures.', 7),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-real-estate-q01', 'cfa-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'A property generates annual gross rental income of $500,000 with vacancy losses of $25,000 and operating expenses of $175,000. What is the Net Operating Income (NOI)?', '$300,000', '$325,000', '$500,000', '$475,000', 'A', 'NOI = Gross rent - Vacancy losses - Operating expenses = $500,000 - $25,000 - $175,000 = $300,000. Debt service is excluded from NOI.', 0),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-real-estate-q02', 'cfa-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'A property has an NOI of $600,000 and is valued at $8,000,000. What is its capitalization rate?', '13.3%', '5.5%', '8.0%', '7.5%', 'D', 'Cap rate = NOI / Property value = $600,000 / $8,000,000 = 7.5%. A lower cap rate signals a higher valuation relative to income.', 1),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-real-estate-q03', 'cfa-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'Compared with direct real estate ownership, Real Estate Investment Trusts (REITs) primarily offer investors:', 'Greater control over property management decisions', 'Lower correlation with equity markets', 'Improved liquidity and easier diversification', 'Tax advantages unavailable in direct ownership', 'C', 'REITs are publicly traded, providing daily liquidity and easy diversification across property types and regions that direct ownership cannot match.', 2),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-real-estate-q04', 'cfa-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'During which phase of the real estate cycle do vacancy rates typically fall and rents begin to rise, but new construction has not yet responded?', 'Hyper-supply', 'Recession', 'Expansion', 'Recovery', 'C', 'In the expansion phase, demand absorbs existing supply, vacancy falls, and rents rise, though new development is still ramping up.', 3),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-real-estate-q05', 'cfa-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'Which real estate property type is most sensitive to economic cycles due to its high operational complexity?', 'Industrial warehouses', 'Hospitality (hotels)', 'Multifamily residential', 'Net-lease retail', 'B', 'Hospitality real estate has the highest operational complexity and most cyclical demand, as room rates and occupancy are repriced daily based on economic conditions.', 4),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-real-estate-q06', 'cfa-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'If cap rates in a market rise from 5% to 6% and the property''s NOI remains constant at $300,000, the property''s value will:', 'Increase from $5,000,000 to $6,000,000', 'Decrease from $5,000,000 to $4,545,455', 'Remain unchanged because NOI is constant', 'Decrease from $6,000,000 to $5,000,000', 'D', 'Value = NOI / Cap rate. At 5% the value is $6,000,000; at 6% it falls to $5,000,000. Rising cap rates compress property values when NOI is fixed.', 5),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-real-estate-q07', 'cfa-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'Which of the following best describes the Gross Income Multiplier (GIM)?', 'Property price divided by annual gross income', 'Net operating income divided by the property''s market value', 'Annual gross income minus operating expenses', 'Property value multiplied by the cap rate', 'A', 'GIM = Property price / Gross income. It is a quick valuation shortcut but ignores vacancy and operating expenses, unlike the cap rate approach.', 6),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-real-estate-q08', 'cfa-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'Industrial real estate has experienced strong secular demand growth primarily because of:', 'Declining interest rates reducing cap rates', 'Increased remote work reducing office demand', 'The expansion of e-commerce requiring logistics and warehouse space', 'Demographic shifts toward urban apartment living', 'C', 'E-commerce growth has driven sustained demand for distribution centers and last-mile logistics warehouses, benefiting the industrial property sector.', 7),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-commodities-q01', 'cfa-alternative-investments-commodities', 'A commodity futures market is in contango when:', 'The futures price exceeds the current spot price', 'Futures prices are below expected future spot prices', 'The spot price exceeds the futures price', 'Roll yield is positive for long futures holders', 'A', 'Contango exists when futures prices are higher than the current spot price. Long investors rolling futures forward incur a negative roll yield in contango.', 0),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-commodities-q02', 'cfa-alternative-investments-commodities', 'The total return of a long commodity futures position consists of which three components?', 'Spot return, roll yield, and collateral yield', 'Dividend yield, price return, and currency return', 'Convenience yield, storage costs, and risk-free rate', 'Income return, capital return, and inflation adjustment', 'A', 'The three components are: spot return (change in commodity price), roll yield (gain or loss from rolling futures contracts), and collateral yield (return on the margin collateral).', 1),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-commodities-q03', 'cfa-alternative-investments-commodities', 'Convenience yield is best described as:', 'The tax benefit of holding commodity futures rather than physical goods', 'The non-monetary benefit from holding physical inventory of a commodity', 'The yield earned on cash posted as futures margin collateral', 'The premium futures traders earn for providing liquidity to the market', 'B', 'Convenience yield reflects the benefit of having immediate access to physical inventory (e.g., uninterrupted production), which futures holders do not enjoy.', 2),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-commodities-q04', 'cfa-alternative-investments-commodities', 'A commodity market is in backwardation. What does this imply for a long futures investor rolling contracts forward?', 'The investor incurs a negative roll yield, reducing total return', 'The investor must pay the convenience yield to the seller', 'Roll yield is zero because the spot price equals the futures price', 'The investor earns a positive roll yield, enhancing total return', 'D', 'In backwardation, near-dated futures are priced above longer-dated ones. Rolling from an expiring contract into a cheaper forward contract generates positive roll yield.', 3),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-commodities-q05', 'cfa-alternative-investments-commodities', 'Which commodity index is most heavily weighted toward energy commodities?', 'Bloomberg Commodity Index', 'S&P GSCI', 'Rogers International Commodity Index', 'Deutsche Bank Liquid Commodity Index', 'B', 'The S&P GSCI weights commodities by world production, which makes it heavily concentrated in energy (often 60-70% or more), unlike the more diversified Bloomberg Commodity Index.', 4),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-commodities-q06', 'cfa-alternative-investments-commodities', 'The primary reason commodities are included in a diversified multi-asset portfolio is:', 'They provide high current income through dividends', 'They have historically offered a partial inflation hedge and low correlation with stocks and bonds', 'Commodity futures always outperform equities during bull markets', 'Physical commodities are easy to store and transport', 'B', 'Commodities provide diversification because their prices often rise with inflation and they have historically shown low correlation with traditional asset classes.', 5),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-commodities-q07', 'cfa-alternative-investments-commodities', 'Which of the following is the spot price of a commodity?', 'The price for delivery at a specified future date', 'The current market price for immediate delivery', 'The average price over the past 12 months', 'The price adjusted for storage costs and financing', 'B', 'The spot price is the price at which a commodity can be bought or sold for immediate delivery, as opposed to the futures price for future delivery.', 6),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-commodities-q08', 'cfa-alternative-investments-commodities', 'An investor holds a long position in crude oil futures. After one month, the near-month contract expires and the investor rolls into the next contract at a higher price. The roll yield in this transaction is:', 'Positive, because the investor moved to a higher-priced contract', 'Positive, because crude oil is always in backwardation', 'Zero, because the gain on the expiring contract offsets the cost of the new one', 'Negative, because buying the more expensive forward contract results in a loss', 'D', 'Rolling from an expiring contract into a more expensive forward contract (contango) generates negative roll yield, reducing total return for the long investor.', 7),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-infrastructure-q01', 'cfa-alternative-investments-infrastructure', 'Which of the following characteristics most distinguishes infrastructure from other real assets?', 'Regulated or contracted revenues providing predictable cash flows', 'High liquidity and ease of trading on secondary markets', 'Short economic lives requiring frequent asset replacement', 'High sensitivity to commodity price fluctuations', 'A', 'Infrastructure assets typically operate under regulatory frameworks or long-term contracts that provide predictable, often inflation-linked revenue streams, which is their defining characteristic.', 0),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-infrastructure-q02', 'cfa-alternative-investments-infrastructure', 'A greenfield infrastructure investment differs from a brownfield investment primarily in that:', 'Greenfield investments involve acquiring operating assets with established cash flows', 'Greenfield investments involve building new assets and bear construction and ramp-up risk', 'Greenfield investments are exclusively financed by governments', 'Greenfield investments have lower required returns due to lower risk', 'B', 'Greenfield projects involve new construction and face risks including cost overruns, delays, and uncertain demand during ramp-up, requiring higher return expectations than brownfield assets.', 1),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-infrastructure-q03', 'cfa-alternative-investments-infrastructure', 'Why are infrastructure investments particularly attractive to pension funds with defined-benefit liabilities?', 'Infrastructure offers the highest expected returns of all asset classes', 'Infrastructure has no correlation with interest rates, eliminating duration risk', 'Infrastructure assets are highly liquid and can be sold quickly when liabilities come due', 'Long-dated, often inflation-linked cash flows match the duration of long-term liabilities', 'D', 'Defined-benefit pension funds have long-duration liabilities; infrastructure''s long-life, inflation-linked cash flows provide natural asset-liability matching.', 2),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-infrastructure-q04', 'cfa-alternative-investments-infrastructure', 'In a Public-Private Partnership (PPP), risk is:', 'Borne entirely by the private sector operator', 'Borne entirely by the government entity', 'Transferred fully to the end-users through user fees', 'Shared between the government and the private operator', 'D', 'PPPs are structured to allocate each type of risk (construction, demand, political) to the party best able to manage it, creating a shared risk arrangement.', 3),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-infrastructure-q05', 'cfa-alternative-investments-infrastructure', 'Which statement best describes timberland as an alternative investment?', 'Timberland generates returns solely from land appreciation', 'Income comes from timber harvests and the asset benefits from biological growth of trees', 'Timberland is highly correlated with commodity futures markets', 'Returns are driven primarily by government subsidy programs', 'B', 'Timberland generates income from harvesting timber and benefits from biological growth (trees grow larger over time), giving investors flexibility to time harvests based on price conditions.', 4),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-infrastructure-q06', 'cfa-alternative-investments-infrastructure', 'Brownfield infrastructure assets are expected to earn lower returns than greenfield assets because:', 'Brownfield assets are smaller and less strategically important', 'Brownfield assets have established cash flows and lower construction risk', 'Brownfield assets require higher ongoing capital expenditure', 'Brownfield assets are subject to more regulatory oversight', 'B', 'The risk premium on brownfield assets is lower because they are already operational, have demonstrable cash flow histories, and do not carry construction or demand ramp-up risk.', 5),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-infrastructure-q07', 'cfa-alternative-investments-infrastructure', 'The demand for infrastructure services is best described as:', 'Highly elastic, as users can easily substitute alternative services', 'Relatively inelastic, because infrastructure provides essential services with few substitutes', 'Perfectly elastic, because prices are set by competitive markets', 'Pro-cyclical, with demand falling sharply during economic recessions', 'B', 'Infrastructure provides essential services such as water, electricity, and transportation, for which demand is relatively price-inelastic and less sensitive to economic cycles.', 6),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-infrastructure-q08', 'cfa-alternative-investments-infrastructure', 'Farmland investments generate returns primarily from:', 'Crop production income and potential land appreciation', 'Government agricultural subsidies and tax credits', 'Rising commodity futures prices on organized exchanges', 'Biological growth similar to timberland investments', 'A', 'Farmland returns come from leasing or operating the land to produce crops (income return) and from appreciation in land values over time (capital return).', 7),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q01', 'cfa-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'Operational due diligence (ODD) in alternative investments primarily focuses on:', 'Assessing non-investment risks such as fund administration, custody, and valuation procedures', 'Evaluating the attractiveness of the manager''s investment strategy and return potential', 'Reviewing the manager''s historical return record relative to peers', 'Analyzing the portfolio''s factor exposures and concentration risk', 'A', 'ODD focuses on non-investment operational risks including back-office controls, custody arrangements, valuation processes, compliance, and IT systems.', 0),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q02', 'cfa-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'When evaluating an alternative investment manager, which factor represents key-person risk?', 'The manager charges fees above the industry median', 'The fund relies heavily on one or a few individuals whose departure could impair performance', 'The manager invests in geographically concentrated assets', 'The fund uses a complex valuation methodology for illiquid assets', 'B', 'Key-person risk arises when a fund''s performance depends critically on specific individuals; if they depart, the investment thesis and returns may be materially impaired.', 1),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q03', 'cfa-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'The TVPI (Total Value to Paid-In) multiple in private equity measures:', 'The ratio of distributions plus remaining net asset value to total capital invested', 'The annualized rate of return earned since the fund''s inception', 'The percentage of committed capital that has been called and invested', 'The excess return earned above a public market benchmark', 'A', 'TVPI = (Distributions + Residual NAV) / Paid-in capital. It captures total value created relative to capital deployed, combining realized and unrealized returns.', 2),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q04', 'cfa-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'An investor reviewing an alternative fund notices that the portfolio currently holds assets in strategies different from those described in the fund''s offering documents. This is an example of:', 'Operational risk, identified through back-office review', 'Benchmark misalignment, identified through performance attribution', 'Liquidity risk, identified through cash flow analysis', 'Style drift, identified through portfolio-level due diligence', 'D', 'Style drift occurs when a manager deviates from the stated investment strategy. It is identified by comparing current holdings to the strategy described in the fund documents.', 3),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q05', 'cfa-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'Which of the following is the most appropriate performance benchmark for a private equity buyout fund?', 'The S&P 500 total return index', 'The 90-day Treasury bill rate', 'A peer universe of comparable private equity funds with a similar vintage year and strategy', 'An equal-weighted basket of publicly traded financial companies', 'C', 'Private equity is best evaluated against a peer universe of funds with the same vintage year, geography, and strategy, as public benchmarks do not reflect the same risk profile or return timing.', 4),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q06', 'cfa-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'A strong historical track record is insufficient for manager selection because:', 'The same team, strategy, and risk level may not be in place going forward', 'Past returns are always higher than future returns due to mean reversion', 'Historical returns are never audited and therefore unreliable', 'Regulators prohibit using past performance in manager selection decisions', 'A', 'Past returns may reflect a different team, strategy, market environment, or leverage level. Investors must confirm the investment process generating past returns will continue unchanged.', 5),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q07', 'cfa-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'The DPI (Distributed to Paid-In) multiple is preferred over TVPI when an investor wants to assess:', 'Realized cash returned to investors relative to capital invested', 'The annualized return relative to a public market equivalent', 'Total paper gains including unrealized appreciation', 'The fund''s remaining investment capacity', 'A', 'DPI measures only realized distributions; unlike TVPI, it excludes unrealized NAV and therefore reflects cash actually returned to investors, making it a more conservative measure.', 6),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q08', 'cfa-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'Strategy fit in alternative manager selection means:', 'The manager''s strategy matches the investor''s portfolio objectives, risk budget, and liquidity needs', 'The manager''s fee structure aligns with industry norms', 'The manager''s returns have historically beaten the benchmark', 'The manager''s team has more than 10 years of experience', 'A', 'Strategy fit ensures that an alternative manager''s approach, risk level, liquidity terms, and expected return are consistent with the investor''s overall portfolio objectives and constraints.', 7),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-ips-planning-q01', 'cfa-portfolio-management-ips-planning', 'Which of the following best describes the purpose of an Investment Policy Statement (IPS)?', 'To specify the exact securities a portfolio manager must purchase', 'To translate a client''s situation and goals into guidelines governing portfolio management', 'To legally protect the investment manager from client lawsuits', 'To record the portfolio''s historical performance and benchmark comparisons', 'B', 'The IPS translates a client''s objectives and constraints into actionable investment guidelines, serving as the governing document for the entire investment process.', 0),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-ips-planning-q02', 'cfa-portfolio-management-ips-planning', 'A client expresses a strong desire to take on significant investment risk to achieve high returns, but her financial situation shows she cannot afford meaningful capital losses. How should the portfolio manager determine the appropriate risk level?', 'Use the higher of willingness and ability to take risk, to honor client preferences', 'Use the lower of willingness and ability to take risk, since that is the binding constraint', 'Average the willingness and ability to take risk', 'Focus only on the client''s stated willingness because the manager cannot assess ability', 'B', 'When willingness and ability to bear risk conflict, the more conservative (lower) tolerance governs. The manager should counsel the client on the gap while respecting the binding constraint.', 1),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-ips-planning-q03', 'cfa-portfolio-management-ips-planning', 'The TTLLU acronym in IPS construction stands for which five constraints?', 'Tax, Trend, Liquidity, Leverage, Uncertainty', 'Trading costs, Turnover, Leverage, Liquidity, Uncertainty', 'Time horizon, Tax, Liquidity, Legal & regulatory, Unique circumstances', 'Time horizon, Tracking error, Leverage, Liquidity, Upside', 'C', 'TTLLU covers the five standard IPS constraints: Time horizon, Tax concerns, Liquidity needs, Legal and regulatory requirements, and Unique circumstances such as ESG preferences.', 2),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-ips-planning-q04', 'cfa-portfolio-management-ips-planning', 'A 35-year-old investor saving for retirement at age 65 has a time horizon that is best described as:', 'Short-term, requiring a conservative fixed-income-heavy allocation', 'Medium-term, because markets are uncertain beyond 10 years', 'Multi-stage, because the investor also has near-term spending needs', 'Long-term, supporting a higher allocation to growth assets', 'D', 'A 30-year horizon until retirement is long-term, allowing the investor to tolerate short-term volatility in exchange for higher expected returns from equities and growth assets.', 3),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-ips-planning-q05', 'cfa-portfolio-management-ips-planning', 'Which institution typically has the longest time horizon and highest risk tolerance among institutional investors?', 'Life insurance companies', 'Defined-benefit pension funds in pay-out phase', 'Commercial banks', 'University endowment funds', 'D', 'University endowments have effectively perpetual time horizons and no fixed liabilities, allowing them to maintain high allocations to illiquid alternatives and equities.', 4),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-ips-planning-q06', 'cfa-portfolio-management-ips-planning', 'A client''s return objective states they need a 6% annual return to meet their goals. Their portfolio currently has a risk tolerance consistent with a maximum 12% standard deviation. This information should be:', 'Used independently; return and risk objectives need not be consistent with each other', 'Overridden by the manager''s own capital market assumptions', 'Reconciled so that the risk level can realistically support the return objective', 'Set aside until markets are more predictable', 'C', 'Return and risk objectives must be consistent. If the stated risk level cannot realistically generate the required return, the conflict must be resolved before portfolio construction begins.', 5),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-ips-planning-q07', 'cfa-portfolio-management-ips-planning', 'An investor specifies in her IPS that she will not hold tobacco or weapons manufacturers. This would be categorized under which IPS constraint?', 'Liquidity needs', 'Unique circumstances', 'Legal and regulatory', 'Time horizon', 'B', 'ESG restrictions, ethical preferences, and social screens are classified under unique circumstances, as they reflect individual values not captured by the other standard constraints.', 6),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-ips-planning-q08', 'cfa-portfolio-management-ips-planning', 'After constructing a portfolio consistent with a client''s IPS, when should the IPS be reviewed and potentially updated?', 'Only when the portfolio significantly underperforms its benchmark', 'On a fixed schedule only, regardless of changes in the client''s circumstances', 'Whenever there is a material change in the client''s goals, financial situation, or constraints', 'Every 10 years to reflect long-run changes in capital markets', 'C', 'The IPS should be reviewed and updated whenever a significant life event or change in financial circumstances alters the client''s objectives or constraints, in addition to regular periodic reviews.', 7),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-asset-allocation-q01', 'cfa-portfolio-management-asset-allocation', 'Strategic asset allocation (SAA) is best defined as:', 'Short-term deviations from target weights to exploit market inefficiencies', 'The allocation to a single asset class that minimizes portfolio variance', 'Long-term target weights across asset classes consistent with the investor''s IPS', 'The process of selecting individual securities within each asset class', 'C', 'SAA establishes long-term target weights based on the investor''s objectives, constraints, and long-run capital market expectations, forming the baseline portfolio.', 0),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-asset-allocation-q02', 'cfa-portfolio-management-asset-allocation', 'Tactical asset allocation (TAA) differs from strategic asset allocation in that TAA:', 'Is based on long-run capital market expectations rather than short-term views', 'Focuses exclusively on rebalancing the portfolio back to its SAA targets', 'Eliminates all tracking error relative to the benchmark', 'Introduces deliberate short-term deviations from SAA based on market forecasts', 'D', 'TAA involves intentional deviations from SAA to exploit perceived short-term mispricings, adding active risk (tracking error) relative to the strategic benchmark.', 1),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-asset-allocation-q03', 'cfa-portfolio-management-asset-allocation', 'The minimum-variance portfolio on the efficient frontier is characterized by:', 'The highest expected return for any given risk level', 'Equal weighting across all asset classes to maximize diversification', 'The lowest possible portfolio standard deviation across all investable combinations', 'A portfolio consisting entirely of the risk-free asset', 'C', 'The minimum-variance portfolio is the leftmost point on the efficient frontier, representing the combination of risky assets with the lowest achievable standard deviation.', 2),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-asset-allocation-q04', 'cfa-portfolio-management-asset-allocation', 'A key advantage of the Black-Litterman model over standard mean-variance optimization (MVO) is that it:', 'Requires no input assumptions about expected returns or correlations', 'Guarantees the highest possible Sharpe ratio for any set of investor views', 'Starts from market equilibrium returns and blends in investor views, producing more diversified portfolios', 'Eliminates the need for rebalancing by automatically adjusting to market changes', 'C', 'Black-Litterman combines implied equilibrium returns (from market cap weights) with investor views, addressing MVO''s tendency to produce concentrated, input-sensitive portfolios.', 3),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-asset-allocation-q05', 'cfa-portfolio-management-asset-allocation', 'Calendar rebalancing differs from threshold (corridor) rebalancing in that calendar rebalancing:', 'Maintains tighter risk control because it responds immediately to significant price moves', 'Always results in lower trading costs than threshold rebalancing', 'Rebalances the portfolio on a fixed schedule regardless of how much weights have drifted', 'Is only appropriate for equity-only portfolios with no fixed income', 'C', 'Calendar rebalancing occurs at fixed intervals (monthly, quarterly) regardless of drift, while threshold rebalancing triggers only when weights deviate beyond a specified band from targets.', 4),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-asset-allocation-q06', 'cfa-portfolio-management-asset-allocation', 'Mean-variance optimization is most criticized for which of the following limitations?', 'It is highly sensitive to input assumptions and often produces concentrated portfolios', 'It requires real-time pricing, which is unavailable for most asset classes', 'It cannot model more than two asset classes simultaneously', 'It ignores the investor''s return objective and risk tolerance', 'A', 'Small changes in expected return assumptions can dramatically shift MVO weights, often yielding concentrated, unstable portfolios that are fragile to estimation errors.', 5),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-asset-allocation-q07', 'cfa-portfolio-management-asset-allocation', 'When an equity allocation grows from its 60% SAA target to 68% due to strong stock market performance, a portfolio manager practicing threshold rebalancing with a 5% corridor would:', 'Take no action, because only calendar rebalancing monitors weight drift', 'Widen the corridor to accommodate the higher equity weight', 'Wait until the next scheduled calendar date to rebalance', 'Immediately rebalance back to 60% because the weight has exceeded the 65% upper boundary', 'D', 'A 5% corridor around 60% sets upper and lower bounds of 65% and 55%. At 68%, the upper bound is breached, triggering a rebalance back to the 60% target.', 6),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-asset-allocation-q08', 'cfa-portfolio-management-asset-allocation', 'Research on asset allocation suggests that strategic asset allocation explains the majority of long-run portfolio return variability primarily because:', 'Individual security selection adds no value in efficient markets', 'Broad asset class exposures drive most of the variation in portfolio returns over time', 'Tactical allocation always destroys value and should be avoided entirely', 'Rebalancing automatically captures alpha from mean-reverting asset prices', 'B', 'The Brinson, Hood, and Beebower research showed that the policy mix across asset classes (SAA) explains the preponderance of the variability in total portfolio returns over time.', 7),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-performance-evaluation-q01', 'cfa-portfolio-management-performance-evaluation', 'The Sharpe ratio is most appropriate for evaluating performance when:', 'The portfolio represents only one component within a larger diversified fund', 'The portfolio is an investor''s entire investment portfolio', 'The benchmark has a different standard deviation than the portfolio', 'The portfolio holds only fixed income securities with low variance', 'B', 'The Sharpe ratio uses total risk (standard deviation) and is appropriate when the portfolio is the investor''s entire holding. For a sub-portfolio within a diversified fund, the Treynor ratio is preferred.', 0),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-performance-evaluation-q02', 'cfa-portfolio-management-performance-evaluation', 'A portfolio earns a return of 12%, the risk-free rate is 3%, and the portfolio beta is 1.2. The market return is 9%. What is Jensen''s alpha?', '1.8%', '3.0%', '0.6%', '2.4%', 'A', 'Jensen''s alpha = Rp - [Rf + beta(Rm - Rf)] = 12% - [3% + 1.2*(9% - 3%)] = 12% - [3% + 7.2%] = 12% - 10.2% = 1.8%.', 1),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-performance-evaluation-q03', 'cfa-portfolio-management-performance-evaluation', 'The Information Ratio (IR) measures:', 'The excess return over the benchmark per unit of tracking error', 'The excess return of the portfolio over the risk-free rate per unit of total risk', 'The portfolio''s alpha per unit of systematic risk (beta)', 'The ratio of positive return months to negative return months', 'A', 'IR = Active return / Tracking error = (Rp - Rb) / TE. A higher IR indicates the manager is generating more consistent active return per unit of active risk taken.', 2),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-performance-evaluation-q04', 'cfa-portfolio-management-performance-evaluation', 'In the Brinson, Hood, and Beebower (BHB) performance attribution model, the allocation effect measures:', 'Whether the manager selected better securities than the benchmark within each asset class', 'Whether the manager overweighted or underweighted asset classes that outperformed or underperformed', 'The combined impact of allocation and selection decisions in each segment', 'The total active return relative to the strategic benchmark', 'B', 'The allocation effect captures value added by over/underweighting asset classes: it is positive when the manager overweights (underweights) sectors that outperform (underperform) the overall benchmark.', 3),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-performance-evaluation-q05', 'cfa-portfolio-management-performance-evaluation', 'The Treynor ratio is preferred over the Sharpe ratio for evaluating a sub-portfolio within a larger diversified fund because:', 'Beta is more accurate than standard deviation for measuring total portfolio risk', 'The Treynor ratio does not require knowledge of the risk-free rate', 'When the portfolio is one of many holdings, idiosyncratic risk is diversified away, making systematic risk the relevant measure', 'The Sharpe ratio cannot be calculated for sub-portfolios with negative returns', 'C', 'In a diversified fund, unsystematic risk is eliminated at the total portfolio level; only systematic risk (beta) is relevant for evaluating a component, making the Treynor ratio appropriate.', 4),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-performance-evaluation-q06', 'cfa-portfolio-management-performance-evaluation', 'M-squared (M2) is calculated by:', 'Multiplying the portfolio''s Sharpe ratio by the benchmark standard deviation plus the risk-free rate', 'Subtracting Jensen''s alpha from the Treynor ratio', 'Dividing the portfolio''s excess return by beta minus the benchmark excess return', 'Adding the information ratio to the Sharpe ratio', 'A', 'M2 = (Sharpe_p - Sharpe_m) * sigma_m + Rf, or equivalently, it levers/de-levers the portfolio to the benchmark''s risk level and compares returns. It expresses risk-adjusted outperformance in return percentage terms.', 5),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-performance-evaluation-q07', 'cfa-portfolio-management-performance-evaluation', 'Which of the following is NOT a required characteristic of a valid benchmark?', 'Higher historical return than the portfolio', 'Specified in advance', 'Investable', 'Unambiguous and measurable', 'A', 'A valid benchmark does not need to outperform the portfolio; it must be investable, specified in advance, measurable, appropriate, reflective of investment opinion, accountable, and unambiguous.', 6),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-performance-evaluation-q08', 'cfa-portfolio-management-performance-evaluation', 'A portfolio manager consistently generates positive Jensen''s alpha over a 5-year period. This most likely indicates:', 'The portfolio took on more total risk than the market portfolio', 'The portfolio''s Sharpe ratio exceeded the market Sharpe ratio', 'The manager earned returns exceeding what the CAPM predicts given the portfolio''s systematic risk', 'The portfolio is fully diversified with no idiosyncratic risk', 'C', 'Jensen''s alpha measures excess return above the CAPM-predicted return for the portfolio''s beta level. Positive alpha over multiple periods suggests manager skill or persistent factor exposures not captured by beta.', 7),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-risk-management-q01', 'cfa-portfolio-management-risk-management', 'Risk budgeting in portfolio management refers to:', 'Setting a maximum loss limit for each calendar year', 'Limiting each position to a fixed percentage of the total portfolio', 'Determining the budget required to hedge all portfolio risks using derivatives', 'Allocating a total risk budget across positions or asset classes to maximize return per unit of risk', 'D', 'Risk budgeting allocates a total risk allowance (expressed as volatility, VaR, or tracking error) across portfolio components, ensuring managers seek the highest return per unit of risk consumed.', 0),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-risk-management-q02', 'cfa-portfolio-management-risk-management', 'A portfolio has a daily 99% VaR of $2 million. This means:', 'The portfolio will lose exactly $2 million on 99% of trading days', 'The portfolio''s maximum possible loss is $2 million', 'Losses exceeding $2 million are expected on approximately 1% of trading days', 'The portfolio will lose at least $2 million on 1% of days with 99% confidence', 'C', 'A 99% VaR of $2 million means that on approximately 1% of trading days (about 2-3 days per year), losses are expected to exceed $2 million. VaR does not cap the loss magnitude.', 1),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-risk-management-q03', 'cfa-portfolio-management-risk-management', 'Conditional VaR (CVaR), also called Expected Shortfall, improves on VaR by:', 'Assuming a normal distribution, which reduces computation complexity', 'Eliminating the need to specify a confidence level', 'Measuring the average loss in the tail beyond the VaR threshold', 'Providing a guaranteed maximum loss figure for risk budgeting purposes', 'C', 'CVaR is the expected loss conditional on the loss exceeding the VaR level. It describes the severity of tail losses that VaR does not capture, addressing VaR''s key limitation.', 2),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-risk-management-q04', 'cfa-portfolio-management-risk-management', 'Which VaR estimation method requires no assumption about the shape of the return distribution?', 'Parametric (variance-covariance) method', 'Monte Carlo simulation', 'Analytical approximation using modified duration', 'Historical simulation', 'D', 'Historical simulation uses the actual distribution of past returns directly, making no normality assumption. It naturally captures fat tails and skewness present in actual market data.', 3),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-risk-management-q05', 'cfa-portfolio-management-risk-management', 'Maximum drawdown is best defined as:', 'The standard deviation of monthly portfolio returns over a given period', 'The VaR of the portfolio measured at the 95% confidence level', 'The average loss during months when the portfolio produces negative returns', 'The largest peak-to-trough decline in portfolio value over a specified period', 'D', 'Maximum drawdown measures the largest cumulative loss from a prior peak to a subsequent trough, capturing the worst sustained loss an investor could have experienced.', 4),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-risk-management-q06', 'cfa-portfolio-management-risk-management', 'A factor-based risk model decomposes portfolio risk into:', 'VaR contributions from each individual position', 'Market risk and credit risk components only', 'Systematic factor exposures and idiosyncratic (security-specific) risk', 'Historical and forward-looking risk components', 'C', 'Factor models explain portfolio returns and risk through exposures to systematic factors (market, size, value, momentum) plus residual idiosyncratic risk unique to individual securities.', 5),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-risk-management-q07', 'cfa-portfolio-management-risk-management', 'The parametric VaR method is least reliable for portfolios that:', 'Hold only investment-grade bonds with no embedded options', 'Are well-diversified across many asset classes with symmetric returns', 'Have a long track record of returns available for analysis', 'Contain options or other instruments with non-linear payoffs and fat-tailed return distributions', 'D', 'Parametric VaR assumes normally distributed, linear returns. Options introduce non-linearity and actual returns often have fat tails, making the parametric method unreliable for such portfolios.', 6),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-risk-management-q08', 'cfa-portfolio-management-risk-management', 'An investment mandate specifies a maximum drawdown limit of 15%. Once this limit is breached, the most likely required action is:', 'Increase leverage to recover the loss more quickly', 'Report the breach only at the end of the fiscal year', 'Switch the entire portfolio to cash until the drawdown limit is reset', 'Reduce risk exposures to prevent further losses and notify stakeholders', 'D', 'A drawdown limit breach triggers risk reduction measures to prevent compounding losses and requires prompt disclosure to stakeholders; the appropriate response is de-risking, not increasing leverage.', 7),
  ('cfa-ethics-independence-objectivity-q01', 'cfa-ethics-independence-objectivity', 'An analyst is invited by a company she covers to tour a remote mine, with the company paying for chartered flights and lodging. To best comply with Standard I(B), she should:', 'Accept, because issuer-paid travel is customary', 'Pay for her own commercial travel and lodging where practical', 'Accept but disclose the trip in her report', 'Decline to cover the company entirely', 'B', 'To preserve independence and objectivity, members should pay for their own travel when feasible rather than accept lavish issuer-paid arrangements that could bias their analysis.', 0),
  ('cfa-ethics-independence-objectivity-q02', 'cfa-ethics-independence-objectivity', 'A client gives a portfolio manager a luxury watch as thanks for strong performance last year. Under the Standards, the manager should:', 'Decline the gift outright', 'Accept it without any further action', 'Accept it but disclose it to the employer', 'Accept only if its value is under $100', 'C', 'Gifts from clients for past performance are permitted but must be disclosed to the employer, since future client relationships could create partiality.', 1),
  ('cfa-ethics-independence-objectivity-q03', 'cfa-ethics-independence-objectivity', 'Including a respected economist''s published GDP forecast in a report without citing the source most likely violates the Standard prohibiting:', 'Misrepresentation through plagiarism', 'Use of material nonpublic information', 'Failure to maintain independence', 'Misconduct', 'A', 'Using another party''s analysis, forecasts, or models without attribution is plagiarism, a form of misrepresentation under Standard I(C).', 2),
  ('cfa-ethics-independence-objectivity-q04', 'cfa-ethics-independence-objectivity', 'Which statement to a prospective client most clearly violates Standard I(C) Misrepresentation?', '"This bond''s coupon is fixed at 5%."', '"Our equity strategy has averaged 8% over ten years, net of fees."', '"I guarantee this equity fund will return at least 10% annually."', '"Past performance does not guarantee future results."', 'C', 'Guaranteeing a specific return on a risky investment is a misrepresentation; only the contractual cash flows of certain instruments (e.g., a fixed coupon) may be stated as assured.', 3),
  ('cfa-ethics-independence-objectivity-q05', 'cfa-ethics-independence-objectivity', 'A sell-side analyst''s firm is underwriting an IPO for a company the analyst rates. The best way to protect objectivity is to:', 'Issue only buy recommendations during the offering period', 'Maintain firewalls between research and investment banking', 'Have investment banking approve the research rating', 'Suspend coverage permanently', 'B', 'Information barriers (firewalls) between research and investment banking reduce pressure on analysts to bias ratings to win or support banking business.', 4),
  ('cfa-ethics-independence-objectivity-q06', 'cfa-ethics-independence-objectivity', 'Using factual macroeconomic data published by a national statistics agency without attribution is:', 'Always a violation of the plagiarism prohibition', 'Permitted, as recognized factual/statistical data need not be cited', 'Permitted only with the agency''s written consent', 'A violation of the duty of loyalty to employers', 'B', 'Members may use recognized factual or statistical information from sources such as government agencies without attribution; the plagiarism rule targets others'' analysis and opinions.', 5),
  ('cfa-ethics-independence-objectivity-q07', 'cfa-ethics-independence-objectivity', 'A credit rating agency lets the team that negotiates fees with issuers also determine the credit ratings. This most likely compromises:', 'Fair dealing among clients', 'Independence and objectivity of the ratings', 'Preservation of confidentiality', 'Suitability', 'B', 'Allowing fee negotiation and rating determination to mix creates a conflict that threatens the independence and objectivity of the ratings.', 6),
  ('cfa-ethics-independence-objectivity-q08', 'cfa-ethics-independence-objectivity', 'A candidate lists ''CFA Level II candidate'' but has not registered for the next exam. This most likely violates the Standard on:', 'Reference to the CFA designation / candidacy misrepresentation', 'Independence and objectivity', 'Priority of transactions', 'Diligence and reasonable basis', 'A', 'One may claim candidacy only when actively registered/enrolled for the next sitting; otherwise it misrepresents the candidate''s status.', 7),
  ('cfa-ethics-independence-objectivity-q09', 'cfa-ethics-independence-objectivity', 'An analyst accepts a modest meal and a branded pen at a company''s investor day. This is most likely:', 'A violation requiring disclosure to clients', 'Acceptable, as token items of minimal value are unlikely to impair objectivity', 'A violation of the prohibition on gifts from clients', 'Permitted only if reciprocated', 'B', 'Modest, token benefits that could not reasonably be expected to influence judgement are acceptable; the concern is with lavish or influence-seeking arrangements.', 8),
  ('cfa-ethics-independence-objectivity-q10', 'cfa-ethics-independence-objectivity', 'A manager''s firm pressures him to maintain a favorable rating on a poorly performing affiliate. Maintaining the rating against his analysis would primarily violate the duty to:', 'Preserve confidentiality', 'Maintain independence and objectivity', 'Ensure fair dealing', 'Disclose referral fees', 'B', 'Allowing internal pressure to override an objective, well-founded opinion violates Standard I(B) independence and objectivity.', 9),
  ('cfa-ethics-independence-objectivity-q11', 'cfa-ethics-independence-objectivity', 'Reusing your own firm''s prior research reports in a new report, with the firm''s permission and consistent with policy, is:', 'Plagiarism in all cases', 'Generally acceptable; using one''s own employer''s work product is not plagiarism', 'Only acceptable if each prior author consents individually', 'A violation of independence', 'B', 'Drawing on work produced within one''s own firm, per firm policy, is not plagiarism; plagiarism concerns passing off others'' external work as one''s own.', 10),
  ('cfa-ethics-independence-objectivity-q12', 'cfa-ethics-independence-objectivity', 'An issuer offers to pay an analyst a bonus tied to a favorable report. The analyst should:', 'Accept if disclosed to clients', 'Decline the arrangement', 'Accept if the report is accurate', 'Accept but cap the bonus amount', 'B', 'Compensation contingent on the content/conclusion of research is a clear threat to independence and objectivity and should be declined.', 11),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-distributions-q01', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-distributions', 'For a binomial random variable with n = 20 and p = 0.3, the expected number of successes is:', '3', '6', '14', '0.3', 'B', 'The mean of a binomial distribution is np = 20 × 0.3 = 6.', 0),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-distributions-q02', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-distributions', 'The variance of a binomial variable with n = 20 and p = 0.3 is:', '6.0', '4.2', '2.05', '14.0', 'B', 'Binomial variance = np(1 − p) = 20 × 0.3 × 0.7 = 4.2.', 1),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-distributions-q03', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-distributions', 'Approximately what percentage of observations from a normal distribution lie within ±1.96 standard deviations of the mean?', '68%', '90%', '95%', '99%', 'C', 'About 95% of a normal distribution lies within ±1.96σ; ±1.65σ captures 90% and ±2.58σ captures 99%.', 2),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-distributions-q04', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-distributions', 'Why is the lognormal distribution often used to model asset prices?', 'It is symmetric around zero', 'It is bounded below by zero and right-skewed', 'It can take negative values', 'It has constant variance', 'B', 'Prices cannot be negative; the lognormal distribution is bounded at zero and right-skewed, matching the behavior of asset prices.', 3),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-distributions-q05', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-distributions', 'A portfolio has expected return 12% and standard deviation 18%. Using Roy''s safety-first criterion with a threshold return of 3%, the safety-first ratio is:', '0.50', '0.83', '0.67', '1.50', 'A', 'SFRatio = (12% − 3%) / 18% = 9 / 18 = 0.50. The optimal portfolio maximizes this ratio.', 4),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-distributions-q06', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-distributions', 'The Sharpe ratio is a special case of the safety-first ratio when the threshold return equals the:', 'Market return', 'Risk-free rate', 'Mean return', 'Median return', 'B', 'When the minimum acceptable (threshold) return is set to the risk-free rate, Roy''s safety-first ratio is identical to the Sharpe ratio.', 5),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-distributions-q07', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-distributions', 'A stock return is normally distributed with mean 10% and standard deviation 5%. The z-value for a 2% return is:', '−1.6', '+1.6', '−0.4', '+0.4', 'A', 'z = (x − μ)/σ = (2% − 10%)/5% = −8/5 = −1.6.', 6),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-distributions-q08', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-distributions', 'A key property of the continuous uniform distribution over [a, b] is that:', 'Probability density is highest at the mean', 'Probability density is constant across the interval', 'It is right-skewed', 'Its variance is zero', 'B', 'The continuous uniform distribution assigns equal (constant) probability density across its interval [a, b].', 7),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-distributions-q09', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-distributions', 'If continuously compounded returns are normally distributed, then the corresponding price relatives (1 + holding-period return) are:', 'Normally distributed', 'Lognormally distributed', 'Uniformly distributed', 'Binomially distributed', 'B', 'When continuously compounded returns are normal, the gross return / price relative follows a lognormal distribution.', 8),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-distributions-q10', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-distributions', 'A ''tracking error'' constraint that limits the probability of underperforming a benchmark by more than a set amount is an application of:', 'The central limit theorem', 'Shortfall risk / safety-first concepts', 'Bayes'' formula', 'The binomial distribution', 'B', 'Limiting the probability of falling below a threshold (shortfall risk) is a direct application of safety-first / downside-risk thinking.', 9),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-distributions-q11', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-distributions', 'Which distribution would best model the number of bonds defaulting out of 50 independent issuers, each with a 4% default probability?', 'Normal', 'Lognormal', 'Binomial', 'Continuous uniform', 'C', 'A count of successes/defaults over a fixed number of independent trials with constant probability is binomial.', 10),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-distributions-q12', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-distributions', 'Standardizing a normal random variable produces a distribution with:', 'Mean 1 and standard deviation 0', 'Mean 0 and standard deviation 1', 'Mean 0 and variance 0', 'Mean equal to the original mean', 'B', 'The standard normal distribution, obtained via z = (x − μ)/σ, has a mean of 0 and a standard deviation (and variance) of 1.', 11),
  ('cfa-economics-currency-parity-q01', 'cfa-economics-currency-parity', 'Which parity condition is enforced by arbitrage and therefore essentially always holds?', 'Uncovered interest rate parity', 'Covered interest rate parity', 'Relative purchasing power parity', 'The international Fisher effect', 'B', 'Covered interest rate parity is enforced by riskless arbitrage using forward contracts, so it holds continuously; the others are equilibrium tendencies.', 0),
  ('cfa-economics-currency-parity-q02', 'cfa-economics-currency-parity', 'Under covered interest rate parity, the currency with the higher interest rate will trade at a forward:', 'Premium', 'Discount', 'Rate equal to spot', 'Rate that is indeterminate', 'B', 'The higher-yielding currency trades at a forward discount, exactly offsetting its interest-rate advantage so no arbitrage profit exists.', 1),
  ('cfa-economics-currency-parity-q03', 'cfa-economics-currency-parity', 'Relative purchasing power parity predicts that a country with persistently higher inflation will see its currency:', 'Appreciate by the inflation differential', 'Depreciate by the inflation differential', 'Remain unchanged', 'Appreciate by the interest differential', 'B', 'Under relative PPP, the higher-inflation country''s currency depreciates by approximately the inflation differential, preserving relative purchasing power.', 2),
  ('cfa-economics-currency-parity-q04', 'cfa-economics-currency-parity', 'Uncovered interest rate parity differs from covered interest rate parity primarily because it:', 'Uses forward contracts to eliminate currency risk', 'Relies on the expected future spot rate and carries currency risk', 'Assumes equal inflation rates', 'Always holds in the short run', 'B', 'UIRP is based on the expected future spot rate without hedging, so it carries currency risk and is not enforced by arbitrage—hence it often fails short-term.', 3),
  ('cfa-economics-currency-parity-q05', 'cfa-economics-currency-parity', 'The international Fisher effect states that the difference in nominal interest rates between two countries equals the difference in their expected:', 'Real interest rates', 'Inflation rates', 'GDP growth rates', 'Current account balances', 'B', 'The international Fisher effect assumes equal real rates across countries, so nominal rate differences reflect expected inflation differences.', 4),
  ('cfa-economics-currency-parity-q06', 'cfa-economics-currency-parity', 'The persistent profitability of the carry trade is evidence that which condition often fails in the short run?', 'Covered interest rate parity', 'Uncovered interest rate parity', 'Absolute purchasing power parity', 'The law of one price for forwards', 'B', 'Carry-trade profits (borrowing low-yield, investing high-yield currencies) imply UIRP—which predicts no expected gain—does not hold short-term.', 5),
  ('cfa-economics-currency-parity-q07', 'cfa-economics-currency-parity', 'Absolute purchasing power parity is built directly on the:', 'Law of one price', 'Quantity theory of money', 'Marshall-Lerner condition', 'Fisher equation', 'A', 'Absolute PPP extends the law of one price (identical goods cost the same everywhere when priced in a common currency) to national price levels.', 6),
  ('cfa-economics-currency-parity-q08', 'cfa-economics-currency-parity', 'Spot USD/EUR is 1.20 and the 1-year forward is 1.176 (USD per EUR). The euro is trading at a forward:', 'Premium, implying lower euro interest rates', 'Discount, implying higher euro interest rates', 'Premium, implying higher euro interest rates', 'Discount, implying lower euro interest rates', 'B', 'The euro buys fewer dollars forward (1.176 < 1.20), so it is at a forward discount—consistent with euro interest rates being higher than USD rates under CIRP.', 7),
  ('cfa-economics-currency-parity-q09', 'cfa-economics-currency-parity', 'If covered interest rate parity is violated, market participants can earn a riskless profit through:', 'Covered interest arbitrage', 'A carry trade', 'Triangular arbitrage only', 'Speculation on inflation', 'A', 'When CIRP fails, covered interest arbitrage (borrow one currency, convert spot, invest, and lock in the forward) earns a riskless profit, quickly restoring parity.', 8),
  ('cfa-economics-currency-parity-q10', 'cfa-economics-currency-parity', 'Combining relative PPP with uncovered interest rate parity implies that real exchange rates are expected to:', 'Rise steadily over time', 'Remain constant', 'Fall steadily over time', 'Equal nominal exchange rates', 'B', 'When relative PPP and UIRP both hold, expected changes in nominal rates offset inflation differentials, leaving the real exchange rate expected to be constant.', 9),
  ('cfa-economics-currency-parity-q11', 'cfa-economics-currency-parity', 'Which factor is NOT a determinant emphasized by the parity conditions for exchange rates?', 'Interest rate differentials', 'Expected inflation differentials', 'Corporate dividend policy', 'Expected future spot rates', 'C', 'The parity framework links exchange rates to interest rates, inflation, and expected spot rates—not to firms'' dividend policies.', 10),
  ('cfa-economics-currency-parity-q12', 'cfa-economics-currency-parity', 'A ''forward rate bias'' refers to the empirical finding that the forward rate is a:', 'Perfect predictor of the future spot rate', 'Biased predictor, with high-yield currencies tending to outperform UIRP''s prediction', 'Predictor only of inflation, not exchange rates', 'Measure of covered arbitrage profit', 'B', 'The forward rate bias is the observation that forwards are biased predictors of future spot rates—high-yield currencies often do not depreciate as UIRP predicts, enabling carry profits.', 11),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-reporting-quality-q01', 'cfa-financial-reporting-reporting-quality', 'The distinction between ''reporting quality'' and ''earnings quality'' is best described as:', 'They are identical concepts', 'Reporting quality refers to decision-usefulness of information; earnings quality refers to sustainability of results', 'Reporting quality refers to the level of profit; earnings quality refers to disclosure', 'Earnings quality refers to GAAP compliance only', 'B', 'Reporting quality concerns the decision-usefulness and faithfulness of the information; earnings (results) quality concerns whether the reported earnings are sustainable and adequate.', 0),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-reporting-quality-q02', 'cfa-financial-reporting-reporting-quality', 'A firm can have HIGH reporting quality while having LOW earnings quality when it:', 'Uses fraudulent transactions', 'Faithfully reports genuinely poor, unsustainable results', 'Capitalizes operating expenses', 'Recognizes revenue prematurely', 'B', 'High reporting quality means the information honestly represents the economics—even when the underlying earnings are low or unsustainable (low earnings quality).', 1),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-reporting-quality-q03', 'cfa-financial-reporting-reporting-quality', 'Which is an example of ''earnings management'' through aggressive accounting choices rather than outright fraud?', 'Recording fictitious sales', 'Lengthening the estimated useful lives of depreciable assets to boost income', 'Creating fake invoices', 'Omitting a known liability entirely', 'B', 'Stretching depreciable lives is an aggressive but within-GAAP estimate change used to manage earnings, distinct from fraudulent, non-GAAP fabrications.', 2),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-reporting-quality-q04', 'cfa-financial-reporting-reporting-quality', 'A high level of accruals relative to cash flow from operations generally signals:', 'Higher earnings quality', 'Lower earnings quality', 'Higher reporting quality', 'No information about quality', 'B', 'Large accruals relative to cash flows indicate earnings rely heavily on estimates/accruals rather than cash, which is associated with lower (less persistent) earnings quality.', 3),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-reporting-quality-q05', 'cfa-financial-reporting-reporting-quality', 'Which is a common MOTIVATION for management to manipulate earnings?', 'To increase audit fees', 'To meet analyst forecasts or debt covenant thresholds', 'To reduce executive compensation', 'To lower the stock price', 'B', 'Managers are often motivated to manipulate earnings to meet/beat analyst expectations, avoid breaching debt covenants, or maximize compensation tied to results.', 4),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-reporting-quality-q06', 'cfa-financial-reporting-reporting-quality', 'On the spectrum of financial reporting quality, which is the LOWEST quality?', 'GAAP-compliant but with biased estimates', 'GAAP-compliant with low earnings quality', 'Fraudulent / fictitious transactions', 'GAAP-compliant and decision-useful', 'C', 'Fabricated or fraudulent transactions sit at the bottom of the quality spectrum, below merely biased-but-compliant or non-compliant-but-non-fraudulent reporting.', 5),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-reporting-quality-q07', 'cfa-financial-reporting-reporting-quality', 'Capitalizing a cost that should be expensed will, in the year incurred, tend to:', 'Decrease net income and decrease assets', 'Increase net income and increase assets', 'Decrease net income and increase liabilities', 'Have no effect on net income', 'B', 'Improperly capitalizing an expense moves it to the balance sheet, raising assets and shifting the cost out of the current period—boosting current net income.', 6),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-reporting-quality-q08', 'cfa-financial-reporting-reporting-quality', 'The Beneish M-score is primarily used to:', 'Estimate a firm''s cost of capital', 'Assess the probability of earnings manipulation', 'Forecast dividend growth', 'Measure liquidity risk', 'B', 'The Beneish M-score combines accounting ratios into a probability score for the likelihood that a firm has manipulated its earnings.', 7),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-reporting-quality-q09', 'cfa-financial-reporting-reporting-quality', '''Channel stuffing'' (pushing excess product to distributors near period-end) is an example of:', 'Conservative accounting', 'Real-activities earnings management', 'A change in accounting principle', 'Improved earnings quality', 'B', 'Channel stuffing manipulates reported results through real operating decisions (accelerating shipments), a form of real-activities earnings management.', 8),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-reporting-quality-q10', 'cfa-financial-reporting-reporting-quality', 'Understating the allowance for doubtful accounts will most likely:', 'Decrease current-period earnings', 'Increase current-period earnings', 'Have no effect on earnings', 'Increase reported liabilities', 'B', 'A smaller bad-debt allowance means lower expense and higher net receivables, increasing current-period earnings—an aggressive choice that lowers earnings quality.', 9),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-reporting-quality-q11', 'cfa-financial-reporting-reporting-quality', 'Which condition from the ''fraud triangle'' relates to weak internal controls or board oversight?', 'Incentive/pressure', 'Opportunity', 'Rationalization', 'Materiality', 'B', 'Opportunity—often arising from weak controls or governance—is the leg of the fraud triangle that enables manipulation, alongside incentive and rationalization.', 10),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-reporting-quality-q12', 'cfa-financial-reporting-reporting-quality', 'Conservative accounting choices (e.g., expensing rather than capitalizing) are best described as:', 'Always indicating high earnings quality', 'A bias that can also reduce decision-usefulness, even though it understates current income', 'A form of fraud', 'Required under IFRS but banned under US GAAP', 'B', 'Although conservatism understates current income, persistent bias in either direction reduces the faithful representation and decision-usefulness of the statements.', 11),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-working-capital-q01', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-working-capital', 'The cash conversion cycle is calculated as:', 'DSO + DOH − DPO', 'DSO − DOH + DPO', 'DPO + DOH − DSO', 'DSO + DPO − DOH', 'A', 'Cash conversion cycle = Days Sales Outstanding + Days of Inventory on Hand − Days Payables Outstanding.', 0),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-working-capital-q02', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-working-capital', 'All else equal, a shorter cash conversion cycle indicates:', 'Weaker liquidity', 'More cash tied up in operations', 'Stronger liquidity and less financing needed', 'Higher inventory levels', 'C', 'A shorter CCC means the firm converts inputs to cash faster, tying up less cash in working capital and improving liquidity.', 1),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-working-capital-q03', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-working-capital', 'The quick (acid-test) ratio differs from the current ratio by excluding:', 'Cash', 'Receivables', 'Inventory', 'Marketable securities', 'C', 'The quick ratio excludes inventory (the least liquid current asset), focusing on cash, marketable securities, and receivables.', 2),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-working-capital-q04', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-working-capital', 'A company with credit terms ''2/10 net 30'' that forgoes the discount and pays on day 30 incurs an implicit annualized cost closest to:', '2%', '24%', '45%', '73%', 'C', 'Cost = (1 + 0.02/0.98)^(365/20) − 1 ≈ 44.6%, so forgoing the discount is expensive financing.', 3),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-working-capital-q05', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-working-capital', 'Days of inventory on hand (DOH) is calculated as:', '365 × inventory turnover', '365 / inventory turnover', 'Inventory / sales', 'Cost of goods sold / inventory', 'B', 'DOH = 365 / inventory turnover, where inventory turnover = COGS / average inventory.', 4),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-working-capital-q06', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-working-capital', 'Which short-term funding source is generally considered the MOST reliable for a borrower?', 'An uncommitted line of credit', 'A committed (revolving) line of credit', 'Commercial paper for a low-rated issuer', 'Trade credit from a single supplier', 'B', 'A committed/revolving line obligates the lender to provide funds (for a fee), making it more reliable than an uncommitted line that can be withdrawn.', 5),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-working-capital-q07', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-working-capital', 'A negative cash conversion cycle, common at some large retailers, implies that:', 'The firm is insolvent', 'Suppliers effectively finance the firm''s operations', 'The firm holds excessive inventory', 'Receivables are collected very slowly', 'B', 'A negative CCC means the firm collects cash from customers before paying suppliers, so suppliers'' trade credit funds operations.', 6),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-working-capital-q08', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-working-capital', 'When managing a short-term investment portfolio for excess cash, the PRIMARY objective is typically:', 'Maximizing yield regardless of risk', 'Safety and liquidity of principal', 'Maximizing duration', 'Currency speculation', 'B', 'Short-term cash management prioritizes preservation of principal and liquidity over yield, since the funds may be needed on short notice.', 7),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-working-capital-q09', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-working-capital', 'Factoring accounts receivable involves:', 'Borrowing against inventory', 'Selling receivables to a third party for immediate cash', 'Extending customer payment terms', 'Issuing commercial paper', 'B', 'Factoring is the sale of receivables (usually at a discount) to a factor in exchange for immediate cash, improving liquidity.', 8),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-working-capital-q10', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-working-capital', 'Days payables outstanding increasing while sales are flat most likely indicates the firm is:', 'Paying suppliers faster', 'Stretching payments to suppliers, conserving cash', 'Collecting receivables faster', 'Reducing inventory', 'B', 'A rising DPO means the firm takes longer to pay suppliers, which conserves cash—though excessive stretching can harm supplier relationships.', 9),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-working-capital-q11', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-working-capital', 'The cash ratio is the most conservative liquidity measure because it includes only:', 'Cash and marketable securities relative to current liabilities', 'Cash, receivables, and inventory', 'All current assets', 'Working capital', 'A', 'The cash ratio counts only cash and (highly liquid) marketable securities against current liabilities, the strictest of the common liquidity ratios.', 10),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-working-capital-q12', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-working-capital', 'A firm reduces its DSO from 45 to 30 days while holding DOH and DPO constant. The cash conversion cycle will:', 'Increase by 15 days', 'Decrease by 15 days', 'Remain unchanged', 'Decrease by 45 days', 'B', 'Since CCC = DSO + DOH − DPO, a 15-day fall in DSO with the other terms constant lowers the CCC by 15 days.', 11),
  ('cfa-equity-residual-income-valuation-q01', 'cfa-equity-residual-income-valuation', 'Residual income for a period is best defined as net income minus a charge for:', 'The cost of debt', 'The cost of equity capital', 'Operating expenses', 'Taxes', 'B', 'Residual income = net income − (required return on equity × beginning book value of equity); it is income after charging for equity capital.', 0),
  ('cfa-equity-residual-income-valuation-q02', 'cfa-equity-residual-income-valuation', 'A firm has beginning book value of equity of $50, net income of $8, and a required return on equity of 12%. Its residual income is:', '$8.00', '$6.00', '$2.00', '$0.96', 'C', 'Equity charge = 12% × $50 = $6. Residual income = $8 − $6 = $2.', 1),
  ('cfa-equity-residual-income-valuation-q03', 'cfa-equity-residual-income-valuation', 'A key advantage of the residual income model over the dividend discount model is that it:', 'Requires no estimate of the cost of equity', 'Recognizes value earlier and relies less on a terminal value', 'Ignores book value entirely', 'Works only for dividend-paying firms', 'B', 'RI models capture much of the value in the current book value and near-term residual income, reducing dependence on a hard-to-estimate terminal value.', 2),
  ('cfa-equity-residual-income-valuation-q04', 'cfa-equity-residual-income-valuation', 'The single-stage residual income model gives V₀ = B₀ + [(ROE − r) × B₀] / (r − g). A firm adds value above book when:', 'ROE < r', 'ROE = r', 'ROE > r', 'g > r', 'C', 'Value exceeds book value only when ROE exceeds the required return r; if ROE = r, value equals book value.', 3),
  ('cfa-equity-residual-income-valuation-q05', 'cfa-equity-residual-income-valuation', 'A major accounting concern when applying the residual income model is that it relies on:', 'Cash flow forecasts only', 'Clean-surplus accounting and book-value quality', 'Dividend policy', 'Market multiples', 'B', 'RI models assume clean-surplus accounting (all income flows through retained earnings) and are sensitive to the quality of reported book value.', 4),
  ('cfa-equity-residual-income-valuation-q06', 'cfa-equity-residual-income-valuation', 'Which is one of the three broad approaches to private company valuation?', 'The arbitrage approach', 'The market (guideline) approach', 'The duration approach', 'The parity approach', 'B', 'Private company valuation uses income, market (guideline public companies/transactions), and asset-based approaches.', 5),
  ('cfa-equity-residual-income-valuation-q07', 'cfa-equity-residual-income-valuation', 'A discount for lack of marketability (DLOM) is applied to private company values because:', 'Private firms always have higher growth', 'Private equity interests cannot be sold quickly without price concession', 'Private firms pay higher dividends', 'Private firms have lower tax rates', 'B', 'Private interests are illiquid and hard to sell promptly, so a DLOM reduces value relative to comparable, freely traded public shares.', 6),
  ('cfa-equity-residual-income-valuation-q08', 'cfa-equity-residual-income-valuation', 'When valuing a controlling interest in a private firm using guideline public companies (which trade as minority interests), an analyst would typically add a:', 'Discount for lack of control', 'Control premium', 'Liquidity discount', 'Size discount', 'B', 'Public minority prices lack control; valuing a controlling stake requires adding a control premium to reflect the power to direct the firm.', 7),
  ('cfa-equity-residual-income-valuation-q09', 'cfa-equity-residual-income-valuation', 'The ''excess earnings method'' of private company valuation is a form of which approach?', 'Market approach', 'Asset-based approach', 'Income approach', 'Option-pricing approach', 'C', 'The excess earnings method values intangible value from earnings above a fair return on tangible assets—an income-based approach.', 8),
  ('cfa-equity-residual-income-valuation-q10', 'cfa-equity-residual-income-valuation', 'Discount rates for private companies are typically higher than for comparable public firms primarily because of:', 'Lower expected growth and added size/company-specific risk premia', 'Lower informational risk', 'Greater liquidity', 'Lower required returns', 'A', 'Private firms warrant additional size and company-specific risk premia (and face illiquidity), raising the required rate of return used in valuation.', 9),
  ('cfa-equity-residual-income-valuation-q11', 'cfa-equity-residual-income-valuation', 'In the residual income model, persistent positive residual income that is expected to fade over time is captured through a:', 'Higher dividend payout', 'Persistence factor that reduces future residual income toward zero', 'Lower book value', 'Negative beta', 'B', 'A persistence factor models how quickly competitive forces erode residual income, scaling down future RI in the continuing-value term.', 10),
  ('cfa-equity-residual-income-valuation-q12', 'cfa-equity-residual-income-valuation', 'The guideline transactions method values a private firm using:', 'Multiples from acquisitions of similar companies', 'The firm''s own prior dividends', 'The risk-free rate only', 'Replacement cost of assets', 'A', 'The guideline transactions method derives valuation multiples from prices paid in acquisitions of comparable companies.', 11),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-spot-forward-rates-q01', 'cfa-fixed-income-spot-forward-rates', 'A spot rate is best defined as the yield on a:', 'Coupon bond priced at par', 'Zero-coupon bond for a given maturity', 'Floating-rate note', 'Bond with embedded options', 'B', 'A spot rate is the yield-to-maturity of a default-free zero-coupon bond maturing at a specific date.', 0),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-spot-forward-rates-q02', 'cfa-fixed-income-spot-forward-rates', 'Given 1-year spot = 3% and 2-year spot = 4%, the implied 1-year forward rate one year from now (1y1y) is closest to:', '3.0%', '4.0%', '5.0%', '7.0%', 'C', '(1.04)² / (1.03) − 1 = 1.0816/1.03 − 1 ≈ 5.01%.', 1),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-spot-forward-rates-q03', 'cfa-fixed-income-spot-forward-rates', 'The par rate for a given maturity is the coupon rate that makes a bond''s price equal to:', 'Its face (par) value', 'Zero', 'The forward price', 'The spot rate', 'A', 'The par rate is the coupon rate, consistent with the spot curve, that prices the bond exactly at par (100).', 2),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-spot-forward-rates-q04', 'cfa-fixed-income-spot-forward-rates', 'When the spot curve is upward sloping, forward rates will be:', 'Below spot rates', 'Equal to spot rates', 'Above spot rates', 'Negative', 'C', 'An upward-sloping spot curve implies forward rates lie above the corresponding spot rates.', 3),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-spot-forward-rates-q05', 'cfa-fixed-income-spot-forward-rates', 'The no-arbitrage relationship between spot and forward rates for two periods is:', '(1+z₂)² = (1+z₁)(1+f(1,1))', '(1+z₂)² = (1+z₁)² + f(1,1)', 'z₂ = z₁ + f(1,1)', '(1+z₂) = (1+z₁)(1+f(1,1))', 'A', 'By no arbitrage, investing for two years at the 2-year spot equals investing one year at z₁ then rolling at the 1y1y forward: (1+z₂)² = (1+z₁)(1+f(1,1)).', 4),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-spot-forward-rates-q06', 'cfa-fixed-income-spot-forward-rates', 'Forward rates can be interpreted as the market''s:', 'Guaranteed future spot rates', 'Break-even future spot rates', 'Risk-free rates only', 'Realized historical rates', 'B', 'Forward rates are break-even rates: an investor is indifferent between strategies only if future spot rates equal today''s forwards.', 5),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-spot-forward-rates-q07', 'cfa-fixed-income-spot-forward-rates', 'An investor expecting future spot rates to be LOWER than current forward rates would prefer to:', 'Roll over short-term bonds', 'Buy and hold a longer-term bond', 'Hold only cash', 'Short all bonds', 'B', 'If realized future spots come in below today''s forwards, locking in longer-term yields (buying the longer bond) outperforms rolling short-term positions.', 6),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-spot-forward-rates-q08', 'cfa-fixed-income-spot-forward-rates', '''Riding (rolling) down the yield curve'' as a return-enhancing strategy works best when the curve is:', 'Downward sloping and shifting up', 'Upward sloping and stable', 'Flat and volatile', 'Inverted and steepening', 'B', 'With a stable, upward-sloping curve, a bond''s yield falls as it ages toward shorter maturities, producing price gains beyond its coupon.', 7),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-spot-forward-rates-q09', 'cfa-fixed-income-spot-forward-rates', 'Discounting each of a bond''s cash flows at its maturity-specific spot rate, rather than a single YTM, produces the bond''s:', 'Forward price', 'Arbitrage-free (no-arbitrage) value', 'Par value', 'Duration', 'B', 'Using the full set of spot rates to discount each cash flow yields the arbitrage-free value, preventing riskless profit from stripping/reconstitution.', 8),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-spot-forward-rates-q10', 'cfa-fixed-income-spot-forward-rates', 'Deriving spot rates sequentially from coupon-bond (par) yields is known as:', 'Bootstrapping', 'Immunization', 'Convexity adjustment', 'Laddering', 'A', 'Bootstrapping iteratively solves for spot rates from observed par/coupon yields, building the zero-coupon (spot) curve.', 9),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-spot-forward-rates-q11', 'cfa-fixed-income-spot-forward-rates', 'If the 1-year spot is 2% and the 2-year spot is 2%, the 1y1y forward rate is closest to:', '0%', '2%', '4%', '1%', 'B', 'With a flat spot curve, forward rates equal spot rates: (1.02)²/(1.02) − 1 = 2%.', 10),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-spot-forward-rates-q12', 'cfa-fixed-income-spot-forward-rates', 'A bond''s realized return equals its YTM only if coupons are reinvested at the YTM and the bond is held to maturity, assuming the yield curve is:', 'Steeply upward sloping', 'Flat and unchanged', 'Inverted', 'Highly volatile', 'B', 'The YTM is realized only under a flat, unchanged curve with reinvestment at the YTM; otherwise reinvestment and price effects cause realized return to differ.', 11),
  ('cfa-derivatives-binomial-option-valuation-q01', 'cfa-derivatives-binomial-option-valuation', 'In the one-period binomial model, the risk-neutral probability of an up move is:', '(u − d) / (1 + r − d)', '(1 + r − d) / (u − d)', '(u − 1 − r) / (u − d)', '(1 + r) / (u × d)', 'B', 'The risk-neutral up-probability is π = (1 + r − d) / (u − d), where u and d are the up/down factors.', 0),
  ('cfa-derivatives-binomial-option-valuation-q02', 'cfa-derivatives-binomial-option-valuation', 'A defining feature of risk-neutral valuation is that the option value does NOT depend on:', 'The risk-free rate', 'The size of the up and down moves', 'Investors'' actual (real-world) probabilities of up/down moves', 'The current price of the underlying', 'C', 'Once the option is replicated/hedged, real-world probabilities and risk preferences are irrelevant—only the risk-neutral probabilities matter.', 1),
  ('cfa-derivatives-binomial-option-valuation-q03', 'cfa-derivatives-binomial-option-valuation', 'A stock is $100; it can rise to $120 (u = 1.2) or fall to $90 (d = 0.9). With r = 5%, the risk-neutral probability of an up move is closest to:', '0.50', '0.40', '0.60', '0.33', 'A', 'π = (1 + 0.05 − 0.9) / (1.2 − 0.9) = 0.15 / 0.30 = 0.50.', 2),
  ('cfa-derivatives-binomial-option-valuation-q04', 'cfa-derivatives-binomial-option-valuation', 'Using the previous setup (π = 0.50, r = 5%), a one-period call with strike $100 (payoffs $20 up, $0 down) is worth closest to:', '$20.00', '$10.00', '$9.52', '$0.00', 'C', 'c₀ = [0.5 × 20 + 0.5 × 0] / 1.05 = 10 / 1.05 ≈ $9.52.', 3),
  ('cfa-derivatives-binomial-option-valuation-q05', 'cfa-derivatives-binomial-option-valuation', 'The binomial ''hedge ratio'' for a call equals the change in option value divided by the change in:', 'The risk-free rate', 'The underlying''s price across states', 'Time', 'Volatility', 'B', 'The hedge ratio = (c_u − c_d) / (S·u − S·d): the number of underlying units needed to replicate the option payoff.', 4),
  ('cfa-derivatives-binomial-option-valuation-q06', 'cfa-derivatives-binomial-option-valuation', 'Early exercise can add value to an American-style option most notably for:', 'Calls on a non-dividend-paying stock', 'Puts that are deep in the money, and calls on dividend-paying stocks', 'All European options', 'Options with zero time to expiry', 'B', 'American puts (deep in the money) and American calls on dividend-paying stocks can be worth more than their European counterparts due to early exercise.', 5),
  ('cfa-derivatives-binomial-option-valuation-q07', 'cfa-derivatives-binomial-option-valuation', 'The no-arbitrage principle in option pricing rests on the ability to:', 'Forecast the real probability of an up move', 'Replicate the option with the underlying and risk-free borrowing/lending', 'Diversify away all risk', 'Predict interest rates', 'B', 'Because a portfolio of the underlying plus borrowing/lending replicates the option''s payoff, the option must trade at the cost of that replicating portfolio.', 6),
  ('cfa-derivatives-binomial-option-valuation-q08', 'cfa-derivatives-binomial-option-valuation', 'Holding other factors constant, increasing the spread between u and d (higher volatility) will cause a call option''s value to:', 'Decrease', 'Increase', 'Stay the same', 'Become negative', 'B', 'Greater dispersion in possible outcomes raises the option''s value because of its asymmetric (limited downside) payoff.', 7),
  ('cfa-derivatives-binomial-option-valuation-q09', 'cfa-derivatives-binomial-option-valuation', 'To value a two-period binomial option, an analyst should:', 'Discount the final payoffs at the real expected return', 'Work backward through the tree, discounting risk-neutral expected values node by node', 'Use only the first-period probabilities', 'Ignore intermediate nodes', 'B', 'Multi-period binomial valuation proceeds by backward induction: compute payoffs at the final nodes, then discount risk-neutral expectations back step by step.', 8),
  ('cfa-derivatives-binomial-option-valuation-q10', 'cfa-derivatives-binomial-option-valuation', 'For the risk-neutral probability to be valid (between 0 and 1), the model requires:', 'd < 1 + r < u', 'u < 1 + r < d', 'u = d', 'r < 0', 'A', 'No-arbitrage requires d < (1 + r) < u; otherwise the risk-neutral probability would fall outside [0, 1] and arbitrage would exist.', 9),
  ('cfa-derivatives-binomial-option-valuation-q11', 'cfa-derivatives-binomial-option-valuation', 'Using the put-call parity check, a one-period put in the earlier example (π = 0.5, r = 5%, strike $100, payoffs $0 up, $10 down) is worth closest to:', '$4.76', '$10.00', '$5.00', '$0.00', 'A', 'p₀ = [0.5 × 0 + 0.5 × 10] / 1.05 = 5 / 1.05 ≈ $4.76.', 10),
  ('cfa-derivatives-binomial-option-valuation-q12', 'cfa-derivatives-binomial-option-valuation', 'As the number of periods in a binomial tree increases toward infinity (with appropriate u and d), the model''s option values converge to those of the:', 'Capital asset pricing model', 'Black-Scholes-Merton model', 'Dividend discount model', 'Arbitrage pricing theory', 'B', 'A finely subdivided binomial tree converges to the continuous-time Black-Scholes-Merton option value.', 11),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-private-debt-q01', 'cfa-alternative-investments-private-debt', 'Private debt is best described as:', 'Publicly traded investment-grade bonds', 'Loans or bonds extended directly to borrowers outside public markets', 'Government treasury securities', 'Listed equity of lending banks', 'B', 'Private debt comprises directly negotiated loans/bonds to (often mid-market) borrowers, made outside public capital markets, typically by funds.', 0),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-private-debt-q02', 'cfa-alternative-investments-private-debt', 'Direct lending strategies most commonly involve loans that are:', 'Subordinated and equity-linked', 'Senior, secured, and often floating-rate', 'Unsecured and fixed-rate government bonds', 'Convertible into common shares', 'B', 'Direct lending typically provides senior, secured, floating-rate loans to private companies, emphasizing current income.', 1),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-private-debt-q03', 'cfa-alternative-investments-private-debt', 'Mezzanine debt is characterized by being:', 'Senior to all other claims', 'Subordinated debt that often includes equity warrants', 'Risk-free', 'Always investment grade', 'B', 'Mezzanine financing is subordinated, higher-yielding debt frequently bundled with equity warrants—a hybrid between debt and equity.', 2),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-private-debt-q04', 'cfa-alternative-investments-private-debt', 'A ''loan-to-own'' approach is most associated with which private debt strategy?', 'Venture debt', 'Investment-grade direct lending', 'Distressed debt investing', 'Infrastructure debt', 'C', 'Distressed-debt investors may buy a troubled firm''s debt intending to convert it into a controlling equity stake through restructuring (''loan-to-own'').', 3),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-private-debt-q05', 'cfa-alternative-investments-private-debt', 'The rapid post-2008 growth of private debt is largely attributed to:', 'Banks expanding mid-market lending', 'Tighter bank capital rules causing banks to retrench from such lending', 'Falling investor demand for yield', 'A ban on syndicated loans', 'B', 'Stricter post-crisis capital and regulatory requirements caused banks to pull back from middle-market lending, creating space for private debt funds.', 4),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-private-debt-q06', 'cfa-alternative-investments-private-debt', 'Investors in private debt are primarily compensated for bearing:', 'Only inflation risk', 'Illiquidity and credit risk', 'Currency risk only', 'No incremental risk', 'B', 'Private debt offers a yield premium chiefly to compensate for illiquidity and the credit risk of often-leveraged, non-public borrowers.', 5),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-private-debt-q07', 'cfa-alternative-investments-private-debt', 'Because direct loans are frequently floating-rate, they tend to have:', 'High interest-rate (duration) risk', 'Low interest-rate (duration) risk', 'No credit risk', 'Guaranteed returns', 'B', 'Floating-rate coupons reset with reference rates, giving direct loans low duration/interest-rate risk relative to fixed-rate bonds.', 6),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-private-debt-q08', 'cfa-alternative-investments-private-debt', '''Covenant-lite'' (cov-lite) loans are a concern because they:', 'Carry no interest', 'Offer lenders fewer protections and early-warning triggers', 'Are always senior secured', 'Eliminate default risk', 'B', 'Cov-lite loans weaken or remove maintenance covenants, reducing lender protections and the early warnings that covenants normally provide.', 7),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-private-debt-q09', 'cfa-alternative-investments-private-debt', 'Venture debt is most commonly extended to:', 'Mature investment-grade corporations', 'Venture-capital-backed startups alongside their equity investors', 'Sovereign governments', 'Real estate investment trusts', 'B', 'Venture debt provides loans to early-stage, venture-backed companies, typically complementing their VC equity financing.', 8),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-private-debt-q10', 'cfa-alternative-investments-private-debt', 'Beyond interest income, an additional return driver for private debt funds is:', 'Dividend imputation credits', 'Origination and prepayment fees', 'Index rebalancing', 'Stock buybacks', 'B', 'Private debt returns include fee income (e.g., origination and prepayment fees) on top of interest, and capital gains in distressed/mezzanine strategies.', 9),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-private-debt-q11', 'cfa-alternative-investments-private-debt', 'Compared with public high-yield bonds, private debt instruments are generally:', 'More liquid', 'Less liquid', 'Free of credit risk', 'Always lower yielding', 'B', 'Private debt is less liquid than publicly traded high-yield bonds, which is a key reason it offers an illiquidity yield premium.', 10),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-private-debt-q12', 'cfa-alternative-investments-private-debt', 'Fund-level leverage in a private debt fund primarily affects:', 'Only the management fee', 'The amplification of both returns and risk to investors', 'The borrowers'' credit ratings', 'Nothing material', 'B', 'Leverage applied at the fund level magnifies returns to limited partners but also amplifies losses and risk, an important due-diligence consideration.', 11),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-behavioral-finance-q01', 'cfa-portfolio-management-behavioral-finance', 'Behavioral biases are commonly classified into which two broad categories?', 'Systematic and unsystematic', 'Cognitive errors and emotional biases', 'Liquid and illiquid', 'Active and passive', 'B', 'Behavioral finance splits biases into cognitive errors (faulty reasoning) and emotional biases (feeling-driven).', 0),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-behavioral-finance-q02', 'cfa-portfolio-management-behavioral-finance', 'The disposition effect—selling winners too early and holding losers too long—stems mainly from:', 'Anchoring', 'Loss aversion', 'Confirmation bias', 'Availability bias', 'B', 'Loss aversion makes investors reluctant to realize losses, so they hold losers and sell winners—the disposition effect.', 1),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-behavioral-finance-q03', 'cfa-portfolio-management-behavioral-finance', 'An investor who seeks out only information confirming an existing view exhibits:', 'Confirmation bias', 'Hindsight bias', 'Mental accounting', 'Regret aversion', 'A', 'Confirmation bias is the tendency to notice and favor evidence that supports prior beliefs while ignoring contradictory information.', 2),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-behavioral-finance-q04', 'cfa-portfolio-management-behavioral-finance', 'According to prospect theory, individuals tend to be:', 'Risk-seeking in gains and risk-averse in losses', 'Risk-averse in gains and risk-seeking in losses', 'Always risk-neutral', 'Always risk-averse', 'B', 'Prospect theory finds people are risk-averse over gains but risk-seeking over losses, evaluated relative to a reference point.', 3),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-behavioral-finance-q05', 'cfa-portfolio-management-behavioral-finance', 'Cognitive errors, relative to emotional biases, are generally:', 'Impossible to correct', 'Easier to mitigate through education and better information', 'Only found in institutional investors', 'Always beneficial', 'B', 'Because cognitive errors arise from faulty reasoning, they can often be reduced with education, data, and disciplined processes, unlike deeper emotional biases.', 4),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-behavioral-finance-q06', 'cfa-portfolio-management-behavioral-finance', 'Treating money differently based on its source or intended use is known as:', 'Mental accounting', 'Anchoring', 'Overconfidence', 'Herding', 'A', 'Mental accounting is the bias of segregating money into separate ''accounts'' and treating each differently, violating fungibility.', 5),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-behavioral-finance-q07', 'cfa-portfolio-management-behavioral-finance', 'An advisor should generally MODERATE cognitive biases but ADAPT to:', 'Market risk', 'Emotional biases', 'Liquidity needs', 'Tax constraints', 'B', 'Standard guidance is to moderate (correct) cognitive errors while adapting the plan around harder-to-change emotional biases.', 6),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-behavioral-finance-q08', 'cfa-portfolio-management-behavioral-finance', 'Investors relying too heavily on an initial reference figure (e.g., a purchase price) when estimating value exhibit:', 'Anchoring and adjustment', 'Self-control bias', 'Endowment bias', 'Status quo bias', 'A', 'Anchoring is the tendency to fixate on an initial value and adjust insufficiently away from it when forming new estimates.', 7),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-behavioral-finance-q09', 'cfa-portfolio-management-behavioral-finance', 'Overconfidence bias most commonly leads investors to:', 'Hold excessive cash', 'Trade too frequently and underdiversify', 'Avoid all equities', 'Over-rely on index funds', 'B', 'Overconfident investors overestimate their skill/information, leading to excessive trading and insufficient diversification.', 8),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-behavioral-finance-q10', 'cfa-portfolio-management-behavioral-finance', 'Following the crowd into popular trades, even against one''s own analysis, is:', 'Herding behavior', 'Conservatism', 'Framing', 'Illusion of control', 'A', 'Herding is the tendency to mimic the actions of a larger group, which can contribute to bubbles and crashes.', 9),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-behavioral-finance-q11', 'cfa-portfolio-management-behavioral-finance', 'Endowment bias is best illustrated by an investor who:', 'Demands a higher price to sell an inherited stock than they would pay to buy it', 'Trades on insider information', 'Diversifies across many assets', 'Rebalances mechanically', 'A', 'Endowment bias causes people to value an asset they own more highly simply because they own it, creating a gap between selling and buying prices.', 10),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-behavioral-finance-q12', 'cfa-portfolio-management-behavioral-finance', 'Market anomalies such as momentum and value effects are significant because they:', 'Confirm strong-form market efficiency', 'Appear to contradict fully efficient markets and may reflect behavioral factors', 'Prove markets are completely random', 'Eliminate the need for risk management', 'B', 'Persistent anomalies challenge the efficient-market hypothesis and are often attributed (at least partly) to behavioral biases among investors.', 11),
  ('cfa-ethics-code-of-ethics-standards-q09', 'cfa-ethics-code-of-ethics-standards', 'The first of the seven Standards of Professional Conduct is:', 'Professionalism', 'Duties to Clients', 'Integrity of Capital Markets', 'Investment Analysis', 'A', 'Standard I is Professionalism, covering knowledge of the law, independence, misrepresentation and misconduct.', 8),
  ('cfa-ethics-code-of-ethics-standards-q10', 'cfa-ethics-code-of-ethics-standards', 'Under Standard I(A) Knowledge of the Law, when applicable law and the Code & Standards differ, a member must follow:', 'The stricter of the two', 'Local law always', 'The Code & Standards always', 'Whichever is easier to apply', 'A', 'Members must comply with the more strict of applicable law or the Code and Standards.', 9),
  ('cfa-ethics-code-of-ethics-standards-q11', 'cfa-ethics-code-of-ethics-standards', 'Standard I(B) requires members to maintain:', 'Independence and objectivity', 'Confidentiality of law', 'Priority of transactions', 'Record retention', 'A', 'Standard I(B) Independence and Objectivity requires members to use reasonable care and judgement and resist influences that compromise it.', 10),
  ('cfa-ethics-code-of-ethics-standards-q12', 'cfa-ethics-code-of-ethics-standards', 'The Code of Ethics requires members to place the integrity of the profession and the interests of clients:', 'Above their own personal interests', 'Below employer targets', 'Equal to their own interests', 'After regulatory interests', 'A', 'Members must place the integrity of the profession and client interests above their own personal interests.', 11),
  ('cfa-ethics-material-non-public-info-q09', 'cfa-ethics-material-non-public-info', 'Standard II(A) prohibits members from acting or causing others to act on:', 'Material non-public information', 'Any public information', 'Analyst consensus estimates', 'Historical price data', 'A', 'Standard II(A) prohibits trading or causing others to trade on material non-public information.', 8),
  ('cfa-ethics-material-non-public-info-q10', 'cfa-ethics-material-non-public-info', 'Information is ''material'' if:', 'Its disclosure would likely affect a security''s price or a reasonable investor would want it', 'It is longer than one page', 'It comes from management only', 'It is already widely known', 'A', 'Materiality turns on whether disclosure would move the price or a reasonable investor would consider it important.', 9),
  ('cfa-ethics-material-non-public-info-q11', 'cfa-ethics-material-non-public-info', 'The ''mosaic theory'' permits an analyst to:', 'Combine public and non-material non-public information to reach a conclusion', 'Trade on any tip received', 'Ignore public disclosures', 'Share inside information with clients', 'A', 'Under the mosaic theory, analysts may reach conclusions from public and non-material non-public information without violating the standard.', 10),
  ('cfa-ethics-material-non-public-info-q12', 'cfa-ethics-material-non-public-info', 'Standard II(B) Market Manipulation prohibits:', 'Practices that distort prices or artificially inflate trading volume', 'Publishing independent research', 'Disclosing conflicts', 'Fair dealing with clients', 'A', 'Market manipulation covers information-based and transaction-based practices that mislead market participants.', 11),
  ('cfa-ethics-duties-to-clients-q09', 'cfa-ethics-duties-to-clients', 'Standard III(A) Loyalty, Prudence, and Care requires members to place:', 'Clients'' interests before their own and their employer''s', 'Employer interests first', 'Their own interests first', 'Regulators'' interests first', 'A', 'Members owe clients loyalty and must act for their benefit, placing client interests first.', 8),
  ('cfa-ethics-duties-to-clients-q10', 'cfa-ethics-duties-to-clients', 'Standard III(B) Fair Dealing requires members to:', 'Treat all clients fairly when disseminating recommendations or taking action', 'Give the largest clients priority', 'Favour discretionary accounts', 'Disclose trades to the public first', 'A', 'Fair dealing requires acting fairly and objectively with all clients when providing investment advice or taking action.', 9),
  ('cfa-ethics-duties-to-clients-q11', 'cfa-ethics-duties-to-clients', 'Standard III(C) Suitability requires that recommendations be:', 'Consistent with the client''s written objectives, mandate and constraints', 'The highest-returning available', 'The same for every client', 'Based only on the member''s view', 'A', 'Suitability requires matching recommendations to the client''s IPS — objectives, risk tolerance and constraints.', 10),
  ('cfa-ethics-duties-to-clients-q12', 'cfa-ethics-duties-to-clients', 'Under Standard III(E), a member may disclose confidential client information when:', 'It concerns illegal activities, disclosure is required by law, or the client permits it', 'A colleague asks for it', 'It would help marketing', 'Never, under any circumstance', 'A', 'Confidentiality must be preserved except where the law requires disclosure, the client permits, or it concerns illegal client activity.', 11),
  ('cfa-ethics-conflicts-of-interest-q09', 'cfa-ethics-conflicts-of-interest', 'Standard VI(A) Disclosure of Conflicts requires that conflicts be:', 'Disclosed prominently, in plain language, and fully', 'Hidden from clients', 'Disclosed only if asked', 'Reported to regulators only', 'A', 'Members must fully and fairly disclose all matters that could impair independence or interfere with duties.', 8),
  ('cfa-ethics-conflicts-of-interest-q10', 'cfa-ethics-conflicts-of-interest', 'Standard VI(B) Priority of Transactions requires that transactions for clients come before those of:', 'The member and the firm', 'Other clients', 'Charitable accounts', 'Index funds', 'A', 'Client transactions take priority over transactions in which a member or the firm is a beneficial owner.', 9),
  ('cfa-ethics-conflicts-of-interest-q11', 'cfa-ethics-conflicts-of-interest', 'Standard VI(C) Referral Fees requires members to:', 'Disclose referral compensation to clients and employer', 'Refuse all referral fees', 'Keep referral fees private', 'Pay referral fees to clients', 'A', 'Any consideration paid or received for recommendations must be disclosed to clients and the employer.', 10),
  ('cfa-ethics-conflicts-of-interest-q12', 'cfa-ethics-conflicts-of-interest', 'A member who receives a bonus gift from a client for strong past performance should:', 'Disclose it to the employer', 'Keep it secret', 'Return it always', 'Share it with other clients', 'A', 'Additional compensation or gifts from clients must be disclosed to the employer to manage the conflict.', 11),
  ('cfa-ethics-gips-standards-q09', 'cfa-ethics-gips-standards', 'GIPS stands for:', 'Global Investment Performance Standards', 'General Investment Pricing System', 'Global Index Performance Series', 'Governed Investment Portfolio Standards', 'A', 'GIPS are the Global Investment Performance Standards for fair, comparable performance reporting.', 8),
  ('cfa-ethics-gips-standards-q10', 'cfa-ethics-gips-standards', 'GIPS compliance must be applied on a:', 'Firm-wide basis', 'Single-portfolio basis', 'Per-client basis', 'Per-strategy basis only', 'A', 'A firm must apply GIPS on a firm-wide basis; it cannot claim compliance for only some composites.', 9),
  ('cfa-ethics-gips-standards-q11', 'cfa-ethics-gips-standards', 'Under GIPS, a ''composite'' is:', 'An aggregation of portfolios managed to a similar strategy or mandate', 'A single flagship fund', 'A benchmark index', 'The firm''s total assets', 'A', 'A composite groups all discretionary portfolios managed according to a similar investment mandate or strategy.', 10),
  ('cfa-ethics-gips-standards-q12', 'cfa-ethics-gips-standards', 'Compliance with the GIPS standards is:', 'Voluntary', 'Legally mandatory worldwide', 'Required only in the US', 'Required for all public companies', 'A', 'GIPS compliance is voluntary but widely adopted to demonstrate fair performance presentation.', 11),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-time-value-of-money-q09', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-time-value-of-money', 'The future value of a single sum invested for n periods at rate r grows by the factor:', '(1 + r)^n', '1 + rn', '(1 − r)^n', 'n / (1 + r)', 'A', 'FV = PV × (1 + r)^n under compound interest.', 8),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-time-value-of-money-q10', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-time-value-of-money', 'An annuity due differs from an ordinary annuity in that its payments occur at the:', 'Beginning of each period', 'End of each period', 'Midpoint of each period', 'Maturity date only', 'A', 'In an annuity due, payments are made at the start of each period, so its value is higher than an ordinary annuity.', 9),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-time-value-of-money-q11', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-time-value-of-money', 'The present value of a level perpetuity paying PMT with discount rate r is:', 'PMT / r', 'PMT × r', 'PMT / (1 + r)', 'PMT × (1 + r)', 'A', 'A perpetuity''s present value is PMT divided by the discount rate.', 10),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-time-value-of-money-q12', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-time-value-of-money', 'The effective annual rate for a stated rate r compounded m times per year is:', '(1 + r/m)^m − 1', 'r/m', 'r × m', '(1 + r)^m', 'A', 'EAR = (1 + periodic rate)^m − 1, where the periodic rate is r/m.', 11),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-q09', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-probability', 'For two mutually exclusive events A and B, P(A or B) equals:', 'P(A) + P(B)', 'P(A) × P(B)', 'P(A) − P(B)', 'P(A) / P(B)', 'A', 'Mutually exclusive events cannot occur together, so the addition rule simplifies to P(A) + P(B).', 8),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-q10', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-probability', 'For two independent events, P(A and B) equals:', 'P(A) × P(B)', 'P(A) + P(B)', 'P(A) − P(B)', 'P(A | B)', 'A', 'For independent events the joint probability is the product of their individual probabilities.', 9),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-q11', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-probability', 'Bayes'' formula is used to:', 'Update a prior probability given new information', 'Compute a bond''s duration', 'Estimate a regression slope', 'Find the mode of a distribution', 'A', 'Bayes'' formula revises a prior probability into a posterior after observing new evidence.', 10),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-probability-q12', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-probability', 'The expected value of a random variable is:', 'The probability-weighted average of its possible outcomes', 'Its largest outcome', 'Its median outcome', 'Its variance', 'A', 'Expected value is the sum of each outcome multiplied by its probability.', 11),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-statistical-inference-q09', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-statistical-inference', 'The central limit theorem states that the sampling distribution of the sample mean approaches:', 'A normal distribution as sample size increases', 'A uniform distribution', 'The population''s exact shape', 'A t-distribution with 1 df', 'A', 'For a large enough sample, the distribution of the sample mean is approximately normal regardless of the population.', 8),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-statistical-inference-q10', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-statistical-inference', 'The standard error of the sample mean equals:', 'σ / √n', 'σ × n', 'σ² / n', '√σ / n', 'A', 'The standard error is the population (or sample) standard deviation divided by the square root of n.', 9),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-statistical-inference-q11', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-statistical-inference', 'An unbiased estimator has an expected value equal to:', 'The true population parameter', 'Zero', 'The sample size', 'The standard error', 'A', 'Unbiasedness means the estimator''s expected value equals the parameter it estimates.', 10),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-statistical-inference-q12', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-statistical-inference', 'A sampling method in which every population member has an equal chance of selection is:', 'Simple random sampling', 'Stratified sampling', 'Judgemental sampling', 'Convenience sampling', 'A', 'Simple random sampling gives each element an equal probability of being chosen.', 11),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing-q09', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing', 'A Type I error occurs when a researcher:', 'Rejects a true null hypothesis', 'Fails to reject a false null', 'Accepts the alternative correctly', 'Uses the wrong test statistic', 'A', 'A Type I error is rejecting the null hypothesis when it is actually true (a false positive).', 8),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing-q10', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing', 'The significance level (α) of a test is the probability of:', 'A Type I error', 'A Type II error', 'A correct decision', 'The power of the test', 'A', 'α is the probability of making a Type I error — rejecting a true null.', 9),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing-q11', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing', 'The p-value of a test is:', 'The smallest significance level at which the null can be rejected', 'The probability the null is true', 'Always equal to 0.05', 'The test''s power', 'A', 'The p-value is the smallest level of significance at which the null hypothesis can be rejected.', 10),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing-q12', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing', 'If the test statistic falls in the rejection region, the analyst should:', 'Reject the null hypothesis', 'Fail to reject the null', 'Increase the sample size', 'Accept the null as proven', 'A', 'A test statistic in the rejection region leads to rejecting the null hypothesis.', 11),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-regression-q09', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-regression', 'In a simple linear regression, the slope coefficient measures:', 'The change in the dependent variable per unit change in the independent variable', 'The average of Y', 'The correlation squared', 'The intercept', 'A', 'The slope estimates how much Y changes for a one-unit change in X.', 8),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-regression-q10', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-regression', 'The coefficient of determination (R²) measures:', 'The proportion of the variation in Y explained by the regression', 'The slope of the line', 'The residual sum of squares', 'The standard error of the estimate', 'A', 'R² is the fraction of total variation in the dependent variable explained by the independent variable(s).', 9),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-regression-q11', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-regression', 'The assumption that the variance of the error term is constant across observations is called:', 'Homoskedasticity', 'Heteroskedasticity', 'Autocorrelation', 'Multicollinearity', 'A', 'Homoskedasticity means the error variance is constant; its violation is heteroskedasticity.', 10),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-regression-q12', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-regression', 'Multicollinearity in a multiple regression refers to:', 'High correlation among the independent variables', 'Correlated error terms', 'Non-constant error variance', 'A non-linear relationship', 'A', 'Multicollinearity is a high correlation among two or more independent variables, inflating coefficient standard errors.', 11),
  ('cfa-economics-market-structures-q09', 'cfa-economics-market-structures', 'In perfect competition, firms are:', 'Price takers producing identical products', 'Price makers with unique products', 'A single dominant seller', 'Colluding oligopolists', 'A', 'Perfectly competitive firms are price takers selling homogeneous products with free entry and exit.', 8),
  ('cfa-economics-market-structures-q10', 'cfa-economics-market-structures', 'A market with a single seller and high barriers to entry is a:', 'Monopoly', 'Perfect competition', 'Monopolistic competition', 'Duopoly', 'A', 'A monopoly has one seller of a product with no close substitutes and high entry barriers.', 9),
  ('cfa-economics-market-structures-q11', 'cfa-economics-market-structures', 'The concentration of an industry is often measured by the:', 'Herfindahl-Hirschman Index (HHI)', 'Consumer Price Index', 'Gini coefficient', 'Sharpe ratio', 'A', 'The HHI sums the squared market shares of firms to gauge industry concentration.', 10),
  ('cfa-economics-market-structures-q12', 'cfa-economics-market-structures', 'A profit-maximising firm produces where:', 'Marginal revenue equals marginal cost', 'Price equals total cost', 'Average cost is highest', 'Revenue is highest', 'A', 'Firms maximise profit at the output where marginal revenue equals marginal cost.', 11),
  ('cfa-economics-gdp-business-cycles-q09', 'cfa-economics-gdp-business-cycles', 'Under the expenditure approach, GDP equals:', 'C + I + G + (X − M)', 'Wages + rent + interest', 'Exports − imports', 'Consumption only', 'A', 'GDP = consumption + investment + government spending + net exports.', 8),
  ('cfa-economics-gdp-business-cycles-q10', 'cfa-economics-gdp-business-cycles', 'A recession is commonly defined as:', 'Two consecutive quarters of declining real GDP', 'One month of falling prices', 'A rising stock market', 'Any fall in nominal GDP', 'A', 'A common rule of thumb defines a recession as two successive quarters of declining real GDP.', 9),
  ('cfa-economics-gdp-business-cycles-q11', 'cfa-economics-gdp-business-cycles', 'Leading economic indicators are used to:', 'Anticipate future economic activity', 'Confirm past turning points', 'Measure current output only', 'Set tax rates', 'A', 'Leading indicators (e.g., new orders, stock prices) tend to change before the overall economy does.', 10),
  ('cfa-economics-gdp-business-cycles-q12', 'cfa-economics-gdp-business-cycles', 'Dividing nominal GDP by the GDP deflator (and scaling) gives:', 'Real GDP', 'Potential GDP', 'Nominal GNP', 'The unemployment rate', 'A', 'Real GDP adjusts nominal GDP for price changes using the GDP deflator.', 11),
  ('cfa-economics-monetary-fiscal-policy-q09', 'cfa-economics-monetary-fiscal-policy', 'Expansionary monetary policy typically involves:', 'Lowering interest rates and increasing the money supply', 'Raising reserve requirements', 'Selling government bonds', 'Cutting government spending', 'A', 'Expansionary monetary policy eases financial conditions by lowering rates and expanding the money supply.', 8),
  ('cfa-economics-monetary-fiscal-policy-q10', 'cfa-economics-monetary-fiscal-policy', 'A tool of contractionary fiscal policy is:', 'Raising taxes or cutting government spending', 'Lowering the policy rate', 'Buying government securities', 'Quantitative easing', 'A', 'Fiscal policy works through taxes and government spending; contractionary fiscal policy raises taxes or cuts spending.', 9),
  ('cfa-economics-monetary-fiscal-policy-q11', 'cfa-economics-monetary-fiscal-policy', 'To increase the money supply through open market operations, a central bank:', 'Buys government securities', 'Sells government securities', 'Raises the reserve requirement', 'Increases the discount rate', 'A', 'Buying securities injects reserves into the banking system, expanding the money supply.', 10),
  ('cfa-economics-monetary-fiscal-policy-q12', 'cfa-economics-monetary-fiscal-policy', 'The neutral policy interest rate is one that:', 'Neither stimulates nor restrains the economy', 'Is always zero', 'Maximises inflation', 'Equals the coupon rate on bonds', 'A', 'The neutral rate is consistent with stable inflation and output at potential — neither expansionary nor contractionary.', 11),
  ('cfa-economics-international-trade-forex-q09', 'cfa-economics-international-trade-forex', 'A country has a comparative advantage in a good when it can produce it at a lower:', 'Opportunity cost', 'Absolute quantity', 'Selling price only', 'Tax rate', 'A', 'Comparative advantage rests on lower opportunity cost, not merely lower absolute cost.', 8),
  ('cfa-economics-international-trade-forex-q10', 'cfa-economics-international-trade-forex', 'A tariff is best described as:', 'A tax on imported goods', 'A limit on export quantity', 'A subsidy to consumers', 'A currency peg', 'A', 'A tariff is a tax levied on imports, raising their domestic price.', 9),
  ('cfa-economics-international-trade-forex-q11', 'cfa-economics-international-trade-forex', 'In a direct exchange-rate quote, the rate is expressed as:', 'Units of domestic currency per unit of foreign currency', 'Units of foreign currency per domestic unit', 'A percentage return', 'The interest rate differential', 'A', 'A direct quote states the price of one unit of foreign currency in domestic-currency terms.', 10),
  ('cfa-economics-international-trade-forex-q12', 'cfa-economics-international-trade-forex', 'A quota differs from a tariff in that a quota:', 'Limits the quantity of imports rather than taxing them', 'Raises government tax revenue directly', 'Applies only to exports', 'Is a type of subsidy', 'A', 'A quota caps the physical quantity of a good that may be imported, whereas a tariff taxes each unit.', 11),
  ('cfa-economics-geopolitics-globalisation-q09', 'cfa-economics-geopolitics-globalisation', 'Globalisation generally leads to:', 'Greater cross-border trade and capital flows', 'Reduced international trade', 'Fewer multinational firms', 'Higher tariffs everywhere', 'A', 'Globalisation integrates economies through expanded trade, capital and labour flows.', 8),
  ('cfa-economics-geopolitics-globalisation-q10', 'cfa-economics-geopolitics-globalisation', 'A country pursuing autarky seeks:', 'Economic self-sufficiency with minimal trade', 'Maximum exports', 'A common currency', 'Free capital flows', 'A', 'Autarky is a policy of national economic self-sufficiency and non-cooperation with other economies.', 9),
  ('cfa-economics-geopolitics-globalisation-q11', 'cfa-economics-geopolitics-globalisation', 'The risk that a government seizes foreign-owned assets is a form of:', 'Political (nationalisation) risk', 'Interest rate risk', 'Liquidity risk', 'Reinvestment risk', 'A', 'Nationalisation or expropriation of assets is a political risk faced by cross-border investors.', 10),
  ('cfa-economics-geopolitics-globalisation-q12', 'cfa-economics-geopolitics-globalisation', 'Regional trading blocs such as the EU primarily aim to:', 'Reduce trade barriers among member countries', 'Raise tariffs against members', 'Fix all exchange rates globally', 'Eliminate private capital', 'A', 'Trading blocs lower or remove barriers among members to promote intra-bloc trade.', 11),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-financial-ratios-analysis-q09', 'cfa-financial-reporting-financial-ratios-analysis', 'The current ratio is calculated as:', 'Current assets / current liabilities', 'Net income / sales', 'Debt / equity', 'Sales / total assets', 'A', 'The current ratio, a liquidity measure, divides current assets by current liabilities.', 8),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-financial-ratios-analysis-q10', 'cfa-financial-reporting-financial-ratios-analysis', 'Return on equity (ROE) equals:', 'Net income / average shareholders'' equity', 'Net income / total assets', 'Gross profit / sales', 'EBIT / interest', 'A', 'ROE measures profit generated per unit of shareholders'' equity.', 9),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-financial-ratios-analysis-q11', 'cfa-financial-reporting-financial-ratios-analysis', 'The three-part DuPont analysis decomposes ROE into net profit margin, asset turnover, and:', 'Financial leverage', 'The quick ratio', 'Interest coverage', 'The dividend payout', 'A', 'DuPont: ROE = net profit margin × total asset turnover × financial leverage (equity multiplier).', 10),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-financial-ratios-analysis-q12', 'cfa-financial-reporting-financial-ratios-analysis', 'The quick (acid-test) ratio excludes which item from current assets?', 'Inventory', 'Cash', 'Receivables', 'Marketable securities', 'A', 'The quick ratio excludes inventory (the least liquid current asset) from the numerator.', 11),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-income-statement-q09', 'cfa-financial-reporting-income-statement', 'Under the converged revenue standard, revenue is recognised when:', 'Control of the good or service transfers to the customer', 'Cash is collected', 'The contract is signed', 'Production begins', 'A', 'The five-step model recognises revenue as performance obligations are satisfied — when control transfers.', 8),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-income-statement-q10', 'cfa-financial-reporting-income-statement', 'Basic EPS equals:', '(Net income − preferred dividends) / weighted average common shares', 'Net income / total assets', 'Net income / ending shares', 'Dividends / shares', 'A', 'Basic EPS divides income available to common shareholders by the weighted average number of common shares.', 9),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-income-statement-q11', 'cfa-financial-reporting-income-statement', 'Gross profit is:', 'Revenue minus cost of goods sold', 'Revenue minus all expenses', 'Operating income minus taxes', 'Net income plus interest', 'A', 'Gross profit equals revenue less the cost of goods sold.', 10),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-income-statement-q12', 'cfa-financial-reporting-income-statement', 'Recognising an expense in the same period as the revenue it helps generate reflects:', 'The matching principle', 'Conservatism', 'The going-concern assumption', 'Historical cost', 'A', 'The matching principle recognises expenses in the period of the related revenues.', 11),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-balance-sheet-equity-q09', 'cfa-financial-reporting-balance-sheet-equity', 'The basic accounting equation is:', 'Assets = Liabilities + Equity', 'Assets = Revenue − Expenses', 'Equity = Assets + Liabilities', 'Liabilities = Assets + Equity', 'A', 'The balance sheet balances because assets are financed by liabilities plus owners'' equity.', 8),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-balance-sheet-equity-q10', 'cfa-financial-reporting-balance-sheet-equity', 'Unrealised gains on debt securities measured at fair value through OCI are reported in:', 'Other comprehensive income', 'Net income', 'Retained earnings directly', 'Cost of goods sold', 'A', 'For FVOCI (available-for-sale) debt securities, unrealised gains and losses bypass the income statement and go to OCI.', 9),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-balance-sheet-equity-q11', 'cfa-financial-reporting-balance-sheet-equity', 'Treasury stock is:', 'A company''s own repurchased shares, shown as a contra-equity item', 'Government bonds held as assets', 'A current liability', 'Preferred stock issued', 'A', 'Treasury stock is a firm''s reacquired shares, reducing total shareholders'' equity.', 10),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-balance-sheet-equity-q12', 'cfa-financial-reporting-balance-sheet-equity', 'Goodwill arises in an acquisition when the purchase price exceeds:', 'The fair value of identifiable net assets acquired', 'The book value of total assets', 'Retained earnings', 'The market capitalisation', 'A', 'Goodwill is the excess of the purchase price over the fair value of identifiable net assets.', 11),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-cash-flow-statement-q09', 'cfa-financial-reporting-cash-flow-statement', 'Under US GAAP, interest paid is classified as a:', 'Operating cash flow', 'Financing cash flow', 'Investing cash flow', 'Non-cash item', 'A', 'US GAAP requires interest paid to be reported in operating cash flows (IFRS allows operating or financing).', 8),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-cash-flow-statement-q10', 'cfa-financial-reporting-cash-flow-statement', 'The indirect method of preparing the cash flow statement begins with:', 'Net income', 'Cash receipts from customers', 'Total revenue', 'Ending cash balance', 'A', 'The indirect method starts with net income and adjusts for non-cash items and working-capital changes.', 9),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-cash-flow-statement-q11', 'cfa-financial-reporting-cash-flow-statement', 'Free cash flow to the firm (FCFF) adds back to CFO the after-tax amount of:', 'Interest expense', 'Depreciation', 'Dividends', 'Taxes', 'A', 'FCFF = CFO + interest×(1 − tax) − capex, adding back after-tax interest since FCFF is pre-financing.', 10),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-cash-flow-statement-q12', 'cfa-financial-reporting-cash-flow-statement', 'The purchase of new equipment is classified as a(n):', 'Investing cash outflow', 'Operating cash outflow', 'Financing cash inflow', 'Non-cash transaction', 'A', 'Capital expenditures on long-lived assets are investing activities.', 11),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-inventory-ppe-taxes-q09', 'cfa-financial-reporting-inventory-ppe-taxes', 'In a period of rising prices, the LIFO method (vs FIFO) produces:', 'Higher COGS and lower net income', 'Lower COGS and higher net income', 'The same COGS', 'Higher ending inventory', 'A', 'LIFO expenses the most recent (higher) costs first, raising COGS and lowering reported income when prices rise.', 8),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-inventory-ppe-taxes-q10', 'cfa-financial-reporting-inventory-ppe-taxes', 'Under IFRS, inventory is carried at:', 'The lower of cost and net realisable value', 'Replacement cost always', 'Historical cost only', 'Fair value through profit or loss', 'A', 'IFRS measures inventory at the lower of cost and net realisable value (LIFO is not permitted).', 9),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-inventory-ppe-taxes-q11', 'cfa-financial-reporting-inventory-ppe-taxes', 'A deferred tax liability generally arises when:', 'Taxable income is temporarily lower than pretax accounting income', 'The firm overpays taxes', 'There is a permanent difference', 'The tax rate is zero', 'A', 'A DTL results from a temporary difference where accounting income exceeds taxable income now, reversing later.', 10),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-inventory-ppe-taxes-q12', 'cfa-financial-reporting-inventory-ppe-taxes', 'An accelerated depreciation method produces, relative to straight-line:', 'Higher depreciation expense in the early years', 'Constant expense each year', 'Lower total depreciation', 'No effect on net income', 'A', 'Accelerated methods (e.g., double-declining balance) front-load depreciation into the early years.', 11),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital-q09', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital', 'The weighted average cost of capital (WACC) weights the cost of each source of capital by its:', 'Market-value proportion in the capital structure', 'Book-value proportion only', 'Coupon rate', 'Dividend yield', 'A', 'WACC uses market-value weights of debt, preferred and equity times their respective costs.', 8),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital-q10', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital', 'The after-tax cost of debt equals the pre-tax cost of debt multiplied by:', '(1 − tax rate)', '(1 + tax rate)', 'The tax rate', 'The beta', 'A', 'Interest is tax-deductible, so the after-tax cost of debt is rd × (1 − t).', 9),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital-q11', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital', 'The CAPM estimates the cost of equity as:', 'Risk-free rate + beta × (market risk premium)', 'Dividend / price', 'Risk-free rate only', 'WACC minus debt cost', 'A', 'CAPM: cost of equity = Rf + β(E[Rm] − Rf).', 10),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital-q12', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital', 'A project''s marginal cost of capital tends to ___ as more capital is raised.', 'Increase', 'Decrease', 'Stay constant forever', 'Fall to zero', 'A', 'As a firm raises more capital, its marginal cost of capital generally rises (upward-sloping MCC schedule).', 11),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-q09', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-structure', 'Modigliani-Miller Proposition I (no taxes) states that firm value is:', 'Independent of the capital structure', 'Maximised with all equity', 'Maximised with all debt', 'Equal to net income', 'A', 'Under MM with no taxes, capital structure is irrelevant to firm value.', 8),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-q10', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-structure', 'With corporate taxes, MM shows firm value rises with debt because of:', 'The interest tax shield', 'Higher dividends', 'Lower business risk', 'Reduced leverage', 'A', 'Tax-deductible interest creates a tax shield, so adding debt increases firm value in the MM-with-taxes model.', 9),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-q11', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-structure', 'The trade-off theory of capital structure balances the tax shield against:', 'The costs of financial distress', 'The dividend payout', 'The cost of equity only', 'Working capital needs', 'A', 'The optimal structure trades off the tax benefit of debt against expected financial-distress and bankruptcy costs.', 10),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-q12', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-structure', 'Under the pecking-order theory, firms prefer to finance first with:', 'Internally generated funds', 'New equity', 'Convertible bonds', 'Preferred stock', 'A', 'The pecking order is internal funds first, then debt, and new equity as a last resort.', 11),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-dividends-buybacks-q09', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-dividends-buybacks', 'On the ex-dividend date, a share price typically:', 'Falls by approximately the dividend amount', 'Rises by the dividend amount', 'Is unchanged', 'Doubles', 'A', 'All else equal, the price drops by about the dividend on the ex-dividend date.', 8),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-dividends-buybacks-q10', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-dividends-buybacks', 'A share repurchase, all else equal, tends to:', 'Reduce shares outstanding and can raise EPS', 'Increase shares outstanding', 'Raise total equity', 'Reduce the share price permanently', 'A', 'Buybacks lower share count; if funded below the earnings yield they can increase EPS.', 9),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-dividends-buybacks-q11', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-dividends-buybacks', 'A stock dividend:', 'Increases shares outstanding but leaves total equity unchanged', 'Reduces retained earnings to zero', 'Pays cash to shareholders', 'Increases total assets', 'A', 'A stock dividend capitalises retained earnings into share capital; total equity and each holder''s proportional stake are unchanged.', 10),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-dividends-buybacks-q12', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-dividends-buybacks', 'The dividend payout ratio equals:', 'Dividends / net income', 'Dividends / share price', 'Net income / dividends', 'Retained earnings / assets', 'A', 'The payout ratio is common dividends divided by net income.', 11),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-governance-q09', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-governance', 'Corporate governance primarily addresses conflicts between:', 'Managers and shareholders (the principal-agent problem)', 'Customers and suppliers', 'Auditors and regulators', 'Employees and unions', 'A', 'A central governance issue is the principal-agent conflict between owners (shareholders) and managers.', 8),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-governance-q10', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-governance', 'In ESG analysis, the ''E'' stands for:', 'Environmental', 'Economic', 'Equity', 'Efficiency', 'A', 'ESG stands for Environmental, Social and Governance factors.', 9),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-governance-q11', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-governance', 'A staggered (classified) board tends to:', 'Entrench management and weaken shareholder control', 'Strengthen shareholder rights', 'Eliminate the CEO role', 'Increase dividend payouts', 'A', 'Because only part of a staggered board is elected each year, it makes takeovers and board change harder, entrenching management.', 10),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-governance-q12', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-governance', 'Which of the following is a stakeholder in corporate governance?', 'Creditors', 'Only the CEO', 'Only the board', 'Only regulators', 'A', 'Stakeholders include shareholders, creditors, employees, customers, suppliers and others affected by the firm.', 11),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-leverage-analysis-q09', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-leverage-analysis', 'The degree of operating leverage (DOL) measures the sensitivity of operating income to changes in:', 'Units sold (revenue)', 'Interest rates', 'The tax rate', 'Dividends', 'A', 'DOL is the percentage change in operating income (EBIT) for a percentage change in units sold.', 8),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-leverage-analysis-q10', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-leverage-analysis', 'The degree of financial leverage (DFL) measures the sensitivity of net income (EPS) to changes in:', 'Operating income (EBIT)', 'Sales units', 'The dividend payout', 'The share price', 'A', 'DFL is the percentage change in EPS for a percentage change in EBIT, driven by fixed financing costs.', 9),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-leverage-analysis-q11', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-leverage-analysis', 'A firm with higher fixed operating costs will generally have a:', 'Higher degree of operating leverage', 'Lower business risk', 'Lower breakeven point', 'Higher dividend payout', 'A', 'Greater fixed costs magnify the effect of sales changes on operating income, raising DOL and business risk.', 10),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-leverage-analysis-q12', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-leverage-analysis', 'The degree of total leverage (DTL) equals:', 'DOL × DFL', 'DOL + DFL', 'DOL / DFL', 'DFL − DOL', 'A', 'Total leverage combines operating and financial leverage: DTL = DOL × DFL.', 11),
  ('cfa-equity-equity-valuation-ddm-q09', 'cfa-equity-equity-valuation-ddm', 'The Gordon (constant) growth model values a share as:', 'D₁ / (r − g)', 'D₁ × (r − g)', 'D₁ / (r + g)', 'D₀ × r', 'A', 'The Gordon growth model: value = next year''s dividend ÷ (required return − growth rate).', 8),
  ('cfa-equity-equity-valuation-ddm-q10', 'cfa-equity-equity-valuation-ddm', 'The Gordon growth model requires that the growth rate g be:', 'Less than the required return r', 'Greater than r', 'Equal to r', 'Negative', 'A', 'The model only converges when g < r; otherwise the denominator is non-positive.', 9),
  ('cfa-equity-equity-valuation-ddm-q11', 'cfa-equity-equity-valuation-ddm', 'The sustainable growth rate equals:', 'Retention ratio × ROE', 'Payout ratio × ROE', 'ROE / dividend', 'Dividend yield × r', 'A', 'g = b × ROE, where b is the earnings retention ratio (1 − payout).', 10),
  ('cfa-equity-equity-valuation-ddm-q12', 'cfa-equity-equity-valuation-ddm', 'A multistage DDM is most appropriate for a firm that is:', 'Growing rapidly now but expected to settle to stable growth later', 'Paying no dividends ever', 'In constant growth from day one', 'About to be liquidated', 'A', 'Multistage models handle firms with high near-term growth that transitions to a stable long-run rate.', 11),
  ('cfa-equity-market-organisation-efficiency-q09', 'cfa-equity-market-organisation-efficiency', 'In a semi-strong-form efficient market, prices fully reflect:', 'All publicly available information', 'Only past prices', 'All public and private information', 'No information', 'A', 'Semi-strong efficiency means public information is already impounded in prices, so fundamental analysis can''t earn abnormal returns.', 8),
  ('cfa-equity-market-organisation-efficiency-q10', 'cfa-equity-market-organisation-efficiency', 'A short sale involves:', 'Selling borrowed securities in the hope of buying them back cheaper', 'Buying on margin', 'Writing a covered call', 'Holding to maturity', 'A', 'A short seller borrows and sells a security, aiming to profit if the price falls.', 9),
  ('cfa-equity-market-organisation-efficiency-q11', 'cfa-equity-market-organisation-efficiency', 'A margin call is triggered when the investor''s equity falls below the:', 'Maintenance margin', 'Initial margin', 'Coupon rate', 'Bid-ask spread', 'A', 'If account equity drops below the maintenance margin, the broker issues a margin call for more funds.', 10),
  ('cfa-equity-market-organisation-efficiency-q12', 'cfa-equity-market-organisation-efficiency', 'If markets are weak-form efficient, which approach cannot produce abnormal returns?', 'Technical analysis', 'Insider trading', 'Private information use', 'Fundamental analysis', 'A', 'Weak-form efficiency means past prices are already reflected, so technical analysis cannot earn abnormal returns.', 11),
  ('cfa-equity-industry-company-analysis-q09', 'cfa-equity-industry-company-analysis', 'Porter''s five forces include rivalry, threat of new entrants, threat of substitutes, and the bargaining power of buyers and:', 'Suppliers', 'Regulators', 'Employees', 'Auditors', 'A', 'The fifth force is the bargaining power of suppliers.', 8),
  ('cfa-equity-industry-company-analysis-q10', 'cfa-equity-industry-company-analysis', 'A cyclical company is one whose earnings are:', 'Highly sensitive to the business cycle', 'Stable regardless of the economy', 'Independent of demand', 'Always growing', 'A', 'Cyclical firms (e.g., autos, steel) see earnings rise and fall sharply with the economic cycle.', 9),
  ('cfa-equity-industry-company-analysis-q11', 'cfa-equity-industry-company-analysis', 'High barriers to entry in an industry tend to support:', 'More sustainable margins and pricing power', 'Rapid new competition', 'Lower profitability', 'Commoditised products', 'A', 'Barriers to entry protect incumbents, supporting higher, more durable profitability.', 10),
  ('cfa-equity-industry-company-analysis-q12', 'cfa-equity-industry-company-analysis', 'Grouping companies by their principal business activity is called:', 'Industry classification', 'Credit rating', 'Index weighting', 'Sector rotation', 'A', 'Industry classification systems group firms by their main products or activities for comparison.', 11),
  ('cfa-equity-dcf-fcf-valuation-q09', 'cfa-equity-dcf-fcf-valuation', 'Free cash flow to the firm (FCFF) is the cash available to:', 'All capital providers (debt and equity)', 'Common shareholders only', 'Preferred holders only', 'Bondholders only', 'A', 'FCFF is pre-financing cash flow available to all suppliers of capital.', 8),
  ('cfa-equity-dcf-fcf-valuation-q10', 'cfa-equity-dcf-fcf-valuation', 'Free cash flow to equity (FCFE) is the cash available to:', 'Common equity holders', 'All capital providers', 'The government', 'Suppliers', 'A', 'FCFE is the cash flow left for common shareholders after debt payments and reinvestment.', 9),
  ('cfa-equity-dcf-fcf-valuation-q11', 'cfa-equity-dcf-fcf-valuation', 'In a DCF valuation, the terminal value is frequently estimated using:', 'The Gordon (constant) growth model', 'The current P/E only', 'Book value', 'The coupon rate', 'A', 'A common terminal-value approach applies constant-growth (Gordon) assumptions to the final forecast cash flow.', 10),
  ('cfa-equity-dcf-fcf-valuation-q12', 'cfa-equity-dcf-fcf-valuation', 'FCFF should be discounted at the:', 'Weighted average cost of capital (WACC)', 'Cost of equity', 'Risk-free rate', 'Coupon rate', 'A', 'Because FCFF belongs to all providers of capital, it is discounted at the WACC; FCFE uses the cost of equity.', 11),
  ('cfa-equity-price-multiples-ev-q09', 'cfa-equity-price-multiples-ev', 'The price-to-earnings (P/E) ratio equals:', 'Price per share / earnings per share', 'Earnings / price', 'Price / sales', 'Dividend / price', 'A', 'The P/E ratio divides the share price by earnings per share.', 8),
  ('cfa-equity-price-multiples-ev-q10', 'cfa-equity-price-multiples-ev', 'Enterprise value (EV) is calculated as:', 'Market value of equity + debt − cash', 'Market cap only', 'Total assets − liabilities', 'Net income × P/E', 'A', 'EV adds net debt to equity value: market cap + total debt − cash and equivalents.', 9),
  ('cfa-equity-price-multiples-ev-q11', 'cfa-equity-price-multiples-ev', 'The justified leading P/E under constant growth equals:', 'Payout ratio / (r − g)', '(r − g) / payout', '1 / (r + g)', 'ROE × payout', 'A', 'Dividing the Gordon model by earnings gives leading P/E = payout / (r − g).', 10),
  ('cfa-equity-price-multiples-ev-q12', 'cfa-equity-price-multiples-ev', 'EV/EBITDA is often preferred to P/E because it is:', 'Independent of capital structure', 'Based only on dividends', 'Unaffected by operations', 'Always lower', 'A', 'EV/EBITDA is capital-structure neutral and can compare firms with different leverage or tax positions.', 11),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-duration-convexity-q09', 'cfa-fixed-income-duration-convexity', 'Modified duration measures a bond''s price sensitivity to:', 'A change in its yield', 'A change in the coupon', 'Time to the next coupon', 'The credit rating', 'A', 'Modified duration estimates the percentage price change for a 1% change in yield.', 8),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-duration-convexity-q10', 'cfa-fixed-income-duration-convexity', 'All else equal, a bond with a longer maturity has a:', 'Higher duration', 'Lower duration', 'Zero duration', 'Negative duration', 'A', 'Longer maturity generally raises duration and interest-rate sensitivity.', 9),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-duration-convexity-q11', 'cfa-fixed-income-duration-convexity', 'Convexity captures the fact that the price-yield relationship of an option-free bond is:', 'Curved, not linear', 'Perfectly linear', 'Always downward-sloping to zero', 'Flat', 'A', 'Duration is a linear approximation; convexity corrects for the curvature of the price-yield curve.', 10),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-duration-convexity-q12', 'cfa-fixed-income-duration-convexity', 'A higher coupon rate, all else equal, results in a:', 'Lower duration', 'Higher duration', 'Unchanged duration', 'Negative duration', 'A', 'Higher coupons return cash sooner, lowering the bond''s duration.', 11),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-bond-features-valuation-q09', 'cfa-fixed-income-bond-features-valuation', 'The price of a bond equals the present value of its:', 'Coupon payments and principal repayment', 'Coupon payments only', 'Face value only', 'Dividends', 'A', 'A bond''s value is the discounted sum of its coupons and its par (principal) repayment.', 8),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-bond-features-valuation-q10', 'cfa-fixed-income-bond-features-valuation', 'When the market yield exceeds the coupon rate, the bond trades at a:', 'Discount to par', 'Premium to par', 'Price equal to par', 'Price of zero', 'A', 'If required yield > coupon, the bond''s price is below par (a discount).', 9),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-bond-features-valuation-q11', 'cfa-fixed-income-bond-features-valuation', 'Relative to an otherwise identical option-free bond, a callable bond typically has a:', 'Lower price and higher yield', 'Higher price and lower yield', 'The same price', 'Zero yield', 'A', 'The call option benefits the issuer, so investors demand a higher yield (lower price).', 10),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-bond-features-valuation-q12', 'cfa-fixed-income-bond-features-valuation', 'A zero-coupon bond is issued at:', 'A discount to par, paying par at maturity', 'A premium to par', 'Par value with coupons', 'Above par always', 'A', 'Zero-coupon bonds pay no periodic interest and are issued below par, returning full par at maturity.', 11),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-yield-measures-term-structure-q09', 'cfa-fixed-income-yield-measures-term-structure', 'Yield to maturity assumes that coupons are reinvested at:', 'The yield to maturity', 'The coupon rate', 'The risk-free rate', 'Zero', 'A', 'The YTM realised return assumes all coupons are reinvested at the YTM and the bond is held to maturity.', 8),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-yield-measures-term-structure-q10', 'cfa-fixed-income-yield-measures-term-structure', 'The yield curve plots yield against:', 'Time to maturity', 'Credit rating', 'Coupon rate', 'Issue size', 'A', 'The (spot/yield) curve shows yields across different maturities for similar-quality bonds.', 9),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-yield-measures-term-structure-q11', 'cfa-fixed-income-yield-measures-term-structure', 'A normal (upward-sloping) yield curve has long-term yields that are:', 'Higher than short-term yields', 'Lower than short-term yields', 'Equal to short-term yields', 'Always negative', 'A', 'A normal curve slopes upward, with longer maturities offering higher yields.', 10),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-yield-measures-term-structure-q12', 'cfa-fixed-income-yield-measures-term-structure', 'The spread of a corporate bond over a government benchmark compensates mainly for:', 'Credit and liquidity risk', 'Reinvestment of coupons', 'Currency risk only', 'Inflation only', 'A', 'The credit spread compensates investors for default (credit) and liquidity risk relative to government bonds.', 11),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-credit-risk-analysis-q09', 'cfa-fixed-income-credit-risk-analysis', 'Credit risk comprises default risk and:', 'Credit spread (migration) risk', 'Reinvestment risk', 'Purchasing-power risk', 'Call risk', 'A', 'Credit risk includes the risk of default and the risk that spreads widen or the rating is downgraded.', 8),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-credit-risk-analysis-q10', 'cfa-fixed-income-credit-risk-analysis', 'The ''four Cs'' of credit analysis include capacity, collateral, covenants, and:', 'Character', 'Cash only', 'Convexity', 'Coupon', 'A', 'The four Cs are capacity, collateral, covenants and character.', 9),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-credit-risk-analysis-q11', 'cfa-fixed-income-credit-risk-analysis', 'A bond is considered investment grade if it is rated at least:', 'BBB− / Baa3', 'BB+ / Ba1', 'CCC / Caa', 'D', 'A', 'Investment-grade bonds are rated BBB−/Baa3 or higher; below that is high-yield (speculative).', 10),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-credit-risk-analysis-q12', 'cfa-fixed-income-credit-risk-analysis', 'Expected loss on a bond is approximately:', 'Probability of default × loss given default', 'Coupon × maturity', 'Duration × yield', 'Par − coupon', 'A', 'Expected loss = probability of default × loss given default (times exposure).', 11),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-securitisation-abs-q09', 'cfa-fixed-income-securitisation-abs', 'Securitisation involves:', 'Pooling assets and issuing securities backed by their cash flows', 'Issuing common equity', 'Buying back debt', 'Paying dividends', 'A', 'Securitisation pools financial assets (e.g., loans) and sells claims on their cash flows to investors.', 8),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-securitisation-abs-q10', 'cfa-fixed-income-securitisation-abs', 'Mortgage-backed securities are particularly exposed to:', 'Prepayment risk', 'Currency risk', 'Equity risk', 'Commodity risk', 'A', 'Borrowers can prepay mortgages, creating prepayment (contraction/extension) risk for MBS investors.', 9),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-securitisation-abs-q11', 'cfa-fixed-income-securitisation-abs', 'In a securitisation, the pooled assets are typically held by a:', 'Special purpose vehicle (SPV/SPE)', 'Central bank', 'Stock exchange', 'Rating agency', 'A', 'A bankruptcy-remote SPV holds the assets, isolating them from the originator''s credit risk.', 10),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-securitisation-abs-q12', 'cfa-fixed-income-securitisation-abs', 'In a tranched structure, losses are absorbed first by the:', 'Most junior (equity) tranche', 'Most senior tranche', 'SPV sponsor', 'Rating agency', 'A', 'Subordination means the junior/equity tranche takes first losses, protecting senior tranches.', 11),
  ('cfa-derivatives-options-put-call-parity-q09', 'cfa-derivatives-options-put-call-parity', 'Put-call parity for European options states that a call plus the PV of the strike equals:', 'A put plus the underlying asset', 'Two calls', 'The strike price', 'A forward minus a put', 'A', 'c + PV(X) = p + S₀ links the prices of European calls and puts on the same underlying and strike.', 8),
  ('cfa-derivatives-options-put-call-parity-q10', 'cfa-derivatives-options-put-call-parity', 'A call option gives the holder the right to:', 'Buy the underlying at the strike price', 'Sell the underlying at the strike', 'Receive a fixed coupon', 'Short the underlying', 'A', 'A call is the right (not obligation) to buy the underlying at the exercise (strike) price.', 9),
  ('cfa-derivatives-options-put-call-parity-q11', 'cfa-derivatives-options-put-call-parity', 'The intrinsic value of a call option is:', 'Max(0, S − X)', 'Max(0, X − S)', 'S + X', 'The premium paid', 'A', 'A call''s intrinsic value is the greater of zero and the underlying price minus the strike.', 10),
  ('cfa-derivatives-options-put-call-parity-q12', 'cfa-derivatives-options-put-call-parity', 'Deviations from put-call parity create opportunities for:', 'Riskless arbitrage', 'Guaranteed losses', 'Dividend capture', 'Higher taxes', 'A', 'If parity is violated, arbitrageurs can construct a riskless profit until prices realign.', 11),
  ('cfa-derivatives-forwards-futures-q09', 'cfa-derivatives-forwards-futures', 'Compared with forwards, futures contracts are:', 'Exchange-traded and standardised', 'Always customised', 'Free of counterparty oversight', 'Non-transferable', 'A', 'Futures are standardised, exchange-traded and centrally cleared, unlike customised OTC forwards.', 8),
  ('cfa-derivatives-forwards-futures-q10', 'cfa-derivatives-forwards-futures', 'Futures positions are marked to market:', 'Daily', 'Only at expiry', 'Monthly', 'Never', 'A', 'Daily settlement (marking to market) adjusts margin accounts each day, reducing counterparty risk.', 9),
  ('cfa-derivatives-forwards-futures-q11', 'cfa-derivatives-forwards-futures', 'The party that agrees to buy the underlying in a forward contract holds the:', 'Long position', 'Short position', 'Option to cancel', 'Call option', 'A', 'The buyer is long the forward; the seller is short.', 10),
  ('cfa-derivatives-forwards-futures-q12', 'cfa-derivatives-forwards-futures', 'When the futures price is above the spot price, the market is said to be in:', 'Contango', 'Backwardation', 'Equilibrium', 'Default', 'A', 'Contango is when futures trade above spot (often reflecting positive cost of carry).', 11),
  ('cfa-derivatives-swaps-q09', 'cfa-derivatives-swaps', 'A plain-vanilla interest rate swap exchanges:', 'Fixed-rate for floating-rate interest payments', 'Two floating rates', 'Principal for dividends', 'Equity for debt', 'A', 'In a plain-vanilla swap, one party pays fixed and receives floating (or vice versa) on a notional principal.', 8),
  ('cfa-derivatives-swaps-q10', 'cfa-derivatives-swaps', 'A swap can be analytically viewed as:', 'A series of forward contracts', 'A single option', 'A perpetual bond', 'A stock index', 'A', 'A swap is economically equivalent to a portfolio of forward contracts on successive settlement dates.', 9),
  ('cfa-derivatives-swaps-q11', 'cfa-derivatives-swaps', 'In a currency swap, the counterparties exchange:', 'Principal and interest in two different currencies', 'Only floating interest', 'Equity dividends', 'Commodity deliveries', 'A', 'Currency swaps exchange principal and interest payments denominated in different currencies.', 10),
  ('cfa-derivatives-swaps-q12', 'cfa-derivatives-swaps', 'At initiation, the value of a plain-vanilla interest rate swap is:', 'Approximately zero', 'Equal to the notional', 'Equal to the fixed leg', 'Negative for both parties', 'A', 'The fixed rate is set so the swap has zero value to both parties at inception.', 11),
  ('cfa-derivatives-option-strategies-q09', 'cfa-derivatives-option-strategies', 'A protective put combines a long position in the underlying with a:', 'Long put', 'Short put', 'Long call', 'Short call', 'A', 'A protective put buys a put to insure a long stock position against downside.', 8),
  ('cfa-derivatives-option-strategies-q10', 'cfa-derivatives-option-strategies', 'A covered call combines a long position in the underlying with a:', 'Short call', 'Long call', 'Long put', 'Short put', 'A', 'A covered call writes (sells) a call against stock owned, earning premium but capping upside.', 9),
  ('cfa-derivatives-option-strategies-q11', 'cfa-derivatives-option-strategies', 'Delta measures the sensitivity of an option''s price to a change in:', 'The underlying asset''s price', 'Time to expiry', 'Volatility', 'The risk-free rate', 'A', 'Delta is the change in option price for a small change in the underlying price.', 10),
  ('cfa-derivatives-option-strategies-q12', 'cfa-derivatives-option-strategies', 'The maximum loss for the buyer of a call option is:', 'The premium paid', 'Unlimited', 'The strike price', 'The underlying price', 'A', 'A long call''s loss is limited to the premium; its upside is theoretically unlimited.', 11),
  ('cfa-derivatives-risk-management-applications-q09', 'cfa-derivatives-risk-management-applications', 'To hedge the downside of a long equity holding, an investor could:', 'Buy put options', 'Buy call options', 'Write puts', 'Buy more stock', 'A', 'Buying puts (or selling index futures) protects a long equity position against price declines.', 8),
  ('cfa-derivatives-risk-management-applications-q10', 'cfa-derivatives-risk-management-applications', 'A forward rate agreement (FRA) is used to manage:', 'Interest rate risk', 'Equity risk', 'Commodity price risk', 'Currency translation risk', 'A', 'An FRA locks in an interest rate on a future borrowing or deposit, hedging rate risk.', 9),
  ('cfa-derivatives-risk-management-applications-q11', 'cfa-derivatives-risk-management-applications', 'An interest rate cap protects a floating-rate borrower against:', 'Rising interest rates', 'Falling interest rates', 'Currency depreciation', 'Equity declines', 'A', 'A cap pays off when rates rise above the strike, limiting a floating-rate borrower''s cost.', 10),
  ('cfa-derivatives-risk-management-applications-q12', 'cfa-derivatives-risk-management-applications', 'A key advantage of using derivatives to adjust portfolio risk is:', 'Lower transaction costs and greater flexibility than trading the underlying', 'The elimination of all risk', 'Guaranteed profits', 'No need for margin', 'A', 'Derivatives let managers change exposures quickly and cheaply without trading the underlying assets.', 11),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-private-equity-q09', 'cfa-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-private-equity', 'A leveraged buyout (LBO) is a private equity strategy that acquires a company primarily using:', 'A large amount of borrowed money', 'Only common equity', 'Government grants', 'Retained earnings', 'A', 'In an LBO, a firm is bought using significant debt, with the target''s cash flows servicing the borrowing.', 8),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-private-equity-q10', 'cfa-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-private-equity', 'Venture capital typically invests in companies that are:', 'Early-stage and high-growth', 'Large and mature', 'About to be liquidated', 'Government-owned', 'A', 'Venture capital funds early-stage, high-growth private companies.', 9),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-private-equity-q11', 'cfa-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-private-equity', 'A market-neutral hedge fund strategy aims to:', 'Remove overall market exposure by balancing longs and shorts', 'Take only long positions', 'Maximise market beta', 'Hold cash only', 'A', 'Market-neutral funds offset long and short positions to profit from relative moves with little net market exposure.', 10),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-private-equity-q12', 'cfa-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-private-equity', 'Private equity investments are characterised by:', 'Illiquidity and long lock-up periods', 'Daily liquidity', 'Exchange listing', 'Guaranteed returns', 'A', 'PE capital is typically committed for years with limited liquidity until an exit.', 11),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-real-estate-q09', 'cfa-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'A REIT is:', 'A company or trust owning income-producing real estate that trades like a stock', 'A government housing agency', 'A type of mortgage', 'A commodity fund', 'A', 'A real estate investment trust holds property assets and is typically publicly traded, distributing most income.', 8),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-real-estate-q10', 'cfa-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'Under the direct capitalisation (income) approach, property value equals:', 'Net operating income / capitalisation rate', 'NOI × cap rate', 'Rent × 10', 'Cost minus depreciation', 'A', 'Value = NOI ÷ cap rate under the income approach.', 9),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-real-estate-q11', 'cfa-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'Net operating income (NOI) is:', 'Rental income minus operating expenses, before financing and taxes', 'Net income after taxes', 'Gross rent only', 'Cash flow after debt service', 'A', 'NOI is property revenue less operating expenses, excluding financing costs and taxes.', 10),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-real-estate-q12', 'cfa-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'A key portfolio benefit of real estate is:', 'Diversification through low correlation with stocks and bonds', 'Daily liquidity', 'Zero maintenance cost', 'Guaranteed appreciation', 'A', 'Real estate historically has relatively low correlation with traditional assets, aiding diversification and income.', 11),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-commodities-q09', 'cfa-alternative-investments-commodities', 'Investors most commonly gain commodity exposure through:', 'Derivatives such as futures', 'Physical storage of the commodity', 'Corporate bonds', 'Bank deposits', 'A', 'Because physical storage is costly, most investors use commodity futures and related derivatives.', 8),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-commodities-q10', 'cfa-alternative-investments-commodities', 'The total return on a commodity futures position includes the spot return, the collateral return, and the:', 'Roll return', 'Dividend yield', 'Coupon return', 'Franking credit', 'A', 'Commodity futures total return = spot (price) + collateral (risk-free) + roll return.', 9),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-commodities-q11', 'cfa-alternative-investments-commodities', 'A commodity futures market in backwardation has futures prices:', 'Below the spot price', 'Above the spot price', 'Equal to spot', 'At zero', 'A', 'Backwardation is when futures trade below spot; contango is the opposite.', 10),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-commodities-q12', 'cfa-alternative-investments-commodities', 'Commodities are frequently held to hedge against:', 'Inflation', 'Deflation', 'Currency appreciation', 'Interest income', 'A', 'Commodity prices often rise with inflation, giving them a role as an inflation hedge.', 11),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-infrastructure-q09', 'cfa-alternative-investments-infrastructure', 'Infrastructure investments typically offer:', 'Stable, long-term cash flows', 'Highly volatile short-term returns', 'No income', 'Daily liquidity', 'A', 'Infrastructure assets (toll roads, utilities) generally provide steady, long-duration cash flows.', 8),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-infrastructure-q10', 'cfa-alternative-investments-infrastructure', 'A ''brownfield'' infrastructure investment involves:', 'Existing operational assets', 'New construction from scratch', 'Undeveloped land only', 'Distressed equities', 'A', 'Brownfield refers to existing assets; greenfield refers to new construction.', 9),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-infrastructure-q11', 'cfa-alternative-investments-infrastructure', 'Infrastructure assets often exhibit:', 'High barriers to entry and monopolistic characteristics', 'Intense daily competition', 'Rapid obsolescence', 'No regulation', 'A', 'Many infrastructure assets are natural monopolies with high entry barriers and regulated returns.', 10),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-infrastructure-q12', 'cfa-alternative-investments-infrastructure', 'A common way governments involve private capital in infrastructure is through a:', 'Public-private partnership (PPP)', 'Central bank swap', 'Stock buyback', 'Tax amnesty', 'A', 'PPPs share the financing, risks and returns of infrastructure between government and private investors.', 11),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q09', 'cfa-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'The classic hedge fund fee structure of ''2 and 20'' refers to:', 'A 2% management fee and 20% incentive fee', 'A 2-year lock-up and 20 investors', '20% management and 2% incentive', 'A 2% hurdle rate', 'A', '''2 and 20'' is a 2% annual management fee plus a 20% share of profits (incentive fee).', 8),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q10', 'cfa-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'A hurdle rate in a private fund is:', 'The minimum return that must be earned before incentive fees apply', 'The management fee', 'The redemption penalty', 'The risk-free rate ceiling', 'A', 'Incentive fees are charged only on returns above the hurdle rate.', 9),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q11', 'cfa-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'Survivorship bias in hedge fund index data tends to:', 'Overstate historical returns', 'Understate historical returns', 'Have no effect', 'Eliminate volatility', 'A', 'Dropping failed funds from an index leaves only survivors, biasing reported returns upward.', 10),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q12', 'cfa-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'A high-water mark ensures that incentive fees are charged only on:', 'New net profits above the previous peak value', 'Gross assets each year', 'The management fee base', 'The hurdle rate itself', 'A', 'A high-water mark prevents charging performance fees twice on the same gains after a loss is recovered.', 11),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-portfolio-risk-return-capm-q09', 'cfa-portfolio-management-portfolio-risk-return-capm', 'The CAPM expresses a security''s expected return as:', 'Risk-free rate + beta × (market return − risk-free rate)', 'Dividend yield + growth', 'Risk-free rate only', 'Beta × standard deviation', 'A', 'CAPM: E[R] = Rf + β(E[Rm] − Rf), pricing systematic risk via beta.', 8),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-portfolio-risk-return-capm-q10', 'cfa-portfolio-management-portfolio-risk-return-capm', 'Beta measures a security''s:', 'Systematic (market) risk', 'Total risk', 'Unsystematic risk', 'Liquidity risk', 'A', 'Beta measures sensitivity to market movements — the security''s systematic (non-diversifiable) risk.', 9),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-portfolio-risk-return-capm-q11', 'cfa-portfolio-management-portfolio-risk-return-capm', 'The benefit of diversification is greatest when assets have:', 'Low or negative correlation', 'A correlation of +1', 'Identical returns', 'The same issuer', 'A', 'Combining assets with low or negative correlation reduces portfolio risk the most.', 10),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-portfolio-risk-return-capm-q12', 'cfa-portfolio-management-portfolio-risk-return-capm', 'The capital market line (CML) uses which measure of risk on its x-axis?', 'Total risk (standard deviation)', 'Beta', 'Duration', 'Tracking error', 'A', 'The CML plots expected return against total risk (standard deviation) for efficient portfolios; the SML uses beta.', 11),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-ips-planning-q09', 'cfa-portfolio-management-ips-planning', 'The two main components of an investor''s objectives in an IPS are:', 'Return objectives and risk tolerance', 'Taxes and liquidity', 'Legal and unique factors', 'Time horizon and taxes', 'A', 'IPS objectives cover the required/desired return and the investor''s willingness and ability to bear risk.', 8),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-ips-planning-q10', 'cfa-portfolio-management-ips-planning', 'Which of the following is an IPS constraint?', 'Liquidity needs', 'Risk tolerance', 'Return objective', 'Expected alpha', 'A', 'Constraints include liquidity, time horizon, taxes, legal/regulatory and unique circumstances.', 9),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-ips-planning-q11', 'cfa-portfolio-management-ips-planning', 'A young investor with stable income and a long horizon typically has:', 'A higher ability to take risk', 'A lower ability to take risk', 'No return objective', 'High liquidity needs', 'A', 'A long time horizon and stable income generally raise the ability to bear investment risk.', 10),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-ips-planning-q12', 'cfa-portfolio-management-ips-planning', 'An investment policy statement should be reviewed:', 'Periodically and whenever the client''s circumstances change', 'Only once at inception', 'Every day', 'Never after signing', 'A', 'The IPS is a living document, revisited regularly and when objectives or constraints change.', 11),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-asset-allocation-q09', 'cfa-portfolio-management-asset-allocation', 'Strategic asset allocation sets:', 'Long-term target weights based on the IPS and capital market expectations', 'Daily trading limits', 'Individual stock picks', 'The management fee', 'A', 'SAA establishes long-run target asset-class weights aligned with the investor''s objectives and constraints.', 8),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-asset-allocation-q10', 'cfa-portfolio-management-asset-allocation', 'Empirically, the largest driver of a portfolio''s return variability over time is:', 'The asset allocation policy', 'Individual security selection', 'Market timing', 'Trading costs', 'A', 'Studies attribute most of the variability in returns over time to the strategic asset allocation decision.', 9),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-asset-allocation-q11', 'cfa-portfolio-management-asset-allocation', 'Tactical asset allocation involves:', 'Short-term deviations from targets to exploit perceived opportunities', 'Never changing the weights', 'Setting the long-run policy', 'Avoiding all equities', 'A', 'TAA makes temporary, deliberate shifts away from strategic weights to add value from market views.', 10),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-asset-allocation-q12', 'cfa-portfolio-management-asset-allocation', 'Rebalancing a portfolio back to its target weights is:', 'A disciplined, often contrarian, risk-control practice', 'A form of market timing', 'Prohibited under an IPS', 'Only for bonds', 'A', 'Rebalancing sells winners and buys losers to restore target weights, controlling risk in a disciplined way.', 11),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-performance-evaluation-q09', 'cfa-portfolio-management-performance-evaluation', 'The Sharpe ratio measures excess return per unit of:', 'Total risk (standard deviation)', 'Systematic risk (beta)', 'Tracking error', 'Duration', 'A', 'Sharpe ratio = (portfolio return − risk-free rate) ÷ standard deviation of returns.', 8),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-performance-evaluation-q10', 'cfa-portfolio-management-performance-evaluation', 'The Treynor ratio measures excess return per unit of:', 'Systematic risk (beta)', 'Total risk', 'Active risk', 'Liquidity risk', 'A', 'The Treynor ratio divides excess return by beta, rewarding return per unit of market risk.', 9),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-performance-evaluation-q11', 'cfa-portfolio-management-performance-evaluation', 'Jensen''s alpha measures a portfolio''s return in excess of that predicted by:', 'The CAPM', 'The dividend discount model', 'Put-call parity', 'The yield curve', 'A', 'Alpha is the return above the CAPM-required return given the portfolio''s beta.', 10),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-performance-evaluation-q12', 'cfa-portfolio-management-performance-evaluation', 'The information ratio equals active return divided by:', 'Active risk (tracking error)', 'Total risk', 'Beta', 'The risk-free rate', 'A', 'The information ratio is active (excess-over-benchmark) return divided by tracking error.', 11),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-risk-management-q09', 'cfa-portfolio-management-risk-management', 'Value at Risk (VaR) estimates:', 'The minimum loss expected over a period at a given confidence level', 'The guaranteed maximum profit', 'The average annual return', 'The portfolio''s beta', 'A', 'VaR gives the loss threshold that will not be exceeded with a specified probability over a horizon.', 8),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-risk-management-q10', 'cfa-portfolio-management-risk-management', 'A risk budget:', 'Allocates an organisation''s total risk across positions or activities', 'Sets the marketing spend', 'Caps management fees', 'Fixes the dividend', 'A', 'Risk budgeting distributes a total risk limit across the portfolio''s positions or strategies.', 9),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-risk-management-q11', 'cfa-portfolio-management-risk-management', 'Risk that can be eliminated through diversification is:', 'Unsystematic (idiosyncratic) risk', 'Systematic risk', 'Market risk', 'Interest rate risk', 'A', 'Unsystematic, security-specific risk can be diversified away; systematic (market) risk cannot.', 10),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-risk-management-q12', 'cfa-portfolio-management-risk-management', 'The first step in the risk management process is to:', 'Establish risk governance and identify the risk tolerance', 'Buy insurance', 'Measure VaR', 'Rebalance the portfolio', 'A', 'Effective risk management begins with governance and defining the organisation''s risk tolerance and objectives.', 11),
  ('cfa-ethics-duties-to-employers-q01', 'cfa-ethics-duties-to-employers', 'Standard IV(A) Loyalty requires members to:', 'Act for the benefit of their employer and not deprive it of their skills', 'Always prioritise personal profit', 'Share confidential files with competitors', 'Ignore employer interests', 'A', 'Loyalty requires acting for the employer''s benefit and not harming it or divulging confidential information.', 0),
  ('cfa-ethics-duties-to-employers-q02', 'cfa-ethics-duties-to-employers', 'Under Standard IV(B), a member may accept outside compensation that competes with the employer only if:', 'Written consent is obtained from all parties', 'It is kept secret', 'It is under a certain amount', 'The client approves verbally', 'A', 'Additional compensation arrangements require written consent from all parties involved.', 1),
  ('cfa-ethics-duties-to-employers-q03', 'cfa-ethics-duties-to-employers', 'Standard IV(C) Responsibilities of Supervisors requires a member to:', 'Make reasonable efforts to prevent and detect violations by subordinates', 'Personally execute all trades', 'Guarantee no violations ever occur', 'Avoid all supervisory roles', 'A', 'Supervisors must take reasonable steps, including adequate compliance systems, to prevent and detect violations.', 2),
  ('cfa-ethics-duties-to-employers-q04', 'cfa-ethics-duties-to-employers', 'Whistle-blowing that protects the integrity of the market:', 'Can override the duty of loyalty to the employer', 'Always violates Standard IV(A)', 'Is never permitted', 'Requires client payment', 'A', 'Loyalty does not require concealing wrongdoing; protecting clients and the market can justify whistle-blowing.', 3),
  ('cfa-ethics-duties-to-employers-q05', 'cfa-ethics-duties-to-employers', 'While still employed, a member who wishes to start a competing business may:', 'Make preparations but not compete until employment ends', 'Solicit the employer''s clients immediately', 'Take client files home', 'Use employer resources freely', 'A', 'Members may prepare to compete but must not actually compete with, or misappropriate from, the employer while employed.', 4),
  ('cfa-ethics-duties-to-employers-q06', 'cfa-ethics-duties-to-employers', 'A supervisor who lacks adequate compliance procedures should:', 'Decline supervisory responsibility in writing until systems are adopted', 'Proceed and hope for the best', 'Delegate all responsibility informally', 'Resign immediately', 'A', 'If a supervisor cannot discharge the duty, they should decline in writing until adequate procedures are in place.', 5),
  ('cfa-ethics-duties-to-employers-q07', 'cfa-ethics-duties-to-employers', 'Which of the following generally belongs to the employer, not the departing member?', 'Client lists and files', 'The member''s general skills', 'The member''s professional knowledge', 'The member''s experience', 'A', 'Client lists, records and confidential data are employer property; a member''s skills and experience are their own.', 6),
  ('cfa-ethics-duties-to-employers-q08', 'cfa-ethics-duties-to-employers', 'Standard IV covers a member''s duties to their:', 'Employer', 'Regulator', 'Family', 'Competitors', 'A', 'Standard IV — Duties to Employers — addresses loyalty, additional compensation and supervisory responsibilities.', 7),
  ('cfa-ethics-duties-to-employers-q09', 'cfa-ethics-duties-to-employers', 'Independent practice for compensation during employment requires:', 'The employer''s consent', 'No disclosure', 'A regulator''s licence only', 'Client anonymity', 'A', 'Undertaking independent, compensated practice that could conflict with the employer requires the employer''s consent.', 8),
  ('cfa-ethics-duties-to-employers-q10', 'cfa-ethics-duties-to-employers', 'A gift from a client to a member for future performance is best treated under Standard IV(B) by:', 'Disclosing it and obtaining written consent', 'Accepting it quietly', 'Refusing to inform anyone', 'Returning it without disclosure', 'A', 'Compensation arrangements that may create a conflict require disclosure and written consent from all parties.', 9),
  ('cfa-ethics-duties-to-employers-q11', 'cfa-ethics-duties-to-employers', 'The primary purpose of Standard IV(C) is to:', 'Ensure those under supervision comply with laws and the Code', 'Increase trading volume', 'Maximise the supervisor''s pay', 'Avoid record-keeping', 'A', 'Supervisors are responsible for guiding and monitoring subordinates to comply with applicable rules and the Code.', 10),
  ('cfa-ethics-duties-to-employers-q12', 'cfa-ethics-duties-to-employers', 'Divulging an employer''s confidential business information to outsiders violates:', 'Standard IV(A) Loyalty', 'Standard VI(C) Referral Fees', 'GIPS', 'Standard II(B)', 'A', 'Disclosing confidential employer information breaches the duty of loyalty under Standard IV(A).', 11),
  ('cfa-ethics-investment-analysis-recommendations-q01', 'cfa-ethics-investment-analysis-recommendations', 'Standard V(A) Diligence and Reasonable Basis requires that recommendations be:', 'Supported by appropriate research and a reasonable basis', 'Based on rumours', 'Identical for all clients', 'Made without analysis', 'A', 'Members must have a reasonable and adequate basis, supported by research and investigation, for their recommendations.', 0),
  ('cfa-ethics-investment-analysis-recommendations-q02', 'cfa-ethics-investment-analysis-recommendations', 'Standard V(B) Communication with Clients requires members to:', 'Distinguish fact from opinion and disclose the investment process', 'Hide all risks', 'Guarantee returns', 'Present opinions as facts', 'A', 'Members must separate fact from opinion and disclose the basic process, limitations and risks of their analysis.', 1),
  ('cfa-ethics-investment-analysis-recommendations-q03', 'cfa-ethics-investment-analysis-recommendations', 'In the absence of regulatory requirements, CFA Institute recommends retaining supporting records for at least:', '7 years', '1 year', '3 months', '30 years', 'A', 'Standard V(C) Record Retention recommends keeping records for a minimum of seven years absent other requirements.', 2),
  ('cfa-ethics-investment-analysis-recommendations-q04', 'cfa-ethics-investment-analysis-recommendations', 'When relying on third-party research, a member must:', 'Make reasonable efforts to verify its soundness', 'Accept it without question', 'Never use it', 'Claim it as original work', 'A', 'Members using others'' research should take reasonable steps to confirm it has a sound basis.', 3),
  ('cfa-ethics-investment-analysis-recommendations-q05', 'cfa-ethics-investment-analysis-recommendations', 'Investment records that support a member''s analysis are the property of the:', 'Firm (employer)', 'Member personally', 'Client only', 'Regulator', 'A', 'Supporting records belong to the firm; a departing member may not take them.', 4),
  ('cfa-ethics-investment-analysis-recommendations-q06', 'cfa-ethics-investment-analysis-recommendations', 'A member presenting a projection as a certainty would violate:', 'Standard V(B) Communication with Clients', 'Standard III(A)', 'GIPS', 'Standard IV(A)', 'A', 'Presenting opinion or projection as fact breaches the requirement to distinguish fact from opinion.', 5),
  ('cfa-ethics-investment-analysis-recommendations-q07', 'cfa-ethics-investment-analysis-recommendations', 'The level of diligence required under V(A) should be:', 'Proportionate to the significance of the recommendation', 'The same regardless of context', 'Minimal for large clients', 'Skipped for repeat clients', 'A', 'Reasonable basis scales with the importance of the recommendation and the client''s reliance on it.', 6),
  ('cfa-ethics-investment-analysis-recommendations-q08', 'cfa-ethics-investment-analysis-recommendations', 'Standard V(B) also requires members to identify:', 'The limitations and risks inherent in an investment', 'Only the upside', 'The employer''s profit', 'Competitors'' fees', 'A', 'Members must disclose material limitations and risks so clients can judge the analysis.', 7),
  ('cfa-ethics-investment-analysis-recommendations-q09', 'cfa-ethics-investment-analysis-recommendations', 'Which factors in a report are members required to highlight?', 'Those material to the investment decision', 'Every trivial detail equally', 'None, to keep it short', 'Only positive factors', 'A', 'Members should identify which factors are material to the analysis and decision.', 8),
  ('cfa-ethics-investment-analysis-recommendations-q10', 'cfa-ethics-investment-analysis-recommendations', 'Standard V(C) Record Retention primarily supports:', 'The reasonable basis for recommendations and actions', 'Marketing campaigns', 'Tax evasion', 'Higher fees', 'A', 'Retained records document the analysis and reasonable basis behind recommendations and actions.', 9),
  ('cfa-ethics-investment-analysis-recommendations-q11', 'cfa-ethics-investment-analysis-recommendations', 'A member develops a quantitative model; under V(B) they should disclose:', 'The basic principles and known limitations of the process', 'The full proprietary code to everyone', 'Nothing about the method', 'Only the fee', 'A', 'Members should communicate the general principles and limitations of their process without necessarily revealing proprietary details.', 10),
  ('cfa-ethics-investment-analysis-recommendations-q12', 'cfa-ethics-investment-analysis-recommendations', 'Standard V as a whole governs:', 'Investment analysis, recommendations and actions', 'Duties to employers', 'Conflicts of interest', 'GIPS compliance', 'A', 'Standard V covers diligence, communication with clients, and record retention for analysis and recommendations.', 11),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-descriptive-statistics-q01', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-descriptive-statistics', 'The measure of central tendency most affected by extreme outliers is the:', 'Arithmetic mean', 'Median', 'Mode', 'Range', 'A', 'The arithmetic mean uses every observation, so it is sensitive to outliers; the median is more robust.', 0),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-descriptive-statistics-q02', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-descriptive-statistics', 'The best measure of the compound rate of growth over multiple periods is the:', 'Geometric mean', 'Arithmetic mean', 'Mode', 'Range', 'A', 'The geometric mean captures compounding and is appropriate for average growth/return rates.', 1),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-descriptive-statistics-q03', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-descriptive-statistics', 'For a data set, the geometric mean is always:', 'Less than or equal to the arithmetic mean', 'Greater than the arithmetic mean', 'Equal to the mode', 'Equal to the range', 'A', 'The geometric mean is less than or equal to the arithmetic mean, with equality only when all values are identical.', 2),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-descriptive-statistics-q04', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-descriptive-statistics', 'The coefficient of variation (CV) is calculated as:', 'Standard deviation / mean', 'Mean / standard deviation', 'Variance / mean', 'Range / median', 'A', 'CV = σ/mean measures risk per unit of return; a lower CV is preferred.', 3),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-descriptive-statistics-q05', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-descriptive-statistics', 'In a positively (right) skewed distribution, the typical ordering is:', 'Mean > median > mode', 'Mean < median < mode', 'Mean = median = mode', 'Median > mean > mode', 'A', 'A long right tail pulls the mean above the median and mode.', 4),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-descriptive-statistics-q06', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-descriptive-statistics', 'A distribution with fatter tails than the normal distribution is described as:', 'Leptokurtic', 'Platykurtic', 'Mesokurtic', 'Symmetric', 'A', 'Leptokurtic distributions have positive excess kurtosis — more peaked with fatter tails than normal.', 5),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-descriptive-statistics-q07', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-descriptive-statistics', 'The excess kurtosis of a normal distribution is:', '0', '3', '1', 'Undefined', 'A', 'Kurtosis of the normal distribution is 3, so its excess kurtosis (kurtosis − 3) is 0.', 6),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-descriptive-statistics-q08', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-descriptive-statistics', 'Values that divide a distribution into four equal parts are:', 'Quartiles', 'Deciles', 'Percentiles', 'Modes', 'A', 'Quartiles split ranked data into four equal parts; deciles into ten and percentiles into a hundred.', 7),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-descriptive-statistics-q09', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-descriptive-statistics', 'The harmonic mean is most appropriate for:', 'Averaging ratios such as cost per share when investing a fixed amount', 'Measuring skewness', 'Compound growth', 'Counting outliers', 'A', 'The harmonic mean suits averaging rates/ratios, e.g., dollar-cost-averaging purchase prices.', 8),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-descriptive-statistics-q10', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-descriptive-statistics', 'Chebyshev''s inequality states that at least 1 − 1/k² of observations lie within:', 'k standard deviations of the mean', 'k means of the median', 'One range of the mode', 'The interquartile range', 'A', 'Chebyshev''s inequality bounds the proportion of data within k standard deviations of the mean for any distribution.', 9),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-descriptive-statistics-q11', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-descriptive-statistics', 'Which measure of dispersion is in the same units as the data and most commonly used?', 'Standard deviation', 'Variance', 'Skewness', 'Kurtosis', 'A', 'Standard deviation, the square root of variance, is expressed in the data''s own units.', 10),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-descriptive-statistics-q12', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-descriptive-statistics', 'In a negatively (left) skewed distribution, the mean is:', 'Less than the median', 'Greater than the median', 'Equal to the mode', 'Equal to the range', 'A', 'A long left tail pulls the mean below the median (mean < median < mode).', 11),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-rates-of-return-q01', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-rates-of-return', 'The money-weighted rate of return is equivalent to the:', 'Internal rate of return of the portfolio''s cash flows', 'Geometric mean of sub-period returns', 'Arithmetic mean return', 'Risk-free rate', 'A', 'The MWR is the IRR that sets the present value of all cash flows (in and out) to zero.', 0),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-rates-of-return-q02', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-rates-of-return', 'The time-weighted rate of return is preferred for evaluating a manager because it:', 'Is not affected by the timing of client cash flows', 'Rewards good deposit timing', 'Ignores compounding', 'Equals the arithmetic mean', 'A', 'TWR removes the distorting effect of client deposits/withdrawals the manager does not control.', 1),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-rates-of-return-q03', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-rates-of-return', 'The holding period return equals:', '(Ending value − beginning value + income) / beginning value', 'Ending value / income', 'Income − beginning value', 'Beginning value / ending value', 'A', 'HPR combines price change and income relative to the beginning value.', 2),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-rates-of-return-q04', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-rates-of-return', 'The money-weighted return is most sensitive to:', 'The size and timing of external cash flows', 'The number of holdings', 'The benchmark used', 'The custodian', 'A', 'Because it is an IRR, the MWR is heavily influenced by when and how much cash is added or withdrawn.', 3),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-rates-of-return-q05', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-rates-of-return', 'The time-weighted return over multiple sub-periods is computed as the:', 'Geometric mean of the sub-period returns', 'Simple sum of returns', 'Arithmetic mean of returns', 'Largest sub-period return', 'A', 'TWR chains sub-period returns geometrically: (1+r₁)(1+r₂)…−1.', 4),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-rates-of-return-q06', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-rates-of-return', 'A return adjusted for inflation is called a:', 'Real return', 'Nominal return', 'Gross return', 'Leveraged return', 'A', 'The real return strips out inflation from the nominal return.', 5),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-rates-of-return-q07', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-rates-of-return', 'A gross return differs from a net return in that the net return is:', 'After deducting management and administrative fees', 'Before all fees', 'Before taxes only', 'Always higher', 'A', 'Net return subtracts fees (and sometimes other costs) from the gross return.', 6),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-rates-of-return-q08', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-rates-of-return', 'When there are no external cash flows during the period, the money-weighted and time-weighted returns are:', 'Equal', 'Always different', 'Both zero', 'Negatively related', 'A', 'Without interim cash flows, the two return measures coincide.', 7),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-rates-of-return-q09', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-rates-of-return', 'The best single-period expected-return estimate is usually the:', 'Arithmetic mean return', 'Geometric mean return', 'Harmonic mean', 'Median return', 'A', 'The arithmetic mean is the unbiased estimate of a single-period expected return; the geometric mean measures realized compound growth.', 8),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-rates-of-return-q10', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-rates-of-return', 'A leveraged return, compared with the unleveraged return, tends to:', 'Magnify both gains and losses', 'Reduce risk', 'Eliminate volatility', 'Equal the risk-free rate', 'A', 'Leverage amplifies the portfolio''s returns in both directions.', 9),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-rates-of-return-q11', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-rates-of-return', 'If large deposits were made just before a strong market rally, the money-weighted return will be ___ the time-weighted return.', 'Higher than', 'Lower than', 'Equal to', 'Unrelated to', 'A', 'Good timing of contributions boosts the MWR relative to the flow-neutral TWR.', 10),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-rates-of-return-q12', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-rates-of-return', 'The measure that best reflects the actual return earned by the investor is the:', 'Money-weighted return', 'Time-weighted return', 'Benchmark return', 'Gross return', 'A', 'The MWR reflects the investor''s actual experience given the timing of their cash flows.', 11),
  ('cfa-economics-demand-supply-elasticity-q01', 'cfa-economics-demand-supply-elasticity', 'The law of demand states that, all else equal, as price rises the quantity demanded:', 'Falls', 'Rises', 'Stays constant', 'Becomes infinite', 'A', 'The demand curve slopes downward: higher prices reduce quantity demanded.', 0),
  ('cfa-economics-demand-supply-elasticity-q02', 'cfa-economics-demand-supply-elasticity', 'Market equilibrium occurs where:', 'Quantity demanded equals quantity supplied', 'Demand is zero', 'Price is highest', 'Supply is vertical', 'A', 'Equilibrium is the price and quantity at which the demand and supply curves intersect.', 1),
  ('cfa-economics-demand-supply-elasticity-q03', 'cfa-economics-demand-supply-elasticity', 'If the price elasticity of demand is greater than 1 in absolute value, demand is:', 'Elastic', 'Inelastic', 'Unit elastic', 'Perfectly inelastic', 'A', 'An elasticity above 1 means quantity is relatively responsive to price — elastic demand.', 2),
  ('cfa-economics-demand-supply-elasticity-q04', 'cfa-economics-demand-supply-elasticity', 'Demand for a good tends to be more elastic when:', 'Close substitutes are available', 'It is a necessity', 'It is a tiny share of the budget', 'There is little time to adjust', 'A', 'Availability of substitutes, luxury status, a large budget share and longer time horizons all raise elasticity.', 3),
  ('cfa-economics-demand-supply-elasticity-q05', 'cfa-economics-demand-supply-elasticity', 'A good with a negative income elasticity of demand is:', 'An inferior good', 'A normal good', 'A luxury good', 'A Giffen good only', 'A', 'For inferior goods, demand falls as income rises, giving a negative income elasticity.', 4),
  ('cfa-economics-demand-supply-elasticity-q06', 'cfa-economics-demand-supply-elasticity', 'Two goods with a positive cross-price elasticity of demand are:', 'Substitutes', 'Complements', 'Inferior goods', 'Unrelated', 'A', 'A positive cross elasticity means a rise in one good''s price increases demand for the other — they are substitutes.', 5),
  ('cfa-economics-demand-supply-elasticity-q07', 'cfa-economics-demand-supply-elasticity', 'Complementary goods have a cross-price elasticity that is:', 'Negative', 'Positive', 'Zero', 'Greater than 1', 'A', 'For complements, a higher price of one reduces demand for the other, giving a negative cross elasticity.', 6),
  ('cfa-economics-demand-supply-elasticity-q08', 'cfa-economics-demand-supply-elasticity', 'If demand is inelastic, a price increase will cause total revenue to:', 'Rise', 'Fall', 'Stay the same', 'Become zero', 'A', 'With inelastic demand, quantity falls less than proportionately, so total revenue rises when price increases.', 7),
  ('cfa-economics-demand-supply-elasticity-q09', 'cfa-economics-demand-supply-elasticity', 'Consumer surplus is:', 'The value buyers place on a good above the price they pay', 'The producer''s profit', 'The tax revenue', 'The total cost of production', 'A', 'Consumer surplus is the difference between buyers'' willingness to pay and the actual price.', 8),
  ('cfa-economics-demand-supply-elasticity-q10', 'cfa-economics-demand-supply-elasticity', 'Producer surplus is:', 'The price received minus the producer''s cost', 'Total revenue', 'Consumer willingness to pay', 'Government subsidy', 'A', 'Producer surplus is the difference between the price received and the minimum the producer would accept.', 9),
  ('cfa-economics-demand-supply-elasticity-q11', 'cfa-economics-demand-supply-elasticity', 'Price elasticity of demand is calculated as:', '% change in quantity demanded / % change in price', '% change in price / % change in income', 'Quantity / price', 'Price × quantity', 'A', 'Own-price elasticity is the percentage change in quantity demanded divided by the percentage change in price.', 10),
  ('cfa-economics-demand-supply-elasticity-q12', 'cfa-economics-demand-supply-elasticity', 'A binding price ceiling set below equilibrium typically causes:', 'A shortage', 'A surplus', 'No effect', 'Higher supply', 'A', 'A price ceiling below equilibrium raises quantity demanded above quantity supplied, creating a shortage.', 11),
  ('cfa-economics-aggregate-output-growth-q01', 'cfa-economics-aggregate-output-growth', 'The long-run aggregate supply (LRAS) curve is:', 'Vertical at potential GDP', 'Horizontal', 'Downward sloping', 'Upward sloping without limit', 'A', 'In the long run, output is at potential (full-employment) GDP regardless of the price level, so LRAS is vertical.', 0),
  ('cfa-economics-aggregate-output-growth-q02', 'cfa-economics-aggregate-output-growth', 'The main driver of sustained growth in output per capita is:', 'Total factor productivity (technology)', 'Population growth alone', 'Inflation', 'Government deficits', 'A', 'Because capital deepening faces diminishing returns, long-run per-capita growth is driven mainly by technological progress (TFP).', 1),
  ('cfa-economics-aggregate-output-growth-q03', 'cfa-economics-aggregate-output-growth', 'The order of business cycle phases is:', 'Expansion, peak, contraction, trough', 'Peak, trough, expansion, contraction', 'Trough, contraction, peak, expansion', 'Contraction, expansion, peak, trough', 'A', 'The cycle runs expansion → peak → contraction → trough, then repeats.', 2),
  ('cfa-economics-aggregate-output-growth-q04', 'cfa-economics-aggregate-output-growth', 'Aggregate demand consists of:', 'Consumption, investment, government spending and net exports', 'Only consumer spending', 'Exports minus imports only', 'Wages plus profits', 'A', 'AD = C + I + G + (X − M), mirroring the expenditure approach to GDP.', 3),
  ('cfa-economics-aggregate-output-growth-q05', 'cfa-economics-aggregate-output-growth', 'Potential GDP is best described as:', 'The economy''s sustainable, non-inflationary level of output', 'The highest GDP ever recorded', 'Nominal GDP', 'GDP during a recession', 'A', 'Potential GDP is the output an economy can sustain at full employment without accelerating inflation.', 4),
  ('cfa-economics-aggregate-output-growth-q06', 'cfa-economics-aggregate-output-growth', 'Capital deepening (more capital per worker) is subject to:', 'Diminishing marginal returns', 'Constant returns forever', 'Increasing returns without limit', 'Negative output', 'A', 'Adding capital per worker raises output but at a declining rate — diminishing marginal returns.', 5),
  ('cfa-economics-aggregate-output-growth-q07', 'cfa-economics-aggregate-output-growth', 'In the Solow (neoclassical) growth model, long-run per-capita growth is ultimately driven by:', 'Technological progress', 'Higher savings alone', 'Population growth', 'Government spending', 'A', 'At the steady state, only technological progress raises long-run output per capita.', 6),
  ('cfa-economics-aggregate-output-growth-q08', 'cfa-economics-aggregate-output-growth', 'A rightward shift in aggregate demand, all else equal, tends to:', 'Increase output and the price level in the short run', 'Reduce output', 'Have no effect', 'Lower prices', 'A', 'Higher AD raises both real output and the price level along an upward-sloping short-run supply curve.', 7),
  ('cfa-economics-aggregate-output-growth-q09', 'cfa-economics-aggregate-output-growth', 'Real GDP differs from nominal GDP in that real GDP:', 'Is adjusted for changes in the price level', 'Uses current prices', 'Excludes services', 'Includes only exports', 'A', 'Real GDP holds prices constant (via a base year/deflator) to isolate changes in output.', 8),
  ('cfa-economics-aggregate-output-growth-q10', 'cfa-economics-aggregate-output-growth', 'Unemployment typically ___ during the contraction phase of the business cycle.', 'Rises', 'Falls', 'Stays flat', 'Disappears', 'A', 'As output falls in a contraction, firms cut jobs and unemployment rises.', 9),
  ('cfa-economics-aggregate-output-growth-q11', 'cfa-economics-aggregate-output-growth', 'The production function relates output to inputs of:', 'Labour, capital and technology', 'Only labour', 'Interest rates', 'Tax rates', 'A', 'Output is produced from labour and capital, scaled by the level of technology/productivity.', 10),
  ('cfa-economics-aggregate-output-growth-q12', 'cfa-economics-aggregate-output-growth', 'When short-run equilibrium output is below potential GDP, the economy has a:', 'Recessionary gap', 'Inflationary gap', 'Trade surplus', 'Budget surplus', 'A', 'Output below potential is a recessionary (deflationary) gap, associated with higher unemployment.', 11),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-standards-framework-q01', 'cfa-financial-reporting-standards-framework', 'IFRS standards are set by the:', 'IASB', 'FASB', 'SEC', 'PCAOB', 'A', 'The International Accounting Standards Board (IASB) sets IFRS; the FASB sets US GAAP.', 0),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-standards-framework-q02', 'cfa-financial-reporting-standards-framework', 'The two fundamental qualitative characteristics of useful financial information are relevance and:', 'Faithful representation', 'Comparability', 'Timeliness', 'Verifiability', 'A', 'Relevance and faithful representation are fundamental; comparability, verifiability, timeliness and understandability are enhancing.', 1),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-standards-framework-q03', 'cfa-financial-reporting-standards-framework', 'The use of LIFO for inventory is:', 'Permitted under US GAAP but prohibited under IFRS', 'Permitted under IFRS but not US GAAP', 'Prohibited under both', 'Required under both', 'A', 'US GAAP allows LIFO; IFRS does not permit it.', 2),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-standards-framework-q04', 'cfa-financial-reporting-standards-framework', 'Under IFRS, development costs meeting specified criteria are:', 'Capitalised', 'Always expensed', 'Ignored', 'Treated as revenue', 'A', 'IFRS allows capitalising development costs when technical and commercial feasibility criteria are met; US GAAP generally expenses them.', 3),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-standards-framework-q05', 'cfa-financial-reporting-standards-framework', 'Revaluation of property, plant and equipment to fair value is:', 'Allowed under IFRS but not US GAAP', 'Allowed under US GAAP but not IFRS', 'Prohibited under both', 'Required under both', 'A', 'IFRS permits the revaluation model for PP&E; US GAAP requires historical cost.', 4),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-standards-framework-q06', 'cfa-financial-reporting-standards-framework', 'The assumption that a company will continue operating for the foreseeable future is the:', 'Going-concern assumption', 'Accrual assumption', 'Matching principle', 'Materiality constraint', 'A', 'The going-concern assumption presumes the entity will continue in operation.', 5),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-standards-framework-q07', 'cfa-financial-reporting-standards-framework', 'Recognising revenues and expenses when earned/incurred rather than when cash moves reflects:', 'Accrual accounting', 'Cash accounting', 'Tax accounting', 'Fund accounting', 'A', 'Accrual accounting records transactions when the economic event occurs, not when cash changes hands.', 6),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-standards-framework-q08', 'cfa-financial-reporting-standards-framework', 'Which is an enhancing qualitative characteristic of financial information?', 'Comparability', 'Relevance', 'Faithful representation', 'Materiality', 'A', 'Comparability (with verifiability, timeliness, understandability) is enhancing; relevance and faithful representation are fundamental.', 7),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-standards-framework-q09', 'cfa-financial-reporting-standards-framework', 'US GAAP is best described as more:', 'Rules-based', 'Principles-based', 'Cash-based', 'Tax-based', 'A', 'US GAAP is relatively rules-based, whereas IFRS is more principles-based.', 8),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-standards-framework-q10', 'cfa-financial-reporting-standards-framework', 'The five elements of financial statements include assets, liabilities, equity, income and:', 'Expenses', 'Dividends', 'Cash flows', 'Taxes', 'A', 'The elements are assets, liabilities, equity, income and expenses.', 9),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-standards-framework-q11', 'cfa-financial-reporting-standards-framework', 'Under IFRS, the reversal of a prior inventory write-down is:', 'Permitted (up to the original cost)', 'Prohibited', 'Required annually', 'Recorded as revenue', 'A', 'IFRS allows reversing inventory write-downs when value recovers; US GAAP prohibits reversal.', 10),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-standards-framework-q12', 'cfa-financial-reporting-standards-framework', 'A constraint on financial reporting that weighs benefits against the effort to produce information is:', 'The cost constraint', 'Relevance', 'Comparability', 'Going concern', 'A', 'The cost constraint recognises that the benefits of information should justify the cost of providing it.', 11),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-liabilities-leases-q01', 'cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-liabilities-leases', 'A bond whose coupon rate is below the market rate at issuance is issued at a:', 'Discount', 'Premium', 'Par', 'Zero value', 'A', 'When the coupon is below the market yield, investors pay less than par — a discount.', 0),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-liabilities-leases-q02', 'cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-liabilities-leases', 'Under the effective interest method, interest expense equals:', 'The carrying value times the market yield at issuance', 'The coupon payment always', 'Face value times coupon rate', 'Zero', 'A', 'Interest expense = beginning carrying (book) value × the market interest rate at issuance.', 1),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-liabilities-leases-q03', 'cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-liabilities-leases', 'For a bond issued at a discount, the carrying value over time:', 'Increases toward par', 'Decreases toward zero', 'Stays constant', 'Falls below zero', 'A', 'Discount amortisation raises the carrying value each period until it reaches par at maturity.', 2),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-liabilities-leases-q04', 'cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-liabilities-leases', 'A lease that transfers substantially all the risks and rewards of ownership is a:', 'Finance (capital) lease', 'Operating lease', 'Short-term rental', 'Sublease only', 'A', 'A finance lease effectively transfers ownership risks and rewards, so the lessee records an asset and liability.', 3),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-liabilities-leases-q05', 'cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-liabilities-leases', 'Under IFRS 16, a lessee recognises for most leases a:', 'Right-of-use asset and a lease liability', 'Only an expense, off balance sheet', 'Revenue item', 'Contra-equity account', 'A', 'IFRS 16 uses a single lessee model: nearly all leases appear on the balance sheet as a right-of-use asset and lease liability.', 4),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-liabilities-leases-q06', 'cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-liabilities-leases', 'A bond issued at a premium has a coupon rate that is:', 'Above the market rate at issuance', 'Below the market rate', 'Equal to the market rate', 'Zero', 'A', 'A coupon above the market yield makes the bond worth more than par — a premium.', 5),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-liabilities-leases-q07', 'cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-liabilities-leases', 'Loan covenants primarily serve to:', 'Protect lenders by restricting the borrower''s actions', 'Increase the coupon rate', 'Guarantee equity returns', 'Eliminate default risk entirely', 'A', 'Covenants restrict borrower behaviour (e.g., additional debt, dividends) to protect creditors.', 6),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-liabilities-leases-q08', 'cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-liabilities-leases', 'For a premium bond, interest expense under the effective interest method is:', 'Less than the coupon payment', 'More than the coupon payment', 'Equal to the face value', 'Zero', 'A', 'For a premium bond, the market yield is below the coupon, so interest expense is less than the cash coupon paid.', 7),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-liabilities-leases-q09', 'cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-liabilities-leases', 'Compared with a finance lease, an operating lease (US GAAP lessee) reports:', 'A single straight-line lease expense', 'Separate interest and amortisation with higher early expense', 'No income-statement effect', 'Revenue recognition', 'A', 'US GAAP operating leases record a right-of-use asset/liability but expense a single straight-line lease cost.', 8),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-liabilities-leases-q10', 'cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-liabilities-leases', 'At maturity, the carrying value of any bond equals its:', 'Face (par) value', 'Issue price', 'Market price at issuance', 'Zero', 'A', 'Premiums and discounts fully amortise, so carrying value converges to par at maturity.', 9),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-liabilities-leases-q11', 'cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-liabilities-leases', 'A key motivation for structuring a lease as ''operating'' historically was to:', 'Keep the obligation off the balance sheet', 'Raise reported debt', 'Increase taxes', 'Reduce cash flow', 'A', 'Older rules let operating leases stay off-balance-sheet; new standards (IFRS 16) largely removed this.', 10),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-liabilities-leases-q12', 'cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-liabilities-leases', 'The amount at which a bond liability is reported on the balance sheet is its:', 'Carrying (book) value', 'Face value always', 'Coupon rate', 'Yield to maturity', 'A', 'Bonds payable are reported at carrying value — face value adjusted for unamortised premium or discount.', 11),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting-q01', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting', 'A project should be accepted under the NPV rule if its NPV is:', 'Greater than zero', 'Less than zero', 'Equal to the IRR', 'Equal to the payback period', 'A', 'A positive NPV means the project adds value, so it should be accepted.', 0),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting-q02', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting', 'The internal rate of return (IRR) is the discount rate that makes a project''s:', 'NPV equal to zero', 'Payback equal to one year', 'Profitability index equal to zero', 'Cash flows negative', 'A', 'The IRR is the rate at which the present value of inflows equals the initial outlay (NPV = 0).', 1),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting-q03', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting', 'Under the IRR rule, an independent project is accepted if the IRR is:', 'Greater than the required rate of return', 'Less than zero', 'Equal to the payback', 'Below the risk-free rate', 'A', 'Accept if IRR exceeds the hurdle (required) rate of return.', 2),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting-q04', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting', 'When NPV and IRR conflict for two mutually exclusive projects, an analyst should rely on:', 'NPV', 'IRR', 'Payback period', 'Discounted payback', 'A', 'NPV is theoretically superior; it measures value added and assumes reinvestment at the cost of capital.', 3),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting-q05', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting', 'A major weakness of the payback period method is that it:', 'Ignores the time value of money and cash flows after payback', 'Requires a discount rate', 'Always rejects good projects', 'Overstates NPV', 'A', 'Payback ignores the time value of money and any cash flows occurring after the payback point.', 4),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting-q06', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting', 'The profitability index (PI) equals:', 'PV of future cash flows / initial investment', 'NPV × IRR', 'Payback / IRR', 'Initial investment / NPV', 'A', 'PI = PV of future cash flows ÷ initial outlay = 1 + NPV/outlay; accept if PI > 1.', 5),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting-q07', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting', 'In capital budgeting, sunk costs should be:', 'Ignored', 'Included as outflows', 'Added to NPV', 'Treated as revenue', 'A', 'Sunk costs are already incurred and irrelevant to the incremental decision, so they are excluded.', 6),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting-q08', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting', 'The opportunity cost of using a resource the firm already owns should be:', 'Included in the project''s cash flows', 'Ignored as sunk', 'Added to NPV twice', 'Treated as financing', 'A', 'Opportunity costs represent forgone alternatives and must be included in incremental cash flows.', 7),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting-q09', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting', 'The NPV of a project represents the:', 'Expected increase in shareholder wealth', 'Payback period in years', 'The IRR', 'The firm''s total assets', 'A', 'A project''s NPV is the theoretical addition to the value of the firm and to shareholder wealth.', 8),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting-q10', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting', 'The IRR rule can be unreliable when a project has:', 'Non-conventional cash flows (multiple sign changes)', 'A single outflow then inflows', 'Only positive cash flows', 'A zero discount rate', 'A', 'Non-conventional cash-flow patterns can produce multiple IRRs, making the IRR rule ambiguous.', 9),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting-q11', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting', 'The discounted payback period improves on the payback period by:', 'Accounting for the time value of money', 'Ignoring cash flows entirely', 'Using undiscounted flows', 'Adding the IRR', 'A', 'Discounted payback uses discounted cash flows, though it still ignores flows after the payback point.', 10),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting-q12', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting', 'Analysts evaluate projects using ___ cash flows.', 'Incremental after-tax', 'Total historical', 'Pre-tax accounting', 'Sunk', 'A', 'Capital budgeting uses incremental, after-tax cash flows attributable to the project.', 11),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-business-models-q01', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-business-models', 'A key advantage of the corporate form of organisation is:', 'Limited liability for shareholders', 'Unlimited owner liability', 'A finite one-year life', 'Inability to raise capital', 'A', 'Corporations give shareholders limited liability and have perpetual life, easing capital raising.', 0),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-business-models-q02', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-business-models', 'A business model describes primarily how a firm:', 'Creates and captures value', 'Files its taxes', 'Sets its share price', 'Chooses its auditor', 'A', 'A business model explains who the customers are, what is offered, how it is delivered, and how value is captured.', 1),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-business-models-q03', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-business-models', 'In a sole proprietorship, the owner has:', 'Unlimited personal liability', 'Limited liability', 'No liability', 'Perpetual succession', 'A', 'A sole proprietor is personally liable for all business obligations.', 2),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-business-models-q04', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-business-models', 'A ''razor-and-blades'' model earns most profit from:', 'Recurring consumable sales after a low-priced base product', 'A single upfront sale', 'Advertising only', 'Government grants', 'A', 'The base product is sold cheaply to drive ongoing sales of higher-margin consumables.', 3),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-business-models-q05', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-business-models', 'A subscription business model generates revenue through:', 'Recurring periodic payments for ongoing access', 'One-time product sales only', 'Barter', 'Donations', 'A', 'Subscriptions produce predictable recurring revenue for continued access to a product or service.', 4),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-business-models-q06', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-business-models', 'A defensive company is one whose demand is:', 'Relatively stable across the business cycle', 'Highly cyclical', 'Zero in recessions', 'Dependent on exports only', 'A', 'Defensive (non-cyclical) firms, such as utilities and staples, have steady demand regardless of the cycle.', 5),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-business-models-q07', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-business-models', 'In a limited partnership, limited partners have:', 'Liability limited to their investment', 'Unlimited liability', 'Full management control', 'No ownership stake', 'A', 'Limited partners'' liability is capped at their capital contribution; general partners have unlimited liability.', 6),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-business-models-q08', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-business-models', 'A potential drawback of the corporate form is:', 'Double taxation of profits and dividends', 'Unlimited liability', 'A one-year life', 'Difficulty raising equity', 'A', 'Corporate profits may be taxed at the entity level and again as shareholder dividends — double taxation.', 7),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-business-models-q09', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-business-models', 'A freemium model offers:', 'A basic service free while charging for premium features', 'Everything free forever', 'Only paid access', 'Products by barter', 'A', 'Freemium gives a free tier to attract users and monetises upgraded, premium features.', 8),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-business-models-q10', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-business-models', 'Beyond shareholders, a company''s stakeholders include:', 'Employees, creditors, customers and suppliers', 'Only the CEO', 'Only regulators', 'Only competitors', 'A', 'Stakeholders are all parties affected by the firm — employees, creditors, customers, suppliers, government and community.', 9),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-business-models-q11', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-business-models', 'A growth company is typically characterised by:', 'Heavy reinvestment and low or no dividends', 'High, stable dividend payouts', 'Declining revenue', 'No reinvestment', 'A', 'Growth firms reinvest earnings to fund expansion, so they usually pay little or no dividend.', 10),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-business-models-q12', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-business-models', 'A platform (network) business model creates value primarily by:', 'Connecting groups of users who benefit from each other', 'Manufacturing physical goods', 'Holding inventory', 'Paying dividends', 'A', 'Platform models create value through network effects by connecting complementary user groups (e.g., buyers and sellers).', 11),
  ('cfa-equity-securities-types-q01', 'cfa-equity-securities-types', 'Common shareholders have a claim on a company''s assets that is:', 'Residual (after creditors and preferred)', 'First priority', 'Ahead of bondholders', 'Fixed and guaranteed', 'A', 'Common equity is the residual claim, paid after debt and preferred holders.', 0),
  ('cfa-equity-securities-types-q02', 'cfa-equity-securities-types', 'Compared with common shares, preferred shares typically have:', 'A fixed dividend and priority over common', 'Full voting rights', 'A residual claim only', 'No dividend', 'A', 'Preferred shares pay a fixed dividend ahead of common and usually carry no vote.', 1),
  ('cfa-equity-securities-types-q03', 'cfa-equity-securities-types', 'For cumulative preferred shares, unpaid dividends:', 'Accumulate and must be paid before common dividends', 'Are permanently forfeited', 'Convert to debt', 'Reduce par value', 'A', 'Cumulative preferred dividends in arrears must be paid before any common dividend.', 2),
  ('cfa-equity-securities-types-q04', 'cfa-equity-securities-types', 'A depository receipt (e.g., an ADR) allows investors to:', 'Trade foreign shares on a domestic exchange', 'Buy government bonds', 'Short commodities', 'Avoid all currency risk', 'A', 'Depository receipts represent foreign shares and trade on a local exchange in local currency.', 3),
  ('cfa-equity-securities-types-q05', 'cfa-equity-securities-types', 'Convertible preferred shares give the holder the right to:', 'Exchange them for common shares', 'Demand a higher coupon', 'Vote twice', 'Receive principal early always', 'A', 'Convertible preferred can be exchanged into a set number of common shares.', 4),
  ('cfa-equity-securities-types-q06', 'cfa-equity-securities-types', 'Private equity, compared with public equity, is generally:', 'Less liquid and not exchange-listed', 'More liquid', 'Traded continuously', 'Risk-free', 'A', 'Private equity is illiquid and not listed on public exchanges.', 5),
  ('cfa-equity-securities-types-q07', 'cfa-equity-securities-types', 'The accounting value of shareholders'' equity is called:', 'Book value', 'Market value', 'Face value', 'Intrinsic value', 'A', 'Book value is equity per the balance sheet; market value is what investors pay.', 6),
  ('cfa-equity-securities-types-q08', 'cfa-equity-securities-types', 'Return on equity (ROE) is calculated as:', 'Net income / shareholders'' equity', 'Net income / total debt', 'Dividends / price', 'Sales / assets', 'A', 'ROE measures profitability relative to book equity.', 7),
  ('cfa-equity-securities-types-q09', 'cfa-equity-securities-types', 'Participating preferred shares may:', 'Receive additional dividends beyond the fixed rate', 'Never receive dividends', 'Convert to bonds automatically', 'Lose priority to common', 'A', 'Participating preferred can share in extra distributions above the stated dividend.', 8),
  ('cfa-equity-securities-types-q10', 'cfa-equity-securities-types', 'A callable preferred share can be:', 'Redeemed by the issuer at a set price', 'Sold back by the holder at will', 'Never retired', 'Converted to common only', 'A', 'A call feature lets the issuer redeem the shares, typically when it is advantageous to refinance.', 9),
  ('cfa-equity-securities-types-q11', 'cfa-equity-securities-types', 'The main advantage of equity ownership over debt is the:', 'Potential to share in the company''s growth and profits', 'Fixed, guaranteed return', 'First claim in liquidation', 'Tax-deductible payments', 'A', 'Equity holders participate in the upside of the firm, unlike fixed-claim debt holders.', 10),
  ('cfa-equity-securities-types-q12', 'cfa-equity-securities-types', 'In a company liquidation, the order of claims is:', 'Debt holders, then preferred, then common', 'Common, then preferred, then debt', 'Preferred, then debt, then common', 'All paid equally', 'A', 'Creditors are paid first, then preferred shareholders, with common shareholders last (residual).', 11),
  ('cfa-equity-security-market-indexes-q01', 'cfa-equity-security-market-indexes', 'In a price-weighted index, the stocks with the greatest influence are those with the:', 'Highest share price', 'Largest market cap', 'Highest dividend', 'Lowest price', 'A', 'A price-weighted index (e.g., the Dow) gives higher-priced shares more influence regardless of company size.', 0),
  ('cfa-equity-security-market-indexes-q02', 'cfa-equity-security-market-indexes', 'In a market-cap-weighted index, the largest weights belong to companies with the:', 'Largest market capitalisation', 'Highest share price', 'Most shares outstanding only', 'Highest dividend yield', 'A', 'Market-cap weighting (e.g., the S&P 500) weights by total market value, so large caps dominate.', 1),
  ('cfa-equity-security-market-indexes-q03', 'cfa-equity-security-market-indexes', 'An equal-weighted index requires:', 'Frequent rebalancing to maintain equal weights', 'No maintenance', 'Weighting by price', 'Only large caps', 'A', 'Because prices drift, equal-weighted indexes must be rebalanced regularly to restore equal weights.', 2),
  ('cfa-equity-security-market-indexes-q04', 'cfa-equity-security-market-indexes', 'A total-return index differs from a price-return index in that it:', 'Reinvests dividends and other income', 'Excludes the largest stocks', 'Ignores price changes', 'Uses only bonds', 'A', 'A total-return index includes reinvested income, so it exceeds the price-return version over time.', 3),
  ('cfa-equity-security-market-indexes-q05', 'cfa-equity-security-market-indexes', 'Most modern market-cap indexes adjust weights for:', 'Free float (shares available to the public)', 'Dividend yield', 'Share price only', 'Book value', 'A', 'Float-adjusting counts only publicly tradable shares, excluding closely held stakes.', 4),
  ('cfa-equity-security-market-indexes-q06', 'cfa-equity-security-market-indexes', 'A fundamental-weighted index weights constituents by:', 'Fundamental measures such as sales or book value', 'Share price', 'Market cap', 'Alphabetical order', 'A', 'Fundamental weighting uses metrics like sales, earnings or book value, breaking the link to price.', 5),
  ('cfa-equity-security-market-indexes-q07', 'cfa-equity-security-market-indexes', 'A stock split in a price-weighted index:', 'Reduces that stock''s influence and changes the divisor', 'Has no effect', 'Doubles the index', 'Removes the stock', 'A', 'A split lowers the share price, reducing its weight; the divisor is adjusted to keep the index continuous.', 6),
  ('cfa-equity-security-market-indexes-q08', 'cfa-equity-security-market-indexes', 'Reconstitution of an index refers to:', 'Changing which securities are included', 'Resetting weights to target only', 'Paying dividends', 'Splitting shares', 'A', 'Reconstitution changes index membership; rebalancing adjusts the weights of existing members.', 7),
  ('cfa-equity-security-market-indexes-q09', 'cfa-equity-security-market-indexes', 'A primary use of a security market index is as a:', 'Benchmark for portfolio performance', 'Source of guaranteed returns', 'Tax shelter', 'Credit rating', 'A', 'Indexes serve as benchmarks, market gauges, and the basis for index funds and ETFs.', 8),
  ('cfa-equity-security-market-indexes-q10', 'cfa-equity-security-market-indexes', 'Market-cap-weighted indexes are said to be self-rebalancing because:', 'Weights adjust automatically as prices change', 'They never change', 'They use equal weights', 'Managers pick weights daily', 'A', 'As prices move, market-cap weights update automatically without manual rebalancing.', 9),
  ('cfa-equity-security-market-indexes-q11', 'cfa-equity-security-market-indexes', 'An equal-weighted index tends to tilt toward:', 'Smaller-cap stocks relative to a cap-weighted index', 'Only the largest stocks', 'Bonds', 'High-priced stocks', 'A', 'Giving each stock equal weight raises the relative importance of smaller companies.', 10),
  ('cfa-equity-security-market-indexes-q12', 'cfa-equity-security-market-indexes', 'The value of an index is best described as:', 'A weighted average of its constituent securities'' values', 'The single highest stock price', 'The total number of shares', 'The risk-free rate', 'A', 'An index aggregates its constituents using a defined weighting method into a single value.', 11),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-markets-instruments-q01', 'cfa-fixed-income-markets-instruments', 'A bond issued by an entity such as the World Bank is classified as:', 'Supranational', 'Sovereign', 'Corporate', 'Municipal', 'A', 'Supranational issuers are multinational agencies like the World Bank or the IMF.', 0),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-markets-instruments-q02', 'cfa-fixed-income-markets-instruments', 'Money-market instruments have original maturities of:', 'One year or less', 'Over ten years', 'Exactly five years', 'No maturity', 'A', 'The money market covers short-term debt maturing in one year or less (T-bills, commercial paper, CDs).', 1),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-markets-instruments-q03', 'cfa-fixed-income-markets-instruments', 'A floating-rate note pays a coupon equal to:', 'A reference rate plus a spread', 'A fixed rate for life', 'Zero until maturity', 'The share price', 'A', 'FRNs reset periodically to a reference (benchmark) rate plus a credit spread.', 2),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-markets-instruments-q04', 'cfa-fixed-income-markets-instruments', 'Government bonds are frequently sold in the primary market through:', 'Auctions', 'Stock splits', 'Dividend reinvestment', 'Buybacks', 'A', 'Sovereign issuers commonly use auctions to distribute new bonds.', 3),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-markets-instruments-q05', 'cfa-fixed-income-markets-instruments', 'A repurchase agreement (repo) is essentially:', 'A short-term collateralised loan', 'An equity issue', 'A perpetual bond', 'A dividend payment', 'A', 'In a repo, a security is sold and repurchased later at a higher price — a collateralised short-term borrowing.', 4),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-markets-instruments-q06', 'cfa-fixed-income-markets-instruments', 'Commercial paper is best described as:', 'Short-term unsecured corporate debt', 'A long-term government bond', 'A share of stock', 'A mortgage', 'A', 'Commercial paper is short-term, usually unsecured debt issued by corporations to fund working capital.', 5),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-markets-instruments-q07', 'cfa-fixed-income-markets-instruments', 'Most secondary-market bond trading takes place:', 'Over the counter (dealer markets)', 'On a single central exchange', 'Only at auctions', 'Through IPOs', 'A', 'Unlike equities, bonds trade largely OTC through dealer networks.', 6),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-markets-instruments-q08', 'cfa-fixed-income-markets-instruments', 'A zero-coupon bond:', 'Pays no periodic interest and is issued at a discount', 'Pays a floating coupon', 'Pays monthly dividends', 'Never matures', 'A', 'Zeros make no interim payments; the return comes from the discount to par.', 7),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-markets-instruments-q09', 'cfa-fixed-income-markets-instruments', 'A quasi-government (agency) bond is issued by:', 'An entity affiliated with, but not, the central government', 'A private hedge fund', 'A stock exchange', 'A foreign household', 'A', 'Quasi-government issuers are agencies or government-sponsored entities linked to, but distinct from, the sovereign.', 8),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-markets-instruments-q10', 'cfa-fixed-income-markets-instruments', 'In an underwritten (firm-commitment) offering, the investment bank:', 'Buys the entire issue and resells it, bearing the risk', 'Only advises, taking no risk', 'Guarantees the coupon', 'Sets monetary policy', 'A', 'Under a firm commitment, the underwriter purchases the whole issue and assumes the placement risk.', 9),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-markets-instruments-q11', 'cfa-fixed-income-markets-instruments', 'A private placement bond is:', 'Sold directly to a small number of investors, not the public', 'Listed on every exchange', 'Always a government bond', 'Free of default risk', 'A', 'Private placements are sold to a limited group of qualified investors without a public offering.', 10),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-markets-instruments-q12', 'cfa-fixed-income-markets-instruments', 'The capital market, versus the money market, covers debt with maturities of:', 'More than one year', 'Less than one month', 'Exactly 90 days', 'No fixed term', 'A', 'Capital-market instruments have maturities beyond one year; money-market instruments are one year or less.', 11),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-interest-rate-risk-measures-q01', 'cfa-fixed-income-interest-rate-risk-measures', 'Money (dollar) duration equals:', 'Modified duration × full price', 'Coupon × maturity', 'Yield × par', 'Convexity × price', 'A', 'Money duration is modified duration multiplied by the bond''s full price, giving the money price change per 1% yield move.', 0),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-interest-rate-risk-measures-q02', 'cfa-fixed-income-interest-rate-risk-measures', 'The price value of a basis point (PVBP) measures the price change for a yield move of:', 'One basis point (0.01%)', 'One percent', 'One dollar', 'One year', 'A', 'PVBP (PV01) is the money-price change for a 1 basis point change in yield.', 1),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-interest-rate-risk-measures-q03', 'cfa-fixed-income-interest-rate-risk-measures', 'For a bond with embedded options, the appropriate sensitivity measure is:', 'Effective duration', 'Modified duration', 'Macaulay duration only', 'Coupon duration', 'A', 'Effective duration accounts for cash flows that change with yields, as with callable or putable bonds.', 2),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-interest-rate-risk-measures-q04', 'cfa-fixed-income-interest-rate-risk-measures', 'Key rate (partial) duration measures sensitivity to:', 'A yield change at a specific maturity on the curve', 'A parallel shift only', 'The coupon rate', 'Credit spreads', 'A', 'Key rate durations capture the effect of non-parallel shifts at particular points on the yield curve.', 3),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-interest-rate-risk-measures-q05', 'cfa-fixed-income-interest-rate-risk-measures', 'When a bond''s Macaulay duration equals the investment horizon:', 'Price and reinvestment risk roughly offset (immunisation)', 'The bond defaults', 'Duration becomes negative', 'The coupon doubles', 'A', 'Matching Macaulay duration to the horizon balances price and reinvestment risk — the basis of immunisation.', 4),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-interest-rate-risk-measures-q06', 'cfa-fixed-income-interest-rate-risk-measures', 'Convexity is used to:', 'Improve the duration-based estimate for larger yield changes', 'Measure credit risk', 'Replace duration entirely', 'Compute the coupon', 'A', 'Convexity corrects the linear duration approximation, mattering more for large yield moves.', 5),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-interest-rate-risk-measures-q07', 'cfa-fixed-income-interest-rate-risk-measures', 'A higher money duration means a bond''s price is:', 'More sensitive in money terms to yield changes', 'Less sensitive to yields', 'Immune to yields', 'Fixed', 'A', 'Larger money duration implies a bigger money-price change for a given yield move.', 6),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-interest-rate-risk-measures-q08', 'cfa-fixed-income-interest-rate-risk-measures', 'PVBP is calculated as:', 'Money duration × 0.0001', 'Modified duration × yield', 'Price × coupon', 'Convexity × maturity', 'A', 'PVBP scales money duration by one basis point (0.0001).', 7),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-interest-rate-risk-measures-q09', 'cfa-fixed-income-interest-rate-risk-measures', 'Modified duration is most appropriate for a bond that is:', 'Option-free with fixed cash flows', 'A callable mortgage security', 'A putable bond', 'Convertible', 'A', 'Modified duration assumes fixed cash flows, so it fits straight, option-free bonds.', 8),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-interest-rate-risk-measures-q10', 'cfa-fixed-income-interest-rate-risk-measures', 'A portfolio''s money duration is useful because it:', 'Expresses interest-rate risk in currency terms', 'Removes all risk', 'Ignores position size', 'Measures equity beta', 'A', 'Money duration translates rate sensitivity into a money amount, aiding position-level risk management.', 9),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-interest-rate-risk-measures-q11', 'cfa-fixed-income-interest-rate-risk-measures', 'Non-parallel shifts in the yield curve are best captured by:', 'Key rate durations', 'A single modified duration', 'The coupon rate', 'The current yield', 'A', 'Key rate durations reveal exposure to changes at individual maturities, unlike one aggregate duration.', 10),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-interest-rate-risk-measures-q12', 'cfa-fixed-income-interest-rate-risk-measures', 'Effective duration is estimated using:', 'Price changes from up and down yield-curve shifts', 'The coupon divided by price', 'Only the maturity', 'The credit rating', 'A', 'Effective duration uses the bond''s prices under small upward and downward curve shifts to gauge sensitivity.', 11),
  ('cfa-derivatives-markets-instruments-q01', 'cfa-derivatives-markets-instruments', 'A derivative is an instrument whose value is:', 'Derived from an underlying asset, rate or index', 'Fixed by the issuer', 'Independent of any asset', 'Equal to par always', 'A', 'Derivatives derive their value from the performance of an underlying.', 0),
  ('cfa-derivatives-markets-instruments-q02', 'cfa-derivatives-markets-instruments', 'Forwards, futures and swaps are examples of:', 'Forward commitments', 'Contingent claims', 'Equity securities', 'Money-market instruments', 'A', 'Forward commitments obligate both parties to transact; options are contingent claims.', 1),
  ('cfa-derivatives-markets-instruments-q03', 'cfa-derivatives-markets-instruments', 'An option is a contingent claim because the buyer has:', 'The right, but not the obligation, to transact', 'An obligation to buy', 'No payoff ever', 'A fixed coupon', 'A', 'Option buyers may exercise if favourable but are not obligated, unlike forward-commitment holders.', 2),
  ('cfa-derivatives-markets-instruments-q04', 'cfa-derivatives-markets-instruments', 'Exchange-traded derivatives are:', 'Standardised and centrally cleared', 'Fully customised', 'Free of margin requirements', 'Unregulated', 'A', 'Exchange-traded contracts have standard terms and use central clearing, reducing counterparty risk.', 3),
  ('cfa-derivatives-markets-instruments-q05', 'cfa-derivatives-markets-instruments', 'OTC derivatives, versus exchange-traded ones, generally have:', 'More customisation but higher counterparty risk', 'Standardised terms', 'No counterparty risk', 'Lower flexibility', 'A', 'OTC contracts are tailored between two parties but carry bilateral counterparty risk.', 4),
  ('cfa-derivatives-markets-instruments-q06', 'cfa-derivatives-markets-instruments', 'A clearinghouse in a futures market:', 'Guarantees performance and requires margin', 'Sets interest rates', 'Issues the underlying stock', 'Eliminates price movement', 'A', 'The clearinghouse acts as counterparty to both sides and enforces margin, reducing default risk.', 5),
  ('cfa-derivatives-markets-instruments-q07', 'cfa-derivatives-markets-instruments', 'A commonly cited benefit of derivatives is:', 'Efficient risk transfer and price discovery', 'Guaranteed profits', 'Elimination of all risk', 'No need for a counterparty', 'A', 'Derivatives enable hedging/risk transfer, price discovery and low-cost exposure.', 6),
  ('cfa-derivatives-markets-instruments-q08', 'cfa-derivatives-markets-instruments', 'A frequently cited criticism of derivatives is that they:', 'Enable high leverage that can magnify losses', 'Cannot be used to hedge', 'Have no underlying', 'Are always unprofitable', 'A', 'The leverage embedded in derivatives can amplify losses and contribute to systemic risk.', 7),
  ('cfa-derivatives-markets-instruments-q09', 'cfa-derivatives-markets-instruments', 'Futures differ from forwards mainly in that futures are:', 'Standardised, exchange-traded and marked to market daily', 'Fully customised', 'Traded only OTC', 'Never cleared', 'A', 'Futures are standardised, exchange-listed and settled daily; forwards are customised OTC contracts.', 8),
  ('cfa-derivatives-markets-instruments-q10', 'cfa-derivatives-markets-instruments', 'Using derivatives, an investor can gain exposure to an underlying with:', 'Less capital than buying it outright', 'No possibility of loss', 'Guaranteed gains', 'No counterparty', 'A', 'Derivatives require only margin/premium, giving leveraged exposure with less upfront capital.', 9),
  ('cfa-derivatives-markets-instruments-q11', 'cfa-derivatives-markets-instruments', 'Price discovery refers to derivatives helping to:', 'Reveal expected future prices of the underlying', 'Hide market information', 'Fix prices permanently', 'Eliminate trading', 'A', 'Active derivatives markets aggregate views and reveal information about expected future prices.', 10),
  ('cfa-derivatives-markets-instruments-q12', 'cfa-derivatives-markets-instruments', 'A swap is best classified as a:', 'Forward commitment (series of forwards)', 'Contingent claim', 'Money-market security', 'Equity index', 'A', 'A swap obligates both parties to exchange cash flows and is economically a series of forward commitments.', 11),
  ('cfa-derivatives-arbitrage-cost-of-carry-q01', 'cfa-derivatives-arbitrage-cost-of-carry', 'Arbitrage is defined as:', 'A riskless profit earned with no net investment', 'A risky bet on prices', 'A guaranteed loss', 'A long-term investment', 'A', 'Arbitrage exploits mispricing to earn a riskless profit with zero net investment.', 0),
  ('cfa-derivatives-arbitrage-cost-of-carry-q02', 'cfa-derivatives-arbitrage-cost-of-carry', 'The law of one price states that:', 'Assets with identical cash flows must have the same price', 'All assets cost the same', 'Prices never change', 'Higher risk means lower price', 'A', 'Identical payoffs must trade at the same price; otherwise arbitrage forces them together.', 1),
  ('cfa-derivatives-arbitrage-cost-of-carry-q03', 'cfa-derivatives-arbitrage-cost-of-carry', 'Replication in derivatives pricing means:', 'Recreating a payoff using the underlying and risk-free borrowing/lending', 'Copying a competitor''s fund', 'Doubling the position', 'Splitting the contract', 'A', 'If a portfolio replicates a derivative''s payoff, no-arbitrage requires they have the same price.', 2),
  ('cfa-derivatives-arbitrage-cost-of-carry-q04', 'cfa-derivatives-arbitrage-cost-of-carry', 'In cost-of-carry pricing, carry costs such as storage:', 'Increase the forward price', 'Decrease the forward price', 'Have no effect', 'Set it to zero', 'A', 'Costs of holding the asset (storage, insurance) raise the no-arbitrage forward price.', 3),
  ('cfa-derivatives-arbitrage-cost-of-carry-q05', 'cfa-derivatives-arbitrage-cost-of-carry', 'Carry benefits such as dividends on the underlying:', 'Lower the forward price', 'Raise the forward price', 'Have no effect', 'Eliminate arbitrage', 'A', 'Income received from holding the asset reduces the net cost of carry and thus the forward price.', 4),
  ('cfa-derivatives-arbitrage-cost-of-carry-q06', 'cfa-derivatives-arbitrage-cost-of-carry', 'The no-arbitrage forward price of an asset paying no income is approximately:', 'S₀ × (1 + r)^T', 'S₀ / (1 + r)^T', 'S₀ − r', 'S₀ × r × T', 'A', 'The forward equals the spot compounded at the risk-free rate over the contract''s life.', 5),
  ('cfa-derivatives-arbitrage-cost-of-carry-q07', 'cfa-derivatives-arbitrage-cost-of-carry', 'A cash-and-carry arbitrage is executed when the forward is:', 'Overpriced relative to its no-arbitrage value', 'Fairly priced', 'Below spot only', 'Equal to the coupon', 'A', 'If the forward is too high, an arbitrageur buys the asset (carries it) and sells the forward for a riskless gain.', 6),
  ('cfa-derivatives-arbitrage-cost-of-carry-q08', 'cfa-derivatives-arbitrage-cost-of-carry', 'The no-arbitrage principle is important because it:', 'Underlies the pricing of all derivatives', 'Guarantees high returns', 'Applies only to stocks', 'Ignores the underlying', 'A', 'Derivatives are priced on the assumption that no riskless arbitrage profit should exist.', 7),
  ('cfa-derivatives-arbitrage-cost-of-carry-q09', 'cfa-derivatives-arbitrage-cost-of-carry', 'If two portfolios have identical future payoffs, arbitrage ensures they have:', 'The same current price', 'Different prices', 'Zero value', 'Opposite risks', 'A', 'Identical payoffs must have identical prices; any gap is arbitraged away.', 8),
  ('cfa-derivatives-arbitrage-cost-of-carry-q10', 'cfa-derivatives-arbitrage-cost-of-carry', 'The convenience yield on a commodity behaves like a:', 'Carry benefit that lowers the forward price', 'Storage cost', 'Financing cost', 'Tax', 'A', 'The convenience yield is a benefit of holding the physical commodity, reducing the net cost of carry.', 9),
  ('cfa-derivatives-arbitrage-cost-of-carry-q11', 'cfa-derivatives-arbitrage-cost-of-carry', 'Arbitrage activity tends to:', 'Push mispriced assets back toward fair value', 'Widen price gaps', 'Increase risk-free rates', 'Eliminate all trading', 'A', 'Arbitrageurs buying the cheap and selling the dear drive prices back into alignment.', 10),
  ('cfa-derivatives-arbitrage-cost-of-carry-q12', 'cfa-derivatives-arbitrage-cost-of-carry', 'Net cost of carry equals:', 'Financing and storage costs minus any income/benefits', 'Only the coupon', 'The spot price', 'The strike price', 'A', 'Net carry is the cost of holding the asset (financing + storage) less benefits (income, convenience yield).', 11),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-overview-q01', 'cfa-alternative-investments-overview', 'A common characteristic of alternative investments is:', 'Illiquidity and limited transparency', 'Daily exchange liquidity', 'Heavy regulation', 'Guaranteed returns', 'A', 'Alternatives are typically illiquid, lightly regulated and less transparent than traditional assets.', 0),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-overview-q02', 'cfa-alternative-investments-overview', 'The classic ''2 and 20'' fee structure refers to:', 'A 2% management fee plus a 20% incentive fee', 'A 2-year lock-up and 20 investors', 'A 20% management fee', 'A 2% hurdle rate', 'A', '''2 and 20'' is a 2% management fee and a 20% performance (incentive) fee.', 1),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-overview-q03', 'cfa-alternative-investments-overview', 'A hurdle rate in a private fund is:', 'The minimum return before incentive fees are charged', 'The management fee', 'The redemption penalty', 'The fund''s benchmark index', 'A', 'Incentive fees apply only to returns above the hurdle rate.', 2),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-overview-q04', 'cfa-alternative-investments-overview', 'A high-water mark ensures incentive fees are charged only on:', 'Gains above the previous peak value', 'Gross assets', 'The management fee', 'The hurdle rate', 'A', 'The high-water mark prevents charging performance fees twice for recovering earlier losses.', 3),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-overview-q05', 'cfa-alternative-investments-overview', 'A frequently cited portfolio benefit of alternatives is:', 'Low correlation with traditional assets (diversification)', 'Zero fees', 'Guaranteed liquidity', 'No manager risk', 'A', 'Many alternatives have low correlation with stocks and bonds, aiding diversification.', 4),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-overview-q06', 'cfa-alternative-investments-overview', 'Most private funds are organised as:', 'Limited partnerships (GP manages, LPs invest)', 'Public corporations', 'Exchange-traded funds', 'Money-market funds', 'A', 'A general partner manages the fund while limited partners provide capital with limited liability.', 5),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-overview-q07', 'cfa-alternative-investments-overview', 'The ''J-curve'' in private equity describes:', 'Early negative returns followed by later gains', 'Constant returns', 'Immediate high returns', 'A steady decline', 'A', 'Fees and early write-downs depress initial returns, which typically improve as investments mature.', 6),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-overview-q08', 'cfa-alternative-investments-overview', 'Committed capital that a private fund can call over time is drawn down via:', 'Capital calls', 'Dividend reinvestment', 'Coupon payments', 'Stock splits', 'A', 'GPs issue capital calls to draw committed capital from LPs as investments are made.', 7),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-overview-q09', 'cfa-alternative-investments-overview', 'Which is a category of alternative investment?', 'Private equity', 'Listed government bonds', 'Publicly traded blue-chip stock', 'A savings account', 'A', 'Alternatives include hedge funds, private equity/debt, real estate, infrastructure and commodities.', 8),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-overview-q10', 'cfa-alternative-investments-overview', 'A lock-up period in a hedge fund:', 'Restricts investor redemptions for a set time', 'Guarantees returns', 'Eliminates fees', 'Sets the benchmark', 'A', 'Lock-ups (and gates) limit withdrawals, giving managers time to execute illiquid strategies.', 9),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-overview-q11', 'cfa-alternative-investments-overview', 'Compared with traditional investments, alternatives typically charge:', 'Higher fees', 'No fees', 'Lower fees', 'Only exit fees', 'A', 'Alternatives generally carry higher management and performance fees than traditional funds.', 10),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-overview-q12', 'cfa-alternative-investments-overview', 'Because of their complexity and illiquidity, alternatives require:', 'Extensive due diligence', 'No analysis', 'Daily marking only', 'Public disclosure of all trades', 'A', 'Thorough operational and investment due diligence is essential given limited transparency.', 11),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-digital-assets-q01', 'cfa-alternative-investments-digital-assets', 'Most digital assets are recorded on a:', 'Distributed ledger (blockchain)', 'Central bank spreadsheet', 'Single private server', 'Paper register', 'A', 'Digital assets are typically tracked on decentralised blockchains.', 0),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-digital-assets-q02', 'cfa-alternative-investments-digital-assets', 'A defining feature of a cryptocurrency such as Bitcoin is that it is:', 'Decentralised with no central issuing authority', 'Issued by a central bank', 'Backed by gold by law', 'Guaranteed by governments', 'A', 'Cryptocurrencies operate on decentralised networks without a central issuer.', 1),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-digital-assets-q03', 'cfa-alternative-investments-digital-assets', 'The mechanism that lets a blockchain network agree on valid transactions is a:', 'Consensus mechanism (e.g., proof of work)', 'Central clearinghouse', 'Credit rating', 'Coupon schedule', 'A', 'Consensus mechanisms like proof of work or proof of stake validate and secure the ledger.', 2),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-digital-assets-q04', 'cfa-alternative-investments-digital-assets', 'A stablecoin is a digital asset designed to:', 'Maintain a stable value relative to a reference such as a fiat currency', 'Maximise volatility', 'Pay a fixed coupon', 'Represent a unique artwork', 'A', 'Stablecoins peg their value to a reference asset (often a fiat currency) to reduce volatility.', 3),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-digital-assets-q05', 'cfa-alternative-investments-digital-assets', 'A non-fungible token (NFT) represents:', 'A unique, non-interchangeable digital item', 'A fixed-income bond', 'A share of stock', 'A stable currency', 'A', 'NFTs are unique tokens representing ownership of a specific digital (or linked physical) item.', 4),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-digital-assets-q06', 'cfa-alternative-investments-digital-assets', 'A smart contract is best described as:', 'Self-executing code that runs when conditions are met', 'A paper legal agreement', 'A central bank rule', 'A mutual fund prospectus', 'A', 'Smart contracts automatically execute agreed terms on a blockchain when conditions are satisfied.', 5),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-digital-assets-q07', 'cfa-alternative-investments-digital-assets', 'Tokenisation refers to:', 'Representing claims on real assets as blockchain tokens', 'Printing paper certificates', 'Splitting a stock', 'Issuing a government bond', 'A', 'Tokenisation records ownership of real assets on a blockchain to improve divisibility and liquidity.', 6),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-digital-assets-q08', 'cfa-alternative-investments-digital-assets', 'A key risk of investing in digital assets is:', 'Extreme price volatility and regulatory uncertainty', 'Guaranteed low returns', 'Excessive liquidity', 'Government insurance', 'A', 'Digital assets face high volatility, evolving regulation, and custody/security risks.', 7),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-digital-assets-q09', 'cfa-alternative-investments-digital-assets', 'The immutability of a blockchain means records are:', 'Extremely difficult to alter once confirmed', 'Editable by anyone', 'Deleted daily', 'Stored centrally', 'A', 'Cryptographic linking makes confirmed blockchain records very hard to change, supporting trust.', 8),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-digital-assets-q10', 'cfa-alternative-investments-digital-assets', 'Investors can gain exposure to cryptocurrencies through:', 'Direct holdings, futures, or funds', 'Only physical coins', 'Only government bonds', 'Bank deposits exclusively', 'A', 'Exposure is available via direct ownership, listed futures, and investment funds/ETPs.', 9),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-digital-assets-q11', 'cfa-alternative-investments-digital-assets', 'A significant custody risk unique to digital assets is:', 'Loss or theft of private keys', 'Coupon default', 'Dividend cuts', 'Index rebalancing', 'A', 'Whoever controls the private keys controls the assets; lost or stolen keys mean permanent loss.', 10),
  ('cfa-alternative-investments-digital-assets-q12', 'cfa-alternative-investments-digital-assets', '''Proof of stake'' is an example of a:', 'Consensus mechanism', 'Fee structure', 'Derivative contract', 'Credit rating', 'A', 'Proof of stake is a consensus mechanism that validates transactions based on staked holdings, an alternative to proof of work.', 11),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-process-q01', 'cfa-portfolio-management-process', 'The three steps of the portfolio management process are planning, execution and:', 'Feedback', 'Taxation', 'Marketing', 'Auditing', 'A', 'The process is plan (IPS), execute (allocate and select), and feedback (monitor, rebalance, evaluate).', 0),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-process-q02', 'cfa-portfolio-management-process', 'The planning step culminates in the:', 'Investment Policy Statement (IPS)', 'Trade blotter', 'Annual report', 'Tax return', 'A', 'Planning produces the IPS, which documents objectives and constraints.', 1),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-process-q03', 'cfa-portfolio-management-process', 'A defined-benefit pension plan''s investment risk is borne primarily by the:', 'Plan sponsor (employer)', 'Employee only', 'Government', 'Custodian', 'A', 'In a DB plan the sponsor promises defined benefits and bears the investment risk; in a DC plan the employee does.', 2),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-process-q04', 'cfa-portfolio-management-process', 'An open-end mutual fund trades at:', 'Its net asset value (NAV)', 'A premium set by the exchange', 'A fixed price forever', 'The coupon rate', 'A', 'Open-end funds issue and redeem shares at NAV; closed-end funds trade on an exchange at market prices.', 3),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-process-q05', 'cfa-portfolio-management-process', 'A key advantage of ETFs is that they:', 'Trade intraday on an exchange and are often tax-efficient', 'Cannot be bought by individuals', 'Never track an index', 'Guarantee returns', 'A', 'ETFs trade like stocks throughout the day, typically track indexes, and are relatively tax-efficient.', 4),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-process-q06', 'cfa-portfolio-management-process', 'The primary rationale for holding a diversified portfolio is to:', 'Reduce risk for a given level of expected return', 'Guarantee the highest return', 'Avoid all taxes', 'Eliminate market risk', 'A', 'Diversification across imperfectly correlated assets lowers risk without necessarily sacrificing expected return.', 5),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-process-q07', 'cfa-portfolio-management-process', 'Endowments and foundations typically have:', 'Long time horizons and ongoing spending needs', 'One-year horizons', 'No return objective', 'Guaranteed liabilities like insurers', 'A', 'Endowments/foundations invest for the long term to support perpetual spending.', 6),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-process-q08', 'cfa-portfolio-management-process', 'The execution step includes:', 'Asset allocation and security selection', 'Writing the IPS', 'Only performance review', 'Setting tax law', 'A', 'Execution translates the plan into a portfolio via allocation and security selection.', 7),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-process-q09', 'cfa-portfolio-management-process', 'Insurance companies and banks are examples of investors that are largely:', 'Liability-driven', 'Return-only focused', 'Unconstrained', 'Tax-exempt endowments', 'A', 'Insurers and banks manage assets against specific liabilities, shaping their risk and liquidity needs.', 8),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-process-q10', 'cfa-portfolio-management-process', 'The feedback step involves monitoring, rebalancing and:', 'Performance evaluation', 'Underwriting bonds', 'Issuing shares', 'Setting monetary policy', 'A', 'Feedback monitors the portfolio and client, rebalances, and evaluates results versus the benchmark.', 9),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-process-q11', 'cfa-portfolio-management-process', 'A separately managed account (SMA) differs from a mutual fund in that the investor:', 'Directly owns the underlying securities', 'Owns fund shares only', 'Cannot customise', 'Has no manager', 'A', 'In an SMA the client directly owns the securities, allowing customisation, unlike pooled fund shares.', 10),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-process-q12', 'cfa-portfolio-management-process', 'A defined-contribution plan differs from a defined-benefit plan in that:', 'The employee bears the investment risk', 'The employer guarantees the benefit', 'Benefits are fixed by formula', 'It cannot be invested', 'A', 'In a DC plan, contributions are defined and the employee bears the investment outcome and risk.', 11),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-fintech-q01', 'cfa-portfolio-management-fintech', 'Fintech is best described as:', 'Technology-driven innovation in financial services', 'A type of government bond', 'A tax strategy', 'A stock exchange', 'A', 'Fintech refers to technological innovation applied to financial products and services.', 0),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-fintech-q02', 'cfa-portfolio-management-fintech', 'The ''three Vs'' commonly used to characterise big data are volume, velocity and:', 'Variety', 'Volatility', 'Value only', 'Verification', 'A', 'Big data is described by volume, velocity and variety.', 1),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-fintech-q03', 'cfa-portfolio-management-fintech', 'Machine learning that uses labelled data to make predictions is:', 'Supervised learning', 'Unsupervised learning', 'Reinforcement only', 'Manual analysis', 'A', 'Supervised learning trains on labelled inputs and outputs to predict or classify.', 2),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-fintech-q04', 'cfa-portfolio-management-fintech', 'Unsupervised learning is typically used to:', 'Find structure or clusters in unlabelled data', 'Predict a known label', 'Execute trades', 'Set interest rates', 'A', 'Unsupervised algorithms discover patterns or groupings without predefined labels.', 3),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-fintech-q05', 'cfa-portfolio-management-fintech', 'Natural language processing (NLP) is used in finance to:', 'Extract information and sentiment from text', 'Store trades', 'Set margin requirements', 'Print reports', 'A', 'NLP analyses text such as filings, news and social media for information and sentiment.', 4),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-fintech-q06', 'cfa-portfolio-management-fintech', 'A robo-adviser provides:', 'Automated, low-cost investment advice and allocation', 'Guaranteed returns', 'Only tax filing', 'Physical bank branches', 'A', 'Robo-advisers deliver algorithm-driven advice and portfolio management at low cost.', 5),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-fintech-q07', 'cfa-portfolio-management-fintech', 'Alternative data includes sources such as:', 'Social media and satellite/sensor data', 'Audited financial statements only', 'Official GDP figures only', 'Company annual reports only', 'A', 'Alternative data comes from non-traditional sources like social media, sensors and transaction records.', 6),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-fintech-q08', 'cfa-portfolio-management-fintech', 'Algorithmic trading refers to:', 'Executing orders automatically according to predefined rules', 'Manual floor trading', 'Long-term buy and hold only', 'Central bank operations', 'A', 'Algorithmic trading uses computer rules to place and manage orders, often at high speed.', 7),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-fintech-q09', 'cfa-portfolio-management-fintech', 'Deep learning is based on:', 'Multi-layered neural networks', 'Simple linear regression only', 'Manual rules', 'Spreadsheets', 'A', 'Deep learning uses neural networks with many layers to model complex patterns.', 8),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-fintech-q10', 'cfa-portfolio-management-fintech', 'A key risk when applying machine learning to investing is:', 'Overfitting the model to noise in the data', 'Too little data always', 'Guaranteed profits', 'No need for validation', 'A', 'Overfitting captures noise rather than signal, producing models that fail out of sample.', 9),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-fintech-q11', 'cfa-portfolio-management-fintech', 'Distributed ledger technology can benefit finance by:', 'Enabling faster, more secure settlement and tokenisation', 'Eliminating all market risk', 'Guaranteeing returns', 'Removing the need for data', 'A', 'DLT can streamline clearing/settlement and support tokenised assets with strong security.', 10),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-fintech-q12', 'cfa-portfolio-management-fintech', 'High-frequency trading is a form of algorithmic trading characterised by:', 'Very high speed and large order volumes', 'Long holding periods', 'Manual execution', 'Annual rebalancing', 'A', 'HFT executes many orders at extremely high speed, holding positions very briefly.', 11),
  ('cfa-ethics-ethics-and-trust-q01', 'cfa-ethics-ethics-and-trust', 'The investment profession fundamentally depends on:', 'Client trust', 'High leverage', 'Frequent trading', 'Tax avoidance', 'A', 'Because clients entrust their capital to professionals, trust is the foundation of the industry.', 0),
  ('cfa-ethics-ethics-and-trust-q02', 'cfa-ethics-ethics-and-trust', 'Ethical standards, relative to legal standards, generally:', 'Go beyond the minimum set by law', 'Are always identical to law', 'Are less demanding than law', 'Replace the law', 'A', 'Laws set a minimum; ethics often demand more, and high conduct meets the stricter of the two.', 1),
  ('cfa-ethics-ethics-and-trust-q03', 'cfa-ethics-ethics-and-trust', 'An act can be:', 'Legal but unethical', 'Never legal and ethical at once', 'Only ethical if legal', 'Only legal if ethical', 'A', 'Legality and ethics do not always coincide — some legal acts are unethical, and vice versa.', 2),
  ('cfa-ethics-ethics-and-trust-q04', 'cfa-ethics-ethics-and-trust', 'A common situational influence that undermines ethical behaviour is:', 'Money and loyalty pressures', 'Reading the Standards', 'Reflecting on outcomes', 'Disclosure', 'A', 'Money, loyalty and peer pressure are situational influences that can override principles.', 3),
  ('cfa-ethics-ethics-and-trust-q05', 'cfa-ethics-ethics-and-trust', 'A robust ethical decision-making framework typically begins by:', 'Identifying the facts, stakeholders and conflicts', 'Executing the trade', 'Ignoring the Code', 'Maximising personal gain', 'A', 'The first step is to identify relevant facts, stakeholders, duties and conflicts of interest.', 4),
  ('cfa-ethics-ethics-and-trust-q06', 'cfa-ethics-ethics-and-trust', 'Overconfidence in one''s own ethics tends to:', 'Increase the risk of misconduct', 'Guarantee ethical behaviour', 'Have no effect', 'Improve compliance', 'A', 'People who overrate their ethical judgement are less vigilant and more prone to lapses.', 5),
  ('cfa-ethics-ethics-and-trust-q07', 'cfa-ethics-ethics-and-trust', 'A defining feature of a ''profession'' is that it:', 'Serves society and holds members to a code of conduct', 'Maximises members'' pay only', 'Has no standards', 'Avoids regulation', 'A', 'A profession serves the greater good and enforces ethical standards among its members.', 6),
  ('cfa-ethics-ethics-and-trust-q08', 'cfa-ethics-ethics-and-trust', 'Unethical behaviour by a few investment professionals tends to:', 'Damage trust across the whole profession', 'Only affect those individuals', 'Improve market confidence', 'Reduce systemic risk', 'A', 'Misconduct erodes trust broadly, harming clients and markets beyond the wrongdoers.', 7),
  ('cfa-ethics-ethics-and-trust-q09', 'cfa-ethics-ethics-and-trust', 'When ethics and law conflict, a member should follow:', 'The stricter standard', 'Whichever is cheaper', 'The law only', 'Personal preference', 'A', 'Members should adhere to the more demanding of the ethical or legal standard.', 8),
  ('cfa-ethics-ethics-and-trust-q10', 'cfa-ethics-ethics-and-trust', 'Trust in the investment industry is best described as:', 'Hard to build and easily lost', 'Automatic and permanent', 'Irrelevant to returns', 'Guaranteed by law', 'A', 'Trust takes time to earn but can be destroyed quickly by misconduct.', 9),
  ('cfa-ethics-ethics-and-trust-q11', 'cfa-ethics-ethics-and-trust', 'The final step of an ethical decision-making framework is usually to:', 'Reflect on the outcome', 'Forget the decision', 'Hide the result', 'Blame others', 'A', 'Reflecting on the outcome supports learning and better future decisions.', 10),
  ('cfa-ethics-ethics-and-trust-q12', 'cfa-ethics-ethics-and-trust', 'A loss of trust in financial markets tends to:', 'Raise costs and reduce participation', 'Lower transaction costs', 'Increase liquidity', 'Have no economic effect', 'A', 'When trust falls, participants demand more protection, raising costs and shrinking markets.', 11),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-portfolio-mathematics-q01', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-portfolio-mathematics', 'The expected return of a portfolio is the:', 'Weighted average of the assets'' expected returns', 'Sum of the assets'' variances', 'Highest asset return', 'Product of the returns', 'A', 'Portfolio expected return is the weight-weighted average of the component expected returns.', 0),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-portfolio-mathematics-q02', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-portfolio-mathematics', 'Unlike expected return, portfolio variance depends on:', 'The covariance/correlation between assets', 'Only the weights', 'The largest variance alone', 'The number of assets only', 'A', 'Portfolio variance is driven by how assets co-move, captured by covariance/correlation.', 1),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-portfolio-mathematics-q03', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-portfolio-mathematics', 'The correlation coefficient ranges between:', '−1 and +1', '0 and 1', '−∞ and +∞', '0 and 100', 'A', 'Correlation is a standardised measure of co-movement bounded by −1 and +1.', 2),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-portfolio-mathematics-q04', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-portfolio-mathematics', 'Covariance between two assets equals:', 'Correlation × σ₁ × σ₂', 'Correlation / (σ₁σ₂)', 'σ₁ + σ₂', 'σ₁ − σ₂', 'A', 'Cov(1,2) = ρ₁₂ × σ₁ × σ₂.', 3),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-portfolio-mathematics-q05', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-portfolio-mathematics', 'The diversification benefit is greatest when the correlation between assets is:', 'As low (or negative) as possible', 'Exactly +1', 'Zero only', 'Undefined', 'A', 'Lower correlation reduces portfolio risk more; at ρ = −1 risk can approach zero.', 4),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-portfolio-mathematics-q06', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-portfolio-mathematics', 'If two assets have a correlation of +1, combining them provides:', 'No diversification benefit', 'Maximum diversification', 'Zero risk', 'Negative risk', 'A', 'Perfectly correlated assets move together, so no risk reduction is achieved.', 5),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-portfolio-mathematics-q07', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-portfolio-mathematics', 'In the two-asset variance formula, the term 2w₁w₂ρσ₁σ₂ represents the:', 'Covariance contribution', 'Total variance', 'Expected return', 'Risk-free rate', 'A', 'That cross term is the covariance contribution to portfolio variance.', 6),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-portfolio-mathematics-q08', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-portfolio-mathematics', 'Adding an asset with low correlation to a portfolio tends to:', 'Reduce total portfolio variance', 'Increase total variance', 'Leave variance unchanged', 'Eliminate expected return', 'A', 'Low-correlation assets lower overall portfolio variance through diversification.', 7),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-portfolio-mathematics-q09', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-portfolio-mathematics', 'A positive covariance between two assets means they tend to:', 'Move in the same direction', 'Move in opposite directions', 'Be uncorrelated', 'Have zero variance', 'A', 'Positive covariance indicates the returns tend to rise and fall together.', 8),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-portfolio-mathematics-q10', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-portfolio-mathematics', 'The correlation coefficient is preferred to covariance because it is:', 'Standardised and unit-free', 'Always positive', 'Larger', 'Equal to variance', 'A', 'Correlation rescales covariance to a bounded, unit-free measure that is easier to interpret.', 9),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-portfolio-mathematics-q11', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-portfolio-mathematics', 'A correlation of 0 between two assets implies:', 'No linear relationship between their returns', 'They are identical', 'Perfect negative co-movement', 'Zero variance', 'A', 'Zero correlation means no linear co-movement between the two return series.', 10),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-portfolio-mathematics-q12', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-portfolio-mathematics', 'Portfolio risk is generally ___ the weighted average of the individual asset risks when correlations are below 1.', 'Less than', 'Greater than', 'Equal to', 'Unrelated to', 'A', 'Imperfect correlation makes portfolio standard deviation less than the weighted average of the components.', 11),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-simulation-methods-q01', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-simulation-methods', 'Monte Carlo simulation works by:', 'Repeatedly generating random inputs to build a distribution of outcomes', 'Solving one exact equation', 'Averaging two data points', 'Ignoring uncertainty', 'A', 'Monte Carlo draws many random scenarios for uncertain inputs and aggregates the results.', 0),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-simulation-methods-q02', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-simulation-methods', 'A common application of Monte Carlo simulation is:', 'Estimating Value at Risk', 'Filing taxes', 'Auditing', 'Setting coupon rates', 'A', 'Monte Carlo is widely used for VaR, option pricing and retirement planning.', 1),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-simulation-methods-q03', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-simulation-methods', 'Bootstrapping generates a sampling distribution by:', 'Resampling with replacement from observed data', 'Assuming a normal distribution', 'Using a single observation', 'Ignoring the data', 'A', 'Bootstrapping repeatedly resamples the actual data set to approximate the sampling distribution.', 2),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-simulation-methods-q04', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-simulation-methods', 'A key limitation of Monte Carlo simulation is that:', 'Its output is only as good as the input assumptions', 'It gives an exact analytical answer', 'It cannot handle uncertainty', 'It needs no computer', 'A', '''Garbage in, garbage out'' — poor assumptions produce misleading simulated results.', 3),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-simulation-methods-q05', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-simulation-methods', 'Asset prices are commonly modelled as following a ___ distribution.', 'Lognormal', 'Uniform', 'Binomial only', 'Negative', 'A', 'Prices are modelled as lognormal because they cannot be negative; continuously compounded returns are modelled as normal.', 4),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-simulation-methods-q06', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-simulation-methods', 'A strength of simulation methods is that they can:', 'Handle complex problems that lack closed-form solutions', 'Eliminate all uncertainty', 'Guarantee accuracy', 'Avoid assumptions', 'A', 'Simulation is valuable for complex, path-dependent problems where analytical solutions do not exist.', 5),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-simulation-methods-q07', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-simulation-methods', 'Bootstrapping is especially useful when:', 'The true underlying distribution is unknown', 'The distribution is known exactly', 'There is no data', 'Only the mean matters', 'A', 'Bootstrapping avoids assuming a theoretical distribution, using the observed data instead.', 6),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-simulation-methods-q08', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-simulation-methods', 'A Monte Carlo result is best described as a:', 'Statistical estimate, not an exact value', 'Precise closed-form answer', 'Deterministic constant', 'Guaranteed forecast', 'A', 'Simulation produces an approximate, statistical estimate that varies with the random draws.', 7),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-simulation-methods-q09', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-simulation-methods', 'Increasing the number of Monte Carlo trials generally:', 'Improves the precision of the estimate', 'Reduces precision', 'Has no effect', 'Changes the assumptions', 'A', 'More simulation runs reduce sampling error and sharpen the estimated distribution.', 8),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-simulation-methods-q10', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-simulation-methods', 'In Monte Carlo simulation, each uncertain input is drawn from:', 'An assumed probability distribution', 'A single fixed value', 'The mean only', 'The risk-free rate', 'A', 'Each simulated input is sampled from a specified probability distribution.', 9),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-simulation-methods-q11', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-simulation-methods', 'Continuously compounded asset returns are typically assumed to be:', 'Normally distributed', 'Lognormally distributed', 'Uniformly distributed', 'Always positive', 'A', 'If prices are lognormal, the continuously compounded returns are normally distributed.', 10),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-simulation-methods-q12', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-simulation-methods', 'Both Monte Carlo and bootstrapping are used to:', 'Model uncertainty and build distributions of outcomes', 'Eliminate randomness', 'Replace the balance sheet', 'Set interest rates', 'A', 'Both are simulation techniques for characterising uncertainty in outcomes.', 11),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-big-data-techniques-q01', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-big-data-techniques', 'The ''three Vs'' of big data are volume, velocity and:', 'Variety', 'Volatility', 'Value', 'Verification', 'A', 'Big data is characterised by volume, velocity and variety (sometimes plus veracity/value).', 0),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-big-data-techniques-q02', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-big-data-techniques', 'Text, images and social-media posts are examples of:', 'Unstructured data', 'Structured data', 'Tabular data', 'Numeric-only data', 'A', 'Unstructured data lacks a predefined row/column format, unlike structured data.', 1),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-big-data-techniques-q03', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-big-data-techniques', 'Fixing errors and handling missing values in a dataset is part of:', 'Data cleansing', 'Data capture only', 'Model deployment', 'Forecasting', 'A', 'Cleansing corrects errors and missing values before analysis.', 2),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-big-data-techniques-q04', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-big-data-techniques', 'Supervised machine learning uses:', 'Labelled data to predict or classify', 'Only unlabelled data', 'No data', 'Manual rules only', 'A', 'Supervised learning trains on labelled inputs/outputs to make predictions.', 3),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-big-data-techniques-q05', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-big-data-techniques', 'Clustering is an example of:', 'Unsupervised learning', 'Supervised learning', 'Regression', 'Manual sorting', 'A', 'Clustering finds structure in unlabelled data — a form of unsupervised learning.', 4),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-big-data-techniques-q06', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-big-data-techniques', 'Natural language processing (NLP) is used to:', 'Extract meaning and sentiment from text', 'Store numbers', 'Set coupon rates', 'Audit accounts', 'A', 'NLP analyses text data such as filings, news and social media.', 5),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-big-data-techniques-q07', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-big-data-techniques', '''Alternative data'' refers to:', 'Non-traditional sources such as satellite images and social media', 'Audited financial statements', 'Official GDP data', 'Company filings only', 'A', 'Alternative data comes from non-traditional sources like sensors, satellites and web activity.', 6),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-big-data-techniques-q08', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-big-data-techniques', 'Deep learning relies on:', 'Multi-layered neural networks', 'A single linear equation', 'Manual rules', 'Spreadsheets only', 'A', 'Deep learning uses neural networks with many layers to model complex patterns.', 7),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-big-data-techniques-q09', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-big-data-techniques', 'A major risk when fitting models to big data is:', 'Overfitting to noise', 'Too little computation', 'Excessive simplicity always', 'Having no data', 'A', 'Overfitting captures noise rather than signal, harming out-of-sample performance.', 8),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-big-data-techniques-q10', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-big-data-techniques', 'Transforming raw data into a usable analytical format is called:', 'Data wrangling (preparation)', 'Data destruction', 'Backtesting', 'Reporting', 'A', 'Wrangling/preparation reshapes and transforms cleansed data for analysis.', 9),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-big-data-techniques-q11', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-big-data-techniques', 'Structured data are best described as:', 'Organised in rows and columns', 'Free-form text', 'Images only', 'Audio recordings', 'A', 'Structured data fit neatly into a predefined tabular (rows/columns) format.', 10),
  ('cfa-quantitative-methods-big-data-techniques-q12', 'cfa-quantitative-methods-big-data-techniques', 'The reliability/accuracy dimension sometimes added to big data''s characteristics is:', 'Veracity', 'Velocity', 'Volume', 'Variety', 'A', 'Veracity refers to the trustworthiness and accuracy of the data.', 11),
  ('cfa-economics-capital-flows-fx-q01', 'cfa-economics-capital-flows-fx', 'A spot exchange rate is for:', 'Near-immediate delivery', 'Delivery in one year', 'A future date only', 'Interest payments', 'A', 'The spot rate settles almost immediately; forward rates apply to future dates.', 0),
  ('cfa-economics-capital-flows-fx-q02', 'cfa-economics-capital-flows-fx', 'A country''s trade in goods and services is recorded in the:', 'Current account', 'Capital account only', 'Financial account only', 'Reserve account', 'A', 'The current account captures trade, income and transfers.', 1),
  ('cfa-economics-capital-flows-fx-q03', 'cfa-economics-capital-flows-fx', 'Cross-border investment flows are recorded in the:', 'Capital and financial account', 'Current account', 'Income statement', 'Trade balance only', 'A', 'The capital and financial account records international investment (capital) flows.', 2),
  ('cfa-economics-capital-flows-fx-q04', 'cfa-economics-capital-flows-fx', 'A current-account deficit is generally financed by:', 'A financial-account surplus (net capital inflows)', 'A larger trade surplus', 'Lower investment', 'Higher exports only', 'A', 'A current-account deficit must be offset by net capital inflows in the financial account.', 3),
  ('cfa-economics-capital-flows-fx-q05', 'cfa-economics-capital-flows-fx', 'All else equal, higher relative inflation tends to:', 'Weaken a country''s currency', 'Strengthen the currency', 'Have no effect', 'Fix the exchange rate', 'A', 'Higher relative inflation erodes purchasing power and tends to depreciate the currency.', 4),
  ('cfa-economics-capital-flows-fx-q06', 'cfa-economics-capital-flows-fx', 'The real exchange rate adjusts the nominal rate for:', 'Relative price levels (inflation)', 'Interest income', 'Trade volume', 'Coupon payments', 'A', 'The real exchange rate accounts for relative price levels between countries.', 5),
  ('cfa-economics-capital-flows-fx-q07', 'cfa-economics-capital-flows-fx', 'Strong foreign investment inflows into a country tend to:', 'Increase demand for its currency (appreciation)', 'Weaken its currency', 'Have no effect', 'Cause deflation', 'A', 'Capital inflows raise demand for the domestic currency, tending to appreciate it.', 6),
  ('cfa-economics-capital-flows-fx-q08', 'cfa-economics-capital-flows-fx', 'A stronger domestic currency tends to make exports:', 'More expensive to foreign buyers', 'Cheaper abroad', 'Free', 'Unaffected', 'A', 'Currency appreciation raises the foreign-currency price of exports, hurting competitiveness.', 7),
  ('cfa-economics-capital-flows-fx-q09', 'cfa-economics-capital-flows-fx', 'The largest financial market in the world by volume is the:', 'Foreign exchange market', 'Equity market', 'Corporate bond market', 'Commodity market', 'A', 'The FX market has the highest daily trading volume of any financial market.', 8),
  ('cfa-economics-capital-flows-fx-q10', 'cfa-economics-capital-flows-fx', 'An exchange-rate regime in which the currency''s value floats freely is called a:', 'Floating (flexible) regime', 'Fixed peg', 'Currency board', 'Gold standard', 'A', 'Under a floating regime, market forces determine the exchange rate.', 9),
  ('cfa-economics-capital-flows-fx-q11', 'cfa-economics-capital-flows-fx', 'Higher relative real interest rates in a country tend to:', 'Attract capital and strengthen its currency', 'Repel capital', 'Weaken the currency', 'Cause a trade surplus only', 'A', 'Higher real rates draw yield-seeking capital, tending to appreciate the currency.', 10),
  ('cfa-economics-capital-flows-fx-q12', 'cfa-economics-capital-flows-fx', 'A forward exchange rate is:', 'Agreed now for currency exchange on a future date', 'The current spot rate', 'An interest rate', 'A tax on trade', 'A', 'A forward rate locks in today the exchange rate for a transaction settling in the future.', 11),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-assets-q01', 'cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-assets', 'A cost that provides benefits beyond the current period should generally be:', 'Capitalised', 'Expensed immediately', 'Ignored', 'Recorded as revenue', 'A', 'Costs with future benefits are capitalised as assets and depreciated/amortised over time.', 0),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-assets-q02', 'cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-assets', 'Capitalising a cost rather than expensing it initially results in:', 'Higher current-period net income', 'Lower current assets', 'Lower current income', 'No effect on income', 'A', 'Capitalising defers the expense, raising current income (and assets) but lowering future income.', 1),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-assets-q03', 'cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-assets', 'Goodwill is:', 'Not amortised but tested for impairment', 'Amortised over 10 years', 'Expensed at purchase', 'A current asset', 'A', 'Goodwill has an indefinite life; it is not amortised but tested for impairment at least annually.', 2),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-assets-q04', 'cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-assets', 'A finite-life intangible asset such as a patent is:', 'Amortised over its useful life', 'Never expensed', 'Revalued upward under US GAAP', 'A liability', 'A', 'Finite-life intangibles are amortised; indefinite-life intangibles are tested for impairment instead.', 3),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-assets-q05', 'cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-assets', 'The depreciation method producing the highest expense in the early years is:', 'Double-declining balance', 'Straight-line', 'Units-of-production always', 'No depreciation', 'A', 'Double-declining balance is an accelerated method, front-loading depreciation.', 4),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-assets-q05b', 'cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-assets', 'Under IFRS, the model that allows carrying PP&E at fair value is the:', 'Revaluation model', 'LIFO model', 'Historical cost model only', 'Cash model', 'A', 'IFRS permits the revaluation model for PP&E; US GAAP requires historical cost.', 5),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-assets-q07', 'cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-assets', 'An asset is impaired when its carrying value:', 'Exceeds its recoverable amount', 'Equals its cost', 'Is below salvage value', 'Rises with inflation', 'A', 'Impairment occurs when carrying value exceeds the recoverable amount, requiring a write-down.', 6),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-assets-q08', 'cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-assets', 'Under US GAAP, reversal of a previously recognised impairment loss (other than assets held for sale) is:', 'Generally prohibited', 'Required annually', 'Always allowed', 'Recorded as revenue', 'A', 'US GAAP generally prohibits reversing impairments; IFRS allows reversal (except goodwill).', 7),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-assets-q09', 'cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-assets', 'The units-of-production depreciation method bases expense on:', 'Actual usage/output of the asset', 'Time only', 'The tax rate', 'The market price', 'A', 'Units-of-production allocates cost according to actual output or usage each period.', 8),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-assets-q10', 'cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-assets', 'Indefinite-life intangible assets are:', 'Tested for impairment rather than amortised', 'Amortised straight-line', 'Expensed immediately', 'Recorded as liabilities', 'A', 'Indefinite-life intangibles (like some brands) are not amortised but tested for impairment.', 9),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-assets-q11', 'cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-assets', 'Compared with expensing, capitalising costs generally makes early-year cash flow from operations:', 'Higher (the outflow is shown as investing)', 'Lower', 'Unchanged', 'Negative', 'A', 'Capitalised costs are classified as investing outflows, so operating cash flow appears higher.', 10),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-assets-q12', 'cfa-financial-reporting-long-term-assets', 'Straight-line depreciation allocates:', 'An equal expense to each year of useful life', 'More expense early', 'More expense late', 'No expense', 'A', 'Straight-line spreads the depreciable cost evenly over the asset''s useful life.', 11),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-financial-modeling-q01', 'cfa-financial-reporting-financial-modeling', 'Financial statement modelling produces:', 'Pro-forma (forecast) financial statements', 'Audited historical accounts only', 'Tax returns', 'Legal contracts', 'A', 'Modelling builds forecast (pro-forma) statements to value a firm or test scenarios.', 0),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-financial-modeling-q02', 'cfa-financial-reporting-financial-modeling', 'Forecasting revenue from the overall economy down to a company''s market share is a:', 'Top-down approach', 'Bottom-up approach', 'Random approach', 'Backward approach', 'A', 'Top-down forecasting moves from macro/industry conditions to the individual company.', 1),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-financial-modeling-q03', 'cfa-financial-reporting-financial-modeling', 'Building revenue from a company''s individual products or segments upward is a:', 'Bottom-up approach', 'Top-down approach', 'Hybrid only', 'Sensitivity test', 'A', 'Bottom-up forecasting aggregates from the company''s own units to a total.', 2),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-financial-modeling-q04', 'cfa-financial-reporting-financial-modeling', 'Varying a single input to see its effect on the output is:', 'Sensitivity analysis', 'Scenario analysis', 'Auditing', 'Consolidation', 'A', 'Sensitivity analysis changes one variable at a time; scenario analysis changes a coherent set.', 3),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-financial-modeling-q05', 'cfa-financial-reporting-financial-modeling', 'A financing ''plug'' in a model is used to:', 'Balance the balance sheet (e.g., via debt or cash)', 'Increase revenue', 'Lower taxes', 'Pay dividends', 'A', 'The plug (revolver/cash) forces the projected balance sheet to balance.', 4),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-financial-modeling-q06', 'cfa-financial-reporting-financial-modeling', 'The behavioural bias of relying too heavily on an initial figure is:', 'Anchoring', 'Diversification', 'Hedging', 'Arbitrage', 'A', 'Anchoring is over-reliance on an initial reference point when making estimates.', 5),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-financial-modeling-q07', 'cfa-financial-reporting-financial-modeling', 'Analysing coherent combinations of inputs (e.g., recession vs boom) is:', 'Scenario analysis', 'Sensitivity analysis', 'Regression', 'Reconciliation', 'A', 'Scenario analysis evaluates outcomes under internally consistent sets of assumptions.', 6),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-financial-modeling-q08', 'cfa-financial-reporting-financial-modeling', 'The three financial statements in a model must:', 'Articulate (tie together consistently)', 'Be independent of each other', 'Ignore cash flow', 'Use different periods', 'A', 'The income statement, balance sheet and cash flow statement must link consistently.', 7),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-financial-modeling-q09', 'cfa-financial-reporting-financial-modeling', 'The bias of seeking information that confirms one''s existing view is:', 'Confirmation bias', 'Anchoring', 'Recency', 'Framing', 'A', 'Confirmation bias leads analysts to favour evidence supporting their prior beliefs.', 8),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-financial-modeling-q10', 'cfa-financial-reporting-financial-modeling', 'Modelling a company''s cost structure requires distinguishing:', 'Fixed from variable costs', 'Assets from equity', 'Dividends from buybacks', 'Spot from forward rates', 'A', 'Separating fixed and variable costs drives margin and operating-leverage forecasts.', 9),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-financial-modeling-q11', 'cfa-financial-reporting-financial-modeling', 'Incorporating Porter''s five forces into a model helps assess:', 'The industry''s competitive environment', 'The tax rate', 'The coupon rate', 'The audit opinion', 'A', 'The competitive environment shapes pricing power, margins and sustainable growth.', 10),
  ('cfa-financial-reporting-financial-modeling-q12', 'cfa-financial-reporting-financial-modeling', 'Good modelling practice checks forecast assumptions against:', 'Historical results and the competitive reality', 'Nothing', 'Only the best case', 'The risk-free rate', 'A', 'Grounding assumptions in history and competitive analysis reduces bias and improves realism.', 11),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring-q01', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring', 'A spin-off is a form of corporate:', 'Divestment (contraction)', 'Investment (expansion)', 'Dividend', 'Share issuance', 'A', 'A spin-off separates a business into an independent company — a form of divestment.', 0),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring-q02', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring', 'An acquisition is an example of corporate:', 'Investment (expansion)', 'Divestment', 'Liquidation', 'Buyback', 'A', 'Acquisitions and joint ventures expand the firm — investment actions.', 1),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring-q03', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring', 'A primary motivation for mergers is to achieve:', 'Synergies', 'Higher taxes', 'Lower revenue', 'Fewer customers', 'A', 'Synergies (cost or revenue) are a leading rationale for M&A.', 2),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring-q04', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring', 'Synergy means the combined firm is worth:', 'More than the sum of the separate parts', 'Less than each part', 'Exactly the sum of the parts', 'Zero', 'A', 'Synergy implies the whole is worth more than the standalone values combined.', 3),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring-q05', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring', 'Valuing a target using multiples of similar listed firms is:', 'Comparable company analysis', 'Comparable transaction analysis', 'Premium-paid analysis', 'DDM', 'A', 'Comparable company analysis applies peer multiples (adding a takeover premium).', 4),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring-q06', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring', 'Valuing a target using multiples paid in past similar deals is:', 'Comparable transaction analysis', 'Comparable company analysis', 'Residual income', 'Cost approach', 'A', 'Comparable transaction analysis uses precedent deal multiples, which embed the premium.', 5),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring-q07', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring', 'A carve-out involves:', 'Selling a stake in a subsidiary to the public via an IPO', 'Buying a competitor', 'Paying a special dividend', 'Issuing bonds', 'A', 'In an equity carve-out, the parent sells shares of a subsidiary to outside investors.', 6),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring-q08', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring', 'Cost cutting and reorganisation in response to distress is a form of:', 'Restructuring', 'Expansion', 'Acquisition', 'Dividend policy', 'A', 'Operational/financial restructuring reshapes a firm, often under distress.', 7),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring-q09', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring', 'Premium-paid analysis measures the premium over the target''s:', 'Pre-announcement share price', 'Book value', 'Coupon rate', 'Dividend', 'A', 'Premium-paid analysis looks at the acquisition premium relative to the unaffected pre-deal price.', 8),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring-q10', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring', 'A joint venture is best classified as a corporate:', 'Investment action', 'Divestment action', 'Liquidation', 'Buyback', 'A', 'A joint venture is an expansion/investment where firms combine resources in a new entity.', 9),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring-q11', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring', 'Firms may restructure when management believes the company is:', 'Undervalued or in need of refocusing', 'Perfectly valued', 'Unable to change', 'Barred from restructuring', 'A', 'Perceived undervaluation, distress, or a desire to refocus commonly drive restructuring.', 10),
  ('cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring-q12', 'cfa-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring', 'The three broad categories of corporate restructuring are investment, divestment and:', 'Restructuring (reorganisation)', 'Dividends', 'Auditing', 'Hedging', 'A', 'Actions are grouped as investment (expansion), divestment (contraction), and restructuring/reorganisation.', 11),
  ('cfa-equity-company-forecasting-q01', 'cfa-equity-company-forecasting', 'The main objective of company analysis forecasting is to:', 'Project future revenues, costs and cash flows', 'Audit past accounts', 'Set the coupon rate', 'File taxes', 'A', 'Forecasting builds projections of a firm''s financials to support valuation.', 0),
  ('cfa-equity-company-forecasting-q02', 'cfa-equity-company-forecasting', 'A top-down revenue forecast starts from:', 'The economy and industry, then the company', 'The company''s products only', 'The share price', 'The audit report', 'A', 'Top-down forecasting moves from macro/industry conditions down to the company.', 1),
  ('cfa-equity-company-forecasting-q03', 'cfa-equity-company-forecasting', 'A sustainable competitive advantage is often called a:', 'Moat', 'Coupon', 'Hedge', 'Spread', 'A', 'A ''moat'' (brands, patents, network effects, cost advantage) protects durable high returns.', 2),
  ('cfa-equity-company-forecasting-q04', 'cfa-equity-company-forecasting', 'Modelling operating leverage requires separating:', 'Fixed and variable costs', 'Debt and equity', 'Spot and forward rates', 'Dividends and buybacks', 'A', 'Operating leverage depends on the mix of fixed versus variable costs.', 3),
  ('cfa-equity-company-forecasting-q05', 'cfa-equity-company-forecasting', 'Using base, bull and bear cases is an example of:', 'Scenario analysis', 'Reconciliation', 'Consolidation', 'Amortisation', 'A', 'Scenario analysis captures a range of coherent outcomes under uncertainty.', 4),
  ('cfa-equity-company-forecasting-q06', 'cfa-equity-company-forecasting', 'Key drivers of revenue growth include volume, price and:', 'Market share', 'The tax rate', 'Depreciation', 'The coupon', 'A', 'Revenue is driven by volume, price and the company''s share of its market.', 5),
  ('cfa-equity-company-forecasting-q07', 'cfa-equity-company-forecasting', 'Porter''s five forces help a forecaster assess:', 'Industry attractiveness and competitive pressure', 'The audit opinion', 'The bond rating', 'The dividend', 'A', 'The five forces gauge competitive intensity, which shapes margins and growth.', 6),
  ('cfa-equity-company-forecasting-q08', 'cfa-equity-company-forecasting', 'Extrapolating recent strong growth indefinitely reflects the bias of:', 'Overconfidence/recency', 'Diversification', 'Hedging', 'Arbitrage', 'A', 'Over-extrapolating recent trends reflects overconfidence and recency bias.', 7),
  ('cfa-equity-company-forecasting-q09', 'cfa-equity-company-forecasting', 'A hybrid forecasting approach:', 'Combines top-down and bottom-up methods', 'Ignores the industry', 'Uses only history', 'Avoids assumptions', 'A', 'A hybrid approach blends macro/industry (top-down) with company-level (bottom-up) inputs.', 8),
  ('cfa-equity-company-forecasting-q10', 'cfa-equity-company-forecasting', 'Company forecasts feed directly into:', 'Valuation models such as DCF and multiples', 'The audit', 'The tax filing only', 'The coupon schedule', 'A', 'Forecast financials are inputs to DCF and multiple-based valuations.', 9),
  ('cfa-equity-company-forecasting-q11', 'cfa-equity-company-forecasting', 'Economies of scale, if present, tend to:', 'Lower unit costs as output rises', 'Raise unit costs with output', 'Have no effect on costs', 'Eliminate revenue', 'A', 'Economies of scale reduce average cost per unit as production increases.', 10),
  ('cfa-equity-company-forecasting-q12', 'cfa-equity-company-forecasting', 'A wider economic moat generally supports:', 'More sustainable margins and returns', 'Rapid margin erosion', 'Immediate bankruptcy', 'Lower pricing power', 'A', 'A durable competitive advantage helps sustain high margins and returns on capital.', 11),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-cash-flows-types-q01', 'cfa-fixed-income-cash-flows-types', 'A bullet bond repays its principal:', 'Entirely at maturity', 'In equal instalments', 'Never', 'Before the coupons', 'A', 'A bullet structure pays coupons over the life and the full principal at maturity.', 0),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-cash-flows-types-q02', 'cfa-fixed-income-cash-flows-types', 'A fully amortising bond:', 'Repays principal gradually so nothing remains at maturity', 'Repays all principal at maturity', 'Never repays principal', 'Pays only at issuance', 'A', 'Level payments in a fully amortising bond retire the principal to zero by maturity.', 1),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-cash-flows-types-q03', 'cfa-fixed-income-cash-flows-types', 'A partially amortising bond leaves a large final payment called a:', 'Balloon payment', 'Coupon', 'Premium', 'Discount', 'A', 'Partial amortisation reduces principal partly, leaving a balloon payment at maturity.', 2),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-cash-flows-types-q04', 'cfa-fixed-income-cash-flows-types', 'A floating-rate note pays a coupon of:', 'A reference rate plus a spread', 'A fixed rate always', 'Zero', 'The share price', 'A', 'FRNs reset periodically to a benchmark reference rate plus a fixed spread.', 3),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-cash-flows-types-q05', 'cfa-fixed-income-cash-flows-types', 'A step-up coupon bond has a coupon that:', 'Rises on a predetermined schedule', 'Falls to zero', 'Stays fixed forever', 'Equals inflation', 'A', 'Step-up bonds increase the coupon at scheduled dates.', 4),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-cash-flows-types-q06', 'cfa-fixed-income-cash-flows-types', 'A payment-in-kind (PIK) bond pays interest by:', 'Issuing additional bonds instead of cash', 'Paying cash coupons', 'Repaying principal early', 'Converting to equity', 'A', 'PIK bonds pay coupons in the form of more bonds/notes rather than cash.', 5),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-cash-flows-types-q07', 'cfa-fixed-income-cash-flows-types', 'A sinking fund provision requires the issuer to:', 'Retire part of the principal on a schedule', 'Pay a higher coupon', 'Issue more debt', 'Convert the bond', 'A', 'A sinking fund repays portions of the principal over the bond''s life, reducing credit risk.', 6),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-cash-flows-types-q08', 'cfa-fixed-income-cash-flows-types', 'A callable bond gives the ___ the right to redeem it early.', 'Issuer', 'Bondholder', 'Trustee', 'Exchange', 'A', 'A call option benefits the issuer, who may redeem the bond before maturity.', 7),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-cash-flows-types-q09', 'cfa-fixed-income-cash-flows-types', 'A putable bond gives the ___ the right to sell it back early.', 'Bondholder', 'Issuer', 'Government', 'Rating agency', 'A', 'A put option benefits the investor, who may sell the bond back to the issuer.', 8),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-cash-flows-types-q10', 'cfa-fixed-income-cash-flows-types', 'A convertible bond can be exchanged by the holder into:', 'Common shares of the issuer', 'Another bond only', 'Cash equal to par always', 'A commodity', 'A', 'Convertibles let the holder convert the bond into a set number of common shares.', 9),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-cash-flows-types-q11', 'cfa-fixed-income-cash-flows-types', 'An inflation-linked bond ties its cash flows to:', 'A price index', 'The stock market', 'A commodity future', 'The exchange rate only', 'A', 'Index-linked (inflation-linked) bonds adjust coupon and/or principal to an inflation index.', 10),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-cash-flows-types-q12', 'cfa-fixed-income-cash-flows-types', 'Embedded options such as calls and puts are examples of:', 'Contingency provisions', 'Coupon structures only', 'Amortisation schedules', 'Credit ratings', 'A', 'Callable, putable and convertible features are contingency provisions (embedded options).', 11),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-government-corporate-markets-q01', 'cfa-fixed-income-government-corporate-markets', 'Treasury bills are:', 'Short-term government securities issued at a discount', 'Long-term coupon bonds', 'Corporate equities', 'Perpetual bonds', 'A', 'T-bills mature in one year or less, pay no coupon, and are sold at a discount to par.', 0),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-government-corporate-markets-q02', 'cfa-fixed-income-government-corporate-markets', 'The most recently issued and most liquid government bond of a maturity is called:', 'On-the-run', 'Off-the-run', 'Callable', 'Subordinated', 'A', 'The on-the-run issue is the newest, most actively traded, most liquid security.', 1),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-government-corporate-markets-q03', 'cfa-fixed-income-government-corporate-markets', 'A loan provided by a group of lenders is a:', 'Syndicated loan', 'Commercial paper', 'Treasury note', 'Convertible bond', 'A', 'A syndicated loan spreads a large loan across a group (syndicate) of lenders.', 2),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-government-corporate-markets-q04', 'cfa-fixed-income-government-corporate-markets', 'Commercial paper is:', 'Short-term unsecured corporate debt', 'A long-term secured bond', 'A government bond', 'An equity share', 'A', 'Commercial paper is short-term, typically unsecured funding used by corporations.', 3),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-government-corporate-markets-q05', 'cfa-fixed-income-government-corporate-markets', 'Banks (financial issuers) rely heavily for funding on:', 'Deposits, interbank funding and the repo market', 'Only equity', 'Only government grants', 'Dividends', 'A', 'Financial institutions fund themselves largely through deposits, interbank markets and repos.', 4),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-government-corporate-markets-q06', 'cfa-fixed-income-government-corporate-markets', 'A corporate bond''s yield spread over a government benchmark compensates for:', 'Credit and liquidity risk', 'Reinvestment of coupons', 'Currency risk only', 'Inflation only', 'A', 'The spread rewards investors for the corporate bond''s default (credit) and liquidity risk.', 5),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-government-corporate-markets-q07', 'cfa-fixed-income-government-corporate-markets', 'In the capital structure, which ranks highest in a default?', 'Secured (senior) debt', 'Subordinated debt', 'Common equity', 'Preferred equity', 'A', 'Secured/senior debt is repaid before subordinated debt and equity in liquidation.', 6),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-government-corporate-markets-q08', 'cfa-fixed-income-government-corporate-markets', 'Medium-term notes (MTNs) are:', 'Debt offered continuously to investors through a program', 'Short-term T-bills', 'Equity shares', 'Commodities', 'A', 'MTNs are issued on an ongoing basis under a program, with flexible maturities.', 7),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-government-corporate-markets-q09', 'cfa-fixed-income-government-corporate-markets', 'Older, less liquid government issues are described as:', 'Off-the-run', 'On-the-run', 'Callable', 'Convertible', 'A', 'Off-the-run securities are previously issued bonds that trade less actively than on-the-run issues.', 8),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-government-corporate-markets-q10', 'cfa-fixed-income-government-corporate-markets', 'A repo (repurchase agreement) is used mainly for:', 'Short-term secured financing', 'Long-term equity investment', 'Paying dividends', 'Issuing shares', 'A', 'Repos provide short-term collateralised funding, widely used by financial institutions.', 9),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-government-corporate-markets-q11', 'cfa-fixed-income-government-corporate-markets', 'Bonds issued by a national government in its own currency are:', 'Sovereign bonds', 'Corporate bonds', 'Municipal bonds', 'Agency MBS', 'A', 'Sovereign bonds are issued by national governments and are typically the benchmark for a currency.', 10),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-government-corporate-markets-q12', 'cfa-fixed-income-government-corporate-markets', 'Treasury bonds, versus Treasury notes, generally have:', 'Longer maturities (over 10 years)', 'Shorter maturities', 'No coupon', 'Equity features', 'A', 'Treasury bonds have the longest maturities (typically over 10 years); notes are 2–10 years.', 11),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-curve-empirical-duration-q01', 'cfa-fixed-income-curve-empirical-duration', 'Effective duration measures sensitivity to a:', 'Parallel shift in the benchmark yield curve', 'Change in the coupon', 'Change in the credit rating', 'Change in maturity', 'A', 'Effective (curve-based) duration estimates price change for a parallel curve shift.', 0),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-curve-empirical-duration-q02', 'cfa-fixed-income-curve-empirical-duration', 'Effective duration is required for bonds that:', 'Have embedded options', 'Are option-free', 'Never pay coupons', 'Are equities', 'A', 'Bonds with embedded options have cash flows that change with rates, requiring effective duration.', 1),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-curve-empirical-duration-q03', 'cfa-fixed-income-curve-empirical-duration', 'Key rate durations capture the effect of:', 'Non-parallel (curve-reshaping) yield changes', 'Only parallel shifts', 'Coupon changes', 'Currency moves', 'A', 'Key rate durations measure sensitivity to yield changes at specific maturities, capturing curve reshaping.', 2),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-curve-empirical-duration-q04', 'cfa-fixed-income-curve-empirical-duration', 'Empirical duration is estimated using:', 'Historical price and yield data (regression)', 'A pricing formula only', 'The coupon rate', 'The credit rating', 'A', 'Empirical duration is derived statistically from observed price/yield movements.', 3),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-curve-empirical-duration-q05', 'cfa-fixed-income-curve-empirical-duration', 'Analytical (yield) duration is computed from:', 'A bond pricing formula', 'Historical regression', 'Market sentiment', 'The issuer''s guidance', 'A', 'Analytical duration comes from the mathematical relationship in a pricing model.', 4),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-curve-empirical-duration-q06', 'cfa-fixed-income-curve-empirical-duration', 'Empirical duration is especially useful for:', 'Credit-risky bonds where yields and spreads move together', 'Risk-free zero-coupon bonds only', 'Equities', 'Commodities', 'A', 'It captures the joint movement of benchmark yields and credit spreads, important for credit-risky bonds.', 5),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-curve-empirical-duration-q07', 'cfa-fixed-income-curve-empirical-duration', 'For a high-yield bond in a risk-off episode, empirical duration is often:', 'Lower than its analytical duration', 'Much higher than analytical', 'Exactly equal', 'Negative and large', 'A', 'When government yields fall but spreads widen, the high-yield bond moves less, lowering empirical duration.', 6),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-curve-empirical-duration-q08', 'cfa-fixed-income-curve-empirical-duration', 'Effective duration is calculated using the bond''s prices from:', 'Small upward and downward curve shifts', 'A single price', 'The coupon only', 'The maturity date', 'A', 'Effective duration uses prices under small up and down shifts to estimate curve sensitivity.', 7),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-curve-empirical-duration-q09', 'cfa-fixed-income-curve-empirical-duration', 'A single duration number can miss risk when the yield curve:', 'Reshapes (steepens or flattens)', 'Shifts perfectly in parallel', 'Does not move', 'Is flat', 'A', 'Non-parallel moves affect maturities unevenly, which key rate durations capture but one duration cannot.', 8),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-curve-empirical-duration-q10', 'cfa-fixed-income-curve-empirical-duration', 'Empirical duration reflects the observed relationship between a bond''s price and:', 'Benchmark yield changes', 'Its coupon schedule', 'Its issue size', 'The exchange rate', 'A', 'Empirical duration regresses the bond''s price changes on benchmark yield changes.', 9),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-curve-empirical-duration-q11', 'cfa-fixed-income-curve-empirical-duration', 'Curve-based effective duration is most appropriate for:', 'Mortgage-backed and callable securities', 'Simple option-free bonds only', 'Common stocks', 'Commodities', 'A', 'Securities with prepayment or call features need effective (curve-based) duration.', 10),
  ('cfa-fixed-income-curve-empirical-duration-q12', 'cfa-fixed-income-curve-empirical-duration', 'The advantage of empirical over analytical duration is that it:', 'Reflects real-world co-movement of yields and spreads', 'Requires no data', 'Is always exact', 'Ignores prices', 'A', 'Empirical duration captures how yields and spreads actually move together in the market.', 11),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-active-management-q01', 'cfa-portfolio-management-active-management', 'Active return is defined as:', 'Portfolio return minus benchmark return', 'Portfolio return minus risk-free rate', 'Benchmark return only', 'Total return', 'A', 'Active return (value added) is the portfolio''s return in excess of its benchmark.', 0),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-active-management-q02', 'cfa-portfolio-management-active-management', 'Active risk is also known as:', 'Tracking error', 'Total risk', 'Systematic risk', 'The risk-free rate', 'A', 'Active risk is the standard deviation of active returns — the tracking error.', 1),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-active-management-q03', 'cfa-portfolio-management-active-management', 'The information ratio equals active return divided by:', 'Active risk (tracking error)', 'Total risk', 'Beta', 'The market return', 'A', 'IR = active return ÷ tracking error, measuring risk-adjusted active performance.', 2),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-active-management-q04', 'cfa-portfolio-management-active-management', 'In the fundamental law of active management, IR ≈ IC × √(breadth), IC represents:', 'The information coefficient (forecasting skill)', 'The interest cost', 'The index change', 'The inflation rate', 'A', 'IC (information coefficient) measures the quality/skill of the manager''s forecasts.', 3),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-active-management-q05', 'cfa-portfolio-management-active-management', 'In the fundamental law, ''breadth'' refers to:', 'The number of independent active decisions', 'The portfolio''s total value', 'The benchmark''s size', 'The management fee', 'A', 'Breadth is the number of independent active investment decisions made per period.', 4),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-active-management-q06', 'cfa-portfolio-management-active-management', 'According to the fundamental law, performance improves with:', 'More skill and more independent bets', 'Fewer decisions', 'Higher fees', 'Less information', 'A', 'A higher IR comes from greater skill (IC) and greater breadth of independent decisions.', 5),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-active-management-q07', 'cfa-portfolio-management-active-management', 'A higher information ratio indicates:', 'Better risk-adjusted active performance', 'Higher fees', 'More leverage', 'Lower skill', 'A', 'A higher IR means more value added per unit of active risk taken.', 6),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-active-management-q08', 'cfa-portfolio-management-active-management', 'Active management must overcome its:', 'Higher fees and trading costs versus passive investing', 'Guaranteed losses', 'Zero tracking error', 'Lack of a benchmark', 'A', 'If expected value added doesn''t exceed the higher costs, low-cost passive investing is preferable.', 7),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-active-management-q09', 'cfa-portfolio-management-active-management', 'A portfolio that closely tracks its benchmark has a:', 'Low tracking error', 'High tracking error', 'High active return always', 'Zero beta', 'A', 'Small deviations from the benchmark produce a low tracking error (active risk).', 8),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-active-management-q10', 'cfa-portfolio-management-active-management', 'The information ratio is useful because it is:', 'Largely unaffected by the scale of active positions', 'Dependent only on fees', 'Always equal to the Sharpe ratio', 'Based on total risk', 'A', 'Scaling active positions changes active return and risk proportionally, leaving the IR roughly unchanged.', 9),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-active-management-q11', 'cfa-portfolio-management-active-management', 'Value added by a manager is measured relative to the:', 'Benchmark', 'Risk-free rate', 'Inflation rate', 'Coupon', 'A', 'Active value added is judged against the portfolio''s benchmark, not an absolute standard.', 10),
  ('cfa-portfolio-management-active-management-q12', 'cfa-portfolio-management-active-management', 'If a manager''s forecasting skill (IC) is zero, the expected information ratio is:', 'Zero', 'Very high', 'Negative and large', 'Equal to breadth', 'A', 'With no skill (IC = 0), IR ≈ IC × √breadth = 0 — active management adds no expected value.', 11),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-intercorporate-investments-q01', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-intercorporate-investments', 'An investor with significant influence (typically a 20–50% stake) over an investee accounts for it using the:', 'Fair value method', 'Equity method', 'Consolidation (acquisition) method', 'Cost method with no income recognition', 'B', 'Significant influence (usually 20–50% ownership) triggers the equity method, recognizing a proportionate share of the investee''s earnings.', 0),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-intercorporate-investments-q02', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-intercorporate-investments', 'Under the equity method, dividends received from the associate are:', 'Recognized as income', 'A reduction of the carrying value of the investment', 'Added to goodwill', 'Recorded as a liability', 'B', 'Dividends are treated as a return of investment, reducing the investment''s carrying value rather than being booked as income.', 1),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-intercorporate-investments-q03', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-intercorporate-investments', 'When one company obtains control (>50%) of another, it uses:', 'The equity method', 'Consolidation under the acquisition method', 'Fair value through profit or loss', 'Proportionate consolidation only', 'B', 'Control requires full consolidation using the acquisition method, combining 100% of the subsidiary''s assets and liabilities.', 2),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-intercorporate-investments-q04', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-intercorporate-investments', 'Goodwill arising in a business combination is:', 'Amortized over 40 years', 'Not amortized but tested for impairment', 'Expensed immediately', 'Recorded as a liability', 'B', 'Under both IFRS and US GAAP, goodwill is not amortized; it is carried and tested for impairment.', 3),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-intercorporate-investments-q05', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-intercorporate-investments', 'Goodwill in an acquisition equals the purchase price minus the:', 'Book value of the target''s equity', 'Fair value of identifiable net assets acquired', 'Market capitalization of the acquirer', 'Non-controlling interest', 'B', 'Goodwill is the excess of consideration paid over the fair value of the identifiable net assets acquired.', 4),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-intercorporate-investments-q06', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-intercorporate-investments', 'A non-controlling interest appears on the consolidated balance sheet when the parent owns:', '100% of the subsidiary', 'Less than 100% but a controlling stake', 'Under 20%', 'Exactly 50% with no control', 'B', 'When a parent controls but does not wholly own a subsidiary, the portion not owned is reported as non-controlling (minority) interest.', 5),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-intercorporate-investments-q07', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-intercorporate-investments', 'Equity securities held with no significant influence are generally measured at:', 'Amortized cost', 'Fair value', 'The equity method', 'Historical cost less impairment', 'B', 'Passive equity investments (<20%, no influence) are carried at fair value, with changes in FVPL or FVOCI.', 6),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-intercorporate-investments-q08', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-intercorporate-investments', 'Compared with the equity method, full consolidation generally results in reported revenue and total assets that are:', 'Lower', 'Higher', 'Identical', 'Always negative', 'B', 'Consolidation brings in 100% of the subsidiary''s revenue and assets, so reported totals are higher than the one-line equity method, though net income to the parent is the same.', 7),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-intercorporate-investments-q09', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-intercorporate-investments', 'A joint venture with shared control is typically accounted for under IFRS using the:', 'Equity method', 'Full consolidation', 'Fair value method', 'Cost method', 'A', 'IFRS requires the equity method for joint ventures (shared control), rather than proportionate consolidation in most cases.', 8),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-intercorporate-investments-q10', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-intercorporate-investments', 'In the acquisition method, the acquired company''s identifiable assets and liabilities are recorded at their:', 'Original book values', 'Fair values at the acquisition date', 'Replacement cost', 'Tax basis', 'B', 'The acquisition method records identifiable assets and liabilities at their acquisition-date fair values.', 9),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-intercorporate-investments-q11', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-intercorporate-investments', 'Net income attributable to the parent is generally the same under the equity method and consolidation because:', 'Both recognize the parent''s economic share of the subsidiary''s profit', 'Goodwill is amortized identically', 'Dividends are income in both', 'Revenue is identical in both', 'A', 'Both methods ultimately attribute the same economic share of subsidiary earnings to the parent; only the gross presentation differs.', 10),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-intercorporate-investments-q12', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-intercorporate-investments', 'Classifying a debt investment as ''held to maturity'' (amortized cost) rather than fair value requires:', 'Intent and ability to hold until maturity', 'Ownership above 50%', 'Significant influence', 'Shared control', 'A', 'Amortized-cost (held-to-maturity) treatment requires both the intent and the ability to hold the debt security to maturity.', 11),
  ('cfa2-equity-fcf-valuation-q01', 'cfa2-equity-fcf-valuation', 'Free cash flow to the firm (FCFF) represents cash available to:', 'Common shareholders only', 'All capital providers (debt and equity)', 'Preferred shareholders only', 'The government', 'B', 'FCFF is the cash flow available to all suppliers of capital after operating expenses and investments, before financing payments.', 0),
  ('cfa2-equity-fcf-valuation-q02', 'cfa2-equity-fcf-valuation', 'FCFF should be discounted at the:', 'Cost of equity', 'Weighted average cost of capital (WACC)', 'Risk-free rate', 'Cost of debt', 'B', 'Because FCFF accrues to all capital providers, it is discounted at the WACC to obtain firm value.', 1),
  ('cfa2-equity-fcf-valuation-q03', 'cfa2-equity-fcf-valuation', 'FCFE is most appropriately discounted at the:', 'WACC', 'Cost of equity', 'After-tax cost of debt', 'Government bond yield', 'B', 'FCFE belongs to equity holders, so it is discounted at the required return on equity to value equity directly.', 2),
  ('cfa2-equity-fcf-valuation-q04', 'cfa2-equity-fcf-valuation', 'Starting from net income, FCFF adds back:', 'Dividends paid', 'Non-cash charges and after-tax interest expense', 'Net borrowing', 'Share repurchases', 'B', 'FCFF = NI + non-cash charges + Int(1−t) − FCInv − WCInv; after-tax interest is added back because FCFF is pre-financing.', 3),
  ('cfa2-equity-fcf-valuation-q05', 'cfa2-equity-fcf-valuation', 'The relationship between FCFF and FCFE is FCFE =', 'FCFF + Int(1−t) − Net borrowing', 'FCFF − Int(1−t) + Net borrowing', 'FCFF + FCInv', 'FCFF − dividends', 'B', 'FCFE = FCFF − after-tax interest + net borrowing, reflecting cash flows to and from debt holders.', 4),
  ('cfa2-equity-fcf-valuation-q06', 'cfa2-equity-fcf-valuation', 'To move from firm value (from FCFF) to equity value, an analyst:', 'Adds the market value of debt', 'Subtracts the market value of debt', 'Adds back interest', 'Subtracts dividends', 'B', 'Equity value = firm value − market value of debt (and other non-equity claims).', 5),
  ('cfa2-equity-fcf-valuation-q07', 'cfa2-equity-fcf-valuation', 'FCFE is often preferred over a dividend discount model when:', 'Dividends differ significantly from the firm''s capacity to pay', 'The firm never invests', 'There is no debt', 'Earnings are negative forever', 'A', 'FCFE values the capacity to distribute cash, which is more meaningful than actual dividends when payout policy diverges from cash generation.', 6),
  ('cfa2-equity-fcf-valuation-q08', 'cfa2-equity-fcf-valuation', 'Using the single-stage model, firm value equals FCFF₁ divided by:', '(WACC − g)', '(r − g)', 'WACC', '(g − WACC)', 'A', 'The constant-growth FCFF model gives firm value = FCFF₁ / (WACC − g).', 7),
  ('cfa2-equity-fcf-valuation-q09', 'cfa2-equity-fcf-valuation', 'An increase in working capital investment (WCInv), all else equal, will:', 'Increase FCFF', 'Decrease FCFF', 'Not affect FCFF', 'Increase net borrowing', 'B', 'Investment in working capital is a use of cash, so a higher WCInv reduces FCFF (and FCFE).', 8),
  ('cfa2-equity-fcf-valuation-q10', 'cfa2-equity-fcf-valuation', 'FCFF is generally preferred to FCFE when a company:', 'Has a stable capital structure', 'Has volatile or high leverage that can make FCFE negative', 'Pays no taxes', 'Has no fixed assets', 'B', 'With changing or high leverage, FCFE can be volatile or negative; FCFF (discounted at WACC) is more stable in such cases.', 9),
  ('cfa2-equity-fcf-valuation-q11', 'cfa2-equity-fcf-valuation', 'Net borrowing (new debt minus repayments) is added when computing:', 'FCFF from net income', 'FCFE', 'WACC', 'EBIT', 'B', 'FCFE includes net borrowing because new debt provides cash to equity holders and repayments use it.', 10),
  ('cfa2-equity-fcf-valuation-q12', 'cfa2-equity-fcf-valuation', 'Non-cash charges such as depreciation are added back in FCF calculations because they:', 'Are cash outflows', 'Reduced net income without using cash', 'Represent new investment', 'Are financing flows', 'B', 'Depreciation and similar non-cash charges lowered accounting income but did not consume cash, so they are added back.', 11),
  ('cfa2-equity-residual-income-q01', 'cfa2-equity-residual-income', 'Residual income equals net income minus a charge for:', 'The cost of debt', 'Equity capital (required return × beginning book value)', 'Taxes', 'Depreciation', 'B', 'Residual income is the profit remaining after charging for the cost of equity capital employed.', 0),
  ('cfa2-equity-residual-income-q02', 'cfa2-equity-residual-income', 'The residual income model expresses intrinsic value as:', 'Only the present value of future dividends', 'Current book value plus the present value of future residual income', 'Book value minus debt', 'The sum of all future sales', 'B', 'Value = current book value + PV of expected future residual income.', 1),
  ('cfa2-equity-residual-income-q03', 'cfa2-equity-residual-income', 'A firm creates value above book value only when:', 'ROE exceeds the required return on equity', 'ROE equals the required return', 'ROE is below the required return', 'Growth exceeds the required return', 'A', 'Positive residual income (value above book) requires ROE > r, the cost of equity.', 2),
  ('cfa2-equity-residual-income-q04', 'cfa2-equity-residual-income', 'Relative to the dividend discount model, the residual income model typically:', 'Relies more heavily on the terminal value', 'Recognizes value earlier and depends less on the terminal value', 'Cannot value non-dividend payers', 'Ignores book value', 'B', 'RI front-loads value into current book value and near-term RI, reducing dependence on uncertain terminal values.', 3),
  ('cfa2-equity-residual-income-q05', 'cfa2-equity-residual-income', 'The residual income model assumes ____ accounting, where all changes in equity except transactions with owners flow through income.', 'Cash-basis', 'Clean-surplus', 'Tax-basis', 'Fair-value-only', 'B', 'RI relies on clean-surplus accounting; violations (e.g., certain OCI items) distort the model.', 4),
  ('cfa2-equity-residual-income-q06', 'cfa2-equity-residual-income', 'A firm with beginning book value $40, ROE of 15%, required return 10%, and growth 4% has an intrinsic value (single-stage) closest to:', '$40', '$73', '$60', '$33', 'B', 'V = 40 + [(0.15 − 0.10)/(0.10 − 0.04)]×40 = 40 + (0.05/0.06)×40 ≈ 40 + 33.3 = $73.3.', 5),
  ('cfa2-equity-residual-income-q07', 'cfa2-equity-residual-income', 'A persistence factor (ω) in a residual income model controls:', 'The tax rate', 'How quickly abnormal residual income decays toward zero', 'The risk-free rate', 'Dividend payout', 'B', 'The persistence factor models the fade of residual income as competition erodes abnormal returns.', 6),
  ('cfa2-equity-residual-income-q08', 'cfa2-equity-residual-income', 'A weakness of the residual income model is its sensitivity to:', 'Dividend policy', 'Book value and accounting quality', 'The number of shares outstanding', 'Stock liquidity', 'B', 'Because it builds on book value and reported earnings, RI is sensitive to accounting choices and quality.', 7),
  ('cfa2-equity-residual-income-q09', 'cfa2-equity-residual-income', 'Residual income valuation is especially useful for firms that:', 'Pay large stable dividends', 'Do not pay dividends or have negative near-term free cash flow', 'Have no book value', 'Are risk-free', 'B', 'RI handles non-dividend payers and negative-FCF firms better than DDM/FCF models.', 8),
  ('cfa2-equity-residual-income-q10', 'cfa2-equity-residual-income', 'If a company''s ROE exactly equals its cost of equity, its intrinsic value under the RI model equals:', 'Zero', 'Book value', 'Twice book value', 'The terminal value', 'B', 'With ROE = r, residual income is zero and intrinsic value equals current book value.', 9),
  ('cfa2-equity-residual-income-q11', 'cfa2-equity-residual-income', 'The equity charge in a residual income calculation is computed using:', 'Ending book value × WACC', 'Beginning book value of equity × required return on equity', 'Net income × tax rate', 'Market cap × beta', 'B', 'The equity charge = beginning book value of equity multiplied by the required return on equity.', 10),
  ('cfa2-equity-residual-income-q12', 'cfa2-equity-residual-income', 'Continuing (terminal) residual income that persists indefinitely at a constant level would be valued as a:', 'Growing annuity at the growth rate', 'Perpetuity discounted at the required return', 'Zero', 'Lump sum at book value', 'B', 'Constant perpetual residual income is valued as a perpetuity (RI / r) in the continuing-value term.', 11),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-arbitrage-free-oas-q01', 'cfa2-fixed-income-arbitrage-free-oas', 'Arbitrage-free valuation of a bond discounts each cash flow at the:', 'Single yield to maturity', 'Maturity-matched spot rate', 'Coupon rate', 'Forward rate only', 'B', 'Each cash flow is discounted at its corresponding spot rate so that stripping and reconstitution offer no arbitrage.', 0),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-arbitrage-free-oas-q02', 'cfa2-fixed-income-arbitrage-free-oas', 'The value of a callable bond equals the value of an otherwise identical straight bond:', 'Plus the value of the call option', 'Minus the value of the call option', 'Plus the value of a put option', 'Unchanged', 'B', 'The issuer''s call option has value to the issuer, so Value_callable = Value_straight − Value_call.', 1),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-arbitrage-free-oas-q03', 'cfa2-fixed-income-arbitrage-free-oas', 'The value of a putable bond equals the straight-bond value:', 'Minus the value of the put', 'Plus the value of the put', 'Minus the value of a call', 'Times the put delta', 'B', 'The investor''s put option adds value, so Value_putable = Value_straight + Value_put.', 2),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-arbitrage-free-oas-q04', 'cfa2-fixed-income-arbitrage-free-oas', 'Increasing interest-rate volatility will, all else equal, cause the price of a callable bond to:', 'Increase', 'Decrease', 'Stay the same', 'Become negative', 'B', 'Higher volatility raises the call option''s value; since it is subtracted, the callable bond''s price falls.', 3),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-arbitrage-free-oas-q05', 'cfa2-fixed-income-arbitrage-free-oas', 'Increasing interest-rate volatility will cause the price of a putable bond to:', 'Increase', 'Decrease', 'Stay the same', 'Always equal par', 'A', 'Higher volatility raises the put''s value, which is added to the straight-bond value, increasing the putable bond''s price.', 4),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-arbitrage-free-oas-q06', 'cfa2-fixed-income-arbitrage-free-oas', 'The option-adjusted spread (OAS) is best described as the Z-spread:', 'Plus the option cost', 'After removing the effect of the embedded option', 'Computed at a single discount rate', 'For a risk-free bond', 'B', 'OAS strips out the embedded option''s effect, leaving a spread comparable across bonds with different optionality.', 5),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-arbitrage-free-oas-q07', 'cfa2-fixed-income-arbitrage-free-oas', 'For a callable bond, the OAS is generally:', 'Greater than the Z-spread', 'Less than the Z-spread', 'Equal to the Z-spread', 'Negative', 'B', 'The call option''s cost is subtracted from the Z-spread, so OAS < Z-spread for callables.', 6),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-arbitrage-free-oas-q08', 'cfa2-fixed-income-arbitrage-free-oas', 'Bonds with embedded options are best valued using:', 'A single discount rate', 'A calibrated, arbitrage-free interest-rate tree with backward induction', 'Only the current spot rate', 'The dividend discount model', 'B', 'An arbitrage-free binomial interest-rate tree lets the option be exercised optimally at each node via backward induction.', 7),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-arbitrage-free-oas-q09', 'cfa2-fixed-income-arbitrage-free-oas', 'The Z-spread differs from the nominal spread in that it is measured relative to the:', 'Single benchmark YTM', 'Entire spot-rate (zero) curve', 'Coupon rate', 'Forward rate', 'B', 'The Z-spread is the constant spread added to every spot rate on the benchmark curve to price the bond.', 8),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-arbitrage-free-oas-q10', 'cfa2-fixed-income-arbitrage-free-oas', 'If two callable bonds have the same Z-spread but different option costs, the one with the higher option cost has the:', 'Higher OAS', 'Lower OAS', 'Same OAS', 'Higher coupon', 'B', 'OAS = Z-spread − option cost, so a larger option cost yields a lower OAS at the same Z-spread.', 9),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-arbitrage-free-oas-q11', 'cfa2-fixed-income-arbitrage-free-oas', 'An OAS that is higher than that of comparable bonds suggests the bond is, all else equal:', 'Overvalued', 'Undervalued (cheap)', 'Fairly valued', 'Risk-free', 'B', 'A wider OAS means more compensation for the same option-adjusted risk, signaling the bond may be relatively cheap.', 10),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-arbitrage-free-oas-q12', 'cfa2-fixed-income-arbitrage-free-oas', 'Reconstitution arbitrage is prevented when a bond is priced using:', 'Its yield to maturity', 'Spot rates for each cash flow (arbitrage-free value)', 'The coupon rate', 'A flat discount rate', 'B', 'Pricing each cash flow at the appropriate spot rate equates the bond to its stripped components, eliminating reconstitution arbitrage.', 11),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-option-valuation-q01', 'cfa2-derivatives-option-valuation', 'In risk-neutral option valuation, the option price does NOT depend on:', 'The risk-free rate', 'Investors'' real-world probabilities of up/down moves', 'The strike price', 'The volatility of the underlying', 'B', 'Once the option is replicated/hedged, only risk-neutral probabilities matter; real-world probabilities are irrelevant.', 0),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-option-valuation-q02', 'cfa2-derivatives-option-valuation', 'The Black-Scholes-Merton model assumes the underlying price is:', 'Normally distributed', 'Lognormally distributed', 'Uniformly distributed', 'Constant', 'B', 'BSM assumes lognormal prices (normally distributed continuously compounded returns), constant volatility, and a constant risk-free rate.', 1),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-option-valuation-q03', 'cfa2-derivatives-option-valuation', 'Delta measures an option''s sensitivity to a change in:', 'Volatility', 'The price of the underlying', 'Time', 'Interest rates', 'B', 'Delta is the rate of change of the option price with respect to the underlying''s price (0→1 for calls).', 2),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-option-valuation-q04', 'cfa2-derivatives-option-valuation', 'Gamma is largest for options that are:', 'Deep in the money', 'At the money', 'Deep out of the money', 'Already expired', 'B', 'Gamma (the rate of change of delta) peaks for at-the-money options near expiration.', 3),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-option-valuation-q05', 'cfa2-derivatives-option-valuation', 'Vega measures sensitivity to changes in:', 'The underlying price', 'Implied volatility', 'Time to expiry', 'The risk-free rate', 'B', 'Vega captures how the option value changes with volatility; it is largest for at-the-money, longer-dated options.', 4),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-option-valuation-q06', 'cfa2-derivatives-option-valuation', 'In the one-period binomial model, the risk-neutral probability of an up move is:', '(1 + r − d)/(u − d)', '(u − d)/(1 + r)', '(u − 1 − r)/(u − d)', '(1 + r)/(u·d)', 'A', 'π = (1 + r − d)/(u − d) is the risk-neutral up-probability.', 5),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-option-valuation-q07', 'cfa2-derivatives-option-valuation', 'Theta generally reflects an option''s:', 'Gain from volatility', 'Loss of value as time passes (time decay)', 'Sensitivity to interest rates', 'Delta hedge ratio', 'B', 'Theta measures time decay—the erosion of an option''s value as expiration approaches, all else equal.', 6),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-option-valuation-q08', 'cfa2-derivatives-option-valuation', 'A delta-neutral hedge must be rebalanced because of:', 'Rho', 'Gamma (delta changes as the underlying moves)', 'Theta', 'The strike price', 'B', 'Gamma means delta itself changes with the underlying, so a delta hedge drifts and requires rebalancing.', 7),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-option-valuation-q09', 'cfa2-derivatives-option-valuation', 'In the BSM call formula c = S·N(d₁) − Ke^(−rT)·N(d₂), N(d₂) is interpreted as the:', 'Option''s delta', 'Risk-neutral probability the call expires in the money', 'Gamma of the option', 'Time value', 'B', 'N(d₂) is the risk-neutral probability that the option is exercised (finishes in the money); N(d₁) relates to delta.', 8),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-option-valuation-q10', 'cfa2-derivatives-option-valuation', 'The only input to the BSM model that is not directly observable is:', 'The spot price', 'Volatility', 'The strike price', 'Time to expiry', 'B', 'Spot, strike, time, and rate are observable; volatility must be estimated (or implied from market prices).', 9),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-option-valuation-q11', 'cfa2-derivatives-option-valuation', 'Increasing the volatility input in an option model raises the value of:', 'Calls only', 'Both calls and puts', 'Puts only', 'Neither', 'B', 'Greater volatility increases the value of both calls and puts because of their asymmetric (limited-loss) payoffs.', 10),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-option-valuation-q12', 'cfa2-derivatives-option-valuation', 'As the number of steps in a binomial tree increases toward infinity, its option value converges to the:', 'Intrinsic value', 'Black-Scholes-Merton value', 'Forward price', 'Strike price', 'B', 'A finely subdivided binomial tree converges to the continuous-time BSM value.', 11),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-multiple-regression-q01', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-multiple-regression', 'A slope coefficient in a multiple regression measures the effect of its variable on Y while:', 'Ignoring all other variables', 'Holding the other independent variables constant', 'Maximizing R-squared', 'Setting the intercept to zero', 'B', 'Each partial slope is the marginal effect of that regressor with the other independent variables held constant.', 0),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-multiple-regression-q02', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-multiple-regression', 'Adjusted R-squared is preferred to R-squared because it:', 'Always increases with more regressors', 'Penalizes the addition of variables that don''t improve the model', 'Measures serial correlation', 'Equals the F-statistic', 'B', 'Adjusted R² adds a penalty for extra regressors, so it can fall when an added variable adds little explanatory power.', 1),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-multiple-regression-q03', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-multiple-regression', 'The F-test in a multiple regression evaluates the null hypothesis that:', 'The intercept is zero', 'All slope coefficients jointly equal zero', 'Residuals are normal', 'There is multicollinearity', 'B', 'The F-test assesses whether the regressors jointly explain a significant portion of the variation in Y.', 2),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-multiple-regression-q04', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-multiple-regression', 'Heteroskedasticity primarily affects a regression by:', 'Biasing the coefficient estimates', 'Biasing the standard errors, distorting t-tests', 'Increasing R-squared', 'Removing serial correlation', 'B', 'Heteroskedasticity leaves coefficients unbiased but makes standard errors (and thus t-statistics) unreliable.', 3),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-multiple-regression-q05', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-multiple-regression', 'A classic symptom of multicollinearity is:', 'A low R-squared with significant t-statistics', 'A high R-squared with insignificant individual t-statistics', 'A Durbin-Watson statistic near 2', 'Zero correlation among regressors', 'B', 'Multicollinearity inflates standard errors so individual coefficients look insignificant even though the model overall (high R²/F) is significant.', 4),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-multiple-regression-q06', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-multiple-regression', 'The Durbin-Watson statistic is used to detect:', 'Heteroskedasticity', 'Serial (auto)correlation of residuals', 'Multicollinearity', 'Omitted variables', 'B', 'The Durbin-Watson statistic tests for first-order serial correlation in the regression residuals.', 5),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-multiple-regression-q07', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-multiple-regression', 'Omitting a relevant explanatory variable that is correlated with included regressors causes:', 'Unbiased coefficients', 'Biased coefficient estimates', 'Only a lower R-squared with no bias', 'Perfect multicollinearity', 'B', 'Omitted-variable bias arises when a relevant, correlated variable is left out, biasing the estimated coefficients.', 6),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-multiple-regression-q08', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-multiple-regression', 'A common correction for serial correlation in standard errors is:', 'White (robust) standard errors', 'Newey-West standard errors', 'Dropping the intercept', 'Adding more regressors', 'B', 'Newey-West (HAC) standard errors correct for both serial correlation and heteroskedasticity.', 7),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-multiple-regression-q09', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-multiple-regression', 'A high variance inflation factor (VIF) for a regressor indicates:', 'Strong multicollinearity with other regressors', 'Heteroskedasticity', 'A good model fit', 'Serial correlation', 'A', 'VIF measures how much a coefficient''s variance is inflated by correlation with other regressors; high VIF signals multicollinearity.', 8),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-multiple-regression-q10', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-multiple-regression', 'Building a model by searching many variables until statistical significance appears risks:', 'Heteroskedasticity', 'Data mining and overfitting', 'A unit root', 'Lower R-squared', 'B', 'Data mining finds chance relationships that fail out of sample; models should rest on economic reasoning.', 9),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-multiple-regression-q11', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-multiple-regression', 'Robust (White) standard errors specifically correct for:', 'Serial correlation only', 'Heteroskedasticity', 'Omitted variables', 'Multicollinearity', 'B', 'White''s heteroskedasticity-consistent standard errors give valid inference when error variance is non-constant.', 10),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-multiple-regression-q12', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-multiple-regression', 'Using a logarithmic transformation of a variable is one way to address:', 'An incorrect functional-form misspecification', 'Perfect collinearity', 'A small sample only', 'The intercept term', 'A', 'Transformations (e.g., logs) can correct functional-form misspecification when relationships are non-linear in levels.', 11),
  ('cfa2-economics-currency-forecasting-q01', 'cfa2-economics-currency-forecasting', 'Which parity relationship is enforced by arbitrage and therefore reliably holds?', 'Uncovered interest rate parity', 'Covered interest rate parity', 'Purchasing power parity', 'The international Fisher effect', 'B', 'Covered interest rate parity is enforced by riskless arbitrage with forwards; the others are equilibrium tendencies.', 0),
  ('cfa2-economics-currency-forecasting-q02', 'cfa2-economics-currency-forecasting', 'A carry trade profits when:', 'Uncovered interest rate parity holds exactly', 'High-yield currencies fail to depreciate as UIRP predicts', 'Covered interest parity is violated', 'Inflation is identical across countries', 'B', 'Carry trades earn the rate differential when high-yield currencies don''t depreciate enough to offset it, i.e., UIRP fails.', 1),
  ('cfa2-economics-currency-forecasting-q03', 'cfa2-economics-currency-forecasting', 'Under the balance-of-payments (flow) approach, a country with persistent large current-account deficits tends to face:', 'Upward pressure on its currency', 'Downward pressure on its currency', 'No effect on its currency', 'Guaranteed appreciation', 'B', 'Persistent current-account deficits imply net selling of the currency, creating downward pressure—though capital flows can offset this.', 2),
  ('cfa2-economics-currency-forecasting-q04', 'cfa2-economics-currency-forecasting', 'A key risk of the carry trade is that returns exhibit:', 'Symmetric, normally distributed payoffs', 'Negative skew — occasional large crashes', 'Zero volatility', 'Guaranteed positive returns', 'B', 'Carry-trade returns are negatively skewed: steady small gains punctuated by sudden, sharp losses when high-yield currencies crash.', 3),
  ('cfa2-economics-currency-forecasting-q05', 'cfa2-economics-currency-forecasting', 'Relative purchasing power parity predicts that the currency of a higher-inflation country will:', 'Appreciate by the inflation differential', 'Depreciate by the inflation differential', 'Stay constant', 'Track the interest differential', 'B', 'Under relative PPP, the higher-inflation currency depreciates by roughly the inflation differential.', 4),
  ('cfa2-economics-currency-forecasting-q06', 'cfa2-economics-currency-forecasting', 'Monetary models of exchange rates emphasize the role of:', 'Replacement cost of assets', 'Relative money supply growth and inflation', 'Dividend yields', 'Beta', 'B', 'Monetary approaches link long-run currency value to relative money-supply growth and inflation between countries.', 5),
  ('cfa2-economics-currency-forecasting-q07', 'cfa2-economics-currency-forecasting', 'The portfolio-balance/asset-market approach to FX focuses on:', 'Trade flows only', 'Capital flows driven by relative returns and risk premia', 'Inflation only', 'Tariffs', 'B', 'The asset-market view holds that capital flows responding to relative expected returns and risk dominate exchange-rate movements, especially short-term.', 6),
  ('cfa2-economics-currency-forecasting-q08', 'cfa2-economics-currency-forecasting', 'The effectiveness of central-bank FX intervention depends importantly on its:', 'Reserves and credibility', 'Dividend policy', 'Equity market cap', 'Corporate tax rate', 'A', 'Intervention works best when the central bank has ample reserves and market credibility; otherwise it has limited lasting effect.', 7),
  ('cfa2-economics-currency-forecasting-q09', 'cfa2-economics-currency-forecasting', 'A long forward position on the base currency gains value when the:', 'Spot/forward rate moves against the position', 'Spot/forward rate moves in the position''s favor', 'Risk-free rate is zero', 'Position is closed early at a loss', 'B', 'The forward''s mark-to-market value rises when the prevailing forward rate moves favorably relative to the contracted rate.', 8),
  ('cfa2-economics-currency-forecasting-q10', 'cfa2-economics-currency-forecasting', 'Capital controls are most likely to:', 'Permanently fix the real exchange rate', 'Affect exchange rates and flows in the short term', 'Eliminate inflation', 'Guarantee appreciation', 'B', 'Capital controls can influence flows and rates temporarily but rarely override fundamentals over the long run.', 9),
  ('cfa2-economics-currency-forecasting-q11', 'cfa2-economics-currency-forecasting', 'If covered interest rate parity is violated, traders can profit via:', 'Covered interest arbitrage', 'A buy-and-hold equity strategy', 'Pure speculation on inflation', 'Dividend capture', 'A', 'A CIRP violation enables riskless covered interest arbitrage, which trades quickly restore parity.', 10),
  ('cfa2-economics-currency-forecasting-q12', 'cfa2-economics-currency-forecasting', 'Combining relative PPP with uncovered interest rate parity implies the expected real exchange rate is:', 'Rising', 'Constant', 'Falling', 'Equal to the nominal rate', 'B', 'When both hold, nominal rate changes offset inflation differentials, leaving the expected real exchange rate constant.', 11),
  ('cfa2-equity-price-multiples-q01', 'cfa2-equity-price-multiples', 'A leading (forward) P/E ratio uses:', 'Trailing 12-month earnings', 'Expected next-period earnings', 'Book value', 'Sales', 'B', 'The forward P/E divides price by expected (forecast) earnings, whereas trailing P/E uses the last 12 months'' EPS.', 0),
  ('cfa2-equity-price-multiples-q02', 'cfa2-equity-price-multiples', 'The justified leading P/E based on the Gordon model equals:', '(1 − payout)/(r − g)', 'payout/(r − g)', '(r − g)/payout', 'g/(r − g)', 'B', 'Dividing the Gordon model by E₁ gives justified leading P/E = payout ratio / (r − g).', 1),
  ('cfa2-equity-price-multiples-q03', 'cfa2-equity-price-multiples', 'Price-to-book is especially useful for valuing:', 'Early-stage tech startups', 'Banks and other asset-intensive financials', 'Firms with no assets', 'Pre-revenue biotech', 'B', 'P/B suits firms whose value rests on marked-to-market assets (e.g., banks) and works even when earnings are negative.', 2),
  ('cfa2-equity-price-multiples-q04', 'cfa2-equity-price-multiples', 'A key advantage of price-to-sales over P/E is that sales are:', 'Always larger than earnings', 'Less easily distorted by accounting choices and usable for unprofitable firms', 'Unaffected by the business cycle', 'Equal to cash flow', 'B', 'Revenue is harder to manipulate than earnings and is positive even for loss-making firms, making P/S broadly applicable.', 3),
  ('cfa2-equity-price-multiples-q05', 'cfa2-equity-price-multiples', 'Enterprise value (EV) is calculated as market cap plus debt, preferred and minority interest, minus:', 'Inventory', 'Cash and cash equivalents', 'Goodwill', 'Retained earnings', 'B', 'EV = equity value + debt + preferred + minority interest − cash, reflecting the cost to acquire the operating business.', 4),
  ('cfa2-equity-price-multiples-q06', 'cfa2-equity-price-multiples', 'EV/EBITDA is often preferred to P/E when comparing firms with different:', 'Capital structures (leverage)', 'Tickers', 'Fiscal year-ends', 'Dividend dates', 'A', 'Because EV and EBITDA are pre-financing, EV/EBITDA is capital-structure neutral, aiding comparison across different leverage.', 5),
  ('cfa2-equity-price-multiples-q07', 'cfa2-equity-price-multiples', 'The ''method of comparables'' values a stock by:', 'Discounting its dividends', 'Comparing its multiple to those of similar companies', 'Estimating replacement cost', 'Using the risk-free rate', 'B', 'Comparables benchmark a firm''s multiple against peers/industry to judge relative cheapness or richness.', 6),
  ('cfa2-equity-price-multiples-q08', 'cfa2-equity-price-multiples', 'A ''justified'' multiple differs from a comparables multiple in that it is:', 'Derived from fundamentals via a discounted-cash-flow model', 'Always lower', 'Based only on peers', 'Independent of growth and risk', 'A', 'A justified multiple is what the multiple should be given the firm''s required return and growth, derived from a valuation model.', 7),
  ('cfa2-equity-price-multiples-q09', 'cfa2-equity-price-multiples', 'Trailing P/E can be misleading for a cyclical firm because earnings:', 'Are constant over the cycle', 'Peak and trough with the cycle, distorting the ratio (Molodovsky effect)', 'Equal sales', 'Are always negative', 'B', 'Cyclical earnings swing widely, so P/E can look low at the peak and high at the trough—normalized earnings help (the Molodovsky effect).', 8),
  ('cfa2-equity-price-multiples-q10', 'cfa2-equity-price-multiples', 'Price-to-cash-flow is sometimes favored over P/E because cash flow is:', 'Always larger', 'Harder to manipulate than reported earnings', 'Unrelated to operations', 'Equal to dividends', 'B', 'Cash flow measures are less susceptible to accrual-based manipulation than net earnings.', 9),
  ('cfa2-equity-price-multiples-q11', 'cfa2-equity-price-multiples', 'When normalizing EPS for a price multiple, an analyst removes:', 'All revenue', 'Transitory, non-recurring items', 'The dividend', 'The risk-free rate', 'B', 'Normalizing strips out one-off, non-recurring items to reflect sustainable earnings power.', 10),
  ('cfa2-equity-price-multiples-q12', 'cfa2-equity-price-multiples', 'A stock trading at a lower P/E than peers with similar fundamentals is, all else equal, potentially:', 'Overvalued', 'Undervalued', 'Risk-free', 'Fairly valued by definition', 'B', 'A lower multiple than comparable peers with similar growth/risk suggests the stock may be relatively undervalued.', 11),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-pe-real-estate-q01', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-pe-real-estate', 'Leveraged buyouts typically target companies that are:', 'Early-stage with no revenue', 'Mature with stable cash flows', 'Pre-product startups', 'Government agencies', 'B', 'Buyouts apply leverage to mature firms with steady cash flows able to service the acquisition debt.', 0),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-pe-real-estate-q02', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-pe-real-estate', 'The private-equity ''J-curve'' describes:', 'Early negative returns (fees/markdowns) before later gains from exits', 'Returns that always decline', 'A risk-free payoff', 'Linear constant returns', 'A', 'The J-curve reflects early negative net returns from fees and conservative marks, with positive returns emerging as investments are exited.', 1),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-pe-real-estate-q03', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-pe-real-estate', 'In a ''2 and 20'' fee structure, the ''20'' refers to:', 'A 20% management fee', 'Carried interest of 20% of profits', 'A 20-year lockup', '20 portfolio companies', 'B', 'The ''20'' is carried interest—the GP''s 20% share of profits—on top of the 2% management fee.', 2),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-pe-real-estate-q04', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-pe-real-estate', 'A clawback provision protects limited partners by:', 'Increasing management fees', 'Requiring the GP to return excess carried interest if later losses occur', 'Guaranteeing returns', 'Eliminating the hurdle rate', 'B', 'A clawback forces the GP to repay carried interest taken early if subsequent results mean the LPs were overcharged.', 3),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-pe-real-estate-q05', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-pe-real-estate', 'Which is a common exit route for a private-equity investment?', 'A coupon payment', 'An initial public offering (IPO)', 'A stock split', 'A dividend reinvestment plan', 'B', 'PE exits include trade sales, secondary sales, IPOs, and recapitalizations; an IPO is a classic route.', 4),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-pe-real-estate-q06', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-pe-real-estate', 'In the income approach to real estate, value equals net operating income divided by the:', 'Coupon rate', 'Capitalization (cap) rate', 'Beta', 'Inflation rate', 'B', 'The direct capitalization method values property as NOI / cap rate.', 5),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-pe-real-estate-q07', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-pe-real-estate', 'A lower capitalization rate, all else equal, implies a property value that is:', 'Lower', 'Higher', 'Unchanged', 'Negative', 'B', 'Since value = NOI / cap rate, a lower cap rate produces a higher value (and a lower income yield).', 6),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-pe-real-estate-q08', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-pe-real-estate', 'The four quadrants of real estate investing are private/public crossed with:', 'Equity and debt', 'Large-cap and small-cap', 'Domestic and foreign', 'Growth and value', 'A', 'Real estate spans private equity (direct), public equity (REITs), private debt (mortgages), and public debt (MBS).', 7),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-pe-real-estate-q09', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-pe-real-estate', 'REITs are commonly valued using price-to-FFO, where FFO adjusts net income by adding back:', 'Interest expense', 'Real-estate depreciation (and removing property sale gains)', 'Dividends', 'Taxes', 'B', 'Funds from operations adds back non-cash real-estate depreciation and excludes gains/losses on property sales to better reflect cash earnings.', 8),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-pe-real-estate-q10', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-pe-real-estate', 'The cap rate is approximately equal to the discount rate minus the:', 'Inflation rate', 'Growth rate of NOI', 'Risk-free rate', 'Vacancy rate', 'B', 'Analogous to the Gordon model, cap rate ≈ required return − expected growth in NOI.', 9),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-pe-real-estate-q11', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-pe-real-estate', 'Venture capital differs from buyout investing primarily in that VC funds:', 'Use heavy leverage on mature firms', 'Finance early-stage, high-growth companies with little leverage', 'Invest only in government bonds', 'Avoid equity entirely', 'B', 'VC targets young, high-growth companies using mostly equity, whereas buyouts use significant debt on established firms.', 10),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-pe-real-estate-q12', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-pe-real-estate', 'DPI (distributions to paid-in capital) measures a private fund''s:', 'Unrealized value only', 'Realized cash returned to investors relative to capital contributed', 'Management fee', 'Leverage ratio', 'B', 'DPI is the ratio of cumulative distributions to paid-in capital—the realized, cash-on-cash return to LPs.', 11),
  ('cfa2-ethics-application-soft-dollar-q01', 'cfa2-ethics-application-soft-dollar', 'Client brokerage (''soft dollars'') should be used to purchase:', 'Office rent and general overhead', 'Research that benefits the client''s investment decision-making', 'The manager''s personal travel', 'Marketing for the firm', 'B', 'Client brokerage belongs to the client and must buy research that aids the investment process, not the manager''s overhead.', 0),
  ('cfa2-ethics-application-soft-dollar-q02', 'cfa2-ethics-application-soft-dollar', 'Tipping a favored client about a recommendation change before others most directly violates:', 'Fair Dealing [III(B)]', 'Independence and Objectivity', 'Record Retention', 'Knowledge of the Law', 'A', 'Standard III(B) Fair Dealing requires recommendations and changes be disseminated fairly to all clients.', 1),
  ('cfa2-ethics-application-soft-dollar-q03', 'cfa2-ethics-application-soft-dollar', 'Under the duty of Loyalty, Prudence and Care [III(A)], a manager of a pension plan must act in the interest of the:', 'Plan sponsor''s management', 'Plan beneficiaries', 'Brokerage firm', 'Manager''s employer', 'B', 'The manager''s loyalty runs to the ultimate beneficiaries of the plan, not the sponsor''s executives.', 2),
  ('cfa2-ethics-application-soft-dollar-q04', 'cfa2-ethics-application-soft-dollar', 'Best execution may NOT be sacrificed in order to:', 'Reduce the client''s taxes', 'Direct soft-dollar business to a particular broker', 'Comply with the law', 'Diversify the portfolio', 'B', 'Managers must seek best execution; they cannot give up execution quality to generate soft-dollar benefits.', 3),
  ('cfa2-ethics-application-soft-dollar-q05', 'cfa2-ethics-application-soft-dollar', 'A board seat held at a company the analyst covers should be:', 'Kept secret', 'Disclosed as a conflict of interest [VI(A)]', 'Ignored if immaterial to the analyst', 'Disclosed only to regulators', 'B', 'Standard VI(A) requires disclosure of conflicts such as board membership that could impair objectivity.', 4),
  ('cfa2-ethics-application-soft-dollar-q06', 'cfa2-ethics-application-soft-dollar', 'Directed brokerage, where a client directs trades to a broker for the client''s own benefit, is:', 'Prohibited', 'Permitted, though it may forgo best execution/soft-dollar benefits and should be disclosed', 'Required', 'Only allowed for institutions', 'B', 'Directed brokerage is allowed but the client should understand it may sacrifice best execution or soft-dollar value.', 5),
  ('cfa2-ethics-application-soft-dollar-q07', 'cfa2-ethics-application-soft-dollar', 'The CFA Institute Soft Dollar Standards are:', 'Mandatory regulations', 'Voluntary best-practice guidance built on the Code', 'Tax rules', 'Accounting standards', 'B', 'The Soft Dollar Standards are voluntary guidance supplementing the mandatory Code and Standards.', 6),
  ('cfa2-ethics-application-soft-dollar-q08', 'cfa2-ethics-application-soft-dollar', 'Referral fees received for recommending a service to clients must be:', 'Disclosed to clients and employer [VI(C)]', 'Kept confidential', 'Paid to charity', 'Reported only if over $100', 'A', 'Standard VI(C) requires disclosure of referral fees to clients and the employer so they can assess any bias.', 7),
  ('cfa2-ethics-application-soft-dollar-q09', 'cfa2-ethics-application-soft-dollar', 'When local law and the Code differ, members must follow the:', 'Less strict of the two', 'More strict of the applicable law or the Code', 'Local law always', 'Code always, ignoring law', 'B', 'Standard I(A) requires adhering to the stricter of applicable law or the Code and Standards.', 8),
  ('cfa2-ethics-application-soft-dollar-q10', 'cfa2-ethics-application-soft-dollar', 'Allocating a hot IPO disproportionately to the manager''s own account breaches:', 'Priority of Transactions [VI(B)]', 'Knowledge of the Law', 'Record Retention', 'Market Manipulation', 'A', 'Standard VI(B) Priority of Transactions requires client and employer trades take precedence over the member''s own.', 9),
  ('cfa2-ethics-application-soft-dollar-q11', 'cfa2-ethics-application-soft-dollar', 'When a recommendation is suitable for some but not all clients, the manager should apply:', 'A blanket trade for everyone', 'Suitability [III(C)] to each client''s objectives and constraints', 'Fair dealing only to large clients', 'No analysis', 'B', 'Standard III(C) Suitability requires matching recommendations to each client''s situation, not applying them uniformly.', 10),
  ('cfa2-ethics-application-soft-dollar-q12', 'cfa2-ethics-application-soft-dollar', 'Mixed-use research (part investment decision-making, part overhead) paid with client brokerage should be:', 'Fully charged to clients', 'Reasonably allocated, with only the research portion paid by client brokerage', 'Charged entirely to the firm regardless', 'Never disclosed', 'B', 'Mixed-use products must be allocated; only the portion that assists investment decisions may be paid with client brokerage.', 11),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-payout-q01', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-payout', 'Under Modigliani-Miller with no taxes, a firm''s WACC as leverage increases:', 'Falls continuously', 'Stays constant', 'Rises continuously', 'Is undefined', 'B', 'With no taxes or distress costs, capital structure is irrelevant and WACC is constant.', 0),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-payout-q02', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-payout', 'Adding corporate taxes to the MM framework makes debt attractive because of the:', 'Interest tax shield', 'Higher bankruptcy risk', 'Dividend tax', 'Equity premium', 'A', 'Tax-deductible interest creates a tax shield, so firm value rises with leverage in MM-with-taxes.', 1),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-payout-q03', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-payout', 'The static trade-off theory sets optimal leverage by balancing the tax shield against:', 'Dividend payments', 'Financial distress / bankruptcy costs', 'Share count', 'The risk-free rate', 'B', 'The trade-off theory finds the leverage that maximizes value by offsetting the tax benefit with expected distress costs.', 2),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-payout-q04', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-payout', 'Pecking-order theory predicts firms prefer financing in the order:', 'Equity, then debt, then internal funds', 'Internal funds, then debt, then equity', 'Debt, then equity, then internal funds', 'Equity only', 'B', 'Because of asymmetric information, firms favor internal funds first, then debt, and issue equity only as a last resort.', 3),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-payout-q05', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-payout', 'Issuing new equity is often read by the market as a signal that management believes the shares are:', 'Undervalued', 'Overvalued', 'Fairly valued', 'Risk-free', 'B', 'Under information asymmetry, equity issuance can signal that managers think the stock is overvalued, pressuring the price.', 4),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-payout-q06', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-payout', 'A firm with high business risk and largely intangible assets should generally carry:', 'More debt', 'Less debt', 'Only debt', 'No equity', 'B', 'High operating risk and few tangible (collateralizable) assets raise distress costs, arguing for lower leverage.', 5),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-payout-q07', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-payout', 'A dividend cut is typically interpreted by investors as:', 'A positive signal', 'A negative signal about future prospects', 'Neutral', 'A tax benefit', 'B', 'Because managers are reluctant to cut, a reduction signals expected weakness and is usually penalized.', 6),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-payout-q08', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-payout', 'Relative to dividends, share repurchases are generally:', 'Less flexible', 'More flexible and can be tax-advantaged', 'Always taxed more heavily', 'A signal of distress', 'B', 'Buybacks are discretionary (more flexible) and, where capital-gains tax is lower than dividend tax, more tax-efficient.', 7),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-payout-q09', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-payout', 'A share repurchase increases EPS when the funds'' after-tax cost (earnings yield given up) is:', 'Greater than the earnings yield of the stock', 'Less than the earnings yield of the stock', 'Equal to the dividend yield', 'Zero', 'B', 'If the after-tax financing cost is below the stock''s earnings yield, buying back shares is accretive to EPS.', 8),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-payout-q10', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-payout', 'The clientele effect refers to investors:', 'Self-selecting firms based on payout/tax preferences', 'Always preferring dividends', 'Ignoring taxes', 'Trading on insider information', 'A', 'Different investor groups (clienteles) gravitate to firms whose payout policies suit their tax and income preferences.', 9),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-payout-q11', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-payout', 'A buyback executed below intrinsic value:', 'Transfers wealth to remaining shareholders', 'Destroys value for remaining shareholders', 'Has no effect', 'Increases shares outstanding', 'A', 'Repurchasing undervalued shares benefits continuing holders by retiring stock for less than it is worth.', 10),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-payout-q12', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-structure-payout', 'In MM with taxes but ignoring distress costs, the value-maximizing capital structure is theoretically:', 'All equity', 'Nearly all debt', '50/50', 'Irrelevant', 'B', 'With only the tax shield and no offsetting costs, value rises with leverage, implying a corner solution of (almost) all debt.', 11),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-multifactor-models-q01', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-multifactor-models', 'Arbitrage Pricing Theory expresses expected return as a function of:', 'A single market factor', 'Multiple systematic risk factors', 'Only idiosyncratic risk', 'Dividend yield', 'B', 'APT is a multifactor, no-arbitrage model generalizing the single-factor CAPM.', 0),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-multifactor-models-q02', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-multifactor-models', 'A fundamental factor model uses factors defined by:', 'Macroeconomic surprises', 'Firm characteristics such as size and value', 'Principal components only', 'The risk-free rate', 'B', 'Fundamental models build factors from observable firm attributes (size, value, momentum, quality).', 1),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-multifactor-models-q03', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-multifactor-models', 'Active risk (tracking error) squared can be decomposed into active factor variance plus:', 'Active specific (idiosyncratic) variance', 'The risk-free rate', 'Beta', 'Total market variance', 'A', 'Active risk² = active factor risk + active specific risk, separating systematic tilts from security-selection risk.', 2),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-multifactor-models-q04', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-multifactor-models', 'The information ratio equals active return divided by:', 'Beta', 'Active risk (tracking error)', 'Total volatility', 'The risk-free rate', 'B', 'IR = active return / active risk, the core measure of active management efficiency.', 3),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-multifactor-models-q05', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-multifactor-models', 'A macroeconomic factor model would most likely include which factor?', 'Book-to-market', 'Unexpected change in inflation', 'Firm size', 'Momentum', 'B', 'Macro models use economic surprises like unexpected inflation, GDP growth, and credit-spread changes.', 4),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-multifactor-models-q06', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-multifactor-models', 'APT rests primarily on the principle of:', 'Mean-variance optimization', 'No-arbitrage', 'Purchasing power parity', 'Dividend irrelevance', 'B', 'APT is derived from the absence of arbitrage among well-diversified portfolios, not CAPM''s restrictive assumptions.', 5),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-multifactor-models-q07', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-multifactor-models', 'Return attribution using a factor model splits active return into factor tilts and:', 'Security selection', 'The risk-free rate', 'Beta drift', 'Inflation', 'A', 'Active return = Σ(factor exposure × factor return) + a security-selection (specific) component.', 6),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-multifactor-models-q08', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-multifactor-models', 'A factor-mimicking portfolio is constructed to:', 'Have unit exposure to one factor and zero to others', 'Maximize idiosyncratic risk', 'Eliminate all returns', 'Track the risk-free rate', 'A', 'A factor-mimicking portfolio isolates a single factor (unit exposure, zero to the rest) for hedging or targeting it.', 7),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-multifactor-models-q09', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-multifactor-models', 'The fundamental law of active management states IR is approximately the information coefficient times the:', 'Square root of breadth', 'Beta', 'Tracking error', 'Risk-free rate', 'A', 'IR ≈ IC × √breadth: skill scaled by the number of independent active decisions.', 8),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-multifactor-models-q10', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-multifactor-models', 'Statistical factor models derive their factors using techniques such as:', 'Principal components analysis', 'Surveying CEOs', 'The Taylor rule', 'Cap-rate analysis', 'A', 'Statistical models extract latent factors from the return covariance matrix via PCA or factor analysis.', 9),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-multifactor-models-q11', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-multifactor-models', 'Multifactor models help a manager hedge a specific risk by:', 'Taking an offsetting position in that factor''s exposure', 'Increasing idiosyncratic risk', 'Buying the risk-free asset only', 'Removing diversification', 'A', 'Knowing factor betas lets a manager offset (hedge) a targeted factor while leaving other exposures intact.', 10),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-multifactor-models-q12', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-multifactor-models', 'Compared with the CAPM, a key practical advantage of multifactor models is that they:', 'Need no data', 'Capture multiple sources of systematic risk, improving risk/return explanation', 'Eliminate all risk', 'Ignore systematic risk', 'B', 'By modeling several systematic factors, multifactor models explain returns and risk better than the single-factor CAPM.', 11),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-credit-analysis-q01', 'cfa2-fixed-income-credit-analysis', 'Expected credit loss is the product of probability of default, exposure at default and:', 'Loss given default', 'The coupon rate', 'Duration', 'The risk-free rate', 'A', 'Expected loss = PD × LGD × EAD, where LGD = 1 − recovery rate.', 0),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-credit-analysis-q02', 'cfa2-fixed-income-credit-analysis', 'Loss given default equals:', '1 − the recovery rate', 'The coupon rate', 'The probability of default', 'The spread duration', 'A', 'LGD is the fraction not recovered: 1 − recovery rate.', 1),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-credit-analysis-q03', 'cfa2-fixed-income-credit-analysis', 'Which spread measure removes the effect of embedded options for comparing bonds?', 'Nominal spread', 'Option-adjusted spread (OAS)', 'Current yield', 'Coupon', 'B', 'OAS strips out the option''s effect, enabling apples-to-apples comparison of bonds with optionality.', 2),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-credit-analysis-q04', 'cfa2-fixed-income-credit-analysis', 'The approximate price impact of a spread change is −(spread duration ×):', 'Change in spread', 'Coupon', 'Recovery rate', 'Time to maturity', 'A', 'Return impact ≈ −spread duration × Δspread (plus a small convexity term).', 3),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-credit-analysis-q05', 'cfa2-fixed-income-credit-analysis', 'A structural (Merton) credit model treats default as occurring when:', 'A statistical hazard fires randomly', 'Firm asset value falls below the value of debt', 'The coupon is missed by one day', 'Interest rates rise', 'B', 'Structural models link default to the firm''s asset value falling below its debt (equity as a call on assets).', 4),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-credit-analysis-q06', 'cfa2-fixed-income-credit-analysis', 'Reduced-form credit models, in contrast, model default as:', 'A deterministic event', 'A statistical hazard driven by observable variables', 'Impossible', 'A function of duration only', 'B', 'Reduced-form models treat default as a random hazard whose intensity depends on macro/firm variables, without modeling asset value directly.', 5),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-credit-analysis-q07', 'cfa2-fixed-income-credit-analysis', 'Anticipating an economic downturn, a credit manager would typically:', 'Move down in quality and extend spread duration', 'Move up in quality and shorten spread duration', 'Buy only equities', 'Ignore credit risk', 'B', 'Defensively, managers upgrade quality and cut spread duration before a downturn widens spreads.', 6),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-credit-analysis-q08', 'cfa2-fixed-income-credit-analysis', 'The G-spread is measured relative to:', 'The government (Treasury) yield', 'The swap curve', 'The spot curve', 'The coupon', 'A', 'The G-spread is the yield spread over a comparable-maturity government bond.', 7),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-credit-analysis-q09', 'cfa2-fixed-income-credit-analysis', 'A bottom-up credit strategy focuses on:', 'Sector rotation over the cycle', 'Selecting individual mispriced issuers', 'Government policy', 'Currency forecasting', 'B', 'Bottom-up credit picks specific issuers/bonds believed to be mispriced, versus top-down sector/quality rotation.', 8),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-credit-analysis-q10', 'cfa2-fixed-income-credit-analysis', 'The credit spread compensates investors mainly for expected loss plus:', 'A risk premium and liquidity', 'Dividends', 'The coupon', 'Inflation only', 'A', 'Spreads cover expected credit loss, a risk/uncertainty premium, and a liquidity premium.', 9),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-credit-analysis-q11', 'cfa2-fixed-income-credit-analysis', 'Holding many uncorrelated issuers reduces which component of credit risk?', 'Idiosyncratic (issuer-specific) default risk', 'Systematic spread risk', 'Interest-rate risk', 'Inflation risk', 'A', 'Diversification across issuers reduces idiosyncratic default risk, though systematic spread risk remains.', 10),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-credit-analysis-q12', 'cfa2-fixed-income-credit-analysis', 'In a recovery phase, a credit manager seeking higher returns would most likely:', 'Add lower-rated, higher-spread credit', 'Move entirely to Treasuries', 'Shorten spread duration sharply', 'Sell all credit', 'A', 'As conditions improve and spreads tighten, adding lower-rated, higher-spread bonds increases return potential.', 11),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-pensions-q01', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-pensions', 'In a defined contribution plan, investment risk is borne by the:', 'Employer', 'Employee', 'Government', 'Plan auditor', 'B', 'In DC plans the employer''s obligation is just the contribution; the employee bears the investment outcome.', 0),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-pensions-q02', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-pensions', 'A defined benefit plan''s funded status equals plan assets minus the:', 'Projected benefit obligation (PBO)', 'Service cost', 'Discount rate', 'Contribution', 'A', 'Funded status = fair value of plan assets − PBO; a deficit is reported as a net pension liability.', 1),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-pensions-q03', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-pensions', 'All else equal, increasing the discount rate used for a DB obligation will:', 'Increase the PBO', 'Decrease the PBO', 'Not affect the PBO', 'Increase plan assets', 'B', 'A higher discount rate lowers the present value of future benefits, reducing the PBO.', 2),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-pensions-q04', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-pensions', 'Under IFRS, remeasurements (actuarial gains/losses) of a DB plan are reported in:', 'Profit or loss', 'Other comprehensive income (not recycled)', 'Retained earnings directly', 'The cash flow statement', 'B', 'IFRS records remeasurements in OCI and does not subsequently recycle them to P&L.', 3),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-pensions-q05', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-pensions', 'Service cost in a DB plan represents:', 'Interest on the obligation', 'The present value of benefits earned by employees in the period', 'The return on plan assets', 'Employer contributions', 'B', 'Service cost is the additional benefit obligation from employees'' service during the period.', 4),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-pensions-q06', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-pensions', 'A higher assumed rate of compensation growth will, all else equal:', 'Decrease the PBO', 'Increase the PBO', 'Not affect the PBO', 'Reduce service cost', 'B', 'Higher expected salary growth raises the projected benefits, increasing the PBO and service cost.', 5),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-pensions-q07', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-pensions', 'For a DC plan, the periodic pension expense is generally equal to:', 'The change in the PBO', 'The employer''s contribution for the period', 'Net interest cost', 'Actuarial losses', 'B', 'DC expense is simply the contribution owed; there is no actuarial obligation to measure.', 6),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-pensions-q08', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-pensions', 'Net interest expense on a DB net liability is calculated using the:', 'Expected return on assets', 'Discount rate applied to the net pension liability', 'Risk-free rate on contributions', 'Compensation growth rate', 'B', 'Under IFRS, net interest = discount rate × the opening net pension liability (or asset).', 7),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-pensions-q09', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-pensions', 'A plan whose assets exceed its PBO reports a:', 'Net pension asset (capped by the asset ceiling)', 'Net pension liability', 'Deferred tax liability only', 'No balance-sheet item', 'A', 'A surplus is recognized as a net pension asset, subject to the asset-ceiling test under IFRS.', 8),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-pensions-q10', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-pensions', 'Analysts adjust pension disclosures mainly to:', 'Inflate earnings', 'Improve comparability and detect aggressive assumptions', 'Hide the obligation', 'Avoid taxes', 'B', 'Reclassifying components and testing assumptions makes cross-company comparisons meaningful and reveals bias.', 9),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-pensions-q11', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-pensions', 'Total periodic pension cost (economic) is best approximated by:', 'Contributions only', 'Ending net liability − beginning net liability + employer contributions', 'Service cost only', 'The discount rate', 'B', 'Economic pension cost = change in funded status adjusted for employer contributions during the period.', 10),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-pensions-q12', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-pensions', 'Moving actuarial losses from the corridor into P&L over time (US GAAP) is called:', 'Amortization of actuarial gains/losses', 'Capitalization', 'Remeasurement to OCI', 'Settlement', 'A', 'US GAAP allows deferral with corridor amortization of actuarial gains/losses into pension expense over time.', 11),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-multinational-q01', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-multinational', 'The current-rate translation method is used when the subsidiary''s functional currency is the:', 'Parent''s presentation currency', 'Local (subsidiary''s own) currency', 'US dollar always', 'Currency of hyperinflation', 'B', 'When the functional currency is the local currency, the current-rate method applies and the gain/loss goes to OCI (CTA).', 0),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-multinational-q02', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-multinational', 'Under the temporal method, non-monetary assets are translated at the:', 'Current rate', 'Historical rate', 'Average rate', 'Forward rate', 'B', 'The temporal method translates non-monetary items (inventory, PP&E) at historical rates; monetary items at the current rate.', 1),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-multinational-q03', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-multinational', 'Translation gains or losses under the current-rate method are reported in:', 'Net income', 'Other comprehensive income (cumulative translation adjustment)', 'Retained earnings directly', 'The cash flow statement', 'B', 'Current-rate translation adjustments accumulate in OCI as the CTA, not in net income.', 2),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-multinational-q04', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-multinational', 'Remeasurement gains/losses under the temporal method are reported in:', 'OCI', 'Net income', 'A separate equity reserve only', 'The notes only', 'B', 'The temporal method runs the remeasurement gain/loss through the income statement.', 3),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-multinational-q05', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-multinational', 'The functional currency is determined by the:', 'Parent''s home country always', 'Primary economic environment in which the entity operates', 'Auditor''s preference', 'Tax authority', 'B', 'Functional currency reflects where the entity primarily generates and spends cash, prices, and obtains financing.', 4),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-multinational-q06', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-multinational', 'Under the current-rate method, revenues and expenses are translated at the:', 'Historical rate', 'Average rate for the period', 'Closing rate', 'Forward rate', 'B', 'Income-statement items use the period''s average rate under the current-rate method.', 5),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-multinational-q07', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-multinational', 'Under the temporal method, COGS and depreciation are translated at:', 'The current rate', 'Historical rates tied to the related assets', 'The average rate', 'The forward rate', 'B', 'Because they relate to non-monetary assets carried at historical cost, COGS and depreciation use historical rates.', 6),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-multinational-q08', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-multinational', 'Financial ratios of the subsidiary are best preserved under the:', 'Temporal method', 'Current-rate method', 'Neither method', 'Both equally', 'B', 'Because all balance-sheet items use one (current) rate, the current-rate method largely preserves the sub''s ratios.', 7),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-multinational-q09', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-multinational', 'In a hyperinflationary economy, US GAAP requires the foreign entity to use the:', 'Current-rate method', 'Temporal method (functional currency = parent''s)', 'Average-rate method', 'No translation', 'B', 'US GAAP treats the parent''s currency as functional in hyperinflation, applying the temporal method.', 8),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-multinational-q10', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-multinational', 'Under IFRS, a subsidiary in a hyperinflationary economy first:', 'Restates for inflation, then translates at the current rate', 'Uses historical rates only', 'Ignores inflation', 'Converts to gold', 'A', 'IFRS restates the statements for inflation and then translates everything at the current rate.', 9),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-multinational-q11', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-multinational', 'A depreciating local currency with a net asset exposure produces, under the current-rate method, a:', 'Positive CTA', 'Negative CTA in OCI', 'Gain in net income', 'No effect', 'B', 'Translating a net asset position at a weakening rate yields a negative cumulative translation adjustment.', 10),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-multinational-q12', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-multinational', 'Monetary assets and liabilities under the temporal method are translated at the:', 'Historical rate', 'Current (closing) rate', 'Average rate', 'Forward rate', 'B', 'The temporal method translates monetary items (cash, receivables, debt) at the current rate.', 11),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-swaps-fra-q01', 'cfa2-derivatives-swaps-fra', 'The long position in a forward rate agreement benefits when the reference rate at expiry is:', 'Below the FRA rate', 'Above the FRA rate', 'Equal to the FRA rate', 'Zero', 'B', 'The long FRA gains when the realized reference rate exceeds the contracted FRA rate.', 0),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-swaps-fra-q02', 'cfa2-derivatives-swaps-fra', 'A plain-vanilla interest-rate swap is economically equivalent to a:', 'Single forward', 'Series (portfolio) of forward rate agreements', 'Equity option', 'Currency forward', 'B', 'Exchanging fixed for floating across multiple dates is equivalent to a strip of FRAs (or a long/short bond pair).', 1),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-swaps-fra-q03', 'cfa2-derivatives-swaps-fra', 'The fixed rate on a newly initiated swap is set so that the swap''s initial value is:', 'Positive to the payer', 'Zero', 'Equal to the notional', 'Negative to the receiver', 'B', 'The swap fixed rate equates the PV of the fixed and floating legs, giving an initial value of zero.', 2),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-swaps-fra-q04', 'cfa2-derivatives-swaps-fra', 'The floating leg of a swap resets to par value on each:', 'Trade date only', 'Reset/payment date', 'Maturity date only', 'Calendar year-end', 'B', 'Immediately after each reset, the floating leg is worth par, which simplifies swap valuation.', 3),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-swaps-fra-q05', 'cfa2-derivatives-swaps-fra', 'After rates rise, the value of a pay-fixed/receive-floating swap to the fixed-rate payer:', 'Increases', 'Decreases', 'Is unchanged', 'Becomes the notional', 'A', 'Rising rates make the floating leg the payer receives more valuable, increasing the pay-fixed position''s value.', 4),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-swaps-fra-q06', 'cfa2-derivatives-swaps-fra', 'A currency swap involves exchanging:', 'Only floating interest', 'Principal and interest payments in two different currencies', 'Equity returns', 'Commodities', 'B', 'Currency swaps exchange principal and interest streams denominated in two currencies.', 5),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-swaps-fra-q07', 'cfa2-derivatives-swaps-fra', 'A firm with floating-rate debt that wants fixed payments can enter a swap to:', 'Receive fixed, pay floating', 'Pay fixed, receive floating', 'Pay floating, receive floating', 'Buy a call option', 'B', 'Paying fixed and receiving floating offsets the floating debt, synthetically converting it to fixed-rate.', 6),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-swaps-fra-q08', 'cfa2-derivatives-swaps-fra', 'The FRA settlement amount is paid on a discounted basis because:', 'It is settled at expiry but covers a future period''s interest', 'Interest rates are negative', 'The notional is exchanged', 'It is an equity product', 'A', 'Settlement occurs at the FRA''s expiry, so the interest differential for the upcoming period is discounted back.', 7),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-swaps-fra-q09', 'cfa2-derivatives-swaps-fra', 'An equity swap most commonly exchanges:', 'An equity return for a fixed or floating rate', 'Two fixed rates', 'Two currencies'' principal', 'Commodities for bonds', 'A', 'Equity swaps exchange the total return on a stock/index for a fixed or floating interest stream.', 8),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-swaps-fra-q10', 'cfa2-derivatives-swaps-fra', 'The swap fixed rate is computed from the:', 'Set of discount factors implied by the spot/forward curve', 'Dividend yield', 'Equity beta', 'Recovery rate', 'A', 'The fixed rate is a par-rate calculation using discount factors derived from the current term structure.', 9),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-swaps-fra-q11', 'cfa2-derivatives-swaps-fra', 'The value of an existing swap to the fixed payer can be found as:', 'PV(floating leg) − PV(fixed leg)', 'PV(fixed leg) − PV(floating leg)', 'Notional × coupon', 'Zero at all times', 'A', 'For the fixed-rate payer, swap value = PV of the floating leg received minus PV of the fixed leg paid.', 10),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-swaps-fra-q12', 'cfa2-derivatives-swaps-fra', 'A key advantage of using swaps to change exposure is that they:', 'Require reissuing debt', 'Adjust exposure cheaply without altering the underlying assets/liabilities', 'Eliminate all risk', 'Guarantee profits', 'B', 'Swaps efficiently convert fixed↔floating or currency exposures without buying/selling the underlying instruments.', 11),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-time-series-q01', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-time-series', 'A log-linear trend model is appropriate when a series grows at a constant:', 'Absolute amount per period', 'Growth rate (percentage) per period', 'Variance', 'Mean only', 'B', 'Log-linear (exponential) trends fit series with a constant percentage growth rate; linear trends fit constant absolute change.', 0),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-time-series-q02', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-time-series', 'An AR(1) model is covariance-stationary only if the slope coefficient is:', 'Exactly 1', 'Less than 1 in absolute value', 'Greater than 1', 'Negative only', 'B', '|b₁| < 1 is required for stationarity; b₁ = 1 is a non-stationary random walk (unit root).', 1),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-time-series-q03', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-time-series', 'A series with a unit root can usually be made stationary by:', 'Squaring it', 'First-differencing', 'Adding a trend', 'Multiplying by its lag', 'B', 'Differencing removes a unit root, producing a stationary series for AR modeling.', 2),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-time-series-q04', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-time-series', 'The Dickey-Fuller test is used to test for:', 'Heteroskedasticity', 'A unit root (non-stationarity)', 'Multicollinearity', 'Normality', 'B', 'Dickey-Fuller tests the null that the series has a unit root and is therefore non-stationary.', 3),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-time-series-q05', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-time-series', 'The mean-reverting level of an AR(1) model is:', 'b₀ × b₁', 'b₀ / (1 − b₁)', 'b₁ / b₀', '1 − b₁', 'B', 'The long-run mean of a stationary AR(1) is the intercept divided by (1 − slope).', 4),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-time-series-q06', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-time-series', 'Significant autocorrelation in a model''s residuals indicates the model is:', 'Correctly specified', 'Misspecified (e.g., needs more lags)', 'Stationary by definition', 'Free of a unit root', 'B', 'Residual autocorrelation signals misspecification; adding lags or seasonal terms may be needed.', 5),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-time-series-q07', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-time-series', 'Out-of-sample forecast accuracy is best compared using the:', 'Root mean squared error (RMSE)', 'R-squared', 'Durbin-Watson statistic', 'Intercept', 'A', 'Lower RMSE indicates better out-of-sample forecasting performance.', 6),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-time-series-q08', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-time-series', 'ARCH is present when the variance of the error term:', 'Is constant', 'Depends on prior periods'' squared errors', 'Is always zero', 'Equals the mean', 'B', 'Autoregressive conditional heteroskedasticity means error variance depends on past squared errors.', 7),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-time-series-q09', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-time-series', 'Seasonality in a quarterly AR model is often handled by adding:', 'A lag at the seasonal interval (e.g., the fourth lag)', 'More observations only', 'A higher intercept', 'A unit root', 'A', 'A seasonal lag (e.g., x_{t-4} for quarterly data) captures recurring seasonal patterns.', 8),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-time-series-q10', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-time-series', 'Regressing two unrelated unit-root (non-stationary) series tends to produce a:', 'Spurious regression', 'Perfectly valid result', 'Zero R-squared', 'Stationary residual always', 'A', 'Two trending non-stationary series can show a high but meaningless (spurious) relationship.', 9),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-time-series-q11', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-time-series', 'Two non-stationary series can be validly regressed together if they are:', 'Cointegrated', 'Heteroskedastic', 'Seasonal', 'Uncorrelated', 'A', 'Cointegration means a long-run equilibrium relationship exists, making the regression meaningful.', 10),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-time-series-q12', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-time-series', 'A random walk is best described as an AR(1) with:', 'b₁ = 0', 'b₁ = 1 (a unit root)', 'A negative intercept', 'Constant variance', 'B', 'A random walk has slope 1 (unit root); its variance grows over time, so it is non-stationary.', 11),
  ('cfa2-economics-growth-q01', 'cfa2-economics-growth', 'In growth accounting, output growth not explained by capital and labor growth is attributed to:', 'Inflation', 'Total factor productivity (TFP)', 'Population only', 'The exchange rate', 'B', 'TFP is the residual capturing technology/efficiency gains beyond measured factor inputs.', 0),
  ('cfa2-economics-growth-q02', 'cfa2-economics-growth', 'The neoclassical (Solow) model predicts that long-run per-capita growth comes from:', 'Capital accumulation alone', 'Exogenous technological progress', 'Higher savings forever', 'Population growth', 'B', 'Due to diminishing returns to capital, sustained per-capita growth requires exogenous technological progress.', 1),
  ('cfa2-economics-growth-q03', 'cfa2-economics-growth', 'Endogenous growth theory differs from the Solow model in that it holds:', 'Capital has diminishing returns always', 'R&D and human capital can yield constant/increasing returns and raise long-run growth', 'Growth is impossible', 'Technology is fixed', 'B', 'Endogenous models let investment in knowledge generate non-diminishing returns, so policy can lift the long-run growth rate.', 2),
  ('cfa2-economics-growth-q04', 'cfa2-economics-growth', 'The convergence hypothesis predicts that, all else equal, poorer economies will:', 'Grow more slowly', 'Grow faster and catch up to richer economies', 'Never grow', 'Have higher inflation', 'B', 'Neoclassical convergence implies lower-income economies grow faster due to higher marginal returns to capital.', 3),
  ('cfa2-economics-growth-q05', 'cfa2-economics-growth', '''Capital deepening'' refers to:', 'An increase in capital per worker', 'More workers', 'Higher TFP', 'Lower savings', 'A', 'Capital deepening raises the capital-to-labor ratio; its growth contribution diminishes as the ratio rises.', 4),
  ('cfa2-economics-growth-q06', 'cfa2-economics-growth', 'Sustainable economic growth tends to support long-run equity returns through:', 'Higher inflation', 'Higher corporate earnings growth', 'Lower productivity', 'Currency depreciation', 'B', 'Durable growth feeds aggregate earnings, a key driver of long-horizon equity returns.', 5),
  ('cfa2-economics-growth-q07', 'cfa2-economics-growth', 'Which institutional factor most supports long-run growth?', 'Weak property rights', 'Strong property rights and rule of law', 'Closed trade', 'High corruption', 'B', 'Secure property rights and rule of law encourage investment and innovation, supporting growth.', 6),
  ('cfa2-economics-growth-q08', 'cfa2-economics-growth', 'In the production function Y = A·F(K, L), ''A'' represents:', 'Labor hours', 'Total factor productivity / technology', 'The capital stock', 'The savings rate', 'B', 'A scales output for given inputs and represents technology/total factor productivity.', 7),
  ('cfa2-economics-growth-q09', 'cfa2-economics-growth', 'Growth driven mainly by capital deepening (rather than TFP) is generally:', 'More sustainable indefinitely', 'Subject to diminishing returns and less durable', 'Impossible', 'Independent of the steady state', 'B', 'Because capital faces diminishing returns, growth relying on capital deepening fades without TFP gains.', 8),
  ('cfa2-economics-growth-q10', 'cfa2-economics-growth', 'Favorable demographics raise growth potential primarily by increasing:', 'The labor input', 'Inflation', 'The discount rate', 'Currency value', 'A', 'A growing working-age population expands the labor input component of potential output.', 9),
  ('cfa2-economics-growth-q11', 'cfa2-economics-growth', '''Club convergence'' holds that convergence occurs only among economies that:', 'Speak the same language', 'Share similar institutions and characteristics', 'Have identical GDP', 'Use the same currency', 'B', 'Club convergence says only economies with comparable institutions/structures converge to similar income levels.', 10),
  ('cfa2-economics-growth-q12', 'cfa2-economics-growth', 'Openness to trade is generally associated with:', 'Lower productivity', 'Higher growth via technology diffusion and competition', 'No growth effect', 'Guaranteed deflation', 'B', 'Trade openness spreads technology and intensifies competition, supporting productivity and growth.', 11),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-q01', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-hedge-funds', 'An equity market-neutral strategy aims for a portfolio beta of approximately:', '1.0', '0', '2.0', '−1.0', 'B', 'Market-neutral funds balance longs and shorts to target near-zero net market (beta) exposure, isolating alpha.', 0),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-q02', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-hedge-funds', 'Merger (risk) arbitrage is an example of which strategy category?', 'Macro', 'Event-driven', 'Relative value', 'Dedicated short', 'B', 'Merger arbitrage trades around announced corporate events, a classic event-driven approach.', 1),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-q03', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-hedge-funds', 'Global macro and managed futures (CTA) strategies primarily trade on:', 'Single-stock fundamentals only', 'Macroeconomic trends across asset classes', 'Merger spreads', 'Convertible bonds', 'B', 'Macro/CTA strategies take directional positions based on macro views and price trends across markets.', 2),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-q04', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-hedge-funds', 'A ''high-water mark'' ensures that incentive fees are charged only on:', 'Gross assets', 'New profits above the prior peak value', 'Every gain regardless of past losses', 'Management fees', 'B', 'The high-water mark prevents charging performance fees on gains that merely recover earlier losses.', 3),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-q05', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-hedge-funds', 'Fund-of-funds add diversification but suffer from:', 'No fees', 'An extra layer of fees (double fees)', 'Guaranteed returns', 'Daily liquidity always', 'B', 'Fund-of-funds charge their own fee on top of the underlying managers'' fees, reducing net returns.', 4),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-q06', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-hedge-funds', 'Many hedge-fund return streams are described as ''short volatility'' because they:', 'Always profit in crises', 'Earn steady gains then suffer large losses in stress (negative skew)', 'Have no risk', 'Are perfectly normal', 'B', 'Such strategies resemble selling insurance: small consistent gains punctuated by occasional large drawdowns.', 5),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-q07', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-hedge-funds', 'Lock-ups, gates, and notice periods are features that limit a hedge fund''s:', 'Leverage', 'Investor liquidity / redemptions', 'Fees', 'Returns', 'B', 'These provisions restrict how and when investors can withdraw capital, managing liquidity for the manager.', 6),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-q08', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-hedge-funds', 'Convertible arbitrage is best classified as a ____ strategy.', 'Relative value', 'Macro', 'Event-driven', 'Long-only', 'A', 'Convertible arbitrage exploits pricing differences between a convertible bond and its components — a relative-value trade.', 7),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-q09', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-hedge-funds', 'Because hedge-fund returns are often non-normal, risk should be assessed with:', 'Standard deviation alone', 'Measures capturing skew, kurtosis and tail/drawdown risk', 'The dividend yield', 'Beta only', 'B', 'Fat tails and negative skew mean volatility understates risk; downside/tail measures are needed.', 8),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-q10', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-hedge-funds', 'A dedicated short-bias fund profits when:', 'Targeted stocks decline', 'The market rises', 'Rates fall only', 'Volatility is zero', 'A', 'Dedicated short managers are net short and gain when their shorted securities fall in price.', 9),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-q11', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-hedge-funds', 'Operational due diligence on a hedge fund focuses on:', 'Only past returns', 'Independent administration/custody, valuation, controls and key-person risk', 'The benchmark index', 'The risk-free rate', 'B', 'ODD assesses the non-investment infrastructure whose failure is a leading cause of fund losses.', 10),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-q12', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-hedge-funds', 'Reported hedge-fund correlations to equities tend to:', 'Stay constant', 'Rise during market crises, reducing diversification when needed', 'Fall to −1 in crises', 'Be exactly 1 always', 'B', 'Like other alternatives, hedge-fund correlations often spike in stress, eroding diversification benefits.', 11),
  ('cfa2-ethics-analysis-recommendations-q01', 'cfa2-ethics-analysis-recommendations', 'Standard V(A) requires that recommendations have a:', 'Guaranteed return', 'Reasonable and adequate basis supported by research', 'Majority vote', 'Regulatory pre-approval', 'B', 'Diligence and Reasonable Basis [V(A)] requires sufficient research/analysis behind any recommendation or action.', 0),
  ('cfa2-ethics-analysis-recommendations-q02', 'cfa2-ethics-analysis-recommendations', 'When relying on third-party research, a member must:', 'Assume it is always correct', 'Make reasonable efforts to verify its soundness', 'Never use it', 'Disclose nothing', 'B', 'Members remain responsible and should take reasonable steps to confirm secondary/third-party research is sound.', 1),
  ('cfa2-ethics-analysis-recommendations-q03', 'cfa2-ethics-analysis-recommendations', 'Standard V(B) requires members to communicate by:', 'Mixing fact and opinion freely', 'Distinguishing fact from opinion and disclosing the basic process and risks', 'Hiding assumptions', 'Omitting limitations', 'B', 'V(B) Communication requires separating fact from opinion and disclosing the process, risks, and limitations.', 2),
  ('cfa2-ethics-analysis-recommendations-q04', 'cfa2-ethics-analysis-recommendations', 'Absent a regulatory requirement, CFA Institute recommends retaining records for at least:', '1 year', '7 years', '20 years', 'No minimum', 'B', 'The recommended minimum record-retention period is seven years when no stricter law applies.', 3),
  ('cfa2-ethics-analysis-recommendations-q05', 'cfa2-ethics-analysis-recommendations', 'Research files and records created for the employer are generally:', 'The member''s personal property to take when leaving', 'The firm''s property', 'Public domain', 'Owned by clients', 'B', 'Supporting records belong to the firm; a departing member cannot take them to a new employer.', 4),
  ('cfa2-ethics-analysis-recommendations-q06', 'cfa2-ethics-analysis-recommendations', 'A member who disagrees with a team''s consensus research may:', 'Never be associated with the report', 'Remain associated if the report has a reasonable basis they can support', 'Be forced to sign it', 'Publicly denounce it always', 'B', 'A member need not dissent publicly if the report rests on a reasonable basis they can accept.', 5),
  ('cfa2-ethics-analysis-recommendations-q07', 'cfa2-ethics-analysis-recommendations', 'Using a quantitative model without understanding its assumptions most likely violates:', 'V(A) Diligence and Reasonable Basis', 'Fair Dealing', 'Priority of Transactions', 'Record Retention', 'A', 'Relying on a model without grasping its inputs/limitations breaches the diligence/reasonable-basis requirement.', 6),
  ('cfa2-ethics-analysis-recommendations-q08', 'cfa2-ethics-analysis-recommendations', 'Material changes to the investment process should be disclosed to clients:', 'Only at year-end', 'Promptly', 'Never', 'Only if asked', 'B', 'V(B) requires prompt disclosure of material changes that could affect the investment process.', 7),
  ('cfa2-ethics-analysis-recommendations-q09', 'cfa2-ethics-analysis-recommendations', 'When communicating a recommendation, a member should identify:', 'Only the upside', 'Key assumptions, limitations and risks', 'Competitors'' fees', 'Other clients'' positions', 'B', 'Clients need the important assumptions, limitations, and risk factors to evaluate a recommendation.', 8),
  ('cfa2-ethics-analysis-recommendations-q10', 'cfa2-ethics-analysis-recommendations', 'Maintaining records of the rationale for investment actions primarily supports:', 'Marketing', 'Demonstrating a reasonable basis and compliance', 'Tax evasion', 'Higher fees', 'B', 'Records substantiate that analysis and actions had a reasonable basis, supporting V(A) and V(C).', 9),
  ('cfa2-ethics-analysis-recommendations-q11', 'cfa2-ethics-analysis-recommendations', 'Presenting an opinion as if it were established fact violates:', 'V(B) Communication with Clients', 'Priority of Transactions', 'Loyalty to Employer', 'Knowledge of the Law', 'A', 'V(B) requires clearly distinguishing fact from opinion in communications.', 10),
  ('cfa2-ethics-analysis-recommendations-q12', 'cfa2-ethics-analysis-recommendations', 'The level of diligence required for a recommendation:', 'Is the same for all decisions', 'Varies with the nature and complexity of the analysis', 'Is set by clients', 'Is unnecessary for buys', 'B', 'What constitutes a ''reasonable basis'' depends on the complexity and significance of the decision.', 11),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-governance-esg-q01', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-governance-esg', 'The principal-agent problem in corporate governance arises between:', 'Customers and suppliers', 'Shareholders (principals) and managers (agents)', 'Regulators and auditors', 'Employees and customers', 'B', 'Managers (agents) may pursue their own interests over those of shareholders (principals), the core agency conflict.', 0),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-governance-esg-q02', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-governance-esg', 'A strong board is characterized by:', 'A combined chair/CEO with no committees', 'Independent, diverse directors and key committees', 'Directors appointed by management only', 'No audit committee', 'B', 'Independence, diversity, and functioning audit/remuneration/nomination committees signal strong governance.', 1),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-governance-esg-q03', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-governance-esg', 'A poison pill (takeover defense) generally:', 'Increases management accountability', 'Entrenches management and can reduce accountability', 'Has no governance effect', 'Guarantees a higher takeover price', 'B', 'Takeover defenses can insulate management from the market for corporate control, weakening accountability.', 2),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-governance-esg-q04', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-governance-esg', 'Negative (exclusionary) screening in ESG investing means:', 'Selecting the best ESG performers', 'Excluding certain sectors or companies on ESG grounds', 'Ignoring ESG', 'Buying only bonds', 'B', 'Negative screening removes objectionable industries/companies (e.g., tobacco, weapons) from the universe.', 3),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-governance-esg-q05', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-governance-esg', 'ESG ''materiality'' refers to focusing on factors that:', 'Are always environmental', 'Affect a company''s cash flows or risk', 'Are immaterial to value', 'Only matter to regulators', 'B', 'Material ESG factors are those with a plausible impact on financial performance and risk.', 4),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-governance-esg-q06', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-governance-esg', 'A shareholder-vs-creditor conflict can arise when a firm:', 'Pays down all debt', 'Takes on riskier projects that benefit shareholders at creditors'' expense (risk-shifting)', 'Issues equity', 'Holds cash', 'B', 'Risk-shifting transfers value from creditors to shareholders by increasing asset risk after debt is issued.', 5),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-governance-esg-q07', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-governance-esg', 'Active ownership (engagement) as an ESG approach involves:', 'Selling all holdings', 'Using voting and dialogue to influence company practices', 'Ignoring management', 'Avoiding equities', 'B', 'Engagement uses proxy voting and direct dialogue to push companies toward better ESG practices.', 6),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-governance-esg-q08', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-governance-esg', 'Strong corporate governance tends to:', 'Raise the cost of capital', 'Lower risk and the cost of capital', 'Have no effect on value', 'Guarantee higher earnings', 'B', 'Better governance reduces agency and operational risk, which can lower the firm''s cost of capital.', 7),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-governance-esg-q09', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-governance-esg', '''Greenwashing'' refers to:', 'Genuine emissions cuts', 'Overstating or misrepresenting ESG credentials', 'A type of bond', 'An accounting method', 'B', 'Greenwashing is misleading promotion of a company/product as more sustainable than it is.', 8),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-governance-esg-q10', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-governance-esg', 'A dual-class share structure typically:', 'Gives all shareholders equal votes', 'Concentrates voting control with insiders despite less economic ownership', 'Eliminates voting', 'Is required by law', 'B', 'Dual-class shares grant insiders disproportionate voting power relative to their cash-flow stake, a governance concern.', 9),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-governance-esg-q11', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-governance-esg', 'ESG integration into valuation most directly affects:', 'Only marketing', 'Cash-flow forecasts and/or the discount rate', 'Share count', 'The dividend payment date', 'B', 'Integrating material ESG factors adjusts projected cash flows and the required return used in valuation.', 10),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-governance-esg-q12', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-governance-esg', 'Stakeholder management seeks to balance the interests of:', 'Shareholders only', 'Shareholders, board, management, employees, creditors, customers, suppliers and regulators', 'Regulators only', 'Competitors', 'B', 'Governance addresses the full set of stakeholders whose interests intersect with the firm.', 11),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-market-risk-var-q01', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-market-risk-var', 'A ''5% one-day VaR of $1 million'' means there is a 5% probability the portfolio will lose:', 'Exactly $1 million in a day', 'At least $1 million in a day', 'At most $1 million ever', 'Nothing', 'B', 'VaR is the minimum loss at the given tail probability: a 5% chance of losing $1m or more in a day.', 0),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-market-risk-var-q02', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-market-risk-var', 'The parametric (variance-covariance) VaR method assumes returns are:', 'Lognormal with fat tails', 'Normally distributed', 'Uniform', 'Non-stationary', 'B', 'Parametric VaR assumes (multivariate) normal returns, which understates risk for options and fat-tailed assets.', 1),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-market-risk-var-q03', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-market-risk-var', 'Historical simulation VaR is computed by:', 'Assuming a normal distribution', 'Applying the distribution of actual past returns', 'Running random simulations', 'Using duration only', 'B', 'Historical simulation reuses the empirical distribution of past returns, requiring no distributional assumption.', 2),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-market-risk-var-q04', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-market-risk-var', 'Monte Carlo VaR is particularly useful for portfolios with:', 'Only linear instruments', 'Non-linear payoffs such as options', 'No risk', 'Constant returns', 'B', 'Monte Carlo can model complex, non-linear payoffs by simulating many scenarios, at higher computational cost.', 3),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-market-risk-var-q05', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-market-risk-var', 'Conditional VaR (expected shortfall) measures the:', 'Most likely gain', 'Average loss given that the loss exceeds the VaR threshold', 'Minimum loss', 'Standard deviation', 'B', 'Expected shortfall captures the average severity of losses beyond VaR, addressing VaR''s tail blind spot.', 4),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-market-risk-var-q06', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-market-risk-var', 'A key limitation of VaR is that it:', 'Describes losses beyond the threshold', 'Says nothing about the magnitude of losses beyond the VaR level', 'Cannot be computed', 'Requires no assumptions', 'B', 'VaR ignores how bad losses get past the cutoff — a major reason expected shortfall is used alongside it.', 5),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-market-risk-var-q07', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-market-risk-var', 'Stress testing and scenario analysis complement VaR by:', 'Assuming normal markets', 'Examining extreme or hypothetical adverse conditions', 'Lowering risk estimates', 'Ignoring tail events', 'B', 'Stress tests probe severe scenarios that statistical VaR, based on normal periods, may miss.', 6),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-market-risk-var-q08', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-market-risk-var', 'Increasing the confidence level from 95% to 99% will, all else equal, make VaR:', 'Smaller', 'Larger', 'Unchanged', 'Negative', 'B', 'A higher confidence level moves further into the tail, increasing the reported VaR loss.', 7),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-market-risk-var-q09', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-market-risk-var', 'A drawback of historical simulation is that it:', 'Requires a normality assumption', 'Assumes the past distribution will repeat (backward-looking)', 'Cannot use real data', 'Ignores history', 'B', 'Historical simulation depends entirely on the chosen sample and may miss risks absent from that history.', 8),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-market-risk-var-q10', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-market-risk-var', 'Position limits, risk budgets and stop-loss rules are examples of:', 'Return objectives', 'Market-risk management constraints', 'VaR methods', 'Performance benchmarks', 'B', 'These are governance tools that constrain and manage market-risk exposures.', 9),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-market-risk-var-q11', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-market-risk-var', 'For an options portfolio, parametric VaR is least reliable because options have:', 'Linear payoffs', 'Non-linear (convex) payoffs not captured by a normal/linear approach', 'No risk', 'Constant delta', 'B', 'Option convexity (gamma) violates the linear/normal assumptions of parametric VaR, distorting the estimate.', 10),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-market-risk-var-q12', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-market-risk-var', 'A criticism that VaR can be ''gamed'' refers to managers:', 'Reducing genuine risk', 'Structuring positions to lower reported VaR while keeping tail risk', 'Disclosing all risks', 'Using expected shortfall', 'B', 'Because VaR ignores the tail, exposures can be arranged to report a low VaR while retaining large tail risk.', 11),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-machine-learning-q01', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-machine-learning', 'Supervised learning differs from unsupervised learning in that it uses:', 'No data', 'Labeled data (known target outputs)', 'Only text', 'Random outputs', 'B', 'Supervised learning trains on labeled examples to predict a target; unsupervised learning finds structure without labels.', 0),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-machine-learning-q02', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-machine-learning', 'K-means clustering is an example of:', 'Supervised regression', 'Unsupervised learning', 'Reinforcement learning', 'A trend model', 'B', 'Clustering groups unlabeled observations by similarity — an unsupervised technique.', 1),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-machine-learning-q03', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-machine-learning', 'Overfitting is characterized by a model that has:', 'High bias, low variance', 'Low bias but high variance (fits noise, fails out of sample)', 'No errors ever', 'Constant accuracy', 'B', 'Overfit models capture noise, performing well in-sample but poorly on new data (high variance).', 2),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-machine-learning-q04', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-machine-learning', 'LASSO and ridge regression help prevent overfitting through:', 'Adding more features', 'Regularization (penalizing coefficient size)', 'Removing the target', 'Increasing variance', 'B', 'Penalized regressions shrink coefficients, reducing model complexity and overfitting.', 3),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-machine-learning-q05', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-machine-learning', 'Principal components analysis (PCA) is primarily used for:', 'Labeling data', 'Dimension reduction', 'Reinforcement learning', 'Forecasting interest rates', 'B', 'PCA reduces many correlated features into a few uncorrelated components capturing most variance.', 4),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-machine-learning-q06', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-machine-learning', 'Cross-validation is used to:', 'Increase overfitting', 'Assess out-of-sample performance and tune the model', 'Clean data only', 'Label data', 'B', 'Cross-validation partitions data to estimate generalization error and guide hyperparameter tuning.', 5),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-machine-learning-q07', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-machine-learning', 'In the bias-variance tradeoff, an overly simple model tends to:', 'Overfit', 'Underfit (high bias)', 'Have zero error', 'Generalize perfectly', 'B', 'Too-simple models underfit, exhibiting high bias and missing real patterns.', 6),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-machine-learning-q08', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-machine-learning', 'The first step of a typical data-analytics project is:', 'Model tuning', 'Conceptualization of the problem', 'Reporting results', 'Deployment', 'B', 'Projects start by conceptualizing the task before collecting, preparing, exploring, and modeling the data.', 7),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-machine-learning-q09', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-machine-learning', 'In text analytics, converting words to a normalized base form is called:', 'Tokenization', 'Stemming/lemmatization', 'Clustering', 'Regularization', 'B', 'Stemming/lemmatization reduces words to a root form; tokenization splits text into tokens.', 8),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-machine-learning-q10', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-machine-learning', 'The ''three Vs'' commonly used to describe big data are volume, velocity and:', 'Value', 'Variety', 'Volatility', 'Validity', 'B', 'Big data is classically described by volume, velocity, and variety (with veracity often added).', 9),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-machine-learning-q11', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-machine-learning', 'A random forest is an ensemble of:', 'Linear regressions', 'Decision trees', 'Clusters', 'Principal components', 'B', 'Random forests aggregate many decision trees to improve accuracy and reduce overfitting.', 10),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-machine-learning-q12', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-machine-learning', 'Data wrangling (preparation) in a project mainly involves:', 'Cleaning and organizing raw data (handling outliers/missing values)', 'Writing the final report', 'Choosing the confidence level', 'Deploying the model', 'A', 'Wrangling cleans, transforms and structures raw data so it is usable for exploration and modeling.', 11),
  ('cfa2-equity-multistage-ddm-q01', 'cfa2-equity-multistage-ddm', 'The Gordon growth model is appropriate only for firms with:', 'Volatile, unpredictable growth', 'Stable growth below the required return', 'No dividends', 'Negative earnings', 'B', 'Gordon assumes a single constant growth rate g < r indefinitely — suitable for mature, stable dividend payers.', 0),
  ('cfa2-equity-multistage-ddm-q02', 'cfa2-equity-multistage-ddm', 'In a two-stage DDM, the terminal value is usually computed using:', 'The high-growth rate', 'The Gordon model with the long-run (stable) growth rate', 'Book value', 'The risk-free rate', 'B', 'After the explicit high-growth phase, the terminal value applies Gordon growth at the sustainable long-run rate.', 1),
  ('cfa2-equity-multistage-ddm-q03', 'cfa2-equity-multistage-ddm', 'The H-model approximates a growth rate that:', 'Jumps suddenly', 'Declines linearly from a high to a long-run rate', 'Is constant', 'Rises over time', 'B', 'The H-model assumes growth fades linearly over 2H years from the initial high rate to the stable rate.', 2),
  ('cfa2-equity-multistage-ddm-q04', 'cfa2-equity-multistage-ddm', 'Sustainable growth rate equals:', 'ROE × retention ratio', 'Dividend yield × payout', 'r − g', 'ROE × payout ratio', 'A', 'g = b × ROE, where b is the earnings retention ratio (1 − payout).', 3),
  ('cfa2-equity-multistage-ddm-q05', 'cfa2-equity-multistage-ddm', 'In most multistage models, the largest component of value is typically the:', 'First dividend', 'Terminal value', 'Risk-free rate', 'Book value', 'B', 'The terminal (continuing) value usually dominates, so models are highly sensitive to its r and g inputs.', 4),
  ('cfa2-equity-multistage-ddm-q06', 'cfa2-equity-multistage-ddm', 'A three-stage DDM is most suitable for a:', 'Mature utility', 'Young firm moving through growth, transition and maturity', 'Firm in liquidation', 'Risk-free bond', 'B', 'Three-stage models capture an initial high-growth phase, a transition, and a stable maturity phase.', 5),
  ('cfa2-equity-multistage-ddm-q07', 'cfa2-equity-multistage-ddm', 'If the long-run growth rate assumption rises (toward r), the estimated value will:', 'Fall sharply', 'Rise sharply', 'Stay constant', 'Become zero', 'B', 'As g_L approaches r, the terminal value (and thus the estimate) increases sharply — a key sensitivity.', 6),
  ('cfa2-equity-multistage-ddm-q08', 'cfa2-equity-multistage-ddm', 'DDMs are least appropriate for firms that:', 'Pay stable dividends', 'Pay no dividends and have no clear dividend policy', 'Are mature', 'Have positive ROE', 'B', 'Without dividends, FCFE or residual-income models are preferable to a DDM.', 7),
  ('cfa2-equity-multistage-ddm-q09', 'cfa2-equity-multistage-ddm', 'A long-run growth rate used in a terminal value should generally not exceed the:', 'Dividend yield', 'Long-run growth rate of the overall economy', 'Risk-free rate', 'Inflation rate only', 'B', 'A perpetual growth rate above economy-wide growth is implausible, so g_L is capped near nominal GDP growth.', 8),
  ('cfa2-equity-multistage-ddm-q10', 'cfa2-equity-multistage-ddm', 'In the H-model, a larger H (longer fade period) will, all else equal:', 'Lower the value', 'Raise the value (more years of above-normal growth)', 'Not affect value', 'Equal the Gordon model', 'B', 'A longer high-growth fade adds more excess growth, increasing the estimated value.', 9),
  ('cfa2-equity-multistage-ddm-q11', 'cfa2-equity-multistage-ddm', 'The required return r in a DDM is most commonly estimated using:', 'The CAPM', 'The dividend yield', 'The payout ratio', 'Book value', 'A', 'CAPM (or a build-up/multifactor model) provides the cost of equity used to discount dividends.', 10),
  ('cfa2-equity-multistage-ddm-q12', 'cfa2-equity-multistage-ddm', 'A two-stage model discounts the terminal value back to the present using:', 'The terminal growth rate', 'The required return over the number of explicit-forecast years', 'The risk-free rate', 'No discounting', 'B', 'The TV computed at year n is discounted to today at (1+r)^n, like any future cash flow.', 11),
  ('cfa2-equity-private-company-q01', 'cfa2-equity-private-company', 'The three broad approaches to private company valuation are income, market, and:', 'Arbitrage', 'Asset-based', 'Parity', 'Duration', 'B', 'Private valuation uses income, market (guideline), and asset-based approaches.', 0),
  ('cfa2-equity-private-company-q02', 'cfa2-equity-private-company', 'A discount for lack of marketability (DLOM) reflects that private interests:', 'Pay higher dividends', 'Cannot be sold quickly without a price concession', 'Have no risk', 'Are tax-free', 'B', 'Illiquidity of private equity warrants a DLOM relative to freely traded shares.', 1),
  ('cfa2-equity-private-company-q03', 'cfa2-equity-private-company', 'Valuing a controlling stake using guideline public (minority) prices usually requires adding a:', 'Control premium', 'Liquidity discount', 'Size discount', 'Tax penalty', 'A', 'Public minority prices lack control, so a control premium is added for a controlling interest.', 2),
  ('cfa2-equity-private-company-q04', 'cfa2-equity-private-company', 'Normalizing a private firm''s earnings involves adjusting for:', 'Market beta', 'Owner compensation, related-party transactions and one-offs', 'The risk-free rate', 'Index weights', 'B', 'Normalization removes non-market owner pay, related-party items and non-recurring effects to show true earnings power.', 3),
  ('cfa2-equity-private-company-q05', 'cfa2-equity-private-company', 'The excess earnings method is a form of which approach?', 'Market', 'Income', 'Asset-based', 'Option-pricing', 'B', 'Excess earnings values intangible value from earnings above a fair return on tangible assets — an income method.', 4),
  ('cfa2-equity-private-company-q06', 'cfa2-equity-private-company', 'Discount rates for private companies are generally ____ than for comparable public firms.', 'Lower', 'Higher', 'Identical', 'Zero', 'B', 'Illiquidity, smaller size and company-specific risk raise the required return for private firms.', 5),
  ('cfa2-equity-private-company-q07', 'cfa2-equity-private-company', 'The guideline transactions method derives multiples from:', 'The firm''s own dividends', 'Prices paid in acquisitions of comparable companies', 'The risk-free rate', 'Replacement cost', 'B', 'It uses valuation multiples observed in M&A transactions of similar businesses.', 6),
  ('cfa2-equity-private-company-q08', 'cfa2-equity-private-company', 'The appropriate standard (definition) of value depends mainly on the:', 'Purpose of the valuation', 'Risk-free rate', 'Auditor', 'Share price', 'A', 'Tax, litigation, M&A or financial-reporting purposes dictate which value standard (e.g., fair market value) applies.', 7),
  ('cfa2-equity-private-company-q09', 'cfa2-equity-private-company', 'A build-up or expanded-CAPM discount rate for a small private firm typically adds:', 'A size premium and company-specific premium', 'A liquidity discount to returns', 'Nothing extra', 'A negative premium', 'A', 'Private/small-firm discount rates layer size and specific-risk premia onto the base CAPM return.', 8),
  ('cfa2-equity-private-company-q10', 'cfa2-equity-private-company', 'The asset-based approach is generally least appropriate for:', 'A holding company of liquid assets', 'A going concern with significant intangible value', 'A firm in liquidation', 'A real-estate holding entity', 'B', 'Asset-based NAV misses going-concern/intangible value, so it suits asset-holding or liquidation cases, not operating firms.', 9),
  ('cfa2-equity-private-company-q11', 'cfa2-equity-private-company', 'A discount for lack of control (DLOC) applies to:', 'Controlling stakes', 'Minority interests', 'Public indices', 'Risk-free bonds', 'B', 'Minority holders can''t direct the firm, so a DLOC reduces the per-share value of non-controlling interests.', 10),
  ('cfa2-equity-private-company-q12', 'cfa2-equity-private-company', 'Capitalized cash flow valuation is most appropriate when a private firm has:', 'Highly erratic cash flows', 'Stable, predictable growth', 'No cash flows', 'Only intangible assets', 'B', 'The capitalized-cash-flow (single-period) method fits firms with steady, predictable growth, akin to Gordon.', 11),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-term-structure-q01', 'cfa2-fixed-income-term-structure', 'The pure (unbiased) expectations theory holds that forward rates equal:', 'Expected future spot rates', 'The risk-free rate plus a fixed premium', 'Current spot rates', 'Zero', 'A', 'Under pure expectations, forwards are unbiased forecasts of future spot rates with no risk premium.', 0),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-term-structure-q02', 'cfa2-fixed-income-term-structure', 'The liquidity preference theory adds to expectations a:', 'Negative term premium', 'Positive premium for longer maturities', 'Tax adjustment', 'Currency premium', 'B', 'Investors require a liquidity/term premium to hold longer maturities, biasing forwards above expected spots.', 1),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-term-structure-q03', 'cfa2-fixed-income-term-structure', 'Market segmentation theory explains the curve by:', 'A single global rate', 'Supply and demand within distinct maturity segments', 'Only inflation', 'Equity returns', 'B', 'Segmentation holds that distinct investor clienteles dominate maturity buckets, shaping the curve.', 2),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-term-structure-q04', 'cfa2-fixed-income-term-structure', 'Empirically, most of the variation in yield-curve movements is explained by changes in the:', 'Level (parallel shifts)', 'Curvature', 'Convexity', 'Coupon', 'A', 'The first principal component — the level (parallel) shift — accounts for the bulk of curve variance.', 3),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-term-structure-q05', 'cfa2-fixed-income-term-structure', 'Key rate durations are useful because they capture exposure to:', 'Only parallel shifts', 'Shifts at specific maturities (non-parallel moves)', 'Credit spreads', 'Currency moves', 'B', 'Key rate (partial) durations measure sensitivity to changes at individual points on the curve, unlike a single effective duration.', 4),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-term-structure-q06', 'cfa2-fixed-income-term-structure', 'An arbitrage-free interest-rate model (e.g., Ho-Lee) is calibrated to:', 'Economic equilibrium assumptions only', 'Fit the current observed yield curve exactly', 'Historical averages', 'Equity volatility', 'B', 'Arbitrage-free models are fit to today''s curve so they price on-the-run bonds without arbitrage.', 5),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-term-structure-q07', 'cfa2-fixed-income-term-structure', 'Equilibrium term-structure models (e.g., CIR, Vasicek) start from:', 'The current curve', 'Assumptions about economic factors and the short rate', 'Option prices', 'Credit ratings', 'B', 'Equilibrium models derive the curve from assumptions about the short-rate process and economic drivers.', 6),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-term-structure-q08', 'cfa2-fixed-income-term-structure', 'The swap spread is the difference between the swap rate and the:', 'Coupon rate', 'Government bond yield of the same maturity', 'Dividend yield', 'Risk-free real rate', 'B', 'The swap spread (swap rate minus matched-maturity government yield) reflects bank credit/liquidity conditions.', 7),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-term-structure-q09', 'cfa2-fixed-income-term-structure', 'Under the preferred-habitat theory, investors leave their preferred maturity only if:', 'Forced by regulators', 'Compensated by a sufficient premium', 'Rates are zero', 'Never', 'B', 'Preferred-habitat extends segmentation: investors will shift maturities for adequate extra yield.', 8),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-term-structure-q10', 'cfa2-fixed-income-term-structure', 'A ''steepening'' of the yield curve is a change primarily in its:', 'Level', 'Slope', 'Curvature', 'Credit spread', 'B', 'Steepening/flattening describes changes in the slope (the gap between long and short rates).', 9),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-term-structure-q11', 'cfa2-fixed-income-term-structure', 'Arbitrage-free models are particularly used to value:', 'Risk-free zero-coupon bonds only', 'Bonds with embedded options (on an interest-rate tree)', 'Equities', 'Commodities', 'B', 'Calibrated arbitrage-free trees value callable/putable bonds via backward induction.', 10),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-term-structure-q12', 'cfa2-fixed-income-term-structure', 'If the pure expectations theory holds and the curve is upward sloping, the market expects future short rates to:', 'Fall', 'Rise', 'Stay flat', 'Be negative', 'B', 'An upward-sloping curve under pure expectations implies expected increases in future short rates.', 11),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-cds-q01', 'cfa2-fixed-income-cds', 'In a CDS, the protection buyer:', 'Receives a premium and pays on default', 'Pays a periodic premium and is compensated on a credit event', 'Owns the reference bond always', 'Sets the recovery rate', 'B', 'The buyer pays the CDS spread and receives a payout if the reference entity has a credit event.', 0),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-cds-q02', 'cfa2-fixed-income-cds', 'A CDS payout on default is approximately notional times:', 'The recovery rate', '(1 − recovery rate)', 'The coupon', 'Duration', 'B', 'The protection payment compensates for loss given default = notional × (1 − recovery).', 1),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-cds-q03', 'cfa2-fixed-income-cds', 'Buying CDS protection is economically similar to:', 'Going long the credit', 'Going short the credit (shorting the bond)', 'Buying equity', 'Lending risk-free', 'B', 'Protection buyers profit when credit deteriorates, equivalent to a short credit position.', 2),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-cds-q04', 'cfa2-fixed-income-cds', 'Which is a standard CDS credit event?', 'A dividend increase', 'Bankruptcy, failure to pay, or restructuring', 'A stock split', 'A rating upgrade', 'B', 'Defined credit events typically include bankruptcy, failure to pay, and (sometimes) restructuring.', 3),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-cds-q05', 'cfa2-fixed-income-cds', 'The CDS upfront payment is approximately:', '(CDS spread − coupon) × duration', 'Notional × coupon', 'Recovery × notional', 'Zero always', 'A', 'Because CDS trade with standard coupons, the spread/coupon difference is settled upfront, scaled by duration.', 4),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-cds-q06', 'cfa2-fixed-income-cds', 'A single-name CDS references:', 'A basket of issuers', 'One specific issuer', 'An equity index', 'A currency', 'B', 'Single-name CDS cover one reference entity; index CDS (CDX/iTraxx) cover a basket.', 5),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-cds-q07', 'cfa2-fixed-income-cds', 'The CDS spread is approximately equal to:', 'Hazard rate × (1 − recovery)', 'The risk-free rate', 'Duration × convexity', 'The coupon rate', 'A', 'Intuitively the spread compensates for expected loss ≈ default intensity × loss given default.', 6),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-cds-q08', 'cfa2-fixed-income-cds', 'As a reference entity''s credit quality worsens, the value of a long-protection CDS position:', 'Falls', 'Rises', 'Is unchanged', 'Goes to zero', 'B', 'Widening spreads increase the mark-to-market value to the protection buyer.', 7),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-cds-q09', 'cfa2-fixed-income-cds', 'Index CDS products such as CDX and iTraxx allow investors to:', 'Trade a single bond', 'Take diversified credit exposure across many names', 'Buy equities', 'Hedge currency only', 'B', 'Index CDS give efficient long/short exposure to a basket of credits.', 8),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-cds-q10', 'cfa2-fixed-income-cds', 'The CDS-cash basis is the difference between the CDS spread and the:', 'Coupon', 'Cash bond''s credit spread', 'Risk-free rate', 'Dividend yield', 'B', 'The basis compares the CDS spread to the same issuer''s cash-bond spread; deviations create relative-value trades.', 9),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-cds-q11', 'cfa2-fixed-income-cds', 'A bond investor worried about default can hedge by:', 'Selling CDS protection', 'Buying CDS protection on the issuer', 'Buying more of the bond', 'Shorting Treasuries', 'B', 'Buying protection offsets the credit risk of the held bond.', 10),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-cds-q12', 'cfa2-fixed-income-cds', 'CDS enable ''synthetic'' credit exposure because they let investors take a view:', 'Only by owning the bond', 'Without buying or shorting the underlying bond', 'On equities only', 'Risk-free', 'B', 'Selling or buying protection creates long/short credit exposure without trading the cash bond.', 11),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-forwards-futures-q01', 'cfa2-derivatives-forwards-futures', 'Ignoring income and costs, the no-arbitrage forward price is:', 'S₀ / (1 + r)^T', 'S₀ × (1 + r)^T', 'S₀ − r', 'S₀ × r × T', 'B', 'Cost-of-carry: F₀ = S₀(1 + r)^T compounds the spot at the risk-free rate to delivery.', 0),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-forwards-futures-q02', 'cfa2-derivatives-forwards-futures', 'Carry benefits such as dividends or coupons ____ the forward price.', 'Raise', 'Lower', 'Do not affect', 'Double', 'B', 'Income accruing to the asset reduces the cost of carry, lowering the forward price.', 1),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-forwards-futures-q03', 'cfa2-derivatives-forwards-futures', 'Carry costs such as storage ____ the forward price.', 'Lower', 'Raise', 'Do not affect', 'Eliminate', 'B', 'Storage and similar costs add to carry, increasing the forward price.', 2),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-forwards-futures-q04', 'cfa2-derivatives-forwards-futures', 'At initiation, the value of a forward contract is:', 'Equal to the spot', 'Zero', 'Equal to the forward price', 'Negative', 'B', 'The forward price is set so neither party pays at inception; the contract''s value starts at zero.', 3),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-forwards-futures-q05', 'cfa2-derivatives-forwards-futures', 'The main difference between futures and forwards is that futures are:', 'Customized and OTC', 'Standardized, exchange-traded and marked-to-market daily', 'Free of margin', 'Settled only at maturity', 'B', 'Futures are standardized, cleared, and margined with daily settlement, reducing counterparty risk.', 4),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-forwards-futures-q06', 'cfa2-derivatives-forwards-futures', '''Contango'' describes a market where futures prices are:', 'Below spot', 'Above spot', 'Equal to spot', 'Negative', 'B', 'Contango: futures > spot, typical when carry costs dominate convenience yield.', 5),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-forwards-futures-q07', 'cfa2-derivatives-forwards-futures', '''Backwardation'' occurs when futures prices are:', 'Above spot', 'Below spot', 'Equal to spot', 'Zero', 'B', 'Backwardation: futures < spot, often due to a high convenience yield/benefits.', 6),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-forwards-futures-q08', 'cfa2-derivatives-forwards-futures', 'The value of a forward before expiry is approximately:', '(F_t − F₀) discounted to today', 'S₀ × r', 'The coupon', 'Always zero', 'A', 'The forward''s mark-to-market value is the PV of the difference between the current and contracted forward prices.', 7),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-forwards-futures-q09', 'cfa2-derivatives-forwards-futures', 'A convenience yield on a commodity behaves like a:', 'Carry cost (raises the forward)', 'Carry benefit (lowers the forward)', 'Tax', 'Margin call', 'B', 'The convenience yield is a benefit of holding the physical asset, reducing the forward price.', 8),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-forwards-futures-q10', 'cfa2-derivatives-forwards-futures', 'Daily mark-to-market on futures introduces, relative to forwards:', 'Counterparty risk', 'Interim cash flows (margin variation) that can cause a small price difference', 'No difference at all', 'Higher credit risk', 'B', 'When rates correlate with the asset, the timing of margin cash flows makes futures and forward prices differ slightly.', 9),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-forwards-futures-q11', 'cfa2-derivatives-forwards-futures', 'If the observed forward price exceeds the no-arbitrage price, an arbitrageur would:', 'Buy the forward and the asset', 'Sell the forward and buy the underlying (cash-and-carry)', 'Do nothing', 'Short the underlying only', 'B', 'An overpriced forward is sold while buying/holding the underlying — a cash-and-carry arbitrage.', 10),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-forwards-futures-q12', 'cfa2-derivatives-forwards-futures', 'With continuous compounding, the forward price is:', 'S₀ × e^(rT) adjusted for carry', 'S₀ − rT', 'S₀ / r', 'S₀ × r', 'A', 'Under continuous compounding, F₀ = S₀·e^((r − income + cost)T).', 11),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-income-taxes-q01', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-income-taxes', 'A deferred tax liability arises when taxable income is currently ____ accounting income.', 'Greater than', 'Less than', 'Equal to', 'Unrelated to', 'B', 'When tax paid now is lower than book tax expense (e.g., accelerated tax depreciation), a DTL builds up.', 0),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-income-taxes-q02', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-income-taxes', 'A temporary difference is one that:', 'Never reverses', 'Reverses in future periods, creating a DTA or DTL', 'Affects only the effective tax rate', 'Is a permanent exemption', 'B', 'Temporary differences reverse over time and give rise to deferred tax assets/liabilities.', 1),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-income-taxes-q03', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-income-taxes', 'Tax-exempt interest income is an example of a:', 'Temporary difference', 'Permanent difference', 'Deferred tax asset', 'Deferred tax liability', 'B', 'Permanent differences (like tax-exempt income) never reverse and alter the effective tax rate, not deferred taxes.', 2),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-income-taxes-q04', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-income-taxes', 'A net operating loss carryforward typically creates a:', 'Deferred tax liability', 'Deferred tax asset', 'Permanent difference', 'Goodwill', 'B', 'Loss carryforwards can offset future taxable income, so they are recognized as deferred tax assets.', 3),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-income-taxes-q05', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-income-taxes', 'Under US GAAP, a valuation allowance is recorded against a DTA when:', 'Realization is more likely than not to fail', 'The DTA will certainly be used', 'There is a DTL', 'Tax rates rise', 'A', 'A valuation allowance reduces a DTA to the amount expected to be realized when realization is doubtful.', 4),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-income-taxes-q06', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-income-taxes', 'Accelerated depreciation for tax versus straight-line for books initially produces a:', 'Deferred tax asset', 'Deferred tax liability', 'Permanent difference', 'No difference', 'B', 'Higher early tax depreciation lowers current taxable income relative to book, creating a DTL that reverses later.', 5),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-income-taxes-q07', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-income-taxes', 'A reduction in the statutory tax rate will, for a company with a net DTL:', 'Increase the DTL', 'Decrease the DTL (a one-time gain to tax expense)', 'Have no effect', 'Create a DTA', 'B', 'Remeasuring a DTL at a lower rate reduces it, lowering income tax expense in the period of change.', 6),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-income-taxes-q08', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-income-taxes', 'Income tax expense equals taxes payable plus:', 'ΔDTL − ΔDTA', 'ΔDTA − ΔDTL', 'Dividends', 'Depreciation', 'A', 'Tax expense = current taxes payable + the change in deferred tax liabilities − the change in deferred tax assets.', 7),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-income-taxes-q09', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-income-taxes', 'For analysis, a DTL expected to keep growing and never reverse is often treated as:', 'Debt', 'Equity', 'A current asset', 'Revenue', 'B', 'If a DTL is not expected to reverse, analysts may reclassify it toward equity rather than a liability.', 8),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-income-taxes-q10', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-income-taxes', 'Permanent differences affect a company''s:', 'Deferred tax balances', 'Effective tax rate', 'Revenue recognition', 'Inventory method', 'B', 'Because they never reverse, permanent differences change the effective tax rate, not deferred taxes.', 9),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-income-taxes-q11', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-income-taxes', 'A warranty expense accrued for books but deductible only when paid creates a:', 'Deferred tax liability', 'Deferred tax asset', 'Permanent difference', 'Goodwill', 'B', 'Book expense recognized before the tax deduction means more tax is paid now, creating a DTA that reverses when paid.', 10),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-income-taxes-q12', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-income-taxes', 'Deferred tax assets and liabilities are measured using the:', 'Historical tax rate', 'Tax rate expected to apply when the difference reverses', 'Risk-free rate', 'Average industry rate', 'B', 'Deferred taxes use the enacted/expected future tax rate applicable when the temporary difference reverses.', 11),
  ('cfa2-equity-industry-analysis-q01', 'cfa2-equity-industry-analysis', 'Porter''s Five Forces include rivalry, new entrants, substitutes, supplier power and:', 'Buyer (customer) power', 'Inflation', 'The risk-free rate', 'Dividend policy', 'A', 'The fifth force is the bargaining power of buyers/customers.', 0),
  ('cfa2-equity-industry-analysis-q02', 'cfa2-equity-industry-analysis', 'A defensive (non-cyclical) industry is characterized by demand that is:', 'Highly sensitive to the business cycle', 'Relatively stable across the business cycle', 'Always declining', 'Zero', 'B', 'Defensive industries (e.g., utilities, staples) have stable demand regardless of the economic cycle.', 1),
  ('cfa2-equity-industry-analysis-q03', 'cfa2-equity-industry-analysis', 'In the growth stage of the industry life cycle, firms typically experience:', 'Declining demand', 'Rapid demand growth and often falling prices', 'Stable mature margins', 'Liquidation', 'B', 'The growth phase sees fast volume growth, new entrants, and price declines as scale builds.', 2),
  ('cfa2-equity-industry-analysis-q04', 'cfa2-equity-industry-analysis', 'High barriers to entry tend to:', 'Reduce incumbents'' pricing power', 'Protect incumbents and support higher, more durable margins', 'Guarantee losses', 'Eliminate rivalry', 'B', 'Entry barriers shield incumbents from new competition, supporting profitability.', 3),
  ('cfa2-equity-industry-analysis-q05', 'cfa2-equity-industry-analysis', 'A highly concentrated industry (few large players) generally has:', 'Less pricing power', 'More pricing power', 'No competitive advantage', 'Zero margins', 'B', 'Fewer competitors typically means greater pricing power and less destructive rivalry.', 4),
  ('cfa2-equity-industry-analysis-q06', 'cfa2-equity-industry-analysis', 'Porter''s generic competitive strategies are cost leadership, focus and:', 'Differentiation', 'Diversification', 'Vertical integration', 'Hedging', 'A', 'The three generic strategies are cost leadership, differentiation, and focus.', 5),
  ('cfa2-equity-industry-analysis-q07', 'cfa2-equity-industry-analysis', 'The experience (learning) curve implies that unit costs:', 'Rise with cumulative output', 'Fall with cumulative output', 'Are constant', 'Are random', 'B', 'Cumulative production experience lowers unit costs, advantaging high-volume incumbents.', 6),
  ('cfa2-equity-industry-analysis-q08', 'cfa2-equity-industry-analysis', 'A firm can sustain returns above its cost of capital only with a durable:', 'Dividend', 'Competitive advantage (moat)', 'Share buyback', 'Tax loss', 'B', 'Without a moat, competition erodes excess returns toward the cost of capital.', 7),
  ('cfa2-equity-industry-analysis-q09', 'cfa2-equity-industry-analysis', 'Strong supplier power in an industry tends to:', 'Raise input costs and squeeze margins', 'Lower input costs', 'Have no effect', 'Eliminate rivalry', 'A', 'Powerful suppliers can charge more, compressing the industry''s profitability.', 8),
  ('cfa2-equity-industry-analysis-q10', 'cfa2-equity-industry-analysis', 'The threat of substitutes is greatest when:', 'Substitutes are costly and inferior', 'Close substitutes are cheap and readily available', 'There are no substitutes', 'Switching costs are high', 'B', 'Cheap, comparable substitutes cap prices and limit industry profitability.', 9),
  ('cfa2-equity-industry-analysis-q11', 'cfa2-equity-industry-analysis', 'Industry analysis feeds equity valuation primarily by informing forecasts of:', 'Revenue growth, margins and reinvestment', 'The risk-free rate', 'The number of shares', 'Dividend dates', 'A', 'Industry structure shapes the revenue, margin, and reinvestment assumptions in a DCF.', 10),
  ('cfa2-equity-industry-analysis-q12', 'cfa2-equity-industry-analysis', 'A mature-stage industry is typically characterized by:', 'Explosive growth', 'Consolidation and stable margins', 'No competition', 'Rapid new entry', 'B', 'Maturity brings slower growth, consolidation, and relatively stable margins among survivors.', 11),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-option-strategies-q01', 'cfa2-derivatives-option-strategies', 'A covered call position consists of:', 'Long stock and a short call', 'Long stock and a long put', 'Two long calls', 'A short put only', 'A', 'A covered call is long the underlying plus a written (short) call, earning premium while capping upside.', 0),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-option-strategies-q02', 'cfa2-derivatives-option-strategies', 'A protective put provides:', 'Income with capped upside', 'A downside floor while keeping upside', 'Unlimited downside', 'No effect on risk', 'B', 'Buying a put on a held asset sets a loss floor while retaining upside (less the premium).', 1),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-option-strategies-q03', 'cfa2-derivatives-option-strategies', 'A long straddle profits when the underlying:', 'Stays flat', 'Makes a large move in either direction', 'Rises only', 'Pays a dividend', 'B', 'A long straddle (call + put at the same strike) is long volatility and gains on large moves up or down.', 2),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-option-strategies-q04', 'cfa2-derivatives-option-strategies', 'A collar combines a long put and a:', 'Short call (to finance the put)', 'Long call', 'Short stock', 'Long bond', 'A', 'A collar buys a protective put and sells a call to offset its cost, bounding both downside and upside.', 3),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-option-strategies-q05', 'cfa2-derivatives-option-strategies', 'Delta measures option sensitivity to a change in:', 'Volatility', 'The underlying''s price', 'Time', 'Interest rates', 'B', 'Delta is the change in option value per unit change in the underlying — the hedge ratio.', 4),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-option-strategies-q06', 'cfa2-derivatives-option-strategies', 'Vega is largest for options that are:', 'Deep in the money and short-dated', 'At the money and longer-dated', 'Already expired', 'Deep out of the money', 'B', 'Vega (volatility sensitivity) peaks for at-the-money, longer-maturity options.', 5),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-option-strategies-q07', 'cfa2-derivatives-option-strategies', 'Theta generally represents an option''s:', 'Gain from volatility', 'Time decay (loss of value as expiry nears)', 'Rate sensitivity', 'Delta', 'B', 'Theta measures the erosion of option value with the passage of time.', 6),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-option-strategies-q08', 'cfa2-derivatives-option-strategies', 'A bull call spread is constructed by:', 'Buying a lower-strike call and selling a higher-strike call', 'Selling both calls', 'Buying two puts', 'Shorting the stock', 'A', 'A bull call spread buys a lower-strike call and writes a higher-strike call — bullish, with limited risk and reward.', 7),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-option-strategies-q09', 'cfa2-derivatives-option-strategies', 'A long butterfly spread profits most when the underlying:', 'Moves sharply', 'Stays near the middle strike at expiry', 'Falls to zero', 'Doubles', 'B', 'A long butterfly is a short-volatility strategy that pays off when the price stays near the central strike.', 8),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-option-strategies-q10', 'cfa2-derivatives-option-strategies', 'Gamma is largest for options that are:', 'At the money near expiration', 'Deep in the money', 'Long-dated and far OTM', 'Expired', 'A', 'Gamma (rate of change of delta) peaks for at-the-money options close to expiry.', 9),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-option-strategies-q11', 'cfa2-derivatives-option-strategies', 'A covered call''s maximum gain occurs when the stock at expiry is:', 'Far below the strike', 'At or above the call''s strike', 'Zero', 'Irrelevant', 'B', 'Upside is capped at the strike; max gain = (strike − purchase price) + premium received.', 10),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-option-strategies-q12', 'cfa2-derivatives-option-strategies', 'A delta-neutral hedge must be rebalanced because of:', 'Rho', 'Gamma (delta changes as the underlying moves)', 'Theta only', 'The strike price', 'B', 'Gamma causes delta to change with the underlying, so a static hedge drifts and needs dynamic rebalancing.', 11),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-simulation-q01', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-simulation', 'Monte Carlo simulation produces a:', 'Single point estimate', 'Distribution of possible outcomes', 'Risk-free rate', 'Closed-form solution', 'B', 'By running many random scenarios, Monte Carlo builds a full distribution of outcomes.', 0),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-simulation-q02', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-simulation', 'A key limitation of Monte Carlo simulation is that it:', 'Cannot handle complexity', 'Is only as reliable as its input assumptions', 'Requires no data', 'Gives an analytical answer', 'B', 'Results depend entirely on the assumed input distributions and relationships (''garbage in, garbage out'').', 1),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-simulation-q03', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-simulation', 'Monte Carlo is especially valuable for valuing instruments with:', 'Linear payoffs only', 'Complex, path-dependent or non-linear payoffs', 'No risk', 'Fixed coupons', 'B', 'Simulation handles path-dependency and non-linearity that closed-form models cannot easily address.', 2),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-simulation-q04', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-simulation', 'Bootstrapping estimates a sampling distribution by:', 'Assuming normality', 'Repeatedly resampling, with replacement, from observed data', 'Using a single sample once', 'Ignoring the data', 'B', 'Bootstrapping resamples the observed data many times to approximate the sampling distribution and standard errors.', 3),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-simulation-q05', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-simulation', 'Historical simulation differs from Monte Carlo in that it:', 'Assumes a parametric distribution', 'Resamples actual past data without a distribution assumption', 'Cannot use real data', 'Is purely analytical', 'B', 'Historical simulation reuses the empirical distribution of past observations rather than a modeled one.', 4),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-simulation-q06', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-simulation', 'Bayes'' formula is used to:', 'Update a prior probability with new evidence', 'Compute duration', 'Estimate beta', 'Price a bond', 'A', 'Bayes'' rule revises a prior probability given new information: P(A|B) = P(B|A)P(A)/P(B).', 5),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-simulation-q07', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-simulation', 'Sensitivity analysis differs from scenario analysis in that sensitivity analysis:', 'Changes many inputs at once', 'Varies one input at a time', 'Uses no inputs', 'Is a simulation', 'B', 'Sensitivity analysis changes a single variable; scenario analysis changes a coherent set of variables together.', 6),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-simulation-q08', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-simulation', 'Ignoring correlations among inputs in a simulation will most likely:', 'Have no effect', 'Misstate the portfolio''s risk', 'Eliminate risk', 'Guarantee accuracy', 'B', 'Correlations drive aggregate risk; omitting them biases the simulated outcome distribution.', 7),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-simulation-q09', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-simulation', 'Increasing the number of Monte Carlo trials primarily improves:', 'The validity of the assumptions', 'The convergence/precision of the estimate', 'The input data quality', 'The risk-free rate', 'B', 'More trials reduce simulation noise so the estimate converges, but cannot fix flawed assumptions.', 8),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-simulation-q10', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-simulation', 'A retirement-wealth projection that accounts for uncertain returns is best done with:', 'A single deterministic forecast', 'Monte Carlo simulation', 'A coupon calculation', 'A trend line', 'B', 'Monte Carlo captures the distribution of possible wealth paths under uncertain returns.', 9),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-simulation-q11', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-simulation', 'An advantage of Monte Carlo over an analytical (closed-form) model is its ability to:', 'Always be faster', 'Incorporate many interacting variables and constraints flexibly', 'Avoid assumptions entirely', 'Guarantee the true answer', 'B', 'Simulation flexibly models complex, multi-variable problems that lack tractable closed-form solutions.', 10),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-simulation-q12', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-simulation', 'In Monte Carlo, random draws are generated from:', 'The realized future', 'Specified probability distributions for the inputs', 'A single historical value', 'The benchmark only', 'B', 'Each input is sampled from an assumed probability distribution to create scenarios.', 11),
  ('cfa2-economics-regulation-q01', 'cfa2-economics-regulation', 'A natural monopoly is a market failure best addressed by:', 'Encouraging many small entrants', 'Price/economic regulation of the single efficient provider', 'Banning the product', 'No intervention', 'B', 'When one provider is most efficient, regulators often control prices/entry rather than force competition.', 0),
  ('cfa2-economics-regulation-q02', 'cfa2-economics-regulation', 'An externality is an example of a:', 'Market failure justifying regulation', 'Perfectly efficient market', 'Tax shelter', 'Risk-free asset', 'A', 'Externalities (costs/benefits not borne by the transacting parties) are a classic rationale for regulation.', 1),
  ('cfa2-economics-regulation-q03', 'cfa2-economics-regulation', '''Regulatory capture'' describes a situation where regulators:', 'Serve the public exclusively', 'Come to serve the interests of the regulated industry', 'Are abolished', 'Set zero rules', 'B', 'Capture occurs when the regulated industry unduly influences its regulator''s decisions.', 2),
  ('cfa2-economics-regulation-q04', 'cfa2-economics-regulation', 'Firms exploiting differences in rules across jurisdictions engage in:', 'Regulatory arbitrage', 'Tax-loss harvesting', 'Index rebalancing', 'Greenmail', 'A', 'Regulatory arbitrage shifts activity to where rules are most favorable.', 3),
  ('cfa2-economics-regulation-q05', 'cfa2-economics-regulation', 'Prudential regulation primarily aims to ensure the:', 'Safety and soundness of financial institutions', 'Maximum number of competitors', 'Lowest possible prices', 'Highest taxes', 'A', 'Prudential regulation focuses on the stability/solvency of financial firms and the system.', 4),
  ('cfa2-economics-regulation-q06', 'cfa2-economics-regulation', 'A self-regulating organization (SRO) is one that:', 'Has no authority', 'Is granted regulatory authority over its members (e.g., an exchange)', 'Replaces all government', 'Only sets taxes', 'B', 'SROs (like exchanges) are private bodies delegated authority to regulate their members.', 5),
  ('cfa2-economics-regulation-q07', 'cfa2-economics-regulation', 'Disclosure requirements address which market failure?', 'Natural monopoly', 'Information asymmetry', 'Public goods', 'Externalities', 'B', 'Mandatory disclosure reduces information asymmetry between firms and investors/consumers.', 6),
  ('cfa2-economics-regulation-q08', 'cfa2-economics-regulation', 'When assessing a regulation, analysts weigh its benefits against its:', 'Compliance costs and unintended consequences', 'Dividend yield', 'Beta', 'Coupon', 'A', 'Sound regulatory analysis is cost-benefit, accounting for compliance burdens and side effects.', 7),
  ('cfa2-economics-regulation-q09', 'cfa2-economics-regulation', 'Regulation that raises barriers to entry tends to:', 'Reduce incumbents'' profitability', 'Protect incumbents'' pricing power', 'Have no valuation impact', 'Lower all prices', 'B', 'Higher regulatory entry barriers can shield incumbents, affecting industry economics and valuation.', 8),
  ('cfa2-economics-regulation-q10', 'cfa2-economics-regulation', 'A public good (e.g., national defense) is under-provided by markets because it is:', 'Excludable and rival', 'Non-excludable and non-rival', 'Highly profitable', 'Easy to price', 'B', 'Non-excludable, non-rival goods invite free-riding, so markets under-provide them — a regulation rationale.', 9),
  ('cfa2-economics-regulation-q11', 'cfa2-economics-regulation', 'Administrative regulations are issued by:', 'Legislatures', 'Government agencies', 'Courts', 'Shareholders', 'B', 'Agencies issue administrative regulations under authority delegated by statute.', 10),
  ('cfa2-economics-regulation-q12', 'cfa2-economics-regulation', 'From an investment standpoint, a major regulatory change is significant because it:', 'Never affects companies', 'Can alter industry structure, costs and risk — hence valuation', 'Only changes accounting', 'Is irrelevant to risk', 'B', 'Regulation reshapes barriers, pricing, and risk, feeding directly into cash-flow and discount-rate assumptions.', 11),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-commodities-q01', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-commodities', 'Most commodity investors gain exposure through:', 'Holding the physical spot commodity', 'Futures contracts', 'Dividends', 'Coupons', 'B', 'Because physical storage is costly/impractical, investors usually use commodity futures.', 0),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-commodities-q02', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-commodities', 'The total return of a commodity futures position is price return, collateral return and:', 'Roll return', 'Dividend return', 'Coupon return', 'Tax return', 'A', 'Futures total return = price return + roll return + collateral (cash) return.', 1),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-commodities-q03', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-commodities', 'A market in backwardation (futures below spot) produces a roll return that is:', 'Positive', 'Negative', 'Zero', 'Undefined', 'A', 'Rolling up toward a higher spot in backwardation generates a positive roll yield.', 2),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-commodities-q04', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-commodities', 'Contango (futures above spot) generates a roll return that is:', 'Positive', 'Negative', 'Zero', 'Equal to the coupon', 'B', 'Rolling into successively cheaper-to-hold-but-higher-priced contracts in contango creates a negative roll yield.', 3),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-commodities-q05', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-commodities', 'The theory of storage relates futures prices to spot plus storage cost minus the:', 'Convenience yield', 'Dividend yield', 'Risk-free rate', 'Beta', 'A', 'Futures ≈ spot + storage − convenience yield; a high convenience yield pushes toward backwardation.', 4),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-commodities-q06', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-commodities', 'The insurance/hedging-pressure theory suggests producers selling futures cause prices to sit:', 'Above expected spot', 'Below expected future spot (normal backwardation)', 'At the risk-free rate', 'At zero', 'B', 'Producer hedging pressure depresses futures below the expected spot, compensating long speculators.', 5),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-commodities-q07', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-commodities', 'Commodities are often added to portfolios as a(n):', 'Inflation hedge and diversifier', 'Source of dividends', 'Risk-free asset', 'Duration hedge', 'A', 'Commodities tend to rise with unexpected inflation and diversify stock/bond portfolios.', 6),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-commodities-q08', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-commodities', 'Unlike stocks and bonds, commodities provide:', 'No income (dividends or coupons)', 'Guaranteed coupons', 'Voting rights', 'A fixed maturity value', 'A', 'Commodities generate no cash income; return comes from price, roll, and collateral yield.', 7),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-commodities-q09', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-commodities', 'The collateral return component of a futures strategy comes from:', 'The cash held to back the futures', 'Dividends on the commodity', 'The convenience yield', 'Storage costs', 'A', 'Futures require little cash outlay, so the backing collateral earns a (cash/T-bill) yield.', 8),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-commodities-q10', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-commodities', 'A high convenience yield typically signals:', 'Abundant supply and contango', 'Tight supply and a tendency toward backwardation', 'Zero demand', 'Negative prices', 'B', 'Scarcity raises the convenience yield of holding the physical, pushing the curve toward backwardation.', 9),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-commodities-q11', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-commodities', 'Commodity returns are primarily driven by:', 'Dividend policy', 'Supply-demand dynamics and the futures-curve shape', 'Share buybacks', 'Credit ratings', 'B', 'Physical supply/demand and the resulting curve (roll yield) drive commodity futures returns.', 10),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-commodities-q12', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-commodities', 'Major commodity sub-sectors include energy, metals, livestock and:', 'Agriculture', 'Equities', 'Government bonds', 'Currencies', 'A', 'Commodity indices span energy, industrial/precious metals, agriculture, and livestock.', 11),
  ('cfa2-ethics-gips-q01', 'cfa2-ethics-gips', 'GIPS compliance is claimed on a:', 'Composite basis', 'Firm-wide basis', 'Per-client basis', 'Per-security basis', 'B', 'A firm either complies with GIPS firm-wide or not at all; partial compliance is prohibited.', 0),
  ('cfa2-ethics-gips-q02', 'cfa2-ethics-gips', 'A GIPS composite must include all:', 'Best-performing portfolios', 'Actual fee-paying, discretionary portfolios of a similar strategy', 'Model portfolios', 'Terminated accounts only', 'B', 'Composites group every actual fee-paying discretionary portfolio of a strategy to prevent cherry-picking.', 1),
  ('cfa2-ethics-gips-q03', 'cfa2-ethics-gips', 'GIPS requires performance to be reported using ____ returns.', 'Money-weighted', 'Time-weighted', 'Simple price', 'Nominal coupon', 'B', 'Time-weighted returns remove the effect of client cash-flow timing, the GIPS default.', 2),
  ('cfa2-ethics-gips-q04', 'cfa2-ethics-gips', 'When first claiming compliance, a firm must show at least ____ of compliant history.', '1 year', '5 years (or since inception)', '20 years', 'No history', 'B', 'A minimum of five years (or since inception) is required, building toward ten years.', 3),
  ('cfa2-ethics-gips-q05', 'cfa2-ethics-gips', 'GIPS verification is:', 'Mandatory', 'Voluntary, firm-wide, and performed by an independent third party', 'Done per composite by CFA Institute', 'Prohibited', 'B', 'Verification is recommended (not required), covers the whole firm, and is done by an independent verifier.', 4),
  ('cfa2-ethics-gips-q06', 'cfa2-ethics-gips', 'Non-discretionary portfolios are:', 'Required in composites', 'Excluded from composites', 'The only ones included', 'Reported separately as fact', 'B', 'Only discretionary portfolios belong in composites; client-imposed constraints make accounts non-discretionary.', 5),
  ('cfa2-ethics-gips-q07', 'cfa2-ethics-gips', 'The primary purpose of GIPS is to ensure:', 'Higher returns', 'Fair representation and full disclosure of performance', 'Lower fees', 'Tax efficiency', 'B', 'GIPS standardizes performance presentation for fairness and transparency to prospective clients.', 6),
  ('cfa2-ethics-gips-q08', 'cfa2-ethics-gips', 'Including only the firm''s surviving, successful portfolios would introduce:', 'Survivorship bias (which GIPS prevents via composites)', 'No bias', 'A tax', 'A discount', 'A', 'Composite construction including all relevant portfolios prevents survivorship and selection bias.', 7),
  ('cfa2-ethics-gips-q09', 'cfa2-ethics-gips', 'GIPS presentations must include a benchmark and a measure of:', 'Dispersion', 'Beta only', 'Duration', 'Tax rate', 'A', 'Composites show an appropriate benchmark and internal dispersion of portfolio returns.', 8),
  ('cfa2-ethics-gips-q10', 'cfa2-ethics-gips', 'Presenting deliberately misleading performance figures:', 'Is allowed if disclosed', 'Violates GIPS'' fair-representation principle', 'Is required', 'Has no consequence', 'B', 'GIPS prohibits false or misleading performance and requires full, fair disclosure.', 9),
  ('cfa2-ethics-gips-q11', 'cfa2-ethics-gips', 'If local law conflicts with GIPS, a firm should:', 'Ignore the law', 'Follow the law and disclose the conflict', 'Abandon GIPS entirely', 'Do nothing', 'B', 'Where law conflicts with GIPS, comply with law and disclose the conflict.', 10),
  ('cfa2-ethics-gips-q12', 'cfa2-ethics-gips', 'GIPS standards are best described as:', 'Mandatory regulations', 'Voluntary ethical performance-presentation standards', 'Tax rules', 'Accounting principles', 'B', 'GIPS are voluntary, ethical standards for presenting investment performance.', 11),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-mergers-acquisitions-q01', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-mergers-acquisitions', 'A merger between two firms in the same industry and stage of production is:', 'Vertical', 'Horizontal', 'Conglomerate', 'A spin-off', 'B', 'Horizontal mergers combine competitors in the same business, often for synergies or market power.', 0),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-mergers-acquisitions-q02', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-mergers-acquisitions', 'A vertical merger combines firms that are:', 'Direct competitors', 'At different stages of the same supply chain', 'In unrelated industries', 'Identical', 'B', 'Vertical mergers join a company with a supplier or customer along its value chain.', 1),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-mergers-acquisitions-q03', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-mergers-acquisitions', 'In an all-stock deal, the target shareholders:', 'Bear none of the deal risk', 'Share in the combined firm''s risk and reward', 'Receive only cash', 'Lose all upside', 'B', 'Stock consideration gives target holders an ongoing stake, sharing post-merger risk/reward with the acquirer.', 2),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-mergers-acquisitions-q04', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-mergers-acquisitions', 'A ''poison pill'' is a:', 'Takeover defense that makes acquisition more costly', 'Type of dividend', 'Accounting method', 'Synergy estimate', 'A', 'A poison pill lets existing holders buy shares cheaply if a raider crosses a threshold, deterring hostile bids.', 3),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-mergers-acquisitions-q05', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-mergers-acquisitions', 'The gain to target shareholders in an acquisition is generally the:', 'Premium paid over the pre-bid price', 'Entire synergy', 'Acquirer''s market cap', 'Risk-free rate', 'A', 'Target holders capture the takeover premium; acquirer gains = synergies − premium.', 4),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-mergers-acquisitions-q06', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-mergers-acquisitions', 'The ''winner''s curse'' in M&A refers to:', 'Underpaying for a target', 'Overpaying such that the premium exceeds the synergies', 'Avoiding all deals', 'Paying in cash', 'B', 'Aggressive bidding can lead the acquirer to overpay, destroying its own shareholders'' value.', 5),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-mergers-acquisitions-q07', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-mergers-acquisitions', 'Comparable transaction analysis includes a control premium because it uses:', 'Minority public prices', 'Prices actually paid to acquire whole companies', 'Book values', 'Risk-free rates', 'B', 'Transaction comps reflect prices paid for control, so they embed a control premium.', 6),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-mergers-acquisitions-q08', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-mergers-acquisitions', 'A spin-off is a form of:', 'Acquisition', 'Corporate restructuring/divestiture', 'Dividend reinvestment', 'Stock split', 'B', 'A spin-off separates a business unit into an independent company — a restructuring/divestiture.', 7),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-mergers-acquisitions-q09', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-mergers-acquisitions', 'Antitrust regulators assess a horizontal merger partly using the:', 'Herfindahl-Hirschman Index (HHI)', 'Sharpe ratio', 'Dividend yield', 'Duration', 'A', 'The HHI measures market concentration; large increases can trigger antitrust scrutiny.', 8),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-mergers-acquisitions-q10', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-mergers-acquisitions', 'Managerial ''hubris'' as a merger motive tends to:', 'Increase deal discipline', 'Lead to overpaying for targets', 'Reduce premiums', 'Improve synergies', 'B', 'Overconfident managers may overestimate synergies and overpay, harming acquirer value.', 9),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-mergers-acquisitions-q11', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-mergers-acquisitions', 'Inviting a friendly ''white knight'' is a response to a:', 'Dividend cut', 'Hostile takeover bid', 'Stock split', 'Bond default', 'B', 'A white knight is a preferred acquirer sought to fend off a hostile bidder.', 10),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-mergers-acquisitions-q12', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-mergers-acquisitions', 'A cash offer, versus a stock offer, transfers more of the deal''s risk and reward to the:', 'Target shareholders', 'Acquirer', 'Regulators', 'Bondholders', 'B', 'With cash, target holders exit at a fixed price and the acquirer bears all subsequent risk/reward.', 11),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-trading-execution-q01', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-trading-execution', 'Which is an implicit trading cost?', 'Commission', 'Market impact', 'Exchange fee', 'Transaction tax', 'B', 'Market impact is implicit; commissions, fees and taxes are explicit costs.', 0),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-trading-execution-q02', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-trading-execution', 'Implementation shortfall measures the gap between:', 'Two benchmarks', 'A paper-portfolio return and the actual executed return', 'Beta and alpha', 'Bid and ask only', 'B', 'Implementation shortfall captures total cost as the difference between a decision-price portfolio and the real one.', 1),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-trading-execution-q03', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-trading-execution', 'A market order prioritizes:', 'Price control', 'Immediacy of execution (at the cost of impact)', 'Avoiding execution', 'Zero cost', 'B', 'Market orders execute immediately but pay the spread/impact; limit orders control price but risk non-execution.', 2),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-trading-execution-q04', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-trading-execution', 'A limit order''s main risk is:', 'Paying high impact', 'Non-execution if the price isn''t reached', 'Immediate fill', 'Zero risk', 'B', 'Limit orders may not fill, creating opportunity cost if the price moves away.', 3),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-trading-execution-q05', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-trading-execution', 'VWAP is a benchmark comparing fills to the:', 'Closing price', 'Volume-weighted average price over the trading period', 'Risk-free rate', 'Prior day''s open', 'B', 'VWAP measures execution quality against the volume-weighted average traded price.', 4),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-trading-execution-q06', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-trading-execution', 'An implementation-shortfall algorithm explicitly trades off market impact against:', 'Dividends', 'Timing (price) risk', 'Beta', 'Duration', 'B', 'IS algos balance trading fast (impact) against trading slow (price-drift/timing risk).', 5),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-trading-execution-q07', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-trading-execution', 'Slicing a large order into smaller child orders aims to:', 'Increase market impact', 'Reduce market impact', 'Raise commissions', 'Guarantee a fill', 'B', 'Breaking up a large order limits the price impact of demanding too much liquidity at once.', 6),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-trading-execution-q08', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-trading-execution', 'Corridor (percentage-range) rebalancing triggers a trade when:', 'A set date arrives', 'A weight breaches its tolerance band', 'A dividend is paid', 'Volatility is zero', 'B', 'Corridor rebalancing acts on drift past a band rather than on the calendar.', 7),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-trading-execution-q09', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-trading-execution', 'Rebalancing corridors should be wider for assets with:', 'Higher transaction costs', 'Higher volatility', 'Higher correlation only', 'Lower risk tolerance', 'A', 'Higher trading costs justify wider bands; higher volatility argues for narrower bands.', 8),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-trading-execution-q10', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-trading-execution', 'Disciplined rebalancing is inherently a ____ strategy.', 'Momentum', 'Contrarian', 'Buy-and-hold', 'Risk-free', 'B', 'Rebalancing sells winners and buys losers — a contrarian action that can earn a diversification return.', 9),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-trading-execution-q11', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-trading-execution', 'The opportunity-cost component of implementation shortfall arises from:', 'Commissions', 'Unfilled portions of an order as the price moves away', 'Taxes', 'The spread only', 'B', 'Shares left unexecuted whose price moves adversely create a missed-trade (opportunity) cost.', 10),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-trading-execution-q12', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-trading-execution', 'A more aggressive (liquidity-demanding) execution generally has:', 'Higher market impact but lower timing risk', 'Lower impact and lower timing risk', 'No cost', 'Higher opportunity cost', 'A', 'Trading quickly raises impact cost but reduces exposure to adverse price drift.', 11),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-analysis-techniques-q01', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-analysis-techniques', 'The 3-way DuPont decomposition expresses ROE as net margin × asset turnover ×:', 'Financial leverage', 'The tax rate', 'Interest coverage', 'The current ratio', 'A', 'ROE = net profit margin × total asset turnover × financial leverage (assets/equity).', 0),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-analysis-techniques-q02', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-analysis-techniques', 'Common-size income statements express each line item as a percentage of:', 'Total assets', 'Revenue (sales)', 'Equity', 'Net income', 'B', 'Common-size income statements scale items by revenue; common-size balance sheets scale by total assets.', 1),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-analysis-techniques-q03', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-analysis-techniques', 'A large and growing gap between net income and operating cash flow suggests:', 'High earnings quality', 'Lower earnings quality (high accruals)', 'No information', 'A tax benefit', 'B', 'Earnings well above operating cash flow indicate accrual-heavy, less sustainable results.', 2),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-analysis-techniques-q04', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-analysis-techniques', 'The interest coverage ratio is a measure of:', 'Liquidity', 'Solvency', 'Profitability', 'Activity', 'B', 'Interest coverage (EBIT/interest) gauges the ability to service debt — a solvency metric.', 3),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-analysis-techniques-q05', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-analysis-techniques', 'Rising days sales outstanding (DSO) with flat sales may indicate:', 'Faster collections', 'Aggressive revenue recognition or collection problems', 'Lower inventory', 'Higher cash', 'B', 'Receivables growing faster than sales can signal channel stuffing or deteriorating collections — an earnings-quality concern.', 4),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-analysis-techniques-q06', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-analysis-techniques', 'The 5-way DuPont model adds which effects to the analysis?', 'Tax burden and interest burden', 'Dividend and buyback', 'Beta and alpha', 'Duration and convexity', 'A', 'The 5-way decomposition isolates tax burden × interest burden × EBIT margin × asset turnover × leverage.', 5),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-analysis-techniques-q07', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-analysis-techniques', 'Total asset turnover measures:', 'Profit per share', 'Revenue generated per unit of assets', 'Debt per equity', 'Cash per liability', 'B', 'Asset turnover = revenue / average total assets — an efficiency (activity) ratio.', 6),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-analysis-techniques-q08', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-analysis-techniques', 'Capitalizing costs that should be expensed will, in the current year, tend to:', 'Lower earnings', 'Inflate earnings and assets (an earnings-quality red flag)', 'Have no effect', 'Reduce assets', 'B', 'Improper capitalization shifts an expense to the balance sheet, boosting current income — a quality warning sign.', 7),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-analysis-techniques-q09', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-analysis-techniques', 'High-quality earnings are best described as:', 'Large one-time gains', 'Sustainable and backed by cash flow', 'Maximized by accruals', 'Volatile', 'B', 'Quality earnings are repeatable and supported by operating cash, not one-offs or aggressive accruals.', 8),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-analysis-techniques-q10', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-analysis-techniques', 'Ratios are most meaningful when compared against:', 'Nothing', 'Peers and the firm''s own historical trend', 'The risk-free rate', 'Random benchmarks', 'B', 'Context from peer and historical comparisons turns a raw ratio into a useful signal.', 9),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-analysis-techniques-q11', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-analysis-techniques', 'The accruals ratio is used to assess:', 'Liquidity', 'Earnings quality', 'Currency risk', 'Duration', 'B', 'A high accruals ratio (accruals relative to earnings/assets) signals lower earnings quality.', 10),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-analysis-techniques-q12', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-analysis-techniques', 'Before forecasting, analysts should adjust statements for:', 'Off-balance-sheet items and non-recurring effects', 'Only the share price', 'The dividend date', 'Nothing', 'A', 'Normalizing for off-balance-sheet exposures and one-offs improves comparability and forecast reliability.', 11),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-mbs-abs-q01', 'cfa2-fixed-income-mbs-abs', 'Securitization typically moves the pooled loans into a:', 'The originator''s balance sheet', 'Bankruptcy-remote special purpose vehicle (SPV)', 'A government agency', 'An equity fund', 'B', 'Loans are sold to a bankruptcy-remote SPV that issues the securities, isolating them from the originator.', 0),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-mbs-abs-q02', 'cfa2-fixed-income-mbs-abs', 'The defining risk of mortgage-backed pass-throughs is:', 'Prepayment risk', 'Currency risk', 'Equity risk', 'Liquidity-only risk', 'A', 'Borrowers'' ability to prepay creates contraction/extension (prepayment) risk and negative convexity.', 1),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-mbs-abs-q03', 'cfa2-fixed-income-mbs-abs', 'When interest rates fall, MBS investors face:', 'Extension risk', 'Contraction risk (faster prepayments)', 'No prepayment effect', 'Higher coupons', 'B', 'Falling rates spur refinancing, returning principal early (contraction risk) to be reinvested at lower rates.', 2),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-mbs-abs-q04', 'cfa2-fixed-income-mbs-abs', 'When interest rates rise, MBS face:', 'Contraction risk', 'Extension risk (slower prepayments)', 'No risk', 'Negative coupons', 'B', 'Rising rates slow prepayments, extending the security''s life just as rates become less favorable.', 3),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-mbs-abs-q05', 'cfa2-fixed-income-mbs-abs', 'CMOs are created primarily to:', 'Eliminate credit risk', 'Redistribute prepayment risk across tranches', 'Increase the coupon', 'Remove all risk', 'B', 'Collateralized mortgage obligations carve cash flows into tranches with different prepayment exposures.', 4),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-mbs-abs-q06', 'cfa2-fixed-income-mbs-abs', 'In an ABS waterfall, the senior tranche:', 'Absorbs losses first', 'Is paid first and protected by subordinated tranches', 'Has the highest yield', 'Has no rating', 'B', 'Credit tranching pays senior holders first; subordinated tranches absorb losses, enhancing senior credit quality.', 5),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-mbs-abs-q07', 'cfa2-fixed-income-mbs-abs', 'Agency MBS, versus non-agency, generally have:', 'Higher credit risk', 'Minimal credit risk (government backing)', 'No prepayment risk', 'Equity exposure', 'B', 'Agency MBS carry government/agency guarantees, so credit risk is minimal; prepayment risk remains.', 6),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-mbs-abs-q08', 'cfa2-fixed-income-mbs-abs', 'A PAC tranche''s prepayment risk is reduced by shifting it to the:', 'Senior tranche', 'Support (companion) tranche', 'Equity tranche', 'Government', 'B', 'Support tranches absorb prepayment variability so PAC tranches enjoy a more stable schedule.', 7),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-mbs-abs-q09', 'cfa2-fixed-income-mbs-abs', 'Auto-loan ABS are ____ , while credit-card ABS are typically ____ .', 'amortizing; non-amortizing (revolving)', 'revolving; amortizing', 'both amortizing', 'both revolving', 'A', 'Auto loans amortize; credit-card receivables are revolving/non-amortizing with a lockout period.', 8),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-mbs-abs-q10', 'cfa2-fixed-income-mbs-abs', 'Overcollateralization and reserve accounts are forms of:', 'Credit enhancement', 'Prepayment', 'Duration', 'Leverage only', 'A', 'These internal credit enhancements provide a loss buffer that protects senior ABS investors.', 9),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-mbs-abs-q11', 'cfa2-fixed-income-mbs-abs', 'Prepayment speeds are commonly measured using:', 'CPR/SMM (and the PSA benchmark)', 'Beta', 'Duration only', 'The dividend yield', 'A', 'Conditional prepayment rate (CPR), single monthly mortality (SMM), and the PSA model quantify prepayments.', 10),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-mbs-abs-q12', 'cfa2-fixed-income-mbs-abs', 'Negative convexity in MBS means that as rates fall, price appreciation is:', 'Greater than a comparable option-free bond', 'Limited because prepayments rise', 'Unaffected', 'Infinite', 'B', 'Faster prepayments cap upside as rates fall, producing the negative convexity of MBS.', 11),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-interest-rate-q01', 'cfa2-derivatives-interest-rate', 'An interest-rate cap protects a floating-rate:', 'Lender against falling rates', 'Borrower against rising rates', 'Equity investor', 'Bondholder against default', 'B', 'A cap pays off when the reference rate exceeds the strike, limiting a borrower''s interest cost.', 0),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-interest-rate-q02', 'cfa2-derivatives-interest-rate', 'An interest-rate cap is economically a series of:', 'Caplets (calls on the reference rate)', 'Floorlets (puts)', 'Forwards only', 'Equity options', 'A', 'A cap is a portfolio of caplets, each a call option on the reference interest rate.', 1),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-interest-rate-q03', 'cfa2-derivatives-interest-rate', 'An interest-rate floor protects a floating-rate:', 'Borrower against rising rates', 'Lender/investor against falling rates', 'Equity short seller', 'Issuer of fixed debt', 'B', 'A floor pays when the reference rate falls below the strike, protecting an investor''s income.', 2),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-interest-rate-q04', 'cfa2-derivatives-interest-rate', 'An interest-rate collar is created by:', 'Buying a cap and selling a floor', 'Buying two caps', 'Selling two floors', 'Buying a swap', 'A', 'A borrower''s collar buys a cap and sells a floor, lowering net premium while bounding the rate.', 3),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-interest-rate-q05', 'cfa2-derivatives-interest-rate', 'A payer swaption gives the holder the right to:', 'Receive fixed / pay floating', 'Pay fixed / receive floating', 'Buy a stock', 'Sell a bond at par', 'B', 'A payer swaption is the right to enter a swap paying fixed and receiving floating.', 4),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-interest-rate-q06', 'cfa2-derivatives-interest-rate', 'A payer swaption gains value when interest rates:', 'Fall', 'Rise', 'Stay flat', 'Are negative', 'B', 'Paying a now-below-market fixed rate becomes valuable as rates rise — a payer swaption is like a call on rates.', 5),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-interest-rate-q07', 'cfa2-derivatives-interest-rate', 'A receiver swaption gains value when rates:', 'Rise', 'Fall', 'Are unchanged', 'Double', 'B', 'The right to receive a now-above-market fixed rate is worth more as rates fall.', 6),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-interest-rate-q08', 'cfa2-derivatives-interest-rate', 'An interest-rate swap can be viewed as a portfolio of:', 'Equity options', 'Forward rate agreements', 'Currency spots', 'Dividends', 'B', 'Each swap payment is equivalent to an FRA, so a swap is a strip of FRAs.', 7),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-interest-rate-q09', 'cfa2-derivatives-interest-rate', 'A company expecting to borrow in 6 months and worried about rising rates could hedge with a:', 'Receiver swaption', 'Payer swaption (or buying a cap/FRA)', 'Long equity call', 'Short floor only', 'B', 'A payer swaption (or cap/long FRA) locks in protection against higher future borrowing rates.', 8),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-interest-rate-q10', 'cfa2-derivatives-interest-rate', 'Issuers of callable debt can monetize or hedge the embedded option using:', 'Receiver swaptions', 'Equity futures', 'Commodity forwards', 'Currency swaps only', 'A', 'A callable bond embeds a receiver-swaption-like option; issuers use swaptions to hedge/monetize call risk.', 9),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-interest-rate-q11', 'cfa2-derivatives-interest-rate', 'Valuing caps, floors and swaptions requires an input for interest-rate:', 'Dividends', 'Volatility', 'Beta', 'Recovery rate', 'B', 'Like all options, these are valued using a rate-volatility input within an arbitrage-free model.', 10),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-interest-rate-q12', 'cfa2-derivatives-interest-rate', 'A floating-rate lender wanting a minimum income could buy a(n):', 'Cap', 'Floor', 'Payer swaption', 'Equity put', 'B', 'Buying a floor guarantees a minimum interest rate on the lender''s floating-rate asset.', 11),
  ('cfa2-economics-currency-determination-q01', 'cfa2-economics-currency-determination', 'In a P/B (price/base) quote, the rate gives:', 'Units of base per 1 price currency', 'Units of price currency per 1 base currency', 'The interest differential', 'The inflation rate', 'B', 'A price/base quote states how many units of the price currency buy one unit of the base currency.', 0),
  ('cfa2-economics-currency-determination-q02', 'cfa2-economics-currency-determination', 'Forward exchange points are determined by the:', 'Dividend differential', 'Interest-rate differential between the two currencies', 'Equity risk premium', 'Recovery rate', 'B', 'Covered interest rate parity ties the forward premium/discount to the interest-rate differential.', 1),
  ('cfa2-economics-currency-determination-q03', 'cfa2-economics-currency-determination', 'Under covered interest rate parity, the higher-yielding currency trades at a forward:', 'Premium', 'Discount', 'Rate equal to spot', 'Indeterminate rate', 'B', 'The higher-yield currency is at a forward discount, offsetting its interest advantage.', 2),
  ('cfa2-economics-currency-determination-q04', 'cfa2-economics-currency-determination', 'A cross rate is computed from:', 'A single currency pair', 'Two pairs that share a common currency', 'The risk-free rate', 'Inflation only', 'B', 'Cross rates are derived by combining two quotes that share a common currency.', 3),
  ('cfa2-economics-currency-determination-q05', 'cfa2-economics-currency-determination', 'In the Mundell-Fleming model with high capital mobility, expansionary monetary policy tends to:', 'Appreciate the currency', 'Depreciate the currency (via lower rates)', 'Leave it unchanged', 'Raise interest rates', 'B', 'Lower domestic rates from easy money spur capital outflows, depreciating the currency.', 4),
  ('cfa2-economics-currency-determination-q06', 'cfa2-economics-currency-determination', 'Monetary models link long-run exchange rates to relative money supply and:', 'Inflation', 'Dividends', 'Beta', 'Duration', 'A', 'Monetary approaches emphasize relative money growth and inflation as long-run currency drivers.', 5),
  ('cfa2-economics-currency-determination-q07', 'cfa2-economics-currency-determination', 'The portfolio-balance approach suggests persistent fiscal deficits eventually:', 'Strengthen the currency', 'Pressure the currency lower', 'Have no effect', 'Eliminate inflation', 'B', 'Mounting government debt can erode confidence and weigh on the currency over time.', 6),
  ('cfa2-economics-currency-determination-q08', 'cfa2-economics-currency-determination', 'The real exchange rate adjusts the nominal rate for:', 'Relative price levels (inflation)', 'Dividend yields', 'Beta', 'Coupon rates', 'A', 'The real exchange rate reflects relative purchasing power by accounting for relative price levels.', 7),
  ('cfa2-economics-currency-determination-q09', 'cfa2-economics-currency-determination', 'Short-term exchange-rate movements are often dominated by:', 'Trade flows', 'Capital flows', 'Dividend payments', 'Coupon resets', 'B', 'Capital flows responding to relative returns typically dominate trade flows in the short run.', 8),
  ('cfa2-economics-currency-determination-q10', 'cfa2-economics-currency-determination', 'Central-bank intervention is generally most effective when backed by:', 'No reserves', 'Ample reserves and credibility', 'Higher dividends', 'A stock buyback', 'B', 'Intervention works best when the central bank has the reserves and credibility to sustain it.', 9),
  ('cfa2-economics-currency-determination-q11', 'cfa2-economics-currency-determination', 'Capital controls primarily aim to:', 'Permanently fix the real rate', 'Influence capital flows and the currency, usually temporarily', 'Eliminate inflation', 'Raise dividends', 'B', 'Controls can affect flows and exchange rates in the short run but rarely override fundamentals long-term.', 10),
  ('cfa2-economics-currency-determination-q12', 'cfa2-economics-currency-determination', 'Expansionary fiscal policy with high capital mobility tends to ____ the currency in the Mundell-Fleming model.', 'Depreciate', 'Appreciate (higher rates attract capital)', 'Not affect', 'Eliminate', 'B', 'Looser fiscal policy can raise interest rates, attracting capital inflows that appreciate the currency.', 11),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-real-estate-q01', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'Net operating income (NOI) is:', 'Rental income minus operating expenses (before financing and taxes)', 'Net income after taxes', 'Gross rent only', 'Cash flow after debt service', 'A', 'NOI = property rental income less operating expenses, excluding financing and income taxes.', 0),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-real-estate-q02', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'Direct capitalization values a property as:', 'NOI × cap rate', 'NOI / cap rate', 'Cap rate / NOI', 'NOI − cap rate', 'B', 'The income approach divides stabilized NOI by the capitalization rate.', 1),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-real-estate-q03', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'A lower capitalization rate implies a property value that is:', 'Lower', 'Higher', 'Unchanged', 'Negative', 'B', 'Since value = NOI / cap rate, a lower cap rate yields a higher value (lower income yield).', 2),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-real-estate-q04', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'The cap rate is approximately the discount rate minus the:', 'Growth rate of NOI', 'Inflation rate', 'Risk-free rate', 'Vacancy rate', 'A', 'Analogous to Gordon growth, cap rate ≈ required return − expected NOI growth.', 3),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-real-estate-q05', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'The four quadrants of real estate are private/public crossed with:', 'Equity and debt', 'Large and small cap', 'Domestic and foreign', 'Growth and value', 'A', 'Real estate spans private/public equity (property, REITs) and private/public debt (mortgages, MBS).', 4),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-real-estate-q06', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'Funds from operations (FFO) adjusts net income by adding back:', 'Interest expense', 'Real-estate depreciation (and removing property sale gains)', 'Dividends', 'Taxes', 'B', 'FFO adds back non-cash depreciation and excludes one-off property sale gains to reflect recurring cash earnings.', 5),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-real-estate-q07', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'AFFO improves on FFO by subtracting:', 'Dividends', 'Maintenance (recurring) capital expenditures', 'Interest', 'Taxes', 'B', 'Adjusted FFO deducts recurring maintenance capex, giving a cleaner measure of distributable cash.', 6),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-real-estate-q08', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'Public REITs, versus direct property, are generally:', 'Less liquid', 'More liquid but more correlated with equities', 'Risk-free', 'Free of management fees', 'B', 'Listed REITs trade like stocks (more liquid) but show higher short-run correlation with equity markets.', 7),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-real-estate-q09', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'The cost approach values property as:', 'NOI / cap rate', 'Replacement cost − depreciation + land value', 'PV of dividends', 'Comparable P/E', 'B', 'The cost approach estimates the cost to rebuild less depreciation, plus land.', 8),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-real-estate-q10', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'REITs are tax-advantaged because they must:', 'Retain all earnings', 'Distribute most of their income to shareholders', 'Avoid real estate', 'Hold only cash', 'B', 'REITs largely escape entity-level tax by distributing the bulk of taxable income to investors.', 9),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-real-estate-q11', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'Real estate in a portfolio is valued for income, diversification and:', 'Inflation sensitivity', 'Guaranteed returns', 'Zero risk', 'Daily liquidity', 'A', 'Property income tends to rise with inflation, adding an inflation-hedging, diversifying role.', 10),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-real-estate-q12', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'The DCF approach to property value sums the PV of forecast NOI and the:', 'Reversion (terminal) value', 'Coupon', 'Dividend', 'Risk-free rate', 'A', 'Property DCF discounts interim NOI plus a terminal (reversion) sale value at the end of the holding period.', 11),
  ('cfa2-ethics-professionalism-integrity-q01', 'cfa2-ethics-professionalism-integrity', 'When applicable law is less strict than the Code and Standards, a member must follow:', 'The local law', 'The Code and Standards (the stricter)', 'Neither', 'Client preference', 'B', 'Standard I(A) requires adhering to the stricter of law or the Code and Standards.', 0),
  ('cfa2-ethics-professionalism-integrity-q02', 'cfa2-ethics-professionalism-integrity', 'Acting on material nonpublic information violates Standard:', 'II(A) Material Nonpublic Information', 'I(B) Independence', 'III(A) Loyalty', 'VI(B) Priority', 'A', 'Trading or causing trades on MNPI breaches Standard II(A) Integrity of Capital Markets.', 1),
  ('cfa2-ethics-professionalism-integrity-q03', 'cfa2-ethics-professionalism-integrity', 'The mosaic theory permits an analyst to:', 'Trade on insider tips', 'Combine public and non-material non-public information to reach a conclusion', 'Spread rumors', 'Front-run clients', 'B', 'Under the mosaic theory, conclusions from public plus non-material non-public information are permissible.', 2),
  ('cfa2-ethics-professionalism-integrity-q04', 'cfa2-ethics-professionalism-integrity', 'Wash trades and spoofing are forms of:', 'Market manipulation [II(B)]', 'Fair dealing', 'Diligence', 'Record retention', 'A', 'Transaction-based manipulation like wash trades/spoofing violates Standard II(B).', 3),
  ('cfa2-ethics-professionalism-integrity-q05', 'cfa2-ethics-professionalism-integrity', 'Plagiarizing another analyst''s report violates Standard:', 'I(C) Misrepresentation', 'II(B) Market Manipulation', 'IV(A) Loyalty', 'VII Conduct', 'A', 'Plagiarism — using others'' work without attribution — is a misrepresentation under I(C).', 4),
  ('cfa2-ethics-professionalism-integrity-q06', 'cfa2-ethics-professionalism-integrity', 'Firewalls (information barriers) are a recommended procedure for complying with:', 'II(A) Material Nonpublic Information', 'VI(B) Priority of Transactions', 'III(C) Suitability', 'V(C) Record Retention', 'A', 'Firewalls prevent the flow of MNPI between departments, supporting II(A) compliance.', 5),
  ('cfa2-ethics-professionalism-integrity-q07', 'cfa2-ethics-professionalism-integrity', 'Dishonesty, fraud, or deceit reflecting on professional integrity violates:', 'I(D) Misconduct', 'II(A) MNPI', 'III(B) Fair Dealing', 'VI(A) Disclosure', 'A', 'Standard I(D) Misconduct addresses behavior reflecting adversely on professional integrity.', 6),
  ('cfa2-ethics-professionalism-integrity-q08', 'cfa2-ethics-professionalism-integrity', 'A ''pump-and-dump'' scheme is an example of:', 'Information-based market manipulation', 'Fair dealing', 'Suitability analysis', 'Independence', 'A', 'Spreading false positive information to inflate a price then selling is information-based manipulation under II(B).', 7),
  ('cfa2-ethics-professionalism-integrity-q09', 'cfa2-ethics-professionalism-integrity', 'Accepting a lavish, influence-seeking gift from a company you cover risks violating:', 'I(B) Independence and Objectivity', 'II(B) Manipulation', 'V(C) Records', 'VII Conduct', 'A', 'Lavish benefits that could bias judgement threaten independence and objectivity under I(B).', 8),
  ('cfa2-ethics-professionalism-integrity-q10', 'cfa2-ethics-professionalism-integrity', 'If a member discovers a colleague is violating the law, the member should:', 'Participate', 'Dissociate from the activity (and report as appropriate)', 'Ignore it', 'Profit from it', 'B', 'Standard I(A) requires members to dissociate from any known violation of law or the Code.', 9),
  ('cfa2-ethics-professionalism-integrity-q11', 'cfa2-ethics-professionalism-integrity', 'Standard II(A) prohibits a member from MNPI use that would:', 'Benefit clients fairly', 'Harm the integrity of capital markets and disadvantage other investors', 'Improve disclosure', 'Support diligence', 'B', 'Acting on MNPI undermines market integrity and unfairly disadvantages those without the information.', 10),
  ('cfa2-ethics-professionalism-integrity-q12', 'cfa2-ethics-professionalism-integrity', 'Guaranteeing a specific return on a volatile fund violates:', 'I(C) Misrepresentation', 'II(A) MNPI', 'III(D) Performance', 'VI(C) Referral Fees', 'A', 'Promising a return on a risky investment is a misrepresentation under Standard I(C).', 11),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting-q01', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting', 'The theoretically preferred capital-budgeting rule is to accept projects with:', 'Positive NPV', 'The shortest payback', 'The highest IRR regardless of NPV', 'Any positive accounting profit', 'A', 'NPV directly measures the value added to shareholders; accept when NPV > 0.', 0),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting-q02', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting', 'The IRR is the discount rate at which a project''s NPV equals:', 'The initial outlay', 'Zero', 'The terminal value', 'The cost of capital', 'B', 'IRR is the rate that makes the project''s NPV zero; accept if IRR exceeds the cost of capital.', 1),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting-q03', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting', 'When NPV and IRR rankings conflict for mutually exclusive projects, an analyst should follow:', 'IRR', 'NPV', 'Payback', 'Accounting return', 'B', 'NPV is the more reliable rule; IRR can mislead due to scale and reinvestment-rate assumptions.', 2),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting-q04', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting', 'Sunk costs should be:', 'Included in the analysis', 'Excluded from the incremental cash flows', 'Added to NPV', 'Treated as opportunity costs', 'B', 'Sunk costs are already incurred and irrelevant to the incremental decision.', 3),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting-q05', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting', 'Financing costs (interest) are excluded from project cash flows because they are:', 'Captured in the discount rate (WACC)', 'Sunk costs', 'Opportunity costs', 'Non-cash', 'A', 'The required return/WACC reflects financing costs, so including interest in cash flows would double-count.', 4),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting-q06', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting', 'A drawback of the payback period is that it:', 'Uses NPV', 'Ignores the time value of money and cash flows after payback', 'Overstates risk', 'Requires a discount rate', 'B', 'Plain payback ignores discounting and any cash flows beyond the payback point.', 5),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting-q07', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting', 'The profitability index equals:', 'PV of future cash flows / initial investment', 'NPV − outlay', 'IRR / WACC', 'Payback / life', 'A', 'PI = PV(inflows)/initial outlay; PI > 1 corresponds to a positive NPV.', 6),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting-q08', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting', 'Non-conventional cash flows (sign changes) can cause IRR to:', 'Always be unique', 'Produce multiple or no IRRs', 'Equal the WACC', 'Be negative only', 'B', 'Multiple sign changes can yield multiple or no real IRRs, a key IRR weakness.', 7),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting-q09', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting', 'Changes in net working capital should be:', 'Ignored', 'Included as part of the project''s cash flows', 'Treated as sunk', 'Added to the discount rate', 'B', 'Investment in (and recovery of) working capital is an incremental project cash flow.', 8),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting-q10', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting', 'The depreciation tax shield in a project equals depreciation times the:', 'Tax rate', 'WACC', 'Growth rate', 'Beta', 'A', 'Depreciation reduces taxable income, saving cash equal to depreciation × tax rate.', 9),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting-q11', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting', 'A foregone alternative use of an existing resource is a(n):', 'Sunk cost', 'Opportunity cost (include it)', 'Financing cost', 'Permanent difference', 'B', 'Opportunity costs of using resources elsewhere are relevant incremental costs.', 10),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting-q12', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-capital-budgeting', 'Real options (e.g., to expand or abandon) add value because they:', 'Are ignored by NPV''s flexibility', 'Give management flexibility that static NPV omits', 'Reduce all risk to zero', 'Lower the discount rate', 'B', 'Real options capture managerial flexibility to adapt, value that a static NPV calculation misses.', 11),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-economics-markets-q01', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-economics-markets', 'An asset''s value is fundamentally the present value of its:', 'Book value', 'Expected future cash flows', 'Dividend yield', 'Beta', 'B', 'All valuation reduces to discounting expected future cash flows at a required return.', 0),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-economics-markets-q02', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-economics-markets', 'The required return on an asset can be decomposed into the real risk-free rate, expected inflation and a:', 'Risk premium', 'Dividend', 'Coupon', 'Tax shield', 'A', 'Required return = real risk-free rate + expected inflation + a risk premium for the asset''s risk.', 1),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-economics-markets-q03', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-economics-markets', 'The real risk-free rate is most closely tied to expected:', 'Real GDP growth', 'The dividend yield', 'Credit spreads', 'The VIX', 'A', 'The real risk-free rate reflects the economy''s real growth and the inter-temporal rate of substitution.', 2),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-economics-markets-q04', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-economics-markets', 'Credit spreads typically ____ during recessions.', 'Narrow', 'Widen', 'Stay constant', 'Disappear', 'B', 'Rising default risk in downturns widens credit spreads (credit is pro-cyclical).', 3),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-economics-markets-q05', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-economics-markets', 'The term premium in default-free bond yields compensates investors for:', 'Default risk', 'Uncertainty about future short rates over a longer horizon', 'Currency risk', 'Equity risk', 'B', 'Longer maturities carry a term premium for bearing interest-rate (reinvestment/price) uncertainty.', 4),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-economics-markets-q06', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-economics-markets', 'Equities, relative to government bonds, are most sensitive to:', 'The business cycle', 'Coupon payments', 'Storage costs', 'Prepayment', 'A', 'Equity cash flows are pro-cyclical, so stocks bear a higher, cycle-sensitive risk premium.', 5),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-economics-markets-q07', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-economics-markets', 'A ''pro-cyclical'' asset earns a risk premium because it tends to perform poorly:', 'In expansions', 'During recessions (bad times)', 'Never', 'Only in inflation', 'B', 'Assets that pay off poorly in bad times must offer a premium to compensate investors.', 6),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-economics-markets-q08', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-economics-markets', 'Higher expected inflation generally raises:', 'Nominal bond yields', 'Real GDP', 'The dividend payout', 'Recovery rates', 'A', 'Nominal yields rise with expected inflation (the inflation component of required return).', 7),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-economics-markets-q09', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-economics-markets', 'Real estate''s return profile is best described as:', 'Purely bond-like', 'A blend of bond-like (income) and equity-like (cyclical) features', 'Risk-free', 'Identical to commodities', 'B', 'Lease income is bond-like while property values are cyclical, giving real estate hybrid characteristics.', 8),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-economics-markets-q10', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-economics-markets', 'Investors'' required returns generally rise when risk aversion:', 'Falls', 'Rises', 'Is constant', 'Is zero', 'B', 'Greater risk aversion increases the risk premium demanded, raising required returns and lowering prices.', 9),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-economics-markets-q11', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-economics-markets', 'The slope of the yield curve embeds market expectations about:', 'Future growth and inflation', 'Dividend policy', 'Share count', 'Audit quality', 'A', 'The curve''s shape reflects expected future short rates, which depend on growth and inflation expectations.', 10),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-economics-markets-q12', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-economics-markets', 'During an economic expansion, credit spreads typically:', 'Widen', 'Narrow', 'Stay fixed', 'Go negative', 'B', 'Improving conditions lower default risk, narrowing credit spreads in expansions.', 11),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-long-lived-assets-q01', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-long-lived-assets', 'Capitalizing a cost rather than expensing it will, in the year incurred:', 'Lower net income and assets', 'Raise net income and assets', 'Have no effect', 'Lower assets only', 'B', 'Capitalizing moves the cost to the balance sheet, raising assets and current income (then depreciating over time).', 0),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-long-lived-assets-q02', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-long-lived-assets', 'Under IFRS, an impairment loss on PP&E may be:', 'Never reversed', 'Reversed if the recoverable amount recovers', 'Reversed only for goodwill', 'Ignored', 'B', 'IFRS permits reversal of impairments (except goodwill); US GAAP prohibits reversal.', 1),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-long-lived-assets-q03', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-long-lived-assets', 'US GAAP tests PP&E for impairment by first comparing carrying value to:', 'Undiscounted expected future cash flows', 'Fair value immediately', 'Replacement cost', 'Book value of equity', 'A', 'If carrying value exceeds undiscounted future cash flows, the asset is written down to fair value.', 2),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-long-lived-assets-q04', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-long-lived-assets', 'Using a longer estimated useful life for depreciation will:', 'Increase annual depreciation and lower income', 'Decrease annual depreciation and raise income', 'Have no effect', 'Increase assets'' salvage', 'B', 'A longer life spreads cost over more years, lowering annual expense and boosting income — an earnings-quality lever.', 3),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-long-lived-assets-q05', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-long-lived-assets', 'The IFRS revaluation model allows long-lived assets to be:', 'Revalued upward, generally to OCI', 'Only written down', 'Held at original cost forever', 'Expensed immediately', 'A', 'IFRS permits upward revaluation (typically through OCI/revaluation surplus); US GAAP does not.', 4),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-long-lived-assets-q06', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-long-lived-assets', 'Goodwill and indefinite-life intangibles are:', 'Amortized straight-line', 'Not amortized but tested for impairment', 'Expensed at purchase', 'Depreciated', 'B', 'These are not amortized; instead they are tested for impairment (annually or on indicators).', 5),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-long-lived-assets-q07', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-long-lived-assets', 'Accelerated depreciation (vs straight-line) produces, in early years:', 'Lower expense and higher income', 'Higher expense and lower income', 'No difference', 'Higher salvage', 'B', 'Accelerated methods front-load depreciation, lowering early-year income relative to straight-line.', 6),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-long-lived-assets-q08', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-long-lived-assets', 'Borrowing costs on a qualifying asset under construction are generally:', 'Expensed immediately', 'Capitalized as part of the asset''s cost', 'Ignored', 'Recorded as revenue', 'B', 'Interest on funds used to construct qualifying assets is capitalized while construction is in progress.', 7),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-long-lived-assets-q09', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-long-lived-assets', 'Under IFRS, the recoverable amount is the higher of fair value less costs to sell and:', 'Value in use', 'Replacement cost', 'Historical cost', 'Salvage value', 'A', 'IFRS impairs when carrying value exceeds recoverable amount = max(fair value less costs, value in use).', 8),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-long-lived-assets-q10', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-long-lived-assets', 'Finite-life intangible assets are:', 'Tested for impairment annually but not amortized', 'Amortized over their useful life and tested on indicators', 'Never amortized', 'Expensed when acquired', 'B', 'Finite-life intangibles are amortized; impairment is tested when indicators suggest it.', 9),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-long-lived-assets-q11', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-long-lived-assets', 'A firm that aggressively capitalizes ordinary operating costs will likely show:', 'Lower current profit and cash from operations', 'Higher current profit and higher reported operating cash flow', 'No change to cash flows', 'Lower assets', 'B', 'Capitalizing operating costs boosts income and shifts the outflow to investing, inflating operating cash flow.', 10),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-long-lived-assets-q12', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-long-lived-assets', 'An impairment write-down reduces:', 'The asset''s carrying value and current income', 'Only cash', 'Revenue', 'The tax rate', 'A', 'Impairment lowers the asset''s carrying amount and recognizes a loss in the income statement.', 11),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-yield-curve-strategies-q01', 'cfa2-fixed-income-yield-curve-strategies', 'Expecting interest rates to fall, a manager should:', 'Shorten duration', 'Extend duration', 'Hold cash only', 'Buy floating-rate notes', 'B', 'Longer duration maximizes price gains when rates fall.', 0),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-yield-curve-strategies-q02', 'cfa2-fixed-income-yield-curve-strategies', '''Riding (rolling) down the yield curve'' adds return when the curve is:', 'Inverted and volatile', 'Upward-sloping and stable', 'Flat and falling', 'Downward-sloping', 'B', 'On a stable upward-sloping curve, a bond''s yield falls as it ages, producing price gains beyond its coupon.', 1),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-yield-curve-strategies-q03', 'cfa2-fixed-income-yield-curve-strategies', 'For a given duration, a barbell portfolio versus a bullet has:', 'Lower convexity', 'Higher convexity', 'Identical convexity', 'No convexity', 'B', 'Spreading maturities (barbell) increases convexity relative to a concentrated bullet of equal duration.', 2),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-yield-curve-strategies-q04', 'cfa2-fixed-income-yield-curve-strategies', 'A manager expecting the curve to flatten (long rates fall relative to short) might:', 'Buy the long end / use a barbell', 'Shorten everything to cash', 'Buy only short bills', 'Sell all bonds', 'A', 'Anticipating flattening, overweighting the long end benefits as long-end yields fall.', 3),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-yield-curve-strategies-q05', 'cfa2-fixed-income-yield-curve-strategies', 'Higher convexity is most valuable when:', 'Rates are constant', 'Rate volatility / large moves are expected', 'The curve is flat forever', 'Coupons are zero', 'B', 'Convexity improves price response asymmetrically, helping most during large or volatile rate moves.', 4),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-yield-curve-strategies-q06', 'cfa2-fixed-income-yield-curve-strategies', 'Key rate durations are used to:', 'Measure only parallel-shift risk', 'Target exposure to specific points on the curve', 'Eliminate credit risk', 'Compute the coupon', 'B', 'Key rate durations capture sensitivity to changes at individual maturities for non-parallel curve trades.', 5),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-yield-curve-strategies-q07', 'cfa2-fixed-income-yield-curve-strategies', 'A bullet structure concentrates maturities, giving it, vs a barbell of equal duration:', 'Higher convexity but lower yield', 'Lower convexity, often a higher yield', 'Identical risk', 'No interest-rate risk', 'B', 'Bullets have less convexity than barbells but typically offer a higher yield for the same duration.', 6),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-yield-curve-strategies-q08', 'cfa2-fixed-income-yield-curve-strategies', 'An active manager profits from a buy-long/roll-short trade only if future spot rates come in:', 'Above the curve''s implied forwards', 'Below the curve''s implied forwards', 'Exactly at the forwards', 'At zero', 'B', 'Forwards are the break-even; you profit from extending only if realized rates are below today''s forwards.', 7),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-yield-curve-strategies-q09', 'cfa2-fixed-income-yield-curve-strategies', 'A butterfly trade is designed to exploit a change in the curve''s:', 'Level', 'Curvature', 'Credit spread', 'Coupon', 'B', 'Butterfly trades (wings vs. belly) target curvature changes while staying roughly duration-neutral.', 8),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-yield-curve-strategies-q10', 'cfa2-fixed-income-yield-curve-strategies', 'A laddered portfolio holds bonds:', 'Only at the short end', 'Spread evenly across maturities', 'Only at the long end', 'All at one maturity', 'B', 'A ladder distributes holdings evenly across maturities, balancing reinvestment and price risk.', 9),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-yield-curve-strategies-q11', 'cfa2-fixed-income-yield-curve-strategies', 'Active fixed-income managers commonly use repo (leverage) to:', 'Reduce returns', 'Amplify carry and curve positions', 'Remove all risk', 'Avoid the curve', 'B', 'Repo financing levers positions to enhance carry/strategy returns — and risk.', 10),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-yield-curve-strategies-q12', 'cfa2-fixed-income-yield-curve-strategies', 'If a manager expects the curve to be stable, a sensible strategy is to:', 'Maximize convexity at any cost', 'Buy-and-hold / roll down and harvest carry', 'Move entirely to cash', 'Short all bonds', 'B', 'With a stable curve, carry and rolldown strategies capture yield without needing rate forecasts.', 11),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-currency-equity-forwards-q01', 'cfa2-derivatives-currency-equity-forwards', 'The forward price of a dividend-paying stock equals:', '(S₀ − PV(dividends)) × (1 + r)^T', 'S₀ × (1 + r)^T + dividends', 'S₀ / (1 + r)^T', 'S₀ × dividends', 'A', 'Dividends are a carry benefit, subtracted (in PV) before compounding at the risk-free rate.', 0),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-currency-equity-forwards-q02', 'cfa2-derivatives-currency-equity-forwards', 'For an equity index with continuous dividend yield q, the forward is:', 'S₀·e^((r + q)T)', 'S₀·e^((r − q)T)', 'S₀·e^(qT)', 'S₀·e^(−rT)', 'B', 'The dividend yield reduces the cost of carry: F₀ = S₀·e^((r − q)T).', 1),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-currency-equity-forwards-q03', 'cfa2-derivatives-currency-equity-forwards', 'Under covered interest rate parity, the currency with the higher interest rate trades at a forward:', 'Premium', 'Discount', 'Rate equal to spot', 'Indeterminate rate', 'B', 'The higher-yield currency is at a forward discount, offsetting its yield advantage (no arbitrage).', 2),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-currency-equity-forwards-q04', 'cfa2-derivatives-currency-equity-forwards', 'The currency forward formula F = S × (1 + r_price)^T / (1 + r_base)^T is enforced by:', 'Covered interest arbitrage', 'The dividend discount model', 'PPP only', 'Random pricing', 'A', 'Covered interest arbitrage forces the forward to this no-arbitrage level.', 3),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-currency-equity-forwards-q05', 'cfa2-derivatives-currency-equity-forwards', 'For a coupon bond forward, one subtracts the:', 'PV of coupons before compounding', 'Dividend yield', 'Convenience yield', 'Beta', 'A', 'Coupons are carry benefits: F₀ = (S₀ − PV(coupons))(1 + r)^T.', 4),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-currency-equity-forwards-q06', 'cfa2-derivatives-currency-equity-forwards', 'The value of an equity forward at initiation is:', 'Equal to the spot', 'Zero', 'The dividend', 'Negative', 'B', 'The forward price is set so the contract''s initial value is zero.', 5),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-currency-equity-forwards-q07', 'cfa2-derivatives-currency-equity-forwards', 'Higher dividends, all else equal, will ____ a stock''s forward price.', 'Raise', 'Lower', 'Not affect', 'Double', 'B', 'Dividends reduce the net cost of carry, lowering the forward price.', 6),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-currency-equity-forwards-q08', 'cfa2-derivatives-currency-equity-forwards', 'The value of a forward before expiry equals the PV of:', '(current forward − contracted forward)', 'the spot price', 'the dividend yield', 'the coupon', 'A', 'A forward''s mark-to-market value is the discounted difference between the current and contracted forward prices.', 7),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-currency-equity-forwards-q09', 'cfa2-derivatives-currency-equity-forwards', 'If the actual currency forward is above its no-arbitrage value, an arbitrageur would:', 'Sell the forward and execute covered interest arbitrage', 'Buy the forward only', 'Do nothing', 'Short the domestic bill only', 'A', 'An overpriced forward is sold while replicating the position via spot + money-market trades.', 8),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-currency-equity-forwards-q10', 'cfa2-derivatives-currency-equity-forwards', 'A higher domestic (price-currency) interest rate, all else equal, makes the currency forward:', 'Lower (base at discount)', 'Higher (base currency at a forward premium)', 'Unchanged', 'Negative', 'B', 'A higher price-currency rate raises F (price/base), putting the base currency at a forward premium.', 9),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-currency-equity-forwards-q11', 'cfa2-derivatives-currency-equity-forwards', 'Equity forward/futures pricing relies on:', 'Dividend forecasts and the risk-free rate (cost of carry)', 'The equity risk premium', 'Beta only', 'Credit spreads', 'A', 'Cost-of-carry pricing uses the risk-free rate and expected dividends, not the equity risk premium.', 10),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-currency-equity-forwards-q12', 'cfa2-derivatives-currency-equity-forwards', 'The no-arbitrage forward price is enforced through:', 'Cash-and-carry / reverse cash-and-carry arbitrage', 'Dividend reinvestment', 'Random walk', 'Tax arbitrage', 'A', 'If price deviates, traders execute cash-and-carry (or its reverse), pushing the forward back to fair value.', 11),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing-q01', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing', 'The probability of a Type I error equals the:', 'Power of the test', 'Significance level (α)', 'p-value always', 'Confidence level', 'B', 'α is the probability of rejecting a true null hypothesis (a Type I error).', 0),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing-q02', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing', 'A Type II error is:', 'Rejecting a true null', 'Failing to reject a false null', 'Using the wrong test', 'A data entry error', 'B', 'A Type II error fails to reject H₀ when it is actually false; its probability is β.', 1),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing-q03', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing', 'The power of a test equals:', 'α', '1 − β', '1 − α', 'β', 'B', 'Power = 1 − β, the probability of correctly rejecting a false null.', 2),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing-q04', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing', 'When the population variance is unknown and the sample is from a normal distribution, the appropriate test uses the:', 'z-distribution', 't-distribution', 'Chi-square', 'F-distribution', 'B', 'The t-test is used when the variance is unknown (estimated from the sample).', 3),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing-q05', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing', 'We reject the null hypothesis when the p-value is:', 'Greater than α', 'Less than α', 'Equal to 1', 'Negative', 'B', 'A p-value below the significance level α leads to rejecting H₀.', 4),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing-q06', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing', 'Lowering the significance level (e.g., from 5% to 1%):', 'Reduces Type I error but raises Type II error', 'Reduces both error types', 'Increases Type I error', 'Has no effect on errors', 'A', 'A stricter α cuts false positives (Type I) but increases the chance of a Type II error.', 5),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing-q07', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing', 'A test of whether a single population variance equals a value uses the:', 't-test', 'Chi-square test', 'z-test', 'Durbin-Watson', 'B', 'The chi-square test assesses hypotheses about a single variance.', 6),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing-q08', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing', 'An F-test is commonly used to test:', 'The equality of two variances or overall regression significance', 'A single mean', 'Serial correlation only', 'Normality', 'A', 'The F-test compares two variances and tests joint significance of regression slopes.', 7),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing-q09', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing', 'A one-tailed test is appropriate when the alternative hypothesis specifies a:', 'Direction (greater than or less than)', 'Two-sided difference', 'Variance only', 'Zero effect', 'A', 'Directional alternatives (e.g., > 0) call for a one-tailed test.', 8),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing-q10', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing', 'Statistical significance differs from economic significance because the latter considers:', 'Only the p-value', 'Transaction costs, taxes and risk after implementation', 'The sample size', 'The test statistic', 'B', 'A statistically significant result may not be economically significant once real-world costs/taxes/risk are included.', 9),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing-q11', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing', 'Non-parametric tests are used when:', 'Distribution assumptions are not met or data are ranks', 'The variance is known', 'The sample is huge and normal', 'Only for regression', 'A', 'Non-parametric methods (e.g., Spearman rank) apply when parametric assumptions fail or data are ordinal.', 10),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing-q12', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-hypothesis-testing', 'Increasing the sample size, all else equal, generally:', 'Lowers the test''s power', 'Increases power (smaller standard error)', 'Raises Type I error', 'Has no effect', 'B', 'Larger samples reduce the standard error, increasing the ability to detect a true effect (power).', 11),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-probability-bayes-q01', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-probability-bayes', 'For two independent events, P(A and B) equals:', 'P(A) + P(B)', 'P(A) × P(B)', 'P(A|B)', 'P(A) − P(B)', 'B', 'Independence means the joint probability is the product of the individual probabilities.', 0),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-probability-bayes-q02', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-probability-bayes', 'The addition rule states P(A or B) =', 'P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B)', 'P(A) × P(B)', 'P(A|B) × P(B)', 'P(A) − P(B)', 'A', 'Subtracting the joint probability avoids double-counting the overlap.', 1),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-probability-bayes-q03', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-probability-bayes', 'Bayes'' formula is used to:', 'Compute variance', 'Update a prior probability with new information', 'Estimate beta', 'Price a forward', 'B', 'Bayes'' rule revises a prior into a posterior given new evidence.', 2),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-probability-bayes-q04', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-probability-bayes', 'The expected value of a random variable is:', 'Σ probability × outcome', 'The largest outcome', 'The variance', 'The median', 'A', 'E[X] is the probability-weighted average of the possible outcomes.', 3),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-probability-bayes-q05', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-probability-bayes', 'In a two-asset portfolio, variance depends on the weights, variances and the:', 'Covariance/correlation between assets', 'Dividend yield', 'Coupon', 'Beta of the market', 'A', 'Var(aX+bY) = a²σ²_X + b²σ²_Y + 2ab·Cov(X,Y); covariance is essential.', 4),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-probability-bayes-q06', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-probability-bayes', 'The total probability rule expresses P(A) as:', 'Σ P(A|Sᵢ)P(Sᵢ) over a mutually exclusive, exhaustive set', 'P(A) × P(B)', '1 − P(A)', 'P(A and B)', 'A', 'It weights conditional probabilities by the probabilities of an exhaustive set of scenarios.', 5),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-probability-bayes-q07', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-probability-bayes', 'Combinations (nCr) are used when:', 'Order matters', 'Order does not matter', 'Events are dependent', 'Computing variance', 'B', 'Combinations count selections where order is irrelevant; permutations count ordered arrangements.', 6),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-probability-bayes-q08', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-probability-bayes', 'A conditional probability P(A|B) is:', 'P(A and B) / P(B)', 'P(A) + P(B)', 'P(A) × P(B)', '1 − P(A)', 'A', 'Conditional probability normalizes the joint probability by the conditioning event''s probability.', 7),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-probability-bayes-q09', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-probability-bayes', 'Two events are mutually exclusive if:', 'They cannot occur together (P(A and B)=0)', 'They always occur together', 'They are independent', 'Their probabilities sum to 1', 'A', 'Mutually exclusive events have zero joint probability.', 8),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-probability-bayes-q10', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-probability-bayes', 'If a test is 90% accurate and a condition is rare, Bayes'' rule shows the posterior probability of the condition given a positive test is often:', 'Near 100%', 'Lower than intuition suggests, due to the low prior', 'Exactly 90%', 'Zero', 'B', 'A low base rate (prior) keeps the posterior modest even with an accurate test — the base-rate effect.', 9),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-probability-bayes-q11', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-probability-bayes', 'Variance is computed as:', 'Σ p(x − E[X])²', 'Σ p·x', 'E[X] − 1', 'the square root of E[X]', 'A', 'Variance is the probability-weighted average of squared deviations from the mean.', 10),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-probability-bayes-q12', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-probability-bayes', 'Adding an asset with low correlation to a portfolio tends to:', 'Increase total risk', 'Reduce total portfolio risk (diversification)', 'Have no effect', 'Guarantee higher return', 'B', 'Low or negative correlation lowers portfolio variance, the core benefit of diversification.', 11),
  ('cfa2-economics-business-cycles-q01', 'cfa2-economics-business-cycles', 'GDP using the expenditure approach equals:', 'C + I + G + (X − M)', 'C × I × G', 'Wages + profits only', 'Exports only', 'A', 'GDP = consumption + investment + government spending + net exports.', 0),
  ('cfa2-economics-business-cycles-q02', 'cfa2-economics-business-cycles', 'Real GDP differs from nominal GDP by adjusting for:', 'Taxes', 'Inflation (using the GDP deflator)', 'Population', 'Trade', 'B', 'Real GDP removes price-level changes via the GDP deflator, measuring true output growth.', 1),
  ('cfa2-economics-business-cycles-q03', 'cfa2-economics-business-cycles', 'The output gap is the difference between actual GDP and:', 'Last year''s GDP', 'Potential (full-employment) GDP', 'Nominal GDP', 'Trade balance', 'B', 'The output gap = actual − potential GDP; a negative gap implies slack/recession.', 2),
  ('cfa2-economics-business-cycles-q04', 'cfa2-economics-business-cycles', 'The long-run aggregate supply (LRAS) curve is:', 'Upward sloping', 'Vertical at potential output', 'Downward sloping', 'Horizontal', 'B', 'In the long run, output is determined by productive capacity, so LRAS is vertical at potential GDP.', 3),
  ('cfa2-economics-business-cycles-q05', 'cfa2-economics-business-cycles', 'Unemployment is generally a ____ indicator of the business cycle.', 'Leading', 'Lagging', 'Coincident', 'Random', 'B', 'Unemployment tends to change after the economy turns, making it a lagging indicator.', 4),
  ('cfa2-economics-business-cycles-q06', 'cfa2-economics-business-cycles', 'Which is a leading economic indicator?', 'Average duration of unemployment', 'New manufacturing orders / building permits', 'Industrial production', 'CPI', 'B', 'New orders and permits shift before the broader economy, classic leading indicators.', 5),
  ('cfa2-economics-business-cycles-q07', 'cfa2-economics-business-cycles', 'Cost-push inflation arises primarily from:', 'Rising aggregate demand', 'Higher input costs reducing aggregate supply', 'Lower money supply', 'Falling wages', 'B', 'Cost-push inflation comes from supply-side cost increases (e.g., energy, wages) shifting SRAS left.', 6),
  ('cfa2-economics-business-cycles-q08', 'cfa2-economics-business-cycles', 'The Phillips curve depicts a short-run trade-off between inflation and:', 'GDP growth', 'Unemployment', 'Interest rates', 'The trade balance', 'B', 'The short-run Phillips curve shows an inverse relation between inflation and unemployment.', 7),
  ('cfa2-economics-business-cycles-q09', 'cfa2-economics-business-cycles', 'NAIRU is the unemployment rate at which inflation is:', 'Accelerating', 'Non-accelerating (stable)', 'Zero', 'Negative', 'B', 'NAIRU is the non-accelerating-inflation rate of unemployment — the rate consistent with stable inflation.', 8),
  ('cfa2-economics-business-cycles-q10', 'cfa2-economics-business-cycles', 'Monetarist economists emphasize:', 'Active fiscal fine-tuning', 'Steady, predictable money-supply growth', 'Ignoring inflation', 'Price controls', 'B', 'Monetarists favor stable money growth, viewing inflation as primarily a monetary phenomenon.', 9),
  ('cfa2-economics-business-cycles-q11', 'cfa2-economics-business-cycles', 'Early in a recession, the inventory-to-sales ratio typically:', 'Falls sharply', 'Rises (as sales slow faster than production adjusts)', 'Is unchanged', 'Goes to zero', 'B', 'Unsold goods accumulate as demand drops, raising the inventory-sales ratio early in a downturn.', 10),
  ('cfa2-economics-business-cycles-q12', 'cfa2-economics-business-cycles', 'Demand-pull inflation results from:', 'Excess aggregate demand relative to capacity', 'Falling input costs', 'A money-supply contraction', 'Rising unemployment', 'A', 'Demand-pull inflation occurs when aggregate demand outstrips the economy''s productive capacity.', 11),
  ('cfa2-economics-trade-capital-flows-q01', 'cfa2-economics-trade-capital-flows', 'Comparative advantage means a country should specialize where its:', 'Absolute output is highest', 'Opportunity cost of production is lowest', 'Wages are highest', 'Currency is strongest', 'B', 'Ricardo''s comparative advantage holds that gains arise from specializing where opportunity cost is lowest.', 0),
  ('cfa2-economics-trade-capital-flows-q02', 'cfa2-economics-trade-capital-flows', 'Trade benefits both partners even when one country has an absolute advantage in everything, according to:', 'Comparative advantage', 'Mercantilism', 'Autarky', 'The Phillips curve', 'A', 'Comparative advantage shows mutual gains from trade despite one party''s absolute superiority.', 1),
  ('cfa2-economics-trade-capital-flows-q03', 'cfa2-economics-trade-capital-flows', 'A tariff on imports generally:', 'Lowers domestic prices', 'Raises domestic prices and creates a deadweight loss', 'Helps all consumers', 'Has no effect', 'B', 'Tariffs protect domestic producers but raise prices and reduce overall welfare (deadweight loss).', 2),
  ('cfa2-economics-trade-capital-flows-q04', 'cfa2-economics-trade-capital-flows', 'The Heckscher-Ohlin model predicts countries export goods that intensively use their:', 'Scarce factor', 'Abundant factor', 'Imported inputs', 'Currency reserves', 'B', 'Countries export goods intensive in their relatively abundant (cheaper) factor of production.', 3),
  ('cfa2-economics-trade-capital-flows-q05', 'cfa2-economics-trade-capital-flows', 'A current-account deficit must be offset by a:', 'Current-account surplus', 'Financial/capital-account surplus (capital inflows)', 'Trade surplus', 'Budget surplus', 'B', 'By the balance-of-payments identity, a current-account deficit is financed by net capital inflows.', 4),
  ('cfa2-economics-trade-capital-flows-q06', 'cfa2-economics-trade-capital-flows', 'A quota differs from a tariff in that a quota:', 'Is a tax on imports', 'Limits the quantity of imports', 'Subsidizes exports', 'Has no price effect', 'B', 'A quota caps import quantity, whereas a tariff taxes imports; both raise domestic prices.', 5),
  ('cfa2-economics-trade-capital-flows-q07', 'cfa2-economics-trade-capital-flows', 'The institution that provides balance-of-payments support and promotes monetary stability is the:', 'WTO', 'IMF', 'World Bank', 'OECD', 'B', 'The IMF offers balance-of-payments assistance and fosters international monetary stability.', 6),
  ('cfa2-economics-trade-capital-flows-q08', 'cfa2-economics-trade-capital-flows', 'A persistent current-account deficit often reflects:', 'Saving exceeding investment', 'Investment exceeding domestic saving', 'A trade surplus', 'Zero capital flows', 'B', 'When domestic investment exceeds saving, the gap is filled by foreign capital — a current-account deficit.', 7),
  ('cfa2-economics-trade-capital-flows-q09', 'cfa2-economics-trade-capital-flows', '''Trade diversion'' from a regional bloc occurs when trade shifts from a:', 'Higher-cost to a lower-cost producer', 'Lower-cost outside producer to a higher-cost member', 'Bloc member to itself', 'Service to a good', 'B', 'Trade diversion is welfare-reducing: preferential tariffs divert imports to a less efficient member.', 8),
  ('cfa2-economics-trade-capital-flows-q10', 'cfa2-economics-trade-capital-flows', 'The WTO''s primary role is to:', 'Set exchange rates', 'Administer trade rules and resolve trade disputes', 'Lend to developing nations', 'Control money supply', 'B', 'The WTO oversees the rules of international trade and provides a dispute-resolution forum.', 9),
  ('cfa2-economics-trade-capital-flows-q11', 'cfa2-economics-trade-capital-flows', 'An export subsidy:', 'Reduces exports', 'Lowers the cost of domestic exports, distorting trade', 'Is the same as a quota', 'Has no welfare effect', 'B', 'Subsidies make domestic exports artificially cheap, distorting competition and imposing costs on taxpayers.', 10),
  ('cfa2-economics-trade-capital-flows-q12', 'cfa2-economics-trade-capital-flows', 'A customs union differs from a free-trade area by adding:', 'A common external tariff', 'Free internal trade only', 'A shared currency', 'Free labor movement', 'A', 'A customs union has free internal trade plus a common external tariff; deeper stages add factor mobility/currency.', 11),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-infrastructure-q01', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-infrastructure', 'Brownfield infrastructure investments are:', 'Yet-to-be-built projects', 'Existing assets with current income and lower risk', 'Always the highest risk', 'Equity indices', 'B', 'Brownfield assets already operate, generating income at lower risk than greenfield (to-be-built) projects.', 0),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-infrastructure-q02', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-infrastructure', 'Greenfield infrastructure projects, versus brownfield, generally have:', 'Lower risk and income now', 'Higher risk with development/construction upside', 'No risk', 'Guaranteed returns', 'B', 'Greenfield projects carry construction/ramp-up risk but offer development upside.', 1),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-infrastructure-q03', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-infrastructure', 'A key attraction of infrastructure cash flows is that they are often:', 'Highly volatile', 'Stable and inflation-linked', 'Tied to equity beta', 'Short-dated', 'B', 'Regulated/contracted infrastructure produces stable, frequently inflation-indexed cash flows.', 2),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-infrastructure-q04', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-infrastructure', 'A major risk specific to infrastructure is:', 'Regulatory/political risk', 'Dividend risk', 'No risk', 'Style drift', 'A', 'Because many assets are regulated or government-linked, regulatory/political changes are a key risk.', 3),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-infrastructure-q05', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-infrastructure', 'Infrastructure suits pension investors partly because it:', 'Offers daily liquidity', 'Provides long-dated, inflation-linked cash flows matching liabilities', 'Has no income', 'Is risk-free', 'B', 'Long horizons and inflation linkage help match pension liabilities.', 4),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-infrastructure-q06', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-infrastructure', 'Timberland and farmland returns come from biological growth, land appreciation and:', 'Commodity prices', 'Coupons', 'Dividends', 'The risk-free rate', 'A', 'Real-asset returns include crop/timber prices (commodities), biological growth, and land value changes.', 5),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-infrastructure-q07', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-infrastructure', 'Infrastructure and real assets are commonly used as a(n):', 'Inflation hedge and diversifier', 'Short-term cash substitute', 'Source of guaranteed returns', 'Pure equity bet', 'A', 'These assets hedge inflation and diversify equity/bond portfolios via low correlations.', 6),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-infrastructure-q08', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-infrastructure', 'Digital infrastructure includes assets such as:', 'Cell towers and data centers', 'Government bonds', 'Equities', 'Hedge funds', 'A', 'Towers, fiber, and data centers are increasingly important ''digital'' infrastructure assets.', 7),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-infrastructure-q09', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-infrastructure', 'Investors can access infrastructure via direct ownership, private funds, or:', 'Listed infrastructure funds/REITs', 'Treasury bills only', 'Currency forwards', 'Credit default swaps', 'A', 'Listed vehicles provide liquid access, while direct/private funds offer purer (less equity-correlated) exposure.', 8),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-infrastructure-q10', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-infrastructure', 'Compared with equities, infrastructure typically has:', 'Higher correlation', 'Lower correlation and steadier cash flows', 'Identical risk', 'No diversification value', 'B', 'Lower equity correlation and stable cash flows give infrastructure diversification benefits.', 9),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-infrastructure-q11', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-infrastructure', 'Leverage in infrastructure deals primarily:', 'Eliminates risk', 'Amplifies returns and risk', 'Lowers the cash yield to zero', 'Is prohibited', 'B', 'Infrastructure often uses substantial leverage, magnifying both returns and risk.', 10),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-infrastructure-q12', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-infrastructure', 'Farmland and timberland are generally considered:', 'Inflation hedges', 'Deflation hedges', 'Risk-free', 'Fixed-income substitutes with no growth', 'A', 'Real assets like farmland/timberland tend to preserve value during inflation.', 11),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q01', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'Because alternative returns are non-normal, appraisal should rely on:', 'Volatility and Sharpe alone', 'Downside and tail measures (Sortino, drawdown, CVaR)', 'The dividend yield', 'Beta only', 'B', 'Fat tails/negative skew make volatility-based measures inadequate; downside and tail risk measures are needed.', 0),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q02', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'Smoothed (appraisal-based) returns should be ''unsmoothed'' before optimization because they:', 'Overstate volatility', 'Understate volatility and correlation', 'Have no bias', 'Are risk-free', 'B', 'Appraisal lag understates risk statistics; unsmoothing restores more realistic volatility/correlation.', 1),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q03', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'DPI (distributions to paid-in) measures a private fund''s:', 'Unrealized value', 'Realized cash returned relative to capital contributed', 'Management fee', 'Leverage', 'B', 'DPI is realized, cash-on-cash performance relative to paid-in capital.', 2),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q04', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'Operational due diligence focuses on:', 'Only past returns', 'Independent administration/custody/audit, valuation and controls', 'The benchmark index', 'The risk-free rate', 'B', 'ODD examines the non-investment infrastructure whose failure is a leading cause of fund losses.', 3),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q05', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'A high-water mark in a fee structure ensures incentive fees are charged only on:', 'Gross assets', 'Gains above the prior peak value', 'Every gain', 'Management fees', 'B', 'The high-water mark prevents charging performance fees on the recovery of prior losses.', 4),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q06', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'A clawback provision protects limited partners by:', 'Raising fees', 'Requiring the GP to return excess carried interest after later losses', 'Guaranteeing returns', 'Removing the hurdle', 'B', 'Clawbacks recover carried interest taken early if subsequent results mean LPs were overcharged.', 5),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q07', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'Private fund performance is best benchmarked against:', 'A public equity index only', 'Vintage-year peer groups (plus IRR/multiples)', 'The risk-free rate', 'Nothing', 'B', 'Comparing to same-vintage peers controls for market timing; IRR and multiples gauge realized/unrealized value.', 6),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q08', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'A management fee charged on committed (vs invested) capital:', 'Is always lower', 'Can create a larger drag early, before capital is deployed', 'Has no effect', 'Eliminates carried interest', 'B', 'Fees on committed capital are paid even before investment, increasing the early fee drag.', 7),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q09', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'Investment due diligence assesses the manager''s:', 'Office address', 'Strategy, edge, repeatability and team', 'Auditor''s fee', 'Custodian location', 'B', 'Investment DD evaluates whether the approach and team can plausibly repeat past success.', 8),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q10', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'Max drawdown measures:', 'Average return', 'The largest peak-to-trough loss', 'Volatility', 'Beta', 'B', 'Maximum drawdown captures the worst peak-to-trough decline — a key downside risk gauge for alternatives.', 9),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q11', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'TVPI (total value to paid-in) combines:', 'DPI (realized) and RVPI (unrealized)', 'Beta and alpha', 'Duration and convexity', 'Two benchmarks', 'A', 'TVPI = DPI + RVPI, summing distributed and residual value relative to paid-in capital.', 10),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q12', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'Liquidity terms relevant to alternatives due diligence include:', 'Lockups and redemption gates', 'The dividend date', 'The coupon', 'The risk-free rate', 'A', 'Lockups, gates, and notice periods determine when investors can access capital — a core DD consideration.', 11),
  ('cfa2-ethics-duties-clients-employers-q01', 'cfa2-ethics-duties-clients-employers', 'Under Standard III(A), a manager of a pension plan owes primary loyalty to the:', 'Plan sponsor''s executives', 'Plan beneficiaries', 'Brokerage firm', 'Regulator', 'B', 'Loyalty, Prudence and Care requires acting for the ultimate beneficiaries of the plan.', 0),
  ('cfa2-ethics-duties-clients-employers-q02', 'cfa2-ethics-duties-clients-employers', 'Disseminating a recommendation change to a favored client before others violates:', 'III(B) Fair Dealing', 'I(B) Independence', 'VI(B) Priority', 'IV(A) Loyalty', 'A', 'Fair Dealing requires distributing recommendations and changes fairly among all clients.', 1),
  ('cfa2-ethics-duties-clients-employers-q03', 'cfa2-ethics-duties-clients-employers', 'Standard III(C) Suitability requires recommendations to match the client''s:', 'Objectives and constraints (IPS)', 'Broker''s targets', 'Country of residence only', 'Tax bracket only', 'A', 'Suitability ties recommendations to each client''s objectives, constraints, and overall portfolio (IPS).', 2),
  ('cfa2-ethics-duties-clients-employers-q04', 'cfa2-ethics-duties-clients-employers', 'Keeping a former client''s information confidential is required by:', 'III(E) Preservation of Confidentiality', 'II(A) MNPI', 'VI(C) Referral Fees', 'I(D) Misconduct', 'A', 'Confidentiality applies to current, former, and prospective clients (subject to legal exceptions).', 3),
  ('cfa2-ethics-duties-clients-employers-q05', 'cfa2-ethics-duties-clients-employers', 'Taking client lists or research files to a new employer violates:', 'IV(A) Loyalty to Employer', 'III(D) Performance', 'II(B) Manipulation', 'VI(A) Disclosure', 'A', 'Such records are the employer''s property; taking them breaches the duty of loyalty to employers.', 4),
  ('cfa2-ethics-duties-clients-employers-q06', 'cfa2-ethics-duties-clients-employers', 'Accepting outside compensation that could conflict with the employer''s interest requires:', 'No action', 'Written consent from all parties (IV(B))', 'Only verbal notice', 'Disclosure to clients only', 'B', 'Standard IV(B) requires written consent from the employer (and parties) for competing additional compensation.', 5),
  ('cfa2-ethics-duties-clients-employers-q07', 'cfa2-ethics-duties-clients-employers', 'A supervisor who fails to establish adequate compliance procedures violates:', 'IV(C) Responsibilities of Supervisors', 'III(B) Fair Dealing', 'I(A) Knowledge of the Law', 'VI(B) Priority', 'A', 'Supervisors must take reasonable steps to prevent and detect violations by those they oversee.', 6),
  ('cfa2-ethics-duties-clients-employers-q08', 'cfa2-ethics-duties-clients-employers', 'Standard III(D) Performance Presentation requires performance data that is:', 'Cherry-picked for the best accounts', 'Fair, accurate and complete', 'Only the most recent quarter', 'Hypothetical', 'B', 'Performance must be presented fairly, accurately and completely — no selective presentation.', 7),
  ('cfa2-ethics-duties-clients-employers-q09', 'cfa2-ethics-duties-clients-employers', 'Confidentiality may be broken when:', 'A friend asks', 'Disclosure is legally required or concerns illegal activities', 'It benefits the manager', 'Never', 'B', 'Confidential information may be disclosed when required by law or to report illegal client activity.', 8),
  ('cfa2-ethics-duties-clients-employers-q10', 'cfa2-ethics-duties-clients-employers', 'Independent practice preparing to compete with one''s employer is acceptable if:', 'Done secretly', 'It does not harm the employer and (per policy) is disclosed/approved', 'Client funds are used', 'It uses employer records', 'B', 'Members may prepare to leave but must not deprive the employer of services or misuse its resources before departure.', 9),
  ('cfa2-ethics-duties-clients-employers-q11', 'cfa2-ethics-duties-clients-employers', 'Whistle-blowing that violates the duty of loyalty to an employer is permissible when it:', 'Benefits the member personally', 'Protects clients or market integrity from illegal/unethical acts', 'Is anonymous gossip', 'Is never allowed', 'B', 'Loyalty yields when reporting is necessary to protect clients or the integrity of the market.', 10),
  ('cfa2-ethics-duties-clients-employers-q12', 'cfa2-ethics-duties-clients-employers', 'In an advisory relationship, suitability is judged in the context of the:', 'Single trade in isolation', 'Client''s total portfolio', 'Manager''s portfolio', 'Benchmark only', 'B', 'For advisory clients, suitability considers how a recommendation fits the total portfolio, not just the trade alone.', 11),
  ('cfa2-ethics-conflicts-priority-q01', 'cfa2-ethics-conflicts-priority', 'Standard VI(A) requires members to ____ conflicts of interest.', 'Conceal', 'Fully and fairly disclose', 'Ignore if small', 'Disclose only to regulators', 'B', 'Conflicts must be disclosed prominently, fully and fairly, in plain language.', 0),
  ('cfa2-ethics-conflicts-priority-q02', 'cfa2-ethics-conflicts-priority', 'Standard VI(B) Priority of Transactions requires that:', 'The member trades first', 'Client and employer transactions take precedence over the member''s own', 'All trades are simultaneous', 'Personal trades are banned entirely', 'B', 'Clients and the employer come before the member''s personal transactions.', 1),
  ('cfa2-ethics-conflicts-priority-q03', 'cfa2-ethics-conflicts-priority', 'Standard VI(C) requires disclosure of:', 'Referral fees to clients and the employer', 'Only large fees', 'Nothing', 'Competitor fees', 'A', 'Referral compensation must be disclosed so clients can assess potential bias.', 2),
  ('cfa2-ethics-conflicts-priority-q04', 'cfa2-ethics-conflicts-priority', 'A board seat at a covered company is a conflict that should be:', 'Disclosed under VI(A)', 'Hidden', 'Disclosed only to the board', 'Ignored if unpaid', 'A', 'Board membership can impair objectivity and must be disclosed as a conflict.', 3),
  ('cfa2-ethics-conflicts-priority-q05', 'cfa2-ethics-conflicts-priority', 'Recommended procedures for personal trading include:', 'No rules', 'Restricted lists, pre-clearance and blackout periods', 'Trading ahead of clients', 'Front-running', 'B', 'Pre-clearance, restricted lists and blackout/restricted periods help comply with VI(B).', 4),
  ('cfa2-ethics-conflicts-priority-q06', 'cfa2-ethics-conflicts-priority', 'Participating in an IPO ahead of clients most likely violates:', 'VI(B) Priority of Transactions', 'III(D) Performance', 'I(A) Knowledge of the Law', 'IV(C) Supervisors', 'A', 'Members must not take desirable IPO/private-placement allocations ahead of clients.', 5),
  ('cfa2-ethics-conflicts-priority-q07', 'cfa2-ethics-conflicts-priority', 'Disclosure of a conflict should be:', 'Buried in fine print', 'Prominent and in plain language', 'Verbal only', 'Made after trading', 'B', 'Disclosures must be clear, prominent and understandable so recipients can evaluate the conflict.', 6),
  ('cfa2-ethics-conflicts-priority-q08', 'cfa2-ethics-conflicts-priority', 'The default remedy for a conflict of interest is:', 'Concealment', 'Full disclosure and proper management', 'Resignation always', 'Ignoring it', 'B', 'Conflicts are addressed by disclosing and managing them, not hiding them.', 7),
  ('cfa2-ethics-conflicts-priority-q09', 'cfa2-ethics-conflicts-priority', 'A compensation structure that could bias a recommendation should be:', 'Disclosed to clients', 'Kept secret', 'Disclosed only to the SEC', 'Ignored', 'A', 'Compensation arrangements that may affect objectivity are disclosable conflicts under VI(A).', 8),
  ('cfa2-ethics-conflicts-priority-q10', 'cfa2-ethics-conflicts-priority', 'Personal-trading rules ensure that members do not:', 'Benefit clients', 'Disadvantage clients through their own trades', 'Disclose conflicts', 'Keep records', 'B', 'VI(B) protects clients from being disadvantaged by the member''s personal transactions.', 9),
  ('cfa2-ethics-conflicts-priority-q11', 'cfa2-ethics-conflicts-priority', 'Referral fee disclosure allows clients to:', 'Avoid taxes', 'Evaluate the cost and any partiality in the recommendation', 'Get free advice', 'Trade ahead', 'B', 'Knowing about referral compensation lets clients judge potential bias and the true cost of services.', 10),
  ('cfa2-ethics-conflicts-priority-q12', 'cfa2-ethics-conflicts-priority', 'Cross-department flow of MNPI is best prevented by:', 'Firewalls (information barriers)', 'Higher fees', 'More trading', 'Public disclosure of the MNPI', 'A', 'Firewalls manage conflicts and prevent improper use/flow of material nonpublic information.', 11),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-working-capital-q01', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-working-capital', 'The cash conversion cycle equals:', 'DSO + DOH − DPO', 'DSO − DOH + DPO', 'DPO − DSO', 'DOH + DPO', 'A', 'CCC = days sales outstanding + days inventory on hand − days payables outstanding.', 0),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-working-capital-q02', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-working-capital', 'A shorter cash conversion cycle generally indicates:', 'Weaker liquidity', 'Stronger liquidity and less financing needed', 'Higher inventory', 'Slower collections', 'B', 'A shorter CCC frees up cash, improving liquidity and reducing working-capital financing.', 1),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-working-capital-q03', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-working-capital', 'The quick ratio excludes which current asset?', 'Cash', 'Inventory', 'Receivables', 'Marketable securities', 'B', 'The quick (acid-test) ratio excludes inventory, the least liquid current asset.', 2),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-working-capital-q04', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-working-capital', 'Forgoing a ''2/10 net 30'' discount implies an annualized cost closest to:', '2%', '24%', '45%', '73%', 'C', '(1 + 0.02/0.98)^(365/20) − 1 ≈ 44.6%, so taking the discount is usually worthwhile.', 3),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-working-capital-q05', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-working-capital', 'A negative cash conversion cycle implies that:', 'The firm is insolvent', 'Suppliers effectively finance operations', 'Inventory is excessive', 'Collections are slow', 'B', 'Collecting from customers before paying suppliers means suppliers fund operations (e.g., some retailers).', 4),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-working-capital-q06', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-working-capital', 'The most reliable committed short-term funding source is a:', 'Uncommitted line', 'Committed (revolving) line of credit', 'One-off supplier credit', 'Commercial paper for a low-rated issuer', 'B', 'A committed/revolving line obligates the lender to provide funds, unlike an uncommitted line.', 5),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-working-capital-q07', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-working-capital', 'The cash ratio is the strictest liquidity measure because it counts only:', 'Cash and marketable securities vs current liabilities', 'All current assets', 'Receivables and inventory', 'Working capital', 'A', 'The cash ratio uses only the most liquid assets against current liabilities.', 6),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-working-capital-q08', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-working-capital', 'Factoring receivables involves:', 'Borrowing against inventory', 'Selling receivables for immediate cash', 'Extending customer terms', 'Issuing equity', 'B', 'Factoring sells receivables (at a discount) to obtain immediate liquidity.', 7),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-working-capital-q09', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-working-capital', 'Days of inventory on hand equals:', '365 × inventory turnover', '365 / inventory turnover', 'Inventory / sales', 'COGS / inventory', 'B', 'DOH = 365 / inventory turnover (turnover = COGS / average inventory).', 8),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-working-capital-q10', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-working-capital', 'Rising days payables outstanding (stretching suppliers) primarily:', 'Uses cash', 'Conserves cash', 'Lowers sales', 'Raises inventory', 'B', 'Paying suppliers more slowly retains cash longer — though excessive stretching can harm relationships.', 9),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-working-capital-q11', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-working-capital', 'Excess cash should be invested in instruments that are:', 'Long-dated and illiquid', 'Safe and liquid', 'Equity-only', 'Speculative', 'B', 'Short-term cash management prioritizes safety and liquidity over yield.', 10),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-working-capital-q12', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-working-capital', 'Reducing DSO from 50 to 35 days, with DOH and DPO unchanged, lowers the CCC by:', '5 days', '15 days', '35 days', '0 days', 'B', 'Since CCC = DSO + DOH − DPO, a 15-day fall in DSO cuts the CCC by 15 days.', 11),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital-q01', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital', 'WACC weights should be based on:', 'Book values', 'Target market-value weights', 'Par values', 'Historical cost', 'B', 'WACC uses target (market-value) capital-structure weights, reflecting how the firm intends to finance.', 0),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital-q02', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital', 'The after-tax cost of debt equals r_d ×:', '(1 − tax rate)', '(1 + tax rate)', 'beta', 'the payout ratio', 'A', 'Interest is tax-deductible, so the relevant cost is r_d(1 − t).', 1),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital-q03', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital', 'The cost of equity is most commonly estimated using the:', 'CAPM', 'Current ratio', 'Payback rule', 'Cash conversion cycle', 'A', 'CAPM (R_f + β × market risk premium) is the standard cost-of-equity estimate.', 2),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital-q04', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital', 'The cost of preferred stock equals:', 'Dividend / price', 'Price / dividend', 'YTM × (1 − t)', 'Beta × premium', 'A', 'Preferred cost = preferred dividend divided by the preferred share price.', 3),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital-q05', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital', 'To estimate beta for a private firm, an analyst would unlever a comparable''s beta and then:', 'Ignore leverage', 'Relever it at the subject firm''s capital structure', 'Use the risk-free rate', 'Use the dividend yield', 'B', 'Hamada: unlever the comparable beta, then relever using the subject firm''s D/E to reflect its leverage.', 4),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital-q06', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital', 'WACC is the appropriate discount rate for a project that is:', 'Riskier than the firm', 'Of average risk relative to the firm', 'Risk-free', 'Always, regardless of risk', 'B', 'WACC applies to average-risk projects; project-specific risk warrants an adjusted rate.', 5),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital-q07', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital', 'The relevant cost of capital for decision-making is the ____ cost.', 'Historical', 'Marginal (forward-looking)', 'Average past', 'Book', 'B', 'Capital-budgeting uses the marginal cost of new capital, not historical/embedded costs.', 6),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital-q08', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital', 'The tax shield in WACC reflects that:', 'Dividends are deductible', 'Interest is tax-deductible', 'Equity is cheaper than debt', 'Preferred is deductible', 'B', 'Deductible interest lowers the effective cost of debt via the (1 − t) factor.', 7),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital-q09', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital', 'All else equal, a higher beta raises the:', 'Cost of equity', 'Cost of debt', 'Tax rate', 'Dividend', 'A', 'A higher beta increases the CAPM cost of equity (more systematic risk).', 8),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital-q10', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital', 'The marginal cost of capital schedule typically ____ as more capital is raised.', 'Falls', 'Rises (at break points)', 'Stays flat', 'Goes to zero', 'B', 'Raising larger amounts of new capital tends to increase its marginal cost, creating break points.', 9),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital-q11', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital', 'The bond-yield-plus-risk-premium method estimates the cost of:', 'Debt', 'Equity', 'Preferred', 'Working capital', 'B', 'Adding an equity risk premium to the firm''s bond yield estimates the cost of equity.', 10),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital-q12', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-cost-of-capital', 'Relevering a beta increases it because more debt:', 'Lowers equity risk', 'Raises the financial risk borne by equity holders', 'Has no effect', 'Reduces the tax shield', 'B', 'Higher leverage amplifies equity''s exposure, raising the levered beta.', 11),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-risk-return-capm-q01', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-risk-return-capm', 'Diversification reduces a portfolio''s:', 'Systematic risk', 'Unsystematic (idiosyncratic) risk', 'Expected return', 'Beta to zero', 'B', 'Diversification eliminates firm-specific (unsystematic) risk; systematic/market risk remains.', 0),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-risk-return-capm-q02', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-risk-return-capm', 'Portfolio variance is most reduced when combined assets have:', 'Correlation of +1', 'Correlation below +1 (ideally negative)', 'Zero variance', 'Identical returns', 'B', 'Lower (especially negative) correlation between assets produces greater risk reduction.', 1),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-risk-return-capm-q03', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-risk-return-capm', 'In the CAPM, beta measures:', 'Total risk', 'Systematic risk (Cov(i,m)/Var(m))', 'Unsystematic risk', 'Downside risk', 'B', 'Beta is the asset''s sensitivity to market movements — its systematic risk.', 2),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-risk-return-capm-q04', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-risk-return-capm', 'The CAPM expected return equals:', 'R_f + β(E[R_m] − R_f)', 'R_f × β', 'E[R_m] − R_f', 'β / R_f', 'A', 'CAPM: expected return = risk-free rate + beta times the market risk premium.', 3),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-risk-return-capm-q05', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-risk-return-capm', 'The Security Market Line plots expected return against:', 'Total volatility', 'Beta', 'Tracking error', 'Duration', 'B', 'The SML relates expected return to systematic risk (beta).', 4),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-risk-return-capm-q06', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-risk-return-capm', 'An asset plotting above the SML is:', 'Overvalued', 'Undervalued (positive alpha)', 'Fairly valued', 'Risk-free', 'B', 'Above the SML, the asset offers more return than required for its beta — undervalued (positive alpha).', 5),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-risk-return-capm-q07', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-risk-return-capm', 'The Capital Market Line uses which risk measure?', 'Beta', 'Total risk (standard deviation)', 'Tracking error', 'VaR', 'B', 'The CML relates the expected return of efficient portfolios to total risk (σ).', 6),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-risk-return-capm-q08', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-risk-return-capm', 'Adding a risk-free asset to risky portfolios creates the:', 'Efficient frontier', 'Capital Allocation Line', 'Security Market Line', 'Yield curve', 'B', 'Combining the risk-free asset with a risky portfolio forms the CAL (the CML for the market portfolio).', 7),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-risk-return-capm-q09', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-risk-return-capm', 'The efficient frontier represents portfolios with the:', 'Lowest return for given risk', 'Highest expected return for each level of risk', 'Zero risk', 'Highest beta', 'B', 'Efficient portfolios maximize expected return for a given level of total risk.', 8),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-risk-return-capm-q10', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-risk-return-capm', 'A limitation of CAPM is its assumption of:', 'Multiple priced factors', 'Homogeneous expectations and a single period', 'Transaction costs', 'Taxes', 'B', 'CAPM assumes frictionless markets, homogeneous expectations and a single holding period — unrealistic simplifications.', 9),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-risk-return-capm-q11', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-risk-return-capm', 'A stock with beta 1.2, R_f 3% and market premium 5% has a CAPM required return of:', '6%', '9%', '8.4%', '12%', 'B', '3% + 1.2 × 5% = 3% + 6% = 9%.', 10),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-risk-return-capm-q12', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-risk-return-capm', 'Total risk equals systematic risk plus:', 'Beta', 'Unsystematic (diversifiable) risk', 'The risk-free rate', 'Alpha', 'B', 'Total variance = systematic (market) risk + unsystematic (firm-specific) risk.', 11),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-ips-allocation-q01', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-ips-allocation', 'The document governing the planning stage of portfolio management is the:', 'Prospectus', 'Investment Policy Statement (IPS)', 'Annual report', 'Tax return', 'B', 'The IPS sets out objectives and constraints that guide the entire process.', 0),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-ips-allocation-q02', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-ips-allocation', 'An investor''s risk tolerance is the lower of their willingness and:', 'Ability to take risk', 'Return objective', 'Time horizon', 'Tax rate', 'A', 'When willingness and ability differ, the more conservative (lower) of the two governs.', 1),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-ips-allocation-q03', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-ips-allocation', 'Which is an IPS constraint?', 'Return objective', 'Liquidity needs', 'Risk tolerance', 'Target alpha', 'B', 'Constraints include time horizon, taxes, liquidity, legal/regulatory, and unique circumstances.', 2),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-ips-allocation-q04', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-ips-allocation', 'Strategic asset allocation is derived from the IPS and:', 'Last week''s returns', 'Capital market expectations', 'Broker tips', 'Social media', 'B', 'SAA combines the investor''s objectives/constraints with long-run capital market expectations.', 3),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-ips-allocation-q05', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-ips-allocation', 'Tactical asset allocation involves:', 'Permanent policy weights', 'Short-run deviations from policy to exploit views', 'Never trading', 'Ignoring the IPS', 'B', 'TAA makes temporary shifts around the strategic weights to capture short-term opportunities.', 4),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-ips-allocation-q06', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-ips-allocation', 'Empirically, the largest driver of long-run return variability is:', 'Security selection', 'Strategic asset allocation', 'Market timing', 'Trading costs', 'B', 'Policy (strategic) asset allocation explains most of the variation in long-run portfolio returns.', 5),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-ips-allocation-q07', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-ips-allocation', 'The three steps of the portfolio management process are planning, execution and:', 'Feedback (monitoring/rebalancing)', 'Taxation', 'Marketing', 'Custody', 'A', 'Planning → execution → feedback (monitor, rebalance, and revise the IPS).', 6),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-ips-allocation-q08', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-ips-allocation', 'A young investor with stable income and a long horizon typically has:', 'Low ability to take risk', 'High ability to take risk', 'No need for an IPS', 'Only liquidity constraints', 'B', 'Long horizons and steady income increase the ability to bear risk.', 7),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-ips-allocation-q09', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-ips-allocation', 'A liability-relative asset allocation is most appropriate for a:', 'Young individual with no liabilities', 'Defined-benefit pension fund', 'Day trader', 'Cash-only saver', 'B', 'Managing assets against defined liabilities fits pensions and insurers.', 8),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-ips-allocation-q10', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-ips-allocation', 'Goals-based asset allocation constructs:', 'One portfolio for everything', 'Sub-portfolios matched to specific goals', 'A cash-only portfolio', 'Equal weights', 'B', 'Goals-based investing builds distinct sub-portfolios for each goal''s horizon and required success.', 9),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-ips-allocation-q11', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-ips-allocation', 'Rebalancing serves primarily to:', 'Maximize turnover', 'Keep the portfolio aligned with policy weights / control risk', 'Chase momentum', 'Avoid taxes', 'B', 'Rebalancing returns drifted weights to the strategic target, controlling risk.', 10),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-ips-allocation-q12', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-ips-allocation', 'An IPS should be revised when:', 'Markets move daily', 'The investor''s circumstances or objectives change materially', 'Never', 'Returns are positive', 'B', 'The IPS is reviewed and updated as the investor''s situation, goals, or constraints change materially.', 11),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-financial-institutions-q01', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-financial-institutions', 'The CAMELS supervisory framework''s ''C'' stands for:', 'Capital adequacy', 'Cash flow', 'Credit spread', 'Collateral', 'A', 'CAMELS begins with Capital adequacy, followed by Asset quality, Management, Earnings, Liquidity and Sensitivity.', 0),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-financial-institutions-q02', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-financial-institutions', 'Basel III''s liquidity coverage ratio (LCR) is designed to ensure a bank can survive:', 'A 30-day stress period', 'A 10-year recession', 'One business day', 'A single trade loss', 'A', 'The LCR requires enough high-quality liquid assets to cover 30 days of net outflows under stress.', 1),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-financial-institutions-q03', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-financial-institutions', 'Net interest margin is calculated as net interest income divided by:', 'Average earning assets', 'Total equity', 'Total deposits', 'Net income', 'A', 'NIM = net interest income / average interest-earning assets.', 2),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-financial-institutions-q04', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-financial-institutions', 'A rising provision for loan losses generally signals:', 'Expected credit deterioration', 'Improving asset quality', 'Higher net interest margin', 'Lower leverage', 'A', 'Provisions rise when a bank expects more loans to default, indicating deteriorating asset quality.', 3),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-financial-institutions-q05', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-financial-institutions', 'The Common Equity Tier 1 (CET1) ratio measures capital relative to:', 'Risk-weighted assets', 'Total deposits', 'Market capitalization', 'Net revenue', 'A', 'CET1 and other Basel capital ratios are expressed as capital divided by risk-weighted assets.', 4),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-financial-institutions-q06', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-financial-institutions', 'For a property & casualty insurer, a combined ratio above 100% indicates:', 'An underwriting loss', 'An underwriting profit', 'Excess liquidity', 'High investment income', 'A', 'A combined ratio over 100% means claims plus expenses exceed premiums — an underwriting loss.', 5),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-financial-institutions-q07', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-financial-institutions', 'Why do financial institutions warrant special analytical frameworks?', 'Their assets are largely financial and they are heavily regulated', 'They hold no leverage', 'They have no credit risk', 'They report under a separate calendar', 'A', 'Banks and insurers hold mostly financial assets, are highly leveraged and heavily regulated, so standard industrial analysis is insufficient.', 6),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-financial-institutions-q08', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-financial-institutions', 'The Basel III net stable funding ratio (NSFR) addresses:', 'Longer-term structural funding stability', 'Daily trading limits', 'Dividend policy', 'Tax deferral', 'A', 'The NSFR promotes funding of assets with stable, longer-term liabilities over a one-year horizon.', 7),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-financial-institutions-q09', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-financial-institutions', 'The ''S'' in CAMELS refers to sensitivity to:', 'Market risk', 'Shareholder votes', 'Supplier terms', 'Sales cycles', 'A', 'The S in CAMELS is sensitivity to market risk (e.g., interest-rate and FX exposure).', 8),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-financial-institutions-q10', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-financial-institutions', 'Which item is most useful for judging a bank''s asset quality?', 'Non-performing loan ratio', 'Dividend yield', 'Inventory turnover', 'Days sales outstanding', 'A', 'The non-performing loan ratio directly reflects the quality of a bank''s loan book.', 9),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-financial-institutions-q11', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-financial-institutions', 'Off-balance-sheet exposures are important for banks because they:', 'Can create hidden leverage and risk', 'Are always risk-free', 'Reduce reported assets to zero', 'Eliminate credit risk', 'A', 'Guarantees, commitments and derivatives off the balance sheet can add significant leverage and risk.', 10),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-financial-institutions-q12', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-financial-institutions', 'Tier 1 capital primarily consists of:', 'Common equity and retained earnings', 'Subordinated debt', 'Loan loss reserves', 'Customer deposits', 'A', 'Tier 1 (going-concern) capital is dominated by common equity and retained earnings.', 11),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-share-based-compensation-q01', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-share-based-compensation', 'Share-based compensation expense is based on fair value measured at the:', 'Grant date', 'Exercise date', 'Vesting date', 'Reporting date', 'A', 'Fair value is typically measured at the grant date and recognised over the vesting period.', 0),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-share-based-compensation-q02', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-share-based-compensation', 'Stock-option compensation expense is recognised over the:', 'Vesting period', 'Option''s full 10-year life', 'First year only', 'Period after exercise', 'A', 'Expense is allocated over the service (vesting) period during which the employee earns the award.', 1),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-share-based-compensation-q03', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-share-based-compensation', 'Increasing the assumed volatility in an option-pricing model will:', 'Increase the option''s fair value and expense', 'Decrease the expense', 'Have no effect', 'Eliminate dilution', 'A', 'Higher volatility raises option value, increasing compensation expense.', 2),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-share-based-compensation-q04', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-share-based-compensation', 'Restricted stock units (RSUs) are generally valued at:', 'The grant-date share price', 'The strike price', 'Book value per share', 'Par value', 'A', 'RSUs are valued at the market price of the shares on the grant date.', 3),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-share-based-compensation-q05', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-share-based-compensation', 'Stock options affect which EPS figure through the treasury-stock method?', 'Diluted EPS', 'Basic EPS', 'Cash EPS', 'Trailing EPS only', 'A', 'Potentially dilutive options are reflected in diluted EPS via the treasury-stock method.', 4),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-share-based-compensation-q06', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-share-based-compensation', 'A longer assumed expected life of an option generally:', 'Increases its fair value', 'Decreases its fair value', 'Has no effect', 'Converts it to an RSU', 'A', 'A longer expected life raises option value, all else equal.', 5),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-share-based-compensation-q07', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-share-based-compensation', 'Share-based compensation creates two analyst concerns: expense and:', 'Dilution', 'Deferred revenue', 'Goodwill impairment', 'Inventory obsolescence', 'A', 'Beyond the expense, share awards dilute existing shareholders'' ownership.', 6),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-share-based-compensation-q08', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-share-based-compensation', 'A higher assumed risk-free rate in the option model generally:', 'Increases call-option value', 'Decreases call-option value', 'Has no effect', 'Eliminates expense', 'A', 'A higher risk-free rate raises the value of a call option, increasing expense.', 7),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-share-based-compensation-q09', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-share-based-compensation', 'Management could understate compensation expense by assuming:', 'Unrealistically low volatility', 'High volatility', 'A long option life', 'A high dividend yield only when it raises value', 'A', 'Assuming low volatility lowers option fair value and thus reported expense.', 8),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-share-based-compensation-q10', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-share-based-compensation', 'Compared with cash salary, stock options conserve which resource in the short run?', 'Cash', 'Equity', 'Debt capacity', 'Tax credits', 'A', 'Options reward employees without an immediate cash outflow, conserving cash.', 9),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-share-based-compensation-q11', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-share-based-compensation', 'A higher assumed dividend yield generally has what effect on call-option value?', 'Decreases it', 'Increases it', 'No effect', 'Doubles it', 'A', 'Higher expected dividends reduce a call option''s value because holders forgo dividends.', 10),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-share-based-compensation-q12', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-share-based-compensation', 'Share-based compensation is intended primarily to:', 'Align employee and shareholder interests', 'Reduce audit fees', 'Increase leverage', 'Defer income taxes indefinitely', 'A', 'Equity awards align employees'' incentives with shareholder value creation.', 11),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-reporting-quality-q01', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-reporting-quality', 'Reporting quality refers to:', 'The faithful representation of economic reality', 'The level of sustainable earnings', 'The dividend payout ratio', 'The market''s valuation multiple', 'A', 'Reporting quality is about decision-useful, faithful representation, distinct from earnings quality.', 0),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-reporting-quality-q02', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-reporting-quality', 'Earnings quality refers to earnings that are:', 'Sustainable and provide adequate returns', 'Always increasing', 'Reported under IFRS only', 'Equal to cash flow exactly', 'A', 'High earnings quality means sustainable earnings that provide an adequate return on investment.', 1),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-reporting-quality-q03', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-reporting-quality', 'A widening gap between net income and operating cash flow suggests:', 'High accruals and possible lower earnings quality', 'Strong cash generation', 'Lower leverage', 'Higher reporting quality', 'A', 'Large accruals (net income far above operating cash flow) often signal lower earnings quality.', 2),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-reporting-quality-q04', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-reporting-quality', 'A ''bill-and-hold'' arrangement is a red flag for:', 'Premature revenue recognition', 'Understated revenue', 'Excess conservatism', 'Deferred tax assets', 'A', 'Bill-and-hold sales can be used to recognise revenue before goods are delivered.', 3),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-reporting-quality-q05', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-reporting-quality', 'The Beneish M-score is used to assess the likelihood of:', 'Earnings manipulation', 'Bankruptcy within one year', 'A dividend cut', 'An interest-rate change', 'A', 'The Beneish M-score estimates the probability that earnings have been manipulated.', 4),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-reporting-quality-q06', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-reporting-quality', 'On the quality spectrum, the lowest level is:', 'Fictitious transactions', 'GAAP-compliant and decision-useful', 'Biased choices within GAAP', 'Conservative estimates', 'A', 'Fabricated or fictitious transactions represent the lowest reporting quality.', 5),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-reporting-quality-q07', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-reporting-quality', 'The fraud triangle comprises opportunity, rationalization, and:', 'Motivation (incentive/pressure)', 'Regulation', 'Diversification', 'Liquidity', 'A', 'Fraud typically requires opportunity, motivation/pressure, and rationalization.', 6),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-reporting-quality-q08', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-reporting-quality', 'Capitalising costs that should be expensed will initially:', 'Overstate net income', 'Understate assets', 'Reduce equity', 'Increase cash flow from financing', 'A', 'Improper capitalization defers expense recognition, overstating current net income.', 7),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-reporting-quality-q09', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-reporting-quality', 'Which condition most enables low-quality reporting?', 'Weak corporate governance', 'Strong internal controls', 'Independent audit committee', 'Conservative accounting culture', 'A', 'Weak governance and controls create the opportunity for manipulation.', 8),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-reporting-quality-q10', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-reporting-quality', 'Classifying a recurring operating cost as ''non-recurring'' tends to:', 'Overstate sustainable (core) earnings', 'Understate revenue', 'Reduce total expenses to zero', 'Lower reported assets', 'A', 'Labelling recurring costs as one-time inflates the perceived sustainable earnings.', 9),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-reporting-quality-q11', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-reporting-quality', 'High reporting quality with low earnings quality means the reports:', 'Faithfully show genuinely weak performance', 'Are fraudulent', 'Overstate results', 'Cannot be analysed', 'A', 'Reports can faithfully represent a business that simply has poor, unsustainable earnings.', 10),
  ('cfa2-financial-reporting-reporting-quality-q12', 'cfa2-financial-reporting-reporting-quality', 'A high accruals ratio generally indicates:', 'Lower earnings quality', 'Higher cash conversion', 'Stronger reporting quality', 'Lower leverage', 'A', 'A high proportion of accruals in earnings is associated with lower earnings persistence and quality.', 11),
  ('cfa2-equity-valuation-process-q01', 'cfa2-equity-valuation-process', 'Intrinsic value is best described as:', 'The value justified by an asset''s fundamentals', 'The current market price', 'The book value of equity', 'The 52-week high', 'A', 'Intrinsic value is the value warranted by fundamentals, compared against market price.', 0),
  ('cfa2-equity-valuation-process-q02', 'cfa2-equity-valuation-process', 'A dividend discount model is most appropriate for a firm that:', 'Pays dividends related to its capacity to pay', 'Never pays dividends', 'Has negative equity', 'Is about to be liquidated', 'A', 'DDM suits dividend-paying firms whose dividends track their ability to pay.', 1),
  ('cfa2-equity-valuation-process-q03', 'cfa2-equity-valuation-process', 'Free cash flow models are preferred over DDM when:', 'Dividends differ markedly from the firm''s capacity to pay', 'The firm has a long dividend history', 'Earnings equal dividends', 'The stock is an index fund', 'A', 'FCF models are used when dividends do not reflect the firm''s cash-generating capacity.', 2),
  ('cfa2-equity-valuation-process-q04', 'cfa2-equity-valuation-process', 'Residual income models are especially useful when:', 'Free cash flows are negative and accounting data are informative', 'The firm pays large dividends', 'There is no book value', 'The market is perfectly efficient', 'A', 'Residual income works well when FCF is negative but book value and earnings are meaningful.', 3),
  ('cfa2-equity-valuation-process-q05', 'cfa2-equity-valuation-process', 'An EV/EBITDA multiple is an example of a:', 'Relative (multiplier) valuation model', 'Present-value model', 'Asset-based model', 'Option-pricing model', 'A', 'Multiples like EV/EBITDA are relative valuation tools comparing a firm to peers.', 4),
  ('cfa2-equity-valuation-process-q06', 'cfa2-equity-valuation-process', 'A positive difference between an analyst''s intrinsic value and market price implies the asset is:', 'Undervalued', 'Overvalued', 'Fairly valued', 'Illiquid', 'A', 'If intrinsic value exceeds price, the asset appears undervalued.', 5),
  ('cfa2-equity-valuation-process-q07', 'cfa2-equity-valuation-process', 'Asset-based valuation values equity as:', 'The value of assets minus liabilities', 'Discounted dividends', 'A P/E multiple', 'EBITDA times a factor', 'A', 'Asset-based models value equity as the fair value of net assets.', 6),
  ('cfa2-equity-valuation-process-q08', 'cfa2-equity-valuation-process', 'The realized alpha from a valuation is captured when:', 'The market price converges toward intrinsic value', 'Dividends are paid', 'The firm issues shares', 'Volatility falls', 'A', 'Alpha is realized as price moves toward the analyst''s estimate of intrinsic value.', 7),
  ('cfa2-equity-valuation-process-q09', 'cfa2-equity-valuation-process', 'A control premium may be added when valuing:', 'A controlling equity stake', 'A single share in a liquid market', 'A government bond', 'A money-market fund', 'A', 'Acquiring control confers benefits, so a control premium applies to controlling stakes.', 8),
  ('cfa2-equity-valuation-process-q10', 'cfa2-equity-valuation-process', 'Going-concern value assumes the firm:', 'Continues operating into the future', 'Is immediately liquidated', 'Has no revenue', 'Pays no taxes', 'A', 'Going-concern value assumes continued operations, unlike liquidation value.', 9),
  ('cfa2-equity-valuation-process-q11', 'cfa2-equity-valuation-process', 'A key input common to all present-value equity models is the:', 'Required rate of return (discount rate)', 'P/B ratio', 'Dividend payout only', 'Bid-ask spread', 'A', 'All DCF-type models discount future flows at a required rate of return.', 10),
  ('cfa2-equity-valuation-process-q12', 'cfa2-equity-valuation-process', 'A marketability (liquidity) discount is applied to:', 'Shares that are hard to sell quickly', 'Highly liquid large-cap stocks', 'Treasury bills', 'Index ETFs', 'A', 'Illiquid, hard-to-sell interests warrant a discount for lack of marketability.', 11),
  ('cfa2-equity-return-concepts-q01', 'cfa2-equity-return-concepts', 'In the CAPM, the required return equals the risk-free rate plus:', 'Beta times the equity risk premium', 'The dividend yield', 'The inflation rate', 'The book-to-market ratio', 'A', 'CAPM: r = Rf + β × ERP.', 0),
  ('cfa2-equity-return-concepts-q02', 'cfa2-equity-return-concepts', 'The bond-yield-plus-risk-premium approach adds an equity risk premium to the firm''s:', 'Debt yield to maturity', 'Dividend yield', 'Book value', 'Cash balance', 'A', 'This method adds a risk premium to the yield on the company''s long-term debt.', 1),
  ('cfa2-equity-return-concepts-q03', 'cfa2-equity-return-concepts', 'The required return is best described as:', 'The minimum return investors demand for the risk', 'The historical average return', 'The dividend growth rate', 'The risk-free rate only', 'A', 'The required return is the minimum compensation demanded for bearing the asset''s risk.', 2),
  ('cfa2-equity-return-concepts-q04', 'cfa2-equity-return-concepts', 'If an asset''s expected return exceeds its required return, it is:', 'Undervalued', 'Overvalued', 'Fairly priced', 'Risk-free', 'A', 'A positive expected-minus-required return signals undervaluation.', 3),
  ('cfa2-equity-return-concepts-q05', 'cfa2-equity-return-concepts', 'The equity risk premium is the expected return on equities in excess of the:', 'Risk-free rate', 'Dividend yield', 'Inflation rate', 'Market P/E', 'A', 'The ERP is the extra return equities are expected to earn over the risk-free rate.', 4),
  ('cfa2-equity-return-concepts-q06', 'cfa2-equity-return-concepts', 'A forward-looking estimate of the ERP can be derived from the:', 'Gordon (dividend discount) growth model', 'Durbin-Watson statistic', 'Combined ratio', 'Sharpe ratio', 'A', 'The Gordon growth model gives a forward-looking ERP from dividend yield plus growth minus the risk-free rate.', 5),
  ('cfa2-equity-return-concepts-q07', 'cfa2-equity-return-concepts', 'Multifactor models such as Fama–French add which factors to the market factor?', 'Size and value', 'Dividend and payout', 'Liquidity and leverage only', 'Duration and convexity', 'A', 'The Fama–French model adds size (SMB) and value (HML) factors.', 6),
  ('cfa2-equity-return-concepts-q08', 'cfa2-equity-return-concepts', 'A small, illiquid stock may require adding to CAPM a:', 'Size or illiquidity premium', 'Negative beta', 'Coupon adjustment', 'Currency hedge', 'A', 'Analysts often add size and illiquidity premiums for small, thinly traded stocks.', 7),
  ('cfa2-equity-return-concepts-q09', 'cfa2-equity-return-concepts', 'Beta in the CAPM measures:', 'Systematic (market) risk', 'Total risk', 'Unsystematic risk', 'Default risk', 'A', 'Beta captures an asset''s sensitivity to systematic market risk.', 8),
  ('cfa2-equity-return-concepts-q10', 'cfa2-equity-return-concepts', 'The required return serves in valuation as the:', 'Discount rate for future cash flows', 'Terminal growth rate', 'Payout ratio', 'Tax rate', 'A', 'The required return is the discount rate applied to expected future cash flows.', 9),
  ('cfa2-equity-return-concepts-q11', 'cfa2-equity-return-concepts', 'Historical estimates of the ERP can be biased by:', 'Survivorship bias in the index', 'Perfect foresight', 'Zero volatility', 'Negative interest rates only', 'A', 'Using a surviving index can overstate the historical equity risk premium.', 10),
  ('cfa2-equity-return-concepts-q12', 'cfa2-equity-return-concepts', 'A higher required return, all else equal, leads to a:', 'Lower intrinsic value', 'Higher intrinsic value', 'Unchanged value', 'Higher dividend', 'A', 'A higher discount rate reduces the present value of future cash flows, lowering value.', 11),
  ('cfa2-equity-dividend-discount-q01', 'cfa2-equity-dividend-discount', 'The Gordon growth model values a share as:', 'D₁ / (r − g)', 'D₁ × (r − g)', 'D₁ / (r + g)', 'D₀ × r', 'A', 'The Gordon model gives V₀ = D₁/(r − g) for constant growth.', 0),
  ('cfa2-equity-dividend-discount-q02', 'cfa2-equity-dividend-discount', 'The Gordon growth model is valid only when:', 'The growth rate is less than the required return', 'g equals r', 'g exceeds r', 'Dividends are zero', 'A', 'The model requires g < r for a finite, positive value.', 1),
  ('cfa2-equity-dividend-discount-q03', 'cfa2-equity-dividend-discount', 'Sustainable growth equals the retention ratio times:', 'ROE', 'ROA', 'The payout ratio', 'Beta', 'A', 'g = retention ratio (b) × ROE.', 2),
  ('cfa2-equity-dividend-discount-q04', 'cfa2-equity-dividend-discount', 'With D₁ = $2, r = 10%, g = 4%, the Gordon value per share is:', '$33.33', '$20.00', '$50.00', '$14.29', 'A', 'V = 2/(0.10 − 0.04) = 2/0.06 = $33.33.', 3),
  ('cfa2-equity-dividend-discount-q05', 'cfa2-equity-dividend-discount', 'The H-model is designed to handle growth that:', 'Declines linearly to a stable long-run rate', 'Is constant forever', 'Jumps instantly to zero', 'Rises without bound', 'A', 'The H-model approximates a growth rate that declines linearly toward a long-run rate.', 4),
  ('cfa2-equity-dividend-discount-q06', 'cfa2-equity-dividend-discount', 'The Gordon model is most sensitive to changes in:', 'The spread (r − g)', 'The share count', 'The current price', 'Trading volume', 'A', 'Because value = D₁/(r − g), small changes in the denominator move value sharply.', 5),
  ('cfa2-equity-dividend-discount-q07', 'cfa2-equity-dividend-discount', 'A two-stage DDM uses a terminal value based on:', 'The Gordon model in the stable phase', 'The current price', 'Book value', 'A P/E of 1', 'A', 'The terminal value in a two-stage DDM typically applies the Gordon model to the mature phase.', 6),
  ('cfa2-equity-dividend-discount-q08', 'cfa2-equity-dividend-discount', 'If price = $40 and D₁ = $2 with r = 10%, the implied growth rate g is:', '5%', '10%', '2%', '8%', 'A', 'g = r − D₁/P = 0.10 − 2/40 = 0.10 − 0.05 = 5%.', 7),
  ('cfa2-equity-dividend-discount-q09', 'cfa2-equity-dividend-discount', 'In the retention-growth formula, a higher payout ratio implies:', 'Lower sustainable growth', 'Higher sustainable growth', 'No change in growth', 'Higher beta', 'A', 'A higher payout means lower retention, reducing sustainable growth (g = b × ROE).', 8),
  ('cfa2-equity-dividend-discount-q10', 'cfa2-equity-dividend-discount', 'The DDM values a share as the present value of:', 'Expected future dividends', 'Future earnings', 'Book value', 'Sales', 'A', 'The dividend discount model discounts all expected future dividends.', 9),
  ('cfa2-equity-dividend-discount-q11', 'cfa2-equity-dividend-discount', 'Raising ROE while holding retention constant will:', 'Increase sustainable growth', 'Decrease growth', 'Leave growth unchanged', 'Reduce the payout to zero', 'A', 'Since g = b × ROE, a higher ROE raises sustainable growth.', 10),
  ('cfa2-equity-dividend-discount-q12', 'cfa2-equity-dividend-discount', 'In the H-model, H represents:', 'Half the length of the high-growth period', 'The full high-growth period', 'The dividend payout', 'The risk-free rate', 'A', 'H is half the number of years of the declining high-growth phase.', 11),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-embedded-options-q01', 'cfa2-fixed-income-embedded-options', 'The value of a callable bond equals the straight-bond value:', 'Minus the value of the call option', 'Plus the value of the call option', 'Times the call price', 'Divided by the yield', 'A', 'The issuer''s call option reduces the bond''s value: callable = straight − call.', 0),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-embedded-options-q02', 'cfa2-fixed-income-embedded-options', 'The value of a putable bond equals the straight-bond value:', 'Plus the value of the put option', 'Minus the value of the put option', 'Times the put price', 'Minus accrued interest', 'A', 'The investor''s put option adds value: putable = straight + put.', 1),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-embedded-options-q03', 'cfa2-fixed-income-embedded-options', 'Option-adjusted spread (OAS) is useful because it:', 'Removes the effect of embedded options for comparison', 'Includes the option cost', 'Ignores credit risk', 'Equals the coupon rate', 'A', 'OAS strips out optionality so bonds with different options can be compared.', 2),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-embedded-options-q04', 'cfa2-fixed-income-embedded-options', 'A callable bond can exhibit negative convexity when:', 'Interest rates fall toward the call level', 'Rates rise sharply', 'The bond defaults', 'The coupon is zero', 'A', 'As rates fall and the call becomes likely, price appreciation is capped, giving negative convexity.', 3),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-embedded-options-q05', 'cfa2-fixed-income-embedded-options', 'Embedded options are typically valued using a:', 'Binomial interest-rate tree with backward induction', 'Single discount rate', 'Simple average of coupons', 'Payback period', 'A', 'A binomial rate tree with backward induction values the option-embedded cash flows.', 4),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-embedded-options-q06', 'cfa2-fixed-income-embedded-options', 'Higher interest-rate volatility increases the value of:', 'Both the call and put options', 'Neither option', 'Only the coupon', 'The par value', 'A', 'Greater volatility raises the value of both embedded options.', 5),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-embedded-options-q07', 'cfa2-fixed-income-embedded-options', 'As rate volatility rises, a callable bond''s price:', 'Falls', 'Rises', 'Is unchanged', 'Equals the put value', 'A', 'A more valuable call (subtracted from the straight bond) lowers the callable''s price.', 6),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-embedded-options-q08', 'cfa2-fixed-income-embedded-options', 'Effective duration is the appropriate rate-sensitivity measure for bonds with:', 'Embedded options', 'No cash flows', 'Fixed maturities only', 'No credit risk', 'A', 'Effective duration accounts for how optionality changes cash flows as rates move.', 7),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-embedded-options-q09', 'cfa2-fixed-income-embedded-options', 'At a call node in the tree, the bond''s value is:', 'Capped at the call price', 'Set to par always', 'Doubled', 'Ignored', 'A', 'If the computed value exceeds the call price, the issuer calls, so value is capped at the call price.', 8),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-embedded-options-q10', 'cfa2-fixed-income-embedded-options', 'The call option in a callable bond benefits the:', 'Issuer', 'Bondholder', 'Rating agency', 'Custodian', 'A', 'The issuer holds the call, allowing it to refinance when rates fall.', 9),
  ('cfa2-fixed-income-embedded-options-q11', 'cfa2-fixed-income-embedded-options', 'A wider OAS on a bond generally indicates:', 'Greater compensation for credit/liquidity risk', 'Lower risk', 'A richer valuation', 'A shorter maturity', 'A', 'A larger option-adjusted spread reflects more compensation for non-option risks.', 10),
