  ('cfa2-derivatives-binomial-trees-q06', 'cfa2-derivatives-binomial-trees', 'Early exercise is most relevant for:', 'American puts and dividend-paying calls', 'European calls', 'European puts', 'Zero-coupon bonds', 'A', 'American puts and calls on dividend payers can be optimally exercised early.', 5),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-binomial-trees-q07', 'cfa2-derivatives-binomial-trees', 'As the number of time steps increases, the binomial price converges to the:', 'Black–Scholes–Merton price', 'Par value', 'Intrinsic value only', 'Strike price', 'A', 'With many steps the binomial model approaches the continuous-time BSM value.', 6),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-binomial-trees-q08', 'cfa2-derivatives-binomial-trees', 'The down-move probability in the risk-neutral world is:', '1 − π', 'π', 'u − d', '1/π', 'A', 'If π is the up probability, the down probability is (1 − π).', 7),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-binomial-trees-q09', 'cfa2-derivatives-binomial-trees', 'The value of an option at a node is the discounted expected value using:', 'Risk-neutral probabilities and the risk-free rate', 'Real probabilities and the cost of equity', 'Equal weights and WACC', 'The dividend yield', 'A', 'Each node''s value discounts the risk-neutral expected payoff at the risk-free rate.', 8),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-binomial-trees-q10', 'cfa2-derivatives-binomial-trees', 'A larger up factor u relative to d, holding r fixed, generally:', 'Raises a call''s value', 'Lowers a call''s value', 'Has no effect', 'Makes the option worthless', 'A', 'Greater potential upside increases a call option''s value.', 9),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-binomial-trees-q11', 'cfa2-derivatives-binomial-trees', 'Binomial trees can value which instruments beyond simple options?', 'Bonds with embedded options', 'Only equities', 'Only currencies', 'Nothing else', 'A', 'Interest-rate binomial trees value callable/putable bonds and other path-dependent claims.', 10),
  ('cfa2-derivatives-binomial-trees-q12', 'cfa2-derivatives-binomial-trees', 'The essential insight of risk-neutral pricing is that value depends on:', 'Replication and no-arbitrage, not risk preferences', 'Investor optimism', 'Past returns', 'The dividend policy', 'A', 'The option can be replicated by the underlying and a bond, so pricing is preference-free.', 11),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-regression-diagnostics-q01', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-regression-diagnostics', 'Adjusted R² differs from R² because it:', 'Penalises adding regressors', 'Is always larger', 'Ignores the residuals', 'Equals the F-statistic', 'A', 'Adjusted R² penalises additional independent variables that don''t improve the model.', 0),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-regression-diagnostics-q02', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-regression-diagnostics', 'The F-test in a regression assesses:', 'Overall significance of the model', 'A single coefficient', 'Normality of one variable', 'The intercept only', 'A', 'The F-test evaluates whether the regressors jointly explain the dependent variable.', 1),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-regression-diagnostics-q03', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-regression-diagnostics', 'Heteroskedasticity refers to:', 'Non-constant variance of the residuals', 'Correlated residuals over time', 'Correlated regressors', 'Missing data', 'A', 'Heteroskedasticity is error variance that changes across observations.', 2),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-regression-diagnostics-q04', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-regression-diagnostics', 'Serial correlation is detected using the:', 'Durbin–Watson statistic', 'Variance inflation factor', 'R² alone', 'Combined ratio', 'A', 'The Durbin–Watson test detects first-order serial correlation in residuals.', 3),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-regression-diagnostics-q05', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-regression-diagnostics', 'A classic symptom of multicollinearity is:', 'High R² but few significant t-statistics', 'Low R² and all significant t', 'Zero residuals', 'A negative F-statistic', 'A', 'Multicollinearity inflates standard errors, so t-stats are insignificant despite a high R².', 4),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-regression-diagnostics-q06', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-regression-diagnostics', 'The variance inflation factor (VIF) is used to detect:', 'Multicollinearity', 'Heteroskedasticity', 'Serial correlation', 'Outliers', 'A', 'A high VIF signals that a regressor is highly correlated with others (multicollinearity).', 5),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-regression-diagnostics-q07', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-regression-diagnostics', 'Heteroskedasticity is tested with the:', 'Breusch–Pagan test', 'Durbin–Watson test', 'Dickey–Fuller test', 'Jarque–Bera test only', 'A', 'The Breusch–Pagan test checks for conditional heteroskedasticity.', 6),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-regression-diagnostics-q08', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-regression-diagnostics', 'Robust (White) standard errors are used to correct for:', 'Heteroskedasticity', 'Multicollinearity', 'Omitted variables', 'Small samples', 'A', 'White standard errors adjust inference when heteroskedasticity is present.', 7),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-regression-diagnostics-q09', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-regression-diagnostics', 'The standard error of the estimate (SEE) measures:', 'The typical size of the regression residuals', 'The slope coefficient', 'The intercept', 'The number of regressors', 'A', 'SEE reflects the average magnitude of the model''s prediction errors.', 8),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-regression-diagnostics-q10', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-regression-diagnostics', 'Uncorrected serial correlation most directly biases the:', 'Coefficient standard errors', 'R² only', 'Dependent variable''s mean', 'Sample size', 'A', 'Serial correlation biases standard errors, distorting t- and F-tests.', 9),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-regression-diagnostics-q11', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-regression-diagnostics', 'A t-test on a slope coefficient assesses whether it:', 'Differs significantly from zero', 'Equals R²', 'Exceeds the F-statistic', 'Is normally distributed', 'A', 'The t-test checks whether an individual coefficient is statistically different from zero.', 10),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-regression-diagnostics-q12', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-regression-diagnostics', 'Adding an irrelevant variable to a regression generally:', 'Raises R² but may lower adjusted R²', 'Lowers R²', 'Has no effect on either', 'Guarantees significance', 'A', 'R² never falls when variables are added, but adjusted R² can fall if the variable is useless.', 11),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-logistic-regression-q01', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-logistic-regression', 'Logistic regression is used when the dependent variable is:', 'Binary (0 or 1)', 'Continuous', 'A time series', 'Always positive', 'A', 'Logistic regression models a binary categorical outcome.', 0),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-logistic-regression-q02', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-logistic-regression', 'Logistic regression models the:', 'Log-odds of the outcome', 'Raw dollar value', 'Variance of X', 'Residual sum of squares', 'A', 'It models the natural log of the odds, ln[p/(1 − p)].', 1),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-logistic-regression-q03', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-logistic-regression', 'Logistic regression coefficients are estimated by:', 'Maximum likelihood', 'Ordinary least squares', 'The method of moments only', 'Linear interpolation', 'A', 'Maximum likelihood estimation is used for logistic models.', 2),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-logistic-regression-q04', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-logistic-regression', 'The predicted probability from a logistic model always lies:', 'Between 0 and 1', 'Above 1', 'Below 0', 'Between −1 and 1', 'A', 'The logistic function maps any input to a probability in (0, 1).', 3),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-logistic-regression-q05', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-logistic-regression', 'Exponentiating a logistic coefficient, e^b, gives the:', 'Odds ratio', 'R²', 'Standard error', 'Residual', 'A', 'e^b is the odds ratio for a one-unit change in the predictor.', 4),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-logistic-regression-q06', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-logistic-regression', 'A common finance application of logistic regression is:', 'Estimating probability of default', 'Forecasting GDP growth', 'Pricing a coupon bond', 'Computing duration', 'A', 'Logistic regression is widely used to model default probability (a binary event).', 5),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-logistic-regression-q07', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-logistic-regression', 'A positive logistic coefficient means a higher predictor value:', 'Raises the probability of the event', 'Lowers the probability', 'Has no effect', 'Makes probability negative', 'A', 'A positive coefficient increases the modeled probability of the outcome.', 6),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-logistic-regression-q08', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-logistic-regression', 'Why is a linear probability model often inadequate for binary outcomes?', 'It can predict probabilities outside 0–1', 'It uses maximum likelihood', 'It has no intercept', 'It cannot include predictors', 'A', 'A linear model can produce nonsensical probabilities below 0 or above 1.', 7),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-logistic-regression-q09', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-logistic-regression', 'Logistic model fit is often assessed with a:', 'Likelihood ratio test', 'Durbin–Watson statistic', 'Simple R² only', 'Combined ratio', 'A', 'Likelihood ratio tests compare nested logistic models.', 8),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-logistic-regression-q10', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-logistic-regression', 'The odds of an event equal:', 'p / (1 − p)', '1 − p', 'p × (1 − p)', 'p + 1', 'A', 'Odds are the probability of the event divided by the probability of non-event.', 9),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-logistic-regression-q11', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-logistic-regression', 'The logistic (sigmoid) function has what shape?', 'S-shaped', 'Straight line', 'U-shaped', 'Vertical', 'A', 'The logistic function is an S-shaped curve bounded by 0 and 1.', 10),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-logistic-regression-q12', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-logistic-regression', 'An odds ratio greater than 1 indicates the predictor:', 'Increases the odds of the event', 'Decreases the odds', 'Has no effect', 'Is statistically insignificant', 'A', 'An odds ratio above 1 means higher predictor values raise the event''s odds.', 11),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-big-data-projects-q01', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-big-data-projects', 'Data cleansing primarily involves:', 'Fixing errors and handling missing values', 'Training the model', 'Selecting hyperparameters', 'Publishing results', 'A', 'Cleansing corrects inaccurate, incomplete or inconsistent data.', 0),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-big-data-projects-q02', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-big-data-projects', 'Feature engineering refers to:', 'Creating useful input variables from raw data', 'Deleting all variables', 'Rating the model', 'Collecting more rows only', 'A', 'Feature engineering transforms raw data into informative model inputs.', 1),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-big-data-projects-q03', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-big-data-projects', 'In text analysis, splitting text into individual words is called:', 'Tokenization', 'Normalization', 'Regularization', 'Aggregation', 'A', 'Tokenization breaks text into tokens (typically words).', 2),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-big-data-projects-q04', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-big-data-projects', 'Reducing words to their root form is called:', 'Stemming', 'Tokenizing', 'Scaling', 'Encoding', 'A', 'Stemming trims words to a common root (e.g., ''running'' → ''run'').', 3),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-big-data-projects-q05', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-big-data-projects', 'A document-term matrix records:', 'The frequency of terms across documents', 'The stock prices', 'The regression residuals', 'The confusion matrix', 'A', 'A document-term matrix tabulates term counts for each document.', 4),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-big-data-projects-q06', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-big-data-projects', 'Model precision is defined as true positives divided by:', 'All predicted positives', 'All actual positives', 'Total observations', 'True negatives', 'A', 'Precision = TP / (TP + FP), i.e., true positives over all predicted positives.', 5),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-big-data-projects-q07', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-big-data-projects', 'Recall is defined as true positives divided by:', 'All actual positives', 'All predicted positives', 'True negatives', 'Total errors', 'A', 'Recall = TP / (TP + FN), true positives over all actual positives.', 6),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-big-data-projects-q08', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-big-data-projects', 'The F1 score is the harmonic mean of:', 'Precision and recall', 'Bias and variance', 'R² and adjusted R²', 'Mean and median', 'A', 'F1 balances precision and recall via their harmonic mean.', 7),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-big-data-projects-q09', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-big-data-projects', 'A confusion matrix summarises:', 'Classification true/false positives and negatives', 'Regression slopes', 'Time-series lags', 'Portfolio weights', 'A', 'The confusion matrix cross-tabulates predicted vs actual classes.', 8),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-big-data-projects-q10', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-big-data-projects', 'Overfitting is best guarded against by:', 'Out-of-sample (cross-)validation', 'Maximising in-sample R²', 'Adding more features always', 'Ignoring the test set', 'A', 'Validating on held-out data detects and limits overfitting.', 9),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-big-data-projects-q11', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-big-data-projects', 'The first step of a data-analysis project is to:', 'Conceptualise the modeling task', 'Train the model', 'Tune hyperparameters', 'Deploy to production', 'A', 'Projects begin by conceptualising the objective and outcome.', 10),
  ('cfa2-quantitative-methods-big-data-projects-q12', 'cfa2-quantitative-methods-big-data-projects', 'Removing common words like ''the'' and ''and'' in text processing is called removing:', 'Stop words', 'Root words', 'Tokens', 'Features', 'A', 'Stop words carry little meaning and are removed before analysis.', 11),
  ('cfa2-economics-exchange-rate-parity-q01', 'cfa2-economics-exchange-rate-parity', 'Covered interest rate parity is enforced by:', 'Arbitrage', 'Central bank decree', 'Inflation targeting', 'Fiscal policy', 'A', 'CIRP holds because arbitrageurs exploit any deviation risk-free.', 0),
  ('cfa2-economics-exchange-rate-parity-q02', 'cfa2-economics-exchange-rate-parity', 'Under covered interest rate parity, the forward premium equals the:', 'Interest-rate differential', 'Inflation rate', 'GDP growth rate', 'Current account balance', 'A', 'The forward premium/discount reflects the interest-rate differential between currencies.', 1),
  ('cfa2-economics-exchange-rate-parity-q03', 'cfa2-economics-exchange-rate-parity', 'Uncovered interest rate parity states the expected spot change equals the:', 'Interest-rate differential', 'Forward points squared', 'Trade balance', 'Money supply growth', 'A', 'UIP predicts the higher-yielding currency depreciates by the interest differential.', 2),
  ('cfa2-economics-exchange-rate-parity-q04', 'cfa2-economics-exchange-rate-parity', 'Purchasing power parity links exchange rates to differences in:', 'Inflation', 'Interest rates', 'Tax policy', 'Unemployment', 'A', 'PPP holds that exchange rates adjust to inflation differentials.', 3),
  ('cfa2-economics-exchange-rate-parity-q05', 'cfa2-economics-exchange-rate-parity', 'The international Fisher effect relates interest-rate differentials to expected:', 'Inflation differentials', 'Output gaps', 'Budget deficits', 'Equity returns', 'A', 'It states nominal interest differentials reflect expected inflation differentials.', 4),
  ('cfa2-economics-exchange-rate-parity-q06', 'cfa2-economics-exchange-rate-parity', 'UIP tends to fail in the short run, which enables the:', 'Carry trade', 'Arbitrage-free bond price', 'Efficient frontier', 'Dividend discount model', 'A', 'Because high-yield currencies don''t depreciate as UIP predicts, carry trades can profit.', 5),
  ('cfa2-economics-exchange-rate-parity-q07', 'cfa2-economics-exchange-rate-parity', 'Covered parity uses which market to remove currency risk?', 'The forward market', 'The equity market', 'The commodity spot market', 'The options market only', 'A', 'The forward contract ''covers'' the exposure, locking in the exchange rate.', 6),
  ('cfa2-economics-exchange-rate-parity-q08', 'cfa2-economics-exchange-rate-parity', 'If domestic interest rates exceed foreign rates, CIRP implies the domestic currency trades at a forward:', 'Discount', 'Premium', 'Par with spot', 'Fixed peg', 'A', 'The higher-yielding currency trades at a forward discount under CIRP.', 7),
  ('cfa2-economics-exchange-rate-parity-q09', 'cfa2-economics-exchange-rate-parity', 'Relative PPP predicts the currency of a high-inflation country will:', 'Depreciate', 'Appreciate', 'Stay fixed', 'Become a reserve currency', 'A', 'Higher relative inflation erodes purchasing power, causing depreciation.', 8),
  ('cfa2-economics-exchange-rate-parity-q10', 'cfa2-economics-exchange-rate-parity', 'PPP tends to hold best over:', 'The long run', 'A single day', 'One week', 'Intraday trading', 'A', 'PPP is a long-run relationship; short-run deviations are large and persistent.', 9),
  ('cfa2-economics-exchange-rate-parity-q11', 'cfa2-economics-exchange-rate-parity', 'A violation of covered interest rate parity would allow a:', 'Riskless arbitrage profit', 'Guaranteed loss', 'Tax refund', 'Higher inflation', 'A', 'Any CIRP gap can be exploited for a riskless arbitrage profit, so it rarely persists.', 10),
  ('cfa2-economics-exchange-rate-parity-q12', 'cfa2-economics-exchange-rate-parity', 'The parity conditions collectively link spot rates, forward rates, interest rates and:', 'Inflation', 'Dividends', 'Corporate earnings', 'Bond ratings', 'A', 'The set of parity relationships ties together rates, forwards and inflation expectations.', 11),
  ('cfa2-economics-carry-trade-q01', 'cfa2-economics-carry-trade', 'The carry trade borrows in a low-yield currency and invests in a:', 'High-yield currency', 'Lower-yield currency', 'Zero-coupon bond', 'Domestic equity', 'A', 'The carry trade funds in low-yield and invests in high-yield currencies.', 0),
  ('cfa2-economics-carry-trade-q02', 'cfa2-economics-carry-trade', 'The carry trade is profitable when uncovered interest rate parity:', 'Fails', 'Holds exactly', 'Is enforced by arbitrage', 'Is illegal', 'A', 'Carry profits arise precisely because high-yield currencies don''t depreciate as UIP predicts.', 1),
  ('cfa2-economics-carry-trade-q03', 'cfa2-economics-carry-trade', 'Carry trade returns typically exhibit:', 'Negative skew and fat tails', 'Positive skew', 'A normal distribution', 'Zero volatility', 'A', 'Carry returns show many small gains and occasional large losses (negative skew, crash risk).', 2),
  ('cfa2-economics-carry-trade-q04', 'cfa2-economics-carry-trade', 'Carry trade losses tend to cluster during periods of:', 'High volatility and risk aversion', 'Calm markets', 'Low inflation', 'Rising equities only', 'A', 'When volatility spikes and investors flee risk, funding currencies rally and carry trades lose.', 3),
  ('cfa2-economics-carry-trade-q05', 'cfa2-economics-carry-trade', 'The currency that is borrowed in a carry trade is the:', 'Funding currency', 'Investment currency', 'Reserve currency', 'Pegged currency', 'A', 'The low-yield currency borrowed is called the funding currency.', 4),
  ('cfa2-economics-carry-trade-q06', 'cfa2-economics-carry-trade', 'The ''crash risk'' of the carry trade refers to:', 'Sudden large losses when funding currencies appreciate', 'Equity market crashes only', 'Bond defaults', 'Inflation spikes', 'A', 'Rapid unwinding causes funding currencies to jump, inflicting large carry losses.', 5),
  ('cfa2-economics-carry-trade-q07', 'cfa2-economics-carry-trade', 'Diversifying a carry trade across currency pairs:', 'Reduces but does not eliminate crash risk', 'Eliminates all risk', 'Increases skew to positive', 'Guarantees profit', 'A', 'Diversification lowers idiosyncratic risk but crashes are correlated across carry pairs.', 6),
  ('cfa2-economics-carry-trade-q08', 'cfa2-economics-carry-trade', 'A rising VIX is generally associated with carry trade:', 'Losses', 'Gains', 'No effect', 'Guaranteed returns', 'A', 'Higher volatility/risk aversion typically triggers carry-trade drawdowns.', 7),
  ('cfa2-economics-carry-trade-q09', 'cfa2-economics-carry-trade', 'Volatility trading takes a view on:', 'Future exchange-rate volatility', 'Only spot direction', 'Dividend yields', 'Bond coupons', 'A', 'Volatility trades (options, variance swaps) profit from changes in FX volatility.', 8),
  ('cfa2-economics-carry-trade-q10', 'cfa2-economics-carry-trade', 'The source of carry-trade return is primarily the:', 'Interest-rate differential', 'Dividend yield', 'Equity risk premium', 'Coupon step-up', 'A', 'The carry earns the interest-rate differential between the two currencies.', 9),
  ('cfa2-economics-carry-trade-q11', 'cfa2-economics-carry-trade', 'Carry trades are sometimes described as ''picking up pennies in front of a:', 'Steamroller''', 'Windmill''', 'Fountain''', 'Mirror''', 'A', 'The phrase captures steady small gains punctuated by rare catastrophic losses.', 10),
  ('cfa2-economics-carry-trade-q12', 'cfa2-economics-carry-trade', 'In calm, low-volatility periods, carry trades tend to:', 'Earn steady positive returns', 'Lose money steadily', 'Be impossible', 'Have positive skew', 'A', 'During tranquil markets the carry accrues steady positive returns.', 11),
  ('cfa2-economics-growth-theories-q01', 'cfa2-economics-growth-theories', 'The neoclassical (Solow) model attributes long-run per-capita growth to:', 'Exogenous technological progress', 'Capital accumulation alone', 'Population growth', 'Government spending', 'A', 'With diminishing returns to capital, sustained per-capita growth requires exogenous technology.', 0),
  ('cfa2-economics-growth-theories-q02', 'cfa2-economics-growth-theories', 'In the Solow model, capital exhibits:', 'Diminishing marginal returns', 'Increasing returns', 'Constant returns', 'Zero productivity', 'A', 'Diminishing returns to capital drive the economy toward a steady state.', 1),
  ('cfa2-economics-growth-theories-q03', 'cfa2-economics-growth-theories', 'Endogenous growth theory treats technology as:', 'Determined within the model via R&D', 'Exogenous and fixed', 'Irrelevant', 'Always zero', 'A', 'Endogenous models make knowledge/technology a product of investment, so policy affects growth.', 2),
  ('cfa2-economics-growth-theories-q04', 'cfa2-economics-growth-theories', 'The classical (Malthusian) view holds that output growth is offset by:', 'Population growth', 'Technological change', 'Rising savings', 'Lower taxes', 'A', 'In the Malthusian model, population growth pushes per-capita income back to subsistence.', 3),
  ('cfa2-economics-growth-theories-q05', 'cfa2-economics-growth-theories', 'Absolute convergence predicts that poor countries will:', 'Grow faster and catch up to rich ones', 'Fall further behind', 'Never grow', 'Have identical technology', 'A', 'Absolute convergence says lower-income economies grow faster and converge.', 4),
  ('cfa2-economics-growth-theories-q06', 'cfa2-economics-growth-theories', 'Conditional convergence occurs among countries with similar:', 'Savings, technology and institutions', 'Populations only', 'Currencies', 'Coastlines', 'A', 'Conditional convergence applies to economies sharing key structural characteristics.', 5),
  ('cfa2-economics-growth-theories-q07', 'cfa2-economics-growth-theories', 'In endogenous growth models, capital broadly defined has:', 'Constant returns', 'Sharply diminishing returns', 'Zero returns', 'Negative returns', 'A', 'Constant returns to broad capital (including knowledge) allow permanent growth effects.', 6),
  ('cfa2-economics-growth-theories-q08', 'cfa2-economics-growth-theories', 'A key policy implication of endogenous growth is that governments can:', 'Affect the long-run growth rate', 'Only affect the price level', 'Never influence growth', 'Eliminate business cycles', 'A', 'Because technology is endogenous, R&D and education policy can raise long-run growth.', 7),
  ('cfa2-economics-growth-theories-q09', 'cfa2-economics-growth-theories', 'In the Solow steady state, output per capita grows at the rate of:', 'Technological progress', 'Capital accumulation', 'Zero always', 'Population growth', 'A', 'At steady state, per-capita growth equals the rate of exogenous technological progress.', 8),
  ('cfa2-economics-growth-theories-q10', 'cfa2-economics-growth-theories', 'Sustainable growth in an economy is often decomposed via:', 'Growth accounting (capital, labour, TFP)', 'The dividend discount model', 'Duration analysis', 'The combined ratio', 'A', 'Growth accounting attributes output growth to capital, labour and total factor productivity.', 9),
  ('cfa2-economics-growth-theories-q11', 'cfa2-economics-growth-theories', 'Total factor productivity (TFP) captures growth not explained by:', 'Measured capital and labour inputs', 'Technology', 'Innovation', 'Efficiency', 'A', 'TFP is the residual output growth beyond measured capital and labour.', 10),
  ('cfa2-economics-growth-theories-q12', 'cfa2-economics-growth-theories', 'A higher savings rate in the Solow model raises the:', 'Steady-state level of output per capita', 'Long-run growth rate permanently', 'Rate of technological progress', 'Population growth rate', 'A', 'More saving raises the steady-state income level but not the long-run growth rate.', 11),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-private-capital-q01', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-private-capital', 'A private-equity fund is typically organised as a:', 'Limited partnership', 'Public corporation', 'Mutual fund', 'REIT', 'A', 'Private capital funds use the limited partnership structure with a GP and LPs.', 0),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-private-capital-q02', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-private-capital', 'In a ''2 and 20'' fee structure, the ''20'' refers to:', 'Carried interest on profits', 'The management fee', 'The hurdle rate', 'The redemption fee', 'A', 'The 20% is carried interest — the GP''s share of profits above the hurdle.', 1),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-private-capital-q03', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-private-capital', 'The hurdle rate is the return that must be exceeded before the GP earns:', 'Carried interest', 'Its management fee', 'A redemption', 'A clawback from LPs', 'A', 'Carried interest is earned only on returns above the hurdle rate.', 2),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-private-capital-q04', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-private-capital', 'The J-curve describes a fund''s:', 'Early negative returns before later gains', 'Steady linear returns', 'Constant losses', 'Immediate high returns', 'A', 'Fees and early write-downs create initial negative returns, later reversed as investments mature.', 3),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-private-capital-q05', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-private-capital', 'A clawback provision protects LPs by:', 'Returning excess carried interest to them', 'Increasing management fees', 'Reducing commitments', 'Waiving the hurdle', 'A', 'Clawback forces the GP to return carry if later losses mean it was overpaid.', 4),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-private-capital-q06', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-private-capital', 'DPI (distributed to paid-in) measures:', 'Realized returns relative to capital called', 'Unrealized value', 'The management fee', 'The hurdle rate', 'A', 'DPI is cumulative distributions divided by paid-in capital — realized return.', 5),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-private-capital-q07', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-private-capital', 'TVPI equals DPI plus:', 'RVPI (residual value to paid-in)', 'The hurdle rate', 'The management fee', 'The IRR', 'A', 'Total value to paid-in = distributed (DPI) + residual/unrealized (RVPI).', 6),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-private-capital-q08', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-private-capital', 'Committed capital that has not yet been drawn is called:', 'Uncalled (dry powder) capital', 'Distributed capital', 'Carried interest', 'Net asset value', 'A', 'Uncalled commitments (dry powder) are drawn down over the investment period.', 7),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-private-capital-q09', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-private-capital', 'Private-equity performance is often measured by which return metric?', 'Internal rate of return (IRR)', 'Time-weighted return', 'Arithmetic mean of monthly returns', 'Geometric market return', 'A', 'IRR (money-weighted) captures the timing of PE cash flows.', 8),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-private-capital-q10', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-private-capital', 'Vintage-year benchmarking compares funds that:', 'Were raised in the same year', 'Have the same GP', 'Hold identical assets', 'Charge the same fee', 'A', 'Vintage-year analysis compares funds started in the same year, controlling for market conditions.', 9),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-private-capital-q11', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-private-capital', 'MOIC (multiple of invested capital) measures:', 'Total value relative to invested capital', 'The annualized IRR', 'The hurdle rate', 'The management fee', 'A', 'MOIC is total value divided by capital invested, a simple multiple.', 10),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-private-capital-q12', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-private-capital', 'The general partner (GP) in a private fund is responsible for:', 'Managing the investments', 'Providing most of the capital', 'Auditing the fund', 'Setting tax law', 'A', 'The GP manages the fund; LPs supply most of the capital.', 11),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-digital-assets-q01', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-digital-assets', 'Digital assets record ownership on a:', 'Distributed ledger (blockchain)', 'Central bank spreadsheet', 'Paper certificate', 'Single private server', 'A', 'Blockchain distributed ledgers record digital-asset ownership.', 0),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-digital-assets-q02', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-digital-assets', 'A stablecoin is designed to:', 'Maintain a stable value versus a reference asset', 'Maximize volatility', 'Pay a fixed coupon', 'Track the S&P 500', 'A', 'Stablecoins peg their value to fiat or collateral to reduce volatility.', 1),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-digital-assets-q03', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-digital-assets', 'Tokenization represents real assets as:', 'Blockchain tokens', 'Bank deposits', 'Mutual fund units', 'Physical certificates', 'A', 'Tokenization creates digital tokens representing ownership of real assets.', 2),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-digital-assets-q04', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-digital-assets', 'A potential benefit of tokenization is improved:', 'Liquidity and fractional ownership', 'Guaranteed returns', 'Zero volatility', 'Tax exemption', 'A', 'Tokens can be divided and traded, improving liquidity and enabling fractional ownership.', 3),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-digital-assets-q05', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-digital-assets', 'Most cryptocurrencies are best characterized as:', 'Non-income-producing assets', 'Fixed-coupon bonds', 'Dividend-paying equities', 'Bank deposits', 'A', 'Cryptocurrencies generally produce no income; value derives from adoption and scarcity.', 4),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-digital-assets-q06', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-digital-assets', 'A major risk of holding digital assets directly is:', 'Custody and key-management security', 'Excessive dividends', 'Guaranteed principal', 'Low volatility', 'A', 'Securing private keys/custody is a central operational risk for digital assets.', 5),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-digital-assets-q07', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-digital-assets', 'Investors can gain digital-asset exposure without direct custody via:', 'Futures or exchange-traded products', 'Only physical coins', 'Bank savings accounts', 'Municipal bonds', 'A', 'Futures and ETPs provide exposure while outsourcing custody.', 6),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-digital-assets-q08', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-digital-assets', 'A utility token primarily provides:', 'Access to a product or service', 'An equity claim on profits', 'A bond coupon', 'A currency peg', 'A', 'Utility tokens grant access/usage rights on a platform rather than ownership.', 7),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-digital-assets-q09', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-digital-assets', 'A key uncertainty affecting digital-asset valuation is:', 'Regulatory treatment', 'The dividend policy', 'The coupon schedule', 'The maturity date', 'A', 'Evolving and divergent regulation is a major source of digital-asset risk.', 8),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-digital-assets-q10', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-digital-assets', 'Claims that digital assets diversify a portfolio should be tested because correlations:', 'Can rise in market stress', 'Are always zero', 'Are fixed by regulators', 'Never change', 'A', 'Diversification benefits may weaken as correlations spike during stress.', 9),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-digital-assets-q11', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-digital-assets', 'Compared with traditional assets, cryptocurrencies typically have:', 'Higher volatility', 'Lower volatility', 'Guaranteed income', 'Fixed maturities', 'A', 'Digital assets are markedly more volatile than most traditional assets.', 10),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-digital-assets-q12', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-digital-assets', 'A security token most resembles a:', 'Traditional investment security in tokenized form', 'Loyalty point', 'Gift card', 'Physical commodity', 'A', 'Security tokens represent investment assets (equity/debt) on a blockchain and are regulated as such.', 11),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-natural-resources-q01', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-natural-resources', 'Timberland returns come from timber price changes, land value, and:', 'Biological growth of the trees', 'Dividend payments', 'Coupon income', 'Share buybacks', 'A', 'Trees grow in volume and value over time — a key return driver unique to timberland.', 0),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-natural-resources-q02', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-natural-resources', 'A valuable flexibility of timberland is the ability to:', 'Delay harvest when prices are low', 'Guarantee a coupon', 'Redeem at par', 'Convert to equity', 'A', 'Timber can be stored ''on the stump'', letting owners wait for better prices.', 1),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-natural-resources-q03', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-natural-resources', 'Farmland returns typically include crop income (rents) and:', 'Land appreciation', 'Bond coupons', 'Option premiums', 'Dividend yield', 'A', 'Farmland earns operating/rental income plus appreciation of the land.', 2),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-natural-resources-q04', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-natural-resources', 'Natural resources are often held for their:', 'Inflation protection and low correlation', 'Guaranteed income', 'High liquidity', 'Fixed maturity', 'A', 'Real assets provide inflation hedging and diversification versus financial assets.', 3),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-natural-resources-q05', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-natural-resources', 'Commodities themselves generate return from spot changes and the:', 'Roll yield in futures', 'Dividend yield', 'Coupon', 'Book value', 'A', 'Commodity returns come from spot moves plus the roll yield of futures positions.', 4),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-natural-resources-q06', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-natural-resources', 'Timberland and farmland valuations are usually based on:', 'Appraisals', 'Daily market quotes', 'Bond ratings', 'Option models', 'A', 'Illiquid real assets are valued via periodic appraisals, not continuous prices.', 5),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-natural-resources-q07', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-natural-resources', 'A key risk specific to timberland and farmland is:', 'Weather and environmental factors', 'Coupon reset risk', 'Prepayment risk', 'Call risk', 'A', 'Weather, disease and environmental regulation materially affect real-asset returns.', 6),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-natural-resources-q08', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-natural-resources', 'Permanent crops (e.g., orchards) differ from row crops in that they:', 'Require multi-year investment before yielding', 'Are harvested only once ever', 'Pay coupons', 'Have no price risk', 'A', 'Permanent crops take years to become productive, changing their risk profile.', 7),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-natural-resources-q09', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-natural-resources', 'Compared with financial assets, natural resources tend to be:', 'Illiquid', 'More liquid', 'Risk-free', 'Income-guaranteed', 'A', 'Real assets like timber and farmland are relatively illiquid.', 8),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-natural-resources-q10', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-natural-resources', 'Timberland''s low correlation with equities makes it useful for:', 'Diversification', 'Leverage', 'Tax evasion', 'Currency hedging only', 'A', 'Its distinct return drivers give timberland diversification benefits.', 9),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-natural-resources-q11', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-natural-resources', 'During inflationary periods, real assets tend to:', 'Preserve real value', 'Lose all value', 'Behave like nominal bonds', 'Pay higher coupons', 'A', 'Real assets often keep pace with inflation, preserving purchasing power.', 10),
  ('cfa2-alternative-investments-natural-resources-q12', 'cfa2-alternative-investments-natural-resources', 'The three components of timberland return are biological growth, land value, and:', 'Timber price changes', 'Dividend income', 'Bond coupons', 'Option premiums', 'A', 'Return comes from tree growth, changes in timber prices, and the underlying land value.', 11),
  ('cfa2-ethics-diligence-records-q01', 'cfa2-ethics-diligence-records', 'Standard V(A) Diligence and Reasonable Basis requires members to:', 'Have adequate research supporting their actions', 'Guarantee investment returns', 'Follow the crowd', 'Avoid all documentation', 'A', 'V(A) requires a reasonable and adequate basis for any investment action.', 0),
  ('cfa2-ethics-diligence-records-q02', 'cfa2-ethics-diligence-records', 'When using third-party research, a member should:', 'Verify that it is sound before relying on it', 'Accept it without question', 'Never use it', 'Claim it as original', 'A', 'Members must make reasonable efforts to verify third-party research quality.', 1),
  ('cfa2-ethics-diligence-records-q03', 'cfa2-ethics-diligence-records', 'Standard V(B) Communication requires distinguishing:', 'Fact from opinion', 'Clients from employers', 'Stocks from bonds', 'Fees from commissions', 'A', 'V(B) requires clearly separating fact from opinion in communications.', 2),
  ('cfa2-ethics-diligence-records-q04', 'cfa2-ethics-diligence-records', 'CFA Institute recommends retaining records supporting analysis for at least:', 'Seven years', 'One year', 'Thirty years', 'Ninety days', 'A', 'Standard V(C) recommends a seven-year record-retention period absent other requirements.', 3),
  ('cfa2-ethics-diligence-records-q05', 'cfa2-ethics-diligence-records', 'Standard VII(A) prohibits conduct that compromises the integrity of the:', 'CFA exam and Institute', 'Employer''s website', 'Client''s tax return', 'Stock exchange', 'A', 'VII(A) forbids cheating on or leaking the CFA exam and harming the Institute''s reputation.', 4),
  ('cfa2-ethics-diligence-records-q06', 'cfa2-ethics-diligence-records', 'Under Standard VII(B), ''CFA'' should be used as a:', 'Adjective, not a noun', 'Noun', 'Verb', 'Company name', 'A', 'The CFA marks are adjectives modifying a name; there is no noun form.', 5),
  ('cfa2-ethics-diligence-records-q07', 'cfa2-ethics-diligence-records', 'A candidate who has passed Level II may state:', 'That they passed Levels I and II', 'That they are a partial charterholder', '''CFA (in progress)'' after their name', 'That they hold the CFA designation', 'A', 'Candidates may factually state progress but cannot imply a partial or completed designation.', 6),
  ('cfa2-ethics-diligence-records-q08', 'cfa2-ethics-diligence-records', 'A diligence violation commonly arises from:', 'Relying on research without verification', 'Documenting analysis thoroughly', 'Seeking independent review', 'Disclosing assumptions', 'A', 'Failing to verify external research before acting breaches V(A).', 7),
  ('cfa2-ethics-diligence-records-q09', 'cfa2-ethics-diligence-records', 'When communicating a recommendation, members should disclose the:', 'Basic process and its limitations', 'Exact future price', 'Guaranteed return', 'Personal net worth', 'A', 'V(B) requires disclosing the investment process and its significant limitations.', 8),
  ('cfa2-ethics-diligence-records-q10', 'cfa2-ethics-diligence-records', 'Group research that a member disagrees with can still be used if:', 'It has a reasonable and adequate basis', 'The member signs off blindly', 'It is kept secret', 'It omits assumptions', 'A', 'A member need not agree with every conclusion if the overall research has a sound basis.', 9),
  ('cfa2-ethics-diligence-records-q11', 'cfa2-ethics-diligence-records', 'Record retention under V(C) supports:', 'Reconstructing the basis for recommendations', 'Higher fees', 'Faster trading', 'Tax avoidance', 'A', 'Keeping records lets members document the rationale behind their advice.', 10),
  ('cfa2-ethics-diligence-records-q12', 'cfa2-ethics-diligence-records', 'Exaggerating the meaning of the CFA designation violates Standard:', 'VII(B)', 'III(A)', 'I(C)', 'VI(B)', 'A', 'Misrepresenting the designation''s meaning breaches VII(B) on reference to the CFA program.', 11),
  ('cfa2-ethics-gips-composites-q01', 'cfa2-ethics-gips-composites', 'A GIPS composite must include all:', 'Actual, fee-paying, discretionary portfolios of a strategy', 'Model portfolios only', 'The best-performing accounts', 'Non-discretionary accounts only', 'A', 'Composites include every actual, fee-paying, discretionary portfolio managed to the strategy.', 0),
  ('cfa2-ethics-gips-composites-q02', 'cfa2-ethics-gips-composites', 'GIPS compliance must be claimed on a:', 'Firm-wide basis', 'Single-composite basis', 'Per-client basis', 'Per-security basis', 'A', 'A firm cannot claim partial compliance; GIPS applies firm-wide.', 1),
  ('cfa2-ethics-gips-composites-q03', 'cfa2-ethics-gips-composites', 'Excluding a poorly performing portfolio from its composite is:', 'Prohibited (cherry-picking)', 'Encouraged', 'Required', 'Optional', 'A', 'All eligible portfolios must be included; selectively removing them is cherry-picking.', 2),
  ('cfa2-ethics-gips-composites-q04', 'cfa2-ethics-gips-composites', 'GIPS requires presenting a minimum initial track record of:', 'Five years, building to ten', 'One year only', 'Twenty years', 'Ninety days', 'A', 'Firms must show at least 5 years, then build to a 10-year record.', 3),
  ('cfa2-ethics-gips-composites-q05', 'cfa2-ethics-gips-composites', 'GIPS returns are calculated on a:', 'Time-weighted basis', 'Money-weighted basis only', 'Simple average basis', 'Cash basis', 'A', 'GIPS generally requires time-weighted returns to remove the effect of client cash flows.', 4),
  ('cfa2-ethics-gips-composites-q06', 'cfa2-ethics-gips-composites', 'A terminated portfolio should remain in the composite through:', 'Its last full measurement period', 'Never — removed immediately', 'The next ten years', 'Only the first month', 'A', 'Terminated portfolios stay in the composite up to the last full period they were managed.', 5),
  ('cfa2-ethics-gips-composites-q07', 'cfa2-ethics-gips-composites', 'GIPS verification is performed at the level of the:', 'Firm', 'Single composite', 'Individual security', 'Client household', 'A', 'Verification tests firm-wide compliance, not a single composite.', 6),
  ('cfa2-ethics-gips-composites-q08', 'cfa2-ethics-gips-composites', 'Composite dispersion measures:', 'The spread of returns across portfolios in the composite', 'The benchmark''s volatility', 'The management fee', 'The number of clients', 'A', 'Dispersion shows how widely individual portfolio returns vary within the composite.', 7),
  ('cfa2-ethics-gips-composites-q09', 'cfa2-ethics-gips-composites', 'New portfolios are added to a composite:', 'On a timely, consistent basis per the firm''s policy', 'Only if they outperform', 'After ten years', 'Never', 'A', 'Firms add new discretionary portfolios on a consistent, timely basis.', 8),
  ('cfa2-ethics-gips-composites-q10', 'cfa2-ethics-gips-composites', 'The main purpose of GIPS is to ensure performance is:', 'Fairly represented and comparable', 'Always positive', 'Higher than peers', 'Tax-free', 'A', 'GIPS promotes fair representation and full disclosure so results are comparable.', 9),
  ('cfa2-ethics-gips-composites-q11', 'cfa2-ethics-gips-composites', 'A composite is defined by its:', 'Investment strategy or mandate', 'Client names', 'Custodian', 'Fee schedule', 'A', 'Composites group portfolios with a similar investment objective or strategy.', 10),
  ('cfa2-ethics-gips-composites-q12', 'cfa2-ethics-gips-composites', 'Claiming compliance while only some composites comply is:', 'Not permitted', 'Permitted with disclosure', 'Required', 'Standard practice', 'A', 'Partial compliance is not allowed; the entire firm must comply to claim GIPS.', 11),
  ('cfa2-ethics-ethics-cases-q01', 'cfa2-ethics-ethics-cases', 'Trading on material nonpublic information violates Standard:', 'II(A) Material Nonpublic Information', 'III(B) Fair Dealing', 'V(A) Diligence', 'VII(B)', 'A', 'Standard II(A) prohibits acting on material nonpublic information.', 0),
  ('cfa2-ethics-ethics-cases-q02', 'cfa2-ethics-ethics-cases', 'The mosaic theory permits an analyst to:', 'Combine public and non-material nonpublic information', 'Trade on insider tips', 'Front-run clients', 'Guarantee returns', 'A', 'Conclusions from public plus non-material nonpublic pieces are permissible (mosaic theory).', 1),
  ('cfa2-ethics-ethics-cases-q03', 'cfa2-ethics-ethics-cases', 'When leaving an employer, a departing member may:', 'Use skills and general knowledge', 'Take client lists in advance', 'Solicit clients before leaving', 'Copy the firm''s records', 'A', 'Standard IV(A) permits using one''s skills/experience but not misappropriating employer property.', 2),
  ('cfa2-ethics-ethics-cases-q04', 'cfa2-ethics-ethics-cases', 'Recommending an unsuitable, high-risk product to a conservative client violates:', 'Standard III(C) Suitability', 'Standard I(A)', 'Standard VII(A)', 'Standard II(B)', 'A', 'III(C) requires recommendations to match the client''s objectives and constraints.', 3),
  ('cfa2-ethics-ethics-cases-q05', 'cfa2-ethics-ethics-cases', 'Under priority of transactions (VI-B), whose trades come first?', 'Clients and employer before personal accounts', 'The member''s own account', 'Family accounts', 'Whoever pays most', 'A', 'Client and employer transactions take priority over a member''s personal trades.', 4),
  ('cfa2-ethics-ethics-cases-q06', 'cfa2-ethics-ethics-cases', 'To preserve independence (I-B), a member should:', 'Refuse gifts or pressure that could bias objectivity', 'Accept lavish gifts freely', 'Follow issuer requests', 'Suppress negative research', 'A', 'I(B) requires maintaining independence and objectivity, avoiding compromising benefits.', 5),
  ('cfa2-ethics-ethics-cases-q07', 'cfa2-ethics-ethics-cases', 'A modest gift from a client for good past performance should be:', 'Disclosed to the employer', 'Kept secret', 'Always refused', 'Reported to regulators', 'A', 'Client gifts for prior service are allowed but must be disclosed to the employer.', 6),
  ('cfa2-ethics-ethics-cases-q08', 'cfa2-ethics-ethics-cases', 'A firewall (information barrier) is used to:', 'Prevent the spread of material nonpublic information', 'Boost trading speed', 'Increase fees', 'Avoid taxes', 'A', 'Firewalls isolate departments to stop MNPI from reaching those who could misuse it.', 7),
  ('cfa2-ethics-ethics-cases-q09', 'cfa2-ethics-ethics-cases', 'Fair dealing (III-B) requires that recommendations be disseminated:', 'To all clients fairly', 'To the largest client first', 'Only to friends', 'Randomly', 'A', 'III(B) requires treating all clients fairly when disseminating recommendations and taking action.', 8),
  ('cfa2-ethics-ethics-cases-q10', 'cfa2-ethics-ethics-cases', 'Misrepresenting performance to win a mandate violates Standard:', 'I(C) Misrepresentation', 'V(C)', 'VI(A)', 'IV(B)', 'A', 'Standard I(C) prohibits misrepresentation, including false performance claims.', 9),
  ('cfa2-ethics-ethics-cases-q11', 'cfa2-ethics-ethics-cases', 'The best default action when facing an ethical gray area is to:', 'Disclose, document, and put clients first', 'Do nothing', 'Follow the most profitable path', 'Ask no one', 'A', 'Disclosure, documentation and client priority resolve most ethical uncertainties.', 10),
  ('cfa2-ethics-ethics-cases-q12', 'cfa2-ethics-ethics-cases', 'Loyalty to clients (III-A) requires acting for the client''s:', 'Benefit, placing their interests first', 'Employer''s benefit only', 'Member''s own benefit', 'Regulator''s benefit', 'A', 'III(A) requires acting with reasonable care and loyalty in the client''s best interest.', 11),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-dividends-repurchases-q01', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-dividends-repurchases', 'In a perfect market, a share repurchase and an equal cash dividend leave shareholder wealth:', 'Unchanged', 'Higher for the dividend', 'Higher for the buyback', 'Zero', 'A', 'Absent taxes and signaling, the two methods are wealth-equivalent.', 0),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-dividends-repurchases-q02', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-dividends-repurchases', 'A share buyback increases EPS when the earnings yield is:', 'Greater than the after-tax cost of funds', 'Less than the cost of funds', 'Zero', 'Equal to the dividend yield', 'A', 'Buying back shares raises EPS if the earnings yield exceeds the after-tax financing cost.', 1),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-dividends-repurchases-q03', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-dividends-repurchases', 'Modigliani–Miller dividend irrelevance assumes:', 'Perfect markets with no taxes or costs', 'High transaction costs', 'Asymmetric information', 'Progressive dividend taxes', 'A', 'MM show payout policy doesn''t affect value in frictionless, perfect markets.', 2),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-dividends-repurchases-q04', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-dividends-repurchases', 'The ''bird-in-the-hand'' argument claims investors prefer:', 'Certain dividends over uncertain capital gains', 'Buybacks over dividends', 'No payout', 'Debt over equity', 'A', 'This (disputed) view holds that investors value certain current dividends more highly.', 3),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-dividends-repurchases-q05', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-dividends-repurchases', 'A dividend increase can serve as a signal of:', 'Management''s confidence in future earnings', 'Impending bankruptcy', 'A tax penalty', 'Falling demand', 'A', 'Raising dividends often signals management''s confidence in sustainable earnings.', 4),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-dividends-repurchases-q06', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-dividends-repurchases', 'A dividend cut is typically interpreted as a:', 'Strong negative signal', 'Positive signal', 'Neutral event', 'Tax benefit', 'A', 'Markets usually read dividend cuts as a sign of financial stress.', 5),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-dividends-repurchases-q07', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-dividends-repurchases', 'Compared with dividends, buybacks offer greater:', 'Flexibility', 'Certainty of income', 'Tax on recipients', 'Regulatory burden', 'A', 'Buybacks are more flexible since they carry no ongoing commitment.', 6),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-dividends-repurchases-q08', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-dividends-repurchases', 'The tax-preference argument favors buybacks when capital-gains tax is:', 'Lower than dividend tax', 'Higher than dividend tax', 'Equal to dividend tax', 'Zero for everyone', 'A', 'If capital gains are taxed more favorably, investors may prefer repurchases.', 7),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-dividends-repurchases-q09', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-dividends-repurchases', 'The dividend coverage ratio measures:', 'Earnings relative to dividends paid', 'Debt relative to equity', 'Sales relative to assets', 'Cash relative to inventory', 'A', 'Coverage = earnings / dividends, gauging dividend safety.', 8),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-dividends-repurchases-q10', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-dividends-repurchases', 'FCFE coverage of dividends assesses whether:', 'Free cash flow to equity can fund the payout', 'Sales exceed costs', 'The firm can repay all debt', 'Inventory is sufficient', 'A', 'Comparing dividends to FCFE tests payout sustainability from cash flow.', 9),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-dividends-repurchases-q11', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-dividends-repurchases', 'A buyback financed by debt at a cost above the earnings yield will:', 'Reduce EPS', 'Raise EPS', 'Leave EPS unchanged', 'Eliminate taxes', 'A', 'If financing cost exceeds the earnings yield, the buyback is EPS-dilutive.', 10),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-dividends-repurchases-q12', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-dividends-repurchases', 'Both dividends and buybacks are forms of:', 'Returning cash to shareholders', 'Raising new capital', 'Issuing debt', 'Acquiring competitors', 'A', 'Dividends and repurchases are the two main ways firms distribute cash to shareholders.', 11),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring-q01', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring', 'A spin-off distributes shares of a subsidiary to:', 'Existing shareholders', 'New outside investors only', 'Creditors', 'The government', 'A', 'In a spin-off, a unit''s shares are distributed pro rata to current shareholders.', 0),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring-q02', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring', 'A carve-out involves:', 'A partial IPO of a subsidiary', 'Full liquidation', 'A debt-for-equity swap', 'A stock split', 'A', 'An equity carve-out sells a minority stake in a subsidiary to the public.', 1),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring-q03', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring', 'Restructuring that reduces the scope of the firm is called:', 'Contraction', 'Expansion', 'Consolidation', 'Integration', 'A', 'Divestitures, spin-offs and liquidations are forms of corporate contraction.', 2),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring-q04', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring', 'A common motive for a spin-off is to:', 'Improve management focus and unlock value', 'Increase leverage', 'Avoid all taxes', 'Reduce share count', 'A', 'Spin-offs sharpen focus and can eliminate a conglomerate discount.', 3),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring-q05', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring', 'A leveraged buyout (LBO) is a form of:', 'Financial restructuring', 'Organic growth', 'Dividend policy', 'Working-capital management', 'A', 'An LBO restructures the capital base using substantial debt to acquire the firm.', 4),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring-q06', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring', 'The ''conglomerate discount'' refers to diversified firms trading:', 'Below the sum of their parts', 'Above intrinsic value', 'At exactly fair value', 'At par', 'A', 'Diversified conglomerates often trade below the sum of their standalone business values.', 5),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring-q07', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring', 'A divestiture generates value by:', 'Selling a unit and raising cash or focus', 'Acquiring competitors', 'Issuing more shares', 'Increasing inventory', 'A', 'Divestitures shed non-core units, raising cash and improving focus.', 6),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring-q08', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring', 'Affected business units in a restructuring are valued using:', 'Standard tools like DCF and comparables', 'Only book value', 'The dividend yield', 'Coupon rates', 'A', 'Analysts value the units with DCF and market multiples, as with any business.', 7),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring-q09', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring', 'A split-off lets shareholders:', 'Exchange parent shares for subsidiary shares', 'Receive a cash dividend', 'Buy new bonds', 'Vote out the board', 'A', 'In a split-off, holders choose to swap parent-company shares for subsidiary shares.', 8),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring-q10', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring', 'A debt-for-equity swap is an example of:', 'Financial restructuring', 'A spin-off', 'An acquisition', 'A carve-out', 'A', 'Swapping debt for equity alters the capital structure — a financial restructuring.', 9),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring-q11', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring', 'Restructuring through acquisitions and mergers is a form of:', 'Expansion', 'Contraction', 'Liquidation', 'Recapitalization', 'A', 'Mergers, acquisitions and joint ventures expand the firm''s scope.', 10),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring-q12', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-corporate-restructuring', 'Spin-offs can create value primarily by improving:', 'Management focus and transparency', 'Leverage ratios', 'Tax rates', 'Coupon payments', 'A', 'Separating a unit improves focus and transparency, often unlocking value.', 11),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-esg-analysis-q01', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-esg-analysis', 'The ''G'' in ESG stands for:', 'Governance', 'Growth', 'Guidance', 'Gearing', 'A', 'ESG = Environmental, Social and Governance factors.', 0),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-esg-analysis-q02', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-esg-analysis', 'Excluding tobacco or weapons stocks is an example of:', 'Negative screening', 'Positive screening', 'Impact investing', 'Thematic investing', 'A', 'Negative/exclusionary screening removes specific industries or issuers.', 1),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-esg-analysis-q03', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-esg-analysis', 'Investing to generate measurable social or environmental benefit alongside return is:', 'Impact investing', 'Index investing', 'Negative screening', 'Momentum investing', 'A', 'Impact investing seeks a measurable positive impact plus a financial return.', 2),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-esg-analysis-q04', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-esg-analysis', 'ESG analysis focuses on issues that are:', 'Financially material to the business', 'Purely reputational', 'Never quantifiable', 'Identical across industries', 'A', 'Effective ESG integration targets financially material, industry-specific issues.', 3),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-esg-analysis-q05', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-esg-analysis', '''Stranded asset'' risk is an example of which ESG pillar''s effect?', 'Environmental', 'Social', 'Governance', 'Liquidity', 'A', 'Carbon-heavy assets becoming uneconomic is an environmental (climate) risk.', 4),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-esg-analysis-q06', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-esg-analysis', 'Board independence and executive pay fall under which pillar?', 'Governance', 'Environmental', 'Social', 'Operational', 'A', 'Board structure, shareholder rights and pay are governance factors.', 5),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-esg-analysis-q07', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-esg-analysis', 'Labour practices and product safety are examples of:', 'Social factors', 'Environmental factors', 'Governance factors', 'Market factors', 'A', 'Workforce and product/community issues are social factors.', 6),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-esg-analysis-q08', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-esg-analysis', 'A challenge in ESG analysis is that third-party ratings often:', 'Diverge from one another', 'Agree perfectly', 'Are legally binding', 'Eliminate all risk', 'A', 'ESG ratings from different providers frequently disagree due to differing methodologies.', 7),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-esg-analysis-q09', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-esg-analysis', 'ESG risks can affect a firm''s:', 'Cash flows and cost of capital', 'Ticker symbol only', 'Par value only', 'CUSIP number', 'A', 'Material ESG issues can change expected cash flows and the discount rate.', 8),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-esg-analysis-q10', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-esg-analysis', 'Selecting the best ESG performers within each sector is:', 'Positive/best-in-class screening', 'Negative screening', 'Divestment', 'Short selling', 'A', 'Best-in-class screening favors the strongest ESG performers in each industry.', 9),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-esg-analysis-q11', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-esg-analysis', 'Poor governance can raise the risk of:', 'Litigation and value destruction', 'Higher dividends', 'Lower volatility', 'Guaranteed returns', 'A', 'Weak governance is associated with litigation, fraud and value loss.', 10),
  ('cfa2-corporate-issuers-esg-analysis-q12', 'cfa2-corporate-issuers-esg-analysis', 'A data challenge in ESG investing is:', 'Inconsistent corporate disclosure', 'Too much standardization', 'Perfect comparability', 'Real-time audited metrics', 'A', 'Inconsistent, voluntary disclosure makes ESG data hard to compare.', 11),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-active-management-q01', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-active-management', 'Active return is defined as:', 'Portfolio return minus benchmark return', 'Portfolio return minus risk-free rate', 'Benchmark return minus inflation', 'Total return', 'A', 'Active return is the excess of the portfolio over its benchmark.', 0),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-active-management-q02', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-active-management', 'The information ratio equals active return divided by:', 'Active risk (tracking error)', 'Total risk', 'Beta', 'The risk-free rate', 'A', 'IR = active return / active risk, measuring skill per unit of active risk.', 1),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-active-management-q03', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-active-management', 'In the fundamental law, the information coefficient (IC) measures:', 'Forecasting skill', 'Portfolio size', 'Trading costs', 'The benchmark return', 'A', 'IC is the correlation between forecasts and outcomes — the manager''s skill.', 2),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-active-management-q04', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-active-management', 'Breadth (BR) in the fundamental law refers to the:', 'Number of independent decisions per year', 'Portfolio''s total value', 'Benchmark''s volatility', 'Management fee', 'A', 'Breadth is the number of independent active bets taken per period.', 3),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-active-management-q05', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-active-management', 'The transfer coefficient (TC) reflects how fully signals reach the portfolio given:', 'Constraints', 'The risk-free rate', 'Dividends', 'Inflation', 'A', 'TC measures the degree to which constraints reduce implementation of the manager''s views.', 4),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-active-management-q06', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-active-management', 'According to the fundamental law, doubling breadth raises the information ratio by a factor of:', '√2', '2', '4', '1', 'A', 'Because IR scales with √BR, doubling breadth multiplies IR by √2.', 5),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-active-management-q07', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-active-management', 'Active risk is also known as:', 'Tracking error', 'Total volatility', 'Systematic risk', 'Default risk', 'A', 'Active risk is the standard deviation of active returns (tracking error).', 6),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-active-management-q08', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-active-management', 'A higher information ratio indicates:', 'More skill per unit of active risk', 'Higher total return always', 'Lower fees', 'A larger portfolio', 'A', 'The IR rewards managers who generate more active return for the active risk taken.', 7),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-active-management-q09', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-active-management', 'The Sharpe ratio of an optimal active portfolio rises with the benchmark''s Sharpe² plus:', 'The information ratio squared', 'The beta', 'The dividend yield', 'The tracking error alone', 'A', 'SR² (optimal) = SR_benchmark² + IR², so higher IR raises the achievable Sharpe ratio.', 8),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-active-management-q10', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-active-management', 'A perfect (unconstrained) transfer coefficient equals:', '1', '0', '√2', 'Infinity', 'A', 'TC = 1 means the manager''s forecasts are fully reflected without constraint losses.', 9),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-active-management-q11', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-active-management', 'Alpha in active management refers to:', 'Risk-adjusted excess return from skill', 'The benchmark return', 'The risk-free rate', 'The management fee', 'A', 'Alpha is the excess return attributable to manager skill, not market exposure.', 10),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-active-management-q12', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-active-management', 'The fundamental law implies a manager can improve results by increasing skill or:', 'Breadth of independent bets', 'Portfolio turnover only', 'Management fees', 'Benchmark volatility', 'A', 'Expected active return grows with both the information coefficient and breadth.', 11),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-algorithmic-trading-q01', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-algorithmic-trading', 'A VWAP algorithm aims to execute at a price close to the:', 'Volume-weighted average price', 'Opening price', '52-week high', 'Closing auction only', 'A', 'VWAP algorithms slice orders to match the volume-weighted average price.', 0),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-algorithmic-trading-q02', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-algorithmic-trading', 'Implementation-shortfall algorithms balance market impact against:', 'The risk of adverse price drift while waiting', 'Dividend timing', 'Coupon reinvestment', 'Tax lots', 'A', 'They trade off impact cost against the opportunity cost of delayed execution.', 1),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-algorithmic-trading-q03', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-algorithmic-trading', 'Dark pools are venues that:', 'Hide order size to reduce market impact', 'Publish all orders instantly', 'Only trade bonds', 'Are run by regulators', 'A', 'Dark pools let large orders trade with less pre-trade transparency, limiting impact.', 2),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-algorithmic-trading-q04', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-algorithmic-trading', 'High-frequency trading relies primarily on:', 'Very low latency', 'Long holding periods', 'Fundamental analysis', 'Annual rebalancing', 'A', 'HFT strategies depend on extremely fast, low-latency execution.', 3),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-algorithmic-trading-q05', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-algorithmic-trading', 'A benefit that HFT market makers can provide is:', 'Tighter bid-ask spreads and liquidity', 'Guaranteed profits for clients', 'Elimination of volatility', 'Zero-risk arbitrage for all', 'A', 'HFT market making can narrow spreads and add liquidity in normal conditions.', 4),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-algorithmic-trading-q06', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-algorithmic-trading', 'A risk associated with HFT is:', 'Flash crashes and fleeting liquidity', 'Slower markets', 'Higher dividends', 'Reduced technology use', 'A', 'HFT can contribute to flash crashes and liquidity that disappears under stress.', 5),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-algorithmic-trading-q07', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-algorithmic-trading', 'A TWAP algorithm spreads an order evenly over:', 'Time', 'Sectors', 'Currencies', 'Counterparties', 'A', 'Time-weighted average price algorithms distribute trades uniformly across time.', 6),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-algorithmic-trading-q08', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-algorithmic-trading', 'Spoofing — placing orders with intent to cancel — is:', 'A prohibited manipulative practice', 'A legal hedging technique', 'Required by regulators', 'A type of dividend', 'A', 'Spoofing is a manipulative practice targeted by market-integrity rules.', 7),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-algorithmic-trading-q09', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-algorithmic-trading', 'Liquidity-seeking algorithms are designed to:', 'Find hidden liquidity across venues', 'Trade only at the open', 'Avoid all execution', 'Maximize market impact', 'A', 'These algorithms hunt for available liquidity, including in dark pools.', 8),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-algorithmic-trading-q10', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-algorithmic-trading', 'Circuit breakers are used to:', 'Halt trading during extreme moves', 'Increase leverage', 'Speed up HFT', 'Pay dividends', 'A', 'Circuit breakers pause trading to curb disorderly, extreme price movements.', 9),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-algorithmic-trading-q11', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-algorithmic-trading', 'A primary goal of execution algorithms is to minimize:', 'Trading costs and market impact', 'Portfolio return', 'Diversification', 'Disclosure', 'A', 'Execution algorithms aim to reduce implicit and explicit trading costs.', 10),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-algorithmic-trading-q12', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-algorithmic-trading', 'Technology/system failures in algorithmic trading create:', 'Operational risk', 'Zero risk', 'Guaranteed alpha', 'Lower volatility', 'A', 'Reliance on automated systems introduces significant operational risk.', 11),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-backtesting-q01', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-backtesting', 'Backtesting applies a strategy to:', 'Historical data to estimate past performance', 'Future data', 'Random noise only', 'A single stock forever', 'A', 'Backtesting simulates how a strategy would have performed on historical data.', 0),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-backtesting-q02', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-backtesting', 'Survivorship bias arises when a backtest:', 'Excludes firms that failed or delisted', 'Includes too many firms', 'Uses future prices', 'Ignores returns', 'A', 'Omitting dead/delisted firms overstates historical performance.', 1),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-backtesting-q03', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-backtesting', 'Look-ahead bias occurs when a backtest uses:', 'Data not available at the decision time', 'Only past data', 'Random data', 'No data', 'A', 'Using information that wasn''t yet known biases results upward.', 2),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-backtesting-q04', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-backtesting', 'Testing many strategy variants and reporting the best is called:', 'Data snooping (overfitting)', 'Cross-validation', 'Diversification', 'Rebalancing', 'A', 'Data snooping inflates apparent performance by mining for the best in-sample result.', 3),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-backtesting-q05', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-backtesting', 'A limitation of a single historical backtest is that it:', 'Represents just one realized path', 'Uses too many scenarios', 'Eliminates all risk', 'Guarantees the future', 'A', 'One historical path understates the range of possible outcomes and tail risk.', 4),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-backtesting-q06', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-backtesting', 'Monte Carlo simulation generates:', 'Many random paths to reveal the outcome distribution', 'A single deterministic path', 'Only the mean', 'Historical data only', 'A', 'Monte Carlo draws many random scenarios to map the distribution of results.', 5),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-backtesting-q07', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-backtesting', 'Scenario analysis differs from Monte Carlo by:', 'Stressing specific, defined events', 'Using infinite random draws', 'Ignoring inputs', 'Requiring no assumptions', 'A', 'Scenario analysis evaluates particular hypothetical situations rather than random sampling.', 6),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-backtesting-q08', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-backtesting', 'Backtest results should be reported:', 'Net of realistic transaction costs', 'Gross, ignoring costs', 'Before any data cleaning', 'Only in-sample', 'A', 'Realistic cost assumptions are essential for a credible backtest.', 7),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-backtesting-q09', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-backtesting', 'Monte Carlo simulation is especially useful for capturing:', 'Non-normal, fat-tailed risk', 'Only average returns', 'Zero-variance outcomes', 'Historical survivorship', 'A', 'By sampling many paths, Monte Carlo reveals tail and non-normal risks.', 8),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-backtesting-q10', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-backtesting', 'A robust backtest rolls forward by:', 'Forming and rebalancing portfolios period by period', 'Using the final weights throughout', 'Trading once at the end', 'Ignoring rebalancing', 'A', 'A proper backtest reconstructs the portfolio and rebalances through time.', 9),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-backtesting-q11', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-backtesting', 'Historical backtests may mislead because past relationships:', 'May not persist in the future', 'Always repeat exactly', 'Are irrelevant', 'Remove all uncertainty', 'A', 'Structural change means historical patterns may not hold going forward.', 10),
  ('cfa2-portfolio-management-backtesting-q12', 'cfa2-portfolio-management-backtesting', 'Reporting risk-adjusted backtest performance often uses the:', 'Sharpe or information ratio', 'Coupon rate', 'Combined ratio', 'Dividend yield', 'A', 'Sharpe and information ratios summarize return per unit of risk in backtests.', 11),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-biases-portfolio-q01', 'cfa3-behavioral-biases-portfolio', 'The recommended treatment of cognitive errors versus emotional biases is to:', 'Adapt to cognitive, moderate emotional', 'Moderate cognitive, adapt to emotional', 'Ignore both', 'Eliminate both completely', 'B', 'Cognitive errors can usually be moderated with education/data, while harder-to-change emotional biases are accommodated (adapted to).', 0),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-biases-portfolio-q02', 'cfa3-behavioral-biases-portfolio', 'Holding too much of an inherited stock because selling ''feels wrong'' is an example of:', 'Anchoring (cognitive)', 'Endowment bias (emotional)', 'Confirmation bias', 'Availability bias', 'B', 'Endowment bias is an emotional bias where owners over-value assets they hold, causing under-diversification.', 1),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-biases-portfolio-q03', 'cfa3-behavioral-biases-portfolio', 'Overconfidence most commonly leads investors to:', 'Hold excess cash', 'Trade excessively and under-diversify', 'Avoid all equities', 'Over-rebalance', 'B', 'Overconfident investors overrate their information/skill, leading to excessive trading and concentrated portfolios.', 2),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-biases-portfolio-q04', 'cfa3-behavioral-biases-portfolio', 'A behaviorally modified asset allocation generally allows larger deviations from the rational optimum when the client has:', 'Low ability to take risk and mostly cognitive biases', 'High standard-of-living risk and emotional biases the advisor adapts to', 'No biases at all', 'Only confirmation bias', 'B', 'Greater allowable deviations accompany emotional biases (which are adapted to) when standard-of-living risk is high enough to tolerate them.', 3),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-biases-portfolio-q05', 'cfa3-behavioral-biases-portfolio', 'The disposition effect—selling winners early and holding losers—arises mainly from:', 'Loss aversion', 'Anchoring', 'Framing', 'Hindsight bias', 'A', 'Loss aversion makes realizing losses painful, so investors hold losers and sell winners (the disposition effect).', 4),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-biases-portfolio-q06', 'cfa3-behavioral-biases-portfolio', 'Mental accounting can be used constructively through:', 'Goals-based investing with separate risk buckets', 'Ignoring client goals', 'A single combined portfolio only', 'Maximizing turnover', 'A', 'Goals-based investing leverages the natural tendency to mentally segregate money, assigning each goal its own sub-portfolio and risk level.', 5),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-biases-portfolio-q07', 'cfa3-behavioral-biases-portfolio', 'Representativeness bias is best described as:', 'Over-relying on an initial number', 'Judging probability by similarity to a stereotype/sample', 'Seeking confirming evidence', 'Fear of regret', 'B', 'Representativeness classifies new information based on resemblance to past patterns or stereotypes, often ignoring base rates.', 6),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-biases-portfolio-q08', 'cfa3-behavioral-biases-portfolio', 'Status quo and endowment biases tend to cause portfolios to be:', 'Over-traded', 'Inert and under-diversified', 'Perfectly efficient', 'Fully hedged', 'B', 'These biases create inertia—investors leave holdings unchanged—leading to under-diversification.', 7),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-biases-portfolio-q09', 'cfa3-behavioral-biases-portfolio', 'Confirmation bias is categorized as a:', 'Emotional bias', 'Cognitive error', 'Market anomaly', 'Risk measure', 'B', 'Confirmation bias is a cognitive (belief-perseverance) error: seeking information that supports existing views.', 8),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-biases-portfolio-q10', 'cfa3-behavioral-biases-portfolio', 'According to prospect theory, investors tend to be risk-seeking in the domain of:', 'Gains', 'Losses', 'Neither', 'Both equally', 'B', 'Prospect theory finds risk-averse behavior over gains but risk-seeking behavior over losses, relative to a reference point.', 9),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-biases-portfolio-q11', 'cfa3-behavioral-biases-portfolio', 'Anchoring and adjustment is a bias in which investors:', 'Fixate on an initial value and adjust insufficiently', 'Follow the crowd', 'Overestimate their own skill', 'Avoid realizing losses', 'A', 'Anchoring fixes estimates near an initial reference point, with inadequate adjustment as new information arrives.', 10),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-biases-portfolio-q12', 'cfa3-behavioral-biases-portfolio', 'Home bias—overweighting domestic securities—is most directly a form of:', 'Familiarity/availability bias', 'Loss aversion', 'Self-control bias', 'Hindsight bias', 'A', 'Home bias reflects familiarity (a relative of availability bias): investors favor what they know, reducing diversification.', 11),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-forecasting-q01', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-forecasting', 'The Grinold-Kroner model decomposes expected equity return into income, nominal earnings growth, and:', 'Repricing (change in the P/E ratio)', 'The risk-free rate only', 'Tracking error', 'Beta', 'A', 'Grinold-Kroner ≈ dividend yield − Δshares + (inflation + real growth) + Δ(P/E), where the last term is repricing.', 0),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-forecasting-q02', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-forecasting', 'Using a sample period dominated by a bull market to forecast long-run returns is an example of:', 'Time-period bias', 'Survivorship bias', 'Anchoring', 'The Taylor rule', 'A', 'Time-period bias arises when a forecast over-reflects the particular (e.g., bull-market) window used to estimate it.', 1),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-forecasting-q03', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-forecasting', 'The building-block approach to a bond''s expected return sums the real risk-free rate, expected inflation, and:', 'Term, credit, and liquidity premia', 'The equity risk premium', 'The P/E change', 'Beta times the market premium', 'A', 'Building-block fixed-income returns add a term (maturity) premium, credit premium, and liquidity premium to the real rate plus inflation.', 2),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-forecasting-q04', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-forecasting', 'The Singer-Terhaar approach estimates a risk premium by blending fully integrated and fully segmented markets based on the degree of:', 'Market integration', 'Inflation', 'Leverage', 'Liquidity only', 'A', 'Singer-Terhaar weights the integrated and segmented risk premia by how integrated the market is with global markets.', 3),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-forecasting-q05', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-forecasting', 'The Taylor rule helps forecast the:', 'Equity risk premium', 'Neutral/policy short-term interest rate', 'Credit spread', 'Dividend yield', 'B', 'The Taylor rule estimates the appropriate central-bank policy rate from the neutral rate, inflation gap, and output gap.', 4),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-forecasting-q06', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-forecasting', 'Finding a spurious ''predictor'' by searching many variables until one fits is:', 'Data mining (data snooping)', 'Building-block estimation', 'Risk budgeting', 'Immunization', 'A', 'Data mining/snooping produces relationships that fit past data by chance and typically fail out of sample.', 5),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-forecasting-q07', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-forecasting', 'Using a covariance matrix estimated from historical data assumes the data are:', 'Non-stationary', 'Reasonably stationary over the forecast horizon', 'Risk-free', 'Lognormal only', 'B', 'Historical estimates are valid only if the underlying statistical relationships are roughly stationary; regime shifts undermine them.', 6),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-forecasting-q08', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-forecasting', 'During the ''initial recovery'' phase of the business cycle, which is typically expected?', 'Falling stock prices and rising short rates', 'Rising stock prices with still-accommodative monetary policy', 'Inverted yield curve', 'Deep recession', 'B', 'Early recovery usually features improving confidence, rising equities, and still-low policy rates supporting growth.', 7),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-forecasting-q09', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-forecasting', 'Excluding failed funds from a historical return sample causes:', 'Survivorship bias (overstated returns)', 'Anchoring', 'Understated returns', 'No bias', 'A', 'Survivorship bias inflates measured returns because only the surviving (better-performing) funds remain in the sample.', 8),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-forecasting-q10', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-forecasting', 'In the Grinold-Kroner model, a net share buyback (negative ΔS) will:', 'Decrease expected return', 'Increase expected return', 'Have no effect', 'Eliminate the dividend yield', 'B', 'Net buybacks reduce share count (negative ΔS); since the model subtracts ΔS, buybacks raise the expected per-share return.', 9),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-forecasting-q11', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-forecasting', 'Leading economic indicators are useful in CME because they:', 'Confirm past activity', 'Tend to change ahead of the overall economy', 'Lag the cycle', 'Measure only inflation', 'B', 'Leading indicators (e.g., new orders, building permits) shift before the broader economy, aiding cyclical forecasting.', 10),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-forecasting-q12', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-forecasting', 'A key limitation of relying on past correlations for asset allocation is that correlations:', 'Are always stable', 'Can rise toward 1 in crises, reducing diversification when it''s needed most', 'Are irrelevant to risk', 'Equal beta', 'B', 'Correlations often spike during market stress, undermining the diversification benefits assumed from calmer historical periods.', 11),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-strategic-q01', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-strategic', 'Strategic asset allocation is primarily derived from the:', 'Latest market momentum', 'Investor''s objectives and constraints in the IPS', 'Most recent quarter''s returns', 'Broker recommendations', 'B', 'SAA translates the IPS''s return objectives, risk tolerance, and constraints into long-run policy weights.', 0),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-strategic-q02', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-strategic', 'Mean-variance optimization is often criticized as an ''error maximizer'' because it is highly sensitive to:', 'The number of assets only', 'Estimation error in expected returns', 'The risk-free rate alone', 'Tax rates', 'B', 'MVO concentrates weights in assets with the highest (often mis-estimated) expected returns, magnifying input errors.', 1),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-strategic-q03', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-strategic', 'Which approach is most appropriate for a defined-benefit pension that must fund future obligations?', 'Asset-only optimization', 'Liability-relative asset allocation', 'Pure momentum', 'Single-stock concentration', 'B', 'Liability-relative (surplus) allocation manages assets against the plan''s liabilities, the natural fit for a DB pension.', 2),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-strategic-q04', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-strategic', 'Risk parity allocates so that each asset contributes:', 'Equal capital', 'Equal risk to the portfolio', 'Equal expected return', 'Zero risk', 'B', 'Risk parity sizes positions so each asset contributes an equal share of total portfolio risk, rather than equal capital.', 3),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-strategic-q05', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-strategic', 'The Black-Litterman model improves MVO by:', 'Ignoring market equilibrium', 'Blending market-implied equilibrium returns with the investor''s views', 'Removing all constraints', 'Using only historical returns', 'B', 'Black-Litterman starts from reverse-optimized equilibrium returns and tilts them toward the investor''s views, yielding stabler weights.', 4),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-strategic-q06', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-strategic', 'A percentage-range (corridor) rebalancing policy should generally use WIDER corridors for assets that are:', 'Highly liquid and low-cost to trade', 'Illiquid or costly to trade', 'Perfectly correlated with the portfolio', 'Risk-free', 'B', 'Higher transaction costs/illiquidity justify wider corridors to avoid frequent, expensive rebalancing trades.', 5),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-strategic-q07', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-strategic', 'Goals-based asset allocation constructs:', 'A single portfolio for all goals', 'Separate sub-portfolios matched to each goal''s horizon and required success', 'Only a cash portfolio', 'Equal weights across all assets', 'B', 'Goals-based investing builds distinct sub-portfolios, each sized and invested for a specific goal and probability of success.', 6),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-strategic-q08', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-strategic', 'Disciplined rebalancing back to target weights is inherently:', 'A momentum (trend-following) strategy', 'A contrarian strategy that sells winners and buys losers', 'Identical to buy-and-hold', 'Risk-free', 'B', 'Rebalancing trims appreciated assets and adds to laggards, a contrarian action that can earn a diversification return.', 7),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-strategic-q09', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-strategic', 'Risk budgeting is the process of:', 'Allocating capital equally', 'Allocating the portfolio''s risk across positions/factors', 'Minimizing the number of trades', 'Setting the management fee', 'B', 'Risk budgeting decides how much of the total risk each position or factor is permitted to contribute.', 8),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-strategic-q10', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-strategic', 'Across long horizons, the largest share of the variation in a diversified portfolio''s returns is typically attributed to:', 'Security selection', 'Strategic asset allocation', 'Market timing', 'Trading costs', 'B', 'Empirically, the policy (strategic) asset allocation explains most of the variability of long-run portfolio returns.', 9),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-strategic-q11', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-strategic', 'Reverse optimization is used to:', 'Derive expected returns implied by a set of market-cap weights', 'Eliminate risk', 'Forecast inflation', 'Compute duration', 'A', 'Reverse optimization backs out the expected returns implied by observed (equilibrium) asset weights, a key input to Black-Litterman.', 10),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-strategic-q12', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-strategic', 'Resampling and adding constraints are remedies for which MVO problem?', 'Excessive diversification', 'Concentrated, unstable weights driven by input error', 'Too few asset classes', 'Negative volatility', 'B', 'Constraints and resampled efficiency reduce MVO''s tendency to produce highly concentrated, input-sensitive allocations.', 11),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-ldi-q01', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-ldi', 'To immunize a single liability, the asset portfolio''s Macaulay duration should be set equal to the:', 'Liability''s coupon rate', 'Liability''s (investment) horizon', 'Risk-free rate', 'Portfolio''s convexity', 'B', 'Single-liability immunization matches the portfolio''s Macaulay duration to the liability horizon so price and reinvestment effects offset.', 0),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-ldi-q02', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-ldi', 'Beyond matching duration, immunizing a single liability also requires the present value of assets to be:', 'Less than the PV of the liability', 'At least equal to the PV of the liability', 'Exactly zero', 'Equal to the coupon', 'B', 'The asset PV must be greater than or equal to the liability PV so the obligation can be funded.', 1),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-ldi-q03', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-ldi', 'To reduce structural risk in an immunized portfolio, an analyst should minimize the portfolio''s:', 'Convexity/dispersion of cash flows', 'Duration', 'Present value', 'Coupon', 'A', 'Lower dispersion/convexity makes the asset portfolio behave like the single liability, reducing exposure to non-parallel shifts (structural risk).', 2),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-ldi-q04', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-ldi', 'Cash-flow matching (dedication) for multiple liabilities is characterized as:', 'Highest risk, most flexible', 'Lowest risk, least flexible', 'Identical to duration matching', 'Requiring no bonds', 'B', 'Dedication buys bonds whose cash flows directly fund each liability—minimal risk but the least flexible approach.', 3),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-ldi-q05', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-ldi', 'Immunization most reliably protects against ____ changes in interest rates.', 'Large, non-parallel', 'Small, parallel', 'Only rising', 'Only falling', 'B', 'Classical immunization locks in a return against small, parallel yield-curve shifts; twists create residual structural risk.', 4),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-ldi-q06', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-ldi', 'Duration matching of multiple liabilities, versus cash-flow matching, is generally:', 'Less flexible', 'More flexible but exposed to some curve/structural risk', 'Risk-free', 'Impossible', 'B', 'Matching BPV gives more portfolio flexibility than dedication but leaves exposure to non-parallel curve moves.', 5),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-ldi-q07', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-ldi', 'An immunized portfolio must be rebalanced over time because, as rates and time change, the portfolio''s duration:', 'Stays perfectly matched forever', 'Drifts away from the liability''s duration', 'Becomes negative', 'Equals its convexity', 'B', 'Duration changes with the passage of time and yield moves, so periodic rebalancing is needed to maintain the match.', 6),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-ldi-q08', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-ldi', 'Contingent immunization permits active management as long as the portfolio maintains a sufficient:', 'Number of bonds', 'Surplus (safety cushion) above the required terminal value', 'Credit rating', 'Cash balance only', 'B', 'Contingent immunization allows active management while a cushion exists; if the surplus is exhausted, the manager reverts to pure immunization.', 7),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-ldi-q09', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-ldi', 'A pension using bonds to hedge the interest-rate sensitivity of its liabilities is practicing:', 'Liability-driven investing', 'Momentum investing', 'Pure indexing of equities', 'Currency overlay', 'A', 'Managing assets specifically to hedge/fund the liabilities'' interest-rate exposure is liability-driven investing (LDI).', 8),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-ldi-q10', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-ldi', 'The basis point value (BPV) hedge for liabilities aims to set asset BPV:', 'To zero', 'Equal to the liabilities'' BPV', 'Equal to the coupon', 'Above convexity', 'B', 'Matching the asset and liability BPV neutralizes the net interest-rate sensitivity of the surplus to small parallel moves.', 9),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-ldi-q11', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-ldi', 'Structural risk in immunization comes primarily from:', 'Parallel shifts', 'Non-parallel (twist) yield-curve movements', 'Coupon reinvestment at the YTM', 'Holding to maturity', 'B', 'Twists and non-parallel shifts cause the immunized portfolio to diverge from the liability—this residual is structural risk.', 10),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-ldi-q12', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-ldi', 'Compared with cash-flow matching, immunization with a duration target generally requires:', 'More frequent rebalancing', 'No rebalancing ever', 'Exactly matching every cash flow', 'Only zero-coupon bonds', 'A', 'Duration-based immunization needs ongoing rebalancing as durations drift, whereas a fully dedicated cash-flow match is largely set-and-forget.', 11),
  ('cfa3-performance-attribution-q01', 'cfa3-performance-attribution', 'Brinson-style performance attribution decomposes active return into allocation, selection, and:', 'Interaction', 'Inflation', 'Duration', 'Tracking error', 'A', 'The Brinson model splits active return into allocation, selection, and an interaction term.', 0),
  ('cfa3-performance-attribution-q02', 'cfa3-performance-attribution', 'The information ratio is defined as active return divided by:', 'Beta', 'Tracking error (active risk)', 'Total volatility', 'The risk-free rate', 'B', 'IR = active return / tracking error, measuring active return earned per unit of active risk.', 1),
  ('cfa3-performance-attribution-q03', 'cfa3-performance-attribution', 'The M² (Modigliani) measure expresses risk-adjusted performance in units of:', 'Beta', 'Return (comparable to the benchmark at equal total risk)', 'Tracking error', 'Duration', 'B', 'M² restates the Sharpe ratio as a return: the portfolio''s return if levered/de-levered to the benchmark''s total risk.', 2),
  ('cfa3-performance-attribution-q04', 'cfa3-performance-attribution', 'The Treynor ratio uses which measure of risk in its denominator?', 'Total volatility', 'Beta (systematic risk)', 'Tracking error', 'Downside deviation', 'B', 'The Treynor ratio divides excess return by beta, appropriate for a well-diversified sub-portfolio.', 3),
  ('cfa3-performance-attribution-q05', 'cfa3-performance-attribution', 'The fundamental law of active management approximates the information ratio as the information coefficient times the:', 'Square root of breadth', 'Beta', 'Risk-free rate', 'Tracking error squared', 'A', 'IR ≈ IC × √breadth: skill scaled by the number of independent active decisions.', 4),
  ('cfa3-performance-attribution-q06', 'cfa3-performance-attribution', 'Overweighting a sector that subsequently outperforms contributes positively to the ____ component of attribution.', 'Selection', 'Allocation', 'Interaction only', 'Tracking error', 'B', 'Choosing sector weights that differ from the benchmark drives the allocation effect.', 5),
  ('cfa3-performance-attribution-q07', 'cfa3-performance-attribution', 'A valid benchmark should be, among other properties:', 'Unambiguous, investable, and specified in advance', 'Chosen after performance is known', 'Impossible to replicate', 'Different each period', 'A', 'Quality benchmarks are specified in advance, unambiguous, investable, measurable, appropriate, and reflective of current opinions.', 6),
  ('cfa3-performance-attribution-q08', 'cfa3-performance-attribution', 'Holdings-based attribution, versus returns-based attribution, is generally:', 'Less accurate but needs less data', 'More accurate but needs more granular data', 'Identical in all cases', 'Only valid for bonds', 'B', 'Holdings/transaction-based attribution uses actual positions for greater accuracy, at the cost of more detailed data.', 7),
  ('cfa3-performance-attribution-q09', 'cfa3-performance-attribution', 'Jensen''s alpha measures return in excess of that required by the:', 'Dividend discount model', 'CAPM (given the portfolio''s beta)', 'Sortino target', 'Information ratio', 'B', 'Jensen''s alpha = portfolio return − CAPM-required return based on beta.', 8),
  ('cfa3-performance-attribution-q10', 'cfa3-performance-attribution', 'The Sharpe ratio is most appropriate when evaluating:', 'A single security within a portfolio', 'An investor''s entire portfolio (total risk matters)', 'Only active managers vs a benchmark', 'A risk-free asset', 'B', 'Because it uses total risk, the Sharpe ratio suits the whole portfolio rather than a component.', 9),
  ('cfa3-performance-attribution-q11', 'cfa3-performance-attribution', 'A persistent benchmark misfit (style bias) in attribution can cause:', 'Active return to be measured against an inappropriate standard', 'Lower transaction costs', 'Higher liquidity', 'Zero tracking error', 'A', 'If the benchmark doesn''t match the manager''s style, attributed allocation/selection effects are distorted.', 10),
  ('cfa3-performance-attribution-q12', 'cfa3-performance-attribution', 'Distinguishing manager skill from luck (appraisal) is harder when:', 'The track record is very long', 'The track record is short and returns are volatile', 'Tracking error is zero', 'The benchmark is investable', 'B', 'Short, noisy track records make it statistically difficult to separate genuine skill from random luck.', 11),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-passive-active-factor-q01', 'cfa3-equity-pm-passive-active-factor', 'Active management is most appropriately evaluated using the:', 'Sharpe ratio', 'Information ratio (active return / tracking error)', 'Cap rate', 'Current ratio', 'B', 'The information ratio measures active return per unit of active risk, the natural gauge of active skill.', 0),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-passive-active-factor-q02', 'cfa3-equity-pm-passive-active-factor', 'A passive index fund seeks primarily to minimize:', 'Tracking error relative to its benchmark', 'Beta', 'Dividend income', 'The number of holdings', 'A', 'Passive strategies aim to replicate the benchmark, so minimizing tracking error (and cost) is the objective.', 1),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-passive-active-factor-q03', 'cfa3-equity-pm-passive-active-factor', '''Smart beta'' / factor strategies are best described as:', 'Purely passive cap-weighting', 'Rules-based tilts toward rewarded factors, between passive and active', 'High-frequency trading', 'Risk-free investing', 'B', 'Factor investing uses transparent rules to tilt toward factors like value or momentum, sitting between pure passive and discretionary active.', 2),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-passive-active-factor-q04', 'cfa3-equity-pm-passive-active-factor', 'Stratified sampling is a method of:', 'Active stock picking', 'Passively replicating an index without holding every constituent', 'Hedging currency', 'Estimating beta', 'B', 'Stratified sampling matches index characteristics (sectors, size) with a subset of securities, approximating the index at lower cost.', 3),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-passive-active-factor-q05', 'cfa3-equity-pm-passive-active-factor', 'A market-neutral long/short strategy aims to:', 'Maximize beta exposure', 'Isolate alpha by removing market (beta) exposure', 'Track the index exactly', 'Eliminate all risk', 'B', 'Balancing long and short positions removes net market exposure, isolating the manager''s security-selection alpha.', 4),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-passive-active-factor-q06', 'cfa3-equity-pm-passive-active-factor', 'Which is a commonly cited rewarded equity factor?', 'Ticker length', 'Value (high book-to-market)', 'Share price level', 'Trading hours', 'B', 'Value, size, momentum, quality, and low volatility are widely studied rewarded factors; value is a classic example.', 5),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-passive-active-factor-q07', 'cfa3-equity-pm-passive-active-factor', 'Equity futures or total-return swaps are often used to gain market exposure because they are:', 'Free of any risk', 'Cheap and liquid relative to buying all underlying stocks', 'Always tax-exempt', 'Guaranteed to outperform', 'B', 'Derivatives provide efficient, low-cost, liquid beta exposure and are useful for overlays and completion strategies.', 6),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-passive-active-factor-q08', 'cfa3-equity-pm-passive-active-factor', 'According to the fundamental law of active management, the information ratio rises with:', 'Higher fees', 'Greater skill (IC) and breadth of independent decisions', 'More tracking error alone', 'Higher turnover alone', 'B', 'IR ≈ IC × √breadth: combining skill with many independent bets raises the achievable information ratio.', 7),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-passive-active-factor-q09', 'cfa3-equity-pm-passive-active-factor', 'Higher portfolio turnover most directly increases:', 'Transaction costs and tax drag', 'Dividend yield', 'Book value', 'The risk-free rate', 'A', 'Frequent trading raises explicit and implicit transaction costs and can trigger taxable gains, reducing net returns.', 8),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-passive-active-factor-q10', 'cfa3-equity-pm-passive-active-factor', 'A completion overlay is used to:', 'Eliminate the benchmark', 'Fill gaps so total exposures match the desired target', 'Increase fees', 'Remove all equities', 'B', 'A completion overlay (often via derivatives) aligns the portfolio''s net exposures with the intended target.', 9),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-passive-active-factor-q11', 'cfa3-equity-pm-passive-active-factor', 'Full replication of an index is most practical when the index has:', 'Thousands of illiquid constituents', 'A manageable number of liquid constituents', 'No constituents', 'Only derivatives', 'B', 'Full replication works well for indices with relatively few, liquid names; otherwise sampling/optimization is used.', 10),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-passive-active-factor-q12', 'cfa3-equity-pm-passive-active-factor', 'Relative to passive management, active management generally has:', 'Lower fees and lower tracking error', 'Higher fees and higher tracking error', 'Zero fees', 'No benchmark', 'B', 'Active strategies charge more and deliberately deviate from the benchmark, producing higher tracking error.', 11),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-derivatives-q01', 'cfa3-risk-management-derivatives', 'To reduce a portfolio''s equity beta, a manager would:', 'Buy equity index futures', 'Sell equity index futures', 'Buy more stocks', 'Buy call options', 'B', 'Selling (shorting) index futures lowers the portfolio''s effective beta/market exposure.', 0),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-derivatives-q02', 'cfa3-risk-management-derivatives', 'Interest-rate futures and swaps are most commonly used to adjust a bond portfolio''s:', 'Credit rating', 'Duration', 'Coupon', 'Currency', 'B', 'Rate derivatives let managers raise or lower portfolio duration without buying/selling the underlying bonds.', 1),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-derivatives-q03', 'cfa3-risk-management-derivatives', 'A protective put strategy provides:', 'A floor on losses while retaining upside', 'A cap on gains with no downside protection', 'Income but unlimited downside', 'No effect on the payoff', 'A', 'Buying a put on a held asset sets a price floor while leaving upside intact (less the premium).', 2),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-derivatives-q04', 'cfa3-risk-management-derivatives', 'A covered call (long stock + short call) primarily:', 'Generates premium income but caps upside', 'Provides downside protection below the strike', 'Increases upside without limit', 'Eliminates all risk', 'A', 'Writing a call earns premium income but caps gains at the strike; downside is only cushioned by the premium received.', 3),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-derivatives-q05', 'cfa3-risk-management-derivatives', 'A collar combines a:', 'Long put and short call around a held asset', 'Two long calls', 'Two short puts', 'Long stock only', 'A', 'A collar buys a protective put and sells a call to finance it, bounding both downside and upside.', 4),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-derivatives-q06', 'cfa3-risk-management-derivatives', 'Basis risk in a hedge refers to the risk that:', 'The hedge and the exposure do not move perfectly together', 'Margin is required', 'The option expires worthless', 'Interest rates are zero', 'A', 'Basis risk arises when the hedging instrument and the hedged exposure are imperfectly correlated, leaving residual risk.', 5),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-derivatives-q07', 'cfa3-risk-management-derivatives', 'Currency forwards are used to:', 'Increase equity beta', 'Hedge the FX exposure of foreign-currency assets', 'Adjust credit risk', 'Raise duration', 'B', 'Currency forwards offset the exchange-rate exposure embedded in foreign-currency-denominated holdings.', 6),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-derivatives-q08', 'cfa3-risk-management-derivatives', 'Compared with OTC forwards, exchange-traded futures require:', 'No collateral', 'Margin and daily mark-to-market settlement', 'Bilateral negotiation of every term', 'No clearinghouse', 'B', 'Futures are margined and settled daily through a clearinghouse, reducing counterparty risk versus bilateral forwards.', 7),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-derivatives-q09', 'cfa3-risk-management-derivatives', 'To raise a portfolio''s duration toward a target, a manager would generally:', 'Sell interest-rate futures', 'Buy interest-rate futures (or receive-fixed in a swap)', 'Buy protective puts', 'Short equities', 'B', 'Going long rate futures (or receiving fixed in a swap) adds duration, raising the portfolio toward its target.', 8),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-derivatives-q10', 'cfa3-risk-management-derivatives', 'A drawback of hedging with a symmetric instrument like a forward or future is that it:', 'Removes downside but keeps upside', 'Removes both downside and upside exposure', 'Has unlimited upside', 'Requires no monitoring', 'B', 'Forwards/futures lock in a rate, eliminating both adverse and favorable moves—unlike asymmetric options.', 9),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-derivatives-q11', 'cfa3-risk-management-derivatives', 'Delta hedging an option position must be rebalanced over time because of:', 'Theta', 'Gamma (delta changes as the underlying moves)', 'Rho', 'The strike', 'B', 'Gamma causes delta to change with the underlying, so a static delta hedge drifts and needs dynamic rebalancing.', 10),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-derivatives-q12', 'cfa3-risk-management-derivatives', 'The number of equity futures to change beta depends on the target and current betas and the ratio of:', 'Portfolio value to futures contract value', 'Coupon to yield', 'Dividends to price', 'Duration to convexity', 'A', 'Contracts ≈ [(β_target − β_portfolio)/β_futures] × (portfolio value / futures contract value).', 11),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-tax-estate-q01', 'cfa3-private-wealth-tax-estate', '''Asset location'' refers to:', 'Geographic diversification', 'Placing tax-inefficient assets in tax-advantaged accounts', 'Choosing a custodian', 'Currency hedging', 'B', 'Asset location optimizes after-tax returns by holding tax-inefficient assets (e.g., bonds) in tax-deferred/exempt accounts.', 0),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-tax-estate-q02', 'cfa3-private-wealth-tax-estate', 'Tax drag is most damaging to terminal wealth when:', 'Horizons are short', 'Returns are taxed annually over long horizons', 'There are no returns', 'Assets are tax-exempt', 'B', 'Annual taxation reduces the compounding base each year; over long horizons this drag compounds significantly.', 1),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-tax-estate-q03', 'cfa3-private-wealth-tax-estate', 'A tax-deferred account is most advantageous when the investor expects their future tax rate to be:', 'Higher than today', 'Lower than today', 'Exactly the same', 'Zero forever', 'B', 'Deferring tax (pre-tax contributions, taxed on withdrawal) helps most when withdrawals are taxed at a lower future rate.', 2),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-tax-estate-q04', 'cfa3-private-wealth-tax-estate', 'Tax-loss harvesting improves after-tax returns by:', 'Realizing losses to offset taxable gains', 'Increasing turnover for its own sake', 'Avoiding all equities', 'Deferring dividends', 'A', 'Harvesting losses offsets realized gains (and some income), lowering the current tax bill and deferring taxes.', 3),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-tax-estate-q05', 'cfa3-private-wealth-tax-estate', 'A ''step-up in basis'' at death can benefit heirs by:', 'Eliminating embedded capital gains on inherited assets', 'Increasing the estate tax', 'Forcing immediate sale', 'Removing all dividends', 'A', 'Resetting the cost basis to fair value at death can erase unrealized capital gains for heirs.', 4),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-tax-estate-q06', 'cfa3-private-wealth-tax-estate', 'Lifetime gifting can reduce estate taxes primarily by:', 'Removing future appreciation of the gifted asset from the estate', 'Increasing the taxable estate', 'Raising the donor''s income', 'Eliminating the gift''s value', 'A', 'Gifting transfers an asset—and its future growth—out of the estate, often at a lower transfer-tax cost.', 5),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-tax-estate-q07', 'cfa3-private-wealth-tax-estate', 'Which is a constraint (not an objective) in an individual''s IPS?', 'Required rate of return', 'Time horizon', 'Risk tolerance', 'Target alpha', 'B', 'IPS constraints include time horizon, liquidity, taxes, legal/regulatory, and unique circumstances; return and risk are objectives.', 6),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-tax-estate-q08', 'cfa3-private-wealth-tax-estate', 'An irrevocable trust differs from a revocable trust in that the grantor:', 'Can freely change or revoke it', 'Generally gives up control, often removing assets from the taxable estate', 'Pays no setup cost', 'Retains full ownership', 'B', 'Irrevocable trusts remove assets from the grantor''s control (and often the estate), unlike revocable trusts.', 7),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-tax-estate-q09', 'cfa3-private-wealth-tax-estate', 'Holding low-turnover, tax-efficient equities in a taxable account is consistent with:', 'Poor asset location', 'Sound asset-location practice', 'Maximizing tax drag', 'Avoiding equities', 'B', 'Tax-efficient assets belong in taxable accounts, leaving scarce tax-advantaged space for tax-inefficient holdings.', 8),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-tax-estate-q10', 'cfa3-private-wealth-tax-estate', 'An individual''s ''human capital'' is best described as:', 'The present value of future labor income', 'Their stock portfolio', 'Their home equity', 'Their pension only', 'A', 'Human capital is the present value of expected future earnings; bond-like human capital argues for more equity in financial assets.', 9),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-tax-estate-q11', 'cfa3-private-wealth-tax-estate', 'Longevity risk for a retiree is the risk of:', 'Dying earlier than expected', 'Outliving one''s assets', 'Market volatility only', 'Currency depreciation', 'B', 'Longevity risk is the danger of living longer than planned and exhausting savings; annuities can hedge it.', 10),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-tax-estate-q12', 'cfa3-private-wealth-tax-estate', 'A tax-exempt account (after-tax contributions, tax-free withdrawals) is most attractive when future tax rates are expected to be:', 'Lower than today', 'Higher than today', 'Zero', 'Irrelevant', 'B', 'Paying tax now (tax-exempt account) is favorable when withdrawals would otherwise be taxed at a higher future rate.', 11),
  ('cfa3-institutional-pensions-endowments-q01', 'cfa3-institutional-pensions-endowments', 'A defined-benefit pension generally has a HIGHER risk tolerance when:', 'The workforce is older and the plan is underfunded', 'The workforce is younger and the plan is well funded', 'The sponsor is financially weak', 'The plan is large relative to the sponsor', 'B', 'Younger workforce, strong funded status, and a financially healthy sponsor all raise a DB plan''s ability to bear risk.', 0),
  ('cfa3-institutional-pensions-endowments-q02', 'cfa3-institutional-pensions-endowments', 'Because pension liabilities are bond-like, plans commonly adopt:', 'Pure equity indexing', 'Liability-driven investing (duration matching)', 'Currency speculation', 'Commodity-only portfolios', 'B', 'LDI hedges the interest-rate sensitivity of the bond-like liabilities by matching asset and liability duration.', 1),
  ('cfa3-institutional-pensions-endowments-q03', 'cfa3-institutional-pensions-endowments', 'An endowment''s primary objective is typically to:', 'Maximize short-term income', 'Support spending in perpetuity while preserving real value', 'Avoid all equities', 'Minimize the time horizon', 'B', 'Endowments aim to fund ongoing spending forever while maintaining the inflation-adjusted (real) value of the corpus.', 2),
  ('cfa3-institutional-pensions-endowments-q04', 'cfa3-institutional-pensions-endowments', 'An endowment''s return objective is approximately:', 'The risk-free rate', 'Spending rate + inflation + fees', 'Zero', 'The dividend yield only', 'B', 'To preserve real value and fund spending, the required return ≈ spending rate + expected inflation + management costs.', 3),
  ('cfa3-institutional-pensions-endowments-q05', 'cfa3-institutional-pensions-endowments', 'A rolling 3-year average spending rule is used to:', 'Increase spending volatility', 'Smooth distributions versus a simple percentage of current assets', 'Eliminate spending', 'Track a single year''s return', 'B', 'Averaging asset values over several years smooths spending, reducing year-to-year volatility in distributions.', 4),
  ('cfa3-institutional-pensions-endowments-q06', 'cfa3-institutional-pensions-endowments', 'Endowments typically have a ____ risk tolerance and ____ time horizon.', 'low; short', 'high; long', 'low; long', 'high; short', 'B', 'Their perpetual horizon and limited near-term liquidity needs give endowments high risk tolerance and a long horizon.', 5),
  ('cfa3-institutional-pensions-endowments-q07', 'cfa3-institutional-pensions-endowments', 'A higher proportion of retired lives in a pension plan tends to increase its:', 'Risk tolerance', 'Liquidity needs', 'Time horizon', 'Equity allocation', 'B', 'More retirees means larger, nearer-term benefit payments, raising liquidity needs and lowering risk tolerance.', 6),
  ('cfa3-institutional-pensions-endowments-q08', 'cfa3-institutional-pensions-endowments', 'Banks and insurers, relative to endowments, generally have:', 'Higher risk tolerance and longer horizons', 'Lower risk tolerance with strong liability/ALM and liquidity focus', 'No regulation', 'No liabilities', 'B', 'Regulated, liability-driven institutions like banks/insurers have lower risk tolerance and emphasize asset-liability management and liquidity.', 7),
  ('cfa3-institutional-pensions-endowments-q09', 'cfa3-institutional-pensions-endowments', 'US private foundations are generally required to distribute about:', '1% of assets annually', '5% of assets annually', '20% of assets annually', 'Nothing', 'B', 'US private foundations typically must pay out roughly 5% of assets per year to maintain tax-exempt status.', 8),
  ('cfa3-institutional-pensions-endowments-q10', 'cfa3-institutional-pensions-endowments', 'The ''Yale'' (geometric/smoothing) spending rule blends:', 'Prior spending and current market value', 'Only the risk-free rate', 'Dividends and coupons only', 'Beta and alpha', 'A', 'The geometric/Yale rule weights last year''s (inflation-adjusted) spending and a percentage of current market value to stabilize payouts.', 9),
  ('cfa3-institutional-pensions-endowments-q11', 'cfa3-institutional-pensions-endowments', 'A pension plan whose asset returns are highly correlated with the sponsor''s operating results should generally take:', 'More investment risk', 'Less investment risk', 'The same risk regardless', 'Only currency risk', 'B', 'High correlation between plan assets and sponsor health concentrates risk, arguing for a more conservative plan allocation.', 10),
  ('cfa3-institutional-pensions-endowments-q12', 'cfa3-institutional-pensions-endowments', 'Endowments'' long horizons and high risk tolerance often lead to large allocations to:', 'Cash and T-bills', 'Equities and alternative investments', 'Short-term deposits only', 'A single bond', 'B', 'To meet real-return objectives over perpetual horizons, endowments tilt toward equities and alternatives (the ''endowment model'').', 11),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-rebalancing-execution-q01', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-rebalancing-execution', 'A percentage-range (corridor) rebalancing policy triggers a trade when:', 'A fixed calendar date arrives', 'An asset''s weight breaches its tolerance band', 'The market rises', 'A dividend is paid', 'B', 'Corridor rebalancing acts when an allocation drifts outside its preset band, regardless of the date.', 0),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-rebalancing-execution-q02', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-rebalancing-execution', 'Optimal rebalancing corridors should be WIDER for assets with:', 'Higher transaction costs', 'Higher volatility', 'Higher correlation with the rest of the portfolio', 'Lower risk tolerance', 'A', 'Higher trading costs justify wider bands to avoid frequent, costly rebalancing; higher volatility argues for narrower bands.', 1),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-rebalancing-execution-q03', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-rebalancing-execution', 'Disciplined rebalancing is inherently:', 'A momentum strategy', 'A contrarian strategy (sell winners, buy losers)', 'Identical to buy-and-hold', 'Risk-free', 'B', 'Rebalancing trims appreciated assets and tops up laggards—a contrarian action that can earn a diversification return.', 2),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-rebalancing-execution-q04', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-rebalancing-execution', 'Implementation shortfall measures the difference between:', 'Two benchmarks', 'A paper portfolio''s return and the actual executed return', 'Beta and alpha', 'Bid and ask only', 'B', 'Implementation shortfall captures total trading cost as the gap between a hypothetical (decision-price) portfolio and the real one.', 3),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-rebalancing-execution-q05', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-rebalancing-execution', 'Which is an IMPLICIT trading cost?', 'Brokerage commission', 'Market impact', 'Exchange fee', 'Transaction tax', 'B', 'Market impact (the price move caused by trading) is implicit; commissions, fees and taxes are explicit costs.', 4),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-rebalancing-execution-q06', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-rebalancing-execution', 'VWAP as an execution benchmark compares fills against the:', 'Volume-weighted average price over the period', 'Closing price only', 'Risk-free rate', 'Prior day''s open', 'A', 'VWAP measures execution quality against the volume-weighted average traded price during the order''s horizon.', 5),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-rebalancing-execution-q07', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-rebalancing-execution', 'A more aggressive (liquidity-demanding) execution generally has:', 'Higher market impact but lower timing risk', 'Lower impact and lower timing risk', 'No costs', 'Higher opportunity cost', 'A', 'Trading quickly raises market-impact cost but reduces exposure to adverse price drift (timing risk).', 6),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-rebalancing-execution-q08', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-rebalancing-execution', 'The opportunity cost component of implementation shortfall arises from:', 'Commissions', 'Portions of the order left unfilled as the price moves away', 'Taxes', 'The bid-ask spread only', 'B', 'Unexecuted shares whose price moves adversely create an opportunity (missed-trade) cost.', 7),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-rebalancing-execution-q09', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-rebalancing-execution', 'Slicing a large order into smaller child orders over time primarily aims to:', 'Increase market impact', 'Reduce market impact', 'Raise commissions', 'Avoid all risk', 'B', 'Breaking a large order into pieces limits the price impact of demanding too much liquidity at once.', 8),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-rebalancing-execution-q10', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-rebalancing-execution', 'Calendar rebalancing differs from corridor rebalancing in that it:', 'Responds to market moves continuously', 'Rebalances on fixed dates regardless of drift', 'Never trades', 'Eliminates transaction costs', 'B', 'Calendar rebalancing is time-triggered, ignoring how far weights have drifted between dates.', 9),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-rebalancing-execution-q11', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-rebalancing-execution', 'The diversification return captured by rebalancing is greatest when assets have:', 'Perfectly correlated returns', 'Volatile, less-than-perfectly correlated returns', 'Zero volatility', 'Identical returns', 'B', 'Rebalancing benefits most from volatile assets whose correlations are below 1, harvesting relative mispricings.', 10),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-rebalancing-execution-q12', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-rebalancing-execution', 'An implementation-shortfall execution algorithm explicitly trades off:', 'Market impact against timing/price risk', 'Dividends against coupons', 'Beta against alpha', 'Duration against convexity', 'A', 'IS algorithms balance the cost of trading fast (impact) against the risk of trading slowly (price drift).', 11),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-currency-q01', 'cfa3-risk-management-currency', 'The total return on an unhedged foreign asset comprises the local asset return and the:', 'Risk-free rate', 'Currency (exchange-rate) return', 'Dividend yield only', 'Inflation rate', 'B', 'An unhedged foreign investment earns the foreign asset''s return plus the return from currency movements.', 0),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-currency-q02', 'cfa3-risk-management-currency', 'The most commonly used instrument for currency hedging is the:', 'Currency forward', 'Equity index future', 'Credit default swap', 'Interest-rate cap', 'A', 'Currency forwards are precise, low-cost and widely used; they are rolled at maturity to maintain a hedge.', 1),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-currency-q03', 'cfa3-risk-management-currency', 'Currency risk is typically hedged MORE for foreign bond portfolios than equity portfolios because:', 'Bonds have higher returns', 'Currency volatility is large relative to bond returns', 'Bonds have no issuer risk', 'Equities cannot be hedged', 'B', 'For bonds the currency swing can dwarf the yield, so hedging is common; for equities currency is a smaller, diversifying share.', 2),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-currency-q04', 'cfa3-risk-management-currency', 'A fully hedged foreign-asset position is most appropriate for an investor who is:', 'Long-horizon and risk-seeking', 'Short-horizon and risk-averse', 'Indifferent to risk', 'Seeking maximum currency exposure', 'B', 'Shorter horizons and risk aversion push toward higher hedge ratios to remove currency volatility.', 3),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-currency-q05', 'cfa3-risk-management-currency', 'The minimum-variance hedge ratio is estimated as the:', 'Beta from regressing domestic-currency asset returns on the exchange-rate return', 'Ratio of durations', 'Dividend yield', 'Sharpe ratio', 'A', 'The minimum-variance hedge ratio is the regression slope of the asset''s domestic-currency return on the currency return.', 4),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-currency-q06', 'cfa3-risk-management-currency', 'A proxy (cross) hedge is used when:', 'A direct forward in the exposure currency is costly or unavailable', 'The asset is domestic', 'There is no currency risk', 'Interest rates are zero', 'A', 'A proxy hedge uses a correlated, more liquid/cheaper currency when a direct forward is impractical—introducing basis risk.', 5),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-currency-q07', 'cfa3-risk-management-currency', 'Currency options differ from forwards as a hedge in that options:', 'Provide asymmetric protection (downside hedged, upside kept) for a premium', 'Lock in a single rate with no premium', 'Cannot expire', 'Have no cost', 'A', 'Options hedge adverse moves while retaining favorable ones, at the cost of the premium—unlike symmetric forwards.', 6),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-currency-q08', 'cfa3-risk-management-currency', 'A currency overlay manager is hired to:', 'Manage the underlying stocks and bonds', 'Manage currency exposure (and seek FX alpha) separately from the asset managers', 'Set the strategic asset allocation', 'Run the leaderboard', 'B', 'A currency overlay separates FX management—hedging and active currency bets—from the underlying asset mandates.', 7),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-currency-q09', 'cfa3-risk-management-currency', 'Maintaining a forward hedge requires periodically:', 'Rolling the forward at maturity', 'Paying dividends', 'Exercising an option', 'Issuing equity', 'A', 'Forwards expire, so a continuing hedge is maintained by rolling into a new forward at each maturity.', 8),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-currency-q10', 'cfa3-risk-management-currency', 'Active currency strategies commonly exploit which factor?', 'Carry (interest-rate differentials)', 'Bond convexity', 'Equity book value', 'Operating leverage', 'A', 'Currency alpha tactics include carry, value (PPP), momentum and volatility trading.', 9),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-currency-q11', 'cfa3-risk-management-currency', '''Roll yield'' on a currency hedge refers to the return from:', 'Dividends on the foreign asset', 'The forward premium/discount as the hedge is rolled', 'Equity price changes', 'Coupon reinvestment', 'B', 'Hedging a currency trading at a forward premium or discount adds or subtracts return when the forward is rolled.', 10),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-currency-q12', 'cfa3-risk-management-currency', 'Leaving foreign-equity currency exposure partially unhedged can be justified because currencies may:', 'Guarantee higher returns', 'Provide diversification to the overall portfolio', 'Eliminate equity risk', 'Remove the need for an IPS', 'B', 'Currency returns are imperfectly correlated with equities, so some unhedged exposure can add diversification.', 11),
  ('cfa3-ethics-asset-manager-code-q01', 'cfa3-ethics-asset-manager-code', 'The Asset Manager Code applies at the level of the:', 'Individual member', 'Firm', 'Regulator', 'Client', 'B', 'The AMC is a firm-level voluntary code of conduct, distinct from the individual Code and Standards.', 0),
  ('cfa3-ethics-asset-manager-code-q02', 'cfa3-ethics-asset-manager-code', 'Compliance with the Asset Manager Code is:', 'Verified by CFA Institute before a firm may claim it', 'Self-claimed by the firm; CFA Institute does not verify it', 'Mandatory for all firms', 'Granted by regulators', 'B', 'Firms claim AMC compliance themselves; CFA Institute does not certify or verify those claims.', 1),
  ('cfa3-ethics-asset-manager-code-q03', 'cfa3-ethics-asset-manager-code', 'Which is one of the six components of the Asset Manager Code?', 'Maximizing assets under management', 'Trading (best execution, client brokerage)', 'Beating the benchmark every year', 'Avoiding all derivatives', 'B', 'The AMC''s six areas include Loyalty to Clients, Investment Process, Trading, Risk/Compliance, Performance/Valuation, and Disclosures.', 2),
  ('cfa3-ethics-asset-manager-code-q04', 'cfa3-ethics-asset-manager-code', 'GIPS compliance must be applied on a ____ basis.', 'Composite-by-composite', 'Firm-wide', 'Per-client', 'Per-portfolio', 'B', 'A firm either complies with GIPS firm-wide or it does not; partial (composite-only) compliance is not permitted.', 3),
  ('cfa3-ethics-asset-manager-code-q05', 'cfa3-ethics-asset-manager-code', 'A GIPS composite must include:', 'Only the firm''s best-performing portfolios', 'All fee-paying discretionary portfolios of a similar mandate', 'Hypothetical model portfolios', 'Only terminated accounts', 'B', 'Composites group all actual fee-paying discretionary portfolios of a strategy, preventing cherry-picking and survivorship bias.', 4),
  ('cfa3-ethics-asset-manager-code-q06', 'cfa3-ethics-asset-manager-code', 'GIPS requires returns to be calculated using a ____ return.', 'Money-weighted', 'Time-weighted', 'Simple average', 'Geometric-only nominal', 'B', 'Time-weighted returns remove the distorting effect of client-driven external cash flows, the GIPS default for composites.', 5),
  ('cfa3-ethics-asset-manager-code-q07', 'cfa3-ethics-asset-manager-code', 'Third-party verification under GIPS is:', 'Mandatory', 'Recommended but voluntary, and applies firm-wide', 'Performed by CFA Institute', 'Done per composite only', 'B', 'Verification is recommended, voluntary, and assesses firm-wide compliance—not individual composites.', 6),
  ('cfa3-ethics-asset-manager-code-q08', 'cfa3-ethics-asset-manager-code', 'Under the AMC, client brokerage (soft dollars) must be used:', 'For the manager''s overhead', 'For the benefit of the client', 'To reward the largest clients', 'However the firm wishes', 'B', 'The Trading component requires client brokerage to be used for the client''s benefit, consistent with the Standards.', 7),
  ('cfa3-ethics-asset-manager-code-q09', 'cfa3-ethics-asset-manager-code', 'The AMC requires firms to designate a:', 'Marketing director', 'Compliance officer responsible for policies and controls', 'Chief investment officer only', 'Board chair', 'B', 'The Risk Management, Compliance and Support component requires a designated compliance officer and adequate controls.', 8),
  ('cfa3-ethics-asset-manager-code-q10', 'cfa3-ethics-asset-manager-code', 'When first claiming GIPS compliance, a firm must present at least ____ of compliant history (building toward 10).', '1 month', '5 years (or since inception if shorter)', '20 years', 'No history', 'B', 'Firms must show a minimum of five years (or since inception) of GIPS-compliant performance, then build to ten.', 9),
  ('cfa3-ethics-asset-manager-code-q11', 'cfa3-ethics-asset-manager-code', 'A primary purpose of the GIPS standards is to ensure:', 'Higher returns', 'Fair representation and full disclosure of performance', 'Lower fees', 'Tax efficiency', 'B', 'GIPS exists to standardize performance reporting so it fairly represents results and fully discloses methodology.', 10),
  ('cfa3-ethics-asset-manager-code-q12', 'cfa3-ethics-asset-manager-code', 'Under the AMC Disclosures component, firms must disclose:', 'Only positive performance', 'Conflicts of interest, fees and costs truthfully and on time', 'Competitors'' weaknesses', 'Clients'' identities publicly', 'B', 'Disclosures must be truthful, accurate, complete and timely—covering conflicts, fees, costs and material information.', 11),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-portfolio-role-q01', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-portfolio-role', 'A primary portfolio reason to add alternatives is:', 'Guaranteed returns', 'Diversification and potential return/inflation benefits', 'Daily liquidity', 'Zero fees', 'B', 'Alternatives are added mainly for diversification, return enhancement, and inflation protection—not liquidity or low cost.', 0),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-portfolio-role-q02', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-portfolio-role', 'Appraisal-based (stale) pricing of private assets causes reported returns to have:', 'Overstated volatility', 'Understated volatility and correlation (smoothing)', 'No bias', 'Negative returns', 'B', 'Smoothing from infrequent appraisals understates measured volatility and correlation, flattering risk statistics.', 1),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-portfolio-role-q03', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-portfolio-role', 'Feeding smoothed alternative returns directly into mean-variance optimization tends to:', 'Under-allocate to alternatives', 'Over-allocate to alternatives', 'Have no effect', 'Eliminate them', 'B', 'Because risk looks artificially low, naive MVO over-allocates to alternatives; returns should be ''unsmoothed'' first.', 2),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-portfolio-role-q04', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-portfolio-role', 'The correlation of alternatives with public markets typically:', 'Stays constant', 'Rises during market crises, reducing diversification when most needed', 'Falls to zero in crises', 'Is always 1', 'B', 'Diversification benefits weaken in stress as correlations spike across asset classes.', 3),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-portfolio-role-q05', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-portfolio-role', 'Allocating alternatives by ''role'' means grouping them by:', 'Ticker', 'Function (growth, income, diversification, inflation hedge)', 'Manager surname', 'Country only', 'B', 'A functional/role-based approach allocates by the economic purpose each alternative serves, rather than rigid asset-class labels.', 4),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-portfolio-role-q06', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-portfolio-role', 'Risk measurement for alternatives should account for returns that are:', 'Perfectly normal', 'Non-normal (fat-tailed, negatively skewed)', 'Risk-free', 'Symmetric and thin-tailed', 'B', 'Alternative returns often have fat tails and negative skew, so volatility/VaR alone understate risk—use scenario and downside measures.', 5),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-portfolio-role-q07', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-portfolio-role', 'The ''J-curve'' affects private-fund allocations by producing:', 'Immediate high returns', 'Early negative returns before later gains', 'Constant returns', 'Guaranteed losses', 'B', 'Fees and early markdowns create initial negative returns (the J-curve) before exits generate gains.', 6),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-portfolio-role-q08', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-portfolio-role', 'Commitment pacing and vintage-year diversification are used to manage:', 'Currency risk', 'Liquidity and timing risk in private funds', 'Interest-rate risk only', 'Tax filing', 'B', 'Spreading commitments across vintages manages liquidity, the J-curve, and timing concentration in private programs.', 7),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-portfolio-role-q09', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-portfolio-role', 'A key constraint on sizing alternatives is:', 'Excessive liquidity', 'Illiquidity, lock-ups and high fees', 'Daily pricing', 'Government guarantees', 'B', 'Long lock-ups, illiquidity, high fees and operational risk limit how much a portfolio can prudently allocate.', 8),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-portfolio-role-q10', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-portfolio-role', 'Real assets (e.g., commodities, real estate, infrastructure) are often added for:', 'Inflation protection', 'Guaranteed income', 'Tax exemption', 'Zero volatility', 'A', 'Real assets tend to provide inflation sensitivity, a key diversifying role in multi-asset portfolios.', 9),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-portfolio-role-q11', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-portfolio-role', 'Benchmarking hedge funds is difficult, so investors often use:', 'A government bond index', 'Peer-group or absolute/risk-factor benchmarks', 'The risk-free rate exactly', 'No benchmark is ever possible', 'B', 'Hedge funds are commonly assessed against peer groups, absolute-return targets, or factor-based benchmarks.', 10),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-portfolio-role-q12', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-portfolio-role', '''Unsmoothing'' a private-asset return series generally:', 'Lowers estimated volatility further', 'Raises estimated volatility and correlation toward true levels', 'Removes all risk', 'Converts it to cash', 'B', 'Unsmoothing reverses appraisal lag, increasing measured volatility/correlation to more realistic levels for allocation.', 11),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-due-diligence-q01', 'cfa3-manager-selection-due-diligence', 'A Type I error in manager selection is:', 'Rejecting a skilled manager', 'Hiring/retaining a manager with no real skill', 'A data entry mistake', 'Choosing a benchmark', 'B', 'Type I (false positive) is keeping or hiring an unskilled manager.', 0),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-due-diligence-q02', 'cfa3-manager-selection-due-diligence', 'A Type II error in manager selection is:', 'Hiring an unskilled manager', 'Rejecting or firing a genuinely skilled manager', 'Overpaying fees', 'Using time-weighted returns', 'B', 'Type II (false negative) is failing to hire or wrongly firing a skilled manager.', 1),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-due-diligence-q03', 'cfa3-manager-selection-due-diligence', 'Tightening selection criteria to avoid unskilled managers tends to:', 'Reduce Type I but increase Type II errors', 'Reduce both error types', 'Increase Type I errors', 'Have no effect on errors', 'A', 'Stricter screens cut false positives (Type I) but raise the chance of rejecting skilled managers (Type II).', 2),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-due-diligence-q04', 'cfa3-manager-selection-due-diligence', 'Operational due diligence is important because operational failures are:', 'Never a cause of fund losses', 'A major cause of fund failures', 'Only relevant to equities', 'Irrelevant to selection', 'B', 'Many fund blow-ups stem from operational issues (controls, valuation, fraud), making ODD essential.', 3),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-due-diligence-q05', 'cfa3-manager-selection-due-diligence', 'An independent administrator, custodian and auditor primarily provide:', 'Higher returns', 'Checks on valuation and safekeeping that reduce operational/fraud risk', 'Tax savings', 'Lower volatility', 'B', 'Independent service providers verify assets and valuations, a key operational-risk control in due diligence.', 4),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-due-diligence-q06', 'cfa3-manager-selection-due-diligence', 'Decomposing a manager''s return into alpha and factor exposures helps avoid:', 'Paying alpha fees for what is really cheap beta', 'Using a benchmark', 'Diversification', 'Time-weighted returns', 'A', 'Returns-based analysis separates true skill (alpha) from factor (beta) exposure so investors don''t overpay for beta.', 5),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-due-diligence-q07', 'cfa3-manager-selection-due-diligence', 'Backfill and survivorship biases in a manager database tend to:', 'Understate historical performance', 'Overstate historical performance', 'Have no effect', 'Only affect fees', 'B', 'Both biases inflate reported track records, so due diligence must adjust for them.', 6),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-due-diligence-q08', 'cfa3-manager-selection-due-diligence', 'A short, volatile track record makes it hard to:', 'Compute fees', 'Distinguish skill from luck statistically', 'Define a universe', 'Read disclosures', 'B', 'Limited, noisy data reduces statistical confidence in separating genuine skill from random outcomes.', 7),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-due-diligence-q09', 'cfa3-manager-selection-due-diligence', 'Investment due diligence assesses the manager''s:', 'Office location only', 'Philosophy, repeatable process, team and decision-making', 'Auditor''s fees', 'Custodian''s address', 'B', 'Investment DD evaluates whether the strategy, process and team can plausibly repeat past success.', 8),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-due-diligence-q10', 'cfa3-manager-selection-due-diligence', 'Key-person risk in manager selection refers to:', 'The custodian failing', 'Over-reliance on one critical individual', 'High portfolio turnover', 'Currency exposure', 'B', 'Key-person risk is dependence on a single individual whose departure would impair the strategy.', 9),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-due-diligence-q11', 'cfa3-manager-selection-due-diligence', 'Capacity analysis matters because a strategy that grows too large may:', 'Always improve returns', 'Suffer decayed alpha from market impact and crowding', 'Become risk-free', 'Lower its fees automatically', 'B', 'Excess assets can erode a strategy''s edge through higher transaction costs and reduced opportunity per dollar.', 10),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-due-diligence-q12', 'cfa3-manager-selection-due-diligence', 'The final output of a sound manager-selection process should be:', 'A ranking by last year''s return only', 'A conviction-weighted recommendation grounded in skill and operations', 'The cheapest manager', 'The largest fund', 'B', 'Selection should yield a forward-looking, conviction-based decision integrating skill evidence and operational soundness, not just past returns.', 11),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-market-behavior-q01', 'cfa3-behavioral-market-behavior', 'Momentum (returns persisting short-term) is considered a market anomaly because it appears to contradict:', 'The capital structure theory', 'Weak-form market efficiency', 'Put-call parity', 'The Taylor rule', 'B', 'Predictable continuation of returns from past prices challenges weak-form efficiency.', 0),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-market-behavior-q02', 'cfa3-behavioral-market-behavior', 'Bubbles and crashes are often driven by:', 'Perfect rationality', 'Herding, overconfidence and regret', 'Lower trading volume', 'Risk-free arbitrage', 'B', 'Behavioral forces like herding and overconfidence detach prices from fundamentals, then reverse sharply.', 1),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-market-behavior-q03', 'cfa3-behavioral-market-behavior', 'The Adaptive Markets Hypothesis suggests that market efficiency:', 'Is constant', 'Varies with conditions as strategies compete and adapt', 'Never exists', 'Is guaranteed', 'B', 'Lo''s AMH applies evolutionary ideas: efficiency fluctuates as participants and strategies adapt.', 2),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-market-behavior-q04', 'cfa3-behavioral-market-behavior', 'Many documented anomalies tend to:', 'Strengthen after publication', 'Weaken or disappear after discovery / net of costs', 'Guarantee profits', 'Be risk-free', 'B', 'Anomalies often shrink once known, suggesting risk compensation, data mining, or arbitraged-away mispricing.', 3),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-market-behavior-q05', 'cfa3-behavioral-market-behavior', 'The value premium refers to the tendency of:', 'Growth stocks to outperform', 'High book-to-market (value) stocks to outperform over time', 'Large caps to outperform', 'Bonds to beat stocks', 'B', 'Value stocks have historically earned a premium, partly attributed to risk and partly to behavior.', 4),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-market-behavior-q06', 'cfa3-behavioral-market-behavior', 'The disposition effect contributes to which market pattern?', 'Under-reaction and momentum', 'Instant full efficiency', 'Put-call parity', 'Risk-free arbitrage', 'A', 'Holding losers and selling winners slows price adjustment, feeding under-reaction and momentum.', 5),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-market-behavior-q07', 'cfa3-behavioral-market-behavior', '''This time is different'' narratives during bubbles are an example of:', 'Rational expectations', 'Overconfidence and rationalization', 'Arbitrage', 'Risk aversion', 'B', 'Such narratives rationalize extreme valuations, a hallmark of bubble psychology.', 6),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-market-behavior-q08', 'cfa3-behavioral-market-behavior', 'Post-earnings-announcement drift describes prices that:', 'Adjust instantly to earnings', 'Continue drifting in the direction of an earnings surprise', 'Reverse immediately', 'Ignore earnings', 'B', 'Prices under-react to earnings surprises and drift, an anomaly versus semi-strong efficiency.', 7),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-market-behavior-q09', 'cfa3-behavioral-market-behavior', 'Whether an anomaly is exploitable depends largely on its persistence, capacity and:', 'Transaction costs', 'The risk-free rate', 'The dividend date', 'The auditor', 'A', 'After costs and capacity limits, many anomalies offer little net, exploitable profit.', 8),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-market-behavior-q10', 'cfa3-behavioral-market-behavior', 'Behavioral asset pricing adds to required return a:', 'Sentiment premium', 'Coupon', 'Dividend', 'Tax shield', 'A', 'Behavioral models append a sentiment-driven premium to the standard risk-based required return.', 9),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-market-behavior-q11', 'cfa3-behavioral-market-behavior', 'The size effect is the historical tendency of:', 'Large caps to outperform', 'Small caps to outperform large caps', 'Bonds to outperform', 'Cash to outperform', 'B', 'Smaller firms have historically earned higher returns (the size premium), partly a risk story.', 10),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-market-behavior-q12', 'cfa3-behavioral-market-behavior', 'A practical implication of behavioral finance for markets is that they are:', 'Always perfectly efficient', 'Mostly efficient but subject to periodic behavioral mispricing', 'Completely random and unanalyzable', 'Always mispriced', 'B', 'Evidence supports broadly efficient markets punctuated by behaviorally driven mispricing.', 11),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-investor-types-q01', 'cfa3-behavioral-investor-types', 'A Passive Preserver is dominated by which kind of bias?', 'Cognitive', 'Emotional (loss aversion, status quo)', 'No biases', 'Arbitrage', 'B', 'Passive Preservers are emotionally biased and risk-averse, prioritizing capital preservation.', 0),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-investor-types-q02', 'cfa3-behavioral-investor-types', 'An Active Accumulator typically has:', 'Low risk tolerance and emotional biases', 'High risk tolerance with overconfidence/illusion of control', 'No biases', 'Only cognitive errors', 'B', 'Active Accumulators are aggressive, emotionally biased (overconfidence, illusion of control).', 1),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-investor-types-q03', 'cfa3-behavioral-investor-types', 'An Independent Individualist is characterized mainly by:', 'Emotional biases', 'Cognitive biases (overconfidence, confirmation, self-attribution)', 'No risk tolerance', 'Following trends', 'B', 'Independent Individualists exhibit cognitive biases and contrarian, high-risk tendencies.', 2),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-investor-types-q04', 'cfa3-behavioral-investor-types', 'A Friendly Follower tends to:', 'Lead market trends', 'Follow trends/peers and exhibit recency and availability biases', 'Ignore all advice', 'Take maximum risk', 'B', 'Friendly Followers chase popular ideas, showing cognitive biases like recency and availability.', 3),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-investor-types-q05', 'cfa3-behavioral-investor-types', 'For a client dominated by emotional biases, an adviser should generally:', 'Moderate the biases aggressively', 'Adapt the portfolio to accommodate them', 'Ignore the biases', 'Maximize tracking error', 'B', 'Hard-to-change emotional biases are accommodated; cognitive errors are moderated with education.', 4),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-investor-types-q06', 'cfa3-behavioral-investor-types', 'For a client with mostly cognitive errors, the adviser should:', 'Adapt the plan around them', 'Moderate them with education and data', 'Do nothing', 'Increase leverage', 'B', 'Cognitive errors can be reduced through information and disciplined process.', 5),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-investor-types-q07', 'cfa3-behavioral-investor-types', 'A limitation of behavioral investor type frameworks is that investors:', 'Always fit one type perfectly', 'May show multiple biases and change over time', 'Have no biases', 'Cannot be profiled', 'B', 'People rarely fit a single box and their biases shift, so types are a starting diagnostic only.', 6),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-investor-types-q08', 'cfa3-behavioral-investor-types', 'Larger deviations from the rational optimum are more acceptable when a client has:', 'Low standard-of-living risk tolerance', 'High standard-of-living risk tolerance and emotional biases', 'Only cognitive biases', 'No wealth', 'B', 'Wealthier clients (higher SLR tolerance) with emotional biases can deviate more while staying secure.', 7),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-investor-types-q09', 'cfa3-behavioral-investor-types', 'The Passive Preserver''s low risk tolerance suggests a portfolio that is:', 'Highly aggressive', 'Conservative, capital-preservation oriented', '100% equities', 'Fully leveraged', 'B', 'A risk-averse preserver needs a conservative allocation emphasizing safety.', 8),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-investor-types-q10', 'cfa3-behavioral-investor-types', 'Behavioral typing helps advisers primarily by:', 'Eliminating market risk', 'Tailoring portfolios and communication to the client', 'Predicting returns', 'Removing fees', 'B', 'Understanding type improves suitability, communication, and client adherence.', 9),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-investor-types-q11', 'cfa3-behavioral-investor-types', 'A Friendly Follower may overestimate their risk tolerance because of:', 'Confirmation bias only', 'Recency and social-proof influences', 'Loss aversion only', 'Endowment bias', 'B', 'Following recent winners and peers can lead Friendly Followers to misjudge their true risk appetite.', 10),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-investor-types-q12', 'cfa3-behavioral-investor-types', 'After assigning a behavioral type, the adviser should:', 'Treat it as final and unchangeable', 'Confirm via discussion and a bias questionnaire', 'Ignore the client''s input', 'Maximize turnover', 'B', 'Types are a hypothesis to validate through conversation and structured assessment.', 11),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-adviser-client-q01', 'cfa3-behavioral-adviser-client', 'Understanding client psychology helps mainly by improving:', 'Market efficiency', 'Risk profiling, suitability and client adherence', 'The risk-free rate', 'Tax rates', 'B', 'Behavioral insight yields better risk profiles, more suitable portfolios, and clients who stick with the plan.', 0),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-adviser-client-q02', 'cfa3-behavioral-adviser-client', 'A behaviorally modified asset allocation is designed to:', 'Maximize tracking error', 'Let clients deviate modestly from the optimum so they stay invested', 'Eliminate all risk', 'Remove the IPS', 'B', 'Small, deliberate deviations accommodate biases so clients can adhere through volatility.', 1),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-adviser-client-q03', 'cfa3-behavioral-adviser-client', 'Risk tolerance combines a client''s:', 'Ability and willingness to take risk', 'Income and age only', 'Beta and alpha', 'Duration and convexity', 'A', 'Risk tolerance integrates the ability (financial capacity) and willingness (psychological) to bear risk.', 2),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-adviser-client-q04', 'cfa3-behavioral-adviser-client', 'Emotional biases in a client should generally be:', 'Moderated aggressively', 'Accommodated (adapted to)', 'Ignored', 'Penalized', 'B', 'Deep emotional biases are hard to change, so the plan is built around them.', 3),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-adviser-client-q05', 'cfa3-behavioral-adviser-client', 'Framing information to avoid triggering loss aversion is an example of:', 'Manipulation', 'Behaviorally informed communication', 'Market timing', 'Tax planning', 'B', 'Thoughtful framing helps clients make better decisions and stay disciplined.', 4),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-adviser-client-q06', 'cfa3-behavioral-adviser-client', 'Larger allowed deviations from the optimum occur when the client''s standard-of-living risk tolerance is:', 'Low', 'High', 'Zero', 'Negative', 'B', 'Greater financial security (high SLR tolerance) permits accommodating biases with larger deviations.', 5),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-adviser-client-q07', 'cfa3-behavioral-adviser-client', 'Cognitive errors are best addressed through:', 'Accommodation', 'Education and better information', 'Higher leverage', 'Ignoring them', 'B', 'Cognitive errors respond to education, data, and disciplined process.', 6),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-adviser-client-q08', 'cfa3-behavioral-adviser-client', 'A strong adviser-client relationship tends to result in clients who:', 'Abandon the plan in downturns', 'Stay invested through volatility', 'Trade constantly', 'Avoid the IPS', 'B', 'Trust and understanding help clients adhere to the long-term plan during market stress.', 7),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-adviser-client-q09', 'cfa3-behavioral-adviser-client', 'A risk-tolerance questionnaire primarily helps identify a client''s:', 'Tax bracket', 'Willingness to take risk and biases', 'Beta', 'Duration', 'B', 'Questionnaires gauge willingness and surface biases, complementing the ability assessment.', 8),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-adviser-client-q10', 'cfa3-behavioral-adviser-client', 'When ability and willingness to take risk differ, the adviser should generally adopt the:', 'Higher of the two', 'Lower (more conservative) of the two, then educate', 'Average', 'Neither', 'B', 'Use the more conservative measure, then seek to reconcile via education where appropriate.', 9),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-adviser-client-q11', 'cfa3-behavioral-adviser-client', 'The IPS should ideally be:', 'Imposed by the adviser alone', 'Agreed jointly with the client', 'Kept secret from the client', 'Changed daily', 'B', 'Joint agreement on the IPS improves buy-in and adherence.', 10),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-adviser-client-q12', 'cfa3-behavioral-adviser-client', 'Smaller deviations from the rational optimum are appropriate when:', 'Biases are emotional and wealth is high', 'Biases are cognitive and the client''s security depends on staying near the optimum', 'The client is very wealthy', 'Markets are calm', 'B', 'Cognitive biases should be moderated, and limited financial security argues for staying near the optimum.', 11),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-analyst-biases-q01', 'cfa3-behavioral-analyst-biases', 'Analyst overconfidence often shows up as:', 'Confidence intervals that are too wide', 'Confidence intervals that are too narrow', 'No forecasts', 'Random forecasts', 'B', 'Overconfident analysts produce overly precise (too-narrow) forecast ranges.', 0),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-analyst-biases-q02', 'cfa3-behavioral-analyst-biases', 'Crediting good outcomes to skill and blaming bad ones on luck is:', 'Self-attribution bias', 'Anchoring', 'Availability', 'Loss aversion', 'A', 'Self-attribution reinforces overconfidence by mis-assigning credit and blame.', 1),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-analyst-biases-q03', 'cfa3-behavioral-analyst-biases', 'Over-weighting recent or vivid information reflects:', 'Availability/recency bias', 'Self-control', 'Endowment', 'Regret aversion', 'A', 'Availability/recency leads analysts to overemphasize easily recalled data.', 2),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-analyst-biases-q04', 'cfa3-behavioral-analyst-biases', 'Anchoring in forecasting means:', 'Ignoring all prior data', 'Sticking to an initial estimate and adjusting insufficiently', 'Using base rates', 'Seeking disconfirming evidence', 'B', 'Analysts anchor to prior estimates or management guidance and under-adjust to new information.', 3),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-analyst-biases-q05', 'cfa3-behavioral-analyst-biases', 'Base-rate neglect occurs when analysts:', 'Over-rely on representative but unrepresentative samples', 'Always use base rates', 'Ignore company data', 'Use disconfirming evidence', 'A', 'Representativeness can cause neglect of relevant base rates in favor of vivid specifics.', 4),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-analyst-biases-q06', 'cfa3-behavioral-analyst-biases', 'Confirmation bias leads analysts to:', 'Seek information supporting their thesis', 'Seek disconfirming evidence', 'Use base rates', 'Widen confidence intervals', 'A', 'Confirmation bias favors supportive evidence and discounts contradictory data.', 5),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-analyst-biases-q07', 'cfa3-behavioral-analyst-biases', 'Over-reliance on company management can cause:', 'Independent analysis', 'A halo effect and biased forecasts', 'Lower fees', 'Higher base rates', 'B', 'Trusting management uncritically (and the halo effect) biases analyst conclusions.', 6),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-analyst-biases-q08', 'cfa3-behavioral-analyst-biases', 'A useful remedy for analyst overconfidence is to:', 'Use base rates and track prior forecast accuracy', 'Narrow confidence intervals', 'Avoid disconfirming evidence', 'Match consensus', 'A', 'Calibrating against base rates and past records widens and improves forecast ranges.', 7),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-analyst-biases-q09', 'cfa3-behavioral-analyst-biases', 'The illusion of control bias leads analysts to:', 'Underestimate their influence', 'Overestimate their ability to influence outcomes', 'Use systematic processes', 'Avoid forecasting', 'B', 'Illusion of control inflates confidence in forecasts of outcomes one cannot actually control.', 8),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-analyst-biases-q10', 'cfa3-behavioral-analyst-biases', 'The gambler''s fallacy is a form of:', 'Misapplied representativeness', 'Loss aversion', 'Endowment bias', 'Regret aversion', 'A', 'Expecting reversals in independent events (e.g., ''due'' for a change) misapplies representativeness.', 9),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-analyst-biases-q11', 'cfa3-behavioral-analyst-biases', 'Conducting post-mortems on past forecasts helps reduce bias by:', 'Encouraging self-attribution', 'Providing objective feedback on accuracy', 'Hiding errors', 'Narrowing intervals', 'B', 'Honest reviews of prior calls counter self-attribution and improve calibration.', 10),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-analyst-biases-q12', 'cfa3-behavioral-analyst-biases', 'Pre-committing to a structured, rule-based process primarily mitigates:', 'Market risk', 'Cognitive biases in analysis', 'Currency risk', 'Liquidity risk', 'B', 'Systematic processes and disconfirming checks reduce discretionary cognitive biases.', 11),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-committee-bias-q01', 'cfa3-behavioral-committee-bias', 'Groupthink in an investment committee refers to:', 'Diverse, independent views', 'Members suppressing dissent to conform', 'Anonymous voting', 'A devil''s advocate', 'B', 'Groupthink occurs when conformity pressure suppresses dissent and critical evaluation.', 0),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-committee-bias-q02', 'cfa3-behavioral-committee-bias', 'A useful remedy for committee bias is to:', 'Have the leader state their view first', 'Appoint a devil''s advocate and gather anonymous input', 'Reduce diversity', 'Discourage dissent', 'B', 'Devil''s advocates, anonymity, and diverse membership counter social-proof pressures.', 1),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-committee-bias-q03', 'cfa3-behavioral-committee-bias', 'Social proof in committees describes members:', 'Forming independent opinions', 'Deferring to the group or a dominant voice', 'Voting anonymously', 'Ignoring the chair', 'B', 'Social proof leads members to follow the group/dominant member rather than reason independently.', 2),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-committee-bias-q04', 'cfa3-behavioral-committee-bias', 'Herding among investors can contribute to:', 'Efficient pricing', 'Bubbles and crashes', 'Lower volatility', 'Risk-free arbitrage', 'B', 'Herding amplifies price moves, helping inflate bubbles and deepen crashes.', 3),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-committee-bias-q05', 'cfa3-behavioral-committee-bias', 'A committee leader can reduce bias by:', 'Stating their opinion before others', 'Soliciting dissent before sharing their own view', 'Discouraging debate', 'Voting publicly first', 'B', 'Eliciting independent input before anchoring on the leader''s view improves decisions.', 4),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-committee-bias-q06', 'cfa3-behavioral-committee-bias', 'The disposition effect contributes to which market phenomenon?', 'Momentum/under-reaction', 'Instant efficiency', 'Put-call parity', 'Arbitrage', 'A', 'Selling winners and holding losers slows price adjustment, producing momentum.', 5),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-committee-bias-q07', 'cfa3-behavioral-committee-bias', 'Home bias in portfolios is an example of:', 'Optimal diversification', 'Familiarity bias reducing diversification', 'Loss aversion only', 'Arbitrage', 'B', 'Overweighting domestic assets (familiarity) reduces diversification.', 6),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-committee-bias-q08', 'cfa3-behavioral-committee-bias', 'Diversity of committee membership helps because it:', 'Increases groupthink', 'Brings independent perspectives that challenge consensus', 'Slows all decisions to a halt', 'Removes accountability', 'B', 'Diverse, independent members reduce correlated errors and challenge premature consensus.', 7),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-committee-bias-q09', 'cfa3-behavioral-committee-bias', 'Trend-chasing fund flows tend to:', 'Stabilize prices', 'Amplify market cycles', 'Eliminate volatility', 'Create arbitrage', 'B', 'Money flowing toward recent winners reinforces momentum and amplifies cycles.', 8),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-committee-bias-q10', 'cfa3-behavioral-committee-bias', 'Anonymous polling of committee members helps by:', 'Increasing conformity', 'Reducing social-proof pressure and revealing true views', 'Hiding the decision', 'Eliminating analysis', 'B', 'Anonymity lets members express genuine opinions free of conformity pressure.', 9),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-committee-bias-q11', 'cfa3-behavioral-committee-bias', 'Whether a behavioral anomaly persists depends on its:', 'Capacity, costs, and how widely it is known', 'Coupon', 'Duration', 'Beta only', 'A', 'Limited capacity, high costs, and broad awareness erode an anomaly''s exploitability.', 10),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-committee-bias-q12', 'cfa3-behavioral-committee-bias', 'The halo effect in investing is the tendency to assume that a good company is:', 'A poor investment', 'Automatically a good stock', 'Risk-free', 'Overvalued always', 'B', 'The halo effect conflates a good company with a good investment, ignoring valuation.', 11),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-economic-analysis-q01', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-economic-analysis', 'Equities and credit typically perform best in which business-cycle phase?', 'Deep contraction', 'Early recovery/expansion', 'Peak only', 'Never', 'B', 'Risk assets like equities and credit tend to outperform early in the recovery/expansion.', 0),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-economic-analysis-q02', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-economic-analysis', 'An inverted yield curve is often interpreted as a signal of:', 'Strong growth ahead', 'An impending recession', 'Rising inflation only', 'A bull market', 'B', 'Curve inversion (short rates above long) has historically preceded recessions.', 1),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-economic-analysis-q03', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-economic-analysis', 'The Taylor rule estimates the appropriate:', 'Equity risk premium', 'Central-bank policy rate', 'Credit spread', 'Dividend yield', 'B', 'The Taylor rule sets a policy rate from the neutral rate plus inflation and output gaps.', 2),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-economic-analysis-q04', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-economic-analysis', 'Long-run trend economic growth is driven by labor, capital and:', 'Total factor productivity', 'Inflation', 'Currency moves', 'Dividends', 'A', 'Trend growth depends on labor and capital inputs plus productivity (TFP).', 3),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-economic-analysis-q05', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-economic-analysis', 'Using a sample dominated by a bull market to forecast long-run returns reflects:', 'Time-period bias', 'Survivorship bias', 'Anchoring', 'No bias', 'A', 'Over-reflecting a particular window introduces time-period bias.', 4),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-economic-analysis-q06', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-economic-analysis', 'Rising inflation generally:', 'Helps nominal bonds', 'Hurts nominal bonds (and stocks at high levels)', 'Has no effect on assets', 'Guarantees equity gains', 'B', 'Higher inflation raises nominal yields, hurting bonds; very high inflation also pressures equities.', 5),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-economic-analysis-q07', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-economic-analysis', 'Leading indicators (e.g., new orders, permits) are useful because they:', 'Confirm the past', 'Tend to change ahead of the economy', 'Lag the cycle', 'Measure inflation only', 'B', 'Leading indicators move before the broader economy, aiding cyclical forecasting.', 6),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-economic-analysis-q08', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-economic-analysis', 'Distinguishing secular trends from cyclical noise matters because:', 'They have identical implications', 'Long-run forecasts should reflect durable trends, not temporary cycles', 'Cycles never matter', 'Trends are irrelevant', 'B', 'Capital market expectations should separate persistent (secular) drivers from transient cyclical effects.', 7),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-economic-analysis-q09', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-economic-analysis', 'A common pitfall when using historical data for CME is:', 'Using stationary data', 'Relying on non-stationary data and ignoring regime shifts', 'Adjusting for biases', 'Cross-checking models', 'B', 'Non-stationary data and structural breaks undermine naive historical extrapolation.', 8),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-economic-analysis-q10', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-economic-analysis', 'Late in an expansion, a typical positioning shift is toward:', 'More cyclical, high-beta exposure', 'More defensive assets and quality', 'Maximum leverage', 'Only small caps', 'B', 'As expansion matures, investors often rotate to defensives and higher-quality assets.', 9),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-economic-analysis-q11', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-economic-analysis', 'Data mining in forecasting refers to:', 'Careful theory-based modeling', 'Searching data until a spurious relationship fits', 'Using base rates', 'Cross-validation', 'B', 'Data mining finds chance relationships that fail out of sample.', 10),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-economic-analysis-q12', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-economic-analysis', 'Anchoring on the status quo when forecasting is an example of a:', 'Psychological/behavioral bias in CME', 'Risk premium', 'Data source', 'Hedging tool', 'A', 'Status-quo/anchoring biases distort capital market expectations and should be guarded against.', 11),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-fixed-income-q01', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-fixed-income', 'The building-block expected return for a bond starts with the real risk-free rate plus:', 'Expected inflation and a term premium', 'The dividend yield', 'Beta', 'The equity risk premium', 'A', 'Fixed-income building blocks add expected inflation, term, credit, and liquidity premia to the real rate.', 0),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-fixed-income-q02', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-fixed-income', 'Credit spreads are typically ____ over the business cycle.', 'Counter-cyclical (widen in booms)', 'Pro-cyclical (widen in recessions)', 'Constant', 'Random', 'B', 'Spreads widen as default risk rises in downturns and narrow in expansions.', 1),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-fixed-income-q03', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-fixed-income', 'For a credit bond, the spread compensates for expected loss plus a:', 'Risk and liquidity premium', 'Dividend', 'Tax shield', 'Coupon', 'A', 'Spread = expected loss + risk/uncertainty premium + liquidity premium.', 2),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-fixed-income-q04', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-fixed-income', 'The approximate return impact of a spread change is:', '−(spread duration × Δspread)', '+coupon', 'duration × convexity', 'recovery × notional', 'A', 'Holding rates constant, credit return ≈ carry/roll − spread duration × Δspread − expected loss.', 3),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-fixed-income-q05', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-fixed-income', 'For a government bond held to maturity, the base return estimate is its:', 'Yield to maturity', 'Dividend yield', 'Beta', 'Cap rate', 'A', 'The YTM (with reinvestment assumptions) anchors the expected return for a held-to-maturity bond.', 4),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-fixed-income-q06', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-fixed-income', 'Forward rates serve as the ____ for active fixed-income positioning.', 'Guaranteed outcome', 'Break-even', 'Coupon', 'Dividend', 'B', 'Forwards are break-even rates; active return requires realized rates to differ from them.', 5),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-fixed-income-q07', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-fixed-income', 'Emerging-market debt forecasts should additionally incorporate:', 'Country/political and currency risk', 'Only the risk-free rate', 'Dividend growth', 'Cap rates', 'A', 'EM debt carries extra country, political, and currency considerations beyond developed-market premia.', 6),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-fixed-income-q08', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-fixed-income', 'The term premium compensates investors for:', 'Default risk', 'Uncertainty over future short rates at longer horizons', 'Currency risk', 'Equity risk', 'B', 'Longer maturities bear interest-rate uncertainty, earning a term premium.', 7),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-fixed-income-q09', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-fixed-income', 'Rolldown return arises when, on an upward-sloping stable curve, a bond''s yield:', 'Rises as it ages', 'Falls as it ages toward shorter maturities', 'Stays constant', 'Becomes negative', 'B', 'On a stable upward-sloping curve, aging lowers a bond''s yield, producing price gains (rolldown).', 8),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-fixed-income-q10', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-fixed-income', 'During a recession, a fixed-income forecaster would expect credit spreads to:', 'Narrow', 'Widen', 'Stay flat', 'Vanish', 'B', 'Rising default risk widens spreads in recessions, hurting credit returns.', 9),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-fixed-income-q11', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-fixed-income', 'Loss given default in expected-loss estimates equals:', '1 − recovery rate', 'The coupon', 'Duration', 'The spread', 'A', 'Expected loss = PD × LGD, with LGD = 1 − recovery rate.', 10),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-fixed-income-q12', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-fixed-income', 'A key reinvestment consideration in bond return forecasts is that realized return equals YTM only if coupons are reinvested at:', 'The YTM', 'The risk-free rate', 'Zero', 'The dividend yield', 'A', 'Realized return matches YTM only under reinvestment at the YTM and holding to maturity.', 11),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-equity-q01', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-equity', 'The Grinold-Kroner model expresses expected equity return as income, nominal earnings growth and:', 'Repricing (change in P/E)', 'The risk-free rate', 'Beta', 'Tracking error', 'A', 'E[R] ≈ (D/P − ΔS) + (i + g) + ΔPE; the last term is repricing.', 0),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-equity-q02', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-equity', 'In Grinold-Kroner, net share buybacks (negative ΔS):', 'Lower expected return', 'Raise expected per-share return', 'Have no effect', 'Remove the dividend', 'B', 'Subtracting a negative ΔS adds to return — buybacks reduce share count, raising per-share return.', 1),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-equity-q03', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-equity', 'Over the long run, the repricing term (ΔP/E) is generally assumed to:', 'Dominate returns', 'Average near zero', 'Equal the dividend yield', 'Be negative always', 'B', 'Valuation change tends to average around zero over long horizons, leaving income and growth as drivers.', 2),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-equity-q04', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-equity', 'The Singer-Terhaar approach blends fully integrated and fully segmented risk premia based on the degree of:', 'Market integration', 'Inflation', 'Leverage', 'Liquidity only', 'A', 'Singer-Terhaar weights the two premia by how integrated the market is with global markets.', 3),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-equity-q05', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-equity', 'A forward-looking estimate of the equity risk premium can be derived from a:', 'DDM-implied (Gordon) approach', 'Coupon', 'Cap rate', 'Duration', 'A', 'The DDM/Gordon model can imply the ERP from current prices, dividends, and growth.', 4),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-equity-q06', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-equity', 'Long-run equity earnings growth is often anchored to:', 'Trend GDP growth', 'The dividend date', 'The risk-free rate only', 'Beta', 'A', 'Aggregate earnings growth tends to track trend nominal GDP growth, adjusted for margins/shares.', 5),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-equity-q07', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-equity', 'Using raw historical average returns to forecast the ERP risks:', 'Survivorship and time-period bias', 'No bias', 'Understating returns always', 'Removing risk', 'A', 'Historical estimates must be adjusted for survivorship and the specific sample period.', 6),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-equity-q08', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-equity', 'In Singer-Terhaar, a market''s risk premium when fully integrated equals ρ_{i,M} × σ_i ×:', 'The global market Sharpe ratio', 'The dividend yield', 'Beta of bonds', 'The coupon', 'A', 'RP_i = ρ_{i,M}·σ_i·(Sharpe ratio of the global market) under full integration.', 7),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-equity-q09', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-equity', 'A segmented market generally has a ____ risk premium than the same market when integrated.', 'Lower', 'Higher', 'Identical', 'Zero', 'B', 'Segmentation reduces diversification with the world, so investors demand a higher premium.', 8),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-equity-q10', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-equity', 'Mean reversion in profit margins implies analysts should be cautious about:', 'Extrapolating recent high margins indefinitely', 'Using base rates', 'Diversifying', 'Hedging currency', 'A', 'Margins tend to revert, so projecting current peak margins forever overstates growth.', 9),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-equity-q11', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-equity', 'Cross-checking multiple equity-return models is recommended because:', 'Each has different assumptions/weaknesses', 'They always agree', 'Only one model exists', 'It removes all uncertainty', 'A', 'Triangulating across models mitigates the limitations of any single approach.', 10),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-equity-q12', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-equity', 'The build-up method estimates the cost of equity as the risk-free rate plus:', 'An equity risk premium (and other premia)', 'The coupon', 'The cap rate', 'Duration', 'A', 'Build-up adds an equity risk premium (and size/specific premia) to R_f to get the required equity return.', 11),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-real-estate-q01', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-real-estate', 'A simple expected return for direct real estate is approximately the cap rate plus:', 'NOI growth (less any cap-rate expansion)', 'The dividend yield', 'Beta', 'The coupon', 'A', 'Expected return ≈ income (cap rate) + NOI growth ± valuation (cap-rate) change.', 0),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-real-estate-q02', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-real-estate', 'The cap rate is approximately the discount rate minus the:', 'Growth rate of NOI', 'Inflation rate', 'Risk-free rate', 'Vacancy rate', 'A', 'Like Gordon growth, cap rate ≈ required return − expected NOI growth.', 1),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-real-estate-q03', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-real-estate', 'Real estate exhibits a blend of:', 'Bond-like income and equity-like cyclicality', 'Pure risk-free behavior', 'Only equity behavior', 'Only currency exposure', 'A', 'Lease income is bond-like; property values are cyclical (equity-like).', 2),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-real-estate-q04', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-real-estate', 'Appraisal-based real-estate returns tend to:', 'Overstate volatility', 'Understate volatility and correlation (smoothing)', 'Be perfectly accurate', 'Be risk-free', 'B', 'Infrequent appraisals smooth returns, understating true volatility and correlation.', 3),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-real-estate-q05', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-real-estate', 'A commodity futures total return includes price return, collateral yield and:', 'Roll return', 'Dividend yield', 'Coupon', 'Cap rate', 'A', 'Commodity return = price + roll + collateral; the roll depends on the futures-curve shape.', 4),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-real-estate-q06', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-real-estate', 'Backwardation in a commodity curve produces a roll return that is:', 'Positive', 'Negative', 'Zero', 'Undefined', 'A', 'Rolling up toward a higher spot in backwardation yields a positive roll return.', 5),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-real-estate-q07', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-real-estate', 'Private-equity return forecasts typically build in a(n):', 'Illiquidity premium over public comparables', 'Dividend yield', 'Risk-free discount', 'Negative premium', 'A', 'PE expected returns add an illiquidity premium and model vintage/J-curve effects.', 6),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-real-estate-q08', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-real-estate', 'Before using alternative-asset returns in optimization, analysts should:', 'Use them raw', 'Unsmooth them to restore realistic risk/correlation', 'Ignore correlations', 'Assume normality', 'B', 'Unsmoothing corrects appraisal lag so MVO doesn''t over-allocate to alternatives.', 7),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-real-estate-q09', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-real-estate', 'Commodities are commonly forecast to serve as a(n):', 'Inflation hedge', 'Duration hedge', 'Risk-free asset', 'Dividend source', 'A', 'Commodities tend to rise with unexpected inflation, providing an inflation hedge.', 8),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-real-estate-q10', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-real-estate', 'Hedge-fund return assumptions must account for returns that are:', 'Perfectly normal', 'Non-normal (skewed, fat-tailed) and fee-laden', 'Risk-free', 'Coupon-bearing', 'B', 'Hedge-fund returns are non-normal with high fees, requiring careful, downside-aware assumptions.', 9),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-real-estate-q11', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-real-estate', 'Rising cap rates (cap-rate expansion), all else equal, ____ real estate values.', 'Raise', 'Lower', 'Do not affect', 'Double', 'B', 'Higher cap rates reduce property values (value = NOI / cap rate), a drag on returns.', 10),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-real-estate-q12', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-real-estate', 'Contango in a commodity curve generates a roll return that is:', 'Positive', 'Negative', 'Zero', 'Equal to carry', 'B', 'Rolling into higher-priced deferred contracts in contango produces a negative roll yield.', 11),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-exchange-rates-q01', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-exchange-rates', 'Purchasing power parity is best regarded as a:', 'Reliable short-run predictor', 'Long-run anchor that fails short-term', 'Daily trading signal', 'Risk-free arbitrage', 'B', 'PPP holds loosely over long horizons but predicts poorly in the short run.', 0),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-exchange-rates-q02', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-exchange-rates', 'Under the balance-of-payments view, persistent current-account deficits tend to:', 'Strengthen the currency', 'Pressure the currency lower over time', 'Have no effect', 'Eliminate inflation', 'B', 'Sustained deficits imply net selling pressure, though capital flows can offset short-term.', 1),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-exchange-rates-q03', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-exchange-rates', 'In risk-off (stress) periods, which currencies typically appreciate?', 'High-yield emerging currencies', 'Safe havens (USD, JPY, CHF)', 'Commodity currencies', 'All currencies equally', 'B', 'Safe-haven currencies rally as investors seek safety during stress.', 2),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-exchange-rates-q04', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-exchange-rates', 'Carry trades profit when:', 'Uncovered interest parity holds exactly', 'High-yield currencies fail to depreciate as UIRP predicts', 'Covered parity is violated', 'Inflation is identical', 'B', 'Carry earns the rate differential when high-yield currencies don''t depreciate enough — a UIRP failure.', 3),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-exchange-rates-q05', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-exchange-rates', 'Carry-trade returns are characterized by:', 'Symmetric, normal payoffs', 'Negative skew (occasional large losses)', 'Zero risk', 'Guaranteed gains', 'B', 'Carry trades earn steady gains punctuated by sharp losses — negative skew/crash risk.', 4),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-exchange-rates-q06', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-exchange-rates', 'Short-run exchange-rate moves are most influenced by:', 'Trade flows', 'Capital flows and risk sentiment', 'Long-run PPP', 'Dividends', 'B', 'Capital flows chasing returns and shifts in risk sentiment dominate short-run FX.', 5),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-exchange-rates-q07', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-exchange-rates', 'Currency forecasts feed portfolio decisions mainly through the:', 'Hedge ratio for foreign assets', 'Coupon', 'Cap rate', 'Duration', 'A', 'Expected currency moves inform how much foreign-asset FX exposure to hedge.', 6),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-exchange-rates-q08', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-exchange-rates', 'Relative PPP predicts the higher-inflation country''s currency will:', 'Appreciate', 'Depreciate by the inflation differential', 'Stay constant', 'Track interest rates', 'B', 'Under relative PPP, higher inflation leads to currency depreciation by the differential.', 7),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-exchange-rates-q09', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-exchange-rates', 'Central-bank intervention is most effective when backed by:', 'No reserves', 'Ample reserves and credibility', 'Higher dividends', 'A buyback', 'B', 'Reserves and credibility determine whether intervention can durably move the currency.', 8),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-exchange-rates-q10', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-exchange-rates', 'A practical caution in FX forecasting is that:', 'Forecasts are highly certain', 'Forecast uncertainty is high; combine fundamentals with sentiment', 'Only PPP matters', 'Currencies never move', 'B', 'FX is notoriously hard to forecast; blend fundamentals, technicals/sentiment, and acknowledge uncertainty.', 9),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-exchange-rates-q11', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-exchange-rates', 'The international Fisher effect links nominal interest-rate differentials to differences in expected:', 'Inflation', 'Dividends', 'GDP only', 'Beta', 'A', 'It assumes equal real rates, so nominal differentials reflect expected inflation differences.', 10),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-exchange-rates-q12', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-exchange-rates', 'Monetary models tie long-run currency value to relative money growth and:', 'Inflation', 'Dividends', 'Beta', 'Cap rates', 'A', 'Monetary approaches emphasize relative money supply growth and inflation as long-run FX drivers.', 11),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-approaches-q01', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-approaches', 'Mean-variance optimization is often called an ''error maximizer'' because it is highly sensitive to:', 'Estimation error in expected returns', 'The risk-free rate only', 'The number of assets', 'Tax rates', 'A', 'MVO concentrates weights in high-(mis)estimated-return assets, magnifying input errors.', 0),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-approaches-q02', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-approaches', 'Risk parity allocates so that each asset contributes:', 'Equal capital', 'Equal risk', 'Equal return', 'Zero risk', 'B', 'Risk parity equalizes each asset''s contribution to total portfolio risk, often using leverage.', 1),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-approaches-q03', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-approaches', 'The Black-Litterman model improves MVO by:', 'Ignoring equilibrium', 'Blending equilibrium (reverse-optimized) returns with investor views', 'Removing constraints', 'Using only history', 'B', 'Black-Litterman tilts equilibrium returns toward the investor''s views, stabilizing weights.', 2),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-approaches-q04', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-approaches', 'Resampling and adding constraints help MVO by:', 'Increasing concentration', 'Reducing concentrated, unstable weights', 'Removing diversification', 'Raising input error', 'B', 'These remedies temper MVO''s tendency to produce extreme, input-sensitive allocations.', 3),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-approaches-q05', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-approaches', 'Risk budgeting is the process of:', 'Allocating capital equally', 'Allocating the portfolio''s risk across positions/factors', 'Minimizing trades', 'Setting fees', 'B', 'Risk budgeting decides how much risk each position or factor may contribute.', 4),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-approaches-q06', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-approaches', 'Reverse optimization derives:', 'Expected returns implied by market-cap weights', 'The risk-free rate', 'Duration', 'Cap rates', 'A', 'Reverse optimization backs out the returns implied by equilibrium (market) weights — a Black-Litterman input.', 5),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-approaches-q07', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-approaches', 'Factor-based asset allocation allocates to:', 'Asset-class labels only', 'Rewarded factors (value, momentum, quality, carry)', 'Cash only', 'A single stock', 'B', 'Factor investing targets underlying return drivers rather than traditional asset-class buckets.', 6),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-approaches-q08', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-approaches', 'The MVO objective function maximizes expected return minus:', '½ × risk-aversion × variance', 'The dividend yield', 'Duration', 'The coupon', 'A', 'Utility = E[R] − ½λσ², trading off return against variance scaled by risk aversion.', 7),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-approaches-q09', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-approaches', 'A minimum-variance portfolio is constructed to:', 'Maximize expected return', 'Minimize total portfolio variance', 'Maximize beta', 'Equalize capital', 'B', 'It seeks the lowest-variance combination of assets, ignoring expected-return estimates.', 8),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-approaches-q10', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-approaches', 'Risk parity portfolios often use leverage to:', 'Reduce return', 'Raise the expected return of a low-risk, well-diversified mix', 'Eliminate all risk', 'Avoid bonds', 'B', 'Because risk parity overweights low-risk assets, leverage is used to reach a target return.', 9),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-approaches-q11', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-approaches', 'The choice among asset-only, liability-relative and goals-based approaches depends mainly on:', 'The investor''s situation and objectives', 'The risk-free rate', 'The auditor', 'Beta', 'A', 'The appropriate framework follows from whether liabilities or specific goals must be funded.', 10),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-approaches-q12', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-approaches', 'Despite its flaws, MVO with sensible constraints remains:', 'Never used', 'A widely used workhorse for asset allocation', 'Risk-free', 'Identical to risk parity', 'B', 'Constrained/resampled MVO is still the practical baseline for strategic allocation.', 11),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-liability-relative-q01', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-liability-relative', 'Liability-relative asset allocation is most appropriate for:', 'A young individual with no liabilities', 'Pensions and insurers with defined obligations', 'A day trader', 'A cash-only saver', 'B', 'Institutions with liabilities to fund manage assets relative to those liabilities.', 0),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-liability-relative-q02', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-liability-relative', 'Surplus optimization maximizes expected surplus return per unit of:', 'Surplus volatility', 'Asset volatility only', 'Coupon', 'Beta', 'A', 'Surplus optimization is mean-variance applied to the surplus (assets − liabilities).', 1),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-liability-relative-q03', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-liability-relative', 'The hedging/return-seeking split divides the portfolio into a liability-hedging portfolio and a:', 'Cash buffer only', 'Return-seeking (growth) portfolio', 'Single bond', 'Currency overlay only', 'B', 'One sleeve hedges liabilities (duration-matched bonds); the other seeks return (growth assets).', 2),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-liability-relative-q04', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-liability-relative', 'The funded ratio equals:', 'Assets / PV(liabilities)', 'Liabilities / assets', 'Assets − coupon', 'PV(liabilities) × beta', 'A', 'Funded ratio = assets divided by the present value of liabilities; below 1 is a deficit.', 3),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-liability-relative-q05', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-liability-relative', 'Because pension liabilities are bond-like, the dominant risk to hedge is:', 'Equity beta', 'Interest-rate (duration) risk', 'Currency risk only', 'Liquidity only', 'B', 'Liability values move with interest rates, so duration matching is central to hedging.', 4),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-liability-relative-q06', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-liability-relative', 'A better-funded plan can generally:', 'Take more risk indefinitely', 'De-risk (glide path) toward more hedging assets', 'Ignore liabilities', 'Stop investing', 'B', 'As funded status improves, plans often de-risk toward liability-hedging assets to lock in gains.', 5),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-liability-relative-q07', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-liability-relative', 'Inflation-linked liabilities are best hedged with:', 'Nominal bonds', 'Inflation-linked bonds (linkers)', 'Equities only', 'Cash', 'B', 'Linkers hedge inflation-sensitive liabilities better than nominal bonds.', 6),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-liability-relative-q08', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-liability-relative', 'Integrated asset-liability management (ALM) jointly models:', 'Only assets', 'Both assets and liabilities', 'Only liabilities', 'Neither', 'B', 'ALM optimizes considering both sides of the balance sheet together.', 7),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-liability-relative-q09', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-liability-relative', 'Surplus volatility primarily reflects the mismatch between asset and liability:', 'Coupons', 'Sensitivities (e.g., duration)', 'Auditors', 'Tickers', 'B', 'Differences in interest-rate (and other) sensitivities drive surplus volatility.', 8),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-liability-relative-q10', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-liability-relative', 'A plan that is underfunded generally has:', 'Lower need for returns', 'Pressure to take some return-seeking risk (subject to constraints)', 'No liabilities', 'A surplus', 'B', 'Deficits often require growth assets to close the gap, balanced against funding risk.', 9),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-liability-relative-q11', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-liability-relative', 'A key risk in liability-relative allocation is:', 'Surplus volatility and non-parallel curve shifts', 'Dividend risk only', 'No risk', 'Style drift only', 'A', 'Surplus can swing from rate moves (including twists), plus longevity and contribution risks.', 10),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-liability-relative-q12', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-liability-relative', 'The liability-hedging portfolio is typically composed of:', 'High-growth equities', 'Duration-matched (often high-quality) bonds', 'Commodities', 'Cash only', 'B', 'Hedging assets are bonds matched to the liabilities'' interest-rate sensitivity.', 11),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-goals-based-q01', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-goals-based', 'Goals-based asset allocation is most associated with:', 'Pension funds', 'Individual investors', 'Central banks', 'Insurers only', 'B', 'Goals-based investing organizes an individual''s wealth around specific personal goals.', 0),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-goals-based-q02', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-goals-based', 'Each goal is assigned a sub-portfolio based on its time horizon and:', 'Required probability of success', 'The auditor', 'Beta', 'The coupon', 'A', 'Goals are funded by sub-portfolios sized to a horizon and a required success probability.', 1),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-goals-based-q03', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-goals-based', 'High-priority, near-term goals are typically funded with:', 'Riskier assets', 'Safer (lower-risk) sub-portfolios', 'Leverage', 'Only equities', 'B', 'Critical, soon-due goals get conservative allocations to ensure they are met.', 2),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-goals-based-q04', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-goals-based', 'Goals-based investing leverages which behavioral tendency constructively?', 'Mental accounting', 'Loss aversion only', 'Anchoring', 'Herding', 'A', 'It uses clients'' natural mental accounting (separate buckets per goal) to improve discipline.', 3),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-goals-based-q05', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-goals-based', 'A drawback of goals-based allocation is that it can be ____ at the total-portfolio level.', 'Optimal', 'Sub-optimal (ignores cross-goal diversification)', 'Risk-free', 'Tax-free', 'B', 'Separating goals can miss diversification across the aggregate, a mild efficiency cost.', 4),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-goals-based-q06', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-goals-based', 'Aspirational, long-horizon goals can be funded with:', 'Cash only', 'Riskier, higher-expected-return assets', 'No assets', 'Short bills only', 'B', 'Lower-priority, distant goals can bear more risk for higher expected return.', 5),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-goals-based-q07', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-goals-based', 'A module/overlay approach uses standardized:', 'Risk modules (sub-portfolios at set risk levels)', 'Single stocks', 'Currencies', 'Auditors', 'A', 'Goals are mapped to pre-built risk modules; an overlay manages aggregate exposures/taxes.', 6),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-goals-based-q08', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-goals-based', 'The ''minimum expectations'' return for a goal is the return needed to achieve it at a:', 'Specified high confidence level', 'Zero probability', 'Random level', 'Negative level', 'A', 'Each goal targets a return achievable with its required (often high) probability of success.', 7),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-goals-based-q09', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-goals-based', 'A benefit of goals-based investing is improved:', 'Client adherence/behavior', 'Market efficiency', 'Tax rates', 'Beta', 'A', 'Tying portfolios to tangible goals helps clients stay disciplined through volatility.', 8),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-goals-based-q10', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-goals-based', 'Goals-based sub-portfolios aggregate into:', 'An overall asset allocation', 'A single bond', 'Cash only', 'No allocation', 'A', 'The combined sub-portfolios form the investor''s total allocation.', 9),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-goals-based-q11', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-goals-based', 'Compared with traditional MVO, goals-based allocation is generally:', 'Simpler at the total level', 'More intuitive for clients but potentially less total-portfolio efficient', 'Always more efficient', 'Risk-free', 'B', 'It improves client understanding/behavior at a modest cost to aggregate efficiency.', 10),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-goals-based-q12', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-goals-based', 'Lower-priority goals in a goals-based plan are typically allocated:', 'The safest assets', 'More risk than high-priority goals', 'Only cash', 'No assets', 'B', 'Risk is scaled by priority — less critical goals can take more risk.', 11),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-constraints-q01', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-constraints', 'A very small asset owner may be constrained because it:', 'Faces capacity limits in large strategies', 'Cannot access some alternatives (minimums/scale)', 'Has too much liquidity', 'Has no goals', 'B', 'Small portfolios may be unable to meet minimums or diversify into certain alternatives.', 0),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-constraints-q02', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-constraints', 'Illiquid assets like private equity complicate allocation because they:', 'Can be rebalanced freely', 'Cannot be rebalanced easily and require commitment pacing', 'Have daily liquidity', 'Have no risk', 'B', 'Illiquidity and capital calls require pacing and a liquidity reserve, limiting rebalancing.', 1),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-constraints-q03', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-constraints', 'Asset location places tax-inefficient assets in:', 'Taxable accounts', 'Tax-advantaged (sheltered) accounts', 'Cash', 'Foreign accounts', 'B', 'Tax-inefficient assets (e.g., high-turnover, bonds) belong in sheltered accounts to reduce tax drag.', 2),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-constraints-q04', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-constraints', 'After-tax asset allocation matters because taxes change an asset''s:', 'Ticker', 'Effective return and risk', 'Auditor', 'Coupon date', 'B', 'Taxes reduce returns and alter risk, so optimization should use after-tax inputs.', 3),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-constraints-q05', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-constraints', 'Rebalancing corridors for taxable accounts are typically:', 'Narrower', 'Wider (to limit taxable trades)', 'Zero', 'Irrelevant', 'B', 'Tax costs of trading justify wider rebalancing bands in taxable accounts.', 4),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-constraints-q06', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-constraints', 'Commitment pacing for private funds manages:', 'Currency risk only', 'Liquidity and the timing/J-curve of capital deployment', 'Beta', 'Coupons', 'B', 'Pacing commitments across vintages smooths liquidity needs and the J-curve.', 5),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-constraints-q07', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-constraints', 'Tax-loss harvesting improves after-tax results by:', 'Realizing losses to offset gains', 'Increasing turnover for its own sake', 'Avoiding equities', 'Deferring dividends', 'A', 'Harvesting losses offsets taxable gains, lowering the current tax bill.', 6),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-constraints-q08', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-constraints', 'A liquidity reserve is held primarily to:', 'Maximize return', 'Meet near-term needs and capital calls without forced selling', 'Increase leverage', 'Avoid taxes', 'B', 'Cash/liquid assets cover spending and capital calls, avoiding forced sales of illiquid assets.', 7),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-constraints-q09', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-constraints', 'ESG or legal restrictions are examples of:', 'Return objectives', 'Constraints on the allocation', 'Risk-free assets', 'Benchmarks', 'B', 'Mandated exclusions/limits act as constraints shaping the feasible allocation.', 8),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-constraints-q10', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-constraints', 'A glide path is an example of a(n):', 'Automatic adjustment to allocation over time', 'Single trade', 'Tax form', 'Benchmark', 'A', 'Glide paths systematically shift the allocation (e.g., de-risking) as circumstances change.', 9),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-constraints-q11', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-constraints', 'A large asset owner may face the constraint of:', 'Too few opportunities to deploy capital (capacity)', 'Inability to access any markets', 'No liquidity ever', 'No governance', 'A', 'Very large portfolios can struggle to deploy capital in capacity-limited strategies without moving prices.', 10),
  ('cfa3-asset-allocation-constraints-q12', 'cfa3-asset-allocation-constraints', 'Tactical asset allocation shifts respond to:', 'Permanent policy', 'Short-run changes in capital market expectations', 'The auditor', 'Share count', 'B', 'TAA temporarily deviates from policy to exploit changing near-term opportunities.', 11),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-indexing-q01', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-indexing', 'Full replication of a bond index is usually impractical because the index has:', 'Too few bonds', 'Thousands of issues, many illiquid', 'No duration', 'Only government bonds', 'B', 'Bond indices contain many illiquid issues with changing composition, making full replication infeasible.', 0),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-indexing-q02', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-indexing', 'Stratified sampling (cell matching) replicates an index by:', 'Holding every bond', 'Matching cell weights defined by risk factors like duration and sector', 'Buying only the largest bond', 'Ignoring credit quality', 'B', 'Cells are formed by key risk dimensions; the portfolio matches each cell''s index weight.', 1),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-indexing-q03', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-indexing', 'Enhanced indexing aims to:', 'Take large active bets', 'Match index risk factors while making small value-adding bets at low tracking error', 'Maximize tracking error', 'Ignore the benchmark', 'B', 'Enhanced indexing keeps factor exposures near the index and seeks modest alpha with low active risk.', 2),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-indexing-q04', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-indexing', 'To control interest-rate tracking error, the portfolio should match the benchmark''s:', 'Coupon', 'Duration and key-rate durations', 'Number of issuers only', 'Dividend yield', 'B', 'Matching duration and key-rate durations minimizes interest-rate tracking error versus the index.', 3),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-indexing-q05', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-indexing', 'A source of tracking error in a sampled bond index portfolio is:', 'Perfect replication', 'Mismatches in sector, quality, and convexity exposures', 'Zero transaction costs', 'Holding the entire index', 'B', 'Sampling leaves residual mismatches (sector, quality, convexity) that create tracking error.', 4),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-indexing-q06', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-indexing', 'Bond ETFs and total-return swaps are used for index exposure because they are:', 'Illiquid and costly', 'Cheap and liquid', 'Free of basis risk', 'Guaranteed to outperform', 'B', 'These vehicles provide efficient, liquid index exposure, though basis/liquidity must be watched.', 5),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-indexing-q07', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-indexing', 'Matching portfolio BPV to the benchmark''s BPV controls:', 'Credit risk', 'Interest-rate (duration) tracking error', 'Currency risk', 'Liquidity risk', 'B', 'Equal basis-point values neutralize the net interest-rate sensitivity vs the benchmark.', 6),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-indexing-q08', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-indexing', 'Index reconstitution creates costs because:', 'Indices never change', 'Forced buying/selling at rebalance dates incurs transaction costs', 'There is no turnover', 'Bonds never mature', 'B', 'Periodic index changes force trades that incur costs and potential front-running.', 7),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-indexing-q09', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-indexing', 'Optimization-based replication minimizes tracking error subject to:', 'No constraints', 'Risk and turnover constraints', 'Maximizing turnover', 'Holding one bond', 'B', 'Optimization selects holdings to minimize tracking error within practical risk/turnover limits.', 8),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-indexing-q10', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-indexing', 'Securities lending can help an index bond fund by:', 'Increasing tracking error', 'Generating income that offsets costs', 'Eliminating duration', 'Removing credit risk', 'B', 'Lending out bonds earns fees that can reduce the fund''s net expense/tracking drag.', 9),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-indexing-q11', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-indexing', 'The primary success metric for a passive bond manager is:', 'Absolute return', 'Low tracking error vs the benchmark', 'High turnover', 'Beating peers by a wide margin', 'B', 'Passive mandates are judged on how closely they track the index (minimal tracking error).', 10),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-indexing-q12', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-indexing', 'Compared with equity indexing, bond indexing is harder mainly due to:', 'Fewer securities', 'Many illiquid issues and constant composition changes', 'No duration risk', 'Daily liquidity in all bonds', 'B', 'Bond universes are large, illiquid, and constantly changing (maturities, calls, issuance).', 11),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-yield-curve-q01', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-yield-curve', 'Expecting rates to fall, a manager should:', 'Shorten duration', 'Extend duration', 'Hold cash', 'Buy floaters', 'B', 'Longer duration maximizes price gains when yields fall.', 0),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-yield-curve-q02', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-yield-curve', 'Anticipating curve flattening, a manager might use a:', 'Bullet at the short end', 'Barbell / overweight the long end', 'Cash-only position', 'Equity hedge', 'B', 'If long yields fall relative to short, overweighting the long end (barbell) benefits.', 1),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-yield-curve-q03', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-yield-curve', 'For a given duration, a barbell vs a bullet has:', 'Lower convexity', 'Higher convexity but typically a lower yield', 'No convexity', 'Identical yield and convexity', 'B', 'Barbells have more convexity (helpful in large moves) but usually a lower yield than bullets.', 2),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-yield-curve-q04', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-yield-curve', 'A butterfly trade is used to exploit a change in the curve''s:', 'Level', 'Curvature', 'Credit spread', 'Coupon', 'B', 'Butterfly trades (wings vs belly) target curvature changes, usually duration-neutral.', 3),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-yield-curve-q05', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-yield-curve', 'On a stable, upward-sloping curve, a profitable passive approach is:', 'Constantly shorting bonds', 'Buy-and-hold with rolldown/carry', 'Moving fully to cash', 'Maximizing turnover', 'B', 'A stable curve lets carry and rolldown capture yield without rate forecasts.', 4),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-yield-curve-q06', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-yield-curve', 'Key-rate durations are used to:', 'Capture only parallel shifts', 'Target exposure to specific maturities (non-parallel moves)', 'Measure credit risk', 'Compute the coupon', 'B', 'Key-rate durations measure sensitivity at individual points on the curve for slope/curvature trades.', 5),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-yield-curve-q07', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-yield-curve', 'An active curve trade is profitable only if realized rates differ from the:', 'Coupon', 'Forward-implied rates', 'Risk-free rate', 'Dividend yield', 'B', 'Forwards are the break-even; active positioning pays only if the curve evolves differently than forwards imply.', 6),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-yield-curve-q08', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-yield-curve', 'Anticipating steepening, a manager would favor a:', 'Barbell', 'Bullet / overweight short-intermediate maturities', 'Long-only at the long end', 'Cash', 'B', 'If long yields rise relative to short, concentrating in shorter maturities (bullet) reduces losses.', 7),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-yield-curve-q09', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-yield-curve', 'Higher convexity is most valuable when:', 'Rates are constant', 'Large or volatile rate moves are expected', 'The curve never changes', 'Coupons are zero', 'B', 'Convexity improves the price response asymmetrically, helping most in big/volatile moves.', 8),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-yield-curve-q10', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-yield-curve', 'Empirically, most yield-curve variance is explained by changes in the:', 'Level (parallel shift)', 'Curvature', 'Convexity', 'Coupon', 'A', 'The first principal component — level/parallel shift — accounts for the bulk of curve variance.', 9),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-yield-curve-q11', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-yield-curve', 'A duration-neutral slope trade isolates exposure to:', 'The overall level of rates', 'Changes in the curve''s slope', 'Credit spreads', 'Currency', 'B', 'Holding duration constant while shifting weights toward one end isolates a slope (steepening/flattening) view.', 10),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-yield-curve-q12', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-yield-curve', 'Riding (rolling) down the yield curve adds return when the curve is:', 'Inverted and volatile', 'Upward-sloping and stable', 'Flat and falling', 'Downward-sloping', 'B', 'On a stable upward-sloping curve, a bond''s yield falls as it ages, producing price gains beyond its coupon.', 11),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-credit-q01', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-credit', 'A top-down credit strategy focuses on:', 'Picking individual mispriced issuers', 'Sector, quality and credit-cycle rotation', 'Currency forecasting', 'Duration only', 'B', 'Top-down credit rotates sector/quality exposure based on the macro and credit-cycle outlook.', 0),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-credit-q02', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-credit', 'The key risk metric for a credit portfolio''s spread sensitivity is:', 'Spread duration', 'The coupon', 'Beta', 'Vega', 'A', 'Spread duration measures price sensitivity to changes in credit spreads.', 1),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-credit-q03', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-credit', 'The credit-spread return impact is approximately:', '−(spread duration × Δspread)', '+coupon', 'duration × convexity', 'recovery × notional', 'A', 'With rates held constant, spread return ≈ carry/roll − spread duration × Δspread − expected loss.', 2),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-credit-q04', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-credit', 'Anticipating a recession, a credit manager would typically:', 'Add lower-quality, high-spread credit', 'Move up in quality and shorten spread duration', 'Maximize leverage', 'Buy only equities', 'B', 'Defensively, managers upgrade quality and reduce spread duration before spreads widen.', 3),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-credit-q05', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-credit', 'Expected credit loss equals:', 'PD × LGD', 'Coupon × duration', 'Spread × convexity', 'Beta × premium', 'A', 'Expected loss = probability of default × loss given default (= 1 − recovery).', 4),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-credit-q06', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-credit', 'CDX/iTraxx index CDS are used to:', 'Buy a single bond', 'Take efficient diversified long/short credit exposure', 'Hedge currency only', 'Buy equities', 'B', 'Index CDS provide liquid, diversified credit exposure for hedging or expressing views.', 5),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-credit-q07', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-credit', 'Credit spreads are generally ____ over the business cycle.', 'Counter-cyclical', 'Pro-cyclical (widen in recessions)', 'Constant', 'Random', 'B', 'Spreads widen as default risk rises in downturns and narrow in expansions.', 6),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-credit-q08', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-credit', 'A bottom-up credit approach relies on:', 'Macro sector rotation', 'Fundamental/relative-value analysis of individual issuers', 'Currency views', 'Index replication', 'B', 'Bottom-up selects specific bonds believed mispriced via issuer-level analysis.', 7),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-credit-q09', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-credit', 'Diversifying across many issuers primarily reduces:', 'Systematic spread risk', 'Idiosyncratic default risk', 'Interest-rate risk', 'Inflation risk', 'B', 'Holding many uncorrelated issuers reduces issuer-specific default risk; systematic spread risk remains.', 8),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-credit-q10', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-credit', 'The CDS-cash basis is the difference between:', 'Two coupons', 'The CDS spread and the cash-bond spread', 'Duration and convexity', 'Beta and alpha', 'B', 'The basis compares CDS and cash-bond spreads for the same issuer, enabling relative-value trades.', 9),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-credit-q11', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-credit', 'In a recovery phase, a credit manager seeking return would likely:', 'Buy only Treasuries', 'Add lower-rated, higher-spread credit', 'Shorten spread duration sharply', 'Sell all credit', 'B', 'As spreads tighten in recovery, adding higher-spread credit raises return potential.', 10),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-credit-q12', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-credit', '''Jump-to-default'' is an example of:', 'Tail (idiosyncratic) credit risk', 'Interest-rate risk', 'Currency risk', 'Liquidity-only risk', 'A', 'A sudden default is a tail risk that diversification mitigates but cannot eliminate.', 11),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-leverage-q01', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-leverage', 'The main tool for leveraging a fixed-income portfolio is:', 'Equity issuance', 'Repurchase (repo) agreements', 'Dividends', 'Stock buybacks', 'B', 'Repo financing is the primary way bond managers obtain leverage.', 0),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-leverage-q02', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-leverage', 'Leverage magnifies:', 'Only gains', 'Both gains and losses', 'Only losses', 'Neither', 'B', 'Leverage amplifies returns in both directions and adds funding risk.', 1),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-leverage-q03', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-leverage', 'Positive carry exists when the asset''s yield exceeds the:', 'Coupon', 'Financing (borrowing) cost', 'Dividend', 'Tax rate', 'B', 'Carry = income earned minus financing cost; positive carry profits if the curve is unchanged.', 2),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-leverage-q04', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-leverage', 'A repo haircut represents:', 'Extra interest paid', 'The collateral margin (overcollateralization) required by the lender', 'A dividend', 'A tax', 'B', 'The haircut is the excess collateral the borrower must post; it can rise in stress, tightening funding.', 3),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-leverage-q05', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-leverage', 'Rolldown return arises on a stable upward-sloping curve as a bond''s yield:', 'Rises with age', 'Falls as it ages toward shorter maturities', 'Stays constant', 'Becomes negative', 'B', 'Aging moves the bond down a positively sloped curve, lowering its yield and raising its price.', 4),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-leverage-q06', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-leverage', 'A danger of leverage during market stress is:', 'Lower volatility', 'Margin calls forcing sales at the worst time', 'Higher liquidity', 'No effect', 'B', 'Falling prices and rising haircuts can trigger margin calls, forcing distressed selling.', 5),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-leverage-q07', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-leverage', 'Leveraged return approximately equals the asset return plus leverage times the:', 'Coupon', '(asset return − borrowing cost) spread', 'Dividend', 'Tax shield', 'B', 'Leveraged return ≈ r_assets + (borrowed/equity)(r_assets − r_borrow).', 6),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-leverage-q08', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-leverage', 'Futures and swaps provide leverage because they:', 'Require full cash outlay', 'Have embedded leverage (small margin controls a large notional)', 'Pay dividends', 'Eliminate risk', 'B', 'Derivatives control large notional exposure with little capital, providing implicit leverage.', 7),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-leverage-q09', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-leverage', 'A carry trade in fixed income loses money if:', 'The curve is unchanged', 'Rates rise sharply (prices fall)', 'Carry is positive', 'Rolldown is positive', 'B', 'A sharp rate rise produces capital losses that can exceed the positive carry.', 8),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-leverage-q10', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-leverage', 'The cost of repo financing depends on the repo rate and the:', 'Dividend yield', 'Haircut and collateral quality', 'Equity beta', 'Coupon only', 'B', 'Funding cost reflects the repo rate plus the capital tied up by the haircut on the posted collateral.', 9),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-leverage-q11', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-leverage', 'Leverage applied to a duration position primarily amplifies:', 'Currency risk', 'Interest-rate (and spread) risk', 'Dividend risk', 'Nothing', 'B', 'Leverage scales up the portfolio''s duration/spread exposures, magnifying rate and spread risk.', 10),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-leverage-q12', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-leverage', 'Carry and rolldown strategies perform best when the yield curve is:', 'Rising sharply', 'Upward-sloping and stable', 'Inverting rapidly', 'Highly volatile', 'B', 'A stable, positively sloped curve lets carry and rolldown accrue without adverse rate moves.', 11),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-multiple-liabilities-q01', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-multiple-liabilities', 'Cash-flow matching (dedication) is characterized as:', 'Highest risk, most flexible', 'Lowest risk, least flexible', 'Identical to duration matching', 'Requiring no bonds', 'B', 'Dedication directly funds each liability with matching cash flows — minimal risk, least flexibility.', 0),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-multiple-liabilities-q02', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-multiple-liabilities', 'Duration matching of multiple liabilities sets the portfolio''s BPV equal to the:', 'Coupon', 'Liabilities'' BPV', 'Risk-free rate', 'Equity beta', 'B', 'Matching basis-point values neutralizes net interest-rate sensitivity to small parallel moves.', 1),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-multiple-liabilities-q03', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-multiple-liabilities', 'To reduce structural risk when immunizing multiple liabilities, minimize the portfolio''s:', 'Duration', 'Dispersion/convexity difference vs the liabilities', 'Present value', 'Coupon', 'B', 'Minimizing dispersion makes the assets track the liabilities under non-parallel shifts.', 2),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-multiple-liabilities-q04', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-multiple-liabilities', 'Immunization protects most reliably against ____ rate changes.', 'Large non-parallel', 'Small parallel', 'Only rising', 'Only falling', 'B', 'Classical immunization locks in a return against small parallel shifts; twists leave structural risk.', 3),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-multiple-liabilities-q05', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-multiple-liabilities', 'Contingent immunization allows active management while:', 'There is no surplus', 'A surplus cushion above the required terminal value exists', 'Rates are zero', 'The portfolio is all cash', 'B', 'Active management continues until the safety cushion is exhausted, then the manager immunizes.', 4),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-multiple-liabilities-q06', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-multiple-liabilities', 'Duration matching versus cash-flow matching is generally:', 'Less flexible', 'More flexible but exposed to some curve risk', 'Risk-free', 'Impossible', 'B', 'BPV matching offers more flexibility than dedication but leaves residual non-parallel curve risk.', 5),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-multiple-liabilities-q07', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-multiple-liabilities', 'An immunized portfolio requires rebalancing because, over time, its duration:', 'Stays matched forever', 'Drifts away from the liabilities'' duration', 'Becomes negative', 'Equals its convexity', 'B', 'Durations change with time and yields, so periodic rebalancing maintains the match.', 6),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-multiple-liabilities-q08', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-multiple-liabilities', 'Horizon matching combines:', 'Equities and cash', 'Cash-flow matching of near-term and duration matching of longer liabilities', 'Two equity sleeves', 'Only futures', 'B', 'Horizon matching dedicates near-term liabilities and duration-matches the longer ones.', 7),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-multiple-liabilities-q09', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-multiple-liabilities', 'Structural risk in immunization arises primarily from:', 'Parallel shifts', 'Non-parallel (twist) curve movements', 'Reinvestment at the YTM', 'Holding to maturity', 'B', 'Twists cause the portfolio to diverge from the liabilities — the residual structural risk.', 8),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-multiple-liabilities-q10', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-multiple-liabilities', 'For immunization, the present value of assets should be:', 'Less than PV of liabilities', 'At least equal to PV of liabilities', 'Exactly zero', 'Equal to the coupon', 'B', 'PV(assets) ≥ PV(liabilities) ensures the obligations can be funded.', 9),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-multiple-liabilities-q11', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-multiple-liabilities', 'Cash-flow matching''s main drawback is:', 'High structural risk', 'Less flexibility and often higher cost', 'Daily rebalancing needs', 'Currency exposure', 'B', 'Dedication constrains bond selection and can cost more than duration matching, though it minimizes risk.', 10),
  ('cfa3-fixed-income-pm-multiple-liabilities-q12', 'cfa3-fixed-income-pm-multiple-liabilities', 'A pension hedging the interest-rate sensitivity of its liabilities is practicing:', 'Momentum investing', 'Liability-driven investing', 'Pure equity indexing', 'Currency overlay', 'B', 'Managing assets to hedge/fund the liabilities'' rate exposure is liability-driven investing.', 11),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-passive-q01', 'cfa3-equity-pm-passive', 'The most common index weighting scheme is:', 'Equal-weighted', 'Market-cap weighted', 'Price-weighted', 'Fundamental-weighted', 'B', 'Market-cap weighting dominates; it is self-rebalancing but concentrates in large (possibly overvalued) names.', 0),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-passive-q02', 'cfa3-equity-pm-passive', 'An equal-weighted index, versus cap-weighted, has:', 'A large-cap tilt', 'A small-cap tilt and higher rebalancing needs', 'No rebalancing', 'Zero turnover', 'B', 'Equal weighting tilts toward smaller names and must be periodically rebalanced back to equal weights.', 1),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-passive-q03', 'cfa3-equity-pm-passive', 'Full replication is most practical for an index that is:', 'Broad and illiquid', 'Narrow and liquid', 'Composed of private firms', 'Bond-only', 'B', 'Full replication works well when the index has relatively few, liquid constituents.', 2),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-passive-q04', 'cfa3-equity-pm-passive', 'Optimization-based replication for large indices aims to:', 'Maximize tracking error', 'Minimize tracking error subject to constraints', 'Hold one stock', 'Ignore the benchmark', 'B', 'Optimization selects a subset minimizing tracking error within risk/turnover limits.', 3),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-passive-q05', 'cfa3-equity-pm-passive', 'Equity index futures and total-return swaps provide:', 'Illiquid exposure', 'Cheap, liquid beta and completion-overlay capability', 'Guaranteed alpha', 'No market exposure', 'B', 'Derivatives offer efficient index exposure and let managers complete/adjust exposures cheaply.', 4),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-passive-q06', 'cfa3-equity-pm-passive', 'A cost specific to passive funds around index changes is:', 'Reconstitution / rebalancing turnover', 'Performance fees', 'Carried interest', 'Currency hedging', 'A', 'Index additions/deletions force trades (and front-running risk) at reconstitution dates.', 5),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-passive-q07', 'cfa3-equity-pm-passive', 'Fundamental (smart-beta) weighting selects weights based on:', 'Market cap', 'Accounting measures like sales, book value, or dividends', 'Price only', 'Random selection', 'B', 'Fundamental indices weight by economic size measures, creating a value tilt versus cap weighting.', 6),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-passive-q08', 'cfa3-equity-pm-passive', 'A drawback of market-cap weighting is that it:', 'Requires frequent rebalancing', 'Overweights large, potentially overvalued stocks', 'Has a small-cap tilt', 'Ignores liquidity', 'B', 'Cap weighting allocates more to the largest stocks, which may be overvalued (a ''return drag'' critique).', 7),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-passive-q09', 'cfa3-equity-pm-passive', 'Securities lending in an index fund can:', 'Increase tracking error', 'Generate income that offsets costs', 'Eliminate beta', 'Remove dividends', 'B', 'Lending portfolio holdings earns fees that reduce the fund''s net costs.', 8),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-passive-q10', 'cfa3-equity-pm-passive', 'The primary success metric for a passive equity manager is:', 'Alpha', 'Low tracking error', 'High turnover', 'Beating peers widely', 'B', 'Passive mandates are judged by how tightly they track the index at low cost.', 9),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-passive-q11', 'cfa3-equity-pm-passive', 'Stratified sampling replicates an index by:', 'Holding every stock', 'Matching key characteristics (sector, size) with a subset', 'Buying one stock', 'Using only derivatives', 'B', 'Sampling matches the index''s characteristics with a representative subset, lowering cost.', 10),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-passive-q12', 'cfa3-equity-pm-passive', 'A price-weighted index (e.g., the DJIA) is most influenced by:', 'The largest-cap stock', 'The highest-priced stock', 'The smallest stock', 'Equal contributions', 'B', 'Price weighting gives higher-priced shares more influence regardless of company size.', 11),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-active-fundamental-q01', 'cfa3-equity-pm-active-fundamental', 'Value investing typically seeks stocks with:', 'High multiples and momentum', 'Low valuation multiples (a margin of safety)', 'No earnings', 'The largest market cap', 'B', 'Value managers buy stocks trading cheaply relative to fundamentals, seeking a margin of safety.', 0),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-active-fundamental-q02', 'cfa3-equity-pm-active-fundamental', 'GARP (growth at a reasonable price) blends:', 'Value discipline with growth characteristics', 'Indexing and cash', 'Only deep value', 'Only momentum', 'A', 'GARP seeks growing companies whose valuations remain reasonable.', 1),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-active-fundamental-q03', 'cfa3-equity-pm-active-fundamental', 'Active share measures:', 'Total volatility', 'How much a portfolio''s holdings differ from the benchmark', 'Beta', 'Duration', 'B', 'Active share is the percentage of holdings that differ from the benchmark — a measure of active ''differentness''.', 2),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-active-fundamental-q04', 'cfa3-equity-pm-active-fundamental', 'A high-conviction concentrated portfolio tends to have:', 'Low active share', 'High active share and higher active risk', 'Zero tracking error', 'Index-like returns', 'B', 'Concentration in differentiated bets raises active share and tracking error.', 3),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-active-fundamental-q05', 'cfa3-equity-pm-active-fundamental', 'A behavioral risk for fundamental managers is:', 'Confirmation bias and overconfidence', 'Index reconstitution', 'Currency translation', 'Repo haircuts', 'A', 'Discretionary stock-pickers are prone to cognitive biases like confirmation and overconfidence.', 4),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-active-fundamental-q06', 'cfa3-equity-pm-active-fundamental', 'Top-down fundamental investing starts from:', 'Individual company analysis', 'Macro/sector themes that drive stock selection', 'Index weights', 'Random screens', 'B', 'Top-down derives sector/theme views first, then selects stocks; bottom-up works company-by-company.', 5),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-active-fundamental-q07', 'cfa3-equity-pm-active-fundamental', 'Style drift occurs when a manager:', 'Stays true to the stated strategy', 'Deviates from the stated investment style', 'Indexes the benchmark', 'Hedges currency', 'B', 'Style drift is unintended departure from the declared style, complicating attribution and suitability.', 6),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-active-fundamental-q08', 'cfa3-equity-pm-active-fundamental', 'Manager skill is best gauged by the:', 'Information ratio', 'Coupon', 'Cap rate', 'Dividend yield', 'A', 'The information ratio (active return / active risk) measures active management efficiency.', 7),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-active-fundamental-q09', 'cfa3-equity-pm-active-fundamental', 'A ''margin of safety'' refers to:', 'Buying below estimated intrinsic value', 'Using leverage', 'Hedging all risk', 'Maximizing turnover', 'A', 'Buying at a discount to intrinsic value cushions against estimation error and adverse outcomes.', 8),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-active-fundamental-q10', 'cfa3-equity-pm-active-fundamental', 'Growth investing typically emphasizes:', 'Low multiples', 'Strong earnings/revenue growth (often higher multiples)', 'No earnings growth', 'Distressed firms', 'B', 'Growth managers pay up for companies with superior expected growth.', 9),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-active-fundamental-q11', 'cfa3-equity-pm-active-fundamental', 'High active share combined with a high information ratio suggests:', 'Closet indexing', 'Genuine, well-rewarded conviction', 'No skill', 'Pure luck', 'B', 'Differentiated holdings (active share) that deliver active return per unit of risk indicate real skill.', 10),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-active-fundamental-q12', 'cfa3-equity-pm-active-fundamental', 'A constraint on how much alpha a fundamental strategy can deliver is:', 'Capacity and turnover/costs', 'The risk-free rate', 'The auditor', 'The dividend date', 'A', 'Capacity limits and trading costs erode the alpha a strategy can realize at scale.', 11),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-active-quant-q01', 'cfa3-equity-pm-active-quant', 'Quantitative equity strategies are best described as:', 'Discretionary stock picking', 'Systematic, rules-based selection using factors/models', 'Pure indexing', 'Currency trading', 'B', 'Quant strategies use disciplined, rules-based models and factors to select securities.', 0),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-active-quant-q02', 'cfa3-equity-pm-active-quant', 'Overfitting in a backtest means the model:', 'Generalizes well', 'Fits historical noise and fails out of sample', 'Uses base rates', 'Has no parameters', 'B', 'Overfit models capture noise, performing well in-sample but poorly on new data.', 1),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-active-quant-q03', 'cfa3-equity-pm-active-quant', 'Look-ahead bias occurs when a backtest uses:', 'Only past data', 'Information not available at the decision time', 'Random data', 'Out-of-sample data', 'B', 'Look-ahead bias uses data (e.g., restated financials) not knowable at the historical decision point.', 2),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-active-quant-q04', 'cfa3-equity-pm-active-quant', 'A common rewarded factor in quant models is:', 'Ticker length', 'Momentum', 'Share price level', 'Trading hours', 'B', 'Momentum (along with value, quality, size, low-vol) is a widely used rewarded factor.', 3),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-active-quant-q05', 'cfa3-equity-pm-active-quant', 'A ''quant meltdown'' typically occurs when:', 'Markets are calm', 'Crowded factor trades unwind simultaneously', 'Factors never change', 'There is no leverage', 'B', 'When many quants hold the same crowded factor bets, a simultaneous unwind causes sharp losses.', 4),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-active-quant-q06', 'cfa3-equity-pm-active-quant', 'Relative to fundamental managers, quant strategies rely on:', 'Deep, concentrated bets', 'Breadth — many small, independent bets', 'No diversification', 'A single stock', 'B', 'Quant exploits modest skill across many positions; IR ≈ IC × √breadth.', 5),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-active-quant-q07', 'cfa3-equity-pm-active-quant', 'Survivorship bias inflates backtest results by:', 'Including failed firms', 'Excluding firms that delisted/failed', 'Using out-of-sample data', 'Lowering returns', 'B', 'Dropping failed firms leaves only survivors, overstating historical performance.', 6),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-active-quant-q08', 'cfa3-equity-pm-active-quant', 'Factor crowding/decay refers to a factor''s premium:', 'Growing forever', 'Shrinking as more investors exploit it', 'Being risk-free', 'Never changing', 'B', 'As a factor becomes popular, its excess return tends to erode (crowding/decay).', 7),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-active-quant-q09', 'cfa3-equity-pm-active-quant', 'Out-of-sample (walk-forward) testing helps guard against:', 'Overfitting', 'Lower returns', 'Currency risk', 'Liquidity risk', 'A', 'Testing on data not used to build the model checks whether it generalizes, mitigating overfitting.', 8),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-active-quant-q10', 'cfa3-equity-pm-active-quant', 'Quant portfolio construction usually uses:', 'Gut feel', 'Optimization with risk and turnover constraints', 'A single discretionary bet', 'No model', 'B', 'Quant builds portfolios via optimization, balancing expected return against risk and trading costs.', 9),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-active-quant-q11', 'cfa3-equity-pm-active-quant', 'A key risk for quant strategies is:', 'Regime change breaking a previously working model', 'Too much diversification', 'No data', 'Zero turnover', 'A', 'Structural/regime shifts can invalidate relationships a model relied upon.', 10),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-active-quant-q12', 'cfa3-equity-pm-active-quant', 'The fundamental law of active management is IR ≈ IC ×:', '√breadth', 'beta', 'the risk-free rate', 'tracking error²', 'A', 'IR ≈ information coefficient × square root of breadth — central to quant''s many-small-bets approach.', 11),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-construction-q01', 'cfa3-equity-pm-construction', 'Active return can be decomposed into factor exposures, security selection and:', 'Costs (subtracted)', 'The risk-free rate', 'Dividends', 'The coupon', 'A', 'Active return = rewarded-factor returns + idiosyncratic selection − implementation costs.', 0),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-construction-q02', 'cfa3-equity-pm-construction', 'Active risk (tracking error) measures the:', 'Total volatility', 'Volatility of active (vs benchmark) returns', 'Beta', 'Duration', 'B', 'Tracking error is the standard deviation of the portfolio''s return minus the benchmark''s.', 1),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-construction-q03', 'cfa3-equity-pm-construction', 'A pure stock-picker typically exhibits:', 'High active share, mostly idiosyncratic risk', 'Low active share', 'Only factor risk', 'Zero tracking error', 'A', 'Stock selection produces high active share with risk dominated by security-specific (idiosyncratic) bets.', 2),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-construction-q04', 'cfa3-equity-pm-construction', 'A factor-bet manager''s active risk is dominated by:', 'Idiosyncratic risk', 'Factor (systematic) exposures', 'The risk-free rate', 'Currency', 'B', 'Tilting toward factors makes systematic factor exposure the main source of active risk.', 3),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-construction-q05', 'cfa3-equity-pm-construction', 'A market-neutral long/short portfolio aims to:', 'Maximize beta', 'Isolate alpha by removing net market exposure', 'Track the index', 'Eliminate all risk', 'B', 'Balancing longs and shorts removes net beta, isolating security-selection alpha.', 4),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-construction-q06', 'cfa3-equity-pm-construction', 'Implementation costs (impact, spread) primarily:', 'Increase paper alpha', 'Erode realized alpha', 'Have no effect', 'Lower risk', 'B', 'Trading costs reduce the alpha actually captured relative to a paper portfolio.', 5),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-construction-q07', 'cfa3-equity-pm-construction', 'A closet indexer is characterized by:', 'High active share', 'Low active share with active fees', 'Market-neutral positioning', 'High tracking error', 'B', 'Closet indexers hug the benchmark (low active share) while charging active fees — poor value.', 6),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-construction-q08', 'cfa3-equity-pm-construction', 'A risk budget for an active equity portfolio sets limits on:', 'Dividend dates', 'Position sizes, sector/factor exposures and tracking error', 'The auditor', 'Coupon', 'B', 'Risk budgeting constrains exposures and active risk consistent with the mandate.', 7),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-construction-q09', 'cfa3-equity-pm-construction', 'Higher portfolio concentration generally:', 'Lowers active share and risk', 'Raises active share and active risk', 'Eliminates risk', 'Tracks the index', 'B', 'Fewer, larger positions increase both how different the portfolio is and its active risk.', 8),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-construction-q10', 'cfa3-equity-pm-construction', 'Liquidity management in construction matters because:', 'It has no cost', 'Illiquid positions raise trading costs and exit risk', 'All stocks are liquid', 'It eliminates beta', 'B', 'Position sizing must consider liquidity to avoid high impact costs and difficulty exiting.', 9),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-construction-q11', 'cfa3-equity-pm-construction', 'Active share alone is insufficient to judge skill because it:', 'Measures volatility', 'Shows differentness, not whether bets are rewarded', 'Equals the information ratio', 'Measures beta', 'B', 'High active share can still underperform; pair it with the information ratio to assess rewarded skill.', 10),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-construction-q12', 'cfa3-equity-pm-construction', 'A long-only manager''s ability to underweight is limited by:', 'Unlimited shorting', 'The benchmark weight (cannot go below zero holding)', 'The risk-free rate', 'Currency', 'B', 'Without shorting, the maximum underweight of a stock is its benchmark weight, constraining negative views.', 11),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-corporate-governance-q01', 'cfa3-equity-pm-corporate-governance', 'Strong corporate governance tends to:', 'Raise the cost of capital', 'Lower risk and the cost of capital', 'Have no effect', 'Guarantee higher earnings', 'B', 'Good governance reduces agency/operational risk, which can lower the firm''s cost of capital.', 0),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-corporate-governance-q02', 'cfa3-equity-pm-corporate-governance', 'Shareholder engagement (stewardship) includes:', 'Proxy voting and dialogue with management', 'Ignoring the company', 'Insider trading', 'Front-running', 'A', 'Engagement uses voting and direct dialogue to influence governance and protect value.', 1),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-corporate-governance-q03', 'cfa3-equity-pm-corporate-governance', 'ESG integration adjusts valuation by incorporating material factors into:', 'Cash-flow forecasts and/or the discount rate', 'Only marketing', 'Share count', 'The dividend date', 'A', 'Material ESG factors affect projected cash flows and required returns.', 2),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-corporate-governance-q04', 'cfa3-equity-pm-corporate-governance', 'Negative (exclusionary) screening means:', 'Picking best-in-class ESG firms', 'Excluding certain sectors/companies on ESG grounds', 'Ignoring ESG', 'Buying only bonds', 'B', 'Negative screening removes objectionable industries/firms from the universe.', 3),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-corporate-governance-q05', 'cfa3-equity-pm-corporate-governance', 'Greenwashing refers to:', 'Genuine emission cuts', 'Overstating or misrepresenting ESG credentials', 'A bond type', 'An accounting method', 'B', 'Greenwashing is misleading promotion of sustainability that overstates reality.', 4),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-corporate-governance-q06', 'cfa3-equity-pm-corporate-governance', 'ESG ''materiality'' means focusing on factors that:', 'Are always environmental', 'Affect cash flows or risk', 'Are immaterial to value', 'Only matter to regulators', 'B', 'Material ESG issues are those with a plausible financial impact.', 5),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-corporate-governance-q07', 'cfa3-equity-pm-corporate-governance', 'A governance red flag is:', 'An independent, diverse board', 'A staggered board and dual-class shares', 'One-share-one-vote', 'A strong audit committee', 'B', 'Entrenchment devices (staggered boards, dual-class shares) weaken accountability.', 6),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-corporate-governance-q08', 'cfa3-equity-pm-corporate-governance', 'Activist investors typically seek to:', 'Hold passively forever', 'Drive board/strategic changes to unlock value', 'Avoid voting', 'Index the market', 'B', 'Activists push for changes (board seats, capital returns, strategy) to increase shareholder value.', 7),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-corporate-governance-q09', 'cfa3-equity-pm-corporate-governance', 'Positive (best-in-class) screening:', 'Excludes whole sectors', 'Selects ESG leaders within sectors', 'Ignores ESG', 'Buys only fossil fuels', 'B', 'Best-in-class screening favors the strongest ESG performers within each sector.', 8),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-corporate-governance-q10', 'cfa3-equity-pm-corporate-governance', 'A frameworks like SASB helps investors identify:', 'Industry-specific material ESG issues', 'Coupon rates', 'Currency forwards', 'Duration', 'A', 'SASB maps financially material ESG topics by industry, aiding integration.', 9),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-corporate-governance-q11', 'cfa3-equity-pm-corporate-governance', 'Dual-class share structures concern investors because they:', 'Give all holders equal votes', 'Concentrate voting control disproportionate to economic ownership', 'Eliminate voting', 'Are legally required', 'B', 'Dual-class shares give insiders outsized control relative to their cash-flow stake.', 10),
  ('cfa3-equity-pm-corporate-governance-q12', 'cfa3-equity-pm-corporate-governance', 'Stewardship through proxy voting is considered part of a manager''s:', 'Marketing', 'Fiduciary duty to protect/enhance long-term value', 'Tax planning', 'Trading desk', 'B', 'Voting and engagement are tools to fulfill the fiduciary duty to clients'' long-term interests.', 11),
  ('cfa3-performance-return-measurement-q01', 'cfa3-performance-return-measurement', 'Time-weighted return is preferred for judging a manager because it:', 'Reflects client cash-flow timing', 'Removes the effect of external cash flows', 'Equals the IRR', 'Ignores returns', 'B', 'TWR strips out client-driven flows the manager doesn''t control, isolating investment skill.', 0),
  ('cfa3-performance-return-measurement-q02', 'cfa3-performance-return-measurement', 'Money-weighted return (IRR) reflects the:', 'Manager''s skill only', 'Timing and size of cash flows (the investor''s experience)', 'Benchmark return', 'Risk-free rate', 'B', 'MWR depends on when and how much cash was invested, capturing the investor''s actual outcome.', 1),
  ('cfa3-performance-return-measurement-q03', 'cfa3-performance-return-measurement', 'TWR and MWR diverge most when there are:', 'No cash flows', 'Large external cash flows and volatile sub-period returns', 'Constant returns', 'Only one period', 'B', 'Big flows combined with variable returns drive a wedge between the two measures.', 2),
  ('cfa3-performance-return-measurement-q04', 'cfa3-performance-return-measurement', 'GIPS requires composite returns to be calculated as:', 'Money-weighted', 'Time-weighted', 'Simple average', 'Nominal coupon', 'B', 'Time-weighted returns are the GIPS default, removing client cash-flow effects.', 3),
  ('cfa3-performance-return-measurement-q05', 'cfa3-performance-return-measurement', 'If a large deposit is made just before a strong period, MWR will be:', 'Lower than TWR', 'Higher than TWR', 'Equal to TWR', 'Zero', 'B', 'More capital invested before strong performance lifts the money-weighted return above TWR.', 4),
  ('cfa3-performance-return-measurement-q06', 'cfa3-performance-return-measurement', 'MWR is most appropriate when the manager:', 'Does not control cash-flow timing', 'Controls the timing of cash flows (e.g., private equity)', 'Indexes the benchmark', 'Has no clients', 'B', 'When the manager dictates when capital is called/returned (PE), IRR/MWR fairly measures performance.', 5),
  ('cfa3-performance-return-measurement-q07', 'cfa3-performance-return-measurement', 'More frequent portfolio valuation improves the accuracy of:', 'The benchmark', 'Time-weighted return', 'The coupon', 'Beta', 'B', 'Valuing at each cash flow makes TWR more precise; the linked IRR approximates it when valuations are infrequent.', 6),
  ('cfa3-performance-return-measurement-q08', 'cfa3-performance-return-measurement', 'TWR compounds the returns of:', 'Each sub-period between cash flows', 'Only the first period', 'The benchmark', 'The risk-free asset', 'A', 'TWR geometrically links the returns of sub-periods defined by external cash flows.', 7),
  ('cfa3-performance-return-measurement-q09', 'cfa3-performance-return-measurement', 'A deposit just before a weak period makes MWR:', 'Higher than TWR', 'Lower than TWR', 'Equal to TWR', 'Undefined', 'B', 'Investing more capital before poor performance drags the money-weighted return below TWR.', 8),
  ('cfa3-performance-return-measurement-q10', 'cfa3-performance-return-measurement', 'For comparing two managers fairly, use:', 'Money-weighted return', 'Time-weighted return', 'Their cash balances', 'Dividend yield', 'B', 'TWR is comparable across managers because it ignores client-driven flows.', 9),
  ('cfa3-performance-return-measurement-q11', 'cfa3-performance-return-measurement', 'The money-weighted return is mathematically the:', 'Arithmetic mean', 'Internal rate of return of the cash flows', 'Geometric mean of sub-periods', 'Risk-free rate', 'B', 'MWR is the IRR that sets the present value of all cash flows (including ending value) to zero.', 10),
  ('cfa3-performance-return-measurement-q12', 'cfa3-performance-return-measurement', 'When there are no external cash flows during a period, TWR and MWR are:', 'Very different', 'Equal', 'Both zero', 'Undefined', 'B', 'Without external flows, the two measures coincide.', 11),
  ('cfa3-performance-attribution-approaches-q01', 'cfa3-performance-attribution-approaches', 'Brinson attribution decomposes active return into allocation, selection and:', 'Interaction', 'Inflation', 'Duration', 'Tracking error', 'A', 'The Brinson model splits active return into allocation, selection, and an interaction term.', 0),
  ('cfa3-performance-attribution-approaches-q02', 'cfa3-performance-attribution-approaches', 'Overweighting a sector that outperforms contributes to the ____ effect.', 'Selection', 'Allocation', 'Interaction only', 'Currency', 'B', 'Choosing sector weights different from the benchmark drives the allocation effect.', 1),
  ('cfa3-performance-attribution-approaches-q03', 'cfa3-performance-attribution-approaches', 'Picking outperforming stocks within a sector contributes to the ____ effect.', 'Allocation', 'Selection', 'Interaction', 'Benchmark', 'B', 'Security choices within sectors drive the selection effect.', 2),
  ('cfa3-performance-attribution-approaches-q04', 'cfa3-performance-attribution-approaches', 'Returns-based attribution, versus holdings-based, is:', 'More accurate but data-heavy', 'Easier but less precise and can be gamed', 'Impossible', 'Identical', 'B', 'Returns-based uses only periodic returns/factor models — convenient but less precise and manipulable.', 3),
  ('cfa3-performance-attribution-approaches-q05', 'cfa3-performance-attribution-approaches', 'Transaction-based attribution is the most accurate because it uses:', 'Only returns', 'Holdings and the actual trades', 'The benchmark only', 'No data', 'B', 'Incorporating intra-period trades gives the most precise attribution, at high data cost.', 4),
  ('cfa3-performance-attribution-approaches-q06', 'cfa3-performance-attribution-approaches', 'Fixed-income attribution commonly decomposes return into income, roll, duration, curve and:', 'Spread/credit and currency', 'Dividends', 'Active share', 'Beta', 'A', 'FI attribution separates yield/income, roll, level (duration), curve, spread/credit, and currency effects.', 5),
  ('cfa3-performance-attribution-approaches-q07', 'cfa3-performance-attribution-approaches', 'Holdings-based attribution requires:', 'Only returns', 'Portfolio weights each period', 'No data', 'Manager interviews', 'B', 'Holdings-based attribution uses periodic position weights, improving accuracy over returns-based.', 6),
  ('cfa3-performance-attribution-approaches-q08', 'cfa3-performance-attribution-approaches', 'Attribution primarily explains:', 'Future returns', 'How (the sources by which) active return was earned', 'The risk-free rate', 'The benchmark''s coupon', 'B', 'Attribution identifies the contribution of allocation, selection, and other effects to realized active return.', 7),
  ('cfa3-performance-attribution-approaches-q09', 'cfa3-performance-attribution-approaches', 'The interaction effect captures the:', 'Combined impact of allocation and selection decisions', 'Risk-free rate', 'Currency only', 'Coupon', 'A', 'Interaction is the cross term reflecting the joint effect of weighting and selection within a sector.', 8),
  ('cfa3-performance-attribution-approaches-q10', 'cfa3-performance-attribution-approaches', 'Attribution can reveal ____ that the manager did not intend.', 'Unintended factor/sector bets', 'The risk-free rate', 'Audit fees', 'Coupon dates', 'A', 'Attribution surfaces inadvertent exposures, helping align the portfolio with the strategy.', 9),
  ('cfa3-performance-attribution-approaches-q11', 'cfa3-performance-attribution-approaches', 'A drawback of returns-based attribution is that it can be:', 'Too accurate', 'Gamed or imprecise (a ''black box'')', 'Free of assumptions', 'Identical to holdings-based', 'B', 'Because it relies on a factor model and not actual holdings, returns-based attribution can mislead.', 10),
  ('cfa3-performance-attribution-approaches-q12', 'cfa3-performance-attribution-approaches', 'Currency effects in attribution are most relevant for:', 'Domestic-only portfolios', 'International portfolios', 'Cash holdings', 'Risk-free assets', 'B', 'Cross-border portfolios require isolating currency contributions in attribution.', 11),
  ('cfa3-performance-risk-attribution-q01', 'cfa3-performance-risk-attribution', 'Risk attribution explains the sources of:', 'Realized return', 'Risk taken', 'Fees', 'Dividends', 'B', 'Where return attribution explains return, risk attribution decomposes the risk taken.', 0),
  ('cfa3-performance-risk-attribution-q02', 'cfa3-performance-risk-attribution', 'Active risk² can be decomposed into active factor risk and active:', 'Specific (selection) risk', 'Risk-free rate', 'Coupon', 'Duration', 'A', 'Tracking error² = active factor risk + active idiosyncratic (selection) risk.', 1),
  ('cfa3-performance-risk-attribution-q03', 'cfa3-performance-risk-attribution', 'A stock-picker whose risk is mostly factor-based may be experiencing:', 'Perfect alignment', 'Style drift (risk not matching the stated process)', 'Lower fees', 'Higher liquidity', 'B', 'The dominant risk source should match the strategy; a mismatch signals style drift or unintended bets.', 2),
  ('cfa3-performance-risk-attribution-q04', 'cfa3-performance-risk-attribution', 'Component (or marginal) VaR is used to:', 'Eliminate risk', 'Attribute total portfolio risk to individual positions/factors', 'Compute the coupon', 'Set the benchmark', 'B', 'Component VaR decomposes total risk into additive contributions from positions or factors.', 3),
  ('cfa3-performance-risk-attribution-q05', 'cfa3-performance-risk-attribution', 'Risk budgeting sets ____ limits, while risk attribution checks the ____ outcome.', 'ex-ante; ex-post', 'ex-post; ex-ante', 'no; no', 'coupon; dividend', 'A', 'Risk budgets are set in advance (ex-ante); attribution verifies the realized (ex-post) risk against them.', 4),
  ('cfa3-performance-risk-attribution-q06', 'cfa3-performance-risk-attribution', 'Combining risk and return attribution helps judge whether:', 'The risk taken was rewarded', 'The auditor is independent', 'Dividends were paid', 'The coupon is fixed', 'A', 'Together they show if the active risk produced commensurate active return (the information ratio).', 5),
  ('cfa3-performance-risk-attribution-q07', 'cfa3-performance-risk-attribution', 'For an absolute-return mandate, risk is decomposed into:', 'Active vs benchmark only', 'Factor and idiosyncratic contributions to total risk', 'Coupon and dividend', 'Beta and alpha only', 'B', 'Absolute mandates decompose total (not active) risk into factor and specific sources.', 6),
  ('cfa3-performance-risk-attribution-q08', 'cfa3-performance-risk-attribution', 'A factor-tilt manager''s tracking error is dominated by:', 'Idiosyncratic risk', 'Active factor exposures', 'Cash', 'Currency', 'B', 'Deliberate factor tilts make systematic factor risk the main contributor to active risk.', 7),
  ('cfa3-performance-risk-attribution-q09', 'cfa3-performance-risk-attribution', 'Risk attribution that doesn''t match the strategy may indicate:', 'Good control', 'Unintended exposures requiring correction', 'Lower fees', 'Higher liquidity', 'B', 'Mismatched risk sources flag unintended bets that should be addressed.', 8),
  ('cfa3-performance-risk-attribution-q10', 'cfa3-performance-risk-attribution', 'A pure stock-picker should show risk dominated by:', 'Factor risk', 'Idiosyncratic (specific) risk', 'The risk-free rate', 'Currency', 'B', 'Genuine security selection produces mostly idiosyncratic active risk.', 9),
  ('cfa3-performance-risk-attribution-q11', 'cfa3-performance-risk-attribution', 'Marginal contribution to risk measures how a position changes:', 'Portfolio total/active risk at the margin', 'The coupon', 'Dividends', 'The benchmark', 'A', 'Marginal contribution shows the incremental risk from a small change in a position''s weight.', 10),
  ('cfa3-performance-risk-attribution-q12', 'cfa3-performance-risk-attribution', 'Risk attribution complements return attribution by confirming the manager''s:', 'Process is consistent with the risk taken', 'Audit fees', 'Coupon schedule', 'Dividend policy', 'A', 'It verifies that the realized risk profile aligns with the stated investment process.', 11),
  ('cfa3-performance-benchmarks-q01', 'cfa3-performance-benchmarks', 'A valid benchmark should be specified in advance and:', 'Unambiguous, investable, measurable', 'Chosen after performance is known', 'Impossible to replicate', 'Different each period', 'A', 'Quality benchmarks (SAMURAI) are pre-specified, unambiguous, investable, measurable, appropriate, reflective, and accountable.', 0),
  ('cfa3-performance-benchmarks-q02', 'cfa3-performance-benchmarks', 'A manager (peer) universe is a flawed benchmark mainly because it is:', 'Investable', 'Not investable and subject to survivorship bias', 'Specified in advance', 'Unambiguous', 'B', 'You cannot invest in a peer group, and it suffers survivorship bias — failing key benchmark properties.', 1),
  ('cfa3-performance-benchmarks-q03', 'cfa3-performance-benchmarks', 'A custom (normal) portfolio benchmark is useful for:', 'Generic large-cap mandates', 'Specialized strategies not fit by a standard index', 'Cash', 'Risk-free assets', 'B', 'Normal portfolios capture a manager''s typical investable universe for specialized mandates.', 2),
  ('cfa3-performance-benchmarks-q04', 'cfa3-performance-benchmarks', 'A poorly matched (style-mismatched) benchmark causes:', 'Accurate attribution', 'Distorted attribution and unfair appraisal', 'Lower fees', 'Higher liquidity', 'B', 'A bad benchmark makes allocation/selection effects meaningless and misjudges the manager.', 3),
  ('cfa3-performance-benchmarks-q05', 'cfa3-performance-benchmarks', 'An absolute-return benchmark is a:', 'Market index', 'Fixed target return (e.g., cash + x%)', 'Peer group', 'Factor model', 'B', 'Absolute benchmarks specify a target return independent of any market index.', 4),
  ('cfa3-performance-benchmarks-q06', 'cfa3-performance-benchmarks', 'High benchmark ''coverage'' means:', 'Few holdings are in the benchmark', 'Most of the portfolio''s holdings are in the benchmark', 'Zero overlap', 'Only cash is covered', 'B', 'Coverage measures the share of portfolio holdings represented in the benchmark; high coverage indicates a good fit.', 5),
  ('cfa3-performance-benchmarks-q07', 'cfa3-performance-benchmarks', 'A test of benchmark quality is whether the manager''s active return is ____ with the benchmark''s active return vs the market.', 'Positively correlated', 'Uncorrelated / low systematic bias', 'Always equal', 'Identical to the coupon', 'B', 'A good benchmark leaves no systematic bias — the manager''s and benchmark''s active returns shouldn''t be systematically related.', 6),
  ('cfa3-performance-benchmarks-q08', 'cfa3-performance-benchmarks', '''Reflective of current investment opinions'' means the benchmark contains:', 'Securities the manager has knowledge/views on', 'Random securities', 'Only cash', 'Only bonds', 'A', 'The benchmark should comprise securities within the manager''s expertise and opinion set.', 7),
  ('cfa3-performance-benchmarks-q09', 'cfa3-performance-benchmarks', '''Accountable (owned)'' means the manager:', 'Rejects the benchmark', 'Accepts and is accountable to the benchmark', 'Ignores it', 'Picks it after the fact', 'B', 'The manager should acknowledge the benchmark as the standard they are accountable to.', 8),
  ('cfa3-performance-benchmarks-q10', 'cfa3-performance-benchmarks', 'A style index benchmark is appropriate for a manager who:', 'Has no style', 'Follows a specific style (e.g., large-cap value)', 'Holds only cash', 'Indexes the whole market', 'B', 'Style indices match managers with defined style mandates.', 9),
  ('cfa3-performance-benchmarks-q11', 'cfa3-performance-benchmarks', 'Survivorship bias in a peer-group benchmark causes it to:', 'Understate the median manager return', 'Overstate the median manager return', 'Have no bias', 'Become investable', 'B', 'Dropping failed managers inflates the surviving group''s apparent performance.', 10),
  ('cfa3-performance-benchmarks-q12', 'cfa3-performance-benchmarks', 'The mnemonic SAMURAI is used to remember:', 'Properties of a valid benchmark', 'Types of options', 'Bond risks', 'Behavioral biases', 'A', 'SAMURAI lists the properties of a valid benchmark (specified, appropriate, measurable, unambiguous, reflective, accountable, investable).', 11),
  ('cfa3-performance-appraisal-q01', 'cfa3-performance-appraisal', 'The information ratio measures active return per unit of:', 'Beta', 'Tracking error (active risk)', 'Total volatility', 'The risk-free rate', 'B', 'IR = active return / tracking error, the core appraisal measure of active management.', 0),
  ('cfa3-performance-appraisal-q02', 'cfa3-performance-appraisal', 'The M² measure expresses risk-adjusted performance in units of:', 'Beta', 'Return (at the benchmark''s total risk)', 'Tracking error', 'Duration', 'B', 'M² restates the Sharpe ratio as a return comparable to the benchmark at equal total risk.', 1),
  ('cfa3-performance-appraisal-q03', 'cfa3-performance-appraisal', 'Firing a skilled manager after a stretch of bad luck is a:', 'Type I error', 'Type II error', 'Data error', 'Benchmark error', 'B', 'Rejecting a genuinely skilled manager (false negative) is a Type II error.', 2),
  ('cfa3-performance-appraisal-q04', 'cfa3-performance-appraisal', 'A manager continuation policy (MCP) helps by:', 'Maximizing churn', 'Setting objective criteria/zones to reduce noise-driven firing/hiring', 'Ignoring performance', 'Removing benchmarks', 'B', 'An MCP defines acceptable-performance zones and criteria, reducing decisions driven by random noise.', 3),
  ('cfa3-performance-appraisal-q05', 'cfa3-performance-appraisal', 'The Sharpe ratio uses which risk measure?', 'Beta', 'Total volatility (standard deviation)', 'Tracking error', 'VaR', 'B', 'The Sharpe ratio divides excess return by total standard deviation.', 4),
  ('cfa3-performance-appraisal-q06', 'cfa3-performance-appraisal', 'Jensen''s alpha measures return in excess of that required by the:', 'Dividend discount model', 'CAPM (given the portfolio''s beta)', 'Sortino target', 'Information ratio', 'B', 'Jensen''s alpha = portfolio return − CAPM-required return based on beta.', 5),
  ('cfa3-performance-appraisal-q07', 'cfa3-performance-appraisal', 'Retaining an unskilled manager is a:', 'Type I error', 'Type II error', 'No error', 'Benchmark error', 'A', 'Keeping/hiring a manager with no real skill (false positive) is a Type I error.', 6),
  ('cfa3-performance-appraisal-q08', 'cfa3-performance-appraisal', 'An IR around 1.0 is generally considered:', 'Poor', 'Exceptional', 'Average', 'Negative', 'B', 'An information ratio near 1.0 reflects exceptional active management; ~0.5 is good.', 7),
  ('cfa3-performance-appraisal-q09', 'cfa3-performance-appraisal', 'Short, volatile track records make appraisal hard because:', 'Skill is easy to confirm', 'It is statistically difficult to separate skill from luck', 'Returns are guaranteed', 'Benchmarks are perfect', 'B', 'Limited noisy data lowers statistical confidence in distinguishing skill from random results.', 8),
  ('cfa3-performance-appraisal-q10', 'cfa3-performance-appraisal', 'Treynor ratio is most appropriate for:', 'An investor''s entire portfolio', 'A well-diversified sub-portfolio (uses beta)', 'A single risk-free asset', 'A cash account', 'B', 'Treynor uses beta, fitting a diversified sub-portfolio whose idiosyncratic risk is diversified away.', 9),
  ('cfa3-performance-appraisal-q11', 'cfa3-performance-appraisal', 'When appraising managers, process and operational quality should be weighted because:', 'Past returns alone confirm skill', 'Returns are noisy; process/operations are more persistent indicators', 'They are irrelevant', 'They lower the IR', 'B', 'Because returns are noisy, durable process and operational quality help confirm genuine skill.', 10),
  ('cfa3-performance-appraisal-q12', 'cfa3-performance-appraisal', 'A ''zone of acceptable performance'' in an MCP is designed to:', 'Trigger immediate firing on any underperformance', 'Tolerate normal performance variation before action', 'Ignore all performance', 'Maximize turnover', 'B', 'The zone allows for expected noise so managers aren''t churned over random short-term variation.', 11),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-ips-q01', 'cfa3-private-wealth-ips', 'An individual''s risk tolerance combines ability and willingness; when they differ, use the:', 'Higher of the two', 'Lower (more conservative) of the two, then educate', 'Average', 'Neither', 'B', 'The more conservative measure governs; the adviser then seeks to reconcile via education.', 0),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-ips-q02', 'cfa3-private-wealth-ips', 'Which is the most distinctive constraint for individuals versus institutions?', 'Time horizon', 'Taxes', 'Liquidity', 'Legal', 'B', 'Taxes are a defining, complex constraint in individual portfolio management.', 1),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-ips-q03', 'cfa3-private-wealth-ips', 'An individual''s required return is best derived from their:', 'Benchmark index', 'Goals, spending, and time horizon', 'Beta', 'The risk-free rate only', 'B', 'Solve for the return needed to fund the client''s goals given spending and horizon.', 2),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-ips-q04', 'cfa3-private-wealth-ips', 'Individual time horizons are often:', 'Single-stage and short', 'Multi-stage (e.g., pre- and post-retirement)', 'Always infinite', 'Irrelevant', 'B', 'Individuals commonly have multi-stage horizons that change the allocation over time.', 3),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-ips-q05', 'cfa3-private-wealth-ips', 'A concentrated single-stock position is typically captured under which IPS constraint?', 'Unique circumstances', 'Taxes only', 'Time horizon', 'Liquidity only', 'A', 'Special situations like concentrated holdings or ESG preferences fall under unique circumstances.', 4),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-ips-q06', 'cfa3-private-wealth-ips', 'Ability to take risk is higher when the client has:', 'Large needs relative to wealth and a short horizon', 'Ample wealth relative to needs, a long horizon, and flexibility', 'No income', 'High debt', 'B', 'Greater financial capacity, longer horizon, and spending flexibility raise the ability to bear risk.', 5),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-ips-q07', 'cfa3-private-wealth-ips', 'Liquidity needs in an individual IPS include:', 'Spending, emergencies, and large planned purchases', 'The benchmark', 'Beta', 'The coupon', 'A', 'Individual liquidity covers ongoing spending, reserves, and known upcoming outlays.', 6),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-ips-q08', 'cfa3-private-wealth-ips', 'Goals-based framing helps individual clients by:', 'Eliminating taxes', 'Improving discipline via mental accounting per goal', 'Removing the IPS', 'Guaranteeing returns', 'B', 'Tying portfolios to specific goals leverages mental accounting to improve adherence.', 7),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-ips-q09', 'cfa3-private-wealth-ips', 'Willingness to take risk is primarily a function of the client''s:', 'Psychological attitude toward risk', 'Net worth only', 'Tax bracket', 'Beta', 'A', 'Willingness reflects the client''s psychological comfort with risk, distinct from financial ability.', 8),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-ips-q10', 'cfa3-private-wealth-ips', 'An individual IPS should be reviewed:', 'Never', 'Regularly and when circumstances change materially', 'Only at death', 'Daily', 'B', 'Changing life stages and goals require periodic IPS review and updates.', 9),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-ips-q11', 'cfa3-private-wealth-ips', 'Legal/regulatory constraints for individuals include:', 'Trust structures and jurisdictional rules', 'Index reconstitution', 'Beta limits', 'Coupon schedules', 'A', 'Trusts, estate law, and jurisdiction shape the individual''s legal constraints.', 10),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-ips-q12', 'cfa3-private-wealth-ips', 'Mortality and longevity risks affect the IPS mainly through:', 'Allocation and insurance needs', 'The benchmark', 'Trading costs', 'Currency', 'A', 'These human-capital risks shape asset allocation and the need for life insurance/annuities.', 11),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-human-capital-q01', 'cfa3-private-wealth-human-capital', 'Total wealth equals financial capital plus:', 'Human capital (PV of future labor income)', 'The benchmark', 'Beta', 'The coupon', 'A', 'Human capital — the present value of future earnings — is part of total wealth.', 0),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-human-capital-q02', 'cfa3-private-wealth-human-capital', 'An investor with bond-like (stable) human capital should tilt financial assets toward:', 'Bonds', 'Equities', 'Cash', 'Commodities', 'B', 'Stable, bond-like human capital offsets risk, allowing more equities in financial assets.', 1),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-human-capital-q03', 'cfa3-private-wealth-human-capital', 'An investor with equity-like (volatile, market-correlated) human capital should tilt financial assets toward:', 'Equities', 'Bonds', 'More of the same risky assets', 'Leverage', 'B', 'Risky, market-correlated human capital argues for more bonds to balance total-wealth risk.', 2),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-human-capital-q04', 'cfa3-private-wealth-human-capital', 'Over the life cycle, human capital generally:', 'Rises with age', 'Declines with age as financial capital grows', 'Stays constant', 'Is zero', 'B', 'As people age, remaining earning years fall, so human capital declines while savings accumulate.', 3),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-human-capital-q05', 'cfa3-private-wealth-human-capital', 'Consequently, the equity share of financial assets typically:', 'Rises with age', 'Falls over the life cycle (a glide path)', 'Stays at 100%', 'Is irrelevant', 'B', 'As bond-like human capital shrinks, financial assets are de-risked toward bonds over time.', 4),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-human-capital-q06', 'cfa3-private-wealth-human-capital', 'Life insurance primarily hedges:', 'Longevity risk', 'Mortality risk (loss of human capital from early death)', 'Market risk', 'Inflation', 'B', 'Life insurance replaces lost future income if the earner dies prematurely.', 5),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-human-capital-q07', 'cfa3-private-wealth-human-capital', 'Annuities primarily hedge:', 'Mortality risk', 'Longevity risk (outliving assets)', 'Currency risk', 'Credit risk', 'B', 'Annuities provide lifetime income, hedging the risk of living longer than planned.', 6),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-human-capital-q08', 'cfa3-private-wealth-human-capital', 'Optimal allocation considers human capital''s size and its ____ with risky assets.', 'Correlation', 'Coupon', 'Duration', 'Beta to bonds only', 'A', 'The correlation between human capital and markets affects how much financial risk is appropriate.', 7),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-human-capital-q09', 'cfa3-private-wealth-human-capital', 'A tenured professor''s human capital is best described as:', 'Equity-like', 'Bond-like', 'Cash-like only', 'Worthless', 'B', 'Stable, secure salary makes the professor''s human capital bond-like.', 8),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-human-capital-q10', 'cfa3-private-wealth-human-capital', 'Disability insurance addresses the risk of:', 'Outliving assets', 'Loss of earning ability during working years', 'Market crashes', 'Inflation', 'B', 'Disability cover protects human capital if illness/injury prevents earning income.', 9),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-human-capital-q11', 'cfa3-private-wealth-human-capital', 'A commodities trader''s human capital is most likely:', 'Bond-like', 'Equity-like (volatile, market-correlated)', 'Risk-free', 'Zero', 'B', 'Income tied to markets is volatile and correlated — equity-like human capital.', 10),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-human-capital-q12', 'cfa3-private-wealth-human-capital', 'Young investors typically hold more equities because their:', 'Human capital is large and bond-like', 'Financial capital is large', 'Horizon is short', 'Taxes are higher', 'A', 'Large, bond-like human capital early in life supports a higher equity allocation in financial assets.', 11),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-tax-efficiency-q01', 'cfa3-private-wealth-tax-efficiency', 'Asset location places tax-inefficient assets in:', 'Taxable accounts', 'Tax-advantaged (sheltered) accounts', 'Foreign accounts', 'Cash', 'B', 'Tax-inefficient assets belong in tax-deferred/exempt accounts to reduce tax drag.', 0),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-tax-efficiency-q02', 'cfa3-private-wealth-tax-efficiency', 'Tax-loss harvesting improves after-tax returns by:', 'Realizing losses to offset gains/income', 'Increasing turnover for its own sake', 'Avoiding equities', 'Deferring dividends', 'A', 'Harvesting losses offsets taxable gains, lowering the current tax bill.', 1),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-tax-efficiency-q03', 'cfa3-private-wealth-tax-efficiency', 'Tax drag is most damaging when returns are taxed:', 'Once at the end', 'Annually over long horizons', 'Never', 'Only on cash', 'B', 'Annual taxation reduces the compounding base each year, compounding the drag over time.', 2),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-tax-efficiency-q04', 'cfa3-private-wealth-tax-efficiency', 'A tax-deferred account is most advantageous when future tax rates are expected to be:', 'Higher than today', 'Lower than today', 'The same', 'Zero', 'B', 'Deferring tax helps most when withdrawals are taxed at a lower future rate.', 3),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-tax-efficiency-q05', 'cfa3-private-wealth-tax-efficiency', 'A tax-exempt account (after-tax in, tax-free out) is best when future rates are expected to be:', 'Lower than today', 'Higher than today', 'Zero', 'Irrelevant', 'B', 'Paying tax now is favorable when withdrawals would otherwise face a higher future rate.', 4),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-tax-efficiency-q06', 'cfa3-private-wealth-tax-efficiency', 'The step-up in basis at death can benefit heirs by:', 'Eliminating embedded capital gains', 'Increasing estate taxes', 'Forcing a sale', 'Removing dividends', 'A', 'Resetting basis to fair value at death can erase unrealized gains for heirs.', 5),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-tax-efficiency-q07', 'cfa3-private-wealth-tax-efficiency', 'Holding low-turnover equities in a taxable account reflects:', 'Poor asset location', 'Sound asset location', 'Tax-loss harvesting', 'Estate planning', 'B', 'Tax-efficient assets belong in taxable accounts, preserving sheltered space for inefficient ones.', 6),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-tax-efficiency-q08', 'cfa3-private-wealth-tax-efficiency', 'Taxes reduce both an asset''s return and its:', 'Risk (the government shares gains/losses)', 'Liquidity', 'Duration', 'Coupon', 'A', 'Because the tax authority shares gains and losses, after-tax volatility is lower — optimize after-tax.', 7),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-tax-efficiency-q09', 'cfa3-private-wealth-tax-efficiency', 'Municipal bonds are typically held in:', 'Tax-deferred accounts', 'Taxable accounts (their tax-exempt income is wasted in shelters)', 'Foreign accounts', 'Trusts only', 'B', 'Tax-exempt munis belong in taxable accounts; placing them in shelters wastes their tax advantage.', 8),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-tax-efficiency-q10', 'cfa3-private-wealth-tax-efficiency', 'Lower portfolio turnover improves tax efficiency by:', 'Realizing more gains', 'Deferring capital gains taxes', 'Increasing dividends', 'Raising the tax rate', 'B', 'Holding positions longer defers capital-gains realization, improving after-tax compounding.', 9),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-tax-efficiency-q11', 'cfa3-private-wealth-tax-efficiency', 'Rebalancing corridors in taxable accounts should generally be:', 'Narrower', 'Wider (to limit taxable trades)', 'Zero', 'Irrelevant', 'B', 'Tax costs of trading justify wider bands in taxable accounts.', 10),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-tax-efficiency-q12', 'cfa3-private-wealth-tax-efficiency', 'High-turnover or REIT holdings are best located in:', 'Taxable accounts', 'Tax-advantaged accounts', 'Cash', 'Foreign accounts', 'B', 'These tax-inefficient assets belong in sheltered accounts to minimize tax drag.', 11),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-estate-planning-q01', 'cfa3-private-wealth-estate-planning', 'Lifetime gifting can reduce estate taxes primarily by:', 'Removing future appreciation from the estate', 'Increasing the taxable estate', 'Raising the donor''s income', 'Eliminating the gift''s value', 'A', 'Gifting transfers an asset and its future growth out of the estate, often at lower transfer-tax cost.', 0),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-estate-planning-q02', 'cfa3-private-wealth-estate-planning', 'An irrevocable trust differs from a revocable trust in that the grantor:', 'Can freely revoke it', 'Generally gives up control, often removing assets from the estate', 'Pays no setup cost', 'Retains full ownership', 'B', 'Irrevocable trusts remove assets from the grantor''s control and usually the taxable estate.', 1),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-estate-planning-q03', 'cfa3-private-wealth-estate-planning', 'Life insurance in estate planning is often used to:', 'Avoid all taxes', 'Provide liquidity to pay estate taxes', 'Increase the estate''s size only', 'Replace the IPS', 'B', 'Insurance proceeds (often in an irrevocable trust) supply liquidity for estate taxes/expenses.', 2),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-estate-planning-q04', 'cfa3-private-wealth-estate-planning', 'The choice between gifting and bequeathing depends on relative:', 'After-tax values (gift vs estate tax, growth, rates)', 'Tickers', 'Auditors', 'Coupons', 'A', 'Compare the after-tax value of gifting now versus bequeathing later, given tax rates and growth.', 3),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-estate-planning-q05', 'cfa3-private-wealth-estate-planning', 'A bequest at death may carry a:', 'Step-up in basis', 'Gift-tax credit only', 'Negative basis', 'Mandatory sale', 'A', 'Assets passed at death often receive a basis step-up, potentially eliminating embedded gains.', 4),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-estate-planning-q06', 'cfa3-private-wealth-estate-planning', 'Charitable trusts and donor-advised funds are tools for:', 'Reducing diversification', 'Tax-efficient charitable giving', 'Increasing turnover', 'Avoiding the IPS', 'B', 'These vehicles facilitate philanthropic goals with favorable tax treatment.', 5),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-estate-planning-q07', 'cfa3-private-wealth-estate-planning', 'Forced heirship rules:', 'Are identical worldwide', 'Vary by jurisdiction and can constrain bequests', 'Apply only to trusts', 'Eliminate estate tax', 'B', 'Some jurisdictions mandate minimum inheritances to certain heirs, constraining estate plans.', 6),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-estate-planning-q08', 'cfa3-private-wealth-estate-planning', 'Gifting is more attractive when the asset is expected to:', 'Decline in value', 'Appreciate significantly', 'Stay flat', 'Pay no return', 'B', 'Removing a high-growth asset early shifts more future appreciation out of the estate.', 7),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-estate-planning-q09', 'cfa3-private-wealth-estate-planning', 'Holding an appreciated asset until death rather than selling can be favorable because of:', 'Higher turnover', 'The step-up in basis eliminating the gain', 'Larger dividends', 'Lower returns', 'B', 'The basis step-up at death can erase the unrealized gain, avoiding capital-gains tax.', 8),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-estate-planning-q10', 'cfa3-private-wealth-estate-planning', 'A revocable trust''s assets are generally:', 'Outside the taxable estate', 'Inside the estate (grantor retains control)', 'Tax-free', 'Owned by charity', 'B', 'Because the grantor can revoke it, a revocable trust''s assets remain in the taxable estate.', 9),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-estate-planning-q11', 'cfa3-private-wealth-estate-planning', 'Estate planning should be coordinated with the IPS to ensure:', 'Liquidity for taxes and alignment with legacy goals', 'Maximum turnover', 'The highest beta', 'No diversification', 'A', 'Plans must provide liquidity for transfer taxes and reflect the client''s legacy objectives.', 10),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-estate-planning-q12', 'cfa3-private-wealth-estate-planning', 'Generation-skipping transfers are designed to:', 'Pass wealth to grandchildren, potentially avoiding a layer of estate tax', 'Increase current income', 'Eliminate trusts', 'Raise turnover', 'A', 'Skipping a generation can avoid taxing the estate twice, subject to GST tax rules.', 11),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-risk-insurance-q01', 'cfa3-private-wealth-risk-insurance', 'Insurance is most appropriate for risks that are:', 'High-frequency, low-severity', 'Low-frequency, high-severity', 'Certain', 'Trivial', 'B', 'Transfer (insure) low-probability, high-severity risks; retain small, frequent ones.', 0),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-risk-insurance-q02', 'cfa3-private-wealth-risk-insurance', 'Term life insurance differs from whole life in that term:', 'Builds cash value', 'Provides pure death-benefit cover for a period, no cash value', 'Is permanent', 'Hedges longevity', 'B', 'Term life is temporary protection without a savings/cash-value component.', 1),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-risk-insurance-q03', 'cfa3-private-wealth-risk-insurance', 'An immediate annuity primarily provides:', 'Lump-sum death benefit', 'Guaranteed lifetime income (hedging longevity)', 'Disability cover', 'Property protection', 'B', 'Annuities convert wealth into lifetime income, hedging the risk of outliving assets.', 2),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-risk-insurance-q04', 'cfa3-private-wealth-risk-insurance', 'The four basic risk-management responses are retain, reduce/avoid, transfer and:', 'Ignore', 'Self-insure (where wealth allows)', 'Leverage', 'Speculate', 'B', 'Risks are retained, reduced/avoided, transferred (insured), or self-insured.', 3),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-risk-insurance-q05', 'cfa3-private-wealth-risk-insurance', 'The economic (holistic) balance sheet includes, beyond financial assets:', 'Human capital and the PV of pensions/liabilities', 'Only cash', 'Only equities', 'The benchmark', 'A', 'It adds human capital and the present value of pensions and future liabilities.', 4),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-risk-insurance-q06', 'cfa3-private-wealth-risk-insurance', 'Life insurance and annuities are complementary because they hedge:', 'The same risk', 'Opposite risks (early death vs long life)', 'Only market risk', 'Currency risk', 'B', 'Life insurance hedges premature death; annuities hedge longevity — opposite human-capital risks.', 5),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-risk-insurance-q07', 'cfa3-private-wealth-risk-insurance', 'Umbrella liability insurance protects against:', 'Market losses', 'Large personal liability claims beyond standard policy limits', 'Longevity', 'Inflation', 'B', 'Umbrella coverage adds liability protection above home/auto policy limits.', 6),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-risk-insurance-q08', 'cfa3-private-wealth-risk-insurance', 'Small, frequent, affordable losses are best:', 'Insured fully', 'Retained (self-funded)', 'Avoided entirely', 'Transferred', 'B', 'It is inefficient to insure small, frequent losses; retain them.', 7),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-risk-insurance-q09', 'cfa3-private-wealth-risk-insurance', 'A young breadwinner with dependents most needs:', 'An annuity', 'Life and disability insurance', 'No insurance', 'Only property cover', 'B', 'Large human capital and dependents make life/disability cover a priority.', 8),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-risk-insurance-q10', 'cfa3-private-wealth-risk-insurance', 'A retiree''s chief financial risk is often:', 'Longevity risk', 'Mortality risk', 'Disability risk', 'Earnings risk', 'A', 'Retirees worry most about outliving their assets (longevity), which annuities address.', 9),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-risk-insurance-q11', 'cfa3-private-wealth-risk-insurance', 'A needs analysis sizes life insurance from the:', 'Gap between resources and obligations', 'Stock price', 'Benchmark', 'Coupon', 'A', 'Coverage equals the shortfall between available resources and the family''s financial obligations.', 10),
  ('cfa3-private-wealth-risk-insurance-q12', 'cfa3-private-wealth-risk-insurance', 'Whole/permanent life insurance, unlike term, includes a:', 'Cash-value (savings) component', 'Disability rider only', 'Property cover', 'Longevity hedge', 'A', 'Permanent policies build cash value alongside the death benefit.', 11),
  ('cfa3-institutional-db-pension-q01', 'cfa3-institutional-db-pension', 'A DB plan has higher risk tolerance when the workforce is:', 'Older and the plan underfunded', 'Younger and the plan well funded', 'Near retirement', 'Shrinking with a weak sponsor', 'B', 'A younger workforce and strong funded status lengthen the horizon and raise risk capacity.', 0),
  ('cfa3-institutional-db-pension-q02', 'cfa3-institutional-db-pension', 'Because pension liabilities are bond-like, plans commonly use:', 'Pure equity indexing', 'Liability-driven investing (duration matching)', 'Currency speculation', 'Commodities only', 'B', 'LDI hedges the interest-rate sensitivity of the bond-like liabilities.', 1),
  ('cfa3-institutional-db-pension-q03', 'cfa3-institutional-db-pension', 'A higher proportion of retired lives tends to:', 'Lower liquidity needs', 'Raise liquidity needs and lower risk tolerance', 'Extend the horizon', 'Raise equity allocation', 'B', 'More retirees means larger, nearer-term benefit payments, increasing liquidity needs.', 2),
  ('cfa3-institutional-db-pension-q04', 'cfa3-institutional-db-pension', 'High correlation between plan assets and the sponsor''s business argues for:', 'More investment risk', 'Less investment risk', 'No effect', 'Only currency risk', 'B', 'Correlated risks concentrate exposure, so the plan should be more conservative.', 3),
  ('cfa3-institutional-db-pension-q05', 'cfa3-institutional-db-pension', 'A DB plan''s minimum return objective is to:', 'Beat equities', 'Fund the liabilities (meet the actuarial/discount rate)', 'Maximize turnover', 'Match cash', 'B', 'At minimum, the plan must earn enough to fund its promised benefits.', 4),
  ('cfa3-institutional-db-pension-q06', 'cfa3-institutional-db-pension', 'DB plans are generally:', 'Heavily taxed', 'Tax-exempt', 'Subject to capital-gains tax only', 'Taxed like individuals', 'B', 'Qualified pension plans are typically tax-exempt.', 5),
  ('cfa3-institutional-db-pension-q07', 'cfa3-institutional-db-pension', 'As a plan''s funded ratio improves, a common strategy is to:', 'Take more equity risk', 'De-risk (glide path) toward liability-hedging assets', 'Stop investing', 'Ignore liabilities', 'B', 'Better-funded plans lock in gains by shifting toward hedging assets along a glide path.', 6),
  ('cfa3-institutional-db-pension-q08', 'cfa3-institutional-db-pension', 'Inflation-indexed pension benefits are best hedged with:', 'Nominal bonds', 'Inflation-linked bonds', 'Equities only', 'Cash', 'B', 'Linkers hedge inflation-sensitive liabilities better than nominal bonds.', 7),
  ('cfa3-institutional-db-pension-q09', 'cfa3-institutional-db-pension', 'A financially weak plan sponsor reduces the plan''s:', 'Risk tolerance', 'Liquidity needs', 'Time horizon', 'Tax rate', 'A', 'A weak sponsor that may not fund shortfalls lowers the plan''s ability to take risk.', 8),
  ('cfa3-institutional-db-pension-q10', 'cfa3-institutional-db-pension', 'Surplus risk refers to volatility in:', 'Assets minus liabilities', 'Coupons', 'Dividends', 'The benchmark', 'A', 'Surplus risk is the volatility of the funded surplus (assets − liabilities).', 9),
  ('cfa3-institutional-db-pension-q11', 'cfa3-institutional-db-pension', 'A smaller plan relative to the sponsor''s size implies:', 'Lower risk tolerance', 'Higher risk tolerance (the sponsor can absorb shortfalls)', 'No effect', 'Mandatory de-risking', 'B', 'When the plan is small relative to the sponsor, the sponsor can more easily fund shortfalls, raising risk capacity.', 10),
  ('cfa3-institutional-db-pension-q12', 'cfa3-institutional-db-pension', 'The return-seeking portfolio in an LDI structure is meant to:', 'Hedge the liabilities', 'Generate growth to improve the funded ratio', 'Hold only cash', 'Match duration', 'B', 'Alongside the hedging sleeve, the return-seeking portfolio pursues growth to close/maintain funding.', 11),
  ('cfa3-institutional-foundations-q01', 'cfa3-institutional-foundations', 'Endowments and foundations generally have a ____ time horizon.', 'Short', 'Very long/perpetual', 'One-year', 'Zero', 'B', 'Supporting a mission in perpetuity gives these institutions very long horizons.', 0),
  ('cfa3-institutional-foundations-q02', 'cfa3-institutional-foundations', 'A typical endowment return objective is approximately:', 'The risk-free rate', 'Spending rate + inflation + fees', 'Zero', 'The dividend yield', 'B', 'To preserve real value and fund spending, required return ≈ spending + inflation + costs.', 1),
  ('cfa3-institutional-foundations-q03', 'cfa3-institutional-foundations', 'US private foundations must generally distribute about:', '1% annually', '5% of assets annually', '20% annually', 'Nothing', 'B', 'US private foundations typically must pay out ~5% of assets per year.', 2),
  ('cfa3-institutional-foundations-q04', 'cfa3-institutional-foundations', 'A rolling 3-year average spending rule is used to:', 'Increase spending volatility', 'Smooth distributions vs a simple percentage rule', 'Eliminate spending', 'Track one year', 'B', 'Averaging asset values over years reduces year-to-year spending volatility.', 3),
  ('cfa3-institutional-foundations-q05', 'cfa3-institutional-foundations', 'The endowment model emphasizes:', 'Cash and T-bills', 'Equities and alternative investments', 'Short-term deposits', 'A single bond', 'B', 'Long horizons and high risk tolerance support heavy equity/alternatives allocations.', 4),
  ('cfa3-institutional-foundations-q06', 'cfa3-institutional-foundations', 'Endowments and foundations are generally:', 'Heavily taxed', 'Largely tax-exempt', 'Taxed like individuals', 'Subject to VAT', 'B', 'These institutions are typically tax-exempt.', 5),
  ('cfa3-institutional-foundations-q07', 'cfa3-institutional-foundations', 'A constraint created by large illiquid alternatives allocations is:', 'No liquidity issues', 'Reduced liquidity to meet spending in stress', 'Higher taxes', 'Shorter horizon', 'B', 'Heavy private/illiquid holdings can strain liquidity when distributions are due in downturns.', 6),
  ('cfa3-institutional-foundations-q08', 'cfa3-institutional-foundations', 'The geometric (Yale) spending rule weights:', 'Only current market value', 'Prior spending (inflation-adjusted) and a % of current market value', 'The risk-free rate', 'Coupons', 'B', 'The Yale rule blends last year''s spending and a percentage of market value to stabilize payouts.', 7),
  ('cfa3-institutional-foundations-q09', 'cfa3-institutional-foundations', 'Endowments aim to preserve the corpus''s ____ value over time.', 'Nominal', 'Real (inflation-adjusted)', 'Book', 'Par', 'B', 'They target maintaining the inflation-adjusted (real) value to support spending forever.', 8),
  ('cfa3-institutional-foundations-q10', 'cfa3-institutional-foundations', 'A simple percentage-of-beginning-assets spending rule is criticized for:', 'Smoothing spending too much', 'Volatile spending tied to market swings', 'Ignoring assets', 'Being illegal', 'B', 'Tying spending to current asset values makes distributions swing with markets.', 9),
  ('cfa3-institutional-foundations-q11', 'cfa3-institutional-foundations', 'Donor restrictions on gifts are an example of which constraint?', 'Legal/regulatory (and unique)', 'Time horizon', 'Taxes', 'Beta', 'A', 'Donor-imposed restrictions create legal/unique constraints on how funds may be invested/spent.', 10),
  ('cfa3-institutional-foundations-q12', 'cfa3-institutional-foundations', 'Compared with a foundation, a university endowment''s spending also funds:', 'A meaningful share of the operating budget', 'Only grants', 'Nothing', 'Taxes', 'A', 'University endowments often support a significant portion of the institution''s operating budget.', 11),
  ('cfa3-institutional-insurance-q01', 'cfa3-institutional-insurance', 'Insurance companies are fundamentally:', 'Liability-driven investors', 'Return-maximizing with no liabilities', 'Tax-exempt always', 'Short-horizon traders', 'A', 'Insurers invest to fund future claims, making them liability-driven and heavily regulated.', 0),
  ('cfa3-institutional-insurance-q02', 'cfa3-institutional-insurance', 'Life insurers'' liabilities are typically:', 'Short and lumpy', 'Long-duration and relatively predictable', 'Zero', 'Equity-like', 'B', 'Life liabilities are long-dated and fairly predictable, favoring long bonds and ALM.', 1),
  ('cfa3-institutional-insurance-q03', 'cfa3-institutional-insurance', 'Property-casualty insurers, versus life insurers, need:', 'Less liquidity', 'More liquidity (claims are lumpy/uncertain)', 'Longer-duration assets', 'No bonds', 'B', 'P&C claims (e.g., catastrophes) are uncertain and lumpy, requiring higher liquidity and shorter assets.', 2),
  ('cfa3-institutional-insurance-q04', 'cfa3-institutional-insurance', 'A key risk for life insurers is:', 'Interest-rate/disintermediation risk', 'Catastrophe risk only', 'No risk', 'Equity-only risk', 'A', 'Rate moves affect both asset values and policyholder behavior (disintermediation), a core life-insurer risk.', 3),
  ('cfa3-institutional-insurance-q05', 'cfa3-institutional-insurance', 'Insurers generally have a ____ risk tolerance for reserves backing liabilities.', 'High', 'Low (protect solvency/policyholders)', 'Unlimited', 'Negative', 'B', 'Reserves are invested conservatively to protect policyholders and meet solvency requirements.', 4),
  ('cfa3-institutional-insurance-q06', 'cfa3-institutional-insurance', 'Regulatory capital regimes (e.g., RBC, Solvency II) influence insurers by:', 'Encouraging only equities', 'Driving asset choices toward higher-quality/liquid holdings', 'Removing all constraints', 'Eliminating ALM', 'B', 'Capital rules penalize risky assets, steering insurers toward high-grade, liquid securities.', 5),
  ('cfa3-institutional-insurance-q07', 'cfa3-institutional-insurance', 'The insurer''s ''float'' refers to:', 'Premiums invested before claims are paid', 'A type of bond', 'Equity capital', 'A derivative', 'A', 'Float is the premium money held and invested until claims come due.', 6),
  ('cfa3-institutional-insurance-q08', 'cfa3-institutional-insurance', 'A life insurer''s surplus portfolio, vs reserves, can:', 'Take more investment risk (e.g., equities)', 'Take less risk', 'Hold only cash', 'Not be invested', 'A', 'Surplus (capital beyond reserves) can bear more risk than the assets backing liabilities.', 7),
  ('cfa3-institutional-insurance-q09', 'cfa3-institutional-insurance', 'Asset-liability management for insurers focuses on:', 'Maximizing turnover', 'Matching asset and liability cash flows/durations', 'Currency speculation', 'Ignoring liabilities', 'B', 'ALM aligns the timing/duration of assets with liabilities to manage interest-rate risk.', 8),
  ('cfa3-institutional-insurance-q10', 'cfa3-institutional-insurance', 'P&C insurers typically hold ____ duration assets than life insurers.', 'Longer', 'Shorter', 'Identical', 'Zero', 'B', 'Shorter, uncertain P&C liabilities call for shorter-duration, more liquid assets.', 9),
  ('cfa3-institutional-insurance-q11', 'cfa3-institutional-insurance', 'Insurers are generally:', 'Tax-exempt', 'Taxable, with complex tax rules', 'Subject to no regulation', 'Individuals', 'B', 'Insurance companies are taxable entities facing complex, regime-specific tax treatment.', 10),
  ('cfa3-institutional-insurance-q12', 'cfa3-institutional-insurance', 'Credit quality is emphasized in insurer portfolios mainly to:', 'Maximize yield at any risk', 'Protect solvency and meet capital requirements', 'Increase turnover', 'Avoid bonds', 'B', 'High credit quality supports solvency and reduces regulatory capital charges.', 11),
  ('cfa3-institutional-banks-q01', 'cfa3-institutional-banks', 'A bank''s securities portfolio is primarily managed to:', 'Maximize return at any risk', 'Provide liquidity and manage the duration gap (ALM)', 'Speculate on equities', 'Avoid regulation', 'B', 'The securities book serves liquidity, collateral, and interest-rate (ALM) management within regulatory limits.', 0),
  ('cfa3-institutional-banks-q02', 'cfa3-institutional-banks', 'The duration gap is the mismatch between the durations of a bank''s:', 'Assets and liabilities', 'Equity and debt only', 'Two benchmarks', 'Loans and dividends', 'A', 'ALM manages the gap between asset (loans/securities) and liability (deposits/borrowings) durations.', 1),
  ('cfa3-institutional-banks-q03', 'cfa3-institutional-banks', 'Banks favor high-quality, liquid securities partly because:', 'They have the highest yield', 'Regulatory (Basel) rules and liquidity needs reward them', 'They are tax-free', 'They have no duration', 'B', 'Capital/liquidity rules (risk weights, LCR/NSFR) steer banks toward governments/agencies.', 2),
  ('cfa3-institutional-banks-q04', 'cfa3-institutional-banks', 'Net interest margin is the difference between:', 'Interest earned on assets and interest paid on liabilities', 'Dividends and coupons', 'Two benchmarks', 'Equity and debt', 'A', 'NIM measures the spread the bank earns between its assets'' yield and its funding cost.', 3),
  ('cfa3-institutional-banks-q05', 'cfa3-institutional-banks', 'The risk tolerance of a bank''s securities book is generally:', 'High', 'Low (it backs deposits and regulatory needs)', 'Unlimited', 'Negative', 'B', 'Because it supports liquidity and capital requirements, the securities portfolio is conservative.', 4),
  ('cfa3-institutional-banks-q06', 'cfa3-institutional-banks', 'Banks use interest-rate swaps and futures to:', 'Speculate on equities', 'Manage the duration gap / interest-rate risk', 'Avoid taxes', 'Increase the NIM with no risk', 'B', 'Derivatives adjust the duration gap and hedge interest-rate risk efficiently.', 5),
  ('cfa3-institutional-banks-q07', 'cfa3-institutional-banks', 'A positive duration gap (assets longer than liabilities) means rising rates will:', 'Increase the bank''s net worth', 'Decrease the bank''s economic net worth', 'Have no effect', 'Raise the NIM immediately', 'B', 'If assets are more rate-sensitive (longer) than liabilities, rising rates lower asset values more, reducing net worth.', 6),
  ('cfa3-institutional-banks-q08', 'cfa3-institutional-banks', 'Liquidity regulations affecting banks include the:', 'LCR and NSFR', 'P/E ratio', 'Sharpe ratio', 'Cap rate', 'A', 'The Liquidity Coverage Ratio and Net Stable Funding Ratio are key Basel liquidity rules.', 7),
  ('cfa3-institutional-banks-q09', 'cfa3-institutional-banks', 'Banks are generally:', 'Tax-exempt', 'Taxable', 'Individuals', 'Unregulated', 'B', 'Banks are taxable, regulated entities.', 8),
  ('cfa3-institutional-banks-q10', 'cfa3-institutional-banks', 'The bank securities portfolio also provides:', 'Collateral for borrowing and regulatory liquidity', 'Only dividend income', 'Equity upside only', 'No function', 'A', 'High-quality securities serve as collateral and count toward liquidity requirements.', 9),
  ('cfa3-institutional-banks-q11', 'cfa3-institutional-banks', 'Managing the duration gap toward zero aims to:', 'Maximize interest-rate risk', 'Immunize net worth against interest-rate moves', 'Increase turnover', 'Avoid all bonds', 'B', 'A zero duration gap makes the bank''s net worth approximately insensitive to small rate changes.', 10),
  ('cfa3-institutional-banks-q12', 'cfa3-institutional-banks', 'A bank''s time horizon for its securities book is tied to its:', 'Liability (deposit/funding) structure', 'Dividend policy', 'Equity beta', 'Auditor', 'A', 'The funding/liability structure drives the appropriate horizon and duration of the securities portfolio.', 11),
  ('cfa3-institutional-sovereign-q01', 'cfa3-institutional-sovereign', 'A budget-stabilization sovereign wealth fund typically has a:', 'Long horizon and high risk tolerance', 'Short horizon, low risk, high liquidity', 'Perpetual horizon', 'No objective', 'B', 'Stabilization funds smooth revenue swings, so they stay short, conservative, and liquid.', 0),
  ('cfa3-institutional-sovereign-q02', 'cfa3-institutional-sovereign', 'A savings/future-generations SWF generally has:', 'A very long horizon and high risk tolerance', 'A one-year horizon', 'No risk tolerance', 'Daily liquidity needs', 'A', 'Savings funds preserve wealth across generations, allowing long horizons and aggressive allocations.', 1),
  ('cfa3-institutional-sovereign-q03', 'cfa3-institutional-sovereign', 'A development SWF aims to:', 'Fund domestic economic projects', 'Smooth budget revenue', 'Hold only cash', 'Pay dividends', 'A', 'Development funds invest to support domestic economic development goals.', 2),
  ('cfa3-institutional-sovereign-q04', 'cfa3-institutional-sovereign', 'A reserve-investment SWF is created to:', 'Earn higher returns on excess FX reserves', 'Pay current pensions', 'Fund a single project', 'Avoid taxes', 'A', 'Reserve-investment funds seek better returns on reserves beyond traditional liquidity holdings.', 3),
  ('cfa3-institutional-sovereign-q05', 'cfa3-institutional-sovereign', 'A pension-reserve SWF is liability-aware because it:', 'Pre-funds future pension obligations', 'Has no liabilities', 'Is a stabilization fund', 'Holds only equities', 'A', 'Pension reserve funds set aside assets to meet future pension liabilities, so they consider those liabilities.', 4),
  ('cfa3-institutional-sovereign-q06', 'cfa3-institutional-sovereign', 'Long-horizon savings SWFs most resemble:', 'Liquidity reserves', 'Endowments (equity/alternatives heavy)', 'Banks', 'P&C insurers', 'B', 'Their perpetual, growth-oriented mandate mirrors the endowment model.', 5),
  ('cfa3-institutional-sovereign-q07', 'cfa3-institutional-sovereign', 'Stabilization funds most resemble:', 'Endowments', 'Short-duration liquidity reserves', 'Equity hedge funds', 'Private equity', 'B', 'Their need to deploy funds on short notice makes them like conservative liquidity portfolios.', 6),
  ('cfa3-institutional-sovereign-q08', 'cfa3-institutional-sovereign', 'The Santiago Principles relate to SWF:', 'Tax rates', 'Governance and transparency best practices', 'Currency hedging', 'Duration matching', 'B', 'The Santiago Principles set voluntary governance/transparency standards for sovereign wealth funds.', 7),
  ('cfa3-institutional-sovereign-q09', 'cfa3-institutional-sovereign', 'A key non-financial constraint for SWFs is:', 'Political scrutiny and governance', 'Dividend dates', 'Coupon schedules', 'Beta limits', 'A', 'Political oversight, governance, and transparency are significant constraints for state-owned funds.', 8),
  ('cfa3-institutional-sovereign-q10', 'cfa3-institutional-sovereign', 'Liquidity needs are highest for which SWF type?', 'Savings', 'Budget stabilization', 'Development', 'Pension reserve', 'B', 'Stabilization funds must be ready to deploy on short notice, so liquidity needs are highest.', 9),
  ('cfa3-institutional-sovereign-q11', 'cfa3-institutional-sovereign', 'A resource-rich country smoothing oil-revenue swings would use a:', 'Stabilization fund', 'Pension reserve fund', 'Development bank loan', 'Hedge fund', 'A', 'Stabilization funds buffer government budgets against commodity-revenue volatility.', 10),
  ('cfa3-institutional-sovereign-q12', 'cfa3-institutional-sovereign', 'The objective and risk profile of an SWF depend mainly on its:', 'Type/purpose', 'Currency only', 'Auditor', 'Coupon', 'A', 'Each SWF type (stabilization, savings, development, reserve, pension) implies a distinct horizon/risk profile.', 11),
  ('cfa3-ethics-code-standards-q01', 'cfa3-ethics-code-standards', 'When local law is less strict than the Code and Standards, members must follow:', 'Local law', 'The stricter (Code and Standards)', 'Neither', 'Client preference', 'B', 'Standard I(A) requires adhering to the stricter of applicable law or the Code and Standards.', 0),
  ('cfa3-ethics-code-standards-q02', 'cfa3-ethics-code-standards', 'Standard II concerns:', 'Integrity of capital markets (MNPI, manipulation)', 'Duties to employers', 'Conflicts of interest', 'Professionalism', 'A', 'Standard II covers material nonpublic information and market manipulation.', 1),
  ('cfa3-ethics-code-standards-q03', 'cfa3-ethics-code-standards', 'Suitability falls under which Standard?', 'III Duties to Clients', 'I Professionalism', 'VI Conflicts', 'VII Member responsibilities', 'A', 'Suitability is Standard III(C), part of Duties to Clients.', 2),
  ('cfa3-ethics-code-standards-q04', 'cfa3-ethics-code-standards', 'Record retention and diligence/reasonable basis are under Standard:', 'V Investment Analysis, Recommendations & Actions', 'II Integrity of Capital Markets', 'IV Duties to Employers', 'VI Conflicts', 'A', 'Standard V covers diligence/reasonable basis, communication, and record retention.', 3),
  ('cfa3-ethics-code-standards-q05', 'cfa3-ethics-code-standards', 'Priority of transactions is part of Standard:', 'VI Conflicts of Interest', 'I Professionalism', 'III Duties to Clients', 'VII Member responsibilities', 'A', 'Priority of transactions is Standard VI(B).', 4),
  ('cfa3-ethics-code-standards-q06', 'cfa3-ethics-code-standards', 'Conduct in the CFA Program is governed by Standard:', 'VII Responsibilities as a Member/Candidate', 'I Professionalism', 'V Investment Analysis', 'III Duties to Clients', 'A', 'Standard VII addresses conduct that could compromise CFA Institute or the program.', 5),
  ('cfa3-ethics-code-standards-q07', 'cfa3-ethics-code-standards', 'Independence and objectivity is part of Standard:', 'I Professionalism', 'II Integrity of Markets', 'IV Employers', 'VI Conflicts', 'A', 'Independence and objectivity is Standard I(B).', 6),
  ('cfa3-ethics-code-standards-q08', 'cfa3-ethics-code-standards', 'Loyalty to employer and supervisory responsibilities are under Standard:', 'IV Duties to Employers', 'III Duties to Clients', 'I Professionalism', 'V Investment Analysis', 'A', 'Standard IV covers loyalty, additional compensation, and supervisor responsibilities.', 7),
  ('cfa3-ethics-code-standards-q09', 'cfa3-ethics-code-standards', 'The most heavily weighted topic area across all CFA levels is:', 'Derivatives', 'Ethics and Professional Standards', 'Economics', 'Alternative investments', 'B', 'Ethics is consistently the most heavily tested area at every level.', 8),
  ('cfa3-ethics-code-standards-q10', 'cfa3-ethics-code-standards', 'The recommended approach to an ethics vignette is to:', 'Pick the first plausible answer', 'Map actions to the precise sub-standard and choose the least-compliant', 'Ignore the facts', 'Assume all actions comply', 'B', 'Identify each action, match it to the relevant sub-standard, then select the least compliant choice.', 9),
  ('cfa3-ethics-code-standards-q11', 'cfa3-ethics-code-standards', 'The Code of Ethics differs from the Standards in that the Code:', 'Provides specific rules', 'States the broad ethical principles', 'Is optional', 'Applies only to candidates', 'B', 'The Code states principles; the Standards translate them into specific, actionable conduct rules.', 10),
  ('cfa3-ethics-code-standards-q12', 'cfa3-ethics-code-standards', 'If a member knows of an ongoing violation of law, they should:', 'Participate', 'Dissociate from the activity', 'Ignore it', 'Profit from it', 'B', 'Standard I(A) requires members to dissociate from known violations.', 11),
  ('cfa3-ethics-professionalism-q01', 'cfa3-ethics-professionalism', 'Accepting lavish, influence-seeking gifts from a covered company risks violating:', 'I(B) Independence and Objectivity', 'II(B) Manipulation', 'V(C) Records', 'VII Conduct', 'A', 'Lavish benefits that could bias judgment threaten independence and objectivity.', 0),
  ('cfa3-ethics-professionalism-q02', 'cfa3-ethics-professionalism', 'Trading on material nonpublic information violates:', 'II(A) Material Nonpublic Information', 'I(C) Misrepresentation', 'IV(A) Loyalty', 'VI(B) Priority', 'A', 'Acting on MNPI breaches Standard II(A).', 1),
  ('cfa3-ethics-professionalism-q03', 'cfa3-ethics-professionalism', 'The mosaic theory permits combining:', 'Insider tips and rumors', 'Public and non-material nonpublic information', 'Two material nonpublic items', 'Client and personal trades', 'B', 'Conclusions from public plus non-material nonpublic information are permissible under the mosaic theory.', 2),
  ('cfa3-ethics-professionalism-q04', 'cfa3-ethics-professionalism', 'Wash trades and spoofing are examples of:', 'Market manipulation [II(B)]', 'Fair dealing', 'Diligence', 'Record retention', 'A', 'Transaction-based manipulation (wash trades, spoofing) violates II(B).', 3),
  ('cfa3-ethics-professionalism-q05', 'cfa3-ethics-professionalism', 'Plagiarizing another''s report violates:', 'I(C) Misrepresentation', 'II(A) MNPI', 'IV(A) Loyalty', 'VI(C) Referral fees', 'A', 'Plagiarism is a misrepresentation under Standard I(C).', 4),
  ('cfa3-ethics-professionalism-q06', 'cfa3-ethics-professionalism', 'Firewalls are a recommended procedure to comply with:', 'II(A) Material Nonpublic Information', 'III(C) Suitability', 'V(C) Records', 'VI(B) Priority', 'A', 'Information barriers prevent improper flow/use of MNPI, supporting II(A).', 5),
  ('cfa3-ethics-professionalism-q07', 'cfa3-ethics-professionalism', 'Guaranteeing a specific return on a volatile fund violates:', 'I(C) Misrepresentation', 'II(A) MNPI', 'IV(B) Compensation', 'VI(A) Disclosure', 'A', 'Promising a return on a risky investment is a misrepresentation under I(C).', 6),
  ('cfa3-ethics-professionalism-q08', 'cfa3-ethics-professionalism', 'A ''pump-and-dump'' scheme is:', 'Information-based market manipulation', 'Fair dealing', 'Suitability analysis', 'Independence', 'A', 'Spreading false positives to inflate a price then selling is information-based manipulation [II(B)].', 7),
  ('cfa3-ethics-professionalism-q09', 'cfa3-ethics-professionalism', 'A modest meal and a branded pen at an investor day are:', 'A violation requiring client disclosure', 'Acceptable token items', 'Market manipulation', 'Misconduct', 'B', 'Token items unlikely to bias judgment are acceptable; the concern is lavish/influence-seeking benefits.', 8),
  ('cfa3-ethics-professionalism-q10', 'cfa3-ethics-professionalism', 'Dishonesty or fraud reflecting on professional integrity violates:', 'I(D) Misconduct', 'II(A) MNPI', 'III(B) Fair Dealing', 'VI(A) Disclosure', 'A', 'Standard I(D) Misconduct addresses dishonest/fraudulent conduct affecting professional reputation.', 9),
  ('cfa3-ethics-professionalism-q11', 'cfa3-ethics-professionalism', 'Using recognized government statistical data without attribution is:', 'Always plagiarism', 'Permitted (factual/statistical data need not be cited)', 'A violation of independence', 'Manipulation', 'B', 'Recognized factual/statistical data may be used without attribution; plagiarism concerns others'' analysis.', 10),
  ('cfa3-ethics-professionalism-q12', 'cfa3-ethics-professionalism', 'Pressure from an employer to issue a biased rating should be:', 'Followed', 'Resisted to maintain independence and objectivity [I(B)]', 'Disclosed only to the SEC', 'Ignored', 'B', 'Members must keep independent, objective opinions despite internal pressure.', 11),
  ('cfa3-ethics-duties-clients-q01', 'cfa3-ethics-duties-clients', 'Under III(A), a pension manager''s loyalty runs primarily to the:', 'Plan sponsor''s executives', 'Plan beneficiaries', 'Brokerage firm', 'Regulator', 'B', 'Loyalty, Prudence and Care requires acting for the ultimate beneficiaries.', 0),
  ('cfa3-ethics-duties-clients-q02', 'cfa3-ethics-duties-clients', 'Tipping a favored client before others violates:', 'III(B) Fair Dealing', 'I(B) Independence', 'VI(B) Priority', 'IV(A) Loyalty', 'A', 'Fair Dealing requires fair dissemination of recommendations and changes.', 1),
  ('cfa3-ethics-duties-clients-q03', 'cfa3-ethics-duties-clients', 'Suitability [III(C)] requires matching recommendations to the client''s:', 'Objectives and constraints (IPS)', 'Broker''s targets', 'Country only', 'Tax bracket only', 'A', 'Suitability ties recommendations to each client''s IPS and, in advisory relationships, the total portfolio.', 2),
  ('cfa3-ethics-duties-clients-q04', 'cfa3-ethics-duties-clients', 'Performance presentation [III(D)] must be:', 'Cherry-picked', 'Fair, accurate and complete', 'Only the best account', 'Hypothetical', 'B', 'Performance must be presented fairly, accurately, and completely.', 3),
  ('cfa3-ethics-duties-clients-q05', 'cfa3-ethics-duties-clients', 'Confidentiality [III(E)] applies to:', 'Only current clients', 'Current, former, and prospective clients', 'No one', 'Competitors', 'B', 'Confidentiality extends to current, former, and prospective clients (subject to legal exceptions).', 4),
  ('cfa3-ethics-duties-clients-q06', 'cfa3-ethics-duties-clients', 'Soft dollars (client brokerage) must be used:', 'For firm overhead', 'For the client''s benefit (research)', 'For personal travel', 'However the manager wishes', 'B', 'Client brokerage belongs to the client and must fund research benefiting the investment process.', 5),
  ('cfa3-ethics-duties-clients-q07', 'cfa3-ethics-duties-clients', 'Best execution must not be sacrificed to:', 'Reduce client taxes', 'Direct soft-dollar business to a broker', 'Comply with the law', 'Diversify', 'B', 'Managers must seek best execution; soft-dollar arrangements cannot override it.', 6),
  ('cfa3-ethics-duties-clients-q08', 'cfa3-ethics-duties-clients', 'Confidential client information may be disclosed when:', 'A friend asks', 'Legally required or concerning illegal activity', 'It benefits the manager', 'Never', 'B', 'Disclosure is permitted when required by law or to report illegal client activity.', 7),
  ('cfa3-ethics-duties-clients-q09', 'cfa3-ethics-duties-clients', 'Fair dealing requires that recommendation changes be communicated:', 'To large clients first', 'Fairly to all clients', 'Only to the manager''s account', 'After trading personally', 'B', 'All clients must be treated fairly when disseminating changes.', 8),
  ('cfa3-ethics-duties-clients-q10', 'cfa3-ethics-duties-clients', 'In an advisory relationship, suitability considers the:', 'Single trade alone', 'Client''s total portfolio', 'Manager''s portfolio', 'Benchmark only', 'B', 'Suitability is judged in the context of the client''s overall portfolio, not the trade in isolation.', 9),
  ('cfa3-ethics-duties-clients-q11', 'cfa3-ethics-duties-clients', 'Loyalty, Prudence and Care requires the manager to:', 'Favor the employer', 'Act with reasonable care in the client''s interest', 'Maximize commissions', 'Trade ahead of clients', 'B', 'III(A) requires prudence and care, placing the client''s interest first.', 10),
  ('cfa3-ethics-duties-clients-q12', 'cfa3-ethics-duties-clients', 'Selectively presenting only top-performing composites violates:', 'III(D) Performance Presentation', 'II(A) MNPI', 'I(A) Knowledge of Law', 'VI(C) Referral Fees', 'A', 'Cherry-picking performance breaches the fair, accurate, complete requirement of III(D).', 11),
  ('cfa3-ethics-conflicts-q01', 'cfa3-ethics-conflicts', 'Standard VI(A) requires members to ____ conflicts of interest.', 'Conceal', 'Fully and fairly disclose', 'Ignore if small', 'Disclose only to regulators', 'B', 'Conflicts must be disclosed prominently, fully, and fairly in plain language.', 0),
  ('cfa3-ethics-conflicts-q02', 'cfa3-ethics-conflicts', 'Priority of transactions [VI(B)] requires that:', 'The member trades first', 'Client and employer trades take precedence over the member''s own', 'All trades are simultaneous', 'Personal trades are banned entirely', 'B', 'Clients and the employer come before the member''s personal transactions.', 1),
  ('cfa3-ethics-conflicts-q03', 'cfa3-ethics-conflicts', 'Referral fees [VI(C)] must be disclosed to:', 'Clients and the employer', 'Only the regulator', 'No one', 'Competitors', 'A', 'Disclosing referral compensation lets clients assess potential bias.', 2),
  ('cfa3-ethics-conflicts-q04', 'cfa3-ethics-conflicts', 'Taking a desirable IPO allocation ahead of clients violates:', 'VI(B) Priority of Transactions', 'III(D) Performance', 'I(A) Knowledge of Law', 'IV(C) Supervisors', 'A', 'Members must not take attractive allocations ahead of clients.', 3),
  ('cfa3-ethics-conflicts-q05', 'cfa3-ethics-conflicts', 'Recommended procedures for personal trading include:', 'No rules', 'Restricted lists, pre-clearance, and blackout periods', 'Front-running', 'Trading ahead of clients', 'B', 'These controls help comply with VI(B) priority of transactions.', 4),
  ('cfa3-ethics-conflicts-q06', 'cfa3-ethics-conflicts', 'A board seat at a covered company should be:', 'Hidden', 'Disclosed under VI(A)', 'Disclosed only to the board', 'Ignored if unpaid', 'B', 'Board membership can impair objectivity and must be disclosed as a conflict.', 5),
  ('cfa3-ethics-conflicts-q07', 'cfa3-ethics-conflicts', 'The default remedy for a conflict of interest is:', 'Concealment', 'Full disclosure and proper management', 'Resignation always', 'Ignoring it', 'B', 'Conflicts are addressed by disclosing and managing them.', 6),
  ('cfa3-ethics-conflicts-q08', 'cfa3-ethics-conflicts', 'A compensation structure that could bias a recommendation is:', 'Not a conflict', 'A disclosable conflict under VI(A)', 'Manipulation', 'Misconduct', 'B', 'Compensation arrangements that may affect objectivity must be disclosed.', 7),
  ('cfa3-ethics-conflicts-q09', 'cfa3-ethics-conflicts', 'Disclosure of a conflict should be:', 'Buried in fine print', 'Prominent and in plain language', 'Verbal only', 'After trading', 'B', 'Disclosures must be clear and understandable so recipients can evaluate the conflict.', 8),
  ('cfa3-ethics-conflicts-q10', 'cfa3-ethics-conflicts', 'Standard IV(B) Additional Compensation requires:', 'No action', 'Written consent for outside compensation that competes with the employer', 'Only verbal notice', 'Client disclosure only', 'B', 'Members need written consent for competing additional compensation arrangements.', 9),
  ('cfa3-ethics-conflicts-q11', 'cfa3-ethics-conflicts', 'Supervisors [IV(C)] must take reasonable steps to:', 'Maximize trades', 'Prevent and detect violations by those they supervise', 'Avoid compliance', 'Hide conflicts', 'B', 'Supervisory responsibility requires adequate procedures to prevent/detect violations.', 10),
  ('cfa3-ethics-conflicts-q12', 'cfa3-ethics-conflicts', 'Personal-trading rules ensure members do not:', 'Benefit clients', 'Disadvantage clients through their own trades', 'Disclose conflicts', 'Keep records', 'B', 'VI(B) protects clients from being disadvantaged by a member''s personal transactions.', 11),
  ('cfa3-ethics-application-cases-q01', 'cfa3-ethics-application-cases', 'The first step in analyzing an ethics vignette is to:', 'Pick the longest answer', 'Identify the actors and actions', 'Assume compliance', 'Skip the facts', 'B', 'Start by identifying who did what before mapping to the standards.', 0),
  ('cfa3-ethics-application-cases-q02', 'cfa3-ethics-application-cases', 'Taking a client list to a new employer maps to:', 'IV(A) Loyalty to Employer', 'II(A) MNPI', 'III(C) Suitability', 'VI(C) Referral Fees', 'A', 'Client lists/records are the employer''s property; taking them breaches IV(A).', 1),
  ('cfa3-ethics-application-cases-q03', 'cfa3-ethics-application-cases', 'Acting on a friend''s tip about an unannounced merger maps to:', 'II(A) Material Nonpublic Information', 'III(B) Fair Dealing', 'I(B) Independence', 'V(C) Records', 'A', 'Trading on material nonpublic information violates II(A).', 2),
  ('cfa3-ethics-application-cases-q04', 'cfa3-ethics-application-cases', 'A gift from a client for past performance should be:', 'Declined always', 'Disclosed to the employer', 'Hidden', 'Returned with interest', 'B', 'Client gifts for prior work are permitted but must be disclosed to the employer.', 3),
  ('cfa3-ethics-application-cases-q05', 'cfa3-ethics-application-cases', 'A gift/benefit from a third party seeking to influence research should be:', 'Accepted freely', 'Declined (or strictly limited)', 'Disclosed only to clients', 'Shared with the team', 'B', 'Influence-seeking third-party benefits should be declined to protect independence.', 4),
  ('cfa3-ethics-application-cases-q06', 'cfa3-ethics-application-cases', 'When law and the Code conflict, follow the:', 'Less strict', 'Stricter', 'Local law always', 'Code always', 'B', 'Members must comply with the stricter of applicable law or the Code and Standards.', 5),
  ('cfa3-ethics-application-cases-q07', 'cfa3-ethics-application-cases', 'Most ethics cases can be resolved by recommending procedures such as:', 'Firewalls, restricted lists, disclosure, supervision', 'Higher leverage', 'More trading', 'Ignoring conflicts', 'A', 'Standard recommended procedures (controls/disclosure) typically resolve case scenarios.', 6),
  ('cfa3-ethics-application-cases-q08', 'cfa3-ethics-application-cases', 'Claiming GIPS compliance for only the firm''s best composites is:', 'Acceptable', 'A violation — GIPS compliance is firm-wide', 'Required', 'A tax strategy', 'B', 'GIPS compliance must be firm-wide; partial/cherry-picked claims are prohibited.', 7),
  ('cfa3-ethics-application-cases-q09', 'cfa3-ethics-application-cases', 'Using public reports plus your own non-material observations to reach a buy is:', 'Permissible (mosaic theory)', 'Insider trading', 'Manipulation', 'Misconduct', 'A', 'The mosaic theory permits combining public and non-material nonpublic information.', 8),
  ('cfa3-ethics-application-cases-q10', 'cfa3-ethics-application-cases', 'When choosing among answers, select the one that is:', 'Most compliant / best remedies the issue', 'Longest', 'Most aggressive', 'Cheapest', 'A', 'Pick the most compliant action or best remedy (or identify the least-compliant act, per the question).', 9),
  ('cfa3-ethics-application-cases-q11', 'cfa3-ethics-application-cases', 'Reasonable basis and diligence behind a recommendation is required by Standard:', 'V(A)', 'II(B)', 'VI(A)', 'IV(C)', 'A', 'Standard V(A) requires a reasonable and adequate basis for recommendations and actions.', 10),
  ('cfa3-ethics-application-cases-q12', 'cfa3-ethics-application-cases', 'Mixed-use research paid with client brokerage should be:', 'Fully charged to clients', 'Allocated, with only the research portion paid by client brokerage', 'Charged entirely to the firm regardless', 'Never disclosed', 'B', 'Mixed-use products must be reasonably allocated; only the research portion may use client brokerage.', 11),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-market-risk-q01', 'cfa3-risk-management-market-risk', 'A 5% one-day VaR of $2m means:', 'Losses will never exceed $2m', 'On ~5% of days losses are expected to exceed $2m', 'The portfolio will gain $2m 5% of the time', 'Average daily loss is $2m', 'B', 'VaR gives a threshold loss exceeded with the stated probability — here, on about 5% of days.', 0),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-market-risk-q02', 'cfa3-risk-management-market-risk', 'The parametric (variance–covariance) VaR method primarily assumes:', 'Returns are normally distributed', 'No correlation among assets', 'Zero volatility', 'Returns are fat-tailed', 'A', 'Parametric VaR assumes normally distributed returns, which makes it fast but prone to understating tail risk.', 1),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-market-risk-q03', 'cfa3-risk-management-market-risk', 'Historical simulation VaR is computed by:', 'Assuming a normal distribution', 'Re-pricing the portfolio over an actual past return window', 'Drawing random paths from a model', 'Using only the latest day''s return', 'B', 'Historical simulation applies actual historical returns to the current portfolio, requiring no distributional assumption.', 2),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-market-risk-q04', 'cfa3-risk-management-market-risk', 'A key weakness of VaR is that it:', 'Says nothing about the size of losses beyond the threshold', 'Cannot be computed', 'Always overstates risk', 'Requires no data', 'A', 'VaR ignores the magnitude of tail losses past the cutoff, which is why expected shortfall complements it.', 3),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-market-risk-q05', 'cfa3-risk-management-market-risk', 'Conditional VaR (expected shortfall) measures the:', 'Probability of any loss', 'Average loss given that the VaR threshold is exceeded', 'Maximum possible gain', 'Daily volatility', 'B', 'CVaR/expected shortfall is the mean loss in the tail beyond VaR, capturing tail severity.', 4),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-market-risk-q06', 'cfa3-risk-management-market-risk', 'Monte Carlo VaR is most useful when:', 'The portfolio has complex, non-linear payoffs', 'There is no computing power', 'Returns are exactly normal', 'Only one asset is held', 'A', 'Monte Carlo simulation flexibly handles non-linear instruments like options, at the cost of model risk and computation.', 5),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-market-risk-q07', 'cfa3-risk-management-market-risk', 'For a bond portfolio, the key sensitivity measure is:', 'Delta', 'Duration (and convexity)', 'Vega', 'Beta', 'B', 'Duration (with convexity) measures a bond portfolio''s sensitivity to interest-rate changes.', 6),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-market-risk-q08', 'cfa3-risk-management-market-risk', 'Scenario analysis and stress testing are valuable because they:', 'Replace the need for VaR', 'Capture extreme, non-normal outcomes VaR may miss', 'Guarantee no losses', 'Only use normal distributions', 'B', 'Stress tests examine severe, plausible shocks and regime shifts that standard VaR can understate.', 7),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-market-risk-q09', 'cfa3-risk-management-market-risk', 'Risk budgeting refers to:', 'Setting a marketing budget', 'Allocating a total risk limit across positions or managers', 'Minimizing all risk to zero', 'Forecasting returns', 'B', 'Risk budgeting distributes an overall risk allowance among activities so total risk stays within tolerance.', 8),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-market-risk-q10', 'cfa3-risk-management-market-risk', 'For an options book, the sensitivity to a change in volatility is:', 'Delta', 'Vega', 'Duration', 'Rho', 'B', 'Vega measures the change in an option''s value for a change in implied volatility.', 9),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-market-risk-q11', 'cfa3-risk-management-market-risk', 'Different market participants (banks vs. pensions) set risk limits differently mainly because of their:', 'Office size', 'Distinct objectives, liabilities and regulation', 'Logo colors', 'Number of employees', 'B', 'Mandates, liability profiles, and regulatory regimes drive how each participant frames and limits risk.', 10),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-market-risk-q12', 'cfa3-risk-management-market-risk', 'A common constraint used alongside VaR limits is a:', 'Position/stop-loss limit', 'Marketing quota', 'Dividend target', 'Headcount cap', 'A', 'Position limits and stop-losses are practical constraints used together with VaR and scenario limits.', 11),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-swaps-forwards-q01', 'cfa3-risk-management-swaps-forwards', 'To increase a bond portfolio''s duration with a swap, a manager would:', 'Pay fixed / receive floating', 'Receive fixed / pay floating', 'Sell the portfolio', 'Buy a call option', 'B', 'Receiving fixed adds positive duration; paying floating offsets little, so the swap raises portfolio duration.', 0),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-swaps-forwards-q02', 'cfa3-risk-management-swaps-forwards', 'A pay-fixed interest-rate swap has a duration that is:', 'Positive and large', 'Negative (reduces portfolio duration)', 'Always zero', 'Equal to the coupon', 'B', 'Paying fixed/receiving floating carries negative duration, lowering a portfolio''s interest-rate sensitivity.', 1),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-swaps-forwards-q03', 'cfa3-risk-management-swaps-forwards', 'Equity index futures are used to change a portfolio''s:', 'Credit rating', 'Beta (equity market exposure)', 'Coupon', 'Dividend policy', 'B', 'Long or short equity index futures raise or lower the portfolio''s effective beta without trading shares.', 2),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-swaps-forwards-q04', 'cfa3-risk-management-swaps-forwards', 'A total-return (equity) swap lets an investor:', 'Gain an asset''s return synthetically without owning it', 'Eliminate counterparty risk', 'Avoid all fees', 'Guarantee a profit', 'A', 'A total-return swap exchanges a financing rate for an asset''s total return, giving synthetic exposure.', 3),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-swaps-forwards-q05', 'cfa3-risk-management-swaps-forwards', 'Cash equitization (an overlay) is used to:', 'Keep idle cash exposed to the market via index futures', 'Increase cash holdings', 'Hedge credit risk', 'Lower equity exposure', 'A', 'Buying index futures against cash removes ''cash drag'' by giving idle cash market exposure.', 4),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-swaps-forwards-q06', 'cfa3-risk-management-swaps-forwards', 'A drawback of hedging with forwards/futures versus options is they:', 'Remove upside as well as downside', 'Cost a large premium', 'Have unlimited upside', 'Cannot be closed out', 'A', 'Symmetric instruments lock in a rate, giving up favorable moves along with adverse ones.', 5),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-swaps-forwards-q07', 'cfa3-risk-management-swaps-forwards', 'Compared with OTC forwards, exchange-traded futures involve:', 'No counterparty', 'Margin and daily mark-to-market via a clearinghouse', 'Bilateral term negotiation', 'No standardization', 'B', 'Futures are standardized, margined, and cleared with daily settlement, reducing counterparty risk.', 6),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-swaps-forwards-q08', 'cfa3-risk-management-swaps-forwards', 'The number of bond futures to hit a target duration is based on the ratio of:', 'Portfolio BPV to futures BPV', 'Coupon to yield', 'Price to earnings', 'Dividends to price', 'A', 'The hedge uses the basis-point value (BPV) of the portfolio relative to the futures'' BPV.', 7),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-swaps-forwards-q09', 'cfa3-risk-management-swaps-forwards', 'Counterparty risk on OTC swaps is mitigated mainly by:', 'Collateral and central clearing', 'Higher coupons', 'Longer maturities', 'Ignoring it', 'A', 'Collateral posting and clearinghouses reduce bilateral counterparty exposure on swaps.', 8),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-swaps-forwards-q10', 'cfa3-risk-management-swaps-forwards', 'Basis risk in a futures hedge arises when:', 'The hedge and exposure move imperfectly together', 'Margin is required', 'The future is cleared', 'Rates are zero', 'A', 'Basis risk is residual risk from imperfect correlation between the hedging future and the hedged exposure.', 9),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-swaps-forwards-q11', 'cfa3-risk-management-swaps-forwards', 'A receive-fixed swap is most like adding a position in a:', 'Long fixed-rate bond', 'Short bond', 'Equity index', 'Currency forward', 'A', 'Receiving fixed behaves like owning a fixed-rate bond, adding positive duration.', 10),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-swaps-forwards-q12', 'cfa3-risk-management-swaps-forwards', 'Using a currency swap, an investor can:', 'Convert cash flows from one currency to another over time', 'Increase equity beta', 'Raise credit quality', 'Remove duration', 'A', 'Currency swaps exchange principal and/or interest in different currencies, managing FX exposure over a horizon.', 11),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-options-strategies-q01', 'cfa3-risk-management-options-strategies', 'A protective put consists of:', 'Long asset + long put', 'Long asset + short call', 'Short asset + long call', 'Two short puts', 'A', 'Holding the asset and buying a put sets a floor on losses while keeping upside, less the premium.', 0),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-options-strategies-q02', 'cfa3-risk-management-options-strategies', 'A covered call:', 'Earns premium but caps upside', 'Provides unlimited upside', 'Removes all downside', 'Requires shorting the stock', 'A', 'Long stock plus a short call generates income but limits gains at the strike, cushioning downside only by the premium.', 1),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-options-strategies-q03', 'cfa3-risk-management-options-strategies', 'A collar is built from a:', 'Long put financed by a short call', 'Two long calls', 'Long straddle', 'Short put only', 'A', 'A collar buys a protective put and sells a call to fund it, bounding both downside and upside.', 2),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-options-strategies-q04', 'cfa3-risk-management-options-strategies', 'A long straddle profits when the underlying:', 'Stays flat', 'Makes a large move in either direction', 'Pays a dividend', 'Rises slightly only', 'B', 'A long call plus long put at the same strike gains from big moves up or down (a long-volatility bet).', 3),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-options-strategies-q05', 'cfa3-risk-management-options-strategies', 'A bull call spread is used to:', 'Profit from a moderate rise at lower cost, with capped payoff', 'Hedge currency', 'Add duration', 'Short volatility only', 'A', 'Buying a lower-strike call and selling a higher-strike call lowers cost but caps the upside payoff.', 4),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-options-strategies-q06', 'cfa3-risk-management-options-strategies', 'Delta of an option measures sensitivity to:', 'The underlying price', 'Volatility', 'Time', 'Interest rates', 'A', 'Delta is the change in option value per unit change in the underlying''s price.', 5),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-options-strategies-q07', 'cfa3-risk-management-options-strategies', 'Gamma is important because it explains why:', 'Delta hedges must be rebalanced as the underlying moves', 'Options never expire', 'Premiums are zero', 'Rates rise', 'A', 'Gamma is the rate of change of delta, so a static delta hedge drifts and needs dynamic rebalancing.', 6),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-options-strategies-q08', 'cfa3-risk-management-options-strategies', 'Theta represents an option''s sensitivity to:', 'Time decay', 'The underlying price', 'Volatility', 'Currency', 'A', 'Theta measures how an option''s value erodes as time to expiration passes.', 7),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-options-strategies-q09', 'cfa3-risk-management-options-strategies', 'Writing (shorting) a straddle is a bet that volatility will be:', 'Low (the market stays quiet)', 'Very high', 'Irrelevant', 'Negative', 'A', 'A short straddle earns premium if the underlying barely moves, but has large risk if it moves sharply.', 8),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-options-strategies-q10', 'cfa3-risk-management-options-strategies', 'The volatility ''skew/smile'' refers to:', 'Implied volatility varying across strikes', 'A constant volatility', 'The risk-free rate', 'Dividend yield', 'A', 'Implied volatility typically differs by strike (and maturity), producing a smile or skew in option prices.', 9),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-options-strategies-q11', 'cfa3-risk-management-options-strategies', 'Relative to a forward, an option hedge offers:', 'Asymmetric protection for a premium', 'A symmetric locked rate at no cost', 'No protection', 'Guaranteed gains', 'A', 'Options hedge adverse moves while keeping favorable ones, paid for with the premium — unlike symmetric forwards.', 10),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-options-strategies-q12', 'cfa3-risk-management-options-strategies', 'A calendar spread mainly trades:', 'Differences in time decay between two expirations', 'Currency exposure', 'Credit risk', 'Equity beta', 'A', 'A calendar spread is long and short options of the same strike but different expiries, profiting from time-decay differences.', 11),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-enterprise-q01', 'cfa3-risk-management-enterprise', 'Enterprise Risk Management (ERM) is best described as:', 'Managing each desk''s risk in isolation', 'A centralized, enterprise-wide view of risk against objectives', 'Buying insurance only', 'Ignoring non-financial risk', 'B', 'ERM takes a top-down, organization-wide approach aligning total risk with the firm''s goals and tolerance.', 0),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-enterprise-q02', 'cfa3-risk-management-enterprise', 'Who is ultimately responsible for setting risk tolerance?', 'The board / senior management', 'Individual traders', 'The custodian', 'External auditors', 'A', 'Risk governance places ultimate responsibility for risk tolerance with the board and senior management.', 1),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-enterprise-q03', 'cfa3-risk-management-enterprise', 'Which is a NON-financial risk?', 'Market risk', 'Operational risk', 'Credit risk', 'Liquidity risk', 'B', 'Operational (also model, legal, regulatory, solvency) risks are non-financial; market/credit/liquidity are financial.', 2),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-enterprise-q04', 'cfa3-risk-management-enterprise', 'Aggregating risks across an organization must account for:', 'Correlations among risks', 'Only the largest single risk', 'Office locations', 'Nothing — risks simply add', 'A', 'Risks are not simply additive; correlations determine the true aggregate exposure.', 3),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-enterprise-q05', 'cfa3-risk-management-enterprise', 'Transferring a risk via insurance is appropriate when:', 'The firm has no comparative advantage in bearing it', 'The risk is free to retain', 'The premium exceeds any benefit always', 'The risk cannot be measured at all', 'A', 'Risks the firm is poorly positioned to bear are good candidates to transfer when the cost is justified.', 4),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-enterprise-q06', 'cfa3-risk-management-enterprise', 'Self-insuring (accepting) a risk means the firm:', 'Retains and bears the risk itself', 'Buys an option', 'Sells the business', 'Hedges fully', 'A', 'Acceptance/self-insurance keeps the risk on the firm''s own book rather than transferring or hedging it.', 5),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-enterprise-q07', 'cfa3-risk-management-enterprise', 'The first step in a risk-management process is to:', 'Identify and classify the risks', 'Buy insurance', 'Fire managers', 'Set marketing goals', 'A', 'Effective ERM begins by identifying and classifying the risks the organization faces.', 6),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-enterprise-q08', 'cfa3-risk-management-enterprise', 'A risk budget in ERM serves to:', 'Allocate total acceptable risk across activities', 'Set salaries', 'Eliminate all risk', 'Forecast revenue', 'A', 'A risk budget distributes the firm''s overall risk tolerance across units and activities.', 7),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-enterprise-q09', 'cfa3-risk-management-enterprise', 'Good risk governance locates risk decisions where the firm has:', 'The most knowledge and comparative advantage', 'The fewest employees', 'The lowest pay', 'The newest office', 'A', 'Decisions should sit where expertise and comparative advantage in bearing the risk are greatest.', 8),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-enterprise-q10', 'cfa3-risk-management-enterprise', 'Model risk is the risk that:', 'A pricing/risk model is wrong or misused', 'Markets fall', 'Rates rise', 'A counterparty defaults', 'A', 'Model risk arises when flawed assumptions or misuse of a model produce incorrect valuations or risk estimates.', 9),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-enterprise-q11', 'cfa3-risk-management-enterprise', 'Tail risk is best addressed with:', 'Scenario/stress testing and CVaR', 'Higher leverage', 'Ignoring extreme events', 'More marketing', 'A', 'Extreme, low-probability losses are examined via stress tests and expected-shortfall measures, not standard VaR alone.', 10),
  ('cfa3-risk-management-enterprise-q12', 'cfa3-risk-management-enterprise', 'An effective ERM framework should be:', 'Siloed by department', 'Enterprise-wide and integrated', 'Limited to market risk', 'Run only by traders', 'B', 'ERM is integrated across the whole enterprise rather than fragmented into independent silos.', 11),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-q01', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-hedge-funds', 'Hedge funds generally target:', 'Benchmark-relative returns', 'Absolute returns using leverage, shorting and derivatives', 'Index replication', 'Guaranteed income', 'B', 'Hedge funds pursue absolute returns with flexible tools rather than tracking a benchmark.', 0),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-q02', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-hedge-funds', 'Merger (risk) arbitrage is an example of which category?', 'Event-driven', 'Global macro', 'Equity market-neutral', 'Managed futures', 'A', 'Merger arbitrage trades announced deals and is classified under event-driven strategies.', 1),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-q03', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-hedge-funds', 'Equity market-neutral funds aim to:', 'Maximize market beta', 'Remove net market exposure and profit from relative mispricing', 'Hold only cash', 'Replicate an index', 'B', 'Market-neutral balances longs and shorts to strip out beta, seeking alpha from relative value.', 2),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-q04', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-hedge-funds', 'Managed futures / CTAs are valued in a portfolio because they often:', 'Provide crisis-period diversification (positive in trends)', 'Guarantee returns', 'Have no leverage', 'Track the S&P exactly', 'A', 'Trend-following CTAs can perform well in sustained market moves, adding crisis-period diversification.', 3),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-q05', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-hedge-funds', 'Relative-value arbitrage strategies typically use:', 'High leverage on small mispricings', 'No leverage', 'Only long equities', 'Physical commodities', 'A', 'Arbitrage of small pricing differences relies on significant leverage to make returns meaningful.', 4),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-q06', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-hedge-funds', 'The phrase ''picking up pennies in front of a steamroller'' describes:', 'Steady small gains with rare large losses (tail risk)', 'Guaranteed losses', 'Low-risk indexing', 'High daily volatility', 'A', 'Many arbitrage/short-volatility styles show consistent small profits punctuated by severe drawdowns.', 5),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-q07', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-hedge-funds', 'A global macro fund primarily trades on:', 'Top-down views of economies, rates and currencies', 'Single-stock fundamentals only', 'Real estate appraisals', 'Merger spreads only', 'A', 'Global macro takes directional positions across asset classes based on macroeconomic forecasts.', 6),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-q08', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-hedge-funds', 'Hedge fund correlations to equities tend to:', 'Stay constant', 'Rise during market stress', 'Fall to −1 in crises', 'Always be zero', 'B', 'Correlations are time-varying and often increase in stressed markets, reducing diversification when needed.', 7),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-q09', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-hedge-funds', 'A multi-strategy hedge fund:', 'Allocates dynamically across several strategies', 'Holds only government bonds', 'Uses no leverage', 'Tracks an index', 'A', 'Multi-strategy funds shift capital among strategies to exploit the best opportunities and smooth returns.', 8),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-q10', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-hedge-funds', 'A ''gate'' provision allows a fund to:', 'Limit the amount investors can redeem at once', 'Guarantee returns', 'Avoid all fees', 'Add leverage automatically', 'A', 'Gates cap aggregate redemptions in a period, protecting the fund from forced asset sales.', 9),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-q11', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-hedge-funds', 'Benchmarking hedge funds is difficult, so investors often use:', 'Peer groups, absolute targets, or factor models', 'A single government-bond index', 'The risk-free rate exactly', 'No evaluation at all', 'A', 'Because no single index fits, hedge funds are judged against peers, absolute return goals, or risk-factor benchmarks.', 10),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-hedge-funds-q12', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-hedge-funds', 'A distressed-securities strategy invests in:', 'Securities of firms in or near financial distress', 'AAA government bonds only', 'Index funds', 'Cash equivalents', 'A', 'Distressed investing buys the debt or equity of troubled companies, an event-driven approach.', 11),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-private-equity-q01', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-private-equity', 'A typical private-equity fund is structured as a:', 'Closed-end limited partnership', 'Daily-liquidity mutual fund', 'Bank deposit', 'Exchange-traded fund', 'A', 'PE funds are closed-end LPs with committed capital drawn down over an investment period.', 0),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-private-equity-q02', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-private-equity', '''Carried interest'' refers to the GP''s:', 'Share of fund profits (e.g., 20%)', 'Annual management fee', 'Office rent', 'Hurdle rate', 'A', 'Carried interest is the general partner''s performance share of profits, commonly around 20%.', 1),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-private-equity-q03', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-private-equity', 'A hurdle rate in a PE fund means carry is paid only:', 'Above a minimum return to LPs', 'Before any returns', 'On management fees', 'If the fund loses money', 'A', 'The hurdle (preferred return) requires LPs to earn a minimum before the GP shares in profits.', 2),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-private-equity-q04', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-private-equity', 'The J-curve describes:', 'Early negative returns followed by later gains', 'Steadily rising returns from day one', 'Constant returns', 'Guaranteed losses', 'A', 'Fees and early markdowns produce initial negative returns before exits drive the curve upward.', 3),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-private-equity-q05', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-private-equity', 'Buyout funds create value mainly through:', 'Operational improvement, leverage and multiple expansion', 'Daily trading', 'Currency hedging', 'Holding cash', 'A', 'Leveraged buyouts add value via operating gains, financial leverage, and exit multiple expansion.', 4),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-private-equity-q06', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-private-equity', 'Venture capital is characterized by:', 'Early-stage investing with high dispersion and failure rates', 'Low risk and steady income', 'Daily liquidity', 'Government guarantees', 'A', 'VC funds back early-stage companies, where a few big winners offset many failures.', 5),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-private-equity-q07', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-private-equity', 'PE performance is primarily measured by:', 'IRR and MOIC/TVPI (money-weighted)', 'Time-weighted return only', 'Cap rate', 'Current ratio', 'A', 'Because the GP controls cash-flow timing, money-weighted IRR and multiples like MOIC/TVPI are standard.', 6),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-private-equity-q08', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-private-equity', 'DPI (distributions to paid-in) measures:', 'Realized cash returned relative to capital called', 'Unrealized value only', 'Management fees', 'The hurdle rate', 'A', 'DPI captures cash actually distributed to LPs per dollar of paid-in capital — the realized multiple.', 7),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-private-equity-q09', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-private-equity', 'A public-market equivalent (PME) compares PE returns to:', 'A public-index investment with the same cash-flow timing', 'The risk-free rate', 'Inflation', 'A peer hedge fund', 'A', 'PME benchmarks a private fund against the return of investing the same cash flows in a public index.', 8),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-private-equity-q10', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-private-equity', 'A ''clawback'' provision requires the GP to:', 'Return excess carry if later results disappoint', 'Add capital each year', 'Pay LP taxes', 'Lower its hurdle', 'A', 'Clawbacks return previously paid carried interest if the fund''s overall results fall short of the agreed split.', 9),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-private-equity-q11', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-private-equity', 'Vintage-year diversification helps manage:', 'Timing/concentration and J-curve risk', 'Currency risk only', 'Coupon risk', 'Dividend risk', 'A', 'Spreading commitments across vintages reduces dependence on any one market environment and smooths the J-curve.', 10),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-private-equity-q12', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-private-equity', '''Committed capital'' that has not yet been called is known as:', 'Dry powder', 'Carried interest', 'NOI', 'A hurdle', 'A', 'Uncalled commitments waiting to be drawn are referred to as dry powder.', 11),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-real-estate-q01', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'Under direct capitalization, property value equals:', 'NOI divided by the cap rate', 'NOI times the cap rate', 'Rent minus taxes', 'Price times yield', 'A', 'Value = NOI / cap rate; a lower cap rate implies a higher value and lower expected return.', 0),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-real-estate-q02', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'Net operating income (NOI) excludes:', 'Financing costs and income taxes', 'Rental income', 'Operating expenses', 'Vacancy allowance', 'A', 'NOI is income after operating expenses but before financing and taxes.', 1),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-real-estate-q03', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'A lower capitalization rate generally implies:', 'A higher property price and lower expected return', 'A lower price', 'Higher risk', 'No effect on value', 'A', 'Cap rate moves inversely with price; a low cap rate means investors accept a lower yield for the asset.', 2),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-real-estate-q04', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'The three main valuation approaches for real estate are income, cost, and:', 'Sales comparison', 'Dividend discount', 'Black–Scholes', 'CAPM', 'A', 'Real estate is valued via income, cost, and sales-comparison (comparable transactions) approaches.', 3),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-real-estate-q05', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'REITs differ from direct private real estate by being:', 'Liquid and daily-priced, behaving more equity-like short term', 'Always lower returning', 'Appraisal-priced', 'Free of market risk', 'A', 'Listed REITs trade like equities with daily prices, showing more short-run correlation to stocks.', 4),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-real-estate-q06', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'Private real estate returns are ''smoothed'' because they are:', 'Appraisal-based and infrequently marked', 'Traded every second', 'Government set', 'Risk-free', 'A', 'Infrequent appraisals understate true volatility and correlation, smoothing reported returns.', 5),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-real-estate-q07', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'Leverage in real estate:', 'Amplifies both returns and risk', 'Removes risk', 'Has no effect', 'Guarantees gains', 'A', 'Mortgage financing magnifies equity returns up and down and adds financing/refinancing risk.', 6),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-real-estate-q08', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'Real estate is often added to a portfolio for:', 'Income, diversification, and partial inflation protection', 'Daily liquidity', 'Zero volatility', 'Tax exemption', 'A', 'Property offers income yield, diversification, and some inflation sensitivity over time.', 7),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-real-estate-q09', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'Moving from core to opportunistic real estate strategies generally means:', 'Higher risk, return, and leverage', 'Lower risk', 'More liquidity', 'No development risk', 'A', 'Core→value-add→opportunistic trades up risk, expected return, and the use of leverage and development.', 8),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-real-estate-q10', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'The debt quadrant of real estate refers to:', 'Mortgages and mortgage-backed lending', 'Equity ownership', 'REIT shares', 'Vacant land', 'A', 'Real estate exposure can be equity or debt (mortgages), and public or private — the four quadrants.', 9),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-real-estate-q11', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'A primary risk specific to rental properties is:', 'Vacancy / tenant default', 'Coupon reinvestment', 'Convexity', 'Tracking error', 'A', 'Vacancy and tenant credit directly reduce NOI and therefore value.', 10),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-real-estate-q12', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-real-estate', 'A terminal (reversion) value in a DCF of property represents the:', 'Estimated sale value at the end of the holding period', 'First year''s rent', 'Annual depreciation', 'Loan balance', 'A', 'DCF valuation discounts interim NOI plus a terminal value reflecting the expected resale price.', 11),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-real-assets-q01', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-real-assets', 'Most institutional commodity exposure is obtained through:', 'Futures contracts', 'Physical storage', 'Bank deposits', 'Equities only', 'A', 'Investors typically use commodity futures rather than holding and storing the physical goods.', 0),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-real-assets-q02', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-real-assets', 'Total return on a collateralized commodity futures position is spot + collateral yield +:', 'Roll yield', 'Dividend yield', 'Coupon', 'Cap rate', 'A', 'Commodity futures total return decomposes into spot price change, roll yield, and collateral (cash) yield.', 1),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-real-assets-q03', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-real-assets', 'Roll yield is positive when the futures curve is in:', 'Backwardation (futures below spot)', 'Contango', 'A flat shape', 'Inversion of rates', 'A', 'In backwardation, rolling to cheaper near-dated futures produces a positive roll yield.', 2),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-real-assets-q04', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-real-assets', 'Contango in a futures market means:', 'Futures prices exceed the spot price', 'Futures below spot', 'No futures exist', 'Spot equals zero', 'A', 'Contango is an upward-sloping curve (futures above spot), producing a negative roll yield.', 3),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-real-assets-q05', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-real-assets', 'Commodities are added to portfolios mainly for:', 'Inflation sensitivity and diversification', 'Steady income', 'Daily coupons', 'Guaranteed returns', 'A', 'Commodities historically correlate with inflation surprises and diversify stock/bond portfolios, despite paying no income.', 4),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-real-assets-q06', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-real-assets', 'The ''theory of storage'' explains the futures curve via:', 'Convenience yield and storage costs', 'Dividend policy', 'Bond duration', 'Equity beta', 'A', 'The theory of storage links the curve shape to storage costs, financing, and the convenience yield of holding the physical.', 5),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-real-assets-q07', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-real-assets', 'A defining feature of commodities versus bonds is that commodities:', 'Produce no income', 'Pay regular coupons', 'Have fixed maturities', 'Carry credit ratings', 'A', 'Commodities generate no cash income; return comes from price and roll, not coupons or dividends.', 6),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-real-assets-q08', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-real-assets', 'Timberland and farmland are examples of:', 'Real assets with inflation-linked, long-duration cash flows', 'Money-market instruments', 'Equity derivatives', 'Government bonds', 'A', 'Natural-resource real assets offer long-horizon, often inflation-sensitive returns and diversification.', 7),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-real-assets-q09', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-real-assets', 'The hedging-pressure (insurance) hypothesis says futures prices reflect:', 'Compensation paid by hedgers to speculators', 'Only inflation', 'Dividend yields', 'Bond convexity', 'A', 'Hedgers may accept a lower expected price to offload risk, paying a premium to speculators who bear it.', 8),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-real-assets-q10', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-real-assets', 'Commodity returns tend to be:', 'Volatile, with correlations rising in macro shocks', 'Always stable', 'Risk-free', 'Perfectly normal', 'A', 'Commodities are volatile and can see correlations move with macroeconomic conditions.', 9),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-real-assets-q11', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-real-assets', 'A practical reason to access commodities via futures rather than physical is:', 'Avoiding storage, insurance and spoilage costs', 'Earning dividends', 'Gaining voting rights', 'Lower volatility', 'A', 'Futures avoid the cost and impracticality of storing, insuring, and transporting physical commodities.', 10),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-real-assets-q12', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-real-assets', 'Like other private real assets, infrastructure/timber valuations may be:', 'Appraisal-based and smoothed', 'Marked every second', 'Government guaranteed', 'Free of illiquidity', 'A', 'Private real-asset valuations are often appraisal-based, smoothing returns and understating volatility.', 11),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-private-debt-q01', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-private-debt', 'Direct lending typically provides:', 'Senior secured loans to mid-market firms', 'Common equity stakes', 'Government bonds', 'Listed REIT shares', 'A', 'Direct lending funds make senior secured loans, mostly to middle-market companies.', 0),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-private-debt-q02', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-private-debt', 'Mezzanine debt is best described as:', 'Subordinated debt, often with an equity kicker', 'Senior secured debt', 'Government guaranteed', 'Daily-liquid', 'A', 'Mezzanine sits between senior debt and equity, carrying higher yield and often warrants/equity participation.', 1),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-private-debt-q03', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-private-debt', 'Private-debt returns are driven mainly by:', 'Credit spread plus an illiquidity premium', 'Equity multiple expansion', 'Roll yield', 'Dividend growth', 'A', 'Investors earn the credit spread and an illiquidity premium for locking up capital in private loans.', 2),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-private-debt-q04', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-private-debt', 'Many direct loans are floating-rate, which means they:', 'Have low duration and benefit as rates rise', 'Have very high duration', 'Pay no interest', 'Are equity-like', 'A', 'Floating-rate loans reset with short rates, giving low duration and rising coupons when rates increase.', 3),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-private-debt-q05', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-private-debt', 'The primary risk in private debt is:', 'Credit/default and recovery risk', 'Currency only', 'Tracking error', 'Dividend cuts', 'A', 'Default probability and recovery on the underlying borrowers drive private-debt risk, with covenant quality key.', 4),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-private-debt-q06', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-private-debt', 'Infrastructure assets are typically:', 'Long-lived physical assets with regulated/contracted cash flows', 'Short-term trading instruments', 'Daily-liquid equities', 'Zero-coupon bonds', 'A', 'Infrastructure comprises long-duration physical assets whose cash flows are often regulated or contracted.', 5),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-private-debt-q07', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-private-debt', 'Brownfield infrastructure refers to assets that are:', 'Already operating, offering stable yield', 'Under development', 'Never built', 'Listed equities', 'A', 'Brownfield assets are existing, operating projects with more predictable, lower-risk cash flows than greenfield.', 6),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-private-debt-q08', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-private-debt', 'Greenfield infrastructure carries higher risk because it involves:', 'Construction/development before cash flows begin', 'Guaranteed income', 'Daily liquidity', 'No leverage', 'A', 'Greenfield projects must be built, adding construction, completion, and demand risk before generating revenue.', 7),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-private-debt-q09', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-private-debt', 'Infrastructure cash flows are attractive to long-horizon investors because they are often:', 'Inflation-linked and bond-like', 'Highly volatile', 'Tax-exempt', 'Zero', 'A', 'Regulated/contracted, frequently inflation-indexed cash flows give infrastructure stable, diversifying, bond-like returns.', 8),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-private-debt-q10', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-private-debt', 'A specific risk of infrastructure investing is:', 'Regulatory / political risk', 'Coupon reinvestment only', 'Style drift', 'Survivorship bias', 'A', 'Because many assets are regulated utilities/transport, changes in regulation or politics materially affect returns.', 9),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-private-debt-q11', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-private-debt', 'Both private debt and infrastructure are well-suited to investors seeking:', 'Income and inflation protection over long horizons', 'Daily liquidity', 'Maximum equity beta', 'Zero risk', 'A', 'Their contractual, often inflation-linked income fits long-horizon investors who can bear illiquidity.', 10),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-private-debt-q12', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-private-debt', 'Weak loan covenants (''cov-lite'') in private debt tend to:', 'Reduce lender protection and worsen recoveries', 'Increase coupon guarantees', 'Eliminate default risk', 'Raise liquidity', 'A', 'Looser covenants give lenders fewer early warning rights, often lowering recoveries when borrowers struggle.', 11),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-quantitative-q01', 'cfa3-manager-selection-quantitative', 'Returns-based analysis seeks to separate a manager''s return into:', 'Alpha (skill) and factor (beta) exposures', 'Fees and taxes', 'Income and capital gains', 'Cash and bonds', 'A', 'Regressing returns on factors isolates genuine alpha from cheap factor/beta exposure.', 0),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-quantitative-q02', 'cfa3-manager-selection-quantitative', 'The information ratio is defined as:', 'Active return divided by tracking error', 'Return divided by total volatility', 'Beta times the market return', 'Alpha minus fees', 'A', 'The information ratio measures active return per unit of active risk, gauging consistency of skill.', 1),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-quantitative-q03', 'cfa3-manager-selection-quantitative', 'A short, volatile track record makes it hard to:', 'Distinguish skill from luck statistically', 'Compute fees', 'Define the mandate', 'Read disclosures', 'A', 'Limited, noisy data has low statistical power to separate true skill from random good luck.', 2),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-quantitative-q04', 'cfa3-manager-selection-quantitative', 'Backfill bias in a manager database tends to:', 'Overstate historical performance', 'Understate performance', 'Have no effect', 'Only affect fees', 'A', 'Adding a manager''s favorable past returns when they join inflates the database''s reported performance.', 3),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-quantitative-q05', 'cfa3-manager-selection-quantitative', 'Survivorship bias arises because databases:', 'Drop failed/closed funds, inflating average returns', 'Include too many funds', 'Use net returns', 'Report monthly', 'A', 'Excluding funds that closed leaves only survivors, biasing measured performance upward.', 4),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-quantitative-q06', 'cfa3-manager-selection-quantitative', 'Paying active fees for returns that are really factor exposure means:', 'Overpaying for cheap beta', 'Earning true alpha', 'Reducing risk', 'Avoiding fees', 'A', 'If ''alpha'' is just persistent factor tilt, the investor is paying active fees for beta that could be bought cheaply.', 5),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-quantitative-q07', 'cfa3-manager-selection-quantitative', 'The Sortino ratio differs from the Sharpe ratio by using:', 'Downside deviation instead of total volatility', 'Beta instead of alpha', 'Gross instead of net returns', 'Tracking error', 'A', 'Sortino penalizes only downside volatility, useful when returns are asymmetric.', 6),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-quantitative-q08', 'cfa3-manager-selection-quantitative', 'Smoothed (appraisal-based) returns in a quant evaluation will:', 'Understate volatility and overstate risk-adjusted ratios', 'Overstate volatility', 'Have no impact', 'Eliminate alpha', 'A', 'Smoothing lowers measured volatility, flattering Sharpe/IR; returns should be unsmoothed first.', 7),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-quantitative-q09', 'cfa3-manager-selection-quantitative', 'A high information ratio sustained over time suggests:', 'Consistent active skill', 'Pure luck', 'High fees', 'Low risk only', 'A', 'Persistently high active return per unit of active risk is evidence of repeatable skill.', 8),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-quantitative-q10', 'cfa3-manager-selection-quantitative', 'Quantitative evaluation alone is insufficient because:', 'It must be paired with qualitative/operational due diligence', 'Numbers are always wrong', 'It ignores returns', 'It sets fees', 'A', 'Past statistics weakly predict future results, so quant work is combined with qualitative and operational DD.', 9),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-quantitative-q11', 'cfa3-manager-selection-quantitative', 'Style drift detected in returns-based analysis means the manager:', 'Has moved away from the intended exposures', 'Earned guaranteed alpha', 'Lowered fees', 'Closed the fund', 'A', 'Drift signals exposures inconsistent with the mandate, a flag for monitoring or termination.', 10),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-quantitative-q12', 'cfa3-manager-selection-quantitative', 'Risk-adjusted return measures are preferred over raw returns because they:', 'Account for the risk taken to earn the return', 'Ignore risk', 'Always exceed benchmarks', 'Remove fees', 'A', 'Adjusting for risk reveals whether returns came from skill or simply from taking more risk.', 11),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-fees-q01', 'cfa3-manager-selection-fees', 'An ad valorem fee is charged as:', 'A fixed percentage of assets under management', 'A share of profits', 'A flat dollar amount', 'A percentage of losses', 'A', 'Ad valorem (AUM) fees are a fixed percentage of assets, rewarding asset gathering.', 0),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-fees-q02', 'cfa3-manager-selection-fees', 'A high-water mark ensures performance fees are charged only on:', 'New net profits above the prior peak', 'Total assets', 'Losses', 'Management fees', 'A', 'The high-water mark prevents charging performance fees again on gains that merely recover a prior drawdown.', 1),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-fees-q03', 'cfa3-manager-selection-fees', 'A hurdle rate means a performance fee applies only:', 'Above a specified threshold return', 'On all returns', 'When the fund loses money', 'On the management fee', 'A', 'The hurdle sets a minimum return the manager must beat before earning performance fees.', 2),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-fees-q04', 'cfa3-manager-selection-fees', 'Performance fees can create an incentive for managers to:', 'Take excessive risk for upside', 'Avoid all risk', 'Lower their AUM', 'Reduce returns', 'A', 'Sharing the upside but not the downside can encourage excessive risk-taking, a key alignment concern.', 3),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-fees-q05', 'cfa3-manager-selection-fees', 'A symmetric (fulcrum) fee:', 'Rewards outperformance and penalizes underperformance', 'Only rewards gains', 'Is a fixed AUM fee', 'Never changes', 'A', 'Fulcrum fees adjust up and down around a benchmark, improving alignment by sharing both outcomes.', 4),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-fees-q06', 'cfa3-manager-selection-fees', 'When evaluating managers, investors should compare returns:', 'Net of fees', 'Gross only', 'Before any costs', 'Ignoring fees', 'A', 'What matters to the client is net-of-fee return; high fees can erase a manager''s gross edge.', 5),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-fees-q07', 'cfa3-manager-selection-fees', 'A clawback provision protects investors by:', 'Recovering excess carry if later results disappoint', 'Raising fees', 'Removing the hurdle', 'Adding leverage', 'A', 'Clawbacks return previously paid performance fees when overall results fall short, improving alignment.', 6),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-fees-q08', 'cfa3-manager-selection-fees', 'Paying high fixed fees for a beta-heavy strategy is problematic because:', 'The investor pays active prices for cheap market exposure', 'Beta is unavailable', 'Fees are tax-deductible', 'It lowers risk', 'A', 'If most return is beta, high active fees destroy value relative to cheaply available index exposure.', 7),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-fees-q09', 'cfa3-manager-selection-fees', 'AUM-based fees can misalign incentives because they reward:', 'Asset gathering over performance', 'Risk reduction', 'Closing the fund', 'Lower fees', 'A', 'Because the fee scales with assets, managers may prioritize growing AUM rather than returns.', 8),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-fees-q10', 'cfa3-manager-selection-fees', 'A cap on performance fees serves to:', 'Limit how much performance fee can accrue', 'Guarantee returns', 'Raise the hurdle', 'Remove the high-water mark', 'A', 'A cap bounds the manager''s performance-fee share, a tool in negotiating fee terms.', 9),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-fees-q11', 'cfa3-manager-selection-fees', 'Combining a hurdle with a high-water mark primarily improves:', 'Alignment between manager and client', 'The management fee', 'Leverage', 'Tracking error', 'A', 'Together they ensure performance fees reward only genuine, sustained outperformance.', 10),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-fees-q12', 'cfa3-manager-selection-fees', 'Fee evaluation in selection should weigh:', 'Both the level and the structure of fees', 'Only the headline rate', 'Office location', 'Fund age only', 'A', 'Both how much and how fees are charged (incentives) matter for net value and alignment.', 11),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-style-analysis-q01', 'cfa3-manager-selection-style-analysis', 'Returns-based style analysis (RBSA) estimates style by:', 'Regressing fund returns on style-index returns', 'Listing every holding', 'Reading the prospectus', 'Interviewing the manager', 'A', 'RBSA uses a constrained regression of fund returns on style indexes to infer the effective style mix.', 0),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-style-analysis-q02', 'cfa3-manager-selection-style-analysis', 'Holdings-based style analysis (HBSA) classifies a manager by:', 'The actual portfolio positions', 'Past returns only', 'Fees charged', 'AUM size', 'A', 'HBSA examines actual holdings by size, value/growth, sector, and region for a precise current snapshot.', 1),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-style-analysis-q03', 'cfa3-manager-selection-style-analysis', 'An advantage of RBSA over HBSA is that it is:', 'Cheap and timely, needing only return data', 'Always more precise', 'Forward-looking', 'Free of estimation error', 'A', 'RBSA requires only returns, making it inexpensive and timely, though it is backward-looking and less precise.', 2),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-style-analysis-q04', 'cfa3-manager-selection-style-analysis', 'An advantage of HBSA over RBSA is that it is:', 'More precise and current about actual exposures', 'Cheaper', 'Requires no data', 'Backward-looking', 'A', 'Examining real positions gives a precise, up-to-date view, at the cost of being data-intensive and a snapshot.', 3),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-style-analysis-q05', 'cfa3-manager-selection-style-analysis', 'Style drift refers to a manager:', 'Moving away from the intended style/mandate', 'Lowering fees', 'Closing the fund', 'Beating the benchmark', 'A', 'Style drift is unintended migration away from the mandate''s exposures, a key thing style analysis detects.', 4),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-style-analysis-q06', 'cfa3-manager-selection-style-analysis', 'Style analysis helps reveal whether reported alpha is really:', 'Persistent factor exposure', 'A data error', 'A fee', 'Cash drag', 'A', 'By identifying exposures, style analysis shows whether ''alpha'' is genuine or just hidden factor tilts.', 5),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-style-analysis-q07', 'cfa3-manager-selection-style-analysis', 'A limitation of RBSA is that it:', 'Is backward-looking and can miss rapid changes', 'Requires every holding', 'Cannot use regression', 'Is too expensive', 'A', 'RBSA infers average past exposures and may lag quick shifts in actual positioning.', 6),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-style-analysis-q08', 'cfa3-manager-selection-style-analysis', 'A limitation of HBSA is that it is:', 'Data-intensive and only a point-in-time snapshot', 'Always backward-looking', 'Free of detail', 'Unable to show sectors', 'A', 'HBSA needs full holdings data and captures only the positions at one moment, missing intra-period trading.', 7),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-style-analysis-q09', 'cfa3-manager-selection-style-analysis', 'Style analysis supports selection by confirming a manager:', 'Fits the intended portfolio role without overlap', 'Has the lowest fee', 'Is the largest fund', 'Has the longest history', 'A', 'It verifies the manager delivers the wanted exposures and won''t duplicate existing managers'' bets.', 8),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-style-analysis-q10', 'cfa3-manager-selection-style-analysis', 'Combining RBSA and HBSA is useful because they provide:', 'History/consistency plus current positioning', 'Identical information', 'Only fee data', 'No added value', 'A', 'RBSA shows consistency over time while HBSA shows the present mix; together they give a fuller picture.', 9),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-style-analysis-q11', 'cfa3-manager-selection-style-analysis', 'Detecting hidden factor tilts matters because they can:', 'Concentrate risk and double-count exposures across managers', 'Raise the hurdle rate', 'Lower the AUM fee', 'Remove tracking error', 'A', 'Unseen tilts may concentrate portfolio risk or overlap with other managers, undermining diversification.', 10),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-style-analysis-q12', 'cfa3-manager-selection-style-analysis', 'The constrained coefficients in RBSA are interpreted as:', 'The portfolio''s effective style weights', 'Future returns', 'Fees', 'Tracking error', 'A', 'The regression weights (summing to one, non-negative) estimate the manager''s effective allocation to each style.', 11),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-monitoring-q01', 'cfa3-manager-selection-monitoring', 'Ongoing manager monitoring should track performance, style consistency, and:', 'The original investment thesis (people, process, firm)', 'Only last quarter''s return', 'Office size', 'Logo changes', 'A', 'Monitoring confirms the reasons for hiring still hold — performance in context, style, and the thesis.', 0),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-monitoring-q02', 'cfa3-manager-selection-monitoring', 'An appropriate termination trigger is:', 'Loss of the original rationale (style drift, process change)', 'A single weak quarter', 'A new office', 'A rebranding', 'A', 'Managers should be replaced when the basis for hiring breaks down, not for short-term noise.', 1),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-monitoring-q03', 'cfa3-manager-selection-monitoring', 'Performance chasing — firing losers and hiring recent winners — often:', 'Destroys value due to mean reversion', 'Guarantees outperformance', 'Lowers fees', 'Removes all risk', 'A', 'Chasing past returns tends to buy high and sell low as performance mean-reverts, hurting results.', 2),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-monitoring-q04', 'cfa3-manager-selection-monitoring', 'Prematurely firing a genuinely skilled manager is a:', 'Type II error', 'Type I error', 'Data entry error', 'Benchmark error', 'A', 'Rejecting/firing a skilled manager is a Type II (false-negative) error.', 3),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-monitoring-q05', 'cfa3-manager-selection-monitoring', 'Retaining an unskilled manager is a:', 'Type I error', 'Type II error', 'Rounding error', 'Sampling error', 'A', 'Keeping a manager with no real skill is a Type I (false-positive) error.', 4),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-monitoring-q06', 'cfa3-manager-selection-monitoring', 'Key-person risk monitoring watches for:', 'Departure of a critical individual', 'Currency moves', 'Coupon changes', 'Index rebalancing', 'A', 'Over-reliance on one person means their exit can impair the strategy, a monitoring priority.', 5),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-monitoring-q07', 'cfa3-manager-selection-monitoring', 'Rapid asset growth toward capacity limits is a concern because it can:', 'Erode alpha via market impact and crowding', 'Lower fees automatically', 'Guarantee returns', 'Remove risk', 'A', 'Too many assets raise transaction costs and reduce opportunity per dollar, decaying the strategy''s edge.', 6),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-monitoring-q08', 'cfa3-manager-selection-monitoring', 'Operational red flags during monitoring include:', 'Valuation, control, or service-provider problems', 'Strong returns', 'Low turnover', 'Clear disclosures', 'A', 'Operational issues (weak controls, valuation concerns, provider changes) are leading causes of fund failure.', 7),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-monitoring-q09', 'cfa3-manager-selection-monitoring', 'Performance should be evaluated:', 'In the proper risk and style context, not in isolation', 'Only versus cash', 'Gross of everything', 'Once at hiring', 'A', 'Returns must be judged against the right benchmark/peers and the risk taken, not as a raw number.', 8),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-monitoring-q10', 'cfa3-manager-selection-monitoring', 'Transition (replacement) costs matter because firing a manager involves:', 'Transaction costs and potential market impact', 'No costs at all', 'Higher guaranteed returns', 'Lower risk only', 'A', 'Replacing a manager incurs trading and transition costs that must be weighed against the expected benefit.', 9),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-monitoring-q11', 'cfa3-manager-selection-monitoring', 'A change in the manager''s investment process should prompt:', 'Re-evaluation of whether the thesis still holds', 'An automatic fee cut', 'Immediate ignore', 'More leverage', 'A', 'A process change can invalidate the original reason for hiring and warrants reassessment.', 10),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-monitoring-q12', 'cfa3-manager-selection-monitoring', 'Termination decisions should be based on:', 'A sound, ex-ante framework rather than recent returns alone', 'Last month''s return only', 'The cheapest option', 'Fund size', 'A', 'Disciplined, forward-looking criteria reduce both error types versus reacting to short-term performance.', 11),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-universe-q01', 'cfa3-manager-selection-universe', 'Sound manager selection begins by:', 'Defining the universe / mandate', 'Negotiating fees', 'Firing the incumbent', 'Setting marketing goals', 'A', 'Specifying the asset class, style, region, and constraints defines the opportunity set before screening.', 0),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-universe-q02', 'cfa3-manager-selection-universe', 'A poorly defined universe leads to:', 'Apples-to-oranges comparisons and misleading rankings', 'Lower fees', 'Guaranteed alpha', 'No estimation error', 'A', 'Comparing managers with different mandates produces invalid peer rankings and poor decisions.', 1),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-universe-q03', 'cfa3-manager-selection-universe', 'Quantitative screens typically filter on:', 'Track-record length, AUM/capacity, risk-adjusted performance, fees', 'Office decor', 'Manager surnames', 'Logo color', 'A', 'Quant filters narrow the universe using measurable criteria like performance, capacity, and cost.', 2),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-universe-q04', 'cfa3-manager-selection-universe', 'Qualitative screens assess factors such as:', 'Firm stability, transparency, and alignment', 'Daily price ticks', 'Index weights', 'Coupon rates', 'A', 'Qualitative criteria gauge the organization''s soundness, openness, and incentive alignment.', 3),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-universe-q05', 'cfa3-manager-selection-universe', 'Databases used for screening should be adjusted for:', 'Survivorship and backfill bias', 'Currency only', 'Coupon reinvestment', 'Convexity', 'A', 'Without correcting these biases, screens are built on inflated historical performance.', 4),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-universe-q06', 'cfa3-manager-selection-universe', 'Managers surviving the screen proceed to:', 'Full investment and operational due diligence', 'Immediate funding', 'Termination', 'A fee auction', 'A', 'Screening yields a shortlist that then undergoes deep investment and operational due diligence.', 5),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-universe-q07', 'cfa3-manager-selection-universe', 'Including capacity in the universe definition matters because:', 'A strategy too large for the mandate may decay in alpha', 'Capacity is irrelevant', 'It sets fees', 'It removes risk', 'A', 'Strategies near capacity can struggle to deploy new assets without eroding their edge.', 6),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-universe-q08', 'cfa3-manager-selection-universe', 'Specifying the investment vehicle (e.g., separate account vs. fund) affects:', 'Liquidity, customization, and control', 'Nothing', 'Only the logo', 'The risk-free rate', 'A', 'Vehicle choice influences liquidity terms, customization, fees, and governance, so it belongs in the mandate.', 7),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-universe-q09', 'cfa3-manager-selection-universe', 'Constraints like ESG or leverage limits should be:', 'Built into the universe/mandate definition', 'Ignored', 'Added after funding only', 'Set by the manager alone', 'A', 'Client constraints define which managers are even eligible and belong in the universe specification.', 8),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-universe-q10', 'cfa3-manager-selection-universe', 'The goal of a documented selection process is to:', 'Be repeatable and control both error types', 'Pick the cheapest manager', 'Maximize AUM', 'Avoid all due diligence', 'A', 'A disciplined, repeatable process reduces Type I/II errors and yields defensible decisions.', 9),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-universe-q11', 'cfa3-manager-selection-universe', 'The final output of selection should be:', 'A conviction-weighted recommendation grounded in skill and operations', 'A ranking by last year''s return', 'The largest fund', 'The newest firm', 'A', 'Selection should produce a forward-looking, conviction-based decision integrating skill and operational soundness.', 10),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-universe-q12', 'cfa3-manager-selection-universe', 'Screening on fees in the universe stage helps:', 'Eliminate managers whose costs outweigh expected skill', 'Increase tracking error', 'Guarantee returns', 'Add leverage', 'A', 'Filtering on cost early removes managers unlikely to deliver value net of fees.', 11),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-cognitive-emotional-q01', 'cfa3-behavioral-cognitive-emotional', 'Cognitive errors stem primarily from:', 'Faulty reasoning', 'Feelings and impulses', 'Market structure', 'Tax rules', 'A', 'Cognitive errors arise from statistical or memory-based reasoning mistakes.', 0),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-cognitive-emotional-q02', 'cfa3-behavioral-cognitive-emotional', 'Emotional biases are generally:', 'Accommodated rather than corrected', 'Easily eliminated with data', 'Irrelevant to advisers', 'Always beneficial', 'A', 'Emotional biases are hard to correct, so portfolios are often adapted to them.', 1),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-cognitive-emotional-q03', 'cfa3-behavioral-cognitive-emotional', 'Confirmation bias is an example of a:', 'Cognitive error', 'Emotional bias', 'Market anomaly', 'Tax preference', 'A', 'Seeking confirming evidence is a belief-perseverance cognitive error.', 2),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-cognitive-emotional-q04', 'cfa3-behavioral-cognitive-emotional', 'Loss aversion is classified as a(n):', 'Emotional bias', 'Cognitive error', 'Information-processing error', 'Arbitrage strategy', 'A', 'Loss aversion — feeling losses more than gains — is an emotional bias.', 3),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-cognitive-emotional-q05', 'cfa3-behavioral-cognitive-emotional', 'Anchoring and adjustment is a(n):', 'Information-processing cognitive error', 'Emotional bias', 'Market anomaly', 'Estate-planning tool', 'A', 'Anchoring is an information-processing bias, a type of cognitive error.', 4),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-cognitive-emotional-q06', 'cfa3-behavioral-cognitive-emotional', 'Cognitive errors are best addressed by:', 'Education and better information', 'Ignoring them', 'Accommodation only', 'Increasing leverage', 'A', 'Because they arise from reasoning, cognitive errors respond to education and data.', 5),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-cognitive-emotional-q07', 'cfa3-behavioral-cognitive-emotional', 'Overconfidence bias is a(n):', 'Emotional bias', 'Cognitive error only', 'Market anomaly', 'Regulatory rule', 'A', 'Overconfidence is generally treated as an emotional bias.', 6),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-cognitive-emotional-q08', 'cfa3-behavioral-cognitive-emotional', 'Mental accounting is a(n):', 'Information-processing cognitive error', 'Emotional bias', 'Tax strategy', 'Anomaly', 'A', 'Treating money differently by ''account'' is an information-processing bias.', 7),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-cognitive-emotional-q09', 'cfa3-behavioral-cognitive-emotional', 'The status quo bias is an example of a(n):', 'Emotional bias', 'Cognitive error', 'Arbitrage', 'Benchmark', 'A', 'Preferring to do nothing (status quo) is an emotional bias.', 8),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-cognitive-emotional-q10', 'cfa3-behavioral-cognitive-emotional', 'Whether to moderate or adapt to a bias depends partly on the client''s:', 'Wealth relative to standard of living', 'Zip code', 'Favorite sector', 'Broker''s name', 'A', 'The decision considers the bias type and the client''s wealth versus lifestyle needs.', 9),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-cognitive-emotional-q11', 'cfa3-behavioral-cognitive-emotional', 'Hindsight bias is a form of:', 'Belief perseverance (cognitive error)', 'Emotional bias', 'Herding', 'Framing only', 'A', 'Believing past events were predictable is a belief-perseverance cognitive error.', 10),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-cognitive-emotional-q12', 'cfa3-behavioral-cognitive-emotional', 'Regret aversion can cause investors to:', 'Be too conservative or hold losers too long', 'Always maximize returns', 'Rebalance perfectly', 'Ignore all risk', 'A', 'Fear of regret leads to excessive conservatism and reluctance to realize losses.', 11),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-portfolio-theory-q01', 'cfa3-behavioral-portfolio-theory', 'Behavioral portfolio theory describes portfolios built as:', 'Layered pyramids by goal', 'A single mean-variance optimum', 'Equal-weighted baskets', 'Random selections', 'A', 'BPT says investors build layered pyramids, each layer serving a distinct goal.', 0),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-portfolio-theory-q02', 'cfa3-behavioral-portfolio-theory', 'Each layer in a behavioral portfolio has its own:', 'Goal and risk attitude', 'Custodian', 'Currency', 'Tax rate', 'A', 'Layers correspond to goals with separate risk attitudes.', 1),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-portfolio-theory-q03', 'cfa3-behavioral-portfolio-theory', 'BPT investors tend to ignore:', 'Correlations across layers', 'Their goals', 'Their safety needs', 'Returns', 'A', 'By treating layers separately, investors overlook cross-layer correlations.', 2),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-portfolio-theory-q04', 'cfa3-behavioral-portfolio-theory', 'BPT is closely related to which bias?', 'Mental accounting', 'Anchoring', 'Hindsight', 'Availability', 'A', 'The layered structure reflects mental accounting.', 3),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-portfolio-theory-q05', 'cfa3-behavioral-portfolio-theory', 'A behavioral portfolio typically includes a low-risk layer for:', 'Safety', 'Speculation', 'Leverage', 'Tax deferral', 'A', 'The bottom layer meets safety/downside-protection needs.', 4),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-portfolio-theory-q06', 'cfa3-behavioral-portfolio-theory', 'The high-risk layer of a behavioral portfolio serves:', 'Aspirational goals', 'Emergency cash', 'Fixed income only', 'Immediate liquidity', 'A', 'The top layer pursues upside/aspirational objectives.', 5),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-portfolio-theory-q07', 'cfa3-behavioral-portfolio-theory', 'Relative to the mean-variance frontier, behavioral portfolios may be:', 'Inefficient', 'Always optimal', 'Risk-free', 'Perfectly diversified', 'A', 'Ignoring cross-layer correlations can make BPT portfolios mean-variance inefficient.', 6),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-portfolio-theory-q08', 'cfa3-behavioral-portfolio-theory', 'Goals-based investing leverages BPT by:', 'Framing allocations around client goals', 'Ignoring client goals', 'Maximizing turnover', 'Using one benchmark', 'A', 'Advisers align sub-portfolios with specific goals to improve client adherence.', 7),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-portfolio-theory-q09', 'cfa3-behavioral-portfolio-theory', 'A benefit of goals-based framing is improved:', 'Client adherence to the plan', 'Tax evasion', 'Leverage', 'Trading speed', 'A', 'Goals-based structures help clients stay invested through volatility.', 8),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-portfolio-theory-q10', 'cfa3-behavioral-portfolio-theory', 'BPT contrasts with which classical theory?', 'Modern portfolio theory', 'Behavioral finance', 'Prospect theory', 'Goals-based investing', 'A', 'BPT departs from MPT''s single mean-variance optimal portfolio.', 9),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-portfolio-theory-q11', 'cfa3-behavioral-portfolio-theory', 'In MPT, investors are assumed to consider:', 'Correlations across all assets', 'Only one goal layer', 'No risk', 'Mental accounts', 'A', 'MPT optimizes over the full covariance structure of assets.', 10),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-portfolio-theory-q12', 'cfa3-behavioral-portfolio-theory', 'A slightly suboptimal but goals-based portfolio can still be valuable if it:', 'Keeps the client invested and disciplined', 'Eliminates all risk', 'Guarantees alpha', 'Avoids all taxes', 'A', 'Better behavior (staying the course) can outweigh small mean-variance inefficiency.', 11),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-anomalies-q01', 'cfa3-behavioral-anomalies', 'The momentum anomaly is often linked to:', 'Herding and under-reaction', 'Perfect rationality', 'Tax-loss selling only', 'Dividend policy', 'A', 'Momentum is associated with herding behavior and under-reaction to news.', 0),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-anomalies-q02', 'cfa3-behavioral-anomalies', 'The January effect is an example of a:', 'Calendar anomaly', 'Momentum signal', 'Risk premium', 'Governance factor', 'A', 'The January effect is a calendar-based market anomaly.', 1),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-anomalies-q03', 'cfa3-behavioral-anomalies', 'Asset bubbles are commonly attributed to:', 'Herding and overconfidence', 'Efficient pricing', 'Low volatility', 'High dividends', 'A', 'Bubbles are driven by herding, overconfidence and regret.', 2),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-anomalies-q04', 'cfa3-behavioral-anomalies', 'Some anomalies disappear after discovery because they are:', 'Arbitraged away', 'Made illegal', 'Taxed', 'Ignored forever', 'A', 'Once known, profitable anomalies are often eliminated by arbitrage.', 3),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-anomalies-q05', 'cfa3-behavioral-anomalies', 'The value effect may reflect either mispricing or:', 'Compensation for risk', 'A calendar effect', 'Herding only', 'Data entry errors', 'A', 'Value premia may be a risk premium rather than a pure inefficiency.', 4),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-anomalies-q06', 'cfa3-behavioral-anomalies', 'Long-term reversals are consistent with investor:', 'Overreaction', 'Under-reaction only', 'Rationality', 'Indifference', 'A', 'Reversals suggest prior overreaction that later corrects.', 5),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-anomalies-q07', 'cfa3-behavioral-anomalies', 'A risk that apparent anomalies are illusory arises from:', 'Data mining', 'Diversification', 'Rebalancing', 'Hedging', 'A', 'Testing many patterns can produce spurious ''anomalies'' by chance.', 6),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-anomalies-q08', 'cfa3-behavioral-anomalies', 'Herding behavior tends to:', 'Amplify price trends', 'Stabilize prices', 'Eliminate volatility', 'Increase dividends', 'A', 'When investors follow the crowd, trends and bubbles are amplified.', 7),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-anomalies-q09', 'cfa3-behavioral-anomalies', 'The size effect refers to outperformance by:', 'Small-cap stocks', 'Large-cap stocks', 'Bonds', 'Commodities', 'A', 'Historically small-cap stocks have outperformed, the size effect.', 8),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-anomalies-q10', 'cfa3-behavioral-anomalies', 'Behavioral finance argues anomalies can persist because of:', 'Limits to arbitrage', 'Perfect liquidity', 'Zero transaction costs', 'Unlimited short selling', 'A', 'Costs and constraints on arbitrage let some mispricings persist.', 9),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-anomalies-q11', 'cfa3-behavioral-anomalies', 'Before trading an anomaly, an analyst should confirm it is not:', 'Simply a risk premium or data artifact', 'Profitable', 'Documented', 'Recent', 'A', 'One must rule out risk-based explanations and data mining before acting.', 10),
  ('cfa3-behavioral-anomalies-q12', 'cfa3-behavioral-anomalies', 'Overconfidence among investors tends to increase:', 'Trading volume and volatility', 'Diversification', 'Saving rates', 'Bond yields', 'A', 'Overconfident investors trade more, raising volume and volatility.', 11),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-framework-q01', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-framework', 'Capital market expectations are forecasts of:', 'Risk and return for asset classes', 'Individual stock earnings only', 'Tax rates', 'Trading volumes', 'A', 'CME are risk and return forecasts used to set strategic allocation.', 0),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-framework-q02', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-framework', 'Appraisal-based (smoothed) data tends to:', 'Understate volatility and correlation', 'Overstate volatility', 'Have no effect', 'Eliminate bias', 'A', 'Smoothing in appraisal data understates true volatility and correlations.', 1),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-framework-q03', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-framework', 'Using ex-post (realized) risk as a proxy for ex-ante risk is:', 'Potentially biased', 'Always accurate', 'Risk-free', 'Forward-looking', 'A', 'Historical risk can be a biased estimate of expected future risk.', 2),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-framework-q04', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-framework', 'Non-stationarity in data means that:', 'Statistical relationships change over time (regimes)', 'Data never changes', 'Variance is always zero', 'Returns are constant', 'A', 'Regime changes make historical relationships unstable, complicating forecasts.', 3),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-framework-q05', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-framework', 'The first step in the CME framework is to:', 'Specify the expectations needed and the horizon', 'Trade immediately', 'Pick a benchmark', 'Rebalance', 'A', 'Begin by defining which expectations are needed over what horizon.', 4),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-framework-q06', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-framework', 'Anchoring and overconfidence in forecasting are examples of:', 'Psychological biases', 'Data errors', 'Model-free methods', 'Liquidity risks', 'A', 'These are psychological biases that can distort capital market expectations.', 5),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-framework-q07', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-framework', 'Economic data limitations include revisions and:', 'Reporting lags', 'Perfect timeliness', 'Zero measurement error', 'Guaranteed accuracy', 'A', 'Data are released with lags and later revised, complicating real-time analysis.', 6),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-framework-q08', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-framework', 'Survivorship bias in a historical index tends to:', 'Overstate returns', 'Understate returns', 'Have no effect', 'Increase volatility', 'A', 'Excluding failed constituents inflates the reported historical return.', 7),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-framework-q09', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-framework', 'Good CME combine statistical tools with:', 'Sound economic judgement', 'No judgement at all', 'Only past averages', 'Random guesses', 'A', 'Judgement is needed to interpret models and adjust for regime shifts.', 8),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-framework-q10', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-framework', 'Model uncertainty refers to the risk that:', 'The chosen model is misspecified', 'Data are timely', 'Volatility is zero', 'Returns are guaranteed', 'A', 'Using the wrong model or inputs introduces model/input uncertainty.', 9),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-framework-q11', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-framework', 'CME feed most directly into:', 'Strategic asset allocation', 'Trade settlement', 'Custody selection', 'Tax filing', 'A', 'Return/risk forecasts are inputs to setting strategic allocation policy.', 10),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-framework-q12', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-framework', 'A limitation of historical estimates is that the past:', 'May not represent the future', 'Always repeats', 'Is irrelevant to risk', 'Has no measurement error', 'A', 'Historical samples may not capture future conditions, especially after regime change.', 11),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-macro-linkages-q01', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-macro-linkages', 'Economies are linked primarily through:', 'Trade and capital flows', 'Identical currencies', 'Shared tax codes', 'One central bank', 'A', 'Cross-border trade and capital flows transmit cycles between economies.', 0),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-macro-linkages-q02', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-macro-linkages', 'Countries with strong trade ties tend to have business cycles that are:', 'More correlated', 'Perfectly independent', 'Always inverse', 'Constant', 'A', 'Tight economic linkages produce more correlated cycles.', 1),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-macro-linkages-q03', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-macro-linkages', 'A key extra risk in emerging markets is:', 'Political and legal instability', 'Excess transparency', 'Zero volatility', 'Guaranteed growth', 'A', 'Emerging markets carry heightened political, legal and policy risk.', 2),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-macro-linkages-q04', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-macro-linkages', 'Emerging-market economic risk is raised by:', 'High debt and current-account deficits', 'Large surpluses', 'Low inflation always', 'Diversified exports only', 'A', 'Fiscal/external deficits and high debt increase emerging-market vulnerability.', 3),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-macro-linkages-q05', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-macro-linkages', 'Capital controls in an emerging market create:', 'Currency and repatriation risk', 'Guaranteed liquidity', 'Lower yields', 'No effect on investors', 'A', 'Controls can trap capital and raise currency/repatriation risk.', 4),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-macro-linkages-q06', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-macro-linkages', 'A checklist approach to forecasting is:', 'A structured qualitative method', 'A single regression', 'Purely random', 'A trading algorithm', 'A', 'The checklist approach systematically reviews relevant factors qualitatively.', 5),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-macro-linkages-q07', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-macro-linkages', 'Leading indicators are used to:', 'Anticipate turning points in the cycle', 'Confirm the distant past', 'Set tax policy', 'Value single bonds', 'A', 'Leading indicators help forecast upcoming economic turning points.', 6),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-macro-linkages-q08', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-macro-linkages', 'Commodity dependence makes an emerging economy sensitive to:', 'Terms-of-trade shocks', 'Nothing external', 'Only domestic policy', 'Equity dividends', 'A', 'Reliance on commodity exports exposes the economy to price/terms-of-trade swings.', 7),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-macro-linkages-q09', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-macro-linkages', 'Long-run growth analysis considers savings/investment, demographics, and:', 'Structural policy', 'Daily price moves', 'Option greeks', 'Coupon dates', 'A', 'Trend growth depends on capital formation, demographics and structural/institutional policy.', 8),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-macro-linkages-q10', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-macro-linkages', 'Monetary tightening in a major economy can spill over by:', 'Raising global rates and pressuring EM currencies', 'Lowering all global yields', 'Guaranteeing EM inflows', 'Having no external effect', 'A', 'Tightening abroad can pull capital from emerging markets and pressure their currencies.', 9),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-macro-linkages-q11', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-macro-linkages', 'Econometric models for forecasting are:', 'Quantitative and equation-based', 'Purely intuitive', 'Free of assumptions', 'Always correct', 'A', 'Econometric models use statistical equations to forecast economic variables.', 10),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-macro-linkages-q12', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-macro-linkages', 'Weaker property rights in an emerging market are a form of:', 'Legal/political risk', 'Currency hedge', 'Liquidity buffer', 'Diversification', 'A', 'Weak legal protections raise political/legal risk for investors.', 11),
  ('cfa3-capital-market-expectations-risk-premium-models-q01', 'cfa3-capital-market-expectations-risk-premium-models', 'The building-block approach expresses expected return as a risk-free rate plus:', 'A sum of risk premia', 'A single beta', 'The dividend yield only', 'Zero', 'A', 'It adds compensation for each source of risk to the risk-free rate.', 0),
