  ('cfa3-ethics-duties-employers-q07', 'cfa3-ethics-duties-employers', 'A member leaving a firm may use:', 'General knowledge and experience', 'The firm''s confidential models', 'Stolen client files', 'Prohibited records', 'A', 'Skills and general knowledge remain with the member; employer property does not.', 6),
  ('cfa3-ethics-duties-employers-q08', 'cfa3-ethics-duties-employers', 'Loyalty duties are balanced against the duty to:', 'Comply with the law', 'Maximize personal profit', 'Follow any employer order', 'Avoid all disclosure', 'A', 'A member must not engage in illegal activity even at an employer''s request.', 7),
  ('cfa3-ethics-duties-employers-q09', 'cfa3-ethics-duties-employers', 'Whistleblowing that violates loyalty may be justified when:', 'Necessary to protect clients or the integrity of markets', 'It is personally profitable', 'It embarrasses a rival', 'Never', 'A', 'Loyalty yields when disclosure is needed to prevent harm or illegal activity.', 8),
  ('cfa3-ethics-duties-employers-q10', 'cfa3-ethics-duties-employers', 'An inadequate compliance system is primarily a violation of:', 'Standard IV(C)', 'Standard III(A)', 'Standard VI(B)', 'Standard I(C)', 'A', 'Supervisors failing to maintain compliance systems breach IV(C).', 9),
  ('cfa3-ethics-duties-employers-q11', 'cfa3-ethics-duties-employers', 'Preparing to compete after resignation is acceptable if it does not:', 'Harm the employer or misuse its property', 'Involve any planning', 'Use one''s own skills', 'Follow the law', 'A', 'Members may prepare to leave, but not by damaging the employer or taking its property.', 10),
  ('cfa3-ethics-duties-employers-q12', 'cfa3-ethics-duties-employers', 'Standard IV as a whole governs a member''s duties to their:', 'Employer', 'Clients', 'Regulators', 'Family', 'A', 'Standard IV addresses loyalty, additional compensation, and supervisory duties to the employer.', 11),
  ('cfa3-ethics-investment-actions-q01', 'cfa3-ethics-investment-actions', 'Standard V(A) requires diligence and a:', 'Reasonable and adequate basis', 'Guaranteed outcome', 'Single data point', 'Client''s opinion only', 'A', 'V(A) demands a reasonable, adequate basis for analysis and recommendations.', 0),
  ('cfa3-ethics-investment-actions-q02', 'cfa3-ethics-investment-actions', 'When relying on a quantitative model, a member should:', 'Understand and verify it', 'Use it blindly', 'Hide its use', 'Assume it is perfect', 'A', 'V(A) requires verifying third-party research and models before relying on them.', 1),
  ('cfa3-ethics-investment-actions-q03', 'cfa3-ethics-investment-actions', 'Standard V(B) requires distinguishing:', 'Fact from opinion', 'Clients from employers', 'Stocks from bonds', 'Gross from net fees', 'A', 'V(B) requires clearly separating fact from opinion in communications.', 2),
  ('cfa3-ethics-investment-actions-q04', 'cfa3-ethics-investment-actions', 'V(B) also requires disclosing the investment process''s:', 'Material limitations and risks', 'Exact future returns', 'Client names', 'Employee pay', 'A', 'Members must disclose significant limitations and risks of the process.', 3),
  ('cfa3-ethics-investment-actions-q05', 'cfa3-ethics-investment-actions', 'Standard V(C) Record Retention recommends keeping records for:', 'Seven years', 'One year', 'Thirty days', 'Fifty years', 'A', 'V(C) recommends a seven-year retention period absent other requirements.', 4),
  ('cfa3-ethics-investment-actions-q06', 'cfa3-ethics-investment-actions', 'Records supporting analysis are generally the property of the:', 'Employer', 'Individual member', 'Client', 'Regulator', 'A', 'Work records typically belong to the employer, not the departing member.', 5),
  ('cfa3-ethics-investment-actions-q07', 'cfa3-ethics-investment-actions', 'Presenting opinion as fact violates:', 'Standard V(B)', 'Standard IV(A)', 'Standard VI(A)', 'Standard II(B)', 'A', 'Failing to distinguish fact from opinion breaches V(B).', 6),
  ('cfa3-ethics-investment-actions-q08', 'cfa3-ethics-investment-actions', 'Acting on inadequate research most directly violates:', 'Standard V(A)', 'Standard III(B)', 'Standard VII(A)', 'Standard IV(C)', 'A', 'Insufficient diligence and basis breach V(A).', 7),
  ('cfa3-ethics-investment-actions-q09', 'cfa3-ethics-investment-actions', 'Using group research a member did not personally conduct is acceptable if it has a:', 'Sound and adequate basis', 'High fee', 'Confidential source', 'Guaranteed return', 'A', 'Members may rely on group research if it is well-founded, even without full agreement.', 8),
  ('cfa3-ethics-investment-actions-q10', 'cfa3-ethics-investment-actions', 'V(B) requires communicating the investment process''s:', 'Basic format and general principles', 'Every trade detail', 'Nothing', 'Only the fee', 'A', 'Members disclose the general principles and format of the process to clients.', 9),
  ('cfa3-ethics-investment-actions-q11', 'cfa3-ethics-investment-actions', 'Diligence under V(A) applies to secondary/third-party research by requiring members to:', 'Verify its soundness', 'Accept it uncritically', 'Never use it', 'Claim it as their own', 'A', 'Members must make reasonable efforts to verify external research.', 10),
  ('cfa3-ethics-investment-actions-q12', 'cfa3-ethics-investment-actions', 'Standard V overall governs investment analysis, recommendations, and:', 'Actions', 'Employer loyalty', 'Exam conduct', 'Gifts', 'A', 'Standard V covers investment analysis, recommendations and actions.', 11),
  ('cfa3-ethics-gips-verification-q01', 'cfa3-ethics-gips-verification', 'GIPS verification tests whether a firm''s ____ comply with the standards.', 'Processes and procedures', 'Stock picks', 'Tax filings', 'Marketing slogans', 'A', 'Verification reviews firm-wide processes for GIPS compliance.', 0),
  ('cfa3-ethics-gips-verification-q02', 'cfa3-ethics-gips-verification', 'Verification is applied to the:', 'Whole firm', 'One composite', 'A single account', 'A single security', 'A', 'Verification applies firm-wide, not to an individual composite.', 1),
  ('cfa3-ethics-gips-verification-q03', 'cfa3-ethics-gips-verification', 'GIPS verification is:', 'Voluntary but recommended', 'Mandatory for all firms', 'Prohibited', 'Done by regulators only', 'A', 'Verification is optional but strongly recommended for credibility.', 2),
  ('cfa3-ethics-gips-verification-q04', 'cfa3-ethics-gips-verification', 'A firm cannot claim that a specific composite is:', '''Verified'' on its own', 'Managed to a strategy', 'Time-weighted', 'Fee-paying', 'A', 'Verification is firm-wide; a single composite cannot be described as separately verified.', 3),
  ('cfa3-ethics-gips-verification-q05', 'cfa3-ethics-gips-verification', 'Claiming GIPS compliance requires meeting:', 'All requirements (no partial compliance)', 'Most requirements', 'Any single rule', 'Only fee disclosure', 'A', 'GIPS forbids partial compliance; all requirements must be met.', 4),
  ('cfa3-ethics-gips-verification-q06', 'cfa3-ethics-gips-verification', 'Verification does NOT guarantee the accuracy of:', 'Any specific composite presentation', 'Firm-wide processes', 'The compliance statement', 'The standards', 'A', 'Verification checks processes, not the accuracy of a particular composite presentation.', 5),
  ('cfa3-ethics-gips-verification-q07', 'cfa3-ethics-gips-verification', 'A compliant firm must show a track record of at least:', 'Five years, building to ten', 'One year', 'Twenty years', 'Ninety days', 'A', 'Firms present at least 5 years then build to 10 years of compliant history.', 6),
  ('cfa3-ethics-gips-verification-q08', 'cfa3-ethics-gips-verification', 'Verification adds ____ to a compliance claim.', 'Credibility', 'A return guarantee', 'A tax benefit', 'Nothing', 'A', 'Independent verification increases confidence in the firm''s compliance.', 7),
  ('cfa3-ethics-gips-verification-q09', 'cfa3-ethics-gips-verification', 'Verification can be:', 'Only firm-wide, never partial', 'Applied to one account', 'Split by composite', 'Done for a single year only', 'A', 'Verification must cover the whole firm; it cannot be partial.', 8),
  ('cfa3-ethics-gips-verification-q10', 'cfa3-ethics-gips-verification', 'The official GIPS compliance statement must be:', 'Used exactly as prescribed', 'Rewritten freely', 'Omitted', 'Kept secret', 'A', 'Firms must use the prescribed compliance statement wording.', 9),
  ('cfa3-ethics-gips-verification-q11', 'cfa3-ethics-gips-verification', 'The purpose of GIPS is to make performance:', 'Comparable and fairly presented', 'Always positive', 'Higher than peers', 'Tax-exempt', 'A', 'GIPS ensures fair, comparable performance presentation across firms.', 10),
  ('cfa3-ethics-gips-verification-q12', 'cfa3-ethics-gips-verification', 'Verification is performed by:', 'An independent third party', 'The firm itself', 'The client', 'A regulator only', 'A', 'An independent third party conducts GIPS verification.', 11),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-hf-fees-q01', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-hf-fees', 'The classic hedge fund fee structure is:', '2 and 20', '5 and 50', '0 and 0', '1 and 100', 'A', '''2 and 20'' means a 2% management fee and 20% incentive fee.', 0),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-hf-fees-q02', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-hf-fees', 'A high-water mark ensures incentive fees are paid only after:', 'Prior losses are recovered', 'Every quarter', 'The fund is closed', 'A dividend', 'A', 'The high-water mark prevents charging incentive fees until past losses are made up.', 1),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-hf-fees-q03', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-hf-fees', 'A hurdle rate is the return that must be exceeded before:', 'Incentive fees apply', 'The fund can trade', 'Redemptions occur', 'The manager is paid a salary', 'A', 'Incentive fees accrue only on returns above the hurdle rate.', 2),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-hf-fees-q04', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-hf-fees', 'A fund-of-funds adds:', 'A second layer of fees', 'No fees', 'Guaranteed alpha', 'Tax exemption', 'A', 'Funds-of-funds layer their own fees on top of the underlying managers'' fees.', 3),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-hf-fees-q05', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-hf-fees', 'A benefit of a fund-of-funds is:', 'Diversification and due diligence/access', 'Zero cost', 'Guaranteed returns', 'No manager risk', 'A', 'FoFs provide diversification, manager access and due-diligence expertise.', 4),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-hf-fees-q06', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-hf-fees', 'Netting risk in a multi-manager fund means investors may:', 'Pay incentive fees to winners even if the fund is flat', 'Never pay fees', 'Receive guaranteed profit', 'Avoid all costs', 'A', 'Without fund-level netting, winning managers earn incentive fees despite a flat overall result.', 5),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-hf-fees-q07', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-hf-fees', 'Over-diversification in a fund-of-funds can:', 'Dilute alpha', 'Guarantee alpha', 'Remove all fees', 'Eliminate risk', 'A', 'Holding too many managers can wash out any manager''s alpha.', 6),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-hf-fees-q08', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-hf-fees', 'Fees materially:', 'Reduce net returns and compound over time', 'Raise gross returns', 'Have no effect', 'Are always waived', 'A', 'Fees compound and significantly reduce the investor''s net return.', 7),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-hf-fees-q09', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-hf-fees', 'The incentive fee is charged on:', 'The fund''s profits', 'Total assets always', 'Losses', 'The hurdle only', 'A', 'The incentive (performance) fee is a share of the profits earned.', 8),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-hf-fees-q10', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-hf-fees', 'A fund-level netting arrangement helps mitigate:', 'Netting risk', 'Market risk', 'Currency risk', 'Duration risk', 'A', 'Netting fees across managers at the fund level reduces netting risk.', 9),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-hf-fees-q11', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-hf-fees', 'A typical fund-of-funds fee might be:', '1 and 10 on top of underlying fees', 'Zero', '5 and 50', '10 and 100', 'A', 'FoFs often charge around ''1 and 10'' in addition to the underlying managers'' fees.', 10),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-hf-fees-q12', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-hf-fees', 'The management fee is generally charged on:', 'Assets under management', 'Profits only', 'Losses', 'The hurdle rate', 'A', 'The management fee is a percentage of assets under management.', 11),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q01', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'Due diligence on alternatives spans investment review and:', 'Operational review', 'Tax filing', 'Marketing', 'Custody sales', 'A', 'Thorough due diligence covers both investment merit and operations.', 0),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q02', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'Investment due diligence assesses whether returns are:', 'Repeatable (skill vs beta)', 'Always guaranteed', 'Tax-free', 'Risk-free', 'A', 'It examines whether performance reflects repeatable skill or just market beta.', 1),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q03', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'Operational due diligence checks for an independent:', 'Administrator, custodian and auditor', 'Marketing team', 'Sales desk', 'Regulator', 'A', 'ODD confirms independent, reputable service providers.', 2),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q04', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'A leading cause of hedge-fund losses is:', 'Operational failure/fraud', 'Too much diversification', 'Low fees', 'High liquidity', 'A', 'Operational failures and fraud are a top cause of hedge-fund blowups.', 3),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q05', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'Valuation policy is critical for funds holding:', 'Illiquid assets', 'Only cash', 'Treasury bills', 'Listed equities only', 'A', 'Illiquid holdings require a sound, independent valuation policy.', 4),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q06', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'Key-person risk refers to dependence on:', 'One or a few critical individuals', 'Many junior staff', 'The custodian', 'A regulator', 'A', 'Loss of a key manager can imperil the strategy — a due-diligence concern.', 5),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q07', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'A red flag in ODD is:', 'Self-administration and self-custody', 'A big-four auditor', 'Independent custody', 'Clear valuation policy', 'A', 'Self-administration/custody removes independent checks — a major red flag.', 6),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q08', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'Lockups and gates are examples of a fund''s:', 'Liquidity terms', 'Fee waivers', 'Tax elections', 'Benchmarks', 'A', 'Lockups, gates and notice periods define redemption/liquidity terms.', 7),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q09', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'Analyzing the return drivers helps determine if performance is:', 'Skill or beta', 'Tax-free', 'Guaranteed', 'Risk-free', 'A', 'Understanding return drivers separates genuine skill from market exposure.', 8),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q10', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'Operational due diligence can act as a:', 'Veto over an attractive strategy', 'Guarantee of returns', 'Marketing tool', 'Tax shelter', 'A', 'Serious operational weaknesses can rule out even a strong-performing manager.', 9),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q11', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'Business continuity planning addresses:', 'Operating through disruptions', 'Return targets', 'Fee levels', 'Benchmark choice', 'A', 'Continuity planning ensures the firm can operate through disruptions/disasters.', 10),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-due-diligence-q12', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-due-diligence', 'Leverage in a strategy is examined because it:', 'Amplifies both gains and losses', 'Removes risk', 'Guarantees profit', 'Lowers volatility', 'A', 'Due diligence scrutinizes leverage since it magnifies risk.', 11),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-risk-return-q01', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-risk-return', 'Reported alternative-asset returns are often:', 'Smoothed (appraisal-based)', 'Real-time and precise', 'Guaranteed', 'Zero volatility', 'A', 'Appraisal-based valuations smooth returns, understating true volatility.', 0),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-risk-return-q02', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-risk-return', 'Return smoothing tends to ____ measured volatility and correlation.', 'Understate', 'Overstate', 'Not affect', 'Double', 'A', 'Smoothing biases volatility and correlation estimates downward.', 1),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-risk-return-q03', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-risk-return', 'Smoothing also tends to ____ the Sharpe ratio.', 'Overstate', 'Understate', 'Zero out', 'Not affect', 'A', 'Understated volatility inflates the apparent Sharpe ratio.', 2),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-risk-return-q04', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-risk-return', 'Many alternative strategies exhibit returns with:', 'Negative skew and fat tails', 'Positive skew always', 'No tails', 'Constant returns', 'A', 'Alternatives often show negative skew and fat tails (like selling insurance).', 3),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-risk-return-q05', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-risk-return', 'Backfill and survivorship bias in alt indexes tend to:', 'Flatter historical returns', 'Understate returns', 'Have no effect', 'Guarantee losses', 'A', 'These biases overstate historical alternative-index performance.', 4),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-risk-return-q06', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-risk-return', 'In stress, correlations of alternatives with other assets often:', 'Rise, reducing diversification', 'Fall to zero', 'Stay constant', 'Turn strongly negative', 'A', 'Correlations tend to spike in crises, cutting diversification when needed most.', 5),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-risk-return-q07', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-risk-return', 'Downside measures preferred for alternatives include:', 'Sortino, max drawdown, CVaR', 'Only the mean', 'P/E ratio', 'Dividend yield', 'A', 'Because of non-normality, downside measures better capture alternatives'' risk.', 6),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-risk-return-q08', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-risk-return', 'The illiquidity premium is best understood as:', 'Compensation for bearing illiquidity', 'Free return', 'A guaranteed gain', 'A tax credit', 'A', 'The illiquidity premium rewards investors for accepting illiquidity, not free money.', 7),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-risk-return-q09', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-risk-return', 'Allocations to illiquid alternatives should be sized to the investor''s:', 'Ability to bear illiquidity', 'Favorite sector', 'Custodian', 'Tax bracket only', 'A', 'Illiquid allocations must match the investor''s liquidity needs and horizon.', 8),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-risk-return-q10', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-risk-return', 'Using the Sharpe ratio alone for alternatives can be:', 'Misleading due to non-normal returns', 'Perfectly reliable', 'Required by GIPS', 'Tax-efficient', 'A', 'Sharpe assumes normality, which alternatives violate, so it can mislead.', 9),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-risk-return-q11', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-risk-return', 'Alternatives are added to a portfolio for return enhancement and/or:', 'Diversification', 'Higher fees', 'More turnover', 'Tax evasion', 'A', 'The two main rationales are enhancing return and diversifying risk.', 10),
  ('cfa3-alternative-investments-risk-return-q12', 'cfa3-alternative-investments-risk-return', 'Some alternative strategies resemble:', 'Selling insurance (small gains, rare large losses)', 'Buying lottery tickets', 'Holding cash', 'A risk-free bond', 'A', 'Their negative-skew payoff profile is like collecting premiums until a rare large loss.', 11),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-operational-dd-q01', 'cfa3-manager-selection-operational-dd', 'Operational due diligence evaluates a manager''s:', 'Business and controls (not investment merit)', 'Stock picks only', 'Benchmark', 'Tax bracket', 'A', 'ODD focuses on operations and controls, separate from investment analysis.', 0),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-operational-dd-q02', 'cfa3-manager-selection-operational-dd', 'ODD checks the independence of the administrator, custodian, and:', 'Auditor', 'Marketing team', 'Sales desk', 'Regulator', 'A', 'Independent administrator, custodian and auditor are core ODD checks.', 1),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-operational-dd-q03', 'cfa3-manager-selection-operational-dd', 'A red flag in ODD is a(n):', 'Unqualified or tiny auditor', 'Big-four auditor', 'Independent custodian', 'Clear valuation policy', 'A', 'A weak or obscure auditor is a warning sign in operational due diligence.', 2),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-operational-dd-q04', 'cfa3-manager-selection-operational-dd', 'Segregation of duties reduces the risk of:', 'Fraud', 'Diversification', 'High returns', 'Liquidity', 'A', 'Separating responsibilities makes fraud harder to commit and conceal.', 3),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-operational-dd-q05', 'cfa3-manager-selection-operational-dd', 'Self-custody of assets is:', 'A significant red flag', 'Best practice', 'Required by GIPS', 'Risk-free', 'A', 'Holding one''s own assets removes independent safekeeping — a major concern.', 4),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-operational-dd-q06', 'cfa3-manager-selection-operational-dd', 'Many manager failures are:', 'Operational, not investment-driven', 'Due to low fees', 'Caused by diversification', 'From high liquidity', 'A', 'Operational problems, not poor investing, cause many blowups.', 5),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-operational-dd-q07', 'cfa3-manager-selection-operational-dd', 'Cybersecurity is part of:', 'Operational due diligence', 'Return attribution', 'Benchmark selection', 'Tax planning', 'A', 'ODD includes assessing controls such as cybersecurity.', 6),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-operational-dd-q08', 'cfa3-manager-selection-operational-dd', 'AUM stability is reviewed because large outflows can:', 'Threaten the firm''s viability', 'Guarantee returns', 'Lower risk', 'Raise alpha', 'A', 'Unstable assets can impair the business and force forced selling.', 7),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-operational-dd-q09', 'cfa3-manager-selection-operational-dd', 'Valuation of illiquid assets should rely on:', 'Independent pricing sources', 'The manager''s guess only', 'The marketing team', 'Client opinion', 'A', 'Independent valuation reduces the risk of inflated marks.', 8),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-operational-dd-q10', 'cfa3-manager-selection-operational-dd', 'Past regulatory issues are:', 'A due-diligence concern', 'Irrelevant', 'A guarantee of skill', 'A tax benefit', 'A', 'Prior regulatory problems raise operational and reputational concerns.', 9),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-operational-dd-q11', 'cfa3-manager-selection-operational-dd', 'Succession planning addresses:', 'Key-person risk', 'Benchmark risk', 'Currency risk', 'Duration risk', 'A', 'Succession plans mitigate the risk of losing critical individuals.', 10),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-operational-dd-q12', 'cfa3-manager-selection-operational-dd', 'ODD is ____ investment due diligence.', 'As important as', 'Less important than', 'A substitute for', 'Unrelated to', 'A', 'For alternatives, operational due diligence is as important as investment analysis.', 11),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-type-errors-q01', 'cfa3-manager-selection-type-errors', 'A Type I error in manager selection is:', 'Hiring a manager who adds no value', 'Firing a skilled manager', 'Perfect selection', 'Avoiding all managers', 'A', 'A Type I error keeps/hires a no-skill manager (a false positive).', 0),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-type-errors-q02', 'cfa3-manager-selection-type-errors', 'A Type II error in manager selection is:', 'Rejecting a manager who does add value', 'Hiring a bad manager', 'Perfect selection', 'Diversifying', 'A', 'A Type II error rejects/fires a skilled manager (a false negative).', 1),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-type-errors-q03', 'cfa3-manager-selection-type-errors', 'Chasing recent outperformance tends to raise:', 'Type I errors', 'Type II errors only', 'Neither error', 'The risk-free rate', 'A', 'Buying past winners often means paying for luck (Type I error).', 2),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-type-errors-q04', 'cfa3-manager-selection-type-errors', 'Firing after short-term underperformance tends to raise:', 'Type II errors', 'Type I errors', 'Neither error', 'Liquidity', 'A', 'Dropping a skilled manager during a rough patch is a Type II error.', 3),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-type-errors-q05', 'cfa3-manager-selection-type-errors', 'A Type I error is also called a:', 'False positive', 'False negative', 'True positive', 'True negative', 'A', 'Rejecting the null ''no skill'' when it is true is a false positive.', 4),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-type-errors-q06', 'cfa3-manager-selection-type-errors', 'A Type II error is also called a:', 'False negative', 'False positive', 'True positive', 'True negative', 'A', 'Failing to identify true skill is a false negative.', 5),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-type-errors-q07', 'cfa3-manager-selection-type-errors', 'Both error types are common because performance is:', 'Noisy', 'Perfectly predictable', 'Constant', 'Risk-free', 'A', 'The noise in returns makes both false positives and negatives frequent.', 6),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-type-errors-q08', 'cfa3-manager-selection-type-errors', 'A robust process weighs statistical significance against the cost of:', 'Switching managers', 'Filing taxes', 'Changing custodians only', 'Marketing', 'A', 'Selection balances the evidence of skill against turnover/switching costs.', 7),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-type-errors-q09', 'cfa3-manager-selection-type-errors', 'The null hypothesis in manager selection is typically:', 'The manager has no skill', 'The manager has skill', 'The market is closed', 'Fees are zero', 'A', 'The null is ''no value added''; evidence must overcome it.', 8),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-type-errors-q10', 'cfa3-manager-selection-type-errors', 'Reducing Type I errors often:', 'Increases Type II errors (a trade-off)', 'Eliminates all errors', 'Has no effect', 'Guarantees skill', 'A', 'Being stricter cuts false positives but raises false negatives, and vice versa.', 9),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-type-errors-q11', 'cfa3-manager-selection-type-errors', 'Paying for luck rather than skill is the essence of a:', 'Type I error', 'Type II error', 'Correct decision', 'Benchmark', 'A', 'Hiring an unskilled but lucky manager is a Type I error.', 10),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-type-errors-q12', 'cfa3-manager-selection-type-errors', 'Evidence-based, disciplined processes aim to:', 'Reduce both error types', 'Increase turnover', 'Maximize fees', 'Ignore performance', 'A', 'A rigorous process lowers the frequency of both selection errors.', 11),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-capture-ratios-q01', 'cfa3-manager-selection-capture-ratios', 'Upside capture measures manager return relative to the benchmark in:', 'Up markets', 'Down markets', 'Flat markets', 'All markets equally', 'A', 'Upside capture is the manager/benchmark return ratio during up markets.', 0),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-capture-ratios-q02', 'cfa3-manager-selection-capture-ratios', 'Downside capture below 100% is:', 'Favorable', 'Unfavorable', 'Impossible', 'Irrelevant', 'A', 'Capturing less than 100% of down markets means smaller losses — favorable.', 1),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-capture-ratios-q03', 'cfa3-manager-selection-capture-ratios', 'The overall capture ratio equals upside capture divided by:', 'Downside capture', 'Beta', 'The Sharpe ratio', 'Turnover', 'A', 'Capture ratio = upside capture ÷ downside capture; >1 is favorable.', 2),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-capture-ratios-q04', 'cfa3-manager-selection-capture-ratios', 'A capture ratio greater than 1 indicates:', 'Favorable asymmetry (more up than down)', 'Guaranteed loss', 'No skill', 'High fees', 'A', 'A capture ratio above 1 means the manager captures more upside than downside.', 3),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-capture-ratios-q05', 'cfa3-manager-selection-capture-ratios', 'Batting average measures the fraction of periods a manager:', 'Beats the benchmark', 'Loses money', 'Pays dividends', 'Rebalances', 'A', 'Batting average is the proportion of periods the manager outperforms the benchmark.', 4),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-capture-ratios-q06', 'cfa3-manager-selection-capture-ratios', 'Upside capture above 100% means the manager:', 'Gains more than the benchmark in up markets', 'Loses in up markets', 'Matches the benchmark exactly', 'Holds cash', 'A', 'Upside capture over 100% means outperforming the benchmark when it rises.', 5),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-capture-ratios-q07', 'cfa3-manager-selection-capture-ratios', 'Capture ratios reveal a manager''s:', 'Convexity (asymmetry of returns)', 'Tax rate', 'Custodian', 'Fee schedule', 'A', 'They show whether a manager''s return profile is convex (favorable asymmetry).', 6),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-capture-ratios-q08', 'cfa3-manager-selection-capture-ratios', 'Maximum drawdown measures the:', 'Largest peak-to-trough loss', 'Average gain', 'Dividend yield', 'Turnover', 'A', 'Max drawdown is the biggest peak-to-trough decline over a period.', 7),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-capture-ratios-q09', 'cfa3-manager-selection-capture-ratios', 'The ideal manager profile captures more of the ____ than the downside.', 'Upside', 'Fees', 'Turnover', 'Duration', 'A', 'A favorable profile captures more upside while limiting downside.', 8),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-capture-ratios-q10', 'cfa3-manager-selection-capture-ratios', 'Consistency of the information ratio indicates:', 'Reliable active skill', 'High fees', 'Low liquidity', 'Tax efficiency', 'A', 'A stable IR over time suggests durable, repeatable skill.', 9),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-capture-ratios-q11', 'cfa3-manager-selection-capture-ratios', 'A downside capture of 80% means the manager fell ____ the benchmark in down markets.', 'Less than', 'More than', 'Exactly with', 'Twice as much as', 'A', '80% downside capture means the manager lost only 80% as much as the benchmark.', 10),
  ('cfa3-manager-selection-capture-ratios-q12', 'cfa3-manager-selection-capture-ratios', 'Capture ratios and drawdowns are examples of ____ metrics.', 'Manager-evaluation', 'Tax', 'Custody', 'Benchmark-licensing', 'A', 'These are quantitative metrics used to evaluate and select managers.', 11),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-management-capm-q01', 'frm-foundations-risk-management-capm', 'Under the CAPM, which type of risk earns a return premium?', 'Idiosyncratic risk', 'Systematic (market) risk', 'Diversifiable risk', 'Firm-specific risk', 'B', 'Only non-diversifiable systematic risk, measured by beta, is rewarded; idiosyncratic risk can be diversified away.', 0),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-management-capm-q02', 'frm-foundations-risk-management-capm', 'In the CAPM equation, beta measures an asset''s:', 'Total return volatility', 'Sensitivity to overall market movements', 'Dividend yield', 'Default probability', 'B', 'Beta is the asset''s sensitivity to the market; it scales the market risk premium in CAPM.', 1),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-management-capm-q03', 'frm-foundations-risk-management-capm', 'The Sharpe ratio divides excess return by:', 'Beta', 'Market return', 'Total risk (standard deviation)', 'Tracking error', 'C', 'The Sharpe ratio uses total risk in the denominator, whereas the Treynor ratio uses beta.', 2),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-management-capm-q04', 'frm-foundations-risk-management-capm', 'Jensen''s alpha represents the portfolio return:', 'Equal to the risk-free rate', 'Below the benchmark', 'In excess of the CAPM prediction', 'Equal to the market return', 'C', 'Alpha is the return earned above what CAPM predicts, often used as a measure of manager skill.', 3),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-management-capm-q05', 'frm-foundations-risk-management-capm', 'An asset with a beta of 1.0 is expected to:', 'Outperform the market in every period', 'Have zero risk', 'Move with the overall market', 'Earn only the risk-free rate', 'C', 'A beta of 1.0 means the asset moves in line with the market on average.', 4),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-management-capm-q06', 'frm-foundations-risk-management-capm', 'Which risk can be reduced by holding a well-diversified portfolio?', 'Idiosyncratic risk', 'Systematic risk', 'Market risk', 'Undiversifiable risk', 'A', 'Idiosyncratic, firm-specific risk is diversifiable; systematic market risk remains regardless of diversification.', 5),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-management-capm-q07', 'frm-foundations-risk-management-capm', 'The market risk premium in the CAPM is defined as:', 'The risk-free rate alone', 'The expected market return minus the risk-free rate', 'Beta times the risk-free rate', 'The total volatility of the market', 'B', 'The market risk premium is the expected market return less the risk-free rate.', 6),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-management-capm-q08', 'frm-foundations-risk-management-capm', 'Credit risk is best described as the risk of loss from:', 'Movements in interest rates', 'Failed internal processes', 'An inability to sell at fair value', 'A counterparty failing to meet its obligations', 'D', 'Credit risk is the loss arising when a borrower or counterparty fails to pay as promised.', 7),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-volatility-ewma-garch-q01', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-volatility-ewma-garch', 'In the EWMA model, a higher decay factor lambda produces a volatility series that is:', 'More jagged and faster to react', 'Smoother and slower to react', 'Always constant', 'Equal to the long-run variance', 'B', 'A higher lambda gives recent returns relatively less extra weight, producing a smoother, slower-reacting estimate.', 0),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-volatility-ewma-garch-q02', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-volatility-ewma-garch', 'The RiskMetrics daily EWMA model uses a lambda of approximately:', '0.50', '0.72', '0.94', '1.20', 'C', 'RiskMetrics adopts lambda equal to 0.94 for daily volatility estimation.', 1),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-volatility-ewma-garch-q03', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-volatility-ewma-garch', 'In a GARCH(1,1) model, persistence is measured by:', 'omega only', 'omega divided by alpha', 'alpha minus beta', 'alpha plus beta', 'D', 'Persistence equals alpha plus beta; it must be below 1 for the model to be mean-reverting.', 2),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-volatility-ewma-garch-q04', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-volatility-ewma-garch', 'The long-run variance implied by a GARCH(1,1) model equals:', 'omega divided by (1 minus alpha minus beta)', 'omega times alpha', 'alpha divided by beta', 'alpha plus beta', 'A', 'The unconditional long-run variance is omega divided by (1 minus alpha minus beta).', 3),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-volatility-ewma-garch-q05', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-volatility-ewma-garch', 'EWMA is a special case of GARCH(1,1) in which:', 'omega is zero and alpha plus beta equals 1', 'alpha equals beta equals zero', 'omega equals 1', 'beta is greater than 1', 'A', 'Setting omega to zero and alpha plus beta to 1 reduces GARCH(1,1) to the EWMA recursion.', 4),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-volatility-ewma-garch-q06', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-volatility-ewma-garch', 'The tendency of large return moves to be followed by large moves is called:', 'Volatility clustering', 'Mean reversion', 'Heteroskedastic decay', 'Ghosting', 'A', 'Volatility clustering describes periods of high volatility persisting, a key motivation for EWMA and GARCH.', 5),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-volatility-ewma-garch-q07', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-volatility-ewma-garch', 'A drawback of the equally weighted variance estimate is the ghosting effect, which occurs when:', 'Recent returns are overweighted', 'Volatility reverts to its mean too quickly', 'The variance can never change', 'A large past return abruptly drops out of the window', 'D', 'Ghosting is the sudden jump in the estimate when a large observation leaves the equally weighted window.', 6),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-volatility-ewma-garch-q08', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-volatility-ewma-garch', 'Compared with EWMA, the GARCH(1,1) model additionally incorporates:', 'No weighting of past returns', 'Only the most recent squared return', 'A fixed lambda of 0.94', 'A long-run variance term that drives mean reversion', 'D', 'GARCH(1,1) adds the omega term tied to long-run variance, giving it mean reversion that EWMA lacks.', 7),
  ('frm-financial-markets-futures-forwards-hedging-q01', 'frm-financial-markets-futures-forwards-hedging', 'Which feature distinguishes futures from forward contracts?', 'Futures are customized over-the-counter deals', 'Futures are exchange-traded and marked-to-market daily', 'Futures carry more counterparty credit risk', 'Futures settle only at maturity', 'B', 'Futures are standardized, exchange-traded, and marked-to-market daily through a clearinghouse, unlike forwards.', 0),
  ('frm-financial-markets-futures-forwards-hedging-q02', 'frm-financial-markets-futures-forwards-hedging', 'For a non-dividend asset, the cost-of-carry forward price is:', 'S_0 minus r times T', 'S_0 divided by r', 'S_0 times e raised to (r times T)', 'S_0 times r times T', 'C', 'Under cost of carry with no income, the forward price equals spot compounded at the risk-free rate over T.', 1),
  ('frm-financial-markets-futures-forwards-hedging-q03', 'frm-financial-markets-futures-forwards-hedging', 'The minimum-variance hedge ratio is equal to:', 'The ratio of spot price to futures price', 'One minus the correlation', 'Futures volatility divided by spot volatility', 'Correlation times the ratio of spot volatility to futures volatility', 'D', 'The optimal hedge ratio is the correlation multiplied by the ratio of spot to futures return volatility.', 2),
  ('frm-financial-markets-futures-forwards-hedging-q04', 'frm-financial-markets-futures-forwards-hedging', 'A market is in backwardation when the futures price is:', 'Below the spot price', 'Above the spot price', 'Zero', 'Equal to the spot price', 'A', 'Backwardation describes a futures price below the spot price; contango is the opposite case.', 3),
  ('frm-financial-markets-futures-forwards-hedging-q05', 'frm-financial-markets-futures-forwards-hedging', 'Basis is defined as:', 'Futures price times the hedge ratio', 'Spot price minus futures price', 'The risk-free rate', 'The sum of spot and futures prices', 'B', 'Basis equals the spot price minus the futures price and converges to zero as delivery approaches.', 4),
  ('frm-financial-markets-futures-forwards-hedging-q06', 'frm-financial-markets-futures-forwards-hedging', 'An investor who already owns an asset and fears a price decline should use a:', 'Short hedge', 'Long hedge', 'Naked call', 'Cash-and-carry arbitrage', 'A', 'A short hedge (selling futures) protects the value of an asset already held against falling prices.', 5),
  ('frm-financial-markets-futures-forwards-hedging-q07', 'frm-financial-markets-futures-forwards-hedging', 'Basis risk in a hedge arises mainly when:', 'The hedge perfectly matches the exposure', 'The hedging instrument or maturity does not match the underlying', 'The contract is exchange-cleared', 'Futures are marked-to-market daily', 'B', 'Basis risk results from mismatches in asset, maturity, or quantity between the position and the hedging contract.', 6),
  ('frm-financial-markets-futures-forwards-hedging-q08', 'frm-financial-markets-futures-forwards-hedging', 'Compared with a forward, an exchange-traded future largely removes counterparty credit risk because of:', 'Daily news disclosure', 'The absence of margin', 'Customized contract terms', 'The clearinghouse acting as central counterparty', 'D', 'The clearinghouse interposes itself as central counterparty and collects margin, sharply reducing credit risk.', 7),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-value-at-risk-q01', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-value-at-risk', 'A one-day 99% VaR of 2 million means there is a:', '99% chance of losing exactly 2 million', 'Certainty of losing 2 million', '1% chance of losing more than 2 million in a day', '1% chance of gaining 2 million', 'C', 'A 99% one-day VaR of 2 million implies a 1% probability that the daily loss exceeds 2 million.', 0),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-value-at-risk-q02', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-value-at-risk', 'In the delta-normal VaR formula, the z-value used for 99% confidence is approximately:', '1.28', '1.65', '1.96', '2.33', 'D', 'The one-tailed standard-normal quantile for 99% confidence is about 2.33.', 1),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-value-at-risk-q03', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-value-at-risk', 'To scale a one-day VaR to a 10-day horizon under the square-root-of-time rule, you multiply by:', '10', 'The square root of 10', '100', '1', 'B', 'VaR scales with the square root of time, so the 10-day VaR is the one-day VaR times the square root of 10.', 2),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-value-at-risk-q04', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-value-at-risk', 'Expected Shortfall (Conditional VaR) measures the:', 'Most likely daily gain', 'Loss at exactly the VaR level only', 'Probability of any loss', 'Average loss in the tail beyond the VaR threshold', 'D', 'Expected Shortfall is the average of losses that exceed the VaR cutoff, capturing tail severity.', 3),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-value-at-risk-q05', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-value-at-risk', 'Portfolio VaR is generally less than the sum of component VaRs because of:', 'Diversification from imperfect correlation', 'Higher volatility', 'Leverage', 'Margin requirements', 'A', 'Imperfect correlation among assets produces a diversification benefit, lowering portfolio VaR below the sum of parts.', 4),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-value-at-risk-q06', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-value-at-risk', 'Which property of VaR makes Expected Shortfall preferred by regulators?', 'VaR always overstates risk', 'VaR ignores the confidence level', 'VaR cannot be computed parametrically', 'VaR can fail subadditivity for non-normal portfolios', 'D', 'VaR can violate subadditivity, so it is not always coherent; Expected Shortfall is coherent and captures the tail.', 5),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-value-at-risk-q07', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-value-at-risk', 'In the delta-normal approach, VaR is computed as z times sigma times the square root of t times:', 'The risk-free rate', 'The portfolio value', 'Beta', 'The correlation', 'B', 'Parametric VaR equals z times volatility times the square root of the horizon times the portfolio value.', 6),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-value-at-risk-q08', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-value-at-risk', 'Lowering the confidence level of a VaR estimate from 99% to 95% will, all else equal:', 'Increase the reported VaR', 'Decrease the reported VaR', 'Leave VaR unchanged', 'Make VaR negative', 'B', 'A lower confidence level uses a smaller z-value, producing a smaller VaR figure.', 7),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-types-taxonomy-q01', 'frm-foundations-risk-types-taxonomy', 'A bank cannot exit a large equity position without moving the market price significantly against itself. This is best described as:', 'Funding liquidity risk', 'Credit spread risk', 'Operational risk', 'Market liquidity risk', 'D', 'Market liquidity risk is the inability to transact at or near the current market price due to insufficient market depth. Funding liquidity risk relates to meeting cash obligations, not position exit constraints.', 0),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-types-taxonomy-q02', 'frm-foundations-risk-types-taxonomy', 'Which risk category encompasses losses arising from failed internal processes, people, systems, or external events?', 'Operational risk', 'Credit risk', 'Strategic risk', 'Market risk', 'A', 'Operational risk is explicitly defined in Basel III as losses from failed internal processes, people, systems, or external events. This is distinct from financial risks such as market or credit risk.', 1),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-types-taxonomy-q03', 'frm-foundations-risk-types-taxonomy', 'A sovereign debt downgrade causes an investment-grade bond to fall to sub-investment-grade status, resulting in forced selling and losses for a fund. Which risk type is most directly illustrated?', 'Default risk', 'Reputational risk', 'Market liquidity risk', 'Migration risk', 'D', 'Migration risk is the loss from deterioration in credit quality (credit rating migration) rather than outright default. The downgrade itself, independent of default, creates the loss.', 2),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-types-taxonomy-q04', 'frm-foundations-risk-types-taxonomy', 'Under Basel III, which of the following risk categories explicitly requires regulatory capital allocation?', 'Reputational risk', 'Strategic risk', 'Operational risk', 'Legal risk', 'C', 'Basel III''s Pillar 1 requires explicit capital charges for market, credit, and operational risk. Reputational and strategic risk are Pillar 2 considerations assessed under the ICAAP rather than through standardised capital formulae.', 3),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-types-taxonomy-q05', 'frm-foundations-risk-types-taxonomy', 'An enterprise risk taxonomy is primarily designed to ensure which of the following?', 'That individual business units manage risk independently without central oversight', 'Consistent identification and classification of all material risks across the firm', 'Elimination of all credit and market risks from the balance sheet', 'Compliance with a single international regulatory standard', 'B', 'A risk taxonomy provides a common language and classification framework that enables consistent identification, measurement, and reporting of risks across all business lines and functions.', 4),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-types-taxonomy-q06', 'frm-foundations-risk-types-taxonomy', 'A bank''s decision to exit a profitable business line due to persistent adverse media coverage is driven primarily by which risk?', 'Reputational risk', 'Operational risk', 'Credit risk', 'Funding liquidity risk', 'A', 'Reputational risk reflects the threat of damage to a firm''s standing in the eyes of customers, counterparties, and the public — the adverse media coverage and resulting business exit is a classic reputational risk manifestation.', 5),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-types-taxonomy-q07', 'frm-foundations-risk-types-taxonomy', 'Which metric pair is most closely associated with measuring credit risk exposure?', 'VaR and Expected Shortfall', 'Duration and convexity', 'Liquidity Coverage Ratio and Net Stable Funding Ratio', 'Probability of Default and Loss Given Default', 'D', 'Probability of Default (PD) and Loss Given Default (LGD) are the fundamental building blocks of credit risk measurement, used together with Exposure at Default (EAD) to calculate Expected Loss.', 6),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-types-taxonomy-q08', 'frm-foundations-risk-types-taxonomy', 'When a credit event at one institution triggers a funding crisis at another due to interbank exposures, which risk interaction is most prominently illustrated?', 'Legal risk creating market risk', 'Market risk amplifying operational risk', 'Strategic risk converting to reputational risk', 'Credit risk triggering liquidity risk', 'D', 'The 2008 financial crisis demonstrated how credit events (counterparty defaults) can immediately cascade into funding liquidity stress, illustrating that risk categories are interdependent rather than isolated.', 7),
  ('frm-foundations-corporate-governance-q01', 'frm-foundations-corporate-governance', 'Under the three lines of defense model, which line is responsible for providing independent assurance over risk management and controls?', 'Second line (risk and compliance)', 'Third line (internal audit)', 'First line (business units)', 'External audit', 'B', 'Internal audit constitutes the third line of defense, providing independent assurance that the first and second lines are functioning effectively. External audit is separate from the three-lines model.', 0),
  ('frm-foundations-corporate-governance-q02', 'frm-foundations-corporate-governance', 'A Chief Risk Officer (CRO) reports exclusively to the CEO with no direct access to the board. What governance weakness does this create?', 'Excessive risk appetite', 'Insufficient separation between the CRO and business lines', 'Potential lack of independence, as commercial pressures could override risk assessments', 'Inadequate quantitative risk modelling capability', 'C', 'CRO independence requires direct reporting access to the board, not solely to the CEO. Without board access, the CRO''s risk assessments may be filtered or overridden by commercial considerations.', 1),
  ('frm-foundations-corporate-governance-q03', 'frm-foundations-corporate-governance', 'Which of the following is the BEST indicator of a strong risk culture within a financial institution?', 'Senior leaders visibly endorse prudent risk behaviour and welcome escalation of concerns', 'Employees consistently prioritise short-term revenue targets above limit compliance', 'Risk limits are set conservatively and never reviewed', 'Internal audit findings are addressed only when mandated by regulators', 'A', 'Tone from the top — senior leaders visibly modelling and rewarding prudent risk behaviour — is the primary driver of a strong risk culture, creating an environment where staff escalate concerns without fear.', 2),
  ('frm-foundations-corporate-governance-q04', 'frm-foundations-corporate-governance', 'The Board Risk Committee''s primary responsibility is best described as:', 'Executing day-to-day risk management decisions on behalf of the CEO', 'Conducting internal audits of the risk management function', 'Preparing regulatory filings and stress test submissions', 'Setting and approving the firm''s risk appetite and overseeing major risk exposures', 'D', 'The Board Risk Committee''s core mandate is to set risk appetite at a strategic level and provide oversight of whether management''s risk-taking remains within approved boundaries — it does not execute operational decisions.', 3),
  ('frm-foundations-corporate-governance-q05', 'frm-foundations-corporate-governance', 'A trading desk manager is responsible for both approving trades and reconciling back-office records. This arrangement violates which governance principle?', 'Tone from the top', 'Segregation of duties', 'Risk appetite alignment', 'The three lines of defense model', 'B', 'Segregation of duties requires that no single individual controls multiple stages of a transaction (execution, approval, recording). Combining trading and back-office reconciliation creates unchecked opportunity for fraud, as demonstrated by the Barings Bank collapse.', 4),
  ('frm-foundations-corporate-governance-q06', 'frm-foundations-corporate-governance', 'Which of the following is a characteristic of the SECOND line of defense?', 'Directly generates revenue through customer transactions', 'Provides independent assurance by auditing both the first and second lines', 'Establishes risk policies, frameworks, and oversight without owning business risk', 'Issues regulatory capital requirements to the firm', 'C', 'The second line (risk management and compliance) creates the framework within which the first line operates, providing oversight and challenge without bearing primary responsibility for revenue generation or independent audit.', 5),
  ('frm-foundations-corporate-governance-q07', 'frm-foundations-corporate-governance', 'Regulators increasingly assess risk culture as a standalone supervisory concern. Which observation would MOST likely trigger regulatory concern about a firm''s risk culture?', 'Staff who raise concerns about mis-selling practices face informal sanctions', 'Risk appetite is reviewed annually', 'The CRO attends all board meetings', 'Internal audit reports are shared with external auditors', 'A', 'Suppression of whistleblowing and retaliation against staff raising legitimate concerns are direct indicators of a dysfunctional risk culture where negative information does not flow to decision-makers.', 6),
  ('frm-foundations-corporate-governance-q08', 'frm-foundations-corporate-governance', 'Which governance structure best ensures that the risk management function remains independent from commercial pressures?', 'CRO reports to the head of the trading business to improve information flow', 'Risk staff are rotated through business units to build commercial knowledge', 'CRO has a direct reporting line to the board and independent authority to escalate risk concerns', 'Risk limits are set by trading desks and submitted to the board for information', 'C', 'CRO independence is preserved by direct board access and authority to escalate risk concerns without management filtering. Reporting into commercial units creates conflicts of interest that undermine the second line''s effectiveness.', 7),
  ('frm-foundations-erp-risk-appetite-q01', 'frm-foundations-erp-risk-appetite', 'Which statement best distinguishes risk appetite from risk tolerance?', 'Risk appetite defines the desired level of risk-taking; risk tolerance defines the acceptable deviation around that level', 'Risk appetite is quantitative; risk tolerance is always qualitative', 'Risk tolerance is set by regulators; risk appetite is set by the board', 'Risk appetite applies to operational risk only; risk tolerance applies to market risk', 'A', 'Risk appetite is the aggregate level of risk the firm is willing to accept in pursuit of strategy; risk tolerance is the allowable band of deviation around the appetite within which management operates day-to-day.', 0),
  ('frm-foundations-erp-risk-appetite-q02', 'frm-foundations-erp-risk-appetite', 'A bank observes that its derivative counterparty exposure is rising toward a pre-set threshold before a formal limit is breached. This threshold is best described as a:', 'Risk register entry', 'Stress test scenario', 'Key Risk Indicator (KRI)', 'Value-at-Risk estimate', 'C', 'Key Risk Indicators (KRIs) are forward-looking metrics that provide early warning when risk levels are approaching limits, enabling preventive action before a formal breach occurs.', 1),
  ('frm-foundations-erp-risk-appetite-q03', 'frm-foundations-erp-risk-appetite', 'The primary advantage of Enterprise Risk Management (ERM) over traditional silo-based risk management is:', 'ERM eliminates the need for quantitative risk models within business units', 'ERM recognises risk interdependencies and enables portfolio-level aggregation across all risk types', 'ERM transfers all risk management responsibility to the compliance department', 'ERM reduces regulatory capital requirements to zero', 'B', 'ERM''s key advantage is an integrated, firm-wide view that captures how risks interact and aggregate, whereas silo-based management can miss concentrations and correlations that only become visible at the enterprise level.', 2),
  ('frm-foundations-erp-risk-appetite-q04', 'frm-foundations-erp-risk-appetite', 'A risk register should be updated:', 'Only when a loss event occurs', 'Continuously, as a living document reflecting the current risk environment', 'Annually, immediately before the regulatory inspection', 'Only when new products are launched', 'B', 'A risk register is a living document that must be maintained continuously to reflect emerging risks, changes in the control environment, and resolved risk items — not treated as a static compliance exercise.', 3),
  ('frm-foundations-erp-risk-appetite-q05', 'frm-foundations-erp-risk-appetite', 'Which component of the risk appetite framework translates the broad appetite statement into actionable day-to-day constraints for business units?', 'Risk register', 'Stress test results', 'ICAAP document', 'Risk limits', 'D', 'Risk limits (e.g., VaR limits by desk, concentration limits by counterparty) cascade the board-level appetite statement into specific, enforceable constraints that guide operational decision-making.', 4),
  ('frm-foundations-erp-risk-appetite-q06', 'frm-foundations-erp-risk-appetite', 'Stress testing within an ERM framework primarily serves to:', 'Replace the need for day-to-day risk limit monitoring', 'Determine the probability of default for individual borrowers', 'Eliminate model risk from the risk measurement process', 'Validate whether the firm can withstand severe but plausible adverse scenarios', 'D', 'Stress testing assesses the firm''s resilience under extreme but plausible scenarios, complementing probabilistic measures like VaR by revealing vulnerabilities that tail probabilities alone may not capture.', 5),
  ('frm-foundations-erp-risk-appetite-q07', 'frm-foundations-erp-risk-appetite', 'Which of the following best describes the relationship between risk appetite and strategic objectives?', 'Risk appetite translates strategic objectives into the level and type of risk the firm is willing to accept in pursuing them', 'Risk appetite is set independently of strategy by the risk function', 'Strategic objectives are derived from the risk appetite statement', 'Risk appetite applies only to financial risks, not strategic decisions', 'A', 'The risk appetite framework operationalises strategy by defining what risks are acceptable in its pursuit — appetite cannot be set without reference to the firm''s strategic goals and business model.', 6),
  ('frm-foundations-erp-risk-appetite-q08', 'frm-foundations-erp-risk-appetite', 'Under traditional silo-based risk management, which of the following problems is most likely to emerge?', 'Concentration risks that span multiple categories are missed because no single function has the full picture', 'Excessive regulatory capital requirements for market risk', 'KRIs are set too conservatively relative to risk appetite', 'Internal audit becomes too independent from business operations', 'A', 'Silo-based management optimises individual risk categories in isolation, creating blind spots for cross-category concentrations and correlated risks that only become visible when viewed at the enterprise level.', 7),
  ('frm-foundations-financial-disasters-q01', 'frm-foundations-financial-disasters', 'The collapse of Barings Bank in 1995 is primarily attributed to which risk management failure?', 'Excessive market risk from proprietary currency trading', 'Inadequate credit limits on sovereign bond exposures', 'A single trader controlling both trading and back-office settlement, with no independent oversight', 'Model risk from mispriced equity derivatives', 'C', 'Nick Leeson exploited the absence of segregation of duties by controlling both the trading function and the back-office account (88888) used to conceal losses, a fundamental operational control failure.', 0),
  ('frm-foundations-financial-disasters-q02', 'frm-foundations-financial-disasters', 'Metallgesellschaft''s 1993 losses were primarily caused by:', 'Credit defaults on long-dated energy supply contracts', 'Overconcentration in a single equity position', 'A rogue trader accumulating speculative foreign exchange positions', 'Ignoring funding liquidity risk when using short-dated futures to hedge long-dated oil commitments', 'D', 'MG''s hedging strategy was theoretically sound in terms of price risk, but the short-dated futures generated enormous mark-to-market margin calls when oil prices fell, creating a funding liquidity crisis that forced liquidation of the hedge.', 1),
  ('frm-foundations-financial-disasters-q03', 'frm-foundations-financial-disasters', 'LTCM''s failure in 1998 demonstrated that during a market crisis, correlations between assets tend to:', 'Converge toward 1, eliminating assumed diversification benefits', 'Remain stable as predicted by historical models', 'Decrease, providing unexpected diversification benefits', 'Become negative, amplifying portfolio gains', 'A', 'The Russian debt default triggered a flight to quality in which assets that were historically uncorrelated moved together, causing correlations to converge toward 1 and destroying the diversification assumptions underlying LTCM''s models.', 2),
  ('frm-foundations-financial-disasters-q04', 'frm-foundations-financial-disasters', 'The Gaussian copula model used to price CDOs before the 2008 financial crisis failed primarily because it:', 'Overestimated interest rate volatility', 'Assumed no tail dependency between defaults, underestimating the probability of simultaneous defaults in stress', 'Used an incorrect risk-free rate for discounting cash flows', 'Applied fat-tailed distributions that overstated expected losses', 'B', 'The Gaussian copula has zero tail dependency, meaning it severely underestimated the probability that many mortgages would default simultaneously in a stress scenario — the exact pattern observed in 2008.', 3),
  ('frm-foundations-financial-disasters-q05', 'frm-foundations-financial-disasters', 'Which risk concept describes the situation where counterparty default risk is positively correlated with the size of the exposure to that counterparty?', 'Credit migration risk', 'Funding liquidity risk', 'Wrong-way risk', 'Basis risk', 'C', 'Wrong-way risk exists when exposure to a counterparty increases at the same time as the counterparty''s creditworthiness deteriorates, making the credit loss larger precisely when the counterparty is most likely to default.', 4),
  ('frm-foundations-financial-disasters-q06', 'frm-foundations-financial-disasters', 'A common lesson from multiple financial disasters (Barings, LTCM, 2008 crisis) is that:', 'Quantitative models are inherently unreliable and should be abandoned', 'Regulatory capital requirements are always sufficient to absorb unexpected losses', 'Leverage and model risk amplify losses beyond what intuitive risk assessment anticipates', 'Operational risk is the dominant source of systemic financial crises', 'C', 'Across all major financial disasters, excessive leverage magnified losses while model risk — the failure of models to capture real-world behaviour in stress — left institutions unprepared for the magnitude of actual losses.', 5),
  ('frm-foundations-financial-disasters-q07', 'frm-foundations-financial-disasters', 'The 2008 financial crisis revealed a significant overreliance on which risk assessment tool for structured credit products?', 'Stress testing under regulatory scenarios', 'Duration-based interest rate sensitivity', 'Historical simulation VaR', 'External credit ratings from agencies', 'D', 'Investors and institutions heavily relied on AAA ratings assigned by credit rating agencies to CDO tranches without independently assessing the underlying collateral quality or model assumptions, a key contributor to the crisis.', 6),
  ('frm-foundations-financial-disasters-q08', 'frm-foundations-financial-disasters', 'Model risk is best defined as:', 'The risk that a quantitative model produces outputs with insufficient decimal precision', 'The risk that IT systems used to run models may fail unexpectedly', 'The risk that model documentation is not shared with regulators', 'The risk of adverse consequences from decisions based on incorrect or misused models', 'D', 'Model risk encompasses both errors in model specification or calibration (a model that is wrong) and the misapplication of a correctly specified model to situations outside its intended scope.', 7),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-probability-distributions-q01', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-probability-distributions', 'A risk manager uses a normal distribution to estimate 99% VaR for a trading portfolio. Compared to using a Student-t distribution with 5 degrees of freedom, the normal distribution will likely:', 'Produce a higher VaR estimate due to fatter tails', 'Produce a lower VaR estimate due to thinner tails', 'Produce the same VaR estimate since both are symmetric', 'Produce a higher VaR estimate due to greater skewness', 'B', 'The normal distribution has thinner tails than the Student-t, so at high confidence levels (99%) it assigns less probability mass to extreme losses and therefore produces a lower VaR estimate than the fat-tailed t-distribution.', 0),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-probability-distributions-q02', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-probability-distributions', 'The lognormal distribution is preferred over the normal distribution for modelling equity prices primarily because:', 'It has fatter tails that better capture equity market crashes', 'It ensures prices cannot fall below zero', 'It is easier to estimate parameters than the normal distribution', 'It is symmetric, matching observed equity return distributions', 'B', 'If a stock price followed a normal distribution, it could theoretically become negative. The lognormal distribution constrains prices to positive values, which is consistent with limited liability for equity holders.', 1),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-probability-distributions-q03', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-probability-distributions', 'As the degrees of freedom parameter (nu) in a Student-t distribution increases toward infinity, the distribution:', 'Becomes increasingly skewed to the right', 'Develops progressively fatter tails', 'Converges to the standard normal distribution', 'Converges to a Poisson distribution', 'C', 'The Student-t distribution converges to the standard normal as degrees of freedom approach infinity, because the additional uncertainty from estimating the variance (which drives the fat tails) disappears.', 2),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-probability-distributions-q04', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-probability-distributions', 'In operational risk modelling under the Loss Distribution Approach, the Poisson distribution is used to model:', 'The severity of individual loss events', 'The correlation between different loss event types', 'The frequency of loss events in a given period', 'The capital charge for market risk', 'C', 'The LDA separates frequency and severity: frequency (the number of loss events per period) is typically modelled with a Poisson distribution, while severity (the size of each loss) uses a heavy-tailed distribution such as lognormal or GPD.', 3),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-probability-distributions-q05', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-probability-distributions', 'A credit portfolio model assumes each obligor defaults independently with a probability of 2%. The number of defaults in a pool of 100 obligors is best modelled using which distribution?', 'Normal distribution', 'Lognormal distribution', 'Binomial distribution', 'Student-t distribution', 'C', 'With n independent Bernoulli trials (default/no-default) each with probability p, the total number of defaults follows a Binomial(n, p) distribution. The normal approximation may be used for large n, but the binomial is the exact model.', 4),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-probability-distributions-q06', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-probability-distributions', 'Which property of empirical financial return distributions does the normal distribution most significantly fail to capture?', 'Positive mean returns over long horizons', 'Excess kurtosis (fat tails) and negative skewness', 'The continuous nature of returns', 'Time-varying correlations between assets', 'B', 'Empirical studies consistently show that financial returns exhibit excess kurtosis (more probability mass in the tails than normal) and negative skewness, both of which the normal distribution cannot accommodate.', 5),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-probability-distributions-q07', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-probability-distributions', 'Using a normal distribution to estimate VaR at high confidence levels (e.g., 99.9%) in the presence of fat-tailed returns will most likely result in:', 'Overestimation of VaR, leading to excess capital held', 'Accurate VaR estimates since the CLT applies', 'Underestimation of VaR, leading to insufficient capital', 'No impact, as VaR is insensitive to tail assumptions', 'C', 'At very high confidence levels, the VaR is determined by the extreme tail of the distribution. If returns are fat-tailed but modelled as normal, the normal distribution assigns too little probability to large losses, systematically underestimating VaR.', 6),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-probability-distributions-q08', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-probability-distributions', 'Which distribution is most appropriate for modelling the number of rare operational loss events (e.g., cyber incidents) occurring in a one-year period?', 'Normal distribution', 'Binomial distribution with large n', 'Student-t distribution', 'Poisson distribution', 'D', 'The Poisson distribution is designed for modelling the count of rare, independent events occurring in a fixed time interval at a constant average rate — exactly the characterisation of infrequent operational loss events.', 7),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-statistical-inference-q01', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-statistical-inference', 'The Central Limit Theorem (CLT) states that as sample size increases, the sampling distribution of the sample mean:', 'Converges to a normal distribution regardless of the population distribution', 'Approaches the same distribution as the underlying population', 'Becomes more skewed to match the population''s skewness', 'Has a standard deviation equal to the population standard deviation', 'A', 'The CLT guarantees convergence of the sample mean''s distribution to normal as n grows, regardless of the underlying population distribution shape — this is why normal-based inference is so widely applicable.', 0),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-statistical-inference-q02', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-statistical-inference', 'A 95% confidence interval for a parameter theta means:', 'There is a 95% probability that theta lies within this specific interval', 'The parameter theta equals the midpoint of the interval with 95% certainty', 'In repeated sampling, 95% of such constructed intervals would contain the true theta', 'The sample estimate is within 5% of the true parameter value', 'C', 'A confidence interval is a frequentist concept: it means that if the experiment were repeated many times, 95% of the resulting intervals would contain the true parameter. The true parameter is fixed; it is the interval that is random.', 1),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-statistical-inference-q03', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-statistical-inference', 'Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE) selects parameter values that:', 'Minimise the sum of squared differences between observed and fitted values', 'Match the theoretical moments to the sample moments', 'Maximise the probability of observing the sample data given the parameters', 'Minimise the variance of the parameter estimates', 'C', 'MLE finds the parameter values under which the observed data are most likely — i.e., it maximises the likelihood function L(theta | data). This is distinct from OLS (minimises squared errors) and method of moments.', 2),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-statistical-inference-q04', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-statistical-inference', 'The Kupiec proportion-of-failures (POF) test is used in risk management to:', 'Estimate the parameters of a GARCH model', 'Backtest whether observed VaR exceptions are consistent with the stated confidence level', 'Test for heteroskedasticity in regression residuals', 'Determine whether credit ratings follow a Markov chain', 'B', 'The Kupiec POF test is a hypothesis test for VaR backtesting: it tests whether the observed frequency of exceptions (losses exceeding VaR) is statistically consistent with the confidence level claimed by the model.', 3),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-statistical-inference-q05', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-statistical-inference', 'Compared to Maximum Likelihood Estimation, the Method of Moments estimator is generally:', 'More computationally intensive and statistically efficient', 'Less efficient but simpler to compute, especially with closed-form solutions', 'More accurate in small samples due to lower bias', 'Preferred for heavy-tailed distributions such as the Pareto', 'B', 'Method of Moments is typically simpler than MLE (often yielding closed-form estimates) but is less statistically efficient, meaning it requires larger samples to achieve the same estimation precision as MLE.', 4),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-statistical-inference-q06', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-statistical-inference', 'A risk manager estimates a 99% VaR of $10 million with a 95% confidence interval of [$8 million, $12 million]. What does this confidence interval convey?', 'There is estimation uncertainty around the VaR point estimate; the interval quantifies the precision of the estimate', 'The true VaR is definitely between $8 million and $12 million', 'The VaR will be exceeded between $8 million and $12 million times per year', 'The 95% VaR is $10 million', 'A', 'The confidence interval around a VaR estimate reflects sampling uncertainty — with a finite historical sample, the VaR itself is estimated with error, and the interval conveys the range within which the true VaR plausibly lies.', 5),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-statistical-inference-q07', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-statistical-inference', 'For which purpose is Bayesian estimation most advantageous relative to MLE?', 'When computational resources are severely limited and closed-form estimates are required', 'When the distribution family is unknown and non-parametric methods are needed', 'When the sample size is very large and asymptotic efficiency dominates', 'When prior knowledge or expert judgment about parameters can be formally incorporated into the estimation', 'D', 'Bayesian estimation''s distinguishing feature is the ability to incorporate prior information (expert judgment, historical data from related entities) through a prior distribution, making it especially useful when sample data are scarce.', 6),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-statistical-inference-q08', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-statistical-inference', 'A risk model produces too many VaR exceptions over a backtesting period. A hypothesis test fails to reject the model at the 5% significance level. The risk manager should:', 'Recognise that failing to reject does not prove the model is correct; consider additional diagnostic tests', 'Conclude the model is definitely correct and no action is needed', 'Increase the VaR confidence level to reduce the number of exceptions', 'Replace the model immediately since any exceptions indicate failure', 'A', 'Failing to reject a null hypothesis does not prove it is true — the test may have low power with limited data. Risk managers should use multiple backtesting tests and qualitative judgment rather than relying solely on a single hypothesis test result.', 7),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-regression-q01', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-regression', 'The Gauss-Markov theorem guarantees that OLS is the Best Linear Unbiased Estimator (BLUE) when:', 'The dependent variable follows a normal distribution', 'All regressors are uncorrelated with each other', 'The sample size exceeds 100 observations', 'Errors are homoskedastic, uncorrelated, and have zero conditional mean', 'D', 'The Gauss-Markov theorem requires zero conditional mean of errors, homoskedasticity (constant error variance), and no autocorrelation. Normality of errors is not required for BLUE, though it is needed for exact inference in small samples.', 0),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-regression-q02', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-regression', 'A researcher regresses bond spreads on macroeconomic variables and finds that residual variance increases with the level of interest rates. This is a sign of:', 'Autocorrelation', 'Heteroskedasticity', 'Multicollinearity', 'Structural break', 'B', 'Heteroskedasticity is present when the variance of regression residuals is not constant but varies systematically with a regressor (or the fitted values). Here, the error variance changes with interest rate levels, violating the OLS homoskedasticity assumption.', 1),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-regression-q03', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-regression', 'The Durbin-Watson (DW) statistic is used to test for:', 'First-order autocorrelation in time-series regression residuals', 'Heteroskedasticity in cross-sectional regression', 'Multicollinearity among regressors', 'Non-linearity in the regression functional form', 'A', 'The Durbin-Watson test detects first-order serial correlation (autocorrelation) in OLS residuals. A DW value near 2 indicates no autocorrelation; values near 0 indicate positive autocorrelation and values near 4 indicate negative autocorrelation.', 2),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-regression-q04', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-regression', 'A Variance Inflation Factor (VIF) of 12 for a regressor indicates:', 'The regressor has 12 times the explanatory power of the dependent variable', 'The regressor is highly correlated with other regressors, inflating its standard error approximately 12-fold', 'The regression model has 12% of its variance explained by this regressor', 'The regressor should be multiplied by 12 before inclusion in the model', 'B', 'VIF measures how much the variance of a coefficient estimate is inflated by multicollinearity. A VIF of 12 means the standard error is approximately sqrt(12) times larger than it would be if the regressor were uncorrelated with all others; VIF > 10 is typically cause for concern.', 3),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-regression-q05', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-regression', 'In the GARCH(1,1) model sigma^2_t = omega + alpha * epsilon^2_(t-1) + beta * sigma^2_(t-1), the term alpha * epsilon^2_(t-1) captures:', 'The long-run average variance', 'The impact of the most recent squared return shock on today''s variance', 'The persistence of the conditional variance from yesterday', 'The correlation between return shocks and variance changes', 'B', 'The alpha term in GARCH(1,1) governs the reaction of conditional variance to the most recent squared return shock (the ARCH effect). A large alpha means variance responds quickly and sharply to new information.', 4),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-regression-q06', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-regression', 'When OLS residuals exhibit positive autocorrelation, which of the following consequences is most likely?', 'Standard errors are underestimated, making t-statistics appear more significant than they are', 'Coefficient estimates become biased and inconsistent', 'The R-squared statistic falls to zero', 'Coefficient estimates become inconsistent but standard errors remain valid', 'A', 'With positive autocorrelation, OLS standard errors are underestimated because the effective number of independent observations is overstated. This inflates t-statistics, causing spurious appearance of statistical significance. Coefficient estimates remain unbiased but are no longer efficient.', 5),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-regression-q07', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-regression', 'GARCH models are used in quantitative risk management primarily to:', 'Model time-varying (conditional) volatility, capturing volatility clustering', 'Correct for heteroskedasticity in cross-sectional equity regressions', 'Reduce multicollinearity in factor models', 'Estimate the unconditional mean of financial returns', 'A', 'GARCH captures the empirical observation that volatility clusters — periods of high volatility tend to persist, and periods of low volatility tend to persist. This makes GARCH models the foundation for dynamic VaR and options pricing with time-varying volatility.', 6),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-regression-q08', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-regression', 'When the OLS homoskedasticity assumption is violated, an appropriate remedy is to:', 'Remove all regressors with VIF greater than 5', 'Apply the Durbin-Watson correction to the coefficient estimates', 'Increase the sample size until residual variance stabilises', 'Use White''s heteroskedasticity-consistent standard errors or Weighted Least Squares', 'D', 'White''s robust standard errors correct the inference problem caused by heteroskedasticity without changing the coefficient estimates. WLS re-weights observations to restore efficiency when the variance pattern is known. Both are standard remedies in financial econometrics.', 7),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-monte-carlo-q01', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-monte-carlo', 'The first step in a Monte Carlo simulation of portfolio VaR is to:', 'Calculate the empirical VaR from historical data', 'Select the copula for dependency modelling', 'Specify the stochastic model governing the dynamics of the underlying risk factors', 'Generate pseudo-random numbers from a standard normal distribution', 'C', 'Before generating random numbers, the risk manager must specify the model (e.g., GBM, jump-diffusion) that governs how risk factors evolve. Random number generation is a later step in the simulation procedure.', 0),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-monte-carlo-q02', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-monte-carlo', 'The antithetic variates variance reduction technique works by:', 'Sampling more heavily from the tail region and reweighting results', 'Pairing each random draw u with (1-u) so that deviations above and below the mean partially cancel', 'Using a correlated variable with a known analytical price to adjust simulated values', 'Stratifying the sample space into equal-probability intervals', 'B', 'Antithetic variates pair each uniform draw u with its complement (1-u), creating negatively correlated simulation paths. This negative correlation causes overestimates on one path to be offset by underestimates on the paired path, reducing overall estimator variance.', 1),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-monte-carlo-q03', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-monte-carlo', 'Sklar''s theorem, which underpins copula theory, states that:', 'The sum of correlated normal random variables follows a normal distribution', 'Any joint distribution can be decomposed into its marginal distributions and a copula capturing the dependency structure', 'Copulas are only valid when the marginal distributions are normal', 'Tail dependency is always zero for elliptical copulas', 'B', 'Sklar''s theorem is the mathematical foundation of copula theory: any multivariate joint distribution can be expressed as a function of its marginals and a copula. This allows marginal distributions and dependency structure to be modelled separately.', 2),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-monte-carlo-q04', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-monte-carlo', 'The Gaussian copula was widely criticised after the 2008 financial crisis because it:', 'Is computationally too expensive for large credit portfolios', 'Requires marginal distributions to be normal, which is rarely true', 'Cannot accommodate asymmetric dependencies between assets', 'Assumes zero tail dependency, meaning it underestimates the probability of simultaneous large losses', 'D', 'The Gaussian copula has zero tail dependency — the probability of simultaneous extreme events converges to zero even as correlations increase. This caused systematic underestimation of joint default probabilities in CDO portfolios.', 3),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-monte-carlo-q05', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-monte-carlo', 'Importance sampling improves Monte Carlo efficiency for tail risk estimation by:', 'Reducing the number of model parameters that need to be estimated', 'Correlating simulation paths to reduce variance via negative covariance', 'Replacing random draws with deterministic low-discrepancy sequences', 'Oversampling the tail region of the distribution and reweighting the results to remove bias', 'D', 'Importance sampling concentrates simulation effort in the tail region that matters for VaR and ES estimation, then uses likelihood-ratio weights to correct for the biased sampling. This dramatically reduces the number of paths needed for accurate tail estimates.', 4),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-monte-carlo-q06', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-monte-carlo', 'The Student-t copula, compared to the Gaussian copula, is better suited to credit portfolio modelling primarily because:', 'It exhibits symmetric tail dependency, capturing the tendency for defaults to cluster in stress scenarios', 'It requires fewer parameters to estimate', 'It assumes independence between extreme events', 'It is equivalent to the Gaussian copula for all practical purposes', 'A', 'The Student-t copula has positive tail dependence (both upper and lower), meaning it assigns higher probability than the Gaussian copula to simultaneous extreme losses — making it more realistic for credit portfolio stress scenarios.', 5),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-monte-carlo-q07', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-monte-carlo', 'The probability integral transform is used in Monte Carlo simulation to:', 'Correlate random draws across simulation paths using the Cholesky decomposition', 'Convert normally distributed samples to uniform samples for variance reduction', 'Transform uniform random draws into draws from any target distribution using the inverse CDF', 'Construct antithetic pairs from pseudo-random uniform samples', 'C', 'The probability integral transform states that if U ~ Uniform(0,1), then F^{-1}(U) follows the distribution with CDF F. This allows simulation from any distribution by applying its inverse CDF to uniform random numbers.', 6),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-monte-carlo-q08', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-monte-carlo', 'For modelling the joint default risk of a credit portfolio where defaults are more likely to cluster in economic downturns (lower tail dependency), which copula is most appropriate?', 'Clayton copula', 'Gaussian copula', 'Gumbel copula', 'Frank copula', 'A', 'The Clayton copula exhibits strong lower tail dependency and weak upper tail dependency, making it well-suited to credit default modelling where the key concern is clustering of defaults during economic stress (lower tail events).', 7),
  ('frm-financial-markets-options-pricing-q01', 'frm-financial-markets-options-pricing', 'The payoff of a European call option at expiry is:', 'max(S - K, 0)', 'max(K - S, 0)', 'S minus the premium paid', 'K times the risk-free rate', 'A', 'A call pays the excess of the asset price S over the strike K if positive, and zero otherwise.', 0),
  ('frm-financial-markets-options-pricing-q02', 'frm-financial-markets-options-pricing', 'Which of the following is NOT an input to the Black-Scholes option pricing model?', 'Current asset price', 'Historical dividend growth rate', 'Volatility of the underlying asset', 'Risk-free interest rate', 'B', 'Black-Scholes requires the current price, strike, time, risk-free rate, and volatility; historical dividend growth is not an input.', 1),
  ('frm-financial-markets-options-pricing-q03', 'frm-financial-markets-options-pricing', 'Implied volatility is best described as:', 'The historical standard deviation of past returns', 'The GARCH forecast of future volatility', 'The realized volatility over the option''s life', 'The volatility backed out of a market option price using the pricing model', 'D', 'Implied volatility is derived from the current market price of an option by inverting the pricing model.', 2),
  ('frm-financial-markets-options-pricing-q04', 'frm-financial-markets-options-pricing', 'A volatility smile in options markets indicates that:', 'Implied volatility is identical across all strikes', 'Historical and implied volatilities always match', 'Options are priced using the CAPM', 'Implied volatility varies with the strike price, violating strict Black-Scholes assumptions', 'D', 'The volatility smile shows that implied volatility changes across strikes, a pattern inconsistent with constant-volatility Black-Scholes.', 3),
  ('frm-financial-markets-options-pricing-q05', 'frm-financial-markets-options-pricing', 'The buyer of a call option has:', 'An obligation to buy the asset at the strike price', 'The right to sell the asset at the strike price', 'The right to buy the asset at the strike price', 'Unlimited liability if the asset price falls', 'C', 'A call option gives the holder the right, not the obligation, to purchase the underlying at the strike price.', 4),
  ('frm-financial-markets-options-pricing-q06', 'frm-financial-markets-options-pricing', 'Black-Scholes assumes which of the following about the underlying asset''s returns?', 'Returns follow a lognormal distribution with constant volatility', 'Returns follow a uniform distribution', 'Returns are mean-reverting with GARCH volatility', 'Returns are always positive', 'A', 'Black-Scholes assumes log-returns are normally distributed, implying prices are lognormal, with constant volatility throughout.', 5),
  ('frm-financial-markets-options-pricing-q07', 'frm-financial-markets-options-pricing', 'The maximum loss for the buyer of a put option is:', 'Unlimited, because the asset price can rise indefinitely', 'Equal to the strike price', 'The premium paid for the option', 'Zero, because puts always expire worthless', 'C', 'The put buyer''s maximum loss is limited to the premium paid; the option simply expires worthless if unexercised.', 6),
  ('frm-financial-markets-options-pricing-q08', 'frm-financial-markets-options-pricing', 'All else equal, increasing the time to expiry of an option will generally:', 'Decrease both call and put values', 'Increase both call and put values', 'Increase calls but decrease puts', 'Have no effect on option value', 'B', 'Longer time to expiry gives the option more opportunity to move in-the-money, raising the value of both calls and puts.', 7),
  ('frm-financial-markets-swaps-q01', 'frm-financial-markets-swaps', 'In a plain vanilla interest rate swap, the fixed-rate payer:', 'Pays floating and receives fixed', 'Receives fixed and pays no floating', 'Pays both fixed and floating rates', 'Pays fixed and receives floating', 'D', 'The fixed-rate payer in a vanilla swap pays the fixed swap rate and receives the floating reference rate.', 0),
  ('frm-financial-markets-swaps-q02', 'frm-financial-markets-swaps', 'At initiation, the swap rate in a plain vanilla interest rate swap is set so that the swap has:', 'A positive net present value to the fixed payer', 'A negative net present value', 'Zero value to both counterparties', 'A value equal to the notional principal', 'C', 'The swap rate is chosen so that the present value of fixed payments equals the present value of expected floating payments, giving zero initial value.', 1),
  ('frm-financial-markets-swaps-q03', 'frm-financial-markets-swaps', 'Following the 2008 financial crisis, the standard for discounting collateralized swap cash flows shifted to:', 'LIBOR flat', 'The overnight indexed swap (OIS) rate', 'The repo rate plus a spread', 'The federal funds target rate', 'B', 'Post-crisis best practice is to discount collateralized swaps using the OIS rate, which better reflects the risk-free rate.', 2),
  ('frm-financial-markets-swaps-q04', 'frm-financial-markets-swaps', 'SOFR (Secured Overnight Financing Rate) replaced LIBOR primarily because:', 'SOFR includes a credit-risk premium like LIBOR', 'SOFR is a forward-looking term rate like LIBOR', 'SOFR is published by commercial banks, not central banks', 'SOFR is based on actual overnight repo transactions, making it more robust', 'D', 'SOFR is grounded in real overnight repo transactions, eliminating the manipulation risk associated with LIBOR panel submissions.', 3),
  ('frm-financial-markets-swaps-q05', 'frm-financial-markets-swaps', 'A swap can be valued by treating it as the difference between:', 'Two equity positions', 'A forward contract and a futures contract', 'A fixed-rate bond and a floating-rate bond', 'Two options on interest rates', 'C', 'A swap is equivalent to a long position in one bond (fixed or floating) and a short position in the other, enabling bond-based valuation.', 4),
  ('frm-financial-markets-swaps-q06', 'frm-financial-markets-swaps', 'In an interest rate swap, which party benefits if floating rates rise significantly above the fixed swap rate?', 'Both parties equally', 'The fixed-rate payer', 'The floating-rate payer', 'The clearinghouse', 'B', 'When floating rates exceed the fixed rate, the fixed-rate payer receives more than it pays, gaining from the rate rise.', 5),
  ('frm-financial-markets-swaps-q07', 'frm-financial-markets-swaps', 'In a plain vanilla swap, only net cash flows are exchanged because:', 'Notional principal is always delivered at maturity', 'Both legs are denominated in the same currency and netting reduces credit exposure', 'The floating rate is fixed at initiation', 'Regulators require full gross settlement', 'B', 'Netting same-currency payment obligations reduces settlement risk and operational complexity.', 6),
  ('frm-financial-markets-swaps-q08', 'frm-financial-markets-swaps', 'A currency swap differs from an interest rate swap in that a currency swap:', 'Never involves any principal exchange', 'Cannot be used to hedge foreign exchange risk', 'Always uses the same reference rate on both legs', 'Involves exchange of principal in different currencies at maturity', 'D', 'Currency swaps typically involve exchanging notional principal in different currencies at inception and re-exchange at maturity.', 7),
  ('frm-financial-markets-fixed-income-products-q01', 'frm-financial-markets-fixed-income-products', 'A bond''s yield to maturity is best described as the discount rate that:', 'Equals the coupon rate in all cases', 'Is always equal to the risk-free rate', 'Measures only the credit spread over the risk-free rate', 'Equates the bond''s price to the present value of its future cash flows', 'D', 'YTM is the internal rate of return that sets the sum of discounted cash flows equal to the current bond price.', 0),
  ('frm-financial-markets-fixed-income-products-q02', 'frm-financial-markets-fixed-income-products', 'When market interest rates rise, the price of a fixed-coupon bond will:', 'Also rise proportionally', 'Fall', 'Remain unchanged', 'Equal the coupon rate', 'B', 'Bond prices and yields move inversely; when market rates rise, the present value of fixed cash flows falls.', 1),
  ('frm-financial-markets-fixed-income-products-q03', 'frm-financial-markets-fixed-income-products', 'Modified duration approximates the:', 'Percentage change in bond price per 1% change in yield', 'Dollar change in bond price for a 1% change in yield', 'Change in coupon payment when rates move', 'Credit spread of the bond', 'A', 'Modified duration gives the approximate percentage price change for a 100-basis-point shift in yield.', 2),
  ('frm-financial-markets-fixed-income-products-q04', 'frm-financial-markets-fixed-income-products', 'Convexity improves the duration approximation by capturing:', 'The credit risk component of yield', 'The linear sensitivity to rate moves', 'The curvature in the price-yield relationship', 'The time value of coupon payments', 'C', 'Convexity is the second-order term that corrects for the nonlinear (curved) price response to yield changes.', 3),
  ('frm-financial-markets-fixed-income-products-q05', 'frm-financial-markets-fixed-income-products', 'In a repurchase agreement (repo), the haircut represents:', 'The interest rate charged on the repo', 'The duration of the collateral', 'The coupon rate of the collateral bond', 'The discount applied to collateral value to protect the cash lender', 'D', 'The haircut is the difference between the market value of collateral and the cash lent, protecting the lender against collateral price falls.', 4),
  ('frm-financial-markets-fixed-income-products-q06', 'frm-financial-markets-fixed-income-products', 'Which yield measure assumes all coupon payments are reinvested at the same rate as the YTM?', 'Current yield', 'Coupon rate', 'Spot rate', 'Yield to maturity', 'D', 'YTM implicitly assumes coupon reinvestment at the YTM rate, which is a key limitation of the measure.', 5),
  ('frm-financial-markets-fixed-income-products-q07', 'frm-financial-markets-fixed-income-products', 'A bond with higher convexity, compared to a lower-convexity bond with the same duration, will:', 'Lose more value when yields fall', 'Gain less value when yields fall and lose more when yields rise', 'Gain more value when yields fall and lose less when yields rise', 'Have identical price behavior to the lower-convexity bond', 'C', 'Higher convexity means the price-yield curve is more curved, so the bond outperforms in both yield-up and yield-down scenarios.', 6),
  ('frm-financial-markets-fixed-income-products-q08', 'frm-financial-markets-fixed-income-products', 'Repos are primarily used by fixed-income dealers to:', 'Issue new equity capital', 'Hedge equity market risk', 'Fund their bond holdings with short-term borrowing', 'Calculate yield to maturity', 'C', 'Dealers use the repo market to finance bond inventories by temporarily selling securities and agreeing to repurchase them.', 7),
  ('frm-financial-markets-credit-products-q01', 'frm-financial-markets-credit-products', 'In a credit default swap, the protection buyer:', 'Pays a periodic spread and receives compensation on a credit event', 'Receives a periodic spread and pays on a credit event', 'Holds the reference bonds directly', 'Receives no payment if the reference entity defaults', 'A', 'The protection buyer pays the CDS spread and receives a loss payment from the seller if a credit event occurs.', 0),
  ('frm-financial-markets-credit-products-q02', 'frm-financial-markets-credit-products', 'A wider CDS spread for a reference entity generally reflects:', 'Higher perceived default risk or lower recovery expectations', 'A higher recovery rate', 'Lower perceived default risk', 'A stronger credit rating', 'A', 'CDS spreads widen when markets perceive higher default probability or lower expected recovery, as the protection seller bears greater expected loss.', 1),
  ('frm-financial-markets-credit-products-q03', 'frm-financial-markets-credit-products', 'In a CDO, which tranche absorbs the first losses from the collateral pool?', 'Senior tranche', 'Equity (first-loss) tranche', 'Mezzanine tranche', 'Super-senior tranche', 'B', 'The equity tranche absorbs losses first, protecting the mezzanine and senior tranches.', 2),
  ('frm-financial-markets-credit-products-q04', 'frm-financial-markets-credit-products', 'A synthetic CDO differs from a cash CDO primarily because a synthetic CDO:', 'Gains credit exposure through CDS rather than actual bond holdings', 'Always holds physical bonds as collateral', 'Is always rated AAA', 'Cannot be tranched into senior and junior notes', 'A', 'Synthetic CDOs use CDS contracts to obtain credit exposure without purchasing the underlying bonds.', 3),
  ('frm-financial-markets-credit-products-q05', 'frm-financial-markets-credit-products', 'In a CDO structure, the senior tranche offers the lowest yield because it:', 'Absorbs the first losses', 'Has the highest priority claim and the lowest default risk', 'Has the highest correlation risk', 'Is always unrated', 'B', 'Senior tranches bear losses only after all subordinate tranches are wiped out, so they carry the lowest risk and yield.', 4),
  ('frm-financial-markets-credit-products-q06', 'frm-financial-markets-credit-products', 'A CDS credit event that triggers a payment from the protection seller includes:', 'A rise in interest rates', 'An equity market decline', 'A decline in commodity prices', 'Default or restructuring of the reference entity', 'D', 'CDS credit events are defined in the contract and typically include bankruptcy, failure to pay, and restructuring of the reference entity.', 5),
  ('frm-financial-markets-credit-products-q07', 'frm-financial-markets-credit-products', 'Higher default correlation among CDO reference entities benefits which tranche the most?', 'Senior tranche', 'Equity tranche', 'Mezzanine tranche', 'All tranches equally', 'B', 'Higher correlation makes defaults cluster, increasing the chance of total portfolio loss but also increasing the chance of no losses at all, which benefits equity holders.', 6),
  ('frm-financial-markets-credit-products-q08', 'frm-financial-markets-credit-products', 'The CDS spread at inception is set so that:', 'The present value of premium payments equals the present value of expected loss payments', 'The protection buyer pays more than the expected loss', 'The swap always has positive value for the protection seller', 'The spread equals the risk-free rate', 'A', 'Like any swap, the CDS spread is set at initiation so that the instrument has zero net present value to both parties.', 7),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-bond-valuation-q01', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-bond-valuation', 'DV01 measures the change in bond price for a:', '1% change in yield', '1-basis-point (0.01%) decrease in yield', '1% change in the credit spread', '1-day change in time to maturity', 'B', 'DV01 (dollar value of a basis point) is the price change for a 1-basis-point downward shift in yield.', 0),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-bond-valuation-q02', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-bond-valuation', 'Key rate durations are most useful for hedging:', 'Parallel shifts in the yield curve only', 'Currency risk in foreign bonds', 'Credit spread movements', 'Yield curve shape risk arising from non-parallel shifts', 'D', 'Key rate durations isolate sensitivity at each maturity point, enabling hedges against twists and steepening of the yield curve.', 1),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-bond-valuation-q03', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-bond-valuation', 'Spread duration measures a bond''s price sensitivity to changes in its:', 'Coupon rate', 'Time to maturity', 'Credit spread', 'Recovery rate', 'C', 'Spread duration captures how much the bond price changes when its credit spread over the risk-free rate widens or narrows.', 2),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-bond-valuation-q04', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-bond-valuation', 'The option-adjusted spread (OAS) of a callable bond strips out the option effect in order to:', 'Enable fair comparison with bullet bonds', 'Increase the bond''s yield above its YTM', 'Remove credit risk from the spread', 'Calculate the bond''s modified duration', 'A', 'OAS removes the value of the embedded option, allowing like-for-like yield comparison between callable and non-callable bonds.', 3),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-bond-valuation-q05', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-bond-valuation', 'A portfolio''s total DV01 is computed as:', 'The sum of DV01 across all positions', 'The average DV01 across all positions', 'The DV01 of the largest position only', 'The DV01 of the longest-maturity bond', 'A', 'DV01 is linear and additive, so portfolio DV01 is the sum of individual position DV01s.', 4),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-bond-valuation-q06', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-bond-valuation', 'For a callable bond, a lower OAS relative to a similar non-callable bond reflects:', 'Greater credit risk', 'A steeper yield curve', 'Higher liquidity of the callable bond', 'The cost of the call option embedded in the bond', 'D', 'The callable bond''s OAS is lower than its nominal spread because part of the spread compensates for the call option value retained by the issuer.', 5),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-bond-valuation-q07', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-bond-valuation', 'Which statement about key rate durations is correct?', 'Key rate durations across all maturities sum to zero', 'Key rate durations are independent of position size', 'Key rate durations sum to the total modified duration', 'Key rate durations measure only credit spread risk', 'C', 'The sum of all key rate durations equals the bond''s total modified duration, as they decompose the same overall interest rate sensitivity.', 6),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-bond-valuation-q08', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-bond-valuation', 'A widening of credit spreads will cause the price of a credit-risky bond to:', 'Rise, because the bond becomes cheaper to issue', 'Remain unchanged if duration is constant', 'Rise proportionally to the spread widening', 'Fall, because the discount rate applied to the bond''s cash flows increases', 'D', 'A wider credit spread raises the total yield used to discount cash flows, lowering the present value and hence the bond price.', 7),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-option-valuation-q01', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-option-valuation', 'Delta for a long call option is:', 'Between 0 and 1', 'Always negative', 'Between -1 and 0', 'Always greater than 1', 'A', 'Call delta is positive and bounded between 0 (deep out-of-the-money) and 1 (deep in-the-money).', 0),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-option-valuation-q02', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-option-valuation', 'Gamma is highest for options that are:', 'Deep in-the-money with long time to expiry', 'Deep out-of-the-money with any time to expiry', 'In-the-money with long time to expiry', 'At-the-money and near expiry', 'D', 'Gamma peaks for at-the-money options close to expiry, because small price changes can shift the option from in- to out-of-the-money.', 1),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-option-valuation-q03', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-option-valuation', 'Vega measures the sensitivity of an option''s price to:', 'The risk-free interest rate', 'The passage of time', 'Changes in implied volatility', 'Changes in the underlying asset price', 'C', 'Vega is the partial derivative of the option price with respect to implied volatility; long options have positive vega.', 2),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-option-valuation-q04', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-option-valuation', 'Theta for a long option position is generally:', 'Positive, because the option gains value over time', 'Negative, reflecting time decay as expiry approaches', 'Zero for all options', 'Positive only for deep in-the-money options', 'B', 'Long option positions suffer time decay (theta is negative), losing value as expiry approaches with all else constant.', 3),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-option-valuation-q05', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-option-valuation', 'A delta-neutral portfolio must be rebalanced frequently because:', 'Delta changes as the underlying price moves, driven by gamma', 'Vega increases as options approach expiry', 'Rho changes with the risk-free rate daily', 'Theta becomes positive after hedging', 'A', 'Gamma causes delta to change as the underlying moves, so a delta-neutral hedge drifts and must be rebalanced continuously.', 4),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-option-valuation-q06', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-option-valuation', 'A trader who is long gamma benefits from:', 'Large moves in the underlying asset in either direction', 'Low realized volatility and stable markets', 'Declining implied volatility', 'Slow passage of time', 'A', 'Long gamma positions profit from large price swings because gains accelerate in either direction while losses from rebalancing are limited.', 5),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-option-valuation-q07', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-option-valuation', 'Rho for a long call option is:', 'Negative, because higher rates reduce call value', 'Zero for all standard options', 'Positive, because higher rates increase call value', 'Equal to delta', 'C', 'A higher risk-free rate lowers the present value of the strike price paid at expiry, increasing the call option''s value.', 6),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-option-valuation-q08', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-option-valuation', 'A key limitation of dynamic delta hedging in practice is that:', 'It requires no monitoring after establishment', 'It eliminates all gamma and vega risk simultaneously', 'Discrete rebalancing, transaction costs, and price jumps leave residual risk', 'It can only be applied to at-the-money options', 'C', 'Continuous rebalancing is impossible in practice; gaps between trades, transaction costs, and sudden jumps all create imperfect hedges.', 7),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-stress-testing-q01', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-stress-testing', 'The primary purpose of stress testing is to:', 'Replace VaR as the sole risk measure', 'Set individual trader position limits', 'Calculate expected losses under average market conditions', 'Examine portfolio performance under severe scenarios that statistical models may miss', 'D', 'Stress testing evaluates tail scenarios — typically severe, correlated dislocations — that VaR underestimates or excludes.', 0),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-stress-testing-q02', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-stress-testing', 'DFAST stands for:', 'Dodd-Frank Act Stress Test', 'Diversified Fixed-Asset Stress Test', 'Dynamic Funding and Stability Test', 'Default Frequency Assessment and Stress Test', 'A', 'DFAST is the Dodd-Frank Act Stress Test, an annual U.S. regulatory requirement for large bank holding companies.', 1),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-stress-testing-q03', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-stress-testing', 'Reverse stress testing differs from conventional stress testing in that it:', 'Uses only historical scenarios', 'Works backward from a failure outcome to identify what scenarios could cause it', 'Applies only to market risk, not credit or liquidity risk', 'Is voluntary and not required by any regulator', 'B', 'Reverse stress testing starts with the outcome (failure or capital breach) and works backward to identify plausible triggering scenarios.', 2),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-stress-testing-q04', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-stress-testing', 'A historical scenario stress test using the 2008 financial crisis as a template is limited because it:', 'Only captures risks that materialized in the past, missing novel scenarios', 'Requires Monte Carlo simulation', 'Cannot project capital adequacy', 'Is too expensive to implement', 'A', 'Historical scenarios are anchored to past events and cannot anticipate entirely new risk drivers or unprecedented market structures.', 3),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-stress-testing-q05', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-stress-testing', 'CCAR (Comprehensive Capital Analysis and Review) is primarily used by U.S. regulators to assess banks'':', 'Capital planning processes and capital adequacy under stress', 'Market liquidity of trading books', 'Operational risk event counts', 'Counterparty credit exposure only', 'A', 'CCAR evaluates whether large bank holding companies have sufficient capital and sound capital planning processes under adverse scenarios.', 4),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-stress-testing-q06', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-stress-testing', 'A hypothetical (narrative) stress scenario differs from a historical scenario in that it:', 'Uses only quantitative data without expert judgment', 'Replays observed market moves directly', 'Is forward-looking and can incorporate unprecedented shocks', 'Cannot be used for regulatory reporting', 'C', 'Hypothetical scenarios are constructed around plausible but previously unseen events, allowing assessment of novel systemic risks.', 5),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-stress-testing-q07', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-stress-testing', 'The severely adverse scenario in a DFAST stress test is designed to represent:', 'Expected base-case economic conditions', 'A mild recession with moderate unemployment', 'A severe global recession with sharp asset price declines', 'Only interest rate risk, not credit or market risk', 'C', 'The severely adverse scenario represents a deep recession, typically with large unemployment increases and significant asset price falls.', 6),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-stress-testing-q08', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-stress-testing', 'Stress testing is best viewed as a complement to VaR rather than a replacement because:', 'Stress tests are always more conservative than VaR', 'Stress tests explore specific extreme scenarios while VaR summarizes typical loss distributions', 'VaR captures tail severity while stress testing captures frequency', 'Regulators prohibit using stress tests and VaR together', 'B', 'VaR characterizes the loss distribution statistically; stress tests explore particular severe scenarios, together giving a fuller risk picture.', 7),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-model-risk-q01', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-model-risk', 'The Federal Reserve''s primary supervisory guidance on model risk management is:', 'SR 11-7', 'Basel III Pillar 2', 'Dodd-Frank Section 165', 'FRTB Article 325', 'A', 'SR 11-7 is the Fed''s Supervisory Guidance on Model Risk Management, setting out the framework for model development, validation, and governance.', 0),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-model-risk-q02', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-model-risk', 'Under SR 11-7, which of the following is required of the model validation function?', 'Validation must be performed by the model''s own developers', 'Validation need not assess model conceptual soundness', 'Validation is only required for market risk models', 'Validation must be independent of model development', 'D', 'SR 11-7 requires that validation be conducted independently of the teams that build and use models to ensure objectivity.', 1),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-model-risk-q03', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-model-risk', 'Backtesting a VaR model involves:', 'Simulating future scenarios and comparing them to theoretical values', 'Comparing model-predicted VaR breaches to actual realized losses', 'Rerunning the model with different random seeds', 'Replacing the model with a challenger model', 'B', 'Backtesting counts how often actual losses exceed the VaR estimate, testing whether the model''s predictions match realized outcomes.', 2),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-model-risk-q04', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-model-risk', 'A challenger model in the context of model validation is:', 'A model that is always inferior to the primary model', 'An independently built alternative used to benchmark or validate the primary model', 'A regulatory-prescribed model mandated by SR 11-7', 'A model used exclusively for stress testing', 'B', 'Challenger models are independent alternatives that help validate the primary model and reduce reliance on a single approach.', 3),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-model-risk-q05', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-model-risk', 'Model misuse occurs when a model is:', 'Used within its intended range and assumptions', 'Subjected to sensitivity analysis', 'Applied outside the scope or conditions it was designed for', 'Validated by an independent team', 'C', 'Model misuse is applying a model beyond its intended use case or assumptions, which can produce materially misleading outputs.', 4),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-model-risk-q06', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-model-risk', 'Sensitivity analysis in model validation is used to:', 'Replace backtesting entirely', 'Test how model outputs change under varied input assumptions', 'Determine the model''s historical loss record', 'Set the model''s capital charge directly', 'B', 'Sensitivity analysis probes the stability of model outputs by varying key assumptions, revealing dependence on uncertain inputs.', 5),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-model-risk-q07', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-model-risk', 'According to SR 11-7, a model consists of three components: an information input, a processing component, and:', 'A regulatory approval step', 'A stress testing module', 'A reporting (output) component', 'A benchmarking database', 'C', 'SR 11-7 defines a model as having an input component, a processing component, and a reporting or output component.', 6),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-model-risk-q08', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-model-risk', 'Benchmarking during model validation involves:', 'Setting minimum capital requirements for the model', 'Documenting the model''s assumptions for regulators', 'Counting VaR exceptions over a historical period', 'Comparing the model''s output to those of alternative or industry-standard models', 'D', 'Benchmarking assesses a model by comparing its results to those produced by independent or industry-accepted alternative models.', 7),
  ('frm-foundations-apt-multifactor-q01', 'frm-foundations-apt-multifactor', 'Arbitrage Pricing Theory (APT) differs from the CAPM primarily because APT:', 'Requires the market portfolio to be mean-variance efficient', 'Allows expected return to depend on multiple systematic risk factors', 'Assumes all investors hold only the risk-free asset', 'Ignores systematic risk entirely', 'B', 'APT expresses expected return as a linear function of several systematic factors, generalizing the single-factor CAPM.', 0),
  ('frm-foundations-apt-multifactor-q02', 'frm-foundations-apt-multifactor', 'APT is fundamentally based on which principle?', 'Mean-variance optimization', 'No-arbitrage', 'Purchasing power parity', 'The efficient market hypothesis in strong form', 'B', 'APT rests on the absence of arbitrage opportunities rather than the restrictive assumptions underlying the CAPM.', 1),
  ('frm-foundations-apt-multifactor-q03', 'frm-foundations-apt-multifactor', 'In an APT model, a ''factor loading'' (beta) represents the asset''s:', 'Total volatility', 'Sensitivity to a particular systematic factor', 'Idiosyncratic risk', 'Expected return', 'B', 'Each factor loading measures how sensitive the asset''s return is to movements in the associated systematic risk factor.', 2),
  ('frm-foundations-apt-multifactor-q04', 'frm-foundations-apt-multifactor', 'A notable limitation of APT relative to the CAPM is that APT:', 'Cannot price any assets', 'Does not specify what the relevant factors are', 'Requires a single factor only', 'Assumes no systematic risk', 'B', 'APT does not identify the factors or their number; they must be determined empirically or by theory.', 3),
  ('frm-foundations-apt-multifactor-q05', 'frm-foundations-apt-multifactor', 'The CAPM can be viewed as a special case of APT in which:', 'There are no factors', 'The only priced factor is the market portfolio', 'All betas equal zero', 'Returns are risk-free', 'B', 'When the single priced systematic factor is the market excess return, APT reduces to the CAPM.', 4),
  ('frm-foundations-apt-multifactor-q06', 'frm-foundations-apt-multifactor', 'The Fama-French three-factor model adds which two factors to the market factor?', 'Momentum and liquidity', 'Size (SMB) and value (HML)', 'Inflation and GDP', 'Credit and term spreads', 'B', 'The Fama-French three-factor model augments the market factor with size (small minus big) and value (high minus low book-to-market).', 5),
  ('frm-foundations-apt-multifactor-q07', 'frm-foundations-apt-multifactor', 'A multifactor model used to attribute a fund''s return to its exposures is performing:', 'Return (performance) attribution', 'Covered interest arbitrage', 'Duration matching', 'Scenario analysis', 'A', 'Decomposing realized returns into factor exposures times factor returns is performance/return attribution.', 6),
  ('frm-foundations-apt-multifactor-q08', 'frm-foundations-apt-multifactor', 'Which is a commonly cited MACROECONOMIC factor in multifactor return models?', 'Book-to-market ratio', 'Firm market capitalization', 'Unexpected changes in inflation', 'Dividend payout ratio', 'C', 'Macroeconomic factor models use economic surprises such as unexpected inflation, GDP/production, term-structure shifts, and credit spreads.', 7),
  ('frm-foundations-apt-multifactor-q09', 'frm-foundations-apt-multifactor', 'Under APT, an asset''s expected return in excess of the risk-free rate equals the sum of its:', 'Idiosyncratic variances', 'Factor sensitivities each multiplied by the corresponding factor risk premium', 'Historical average returns', 'Dividends', 'B', 'Expected excess return = Σ (factor beta × factor risk premium) across the systematic factors.', 8),
  ('frm-foundations-apt-multifactor-q10', 'frm-foundations-apt-multifactor', 'If an asset has betas of 0.5 to factor 1 (premium 4%) and 1.5 to factor 2 (premium 2%), with R_f = 3%, its APT expected return is:', '8%', '9%', '11%', '5%', 'A', 'E[R] = 3% + 0.5×4% + 1.5×2% = 3% + 2% + 3% = 8%.', 9),
  ('frm-foundations-apt-multifactor-q11', 'frm-foundations-apt-multifactor', 'Fundamental factor models (e.g., Fama-French) typically define factors using:', 'Macroeconomic surprises', 'Firm characteristics such as size and value', 'Central bank policy rates', 'Currency exchange rates', 'B', 'Fundamental factor models build factors from observable firm attributes (size, value, momentum, profitability, etc.).', 10),
  ('frm-foundations-apt-multifactor-q12', 'frm-foundations-apt-multifactor', 'A risk manager wanting to hedge a portfolio''s exposure to a single factor while keeping others would use a multifactor model to:', 'Eliminate all returns', 'Isolate and offset that factor''s beta', 'Maximize idiosyncratic risk', 'Remove the risk-free rate', 'B', 'Multifactor models let a manager identify the target factor''s loading and take an offsetting position to neutralize just that exposure.', 11),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-time-series-q01', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-time-series', 'A covariance-stationary time series has:', 'A mean and variance that change over time', 'Constant mean, constant variance, and autocovariance depending only on lag', 'Zero autocorrelation at all lags', 'A unit root', 'B', 'Covariance stationarity requires a constant mean and variance and an autocovariance that depends only on the lag between observations.', 0),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-time-series-q02', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-time-series', 'An AR(1) process x_t = c + φ·x_{t−1} + ε_t is covariance-stationary if:', '|φ| < 1', 'φ = 1', 'φ > 1', 'c = 0', 'A', 'Stationarity of an AR(1) requires the autoregressive coefficient to satisfy |φ| < 1; at φ = 1 it becomes a non-stationary random walk.', 1),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-time-series-q03', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-time-series', 'A random walk is an example of a process that:', 'Is covariance-stationary', 'Has a unit root and is non-stationary', 'Always mean-reverts quickly', 'Has zero variance', 'B', 'A random walk has a unit root (φ = 1); its variance grows without bound over time, so it is non-stationary.', 2),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-time-series-q04', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-time-series', 'The Augmented Dickey-Fuller test is used to test for:', 'Heteroskedasticity', 'A unit root (non-stationarity)', 'Normality of residuals', 'Multicollinearity', 'B', 'The (Augmented) Dickey-Fuller test checks the null hypothesis that a series contains a unit root, i.e., is non-stationary.', 3),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-time-series-q05', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-time-series', 'In a moving average MA(q) model, the current value is a function of:', 'Past values of the variable itself', 'Current and past random shocks (error terms)', 'Future expected values', 'The deterministic trend only', 'B', 'An MA(q) process expresses the current observation as a linear combination of the current and q past error (shock) terms.', 4),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-time-series-q06', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-time-series', 'A common way to make a non-stationary series with a unit root stationary is to:', 'Square the series', 'Take first differences', 'Add a constant', 'Multiply by its lag', 'B', 'Differencing (e.g., first differences) typically removes a unit root and yields a stationary series—the ''I'' (integrated) step in ARIMA.', 5),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-time-series-q07', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-time-series', 'For an AR(1) with c = 2 and φ = 0.6, the long-run (unconditional) mean is:', '2.0', '3.3', '5.0', '1.2', 'C', 'The mean of a stationary AR(1) is c/(1 − φ) = 2 / (1 − 0.6) = 2/0.4 = 5.', 6),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-time-series-q08', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-time-series', 'Lower values of the Akaike (AIC) or Bayesian (BIC) information criteria indicate:', 'A worse model fit/parsimony trade-off', 'A preferred model balancing fit and complexity', 'Non-stationarity', 'Perfect forecasts', 'B', 'AIC/BIC penalize complexity; lower values indicate a better balance of fit and parsimony, guiding lag-order selection.', 7),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-time-series-q09', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-time-series', 'After fitting a time-series model, residuals should ideally resemble:', 'A strong upward trend', 'White noise with no remaining autocorrelation', 'A random walk', 'A perfect sine wave', 'B', 'Well-specified models leave residuals that are white noise—no significant remaining autocorrelation (checked with tests like Ljung-Box).', 8),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-time-series-q10', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-time-series', 'An ARMA(p, q) model combines:', 'Autoregressive and moving-average components', 'Two moving-average components', 'A unit root and a trend', 'GARCH and EWMA', 'A', 'ARMA(p, q) blends an autoregressive part (AR, order p) with a moving-average part (MA, order q).', 9),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-time-series-q11', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-time-series', 'The ''I'' in an ARIMA model refers to:', 'Integration order, i.e., the number of times the series is differenced', 'The intercept', 'The information ratio', 'Instrumental variables', 'A', 'ARIMA''s ''I'' denotes the degree of integration—how many times the series is differenced to achieve stationarity.', 10),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-time-series-q12', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-time-series', 'Regressing two independent non-stationary series can produce a ''spurious regression'', characterized by:', 'A low R² and insignificant coefficients', 'A high R² and significant coefficients despite no true relationship', 'Perfectly stationary residuals', 'Zero estimated slope', 'B', 'Spurious regression yields a deceptively high R² and significant t-stats between unrelated trending (non-stationary) series; differencing or cointegration analysis is needed.', 11),
  ('frm-financial-markets-market-structure-q01', 'frm-financial-markets-market-structure', 'Compared with OTC forwards, exchange-traded futures are:', 'Customized and bilaterally cleared', 'Standardized and cleared through a central counterparty', 'Free of margin requirements', 'Subject to higher counterparty credit risk', 'B', 'Futures are standardized contracts cleared by a central counterparty (clearinghouse), which sharply reduces counterparty credit risk via margining.', 0),
  ('frm-financial-markets-market-structure-q02', 'frm-financial-markets-market-structure', 'Through ''novation'', a central counterparty (CCP):', 'Eliminates all market risk', 'Becomes the buyer to every seller and the seller to every buyer', 'Guarantees a profit to members', 'Removes the need for margin', 'B', 'Novation interposes the CCP between the original parties, making it counterparty to both sides of each cleared trade.', 1),
  ('frm-financial-markets-market-structure-q03', 'frm-financial-markets-market-structure', 'Initial margin posted to a CCP is designed primarily to cover:', 'Daily realized mark-to-market gains and losses', 'Potential future exposure over the close-out period', 'The clearing fee', 'Dividends owed', 'B', 'Initial margin covers potential future exposure—the possible adverse move during the time needed to close out a defaulter''s positions—while variation margin settles daily P&L.', 2),
  ('frm-financial-markets-market-structure-q04', 'frm-financial-markets-market-structure', 'Variation margin is exchanged to reflect:', 'Potential future exposure', 'Daily changes in the mark-to-market value of positions', 'The default fund contribution', 'The CCP''s capital', 'B', 'Variation margin transfers the daily mark-to-market gains/losses between counterparties, keeping current exposure near zero.', 3),
  ('frm-financial-markets-market-structure-q05', 'frm-financial-markets-market-structure', 'In a CCP default waterfall, which resource is typically used FIRST when a member defaults?', 'The mutualized default fund of non-defaulting members', 'The CCP''s own capital (''skin in the game'')', 'The defaulting member''s own margin and default-fund contribution', 'Central bank funding', 'C', 'The defaulter''s own posted margin and default-fund contribution are consumed first, before the CCP''s capital and the mutualized fund.', 4),
  ('frm-financial-markets-market-structure-q06', 'frm-financial-markets-market-structure', 'A key benefit of central clearing is multilateral netting, which:', 'Increases gross exposures', 'Reduces total counterparty exposures across members', 'Eliminates market risk', 'Removes the need for a default fund', 'B', 'By netting offsetting positions across all members, a CCP greatly reduces aggregate counterparty exposures compared with bilateral trading.', 5),
  ('frm-financial-markets-market-structure-q07', 'frm-financial-markets-market-structure', 'A frequently cited drawback of mandatory central clearing is that CCPs:', 'Have no risk management', 'Concentrate risk, making a CCP failure potentially systemic', 'Increase customization of contracts', 'Remove the need for any margin', 'B', 'Clearing concentrates exposures in a few CCPs, so a CCP failure could be a systemic event—hence stringent margin and default-fund requirements.', 6),
  ('frm-financial-markets-market-structure-q08', 'frm-financial-markets-market-structure', 'Non-centrally-cleared OTC derivatives generally require dealers to compute a ____ to reflect counterparty default risk.', 'Credit valuation adjustment (CVA)', 'Sharpe ratio', 'Duration', 'Tracking error', 'A', 'Bilateral OTC trades carry counterparty risk, captured via a credit valuation adjustment (CVA) to the derivative''s value.', 7),
  ('frm-financial-markets-market-structure-q09', 'frm-financial-markets-market-structure', 'The push toward central clearing of standardized OTC derivatives accelerated after:', 'The dot-com bust of 2000', 'The 2007-2009 global financial crisis', 'The 2020 oil price collapse', 'The introduction of the euro', 'B', 'Post-2008 reforms (e.g., Dodd-Frank, EMIR) mandated central clearing of standardized OTC derivatives to reduce systemic counterparty risk.', 8),
  ('frm-financial-markets-market-structure-q10', 'frm-financial-markets-market-structure', 'Relative to exchange-traded contracts, OTC contracts offer the advantage of:', 'Greater standardization', 'Customization of terms to the counterparties'' needs', 'Zero counterparty risk', 'Daily settlement through a clearinghouse', 'B', 'OTC contracts can be tailored (notional, maturity, underlying) to specific hedging needs, unlike standardized exchange contracts.', 9),
  ('frm-financial-markets-market-structure-q11', 'frm-financial-markets-market-structure', 'The CCP ''guarantee fund'' (default fund) is primarily funded by:', 'Taxpayers', 'Contributions from clearing members', 'The central bank', 'Retail investors', 'B', 'Clearing members contribute to a mutualized default (guarantee) fund that can absorb losses exceeding a defaulter''s own resources.', 10),
  ('frm-financial-markets-market-structure-q12', 'frm-financial-markets-market-structure', '''Wrong-way risk'' in a bilateral OTC trade arises when:', 'Exposure to a counterparty rises as that counterparty''s credit quality deteriorates', 'Margin is posted daily', 'The trade is centrally cleared', 'Volatility is constant', 'A', 'Wrong-way risk is the adverse correlation where exposure increases just as the counterparty becomes more likely to default.', 11),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-volatility-smiles-q01', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-volatility-smiles', 'The existence of a volatility smile indicates that the Black-Scholes assumption of:', 'Constant volatility across strikes does not hold in practice', 'A risk-free rate is incorrect', 'European exercise is invalid', 'Zero dividends is required', 'A', 'A smile shows implied volatility varies with strike, contradicting Black-Scholes'' single constant-volatility assumption.', 0),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-volatility-smiles-q02', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-volatility-smiles', 'Equity index options typically exhibit a volatility ''skew'' in which implied volatility is:', 'Higher for high-strike (OTM call) options', 'Higher for low-strike (OTM put) options', 'Identical across all strikes', 'Zero at the money', 'B', 'Equity options show a skew/smirk with higher implied vol at low strikes, reflecting crash fear and the leverage effect.', 1),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-volatility-smiles-q03', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-volatility-smiles', 'A symmetric volatility smile (high IV at both wings) is most characteristic of which market?', 'Equity index options', 'Currency (FX) options', 'Single-stock options only', 'Zero-coupon bonds', 'B', 'FX options often show a roughly symmetric smile because large moves are plausible in both directions, implying fat tails on both sides.', 2),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-volatility-smiles-q04', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-volatility-smiles', 'A volatility smile implies that the market''s risk-neutral distribution of the underlying has:', 'Thinner tails than the lognormal', 'Fatter tails than the lognormal', 'Zero variance', 'No skewness or kurtosis', 'B', 'Higher implied vols in the wings price greater probability of extreme moves, implying fatter tails than the Black-Scholes lognormal.', 3),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-volatility-smiles-q05', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-volatility-smiles', 'A volatility surface plots implied volatility against:', 'Strike and time to maturity', 'Delta and gamma', 'Price and volume', 'Interest rate and dividend yield', 'A', 'The volatility surface shows implied volatility as a function of both strike (moneyness) and maturity.', 4),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-volatility-smiles-q06', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-volatility-smiles', 'The equity volatility skew is often attributed to the ''leverage effect'', whereby a fall in equity price:', 'Decreases the firm''s leverage and volatility', 'Increases the firm''s leverage and thus volatility', 'Has no effect on volatility', 'Increases dividends', 'B', 'As equity prices fall, financial leverage rises, raising equity volatility—contributing to higher implied vol at lower strikes.', 5),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-volatility-smiles-q07', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-volatility-smiles', 'Volatility smiles tend to become ____ as option maturity increases.', 'Steeper', 'Flatter', 'Inverted', 'Undefined', 'B', 'Smiles generally flatten with longer maturities, as relative differences in implied vol across strikes diminish.', 6),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-volatility-smiles-q08', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-volatility-smiles', 'Pricing a deep out-of-the-money option with a single at-the-money implied volatility would most likely:', 'Correctly price the option', 'Misprice it because the smile assigns wings a different volatility', 'Always overprice it', 'Eliminate the need for the smile', 'B', 'Because the smile gives wing strikes different implied vols, using a flat ATM vol misprices OTM/ITM options.', 7),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-volatility-smiles-q09', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-volatility-smiles', 'Empirically, the appearance of pronounced equity volatility skews became prominent after:', 'The 1987 stock market crash', 'The introduction of the euro', 'The 2000 dot-com peak only', 'There has never been a skew', 'A', 'Persistent equity skews emerged after the 1987 crash heightened market awareness of large downside moves.', 8),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-volatility-smiles-q10', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-volatility-smiles', 'Jumps and stochastic volatility in the underlying help explain volatility smiles because they create:', 'A perfectly lognormal distribution', 'Heavier tails and skewness not captured by constant-volatility Black-Scholes', 'Zero implied volatility', 'Negative option prices', 'B', 'Jump and stochastic-volatility dynamics produce fat tails/skew, generating the implied-volatility patterns observed as smiles/skews.', 9),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-volatility-smiles-q11', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-volatility-smiles', 'Implied volatility extracted from the smile is best described as:', 'A backward-looking historical estimate', 'The market''s forward-looking volatility expectation embedded in option prices', 'Always equal to realized volatility', 'Independent of option prices', 'B', 'Implied volatility is derived from current option prices and reflects the market''s forward-looking volatility expectation.', 10),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-volatility-smiles-q12', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-volatility-smiles', 'A trader observing a steep put skew in equity options would infer the market is pricing in:', 'A high probability of large upside moves', 'A high probability of large downside moves', 'No directional bias', 'Zero volatility', 'B', 'A steep put (low-strike) skew indicates elevated demand/pricing for downside protection, i.e., greater perceived crash risk.', 11),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-management-capm-q09', 'frm-foundations-risk-management-capm', 'In the CAPM, the only priced (rewarded) risk is:', 'Total volatility', 'Systematic (market) risk', 'Idiosyncratic risk', 'Liquidity risk', 'B', 'CAPM rewards only non-diversifiable systematic risk measured by beta; idiosyncratic risk is diversified away.', 8),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-management-capm-q10', 'frm-foundations-risk-management-capm', 'The security market line (SML) plots expected return against:', 'Standard deviation', 'Beta', 'Variance', 'Tracking error', 'B', 'The SML expresses the linear relationship between expected return and beta under the CAPM.', 9),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-management-capm-q11', 'frm-foundations-risk-management-capm', 'A stock plotting above the SML is best described as:', 'Overvalued', 'Undervalued (offers excess return for its beta)', 'Fairly valued', 'Risk-free', 'B', 'Above the SML the asset offers more return than its beta requires, indicating it is underpriced.', 10),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-management-capm-q12', 'frm-foundations-risk-management-capm', 'The Sharpe ratio measures excess return per unit of:', 'Beta', 'Total risk (standard deviation)', 'Systematic risk', 'Tracking error', 'B', 'The Sharpe ratio divides excess return by total volatility, unlike the Treynor ratio which uses beta.', 11),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-types-taxonomy-q09', 'frm-foundations-risk-types-taxonomy', 'Market, credit, liquidity, and operational risk are examples of:', 'The major financial-risk categories', 'Non-financial regulation', 'Accounting standards', 'Tax categories', 'A', 'These are the core risk-type categories a firm''s framework must identify and manage.', 8),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-types-taxonomy-q10', 'frm-foundations-risk-types-taxonomy', 'The risk that a counterparty fails to meet an obligation is:', 'Market risk', 'Credit risk', 'Operational risk', 'Reputational risk', 'B', 'Credit (default) risk is the risk of loss from a counterparty failing to perform.', 9),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-types-taxonomy-q11', 'frm-foundations-risk-types-taxonomy', 'Losses from failed internal processes, people, or systems are classified as:', 'Operational risk', 'Market risk', 'Credit risk', 'Liquidity risk', 'A', 'Operational risk covers failures of processes, people, systems, and external events.', 10),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-types-taxonomy-q12', 'frm-foundations-risk-types-taxonomy', 'Business and reputational risks are best described as:', 'Always quantifiable like market risk', 'Harder-to-quantify risks affecting strategy and franchise value', 'Forms of credit risk', 'Irrelevant to risk management', 'B', 'Business/strategic and reputational risks affect long-run viability but are difficult to quantify precisely.', 11),
  ('frm-foundations-corporate-governance-q09', 'frm-foundations-corporate-governance', 'The board''s risk committee is primarily responsible for:', 'Day-to-day trading', 'Overseeing the risk framework and tolerance', 'Marketing', 'External audit fees', 'B', 'The board (often via a risk committee) sets and oversees risk appetite and the governance framework.', 8),
  ('frm-foundations-corporate-governance-q10', 'frm-foundations-corporate-governance', 'A key principle of sound governance is separation between:', 'Risk-taking and risk-control functions', 'Marketing and sales', 'IT and HR', 'Audit and tax', 'A', 'Independent risk control from the front office prevents conflicts and unchecked risk-taking.', 9),
  ('frm-foundations-corporate-governance-q11', 'frm-foundations-corporate-governance', 'The Chief Risk Officer (CRO) should ideally report to:', 'The head trader', 'The board/CEO with independence from business lines', 'Sales', 'The external auditor', 'B', 'An independent CRO reporting to the board/CEO preserves objectivity over business units.', 10),
  ('frm-foundations-corporate-governance-q12', 'frm-foundations-corporate-governance', 'Aligning management incentives with long-term risk is achieved partly through:', 'Deferred, clawback-eligible compensation', 'Immediate cash-only bonuses', 'Removing all oversight', 'Higher leverage', 'A', 'Deferral and clawbacks discourage short-term risk-taking that harms the firm later.', 11),
  ('frm-foundations-erp-risk-appetite-q09', 'frm-foundations-erp-risk-appetite', 'A risk appetite statement defines:', 'The amount and type of risk the firm is willing to take', 'The marketing budget', 'Quarterly earnings', 'The dividend policy', 'A', 'Risk appetite articulates the level and kinds of risk acceptable in pursuit of objectives.', 8),
  ('frm-foundations-erp-risk-appetite-q10', 'frm-foundations-erp-risk-appetite', 'Enterprise risk management (ERM) is characterized by:', 'Siloed, department-by-department risk', 'An integrated, firm-wide view of risk', 'Ignoring correlations', 'Only market risk', 'B', 'ERM takes a holistic, enterprise-wide approach aggregating risks against the firm''s appetite.', 9),
  ('frm-foundations-erp-risk-appetite-q11', 'frm-foundations-erp-risk-appetite', 'Risk capacity differs from risk appetite in that capacity is:', 'The maximum risk the firm can bear before distress', 'Always equal to appetite', 'A marketing metric', 'Lower than appetite', 'A', 'Capacity is the absolute maximum a firm can absorb; appetite is the chosen level within that capacity.', 10),
  ('frm-foundations-erp-risk-appetite-q12', 'frm-foundations-erp-risk-appetite', 'Risk limits translate appetite into:', 'Operational constraints on desks and exposures', 'Marketing targets', 'Tax filings', 'Audit schedules', 'A', 'Limits cascade the firm''s appetite down to actionable constraints on positions and activities.', 11),
  ('frm-foundations-financial-disasters-q09', 'frm-foundations-financial-disasters', 'The collapse of LTCM (1998) primarily illustrates the dangers of:', 'Excessive leverage and liquidity/model risk', 'Too little leverage', 'Avoiding derivatives', 'Overdiversification', 'A', 'LTCM''s huge leverage and convergence bets failed when liquidity dried up and correlations spiked.', 8),
  ('frm-foundations-financial-disasters-q10', 'frm-foundations-financial-disasters', 'The Barings Bank failure (1995) is a classic case of:', 'Weak operational controls and rogue trading', 'Sovereign default', 'Inflation risk', 'Currency pegs', 'A', 'Nick Leeson''s unchecked, concealed positions reflected a breakdown in operational controls and segregation of duties.', 9),
  ('frm-foundations-financial-disasters-q11', 'frm-foundations-financial-disasters', 'The 2007–09 crisis highlighted the systemic danger of:', 'Subprime securitization, leverage, and funding liquidity', 'Low housing prices', 'Strong capital buffers', 'Conservative lending', 'A', 'Leveraged exposure to subprime-backed securities plus funding-liquidity runs propagated systemic losses.', 10),
  ('frm-foundations-financial-disasters-q12', 'frm-foundations-financial-disasters', 'A common lesson across financial disasters is the need for:', 'Independent risk oversight and stress testing of tail events', 'More leverage', 'Less disclosure', 'Ignoring liquidity', 'A', 'Recurring themes include weak oversight, underestimating tail/liquidity risk, and poor incentives.', 11),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-volatility-ewma-garch-q09', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-volatility-ewma-garch', 'EWMA gives more weight to:', 'Older observations', 'More recent observations', 'All observations equally', 'The mean only', 'B', 'Exponentially weighted moving average places greater weight on recent data via the decay factor λ.', 8),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-volatility-ewma-garch-q10', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-volatility-ewma-garch', 'A higher λ (lambda) in EWMA implies:', 'Faster reaction to new data', 'Slower decay and smoother, more persistent volatility', 'No weighting', 'Random weights', 'B', 'A λ near 1 decays slowly, giving smoother volatility that reacts less abruptly to shocks.', 9),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-volatility-ewma-garch-q11', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-volatility-ewma-garch', 'GARCH(1,1) adds which feature versus EWMA?', 'Mean reversion to a long-run variance', 'No persistence', 'Equal weights', 'No recent data', 'A', 'GARCH(1,1) includes a constant term producing mean reversion to a long-run average variance; EWMA does not.', 10),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-volatility-ewma-garch-q12', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-volatility-ewma-garch', 'In GARCH(1,1), the sum of the ARCH and GARCH coefficients (α+β) measures:', 'Volatility persistence', 'The mean return', 'Skewness', 'The risk-free rate', 'A', 'α+β close to 1 indicates highly persistent volatility (slow mean reversion); it must be < 1 for stationarity.', 11),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-probability-distributions-q09', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-probability-distributions', 'The binomial distribution models:', 'Number of successes in n independent trials', 'Continuous returns', 'Time between events', 'Infinite outcomes', 'A', 'The binomial counts successes across n independent Bernoulli trials with fixed probability p.', 8),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-probability-distributions-q10', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-probability-distributions', 'A distribution with positive excess kurtosis is:', 'Thin-tailed', 'Fat-tailed (leptokurtic)', 'Always symmetric', 'Uniform', 'B', 'Positive excess kurtosis means fatter tails than the normal, implying more extreme outcomes.', 9),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-probability-distributions-q11', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-probability-distributions', 'Negative skewness implies:', 'A longer left tail (large losses more likely than symmetric)', 'A longer right tail', 'No tail', 'Zero variance', 'A', 'Negative skew has a longer left tail, a concern in risk because of larger-than-normal losses.', 10),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-probability-distributions-q12', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-probability-distributions', 'The Student''s t-distribution is often preferred for returns because it:', 'Has fatter tails than the normal', 'Has no variance', 'Is always skewed', 'Ignores extreme events', 'A', 'The t-distribution''s fatter tails better capture the frequency of extreme financial returns.', 11),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-statistical-inference-q09', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-statistical-inference', 'A Type I error in hypothesis testing is:', 'Rejecting a true null hypothesis', 'Accepting a true null', 'A data error', 'Failing to reject a false null', 'A', 'Type I (false positive) is rejecting a null that is actually true; its probability is the significance level α.', 8),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-statistical-inference-q10', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-statistical-inference', 'The p-value is:', 'The probability of the observed (or more extreme) result if the null is true', 'The probability the null is true', 'The sample mean', 'The variance', 'A', 'A small p-value means the data would be unlikely under the null, supporting rejection.', 9),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-statistical-inference-q11', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-statistical-inference', 'A 95% confidence interval that excludes zero suggests the parameter is:', 'Statistically significant at the 5% level', 'Always zero', 'Insignificant', 'Negative', 'A', 'If zero lies outside the 95% interval, the estimate is significant at the 5% level.', 10),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-statistical-inference-q12', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-statistical-inference', 'The power of a test is:', '1 minus the probability of a Type II error', 'The significance level', 'The sample size', 'The mean', 'A', 'Power = 1 − β, the probability of correctly rejecting a false null hypothesis.', 11),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-regression-q09', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-regression', 'R-squared in a regression measures:', 'The fraction of variance in Y explained by the model', 'The slope', 'The intercept', 'The residual mean', 'A', 'R² is the proportion of the dependent variable''s variance explained by the regressors.', 8),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-regression-q10', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-regression', 'Heteroskedasticity refers to:', 'Non-constant variance of the error term', 'Correlated regressors', 'A perfect fit', 'Zero mean errors', 'A', 'Heteroskedasticity is error variance that changes across observations, biasing standard errors.', 9),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-regression-q11', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-regression', 'Multicollinearity among independent variables tends to:', 'Inflate coefficient standard errors', 'Lower R²', 'Remove all variance', 'Guarantee causation', 'A', 'High correlation among regressors makes individual coefficients imprecise (large standard errors).', 10),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-regression-q12', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-regression', 'Adjusted R-squared differs from R-squared by:', 'Penalizing the addition of explanatory variables', 'Always being larger', 'Ignoring the sample', 'Measuring the slope', 'A', 'Adjusted R² penalizes extra regressors, so it can fall when an added variable adds little explanatory power.', 11),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-monte-carlo-q09', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-monte-carlo', 'Monte Carlo simulation estimates risk by:', 'Generating many random scenarios from an assumed model', 'Using only historical data', 'Assuming normality always', 'A single deterministic path', 'A', 'Monte Carlo draws many random paths from a specified stochastic process to build an outcome distribution.', 8),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-monte-carlo-q10', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-monte-carlo', 'The standard error of a Monte Carlo estimate falls in proportion to:', '1/√N (square root of simulations)', '1/N²', 'N', 'The mean', 'A', 'Monte Carlo accuracy improves with the square root of the number of trials, so 4× trials halve the error.', 9),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-monte-carlo-q11', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-monte-carlo', 'Variance-reduction techniques (e.g., antithetic variates) are used to:', 'Improve accuracy for a given number of simulations', 'Increase model risk', 'Add bias', 'Slow convergence', 'A', 'Techniques like antithetic and control variates reduce sampling variance, improving efficiency.', 10),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-monte-carlo-q12', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-monte-carlo', 'A key drawback of Monte Carlo VaR is:', 'It is computationally intensive and model-dependent', 'It cannot handle options', 'It needs no assumptions', 'It is always exact', 'A', 'Monte Carlo is flexible but slow and only as good as the assumed model (model risk).', 11),
  ('frm-financial-markets-futures-forwards-hedging-q09', 'frm-financial-markets-futures-forwards-hedging', 'Compared with forwards, futures contracts are:', 'Standardized and exchange-traded with daily margining', 'Always customized', 'Free of margin', 'Bilateral only', 'A', 'Futures are standardized, cleared, and marked-to-market daily; forwards are customized OTC contracts.', 8),
  ('frm-financial-markets-futures-forwards-hedging-q10', 'frm-financial-markets-futures-forwards-hedging', 'The minimum-variance hedge ratio equals:', 'The correlation times the ratio of spot to futures volatility', 'Always 1.0', 'The risk-free rate', 'The coupon', 'A', 'h* = ρ × (σ_spot/σ_futures), minimizing the variance of the hedged position.', 9),
  ('frm-financial-markets-futures-forwards-hedging-q11', 'frm-financial-markets-futures-forwards-hedging', 'Basis risk in a futures hedge arises because:', 'Spot and futures prices may not move together perfectly', 'Margin is posted', 'Contracts expire', 'Rates are zero', 'A', 'Basis = spot − futures; its variability leaves residual risk in an otherwise hedged position.', 10),
  ('frm-financial-markets-futures-forwards-hedging-q12', 'frm-financial-markets-futures-forwards-hedging', 'Contango describes a market where futures prices are:', 'Above the current spot price', 'Below spot', 'Equal to spot', 'Negative', 'A', 'Contango is an upward-sloping futures curve (futures above spot); backwardation is the reverse.', 11),
  ('frm-financial-markets-options-pricing-q09', 'frm-financial-markets-options-pricing', 'Put-call parity relates the prices of a call, a put, the underlying, and:', 'A risk-free bond (PV of the strike)', 'A dividend index', 'A currency forward', 'A swap', 'A', 'Put-call parity: C + PV(K) = P + S, linking the two options, the stock, and a bond.', 8),
  ('frm-financial-markets-options-pricing-q10', 'frm-financial-markets-options-pricing', 'An increase in volatility raises the value of:', 'Both calls and puts', 'Only calls', 'Only puts', 'Neither', 'A', 'Higher volatility increases the value of both calls and puts (positive vega).', 9),
  ('frm-financial-markets-options-pricing-q11', 'frm-financial-markets-options-pricing', 'The delta of a deep in-the-money call approaches:', '1.0', '0.0', '−1.0', '0.5', 'A', 'A deep ITM call behaves like the underlying, so its delta tends toward 1.0.', 10),
  ('frm-financial-markets-options-pricing-q12', 'frm-financial-markets-options-pricing', 'Gamma is largest for options that are:', 'At-the-money near expiration', 'Deep in-the-money', 'Deep out-of-the-money', 'Long-dated', 'A', 'Gamma peaks for at-the-money options close to expiry, where delta changes most rapidly.', 11),
  ('frm-financial-markets-swaps-q09', 'frm-financial-markets-swaps', 'In a plain-vanilla interest-rate swap, the parties exchange:', 'Fixed for floating interest payments', 'Principal amounts only', 'Equity for bonds', 'Currencies', 'A', 'A vanilla IRS swaps fixed-rate for floating-rate cash flows on a notional principal.', 8),
  ('frm-financial-markets-swaps-q10', 'frm-financial-markets-swaps', 'At inception, the value of a par interest-rate swap is approximately:', 'Zero', 'The notional', 'The fixed coupon', 'Negative', 'A', 'A par swap is priced so the fixed and floating legs have equal value, giving zero initial value.', 9),
  ('frm-financial-markets-swaps-q11', 'frm-financial-markets-swaps', 'A currency swap differs from an interest-rate swap by exchanging:', 'Principal and interest in two different currencies', 'Only fixed rates', 'Equity returns', 'Nothing', 'A', 'Currency swaps exchange principal and interest in two currencies, typically including principal exchange.', 10),
  ('frm-financial-markets-swaps-q12', 'frm-financial-markets-swaps', 'Swap counterparty credit exposure is largest:', 'During the life of the swap as rates move (mid-life peak)', 'Only at inception', 'Never', 'Only on the notional at maturity', 'A', 'Expected exposure on an IRS typically peaks mid-life, balancing rate diffusion against remaining payments.', 11),
  ('frm-financial-markets-fixed-income-products-q09', 'frm-financial-markets-fixed-income-products', 'The price of a bond and its yield are:', 'Inversely related', 'Directly related', 'Unrelated', 'Always equal', 'A', 'Bond prices fall as yields rise and vice versa — the fundamental inverse relationship.', 8),
  ('frm-financial-markets-fixed-income-products-q10', 'frm-financial-markets-fixed-income-products', 'A callable bond is least attractive to investors when rates:', 'Fall (issuer likely to call)', 'Rise', 'Stay flat', 'Are negative', 'A', 'Falling rates make the issuer''s call option valuable, capping the bondholder''s upside.', 9),
  ('frm-financial-markets-fixed-income-products-q11', 'frm-financial-markets-fixed-income-products', 'The credit spread on a corporate bond compensates for:', 'Default and liquidity risk over Treasuries', 'Inflation only', 'Currency risk', 'Nothing', 'A', 'The spread above the risk-free rate compensates for default risk, recovery uncertainty, and liquidity.', 10),
  ('frm-financial-markets-fixed-income-products-q12', 'frm-financial-markets-fixed-income-products', 'A floating-rate note has low interest-rate duration because its coupon:', 'Resets periodically with market rates', 'Is fixed for life', 'Never pays', 'Equals the principal', 'A', 'Because the coupon resets to current rates, an FRN''s price is relatively insensitive to rate changes.', 11),
  ('frm-financial-markets-credit-products-q09', 'frm-financial-markets-credit-products', 'A credit default swap (CDS) buyer:', 'Pays a premium for protection against a reference default', 'Receives a premium', 'Owns the bond', 'Takes no risk', 'A', 'The protection buyer pays periodic premiums and receives compensation if a credit event occurs.', 8),
  ('frm-financial-markets-credit-products-q10', 'frm-financial-markets-credit-products', 'Widening CDS spreads indicate:', 'Deteriorating perceived credit quality', 'Improving credit', 'Lower default risk', 'No change', 'A', 'Higher CDS spreads mean the market demands more for default protection, signaling worse credit.', 9),
  ('frm-financial-markets-credit-products-q11', 'frm-financial-markets-credit-products', 'In a collateralized debt obligation (CDO), the equity (first-loss) tranche:', 'Absorbs initial losses but offers the highest yield', 'Is the safest', 'Has no risk', 'Is senior', 'A', 'The equity tranche takes the first losses in exchange for the highest potential return.', 10),
  ('frm-financial-markets-credit-products-q12', 'frm-financial-markets-credit-products', 'The recovery rate in credit modeling is:', 'The fraction of exposure recovered after default', 'The default probability', 'The coupon', 'The spread', 'A', 'Recovery rate = 1 − loss given default; it scales the loss when a default occurs.', 11),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-value-at-risk-q09', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-value-at-risk', 'A 99% one-day VaR of $5m means:', 'Losses should exceed $5m on ~1% of days', 'Losses never exceed $5m', 'Average loss is $5m', 'Gains are $5m', 'A', 'VaR is a quantile of the loss distribution; here losses exceed $5m about 1% of the time.', 8),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-value-at-risk-q10', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-value-at-risk', 'VaR is criticized for not being a coherent risk measure because it lacks:', 'Subadditivity in general', 'Monotonicity', 'A confidence level', 'A time horizon', 'A', 'VaR can violate subadditivity (diversification), so it is not generally coherent — expected shortfall is.', 9),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-value-at-risk-q11', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-value-at-risk', 'Scaling one-day VaR to a 10-day horizon (i.i.d. normal) uses a factor of:', '√10', '10', '100', '1', 'A', 'Under the square-root-of-time rule, multiply by √T, so √10 for ten days.', 10),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-value-at-risk-q12', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-value-at-risk', 'The main weakness of VaR relative to expected shortfall is that VaR:', 'Ignores the size of losses beyond the threshold', 'Cannot be computed', 'Overstates risk', 'Has no horizon', 'A', 'VaR says nothing about tail severity past the cutoff, which expected shortfall (CVaR) captures.', 11),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-bond-valuation-q09', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-bond-valuation', 'Modified duration approximates the percentage price change for a:', '1% (100bp) change in yield', '1% change in coupon', 'Change in rating', 'Change in maturity', 'A', 'Modified duration estimates the percent price change per unit (100bp) change in yield.', 8),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-bond-valuation-q10', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-bond-valuation', 'Convexity adjustment improves duration estimates because price-yield is:', 'Curved, not linear', 'Perfectly linear', 'Flat', 'Vertical', 'A', 'Duration is a linear approximation; convexity corrects for the curvature of the price-yield relationship.', 9),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-bond-valuation-q11', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-bond-valuation', 'A bond''s DV01 (dollar value of a basis point) measures:', 'Price change for a 1bp yield move', 'The coupon', 'The yield', 'The maturity', 'A', 'DV01 is the dollar change in price for a one-basis-point change in yield, a key hedging measure.', 10),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-bond-valuation-q12', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-bond-valuation', 'Higher convexity is beneficial to a bondholder because it:', 'Gains more when rates fall and loses less when they rise', 'Reduces coupons', 'Increases default risk', 'Lowers price always', 'A', 'Positive convexity produces asymmetric, favorable price responses to yield changes.', 11),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-option-valuation-q09', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-option-valuation', 'The Black-Scholes-Merton model assumes the underlying follows:', 'Geometric Brownian motion with constant volatility', 'A jump process only', 'A linear trend', 'No randomness', 'A', 'BSM assumes lognormal prices (GBM) with constant volatility and continuous trading.', 8),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-option-valuation-q10', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-option-valuation', 'The binomial option model values an option by:', 'Risk-neutral discounting over a price tree', 'Historical averaging', 'Ignoring volatility', 'Using only the strike', 'A', 'The binomial tree uses risk-neutral probabilities and backward induction to value the option.', 9),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-option-valuation-q11', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-option-valuation', 'Implied volatility is the volatility that:', 'Makes the model price equal the market price', 'Equals historical volatility', 'Is always zero', 'Ignores the option price', 'A', 'Implied volatility is backed out of observed option prices via the pricing model.', 10),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-option-valuation-q12', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-option-valuation', 'American options can be worth more than European options because they allow:', 'Early exercise', 'Lower strikes', 'No premium', 'Infinite maturity', 'A', 'The early-exercise right gives American options value at least equal to otherwise identical Europeans.', 11),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-stress-testing-q09', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-stress-testing', 'Stress testing complements VaR by examining:', 'Severe but plausible scenarios beyond normal conditions', 'Only average outcomes', 'Daily P&L only', 'Marketing data', 'A', 'Stress tests assess losses under extreme scenarios that standard VaR may understate.', 8),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-stress-testing-q10', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-stress-testing', 'A historical scenario stress test uses:', 'Actual past crisis episodes (e.g., 2008)', 'Hypothetical only', 'Random noise', 'Average returns', 'A', 'Historical scenarios replay real stress events to gauge portfolio impact.', 9),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-stress-testing-q11', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-stress-testing', 'Reverse stress testing starts from:', 'A defined failure outcome and works back to scenarios that cause it', 'The base case', 'Average returns', 'A single asset', 'A', 'Reverse stress testing identifies the scenarios that would render the firm unviable.', 10),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-stress-testing-q12', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-stress-testing', 'A limitation of stress testing is that it:', 'Depends on the scenarios chosen and may miss unforeseen risks', 'Always predicts the future', 'Requires no judgment', 'Replaces all risk models', 'A', 'Results are only as good as the imagined scenarios; novel shocks can be overlooked.', 11),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-model-risk-q09', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-model-risk', 'Model risk arises primarily from:', 'Incorrect assumptions, errors, or misuse of a model', 'Market moves only', 'Counterparty default', 'Liquidity', 'A', 'Model risk is loss from flawed model specification, implementation errors, or inappropriate application.', 8),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-model-risk-q10', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-model-risk', 'Model validation should be performed by:', 'An independent team separate from the developers', 'The model developers alone', 'Sales', 'No one', 'A', 'Independent validation provides effective challenge and reduces conflicts of interest.', 9),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-model-risk-q11', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-model-risk', 'Calibrating a model to too few data points raises the risk of:', 'Overfitting', 'Underfitting only', 'No risk', 'Lower model risk', 'A', 'Overfitting captures noise rather than signal, producing poor out-of-sample performance.', 10),
  ('frm-valuation-risk-models-model-risk-q12', 'frm-valuation-risk-models-model-risk', 'A common control for model risk is:', 'Benchmarking against alternative models and ongoing monitoring', 'Hiding the model', 'Removing documentation', 'Ignoring outputs', 'A', 'Comparing outputs to alternative approaches and monitoring performance helps catch model failures.', 11),
  ('frm-foundations-garp-code-q01', 'frm-foundations-garp-code', 'The GARP Code of Conduct applies to:', 'Both candidates and certified members', 'Only certified FRMs', 'Only candidates', 'Only employers', 'A', 'The Code binds FRM candidates and certified members alike.', 0),
  ('frm-foundations-garp-code-q02', 'frm-foundations-garp-code', 'Under the Code, conflicts of interest must be:', 'Disclosed and managed', 'Ignored', 'Kept confidential from all', 'Always avoided by resigning', 'A', 'Members must disclose and appropriately manage conflicts of interest.', 1),
  ('frm-foundations-garp-code-q03', 'frm-foundations-garp-code', 'A member who leaks a client''s confidential exposure data violates the principle of:', 'Confidentiality', 'Timeliness', 'Adaptability', 'Diversification', 'A', 'Protecting client and employer information is a core confidentiality duty.', 2),
  ('frm-foundations-garp-code-q04', 'frm-foundations-garp-code', 'Maintaining professional competence is part of a member''s:', 'Fundamental responsibilities', 'Marketing duties', 'Trading limits', 'Tax obligations', 'A', 'Complying with laws and maintaining competence are fundamental responsibilities.', 3),
  ('frm-foundations-garp-code-q05', 'frm-foundations-garp-code', 'A serious violation of the GARP Code can result in:', 'Loss of the FRM designation', 'A cash bonus', 'Automatic promotion', 'No consequence', 'A', 'Violations can lead to loss of the designation and removal from membership.', 4),
  ('frm-foundations-garp-code-q06', 'frm-foundations-garp-code', 'Acting honestly and avoiding conduct that discredits the profession reflects:', 'Professional integrity', 'Model validation', 'Backtesting', 'Netting', 'A', 'Professional integrity and ethical conduct is the first core principle.', 5),
  ('frm-foundations-garp-code-q07', 'frm-foundations-garp-code', 'Following prevailing risk-management standards reflects the principle of:', 'Generally accepted practices', 'Confidentiality', 'Timeliness', 'Anonymity', 'A', 'Members should adhere to generally accepted risk-management practices.', 6),
  ('frm-foundations-garp-code-q08', 'frm-foundations-garp-code', 'An FRM overstating a model''s accuracy to win approval breaches:', 'Professional integrity', 'Timeliness', 'Adaptability', 'Liquidity', 'A', 'Misrepresenting a model violates the duty of integrity and honesty.', 7),
  ('frm-foundations-garp-code-q09', 'frm-foundations-garp-code', 'The GARP Code is most similar in spirit to the:', 'CFA Code of Ethics', 'Basel III accord', 'IFRS standards', 'Tax code', 'A', 'Both codes set professional ethical standards for practitioners.', 8),
  ('frm-foundations-garp-code-q10', 'frm-foundations-garp-code', 'When a personal investment could bias a risk assessment, the member should:', 'Disclose the conflict', 'Hide it', 'Trade more', 'Ignore the assignment', 'A', 'Potential conflicts must be disclosed and managed under the Code.', 9),
  ('frm-foundations-garp-code-q11', 'frm-foundations-garp-code', 'Complying with applicable laws and regulations is:', 'A fundamental responsibility', 'Optional', 'Only for auditors', 'A marketing choice', 'A', 'Legal and regulatory compliance is a fundamental responsibility.', 10),
  ('frm-foundations-garp-code-q12', 'frm-foundations-garp-code', 'Ethics questions in the FRM exam typically test:', 'Applying principles to realistic dilemmas', 'Memorising dates', 'Pricing bonds', 'Coding models', 'A', 'The exam applies the Code''s principles to practical situations.', 11),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-data-q01', 'frm-foundations-risk-data', 'The Basel standard for risk data aggregation is known as:', 'BCBS 239', 'IFRS 9', 'Basel I', 'SOX 404', 'A', 'BCBS 239 sets principles for risk data aggregation and reporting (RDARR).', 0),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-data-q02', 'frm-foundations-risk-data', 'A key motivation for BCBS 239 was banks'' inability during 2008 to:', 'Quickly measure aggregate exposures', 'Pay dividends', 'Hire staff', 'Issue equity', 'A', 'Poor data aggregation meant banks could not rapidly gauge total exposures.', 1),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-data-q03', 'frm-foundations-risk-data', 'The principle that risk data must be reliable and largely automated is:', 'Accuracy and integrity', 'Timeliness', 'Adaptability', 'Confidentiality', 'A', 'Accuracy and integrity require reliable, largely automated aggregation.', 2),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-data-q04', 'frm-foundations-risk-data', 'Capturing all material risks across the group reflects the principle of:', 'Completeness', 'Timeliness', 'Clarity', 'Governance', 'A', 'Completeness means aggregating all material risk exposures firm-wide.', 3),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-data-q05', 'frm-foundations-risk-data', 'Producing aggregated risk data rapidly, especially in stress, reflects:', 'Timeliness', 'Adaptability', 'Accuracy', 'Confidentiality', 'A', 'Timeliness requires fast production of risk data, particularly under stress.', 4),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-data-q06', 'frm-foundations-risk-data', 'Meeting ad-hoc requests and new scenarios reflects the principle of:', 'Adaptability', 'Completeness', 'Integrity', 'Timeliness', 'A', 'Adaptability is the ability to handle ad-hoc and new risk-data requests.', 5),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-data-q07', 'frm-foundations-risk-data', 'Strong data architecture and IT infrastructure fall under:', 'Governance and infrastructure', 'Clarity', 'Distribution', 'Timeliness', 'A', 'Governance and infrastructure underpin effective risk data aggregation.', 6),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-data-q08', 'frm-foundations-risk-data', 'Risk reports should be accurate, comprehensive, clear, useful and:', 'Timely', 'Anonymous', 'Confidential to no one', 'Random', 'A', 'Effective reporting must be timely alongside accuracy, clarity and usefulness.', 7),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-data-q09', 'frm-foundations-risk-data', 'Reconciliation of aggregated data helps ensure:', 'Accuracy and integrity', 'Marketing appeal', 'Lower taxes', 'Higher leverage', 'A', 'Reconciling data sources supports accuracy and integrity.', 8),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-data-q10', 'frm-foundations-risk-data', 'RDARR stands for risk data aggregation and:', 'Risk reporting', 'Return realization', 'Rate recalibration', 'Reserve requirement', 'A', 'RDARR = Risk Data Aggregation and Risk Reporting.', 9),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-data-q11', 'frm-foundations-risk-data', 'Being able to aggregate exposure to a single counterparty quickly supports:', 'Completeness and timeliness', 'Marketing', 'Dividend policy', 'Tax planning', 'A', 'Rapid, complete aggregation of counterparty exposure is a core RDARR goal.', 10),
  ('frm-foundations-risk-data-q12', 'frm-foundations-risk-data', 'BCBS 239 was issued by the:', 'Basel Committee on Banking Supervision', 'SEC', 'IASB', 'GARP', 'A', 'The Basel Committee published BCBS 239 on risk data aggregation.', 11),
  ('frm-foundations-capital-allocation-q01', 'frm-foundations-capital-allocation', 'RAROC measures return relative to:', 'Economic capital', 'Total assets', 'Revenue', 'Headcount', 'A', 'RAROC divides risk-adjusted return by the economic capital at risk.', 0),
  ('frm-foundations-capital-allocation-q02', 'frm-foundations-capital-allocation', 'Economic capital is the capital needed to absorb:', 'Unexpected losses at a confidence level', 'Expected losses only', 'All revenue', 'Dividends', 'A', 'Economic capital covers unexpected losses to a chosen confidence level.', 1),
  ('frm-foundations-capital-allocation-q03', 'frm-foundations-capital-allocation', 'A project adds value when its RAROC is:', 'Above the hurdle rate', 'Below the hurdle rate', 'Equal to zero', 'Negative', 'A', 'RAROC above the hurdle (often the cost of equity) signals value creation.', 2),
  ('frm-foundations-capital-allocation-q04', 'frm-foundations-capital-allocation', 'Expected loss is typically ____ in the RAROC numerator.', 'Subtracted', 'Added', 'Ignored', 'Squared', 'A', 'Expected loss is deducted from returns in the RAROC numerator.', 3),
  ('frm-foundations-capital-allocation-q05', 'frm-foundations-capital-allocation', 'RAROC is commonly used to allocate capital across:', 'Business lines', 'Countries only', 'Auditors', 'Regulators', 'A', 'RAROC ranks business lines by risk-adjusted performance for capital allocation.', 4),
  ('frm-foundations-capital-allocation-q06', 'frm-foundations-capital-allocation', 'Adjusted RAROC (ARAROC) accounts for a business''s:', 'Systematic risk (beta)', 'Headcount', 'Tax rate only', 'Marketing budget', 'A', 'ARAROC adjusts for beta so comparisons reflect systematic risk.', 5),
  ('frm-foundations-capital-allocation-q07', 'frm-foundations-capital-allocation', 'The hurdle rate in RAROC is often the firm''s:', 'Cost of equity', 'Coupon rate', 'Dividend yield', 'Tax rate', 'A', 'The hurdle is typically the cost of equity capital.', 6),
  ('frm-foundations-capital-allocation-q08', 'frm-foundations-capital-allocation', 'RAROC supports transaction pricing by ensuring each deal:', 'Earns enough to cover its risk', 'Ignores risk', 'Maximizes volume only', 'Avoids capital', 'A', 'RAROC-based pricing ensures returns compensate for the risk taken.', 7),
  ('frm-foundations-capital-allocation-q09', 'frm-foundations-capital-allocation', 'A higher economic capital requirement, all else equal, ____ RAROC.', 'Lowers', 'Raises', 'Does not affect', 'Doubles', 'A', 'More capital in the denominator reduces RAROC.', 8),
  ('frm-foundations-capital-allocation-q10', 'frm-foundations-capital-allocation', 'RAROC is a tool for:', 'Risk-adjusted performance measurement', 'Tax filing', 'Marketing', 'Custody', 'A', 'RAROC measures performance adjusted for the risk/capital consumed.', 9),
  ('frm-foundations-capital-allocation-q11', 'frm-foundations-capital-allocation', 'ARAROC is compared against the ____ to judge value creation.', 'Risk-free rate', 'Dividend yield', 'Coupon', 'Inflation only', 'A', 'After the beta adjustment, ARAROC is benchmarked to the risk-free rate.', 10),
  ('frm-foundations-capital-allocation-q12', 'frm-foundations-capital-allocation', 'Incentive compensation tied to RAROC encourages managers to:', 'Consider risk, not just return', 'Maximize volume only', 'Ignore capital', 'Increase leverage without limit', 'A', 'RAROC-linked pay rewards risk-adjusted, capital-efficient performance.', 11),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-hypothesis-testing-q01', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-hypothesis-testing', 'A Type I error is:', 'Rejecting a true null hypothesis', 'Failing to reject a false null', 'Accepting the alternative correctly', 'A correct decision', 'A', 'A Type I error (α) is rejecting H₀ when it is actually true.', 0),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-hypothesis-testing-q02', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-hypothesis-testing', 'A Type II error is:', 'Failing to reject a false null', 'Rejecting a true null', 'A correct rejection', 'Setting alpha', 'A', 'A Type II error (β) is not rejecting H₀ when it is false.', 1),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-hypothesis-testing-q03', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-hypothesis-testing', 'The power of a test equals:', '1 − β', 'α', '1 − α', 'β', 'A', 'Power is the probability of correctly rejecting a false null, 1 − β.', 2),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-hypothesis-testing-q04', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-hypothesis-testing', 'The significance level α is the probability of a:', 'Type I error', 'Type II error', 'Correct decision', 'Wide interval', 'A', 'α is the chosen probability of committing a Type I error.', 3),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-hypothesis-testing-q05', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-hypothesis-testing', 'The null hypothesis is rejected when the p-value is:', 'Less than α', 'Greater than α', 'Equal to 1', 'Negative', 'A', 'Reject H₀ when the p-value falls below the significance level α.', 4),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-hypothesis-testing-q06', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-hypothesis-testing', 'For a small sample with unknown variance, the appropriate distribution is the:', 't-distribution', 'z (normal)', 'chi-square only', 'uniform', 'A', 'The t-distribution applies to small samples with unknown population variance.', 5),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-hypothesis-testing-q07', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-hypothesis-testing', 'A 95% confidence interval equals the estimate plus or minus:', 'Critical value times the standard error', 'The mean', 'The variance', 'The p-value', 'A', 'A CI is the estimate ± (critical value × standard error).', 6),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-hypothesis-testing-q08', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-hypothesis-testing', 'A one-tailed test compared with a two-tailed test at the same α has a:', 'Smaller critical value in the tail of interest', 'Larger p-value always', 'No critical region', 'Zero power', 'A', 'A one-tailed test concentrates α in one tail, giving a smaller critical value there.', 7),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-hypothesis-testing-q09', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-hypothesis-testing', 'Increasing the sample size generally:', 'Narrows the confidence interval', 'Widens it', 'Has no effect', 'Raises α', 'A', 'A larger sample reduces the standard error, narrowing the interval.', 8),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-hypothesis-testing-q10', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-hypothesis-testing', 'Lowering α from 5% to 1%, all else equal, ____ the Type II error rate.', 'Increases', 'Decreases', 'Eliminates', 'Does not affect', 'A', 'A stricter α makes rejection harder, raising the chance of a Type II error.', 9),
  ('frm-quantitative-analysis-hypothesis-testing-q11', 'frm-quantitative-analysis-hypothesis-testing', 'The p-value is the probability of observing a result at least as extreme, assuming:', 'The null hypothesis is true', 'The alternative is true', 'Alpha equals beta', 'The sample is biased', 'A', 'The p-value is computed under the assumption that H₀ holds.', 10),
